AXALL_US_OMB_2025_00478,US_OMB_2025_00478,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,U.S. Office of Management and Budget,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Department of Energy,Science,The understanding of catalytic reactions has been a long-standing challenge due to the complexity and wide range of time scales involved in their mechanisms.,Machine learning for accelerated understanding of dynamic catalysis,Department of Energy; BNL - Brookhaven National Laboratory (SC43 OIM); Science; United States,"The understanding of catalytic reactions has been a long-standing challenge due to the complexity and wide range of time scales involved in their mechanisms. There remain significant gaps in the understanding of how the catalyst's atomistic structure determines the activity of reactions and how it is transiently transformed under varying operating conditions. The proposed effort seeks to take on this challenge with a data-science-driven approach to computational modeling, joining it with advanced experimental methods of characterization to create new methods for capturing realistic complexity of reactions at heterogenous and disordered interfaces. As a prototype application, we will focus on the water gas shift reaction (WGSR) CO + H2O â†’ CO2 +H2, as carried out over an active oxide (ceria CeO2) supported nanoscale Pt cluster catalyst. The Pt/CeO2 system is a high activity, low temperature catalyst for WGSR in which transient catalyst reconstructions and fluxional oscillating behavior",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,"The understanding of catalytic reactions has been a long-standing challenge due to the complexity and wide range of time scales involved in their mechanisms. There remain significant gaps in the understanding of how the catalyst's atomistic structure determines the activity of reactions and how it is transiently transformed under varying operating conditions. The proposed effort seeks to take on this challenge with a data-science-driven approach to computational modeling, joining it with advanced experimental methods of characterization to create new methods for capturing realistic complexity of reactions at heterogenous and disordered interfaces. As a prototype application, we will focus on the water gas shift reaction (WGSR) CO + H2O â†’ CO2 +H2, as carried out over an active oxide (ceria CeO2) supported nanoscale Pt cluster catalyst. The Pt/CeO2 system is a high activity, low temperature catalyst for WGSR in which transient catalyst reconstructions and fluxional oscillating behavior of active sites at the metal-support interface play an essential role.",The understanding of catalytic reactions has been a long-standing challenge due to the complexity and wide range of time scales involved in their mechanisms. There remain significant gaps in the understanding of how the catalyst's atomistic structure,SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PRE_DEPLOYMENT,도입 전,,,https://github.com/ombegov/2025-Federal-Agency-AI-Use-Case-Inventory,DOE-554,U.S. federal public inventory; no separate repository LICENSE found; retain source attribution and review redistribution conditions,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
