AXALL_US_OMB_2025_00965,US_OMB_2025_00965,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,U.S. Office of Management and Budget,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Department of the Interior,Science,UNKNOWN,Predictive AI applications for estimating water quality constituents as causal factors of harmful algal blooms.,Department of the Interior; USGS; Science; United States,"Estimates of water quality constituents: suspended sediment, total nitrogen, total phosphorus, algal pigments, and algal cell abundances.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,Better understanding of the causes of harmful algal blooms in order for localities and state agencies to take proactive action to prevent harmful algal blooms that potentially have impact on life and property.,"Ensemble regressions to predict suspended sediment, total nitrogen, total phosphorus, algal pigments, and algal cell abundances and image-based estimation of suspended sediment concentration via machine learning used in combination with custom developed python code to help build on our correlative understanding of what drives harmful algal blooms and introduces a technique to better understand and attribute causality.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PRE_DEPLOYMENT,도입 전,,,https://github.com/ombegov/2025-Federal-Agency-AI-Use-Case-Inventory,DOI-0143,U.S. federal public inventory; no separate repository LICENSE found; retain source attribution and review redistribution conditions,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_US_OMB_2025_00966,US_OMB_2025_00966,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,U.S. Office of Management and Budget,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Department of the Interior,Science,UNKNOWN,Biotic and abiotic drivers of the prevalence of a tick and associated vector-borne disease,Department of the Interior; USGS; Science; United States,Identify the relative importance of biotic and abiotic factors driving tick emergence.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,Data will be used to inform management and conservation activities in the region and better understand the risk of emerging disease to Arizona wildlife and people.,"Ticks are one of the most important vectors of disease in North America; however, their presence in desert ecosystems is often underestimated. The Gulf Coast tick (Amblyomma maculatum) has been recently discovered in Arizona and has the potential to transmit a bacterial spotted fever (Rickettsia parkeri) to humans and other animals. We do not yet understand why this tick has recently appeared in Arizona.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PRE_DEPLOYMENT,도입 전,,,https://github.com/ombegov/2025-Federal-Agency-AI-Use-Case-Inventory,DOI-0142,U.S. federal public inventory; no separate repository LICENSE found; retain source attribution and review redistribution conditions,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_US_OMB_2025_00967,US_OMB_2025_00967,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,U.S. Office of Management and Budget,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Department of the Interior,Science,UNKNOWN,Enhancing Community and Wildlife Resilience to Sea?Level Rise and Infrastructure Development in the San Pablo Baylands,Department of the Interior; USGS; Science; United States,Wildlife-habitat relationships will be modeled using boosted regression tree or similar machine learning method,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,The goal of our project is to understand the potential impacts of SLR and transportation redesigns on the Baylands and identify management actions that could be taken to achieve desired future habitat and public access targets.,"Considerable public dollars will be invested in both tidal marsh restoration and transportation upgrades in the Baylands; yet the combined and interactive effects of SLR and infrastructure changes on wildlife habitat, local communities, and public access are largely unknown.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PRE_DEPLOYMENT,도입 전,,,https://github.com/ombegov/2025-Federal-Agency-AI-Use-Case-Inventory,DOI-0141,U.S. federal public inventory; no separate repository LICENSE found; retain source attribution and review redistribution conditions,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_US_OMB_2025_00968,US_OMB_2025_00968,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,U.S. Office of Management and Budget,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Department of the Interior,Science,UNKNOWN,Predicting from the past - identifying characteristics of invasion-resistant and invasion-prone waterbodies to aid horizon scanning,Department of the Interior; USGS; Science; United States,Waterbodies vulnerable to invasion,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,AI/ML will provide a decision support tool for managers and policy makers to identify waterbodies vulnerable to invasion by non-native fishes and potential actions that could be implemented to reduce risk of invasion.,"Machine learning and statistical modeling will be used to leverage region-wide waterbody invasion histories and datasets on the physical, biological, chemical, anthropogenic, and geographic characteristics of these waterbodies to: a) identify those variables that increase or decrease invasion risk, b) categorize all waterbodies in the region based on their invasion risk, and c) provide a decision support tools for managers and policy makers to identify at-risk sites.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PRE_DEPLOYMENT,도입 전,,S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://github.com/ombegov/2025-Federal-Agency-AI-Use-Case-Inventory,DOI-0139,U.S. federal public inventory; no separate repository LICENSE found; retain source attribution and review redistribution conditions,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_US_OMB_2025_00969,US_OMB_2025_00969,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,U.S. Office of Management and Budget,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Department of the Interior,Science,UNKNOWN,Adaptive Management with AMMonitor,Department of the Interior; USGS; Science; United States,Species identification from remotely sensed data,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,Using AI to accurately identify species from remotely sensed data will save thousands of hours of personnel time previously required and would allow researchers to inexpensively capture extensive data sets on species abundances and distributions.,Automated species identification from remote sensed data (images and audio files),SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://github.com/ombegov/2025-Federal-Agency-AI-Use-Case-Inventory,DOI-0144,U.S. federal public inventory; no separate repository LICENSE found; retain source attribution and review redistribution conditions,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_US_OMB_2025_00970,US_OMB_2025_00970,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,U.S. Office of Management and Budget,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Department of the Interior,Science,UNKNOWN,Using High-Resolution Imagery and Artificial Intelligence to Support Climate Change Resilience in Agroforestry Across the Pacific,Department of the Interior; USGS; Science; United States,"Project researchers will build capacity for conducting detailed agroforestry assessment and monitoring in Pacific Island nations, by using imagery collected from small unmanned aerial systems (sUAS or “drones”) and custom computer algorithms to automatically detect and monitor the health of coconut trees and other species of importance.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,"The results from this work can be used by smallholder coconut farmers and processors and local and national government agencies to better manage agroforestry resources for coconut, pandanus, and other species of interest across the Pacific Island region.","On remote Pacific islands and outer atolls, agroforestry (i.e., the cultivation and conservation of trees for agriculture) provides food security and income to local communities. Growing instability from climate change and invasive species like the coconut rhinoceros beetle threaten these resources. Actively managing and sustaining agroforestry resources requires detailed and up-to-date knowledge of forest inventories and conditions.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PRE_DEPLOYMENT,도입 전,,,https://github.com/ombegov/2025-Federal-Agency-AI-Use-Case-Inventory,DOI-0138,U.S. federal public inventory; no separate repository LICENSE found; retain source attribution and review redistribution conditions,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_US_OMB_2025_00971,US_OMB_2025_00971,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,U.S. Office of Management and Budget,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Department of the Interior,Science,UNKNOWN,Forecasting Earthquake Ground Motion Time Series [2024 INV#WO0000000109733],Department of the Interior; USGS; Science; United States,Generate earthquake ground motion time series,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,"Improved Earthquake Early Warning, Operational Aftershock Forecasting, and the National Seismic Hazard Model","Development of a deep learning models to generate earthquake ground motion time series for potential application to Earthquake Early Warning, Operational Aftershock Forecasting, and the National Seismic Hazard Model.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PRE_DEPLOYMENT,도입 전,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION,https://github.com/ombegov/2025-Federal-Agency-AI-Use-Case-Inventory,DOI-0140,U.S. federal public inventory; no separate repository LICENSE found; retain source attribution and review redistribution conditions,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_US_OMB_2025_00972,US_OMB_2025_00972,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,U.S. Office of Management and Budget,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Department of the Interior,Science,UNKNOWN,Refining Flood Risk Predictions in Hawai?i with Generative Machine Learning,Department of the Interior; USGS; Science; United States,This project will use these generative AI advancements to create high-resolution (1km) daily and sub-daily precipitation maps for the Hawaiian Islands and use these maps to quantify the risk of flooding events.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,Recent breakthroughs in generative artificial intelligence (AI) with neural networks offer new possibilities for for statistical downscaling.,"While global climate models (GCMs) offer climate projections, their coarse spatial resolution does not allow regional characteristics to be captured accurately, which are details crucial for managing risks related to climate hazards like floods, droughts, and heatwaves.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PRE_DEPLOYMENT,도입 전,,,https://github.com/ombegov/2025-Federal-Agency-AI-Use-Case-Inventory,DOI-0136,U.S. federal public inventory; no separate repository LICENSE found; retain source attribution and review redistribution conditions,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_US_OMB_2025_00973,US_OMB_2025_00973,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,U.S. Office of Management and Budget,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Department of the Interior,Science,"Currently, models of global climate change lack the resolution needed to model the processes that create most of Hawai?i’s rainfall.",Machine Learning for High-Resolution Downscaling in the Hawaiian Islands,Department of the Interior; USGS; Science; United States,"Using these improved spatial interpolation models, this project will create high-resolution, accurate historical rainfall maps. The project will also test the method for projecting future rainfall and compare predictions to existing statistical downscaling models.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,"predict precipitation at locations where no measurement data is available, using rainfall measurements from nearby locations","Currently, models of global climate change lack the resolution needed to model the processes that create most of Hawai?i’s rainfall.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PRE_DEPLOYMENT,도입 전,,,https://github.com/ombegov/2025-Federal-Agency-AI-Use-Case-Inventory,DOI-0134,U.S. federal public inventory; no separate repository LICENSE found; retain source attribution and review redistribution conditions,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_US_OMB_2025_00974,US_OMB_2025_00974,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,U.S. Office of Management and Budget,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Department of the Interior,Energy and Environment,"While it is important to monitor the water quality of these waterbodies, it can be costly and labor intensive to continuously monitor sites across the US.",Random forest models for predicting water quality of inland waters from remotely sensed imagery,Department of the Interior; USGS; Energy and Environment; United States,Prediction of chlorophyll concentrations and turbidity values in remotely sensed images of water bodies.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,"cost savings by estimating water quality parameters across the entire country on a frequent (5 day) schedule with minimal labor. By making these modeled water quality products publicly available, it may aid in managing water resources","Inland waterbodies (i.e. rivers, lakes, reservoirs, ponds, etc.) can face issues of poor water quality which may pose issues to water users, water infrastructure, and ecosystems. While it is important to monitor the water quality of these waterbodies, it can be costly and labor intensive to continuously monitor sites across the US. This project aims to improve the efficiency of monitoring by using Machine Learning (Random Forest) models to predict two water quality parameters of waterbodies seen in remotely sensed images. These remotely sensed images span the entire Conterminous US (CONUS) and are collected approximately every 5 days, allowing for water quality monitoring at high temporal and spatial resolutions with relatively low effort and cost.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://github.com/ombegov/2025-Federal-Agency-AI-Use-Case-Inventory,DOI-0137,U.S. federal public inventory; no separate repository LICENSE found; retain source attribution and review redistribution conditions,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_US_OMB_2025_00975,US_OMB_2025_00975,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,U.S. Office of Management and Budget,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Department of the Interior,Science,UNKNOWN,Global Detection of Historical Harmful Algal Blooms via combined Satellite Data and Deep Learning Methods,Department of the Interior; USGS; Science; United States,"Create a global-scale geospatial database of HABs occurrences over the past 40 years and compile in-situ measured HAB events and related parameters, remotely sensed data, and machine learning methods.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,"Provide resource managers, researchers, and the public with a novel ability to track and compare HABs occurrences across time and space, which could identify systematic trends and patterns that affect multiple inland water bodies.","Create a global-scale geospatial database of HABs occurrences over the past 40 years and compile in-situ measured HAB events and related parameters, remotely sensed data, and machine learning methods.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PRE_DEPLOYMENT,도입 전,,,https://github.com/ombegov/2025-Federal-Agency-AI-Use-Case-Inventory,DOI-0133,U.S. federal public inventory; no separate repository LICENSE found; retain source attribution and review redistribution conditions,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_US_OMB_2025_00976,US_OMB_2025_00976,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,U.S. Office of Management and Budget,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Department of the Interior,Energy and Environment,UNKNOWN,Classifying CWD-Infected Elk Using Recurrent Neural Networks on GPS Movement Data,Department of the Interior; USGS; Energy and Environment; United States,The AI system outputs a classification label for each elk—CWD-infected or not infected—based on patterns in their GPS movement data.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,"helps wildlife managers detect CWD in elk early using GPS movement data, reducing the need for costly and invasive testing. It supports faster, data-driven decisions for disease control and wildlife management, protecting both animal health and resources","Chronic Wasting Disease is difficult and costly to diagnose using traditional biological testing. However, the disease affects an elk's behavior and movement over time. By analyzing GPS tracking data with AI — specifically, Recurrent Neural Networks (RNNs) — the model aims to: Automatically identify movement patterns indicative of CWD infection, Enable earlier or non-invasive detection of disease, and Support wildlife disease surveillance and management decisions",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://github.com/ombegov/2025-Federal-Agency-AI-Use-Case-Inventory,DOI-0135,U.S. federal public inventory; no separate repository LICENSE found; retain source attribution and review redistribution conditions,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
