AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00002,EU_PSTW_202609_00002,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Public Employment Service Austria,Unemployment.,UNKNOWN,AMS – Algorithmic Profiling in the Austrian Public Employment Service,Public Employment Service Austria; Central-Government; Unemployment.; Austria,"The system applies statistical modelling techniques to historical labour market data, incorporating variables such as education, employment history and demographic indicators to generate probability scores.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Public employment services aim to allocate counselling resources and labour market programmes efficiently while supporting diverse jobseekers with varying employment prospects. In Austria, the Public Employment Service (AMS) introduced an algorithmic profiling system to assist counsellors in classifying jobseekers according to their predicted likelihood of re-entering employment. Stakeholders include jobseekers, employment counsellors, programme administrators and oversight authorities. The objective was to improve the effectiveness of active labour market policies by tailoring support intensity to statistically estimated prospects. The system applies statistical modelling techniques to historical labour market data, incorporating variables such as education, employment history and demographic indicators to generate probability scores. Based on these outputs, individuals are categorised into high, medium or low employment prospects, which inform differentiated support pathways. Expected impacts included more targeted allocation of training resources, improved programme efficiency and data-informed counselling decisions; however, concerns emerged regarding fairness, transparency and potential discrimination in automated classifications.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,NOT_IN_USE,미사용,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00003,EU_PSTW_202609_00003,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Unternehmensservice Portal, Austria","Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.",UNKNOWN,Mona - Public chatbot for companies on the subject of the economy,"Unternehmensservice Portal, Austria; Central-Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Austria","At a conceptual level, the service uses natural language understanding to interpret user requests, retrieve relevant information, and deliver responses through a chatbot integrated into the national business portal.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Businesses often need rapid, reliable guidance on economic measures, employment rules, subsidies, and administrative obligations, especially during periods of disruption such as the coronavirus crisis, when demand for official information rises sharply and questions become more varied. In this context, the Federal Ministry for Digitisation and Business Location introduced Mona through the Company Service Portal to give companies a simpler and more accessible way to obtain practical support on economic matters. The objective is to reduce information barriers for firms, improve access to official guidance across web and mobile channels, and help users navigate complex topics such as financial support, labour law, short-time work, teleworking, and research-related measures. The implementation combines a public-facing conversational interface with structured information flows, enabling both free-form questions and guided step-by-step navigation through key thematic areas. At a conceptual level, the service uses natural language understanding to interpret user requests, retrieve relevant information, and deliver responses through a chatbot integrated into the national business portal. The impact includes faster access to official information, lower demand on traditional support channels, and a more user-friendly experience for companies. It also improves service scalability, strengthens consistency of communication, and creates operational value by making critical business information available in a more efficient and responsive way.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00004,EU_PSTW_202609_00004,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"CitizenLab, Belgium",Environmental protection n.e.c.,"Large-scale participatory initiatives often succeed in collecting substantial volumes of ideas and opinions, yet the real challenge lies in processing that material quickly enough to turn it into meaningful recommendations and actions. It also enhances scalability, reduces manual analysis effort, and increases the value of public engagement by making it easier to convert diverse ideas into usable strategic input.",CitizenLab - Youth for Climate,"CitizenLab, Belgium; Private sector; Environmental protection n.e.c.; Belgium","The objective of this use case is to help organisers transform large-scale citizen participation into structured evidence that can inform climate-related decision-making and policy discussions. The implementation relies on the automated analysis capabilities of the CitizenLab platform, using natural language processing to cluster, classify, and interpret contributions submitted in multiple languages.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Large-scale participatory initiatives often succeed in collecting substantial volumes of ideas and opinions, yet the real challenge lies in processing that material quickly enough to turn it into meaningful recommendations and actions. In the Youth for Climate context, thousands of contributions were gathered in a short period, creating a need for a systematic way to analyse multilingual input without losing sight of recurring themes, priorities, and actionable insights. The objective of this use case is to help organisers transform large-scale citizen participation into structured evidence that can inform climate-related decision-making and policy discussions. The implementation relies on the automated analysis capabilities of the CitizenLab platform, using natural language processing to cluster, classify, and interpret contributions submitted in multiple languages. At a conceptual level, the platform applies text analysis techniques to identify patterns across large numbers of submissions, supporting rapid synthesis while preserving the breadth of participant input. The system is embedded within the wider participation workflow, linking idea collection with analysis and recommendation development. The impact includes much faster processing of large consultation volumes, improved ability to derive insights from multilingual engagement, and stronger support for evidence-based participation outcomes. It also enhances scalability, reduces manual analysis effort, and increases the value of public engagement by making it easier to convert diverse ideas into usable strategic input.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-3,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00005,EU_PSTW_202609_00005,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Département de l'Agriculture,"Agriculture, forestry, fishing and hunting","Monitoring whether agricultural subsidy conditions are met is traditionally a labour-intensive process, often based on limited field inspections that cover only a small proportion of parcels and crops. The impact includes broader coverage, reduced need for costly site inspections, and more effective stewardship of public money.",Walloon - Agricultural subsidy monitoring with the use of GeoAI,"Département de l'Agriculture; Local Government; Agriculture, forestry, fishing and hunting; Belgium","This creates challenges for public authorities seeking to ensure compliance, use public funds efficiently, and maintain consistent oversight across large agricultural areas. The objective of this use case is to improve the accuracy, reach, and efficiency of subsidy monitoring by replacing sporadic manual checks with continuous and comprehensive observation.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Monitoring whether agricultural subsidy conditions are met is traditionally a labour-intensive process, often based on limited field inspections that cover only a small proportion of parcels and crops. This creates challenges for public authorities seeking to ensure compliance, use public funds efficiently, and maintain consistent oversight across large agricultural areas. The objective of this use case is to improve the accuracy, reach, and efficiency of subsidy monitoring by replacing sporadic manual checks with continuous and comprehensive observation. The implementation uses geospatial analysis and image-processing techniques applied to satellite imagery in order to verify whether land use and crop conditions comply with subsidy requirements. At a conceptual level, geo-enabled analytical models process remote sensing data to assess agricultural parcels continuously, enabling authorities to monitor 100 per cent of parcels and crops rather than relying on field visits to only around 5 per cent. The solution is integrated into compliance monitoring workflows to support more timely and evidence-based verification. The impact includes broader coverage, reduced need for costly site inspections, and more effective stewardship of public money. In addition, it improves reliability, scalability, and consistency in agricultural oversight, while enabling authorities to identify potential issues earlier and allocate inspection resources more strategically.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-4,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00006,EU_PSTW_202609_00006,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Digipolis & city of Antwerp,General public services n.e.c.,"This task can be time-consuming and fragmented, particularly in large organisations where APIs, applications, and other reusable assets are documented across multiple repositories and formats.",AcPaas - Technical procurement documents comparison,Digipolis & city of Antwerp; Private sector; General public services n.e.c.; Belgium,"The objective of this use case is to support more efficient procurement preparation and solution design by identifying whether in-house technology components can satisfy requirements described in procurement or offer documentation. The implementation uses natural language processing to compare the text contained in technical files with metadata describing existing IT components, including APIs and applications.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Public procurement processes often involve reviewing complex technical documents and determining whether existing internal digital components could already meet some of the stated requirements. This task can be time-consuming and fragmented, particularly in large organisations where APIs, applications, and other reusable assets are documented across multiple repositories and formats. The objective of this use case is to support more efficient procurement preparation and solution design by identifying whether in-house technology components can satisfy requirements described in procurement or offer documentation. The implementation uses natural language processing to compare the text contained in technical files with metadata describing existing IT components, including APIs and applications. For APIs, descriptive content is extracted and pre-processed from metadata files to improve matching, while an experimental extension also compares technical diagrams against component metadata. Conceptually, the solution links document analysis, metadata enrichment, and semantic comparison within the Antwerp City Platform as a Service environment. The impact includes faster identification of reusable digital assets, reduced duplication of development effort, and more informed procurement decisions. It also supports better use of existing technology investments, improves consistency in technical assessment, and enhances organisational efficiency by making internal component knowledge easier to access and apply in procurement and architecture processes.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-5,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00007,EU_PSTW_202609_00007,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Information Flanders, Belgium","Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.","At the Flemish Infoline, this challenge is particularly important because incoming calls and other enquiries cover a wide range of topics, making manual triage and answer preparation both time-consuming and inconsistent.",Flemish Infoline - Automatic classification of incoming phone calls,"Information Flanders, Belgium; Local Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Belgium","The objective of this use case is to improve the speed and precision with which questions are captured, classified, and directed to the right answer provider, while also making standardised responses easier to deliver. The implementation uses natural language processing to classify incoming telephone questions, transcribe and document conversations in full, and suggest relevant answers based on editorially managed content.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Public contact centres handle large volumes of citizen questions across multiple channels, and service quality often depends on how quickly each request can be understood, categorised, routed, and answered by the most suitable staff member. At the Flemish Infoline, this challenge is particularly important because incoming calls and other enquiries cover a wide range of topics, making manual triage and answer preparation both time-consuming and inconsistent. The objective of this use case is to improve the speed and precision with which questions are captured, classified, and directed to the right answer provider, while also making standardised responses easier to deliver. The implementation uses natural language processing to classify incoming telephone questions, transcribe and document conversations in full, and suggest relevant answers based on editorially managed content. At a conceptual level, speech and text processing components support fine-grained categorisation and connect each query to the most appropriate responder with the right expertise, regardless of whether the channel is spoken or written. The impact includes shorter handling times, more accurate routing, and greater consistency in responses. It also reduces administrative effort, improves service scalability across channels, and strengthens the overall citizen experience through faster and more reliable access to information.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-6,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00008,EU_PSTW_202609_00008,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Instituut voor Landbouw-, Visserij- en Voedingsonderzoek","General economic, commercial and labour affair",UNKNOWN,ILVO supporting Flanders AI research program (FAIR) pilots,"Instituut voor Landbouw-, Visserij- en Voedingsonderzoek; Central-Government; General economic, commercial and labour affair; Belgium","The programme includes multiple experimental use cases, such as early detection of crop diseases using hyperspectral imaging, automated health monitoring of livestock through sensor and image data, and identification of pests and weeds via computer vision.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Institute for Agricultural, Fisheries and Food Research (ILVO) participates in the Flanders AI Research Programme (FAIR) to explore the application of advanced data-driven methods in agriculture and food systems. The sector faces increasing pressure to improve productivity, sustainability, and animal welfare while managing complex biological and environmental variables. The objective of this initiative is to investigate and validate innovative solutions that can support farmers and policymakers through improved monitoring, diagnostics, and decision-making. The programme includes multiple experimental use cases, such as early detection of crop diseases using hyperspectral imaging, automated health monitoring of livestock through sensor and image data, and identification of pests and weeds via computer vision. These applications rely on machine learning models trained on agricultural datasets, integrating sensor data, imagery, and environmental variables. As a research-driven initiative involving collaborations with knowledge centres, the projects are in various stages of development and validation.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-7,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00009,EU_PSTW_202609_00009,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Kind en Gezin, Belgium","Medical products, appliances and equipment.","Accurate recording of vaccine batch numbers is essential for pharmacovigilance, traceability and rapid response to safety issues, yet non-standardised packaging complicates manual data entry. Extracted data are structured and integrated into vaccination registration systems to reduce manual entry errors.",Belgium - Detection batch numbers vaccines,"Kind en Gezin, Belgium; Central-Government; Medical products, appliances and equipment.; Belgium",The proof of concept uses computer vision and optical character recognition techniques embedded in a smartphone application to detect and interpret batch numbers from camera images.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Accurate recording of vaccine batch numbers is essential for pharmacovigilance, traceability and rapid response to safety issues, yet non-standardised packaging complicates manual data entry. Stakeholders include Child and Family services, healthcare professionals and public health authorities. The objective is to streamline vaccination registration by automatically capturing lot numbers from vaccine packaging. The proof of concept uses computer vision and optical character recognition techniques embedded in a smartphone application to detect and interpret batch numbers from camera images. Image preprocessing and pattern recognition algorithms account for varied formats and print quality. Extracted data are structured and integrated into vaccination registration systems to reduce manual entry errors. Expected impacts include improved data accuracy, faster registration processes, enhanced traceability of administered vaccines and strengthened public health monitoring capabilities.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,NOT_IN_USE,미사용,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK|F08_ERROR_COMPLIANCE_RISK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-8,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00010,EU_PSTW_202609_00010,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Kind en Gezin, Belgium",Family and children.,"The Flemish Agency for Child and Family (Kind en Gezin), now part of the Opgroeien agency, faced constraints in conducting comprehensive inspections of childcare facilities due to limited inspection capacity and increasing demand for oversight.",Verontrustingen - Enabling accurate predictions to detect day-care services inspection,"Kind en Gezin, Belgium; Central-Government; Family and children.; Belgium","The use case aims to prioritise inspections by identifying childcare providers with a higher likelihood of non-compliance or quality issues, thereby improving regulatory efficiency and service quality.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Flemish Agency for Child and Family (Kind en Gezin), now part of the Opgroeien agency, faced constraints in conducting comprehensive inspections of childcare facilities due to limited inspection capacity and increasing demand for oversight. Ensuring consistent quality and safeguarding child wellbeing required a more targeted and risk-based inspection approach. The use case aims to prioritise inspections by identifying childcare providers with a higher likelihood of non-compliance or quality issues, thereby improving regulatory efficiency and service quality. The solution integrates supervised machine learning techniques, including logistic regression and gradient boosting (XGBoost), applied to a combination of internal administrative records and external inspection data from the Health Care Inspectorate. These models generate risk scores that support inspectors in selecting facilities for inspection. The system is embedded within existing workflows, complementing professional judgement rather than replacing it. As a result, inspection resources are allocated more effectively, enabling earlier detection of potential issues and improving overall monitoring coverage. The approach enhances evidence-based decision-making, contributes to maintaining high childcare standards, and supports a more proactive regulatory framework.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-9,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00011,EU_PSTW_202609_00011,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Traffic Institute Vias,Police Services,"Traditional enforcement methods rely heavily on manual observation by police, limiting detection rates and scalability.",Camera System - Mobile phone usage on vehicles,Traffic Institute Vias; Local Government; Police Services; Belgium,"The Belgian road safety institute Vias identified the growing risk of traffic accidents caused by drivers using mobile phones while driving, particularly handheld usage. This use case aims to enhance enforcement capabilities by automating the identification of such violations and supporting road safety policies.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Belgian road safety institute Vias identified the growing risk of traffic accidents caused by drivers using mobile phones while driving, particularly handheld usage. Traditional enforcement methods rely heavily on manual observation by police, limiting detection rates and scalability. This use case aims to enhance enforcement capabilities by automating the identification of such violations and supporting road safety policies. The system uses roadside camera infrastructure to capture images of passing vehicles, which are then processed using computer vision techniques to detect whether a driver is holding and using a mobile phone. Images not indicating violations are automatically discarded, while detected cases are anonymised to protect privacy before further review. Law enforcement authorities validate flagged cases before issuing fines. The solution integrates image recognition models trained on annotated datasets to distinguish relevant behaviours. The pilot demonstrates the feasibility of augmenting enforcement through automated pre-selection of violations, improving efficiency and enabling more consistent monitoring. It contributes to increased deterrence, optimised use of police resources, and enhanced road safety outcomes.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-10,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00012,EU_PSTW_202609_00012,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Visit Flanders,"General economic, commercial and labour affair",UNKNOWN,PaveAI 2.0 - Interprets the figures in Google Analytics via machine learning and custom reports,"Visit Flanders; Local Government; General economic, commercial and labour affair; Belgium",VISITFLANDERS explored the use of automated analytics tools to better interpret website performance data and support digital marketing strategies. The objective of this use case was to enhance understanding of user behaviour and improve decision-making related to tourism promotion.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"VISITFLANDERS explored the use of automated analytics tools to better interpret website performance data and support digital marketing strategies. Analysing large volumes of web analytics data requires specialised expertise and can be time-consuming, limiting the ability to quickly derive actionable insights. The objective of this use case was to enhance understanding of user behaviour and improve decision-making related to tourism promotion. The solution involved the use of a tool capable of transforming Google Analytics data into structured insights and customised reports through machine learning techniques. The system processes web traffic data to identify patterns, trends, and performance indicators, presenting them in an accessible format for decision-makers. It integrates with existing analytics platforms to automate reporting workflows. While the pilot demonstrated potential to improve efficiency and insight generation, no recent evidence confirms continued use or operational deployment within VISITFLANDERS.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S6_WORKFLOW_PROCESS_AUTOMATION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-11,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00013,EU_PSTW_202609_00013,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Vlaamse Dienst voor Arbeidsbemiddeling en Beroepsopleiding,Unemployment.,"Without tailored guidance, it can be difficult for jobseekers to identify realistic career paths, understand which skills they already possess, and determine which additional competences are needed to move into new roles.","Jobnet - Reskilling, upskilling and retraining of people",Vlaamse Dienst voor Arbeidsbemiddeling en Beroepsopleiding; Local Government; Unemployment.; Belgium,The objective of this use case is to enrich job matching and support career transitions by helping citizens connect their own backgrounds with current labour market demand.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Labour markets change rapidly, and many citizens need support to understand how their existing experience and capabilities relate to emerging job opportunities, especially when they are seeking to reskill, upskill, or retrain. Without tailored guidance, it can be difficult for jobseekers to identify realistic career paths, understand which skills they already possess, and determine which additional competences are needed to move into new roles. The objective of this use case is to enrich job matching and support career transitions by helping citizens connect their own backgrounds with current labour market demand. The implementation consists of Jobnet, a solution used by VDAB since 2018 that combines multiple analytical models into a broader matching system. At a conceptual level, the platform analyses user experience and skill profiles alongside labour market data to identify suitable jobs and highlight relevant skill gaps that may need to be addressed through training or development. The system therefore links personal profile analysis with market intelligence to support guidance and transition planning. The impact includes more informed career decisions, better targeting of training pathways, and improved matching between people and available work. It also supports employability, makes career services more scalable, and delivers public value by helping align workforce capabilities with changing economic needs.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-12,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00014,EU_PSTW_202609_00014,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Vlaamse Dienst voor Arbeidsbemiddeling en Beroepsopleiding,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.","Jobseekers often need timely, practical support when searching for vacancies, yet traditional guidance channels can struggle to provide immediate and personalised assistance at scale. This creates a challenge for employment services that aim to help large numbers of users navigate vacancies, ask questions, and receive relevant direction throughout their search process.",VDAB Chatbot for job seekers - Chatbots to better match jobseekers with the right vacancies,"Vlaamse Dienst voor Arbeidsbemiddeling en Beroepsopleiding; Local Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Belgium","The objective of this use case is to improve the quality and accessibility of job-search support by using conversational tools that can answer questions and guide users towards suitable opportunities. At a conceptual level, the solution uses conversational language processing to interpret user input, provide answers, and support matching-related interactions between jobseekers and the employment service.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Jobseekers often need timely, practical support when searching for vacancies, yet traditional guidance channels can struggle to provide immediate and personalised assistance at scale. This creates a challenge for employment services that aim to help large numbers of users navigate vacancies, ask questions, and receive relevant direction throughout their search process. The objective of this use case is to improve the quality and accessibility of job-search support by using conversational tools that can answer questions and guide users towards suitable opportunities. The implementation involves chatbot technology designed to participate in typed conversations with jobseekers, automatically responding to enquiries and helping users progress through their search. At a conceptual level, the solution uses conversational language processing to interpret user input, provide answers, and support matching-related interactions between jobseekers and the employment service. The chatbot is positioned as part of a broader exploration of how conversational systems can strengthen vacancy discovery and candidate guidance. The impact includes quicker access to support, a more responsive user experience, and better assistance outside the limits of traditional one-to-one service models. It also increases service scalability, reduces pressure on human support channels, and creates business value by helping more jobseekers engage effectively with vacancies and employment services.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-13,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00015,EU_PSTW_202609_00015,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Departement Cultuur, Jeugd en Media (CJM) - Wallonia","Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.","Grant-making bodies often face a heavy administrative burden when assessing subsidy applications, particularly where they must judge not only the substantive quality of a proposal but also whether the applicant has sufficient organisational and business maturity to deliver it successfully.",Quickscan Organisaties,"Departement Cultuur, Jeugd en Media (CJM) - Wallonia; Central-Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Belgium","At a conceptual level, the solution analyses structured and textual application information to automate elements of admissibility checking and organisational assessment, enabling public officials to compare applicants against defined criteria more efficiently. It also offers improved scalability and greater consistency in handling large numbers of applications, creating business value through better use of public-sector resources.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Grant-making bodies often face a heavy administrative burden when assessing subsidy applications, particularly where they must judge not only the substantive quality of a proposal but also whether the applicant has sufficient organisational and business maturity to deliver it successfully. In this case, the Department of Culture, Youth and Media sought to streamline the admissibility and assessment of grant applications, reduce the time required from both applicants and evaluators, and improve consistency in decision-making. The objective is therefore to support faster, more standardised, and less labour-intensive screening of applications without weakening the underlying governance process. The implementation centred on the development of the Quickscan Organizations tool and its supporting algorithm through an innovative public procurement project. At a conceptual level, the solution analyses structured and textual application information to automate elements of admissibility checking and organisational assessment, enabling public officials to compare applicants against defined criteria more efficiently. The tool was developed through a proof-of-concept phase involving selected suppliers and positioned as a digital support layer for grant evaluation workflows. The expected impact includes shorter assessment cycles, lower administrative effort, and more efficient processing for both applicants and reviewers. It also offers improved scalability and greater consistency in handling large numbers of applications, creating business value through better use of public-sector resources.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-14,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00016,EU_PSTW_202609_00016,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,UMNI,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.",UNKNOWN,Plovdic - City Concierge Chatbot,"UMNI; Local Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Bulgaria",City Concierge is an AI-powered city chatbot like a virtual Tourist Office in the mobile.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"City Concierge is an AI-powered city chatbot like a virtual Tourist Office in the mobile. It is there for the visitors 24/7 in Messenger and provides information about the city Point of Interests pf the city. It is possible for tourists and visitors to search information by category and location, or by keyword, or simply ask question about restaurants, entertainment, sightseeing, shopping, transportation and other",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-15,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00017,EU_PSTW_202609_00017,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Ministry of Health,Public health services.,"Expected impacts include faster dissemination of accurate information, reduced burden on call centres, improved public awareness and more efficient triage during health emergencies.",Andrija - Virtual assistant supporting advising suspected COVID-19 infections,Ministry of Health; Central-Government; Public health services.; Croatia,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"During the COVID-19 pandemic, public health authorities faced high volumes of citizen enquiries regarding symptoms, testing and protective measures. In Croatia, the digital assistant Andrija was developed to provide consistent, evidence-based guidance and reduce pressure on health services. Stakeholders include citizens, epidemiologists, public health authorities and technology partners. The objective is to support early self-assessment and disseminate reliable health information. The system combines natural language processing, rule-based medical triage logic and continuously updated epidemiological guidance to respond to user queries via web and messaging platforms such as WhatsApp. Expert-defined decision trees and symptom-check algorithms guide users through structured dialogues. Expected impacts include faster dissemination of accurate information, reduced burden on call centres, improved public awareness and more efficient triage during health emergencies.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,NOT_IN_USE,미사용,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-16,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00018,EU_PSTW_202609_00018,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Vinkovci munipality,Public health services.,UNKNOWN,Hope - Chatbot informing work of the Civil Protection Staff,Vinkovci munipality; Local Government; Public health services.; Croatia,The chatbot uses natural language processing and predefined knowledge bases to answer common queries and guide users through structured information flows.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Local authorities require effective communication channels to inform citizens during emergencies, particularly during the COVID-19 pandemic. In Vinkovci, the Civil Protection Staff sought a digital solution to address frequently asked questions and reduce administrative workload. Stakeholders include municipal authorities, citizens and public health institutions. The objective is to provide accessible, real-time information on pandemic measures, symptoms and administrative procedures such as e-passes. The chatbot uses natural language processing and predefined knowledge bases to answer common queries and guide users through structured information flows. It is integrated into municipal communication channels and can be updated with new guidance as regulations change. Expected impacts include improved citizen access to reliable information, reduced call volumes for public offices and enhanced responsiveness during crisis management.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,NOT_IN_USE,미사용,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-17,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00019,EU_PSTW_202609_00019,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"The Water Development Department, The Water Development Department, Cyprus",Water supply.,Traditional monitoring approaches generate large volumes of heterogeneous data that are difficult to analyse in a timely and integrated manner.,WaterAnalytics - Water quality monitoring,"The Water Development Department, The Water Development Department, Cyprus; Central-Government; Water supply.; Cyprus",This use case aims to improve water management by enabling more effective analysis of hydraulic and quality-related data to support operational and policy decisions.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Water Development Department in Cyprus is responsible for monitoring water resources, including quality and distribution efficiency, in a context of increasing environmental pressures and resource scarcity. Traditional monitoring approaches generate large volumes of heterogeneous data that are difficult to analyse in a timely and integrated manner. This use case aims to improve water management by enabling more effective analysis of hydraulic and quality-related data to support operational and policy decisions. The system employs data analytics techniques to process inputs from monitoring infrastructure, including sensors capturing parameters such as flow, pressure, and chemical composition. Machine learning algorithms are applied to identify patterns, detect anomalies, and support predictive assessments of water system performance. The solution is designed to integrate multiple data streams into a unified analytical framework. Expected benefits include improved detection of water quality issues, more efficient resource management, and enhanced decision-making capabilities.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F06_HIGH_VOLUME_SCALABILITY_BOTTLENECK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-18,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00020,EU_PSTW_202609_00020,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Ministry of Industry and Trade,Old age.,UNKNOWN,AI in social service to improve long term stay,Ministry of Industry and Trade; Central-Government; Old age.; Czechia,This use case addresses that challenge by using digital support in social services to help citizens remain in their natural home and community environment for as long as possible.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Social-care systems face growing pressure to support older and vulnerable people in ways that preserve independence, quality of life, and continuity of care, while also reducing unnecessary institutionalisation. This use case addresses that challenge by using digital support in social services to help citizens remain in their natural home and community environment for as long as possible. The objective is to strengthen person-centred care, improve early identification of support needs, and enable more sustainable care planning that balances individual wellbeing with public-service capacity. The implementation is described at a high level within the Czech AI strategy context, where AI is envisaged as a support tool for social services rather than as a replacement for professional judgement. At a conceptual level, such a solution would draw on relevant care and contextual data to help identify risks, support care coordination, and guide interventions that make independent living more viable over time. The expected impact includes improved quality of life for service users, reduced pressure on long-term institutional care, and more efficient allocation of social-service resources. It also supports a more scalable and preventive approach to care by helping public services intervene earlier and tailor support more effectively to individual circumstances.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PLANNED,계획,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-19,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00021,EU_PSTW_202609_00021,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Corti,Public health services.,UNKNOWN,Corti's AI - Emergency medical services real-time speech analysis for cardiac predictions,Corti; Private sector; Public health services.; Denmark,"This use case addresses that operational problem by supporting dispatchers and call-takers during emergency calls so that out-of-hospital cardiac arrests can be recognised faster and with fewer errors. The implementation uses real-time speech analysis on live emergency calls, applying predictive models trained to detect linguistic and behavioural patterns associated with cardiac arrest.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Emergency call handlers must identify cardiac arrest within seconds, yet callers often describe symptoms unclearly and high-pressure conditions can lead to delayed recognition or missed warning signs. This use case addresses that operational problem by supporting dispatchers and call-takers during emergency calls so that out-of-hospital cardiac arrests can be recognised faster and with fewer errors. The broader objective is to improve emergency medical response, reduce avoidable mortality, and strengthen the reliability of call-centre decision-making in time-critical situations. The implementation uses real-time speech analysis on live emergency calls, applying predictive models trained to detect linguistic and behavioural patterns associated with cardiac arrest. At a conceptual level, the system listens to the call as it unfolds, analyses speech signals and conversational content, and produces immediate decision support for human operators. The approach is intended to work alongside dispatch professionals rather than replace them, feeding alerts and clinical suggestions into the emergency response workflow. Reported performance from the original deployment described detection accuracy of up to 95 per cent, and the Copenhagen implementation was sufficiently promising to support wider piloting with the European Emergency Number Association. The impact includes quicker diagnosis, reduced error rates, faster dispatch decisions, and stronger overall service quality, with clear public value in improving survival chances during medical emergencies.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-20,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00022,EU_PSTW_202609_00022,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Danish Business Authority,"General economic, commercial and labour affair","Supervising corporate filings and financial statements at scale is difficult for regulators because manual review cannot easily keep pace with the volume of submissions, while material errors and potentially fraudulent reporting may remain hidden among routine cases. The objective is to improve control quality, target inspections more intelligently, and reduce unnecessary burdens on compliant businesses by focusing attention where risks are highest.",Erhvervsstyrelsen - Detection of errors in business statements,"Danish Business Authority; Central-Government; General economic, commercial and labour affair; Denmark","The Danish Business Authority developed this use case to strengthen risk-based supervision by identifying suspicious patterns and highlighting significant errors in business and financial statements more efficiently. The implementation uses machine learning models within the authority’s data and control environment to analyse company filings and financial statements for indicators of fraud, irregularities, or non-compliance.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Supervising corporate filings and financial statements at scale is difficult for regulators because manual review cannot easily keep pace with the volume of submissions, while material errors and potentially fraudulent reporting may remain hidden among routine cases. The Danish Business Authority developed this use case to strengthen risk-based supervision by identifying suspicious patterns and highlighting significant errors in business and financial statements more efficiently. The objective is to improve control quality, target inspections more intelligently, and reduce unnecessary burdens on compliant businesses by focusing attention where risks are highest. The implementation uses machine learning models within the authority’s data and control environment to analyse company filings and financial statements for indicators of fraud, irregularities, or non-compliance. At a conceptual level, the solution combines administrative and statement data with digital risk-scoring methods to prioritise cases for review and support supervisory decision-making. This fits into a broader risk-based control framework used by the authority in relation to company law and accounting oversight. The impact includes more effective detection of problematic filings, better allocation of inspection resources, and stronger trust in business reporting. It also supports scalability in regulatory control, improves consistency in case selection, and creates value by enabling more proportionate and data-driven oversight across very large filing volumes.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK|F08_ERROR_COMPLIANCE_RISK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-21,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00023,EU_PSTW_202609_00023,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Frederiksberg Municipality,General public services n.e.c.,"The pilot was inspired by similar Scandinavian trials and was intentionally limited at first to frequently asked, general enquiries rather than individual case handling.",Kiri - chatbot for citizens' enquiries,Frederiksberg Municipality; Local Government; General public services n.e.c.; Denmark,The implementation uses a public-facing municipal chatbot that interprets natural-language questions and returns answers drawn from predefined service information.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Municipal websites receive large numbers of routine citizen questions, yet residents increasingly expect immediate answers without having to wait for office hours or contact staff directly for simple matters. Kiri was introduced in this context as a pilot chatbot for several Danish municipalities, including Frederiksberg, to improve access to general information in citizen service areas such as passports and driving licences. The objective is to provide instant responses to common questions, reduce pressure on front-line service teams, and test whether conversational digital assistance can improve the overall citizen experience without handling sensitive case-specific issues. The implementation uses a public-facing municipal chatbot that interprets natural-language questions and returns answers drawn from predefined service information. At a conceptual level, the system relies on conversational language technology, structured municipal knowledge content, and website integration so that users can obtain immediate guidance directly on the municipality’s digital channels. The pilot was inspired by similar Scandinavian trials and was intentionally limited at first to frequently asked, general enquiries rather than individual case handling. The impact includes quicker access to information, more convenient self-service, and reduced demand on municipal staff for repetitive queries. It also offers a scalable support mechanism across municipalities and contributes to more consistent communication with citizens across digital service channels.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-22,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00024,EU_PSTW_202609_00024,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Aarhus Municipality,Water supply.,Traditional systems lacked the capability to dynamically optimise operations based on real-time demand and system conditions.,CHAIN - Smart Water optimising energy consumption,Aarhus Municipality; Local Government; Water supply.; Denmark,The objective of this use case is to improve energy efficiency and water quality monitoring through data-driven optimisation of water systems.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The municipality of Aarhus sought to address inefficiencies in water and energy consumption within its water management infrastructure. Traditional systems lacked the capability to dynamically optimise operations based on real-time demand and system conditions. The objective of this use case is to improve energy efficiency and water quality monitoring through data-driven optimisation of water systems. The solution relies on sensor networks that collect detailed data on water consumption, flow, and quality parameters. Advanced analytics and machine learning techniques are applied to process these large datasets, enabling optimisation of pumping operations and early detection of anomalies. The system integrates sensor outputs with analytical models to support operational decision-making. As a pilot project, it demonstrates the potential to reduce energy consumption, improve system efficiency, and enhance sustainability in municipal water management.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-23,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00025,EU_PSTW_202609_00025,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Estonian Agricultural Registers and Information Board (ARIB),"Agriculture, forestry, fishing and hunting","Agricultural agencies need reliable ways to verify whether grasslands have been mown, since this affects compliance with land-management and subsidy rules, yet traditional inspections are costly, selective, and difficult to scale across an entire country. The objective is to support more efficient compliance checking by replacing or reducing manual inspection with systematic, data-driven observation of mowing activity.",SATIKAS - Detecting the agricultural grasslands changes,"Estonian Agricultural Registers and Information Board (ARIB); Central-Government; Agriculture, forestry, fishing and hunting; Estonia","SATIKAS was developed for the Estonian Agricultural Registers and Information Board to automate this monitoring task and improve oversight of grassland management using remote sensing. The implementation uses deep learning and convolutional neural network techniques to analyse satellite imagery from the Copernicus programme, particularly Sentinel data, together with reference information on farmer fields, historical inspection logs, and meteorological data.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Agricultural agencies need reliable ways to verify whether grasslands have been mown, since this affects compliance with land-management and subsidy rules, yet traditional inspections are costly, selective, and difficult to scale across an entire country. SATIKAS was developed for the Estonian Agricultural Registers and Information Board to automate this monitoring task and improve oversight of grassland management using remote sensing. The objective is to support more efficient compliance checking by replacing or reducing manual inspection with systematic, data-driven observation of mowing activity. The implementation uses deep learning and convolutional neural network techniques to analyse satellite imagery from the Copernicus programme, particularly Sentinel data, together with reference information on farmer fields, historical inspection logs, and meteorological data. At a conceptual level, the system fuses geospatial imagery and administrative datasets to classify whether mowing has taken place on specific grassland parcels. The solution is one of Estonia’s earliest government uses of such methods and was described as still under development, with plans to expand its capabilities further. The impact includes broader and more frequent monitoring coverage, reduced inspection effort, and more consistent evidence for subsidy control. It also improves scalability and reliability in agricultural supervision while supporting better use of public resources and more targeted field verification where necessary.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-24,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00026,EU_PSTW_202609_00026,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,e-Residency organization,General services.,"The challenge was to provide faster answers to common enquiries while maintaining service quality for a global user base seeking information on applications, digital identity, company creation, and programme processes.",e-Residency - Chatbot for customer assistance,e-Residency organization; Central-Government; General services.; Estonia,"The objective of this use case was to improve responsiveness and customer experience without proportionally increasing support headcount. The implementation introduced an AI-powered customer service assistant with a live-chat interface, designed to answer common questions instantly and support users as they navigated the e-Residency journey.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"As the Estonian e-Residency programme grew internationally, customer support demand increased and response times risked lengthening unless more staff were added. The challenge was to provide faster answers to common enquiries while maintaining service quality for a global user base seeking information on applications, digital identity, company creation, and programme processes. The objective of this use case was to improve responsiveness and customer experience without proportionally increasing support headcount. The implementation introduced an AI-powered customer service assistant with a live-chat interface, designed to answer common questions instantly and support users as they navigated the e-Residency journey. At a conceptual level, the solution combined conversational automation with structured support content, allowing the organisation to handle routine queries through a chatbot while reserving staff effort for more complex cases. The service was integrated into the programme’s broader digital support model and aligned with an evolving customer strategy focused on accessibility and end-to-end assistance. The impact includes faster first-response times, better support scalability, and lower pressure on human support teams. It also contributes to a more seamless user experience for international applicants and creates operational value by enabling a public digital service to serve growing demand more efficiently across borders.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-25,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00027,EU_PSTW_202609_00027,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Estonian Health Insurance Fund,Public health services.,"General practitioners often face challenges in identifying high-risk patients who require intensified monitoring and intervention. However, the initiative remains at pilot stage with limited evidence of nationwide implementation.",Enhanced Care Management - Prediction model for hospitalisation/assistance,Estonian Health Insurance Fund; Central-Government; Public health services.; Estonia,"The solution uses predictive modelling techniques applied to healthcare utilisation data, particularly medical billing records, to identify patterns associated with hospitalisation risk.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Estonian Health Insurance Fund, in collaboration with the World Bank, initiated a programme to improve the management of patients with chronic conditions by strengthening preventive care and reducing avoidable hospitalisations. General practitioners often face challenges in identifying high-risk patients who require intensified monitoring and intervention. The objective is to support risk-based care management by identifying patients most likely to experience adverse health events. The solution uses predictive modelling techniques applied to healthcare utilisation data, particularly medical billing records, to identify patterns associated with hospitalisation risk. Algorithms analyse patient histories and selected diagnoses to generate risk scores, enabling GPs to prioritise care interventions such as counselling and follow-up. The model is integrated into primary care workflows to support decision-making. Pilot results indicate improved targeting of high-risk patients and potential reductions in unnecessary hospital admissions and healthcare costs. However, the initiative remains at pilot stage with limited evidence of nationwide implementation.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-26,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00028,EU_PSTW_202609_00028,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Estonian Parliament (Riigikogu),"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.","Parliamentary administrations are often required to publish accurate verbatim records very quickly after sittings conclude, yet doing so manually places significant pressure on stenographers and can limit both speed and consistency. At a conceptual level, the system converts live spoken contributions into draft verbatim text that can be processed and published more quickly than through manual transcription alone.",EE Parliament- A system for preparing of verbatim reports,"Estonian Parliament (Riigikogu); Central-Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Estonia","The objective of this use case is therefore to improve transcript production efficiency and accuracy while enabling parliamentary speech records to become machine-readable open data. The implementation uses a speech recognition system called Hans, developed with Estonian-language recognition technology, to transcribe speeches delivered from the parliamentary rostrum.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Parliamentary administrations are often required to publish accurate verbatim records very quickly after sittings conclude, yet doing so manually places significant pressure on stenographers and can limit both speed and consistency. In Estonia, the Chancellery of the Riigikogu needed a more efficient way to produce official transcripts within one hour of the end of a sitting and to open up these records for wider digital reuse. The objective of this use case is therefore to improve transcript production efficiency and accuracy while enabling parliamentary speech records to become machine-readable open data. The implementation uses a speech recognition system called Hans, developed with Estonian-language recognition technology, to transcribe speeches delivered from the parliamentary rostrum. At a conceptual level, the system converts live spoken contributions into draft verbatim text that can be processed and published more quickly than through manual transcription alone. It is integrated into the parliamentary reporting workflow and supports both operational publication and downstream data use. The impact includes reduced manual workload for stenographers, faster delivery of official reports, and improved accessibility of parliamentary proceedings. It also increases the scalability and reusability of legislative records by supporting their publication as structured, machine-readable data for use in other systems and public services.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-27,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00029,EU_PSTW_202609_00029,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Estonian Statistical Office,General services.,UNKNOWN,ITI - Chatbot for Statistics Estonia,Estonian Statistical Office; Central-Government; General services.; Estonia,"ITI is know not in use as it has been substituted by Bürokratt, the Estonian national chatbot.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The virtual assistant called ITI, created in collaboration with the Estonian start-up AlphaBlues (now AlfaChat, part of a larger company), shared knowledge regarding the population of Estonia, the size of the pay gap, average wages and consumer price changes, and many other things. Iti also assisted the data provider, whose typical questions are related to activities in the electronic data submission portal and filling in questionnaires. If Iti did not know the answer, it would redirect the query based on its content to either the customer support specialist of the data provider or the user of the statistics. Iti obtained its knowledge from the questions sent through online chats over one year and gets wiser with every query. ITI helped to find information faster thus allowing the customer support of Statistics Estonia to focus on answering more complicated and detailed queries. ITI is know not in use as it has been substituted by Bürokratt, the Estonian national chatbot.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,NOT_IN_USE,미사용,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-28,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00030,EU_PSTW_202609_00030,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Estonian Unemployment Insurance Fund,Unemployment.,"Employment services must decide how best to support jobseekers with very different skills, histories, and barriers to work, yet this can be difficult to do consistently when advisers are handling large caseloads and labour market conditions are changing.",OTT - Predicting employment pathways,Estonian Unemployment Insurance Fund; Central-Government; Unemployment.; Estonia,"The implementation uses machine-learning methods trained on more than 100,000 client records to estimate the probabilities of different employment outcomes and suggest appropriate positions or interventions based on qualifications and profile characteristics.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Employment services must decide how best to support jobseekers with very different skills, histories, and barriers to work, yet this can be difficult to do consistently when advisers are handling large caseloads and labour market conditions are changing. OTT was developed by the Estonian Unemployment Insurance Fund to improve understanding of client needs and to help identify the most suitable employment pathways for individual jobseekers. The objective is to support more accurate job recommendations, better targeting of retraining or reskilling measures, and more effective allocation of staff attention across client groups. The implementation uses machine-learning methods trained on more than 100,000 client records to estimate the probabilities of different employment outcomes and suggest appropriate positions or interventions based on qualifications and profile characteristics. At a conceptual level, the system combines client data, pathway prediction, and recommendation logic within a tool used by civil servants rather than as a fully autonomous public-facing service. It helps structure client segmentation, identify likely needs, and distribute workload more effectively among advisers. The impact includes more tailored support for jobseekers, improved matching to vacancies, and better-informed retraining guidance. It also enhances service efficiency, supports prioritisation of cases where intervention is most needed, and creates business value by making employment support more targeted and data-driven.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-29,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00031,EU_PSTW_202609_00031,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Ministry of Education and Research,General services.,"Public institutions manage very large document collections, and identifying files that have been published without authorisation, or that still contain personal or restricted information, is difficult to do manually at scale. In Estonia, this challenge arose in relation to document management audits and the need to detect internal documents, personal data, and other content that should not have been publicly disclosed.",Texta Toolkit - Identifying documents published without authorisation,Ministry of Education and Research; Central-Government; General services.; Estonia,"The objective of this use case is to strengthen information governance by automating large-scale document review, supporting anonymisation, and enabling safer republication of sensitive materials where appropriate.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Public institutions manage very large document collections, and identifying files that have been published without authorisation, or that still contain personal or restricted information, is difficult to do manually at scale. In Estonia, this challenge arose in relation to document management audits and the need to detect internal documents, personal data, and other content that should not have been publicly disclosed. The objective of this use case is to strengthen information governance by automating large-scale document review, supporting anonymisation, and enabling safer republication of sensitive materials where appropriate. The implementation used the Texta Toolkit, a no-code natural language processing and machine learning platform, to analyse more than 1.2 million documents, convert them into machine-readable form, and identify or remove personal data. The same toolkit was also used in collaboration with justice-sector bodies to anonymise nearly 80,000 court decisions before republication. At a conceptual level, the system combines document ingestion, text extraction, classification, and anonymisation functions within a scalable analysis environment for public-sector records. The impact includes much faster auditing of large repositories, lower manual effort, and reduced risk of unauthorised disclosure. It also improves compliance, supports open-data publication in safer forms, and creates sustained operational value by giving institutions reusable tools for document governance and privacy protection.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-30,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00032,EU_PSTW_202609_00032,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Police and Border Guard,Police Services,"Road safety enforcement depends on the rapid and consistent identification of speeding offences, yet manual processing of camera evidence can be slow, resource-intensive, and difficult to scale across large traffic networks. The impact includes faster handling of offences, reduced manual workload, and more scalable traffic enforcement operations.",Datel - Speed cameras for detecting speeding violations,Police and Border Guard; Central-Government; Police Services; Estonia,"In Estonia, this use case was introduced to strengthen the handling of speeding violations by automating the recognition of number plates, the retrieval of case data, and the preparation of administrative steps needed to initiate proceedings. The implementation uses machine learning and image recognition to analyse photographs captured by roadside speed cameras, identify licence plate numbers, match them with the relevant vehicle and owner records, and prepare draft penalty decisions where appropriate.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Road safety enforcement depends on the rapid and consistent identification of speeding offences, yet manual processing of camera evidence can be slow, resource-intensive, and difficult to scale across large traffic networks. In Estonia, this use case was introduced to strengthen the handling of speeding violations by automating the recognition of number plates, the retrieval of case data, and the preparation of administrative steps needed to initiate proceedings. The objective is to improve the efficiency, consistency, and timeliness of speed enforcement while allowing police officers to focus on validation and final decision-making rather than routine processing. The implementation uses machine learning and image recognition to analyse photographs captured by roadside speed cameras, identify licence plate numbers, match them with the relevant vehicle and owner records, and prepare draft penalty decisions where appropriate. At a conceptual level, the solution links visual detection, administrative data access, and case-generation functions into a semi-automated enforcement workflow, with the Police and Border Guard Board carrying out verification before finalising the process. The impact includes faster handling of offences, reduced manual workload, and more scalable traffic enforcement operations. It also improves procedural consistency, supports more reliable road safety monitoring, and creates public value by enabling authorities to respond more efficiently to speeding violations.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,S6_WORKFLOW_PROCESS_AUTOMATION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-31,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00033,EU_PSTW_202609_00033,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Public Transport and Traffic Management Transport Board in Tallinn,Transport,"Urban transport planning requires accurate information on how vehicles move through the city, yet municipalities often lack detailed, continuous data on traffic flows across key entry and exit points. In Tallinn, this created a practical challenge for planning decisions related to congestion, parking, road construction, and other transport measures, as authorities needed better evidence on how many vehicles entered and left the city each day.",Traffic load - Machine vision for collecting information on traffic load,Public Transport and Traffic Management Transport Board in Tallinn; Local Government; Transport; Estonia,"The objective of this use case is to generate a clearer picture of traffic load in order to support more informed and timely planning decisions. The implementation uses machine vision developed in collaboration with Sifr OU, with cameras placed at selected intersections to detect and count different categories of vehicles passing through monitored areas.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Urban transport planning requires accurate information on how vehicles move through the city, yet municipalities often lack detailed, continuous data on traffic flows across key entry and exit points. In Tallinn, this created a practical challenge for planning decisions related to congestion, parking, road construction, and other transport measures, as authorities needed better evidence on how many vehicles entered and left the city each day. The objective of this use case is to generate a clearer picture of traffic load in order to support more informed and timely planning decisions. The implementation uses machine vision developed in collaboration with Sifr OU, with cameras placed at selected intersections to detect and count different categories of vehicles passing through monitored areas. Because no ready-made solution matched Tallinn’s local conditions, the system required a dedicated training phase to adapt the model to the city’s environment and traffic patterns. At a conceptual level, the application combines image capture, object recognition, and traffic counting to classify buses, cars, lorries, and motorcycles, with the potential to expand later to pedestrians and cyclists. The impact includes the creation of traffic intelligence where little or no such information previously existed, enabling better planning, more data-driven decisions, and improved scalability in how the city monitors and manages transport demand.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-32,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00034,EU_PSTW_202609_00034,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Estonian Investment Agency (EIA),Public health services.,"During public emergencies, citizens need quick access to trustworthy and consistent information, yet official websites and helplines can struggle to keep pace with rapidly changing demand and the spread of misinformation. In Estonia, this challenge became particularly acute during the coronavirus outbreak, when several government agencies needed a way to answer frequently asked questions efficiently across different audiences and languages.",SUVE - Chatbot for questions about emergency situations,Estonian Investment Agency (EIA); Central-Government; Public health services.; Estonia,"The objective of this use case is to improve public access to reliable crisis information, reduce confusion, and support coordinated communication during emergency situations. At a conceptual level, the chatbot uses conversational language processing to answer questions in Estonian and English, drawing on official information published by government agencies so that responses remain aligned with authoritative sources.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"During public emergencies, citizens need quick access to trustworthy and consistent information, yet official websites and helplines can struggle to keep pace with rapidly changing demand and the spread of misinformation. In Estonia, this challenge became particularly acute during the coronavirus outbreak, when several government agencies needed a way to answer frequently asked questions efficiently across different audiences and languages. The objective of this use case is to improve public access to reliable crisis information, reduce confusion, and support coordinated communication during emergency situations. The implementation took the form of Suve, an automated chatbot developed rapidly through a hackathon involving government bodies and the Estonian start-up community in the early phase of the pandemic. At a conceptual level, the chatbot uses conversational language processing to answer questions in Estonian and English, drawing on official information published by government agencies so that responses remain aligned with authoritative sources. The system is integrated across the websites of several public institutions, allowing users to access guidance through familiar digital channels. The impact includes faster dissemination of official information, reduced pressure on manual support services, and a better user experience during crisis conditions. It also strengthens the reliability of government communication and helps counter misinformation by making verified answers easier to obtain.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-34,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00035,EU_PSTW_202609_00035,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Helsinki University Hospital,Hospital services.,"Citizens, visitors, and businesses increasingly expect public services to be available at any time and through multiple digital channels, yet municipal support is often fragmented across websites, offices, and service teams. In Oulu, this created a need for a more accessible way to guide users across everyday matters relating to tourism, business services, and municipal information without depending solely on working hours or manual support.",Helsinki HUH - Intensive care for premature baby,Helsinki University Hospital; Local Government; Hospital services.; Finland,"The objective of this use case is to provide continuous, multilingual access to guidance and electronic services while improving the overall availability and convenience of city support.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Citizens, visitors, and businesses increasingly expect public services to be available at any time and through multiple digital channels, yet municipal support is often fragmented across websites, offices, and service teams. In Oulu, this created a need for a more accessible way to guide users across everyday matters relating to tourism, business services, and municipal information without depending solely on working hours or manual support. The objective of this use case is to provide continuous, multilingual access to guidance and electronic services while improving the overall availability and convenience of city support. The implementation resulted in OuluBot, a virtual assistant developed through a project co-financed by Business Finland. At a conceptual level, the chatbot retrieves and combines information from city websites, prior chat interactions, guidance materials, databases, and other information sources in order to answer questions or direct users to the right service. It operates continuously across channels and, when it cannot resolve a request, can pass the conversation during office hours to the city’s general counselling service. The impact includes faster access to municipal and business information, reduced dependency on staff for routine questions, and a more seamless digital experience for users. It also improves service scalability, supports more consistent communication, and strengthens the city’s ability to offer responsive support across time, place, language, and device.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-37,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00036,EU_PSTW_202609_00036,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Oulu,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.",UNKNOWN,OuluBot - Virtual assistant for business,"Municipality of Oulu; Local Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Finland",UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The City of Oulu initiated a project co-financed by Business Finland, which brought to the development of a chatbot service, OuluBot. OuluBot is a virtual assistant in all everyday issues, whether they concern tourism, companies or city services. The answer or guidance to an electronic service is provided regardless of time, place, medium and language. Technically, chatbot retrieves information e.g. relevant City of Oulu websites, chat history, guidance information, databases, and other information sources. OuluBot is available 24/7, independently of working hours. If the user is looking for information that OuluBot would not be able to answer, then during office hours the discussion could be directed to the City of Oulu chat service (Oulu10 general counseling customer service).",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-38,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00037,EU_PSTW_202609_00037,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Munipality of Tampere and Turku,Health n.e.c.,"Public services are often organised around administrative structures rather than around the real-life situations people face, which can make it difficult for citizens to find timely, relevant support across different organisations.",AuroraAI - Programme with many AI examples.,Munipality of Tampere and Turku; Local Government; Health n.e.c.; Finland,"Within the AuroraAI programme, one experimental use case focused on the life event of moving to a place of study, where students may need help that cuts across housing, mobility, wellbeing, and local services.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Public services are often organised around administrative structures rather than around the real-life situations people face, which can make it difficult for citizens to find timely, relevant support across different organisations. Within the AuroraAI programme, one experimental use case focused on the life event of moving to a place of study, where students may need help that cuts across housing, mobility, wellbeing, and local services. The objective was to understand student needs more holistically and to design a cross-cutting public service offering that responded better to the factors shaping wellbeing during this transition. The implementation combined wellbeing survey data collected in Tampere and Turku with clustering and analytical methods to identify groups of students requiring different kinds of support. The findings showed that issues such as the reliability of public transport and the quality of the natural environment played an important role in student wellbeing. Based on these insights, a bot was created to provide information on the topics students considered most important. The impact of the experiment lies less in large-scale uptake than in the lessons learned: it demonstrated the value of user insight, data analytics, and life-event-based service design, while also highlighting the need for stronger user experience capabilities and more proactive engagement if municipalities are to make cross-cutting digital services more effective and widely used.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-39,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00038,EU_PSTW_202609_00038,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,French Customs,Police Services,Customs authorities process very large numbers of import declarations and must identify potentially fraudulent cases without slowing down legitimate trade unnecessarily.,French Fraud - Fraud detection value declarations,French Customs; Central-Government; Police Services; France,"The objective of this use case is to strengthen risk detection, support targeted enforcement, and improve the efficiency of customs operations by focusing attention on the declarations most likely to involve non-compliance. The implementation uses a supervised deep learning model to analyse customs value declarations and related data for anomalies, suspicious patterns, and indicators of fraud.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Customs authorities process very large numbers of import declarations and must identify potentially fraudulent cases without slowing down legitimate trade unnecessarily. In France, this created a need for a more effective way to detect irregularities in declared values and identify possible cases of identity fraud or import trafficking within complex customs workflows. The objective of this use case is to strengthen risk detection, support targeted enforcement, and improve the efficiency of customs operations by focusing attention on the declarations most likely to involve non-compliance. The implementation uses a supervised deep learning model to analyse customs value declarations and related data for anomalies, suspicious patterns, and indicators of fraud. At a conceptual level, the solution combines historical enforcement knowledge with predictive analysis to assess declaration risk and help customs officers prioritise cases for further examination. Rather than replacing investigative judgement, the model functions as a decision-support layer within a broader customs control process, enabling staff to concentrate resources where the likelihood of irregularity is highest. The impact includes improved capacity to detect fraud, faster identification of high-risk transactions, and more efficient allocation of control resources. It also supports the scalability of customs supervision, contributes to stronger border and revenue protection, and creates operational value by helping enforcement teams manage growing declaration volumes with greater precision and consistency.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-41,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00039,EU_PSTW_202609_00039,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Interministerial Center for IT Services Relating to Human Resources,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.","The implementation improves accessibility of information, reduces administrative burden, and enhances user experience by enabling faster and more intuitive access to HR guidance across the public sector.",RenoiRH - Chatbot in HR management rules to facilitate access,"Interministerial Center for IT Services Relating to Human Resources; Central-Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; France",This use case aims to simplify access to HR information and improve administrative efficiency for public employees.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The French Interministerial Centre for IT Services Relating to Human Resources (CISIRH) manages complex HR systems across the public administration, where staff must navigate extensive regulatory frameworks governing careers, mobility, and administrative procedures. Accessing accurate and timely information can be challenging due to the volume and complexity of rules. This use case aims to simplify access to HR information and improve administrative efficiency for public employees. The solution includes a conversational interface that enables users to query HR rules and procedures in natural language. Natural language processing techniques are used to interpret user queries and retrieve relevant information from structured HR knowledge bases and regulatory documents. The chatbot is integrated into existing HR information systems, providing real-time assistance. The implementation improves accessibility of information, reduces administrative burden, and enhances user experience by enabling faster and more intuitive access to HR guidance across the public sector.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-42,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00040,EU_PSTW_202609_00040,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Ministry of the Economy, Finance and Recovery - General Directorate of Enterprises (DGE) / Ministry of Labor (DGEFP)","General economic, commercial and labour affair","Companies in financial difficulty often show warning signs before a crisis becomes visible, yet public support bodies may identify those signals too late to offer effective assistance. At a conceptual level, the solution analyses relevant variables associated with business fragility, generates alerts on firms likely to be entering difficulty, and feeds these insights into the work of decentralised public actors responsible for business support.",SignauxFaibles - Predictive analysis of business difficulties,"Ministry of the Economy, Finance and Recovery - General Directorate of Enterprises (DGE) / Ministry of Labor (DGEFP); Central-Government; General economic, commercial and labour affair; France","In France, this use case was developed to help public actors detect vulnerable small and medium-sized enterprises earlier and intervene before deterioration becomes irreversible. The impact includes earlier detection of companies needing help, more effective use of support resources, and stronger public coordination around economic resilience.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Companies in financial difficulty often show warning signs before a crisis becomes visible, yet public support bodies may identify those signals too late to offer effective assistance. In France, this use case was developed to help public actors detect vulnerable small and medium-sized enterprises earlier and intervene before deterioration becomes irreversible. The objective is to strengthen preventive support for businesses by identifying weak signals of distress, improving coordination between institutions, and enabling more timely outreach to company managers. The implementation combines a detection algorithm with an automated data-loading system capable of refreshing information regularly from operational databases such as URSSAF, DIRECCTE, and Banque de France. At a conceptual level, the solution analyses relevant variables associated with business fragility, generates alerts on firms likely to be entering difficulty, and feeds these insights into the work of decentralised public actors responsible for business support. The approach was designed to identify a small but meaningful number of new at-risk SMEs each month within a regional context, demonstrating a targeted and dynamic monitoring capability. The impact includes earlier detection of companies needing help, more effective use of support resources, and stronger public coordination around economic resilience. It also supports digital transformation in state action by creating a more proactive, scalable, and evidence-based way to assist weakened businesses.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-43,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00041,EU_PSTW_202609_00041,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,National Old Age Insurance Fund (Cnav),"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.","Retirees often need straightforward access to information about payments, declared income, and direct debits, yet conventional customer-service channels may be available only during limited hours and can struggle to respond quickly to large volumes of recurring enquiries.",Aria - Chatbot answering retired people,"National Old Age Insurance Fund (Cnav); Central-Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; France",The French National Old Age Insurance Fund introduced this use case to make support more accessible and to help pensioners obtain practical information without waiting for standard service windows.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Retirees often need straightforward access to information about payments, declared income, and direct debits, yet conventional customer-service channels may be available only during limited hours and can struggle to respond quickly to large volumes of recurring enquiries. The French National Old Age Insurance Fund introduced this use case to make support more accessible and to help pensioners obtain practical information without waiting for standard service windows. The objective is to improve the availability, speed, and convenience of pension-related assistance while reducing pressure on traditional customer-support operations. The implementation centres on Aria, a chatbot designed to answer questions from retirees and provide personalised information on topics such as payment schedules, income declarations, and debit arrangements. At a conceptual level, the service combines conversational interaction with access to relevant account or process information, allowing users to obtain responses through a digital interface beyond ordinary office hours. The solution is also being extended to cover a broader range of topics and user groups, indicating a gradual expansion of its role within pension service delivery. The impact includes quicker access to information, a more flexible and user-friendly support experience, and lower demand on human service teams for routine enquiries. It also improves service scalability, supports continuity of access outside working hours, and creates public value by making essential pension information easier to obtain.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F06_HIGH_VOLUME_SCALABILITY_BOTTLENECK,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-44,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00042,EU_PSTW_202609_00042,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Public transport,Health n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,Datakalab - Covid-19 Mask wearing detection In some French cities,Public transport; Central-Government; Health n.e.c.; France,"During the Covid-19 pandemic, the use of surveillance cameras was authorised to monitor whether passengers on public transport respected the requirement to wear masks.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"During the Covid-19 pandemic, the use of surveillance cameras was authorised to monitor whether passengers on public transport respected the requirement to wear masks. However, France’s data protection watchdog CNIL called out the practice, saying it was not governed by any legal framework and was therefore illegal. The authority began a three-month test of its software from French tech company Datakalab this week at the Chatelet-Les-Halles station in the heart of Paris, normally used by about 33 million passengers per year. Monitors will have access to a dashboard with the proportion of riders believed to be wearing masks. The plan was making mask wearing mandatory in public transport from May 2019. AI was regarded to be able to supplement enforcement agents in stations. Still, CNIL, the government body in charge of privacy, raised concerns that this type of technology carries a risk that the identity of the people being analyzed could be reconstructed.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,NOT_IN_USE,미사용,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-45,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00043,EU_PSTW_202609_00043,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Pôle emploi,Unemployment.,UNKNOWN,LaBonneBoîte - Identification of companies with a high probability of hiring,Pôle emploi; Central-Government; Unemployment.; France,"In France, this use case was created to help people identify employers likely to recruit in the near future, even where no vacancy has yet been posted. At a conceptual level, the tool uses predictive modelling over labour market and employer data to estimate hiring probability and then presents these employers through a searchable digital interface.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Many job opportunities are never formally advertised, which means jobseekers can miss suitable openings if they rely only on published vacancies. In France, this use case was created to help people identify employers likely to recruit in the near future, even where no vacancy has yet been posted. The objective is to improve access to hidden job opportunities, strengthen spontaneous application strategies, and increase the chances that jobseekers secure interviews by targeting firms with a high probability of hiring. The implementation takes the form of an online service that predicts which companies are likely to recruit within the next six months in a given sector and area. At a conceptual level, the tool uses predictive modelling over labour market and employer data to estimate hiring probability and then presents these employers through a searchable digital interface. Its code is reusable and an API allows the service to be replicated on other websites, increasing its portability and reach. The impact includes better targeting of job-search efforts, improved access to opportunities beyond traditional vacancy listings, and stronger support for proactive labour-market behaviour. It also enhances scalability by allowing the service to be reused across platforms and user groups, while creating clear business value by helping more people connect with employers likely to recruit in the near term.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-46,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00045,EU_PSTW_202609_00045,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,The General Food Directorate (DGAL) of the Ministry of Agriculture and Food,Public health services.,"Food safety authorities must prioritise inspections across large numbers of restaurants, food service businesses, and distributors, yet traditional inspection planning can struggle to identify which establishments present the greatest immediate risk.",IAlim - Targeting restaurant health inspections,The General Food Directorate (DGAL) of the Ministry of Agriculture and Food; Central-Government; Public health services.; France,"In France, this use case was introduced to help Ministry of Agriculture inspectors target health inspections more effectively in the direct-to-consumer delivery sector, where the rapid growth of online ordering and platform-based services has made oversight more complex. The implementation uses analytical models to process consumer feedback gathered from online notification and review sites, treating these data as signals that can help identify hygiene-related concerns and possible inspection priorities.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Food safety authorities must prioritise inspections across large numbers of restaurants, food service businesses, and distributors, yet traditional inspection planning can struggle to identify which establishments present the greatest immediate risk. In France, this use case was introduced to help Ministry of Agriculture inspectors target health inspections more effectively in the direct-to-consumer delivery sector, where the rapid growth of online ordering and platform-based services has made oversight more complex. The objective is to refine inspection selection, improve the allocation of control resources, and strengthen consumer protection by focusing attention on establishments more likely to require intervention. The implementation uses analytical models to process consumer feedback gathered from online notification and review sites, treating these data as signals that can help identify hygiene-related concerns and possible inspection priorities. At a conceptual level, the system combines digital complaint monitoring with risk-targeting logic to support inspectors in deciding which premises should be checked first. Rather than replacing formal inspection procedures, it functions as a decision-support tool within the broader public health control framework. The impact includes more precise targeting of inspections, reduced inefficiency in inspection planning, and better use of public resources. It also improves the scalability and responsiveness of food safety oversight, helping authorities act more quickly on emerging concerns in a fast-changing sector.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-48,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00046,EU_PSTW_202609_00046,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,French Supreme Court,Law courts,"The impact includes wider public access to case law, more reusable legal data for research and legal-tech services, and a more scalable publication process than manual redaction alone.","OpenJustice - Open case law, pseudonymization and enrichment of court decisions",French Supreme Court; Central-Government; Law courts; France,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The French open-data programme for judicial decisions addresses a structural tension between transparency and privacy: courts must make case law more accessible and reusable, while ensuring that parties and other individuals mentioned in decisions are adequately protected. Led by the Court of Cassation and the Ministry of Justice, the objective is to enable broad publication of court decisions, support legal innovation and legal-tech reuse, and strengthen consistency, accessibility, and public confidence in the judiciary. The implementation combines rule-based and machine-learning techniques to detect and pseudonymise identifying information before publication, within the wider open-data infrastructure for judicial decisions. Official French sources state that the Court of Cassation is responsible for implementing the release of decisions, while the Ministry of Justice describes an ongoing publication rollout and public reporting notes that the release calendar runs by court branch and jurisdiction type, with deployment continuing through the different court systems. This makes the programme a staged implementation rather than a single completed launch. The impact includes wider public access to case law, more reusable legal data for research and legal-tech services, and a more scalable publication process than manual redaction alone. It also creates long-term value by embedding privacy-preserving automation into judicial transparency at national scale.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-49,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00047,EU_PSTW_202609_00047,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Polish Development Fund (PFR),"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.",UNKNOWN,Poland - Fraud Detection COVID-19 support,"Polish Development Fund (PFR); Central-Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Poland","Emergency business-support schemes introduced during the pandemic had to be deployed quickly and at scale, creating a significant challenge for public authorities seeking to prevent fraud while ensuring that legitimate applicants received support without unnecessary delay. In Poland, this use case was designed to strengthen the control of COVID-19 subsidies by automatically verifying whether applications met the required financial and operational criteria.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Emergency business-support schemes introduced during the pandemic had to be deployed quickly and at scale, creating a significant challenge for public authorities seeking to prevent fraud while ensuring that legitimate applicants received support without unnecessary delay. In Poland, this use case was designed to strengthen the control of COVID-19 subsidies by automatically verifying whether applications met the required financial and operational criteria. The objective is to protect public funds, detect irregular claims, and improve the consistency and speed of subsidy review in a period of exceptional demand. The implementation uses an analytical system that checks statements submitted in applications against financial data held by the National Tax Administration and the Social Insurance Institution, while also examining anomalies such as declared amounts that appear disproportionate to employee numbers. At a conceptual level, the solution assesses whether a reported decline in turnover is plausibly linked to the pandemic rather than to unrelated business problems, and also reviews how subsidy funds are subsequently spent to ensure compliance with the rule that support may be used only for business expenses. The impact includes stronger fraud prevention, faster verification of applications, and more focused use of investigative resources. It also improves the scalability of emergency support controls and reinforces trust in public subsidy programmes by enabling more systematic, data-driven scrutiny.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-50,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00048,EU_PSTW_202609_00048,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Federal Office for Migration and Refugees (BAMF),Public order and safety n.e.c.,"Asylum authorities sometimes need to assess the likely origin of applicants who lack documentation, yet this can be difficult where identity evidence is incomplete and decisions must rely on a combination of interviews, records, and expert judgement.",Germany's Federal Office for Migrants and Refugees - Refugee Language Detection,Federal Office for Migration and Refugees (BAMF); Central-Government; Public order and safety n.e.c.; Germany,"In Germany, this use case was introduced to support the Federal Office for Migrants and Refugees in evaluating claims about country of origin, particularly in cases involving Arabic-speaking applicants whose stated nationality could affect the asylum process. The implementation uses automated speech-analysis software to examine spoken language and identify dialect or linguistic patterns associated with particular countries or regions.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Asylum authorities sometimes need to assess the likely origin of applicants who lack documentation, yet this can be difficult where identity evidence is incomplete and decisions must rely on a combination of interviews, records, and expert judgement. In Germany, this use case was introduced to support the Federal Office for Migrants and Refugees in evaluating claims about country of origin, particularly in cases involving Arabic-speaking applicants whose stated nationality could affect the asylum process. The objective is to strengthen identity assessment and provide additional evidence that can support case handling where documentary proof is missing or uncertain. The implementation uses automated speech-analysis software to examine spoken language and identify dialect or linguistic patterns associated with particular countries or regions. At a conceptual level, the system analyses audio samples alongside other sources of information, such as documentation and existing case-evaluation techniques, and its outputs may be complemented by expert linguistic opinion where necessary. The tool is therefore positioned as an additional decision-support element rather than a standalone determination mechanism. The impact lies in the potential to improve the speed and structure of origin assessment and to help officials prioritise further checks in uncertain cases. At the same time, the use case illustrates the limitations of language-based inference, particularly where vocabulary and dialects change over time, making accuracy and reliability ongoing operational concerns.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-51,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00049,EU_PSTW_202609_00049,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Ministerium für Inneres und Kommunales des Landes Nordrhein-Westfalen,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.",UNKNOWN,Govbot - Administrative search engine for citizens' enquiries,"Ministerium für Inneres und Kommunales des Landes Nordrhein-Westfalen; Central-Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Germany","Citizens frequently struggle to find the right administrative information because public services are spread across many institutions, websites, and levels of government, each using its own terminology and structure. The objective of this use case is to simplify access to government information and responsibilities by creating a clearer and more user-friendly search experience.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Citizens frequently struggle to find the right administrative information because public services are spread across many institutions, websites, and levels of government, each using its own terminology and structure. In North Rhine-Westphalia, this created a need for a more accessible way to help users search for public information, identify the correct authority, and navigate administrative matters without already knowing the official vocabulary. The objective of this use case is to simplify access to government information and responsibilities by creating a clearer and more user-friendly search experience. The implementation uses GovBot as part of an administrative search engine that indexes millions of pages across the websites of the state, districts, municipalities, chambers, and regional associations. At a conceptual level, the solution combines large-scale search indexing, query interpretation, search-term recommendation, and authority mapping to guide users towards the correct administrative terms, the relevant level of government, and the responsible office for a given issue. Search results are complemented by structured information and a map view showing the location of the appropriate authority. The impact includes quicker access to administrative guidance, reduced confusion for citizens, and more efficient navigation across a highly distributed public-service landscape. It also improves service scalability, supports consistency in digital information delivery, and creates value by lowering the effort required for users to find and act on the right public information.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-52,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00050,EU_PSTW_202609_00050,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,State Criminal Police Office (LKA) of North Rhine-Westphalia,Police Services,"Police forces must decide how to deploy limited patrol and prevention resources across large territories, yet burglary and related offences often occur in patterns that are difficult to anticipate through manual analysis alone.",Skala - Predictive policing,State Criminal Police Office (LKA) of North Rhine-Westphalia; Local Government; Police Services; Germany,"In North Rhine-Westphalia, this use case was developed to support policing decisions by identifying areas with a higher probability of residential burglary based on prior incidents and spatial-temporal patterns. The implementation uses the Skala software, which applies analytical methods grounded in past crime data and concepts such as near-repeat theory to generate weekly forecasts for local police authorities.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Police forces must decide how to deploy limited patrol and prevention resources across large territories, yet burglary and related offences often occur in patterns that are difficult to anticipate through manual analysis alone. In North Rhine-Westphalia, this use case was developed to support policing decisions by identifying areas with a higher probability of residential burglary based on prior incidents and spatial-temporal patterns. The objective is to improve prevention, target patrol activity more effectively, and support public advice in neighbourhoods considered vulnerable. The implementation uses the Skala software, which applies analytical methods grounded in past crime data and concepts such as near-repeat theory to generate weekly forecasts for local police authorities. At a conceptual level, the system produces maps highlighting residential districts where burglary risk is expected to be elevated, allowing police to integrate those forecasts into operational planning. The outputs are used to support decisions on patrol deployment and preventive engagement with residents on burglary protection. The impact includes more focused use of police resources, improved situational awareness, and a stronger preventive approach to property crime. It also enhances the scalability of crime analysis across multiple districts, creates greater consistency in how risk information is produced, and provides operational value by helping authorities act proactively rather than only react after offences have occurred.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-53,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00051,EU_PSTW_202609_00051,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Irish Revenue Commissioners,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.","Tax administrations receive large volumes of routine telephone enquiries, and many callers seek straightforward information that can be difficult to provide efficiently through human operators alone, especially during peak demand periods.",VDA - Voicebot on calls from Irish taxpayer on tax clearance,"Irish Revenue Commissioners; Central-Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Ireland","In Ireland, this use case was introduced by the Revenue Commissioners to improve customer experience on tax-clearance enquiries while reducing pressure on telephony services. The implementation uses a Virtual Digital Agent operating as a voicebot for a defined subset of tax-clearance calls.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Tax administrations receive large volumes of routine telephone enquiries, and many callers seek straightforward information that can be difficult to provide efficiently through human operators alone, especially during peak demand periods. In Ireland, this use case was introduced by the Revenue Commissioners to improve customer experience on tax-clearance enquiries while reducing pressure on telephony services. The objective is to provide faster, more effective support for taxpayers seeking guidance on how to apply for tax clearance, check their current status, retrieve access numbers, or understand why an application has expired, been refused, or been rescinded. The implementation uses a Virtual Digital Agent operating as a voicebot for a defined subset of tax-clearance calls. At a conceptual level, the system uses conversational voice processing to interpret callers’ requests, provide the relevant information automatically, and guide users through the appropriate steps without requiring immediate human intervention in every case. Where necessary, calls can still be transferred to a human operator. The impact includes substantial automation of routine call handling, with up to 50 per cent of calls reportedly managed end to end by the voicebot, while only 10 per cent required transfer to an agent. It also improves service availability, reduces administrative workload, and creates business value through faster resolution and more scalable taxpayer support.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F06_HIGH_VOLUME_SCALABILITY_BOTTLENECK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-54,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00052,EU_PSTW_202609_00052,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Dublin,R&D general public services.,UNKNOWN,The Dublin Beat - Citizen opinion analysis,Municipality of Dublin; Local Government; R&D general public services.; Ireland,"In the Dublin region, this use case was designed to provide local authorities with a broader and more continuous picture of citizens’ concerns, perceptions, and priorities by analysing opinions expressed online. The implementation uses the Citibeats text-analytics platform to collect public tweets each month and analyse them using models trained to understand opinion in the local Dublin context.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Public authorities need to understand how residents feel about local issues, yet conventional consultation methods may not always capture timely shifts in public sentiment across a fast-changing urban environment. In the Dublin region, this use case was designed to provide local authorities with a broader and more continuous picture of citizens’ concerns, perceptions, and priorities by analysing opinions expressed online. The objective is to support more responsive policy-making and civic action by identifying how people feel about topics such as environmental issues, cultural events, and regional developments. The implementation uses the Citibeats text-analytics platform to collect public tweets each month and analyse them using models trained to understand opinion in the local Dublin context. At a conceptual level, the system applies natural language analysis to large volumes of online text and presents the resulting insights through visual dashboards and monthly reports, enabling public bodies to detect themes, track changes in sentiment, and explore the issues most frequently discussed. The solution complements, rather than replaces, other forms of citizen engagement by offering a faster and broader layer of evidence. The impact includes improved situational awareness for local authorities, more timely understanding of community concerns, and stronger evidence to inform policy and civic initiatives. It also increases the scalability of public listening and helps connect digital public discourse with practical decision-making.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-55,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00053,EU_PSTW_202609_00053,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""social_nonprofit""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Ministry of Education,R&D education.,"Public administrations often face significant challenges in processing and synthesising large volumes of textual input from citizens, limiting their ability to incorporate feedback into policymaking.",Project PIERINO (Platform for the Extraction and Retrieval of Online Information),Ministry of Education; Central-Government; R&D education.; Italy,"The platform uses natural language processing and semantic analysis techniques to automatically process, classify, and summarise textual data.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The PIERINO project was developed through collaboration between the Bruno Kessler Foundation, the Vrije Universiteit Amsterdam, and institutional partners to support the analysis of large-scale public consultation data. Public administrations often face significant challenges in processing and synthesising large volumes of textual input from citizens, limiting their ability to incorporate feedback into policymaking. The objective of the project was to enable efficient and scalable analysis of qualitative data from consultations. The platform uses natural language processing and semantic analysis techniques to automatically process, classify, and summarise textual data. It was first applied by the Italian Ministry of Education to analyse approximately 270,000 contributions from the “La buona scuola” consultation, significantly reducing processing time. The system integrates modular text processing components designed for reuse in future consultations.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F06_HIGH_VOLUME_SCALABILITY_BOTTLENECK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-56,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00054,EU_PSTW_202609_00054,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Reggia di Caserta,Cultural services.,UNKNOWN,Borbot - Virtual assistant for museums,Reggia di Caserta; Local Government; Cultural services.; Italy,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Borbot (acronym for Bourbons and Robotics) was a virtual assistant, adopted by the National Museum of the Royal Palace of Caserta, which through machine learning techniques was able to answer user questions, providing detailed tourist information and news about the monument. The mobile system supports the Museum's front office service and allows customer service staff to concentrate on the services with greater added value. The service is not available anymore.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,NOT_IN_USE,미사용,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-57,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00055,EU_PSTW_202609_00055,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Regional Government of Apulia,R&D general public services.,"The TALIA Semantic Framework, developed and managed by InnovaPuglia on behalf of the Regional Government of Apulia, addresses the challenge of extracting actionable insights from large volumes of textual documentation produced by projects funded under the Interreg MED Programme. These documents contain valuable knowledge for innovation policy development, but their scale and heterogeneity make manual analysis inefficient and time-consuming.",TALIA SF - Extract and organize knowledge coming from the analysis of textual documents,Regional Government of Apulia; Regional Government; R&D general public services.; Italy,"The system applies natural language processing and semantic analysis techniques to automatically extract, classify, and structure information from unstructured text.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The TALIA Semantic Framework, developed and managed by InnovaPuglia on behalf of the Regional Government of Apulia, addresses the challenge of extracting actionable insights from large volumes of textual documentation produced by projects funded under the Interreg MED Programme. These documents contain valuable knowledge for innovation policy development, but their scale and heterogeneity make manual analysis inefficient and time-consuming. The objective is to support evidence-based policymaking and facilitate the reuse and capitalisation of project results. The system applies natural language processing and semantic analysis techniques to automatically extract, classify, and structure information from unstructured text. It builds a knowledge base that organises project outputs, themes, and relationships, enabling advanced querying and analysis. The framework integrates multiple data sources and supports interoperability with regional innovation systems. While the solution demonstrated potential to improve accessibility and reuse of project knowledge, no recent evidence confirms its continued deployment or operational status.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK|F06_HIGH_VOLUME_SCALABILITY_BOTTLENECK,S3_DOCUMENT_INTELLIGENCE,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-58,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00056,EU_PSTW_202609_00056,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""social_nonprofit""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Veneto Region,R&D health.,"Healthcare systems produce vast quantities of clinical documentation, and important diagnostic information is often embedded in free-text discharge letters that are difficult to process consistently at scale. At a conceptual level, the solution combines document analysis, medical term recognition, and coding support to structure unstructured clinical text and feed that information into the regional electronic health record ecosystem.",Noovle - Management of health documentation,Veneto Region; Local Government; R&D health.; Italy,"The objective of this use case is to identify the salient parts of discharge documentation automatically, map diagnoses to standard coding systems, and make health data more usable for clinical and population-level purposes. The implementation uses machine learning techniques to analyse discharge letters and identify diagnoses according to the ICD-9-CM international classification.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Healthcare systems produce vast quantities of clinical documentation, and important diagnostic information is often embedded in free-text discharge letters that are difficult to process consistently at scale. In the Veneto Region, this created a need to extract clinically relevant information more efficiently so that electronic health records could better support both care delivery and regional health governance. The objective of this use case is to identify the salient parts of discharge documentation automatically, map diagnoses to standard coding systems, and make health data more usable for clinical and population-level purposes. The implementation uses machine learning techniques to analyse discharge letters and identify diagnoses according to the ICD-9-CM international classification. At a conceptual level, the solution combines document analysis, medical term recognition, and coding support to structure unstructured clinical text and feed that information into the regional electronic health record ecosystem. This creates a foundation for applications that can support clinical decision-making while also strengthening prevention, planning, and population medicine at governance level. The impact includes more efficient processing of medical documentation, improved quality and usability of clinical data, and stronger support for interoperable digital health services. It also enhances scalability in health information management and creates value by turning routine documentation into structured knowledge for both frontline care and strategic health-system oversight.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F04_UNSTRUCTURED_INFORMATION_COMPLEXITY,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-59,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00057,EU_PSTW_202609_00057,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Regional Conference for Research and Innovation,R&D general public services.,"Research, innovation, and higher-education ecosystems generate complex and distributed data, yet this information is often difficult for non-specialists to explore because databases are organised around technical structures rather than intuitive user needs.","Toscana Open Research - Allows users to access information through ""queries""",Regional Conference for Research and Innovation; Local Government; R&D general public services.; Italy,"In Tuscany, this use case was created to improve transparency and make the regional research and innovation system easier to understand for a wide range of users, including citizens, institutions, and other stakeholders. The implementation uses an integrated data model based on domain ontology, allowing information from different sources to be linked conceptually and queried through user-oriented expressions.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Research, innovation, and higher-education ecosystems generate complex and distributed data, yet this information is often difficult for non-specialists to explore because databases are organised around technical structures rather than intuitive user needs. In Tuscany, this use case was created to improve transparency and make the regional research and innovation system easier to understand for a wide range of users, including citizens, institutions, and other stakeholders. The objective is to support more inclusive governance and easier access to information by enabling people to search and query the system without needing specialist knowledge of how the underlying databases are organised. The implementation uses an integrated data model based on domain ontology, allowing information from different sources to be linked conceptually and queried through user-oriented expressions. At a conceptual level, the ontology acts as a semantic layer between users and the physical databases, translating queries into structured access paths without requiring users to understand technical schemas or internal data relationships. This makes the portal a more accessible interface for exploring the Tuscan landscape of research, innovation, and higher education. The impact includes improved discoverability of public information, greater transparency, and a more user-friendly experience for navigating complex institutional data. It also supports reusability and interoperability of information, while creating public value through more open and intelligible digital access to knowledge about the regional innovation ecosystem.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-60,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00058,EU_PSTW_202609_00058,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Bologna, Trento, Rovereto Municipalities",Transport,UNKNOWN,Lume PlannER - Navigation planning assistant,"Bologna, Trento, Rovereto Municipalities; Local Government; Transport; Italy","This use case was developed to help travellers choose routes that balance travel time with environmental considerations, giving people personalised guidance on the most efficient and sustainable way to reach a destination. At a conceptual level, the solution uses analytical and optimisation techniques to process live and contextual mobility data, compare transport options, and generate personalised route suggestions that reduce both emissions and journey times.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Urban mobility users increasingly need journey-planning tools that do more than identify the quickest route, especially in cities aiming to reduce congestion and emissions while promoting public and shared transport. This use case was developed to help travellers choose routes that balance travel time with environmental considerations, giving people personalised guidance on the most efficient and sustainable way to reach a destination. The objective is to support cleaner and smarter mobility choices by making multimodal journey planning more adaptive and relevant to real urban conditions. The implementation takes the form of a smartphone-based planning application that analyses data from multiple sources and recommends combinations of public, private, or shared transport according to speed and ecological impact. At a conceptual level, the solution uses analytical and optimisation techniques to process live and contextual mobility data, compare transport options, and generate personalised route suggestions that reduce both emissions and journey times. The app is reported to have been used in cities such as Trento and Rovereto as well as in the Emilia-Romagna region, illustrating its adaptability across local mobility settings. The impact includes better user guidance, more informed transport choices, and support for environmentally responsible travel behaviour. It also improves the scalability of smart mobility services and creates public value by aligning everyday navigation with broader sustainability and urban-planning goals.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-61,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00059,EU_PSTW_202609_00059,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""social_nonprofit""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Culture Information Systems Centre Latvia,General services.,"Public administrations often struggle to provide consistent, multilingual digital support across agencies, particularly when regulations change frequently and institutions must keep guidance up to date across many separate websites and service channels.",Hugo - Virtual Assistant for governments,Culture Information Systems Centre Latvia; Central-Government; General services.; Latvia,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Public administrations often struggle to provide consistent, multilingual digital support across agencies, particularly when regulations change frequently and institutions must keep guidance up to date across many separate websites and service channels. In Latvia, the Culture Information Systems Centre developed Hugo.lv as a shared language-technology and virtual-assistant platform for state administration, with the aim of giving public bodies a reusable foundation for digital interaction rather than requiring each institution to build its own standalone solution. The objective is to improve accessibility, consistency, and efficiency in public communication while helping institutions adapt language technologies to their own operational needs. The implementation combines machine translation, speech recognition, speech synthesis, and virtual-assistant capabilities within a common software-as-a-service model for public authorities. At a conceptual level, the platform provides shared natural-language infrastructure that institutions can configure for their own services, while also supporting multilingual e-services and conversational interfaces. Because regulatory content changes over time, the approach depends on institutions maintaining an accurate and current knowledge base. The impact includes lower duplication of effort across government, improved scalability of digital services, and more consistent citizen access to information. It also strengthens the public sector’s language-technology capability and creates value through a common platform that can be adapted across multiple agencies.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-62,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00060,EU_PSTW_202609_00060,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Register of Enterprises of the Republic of Latvia,"General economic, commercial and labour affair",UNKNOWN,UNA - Chatbot for process enterprise registration,"Register of Enterprises of the Republic of Latvia; Central-Government; General economic, commercial and labour affair; Latvia",UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"In 2018, the Register of Enterprises of Latvia introduced the Chatbot UNA (Future assistant for entrepreneurs - FAE) to answer frequently asked questions regarding the process of enterprise registration. Chatbot is available 24/7 and thus able to make communication between citizens and the state accessible and friendly. UNA was available on both the website of the Register of Enterprises as well as on the Facebook page as part of the Facebook messenger application. UNA was able to answer frequently asked questions about the registration of their businesses as well as the liquidation, merchants, companies and organizations. If citizens already have an application in progress, they are also able to ask about the progress of their documents. UNA only worked in the Latvian language. According to the first performance indicators, 44% of the questions asked on UNA were considered to be general of nature and easily taken care of by the Chatbot.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,NOT_IN_USE,미사용,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-63,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00061,EU_PSTW_202609_00061,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Rural Support Service (“LAD”),Transport,"Processing aid applications in the agricultural sector often requires public authorities to check eligibility, verify company-related information, and ensure that beneficiaries have not been subject to relevant penalties, all of which can be administratively burdensome when handled manually at scale. The objective is to reduce manual effort in assessing applicants, strengthen control over eligibility-related conditions, and support more efficient administration of aid schemes.",VARIS - Reviews all the applicants to Rural Support Service,Rural Support Service (“LAD”); Central-Government; Transport; Latvia,"The implementation uses a virtual assistant that reviews applicants recorded in the Rural Support Service database, retrieves information about responsible company representatives from the Lursoft database, and checks penalty information before inserting the consolidated results back into the service’s own system. At a conceptual level, the solution links multiple administrative databases and automates routine cross-checking tasks that would otherwise require repeated manual verification.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Processing aid applications in the agricultural sector often requires public authorities to check eligibility, verify company-related information, and ensure that beneficiaries have not been subject to relevant penalties, all of which can be administratively burdensome when handled manually at scale. In Latvia, the Rural Support Service introduced VARIS to streamline these checks and improve the consistency and speed of application review. The objective is to reduce manual effort in assessing applicants, strengthen control over eligibility-related conditions, and support more efficient administration of aid schemes. The implementation uses a virtual assistant that reviews applicants recorded in the Rural Support Service database, retrieves information about responsible company representatives from the Lursoft database, and checks penalty information before inserting the consolidated results back into the service’s own system. At a conceptual level, the solution links multiple administrative databases and automates routine cross-checking tasks that would otherwise require repeated manual verification. This creates a structured workflow in which relevant data are gathered, validated, and returned to the core application-handling process. The impact includes faster case preparation, reduced administrative burden, and more standardised review of applicants. It also improves the scalability of aid administration and creates operational value by enabling public officials to focus more of their time on exceptions, judgement-based tasks, and more complex cases.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-64,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00062,EU_PSTW_202609_00062,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,State Public Traffic Safety Directorate,Transport,"Road safety authorities need reliable ways to enforce rules on speeding, insurance, and vehicle roadworthiness, yet manual roadside control is resource-intensive and cannot provide the same breadth of coverage as automated monitoring.",FITS ITEMS - Road safety,State Public Traffic Safety Directorate; Central-Government; Transport; Latvia,"The implementation uses automated number-plate recognition and related analytical functions to identify vehicles in relation to speed, insurance status, and roadworthiness-test compliance. The use case is presented as part of a broader intelligent transport approach rather than as a single isolated camera tool.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Road safety authorities need reliable ways to enforce rules on speeding, insurance, and vehicle roadworthiness, yet manual roadside control is resource-intensive and cannot provide the same breadth of coverage as automated monitoring. In Latvia, the State Public Traffic Safety Directorate adopted FITS ITEMS to improve control on the road network and strengthen preventive enforcement in support of safer driving behaviour. The objective is to detect non-compliance more efficiently, increase the effectiveness of traffic supervision, and reduce accidents through more systematic monitoring. The implementation uses automated number-plate recognition and related analytical functions to identify vehicles in relation to speed, insurance status, and roadworthiness-test compliance. At a conceptual level, the system combines roadside camera infrastructure, image recognition, and back-office processing to identify vehicles and connect them to the relevant compliance checks needed for enforcement activity. The use case is presented as part of a broader intelligent transport approach rather than as a single isolated camera tool. Reported results indicate that, by the end of 2018, road accidents had decreased by 43 per cent at roadside sections where photo cameras were installed and operating, falling from 1,084 to 613. The wider impact includes stronger road safety, more efficient enforcement operations, and better use of public resources through automated control at scale.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-65,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00063,EU_PSTW_202609_00063,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,State Revenue Service,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.","Tax administrations receive very large volumes of declarations and supporting documents, and reviewing each submission manually can limit efficiency, delay compliant cases, and divert expert attention away from higher-risk filings.",Automatic decision making of the Electronic Declaration System of the State Revenue Service,"State Revenue Service; Central-Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Latvia","In Latvia, the Electronic Declaration System of the State Revenue Service was designed to automate part of this process by verifying submitted declarations and filtering out cases that do not show signs of non-compliance. In the use case description, up to two thirds of declarations that reveal no non-compliance can be approved through this automated decision-making approach, while experts concentrate on the remaining cases.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Tax administrations receive very large volumes of declarations and supporting documents, and reviewing each submission manually can limit efficiency, delay compliant cases, and divert expert attention away from higher-risk filings. In Latvia, the Electronic Declaration System of the State Revenue Service was designed to automate part of this process by verifying submitted declarations and filtering out cases that do not show signs of non-compliance. The objective is to accelerate routine processing, improve service efficiency for citizens and businesses, and allow specialists to focus on the declarations most likely to require closer examination. The implementation relies on automated verification rules within the Electronic Declaration System, which checks the correctness of document completion and informs users of the result. In the use case description, up to two thirds of declarations that reveal no non-compliance can be approved through this automated decision-making approach, while experts concentrate on the remaining cases. At a conceptual level, the system combines digital submission, validation, and compliance screening within the tax administration workflow. The impact includes shorter processing times for straightforward filings, better allocation of expert capacity, and more scalable tax administration. It also creates value by improving the user experience for compliant taxpayers and by supporting more targeted oversight of declarations where inconsistencies or risks are detected.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK|F06_HIGH_VOLUME_SCALABILITY_BOTTLENECK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-66,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00064,EU_PSTW_202609_00064,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,State Revenue Service,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.","The objective is to make common information easier to obtain, reduce the burden on employees answering standard questions, and improve convenience for users seeking help outside normal service hours.",Toms - Chatbot facilitating customer communication with the State Revenue service,"State Revenue Service; Central-Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Latvia","The implementation uses a virtual assistant integrated into the service environment to answer frequently asked questions about topics such as registration of economic operators, use of the Electronic Declaration System, annual income statements, and eligible expenditure.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Revenue agencies handle high volumes of repetitive public enquiries, ranging from office locations and opening hours to questions about digital filing, annual income declarations, and eligible expenditure. Without a self-service layer, these routine contacts can consume significant staff time and reduce the speed of support for more complex cases. In Latvia, the State Revenue Service introduced Toms to improve customer communication and provide continuous access to general tax-related guidance. The objective is to make common information easier to obtain, reduce the burden on employees answering standard questions, and improve convenience for users seeking help outside normal service hours. The implementation uses a virtual assistant integrated into the service environment to answer frequently asked questions about topics such as registration of economic operators, use of the Electronic Declaration System, annual income statements, and eligible expenditure. At a conceptual level, the solution combines a conversational interface with a structured knowledge base covering standard administrative topics, allowing users to receive guidance automatically at any time. The impact includes faster access to routine information, lower demand on human advisers for repetitive queries, and a more consistent digital support experience. It also improves the scalability of taxpayer assistance and creates operational value by shifting high-volume, low-complexity interactions towards automated service channels.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-67,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00065,EU_PSTW_202609_00065,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Latvian Investment and Development Agency (LAA),Hospital services.,UNKNOWN,LAA - Pilot lung cancer risk assessment,Latvian Investment and Development Agency (LAA); Central-Government; Hospital services.; Latvia,"In 2018, Latvia launched a pilot project to improve lung cancer risk assessment, early diagnosis, and forecasting using large-scale data analysis. Such a centre would leverage a national data lake to drive European-level research and produce unique genomic data.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"In 2018, Latvia launched a pilot project to improve lung cancer risk assessment, early diagnosis, and forecasting using large-scale data analysis. Since treatment is most effective when detected early, the project applied machine learning for cancer diagnostics. Backed by EUR 600K from the Latvian Investment and Development Agency, it also paved the way for a potential genomics centre with advanced infrastructure. Such a centre would leverage a national data lake to drive European-level research and produce unique genomic data.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-68,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00066,EU_PSTW_202609_00066,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Government of the Republic of Lithuania,Public health services.,"The expected impact was faster public access to critical information, reduced demand on manual support channels and a better user experience during a highly disruptive period.",viLTe' - Chatbot answering relevant questions regarding the COVID-19,Government of the Republic of Lithuania; Central-Government; Public health services.; Latvia,"The use case focused on supporting the general public by reducing confusion, improving access to essential information and easing pressure on traditional information channels that were likely to be overwhelmed during the health emergency. The solution was implemented as a chatbot using conversational language-processing capabilities to interpret user queries and match them to relevant, pre-structured information on COVID-19 symptoms, rules and restrictions.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Created during the “Hack the Crisis” hackathon, viLTe' was designed to address an urgent public-information problem during the COVID-19 pandemic: citizens needed fast, reliable and easy-to-understand answers about symptoms, restrictions and related public-health measures at a time of high uncertainty and rapidly changing guidance. The use case focused on supporting the general public by reducing confusion, improving access to essential information and easing pressure on traditional information channels that were likely to be overwhelmed during the health emergency. The stakeholders included citizens, public authorities and health-information providers, with the objective of offering an accessible and responsive digital point of contact capable of answering common pandemic-related questions quickly and consistently. The solution was implemented as a chatbot using conversational language-processing capabilities to interpret user queries and match them to relevant, pre-structured information on COVID-19 symptoms, rules and restrictions. At a conceptual level, the system combined question recognition, intent matching and automated response delivery so that users could receive immediate answers without navigating lengthy websites or waiting for human support. The expected impact was faster public access to critical information, reduced demand on manual support channels and a better user experience during a highly disruptive period. More broadly, viLTe' demonstrated the value of lightweight conversational tools in crisis communication by improving responsiveness, scalability and the accessibility of public-health information.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,NOT_IN_USE,미사용,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-69,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00067,EU_PSTW_202609_00067,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Financial Crimes Investigation Service (FNTT),Public order and safety n.e.c.,"Financial Crime Investigation Service under Lithuanian Customs sought to strengthen its ability to detect suspicious trade and financial-flow patterns across fragmented and high-volume datasets, in a context where investigators and other law-enforcement units needed faster, better-prioritised intelligence to support operational decisions. The objective was to support analysts and decision-makers with a targeted data-processing and analysis capability able to surface anomalies, relationships and priorities that would otherwise remain difficult to detect manually.","FCIS - Machine learning to identify suspicious patterns (trade, financial flows)",Financial Crimes Investigation Service (FNTT); Central-Government; Public order and safety n.e.c.; Lithuania,"The use case focused on improving the linking, filtering and analysis of information gathered from multiple internal and external sources so that relevant cases could be identified earlier and assessed more consistently. The solution was implemented within a Horizon 2020 research and innovation project using machine-learning methods, data-analysis techniques and visual analytics to process heterogeneous datasets conceptually spanning trade records, financial flows and other investigative information.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Financial Crime Investigation Service under Lithuanian Customs sought to strengthen its ability to detect suspicious trade and financial-flow patterns across fragmented and high-volume datasets, in a context where investigators and other law-enforcement units needed faster, better-prioritised intelligence to support operational decisions. The use case focused on improving the linking, filtering and analysis of information gathered from multiple internal and external sources so that relevant cases could be identified earlier and assessed more consistently. The objective was to support analysts and decision-makers with a targeted data-processing and analysis capability able to surface anomalies, relationships and priorities that would otherwise remain difficult to detect manually. The solution was implemented within a Horizon 2020 research and innovation project using machine-learning methods, data-analysis techniques and visual analytics to process heterogeneous datasets conceptually spanning trade records, financial flows and other investigative information. The system combined pattern-detection models, data integration and analytical workflows to help users connect entities, rank leads and explore evidence in a more structured way. The result was a more scalable and informed investigative process, with improved analytical depth, faster triage of complex information and stronger support for operational decision-making across the organisational units involved.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK|F06_HIGH_VOLUME_SCALABILITY_BOTTLENECK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-70,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00068,EU_PSTW_202609_00068,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,About 120 different municipalities in the Netherlands,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.","Dutch municipalities such as Staphorst needed a practical way to make council meetings more accessible and transparent, particularly for citizens who are deaf or hard of hearing and may otherwise struggle to follow political debate in real time. Municipal discussions are central to public accountability, yet live accessibility support can be costly and difficult to provide consistently across all meetings.",Amberscript - Automated speech translation for public organisations,"About 120 different municipalities in the Netherlands; Local Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Netherlands","The use case therefore involves local authorities, elected representatives and residents, with the objective of improving access to public decision-making, strengthening civic inclusion and increasing transparency around how municipal decisions are discussed and taken. The solution is implemented through AmberScript’s automated speech-recognition and translation technology, developed for public-sector meeting environments.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Dutch municipalities such as Staphorst needed a practical way to make council meetings more accessible and transparent, particularly for citizens who are deaf or hard of hearing and may otherwise struggle to follow political debate in real time. Municipal discussions are central to public accountability, yet live accessibility support can be costly and difficult to provide consistently across all meetings. The use case therefore involves local authorities, elected representatives and residents, with the objective of improving access to public decision-making, strengthening civic inclusion and increasing transparency around how municipal decisions are discussed and taken. The solution is implemented through AmberScript’s automated speech-recognition and translation technology, developed for public-sector meeting environments. At a conceptual level, the system was trained on a dataset of more than 1,000 hours of audio recordings and transcriptions from public-sector meetings, which were compiled, cleaned and processed to improve performance on the terminology, speaking styles and formats typical of council proceedings. The platform then converts spoken debate into text that can be followed online by citizens. The impact includes greater accessibility for people with hearing impairments, faster and more scalable production of meeting transcripts and improved visibility of democratic processes. More broadly, the service creates public value by making council proceedings easier to follow, reducing reliance on fully manual transcription and supporting a more open and inclusive model of local governance.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-71,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00069,EU_PSTW_202609_00069,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Amsterdam,Police Services,"The Crime Anticipation System was designed to help police allocate limited resources more effectively by identifying where and when specific categories of crime were more likely to occur. It addressed the practical challenge of turning large volumes of local, time-sensitive information into actionable operational insight, with the objective of anticipating patterns such as repeated offences in particular neighbourhoods or at particular times and enabling a more focused preventive presence.",CAS - Crime anticipation system,City of Amsterdam; Local Government; Police Services; Netherlands,"Operating in a context of recurrent local crime patterns, the use case brought together police forces, local operational teams and public-data providers to improve weekly planning and deployment decisions. The impact lay in more targeted deployment of officers, improved responsiveness to recurring crime trends and better use of available capacity.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Crime Anticipation System was designed to help police allocate limited resources more effectively by identifying where and when specific categories of crime were more likely to occur. Operating in a context of recurrent local crime patterns, the use case brought together police forces, local operational teams and public-data providers to improve weekly planning and deployment decisions. It addressed the practical challenge of turning large volumes of local, time-sensitive information into actionable operational insight, with the objective of anticipating patterns such as repeated offences in particular neighbourhoods or at particular times and enabling a more focused preventive presence. The implementation combined predictive analytics with regular ingestion of local and current data, including police records, neighbourhood and resident data, contextual information on local conditions and statistics from Statistics Netherlands. At a conceptual level, the system used pattern-recognition and spatio-temporal analysis techniques to detect recurring crime behaviours and generate weekly forecasts that could be integrated into policing workflows and resource-planning processes. The impact lay in more targeted deployment of officers, improved responsiveness to recurring crime trends and better use of available capacity. Qualitatively, this increased operational efficiency, supported evidence-based planning and strengthened the police’s ability to intervene earlier and more effectively in areas of elevated risk.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F06_HIGH_VOLUME_SCALABILITY_BOTTLENECK,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-72,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00070,EU_PSTW_202609_00070,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Amsterdam,Police Services,The municipality of Amsterdam needed a better way to coordinate and evaluate law-enforcement activity across a fragmented operational landscape in which relevant information was dispersed across multiple disconnected systems.,Xomnia - Law enforcement optimization system,City of Amsterdam; Local Government; Police Services; Netherlands,"Enforcement teams were generating valuable data through daily activities, but because these records were stored in separate silos, it was difficult for managers and operational staff to obtain a unified view of what was happening in the field or to use that information effectively for planning and oversight. The use case therefore involved municipal enforcement teams, control-room staff and decision-makers, with the objective of improving operational visibility, supporting more informed deployment decisions and making enforcement management more data-driven and efficient.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The municipality of Amsterdam needed a better way to coordinate and evaluate law-enforcement activity across a fragmented operational landscape in which relevant information was dispersed across multiple disconnected systems. Enforcement teams were generating valuable data through daily activities, but because these records were stored in separate silos, it was difficult for managers and operational staff to obtain a unified view of what was happening in the field or to use that information effectively for planning and oversight. The use case therefore involved municipal enforcement teams, control-room staff and decision-makers, with the objective of improving operational visibility, supporting more informed deployment decisions and making enforcement management more data-driven and efficient. The solution was implemented by Xomnia through a user-friendly dashboard built on top of an integrated data architecture. At a conceptual level, the system harvested and reconstructed data from seven different municipal sources, including GPS data from walkie-talkies, text data from fines issued through PDAs and reports from the control room, and then interconnected these datasets to allow dynamic exploration. This created a single analytical environment in which previously isolated operational signals could be examined together. The impact includes better situational awareness, improved ability to monitor enforcement activity and stronger support for planning and resource allocation. More broadly, the project creates value by reducing the limits imposed by data silos, improving operational efficiency and enabling a more coordinated and evidence-based approach to municipal law enforcement.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-73,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00071,EU_PSTW_202609_00071,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Utrecht,Police Services,"Burglary patterns are influenced by a mix of temporal, environmental and neighbourhood factors, yet these are difficult to interpret consistently without a structured analytical model.",Xomnia - The Burglary Predictor,City of Utrecht; Local Government; Police Services; Netherlands,"The use case therefore involved the municipality of Utrecht and local safety stakeholders, with the objective of supporting more data-driven decision-making and enabling preventive action at district level before incidents occur. The solution was implemented by Xomnia as a predictive model that estimates the occurrence of burglaries in each district for a given week.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Utrecht wanted to strengthen burglary prevention by moving beyond reactive policing and municipal response towards a more anticipatory understanding of where burglary risk was likely to emerge. Burglary patterns are influenced by a mix of temporal, environmental and neighbourhood factors, yet these are difficult to interpret consistently without a structured analytical model. The use case therefore involved the municipality of Utrecht and local safety stakeholders, with the objective of supporting more data-driven decision-making and enabling preventive action at district level before incidents occur. The solution was implemented by Xomnia as a predictive model that estimates the occurrence of burglaries in each district for a given week. At a conceptual level, the model combines burglary date and time information with derived contextual variables such as weather conditions, holidays and special events, and enriches this with sociodemographic neighbourhood statistics and points of interest including street lights, cameras, trees, parks and large rubbish containers. These factors are brought together to generate district-level risk predictions that can inform operational choices and preventive planning. The expected impact is earlier identification of higher-risk areas, better targeting of local prevention measures and more efficient use of municipal and safety resources. More broadly, the Burglary Predictor creates value by improving situational foresight, supporting evidence-based urban safety policy and demonstrating how diverse local data can be combined to strengthen crime-prevention strategies.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-75,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00072,EU_PSTW_202609_00072,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Dutch Police,Police Services,"Manual checking is often too slow and resource-intensive to support these activities at scale, particularly when large volumes of vehicles must be processed quickly and accurately. The impact includes faster processing, reduced manual workload and improved enforcement capability, while also supporting scalability across multiple public functions.",ANPR - Car plate recognition,Dutch Police; Central-Government; Police Services; Netherlands,"The use case therefore involves local administrations, traffic authorities and enforcement bodies, with the objective of improving operational efficiency, strengthening compliance and enabling more consistent control across transport-related public services. The solution is implemented through automatic number-plate-recognition software that identifies vehicle registration plates from video or photographic footage captured by cameras.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Public authorities need reliable ways to monitor vehicle movements and enforce rules across a wide range of operational contexts, including paid parking, traffic management, tax enforcement and environmental regulation. Manual checking is often too slow and resource-intensive to support these activities at scale, particularly when large volumes of vehicles must be processed quickly and accurately. The use case therefore involves local administrations, traffic authorities and enforcement bodies, with the objective of improving operational efficiency, strengthening compliance and enabling more consistent control across transport-related public services. The solution is implemented through automatic number-plate-recognition software that identifies vehicle registration plates from video or photographic footage captured by cameras. At a conceptual level, the system combines image capture, optical character recognition and pattern-detection techniques to isolate number plates, extract alphanumeric characters and match them against relevant administrative or enforcement databases. This makes it possible to automate vehicle identification in real time or near real time across different use cases. According to the source, the system is used in more than 2,000 administrations, indicating broad operational uptake and strong adaptability across service areas. The impact includes faster processing, reduced manual workload and improved enforcement capability, while also supporting scalability across multiple public functions. More broadly, ANPR creates business value by providing a versatile digital-control layer that can be reused across parking, regulation, taxation and environmental monitoring.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK|F06_HIGH_VOLUME_SCALABILITY_BOTTLENECK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-76,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00073,EU_PSTW_202609_00073,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Dutch Public Procesecution Service,Police Services,"Manual enforcement is limited by staff availability and by the practical difficulty of identifying violations reliably in moving traffic. The impact includes more targeted and scalable enforcement, reduced reliance on continuous manual observation and improved consistency in identifying offences.",Cameras - Detection holding a phone in car,Dutch Public Procesecution Service; Central-Government; Police Services; Netherlands,"The Dutch Public Prosecution Service introduced this use case to strengthen enforcement against drivers holding mobile phones behind the wheel, a behaviour associated with serious road-safety risks and difficult to police consistently through conventional roadside observation alone. The solution is implemented through smart enforcement cameras that photograph passing vehicles from above in a way that excludes the driver’s face but captures the vehicle interior and number plate clearly enough to show whether a mobile device is being held.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Dutch Public Prosecution Service introduced this use case to strengthen enforcement against drivers holding mobile phones behind the wheel, a behaviour associated with serious road-safety risks and difficult to police consistently through conventional roadside observation alone. Manual enforcement is limited by staff availability and by the practical difficulty of identifying violations reliably in moving traffic. The initiative therefore involves prosecutors, the Central Judicial Collection Agency and road users, with the objective of increasing compliance, improving traffic safety and making enforcement more efficient while preserving a degree of privacy in evidence collection. The solution is implemented through smart enforcement cameras that photograph passing vehicles from above in a way that excludes the driver’s face but captures the vehicle interior and number plate clearly enough to show whether a mobile device is being held. At a conceptual level, the system uses image-analysis techniques to detect likely violations and forwards only those suspected cases to the CJIB, where a staff member performs the final assessment before any sanction is issued. If confirmed, the violation can lead to a fine of EUR 240. The impact includes more targeted and scalable enforcement, reduced reliance on continuous manual observation and improved consistency in identifying offences. More broadly, the system creates value by supporting safer driving behaviour, strengthening operational efficiency and balancing automated detection with human verification in the final decision stage.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-77,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00074,EU_PSTW_202609_00074,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Ministry of Social Affairs and Employment,Social protection n.e.c.,"SyRi was introduced by the Dutch government in 2014 to address a longstanding administrative challenge: municipalities wanted a more systematic way to identify possible welfare fraud and misuse of public benefits across fragmented public-sector datasets. However, the system became highly controversial because of concerns over privacy intrusion, lack of transparency and discriminatory effects on poorer and more vulnerable citizens.",SyRi - Detect welfare fraud,Ministry of Social Affairs and Employment; Central-Government; Social protection n.e.c.; Netherlands,"The use case involved municipalities, civil servants and national authorities, with the objective of improving the targeting of further investigations by identifying addresses considered at higher risk of irregularities in welfare claims. The solution was implemented by combining risk indicators from multiple sources, including tax records, health-insurance data, residence information and education-related datasets, to generate risk profiles for possible fraud or misuse.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"SyRi was introduced by the Dutch government in 2014 to address a longstanding administrative challenge: municipalities wanted a more systematic way to identify possible welfare fraud and misuse of public benefits across fragmented public-sector datasets. Before SyRi, several municipalities had been developing their own approaches, creating inconsistency and duplication in fraud-detection practices. The use case involved municipalities, civil servants and national authorities, with the objective of improving the targeting of further investigations by identifying addresses considered at higher risk of irregularities in welfare claims. SyRi did not make formal decisions itself, but was designed to support civil servants by generating recommendations for additional scrutiny based on aggregated signals from existing governmental systems. The solution was implemented by combining risk indicators from multiple sources, including tax records, health-insurance data, residence information and education-related datasets, to generate risk profiles for possible fraud or misuse. At a conceptual level, the system used cross-dataset matching and risk-scoring techniques to highlight cases for further human review. However, the system became highly controversial because of concerns over privacy intrusion, lack of transparency and discriminatory effects on poorer and more vulnerable citizens. Following legal challenge, a Dutch court ruled in early 2020 that SyRi’s use breached Article 8 of the European Convention on Human Rights, and the system was prohibited. Its impact therefore includes not only lessons on administrative targeting, but also a major cautionary example regarding legality, transparency and rights-based governance.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,NOT_IN_USE,미사용,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-78,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00075,EU_PSTW_202609_00075,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Dordrecht,General public services n.e.c.,"When this sorting is done manually, it can be slow, inconsistent and resource-intensive, especially where incoming reports vary widely in wording and urgency. This creates a more consistent and scalable intake process than fully manual routing.",SIA - Notifications public space,Municipality of Dordrecht; Local Government; General public services n.e.c.; Netherlands,The SIA use case was developed to address this administrative bottleneck by improving how local authorities process citizen notifications about the public realm. The solution is implemented through an automated categorisation tool that analyses incoming citizen notifications and assigns them to the relevant issue type and responsible department.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Municipalities receive large numbers of citizen reports about problems in public space, such as damage, nuisance or maintenance issues, and these notifications must be classified and routed to the appropriate department before action can begin. When this sorting is done manually, it can be slow, inconsistent and resource-intensive, especially where incoming reports vary widely in wording and urgency. The SIA use case was developed to address this administrative bottleneck by improving how local authorities process citizen notifications about the public realm. The stakeholders include residents, municipal contact teams and operational departments responsible for public-space maintenance and response, with the objective of speeding up case handling, reducing internal friction and ensuring that reports reach the right team more quickly. The solution is implemented through an automated categorisation tool that analyses incoming citizen notifications and assigns them to the relevant issue type and responsible department. At a conceptual level, the system uses language-processing and classification techniques to interpret the content of free-text reports and connect them with municipal workflows for follow-up. This creates a more consistent and scalable intake process than fully manual routing. The impact includes faster handling of public-space notifications, reduced administrative burden and improved responsiveness for residents reporting problems. More broadly, SIA creates public value by making local-government service delivery more efficient, improving the user experience and supporting a fairer and more reliable way of processing citizen concerns.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-79,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00076,EU_PSTW_202609_00076,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Ministry of Infrastructure and Water Management of the Netherlands,Public order and safety n.e.c.,"Rijkswaterstaat operates a 24-hour incident-management service in which traffic managers monitor extensive real-time information across more than 15 screens, creating a demanding environment in which accident risks can be difficult to identify early and inspectors may not always be optimally positioned to respond quickly. The operational challenge is not only to detect incidents, but also to anticipate where they are most likely to occur so that patrol deployment can be planned more effectively.",RWS - Machine learning for road accident predictions,Ministry of Infrastructure and Water Management of the Netherlands; Central-Government; Public order and safety n.e.c.; Netherlands,"This use case therefore involves traffic managers, inspectors and the Netherlands Ministry of Water and Infrastructure Management, with the objective of reducing response times and improving road-safety operations by positioning inspectors where risk is greatest. The solution was developed with Data Science for Social Good using a predictive model trained on incident data from 2012 to 2016, combined with road characteristics, traffic speed and flow data, and weather information.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Rijkswaterstaat operates a 24-hour incident-management service in which traffic managers monitor extensive real-time information across more than 15 screens, creating a demanding environment in which accident risks can be difficult to identify early and inspectors may not always be optimally positioned to respond quickly. The operational challenge is not only to detect incidents, but also to anticipate where they are most likely to occur so that patrol deployment can be planned more effectively. This use case therefore involves traffic managers, inspectors and the Netherlands Ministry of Water and Infrastructure Management, with the objective of reducing response times and improving road-safety operations by positioning inspectors where risk is greatest. The solution was developed with Data Science for Social Good using a predictive model trained on incident data from 2012 to 2016, combined with road characteristics, traffic speed and flow data, and weather information. At a conceptual level, the model estimates the probability of an accident occurring within a given time window and road segment, then displays this on a heatmap so that managers can identify high-risk sections and allocate patrols more strategically. The impact is explicitly measurable: the approach delivered a three- to fourfold improvement over a baseline in which inspectors patrolled random road segments. More broadly, the project improves operational efficiency, supports faster incident response and creates value through better use of traffic-management resources.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-80,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00077,EU_PSTW_202609_00077,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Rotterdam Municipality,Housing and community amenities n.e.c.,"With around 14.5 km² of flat roofs across a dense and modern urban environment, the challenge was to move from fragmented visual assessment to a city-wide evidence base that could support planning, prioritisation and investment decisions. The expected impact was improved decision quality, greater scalability than manual inspection and a stronger ability to prioritise interventions across the city.",Rotterdam – Improving the usage of rooftops,Rotterdam Municipality; Local Government; Housing and community amenities n.e.c.; Netherlands,"The use case involved municipal departments and urban-planning stakeholders, with the objective of identifying current rooftop use and uncovering opportunities for more sustainable and productive deployment of roof space across the city. The implementation used computer-vision techniques applied to aerial and satellite imagery to classify rooftops and infer their current characteristics and use.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Municipality of Rotterdam needed a better understanding of how the city’s extensive stock of flat rooftops could contribute to urban sustainability goals, including water storage, green space creation and energy generation. With around 14.5 km² of flat roofs across a dense and modern urban environment, the challenge was to move from fragmented visual assessment to a city-wide evidence base that could support planning, prioritisation and investment decisions. The use case involved municipal departments and urban-planning stakeholders, with the objective of identifying current rooftop use and uncovering opportunities for more sustainable and productive deployment of roof space across the city. The implementation used computer-vision techniques applied to aerial and satellite imagery to classify rooftops and infer their current characteristics and use. At a conceptual level, the approach combined image data, geospatial analysis and classification models to create a scalable mapping layer that could be integrated with municipal planning processes and related environmental datasets. This provided a structured basis for evaluating where roofs might support climate adaptation or energy initiatives. The expected impact was improved decision quality, greater scalability than manual inspection and a stronger ability to prioritise interventions across the city. In business terms, the use case supports more efficient urban planning, better use of public resources and a more informed pathway towards environmental resilience.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-81,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00078,EU_PSTW_202609_00078,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Barcelona,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.",UNKNOWN,Mercé - citizen science for better urban life,"City of Barcelona; Local Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Spain",UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Mercè project in Barcelona was a citizen science experiment run in 2020 where residents helped train algorithms to make cities more livable. By applying machine learning across urban planning, geography, sociology, economics, and urban health, it translated citizens’ perceptions into objective knowledge and open data. This knowledge could then inform urban planning and support the design of healthier, more sustainable cities. The project stands out for combining ICT with diverse disciplines, all converging on the urban environment.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-82,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00079,EU_PSTW_202609_00079,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,ProRail B.V,Transport,"One important challenge was understanding when and where trespassers might enter railway tracks, alongside broader needs such as preventive maintenance and more reliable scheduling. At a conceptual level, predictive algorithms were applied to identify correlations, estimate likely events and support decision-making beyond the limits of manual analysis.",PRORAIL: Rail trespassers prediction,ProRail B.V; Central-Government; Transport; Netherlands,"ProRail aimed to improve the safety and efficiency of the Dutch rail network by using operational data to anticipate risks and optimise infrastructure management. The use case involved asset-management teams, operational planners and safety stakeholders, with the objective of turning continuously generated infrastructure data into practical insight for safer operations and better network performance.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"ProRail aimed to improve the safety and efficiency of the Dutch rail network by using operational data to anticipate risks and optimise infrastructure management. One important challenge was understanding when and where trespassers might enter railway tracks, alongside broader needs such as preventive maintenance and more reliable scheduling. The use case involved asset-management teams, operational planners and safety stakeholders, with the objective of turning continuously generated infrastructure data into practical insight for safer operations and better network performance. The implementation relied on Internet of Things sensor data collected from the rail system, including track-monitoring information used to assess infrastructure condition on different routes. At a conceptual level, predictive algorithms were applied to identify correlations, estimate likely events and support decision-making beyond the limits of manual analysis. These models were integrated into operational processes to inform maintenance planning, improve schedule optimisation and identify locations or circumstances in which trespassing risk might be higher. The impact was greater visibility over infrastructure conditions, stronger preventive maintenance capability and improved support for safety-related interventions. Qualitatively, this enhances network reliability, reduces operational disruption and helps protect the physical rail environment. More broadly, the use case demonstrates how data-driven forecasting can improve both efficiency and public safety across a large-scale transport system.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-83,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00080,EU_PSTW_202609_00080,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Boost.ai,General services.,UNKNOWN,Norway Chatbots Kommune Kiri - Chatbot for public administrations,Boost.ai; Private sector; General services.; Norway,"The use case therefore aims to support a more efficient public sector while also improving the quality and speed of service for citizens. The solution is implemented through chatbot platforms, most commonly based on technologies from providers such as boost.ai or Kindly.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Norwegian government agencies and municipalities have increasingly adopted chatbots to improve how citizens access public services and information. Public administrations often receive large volumes of routine enquiries, which can delay responses, increase pressure on service teams and make it harder for staff to focus on more complex cases requiring human judgement. The use case therefore aims to support a more efficient public sector while also improving the quality and speed of service for citizens. The stakeholders include municipalities, government agencies, residents and public-service staff, with the objective of making public information more accessible, reducing response times and supporting more scalable digital interaction between administrations and users. The solution is implemented through chatbot platforms, most commonly based on technologies from providers such as boost.ai or Kindly. At a conceptual level, these systems use conversational language processing, intent recognition and structured service knowledge to understand citizen questions and return relevant responses through digital channels. This allows common requests to be handled automatically while maintaining the possibility of escalation or further support where needed. The case has also been examined through the EU-funded ETAPAS project, which assessed it as one of its use cases. The impact includes improved service availability, lower administrative pressure on frontline teams and a more responsive user experience. More broadly, these chatbot deployments create value by enabling scalable, citizen-centred public communication and supporting digital transformation across public administration.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-84,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00081,EU_PSTW_202609_00081,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,National Library of Norway,Education n.e.c.,"Large heritage collections are often difficult to navigate because legacy catalogue data may be incomplete, inconsistent or too limited to support rich digital access. This enables the library to move beyond fully manual cataloguing processes and support deeper interaction with its holdings.",Nancy - Access to National library assets,National Library of Norway; Central-Government; Education n.e.c.; Norway,"The use case therefore involves the National Library, researchers, cultural users and the wider public, with the objective of improving access to national collections, enriching discovery and strengthening the quality of catalogue information. The solution is implemented through commercially available analytical technologies used to generate new catalogue data and improve metadata production.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Norwegian National Library needed a more effective way to open up its extensive collections of text, images and video so that users could discover, search and understand cultural assets more deeply. Large heritage collections are often difficult to navigate because legacy catalogue data may be incomplete, inconsistent or too limited to support rich digital access. The use case therefore involves the National Library, researchers, cultural users and the wider public, with the objective of improving access to national collections, enriching discovery and strengthening the quality of catalogue information. The solution is implemented through commercially available analytical technologies used to generate new catalogue data and improve metadata production. At a conceptual level, the system applies machine-learning methods to library assets in order to analyse content, identify relevant descriptive features and produce metadata that can enhance indexing, search and interpretation across different media types. This enables the library to move beyond fully manual cataloguing processes and support deeper interaction with its holdings. The impact includes improved discoverability of texts, images and video, more scalable metadata production and better access to cultural resources for end users. More broadly, Nancy creates value by modernising collection management, reducing the effort required to enrich catalogue records and helping a national cultural institution make its assets more visible, usable and meaningful in the digital environment.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-85,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00082,EU_PSTW_202609_00082,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Norwegian National Security Authority,UNKNOWN,"The operational challenge is that legacy sensor systems and manual analytical processes are no longer sufficient for the scale, speed and complexity of modern threat environments, particularly where rapid identification of malware and coordinated response are essential.",VDI - Protection of digital infrastructure,Norwegian National Security Authority; Central-Government; Norway,"The use case therefore involves national-security authorities, operators of critical infrastructure and cyber-defence stakeholders, with the objective of improving detection, accelerating analysis and strengthening the protection of strategically important systems across the country. The solution is implemented through new sensor technology and a replacement platform designed to process data collected from the upgraded warning system.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Norway’s critical digital infrastructure faces persistent and increasingly sophisticated cyber threats, and the Norwegian National Security Authority has needed to strengthen a long-standing early-warning capability that has been used for almost 20 years to detect targeted attacks. The operational challenge is that legacy sensor systems and manual analytical processes are no longer sufficient for the scale, speed and complexity of modern threat environments, particularly where rapid identification of malware and coordinated response are essential. The use case therefore involves national-security authorities, operators of critical infrastructure and cyber-defence stakeholders, with the objective of improving detection, accelerating analysis and strengthening the protection of strategically important systems across the country. The solution is implemented through new sensor technology and a replacement platform designed to process data collected from the upgraded warning system. At a conceptual level, the platform applies machine-learning and automated-analysis techniques to cyber-event data in order to detect malicious activity, analyse malware automatically and share the resulting intelligence more rapidly across relevant actors. This creates a more integrated and scalable cyber-monitoring environment than the earlier sensor architecture alone. The expected impact is better threat visibility, faster analytical turnaround and improved resilience of critical infrastructure against targeted attacks. More broadly, the initiative creates value by modernising national cyber-defence capabilities, reducing response latency and enabling a more adaptive and intelligence-led model of digital-infrastructure protection.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-86,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00083,EU_PSTW_202609_00083,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Norwegian student loan fund (Lånekassen),"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.","The operational problem was that students claiming to live away from home could receive more favourable support, yet manual or random checks were not sufficiently effective at identifying incorrect declarations.",Lånekassen - Improve control on students with loan payments and identification,"Norwegian student loan fund (Lånekassen); Central-Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Norway","The use case therefore focused on improving control selection so that limited audit resources could be directed towards cases with a higher likelihood of non-compliance, while maintaining a comparison group to assess effectiveness. The impact was a clear improvement in control efficiency, better targeting of investigative effort and stronger use of public funds, with measurable gains in detection performance and a more scalable approach to compliance monitoring.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Lånekassen, the Norwegian State Educational Loan Fund, sought to improve how it selected student cases for account controls related to housing declarations used in grant eligibility. The operational problem was that students claiming to live away from home could receive more favourable support, yet manual or random checks were not sufficiently effective at identifying incorrect declarations. The use case therefore focused on improving control selection so that limited audit resources could be directed towards cases with a higher likelihood of non-compliance, while maintaining a comparison group to assess effectiveness. The implementation used machine-learning models to prioritise which student records should be checked, based on patterns in available administrative data relevant to the control objective. In 2018, 25,000 students were included in the check: 15,000 were selected by the model and 10,000 by random selection as a control group. The results showed that 11.6% of the machine-learning group did not pass the check, compared with 5.5% in the control group, making the model-based approach roughly twice as effective at identifying incorrect housing information. The impact was a clear improvement in control efficiency, better targeting of investigative effort and stronger use of public funds, with measurable gains in detection performance and a more scalable approach to compliance monitoring.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-87,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00084,EU_PSTW_202609_00084,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Statnett, the Norwegian electricity transmission system operator",Fuel and energy,"The operational challenge is that transmission-system operators must continually assess how changing weather conditions may affect critical infrastructure, yet conventional monitoring alone does not always provide a sufficiently forward-looking view of failure risk.",Statnett - Forecast service for weather,"Statnett, the Norwegian electricity transmission system operator; Central-Government; Fuel and energy; Norway","This use case therefore involves grid operators, system planners and maintenance teams, with the objective of improving network reliability by forecasting weather-dependent failure probabilities and enabling earlier operational response. The solution is implemented as a real-time forecasting service that combines Tableau, Python, Splunk and open weather data from the Norwegian Meteorological Institute.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Statnett needed a better way to anticipate weather-related failures on overhead power lines, since wind and lightning can disrupt network reliability and make it harder for operators to plan preventive action in time. The operational challenge is that transmission-system operators must continually assess how changing weather conditions may affect critical infrastructure, yet conventional monitoring alone does not always provide a sufficiently forward-looking view of failure risk. This use case therefore involves grid operators, system planners and maintenance teams, with the objective of improving network reliability by forecasting weather-dependent failure probabilities and enabling earlier operational response. The solution is implemented as a real-time forecasting service that combines Tableau, Python, Splunk and open weather data from the Norwegian Meteorological Institute. At a conceptual level, the service integrates live and forecast weather inputs with analytical models that estimate the probability of failure on overhead lines due to specific weather drivers such as wind and lightning. The outputs are then presented through operational dashboards that support monitoring and decision-making in real time. This allows risk to be viewed not as a static condition but as a changing probability tied to the weather outlook. The expected impact is earlier identification of vulnerable lines, better-informed maintenance and operational planning, and improved service continuity. More broadly, the use case creates value by supporting a more proactive and data-driven approach to grid resilience and infrastructure-risk management.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-88,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00085,EU_PSTW_202609_00085,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Trondheim Municipality,"General economic, commercial and labour affair",UNKNOWN,TrondHeim - Insolvency Prediction,"Trondheim Municipality; Local Government; General economic, commercial and labour affair; Norway",Trondheim Municipality explored this use case in order to identify companies at heightened risk of insolvency early enough to support preventive action. The solution was implemented through a predictive system designed to estimate which companies had a greater probability of becoming insolvent.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Trondheim Municipality explored this use case in order to identify companies at heightened risk of insolvency early enough to support preventive action. The operational problem was that business failure can have significant consequences for local economic stability, municipal planning and public-sector financial exposure, yet warning signs are not always easy to detect through conventional monitoring alone. The stakeholders included municipal authorities and economic-administration teams, with the objective of recognising vulnerable companies earlier, prioritising attention more effectively and creating an evidence base for possible pre-emptive measures. The solution was implemented through a predictive system designed to estimate which companies had a greater probability of becoming insolvent. At a conceptual level, the system used company-related data and predictive-modelling techniques to identify patterns associated with future insolvency and generate risk estimates that could inform municipal follow-up. The reported accuracy was around 80% over a one-year period, while the preferred level would have been closer to 90%, indicating that the system produced useful but still imperfect predictive support. The impact therefore lies in improved early visibility of financial risk and a stronger basis for proactive intervention, even if performance fell short of the more ambitious target. More broadly, the project created value by showing how municipal authorities can use predictive tools to support economic oversight, strengthen risk management and move from reactive to earlier action in relation to business vulnerability.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-89,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00086,EU_PSTW_202609_00086,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Cancer Registry of Norway,Hospital services.,"Conventional pathways can struggle to capture the full complexity of disease variation across patients, which may limit the precision of treatment decisions and the ability to tailor care effectively.",Norway AI for Diagnosing and treating Breast cancer,Cancer Registry of Norway; Central-Government; Hospital services.; Norway,"Norwegian researchers are addressing a critical clinical challenge in breast-cancer care: how to improve the accuracy of diagnosis and treatment selection by making better use of the country’s extensive, well-structured healthcare data. This use case therefore involves researchers, hospitals and clinical teams seeking to improve outcomes through more data-driven and individualised cancer care.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Norwegian researchers are addressing a critical clinical challenge in breast-cancer care: how to improve the accuracy of diagnosis and treatment selection by making better use of the country’s extensive, well-structured healthcare data. Conventional pathways can struggle to capture the full complexity of disease variation across patients, which may limit the precision of treatment decisions and the ability to tailor care effectively. This use case therefore involves researchers, hospitals and clinical teams seeking to improve outcomes through more data-driven and individualised cancer care. The objective is to support earlier and more accurate detection of breast cancer and to enable treatment choices that are more closely matched to each patient’s condition. The solution is implemented through advanced analytical models trained on large-scale national healthcare datasets, using patterns in clinical and diagnostic information to improve both disease identification and treatment precision. At a conceptual level, the system combines structured health data, predictive modelling and clinical interpretation to support more personalised care pathways. According to the source, one hospital has been working on a specific form of this technology for around two and a half years, and performance is already described as highly accurate. The expected impact is improved diagnostic precision, more targeted treatment and better use of health data in oncology. More broadly, the initiative creates value by supporting personalised medicine, improving clinical decision quality and strengthening Norway’s position in data-driven cancer care.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-90,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00087,EU_PSTW_202609_00087,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Ministry of Foreign Affairs,Foreign economic aid.,UNKNOWN,HAL - classification of documents,Ministry of Foreign Affairs; Central-Government; Foreign economic aid.; Norway,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"In a pilot project between 2017 and 2018, the Norwegian Ministry of Foreign Affairs (MFA) applied machine learning and NLP to analyse and classify up to 6,000 annual reports from embassies and delegations, manking it easier to retrieve relevant information, extract key insights, and generate summaries. Developed in cooperation with the University of Oslo, the system leveraged Norwegian-language categorisation and will gradually expand to include archives and external research reports.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-91,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00088,EU_PSTW_202609_00088,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Automated Traffic Supervision Center,Police Services,"The operational challenge was not simply capturing images, but extracting usable information from them so that potential infringements could be identified and prioritised without requiring every case to be reviewed from scratch. The impact was greater operational efficiency for CANARD staff, reduced manual effort in first-line case screening and improved scalability of enforcement processes as camera coverage expanded.",Canard - Speed camera system to flag potential traffic rules infringement,Automated Traffic Supervision Center; Central-Government; Police Services; Poland,"The use case involved road-enforcement personnel and administrative teams responsible for case handling, with the objective of creating an initial sorting mechanism that would support, rather than replace, human decision-making on fines and infringements. In practice, the system supports faster triage, more consistent handling of potential cases and better use of public enforcement resources, while preserving reliability and accountability in the final decision process.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Canard's speed-camera system was developed to improve the efficiency of traffic-rule enforcement at road junctions by helping staff process large volumes of photographic evidence more quickly and consistently. The operational challenge was not simply capturing images, but extracting usable information from them so that potential infringements could be identified and prioritised without requiring every case to be reviewed from scratch. The use case involved road-enforcement personnel and administrative teams responsible for case handling, with the objective of creating an initial sorting mechanism that would support, rather than replace, human decision-making on fines and infringements. The implementation used image-processing and analytical techniques to derive relevant information from photographs captured by cameras installed at junctions. At a conceptual level, the system analysed vehicle images and event data to flag cases that appeared to indicate potential breaches of traffic rules, feeding these into a human review workflow. The final enforcement decision remained non-automatic, ensuring human oversight and procedural control. The impact was greater operational efficiency for CANARD staff, reduced manual effort in first-line case screening and improved scalability of enforcement processes as camera coverage expanded. In practice, the system supports faster triage, more consistent handling of potential cases and better use of public enforcement resources, while preserving reliability and accountability in the final decision process.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,S3_DOCUMENT_INTELLIGENCE,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-92,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00089,EU_PSTW_202609_00089,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Krajowa Izba Rozliczeniowa S.A (State Clearance Chamber), Krajowa Izba Rozliczeniowa S.A (State Clearance Chamber)","Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.","The operational challenge was to assess risk across large volumes of financial information and identify account holders who may be involved in fraud or other illicit conduct, while enabling timely intervention where warranted.",STIR - System Teleinformatyczny Izby Rozliczeniowej (Clearance Chamber ICT System),"Krajowa Izba Rozliczeniowa S.A (State Clearance Chamber), Krajowa Izba Rozliczeniowa S.A (State Clearance Chamber); Central-Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Poland","The solution is implemented through STIR, which compiles a risk indicator from financial data collected from financial institutions.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Polish tax authorities and payment-system institutions needed a stronger mechanism to detect suspicious financial behaviour linked to illegal activity, particularly in a context where unlawful transactions can move quickly through bank accounts before traditional controls can respond. The operational challenge was to assess risk across large volumes of financial information and identify account holders who may be involved in fraud or other illicit conduct, while enabling timely intervention where warranted. The stakeholders include the State Clearance Chamber, tax authorities, banks and the wider financial-control system, with the objective of improving the detection of suspicious activity and strengthening the state’s ability to act before funds are moved or concealed. The solution is implemented through STIR, which compiles a risk indicator from financial data collected from financial institutions. At a conceptual level, the system aggregates and analyses transaction-related and account-level information to generate a risk profile indicating the likelihood that illegal activity may be taking place. Where suspicious activity is identified, tax authorities can request that a bank freeze the account concerned. This embeds the analytical system within the national payment infrastructure and links risk detection directly to enforcement capability. The expected impact is faster identification of high-risk accounts, improved targeting of financial controls and stronger protection of public revenues. More broadly, STIR creates value by increasing the efficiency and reach of financial oversight while supporting a more responsive and coordinated anti-fraud framework.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F06_HIGH_VOLUME_SCALABILITY_BOTTLENECK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-93,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00090,EU_PSTW_202609_00090,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Polish Centre of Digital Administration,Public health services.,"The operational challenge was to verify that quarantined individuals remained at the required location without relying solely on repeated police visits, which would have been resource-intensive and harder to scale during a public-health emergency.",Home Quarantine - app on COVID-19 risk,Polish Centre of Digital Administration; Central-Government; Public health services.; Poland,"The use case therefore involved quarantined residents, police authorities and public-health institutions, with the objective of improving quarantine enforcement, reducing administrative burden and giving users access to relevant information and support. The solution was implemented through the free Home Quarantine app for iPhone and Android, connected to the official database of phone numbers associated with quarantine orders so that only eligible users could activate it.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"During the COVID-19 pandemic, the Polish government needed a way to monitor compliance with mandatory quarantine orders while limiting in-person checks and at the same time offering practical support to people confined at home. The operational challenge was to verify that quarantined individuals remained at the required location without relying solely on repeated police visits, which would have been resource-intensive and harder to scale during a public-health emergency. The use case therefore involved quarantined residents, police authorities and public-health institutions, with the objective of improving quarantine enforcement, reducing administrative burden and giving users access to relevant information and support. The solution was implemented through the free Home Quarantine app for iPhone and Android, connected to the official database of phone numbers associated with quarantine orders so that only eligible users could activate it. After registration using a phone number and SMS code, users submitted a reference photograph, and the app then sent unscheduled prompts requiring a new image within 20 minutes. At a conceptual level, the system combined facial-recognition checks with location verification to confirm identity and presence, effectively replicating the function of an in-person control. The app also included health information and access to a hotline. The impact was improved scalability in quarantine monitoring, reduced dependence on physical inspections and more integrated support for quarantined individuals, while also raising broader questions about proportionality, privacy and emergency digital enforcement.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,NOT_IN_USE,미사용,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-94,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00091,EU_PSTW_202609_00091,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Institute for Employment and Vocational Training (IEFP),Unemployment.,The context was an existing risk-categorisation framework that provided a starting point but lacked the ability to reflect changes in an individual’s circumstances over time.,Predicting long term unemployment in Portugal,Institute for Employment and Vocational Training (IEFP); Central-Government; Unemployment.; Portugal,"Portugal’s Institute for Employment and Vocational Training (IEFP) launched this use case to improve how it identified people at risk of long-term unemployment and to allocate support resources more effectively. The use case involved employment-service teams, caseworkers and programme managers, with the objective of creating a more responsive assessment process that could better flag high-risk individuals and support more tailored intervention.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Portugal’s Institute for Employment and Vocational Training (IEFP) launched this use case to improve how it identified people at risk of long-term unemployment and to allocate support resources more effectively. The context was an existing risk-categorisation framework that provided a starting point but lacked the ability to reflect changes in an individual’s circumstances over time. The use case involved employment-service teams, caseworkers and programme managers, with the objective of creating a more responsive assessment process that could better flag high-risk individuals and support more tailored intervention. The implementation introduced a dynamic predictive model that updated each individual’s risk score over time rather than relying on a static classification. At a conceptual level, the solution used longitudinal administrative and service data, predictive modelling and score recalculation to reflect evolving employment risk and integrate updated assessments into operational decision-making. This allowed the system to support more precise targeting of services and better coordination of case-management activity. The impact was improved prioritisation of individuals most in need, more efficient allocation of public employment resources and stronger potential for earlier intervention. Qualitatively, the approach improves service personalisation, enhances the responsiveness of employment support and increases the overall effectiveness of delivery by aligning scarce resources with changing levels of risk across the beneficiary population.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-95,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00092,EU_PSTW_202609_00092,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Porto,Transport,UNKNOWN,Failstopper - Study the compressed air system of the vehicle,City of Porto; Local Government; Transport; Portugal,"The use case focuses on supporting maintenance teams by improving visibility over asset condition and enabling earlier intervention before faults disrupt service. The expected impact is improved fleet reliability, fewer cancelled trips, better-targeted maintenance activity and more efficient use of technical resources.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Metro do Porto faces a significant operational reliability issue, with around 3,000 journeys cancelled each year due to breakdowns in MP vehicles, many of which are linked to critical onboard systems such as compressed air. The use case focuses on supporting maintenance teams by improving visibility over asset condition and enabling earlier intervention before faults disrupt service. In this context, operators, maintenance engineers and fleet managers require a more timely and data-driven understanding of vehicle behaviour in order to reduce cancellations, improve service continuity and strengthen maintenance planning. The objective is therefore to monitor the compressed air system in operation and provide actionable alerts that help maintenance teams prevent failures rather than react to them after disruption has already occurred. The implementation relies on sensors installed in vehicles to capture operational data from the compressed air system, which is then analysed in real time to detect patterns associated with degradation or emerging faults. At a conceptual level, the solution combines continuous data acquisition, condition-monitoring models and alerting mechanisms that integrate with maintenance workflows so that anomalies can be identified and escalated promptly. The expected impact is improved fleet reliability, fewer cancelled trips, better-targeted maintenance activity and more efficient use of technical resources. In business terms, the project supports service quality, reduces disruption-related costs and creates a scalable predictive-maintenance capability for public transport operations.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-96,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00093,EU_PSTW_202609_00093,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,National Archive of Torre do Tombo,Cultural services.,"EPISA addresses a core challenge in the archival domain: how to improve the accessibility, structure and interoperability of Portuguese cultural heritage records so that archives can be more easily searched, connected and reused. Traditional archival descriptions often contain rich information, but much of it is difficult to process automatically or integrate into broader digital ecosystems.",EPISA - Entity and property inference for semantic archives,National Archive of Torre do Tombo; Central-Government; Cultural services.; Portugal,"The use case therefore involves archivists, cultural-heritage institutions and information-management stakeholders seeking to create a richer and more machine-readable description model for archival material. The implementation uses natural language processing, named-entity recognition and machine-learning methods to analyse archival descriptions and, where available, the digital documents themselves.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"EPISA addresses a core challenge in the archival domain: how to improve the accessibility, structure and interoperability of Portuguese cultural heritage records so that archives can be more easily searched, connected and reused. Traditional archival descriptions often contain rich information, but much of it is difficult to process automatically or integrate into broader digital ecosystems. The use case therefore involves archivists, cultural-heritage institutions and information-management stakeholders seeking to create a richer and more machine-readable description model for archival material. Its objective is to support the semi-automatic creation of metadata, improve the quality and consistency of archival descriptions and connect national archives to the wider network of semantic linked data. The implementation uses natural language processing, named-entity recognition and machine-learning methods to analyse archival descriptions and, where available, the digital documents themselves. Conceptually, the system extracts entities, relationships and relevant properties from texts and uses them to populate a semantic description model that is more expressive and more suitable for automated processing than conventional archival structures. The impact is improved discoverability, stronger interoperability and reduced manual effort in metadata creation. More broadly, the project increases the accessibility and long-term value of archival collections, supports digital transformation in the cultural sector and enables more scalable management of documentary heritage.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-97,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00094,EU_PSTW_202609_00094,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,National Institute of Medical Emergency,Health n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,Data2Help - Optimizing the allocation of resources for a faster response to medical emergencies,National Institute of Medical Emergency; Central-Government; Health n.e.c.; Portugal,"Emergency demand varies across locations and over time, making it difficult to position vehicles and teams optimally using static planning alone. The use case therefore involves emergency-service operators, dispatch teams and planning stakeholders who need better forecasting and decision support to ensure that limited resources are directed where they are most needed.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Data2Help was created to help Portugal’s National Institute for Medical Emergency (INEM) improve operational performance by allocating emergency resources more effectively and reducing response times. Emergency demand varies across locations and over time, making it difficult to position vehicles and teams optimally using static planning alone. The use case therefore involves emergency-service operators, dispatch teams and planning stakeholders who need better forecasting and decision support to ensure that limited resources are directed where they are most needed. The objective is to strengthen emergency preparedness and improve the speed and quality of response by combining operational, geographic and contextual information in a more systematic way. The implementation integrates INEM’s internal information systems with external datasets such as meteorology, epidemic indicators, demography and forest-fire information, and uses historical records to build predictive models for emergency vehicle demand in each geographic area. These forecasts then inform optimisation models that support resource allocation and deployment decisions. At a conceptual level, the solution combines data integration, demand prediction and operational optimisation within emergency-management workflows. The expected impact is faster response to medical emergencies, more efficient use of vehicles and personnel, and improved scalability in managing fluctuating demand. In public-value terms, it supports more reliable emergency coverage, better service outcomes and stronger resilience in critical healthcare operations.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION|S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-98,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00095,EU_PSTW_202609_00095,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Portuguese Public National Health Service (Centro de Controlo e Monitorização do Sistema Nacional de Saúde),Health n.e.c.,"The operational challenge was to monitor very large volumes of prescription and invoicing data across multiple databases in order to identify suspicious claims without relying on slower, fragmented manual checks.",CCM-SNS - Verification of medical prescriptions,Portuguese Public National Health Service (Centro de Controlo e Monitorização do Sistema Nacional de Saúde); Central-Government; Health n.e.c.; Portugal,"The use case therefore involves the Control and Monitoring Centre of the Portuguese Public National Health Service, healthcare-finance authorities and prosecutors, with the objective of protecting public funds, improving oversight of prescription activity and supporting the investigation of fraud within the health system. The solution is implemented through data integration and analytical controls applied to several health and billing databases.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Portugal’s National Health Service needed a stronger way to detect irregular prescribing patterns and possible fraud after introducing paperless prescriptions and mandatory electronic medical prescriptions across the public sector in 2016. The operational challenge was to monitor very large volumes of prescription and invoicing data across multiple databases in order to identify suspicious claims without relying on slower, fragmented manual checks. The use case therefore involves the Control and Monitoring Centre of the Portuguese Public National Health Service, healthcare-finance authorities and prosecutors, with the objective of protecting public funds, improving oversight of prescription activity and supporting the investigation of fraud within the health system. The solution is implemented through data integration and analytical controls applied to several health and billing databases. At a conceptual level, the system cross-checks prescription, invoicing and related administrative records to identify anomalies and inconsistencies that may indicate fraudulent or improper activity. These flagged cases can then be escalated for further review or prosecution. The results reported are substantial: during the first seven years, irregularities amounting to hundreds of millions of euros were detected, and in the first half of 2017 alone the Invoice Control Centre processed EUR 356.2 million in prescriptions and flagged EUR 21.1 million to the State Prosecutor. The impact includes major financial-control benefits, improved fraud detection and stronger accountability in public healthcare spending.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK|F06_HIGH_VOLUME_SCALABILITY_BOTTLENECK,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-99,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00096,EU_PSTW_202609_00096,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Shared Services of the Ministry of Health,R&D health.,"The operational challenge lies in improving referral quality, reducing delays and supporting more consistent triage across different points of care.",Derm.AI - Tele Dermatological Screening,Shared Services of the Ministry of Health; Central-Government; R&D health.; Portugal,"This use case aims to improve teledermatology processes within the National Health Service by strengthening collaboration between Primary Care Units and Hospital Dermatology Departments in the assessment of skin lesions. At a conceptual level, the service uses image analysis, classification models and workflow integration to support case submission, prioritisation and review.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"This use case aims to improve teledermatology processes within the National Health Service by strengthening collaboration between Primary Care Units and Hospital Dermatology Departments in the assessment of skin lesions. In the existing pathway, general practitioners must capture and refer cases efficiently, while dermatologists need to review large numbers of referrals and prioritise those requiring urgent attention. The operational challenge lies in improving referral quality, reducing delays and supporting more consistent triage across different points of care. The stakeholders include general practitioners, dermatologists, hospital teams and healthcare administrators, all of whom require a more effective screening and prioritisation process. The objective is to streamline dermatology referral workflows, improve early assessment and support faster, more appropriate specialist intervention. The implementation combines computer-vision capabilities in a mobile application used by general practitioners with a risk-prioritisation and decision-support platform for dermatologists, both integrated into the SIGA-VAI eReferral system. At a conceptual level, the service uses image analysis, classification models and workflow integration to support case submission, prioritisation and review. The expected impact includes improved referral consistency, better prioritisation of higher-risk lesions, reduced pressure on specialist services and a smoother user experience for clinicians. Overall, the use case supports more timely care, better use of specialist capacity and greater efficiency in teledermatology services.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-100,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00097,EU_PSTW_202609_00097,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Directorate-General for Education and Science Statistics,Education n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,ModEst - Student Flow Modeling,Directorate-General for Education and Science Statistics; Central-Government; Education n.e.c.; Portugal,"The use case therefore involves education planners, public administrators and policy stakeholders who need robust forecasting to support decision-making across the six stages of education. In practical terms, it supports earlier identification of risk areas, better-targeted interventions and more informed use of public education resources.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"ModEst addresses the need for better planning and policy support in the Portuguese education system by producing reliable estimates of student movements across the system at NUTS 3 regional level. Education authorities must understand how learners progress through different stages of education, where dropouts occur and how admission patterns evolve over time in order to design effective policies and corrective measures. The use case therefore involves education planners, public administrators and policy stakeholders who need robust forecasting to support decision-making across the six stages of education. Its objective is to model student flows more accurately and provide a structured evidence base for planning interventions, resource allocation and policy design. The implementation decomposes forecasting into segment-specific models corresponding to each education stage and estimates variables such as admissions, progressions and dropouts on an annual basis. These models are enriched with contextual socio-economic data from sources including the National Statistics Institute and Pordata, enabling regional patterns and external influences to be incorporated into the analysis. At a conceptual level, the solution combines predictive modelling, segmentation and contextual data enrichment. The impact is improved planning precision, stronger policy insight and a more scalable way to monitor educational dynamics over time. In practical terms, it supports earlier identification of risk areas, better-targeted interventions and more informed use of public education resources.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION|S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-101,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00098,EU_PSTW_202609_00098,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""social_nonprofit""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Directorate-General for Territory,R&D environmental protection.,"IPSTERS focuses on the challenge of processing very large volumes of remotely sensed data for land applications with minimal manual intervention. Public-sector and research stakeholders increasingly depend on satellite and other remote-sensing data, yet the scale and complexity of these datasets make traditional processing workflows slow and resource-intensive.",IPSTERS - Remote Sense Data,Directorate-General for Territory; Central-Government; R&D environmental protection.; Portugal,"The use case brings together stakeholders interested in environmental monitoring and land-information products, with the objective of improving the efficiency and autonomy of data digestion and generating level-3 products suitable for operational use. The implementation uses accelerated processing hardware within the IPSentinel framework and investigates two complementary approaches: one based on active learning and another based on fuzzy logic.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"IPSTERS focuses on the challenge of processing very large volumes of remotely sensed data for land applications with minimal manual intervention. Public-sector and research stakeholders increasingly depend on satellite and other remote-sensing data, yet the scale and complexity of these datasets make traditional processing workflows slow and resource-intensive. The use case brings together stakeholders interested in environmental monitoring and land-information products, with the objective of improving the efficiency and autonomy of data digestion and generating level-3 products suitable for operational use. It also explores the practical boundaries of advanced analytical methods when combined with accelerated hardware. The implementation uses accelerated processing hardware within the IPSentinel framework and investigates two complementary approaches: one based on active learning and another based on fuzzy logic. At a conceptual level, the system is designed to automate the interpretation and transformation of large remote-sensing datasets into higher-level analytical products while reducing the need for continuous human intervention. The expected impact is faster processing, improved scalability and greater consistency in producing land-related outputs from remote-sensing data. More broadly, the project supports better exploitation of Earth-observation information, enhances operational efficiency for data-intensive workflows and provides a foundation for more responsive environmental and territorial analysis in the public sector.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK|F06_HIGH_VOLUME_SCALABILITY_BOTTLENECK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-102,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00099,EU_PSTW_202609_00099,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Gambling Inspection and Regulation Service,Social protection n.e.c.,"The operational challenge is that harmful gambling behaviour can be difficult to detect early through manual review alone, especially at scale, despite the importance of timely intervention for consumer protection and regulatory oversight.",GADgET - Online Gambling Addiction Detection,Gambling Inspection and Regulation Service; Central-Government; Social protection n.e.c.; Portugal,"GADgET was designed to help the gambling control authority identify problematic patterns of online gambling behaviour more effectively by making better use of the large volumes of behavioural data generated every day on digital gambling platforms. The use case therefore involves the gambling regulator and associated supervisory stakeholders, with the objective of modelling user behaviour, detecting patterns associated with addiction risk and enabling proportionate follow-up actions where necessary.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"GADgET was designed to help the gambling control authority identify problematic patterns of online gambling behaviour more effectively by making better use of the large volumes of behavioural data generated every day on digital gambling platforms. The operational challenge is that harmful gambling behaviour can be difficult to detect early through manual review alone, especially at scale, despite the importance of timely intervention for consumer protection and regulatory oversight. The use case therefore involves the gambling regulator and associated supervisory stakeholders, with the objective of modelling user behaviour, detecting patterns associated with addiction risk and enabling proportionate follow-up actions where necessary. The implementation uses behavioural data captured from online gambling activity to build detection models capable of identifying behavioural signals associated with addiction. At a conceptual level, the solution combines user-behaviour analysis, pattern-modelling and integration with the authority’s existing operational system so that flagged cases can be reviewed and acted upon within current supervisory processes. The impact is improved detection capability, more efficient monitoring of large user populations and stronger support for targeted regulatory intervention. In qualitative terms, the project enhances scalability, improves the consistency of oversight and contributes to better protection of vulnerable users, while also increasing the effectiveness and responsiveness of the authority’s control mechanisms.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-103,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00100,EU_PSTW_202609_00100,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Polytechnic Institute of Setúbal,Water supply.,"WISDom addresses a core operational challenge for water utilities: how to turn large volumes of network and consumption data into practical insight that improves service continuity, reduces losses and supports reliable water supply in both quantity and quality.",WISDom - Water Intelligence Data,Polytechnic Institute of Setúbal; Central-Government; Water supply.; Portugal,"The use case involves water-network operators, planners and technical decision-makers who need stronger visibility over consumption patterns, infrastructure failures and abnormal events in order to manage increasingly complex systems more effectively. In practical terms, the project supports better resource use, greater scalability in operational control and stronger decision-making for maintaining resilient water services.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"WISDom addresses a core operational challenge for water utilities: how to turn large volumes of network and consumption data into practical insight that improves service continuity, reduces losses and supports reliable water supply in both quantity and quality. The use case involves water-network operators, planners and technical decision-makers who need stronger visibility over consumption patterns, infrastructure failures and abnormal events in order to manage increasingly complex systems more effectively. Its objective is to improve network management by enabling earlier detection of problems, more accurate forecasting and better-informed operational decisions across the water cycle. The implementation combines exploratory data analysis with predictive and spatial modelling techniques applied to Water Intelligence System data. At a conceptual level, the solution covers five analytical capabilities: understanding historical patterns, forecasting water consumption, locating pipe breaks spatially, identifying and anticipating anomalous events, and delivering a prototype recommendation and decision-support system for end users. These components are integrated into a unified analytical framework that supports both monitoring and planning activities. The expected impact is lower water loss, faster identification of incidents, more efficient network management and improved service reliability. In practical terms, the project supports better resource use, greater scalability in operational control and stronger decision-making for maintaining resilient water services.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F06_HIGH_VOLUME_SCALABILITY_BOTTLENECK,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-104,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00101,EU_PSTW_202609_00101,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Bucarest-Henri Coandă Airport,Public order and safety n.e.c.,"Conventional airport surveillance can struggle to process the scale and pace of activity effectively, particularly when security teams need to distinguish priority threats from routine movement in real time.",Bucharest airport Face recognition - Detection of wanted persons and unsafe situations,Bucarest-Henri Coandă Airport; Local Government; Public order and safety n.e.c.; Romania,"This use case therefore involves airport authorities, security services and intelligence-linked stakeholders, with the objective of improving threat detection, supporting faster intervention and enhancing overall passenger and infrastructure safety. The solution is implemented through a facial-recognition-based security system designed both to identify persons on wanted or intelligence-service lists and to detect suspicious behaviour patterns.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Bucharest Henri Coandă Airport sought to strengthen its security operations in a context where major transport hubs must identify wanted individuals, respond to terrorism-related risks and detect suspicious behaviour across fast-moving passenger flows. Conventional airport surveillance can struggle to process the scale and pace of activity effectively, particularly when security teams need to distinguish priority threats from routine movement in real time. This use case therefore involves airport authorities, security services and intelligence-linked stakeholders, with the objective of improving threat detection, supporting faster intervention and enhancing overall passenger and infrastructure safety. The solution is implemented through a facial-recognition-based security system designed both to identify persons on wanted or intelligence-service lists and to detect suspicious behaviour patterns. At a conceptual level, the platform combines live camera feeds with biometric matching and behavioural-analysis functions so that visual information from the airport environment can be screened automatically against relevant watchlists and risk indicators. This creates a dual-layer security capability focused on both person identification and situational anomaly detection. The expected impact is stronger surveillance coverage, faster recognition of high-risk individuals and improved capacity to spot unsafe situations before they escalate. More broadly, the system creates value by enhancing airport-security efficiency, supporting more targeted use of human security resources and contributing to a more proactive and technology-enabled approach to protecting a critical transport hub.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-105,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00102,EU_PSTW_202609_00102,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Rammicu-Valcea,Police Services,"Heavy traffic volumes can make manual identification impractical and can also reduce the effectiveness of conventional camera systems when conditions are complex or fast-moving. While the source does not provide quantitative performance figures, the expected impact is better plate-detection accuracy, reduced manual effort and more reliable road-monitoring outputs.",Metrici LPR cameras - Better license plate detection,City of Rammicu-Valcea; Private sector; Police Services; Romania,"The solution is implemented through an LPR camera system enhanced with image-analysis technology for licence-plate recognition. More broadly, the use case creates value by improving the operational performance of camera infrastructure on high-traffic routes and by supporting scalable vehicle identification where traffic intensity makes conventional methods less effective.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Authorities managing one of Romania’s busiest traffic corridors needed more reliable vehicle-identification capabilities in order to support traffic monitoring and related enforcement or operational functions. Heavy traffic volumes can make manual identification impractical and can also reduce the effectiveness of conventional camera systems when conditions are complex or fast-moving. The operational problem was therefore to improve the accuracy and usability of licence-plate capture in a demanding transport environment. The stakeholders include road authorities, traffic-management teams and organisations relying on vehicle-recognition data, with the objective of improving detection quality, supporting more effective monitoring and making camera-based traffic analysis more dependable at scale. The solution is implemented through an LPR camera system enhanced with image-analysis technology for licence-plate recognition. At a conceptual level, the platform analyses video or image streams captured along the route, isolates vehicle plates and improves character extraction so that registration numbers can be read more accurately under heavy-traffic conditions. This creates a stronger basis for downstream traffic-management, control or investigative processes than standard visual review alone. While the source does not provide quantitative performance figures, the expected impact is better plate-detection accuracy, reduced manual effort and more reliable road-monitoring outputs. More broadly, the use case creates value by improving the operational performance of camera infrastructure on high-traffic routes and by supporting scalable vehicle identification where traffic intensity makes conventional methods less effective.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-106,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00103,EU_PSTW_202609_00103,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Cluj-Napoca,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.","Cluj-Napoca City Hall introduced Antonia to address a common municipal challenge: handling large volumes of standard service requests quickly, consistently and without creating unnecessary queues for residents. Public administrations often spend substantial time on repetitive form processing and on answering routine questions about how applications should be completed, which can slow service delivery and increase frustration for citizens.",Antonia - Automatic processing of requests of public services,"Municipality of Cluj-Napoca; Local Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Romania","The use case therefore involves city-hall staff, local residents and applicants for municipal services, with the objective of simplifying access to public services, reducing administrative bottlenecks and ensuring that requests can be handled efficiently at any time of day. The solution is implemented through a digital system that automatically processes 64 types of forms for public-service requests and includes a chatbot component that answers questions about how to complete them.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Cluj-Napoca City Hall introduced Antonia to address a common municipal challenge: handling large volumes of standard service requests quickly, consistently and without creating unnecessary queues for residents. Public administrations often spend substantial time on repetitive form processing and on answering routine questions about how applications should be completed, which can slow service delivery and increase frustration for citizens. The use case therefore involves city-hall staff, local residents and applicants for municipal services, with the objective of simplifying access to public services, reducing administrative bottlenecks and ensuring that requests can be handled efficiently at any time of day. The solution is implemented through a digital system that automatically processes 64 types of forms for public-service requests and includes a chatbot component that answers questions about how to complete them. At a conceptual level, the platform combines form-recognition, workflow automation and conversational support to guide users through administrative procedures and route applications into the city hall’s operational processes. Antonia has operated around the clock since its launch in April 2018, creating a continuous digital access point for residents. A second development stage is intended to extend the system into a hologram-based interface, with completion of the wider project estimated over a 30-month period, subject to technical progress. The impact includes faster processing, reduced waiting, improved user guidance and a more scalable model for municipal service delivery.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK|F06_HIGH_VOLUME_SCALABILITY_BOTTLENECK,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI|S6_WORKFLOW_PROCESS_AUTOMATION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-107,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00104,EU_PSTW_202609_00104,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Financial Administration,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.","The Slovak Financial Administration introduced Taxana to improve communication with taxpayers and to reduce the burden on traditional support channels in a context of ongoing digitalisation of public finance services. Taxpayers frequently need answers to routine procedural questions, and when these enquiries are handled only through call centres or manual support, staff capacity is quickly absorbed by repetitive issues that do not require specialist judgement.",Taxana - Chatbot for financial administration,"Financial Administration; Central-Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Slovakia","The use case therefore involves taxpayers, financial-administration staff and customer-support teams, with the objective of making tax-related processes easier to navigate, improving service accessibility and allowing staff to focus on more complex matters. The solution is implemented through an automated chatbot available 24/7, making the Slovak Financial Administration the first state institution in the country to launch this type of tool for client communication.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Slovak Financial Administration introduced Taxana to improve communication with taxpayers and to reduce the burden on traditional support channels in a context of ongoing digitalisation of public finance services. Taxpayers frequently need answers to routine procedural questions, and when these enquiries are handled only through call centres or manual support, staff capacity is quickly absorbed by repetitive issues that do not require specialist judgement. The use case therefore involves taxpayers, financial-administration staff and customer-support teams, with the objective of making tax-related processes easier to navigate, improving service accessibility and allowing staff to focus on more complex matters. The solution is implemented through an automated chatbot available 24/7, making the Slovak Financial Administration the first state institution in the country to launch this type of tool for client communication. At a conceptual level, the system uses conversational language processing and structured tax-service knowledge to interpret user questions and provide relevant answers through a digital interface. This creates an always-available first line of support that complements existing channels and fits within the broader shift towards electronic processes in financial administration. The impact includes faster responses to common enquiries, lower pressure on call-centre staff and a more convenient user experience for taxpayers. More broadly, Taxana creates value by supporting public-sector digital transformation, improving service efficiency and enabling financial-administration teams to devote more time to higher-value and more complex client issues.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-108,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00105,EU_PSTW_202609_00105,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,The Office of the Deputy Prime Minister of the Slovak Republic for Investments and Informatization (ODPMII),General services.,"The stakeholders include citizens, public-service users and administrative bodies involved in simplification efforts, with the objective of reducing confusion, speeding up access to guidance and supporting a less burdensome relationship between people and the state. At a conceptual level, the system combines conversational query handling with structured administrative knowledge so that users can ask questions in natural language and receive practical guidance without needing to search through multiple websites or documents manually.",OverSi - Chatbot to faster burocracy,The Office of the Deputy Prime Minister of the Slovak Republic for Investments and Informatization (ODPMII); Central-Government; General services.; Slovakia,"Public procedures are often difficult to navigate, particularly when people are uncertain which rule applies, which document is needed or which process should be followed. The solution is implemented through a chatbot designed to respond to routine public questions and direct users towards the most appropriate information or procedure.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Slovakia’s wider effort to reduce bureaucracy and apply the once-only principle in digital form created a need for simpler ways for citizens to obtain clear answers to common administrative questions. Public procedures are often difficult to navigate, particularly when people are uncertain which rule applies, which document is needed or which process should be followed. The OverSi chatbot was introduced as part of a government initiative to tackle that friction by helping the general public identify the most relevant information and solutions more quickly. The stakeholders include citizens, public-service users and administrative bodies involved in simplification efforts, with the objective of reducing confusion, speeding up access to guidance and supporting a less burdensome relationship between people and the state. The solution is implemented through a chatbot designed to respond to routine public questions and direct users towards the most appropriate information or procedure. At a conceptual level, the system combines conversational query handling with structured administrative knowledge so that users can ask questions in natural language and receive practical guidance without needing to search through multiple websites or documents manually. This supports the broader digital application of the once-only principle by helping people navigate services more efficiently. The impact includes quicker access to answers, less wasted effort for users and a more scalable way of handling recurring public enquiries. More broadly, OverSi creates value by supporting administrative simplification and improving the usability of public services in a digital environment.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-109,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00106,EU_PSTW_202609_00106,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Institute of health policy (IZP),Public health services.,UNKNOWN,PCA - AI algorithms in audit methodology detecting potential fraud when prescribing medicines,Institute of health policy (IZP); Central-Government; Public health services.; Slovakia,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"AI algorithms in audit methodology detecting potential fraud when prescribing medicines. Between 2014–2018, big data analysis was used to examine prescribing of two expensive ophthalmology drugs, Eylea and Lucentis, in Slovakia. The study found that 15.5% of patients were treated atypically, with high concentrations of irregular prescribing in three centres. These anomalies could stem from medical decisions, patient non-compliance, or potential fraud, with estimated savings of €1.8M if practices matched national norms. Following the analysis, health insurers tightened monitoring and restrictions for these treatments. The case highlights how big data can uncover inefficiencies and protect public resources, while final judgments must rest with medical experts.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-110,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00107,EU_PSTW_202609_00107,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,The Financial Supervisory Authority,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.","One of the authority’s operational challenges involved manually entering information on investment services offered by European companies into a database, based on notifications exchanged between authorities. The objective was therefore to reduce manual workload, improve processing accuracy and allow supervisory staff to focus on higher-value activities while maintaining the reliability of regulatory information handling.",Case Fiva (RPA) - Support the Financial supervisory Authority,"The Financial Supervisory Authority; Central-Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Finland","This use case was developed to support the Financial Supervisory Authority in carrying out time-consuming administrative tasks more efficiently in a context of limited resources and high accuracy requirements. The impact was improved operational efficiency, a marked reduction in human error and better use of constrained supervisory resources.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"This use case was developed to support the Financial Supervisory Authority in carrying out time-consuming administrative tasks more efficiently in a context of limited resources and high accuracy requirements. One of the authority’s operational challenges involved manually entering information on investment services offered by European companies into a database, based on notifications exchanged between authorities. The task required significant staff effort, was repetitive in nature and presented substantial scope for human error. The objective was therefore to reduce manual workload, improve processing accuracy and allow supervisory staff to focus on higher-value activities while maintaining the reliability of regulatory information handling. The implementation combined robotic process automation with rule-based processing to capture, validate and enter structured information into the relevant database. At a conceptual level, the solution replicated a clearly defined administrative workflow that could be standardised and executed consistently by software, with appropriate integration into the authority’s existing data-management processes. The impact was improved operational efficiency, a marked reduction in human error and better use of constrained supervisory resources. Qualitatively, the solution increased reliability, reduced the burden of repetitive work and demonstrated a scalable approach for automating similar back-office tasks in financial supervision.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,S6_WORKFLOW_PROCESS_AUTOMATION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-111,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00108,EU_PSTW_202609_00108,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Ministry of Public Administration,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.","The operational problem was that conventional text comparison methods tended to rely heavily on statistical similarity or keyword overlap, which limited their ability to identify documents expressing the same ideas in different wording. The implementation used semantic text-analysis techniques to compare unstructured documents according to their content and intended meaning.",Semantic analyser - Document analyser for the public administration,"Ministry of Public Administration; Central-Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Slovenia","This use case was initiated by the Ministry of Public Administration, in cooperation with the Faculty of Computer Science and Informatics of the University of Ljubljana, to improve how public administration bodies compare and analyse unstructured textual documents. The use case therefore involved public-sector organisations handling large volumes of documents and seeking more accurate ways to identify similarities, overlaps or related content across texts.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"This use case was initiated by the Ministry of Public Administration, in cooperation with the Faculty of Computer Science and Informatics of the University of Ljubljana, to improve how public administration bodies compare and analyse unstructured textual documents. The operational problem was that conventional text comparison methods tended to rely heavily on statistical similarity or keyword overlap, which limited their ability to identify documents expressing the same ideas in different wording. The use case therefore involved public-sector organisations handling large volumes of documents and seeking more accurate ways to identify similarities, overlaps or related content across texts. Its objective was to enable comparison based on meaning and context rather than surface-level word matching alone. The implementation used semantic text-analysis techniques to compare unstructured documents according to their content and intended meaning. At a conceptual level, the solution relied on language-processing methods capable of recognising contextual similarity, semantically related expressions and documents containing equivalent or closely aligned concepts even where vocabulary differed. The impact was improved document analysis, more effective retrieval of related texts and stronger support for knowledge-intensive administrative work. In practical terms, the tool increases efficiency in handling unstructured information, improves consistency in text comparison and provides a more scalable basis for document management within public administration.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F04_UNSTRUCTURED_INFORMATION_COMPLEXITY,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-112,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00109,EU_PSTW_202609_00109,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,INTRASOFT International SA,Transport,UNKNOWN,Optimum - decision making for efficient integration of transport modes,INTRASOFT International SA; Private sector; Transport; Belgium,"In complex city environments, travellers depend on a combination of transport options, yet fragmented information and rapidly changing traffic conditions can make it difficult for operators and planners to maintain quality, accessibility and efficient system use. The use case involved urban mobility stakeholders participating in pilot studies, with the objective of facilitating proactive decision-making through a smart multimodal transit concept that would improve the coordination and utilisation of different transport modes.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"OPTIMUM was created to support urban authorities in making better decisions about the integration of transport modes across interconnected mobility systems. In complex city environments, travellers depend on a combination of transport options, yet fragmented information and rapidly changing traffic conditions can make it difficult for operators and planners to maintain quality, accessibility and efficient system use. The use case involved urban mobility stakeholders participating in pilot studies, with the objective of facilitating proactive decision-making through a smart multimodal transit concept that would improve the coordination and utilisation of different transport modes. The implementation combined multiple transport information sources with advanced real-time predictive analytics to create a complex model of current traffic conditions and a short-term forecast of how those conditions were likely to evolve. At a conceptual level, the system integrated multimodal data streams, predictive models and decision-support capabilities to provide a dynamic view of network conditions and support more effective operational and planning choices. The impact was improved integration of transport services, better accessibility and more efficient use of interconnected mobility networks. More broadly, the use case supports higher service quality, stronger responsiveness to changing conditions and a more scalable approach to managing multimodal urban transport.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-113,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00110,EU_PSTW_202609_00110,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Ministry of Territorial Policy and Public Function,General services.,"A key operational challenge for public administrations is that official terminology is often formal, technical and difficult for citizens to understand, while large volumes of routine questions can create pressure on service teams.",060 Service - Answer FAQ from citizens and burocratic terms into informal language,Ministry of Territorial Policy and Public Function; Central-Government; General services.; Spain,"This use case focused on improving clarity and accessibility by helping citizens obtain answers to frequently asked questions and by translating bureaucratic language into more informal, user-friendly terms. The solution was implemented using natural language processing to interpret citizen queries and match them to relevant answers, while also recognising formal administrative expressions and converting them into plainer language.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Spain’s 060 Service was created as a central contact point to help citizens navigate the complexity of central government services through one short number and a unified access channel. A key operational challenge for public administrations is that official terminology is often formal, technical and difficult for citizens to understand, while large volumes of routine questions can create pressure on service teams. This use case focused on improving clarity and accessibility by helping citizens obtain answers to frequently asked questions and by translating bureaucratic language into more informal, user-friendly terms. The stakeholders included Spanish citizens, central-administration service teams and public-service providers connected through the 060 channel, with the objective of making public information easier to access, easier to understand and more consistent across services. The solution was implemented using natural language processing to interpret citizen queries and match them to relevant answers, while also recognising formal administrative expressions and converting them into plainer language. At a conceptual level, the system combined FAQ retrieval, conversational query handling and terminology simplification so that citizens could interact more naturally with government information. The expected impact was faster access to answers, reduced friction in understanding public services and improved efficiency in handling common enquiries. More broadly, the 060 Service created value by simplifying interaction with central administration, improving the user experience and supporting a more citizen-centred model of public information delivery.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,NOT_IN_USE,미사용,F06_HIGH_VOLUME_SCALABILITY_BOTTLENECK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-114,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00111,EU_PSTW_202609_00111,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Health Institute Carlos III,"Medical products, appliances and equipment.","During the COVID-19 emergency, Spanish authorities faced the urgent challenge of scaling up testing capacity quickly enough to support public-health management and identify infections at a much larger volume than manual processes could comfortably handle. Traditional laboratory and sample-processing workflows risked becoming a bottleneck at a time when testing speed and throughput were critical to the national response.",Robots - Automating Covid-19 tests,"Health Institute Carlos III; Central-Government; Medical products, appliances and equipment.; Spain",The use case therefore focused on increasing testing capacity in order to improve operational efficiency and strengthen the response to the spread of the virus. The solution was implemented through the planned acquisition and testing of four robots designed to automate parts of the COVID-19 testing process.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"During the COVID-19 emergency, Spanish authorities faced the urgent challenge of scaling up testing capacity quickly enough to support public-health management and identify infections at a much larger volume than manual processes could comfortably handle. Traditional laboratory and sample-processing workflows risked becoming a bottleneck at a time when testing speed and throughput were critical to the national response. The use case therefore focused on increasing testing capacity in order to improve operational efficiency and strengthen the response to the spread of the virus. The stakeholders included health authorities, laboratory operators and the wider public, with the objective of processing far more tests per day while reducing dependence on slower, labour-intensive routines. The solution was implemented through the planned acquisition and testing of four robots designed to automate parts of the COVID-19 testing process. At a conceptual level, the system was intended to use robotic automation to handle repetitive steps within the testing workflow, allowing higher throughput and more consistent execution than purely manual operations. The target capacity reported was up to 80,000 tests per day, highlighting the scale of the ambition behind the project. The expected impact was significantly increased testing throughput, reduced operational pressure on staff and stronger capacity to manage pandemic surveillance. More broadly, the initiative illustrated how robotic automation could support rapid scaling in high-demand public-health operations, with value in speed, consistency and resilience during a crisis.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,NOT_IN_USE,미사용,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-115,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00112,EU_PSTW_202609_00112,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Fuengirola,Recreational and sporting services.,"The operational challenge was that beach attendance changes constantly, and manual supervision alone could not provide timely, area-wide visibility of occupancy levels.",Fuengirola Town Hall - measuring beach attendance,Municipality of Fuengirola; Local Government; Recreational and sporting services.; Spain,"During the COVID-19 pandemic, Fuengirola Town Hall needed a practical way to manage crowding on beaches and in public spaces so that residents and visitors could use these areas more safely while reducing the risk of infection. The use case therefore focused on helping the municipality monitor capacity in real time and communicate crowding information clearly to the public.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"During the COVID-19 pandemic, Fuengirola Town Hall needed a practical way to manage crowding on beaches and in public spaces so that residents and visitors could use these areas more safely while reducing the risk of infection. The operational challenge was that beach attendance changes constantly, and manual supervision alone could not provide timely, area-wide visibility of occupancy levels. The use case therefore focused on helping the municipality monitor capacity in real time and communicate crowding information clearly to the public. The stakeholders included town-hall representatives, local residents, visitors and public-space managers, with the objective of avoiding overcrowding, supporting safer use of public areas and improving situational awareness during the health emergency. The solution was implemented through a system developed by a local company using sensors installed in streetlights and virtual occupancy grids with defined maximum capacities. At a conceptual level, the system measured the three-dimensional movement of people entering and leaving monitored spaces and counted how many individuals were present in each measurement grid, producing real-time occupancy calculations. These results were then translated into public warnings using percentages and colour-coded alerts. The expected impact was better crowd management, improved public information and faster response to unsafe density levels. More broadly, the initiative created value by improving public-space management, supporting safer tourism and demonstrating a scalable model for real-time occupancy monitoring in high-footfall environments.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,NOT_IN_USE,미사용,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-116,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00113,EU_PSTW_202609_00113,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Sant Just Desvern,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.","Local authorities often face pressure to reduce waiting times and make services more accessible, yet appointment scheduling and registration management can still generate delays and administrative overhead when handled through conventional channels alone.",Chatbot - Boost relations with citizens,"Municipality of Sant Just Desvern; Local Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Spain","The use case therefore involved the city council, residents and frontline administrative teams, with the objective of speeding up access to services, improving communication with citizens and strengthening day-to-day relations between the administration and the local community. The solution is implemented through two initial chatbot services focused on arranging prior appointments with the City Council and supporting management of registration flyers.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Sant Just Desvern City Council launched its chatbot initiative to improve how citizens interact with municipal services and to streamline routine administrative processes in a town of around 17,200 inhabitants. Local authorities often face pressure to reduce waiting times and make services more accessible, yet appointment scheduling and registration management can still generate delays and administrative overhead when handled through conventional channels alone. The use case therefore involved the city council, residents and frontline administrative teams, with the objective of speeding up access to services, improving communication with citizens and strengthening day-to-day relations between the administration and the local community. The solution is implemented through two initial chatbot services focused on arranging prior appointments with the City Council and supporting management of registration flyers. At a conceptual level, the system uses conversational interaction to guide users through these processes, capture their requests and route them into the relevant municipal workflow, reducing reliance on slower manual exchanges. This creates a more immediate digital access point for common tasks and helps the administration organise routine demand more efficiently. The impact includes shorter waits, smoother appointment handling and a more convenient experience for residents seeking municipal services. More broadly, the project creates value by improving responsiveness, supporting a more citizen-friendly local administration and demonstrating how targeted chatbot deployment can strengthen both efficiency and public engagement at municipal level.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-117,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00114,EU_PSTW_202609_00114,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Spanish Police,Police Services,"The operational challenge is that risk can change across time and across judicial stages, and authorities need a more consistent basis for deciding how to prioritise resources before trial, after sentencing or even in situations where a trial does not take place.",VioGen - Forecast gender violence,Spanish Police; Central-Government; Police Services; Spain,The solution is implemented through an algorithmic system that uses two assessment tests to evaluate the risk level associated with complainants or already sentenced offenders.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Spain introduced VioGen to support one of the most sensitive and high-stakes areas of public protection: assessing the risk faced by victims of gender-based violence and determining the level of police protection required. The operational challenge is that risk can change across time and across judicial stages, and authorities need a more consistent basis for deciding how to prioritise resources before trial, after sentencing or even in situations where a trial does not take place. The stakeholders include victims, police forces and protection services, with the objective of improving risk assessment, tailoring police protection more effectively and reducing repeat violence. The solution is implemented through an algorithmic system that uses two assessment tests to evaluate the risk level associated with complainants or already sentenced offenders. At a conceptual level, the system processes case-related information and translates it into a graded risk classification, which then determines the level of police monitoring and protection to be applied. This embeds the tool directly into protection workflows rather than treating it as a separate analytical exercise. The reported results are significant: according to the source, recurrence of attacks has fallen by 25% since implementation, and whereas recidivism in comparable neighbouring countries reaches around 35%, in Spain it is reported at 15%. The impact therefore includes measurable reductions in repeat violence, more targeted protection and stronger operational consistency in safeguarding victims of gender violence.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-118,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00115,EU_PSTW_202609_00115,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Spanish Police,Police Services,"False police reports, especially for low-level offences, consume investigative resources, distort crime recording and can be linked to wider fraudulent behaviour, making them a costly problem for law-enforcement agencies.",VeriPol - Detect false police reports,Spanish Police; Central-Government; Police Services; Spain,"The solution is implemented as a system integrated into SIDENPOL, the existing information system of the Spanish National Police, so that it can be used directly within standard reporting practices. At a conceptual level, VeriPol was trained on a dataset of 1,122 police reports, including 534 genuine and 588 false reports, and applies natural language processing together with classification models to estimate the probability that a report is false.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"False police reports, especially for low-level offences, consume investigative resources, distort crime recording and can be linked to wider fraudulent behaviour, making them a costly problem for law-enforcement agencies. In Spain, the National Police adopted VeriPol to help identify reports that may be fabricated and to improve the efficiency of case handling within existing policing workflows. The stakeholders include police officers, investigators and administrative staff, with the objective of reducing wasted effort, improving the quality of recorded complaints and supporting earlier detection of potentially fraudulent claims. The solution is implemented as a system integrated into SIDENPOL, the existing information system of the Spanish National Police, so that it can be used directly within standard reporting practices. At a conceptual level, VeriPol was trained on a dataset of 1,122 police reports, including 534 genuine and 588 false reports, and applies natural language processing together with classification models to estimate the probability that a report is false. In addition to producing a risk score, the system also provides insight into linguistic and structural differences between false and true reports, which can support professional understanding and review. The impact includes more efficient screening of complaints, better targeting of police attention and stronger integration into established operational systems. More broadly, VeriPol creates value by reducing misuse of police resources, improving investigative efficiency and supporting a more evidence-based response to fraudulent reporting.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-119,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00116,EU_PSTW_202609_00116,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,San Carlos Hospital,Hospital services.,"Conducted through field trials involving San Carlos Hospital in Madrid, Fujitsu Laboratories of Europe and Fujitsu Spain, the project addressed a high-stakes clinical context in which doctors must make fast decisions based on complex and often fragmented medical information.",Madrid - AI for saving lives of Psychiatric patients,San Carlos Hospital; Central-Government; Hospital services.; Spain,This use case focused on supporting clinicians in identifying suicide risk and other serious health threats among psychiatric patients more rapidly and consistently.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"This use case focused on supporting clinicians in identifying suicide risk and other serious health threats among psychiatric patients more rapidly and consistently. Conducted through field trials involving San Carlos Hospital in Madrid, Fujitsu Laboratories of Europe and Fujitsu Spain, the project addressed a high-stakes clinical context in which doctors must make fast decisions based on complex and often fragmented medical information. The stakeholders included psychiatrists, hospital teams and clinical researchers, with the objective of improving diagnostic support and helping professionals recognise relevant risks earlier in the care pathway. The implementation led to the development of the Advanced Clinical Research Information System, which combined anonymised medical histories from more than 36,000 patients with a large body of external knowledge, including over one million medical papers. At a conceptual level, the system used analytical models to examine this integrated knowledge base and present possible health risks relevant to an individual patient. The impact was improved clinical decision support, faster access to relevant evidence and stronger capacity to identify patients requiring urgent attention. In qualitative terms, the solution enhanced the usefulness of available data, improved the consistency of risk assessment and delivered significant potential value in protecting vulnerable psychiatric patients.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-120,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00117,EU_PSTW_202609_00117,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Xunta de Galicia,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.","In many cases, public e-services remain difficult to navigate, creating barriers to adoption and reducing the efficiency gains that digital transformation is meant to achieve.",SIMPATICO Platform for PS iN Spain,"Xunta de Galicia; Central-Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Spain","The use case, deployed in Galicia, involved public administrations, service managers and end users, with the objective of making existing systems more understandable, more personalised and more responsive to citizen needs.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The SIMPATICO Platform was developed to simplify how citizens and companies interact with public services by improving the usability and relevance of digital administrative systems. In many cases, public e-services remain difficult to navigate, creating barriers to adoption and reducing the efficiency gains that digital transformation is meant to achieve. The use case, deployed in Galicia, involved public administrations, service managers and end users, with the objective of making existing systems more understandable, more personalised and more responsive to citizen needs. It sought not only to improve individual service interactions but also to enable the continuous optimisation of services and administrative processes over time. The implementation used advanced technical components layered onto existing public administration systems to adapt content, support personalised interactions and improve the delivery of customer-centric services. At a conceptual level, the platform combined service integration, user-oriented adaptation and process-improvement mechanisms to reduce complexity and strengthen engagement with digital public services. The impact was better user experience for citizens and businesses, higher accessibility and improved uptake of e-services. More broadly, the platform supports efficiency in service delivery, enables continuous improvement of administrative processes and strengthens the public sector’s ability to provide scalable, user-centred digital services.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-121,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00118,EU_PSTW_202609_00118,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Barcelona,Housing development.,"ARSI addresses a difficult operational challenge for urban infrastructure management: inspecting sewer networks safely, efficiently and in sufficient detail to detect blockages, sediment build-up and structural defects before they compromise system performance. Sewer environments are hazardous, constrained and costly to inspect manually, which limits the frequency and breadth of conventional inspections.",ARSI – Aerial Robot for Sewer Inspection,Barcelona; Local Government; Housing development.; Spain,"The use case therefore involves infrastructure operators and maintenance teams seeking more effective ways to assess sewer condition, monitor system performance and collect environmental samples from hard-to-reach locations. The implementation uses an aerial robotic solution designed to operate within sewer environments and assess infrastructure condition while carrying out additional monitoring functions.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"ARSI addresses a difficult operational challenge for urban infrastructure management: inspecting sewer networks safely, efficiently and in sufficient detail to detect blockages, sediment build-up and structural defects before they compromise system performance. Sewer environments are hazardous, constrained and costly to inspect manually, which limits the frequency and breadth of conventional inspections. The use case therefore involves infrastructure operators and maintenance teams seeking more effective ways to assess sewer condition, monitor system performance and collect environmental samples from hard-to-reach locations. Its objective is to support earlier identification of compromised sewer segments and improve maintenance planning through more capable and less intrusive inspection methods. The implementation uses an aerial robotic solution designed to operate within sewer environments and assess infrastructure condition while carrying out additional monitoring functions. At a conceptual level, the platform combines onboard sensing, navigation and inspection capabilities to identify sediments, detect structural defects, monitor sewer conditions and collect samples of water, air and sediments for further analysis. The impact is improved inspection coverage, reduced reliance on labour-intensive manual procedures and stronger support for preventive maintenance. In operational terms, the solution offers better scalability, improved safety for inspection activities and greater reliability in monitoring critical underground infrastructure.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-122,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00119,EU_PSTW_202609_00119,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,University of Hertfordshire,"General economic, commercial and labour affair","The SCRIPT project was created to address a major challenge in stroke rehabilitation: how to ensure that patients, particularly those in the chronic phase of recovery, can continue to receive intensive and repetitive therapy beyond the clinical setting.",SCRIPT Project - Supervised Care & Rehabilitation Involving Personal Tele-robotics,"University of Hertfordshire; Academic-Research; General economic, commercial and labour affair; United Kingdom","The use case therefore involved stroke patients, clinicians, rehabilitation specialists and carers, with the objective of extending supervised rehabilitation into the home environment in a way that supports better long-term management of recovery. The implementation used robot-mediated rehabilitation technologies adapted for home use, combining tele-robotic devices with remote supervision and monitoring capabilities.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The SCRIPT project was created to address a major challenge in stroke rehabilitation: how to ensure that patients, particularly those in the chronic phase of recovery, can continue to receive intensive and repetitive therapy beyond the clinical setting. Conventional rehabilitation often depends on in-person sessions, which can limit the volume and frequency of exercise that patients are able to complete once they return home. The use case therefore involved stroke patients, clinicians, rehabilitation specialists and carers, with the objective of extending supervised rehabilitation into the home environment in a way that supports better long-term management of recovery. By enabling a higher number of repetitions and more frequent exercise, the project aimed to improve functional and motor outcomes while reducing dependence on hospital-based delivery alone. The implementation used robot-mediated rehabilitation technologies adapted for home use, combining tele-robotic devices with remote supervision and monitoring capabilities. At a conceptual level, the solution enabled structured exercise delivery, capture of performance data and integration with professional oversight so that therapy could be administered safely and consistently outside traditional care settings. The impact was expected to include greater continuity of care, improved rehabilitation intensity and a better user experience for patients managing recovery at home. More broadly, the project supports scalable long-term rehabilitation, more efficient use of clinical resources and stronger recovery potential for stroke patients.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-123,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00120,EU_PSTW_202609_00120,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Vall d'Hebron University Hospital,"Medical products, appliances and equipment.","The da Vinci Surgical System addresses this challenge by supporting minimally invasive surgery in situations where high dexterity and fine control are especially important. The stakeholders include surgeons, hospital teams and patients undergoing complex procedures, with the objective of improving surgical performance, reducing the physical burden of operations on patients and expanding the range of procedures that can be carried out through small incisions.",Robotic System Da Vinci,"Vall d'Hebron University Hospital; Academic-Research; Medical products, appliances and equipment.; Spain",The solution is implemented as a robotic-assisted surgical platform that enables surgeons to operate through a limited number of small entry points while controlling instruments with enhanced precision.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Hospitals and surgical teams continually seek ways to perform delicate and complex operations with greater precision, less trauma to the patient and shorter recovery times, particularly in procedures where conventional open surgery may involve larger incisions and longer rehabilitation. The da Vinci Surgical System addresses this challenge by supporting minimally invasive surgery in situations where high dexterity and fine control are especially important. The stakeholders include surgeons, hospital teams and patients undergoing complex procedures, with the objective of improving surgical performance, reducing the physical burden of operations on patients and expanding the range of procedures that can be carried out through small incisions. The solution is implemented as a robotic-assisted surgical platform that enables surgeons to operate through a limited number of small entry points while controlling instruments with enhanced precision. At a conceptual level, the system combines robotic arms, surgeon-controlled interfaces and high-definition visualisation to translate the surgeon’s movements into highly controlled instrument actions inside the body. This allows intricate procedures to be performed with a level of stability and manoeuvrability that can exceed conventional laparoscopy in certain contexts. The impact includes more precise surgical intervention, reduced invasiveness and potential improvements in patient recovery and hospital efficiency. More broadly, the da Vinci system creates value by modernising the surgical theatre, improving the quality of complex operations and supporting a more advanced model of minimally invasive care.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-124,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00121,EU_PSTW_202609_00121,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Vicomtech,"General economic, commercial and labour affair",UNKNOWN,VI-DAS – Vision Inspired Driver Assistance Systems,"Vicomtech; Academic-Research; General economic, commercial and labour affair; Spain","VI-DAS addressed the need for safer, more adaptive driver-assistance systems by focusing on contextual understanding of driver behaviour and road situations using non-invasive sensing. The use case therefore involved vehicle manufacturers, drivers, road-safety stakeholders and system designers seeking to improve safety, comfort and efficiency through better situational awareness and more personalised driver support.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"VI-DAS addressed the need for safer, more adaptive driver-assistance systems by focusing on contextual understanding of driver behaviour and road situations using non-invasive sensing. Many road incidents are linked to human error, yet traditional vehicle systems do not always interpret behaviour, environment and risk in a sufficiently integrated way. The use case therefore involved vehicle manufacturers, drivers, road-safety stakeholders and system designers seeking to improve safety, comfort and efficiency through better situational awareness and more personalised driver support. Its objective was to understand scene dynamics and driver behaviour in real time, and to provide the most appropriate response through vehicle interfaces that guide safer decisions. The implementation combined computer vision and machine-learning methods with inexpensive and widely available sensors, primarily cameras, to monitor the driving context and model driver behaviour. At a conceptual level, the system generated predictions about likely outcomes in a scene and used these to drive a personalised human-machine interface that recommended appropriate actions. A cloud platform supported large-scale storage and analysis of data, improved the design of sensors and algorithms, and enabled the use of connected and cooperative vehicle systems. The impact was improved safety support, more effective anticipation of risk and stronger potential to reduce incidents linked to human error. It also offered benefits in comfort, efficiency and scalability, while recognising important legal, liability and ethical considerations associated with advanced driver-assistance technologies.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-125,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00122,EU_PSTW_202609_00122,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,State Secretary for Digital Advancement (SEAD),Public health services.,"Corpus Viewer was developed to help public administrations make better-informed policy decisions by analysing large and complex bodies of textual information that would be difficult to process manually. Policy design and implementation increasingly require evidence drawn from diverse sources such as patents, scientific publications and public-aid records, yet these datasets often combine structured metadata with large volumes of unstructured text.",Corpus Viewer: NLP and ML-based Platform for Public Policy Making and Implementation,State Secretary for Digital Advancement (SEAD); Central-Government; Public health services.; Spain,"The use case therefore involved decision-makers and policy implementers who needed a clearer, more navigable view of the research and innovation landscape in order to support evidence-based action.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Corpus Viewer was developed to help public administrations make better-informed policy decisions by analysing large and complex bodies of textual information that would be difficult to process manually. Policy design and implementation increasingly require evidence drawn from diverse sources such as patents, scientific publications and public-aid records, yet these datasets often combine structured metadata with large volumes of unstructured text. The use case therefore involved decision-makers and policy implementers who needed a clearer, more navigable view of the research and innovation landscape in order to support evidence-based action. Its objective was to enable the analysis of this information space in a way that reveals themes, relationships and patterns relevant to public policy. The implementation used natural language processing, machine learning and machine translation techniques to process multilingual textual corpora and associated metadata. At a conceptual level, the platform combined topic modelling, graph analysis and large-scale text exploration to support the discovery of connections across documents and sources. It was developed through collaboration between research groups and companies, and is used by several public administrations, including SEAD, FECYT and SEUIDI. The impact includes improved analytical capacity for policy teams, more robust evidence for decision-making and a more scalable way to explore large knowledge domains. In practical terms, it strengthens policy design, supports implementation oversight and improves the usability of complex R&D information.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK|F04_UNSTRUCTURED_INFORMATION_COMPLEXITY|F06_HIGH_VOLUME_SCALABILITY_BOTTLENECK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-126,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00123,EU_PSTW_202609_00123,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Upplands-Bro,General public services n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,Tengai - Robot in recruitment processes,Municipality of Upplands-Bro; Local Government; General public services n.e.c.; Sweden,"Upplands-Bro Municipality began using Tengai in June 2019 to address a persistent challenge in recruitment: reducing bias in candidate selection while maintaining a structured and efficient hiring process. The use case therefore involved the municipality’s recruitment and staffing agency, hiring managers and job candidates, with the objective of making recruitment more impartial, standardised and evidence-based while preserving a final human assessment of motivation and fit.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Upplands-Bro Municipality began using Tengai in June 2019 to address a persistent challenge in recruitment: reducing bias in candidate selection while maintaining a structured and efficient hiring process. Traditional interviews can be influenced by subjective impressions, inconsistent questioning and uneven evaluation practices, which may affect fairness and the quality of shortlist decisions. The use case therefore involved the municipality’s recruitment and staffing agency, hiring managers and job candidates, with the objective of making recruitment more impartial, standardised and evidence-based while preserving a final human assessment of motivation and fit. The solution was implemented through Tengai, one of the first social interview robots, developed in collaboration between municipal staff and Furhat Robotics. At a conceptual level, the system first identifies candidates with the highest general performance scores to create a shortlist, then conducts structured interviews in which it assesses behaviour, problem-solving ability and other competencies, including the ability to ask follow-up questions where needed. These interview outputs are combined with competency scores and passed on to recruiters, who then carry out the final interview stage. The first results were regarded as successful, and the service was adopted permanently within a week of release. The impact included a more standardised interview process, reduced exposure to bias in early screening and significant reputational value through national and international media attention to the municipality’s innovative recruitment approach.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-128,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00124,EU_PSTW_202609_00124,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Örebro Municipality,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.","The operational problem is that interpreting and standardising plan provisions is labour-intensive, technically complex and slow, especially when older planning documents must be translated into a common format aligned with the standards of the National Board of Housing, Building and Planning. This allows the digitisation process to move beyond fully manual interpretation and supports a more uniform treatment of plan content.",AIDA - Interpreting detailed plan provisions,"Örebro Municipality; Local Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Sweden","The use case therefore involved planning authorities, digitisation teams and national actors working towards a shared planning-data infrastructure, with the objective of accelerating the conversion of existing plans into standardised digital form while reducing manual effort and improving consistency. The solution was implemented through a system designed to interpret detailed-plan regulations according to the relevant national standard.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Sweden’s planning system has long relied on a large stock of existing detailed plans that must be digitised before they can be integrated into a coherent national plan database. The operational problem is that interpreting and standardising plan provisions is labour-intensive, technically complex and slow, especially when older planning documents must be translated into a common format aligned with the standards of the National Board of Housing, Building and Planning. The use case therefore involved planning authorities, digitisation teams and national actors working towards a shared planning-data infrastructure, with the objective of accelerating the conversion of existing plans into standardised digital form while reducing manual effort and improving consistency. The solution was implemented through a system designed to interpret detailed-plan regulations according to the relevant national standard. At a conceptual level, the platform applies document interpretation and classification techniques to extract and structure provisions from existing plans, converting them into standardised digital representations that can feed into the wider national planning database. This allows the digitisation process to move beyond fully manual interpretation and supports a more uniform treatment of plan content. The expected impact was a 20% reduction in the time required for digitisation, which, when applied across the full stock of plans still to be processed, would save years of work. More broadly, the project promised gains in efficiency, consistency and interoperability, supporting wider Smart Built Environment goals through faster, more scalable planning-data modernisation.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-129,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00125,EU_PSTW_202609_00125,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Swedish Social Insurance Agency,Hospital services.,"This is a demanding process because medical records can be detailed, inconsistent in format and difficult for non-clinical staff to interpret without support. SKOSA was developed to address this challenge by helping administrators analyse, structure and understand medical information more effectively when conducting needs assessments.",SKOSA - Interpreting medical information,Swedish Social Insurance Agency; Central-Government; Hospital services.; Sweden,"The solution is implemented as a general platform with a digital knowledge base built in collaboration with doctors and other subject-matter experts. The expected impact is more efficient case handling, better-structured use of health information and improved consistency in needs assessments.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Administrators handling health-related casework often need to review large volumes of medical information and translate complex clinical material into structured assessments that support benefit or needs-related decisions. This is a demanding process because medical records can be detailed, inconsistent in format and difficult for non-clinical staff to interpret without support. SKOSA was developed to address this challenge by helping administrators analyse, structure and understand medical information more effectively when conducting needs assessments. The stakeholders include public administrators, medical experts and case-handling organisations responsible for decisions influenced by health information, with the objective of improving consistency, reducing interpretative burden and strengthening the quality of assessment processes. The solution is implemented as a general platform with a digital knowledge base built in collaboration with doctors and other subject-matter experts. At a conceptual level, the system combines expert-curated knowledge structures with analytical support tools that help caseworkers organise medical data, identify relevant information and interpret it within a decision-support context. Rather than replacing professional judgement, the platform is designed to make medical evidence more accessible and usable in administrative workflows. The expected impact is more efficient case handling, better-structured use of health information and improved consistency in needs assessments. More broadly, SKOSA creates value by reducing the cognitive load on administrators, supporting more reliable decisions and building a reusable infrastructure for interpreting complex medical evidence in public administration.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-131,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00126,EU_PSTW_202609_00126,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""social_nonprofit""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Uppsala, Umeå, Linköping, Malmö/Lund, Gothenburg and Stockholm Hospitals",Hospital services.,"Heart and lung diseases place a major burden on health systems, yet earlier and more individualised risk identification requires data on a scale and depth that routine care alone cannot easily provide.",SCAPIS - Prevention of heart- and lung-diseases,"Uppsala, Umeå, Linköping, Malmö/Lund, Gothenburg and Stockholm Hospitals; Central-Government; Hospital services.; Sweden","The use case brought together all six Swedish healthcare regions, university hospitals and a broad group of scientific stakeholders, with the objective of building a national evidence base to improve understanding of cardiovascular, pulmonary, epidemiological and genetic risk factors.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"SCAPIS was established to strengthen prevention and risk assessment for cardiovascular and pulmonary diseases through a large-scale population study capable of generating deeper insight into how these conditions develop and how they might be prevented or delayed. Heart and lung diseases place a major burden on health systems, yet earlier and more individualised risk identification requires data on a scale and depth that routine care alone cannot easily provide. The use case brought together all six Swedish healthcare regions, university hospitals and a broad group of scientific stakeholders, with the objective of building a national evidence base to improve understanding of cardiovascular, pulmonary, epidemiological and genetic risk factors. The implementation was centred on the Swedish CardioPulmonary bioImage Study, a population-based initiative involving 30,000 participants. At a conceptual level, the programme integrated imaging, clinical, epidemiological and genetic data in order to support the development of more individualised risk assessments and preventive strategies. The study began with a pilot in 2012 to confirm feasibility, followed by the major study launch in 2014, building on work initiated in 2007. The impact lies in creating a world-leading research resource that supports earlier prevention, more precise risk stratification and stronger scientific understanding of heart and lung disease. In broader terms, SCAPIS improves the foundation for long-term public-health planning, supports clinical innovation and strengthens the ability of the health system to move from reactive treatment towards prevention.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-132,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00127,EU_PSTW_202609_00127,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Norwegian Tax Administration,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.","The Norwegian Tax Administration introduced its chat service to address a familiar public-service challenge: large numbers of taxpayers need timely answers to general questions, particularly when they are trying to understand obligations, complete filings correctly or resolve routine issues without delay. The expected impact is faster access to guidance, reduced pressure on manual support functions and a more convenient experience for taxpayers seeking straightforward information.",Norwegian Tax Administration chat service,"Norwegian Tax Administration; Central-Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Norway","The use case therefore involves taxpayers and tax-administration support teams, with the objective of improving access to general information, simplifying communication and making it easier for users to get their taxes right. The solution is implemented as a digital chat service that handles general enquiries through an online interface.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Norwegian Tax Administration introduced its chat service to address a familiar public-service challenge: large numbers of taxpayers need timely answers to general questions, particularly when they are trying to understand obligations, complete filings correctly or resolve routine issues without delay. Relying only on traditional contact channels can create unnecessary waiting times and increase pressure on service staff, especially during peak tax periods. The use case therefore involves taxpayers and tax-administration support teams, with the objective of improving access to general information, simplifying communication and making it easier for users to get their taxes right. The solution is implemented as a digital chat service that handles general enquiries through an online interface. At a conceptual level, the service uses conversational interaction and structured service knowledge to interpret routine questions and return relevant tax information quickly, creating a more immediate support layer than static web pages alone. By focusing on general enquiries, the service can respond at scale while leaving more complex or case-specific matters to other channels. The expected impact is faster access to guidance, reduced pressure on manual support functions and a more convenient experience for taxpayers seeking straightforward information. More broadly, the chat service creates value by improving digital service delivery, supporting greater self-service in tax administration and helping the organisation allocate human support capacity more effectively.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-133,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00128,EU_PSTW_202609_00128,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Karolinska Institutet in Sweden,Hospital services.,UNKNOWN,Stockholm3 - Diagnosing prostate cancer with OncoWatch,Karolinska Institutet in Sweden; Academic-Research; Hospital services.; Sweden,"This use case focuses on improving the diagnosis of prostate cancer by strengthening the consistency, accuracy and scalability of pathology assessment across laboratories and clinical settings. The use case therefore involved pathology laboratories, clinicians, diagnostic innovators and health-system stakeholders seeking to improve diagnostic support and broaden access to reliable assessment tools.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"This use case focuses on improving the diagnosis of prostate cancer by strengthening the consistency, accuracy and scalability of pathology assessment across laboratories and clinical settings. Diagnosing prostate cancer can be complex, and variability in interpretation may affect both efficiency and quality of care. The use case therefore involved pathology laboratories, clinicians, diagnostic innovators and health-system stakeholders seeking to improve diagnostic support and broaden access to reliable assessment tools. Its objective was to accelerate the development and implementation of Stockholm3 together with OncoWatch, so that diagnostic workflows could benefit from more standardised and scalable support. The implementation involved the development and validation of OncoWatch as a diagnostic system for use in pathology contexts, supported by EIT Health through funding and network access. At a conceptual level, the solution was tested in a nine-country multicentre study to assess performance across different laboratories and a wider range of digital pathology scanners, ensuring that the system could operate effectively in heterogeneous real-world environments. This emphasis on cross-site validation supported readiness for broader deployment, with a first CE-marked product expected to launch by the end of the year referenced. The impact includes improved confidence in diagnostic consistency, stronger interoperability across lab settings and better scalability of prostate-cancer diagnostics. More broadly, the use case supports faster translation of innovation into clinical practice and creates business value through improved efficiency, standardisation and potential expansion across multiple healthcare markets.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-134,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00129,EU_PSTW_202609_00129,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Federal Office for Statistics,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.","The expected impact was faster and more accurate checks, reduced manual workload and more efficient handling of correction cycles.",Plausibility check on quality and reliability of administrative data,"Federal Office for Statistics; Central-Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Switzerland","This use case addresses the substantial effort required by statistical offices to validate the quality and reliability of administrative and survey data before it can be used confidently. Existing plausibility checks have typically relied either on manual review or on rule-based validation using thresholds and logical tests, both of which can be labour-intensive, incomplete or insufficiently precise.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"This use case addresses the substantial effort required by statistical offices to validate the quality and reliability of administrative and survey data before it can be used confidently. Existing plausibility checks have typically relied either on manual review or on rule-based validation using thresholds and logical tests, both of which can be labour-intensive, incomplete or insufficiently precise. The use case therefore involved statistical offices and data suppliers seeking to improve validation processes, reduce repeated back-and-forth corrections and strengthen the consistency of data quality assurance. Its objective was to identify questionable or incorrect records more quickly and accurately, while also improving communication with data suppliers about detected issues. The implementation used machine-learning methods trained initially on historical data, with a target variable defined through prior analysis and then predicted by the algorithm. At a conceptual level, the solution compared predicted and actual values to evaluate accuracy, used these results to support plausibility checks and incorporated a feedback mechanism to generate automatic explanations for data suppliers. This moved validation beyond fixed rules towards a more adaptive and evidence-based approach. The expected impact was faster and more accurate checks, reduced manual workload and more efficient handling of correction cycles. In operational terms, the use case improves data reliability, enhances scalability in statistical production and strengthens the overall quality of administrative information used in the public sector.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-135,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00130,EU_PSTW_202609_00130,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Federal Office for Statistics,R&D economic affairs,"The operational challenge is that coding enterprises on the basis of survey responses, commercial-register descriptions and other available information requires human interpretation, which can introduce subjectivity and make standardisation difficult. At the same time, manual coding is resource-intensive and can increase the burden associated with maintaining high-quality classifications across the register.",NOGAuto - Assign or control codes to companies for economic activities,Federal Office for Statistics; Central-Government; R&D economic affairs; Switzerland,The solution is implemented through an automated coding approach that initially relies on information already available within the Federal Statistical Office.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Federal Statistical Office depends on accurate and stable NOGA coding in the Business and Enterprise Register because these economic-activity classifications directly influence the quality of structural, economic and synthetic business statistics. The operational challenge is that coding enterprises on the basis of survey responses, commercial-register descriptions and other available information requires human interpretation, which can introduce subjectivity and make standardisation difficult. At the same time, manual coding is resource-intensive and can increase the burden associated with maintaining high-quality classifications across the register. NOGAuto was therefore developed to support staff responsible for assigning or checking economic-activity codes, with the objective of automating part of the coding process, improving consistency and reducing administrative burden on both the statistical office and enterprises. The solution is implemented through an automated coding approach that initially relies on information already available within the Federal Statistical Office. At a conceptual level, the system analyses existing textual and administrative data, identifies patterns associated with NOGA classifications and generates proposed codes or control suggestions for review. This creates a more standardised and reproducible coding workflow while still allowing staff to validate or adjust outcomes where needed. The expected impact is improved coding quality, greater consistency across enterprises and reduced manual effort in maintaining the register. More broadly, the project creates value by strengthening the statistical production chain, enhancing reliability in enterprise-based statistics and supporting more efficient and scalable classification management.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-136,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00131,EU_PSTW_202609_00131,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""social_nonprofit""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of St.Gallen,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.","City administrations often hold a broad range of service information, but users may find it difficult to locate the right detail at the right moment, whether they are looking for parking, event information or digital administrative services.",Chatbot - Offer residents and tourism information and services from the administration,"Municipality of St.Gallen; Local Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Switzerland","The use case therefore involved residents, visitors and municipal service teams, with the objective of simplifying access, improving convenience and testing public acceptance of conversational service delivery. The solution was implemented through a prototype chatbot integrated into both the city’s website and its urban app, mobileSG.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The City of St. Gallen tested this chatbot to improve how residents and tourists access municipal information and services, particularly in situations where users need quick, practical answers without navigating multiple systems. City administrations often hold a broad range of service information, but users may find it difficult to locate the right detail at the right moment, whether they are looking for parking, event information or digital administrative services. The use case therefore involved residents, visitors and municipal service teams, with the objective of simplifying access, improving convenience and testing public acceptance of conversational service delivery. The solution was implemented through a prototype chatbot integrated into both the city’s website and its urban app, mobileSG. At a conceptual level, the system combined conversational query handling with access to multiple municipal information domains, including the event calendar, parking information, mobility data, disposal guidance, eUmzugCH functionality and user feedback collection. The roadmap also included integration into the city’s own software environment and extension to services such as residence confirmation, debt-enforcement register extracts and tax-deadline extensions. Public testing and subsequent revision were built into the implementation approach. The impact included broader access to municipal and tourism information, a more user-friendly service experience and a scalable foundation for expanding digital interaction across urban administration.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-137,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00132,EU_PSTW_202609_00132,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Social Insurance Institute of Canton of Aargau,Social protection n.e.c.,"Social Insurance Aargau introduced the Maxi chatbot to deal with a high volume of routine enquiries and to reduce the operational burden created by daily incoming questions. Many requests to social-insurance institutions concern general matters that do not involve sensitive personal data, yet handling them manually consumes time that staff could otherwise spend on more complex and case-specific work.",Maxi - Chatbot answering questions on insurance,Social Insurance Institute of Canton of Aargau; Local Government; Social protection n.e.c.; Switzerland,"The use case therefore involved the social-insurance institution, its customer-service teams and citizens seeking information, with the objective of improving responsiveness, reducing repetitive workload and creating a pathway towards more automated handling of incoming communication. The solution was implemented through a chatbot capable of answering general questions that do not require access to sensitive personal information.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Social Insurance Aargau introduced the Maxi chatbot to deal with a high volume of routine enquiries and to reduce the operational burden created by daily incoming questions. Many requests to social-insurance institutions concern general matters that do not involve sensitive personal data, yet handling them manually consumes time that staff could otherwise spend on more complex and case-specific work. The use case therefore involved the social-insurance institution, its customer-service teams and citizens seeking information, with the objective of improving responsiveness, reducing repetitive workload and creating a pathway towards more automated handling of incoming communication. The solution was implemented through a chatbot capable of answering general questions that do not require access to sensitive personal information. At a conceptual level, Maxi uses conversational query handling and structured service knowledge to respond to standard requests, while the broader plan is for future emails to pass through automated, intelligent triage and further processing. In that next stage, requests containing personal data would be classified correctly and, depending on the issue, could trigger processes directly within specialist applications. The impact has already been measurable: the introduction of Maxi reduced mail enquiries by around 30%. More broadly, the use case creates value by improving service efficiency, reducing pressure on support teams and laying the groundwork for a more integrated and scalable model of automated request management in social insurance.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK|F06_HIGH_VOLUME_SCALABILITY_BOTTLENECK,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-138,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00133,EU_PSTW_202609_00133,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Social Insurance Institute of St.Gallen,Social protection n.e.c.,"Access barriers of this kind can reduce uptake of available support and increase administrative pressure when routine questions must be handled manually. While the available description is brief, the intended value lies in making social-security information easier to understand and more readily available, particularly for users who may otherwise struggle to identify the right service path.","Chatbot - Security institution information, find the ones entitled to premium reductions",Social Insurance Institute of St.Gallen; Local Government; Social protection n.e.c.; Switzerland,"The use case therefore involved the cantonal social security organisation and citizens seeking guidance, with the objective of improving access to service information and helping people identify support, including potential entitlement to premium reductions. The solution was implemented through a chatbot developed within the SVA organisation to provide answers about the institution’s services.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"In the Canton of St. Gallen, the social security institution needed a more accessible way to provide citizens with information about its services, particularly where people may be unsure about entitlements or may not know how to navigate the institution’s offerings. Access barriers of this kind can reduce uptake of available support and increase administrative pressure when routine questions must be handled manually. The use case therefore involved the cantonal social security organisation and citizens seeking guidance, with the objective of improving access to service information and helping people identify support, including potential entitlement to premium reductions. The solution was implemented through a chatbot developed within the SVA organisation to provide answers about the institution’s services. At a conceptual level, the system uses conversational interaction and structured benefit-related information to interpret user questions and return relevant guidance through a digital interface. This allows citizens to obtain first-line support more quickly and more independently than through traditional channels alone. While the available description is brief, the intended value lies in making social-security information easier to understand and more readily available, particularly for users who may otherwise struggle to identify the right service path. The expected impact includes improved accessibility of service information, greater convenience for citizens and reduced demand on manual enquiry handling. More broadly, the chatbot creates value by supporting more user-centred access to social-security services and by improving the efficiency and reach of public information delivery.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-139,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00134,EU_PSTW_202609_00134,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Swiss State Secretariat for Migration,Public order and safety n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,Refugees - Assigning asylum seekers,Swiss State Secretariat for Migration; Central-Government; Public order and safety n.e.c.; Switzerland,"This use case was designed to improve how asylum seekers are assigned to Swiss cantons by replacing purely random allocation with a method intended to increase their chances of labour-market integration. The use case therefore involved the Swiss State Secretariat for Migration, canton-level placement officials and policy researchers, with the objective of making placement decisions more effective while still respecting operational constraints such as the limited capacity available in each canton.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"This use case was designed to improve how asylum seekers are assigned to Swiss cantons by replacing purely random allocation with a method intended to increase their chances of labour-market integration. Under the traditional system, individuals were distributed without systematically considering where their profile might best fit local conditions, despite the long-term importance of employment for integration outcomes. The use case therefore involved the Swiss State Secretariat for Migration, canton-level placement officials and policy researchers, with the objective of making placement decisions more effective while still respecting operational constraints such as the limited capacity available in each canton. The implementation used an algorithm developed by the Immigration Policy Lab, together with an affiliated researcher from Dartmouth College, to recommend canton assignments based on the expected likelihood of future employment. At a conceptual level, the system matched individual profiles to cantonal conditions, subject to practical constraints, while preserving human oversight because placement officials retained the final decision in each case. The pilot included 2,000 people, with half assigned using the algorithm’s guidance and half allocated randomly, and outcomes were to be tracked over several years by comparing employment rates between the two groups. The expected impact was improved integration prospects, better use of placement capacity and a more evidence-based approach to allocation. More broadly, the use case supports stronger public-value outcomes by linking operational decisions to long-term social and economic integration results.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-140,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00135,EU_PSTW_202609_00135,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Driver and Vehicle Standards Agency (DVSA),Transport,"The Driver and Vehicle Standards Agency (DVSA) needed a more effective way to prioritise inspections across a very large MOT testing ecosystem, covering 23,000 garages and 66,000 testers, at a time when examiner capacity was limited and manual targeting could not easily keep pace with the volume and complexity of testing behaviour.","DVSA MOT - Intelligent, data-driven approach to better target Driver and Vehicle Standards Agency (DVSA) resources",Driver and Vehicle Standards Agency (DVSA); Central-Government; Transport; United Kingdom,"The use case focused on helping enforcement and compliance teams identify higher-risk testing patterns more consistently, even in the absence of labelled data, so that scarce inspection resources could be directed where they were most likely to have the greatest regulatory impact.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Driver and Vehicle Standards Agency (DVSA) needed a more effective way to prioritise inspections across a very large MOT testing ecosystem, covering 23,000 garages and 66,000 testers, at a time when examiner capacity was limited and manual targeting could not easily keep pace with the volume and complexity of testing behaviour. The use case focused on helping enforcement and compliance teams identify higher-risk testing patterns more consistently, even in the absence of labelled data, so that scarce inspection resources could be directed where they were most likely to have the greatest regulatory impact. Its objective was to improve oversight of MOT testing standards, support more efficient examiner preparation and strengthen road-safety and environmental outcomes by targeting non-compliant behaviour more accurately. The implementation used clustering techniques to group garages according to testing behaviour and combined the resulting patterns with historical disciplinary records to generate risk scores. These scores were refreshed every three months, creating a balance between stability and timeliness in inspection planning. At a conceptual level, the solution integrated behavioural analysis and historical compliance data into DVSA’s operational decision-making processes. The impact included a 50% reduction in examiner preparation time and more targeted enforcement activity. In business and public-value terms, the approach improved scalability, increased regulatory efficiency and helped uphold MOT standards, contributing to safer and more environmentally compliant vehicles on the road.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-141,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00136,EU_PSTW_202609_00136,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Durham City Police,Police Services,UNKNOWN,"HART - Assessment risk tool, predictive policing based on person data",Durham City Police; Local Government; Police Services; United Kingdom,"In a policing context where officers must often make rapid judgements about custody, diversion and case handling, the challenge was to use available information more consistently when distinguishing between low-, medium- and high-risk individuals. The use case involved Durham Constabulary and operational policing stakeholders, with the objective of improving risk assessment by supplementing professional judgement with a more systematic analysis of relevant personal and offending-history data.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Harm Assessment Risk Tool (HART) was introduced to support police decision-making by providing a structured assessment of an individual’s likelihood of future harm-related offending. In a policing context where officers must often make rapid judgements about custody, diversion and case handling, the challenge was to use available information more consistently when distinguishing between low-, medium- and high-risk individuals. The use case involved Durham Constabulary and operational policing stakeholders, with the objective of improving risk assessment by supplementing professional judgement with a more systematic analysis of relevant personal and offending-history data. It addressed the need for more consistent triage in early-stage criminal justice decisions, particularly in a period when predictive policing applications were beginning to emerge in practice. The implementation used algorithmic classification techniques applied to variables such as past offending history, age, postcode and other background characteristics in order to produce a three-level risk categorisation. At a conceptual level, the system transformed person-level data into a structured risk score that could be incorporated into policing workflows as a decision-support input. The impact was a more standardised approach to assessing risk and a clearer basis for prioritising interventions and case handling. More broadly, the use case marked one of the earliest reported deployments of predictive policing in the UK, demonstrating both the operational potential and the governance sensitivity of data-driven risk assessment in policing.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-142,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00137,EU_PSTW_202609_00137,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,London Borough of Enfield,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.","Local residents often struggle to identify which city service they need, where to find it and how to begin an interaction with the relevant department, particularly when public-service structures are complex and spread across multiple channels.",Amelia - Chatbot helping local residents find city services,"London Borough of Enfield; Local Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; United Kingdom","The solution is implemented through a conversational digital assistant that combines natural-language processing, machine-learning techniques and service-knowledge retrieval.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Local residents often struggle to identify which city service they need, where to find it and how to begin an interaction with the relevant department, particularly when public-service structures are complex and spread across multiple channels. This creates frustration for citizens and unnecessary pressure on contact centres and service teams handling routine enquiries. Amelia was introduced to address this access problem by helping residents navigate city services more easily and obtain answers more quickly. The stakeholders include local residents, municipal service teams and customer-support functions, with the objective of simplifying access to public services, improving the user experience and reducing the friction involved in finding the right information or pathway. The solution is implemented through a conversational digital assistant that combines natural-language processing, machine-learning techniques and service-knowledge retrieval. At a conceptual level, the system interprets user questions in everyday language, identifies the relevant intent and matches the query to appropriate city-service information or next steps. This allows residents to interact in a more intuitive way than through static web navigation or complex service menus. The expected impact is faster access to city services, improved satisfaction among residents and lower pressure on frontline support channels for routine issues. More broadly, Amelia creates value by supporting more responsive local-government communication, enabling scalable first-line assistance and improving how citizens connect with municipal services.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,S1_KNOWLEDGE_RETRIEVAL_RAG,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-143,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00138,EU_PSTW_202609_00138,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,National Health Service,"Medical products, appliances and equipment.","Many users struggle to interpret symptoms accurately on their own, while traditional health-information channels can be fragmented, overly generic or difficult to navigate under stress.",Babylon - Health interactive symptom checker,"National Health Service; Central-Government; Medical products, appliances and equipment.; United Kingdom","The use case therefore focused on improving access to informed, up-to-date medical guidance in a format that could respond dynamically to the user’s answers. The solution was implemented as an interactive question-and-answer system designed by doctors and scientists and supported by deep-learning methods.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Babylon’s interactive symptom checker was developed to help people obtain timely, structured health information when they were trying to understand possible symptoms and decide what to do next. Many users struggle to interpret symptoms accurately on their own, while traditional health-information channels can be fragmented, overly generic or difficult to navigate under stress. The use case therefore focused on improving access to informed, up-to-date medical guidance in a format that could respond dynamically to the user’s answers. The stakeholders included patients and members of the public seeking early health advice, with the objective of making symptom assessment more accessible, more personalised and more useful than a simple static database search. The solution was implemented as an interactive question-and-answer system designed by doctors and scientists and supported by deep-learning methods. At a conceptual level, the tool combined structured symptom questioning with analysis of known symptoms and risk factors to assess the user’s condition and return relevant medical information. Rather than functioning as a searchable repository alone, it adapted the questioning flow based on earlier responses and used this evolving input to refine the guidance provided. The expected impact was quicker access to structured health information, improved user understanding of possible conditions and a more engaging symptom-assessment experience. More broadly, Babylon created value by offering scalable digital triage support, improving convenience for users and demonstrating how interactive health tools could extend access to medical guidance outside traditional clinical settings.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,NOT_IN_USE,미사용,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-144,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00139,EU_PSTW_202609_00139,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Transport for London,"General economic, commercial and labour affair","Transport for London launched TravelBot to address a common customer-service problem in urban transport: passengers often need immediate answers about bus arrivals, service disruptions and route information, yet traditional service channels can be slower, less convenient or harder to access during travel.",TravelBot - Chatbot for travel information,"Transport for London; Local Government; General economic, commercial and labour affair; United Kingdom","The use case was designed to improve how customers obtain real-time travel information through familiar digital platforms, while also making it easier to escalate to a human agent when necessary. The solution was implemented as a social-media chatbot on Messenger, using conversational processing to interact with customers and provide travel-related information instantly.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Transport for London launched TravelBot to address a common customer-service problem in urban transport: passengers often need immediate answers about bus arrivals, service disruptions and route information, yet traditional service channels can be slower, less convenient or harder to access during travel. The use case was designed to improve how customers obtain real-time travel information through familiar digital platforms, while also making it easier to escalate to a human agent when necessary. The stakeholders included TfL customers, customer-service teams and transport-information providers, with the objective of improving convenience, reducing friction in journey planning and making service information more responsive to passenger needs. The solution was implemented as a social-media chatbot on Messenger, using conversational processing to interact with customers and provide travel-related information instantly. At a conceptual level, the system linked user queries to live transport data so that it could report when a bus was due, provide service updates and share Tube maps, while also offering a handover route to customer-service agents for more complex requests. This created a blended model combining automated assistance with human support where needed. The expected impact was faster access to travel information, easier handling of routine customer queries and a more convenient user experience. More broadly, TravelBot created value by modernising transport communication, improving service accessibility and providing a scalable digital channel for real-time passenger support.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,NOT_IN_USE,미사용,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-145,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00140,EU_PSTW_202609_00140,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,London Hackney Council,Family and children.,"Hackney Council introduced the Early Help Profiling System to support earlier intervention for children and families considered vulnerable, in a context where public services were under pressure to identify risk sooner and target limited support resources more effectively.",EHPS - Early help profiling system to identify children and families considered vulnerable,London Hackney Council; Local Government; Family and children.; United Kingdom,"The solution was implemented by combining administrative datasets such as school attendance, housing information and financial records to generate risk profiles and ranked recommendations for frontline teams.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Hackney Council introduced the Early Help Profiling System to support earlier intervention for children and families considered vulnerable, in a context where public services were under pressure to identify risk sooner and target limited support resources more effectively. The system was intended to help social workers prioritise households that might require attention by drawing on patterns across education, housing and economic records. Each month, practitioners received a shortlist of 20 families assessed as high risk, with the objective of enabling preventive action before harm escalated. The solution was implemented by combining administrative datasets such as school attendance, housing information and financial records to generate risk profiles and ranked recommendations for frontline teams. At a conceptual level, the system used predictive modelling and cross-dataset analysis to flag cases for further human review rather than making decisions automatically. It was reported to be more than 80% accurate and projected to save around €1 million annually, suggesting potential gains in targeting and efficiency. However, the initiative attracted strong criticism over secrecy, commercial control of data and its perceived relationship to austerity-era service pressures. In the end, Hackney Council discontinued the system after it failed to deliver the expected benefits, making it a notable example of the gap that can emerge between technical promise, public trust and operational value.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,NOT_IN_USE,미사용,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-146,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00141,EU_PSTW_202609_00141,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Norwegian Government Agency for Financial Management,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.","The operational challenge is especially acute where invoice volumes are high and staff must interpret coding requirements repeatedly under time pressure. The stakeholders include DFØ, its customer organisations and accounting staff responsible for invoice handling, with the objective of reducing manual effort, lowering the risk of late payments and improving the reliability of invoice processing.",Automatic posting of invoices - Robot to propose the correct posting,"Norwegian Government Agency for Financial Management; Central-Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Norway",This use case was introduced to improve efficiency and reduce error in financial administration by supporting more accurate posting decisions from the outset. The solution is implemented through a tested accounting robot that proposes the correct posting for incoming invoices.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Customers of the Norwegian Government Agency for Financial Management spend significant time deciding how incoming invoices should be posted correctly, and uncertainty in this process can lead to late payment, interest charges and unnecessary administrative cost. The operational challenge is especially acute where invoice volumes are high and staff must interpret coding requirements repeatedly under time pressure. This use case was introduced to improve efficiency and reduce error in financial administration by supporting more accurate posting decisions from the outset. The stakeholders include DFØ, its customer organisations and accounting staff responsible for invoice handling, with the objective of reducing manual effort, lowering the risk of late payments and improving the reliability of invoice processing. The solution is implemented through a tested accounting robot that proposes the correct posting for incoming invoices. At a conceptual level, the model is trained on historical accounting data and then combines those learned patterns with information from the actual invoice to generate a posting prediction. This allows the system to function as decision support within existing accounting workflows, helping staff resolve uncertainty more quickly and consistently. The expected impact is faster invoice handling, fewer posting errors and reduced risk of additional costs from interest and charges. More broadly, the use case creates value by improving operational efficiency in financial management, increasing consistency in accounting processes and supporting more scalable processing of incoming invoices across public-sector organisations.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-147,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00142,EU_PSTW_202609_00142,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Barcelona,Old age.,"The pilot involved twenty older residents already supported by the City Council’s Area for Social Rights and was launched in the context of a broader challenge focused on improving quality of life for senior citizens through technology. The operational problem was social isolation and the difficulty of maintaining regular reminders, wellbeing monitoring and day-to-day support for people who may be vulnerable but still living independently.",Misty II - Robot for elderly people,Municipality of Barcelona; Local Government; Old age.; Spain,"This use case was designed to explore whether a social robot could improve the daily lives of older people living alone by providing practical support, companionship and routine assistance in the home.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"This use case was designed to explore whether a social robot could improve the daily lives of older people living alone by providing practical support, companionship and routine assistance in the home. The pilot involved twenty older residents already supported by the City Council’s Area for Social Rights and was launched in the context of a broader challenge focused on improving quality of life for senior citizens through technology. The operational problem was social isolation and the difficulty of maintaining regular reminders, wellbeing monitoring and day-to-day support for people who may be vulnerable but still living independently. The objective was therefore to assess whether a domestic assistive solution could reduce loneliness, support adherence to medication and appointments, and contribute to safer and more confident independent living. The implementation used the Misty II social robot as an in-home interaction platform, capable of providing reminders, supporting simple monitoring functions and engaging users in daily routines. At a conceptual level, the solution combined conversational interaction, scheduling support and basic assistive features within a real-world pilot setting so that user acceptance and practical value could be evaluated directly. The expected impact included better user experience, reduced feelings of isolation and improved support for routine health and wellbeing tasks. More broadly, the pilot explored a scalable model for complementing social-care services and extending support for older people living alone.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-148,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00143,EU_PSTW_202609_00143,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"BMVI, Autobahn GmbH of the Federal Government",Transport,"ARC-D addresses the lack of automated, up-to-date condition monitoring across the federal motorway network, a gap that makes it harder for road authorities to detect deterioration early and to plan timely maintenance before defects become more severe and more expensive to repair. Its objective is to create a more systematic and scalable basis for assessing the condition of individual motorway sections, replacing a fragmented approach with one that supports earlier action and better long-term maintenance planning.",ARC-D - Automatic Road Condition Detection,"BMVI, Autobahn GmbH of the Federal Government; Central-Government; Transport; Germany","The use case involves transport infrastructure authorities and maintenance planners who need consistent, network-wide visibility of road quality in order to prioritise interventions and manage public assets more effectively. The implementation uses image data previously classified by experts to train a system capable of partially automating the evaluation of road video footage and classifying stretches of road according to their condition.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"ARC-D addresses the lack of automated, up-to-date condition monitoring across the federal motorway network, a gap that makes it harder for road authorities to detect deterioration early and to plan timely maintenance before defects become more severe and more expensive to repair. The use case involves transport infrastructure authorities and maintenance planners who need consistent, network-wide visibility of road quality in order to prioritise interventions and manage public assets more effectively. Its objective is to create a more systematic and scalable basis for assessing the condition of individual motorway sections, replacing a fragmented approach with one that supports earlier action and better long-term maintenance planning. The implementation uses image data previously classified by experts to train a system capable of partially automating the evaluation of road video footage and classifying stretches of road according to their condition. At a conceptual level, the solution combines supervised classification methods with image-based inspection workflows, with the longer-term intention of integrating additional sensor data to broaden and strengthen detection capability. The expected impact is earlier identification of road degradation, more efficient maintenance targeting and reduced risk of cost-intensive long-term damage. In operational terms, the system improves scalability, enhances the timeliness of infrastructure monitoring and supports more reliable, data-informed management of motorway assets.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-149,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00144,EU_PSTW_202609_00144,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,ITZBund,General public services n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,C-19 - Interdepartmental chatbot on the topic of Corona.,ITZBund; Central-Government; General public services n.e.c.; Germany,"The solution was implemented as an interdepartmental chatbot built from existing FAQ content, which was entered as modular dialogue components to generate automated responses. Although no longer in use, the case remains relevant as an example of rapid chatbot deployment for crisis communication under exceptional service pressure.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"During the early stages of the COVID-19 pandemic, German public services experienced a dramatic increase in citizen enquiries, with volumes reported to rise by up to ten times. This created an urgent operational need for a scalable way to answer common questions quickly and consistently without overwhelming public-service staff. The C-19 chatbot was introduced to address this surge by providing citizens with immediate access to information on coronavirus-related issues and directing them to further official sources where appropriate. The stakeholders included public administrations, service teams and the general public, with the objective of reducing pressure on frontline channels while maintaining accessible communication during a fast-moving crisis. The solution was implemented as an interdepartmental chatbot built from existing FAQ content, which was entered as modular dialogue components to generate automated responses. At a conceptual level, the system combined pre-formulated answer blocks with speech-recognition and dialogue-processing components so that citizens could ask questions in natural language and receive standardised replies. The chatbot also pointed users towards further sources of information, and future ambitions reportedly included support for application creation and status clarification. The impact was improved scalability in handling routine pandemic-related enquiries and faster dissemination of official information at a time of high uncertainty. Although no longer in use, the case remains relevant as an example of rapid chatbot deployment for crisis communication under exceptional service pressure.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,NOT_IN_USE,미사용,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-150,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00145,EU_PSTW_202609_00145,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Federal Employment Agency,Family and children.,"Family benefits offices in Germany face a recurring administrative burden because, to maintain eligibility for child benefit, a student’s current certificate of enrolment must be submitted twice a year. The problem is compounded by the lack of a uniform certificate format across German universities, which means that large volumes of heterogeneous documents must be checked manually.",Study certificates - Automatic recognition for applying for child benefit,Federal Employment Agency; Central-Government; Family and children.; Germany,"This use case was introduced to reduce processing effort, improve consistency and accelerate validation while retaining staff oversight. The solution is implemented through a workflow in which the applicant uploads the certificate via a web portal, the text is extracted using optical character recognition, and the resulting content is classified automatically.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Family benefits offices in Germany face a recurring administrative burden because, to maintain eligibility for child benefit, a student’s current certificate of enrolment must be submitted twice a year. The problem is compounded by the lack of a uniform certificate format across German universities, which means that large volumes of heterogeneous documents must be checked manually. This use case was introduced to reduce processing effort, improve consistency and accelerate validation while retaining staff oversight. The stakeholders include applicants, family benefits offices and case-handling staff, with the objective of making the verification of study certificates faster, more reliable and less labour-intensive. The solution is implemented through a workflow in which the applicant uploads the certificate via a web portal, the text is extracted using optical character recognition, and the resulting content is classified automatically. At a conceptual level, the system checks four points: whether the document is a study certificate, whether it relates to the correct child, whether the semester or validity period is correct, and whether the issuing institution is a recognised German university. The result is displayed within the specialist procedure and must be actively accepted by an employee. The training base currently includes 60,000 semi-automatically annotated certificates, anonymised with a dedicated algorithm, and the classification combines Naive Bayes, Support Vector Machine, Logistic Regression, natural language processing and named-entity recognition. The impact is improved efficiency, reduced manual workload and more scalable certificate processing.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK|F06_HIGH_VOLUME_SCALABILITY_BOTTLENECK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-151,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00146,EU_PSTW_202609_00146,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,German Federal Statistical Office,General services.,"Previously, this task had been performed manually, which consumed significant staff time, was vulnerable to inconsistency and error, and placed pressure on already limited personnel resources.",Trade statistics - Classification system that automatically new data points as relevant,German Federal Statistical Office; Central-Government; General services.; Germany,"This use case was developed to improve the production of official trade statistics by automating the initial classification of incoming units, such as craft enterprises, as relevant or not relevant for inclusion in the statistical process. The use case therefore involved statistical case handlers and official statistics teams seeking to maintain processing quality while increasing efficiency in the treatment of new data points.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"This use case was developed to improve the production of official trade statistics by automating the initial classification of incoming units, such as craft enterprises, as relevant or not relevant for inclusion in the statistical process. Previously, this task had been performed manually, which consumed significant staff time, was vulnerable to inconsistency and error, and placed pressure on already limited personnel resources. The use case therefore involved statistical case handlers and official statistics teams seeking to maintain processing quality while increasing efficiency in the treatment of new data points. Its objective was to pre-sort cases more effectively so that staff could focus their effort within the same operational time window but on a more intelligently ordered set of records. The implementation used previously identified classification patterns for units, drawing on information from business registers, to train a classification system that could assess the likely relevance of new cases. At a conceptual level, the solution applied supervised learning to historical administrative patterns and fed the resulting predictions into the case-handling workflow as a prioritisation layer. The impact was a more efficient and reliable screening process, reduced manual burden and better use of scarce staffing capacity. In practical terms, the approach improved throughput, supported more consistent statistical production and created a scalable mechanism for handling growing volumes of administrative data.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-152,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00147,EU_PSTW_202609_00147,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Federal Ministry of Transport and Digital Infrastructure (BMVI),Housing development.,"The problem was especially significant for people who need barrier-free navigation, including blind people and wheelchair users, because standard plans often lack the information required for independent movement through complex indoor environments.",Indoor Robot - Making a 3D model of a new building,Federal Ministry of Transport and Digital Infrastructure (BMVI); Central-Government; Housing development.; Germany,"This use case was created to improve accessibility and navigation in public buildings by generating more detailed and practical spatial information than conventional building plans typically provide. The use case therefore involved public-building managers, accessibility stakeholders and end users requiring reliable orientation information, with the objective of producing accurate indoor models that could support inclusive navigation and better user autonomy.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"This use case was created to improve accessibility and navigation in public buildings by generating more detailed and practical spatial information than conventional building plans typically provide. The problem was especially significant for people who need barrier-free navigation, including blind people and wheelchair users, because standard plans often lack the information required for independent movement through complex indoor environments. The use case therefore involved public-building managers, accessibility stakeholders and end users requiring reliable orientation information, with the objective of producing accurate indoor models that could support inclusive navigation and better user autonomy. The implementation used a robot equipped with a laser scanner and object camera to measure the entire building and capture detailed spatial data. At a conceptual level, the solution transformed individual measurement points into a 3D vector model of the building through automated modelling techniques, creating a digital representation that could then support orientation maps and display on mobile devices. This provided a richer and more usable description of the indoor environment than static architectural drawings alone. The expected impact was improved accessibility, better navigation support and a stronger basis for inclusive public-space design. More broadly, the use case offers a scalable way to capture building information and translate it into practical guidance tools that enhance independence and user experience for people with disabilities.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-153,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00148,EU_PSTW_202609_00148,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Hamburg Office for IT and Digitalisation,Family and children.,"The operational problem is that these procedures often involve repeated document requests from different institutions and limited electronic data exchange, creating unnecessary bureaucracy at a time when families are already under pressure. The stakeholders include parents, registry services, benefits administrators and hospital staff, with the objective of making the process faster, more accessible and less burdensome.",Child benefit - Voice assistant for parents support,Hamburg Office for IT and Digitalisation; Local Government; Family and children.; Germany,"This use case was designed to simplify that process by allowing parents to initiate the necessary procedures directly at the maternity hospital, thereby reducing duplication and speeding up access to key documents and benefits. The solution is implemented through a combined application process supported by a voice-assistance system available on stationary hospital terminals, tablets or smartphones.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"In the days immediately following childbirth, parents are legally required to complete a range of formal administrative steps, including providing registration information, determining the child’s name and establishing the first official conditions for receiving parental and child benefits. The operational problem is that these procedures often involve repeated document requests from different institutions and limited electronic data exchange, creating unnecessary bureaucracy at a time when families are already under pressure. This use case was designed to simplify that process by allowing parents to initiate the necessary procedures directly at the maternity hospital, thereby reducing duplication and speeding up access to key documents and benefits. The stakeholders include parents, registry services, benefits administrators and hospital staff, with the objective of making the process faster, more accessible and less burdensome. The solution is implemented through a combined application process supported by a voice-assistance system available on stationary hospital terminals, tablets or smartphones. At a conceptual level, the system guides parents verbally through the application steps and supports a more integrated administrative workflow that links registration, birth certification, tax identification, residents’ registry entry and child-benefit notification. As a result, parents receive the child’s birth certificate, tax number, registration confirmation and child-benefit notice more quickly by post, while benefits can be transferred directly to their account. The impact includes reduced bureaucracy, faster processing and a more user-friendly start to family-related administrative services.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-154,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00149,EU_PSTW_202609_00149,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Cologne public prosecutor's office,Police Services,"Public prosecutors and investigators face the operational challenge of reviewing very large volumes of seized digital material in cases involving the distribution, acquisition or possession of child and youth sexual abuse imagery. Its objective was to reduce the burden of manual screening, improve consistency in the handling of digital evidence and support more efficient investigative and prosecutorial workflows while preserving human judgement in the final assessment.",AI against pornography - Analysis of suspicious material,Cologne public prosecutor's office; Local Government; Police Services; Germany,The use case was therefore designed to support case workers by accelerating the initial sorting of suspicious images so that the most potentially relevant material could be prioritised for detailed review.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Public prosecutors and investigators face the operational challenge of reviewing very large volumes of seized digital material in cases involving the distribution, acquisition or possession of child and youth sexual abuse imagery. Much of this material may be entirely irrelevant to the offence under investigation, yet it must still be assessed carefully to identify evidential content. The use case was therefore designed to support case workers by accelerating the initial sorting of suspicious images so that the most potentially relevant material could be prioritised for detailed review. Its objective was to reduce the burden of manual screening, improve consistency in the handling of digital evidence and support more efficient investigative and prosecutorial workflows while preserving human judgement in the final assessment. The implementation used automated image-recognition techniques to analyse visual material and identify items most likely to depict illegal content involving children or adolescents. At a conceptual level, the system functioned as a triage mechanism, separating likely irrelevant material from images requiring closer investigation and forwarding flagged content to case handlers and, where appropriate, to the public prosecutor for decision. The impact was faster evidence screening, reduced manual effort and improved focus of investigative resources on the most relevant material. In operational terms, the approach enhanced scalability, supported reliability in evidence handling and improved the efficiency of highly sensitive prosecutorial work.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK|F06_HIGH_VOLUME_SCALABILITY_BOTTLENECK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-155,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00150,EU_PSTW_202609_00150,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Employer's Liability Insurance Association for Energy, Textiles, Electrical and Media Products (BG ETEM)","General economic, commercial and labour affair","Previously, suspected cases were forwarded through a standardised document flow to a central office, where they were manually pre-checked, creating a slow and resource-intensive process across large case volumes. The impact is faster pre-assessment, better-informed case selection and reduced manual effort, with broader value in improving consistency, focusing resources on higher-potential cases and making recourse management more scalable.",Notification procedure - Automatic control of accident occurring at the workplace,"Employer's Liability Insurance Association for Energy, Textiles, Electrical and Media Products (BG ETEM); Private sector; General economic, commercial and labour affair; Germany","This use case was developed to improve prioritisation and support case handlers with more structured evidence before they decide whether to pursue recourse. The solution is implemented using a model trained on around one million completed recourse and subrogation proceedings, both successful and unsuccessful.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"When an accident at work, or on the journey to or from work, is caused by a third party, the Employer’s Liability Insurance Association must assess whether recourse proceedings against that third party are likely to succeed. Previously, suspected cases were forwarded through a standardised document flow to a central office, where they were manually pre-checked, creating a slow and resource-intensive process across large case volumes. This use case was developed to improve prioritisation and support case handlers with more structured evidence before they decide whether to pursue recourse. The stakeholders include the insurance association, recourse departments and case officers, with the objective of increasing efficiency, improving the quality of triage and strengthening decisions on whether legal recovery is worthwhile. The solution is implemented using a model trained on around one million completed recourse and subrogation proceedings, both successful and unsuccessful. At a conceptual level, the system estimates the plausibility of a potential recourse action, forecasts the likely probability of success and predicts the level of total costs. In parallel, text-mining methods identify critical keywords, such as whether the police were called or whether an assault occurred, to provide additional decision support. These outputs are delivered through a web interface for the recourse department. The impact is faster pre-assessment, better-informed case selection and reduced manual effort, with broader value in improving consistency, focusing resources on higher-potential cases and making recourse management more scalable.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION|S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-156,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00151,EU_PSTW_202609_00151,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,ETAPAS project: Fondazione Don Gnocchi (Italy),"Medical products, appliances and equipment.","The context is one in which patients recovering from injury or managing long-term functional limitations often require frequent support with exercises, daily activities and safety monitoring, while clinical staff must balance intensive care needs with limited time and resources.",Robot-mediated rehabilitation (IIT-FDG),"ETAPAS project: Fondazione Don Gnocchi (Italy); Non-governmental; Medical products, appliances and equipment.; Italy","This use case addresses the need to extend rehabilitation support beyond conventional clinical sessions by introducing an assistive robot that can complement therapists’ work in hospitals, rehabilitation gyms and patients’ homes. The implementation uses the humanoid robot R1, developed as part of the ETAPAS H2020 project, combining advanced mechanics, sensors and intelligent control capabilities to operate as a low-cost assistive-rehabilitation platform.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"This use case addresses the need to extend rehabilitation support beyond conventional clinical sessions by introducing an assistive robot that can complement therapists’ work in hospitals, rehabilitation gyms and patients’ homes. The context is one in which patients recovering from injury or managing long-term functional limitations often require frequent support with exercises, daily activities and safety monitoring, while clinical staff must balance intensive care needs with limited time and resources. The stakeholders include patients, rehabilitation professionals, ward staff and carers, with the objective of improving continuity of support, reinforcing therapy routines and assisting with everyday tasks that help preserve autonomy and safety. The implementation uses the humanoid robot R1, developed as part of the ETAPAS H2020 project, combining advanced mechanics, sensors and intelligent control capabilities to operate as a low-cost assistive-rehabilitation platform. At a conceptual level, the system supports traditional rehabilitation treatments, assists staff in clinical environments and provides in-home functions such as fetching or locating objects, reminding patients about appointments or medication, and detecting falls to support emergency management. The expected impact includes improved continuity of care, better patient experience and stronger support for independent living. More broadly, the solution offers scalability through lower-cost deployment, reduces pressure on clinical staff and strengthens rehabilitation outcomes by extending assistance into everyday environments.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-157,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00152,EU_PSTW_202609_00152,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,ETAPAS project: SINTEF-Prokom,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.","In many councils, residents need clear answers on procedures, rights and local services, yet frontline teams often face repetitive requests that absorb time and slow response delivery.",Kari - Chatbot for local governments,"ETAPAS project: SINTEF-Prokom; Non-governmental; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Sweden","The use case therefore involves municipal administrations and citizens, with the objective of improving access to information, reducing pressure on service staff and strengthening the quality of digital interaction with local government. The solution is implemented through a virtual-agent platform integrated into municipal service channels and supported by a knowledge base containing more than 6,000 intents, covering a very broad range of local-government questions.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Kari was created to help local municipalities manage large volumes of citizen enquiries about public services in a faster and more consistent way. In many councils, residents need clear answers on procedures, rights and local services, yet frontline teams often face repetitive requests that absorb time and slow response delivery. The use case therefore involves municipal administrations and citizens, with the objective of improving access to information, reducing pressure on service staff and strengthening the quality of digital interaction with local government. The solution is implemented through a virtual-agent platform integrated into municipal service channels and supported by a knowledge base containing more than 6,000 intents, covering a very broad range of local-government questions. At a conceptual level, the system uses conversational language-processing and intent-recognition techniques to interpret citizen requests, match them to the relevant service information and return standardised answers that can be maintained centrally and reused across municipalities. Kari is currently available in 80 Norwegian municipalities, covering about 30% of the Norwegian population, and is being introduced in Sweden, Finland and Denmark. The result is improved user experience, faster access to information and greater scalability in handling routine enquiries, while municipalities benefit from more efficient service delivery, better consistency of responses and reduced administrative workload.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-158,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00153,EU_PSTW_202609_00153,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,ETAPAS project: Municipality of Katerini,General public services n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,CERTH-MUKA - Public Organizations Multi-factor Misinformation Handling,ETAPAS project: Municipality of Katerini; Consortium; General public services n.e.c.; Greece,"In the municipality of Katerini, the challenge addressed by this use case is the growing need to identify misleading information quickly and to prioritise emerging issues before they create operational, reputational or social harm. The use case therefore involves municipal decision-makers, communication teams and public-sector organisations seeking to manage information risks more effectively, with the objective of detecting problematic content early and handling it in a way that is operationally useful and socially responsible.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"In the municipality of Katerini, the challenge addressed by this use case is the growing need to identify misleading information quickly and to prioritise emerging issues before they create operational, reputational or social harm. Public authorities must respond in an environment where inaccurate online content can spread rapidly, undermine trust and complicate communication with citizens. The use case therefore involves municipal decision-makers, communication teams and public-sector organisations seeking to manage information risks more effectively, with the objective of detecting problematic content early and handling it in a way that is operationally useful and socially responsible. The solution combines misinformation-detection and issue-prioritisation capabilities with the ETAPAS framework, which is used to assess deployment risks and define mitigation actions. At a conceptual level, the implementation analyses content and signals associated with potentially false or harmful narratives, prioritises relevant issues for attention and embeds an ethical and social impact assessment around the operational system. This allows the municipality to evaluate not only technical performance but also governance risks, unintended consequences and required safeguards. The expected impact is improved responsiveness to misinformation, better prioritisation of public communication efforts and stronger institutional trust. In business and public-value terms, the approach enhances reliability, reduces the risk of harmful side effects and supports scalable adoption through structured risk management.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-159,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00154,EU_PSTW_202609_00154,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Hungarian government,Health n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,Home Quarantine App - Supporting the use of electronic control for home quarantine,Hungarian government; Central-Government; Health n.e.c.; Hungary,"In early May 2020, a government decree enabled the use of electronic control to support home quarantine, responding to the practical challenge of monitoring compliance during the COVID-19 pandemic without relying solely on manual enforcement. The solution was implemented through a mobile application developed by Asura Technologies, a Hungarian company with experience in facial-recognition and GPS-location technologies.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"In early May 2020, a government decree enabled the use of electronic control to support home quarantine, responding to the practical challenge of monitoring compliance during the COVID-19 pandemic without relying solely on manual enforcement. The app was introduced in a context where authorities needed a scalable mechanism to check whether quarantined individuals remained at home, while also attempting to encourage uptake by linking participation to lower fines for breaches of quarantine rules. The stakeholders included quarantined citizens, state authorities and the developer company Asura Technologies, with the objective of strengthening compliance monitoring and supporting pandemic-control measures. The solution was implemented through a mobile application developed by Asura Technologies, a Hungarian company with experience in facial-recognition and GPS-location technologies. At a conceptual level, the system linked identity verification with geolocation checks, while personal data formally remained with the state and the company’s role was described as establishing the connection between the relevant data points rather than accessing the data itself. Although use of the app was voluntary, the incentive structure created practical pressure to adopt it. The impact lay in providing a digital alternative to more labour-intensive monitoring, but the case also raised broader issues around proportionality, governance and the role of private operators in public enforcement technology. The app is no longer in use, but it remains significant as a pandemic-era example of digitally supported quarantine control.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,NOT_IN_USE,미사용,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-160,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00155,EU_PSTW_202609_00155,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Agencia Tributaria,Economic affairs n.e.c,"While this simplifies compliance for taxpayers, it creates an analytical challenge for the tax administration because exact income figures are not directly available for this group, yet they are still needed for the calculation of ratios, statistics and broader economic analysis.",Estimation of income of self-employed and SMES who pay taxes by module,Agencia Tributaria; Central-Government; Economic affairs n.e.c; Spain,"This use case was designed to estimate the likely income of those taxpayers more accurately so that official statistical and analytical work can be based on more robust information. The solution is implemented through predictive estimation based on a wider comparison population of taxpayers with similar characteristics who do declare exact income, as well as taxpayers who switch between the modular system and direct declaration from one period to the next.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"In Spain, certain self-employed workers and small and medium-sized enterprises may opt to pay taxes under a modular system rather than declaring their exact income. While this simplifies compliance for taxpayers, it creates an analytical challenge for the tax administration because exact income figures are not directly available for this group, yet they are still needed for the calculation of ratios, statistics and broader economic analysis. This use case was designed to estimate the likely income of those taxpayers more accurately so that official statistical and analytical work can be based on more robust information. The stakeholders include the tax administration, statistical and analytical teams, and indirectly the wider policy community that relies on income-based indicators, with the objective of improving the quality of tax-based estimates without changing the underlying tax regime. The solution is implemented through predictive estimation based on a wider comparison population of taxpayers with similar characteristics who do declare exact income, as well as taxpayers who switch between the modular system and direct declaration from one period to the next. At a conceptual level, the system uses these comparable datasets to infer likely income levels for the target population and feeds the resulting estimates into the calculation of income-related indicators. The impact is improved statistical reliability, stronger analytical precision and better consistency in fiscal reporting, creating value through more accurate measurement and more useful evidence for policy and administration.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-161,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00156,EU_PSTW_202609_00156,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Hungarian police,Police Services,"Manual processing of speeding and related road-traffic offences can be time-consuming and vulnerable to bottlenecks, particularly when evidence must be matched against vehicle registries and then transferred into case-management systems.",Véda-KAFIR-ROBOTZSARU (‘Robocop’) - System Operated by the Police for Traffic Safety Automated Processing,Hungarian police; Central-Government; Police Services; Hungary,"This use case therefore aims to streamline enforcement by linking roadside capture, data verification and administrative processing in a single workflow. The solution is implemented through the Véda intelligent camera network, which feeds data into KAFIR, the Traffic Safety Automated Processing and Information System, and then into the police’s broader Robotzsaru records and case-management platform.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Hungary’s road-safety enforcement authorities needed a faster and more integrated way to process large volumes of traffic violations with minimal delay and consistent evidential handling. Manual processing of speeding and related road-traffic offences can be time-consuming and vulnerable to bottlenecks, particularly when evidence must be matched against vehicle registries and then transferred into case-management systems. This use case therefore aims to streamline enforcement by linking roadside capture, data verification and administrative processing in a single workflow. The stakeholders include the police, responsible enforcement authorities and road users, with the objective of improving traffic-safety enforcement, accelerating case handling and reducing administrative friction in the imposition of fines. The solution is implemented through the Véda intelligent camera network, which feeds data into KAFIR, the Traffic Safety Automated Processing and Information System, and then into the police’s broader Robotzsaru records and case-management platform. At a conceptual level, the cameras capture the vehicle registration number, speed, location coordinates, date and time, after which KAFIR cross-checks this information against the traffic registry, for example to determine whether a vehicle is stolen, and uploads the data into Robotzsaru. The files are then classified and sent to the competent authority without human intervention at that stage, followed by an ex post administrative check. The impact is highly automated enforcement, faster transmission and classification of cases, and more consistent processing of no-fault traffic infringements.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-162,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00157,EU_PSTW_202609_00157,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Hungarian State Treasury,Family and children.,"Fragmented data access can slow decision-making, increase duplication and make it more difficult for public bodies to manage entitlements consistently. At a conceptual level, TÉBA functions as an integration layer that enables the Treasury to consolidate the information needed for benefit assessment and payment within a more unified workflow, reducing fragmentation in case handling.",Decision on the Maternity Benefit (TÉBA) - The Family Support Scheme of the Hungarian State Treasury,Hungarian State Treasury; Central-Government; Family and children.; Hungary,This use case was therefore designed to improve the management of maternity-related and other family support benefits by integrating or guaranteeing access to the relevant information sources required for decision-making. The solution is implemented through a system architecture that connects or provides access to multiple public databases involved in family and disability allowance administration.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Hungarian State Treasury introduced the TÉBA system to address a structural administrative problem in family support: benefits such as family and disability allowances were managed across different databases and processes, making it harder to deliver a unified, efficient and citizen-centred service. Fragmented data access can slow decision-making, increase duplication and make it more difficult for public bodies to manage entitlements consistently. This use case was therefore designed to improve the management of maternity-related and other family support benefits by integrating or guaranteeing access to the relevant information sources required for decision-making. The stakeholders include the State Treasury, benefit administrators and citizens applying for family-related support, with the objective of simplifying administration, improving coordination across databases and making benefits management more coherent and efficient. The solution is implemented through a system architecture that connects or provides access to multiple public databases involved in family and disability allowance administration. At a conceptual level, TÉBA functions as an integration layer that enables the Treasury to consolidate the information needed for benefit assessment and payment within a more unified workflow, reducing fragmentation in case handling. Although the source provides limited quantitative detail, the expected impact lies in faster processing, improved consistency and lower administrative overhead. More broadly, the system creates value by modernising benefits administration, supporting better data availability and enabling a more integrated public-service model for family support.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F07_DATA_VISIBILITY_INTEGRATION_GAP,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-163,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00158,EU_PSTW_202609_00158,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Norwegian Labour and Welfare Administration (NAV),Unemployment.,UNKNOWN,Predicting sick leave duration,Norwegian Labour and Welfare Administration (NAV); Central-Government; Unemployment.; Norway,The use case therefore focuses on improving prioritisation in sickness-absence management by estimating how long the current period of sick leave is likely to continue. The solution is implemented through a random-forest model that predicts the remaining duration of an ongoing sick-leave episode.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Norwegian Labour and Welfare Service needed a more structured way to support case workers dealing with people on sick leave, particularly when deciding which cases require closer follow-up and which may need less attention. Without predictive support, such decisions depend heavily on individual judgement and can make it harder to allocate case-management effort efficiently across large caseloads. The use case therefore focuses on improving prioritisation in sickness-absence management by estimating how long the current period of sick leave is likely to continue. The stakeholders include case workers, people on sick leave and the wider set of actors involved in return-to-work dialogue, with the objective of improving the quality of follow-up decisions and making case management more targeted. The solution is implemented through a random-forest model that predicts the remaining duration of an ongoing sick-leave episode. At a conceptual level, the prediction is integrated into the information available to the case worker when deciding whether to call a dialogue meeting between the relevant parties. To strengthen interpretability, the outputs are explained using Shapley values, allowing case workers to understand which factors contributed most to the prediction. The impact is better-targeted follow-up, improved allocation of staff attention and more consistent support for decision-making. More broadly, the model creates value by enhancing explainability in predictive public services, supporting more efficient welfare administration and helping case workers focus on the cases where intervention may matter most.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-164,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00159,EU_PSTW_202609_00159,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Vimercate Local Health Authority,Health n.e.c.,"The expected impact includes reduced administrative burden on doctors, more timely and informed clinical predictions, better bed and care-capacity planning and improved service continuity.",Intelligent mechanisms for clinical evaluation - Facilitate the reconciliation of citizens,Vimercate Local Health Authority; Local Government; Health n.e.c.; Italy,"The objective of this use case was therefore to improve clinical evaluation and operational planning at the same time, enabling the facility to maintain continuity of care while optimising resources and reducing costs. The solution is implemented through machine-learning mechanisms that support doctors by analysing larger volumes of clinical information and generating suggestions more quickly than manual review alone.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Vimercate Local Health Authority sought to reconcile two competing pressures in hospital care: offering citizens the best possible treatment while reducing the time clinicians spend on administrative and forecasting tasks that take them away from patients. In departments such as diabetology, nephrology and internal medicine, doctors must assess patients daily in order to estimate likely discharge times, the risk of complications and the possibility of readmission, including urgent readmission, both for those currently in treatment and for those in protected discharge. The objective of this use case was therefore to improve clinical evaluation and operational planning at the same time, enabling the facility to maintain continuity of care while optimising resources and reducing costs. The solution is implemented through machine-learning mechanisms that support doctors by analysing larger volumes of clinical information and generating suggestions more quickly than manual review alone. At a conceptual level, the system processes patient conditions and historical treatment patterns to assist with predictions regarding discharge timing, complication risk and readmission likelihood, integrating these outputs into daily clinical assessment workflows. This does not replace medical judgement but augments it with faster, broader analytical support. The expected impact includes reduced administrative burden on doctors, more timely and informed clinical predictions, better bed and care-capacity planning and improved service continuity. More broadly, the initiative creates value through better operational efficiency, stronger patient management and a more sustainable balance between care quality and hospital resource use.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION|S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-165,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00160,EU_PSTW_202609_00160,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"SPMS - Shared Services of the Ministry of Health, EPE",Health n.e.c.,The operational challenge is that choosing the right algorithm is critical to both patient safety and service efficiency: the selected pathway must avoid missing urgent situations while also discriminating effectively between cases.,Scout.AI - Analyse the information recorded within the SNS 24 TAE Service,"SPMS - Shared Services of the Ministry of Health, EPE; Central-Government; Health n.e.c.; Portugal","The use case therefore involves nurses, the SNS 24 Contact Centre and the Directorate-General of Health, with the objective of improving algorithm selection in real time and strengthening the design of the clinical algorithms themselves. The solution is implemented through decision-support models trained on anonymised SNS 24 contact data from 2017 and 2018, representing around 2 million cases.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The SNS 24 Triage, Counselling and Referral Service handles a very large volume of calls across Portugal and plays an important role in ensuring equitable access to healthcare advice. In 2018 alone, more than 1 million calls were answered, each lasting on average seven to eight minutes, and nurses relied on one of 59 predefined clinical algorithms to triage callers and decide on the most appropriate referral. The operational challenge is that choosing the right algorithm is critical to both patient safety and service efficiency: the selected pathway must avoid missing urgent situations while also discriminating effectively between cases. The use case therefore involves nurses, the SNS 24 Contact Centre and the Directorate-General of Health, with the objective of improving algorithm selection in real time and strengthening the design of the clinical algorithms themselves. The solution is implemented through decision-support models trained on anonymised SNS 24 contact data from 2017 and 2018, representing around 2 million cases. At a conceptual level, the system uses classification methods to recommend the most appropriate clinical algorithms for a given symptom profile, adjusted for age and sex, and is then integrated into the TAE workflow as real-time support for nurses. The expected impact is safer and more consistent triage, reduced uncertainty in algorithm choice and better-informed optimisation of referral logic, creating value through improved clinical decision support and more effective telephone-based healthcare access.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-166,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00161,EU_PSTW_202609_00161,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Federal Statistical Office Germany,General public services n.e.c.,"At the Federal Statistical Office of Germany, editing and imputation are essential steps in data processing, yet manual treatment becomes increasingly difficult as digitisation produces larger and more complex datasets. This creates a more adaptive alternative to purely manual intervention and helps incorporate automation into established statistical processes.",Machine Learning in Editing and Imputation - Automated editing and correcting of data,Federal Statistical Office Germany; Central-Government; General public services n.e.c.; Germany,"The use case therefore involves statistical production teams and data-quality specialists, with the objective of reducing routine manual work while preserving the quality and timeliness of published results. The implementation uses observed values, plausibility rules and machine-learning techniques to support automated editing and imputation within the statistical workflow.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"A key operational issue in official statistics is the need to correct missing, implausible or inconsistent observations quickly enough to keep production cycles on schedule without overloading staff. At the Federal Statistical Office of Germany, editing and imputation are essential steps in data processing, yet manual treatment becomes increasingly difficult as digitisation produces larger and more complex datasets. The use case therefore involves statistical production teams and data-quality specialists, with the objective of reducing routine manual work while preserving the quality and timeliness of published results. The implementation uses observed values, plausibility rules and machine-learning techniques to support automated editing and imputation within the statistical workflow. Conceptually, the system analyses historical and current data patterns, applies prediction methods to infer likely corrections or substitute values, and combines these outputs with existing validation logic so that suspect observations can be treated more consistently and at scale. This creates a more adaptive alternative to purely manual intervention and helps incorporate automation into established statistical processes. The impact is expected to include lower administrative effort, quicker processing of large datasets and improved timeliness of statistical outputs. More broadly, the use case increases scalability, strengthens consistency in data treatment and delivers business value by enabling statistical offices to manage growing data volumes more efficiently without proportionate increases in staffing.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-167,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00162,EU_PSTW_202609_00162,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Federal Statistical Office Germany,General public services n.e.c.,"This coding work is repetitive, labour-intensive and increasingly difficult to sustain as digitisation expands the volume of incoming textual material.",Machine Learning in Text Classification Projects - Relieving staff from coding tasks,Federal Statistical Office Germany; Central-Government; General public services n.e.c.; Germany,"The use case at the Federal Statistical Office of Germany addresses this bottleneck by supporting teams responsible for statistical coding and production, with the objective of reducing manual effort, improving turnaround times and enabling staff to focus on the most complex cases rather than routine assignments. The solution is implemented through text-classification models trained on previously coded examples and embedded within statistical production workflows.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Across many official statistics processes, large amounts of text information must be coded into predefined categories before the data can be analysed and published. This coding work is repetitive, labour-intensive and increasingly difficult to sustain as digitisation expands the volume of incoming textual material. The use case at the Federal Statistical Office of Germany addresses this bottleneck by supporting teams responsible for statistical coding and production, with the objective of reducing manual effort, improving turnaround times and enabling staff to focus on the most complex cases rather than routine assignments. The solution is implemented through text-classification models trained on previously coded examples and embedded within statistical production workflows. At a conceptual level, the system uses machine-learning techniques to detect patterns in textual responses, assign likely categories and pre-sort or directly code new records against the required classification schemes. Human review can then be concentrated on ambiguous or higher-risk cases, allowing the overall process to operate more efficiently. The expected impact is improved timeliness of statistical results, reduced pressure on coding staff and greater consistency in the handling of textual information. In operational terms, the approach offers stronger scalability, better use of limited personnel capacity and a more sustainable way to manage growing data volumes while maintaining quality in official statistical production.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-168,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00163,EU_PSTW_202609_00163,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Federal Office for Information Security (BSI),General public services n.e.c.,"Static Application Security Testing is already used to examine source code without executing it, but conventional approaches can struggle to recognise new or evolving error patterns efficiently. The expected impact is more secure software, lower manual workload for developers and shorter development cycles.",Project ML-SAST - Machine Learning in the Context of Static Application Security Testing,Federal Office for Information Security (BSI); Central-Government; General public services n.e.c.; Germany,"The use case therefore involves the BSI, software-security researchers and development teams, with the objective of strengthening automated code analysis and exploring whether more adaptive methods can make security testing more effective and less burdensome. The solution is implemented as a staged research and development effort focused on applying machine-learning techniques to static security testing.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Federal Office for Information Security launched the ML-SAST project to address a pressing software-security problem: identifying vulnerabilities and coding errors early enough in the development process to reduce remediation effort, shorten delivery times and improve the overall security of software systems. Static Application Security Testing is already used to examine source code without executing it, but conventional approaches can struggle to recognise new or evolving error patterns efficiently. The use case therefore involves the BSI, software-security researchers and development teams, with the objective of strengthening automated code analysis and exploring whether more adaptive methods can make security testing more effective and less burdensome. The solution is implemented as a staged research and development effort focused on applying machine-learning techniques to static security testing. At a conceptual level, the project begins with a feasibility study and literature review to identify the most suitable model families for detecting code patterns associated with vulnerabilities. On the basis of those findings, an open-source prototype is then developed to demonstrate the method in practice. This combines pattern-recognition strengths from machine learning with the static-analysis context of software assurance. The expected impact is more secure software, lower manual workload for developers and shorter development cycles. More broadly, ML-SAST creates value by exploring a scalable route towards earlier and more autonomous vulnerability detection in software engineering.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-169,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00164,EU_PSTW_202609_00164,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Federal Agency for Technical Relief,Public order and safety n.e.c.,"Project ROMATRIS responds to a practical problem in emergency response: transporting essential materials through difficult or obstructed terrain during rescue operations. In scenarios such as collapsed buildings, rail accidents, road incidents or off-road emergencies, responders often need to carry equipment across gravel, slopes or obstacles, which slows interventions and increases physical strain.",Project ROMATRIS,Federal Agency for Technical Relief; Central-Government; Public order and safety n.e.c.; Germany,"The use case therefore involves rescue teams and emergency-service organisations, with the objective of improving field logistics, reducing responder burden and supporting faster, safer access to critical equipment in demanding environments.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Project ROMATRIS responds to a practical problem in emergency response: transporting essential materials through difficult or obstructed terrain during rescue operations. In scenarios such as collapsed buildings, rail accidents, road incidents or off-road emergencies, responders often need to carry equipment across gravel, slopes or obstacles, which slows interventions and increases physical strain. The use case therefore involves rescue teams and emergency-service organisations, with the objective of improving field logistics, reducing responder burden and supporting faster, safer access to critical equipment in demanding environments. The solution is based on a partially automated mobile assistance robot designed to accompany a person through impassable terrain while carrying materials needed for the operation. At a conceptual level, the robot combines autonomous mobility, terrain adaptation, person-following capabilities, gesture recognition and feedback mechanisms so that it can remain aligned with the user and respond appropriately in dynamic rescue conditions. By integrating transport support with human guidance, the system is intended to fit into operational workflows without replacing the rescuer’s judgement. The expected impact is greater efficiency in material transport, reduced physical effort for responders and improved continuity of operations in hazardous settings. More broadly, the project offers scalable support for emergency logistics, enhances reliability under difficult field conditions and contributes to safer, more effective rescue missions.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-170,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00165,EU_PSTW_202609_00165,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Province of South Holland,Transport,UNKNOWN,Bridge sensors to control car and boat traffic,Province of South Holland; Local Government; Transport; Netherlands,"The Province of South Holland introduced this use case to address the operational challenge of keeping both roads and waterways safe, accessible and efficient in an area where bridge openings directly affect two interconnected transport systems. Through the Smart Shipping programme, developed with the Directorate-General for Public Works and Water Management and the Ministry of Infrastructure and Water Management, the province aims to improve the use of inland waterways while reducing unnecessary disruption for road users.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Province of South Holland introduced this use case to address the operational challenge of keeping both roads and waterways safe, accessible and efficient in an area where bridge openings directly affect two interconnected transport systems. Through the Smart Shipping programme, developed with the Directorate-General for Public Works and Water Management and the Ministry of Infrastructure and Water Management, the province aims to improve the use of inland waterways while reducing unnecessary disruption for road users. The central objective is to coordinate bridge operations more intelligently, so that vessels can pass with less waiting time and road traffic remains manageable when bridges open. The implementation relies on a network of sensors installed across 55 provincial bridges to detect whether each bridge is open or closed, combined with induction loops placed in roads to measure traffic volumes. These data streams are used by a mobility application that supports operational decisions on bridge openings by balancing vessel movements with road traffic conditions. By integrating bridge-status data and traffic-flow measurements, the system enables more responsive planning and contributes to smoother multimodal transport management. The expected value lies in reducing waiting times for ships, limiting avoidable congestion for cars and other road users, improving safety around bridge operations, and supporting the wider competitiveness of waterborne transport as a reliable alternative to road-based logistics.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-171,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00166,EU_PSTW_202609_00166,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Province of Friesland,Environmental protection n.e.c.,"At a conceptual level, the system analyses satellite data to classify vegetation patterns across the islands, while areas that are difficult to interpret automatically are flagged for manual review by inspectors. More broadly, the approach creates value by improving ecological oversight, reducing fieldwork burden and enabling a more responsive approach to landscape management.",Vegetation detection Friesland - Remote sensing and image recognition to interpret satellite images,Province of Friesland; Regional Government; Environmental protection n.e.c.; Netherlands,"The use case therefore focused on improving how authorities assess the current state of vegetation, with the added importance that many plant communities in the area are sensitive to nitrogen, making accurate vegetation monitoring relevant for broader environmental modelling as well. The solution is implemented through remote sensing and image-recognition techniques applied to satellite imagery.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Province of Friesland needed a more efficient and timely way to monitor vegetation on the Dutch Wadden Islands, where traditional field-based registration had been carried out only once every six or twelve years by inspectors travelling to the islands. This approach was both labour-intensive and too infrequent to provide the up-to-date environmental insight needed for effective management. The use case therefore focused on improving how authorities assess the current state of vegetation, with the added importance that many plant communities in the area are sensitive to nitrogen, making accurate vegetation monitoring relevant for broader environmental modelling as well. The stakeholders include provincial authorities, environmental inspectors and ecological planners, with the objective of creating a more scalable and current monitoring method while retaining expert oversight for uncertain cases. The solution is implemented through remote sensing and image-recognition techniques applied to satellite imagery. At a conceptual level, the system analyses satellite data to classify vegetation patterns across the islands, while areas that are difficult to interpret automatically are flagged for manual review by inspectors. This creates a hybrid workflow that combines large-scale automated screening with targeted expert validation. The expected impact is more frequent and less labour-intensive vegetation monitoring, better environmental intelligence and stronger support for nitrogen modelling. More broadly, the approach creates value by improving ecological oversight, reducing fieldwork burden and enabling a more responsive approach to landscape management.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-172,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00167,EU_PSTW_202609_00167,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Province of South Holland,Social protection n.e.c.,"The operational challenge was that reviewing applications manually was slow, costly and difficult to scale, which delayed support and limited administrative efficiency.",Automation subsidy requirements - Application for a subsidy are examined by AI algorithm,Province of South Holland; Local Government; Social protection n.e.c.; Netherlands,The Province of South Holland introduced this use case to address a practical barrier to household sustainability: many homeowners wanted to make their homes more energy-efficient but lacked the financial means to do so. The solution is implemented through automated assessment based on cluster data analysis and rule-based eligibility evaluation.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Province of South Holland introduced this use case to address a practical barrier to household sustainability: many homeowners wanted to make their homes more energy-efficient but lacked the financial means to do so. To support this group, the province offered subsidies of between EUR 300 and EUR 3,000 to homeowners whose property value was EUR 200,000 or less, provided that they had taken measures to improve the sustainability of their homes. The operational challenge was that reviewing applications manually was slow, costly and difficult to scale, which delayed support and limited administrative efficiency. The stakeholders therefore included homeowners, subsidy administrators and provincial programme managers, with the objective of accelerating application handling, reducing processing costs and ensuring that eligible applicants received support more quickly. The solution is implemented through automated assessment based on cluster data analysis and rule-based eligibility evaluation. At a conceptual level, the system analyses application data to identify likely eligible cases and processes them automatically, while directing uncertain cases to human staff for review so that applications are not rejected solely by the system. The project was delivered using an innovation-oriented methodology involving funnels, scrum and product owners. The impact includes a reduction in processing time from weeks to days, improved administrative scalability and lower handling costs. More broadly, the initiative creates value by speeding up access to sustainability support and making the subsidy scheme more efficient and citizen-friendly.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-173,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00168,EU_PSTW_202609_00168,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Social Affairs and Employment Inspectorate (SZW),Social protection n.e.c.,"Labour exploitation and human trafficking are difficult to detect because warning signs are often hidden within ordinary-looking financial activity, making traditional investigation methods slow and resource-intensive.",Tracing potential victims of human trafficking and labour exploitation with AI,Social Affairs and Employment Inspectorate (SZW); Central-Government; Social protection n.e.c.; Netherlands,"The use case does not aim to detect perpetrators directly at the initial stage, but to help authorities identify potential victims more effectively and trigger further investigation where justified. The solution is implemented through pattern-recognition scripts applied to banking data using risk patterns supplied by the Inspectorate.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Labour exploitation and human trafficking are difficult to detect because warning signs are often hidden within ordinary-looking financial activity, making traditional investigation methods slow and resource-intensive. To address this, the Inspectorate SZW collaborated with ABN AMRO and the University of Amsterdam to identify patterns in banking data that may indicate possible cases of labour exploitation. The use case does not aim to detect perpetrators directly at the initial stage, but to help authorities identify potential victims more effectively and trigger further investigation where justified. The stakeholders include labour inspectors, banks, researchers, the Financial Intelligence Unit and, ultimately, prosecutors and judges, with the objective of strengthening early detection while preserving human control over escalatory decisions. The solution is implemented through pattern-recognition scripts applied to banking data using risk patterns supplied by the Inspectorate. At a conceptual level, the scripts search for relevant transactional connections, after which human reviewers check the output for false positives. If a pattern is validated, it may justify a further FIU investigation into suspicious transactions, which can then support legal escalation and, where authorised, access to address and transaction details for tracing potential victims. Machine-learning refinement and expansion to four other major banks are under consideration. The impact lies in earlier and more targeted identification, improved investigative efficiency and stronger inter-institutional cooperation in combating labour exploitation.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-174,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00169,EU_PSTW_202609_00169,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Netherlands Food and Consumer Product Safety Authority (NVW,"Agriculture, forestry, fishing and hunting","The Netherlands Food and Consumer Product Safety Authority needed a faster and more accurate way to identify sites where animal cadavers may have been buried illegally, since manual inspection over large areas is resource-intensive and can delay enforcement action. Illegal burial may alter surface vegetation and create subtle irregularities that are difficult to spot consistently from the ground.",Loting illegally buried cadavers with drones - Use of drones to detect illegaly buried cadavers,"Netherlands Food and Consumer Product Safety Authority (NVW; Central-Government; Agriculture, forestry, fishing and hunting; Netherlands","The use case therefore involves inspectors and regulatory authorities seeking to target investigations more effectively, with the objective of obtaining an early and reliable picture of suspicious locations so that field resources can be deployed where they are most needed. The solution uses drones to collect aerial data and analytical models to detect anomalies associated with disturbed vegetation patterns.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Netherlands Food and Consumer Product Safety Authority needed a faster and more accurate way to identify sites where animal cadavers may have been buried illegally, since manual inspection over large areas is resource-intensive and can delay enforcement action. Illegal burial may alter surface vegetation and create subtle irregularities that are difficult to spot consistently from the ground. The use case therefore involves inspectors and regulatory authorities seeking to target investigations more effectively, with the objective of obtaining an early and reliable picture of suspicious locations so that field resources can be deployed where they are most needed. The solution uses drones to collect aerial data and analytical models to detect anomalies associated with disturbed vegetation patterns. At a conceptual level, the implementation combines remote sensing, machine-learning-based irregularity detection and inspection triage, allowing broad areas to be screened quickly and suspect zones to be flagged for human follow-up. Rather than replacing inspectors, the system acts as an early-warning layer that narrows the search area and improves the efficiency of site verification. The expected impact is faster localisation of suspicious burial sites, reduced manual search effort and better prioritisation of inspections. In operational terms, the approach improves scalability, strengthens responsiveness and delivers business value through more efficient environmental and public-health enforcement.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-175,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00170,EU_PSTW_202609_00170,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Netherlands Forensic Institute,Police Services,"Manual searching through data carriers is time-consuming and can slow investigative progress, especially when detectives must examine extensive collections of low-quality or mixed-content images. This creates a practical classification layer that can highlight potentially relevant material for further expert examination rather than requiring investigators to inspect all data manually.",AI for detecting illegal substances - AI to index seized data carrier,Netherlands Forensic Institute; Central-Government; Police Services; Netherlands,The use case was therefore developed to support forensic and investigative teams by accelerating the indexing of seized data and helping them locate potentially relevant material more efficiently. The solution is implemented through a workflow in which seized data carriers are indexed and their contents scanned for images likely to depict illegal substances.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Netherlands Forensic Institute needed a faster and more effective way to review large volumes of seized digital material in investigations where images of illegal substances might be present. Manual searching through data carriers is time-consuming and can slow investigative progress, especially when detectives must examine extensive collections of low-quality or mixed-content images. The use case was therefore developed to support forensic and investigative teams by accelerating the indexing of seized data and helping them locate potentially relevant material more efficiently. The stakeholders include forensic analysts, detectives and criminal-investigation units, with the objective of reducing review time, improving search precision and increasing the effectiveness of digital evidence analysis. The solution is implemented through a workflow in which seized data carriers are indexed and their contents scanned for images likely to depict illegal substances. At a conceptual level, the development process involved labelling examples of illegal substances, selecting an appropriate model architecture based on pre-trained deep-learning methods and then fine-tuning the model to perform better on low-quality images. This creates a practical classification layer that can highlight potentially relevant material for further expert examination rather than requiring investigators to inspect all data manually. The impact includes faster evidence handling, improved investigative productivity and more efficient use of forensic resources. More broadly, the system creates value by strengthening digital-forensics capability and allowing law-enforcement teams to focus their efforts more effectively on high-priority material.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-176,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00171,EU_PSTW_202609_00171,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Rotterdam,Social protection n.e.c.,"Many people experiencing debt problems struggle to find the right form of help quickly, particularly when they do not know which service to contact or feel overwhelmed by the complexity of support pathways. This creates a significant access problem in debt relief, as delays in finding appropriate advice can worsen financial hardship and make later intervention more difficult.",Debt relief - Chatbot to assist people with debts,Rotterdam; Local Government; Social protection n.e.c.; Netherlands,"The solution is implemented partly through a chatbot that asks a series of key questions in order to determine the most appropriate next step for the person concerned. At a conceptual level, the system uses structured conversational triage and decision logic to assess the user’s situation and route them towards the correct entry path for debt support.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Many people experiencing debt problems struggle to find the right form of help quickly, particularly when they do not know which service to contact or feel overwhelmed by the complexity of support pathways. This creates a significant access problem in debt relief, as delays in finding appropriate advice can worsen financial hardship and make later intervention more difficult. The project, developed through a collaboration between IBM and the Municipality of Rotterdam, was designed to make local debt-relief support easier and faster to reach. The stakeholders include debtors, municipal support services and debt-assistance providers, with the objective of lowering access barriers, guiding people to the most suitable support route and improving the overall responsiveness of debt-relief services. The solution is implemented partly through a chatbot that asks a series of key questions in order to determine the most appropriate next step for the person concerned. At a conceptual level, the system uses structured conversational triage and decision logic to assess the user’s situation and route them towards the correct entry path for debt support. This creates a more accessible first contact point than leaving individuals to navigate multiple service options unaided. The impact includes quicker orientation into support pathways, reduced confusion for users and a more efficient front door for debt-relief services. More broadly, the project creates value by improving early access to assistance and helping local authorities organise debt-support journeys more effectively.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-177,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00172,EU_PSTW_202609_00172,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Leibniz Center for Law, University of Amsterdam","Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.","Legal and policy rules are often difficult to interpret consistently, particularly when different professional groups or affected citizens need to understand how norms should be applied in practice. This creates challenges not only for legislative drafting and legal analysis, but also for the legitimacy and acceptance of decisions made under complex regulatory frameworks.",Calculemus FLINT - Ensuring understandability of normative law texts,"Leibniz Center for Law, University of Amsterdam; Academic-Research; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Netherlands","The solution is implemented through a paired method and language framework: Calculemus as a roadmap for solving normative questions, and FLINT as a formal domain-specific language for interpreting judicial and normative sources.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Legal and policy rules are often difficult to interpret consistently, particularly when different professional groups or affected citizens need to understand how norms should be applied in practice. This creates challenges not only for legislative drafting and legal analysis, but also for the legitimacy and acceptance of decisions made under complex regulatory frameworks. Calculemus FLINT was developed to address this problem by making normative legal texts more understandable, more formally interpretable and easier to test for executability. The stakeholders include policymakers, legislative lawyers and citizens subject to legal rules, with the objective of improving the exchange, interpretation and explanation of norms across different groups while supporting more transparent and accountable implementation. The solution is implemented through a paired method and language framework: Calculemus as a roadmap for solving normative questions, and FLINT as a formal domain-specific language for interpreting judicial and normative sources. At a conceptual level, the approach translates legal norms into structured formal representations that allow rules to be analysed, compared and tested more systematically. This can help explain whether laws are operationally executable and whether decisions made under them can be justified clearly. The impact is primarily qualitative, centred on greater interpretability, improved legal transparency and stronger normative grounding for automated or assisted decisions. More broadly, the method creates value by increasing confidence in rule-based public administration and supporting clearer communication between legal experts, policymakers and citizens.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-178,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00173,EU_PSTW_202609_00173,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Amsterdam,Health n.e.c.,"The operational problem was that authorities needed a way to observe crowd behaviour and communicate distancing risks in real time, particularly in locations where pedestrian density could change quickly and manual oversight alone was insufficient.",One and a half meter monitor - Monitor which automatically calculates the distance between people,City of Amsterdam; Local Government; Health n.e.c.; Netherlands,"The solution was implemented through camera-based monitoring that measured the distance between pedestrians and displayed anonymised visual feedback using green, orange and red smileys, accompanied by audio warnings.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Amsterdam introduced the 1.5 metre monitor during the COVID-19 pandemic to support enforcement of public-health guidance on social distancing in busy public spaces. The operational problem was that authorities needed a way to observe crowd behaviour and communicate distancing risks in real time, particularly in locations where pedestrian density could change quickly and manual oversight alone was insufficient. The stakeholders included municipal authorities, public-space managers and pedestrians, with the objective of encouraging safer distancing behaviour, monitoring crowding and helping manage public areas under emergency regulations. The solution was implemented through camera-based monitoring that measured the distance between pedestrians and displayed anonymised visual feedback using green, orange and red smileys, accompanied by audio warnings. At a conceptual level, the system used computer-vision techniques to estimate interpersonal distance and produce immediate behavioural cues without storing photographs, while still recording pedestrian counts to track crowd levels. This allowed the city to test a form of real-time public-space monitoring that combined behaviour feedback with crowd measurement. The impact was primarily operational and situational: it offered a way to promote distancing and understand crowding patterns during the pandemic, while limiting data retention by avoiding image storage. Once COVID-19 restrictions ended, the monitor was discontinued. Although temporary, the case remains an important example of how public-health rules were supported through live behavioural monitoring in urban environments.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,NOT_IN_USE,미사용,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-179,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00174,EU_PSTW_202609_00174,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Olomouc University Hospital,Health n.e.c.,"The operational challenge was to observe whether visitors and others in indoor spaces were wearing respirator masks correctly, maintaining recommended distance and displaying possible signs of illness, while reducing the burden on staff responsible for supervision. Because the system remained in testing, its impact was exploratory rather than fully operational, but its anticipated value lay in strengthening rule adherence, reducing manual checking and supporting safer movement through hospital facilities during the pandemic.",Detection protective equipment,Olomouc University Hospital; Academic-Research; Health n.e.c.; Czechia,"The use case therefore involved hospital management, visitors and industry partners, with the objective of supporting safer indoor conditions and improving adherence to pandemic-related rules within the hospital environment. The solution was implemented as a system under development that combined detection of correct mask use, interpersonal distancing and coughing behaviour.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"University Hospital Olomouc tested this system during April and May 2021, at a time when hospitals and other indoor environments needed practical ways to monitor compliance with COVID-19 protective measures. The operational challenge was to observe whether visitors and others in indoor spaces were wearing respirator masks correctly, maintaining recommended distance and displaying possible signs of illness, while reducing the burden on staff responsible for supervision. The use case therefore involved hospital management, visitors and industry partners, with the objective of supporting safer indoor conditions and improving adherence to pandemic-related rules within the hospital environment. The solution was implemented as a system under development that combined detection of correct mask use, interpersonal distancing and coughing behaviour. At a conceptual level, it used visual and audio analysis technologies, reportedly alongside thermal-camera testing in the wider hospital effort, to identify whether personal protective equipment was being worn properly and whether distancing and symptom-related behaviours suggested heightened risk. This created a single monitoring framework intended to support public-health compliance in interior spaces. Because the system remained in testing, its impact was exploratory rather than fully operational, but its anticipated value lay in strengthening rule adherence, reducing manual checking and supporting safer movement through hospital facilities during the pandemic. The initiative is no longer in use, yet it illustrates the short-term experimentation that many health institutions pursued when trying to manage infection-control measures more effectively.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,NOT_IN_USE,미사용,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-180,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00175,EU_PSTW_202609_00175,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Amsterdam,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.","Manual reporting by residents and reactive clean-up processes created inefficiencies, delayed response and placed unnecessary burden both on citizens and municipal waste services. More broadly, ODK creates value by improving the cleanliness of urban environments, reducing operational friction and offering a scalable open-source model that other municipalities can potentially adapt to their own waste-management challenges.",Object Detection Kit Amsterdam - Automatic detection of garbage,"City of Amsterdam; Local Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Netherlands","The use case therefore involves the municipality, waste-removal teams and local residents, with the objective of keeping public spaces cleaner while deploying removal services more efficiently and reducing the need for residents to report misplaced rubbish repeatedly. The solution is implemented through the open-source Object Detection Kit, a platform initiated by Amsterdam to detect rubbish in public space automatically.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Amsterdam introduced the Object Detection Kit to address a highly visible and operationally demanding urban-management issue: waste placed incorrectly in public space, especially near underground containers, was affecting street cleanliness and requiring frequent reporting and intervention. Manual reporting by residents and reactive clean-up processes created inefficiencies, delayed response and placed unnecessary burden both on citizens and municipal waste services. The use case therefore involves the municipality, waste-removal teams and local residents, with the objective of keeping public spaces cleaner while deploying removal services more efficiently and reducing the need for residents to report misplaced rubbish repeatedly. The solution is implemented through the open-source Object Detection Kit, a platform initiated by Amsterdam to detect rubbish in public space automatically. At a conceptual level, the system uses object-detection techniques to scan public areas, identify waste items visually and transmit this information directly to the service responsible for removing that category of rubbish. This creates an automated chain from detection to operational response, allowing waste issues to be addressed more proactively. The impact includes faster identification of rubbish hotspots, more efficient deployment of cleaning resources and less reliance on citizen reporting. More broadly, ODK creates value by improving the cleanliness of urban environments, reducing operational friction and offering a scalable open-source model that other municipalities can potentially adapt to their own waste-management challenges.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-181,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00176,EU_PSTW_202609_00176,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""social_nonprofit""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Kungsbacka municipality,Pre-primary and primary education.,"The operational challenge is that current analysis of this problem has been limited largely to grades and has been carried out manually, which restricts the ability to uncover broader patterns and understand the full range of contributing factors. At a conceptual level, the system is intended to move beyond manual grade-based review by examining a wider set of patterns and relationships in available student data, helping to surface contributing factors that may not be obvious through conventional analysis alone.",Detection causes early school leavers Kungsbacka - Analysing data of early school leavers,Kungsbacka municipality; Local Government; Pre-primary and primary education.; Sweden,"This use case was developed to help the organisation gain a deeper and more proactive understanding of why some students are at risk of early educational disengagement, with the objective of identifying underlying causes earlier and enabling preventive intervention. The stakeholders include school leaders, education staff and students at risk of exclusion from further education pathways, and the initiative also serves as a first step towards wider use of advanced analytical tools in the organisation.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Each year in Kungsbacka, around 100 students move from primary school to upper secondary education without the eligibility required to continue successfully, creating a significant concern for educators and local authorities. The operational challenge is that current analysis of this problem has been limited largely to grades and has been carried out manually, which restricts the ability to uncover broader patterns and understand the full range of contributing factors. This use case was developed to help the organisation gain a deeper and more proactive understanding of why some students are at risk of early educational disengagement, with the objective of identifying underlying causes earlier and enabling preventive intervention. The stakeholders include school leaders, education staff and students at risk of exclusion from further education pathways, and the initiative also serves as a first step towards wider use of advanced analytical tools in the organisation. The solution is implemented through analytical technology applied to data on students transitioning without eligibility. At a conceptual level, the system is intended to move beyond manual grade-based review by examining a wider set of patterns and relationships in available student data, helping to surface contributing factors that may not be obvious through conventional analysis alone. The expected impact is better understanding of educational risk, stronger capacity for early intervention and a more evidence-based foundation for support measures. More broadly, the project creates value by improving educational planning and opening the way for broader data-driven improvement across other service areas.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-182,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00177,EU_PSTW_202609_00177,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Federal Office for Information Security,Public order and safety n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,Assessment security properties of Symmetric Ciphers - Supporting assessment of security properties of cryptographic algorithms,Federal Office for Information Security; Central-Government; Public order and safety n.e.c.; Germany,This use case was developed to support that evaluation process and to investigate whether advanced computational methods can assist experts in solving cryptographic problems more efficiently. The solution is implemented through a tool based on deep neural networks.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Assessing the security strength of cryptographic algorithms is a technically demanding task, yet it is essential for organisations that rely on encryption to protect sensitive information. In the case of symmetric ciphers, security assessment often requires specialised expertise and careful analysis of whether the cipher can be distinguished from random behaviour under different conditions. This use case was developed to support that evaluation process and to investigate whether advanced computational methods can assist experts in solving cryptographic problems more efficiently. The stakeholders include the BSI, cryptography specialists and organisations that depend on trustworthy encryption mechanisms, with the objective of improving the assessment of cipher security and strengthening the analytical basis for selecting secure algorithms. The solution is implemented through a tool based on deep neural networks. At a conceptual level, the model is trained to distinguish ciphertext pairs produced by round-reduced symmetric ciphers from random text, thereby producing a coarse estimate of the security level that a given cipher may provide. This allows the tool to act as an analytical aid within a broader cryptographic study rather than as a definitive replacement for expert evaluation. The expected impact is faster exploratory assessment, improved support for security analysis and a stronger evidence base for cryptographic review. More broadly, the project creates value by opening a new line of support for complex cipher evaluation within information-security research.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-183,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00178,EU_PSTW_202609_00178,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Federal Office for Security in Information Technology (Bundesamt für Sicherheit in der Informationstechnik - BSI),"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.","Public organisations working with large volumes of unstructured information on IT security need better ways to search, interpret and reuse textual content that is often fragmented across news, reports and background material. The operational challenge is that pure text alone is difficult to query with precision, especially when words have multiple meanings or when users need systems to support more advanced reasoning rather than simple keyword lookup.",Enhancing metadata for textual information - improving the quality of search and automatic reasoning,"Federal Office for Security in Information Technology (Bundesamt für Sicherheit in der Informationstechnik - BSI); Central-Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Germany","This use case therefore focuses on improving the metadata attached to unstructured textual information so that search quality and automated reasoning can be strengthened. The solution is implemented by first creating a corpus of publicly available IT-security news and background texts, pre-processing it to remove artefacts and technical noise, and then enriching it through a combination of semantic and statistical methods.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Public organisations working with large volumes of unstructured information on IT security need better ways to search, interpret and reuse textual content that is often fragmented across news, reports and background material. The operational challenge is that pure text alone is difficult to query with precision, especially when words have multiple meanings or when users need systems to support more advanced reasoning rather than simple keyword lookup. This use case therefore focuses on improving the metadata attached to unstructured textual information so that search quality and automated reasoning can be strengthened. The stakeholders include the BSI, analysts and users of IT-security information, with the objective of creating cleaner, more structured and more semantically useful text resources for retrieval and analysis. The solution is implemented by first creating a corpus of publicly available IT-security news and background texts, pre-processing it to remove artefacts and technical noise, and then enriching it through a combination of semantic and statistical methods. At a conceptual level, manually created taxonomies and ontologies, using standards such as SKOS, are combined with machine-learning approaches that capture local context and support named-entity extraction, while transformer-based NLP models are applied to tasks such as entity recognition, classification and question answering. The impact is expected to include higher-quality search, more reusable metadata and better support for automated interpretation, creating value through improved information access and stronger analytical capability in cybersecurity knowledge management.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F04_UNSTRUCTURED_INFORMATION_COMPLEXITY|F06_HIGH_VOLUME_SCALABILITY_BOTTLENECK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-184,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00179,EU_PSTW_202609_00179,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Barcelona,General public services n.e.c.,"If an incident is assigned to the wrong category, it can be routed to the wrong department, causing delays and reducing service quality. This creates a more intelligent classification layer at the point of submission and reduces the reliance on later manual correction.",IRIS - Support system for classification of citizen inquiries or complaints,City of Barcelona; Local Government; General public services n.e.c.; Spain,"The use case involves residents, the IRIS service and the municipal departments responsible for resolving reported incidents, with the objective of speeding up response times, reducing classification errors and improving the overall quality of citizen service. The solution is implemented through MARIO, a machine-learning-based module developed as part of the IRIS update.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Barcelona City Council relies on IRIS as the channel through which citizens report incidents, submit complaints and suggest service improvements, yet the effectiveness of the process depends heavily on the initial thematic classification chosen by the user. If an incident is assigned to the wrong category, it can be routed to the wrong department, causing delays and reducing service quality. The operational problem was therefore to simplify classification for citizens while improving routing accuracy inside the council. The use case involves residents, the IRIS service and the municipal departments responsible for resolving reported incidents, with the objective of speeding up response times, reducing classification errors and improving the overall quality of citizen service. The solution is implemented through MARIO, a machine-learning-based module developed as part of the IRIS update. At a conceptual level, the module analyses the free text entered by the user and suggests the most likely categories into which the incident fits, allowing the citizen to choose the most appropriate option with guidance from the system. This creates a more intelligent classification layer at the point of submission and reduces the reliance on later manual correction. The reported impact is substantial: whereas previously around 50% of communications had to be reassigned, MARIO now achieves a hit rate of over 85%. More broadly, the project creates value by improving routing efficiency, reducing rework and making citizen reporting more reliable and user-friendly.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-185,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00180,EU_PSTW_202609_00180,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Agency for Administrative Modernisation - AMA,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.",The operational challenge was that citizens often need quick answers about administrative procedures but may struggle to navigate large service portals or understand where to find the right transaction.,Chatbot Sigma,"Agency for Administrative Modernisation - AMA; Central-Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Portugal","The solution was implemented through a combination of three Microsoft technologies: LUIS for intent recognition, QnA for service and chat content, and Cognitive Services APIs for keyword-based interpretation. Although Sigma is no longer in use, it remains a useful example of multi-platform chatbot deployment in government digital services.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Sigma served as the chatbot for Portugal’s public-services portal, ePortugal.gov.pt, helping users navigate state services and complete certain simple interactions through a digital conversational interface. The operational challenge was that citizens often need quick answers about administrative procedures but may struggle to navigate large service portals or understand where to find the right transaction. Sigma was introduced to improve access to public-service information and make some routine interactions more convenient, such as changing an address on the Citizen Card or checking polling stations during elections. The stakeholders included portal users, public-service administrators and the teams managing the national digital-services platform, with the objective of simplifying first-line access to administrative information and tasks. The solution was implemented through a combination of three Microsoft technologies: LUIS for intent recognition, QnA for service and chat content, and Cognitive Services APIs for keyword-based interpretation. At a conceptual level, these components enabled the chatbot to recognise user questions, retrieve relevant content and guide people towards the appropriate service or action. This created a conversational access layer over the wider ePortugal portal rather than requiring users to rely solely on menus and static pages. The impact was improved convenience, quicker access to routine information and broader experimentation with conversational public services. Although Sigma is no longer in use, it remains a useful example of multi-platform chatbot deployment in government digital services.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,NOT_IN_USE,미사용,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-186,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00181,EU_PSTW_202609_00181,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Aiguasol,Housing and community amenities n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,Tool to optimise self-consumption in residential sectors,Aiguasol; Private sector; Housing and community amenities n.e.c.; Spain,"Households and residential communities increasingly want to make better use of locally generated renewable electricity, but optimising self-consumption is difficult because it depends on understanding both generation patterns and highly variable consumption behaviour. This use case was therefore designed to help residential users and energy planners develop more efficient self-consumption scenarios and better align local renewable generation with actual demand.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Households and residential communities increasingly want to make better use of locally generated renewable electricity, but optimising self-consumption is difficult because it depends on understanding both generation patterns and highly variable consumption behaviour. In practice, selecting the right system size and managing hourly distribution of photovoltaic output across users requires forecasting that most available tools do not support well. This use case was therefore designed to help residential users and energy planners develop more efficient self-consumption scenarios and better align local renewable generation with actual demand. The stakeholders include energy users, installers, planners and potentially grid operators, with the objective of improving self-consumption rates, supporting system sizing and enabling more efficient use of distributed renewable energy. The solution is implemented through a set of tools built around three modules: recruitment, planning and execution. At a conceptual level, the platform combines consumption forecasting, photovoltaic production forecasting and optimisation techniques to calculate hourly distribution coefficients and recommend self-consumption scenarios. In addition, automatically generated statistical profiles by user type help determine the optimal number of PV panels during earlier planning stages. The expected impact is better use of renewable generation, more accurate system sizing and reduced difficulty in planning residential self-consumption schemes. More broadly, the tool creates value by helping users adapt consumption habits, supporting future grid-stability services and enabling a more data-driven, scalable approach to distributed energy management.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-187,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00182,EU_PSTW_202609_00182,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Finnish Patent and Registration Office PRH,Economic affairs n.e.c,UNKNOWN,Chatbot Trade Register - Development of Chatbot for Trade Register matters,Finnish Patent and Registration Office PRH; Central-Government; Economic affairs n.e.c; Finland,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,The Finnish Patent Registration Office has developed a Finnish-speaking chatbot to assist citenzs and customers with Trade Register matters. It provides general advice on questions related to the Trade Register and financial statement reporting. The chatbot is continuously trained and its content is developed based on discussions and feedback.,SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-188,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00183,EU_PSTW_202609_00183,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Finnish Tax Administration (FTA),"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.",UNKNOWN,Automation of subtitling videos and audios - Improving accessibility of public websites,"Finnish Tax Administration (FTA); Central-Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Finland",UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,The automation of subtitling videos and audios is a solution that relates to the directive (EU) 2016/2102 of the European Parliament and of the Council of 26 October 2016 on the accessibility of the websites and mobile applications of public sector bodies. The technical solution is intended for subtitling public and internal videos and audios by utilizing Microsoft's Azure cloud computing technology - speech-to-text services and text analytics. The subtitling service is automated in such a way that the user downloads the recording to be subtitled via the web interface and receives an e-mail when the subtitling is completed. The subtitle file (.srt) is returned to the user.,SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S3_DOCUMENT_INTELLIGENCE,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-189,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00184,EU_PSTW_202609_00184,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,The National Library of Finland,Cultural services.,'-The trained model was used to classify 50 000 images and its accuracy was about 70-75% when about 10 000 classified images were manually observed.,Cultural Heritage Tools - AI tools integrated in the National Library of Finland,The National Library of Finland; Central-Government; Cultural services.; Finland,"The Stanford NER system was trained using 220 annotated pages and evaluated using 34 pages. Using the trained model, it was possible to classify images into the following groups: advertisements, drawings, notes, pictures, paintings, maps or garbage.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Two AI tools have been integrated into the IT system of the National Library of Finland (digi.nationallibrary.fi): 1. Named entity recognition (NER) -Stanford NER software to recognize persons and place names from the Finnish historical newspapers. The first training and evaluation data set was created that consists of pages of both journals and newspapers from the time period 1836–1918. The Stanford NER system was trained using 220 annotated pages and evaluated using 34 pages. The results that were achieved were mostly good: 71 % of person names and 79 % of place names were recognized from the evaluation data. 2. Image classification -Google TensorFlow was used to classify images. A training dataset was created that was used to train the classifier model with TensorFlow. Using the trained model, it was possible to classify images into the following groups: advertisements, drawings, notes, pictures, paintings, maps or garbage. -The trained model was used to classify 50 000 images and its accuracy was about 70-75% when about 10 000 classified images were manually observed. -A graphical user interface was created that enabled an easier way to observe achieved results.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-190,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00185,EU_PSTW_202609_00185,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Social Insurance Institution of Finland,Social protection n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,Chatbot - Social Insurance Institution of Finland,Social Insurance Institution of Finland; Central-Government; Social protection n.e.c.; Finland,"The service responds to the need for a more accessible front door to welfare information, reducing dependence on traditional service channels for routine questions and supporting users who may not immediately know which benefit, allowance or administrative process applies to their situation. From a technical perspective, the system uses conversational technology and natural language processing to interpret user queries, identify the relevant topic area and return appropriate guidance from structured welfare-service content.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Social Insurance Institution of Finland developed this chatbot to help citizens access information on public welfare matters more easily, particularly in areas where users often require timely clarification, such as family benefits, income support and issues linked to social security. The service responds to the need for a more accessible front door to welfare information, reducing dependence on traditional service channels for routine questions and supporting users who may not immediately know which benefit, allowance or administrative process applies to their situation. The chatbot operates in Finland’s two official languages, Finnish and Swedish, reflecting the institution’s obligation to serve citizens across linguistic groups. While it can understand questions submitted in English, its responses are provided in Finnish or Swedish, ensuring consistency with the language framework of the service. From a technical perspective, the system uses conversational technology and natural language processing to interpret user queries, identify the relevant topic area and return appropriate guidance from structured welfare-service content. Its role is not to replace case-specific decision-making, but to provide first-level information and direct users towards the correct benefit or process. The qualitative impact is improved availability of basic welfare guidance, faster responses to common questions, reduced pressure on staff handling repetitive enquiries, and a more user-friendly digital channel for citizens seeking support from the Finnish social security system.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-191,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00186,EU_PSTW_202609_00186,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Helsinki City Library,R&D general public services.,UNKNOWN,IMMS (Intelligent Material Management System) for the entire library collection.,Helsinki City Library; Local Government; R&D general public services.; Finland,"Implemented in May 2019, IMMS complements the existing library management infrastructure by adding a more detailed layer of collection, space and logistics management. The system uses data collection tools to track materials at granular locations, including shelves, shelving carts, transport bins and storage areas, thereby creating greater transparency across the material flow.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Helsinki City Library introduced the Intelligent Material Management System during its transition from separate library-specific collections towards one shared collection serving the whole city network. The operational problem was that materials needed to move more flexibly between branches, while library staff required better visibility over where items were located, how they circulated and how available space could be used. The objective was to support a “floating” collection model, in which books and other materials are no longer tied permanently to a single branch but can circulate according to user demand and operational needs. Implemented in May 2019, IMMS complements the existing library management infrastructure by adding a more detailed layer of collection, space and logistics management. The system uses data collection tools to track materials at granular locations, including shelves, shelving carts, transport bins and storage areas, thereby creating greater transparency across the material flow. It supports decision-making on stock distribution, item movement, collection balance and physical space use across the library network. As a result, Helsinki City Library has been able to move towards a model in which most of the collection floats between libraries. The main benefits include more efficient use of the overall collection, improved staff control over logistics, better alignment between material availability and user demand, and a more scalable approach to managing public library resources across multiple branches.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-192,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00187,EU_PSTW_202609_00187,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Helsinki,Health n.e.c.,"Its impact is primarily qualitative: it improves the availability of trusted municipal guidance, helps families obtain answers at any time of day, reduces the burden of repetitive enquiries on maternity and child-health staff, and strengthens the digital service offer of Helsinki’s public health system.",NeRo Chatbot - Chatbot for clients of maternity services,City of Helsinki; Local Government; Health n.e.c.; Finland,"Technically, it applies conversational technology and language understanding to interpret user questions and match them with curated information from municipal health-service content.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The City of Helsinki developed NeRo to support parents, expectant parents and carers who need reliable information about pregnancy, infant health and the early development of children. Maternity and child-health services often receive recurring questions on topics such as growth, nutrition, dental care, speech development and everyday health concerns, and users may need guidance outside normal service hours. NeRo responds to this need by offering an around-the-clock digital support channel in Finnish, giving families an accessible source of basic information before they contact health professionals or attend appointments. The chatbot is designed to answer questions related to pregnancy and the health care of babies and small children, covering both preventive guidance and common development-related issues. Technically, it applies conversational technology and language understanding to interpret user questions and match them with curated information from municipal health-service content. The system provides automated responses within a defined service scope and can guide users towards relevant topics without replacing professional medical assessment where individual care is required. Its impact is primarily qualitative: it improves the availability of trusted municipal guidance, helps families obtain answers at any time of day, reduces the burden of repetitive enquiries on maternity and child-health staff, and strengthens the digital service offer of Helsinki’s public health system.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-193,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00188,EU_PSTW_202609_00188,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Ministry of Economic Affairs and Employment,Unemployment.,"The service addresses a core policy and operational challenge: matching people to suitable vacancies in a context where skills, occupations, training services and employment opportunities are distributed across multiple public and private platforms.",Job Market Finland - Matching employees with right jobs,Ministry of Economic Affairs and Employment; Central-Government; Unemployment.; Finland,"The emerging technology component is centred on an AI-based matching solution that compares jobseeker profiles with vacancies using both structured data, such as skills and occupations, and unstructured natural-language information contained in descriptions.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Job Market Finland was developed by the KEHA Centre and the Ministry of Economic Affairs and Employment to improve how jobseekers, employers and service providers interact within the Finnish labour market. The service addresses a core policy and operational challenge: matching people to suitable vacancies in a context where skills, occupations, training services and employment opportunities are distributed across multiple public and private platforms. Its objective is to create a shared digital environment where jobseekers can present their profiles, employers can identify relevant candidates, and different labour-market services can be connected more coherently. The emerging technology component is centred on an AI-based matching solution that compares jobseeker profiles with vacancies using both structured data, such as skills and occupations, and unstructured natural-language information contained in descriptions. The system scores the degree of fit between candidates and roles and presents matches to both jobseekers and employers. It also assists users in describing the skills they offer or require, helping to improve the quality of profile and vacancy information. The expected impact is a more efficient matching process, better visibility of opportunities, reduced friction in recruitment, and stronger alignment between labour supply and employer demand. By combining public employment services with data-driven matching, the platform supports a more transparent, scalable and user-oriented labour-market infrastructure.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-194,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00189,EU_PSTW_202609_00189,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Finnish Tax Administration,Economic affairs n.e.c,UNKNOWN,AI assisted chat service - Chatbot for tax purposes,Finnish Tax Administration; Central-Government; Economic affairs n.e.c; Finland,This arrangement allows the administration to retain control over service quality and accuracy while using external technology to support delivery.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"OmaVero is a chatbot service that provides the Finnish Tax Administration with a digital channel for answering common tax-related questions from individual taxpayers. The service responds to the recurring need for quick, accessible and low-threshold guidance on tax matters, particularly when users are looking for general clarification rather than case-specific decisions. It allows customers to receive assistance without calling the administration, paying telephone costs or completing identification steps for basic enquiries. During office hours, the interaction can be escalated to a human agent when the automated response is not sufficient, ensuring that more complex issues can still be handled by staff. The technological implementation combines a virtual agent with natural language processing, which converts customer messages into a form that the system can interpret and route towards relevant tax content. The underlying AI-assisted chat solution was developed by a commercial provider, while the Finnish Tax Administration remains responsible for governance, including training, monitoring and content creation through its chat-product team. This arrangement allows the administration to retain control over service quality and accuracy while using external technology to support delivery. The main benefits are faster replies to routine questions, broader service availability regardless of time, reduced demand on telephone and front-office channels, and a more efficient allocation of human agents to cases requiring judgement or personalised assistance.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-195,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00190,EU_PSTW_202609_00190,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Statistics Finland,R&D general public services.,UNKNOWN,MLOps platform for classification purposes - Developing classification models,Statistics Finland; Central-Government; R&D general public services.; Finland,"The operational problem is that statistical organisations increasingly use models in internal workflows, but each project can otherwise require separate decisions on architecture, security, deployment, monitoring and maintenance. By standardising MLOps practices, Statistics Finland can scale machine-learning use while preserving statistical quality and confidentiality requirements.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Statistics Finland’s MLOps platform is designed to make the development, deployment and maintenance of machine-learning classification models more robust within official statistics production. The operational problem is that statistical organisations increasingly use models in internal workflows, but each project can otherwise require separate decisions on architecture, security, deployment, monitoring and maintenance. This creates duplication and weakens consistency across statistical processes. The platform supports expert users working with internal data or statistical production pipelines, where classification models may operate either in batch mode or as online prediction services. Its implementation defines common architecture and security policies for model development, reducing the need to redesign governance and technical arrangements case by case. Conceptually, the system covers the machine-learning lifecycle, from model construction and deployment to monitoring and operational maintenance, while ensuring that outputs remain internal intermediate products before aggregation and publication as official statistics. The expected impact is improved reliability, easier model reuse, stronger governance, reduced operational risk and more efficient development of classification models. By standardising MLOps practices, Statistics Finland can scale machine-learning use while preserving statistical quality and confidentiality requirements.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-196,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00191,EU_PSTW_202609_00191,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Idomsoft ZRT,General services.,"The operational challenge is that modern e-government systems generate large volumes of technical and usage data, while service disruptions or misuse can affect citizens’ ability to complete administrative procedures.",Anomaly detection in e-government administration,Idomsoft ZRT; Central-Government; General services.; Hungary,"IdomSoft, a state-owned company responsible for a wide range of Hungarian digital government services, is introducing machine-learning techniques to predict future system loads and identify abnormal use of public-service systems. The implementation applies anomaly-detection algorithms to data generated by administrative digital services, enabling the authority to analyse usage patterns and detect possible service corruption, overload or misuse.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Hungary’s anomaly-detection initiative focuses on protecting the reliability and availability of critical digital public services. The operational challenge is that modern e-government systems generate large volumes of technical and usage data, while service disruptions or misuse can affect citizens’ ability to complete administrative procedures. IdomSoft, a state-owned company responsible for a wide range of Hungarian digital government services, is introducing machine-learning techniques to predict future system loads and identify abnormal use of public-service systems. The implementation applies anomaly-detection algorithms to data generated by administrative digital services, enabling the authority to analyse usage patterns and detect possible service corruption, overload or misuse. Conceptually, the system combines monitoring data, predictive modelling, anomaly detection and operational alerting to support service continuity. The expected impact is improved resilience of e-government platforms, lower risk of disruption, better targeting of technical interventions and reduced operational costs. By moving from reactive maintenance to predictive monitoring, the initiative can strengthen the dependability of digital administration and improve the user experience for citizens relying on online public services.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,F06_HIGH_VOLUME_SCALABILITY_BOTTLENECK,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-197,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00192,EU_PSTW_202609_00192,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,La Poste,Transport,The operational problem is that uncertain arrival times reduce loading-bay utilisation and force logistics operators to make costly rescheduling decisions.,Prediction supply of trucks - Machine Learning to predict truck scheduling,La Poste; Central-Government; Transport; France,"La Poste’s truck-scheduling use case addresses the difficulty of managing loading operations when incoming trucks arrive early, on time or late because of congestion and other disruptions.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"La Poste’s truck-scheduling use case addresses the difficulty of managing loading operations when incoming trucks arrive early, on time or late because of congestion and other disruptions. The operational problem is that uncertain arrival times reduce loading-bay utilisation and force logistics operators to make costly rescheduling decisions. The solution combines a machine-learning classifier with an optimisation model: the classifier estimates the probability that each truck will arrive early, on time or late, and these probabilities are then used to proactively adjust loading slots. At a conceptual level, the system links real-time truck-location information, historical traffic data, predictive classification and mathematical rescheduling. The expected impact is more efficient loading-bay management, lower disruption costs and better transport-flow optimisation. Research on the case reported that the combined predictive and proactive approach reduced rescheduling costs by around 42% compared with a baseline schedule without real-time arrival predictions. This indicates that predictive information can materially improve operational planning when integrated with optimisation rather than used only as a passive forecast.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-198,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00193,EU_PSTW_202609_00193,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""social_nonprofit""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Bibliothèque nationale de Luxembourg,General services.,"The operational challenge addressed here concerns the conversion of scanned or image-based materials into structured, searchable and better-organised digital resources.",a-z.lu to digitise text documents for Luxembourg's National Library,Bibliothèque nationale de Luxembourg; Central-Government; General services.; Luxembourg,"The Bibliothèque nationale de Luxembourg implemented this use case to improve the accessibility, usability and long-term management of textual documents held in its collections. The implementation uses optical character recognition as a foundation for transforming scanned documents into machine-readable text, followed by correction processes to improve the reliability of the extracted content.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Bibliothèque nationale de Luxembourg implemented this use case to improve the accessibility, usability and long-term management of textual documents held in its collections. National libraries are responsible not only for preserving documentary heritage, but also for making it discoverable and usable for researchers, public institutions and citizens. The operational challenge addressed here concerns the conversion of scanned or image-based materials into structured, searchable and better-organised digital resources. The objective is to enhance the quality of digitised content by correcting errors, identifying meaningful information within texts and improving the organisation of associated image content. The implementation uses optical character recognition as a foundation for transforming scanned documents into machine-readable text, followed by correction processes to improve the reliability of the extracted content. Entity extraction is then applied to identify relevant names, places, organisations or other significant references within the material, while image-content organisation supports better structuring and retrieval of visual components. Together, these techniques make the digitised collections easier to search, classify and reuse. The impact is a stronger digital library service, improved discoverability of national documentary resources, higher-quality metadata and text outputs, and better support for research and public access. Over time, the approach contributes to more scalable digitisation workflows and strengthens the National Library’s capacity to preserve and disseminate Luxembourg’s cultural and documentary heritage.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-199,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00194,EU_PSTW_202609_00194,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""social_nonprofit""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City Municipality of Kranj,R&D general public services.,UNKNOWN,Kranj Digital Twin - Representation of the city digitally,City Municipality of Kranj; Local Government; R&D general public services.; Slovenia,"The City Municipality of Kranj launched this use case in 2021 to explore how a residential district could be managed through more integrated, data-informed urban services.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The City Municipality of Kranj launched this use case in 2021 to explore how a residential district could be managed through more integrated, data-informed urban services. The pilot focused on Mlaka pri Kranju and was carried out with a consortium of local companies, including RIKO d.o.o., 3fs d.o.o., Iskraemeco d.d., Komunala Kranj d.o.o., Domplan d.o.o., Elektro Gorenjska d.d. and Vigred d.o.o. The main operational need was to obtain a clearer, more timely picture of how neighbourhood infrastructure performs, particularly in relation to utilities, environmental conditions, traffic and connectivity. Rather than managing these domains separately, the municipality aimed to create a smart-neighbourhood model that could later be extended to other parts of the city. The technological implementation centres on a digital twin approach, combining data from smart electricity, gas and water meters with environmental and traffic sensors, supported by a telecommunications backbone. These inputs are collected, visualised and analysed to create a digital representation of selected neighbourhood functions and infrastructure conditions. The resulting model can support monitoring, planning and service coordination at local level. Its value lies in giving the municipality and service providers a more coherent operational view, improving evidence for infrastructure decisions, enabling future scalability across Kranj, and establishing a practical foundation for broader smart-city development.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-200,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00195,EU_PSTW_202609_00195,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Ministry of Finance,R&D general public services.,"The underlying challenge was that services were often organised in silos, making it harder for citizens and businesses to receive timely support aligned with real-life situations and harder for authorities to allocate services in a coordinated way. The anticipated impact included better cross-service coordination, less fragmented service supply and stronger conditions for ethically functioning digital support in government.",AuroraAI - Ministry of Finance,Ministry of Finance; Central-Government; R&D general public services.; Finland,"The solution was implemented through a decentralised network architecture designed to connect service providers and allocate services more effectively based on identified needs. At a conceptual level, the model combined distributed data use, service-matching logic and ethical-governance principles, with responsible organisations coordinating the application of the model to selected life events.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"AuroraAI was launched in Finland as a national service model intended to support more human-centric public services and more informed public-sector management. The underlying challenge was that services were often organised in silos, making it harder for citizens and businesses to receive timely support aligned with real-life situations and harder for authorities to allocate services in a coordinated way. AuroraAI sought to respond to this by creating a “well-being snapshot” and linking it to service provision across different life events, with the objective of improving relevance, timing and coordination in public support. The stakeholders included citizens, companies, responsible authorities and public-service organisations, with the broader aim of reshaping how government worked across institutional boundaries. The solution was implemented through a decentralised network architecture designed to connect service providers and allocate services more effectively based on identified needs. At a conceptual level, the model combined distributed data use, service-matching logic and ethical-governance principles, with responsible organisations coordinating the application of the model to selected life events. This was intended to enable knowledge-based management as well as more timely support for end users. The anticipated impact included better cross-service coordination, less fragmented service supply and stronger conditions for ethically functioning digital support in government. Although the service model is no longer in use, AuroraAI remains significant as an ambitious attempt to redesign public services around life events, wellbeing and coordinated digital governance.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,NOT_IN_USE,미사용,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-201,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00196,EU_PSTW_202609_00196,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Danish Business Authority,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.",UNKNOWN,DBA - Intelligent Control Platform,"Danish Business Authority; Central-Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Denmark",UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Danish Business Authority developed the Intelligent Control Platform to strengthen how public-sector control, supervision and decision-support processes are carried out in areas where large volumes of administrative and operational data must be continuously assessed. The research unit behind the initiative was established in 2017, when the Authority put its first two models into production and integrated them directly with internal control systems. The purpose was to move beyond static checks and enable more dynamic monitoring, where changes in data could be evaluated quickly against defined thresholds and used to support operational decisions. The platform has since evolved through several generations and now includes around 30 models in production. Technically, it combines machine-learning models with internal control-system data, allowing new information to be assessed as it becomes available. The Authority is also working with real-time graph machine learning and fraud-detection methods, supported by a data-platform architecture designed to handle continuous analysis and model deployment. The system therefore functions as an intelligent layer over existing control processes, identifying patterns, risks or threshold breaches that may require attention. Its impact is a more scalable and data-driven approach to supervision, improved capacity to detect irregularities, faster interpretation of changing datasets, and better support for staff responsible for control activities and fraud prevention.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-202,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00197,EU_PSTW_202609_00197,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Civocracy,R&D general public services.,UNKNOWN,Civocracy - Online Platform,Civocracy; Non-governmental; R&D general public services.; Germany,"The use case addresses a recurring challenge in public consultation: traditional participation processes can be fragmented, limited in reach and difficult to analyse systematically once large volumes of feedback are collected.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Civocracy provides public authorities with an online participation environment designed to improve dialogue between citizens, businesses, civil-society organisations and governments. The use case addresses a recurring challenge in public consultation: traditional participation processes can be fragmented, limited in reach and difficult to analyse systematically once large volumes of feedback are collected. By offering a structured digital space for discussion, debate and idea generation, the platform aims to make consultation more transparent, inclusive and continuous. It has been used by cities in Germany, the Netherlands, Belgium and France, supporting local authorities that want to involve communities in policy development, urban planning or service improvement. Its implementation combines online discussion-board functionality with tools for crowdsourcing ideas, tracking participation, producing statistics and monitoring the impact of engagement activities in real time. Data analytics and machine-learning capabilities support the analysis of group sentiment and help public bodies understand how participants respond to proposals or policy themes. This makes consultation outputs easier to interpret and compare, particularly where feedback is broad or diverse. The platform’s main benefits include wider participation, clearer evidence from citizen input, more transparent follow-up on public discussions and stronger trust-building between institutions and communities. For administrations, it also creates a reusable and scalable channel for managing participatory processes across multiple policy areas.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-203,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00198,EU_PSTW_202609_00198,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Helsinki, Skansa and others","Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.","Traditional property analysis can be fragmented, slow and insufficiently sensitive to changes in urban environments.",Chaos - Analysis of multiple data sources to forecast property market performance,"Helsinki, Skansa and others; Private sector; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Finland",UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"CHAOS provides AI-driven location intelligence for the real-estate sector by analysing multiple data sources to forecast property-market performance and generate actionable insights. The business problem is that investors, property developers, cities and retail operators need to understand how neighbourhood dynamics, service accessibility, demographics, mobility and local amenities affect real-estate value and urban development potential. Traditional property analysis can be fragmented, slow and insufficiently sensitive to changes in urban environments. CHAOS addresses this by combining data analytics, AI and built-environment expertise to support more informed decision-making. Conceptually, the platform integrates heterogeneous urban and market datasets, models location attractiveness and produces insights that can guide investment, development and planning choices. The expected impact is faster property analysis, more evidence-based investment decisions, improved understanding of local demand and support for more liveable urban development. Recent company information indicates that CHAOS has moved beyond an experimental phase and is scaling its AI-driven location-intelligence platform across the Nordics and DACH region.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-204,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00199,EU_PSTW_202609_00199,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,TeleCare Nord,R&D health.,UNKNOWN,Prediction of heart failure from exacerbations in chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD) patients,TeleCare Nord; Local Government; R&D health.; Denmark,"This use case focuses on reducing avoidable deterioration and hospital admissions among patients with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease and heart failure by identifying warning signs early enough for preventive treatment to begin. The use case therefore involves patients, clinicians and remote-monitoring teams, with the objective of detecting elevated risk before a serious event occurs and enabling earlier, more targeted clinical action.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"This use case focuses on reducing avoidable deterioration and hospital admissions among patients with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease and heart failure by identifying warning signs early enough for preventive treatment to begin. In routine care, exacerbations can escalate quickly, and delayed intervention may result in emergency admission, worsening health status and higher pressure on clinical services. The use case therefore involves patients, clinicians and remote-monitoring teams, with the objective of detecting elevated risk before a serious event occurs and enabling earlier, more targeted clinical action. The solution is implemented through a predictive algorithm that analyses patient-reported measurements such as blood pressure, oxygen saturation and heart rate. At a conceptual level, these physiological data are captured through monitoring processes, assessed for risk patterns associated with exacerbation or heart failure, and translated into alerts that notify healthcare professionals when a patient’s condition appears to be deteriorating. This allows preventive treatment to be initiated before the event fully develops and integrates monitoring with clinical decision support. The expected impact is fewer admissions, better prevention of deterioration and a more proactive model of care for vulnerable patients. More broadly, the use case improves service responsiveness, enhances patient safety and experience, and creates business value through more efficient use of healthcare resources and better continuity of care.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-205,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00200,EU_PSTW_202609_00200,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Sugehus Lillebaelt,R&D health.,"The stakeholders include emergency physicians, nurses, laboratory services and hospital managers, all of whom need better decision support to improve triage, reduce delays and allocate beds and clinical attention more effectively.",Artificial Intelligence for faster and better diagnosis of emergency patients,Sugehus Lillebaelt; Local Government; R&D health.; Denmark,"This use case addresses the operational need for faster, more reliable early assessment of emergency patients, particularly in distinguishing non-critical cases from those requiring urgent hospital care. The solution is implemented through a diagnostic-support tool that analyses blood and urine samples and applies predictive models to identify the 15 most common acute illnesses and infections seen in emergency settings.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"In emergency departments, clinicians must decide quickly whether a patient can be safely discharged or needs immediate admission, often while facing incomplete information, time pressure and high patient volumes. This use case addresses the operational need for faster, more reliable early assessment of emergency patients, particularly in distinguishing non-critical cases from those requiring urgent hospital care. The stakeholders include emergency physicians, nurses, laboratory services and hospital managers, all of whom need better decision support to improve triage, reduce delays and allocate beds and clinical attention more effectively. The objective is to strengthen the initial diagnostic process so that patients receive the right level of care sooner and hospital resources are directed where they are most needed. The solution is implemented through a diagnostic-support tool that analyses blood and urine samples and applies predictive models to identify the 15 most common acute illnesses and infections seen in emergency settings. At a conceptual level, the system combines laboratory data, probabilistic ranking methods and workflow integration to produce a prioritised list of likely diagnoses within 60 minutes of the patient’s arrival. This enables clinicians to incorporate rapid analytical insight into admission and treatment decisions. The expected impact is quicker diagnosis, more accurate triage and improved use of emergency capacity. In practice, the tool supports better patient flow, a more consistent clinical response and greater value through safer, more efficient acute-care delivery.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-206,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00201,EU_PSTW_202609_00201,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Medcom,Hospital services.,This creates an administrative burden that can reduce the time available for direct patient care and increase the risk that urgent findings are not prioritised as quickly as they should be.,Artificial Intelligence in general practice,Medcom; Local Government; Hospital services.; Denmark,"The use case therefore focuses on supporting physicians in general practice by improving the handling of incoming test results and helping surgeries manage clinical information more efficiently. The solution is implemented through algorithms that automatically process selected test results, sort blood-test outputs and flag results requiring immediate physician attention.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"General practitioners routinely receive large volumes of clinical information, including laboratory results, many of which do not require immediate medical action but still demand time and attention to review. This creates an administrative burden that can reduce the time available for direct patient care and increase the risk that urgent findings are not prioritised as quickly as they should be. The use case therefore focuses on supporting physicians in general practice by improving the handling of incoming test results and helping surgeries manage clinical information more efficiently. The stakeholders include GPs, practice staff and patients, with the objective of reducing routine workload, sharpening clinical prioritisation and freeing up professional capacity for treatment and patient interaction. The solution is implemented through algorithms that automatically process selected test results, sort blood-test outputs and flag results requiring immediate physician attention. At a conceptual level, the system uses classification and prioritisation techniques applied to structured clinical data, embedded within the practice’s existing information flows so that incoming results can be routed, filtered and escalated appropriately. This means routine or low-risk results can be handled more efficiently, while urgent cases are surfaced promptly for review. The impact is expected to include lower administrative effort, quicker response to clinically significant findings and better use of time in primary care. More broadly, the approach improves reliability, enhances workflow efficiency and strengthens the overall quality and responsiveness of general-practice services.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-207,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00202,EU_PSTW_202609_00202,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Regionshospitalet Randers,Hospital services.,"Without a current picture of capacity, operational decisions can become reactive, leading to bottlenecks, inefficient resource allocation and pressure on both patients and staff. In operational and business terms, the approach increases scalability, supports more reliable decision-making and enhances the patient experience by reducing avoidable delays and improving the coordination of care delivery.",Real-time predictions and essential information for clinicians,Regionshospitalet Randers; Local Government; Hospital services.; Denmark,"Hospitals need timely and accurate information on ward occupancy, discharges and likely patient returns in order to plan staffing, organise patient pathways and make the best possible use of available beds. This use case addresses that challenge by equipping health professionals with real-time insight into hospital capacity and expected patient movement.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Hospitals need timely and accurate information on ward occupancy, discharges and likely patient returns in order to plan staffing, organise patient pathways and make the best possible use of available beds. Without a current picture of capacity, operational decisions can become reactive, leading to bottlenecks, inefficient resource allocation and pressure on both patients and staff. This use case addresses that challenge by equipping health professionals with real-time insight into hospital capacity and expected patient movement. The stakeholders include ward managers, clinicians, discharge planners and hospital operations teams, with the objective of improving day-to-day planning and supporting smoother patient flow across the organisation. The solution is implemented through predictive models that estimate bed-occupancy rates, the expected number of discharges and the risk of patient readmission. At a conceptual level, the system combines hospital activity data with forecasting techniques to generate a live operational view of capacity, which can be integrated into existing planning and coordination processes. This allows staff to anticipate pressure points rather than simply react to them once they occur. The expected impact is better use of hospital capacity, more efficient organisation of patient pathways and improved staff planning. In operational and business terms, the approach increases scalability, supports more reliable decision-making and enhances the patient experience by reducing avoidable delays and improving the coordination of care delivery.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION|S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-208,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00203,EU_PSTW_202609_00203,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Odense Universitetshospital,R&D health.,"The expected impact is more precise diagnosis-related decision-making, fewer unnecessary operations and a better balance between clinical benefit and treatment burden.",Better diagnosis of prostate cancer,Odense Universitetshospital; Local Government; R&D health.; Denmark,This use case addresses the clinical and operational challenge of determining which patients genuinely need intervention and which can be managed more conservatively without compromising outcomes. The solution is implemented through a risk-assessment model trained on historical disease patterns and clinical outcome data to estimate the likelihood that the cancer will spread from the prostate.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Prostate cancer is frequently identified at an early stage, when the disease has not yet spread beyond the prostate, yet many patients still undergo surgery that may later prove unnecessary and can cause complications or long-term discomfort. This use case addresses the clinical and operational challenge of determining which patients genuinely need intervention and which can be managed more conservatively without compromising outcomes. The stakeholders include urologists, oncologists, multidisciplinary cancer teams and patients, with the objective of improving treatment decisions, reducing overtreatment and ensuring that surgery is reserved for cases where the risk of progression is sufficiently high. The solution is implemented through a risk-assessment model trained on historical disease patterns and clinical outcome data to estimate the likelihood that the cancer will spread from the prostate. At a conceptual level, the system analyses patterns in previous patient trajectories and produces an individualised risk estimate that can be incorporated into medical assessment and treatment planning. This supports clinicians in distinguishing patients who may require surgery to prevent metastases from those who may safely avoid surgical treatment. The expected impact is more precise diagnosis-related decision-making, fewer unnecessary operations and a better balance between clinical benefit and treatment burden. In practical terms, the use case improves patient experience, supports more efficient use of surgical resources and creates value through more personalised and proportionate cancer care.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-209,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00204,EU_PSTW_202609_00204,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Rigshospitalet,R&D health.,"A major operational challenge is to identify which patients are at greater risk and therefore need more intensive treatment or closer monitoring, and which may be managed through less demanding pathways without compromising outcomes.",Artificial Intelligence for better planning of cancer treatments,Rigshospitalet; Local Government; R&D health.; Denmark,This use case focuses on stratifying cancer patients into meaningful risk groups so that care plans can be better aligned with individual needs and clinical resources can be deployed more effectively. The solution is implemented through predictive models that divide patients into high-risk and low-risk groups on the basis of relevant clinical data and disease characteristics.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Cancer services must balance clinical complexity, finite resources and the need to tailor treatment to the individual patient’s likely trajectory. A major operational challenge is to identify which patients are at greater risk and therefore need more intensive treatment or closer monitoring, and which may be managed through less demanding pathways without compromising outcomes. This use case focuses on stratifying cancer patients into meaningful risk groups so that care plans can be better aligned with individual needs and clinical resources can be deployed more effectively. The stakeholders include oncologists, cancer-care teams, hospital planners and patients, with the objective of supporting more personalised treatment planning and improving the overall efficiency of care delivery. The solution is implemented through predictive models that divide patients into high-risk and low-risk groups on the basis of relevant clinical data and disease characteristics. At a conceptual level, the system combines risk stratification, pattern recognition and decision-support integration so that treatment pathways can be adapted according to the patient’s expected level of need. This enables clinicians to supplement their judgement with structured analytical insight when planning therapies and follow-up. The expected impact is better adaptation of treatment plans, improved use of healthcare resources and more consistent targeting of intensive care to those most likely to benefit. More broadly, the approach supports scalability, strengthens clinical precision and enhances patient value through more individualised cancer treatment.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-210,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00205,EU_PSTW_202609_00205,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,West Zealand Region,R&D health.,UNKNOWN,Artificial Intelligence to prevent complications after bowel cancer surgery,West Zealand Region; Local Government; R&D health.; Denmark,"The use case therefore addresses the challenge of improving risk assessment in bowel cancer pathways, with the aim of reducing complications, mortality and recurrence. The solution is implemented through a predictive model trained on health-registry data relating to bowel cancer patients and embedded in a software module for clinical use.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Bowel cancer surgery carries substantial risk: around one in four patients develops complications after the operation, and roughly one in three experiences relapse. This creates a pressing need for earlier identification of patients who are most vulnerable to poor outcomes so that treatment plans can be adjusted before surgery and postoperative care can be better targeted. The use case therefore addresses the challenge of improving risk assessment in bowel cancer pathways, with the aim of reducing complications, mortality and recurrence. The stakeholders include surgeons, oncologists, perioperative teams and patients, all of whom benefit from more precise information when deciding how treatment and follow-up should be organised. The solution is implemented through a predictive model trained on health-registry data relating to bowel cancer patients and embedded in a software module for clinical use. At a conceptual level, the system analyses individual patient data to estimate the risk of death, complications, readmission, re-operation and relapse, providing structured support for preoperative and postoperative planning. This allows clinicians to identify higher-risk cases earlier and adapt treatment plans accordingly. The expected impact is more proactive care, fewer preventable adverse outcomes and stronger support for personalised surgical pathways. In practical and economic terms, the approach can improve patient safety, reduce avoidable readmissions and complications, and generate business value through better resource use and more efficient management of complex cancer care.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-211,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00206,EU_PSTW_202609_00206,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Rigshospitalet,Hospital services.,"The operational challenge is therefore to maintain diagnostic image quality while reducing the radiation exposure faced by patients, particularly when repeated scans may be required over time.",Reduction of radiation dose in relation to scans used in cancer treatment,Rigshospitalet; Local Government; Hospital services.; Denmark,"The solution is implemented through image-processing models that work on scans acquired using a reduced radiation dose. At a conceptual level, the system addresses the increased noise created by lower-dose imaging by applying denoising and reconstruction techniques that enhance image clarity sufficiently for diagnostic use.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"PET imaging is an important tool in cancer diagnosis and treatment planning, but it requires the injection of a radioactive tracer, and higher tracer doses generally produce clearer images. The operational challenge is therefore to maintain diagnostic image quality while reducing the radiation exposure faced by patients, particularly when repeated scans may be required over time. The stakeholders include radiologists, nuclear-medicine specialists, oncology teams and patients, with the objective of making imaging safer and more cost-effective without undermining its clinical usefulness. The solution is implemented through image-processing models that work on scans acquired using a reduced radiation dose. At a conceptual level, the system addresses the increased noise created by lower-dose imaging by applying denoising and reconstruction techniques that enhance image clarity sufficiently for diagnostic use. This allows the scan to retain its practical value while lowering the quantity of tracer administered. The expected impact is a reduction in radiation exposure and, consequently, a lower long-term risk associated with imaging, alongside savings linked to reduced use of radioactive tracer. In broader terms, the use case improves patient safety, supports more sustainable imaging practice and enhances service efficiency by preserving diagnostic quality while lowering both clinical risk and material cost.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-212,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00207,EU_PSTW_202609_00207,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Aalborg,Public health services.,"In municipal rehabilitation services, one recurring challenge is that citizens who could benefit from physical exercise or preventive support are not always identified or referred at the most appropriate time.",AI-Rehabilitering (AIR) - Intelligent rehabilitation and targeted healthcare services for citizens,Municipality of Aalborg; Local Government; Public health services.; Denmark,"This can delay intervention, reduce the likelihood of successful outcomes and limit the efficient use of rehabilitation resources, assistive aids and fall-prevention measures. The AIR use case, anchored in the Municipality of Aalborg, addresses this problem by supporting case workers who must decide which rehabilitation pathway best matches an individual citizen’s needs and prospects.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"In municipal rehabilitation services, one recurring challenge is that citizens who could benefit from physical exercise or preventive support are not always identified or referred at the most appropriate time. This can delay intervention, reduce the likelihood of successful outcomes and limit the efficient use of rehabilitation resources, assistive aids and fall-prevention measures. The AIR use case, anchored in the Municipality of Aalborg, addresses this problem by supporting case workers who must decide which rehabilitation pathway best matches an individual citizen’s needs and prospects. Its objective is to improve the consistency and timing of referrals, strengthen professional judgement and ensure that citizens are directed towards the course of action most likely to deliver benefit. The solution is implemented as a decision-support tool built on statistical modelling and predictive analytics. At a conceptual level, it analyses citizen-level data to estimate current eligibility for a rehabilitation course, the likelihood of completing it successfully and the expected level of benefit from participation. These outputs are designed to complement, rather than replace, the case worker’s assessment and can also support decisions on specific interventions, assistive-device use and fall prevention. The expected impact is more targeted rehabilitation, earlier and better-informed intervention and improved allocation of municipal care resources. In practice, this can enhance service quality, increase consistency in decision-making and create value through more efficient and personalised support for citizens.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-213,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00208,EU_PSTW_202609_00208,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Esbjerg Kommune,General public services n.e.c.,"This variation creates uneven workloads and can slow down decision-making, particularly where straightforward cases consume time that could otherwise be devoted to more complex assessments.",Faster case processing when granting cleaning services using new technology,Esbjerg Kommune; Local Government; General public services n.e.c.; Denmark,"The purpose of this use case is therefore to improve the quality and speed of case processing for citizens while releasing capacity for staff to focus on cases requiring greater professional attention. The solution is implemented through a decision-support tool that uses structured data on the citizen’s condition, housing situation and possible cohabitants to inform recommendations on eligibility for cleaning services.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Assessing whether a citizen should receive municipal cleaning assistance can vary considerably in complexity, as case workers must evaluate individual need on the basis of health, living conditions and household circumstances. This variation creates uneven workloads and can slow down decision-making, particularly where straightforward cases consume time that could otherwise be devoted to more complex assessments. The purpose of this use case is therefore to improve the quality and speed of case processing for citizens while releasing capacity for staff to focus on cases requiring greater professional attention. The stakeholders include municipal case workers, social-care administrators and citizens applying for support, all of whom benefit from a more consistent and efficient assessment process. The solution is implemented through a decision-support tool that uses structured data on the citizen’s condition, housing situation and possible cohabitants to inform recommendations on eligibility for cleaning services. At a conceptual level, the system applies classification and pattern-recognition techniques to comparable historical cases and integrates the resulting output into the case worker’s existing workflow. This allows routine applications to be assessed more efficiently while preserving human oversight in the final decision. The expected impact is shorter processing times, improved consistency in eligibility assessments and better use of staff resources. More broadly, the approach strengthens service quality, improves the citizen experience and supports a more scalable model for handling municipal care applications.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-214,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00209,EU_PSTW_202609_00209,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Norddjurs Municipality,General services.,This enables intake tasks to be handled with greater speed and consistency than purely manual processing.,Intelligent sorting and filing of mail to provide citizens with faster case processing,Norddjurs Municipality; Local Government; General services.; Denmark,"This use case addresses that operational bottleneck by aiming to streamline intake management and reduce the manual burden associated with handling incoming correspondence. The solution is implemented through an automated mail-handling system that classifies incoming digital mail and email, distributes it to the relevant recipients and files it in the appropriate location.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Norddjurs Municipality receives substantial volumes of digital mail and emails every day through a central inbox, and each message must be sorted, routed to the correct case worker and filed appropriately before any substantive action can begin. These are routine but resource-intensive administrative tasks that consume staff time and can delay the start of case processing for citizens. This use case addresses that operational bottleneck by aiming to streamline intake management and reduce the manual burden associated with handling incoming correspondence. The stakeholders include administrative staff, case workers and citizens, with the objective of accelerating distribution, improving filing accuracy and ensuring that enquiries reach the right destination more quickly. The solution is implemented through an automated mail-handling system that classifies incoming digital mail and email, distributes it to the relevant recipients and files it in the appropriate location. At a conceptual level, the system combines text classification, routing logic and document-management integration to interpret message content and connect it with the municipality’s internal case-processing structure. This enables intake tasks to be handled with greater speed and consistency than purely manual processing. The expected impact is reduced administrative workload, quicker initiation of citizen cases and improved reliability in document handling. In practical terms, the use case supports faster case processing, better internal efficiency and a more scalable way to manage growing communication volumes in municipal administration.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-215,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00210,EU_PSTW_202609_00210,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Kobenhavns Kommune,Housing development.,"This creates delays for applicants and additional workload for case workers, who must spend time identifying errors, requesting further documentation and comparing the case with similar precedents. The expected impact is shorter case-processing times, fewer avoidable delays caused by incorrect documentation and greater consistency in planning decisions.",Shorter response time to construction applications and help submitting correct documents,Kobenhavns Kommune; Local Government; Housing development.; Denmark,"The use case therefore focuses on improving the efficiency and consistency of construction-application handling, with the objective of reducing response times and supporting better-quality submissions. The solution is implemented as a decision-support tool that screens applications for likely errors and retrieves relevant decisions from earlier similar cases.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Municipal construction applications often take a long time to process, not only because of the complexity of planning and compliance checks, but also because many submissions are incomplete or incorrectly prepared from the outset. This creates delays for applicants and additional workload for case workers, who must spend time identifying errors, requesting further documentation and comparing the case with similar precedents. The use case therefore focuses on improving the efficiency and consistency of construction-application handling, with the objective of reducing response times and supporting better-quality submissions. The stakeholders include applicants, municipal planning officers and case workers, all of whom benefit when straightforward errors can be identified early and previous comparable decisions are easier to consult. The solution is implemented as a decision-support tool that screens applications for likely errors and retrieves relevant decisions from earlier similar cases. At a conceptual level, the system uses document analysis and case-comparison techniques to highlight missing or problematic elements and present precedent information within the case worker’s assessment process. This helps staff make more informed and consistent decisions while also improving the quality of the material under review. The expected impact is shorter case-processing times, fewer avoidable delays caused by incorrect documentation and greater consistency in planning decisions. More broadly, the approach improves the applicant experience, reduces administrative effort and creates value through more efficient municipal permitting processes.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F08_ERROR_COMPLIANCE_RISK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-216,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00211,EU_PSTW_202609_00211,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Frederiksberg Municipality,Unemployment.,"Deciding whether an unemployed citizen should be subject to an economic sanction is a difficult and sensitive task, as case workers must consider not only the triggering event, such as absence from an interview or activation offer, but also whether a sanction is likely to have the intended effect on the person’s availability for work.",Correct and uniform case management when sanctioning unemployed citizens,Frederiksberg Municipality; Local Government; Unemployment.; Denmark,This use case addresses the need for more consistent and evidence-informed decision-making in employment services. The solution is implemented through a decision-support tool that analyses factors associated with positive or negative effects on a citizen’s likelihood of becoming more available for employment.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Deciding whether an unemployed citizen should be subject to an economic sanction is a difficult and sensitive task, as case workers must consider not only the triggering event, such as absence from an interview or activation offer, but also whether a sanction is likely to have the intended effect on the person’s availability for work. In practice, these judgements are not always applied uniformly, which can lead to inconsistency in case handling and uncertainty for both staff and citizens. This use case addresses the need for more consistent and evidence-informed decision-making in employment services. The stakeholders include case workers, employment-service managers and unemployed citizens, with the objective of supporting fairer and more uniform assessments while helping staff distinguish between cases in which sanctions may be effective and those in which they may not. The solution is implemented through a decision-support tool that analyses factors associated with positive or negative effects on a citizen’s likelihood of becoming more available for employment. At a conceptual level, the system evaluates adverse events and broader citizen characteristics to support judgements on whether a sanction should be triggered. These analytical outputs are integrated into the case worker’s decision process rather than replacing professional discretion. The expected impact is more consistent case management, better-targeted use of sanctions and improved transparency in decision-making. In operational terms, the approach enhances fairness, reliability and resource efficiency across municipal employment services.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-217,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00212,EU_PSTW_202609_00212,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Odense Kommune,Unemployment.,"Employment services often need to decide which programme or activity is most likely to help a job-ready unemployed citizen return to work quickly, yet this is difficult to do consistently without clear evidence on what interventions deliver the strongest results for different profiles. The challenge is not simply to offer support, but to direct each individual towards the activities most likely to shorten unemployment and improve outcomes.",Targeted employment programmes for unemployed citizens,Odense Kommune; Local Government; Unemployment.; Denmark,This use case responds to that need by helping case workers design more targeted employment programmes for unemployed citizens. The solution is implemented as a decision-support tool that uses individual citizen information to propose specific activities expected to reduce the likely period of unemployment.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Employment services often need to decide which programme or activity is most likely to help a job-ready unemployed citizen return to work quickly, yet this is difficult to do consistently without clear evidence on what interventions deliver the strongest results for different profiles. The challenge is not simply to offer support, but to direct each individual towards the activities most likely to shorten unemployment and improve outcomes. This use case responds to that need by helping case workers design more targeted employment programmes for unemployed citizens. The stakeholders include employment advisers, case workers, programme managers and citizens seeking work, with the objective of reducing unemployment duration and improving the relevance and benefit of employment services. The solution is implemented as a decision-support tool that uses individual citizen information to propose specific activities expected to reduce the likely period of unemployment. At a conceptual level, the system combines predictive modelling with recommendation logic, drawing on patterns from previous programme outcomes and citizen characteristics to identify interventions with the greatest expected effect. These suggestions are then incorporated into the case worker’s planning process. The expected impact is more personalised employment support, faster transitions back into work and better use of programme resources. More broadly, the approach strengthens the effectiveness of labour-market services, improves the citizen experience and creates value by aligning support measures more closely with individual needs and likely outcomes.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-218,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00213,EU_PSTW_202609_00213,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Kobenhavns Kommune,Unemployment.,"Even where employers are actively seeking staff, matching unemployed citizens to suitable vacancies can remain difficult because the fit between a person’s experience, transferable skills and available roles is not always obvious from standard job titles or sector boundaries. As a result, case workers may struggle to guide citizens towards realistic opportunities, especially where alternative occupations or industries could offer a viable route back into employment.",Better match between unemployed citizens and companies,Kobenhavns Kommune; Local Government; Unemployment.; Denmark,This use case addresses that challenge by improving how unemployed citizens are matched with company demand and by supporting more targeted job-search strategies. The solution is implemented through a matching tool that compares the professional and personal capabilities described in citizens’ CVs with the requirements listed in current job openings.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Even where employers are actively seeking staff, matching unemployed citizens to suitable vacancies can remain difficult because the fit between a person’s experience, transferable skills and available roles is not always obvious from standard job titles or sector boundaries. As a result, case workers may struggle to guide citizens towards realistic opportunities, especially where alternative occupations or industries could offer a viable route back into employment. This use case addresses that challenge by improving how unemployed citizens are matched with company demand and by supporting more targeted job-search strategies. The stakeholders include case workers, job-ready unemployed citizens and employers, with the objective of increasing the effectiveness of employment programmes and improving the connection between labour supply and labour demand. The solution is implemented through a matching tool that compares the professional and personal capabilities described in citizens’ CVs with the requirements listed in current job openings. At a conceptual level, the system uses skills-matching and recommendation techniques to identify suitable vacancies, including alternatives in other industries or job types that may not have been considered initially. These results support the case worker in guiding the citizen towards a broader and more realistic search strategy. The expected impact is better job matching, a more focused job-search process and improved employment outcomes. In practical terms, the approach enhances service quality, increases the utility of available vacancy data and creates value through more efficient and responsive labour-market intermediation.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-219,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00214,EU_PSTW_202609_00214,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,RSZ/ONSS,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.","Public bodies that handle large volumes of incoming correspondence and forms often face a difficult administrative problem: important information is buried in siloed documents and must be extracted manually before it can be reused in central systems. Smals, as an ICT provider for government, explored this challenge with public-sector partners such as the RSZ/ONSS, where the broader aim was to support data centralisation and reduce the effort required to process incoming material.",NER-based extraction of contract data,"RSZ/ONSS; Private sector; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Belgium","The use case focused on extracting structured information from emails and forms, including names, roles and telephone numbers, so that staff could move faster from unstructured documents to usable records. The solution was implemented through named-entity recognition and related natural-language-processing techniques applied to scanned documents and digital correspondence.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Public bodies that handle large volumes of incoming correspondence and forms often face a difficult administrative problem: important information is buried in siloed documents and must be extracted manually before it can be reused in central systems. Smals, as an ICT provider for government, explored this challenge with public-sector partners such as the RSZ/ONSS, where the broader aim was to support data centralisation and reduce the effort required to process incoming material. The use case focused on extracting structured information from emails and forms, including names, roles and telephone numbers, so that staff could move faster from unstructured documents to usable records. The stakeholders include administrative teams, ICT specialists and the government bodies seeking to consolidate data sources, with the objective of improving efficiency while retaining sufficient control over data quality. The solution was implemented through named-entity recognition and related natural-language-processing techniques applied to scanned documents and digital correspondence. At a conceptual level, the system first converts paper-based material into machine-readable text through scanning and OCR, then applies entity-extraction models to identify specific data fields. The proof-of-concept work showed that highly structured information can be captured with reasonable effectiveness, while context-dependent elements such as job functions remain more difficult to interpret reliably. The impact lies in reduced manual effort and better support for mailroom and intake automation, but also in a clearer understanding of the limits of current extraction tools, including the need for human validation where full accuracy cannot be guaranteed.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK|F06_HIGH_VOLUME_SCALABILITY_BOTTLENECK,S3_DOCUMENT_INTELLIGENCE,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-220,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00215,EU_PSTW_202609_00215,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Roads and Traffic Agency of the Flemish Government,Other industries,UNKNOWN,Creation and management of semantic OTL relationships,Roads and Traffic Agency of the Flemish Government; Regional Government; Other industries; Belgium,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Flemish Roads and Traffic Agency’s semantic OTL project supports its transition towards data-driven infrastructure management. The operational problem is that road infrastructure assets, including roads, cycle paths, tunnels, bridges and related equipment, generate information across design, construction, maintenance and replacement phases, but this information must be standardised to remain usable throughout the asset lifecycle. The project aims to develop a tool for creating and managing semantic relationships in the Object Type Library, allowing asset data to be collected once, structured consistently and exchanged across models, databases and software systems. Its implementation builds on BIM and semantic data-management methods, defining relationships between infrastructure object types so that asset information can be shared uniformly among project partners. The expected impact is improved data quality, more efficient collaboration, reduced duplication and stronger lifecycle management of public infrastructure. By embedding semantic standards into asset management, AWV can strengthen interoperability and make infrastructure information more reliable for planning, maintenance and operational decisions.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-221,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00216,EU_PSTW_202609_00216,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Flemish Agency for Persons with Disabilities,Health n.e.c.,"The service problem is that several comparable services currently operate side by side, creating fragmentation and confusion for users, while existing technical and organisational arrangements do not yet ensure access anytime and anywhere. Its public value lies in treating language accessibility as an essential service rather than a fragmented accommodation.",Remote interpreting services - Communicating in Flemish sign language,Flemish Agency for Persons with Disabilities; Regional Government; Health n.e.c.; Belgium,"The expected impact is improved accessibility, lower organisational costs through consolidation, greater use of remote interpreting and stronger inclusion of deaf users in public, professional and personal communication.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The remote interpreting services project aims to improve communication between deaf and hearing people in Flanders by making Flemish Sign Language interpretation available more flexibly and consistently. The service problem is that several comparable services currently operate side by side, creating fragmentation and confusion for users, while existing technical and organisational arrangements do not yet ensure access anytime and anywhere. The project seeks to harmonise and scale remote interpreting so that deaf users can contact everyone by telephone, including emergency services, and can also be contacted by others such as colleagues or doctors in their preferred communication mode. Implementation requires innovation in hardware, software, service organisation, privacy protection and interoperability between existing systems. Conceptually, the solution combines video-remote interpreting, user authentication, service-routing, privacy safeguards and platform harmonisation. The expected impact is improved accessibility, lower organisational costs through consolidation, greater use of remote interpreting and stronger inclusion of deaf users in public, professional and personal communication. Its public value lies in treating language accessibility as an essential service rather than a fragmented accommodation.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-222,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00217,EU_PSTW_202609_00217,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Flanders Research Institute for Agriculture, Fisheries and Food (ILVO)","Agriculture, forestry, fishing and hunting","The operational challenge is that these data streams are not processed quickly enough, meaning potentially valuable signals for research, testing and operational improvement can be missed.",AI platform for agri-food applications from field to fork,"Flanders Research Institute for Agriculture, Fisheries and Food (ILVO); Academic-Research; Agriculture, forestry, fishing and hunting; Belgium","The use case therefore focuses on helping ILVO researchers make better use of the information already being produced, while reducing the barriers that prevent advanced data methods from being used routinely in day-to-day work. At a conceptual level, the platform is intended to connect heterogeneous data flows, support data science and model deployment, and provide functionalities that simplify the practical use of advanced analytics in research workflows.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Across ILVO’s living labs, food pilot, greenhouses, laboratories and test environments, large volumes of data are generated every day from IoT sensors, machine-mounted cameras, drones, satellites and analytical equipment. The operational challenge is that these data streams are not processed quickly enough, meaning potentially valuable signals for research, testing and operational improvement can be missed. The use case therefore focuses on helping ILVO researchers make better use of the information already being produced, while reducing the barriers that prevent advanced data methods from being used routinely in day-to-day work. Internal stakeholders across research and innovation teams were involved in defining the needs, with the objective of creating a platform that broadens access to data-driven methods and improves the speed and consistency of analysis from field to fork. The solution is being developed as an integrated platform for agri-food data and analytics, designed through a preparatory phase involving user-centred workshops with ILVO stakeholders and supported by consultancy partner Addestino. At a conceptual level, the platform is intended to connect heterogeneous data flows, support data science and model deployment, and provide functionalities that simplify the practical use of advanced analytics in research workflows. The expected impact is faster extraction of useful information, reduced loss of actionable insight and stronger scalability in handling complex agri-food datasets. More broadly, the platform improves research efficiency, supports innovation and creates value by democratising advanced analytical capabilities across ILVO’s operations.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-223,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00218,EU_PSTW_202609_00218,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"DigiPolis, IT provider for the City of Antwerp",Transport,"Digipolis sought to turn street images, which have limited value in raw form, into mobility intelligence for the city, especially in the Smart Zone.",ALTRUIS - Alternative Use of Imaging Systems through Artificial Intelligence,"DigiPolis, IT provider for the City of Antwerp; Local Government; Transport; Belgium","The implementation explored alternative use of imaging systems through artificial intelligence, initially focusing on anonymisation of visual material and then on image recognition and algorithms for mobility insights.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"ALTRUIS was launched to help Antwerp respond to severe mobility pressures by extracting useful traffic and mobility insights from existing camera footage. The operational context was particularly challenging because Antwerp already experienced heavy congestion, while major infrastructure works placed additional pressure on mobility and quality of life. Digipolis sought to turn street images, which have limited value in raw form, into mobility intelligence for the city, especially in the Smart Zone. The implementation explored alternative use of imaging systems through artificial intelligence, initially focusing on anonymisation of visual material and then on image recognition and algorithms for mobility insights. The procurement later combined these phases into a single assignment and shifted the technological scope towards edge AI, meaning that interpretation would occur closer to the camera instead of relying only on cloud or on-premise GPU processing. The expected impact was better understanding of movement patterns, more responsive mobility management and support for smart-city traffic solutions. The case also highlights the importance of anonymisation, privacy-by-design and careful governance when repurposing urban camera infrastructure.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-224,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00219,EU_PSTW_202609_00219,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Department of Agriculture and Fisheries,"Agriculture, forestry, fishing and hunting","Traditionally, supervision of these measures has relied on field spot checks, which are resource-intensive, limited in coverage and often unable to provide rapid insight across large agricultural areas.",Capsat - Information from satellite images for a better agricultural policy in Flanders and Europe,"Department of Agriculture and Fisheries; Regional Government; Agriculture, forestry, fishing and hunting; Belgium","The objective of the use case is therefore to improve follow-up, guidance and control by using remote information that can complement or partially replace manual inspection. The implementation uses high-resolution satellite imagery to map relevant agricultural conditions and detect whether policy measures have been applied correctly and on time.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Capsat was developed to support the Department of Agriculture and Fisheries in Flanders in monitoring compliance with agricultural policy requirements linked to the European Common Agricultural Policy. Farmers receiving direct support must respect several climate and environmentally oriented obligations, including crop diversification, grassland maintenance and the creation of ecological focus areas. Traditionally, supervision of these measures has relied on field spot checks, which are resource-intensive, limited in coverage and often unable to provide rapid insight across large agricultural areas. The objective of the use case is therefore to improve follow-up, guidance and control by using remote information that can complement or partially replace manual inspection. The implementation uses high-resolution satellite imagery to map relevant agricultural conditions and detect whether policy measures have been applied correctly and on time. Image-analysis techniques can help identify crops, land use, grassland status or ecological features, while also supporting faster objectification of crop damage caused by drought or flooding. This creates a more systematic evidence base for both compliance monitoring and farmer support. The expected impact is greater efficiency in controls, broader territorial coverage, faster detection of anomalies or damage, and more objective decision-making. In policy terms, Capsat strengthens the administration’s capacity to implement agricultural rules while supporting sustainable, liveable and competitive farming in Flanders and potentially at wider European scale.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-225,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00220,EU_PSTW_202609_00220,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Flemish Audit Office,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.","The operational problem is that a significant share of audits must be outsourced, often at high cost and with uneven added value, while the Flemish Audit Authority still needs to spend substantial time on quality control when external auditors lack sufficient expertise in public procurement or state aid.",CATE - Continuous Auditing based on Technological Evolution and Data Mining,"Flemish Audit Office; Regional Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Belgium","Conceptually, the system uses data-driven risk analysis, audit-process automation, analytical models and continuous monitoring of subsidised projects to identify where audit attention is most needed. The expected impact is a more efficient and targeted audit function, stronger detection of irregularities, better use of audit capacity and improved protection of public funds.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"CATE was developed to rethink how the Flemish Audit Authority audits projects subsidised through European Structural Funds and Flemish co-financing. The operational problem is that a significant share of audits must be outsourced, often at high cost and with uneven added value, while the Flemish Audit Authority still needs to spend substantial time on quality control when external auditors lack sufficient expertise in public procurement or state aid. The project aims to redesign the audit process methodologically and technologically so that audits can provide greater assurance, lower costs, shorter turnaround times, higher added value and reduced loss of European resources. The implementation is based on continuous auditing, data mining and technological evolution, with an innovation partnership structured in phases from proof of concept to increasingly mature functionality. Conceptually, the system uses data-driven risk analysis, audit-process automation, analytical models and continuous monitoring of subsidised projects to identify where audit attention is most needed. The expected impact is a more efficient and targeted audit function, stronger detection of irregularities, better use of audit capacity and improved protection of public funds.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-226,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00221,EU_PSTW_202609_00221,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Antwerp,Cultural services.,"The City of Antwerp and Digipolis launched SOCRATES to address a growing challenge in cultural participation: citizens are exposed to a broad and fragmented cultural offer, yet it is often difficult to connect individuals with activities and content that genuinely match their interests.",Smart Personalisation of the cultural offer in Antwerp Testing (SOCRATES),City of Antwerp; Local Government; Cultural services.; Belgium,"The solution is implemented through a Digital Experience Platform built around a personal culture profile and enriched by recommendation logic, described as a “wonder algorithm”, that can suggest suitable cultural content. The expected impact is more relevant cultural discovery, better use of digital channels and stronger engagement with Antwerp’s cultural offer.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The City of Antwerp and Digipolis launched SOCRATES to address a growing challenge in cultural participation: citizens are exposed to a broad and fragmented cultural offer, yet it is often difficult to connect individuals with activities and content that genuinely match their interests. The project therefore focuses on whether a more tailored, user-driven cultural experience can increase engagement and improve how residents discover literature, events and other cultural opportunities. The stakeholders include the city, Digipolis, cultural users and the wider digital-services ecosystem around Antwerp’s public platforms, with the objective of testing the feasibility and usefulness of an individual cultural profile that can power more relevant recommendations. The solution is implemented through a Digital Experience Platform built around a personal culture profile and enriched by recommendation logic, described as a “wonder algorithm”, that can suggest suitable cultural content. At a conceptual level, the platform is designed modularly so that it can connect with existing and future architecture, especially Antwerp City Platform as a Service, which already offers reusable backend components. SOCRATES extends this model to application front ends and digital touchpoints, allowing the user’s “digital cultural DNA” to inform personalised suggestions. The expected impact is more relevant cultural discovery, better use of digital channels and stronger engagement with Antwerp’s cultural offer. More broadly, the project creates value by testing a scalable, interoperable model for citizen-centred cultural personalisation.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-227,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00222,EU_PSTW_202609_00222,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Flemish Domestic Governance Agency,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.","Smart Regulation responds to the difficulty of navigating fragmented, divergent and incoherent regulatory information. The Flemish Domestic Governance Agency and Information Flanders aim to create an engine that can process unstructured legal and regulatory sources and provide users with targeted data and insights according to their needs.",Smart regulation - Using Artificial Intelligence to train computers to interpret regulation,"Flemish Domestic Governance Agency; Regional Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Belgium","Regulations are essential for citizens, administrations, businesses and software systems, yet relevant provisions are often spread across basic laws, amendments, additions and corrections, making it hard to retrieve the exact rule that applies to a given situation. The implementation uses artificial intelligence to train computer systems to interpret regulation, extract relevant information and make it available for specific target groups, including people, applications and machines.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Smart Regulation responds to the difficulty of navigating fragmented, divergent and incoherent regulatory information. Regulations are essential for citizens, administrations, businesses and software systems, yet relevant provisions are often spread across basic laws, amendments, additions and corrections, making it hard to retrieve the exact rule that applies to a given situation. The Flemish Domestic Governance Agency and Information Flanders aim to create an engine that can process unstructured legal and regulatory sources and provide users with targeted data and insights according to their needs. The implementation uses artificial intelligence to train computer systems to interpret regulation, extract relevant information and make it available for specific target groups, including people, applications and machines. Conceptually, the solution combines natural language processing, legal text mining, semantic structuring, regulatory knowledge extraction and subscription-based access to structured outputs. The expected impact is faster access to applicable rules, reduced information silos, better reuse of regulatory data and stronger support for digital public services that depend on machine-readable legislation. By turning complex legal texts into more structured and queryable information, the project can improve administrative efficiency and regulatory transparency.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,F04_UNSTRUCTURED_INFORMATION_COMPLEXITY,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-228,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00223,EU_PSTW_202609_00223,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Agency for Roads and Traffic (AWV),Street lighting.,"Turing addresses the challenge of managing motorway lighting in Flanders in a way that balances energy efficiency, climate objectives, road safety and operational workload. The anticipated results include lower energy consumption, stronger contribution to climate targets, maintained or improved road safety, and a significant reduction in manual workload for operators.",Turing - Automatically control Flemish road lighting with local and current data,Agency for Roads and Traffic (AWV); Regional Government; Street lighting.; Belgium,"The Turing use case aims to make lighting control more localised, timely and evidence-based. The use case also demonstrates how existing infrastructure-management systems can be upgraded through data-driven automation.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Turing addresses the challenge of managing motorway lighting in Flanders in a way that balances energy efficiency, climate objectives, road safety and operational workload. The Agency for Roads and Traffic had already adopted a lighting strategy in 2011 in response to the European Union’s objective of reducing energy consumption by 20% by 2020, and the number of illuminated road sections has since fallen by almost half. However, the existing control approach relies mainly on historical traffic intensities and weather forecasts at provincial level, which limits the precision of decisions on whether lighting is necessary for a specific road segment. The Turing use case aims to make lighting control more localised, timely and evidence-based. Its implementation combines rule-based algorithms with machine-learning techniques to integrate internal and external data sources into the current road-lighting control system. Local and real-time information on traffic intensity and weather conditions can be processed to determine more accurately when lighting should be switched on or off. This allows the system to move from broad assumptions towards segment-level operational decisions. The anticipated results include lower energy consumption, stronger contribution to climate targets, maintained or improved road safety, and a significant reduction in manual workload for operators. The use case also demonstrates how existing infrastructure-management systems can be upgraded through data-driven automation.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-229,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00224,EU_PSTW_202609_00224,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Agency for Roads and Traffic (AWV),Transport,UNKNOWN,Elsa - AI-assisted spreading decisions,Agency for Roads and Traffic (AWV); Regional Government; Transport; Belgium,"The expected impact is more uniform decision-making, better road safety, reduced unnecessary salt use, lower environmental burden and stronger support for staff during winter operations.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Elsa addresses the need for more consistent winter-service decisions across Flemish road districts. The Agency for Roads and Traffic is responsible for controlling slippery road conditions on the roads it manages, but spreading decisions depend on local interpretation of real-time measurements, weather forecasts and staff experience. Because expertise varies across districts and road networks do not stop at administrative boundaries, inconsistent spreading policies can affect safety, environmental impact and operational efficiency. Elsa is intended to support district employees in deciding whether, when and how to spread de-icing material. The implementation combines physical rules and machine-learning techniques with data from AWV’s smoothness measurement system, which already integrates real-time measurements from monitoring points and stations with forecasts from the Royal Meteorological Institute. Conceptually, the system assesses road and weather conditions, estimates the risk of slipperiness and recommends the type of spreading material, quantity and timing needed to keep roads ice-free. The expected impact is more uniform decision-making, better road safety, reduced unnecessary salt use, lower environmental burden and stronger support for staff during winter operations.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-230,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00225,EU_PSTW_202609_00225,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Felmish Energy Company (VEB), Agency for Facility Operations (HFB) and Flemish Energy Agency (VEA)",Fuel and energy,UNKNOWN,Innovative functionalities and AI for energy saving in the public sector,"Felmish Energy Company (VEB), Agency for Facility Operations (HFB) and Flemish Energy Agency (VEA); Regional Government; Fuel and energy; Belgium","Within the Terra patrimony and energy database, substantial information already exists on asset characteristics and energy performance, but the challenge lies in using that information actively and systematically to identify savings opportunities, support renewable energy projects and guide long-term property strategy. This use case brings together VEB, Facilities and Services, and VEA, with the objective of improving how public-sector energy data is used to detect inefficiencies faster, plan interventions more effectively and monitor energy-saving measures with greater precision.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Flemish public sector manages a large and diverse portfolio of buildings and infrastructure, yet turning building and energy data into practical action remains a complex task. Within the Terra patrimony and energy database, substantial information already exists on asset characteristics and energy performance, but the challenge lies in using that information actively and systematically to identify savings opportunities, support renewable energy projects and guide long-term property strategy. This use case brings together VEB, Facilities and Services, and VEA, with the objective of improving how public-sector energy data is used to detect inefficiencies faster, plan interventions more effectively and monitor energy-saving measures with greater precision. The solution explores smart data use through advanced analytics, asset analysis and measurement models, while also assessing where algorithmic methods and a Software-as-a-Service model could add value. At a conceptual level, the approach uses the Terra database as the central information layer for identifying energy-saving potential, informing real-estate strategies, supporting the initiation and monitoring of OEPC contracts, and creating synergies with other digital and sustainability initiatives. The expected impact is earlier identification of savings opportunities, better-managed energy programmes and stronger evidence for investment decisions. In business and operational terms, the initiative supports cost reduction, improves the scalability of energy management and increases the long-term value of public assets through more informed and professionally managed interventions.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-231,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00226,EU_PSTW_202609_00226,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Leuven City,Public order and safety n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,Naamsestraat - Reduce night noise through technology and nudging,Leuven City; Local Government; Public order and safety n.e.c.; Belgium,"The use case therefore focuses on making the street more liveable while preserving the vitality of the city centre, involving municipal authorities and a range of local partners with the objective of detecting disturbing noise more accurately, understanding when and where it occurs, and intervening at the right moment. At a conceptual level, the implementation uses automated sound recognition to define and map nuisance, links live detections to behavioural nudges in the street environment, and supports scheduling of interventions through predictive models that identify likely high-disturbance moments.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Naamsestraat in Leuven is a busy transit route for people returning home after a night out, and this regularly generates disturbance for residents through late-night noise. The City of Leuven is addressing this as part of a broader strategy on nightlife nuisance in which preventive and enforcement measures are intended to work together. The use case therefore focuses on making the street more liveable while preserving the vitality of the city centre, involving municipal authorities and a range of local partners with the objective of detecting disturbing noise more accurately, understanding when and where it occurs, and intervening at the right moment. The solution is being developed as a living lab combining real-time data capture, objectified recognition of disturbing night noise, nudging techniques and predictive intervention planning. At a conceptual level, the implementation uses automated sound recognition to define and map nuisance, links live detections to behavioural nudges in the street environment, and supports scheduling of interventions through predictive models that identify likely high-disturbance moments. This creates a dynamic feedback loop between monitoring and action rather than relying solely on retrospective complaint handling. The expected impact is reduced nuisance, more targeted deployment of municipal measures and an improved nighttime environment for residents. More broadly, the initiative enhances urban liveability, supports evidence-based public-space management and creates a scalable model for tackling nightlife-related disturbance.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-232,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00227,EU_PSTW_202609_00227,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Department of Mobility and Public Works (MOW),Environmental protection n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,Clearance of dumped war ammunition in the North Sea,Department of Mobility and Public Works (MOW); Regional Government; Environmental protection n.e.c.; Belgium,"The Flemish government’s Maritime Access department launched this use case to test practical methods for addressing the problem safely and cost-effectively, with the objective of evaluating whether a larger-scale clean-up could be undertaken without creating unacceptable risks for people or the marine environment.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"On the North Sea sandbank De Paardenmarkt near Knokke, around 35,000 tonnes of First World War ammunition, including both conventional and chemical munitions, remain buried beneath the seabed. Although the site is currently regarded as stable, long-term risks persist as corrosion progresses and external factors such as ship strikes could trigger severe pollution, threaten marine safety and constrain coastal and port development. The Flemish government’s Maritime Access department launched this use case to test practical methods for addressing the problem safely and cost-effectively, with the objective of evaluating whether a larger-scale clean-up could be undertaken without creating unacceptable risks for people or the marine environment. The solution is being trialled through a combination of precision detection, environmental monitoring, sediment removal, deepening operations and robotic recovery of buried ammunition. At a conceptual level, the approach integrates seabed sensing, risk monitoring, controlled excavation and remote-handling technologies to locate and retrieve hazardous objects while limiting direct human exposure and minimising ecological disturbance. The expected impact is better understanding of feasible remediation methods, reduced uncertainty around operational risk and stronger preparedness for a full-scale clean-up. In strategic terms, the project supports environmental protection, enhances coastal safety and may unlock future value by reducing obstacles to maritime infrastructure and port development.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-233,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00228,EU_PSTW_202609_00228,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Social Insurance Board,Social protection n.e.c.,"The Social Insurance Board needed a clearer understanding of why citizens were contacting its services and where recurring pain points in service delivery were causing confusion, delays or repeated enquiries. Traditional review methods made it difficult to obtain both a broad statistical overview and a detailed picture of issues emerging across multiple communication channels.",Customer call analysis - Finding patterns in the reasons for customer inquiries,Social Insurance Board; Central-Government; Social protection n.e.c.; Estonia,"This use case therefore focused on helping managers identify patterns in customer concerns more quickly so that services could be improved in a targeted and timely way. The solution was implemented with Feelingstream, which analysed customer contacts across calls, emails and chatbot interactions.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Social Insurance Board needed a clearer understanding of why citizens were contacting its services and where recurring pain points in service delivery were causing confusion, delays or repeated enquiries. Traditional review methods made it difficult to obtain both a broad statistical overview and a detailed picture of issues emerging across multiple communication channels. This use case therefore focused on helping managers identify patterns in customer concerns more quickly so that services could be improved in a targeted and timely way. The pilot involved an information-line manager and two service managers, with the objective of generating insights that would support managerial decision-making and lead to faster, more convenient public services. The solution was implemented with Feelingstream, which analysed customer contacts across calls, emails and chatbot interactions. During the pilot, calls from May to August 2020 were transcribed in Estonian and Russian, enabling the system to detect themes and recurring reasons for contact across language groups. At a conceptual level, the approach combines transcription, multilingual text analysis and cross-channel pattern detection to turn customer-contact data into actionable service intelligence. The expected impact is faster identification of service problems, better prioritisation of improvements and more informed management decisions. In practice, this enhances efficiency, improves the citizen experience and creates value by linking everyday customer interactions directly to service redesign.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-234,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00229,EU_PSTW_202609_00229,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Department of Public Relations of the Ministry of Justice,General public services n.e.c.,The operational challenge was to broaden access to official information without creating unsustainable translation costs or slowing publication processes.,Automatic translation of Riigi Teataja Ametlikud Teadaanded,Department of Public Relations of the Ministry of Justice; Central-Government; General public services n.e.c.; Estonia,"The use case therefore involved the publisher of the official notices service, external technology partners and users who require reliable access to administrative information in a language other than Estonian. The solution is implemented through a neural machine translation system developed with Tilde Eesti OÜ and Inversion OÜ and funded 80% by the European Commission.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Estonia’s Official Gazette introduced automated translation to make website content available in English, responding to the need for greater accessibility for foreign businesses and other international stakeholders while preserving Estonian as the only legally binding language. The operational challenge was to broaden access to official information without creating unsustainable translation costs or slowing publication processes. The use case therefore involved the publisher of the official notices service, external technology partners and users who require reliable access to administrative information in a language other than Estonian. Its objective was to support an open business environment and improve usability for non-Estonian speakers, while maintaining clear legal certainty about which language version is authoritative. The solution is implemented through a neural machine translation system developed with Tilde Eesti OÜ and Inversion OÜ and funded 80% by the European Commission. At a conceptual level, the system generates English versions of website content, captures corrections and feeds those improvements back into the model so that translation quality can improve over time. Quality is also monitored and adjusted by the publisher to maintain accuracy and fitness for use. The expected impact is lower translation cost, broader access to official information and improved convenience for foreign stakeholders. More broadly, the initiative strengthens transparency, supports international engagement and delivers scalable multilingual access without changing the legal status of official publications.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F10_CREATIVE_PRODUCTION_CAPACITY_BOTTLENECK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-235,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00230,EU_PSTW_202609_00230,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Center of Registers and Information Systems,General public services n.e.c.,"Estonia’s Centre of Registers and Information Systems introduced automatic translation in the e-Land Register and related registry environments to address a practical accessibility problem: non-Estonian speakers often found it difficult to understand detailed registry information, even when they needed only to read and interpret on-screen data rather than obtain a legally binding translation.",Automatic translation in the Land Register search portal and Commercial Register,Center of Registers and Information Systems; Central-Government; General public services n.e.c.; Estonia,"The use case therefore involves citizens, businesses and other users of the e-Land Register and Commercial Register, with the objective of improving comprehension of registry data and making these digital public services more accessible internationally. The solution is implemented through a real-time machine translation tool launched in the e-Land Register web environment on 29 October 2021.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Estonia’s Centre of Registers and Information Systems introduced automatic translation in the e-Land Register and related registry environments to address a practical accessibility problem: non-Estonian speakers often found it difficult to understand detailed registry information, even when they needed only to read and interpret on-screen data rather than obtain a legally binding translation. Registry entries can contain highly technical content, such as information on easements and mortgages, and this can create barriers for foreign users engaging with official systems. The use case therefore involves citizens, businesses and other users of the e-Land Register and Commercial Register, with the objective of improving comprehension of registry data and making these digital public services more accessible internationally. The solution is implemented through a real-time machine translation tool launched in the e-Land Register web environment on 29 October 2021. At a conceptual level, the system uses neural-network-based translation methods that draw links across a large language database and improve over time as vocabulary and patterns are refined. The tool was developed in cooperation between Estonia, Latvia and Belgium, with middleware provided by the Latvian partner TILDE SIA. The translated text is explicitly informative and has no legal force, as Estonian remains the official language of the register. The impact includes improved usability for non-Estonian speakers, greater accessibility of registry information and a more inclusive digital-service experience.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-236,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00231,EU_PSTW_202609_00231,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Information System Authority of the Republic of Estonia,General public services n.e.c.,"Bürokratt was conceived to address this fragmentation by creating a more unified service experience in which users can obtain information and complete actions through a single conversational entry point. The expected impact is a more coherent user experience, reduced channel fragmentation and stronger scalability in digital service delivery.",Bürokratt – a virtual assistant for the Public Administration,Information System Authority of the Republic of Estonia; Central-Government; General public services n.e.c.; Estonia,"The solution is implemented as an interoperable network of public-sector virtual assistants, or “kratts”, connected to national information systems and capable of acting together as one channel from the user’s perspective.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Estonia’s public sector has sought to simplify how people access government services by reducing the need to navigate multiple agencies, channels and digital systems separately. The underlying operational problem is that citizens and businesses often have to move between different websites, interfaces and institutions to complete related tasks, even when they experience them as part of one life event or administrative need. Bürokratt was conceived to address this fragmentation by creating a more unified service experience in which users can obtain information and complete actions through a single conversational entry point. The stakeholders include citizens, public authorities, national information-system operators and, in some cases, private-sector service providers, with the objective of making service access more seamless, reducing friction and improving the efficiency of digital public administration. The solution is implemented as an interoperable network of public-sector virtual assistants, or “kratts”, connected to national information systems and capable of acting together as one channel from the user’s perspective. At a conceptual level, the system combines conversational interfaces, service orchestration and interoperability across public and selected private systems so that multiple tasks can be handled within one communication session and across devices. The expected impact is a more coherent user experience, reduced channel fragmentation and stronger scalability in digital service delivery. More broadly, Bürokratt creates value by supporting a whole-of-government approach to virtual assistance and making public services more accessible, integrated and citizen-centred.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-237,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00232,EU_PSTW_202609_00232,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Estonian Road Administration,Transport,"Road budgets are finite, and without stronger predictive insight it can be difficult to prioritise maintenance and improvement projects in a way that maximises long-term benefit.",Forecasting models Road Administration (Roads conditions and return of investments),Estonian Road Administration; Central-Government; Transport; Estonia,"The use case therefore involves transport authorities, infrastructure planners and investment decision-makers, with the objective of improving planning quality by anticipating road conditions and directing funding towards the interventions most likely to deliver the best return. The solution is implemented through forecasting models that estimate both the future condition of roads and the likely return on investment associated with different projects.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Estonian Road Administration is developing forecasting models to address a key infrastructure-management problem: how to decide where road investments will have the greatest effect in terms of network condition and value for money. Road budgets are finite, and without stronger predictive insight it can be difficult to prioritise maintenance and improvement projects in a way that maximises long-term benefit. The use case therefore involves transport authorities, infrastructure planners and investment decision-makers, with the objective of improving planning quality by anticipating road conditions and directing funding towards the interventions most likely to deliver the best return. The solution is implemented through forecasting models that estimate both the future condition of roads and the likely return on investment associated with different projects. At a conceptual level, the system combines historical road data, condition trends and investment-analysis methods to identify where deterioration is likely to occur and where spending can produce the highest practical benefit. These outputs can then support prioritisation, maintenance scheduling and broader infrastructure strategy. The expected impact is better-targeted investment, improved road-condition management and more efficient use of public resources. More broadly, the initiative creates value by enabling evidence-based infrastructure planning, reducing the risk of poorly targeted spending and strengthening the long-term reliability and sustainability of the road network.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-238,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00233,EU_PSTW_202609_00233,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Ministry of Education and Research, University of Tartu, Ministry of Economic Affairs and Communications",General services.,"The operational problem addressed by Kama is that multilingual translation often requires separate tools, uneven language coverage or manual effort that reduces efficiency and consistency. The expected impact is improved translation availability, greater consistency across languages and reduced dependence on manual translation for routine needs.",Kama - Multilingual multidisciplinary neuroma machine translation,"Ministry of Education and Research, University of Tartu, Ministry of Economic Affairs and Communications; Central-Government; General services.; Estonia","This use case therefore focuses on providing a broader, more flexible translation capability that can support different domains and users working across several languages. The solution is implemented as a multilingual neural machine translation system supporting seven input and output languages: Estonian, Latvian, Lithuanian, English, Russian, German and Finnish.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"In multilingual public and professional environments, the need to translate accurately across a range of languages and subject areas is essential for communication, cooperation and access to information. The operational problem addressed by Kama is that multilingual translation often requires separate tools, uneven language coverage or manual effort that reduces efficiency and consistency. This use case therefore focuses on providing a broader, more flexible translation capability that can support different domains and users working across several languages. Its objective is to enable reliable translation between multiple language pairs in a single system, making multilingual communication more practical and accessible. The solution is implemented as a multilingual neural machine translation system supporting seven input and output languages: Estonian, Latvian, Lithuanian, English, Russian, German and Finnish. At a conceptual level, the platform uses neural translation models trained to operate across multiple language directions, allowing users to move between different linguistic contexts within one shared architecture rather than relying on isolated bilingual tools. This design supports more efficient maintenance and broader applicability across disciplines and use cases. The expected impact is improved translation availability, greater consistency across languages and reduced dependence on manual translation for routine needs. In operational and business terms, Kama offers scalability, supports multilingual service delivery and creates value by widening access to information and collaboration across language communities.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-239,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00234,EU_PSTW_202609_00234,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""social_nonprofit""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Education and Youth Board (Harno),General services.,"Estonia’s digital ecosystem depends on high-quality language tools to support public services, research and software development in Estonian, yet language-technology resources for smaller languages often remain fragmented or incomplete. ESTNLTK was created to address this challenge by making existing Estonian language-technology tools easier to access and by developing new ones where gaps remain.",ESTNLTK - Open source tools for Estonian natural language processing,Education and Youth Board (Harno); Central-Government; General services.; Estonia,"The solution is implemented as an open-source toolkit, written mainly in Python, for analysing and editing Estonian text.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Estonia’s digital ecosystem depends on high-quality language tools to support public services, research and software development in Estonian, yet language-technology resources for smaller languages often remain fragmented or incomplete. ESTNLTK was created to address this challenge by making existing Estonian language-technology tools easier to access and by developing new ones where gaps remain. The stakeholders include researchers, developers, public institutions and organisations working with Estonian-language data, with the objective of strengthening the technical foundation for processing Estonian texts and enabling wider reuse of language resources across different domains. The solution is implemented as an open-source toolkit, written mainly in Python, for analysing and editing Estonian text. At a conceptual level, the collection acts both as an integration layer for existing language-technology components and as a development framework for new tools, allowing users to perform a range of text-processing tasks within one extensible environment. Because it is open source and funded through the Estonian National Language Technology Programme, it supports both practical deployment and broader ecosystem building. The expected impact is improved availability of language-processing tools, lower barriers for developers and researchers, and better support for Estonian-language applications. More broadly, ESTNLTK creates value by reinforcing digital capability in a smaller language market, enabling innovation and reuse, and providing shared infrastructure that can support public-sector, academic and commercial work on Estonian text.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-240,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00235,EU_PSTW_202609_00235,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Police and Border Guard Board (PPA),Police Services,"The Police and Border Guard Board needed a better understanding of why customers were contacting its information lines and how service bottlenecks were affecting the uptake of digital channels. Calls concerning the issuance of documents had already been identified as the largest category of enquiries, yet the organisation needed richer insight into customer difficulties, especially across different languages and over long service cycles in which people may only renew documents every five to ten years.",PPA Information Phone call transcription for customers analysis different languages,Police and Border Guard Board (PPA); Central-Government; Police Services; Estonia,"The use case therefore focused on improving service design, encouraging greater use of e-channels and refining the content and presentation of customer information. The stakeholders include the Police and Border Guard Board, customer-service teams and citizens interacting with document-issuance services, with the objective of reducing avoidable calls and making digital services easier to use.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Police and Border Guard Board needed a better understanding of why customers were contacting its information lines and how service bottlenecks were affecting the uptake of digital channels. Calls concerning the issuance of documents had already been identified as the largest category of enquiries, yet the organisation needed richer insight into customer difficulties, especially across different languages and over long service cycles in which people may only renew documents every five to ten years. The use case therefore focused on improving service design, encouraging greater use of e-channels and refining the content and presentation of customer information. The stakeholders include the Police and Border Guard Board, customer-service teams and citizens interacting with document-issuance services, with the objective of reducing avoidable calls and making digital services easier to use. The solution is implemented through an analytical application that processes customer contacts in several languages, including Estonian and Russian, and examines call content to identify recurring patterns and friction points. At a conceptual level, the system combines multilingual transcription and contact analysis to extract service insights from phone interactions and translate them into design improvements. The pilot showed that customers often forgot the correct steps because document requests are infrequent, and it identified specific bottlenecks hindering smoother digital use. The impact includes better-targeted service improvement, stronger support for e-channel adoption and more effective customer communication, creating value through more informed service design and reduced pressure on traditional contact channels.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-241,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00236,EU_PSTW_202609_00236,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Environmental Agency,Protection of biodiversity and landscape.,"The forestry-management problem is that national forest assessment requires up-to-date spatial information on tree species, forest clearings, forest height and other structural characteristics, but traditional methods can be slow, fragmented or dependent on manual inspection.","Identification of forest tree species, forest clearing and forest height",Environmental Agency; Central-Government; Protection of biodiversity and landscape.; Estonia,"The Environmental Agency and KEMIT developed a system that supports the collection and sharing of operational forest information, using remote sensing as the main data source.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Estonia’s remote forest monitoring information system was created to improve how forest resources are recorded, monitored and shared in georeferenced form. The forestry-management problem is that national forest assessment requires up-to-date spatial information on tree species, forest clearings, forest height and other structural characteristics, but traditional methods can be slow, fragmented or dependent on manual inspection. The Environmental Agency and KEMIT developed a system that supports the collection and sharing of operational forest information, using remote sensing as the main data source. The implementation is semi-automatic, combining machine-learning algorithms, visual inspection of remote-sensing data and third-party software tools. Inputs include satellite or other remote-sensing data, forest notifications, soil maps and teaching and validation datasets. The outputs include nationwide map layers for key assessment features, such as raster maps identifying dominant tree species. Conceptually, the system integrates geospatial data processing, classification models, human validation and forest-resource mapping. The expected impact is more consistent national forest monitoring, better evidence for forest management, improved detection of changes such as clearing, and more efficient sharing of forest data among competent bodies.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-242,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00237,EU_PSTW_202609_00237,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Emergency center,Public order and safety n.e.c.,"Spoken interactions often contain valuable information on citizens’ concerns, recurring service needs and operational bottlenecks, yet audio recordings are difficult to analyse at scale and manual review is time-consuming.",Transcription Kratt 1247 - Transcribing calls to to crisis number 1247,Emergency center; Central-Government; Public order and safety n.e.c.; Estonia,Estonia’s crisis-information services introduced this use case to make better use of the substantial volume of calls handled through the national 1247 crisis number.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Estonia’s crisis-information services introduced this use case to make better use of the substantial volume of calls handled through the national 1247 crisis number. Spoken interactions often contain valuable information on citizens’ concerns, recurring service needs and operational bottlenecks, yet audio recordings are difficult to analyse at scale and manual review is time-consuming. The service therefore needed a way to convert calls into a format that could support structured analysis, service improvement and the development of additional digital-support tools. The stakeholders include the emergency and crisis-information service, public-sector innovation teams and future service designers working on Estonia’s “kratt” ecosystem. The technological implementation is based on automated speech transcription, which converts spoken conversations from the 1247 line into machine-readable text. Once transcribed, the material can be searched, analysed and reused to identify frequent topics, recurring questions, information gaps and patterns in public demand during crisis situations. The resulting text can also provide a practical evidence base for training or designing future conversational assistants. The main impact is improved reuse of call-centre knowledge, reduced effort in extracting insight from audio material and stronger support for evidence-based service design. More broadly, the solution transforms routine crisis-service interactions into a scalable knowledge resource for public administration.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-243,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00238,EU_PSTW_202609_00238,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Emergency center,Public order and safety n.e.c.,"Under such pressure, mistakes in risk assessment or delays in gathering the right details can directly affect response quality. The expected impact is faster and more consistent threat assessment, reduced cognitive burden and better coordination of emergency resources, creating value through higher reliability in high-pressure public-safety operations.",Alarm Center Assistant for Risk Assessment,Emergency center; Central-Government; Public order and safety n.e.c.; Estonia,"This use case was developed to support operators in one of the most demanding parts of emergency-call handling, with the objective of reducing error, speeding up assessment and improving the consistency of resource deployment. The solution is implemented through software that transcribes the incoming call and analyses it in light of previous actions in order to suggest the most likely event type and a typical case profile for the operator.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Estonia’s emergency notification process is highly time-critical and operationally complex: rescue operators must assess threats within roughly a minute while taking into account the information needs and resource rules of at least three different agencies. Under such pressure, mistakes in risk assessment or delays in gathering the right details can directly affect response quality. This use case was developed to support operators in one of the most demanding parts of emergency-call handling, with the objective of reducing error, speeding up assessment and improving the consistency of resource deployment. The stakeholders include the emergency centre, rescue operators and the agencies that depend on accurate threat classification, with the wider goal of making emergency call processing more reliable under severe time constraints. The solution is implemented through software that transcribes the incoming call and analyses it in light of previous actions in order to suggest the most likely event type and a typical case profile for the operator. At a conceptual level, the system combines real-time transcription, contextual analysis and historical pattern support to assist rather than replace the human operator during risk assessment. This allows critical information to be surfaced more quickly while keeping the operator in control of the final judgement. The expected impact is faster and more consistent threat assessment, reduced cognitive burden and better coordination of emergency resources, creating value through higher reliability in high-pressure public-safety operations.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-244,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00239,EU_PSTW_202609_00239,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Emergency center,Public order and safety n.e.c.,"Emergency-call centres handle high volumes of urgent voice data, yet spoken calls are difficult to search, analyse or reuse unless they are converted into text. The operational challenge is that emergency communication must be processed instantly for response purposes, but afterwards authorities also need a usable record for review, quality improvement and possible integration into future support systems.",Transcription Kratt 112 - Transcribing emergency calls dialed to the emergency number 112,Emergency center; Central-Government; Public order and safety n.e.c.; Estonia,Estonia introduced this use case to support the handling of 112 emergency calls by beginning the systematic transcription of those conversations. The solution is implemented through automated transcription of calls to 112.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Emergency-call centres handle high volumes of urgent voice data, yet spoken calls are difficult to search, analyse or reuse unless they are converted into text. Estonia introduced this use case to support the handling of 112 emergency calls by beginning the systematic transcription of those conversations. The operational challenge is that emergency communication must be processed instantly for response purposes, but afterwards authorities also need a usable record for review, quality improvement and possible integration into future support systems. The stakeholders include the emergency centre, call operators and related public-safety services, with the objective of making emergency-call content more accessible for analysis and process improvement while supporting a more modern digital workflow around voice data. The solution is implemented through automated transcription of calls to 112. At a conceptual level, the system applies speech-recognition technology to transform emergency conversations into text, allowing spoken interactions to become searchable, analysable and suitable for downstream use in training, quality monitoring or future decision-support development. Even where the immediate use is modest, the transcription layer creates a reusable digital asset from otherwise transient audio. The expected impact is improved access to operational information, better conditions for service analysis and stronger foundations for future emergency-support tools. More broadly, the project creates value by enabling voice-based public-safety interactions to be incorporated into broader data-driven improvement and digital-service development.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F06_HIGH_VOLUME_SCALABILITY_BOTTLENECK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-245,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00240,EU_PSTW_202609_00240,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Estonian National Archives,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.","Historical image collections are rich in public value yet often difficult to navigate when names are unknown, records are incomplete or users are trying to identify ancestors or historical figures from a single photograph.",Appearance - Identification of objects at the Estonian National Archives,"Estonian National Archives; Central-Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Estonia","This use case therefore involved the National Archives, researchers, family historians and the wider public, with the objective of improving discoverability and opening new ways of engaging with archival photo collections. The solution is implemented through Ilme, a facial-recognition-based search service linked to Fotis, the National Archives’ photo database.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Estonian National Archives wanted to make its vast photographic holdings more accessible by allowing users to search not only through metadata, but also through visual similarity in faces. Historical image collections are rich in public value yet often difficult to navigate when names are unknown, records are incomplete or users are trying to identify ancestors or historical figures from a single photograph. This use case therefore involved the National Archives, researchers, family historians and the wider public, with the objective of improving discoverability and opening new ways of engaging with archival photo collections. The solution is implemented through Ilme, a facial-recognition-based search service linked to Fotis, the National Archives’ photo database. At a conceptual level, users upload a photo, faces are detected and compared against more than half a million archive photographs spanning back to the 1840s, representing around three million faces in total, and the most similar matches are returned by email within 24 hours as a PDF. This creates a visual retrieval workflow that complements conventional catalogue search. The expected impact is easier access to archival imagery, improved usability of large photo collections and new opportunities for users to identify individuals across historical materials. More broadly, the system creates value by making cultural heritage more searchable, more personal and more meaningful for both specialist and public audiences.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-246,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00241,EU_PSTW_202609_00241,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Environment Agency,Protection of biodiversity and landscape.,"Estonia’s forest monitoring system relies on annual field measurements from 5,600 plots, which provide the basis for national reporting and forest policy, but translating those point measurements into broader, map-based resource estimates remains a difficult and labour-intensive task. The operational challenge is to extend detailed statistical inventory information across large forest areas in a way that improves spatial coverage and supports both monitoring and stand-level economic decisions.",Calculation of forest resources by remote sensing methods,Environment Agency; Central-Government; Protection of biodiversity and landscape.; Estonia,"This use case therefore involves forest-monitoring authorities, policy planners and users of national forest-resource data, with the objective of developing a methodology that can turn inventory data into maps of species composition, stand height and trunk volume. The solution is implemented by combining multispectral satellite imagery, airborne laser-scanning data and accurate tree measurements from sample plots.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Estonia’s forest monitoring system relies on annual field measurements from 5,600 plots, which provide the basis for national reporting and forest policy, but translating those point measurements into broader, map-based resource estimates remains a difficult and labour-intensive task. The operational challenge is to extend detailed statistical inventory information across large forest areas in a way that improves spatial coverage and supports both monitoring and stand-level economic decisions. This use case therefore involves forest-monitoring authorities, policy planners and users of national forest-resource data, with the objective of developing a methodology that can turn inventory data into maps of species composition, stand height and trunk volume. The solution is implemented by combining multispectral satellite imagery, airborne laser-scanning data and accurate tree measurements from sample plots. At a conceptual level, the system uses the plot-based measurements as model inputs and links them with remote-sensing variables in order to estimate forest characteristics spatially and produce map-based resource assessments. This moves the inventory system from pure sampling towards a hybrid model that supports both national reporting and more operationally detailed forest analysis. The expected impact is better spatial representation of forest resources, improved support for monitoring and stronger evidence for policy and economic planning. More broadly, the project creates value by increasing the usefulness of Estonia’s forest inventory, supporting smarter forest management and enabling more scalable environmental analysis through remote sensing.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-247,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00242,EU_PSTW_202609_00242,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Environment Agency,Environmental protection n.e.c.,"This creates a challenge for flood monitoring and marine forecasting, where broader and more continuous visibility of water conditions can improve both warning and planning.",Water level monitoring in coastal seas and lakes using satellite altimetry,Environment Agency; Central-Government; Environmental protection n.e.c.; Estonia,"The use case was developed to enhance water-level observation by combining conventional monitoring with remote methods, with the objective of improving hydrological information available to authorities and the public. The solution is implemented through prototypes that combine radar remote-sensing data, satellite altimetry, forecasting models and analytical methods.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Hydrological monitoring traditionally depends on in situ measurements of water levels and flows at national stations, but these observations do not always provide the spatial coverage needed for large lakes and coastal seas. This creates a challenge for flood monitoring and marine forecasting, where broader and more continuous visibility of water conditions can improve both warning and planning. The use case was developed to enhance water-level observation by combining conventional monitoring with remote methods, with the objective of improving hydrological information available to authorities and the public. The stakeholders include hydrological-monitoring services, marine forecasters and users of water-level information, with the broader aim of strengthening operational flood monitoring and sea-level prediction. The solution is implemented through prototypes that combine radar remote-sensing data, satellite altimetry, forecasting models and analytical methods. At a conceptual level, the platform integrates remote water-level observations with existing hydrometric data and modelling workflows so that water conditions can be observed over a wider area than is possible through ground stations alone. This is particularly relevant for improving observation coverage over the Great Lakes and for feeding better data into forecasting systems. The expected impact is improved spatial coverage, more accurate sea-level forecasts and stronger flood-monitoring capability. More broadly, the project creates value by making hydrological services more comprehensive and by supporting a more resilient, data-rich approach to water-level monitoring in coastal and inland waters.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-248,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00243,EU_PSTW_202609_00243,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Board of Education and Youth,R&D education.,"Education systems increasingly need to support learners whose interests, prior knowledge and abilities differ widely, yet standardised content delivery can make it difficult to provide genuinely individual learning pathways.",Marakratt - Personal learning path infrastructure,Board of Education and Youth; Central-Government; R&D education.; Estonia,"The use case therefore involved students, educators and digital-learning stakeholders, with the objective of testing whether recommendation models and automation could help build more individualised study paths and improve the relevance of educational material. The solution was implemented through machine-learning experimentation aimed at recommending educational materials and automating the personalisation of learning activities.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Education systems increasingly need to support learners whose interests, prior knowledge and abilities differ widely, yet standardised content delivery can make it difficult to provide genuinely individual learning pathways. Marakratt was developed to explore how students could be supported more effectively through educational content and activities that are better matched to their personal learning needs. The use case therefore involved students, educators and digital-learning stakeholders, with the objective of testing whether recommendation models and automation could help build more individualised study paths and improve the relevance of educational material. Rather than redesigning the entire education process, the project focused on creating a proof-of-concept infrastructure able to personalise parts of the learner experience. The solution was implemented through machine-learning experimentation aimed at recommending educational materials and automating the personalisation of learning activities. At a conceptual level, the prototype used learner-related information such as interests, knowledge and abilities to generate recommendations and adapt content pathways, creating models that could serve as the basis for more personalised digital learning environments. This allowed the project to test how recommendation systems might support both educational planning and learner engagement. The impact is primarily exploratory, but it includes a clearer technical basis for individual study-path support, improved understanding of how recommendation models can function in education and a prototype framework for future scaling. More broadly, Marakratt creates value by laying the groundwork for more adaptive and learner-centred digital education services.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-249,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00244,EU_PSTW_202609_00244,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Courts of Estonia and Registers and Information Systems Center,Law courts,"Estonia’s courts needed a faster and more efficient way to produce hearing transcripts, since manual transcription of court sessions is slow, labour-intensive and places a heavy burden on clerks. According to the Estonian courts, a single criminal case containing about 14 hours of hearings could require an average of 112 working hours to transcribe manually.",Salme - Speech recognition in courts,Courts of Estonia and Registers and Information Systems Center; Central-Government; Law courts; Estonia,"The use case therefore involved the courts, the Centre of Registers and Information Systems and judicial staff, with the objective of reducing transcription effort, speeding up protocolling and improving the handling of spoken courtroom data in both physical and remote hearings. The solution is implemented through Salme, a speech-recognition tool developed with CGI Estonia and Tilde and integrated into the Court Information System via X-Road.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Estonia’s courts needed a faster and more efficient way to produce hearing transcripts, since manual transcription of court sessions is slow, labour-intensive and places a heavy burden on clerks. According to the Estonian courts, a single criminal case containing about 14 hours of hearings could require an average of 112 working hours to transcribe manually. The use case therefore involved the courts, the Centre of Registers and Information Systems and judicial staff, with the objective of reducing transcription effort, speeding up protocolling and improving the handling of spoken courtroom data in both physical and remote hearings. The solution is implemented through Salme, a speech-recognition tool developed with CGI Estonia and Tilde and integrated into the Court Information System via X-Road. At a conceptual level, Salme supports both real-time and offline transcription, assigns microphones to speakers, returns transcript text with only a short delay and links recordings, transcripts and notes back into court workflows. The system was trained using more than 800 hours of transcribed audio for acoustic models and over 800 million words of textual data for the custom language model, producing reported word-error rates between 8% and 15% and overall precision of about 92%. The impact includes major time savings, faster transcript production and better integration of courtroom recordings into digital judicial processes, creating value through efficiency, accuracy and secure operation on authorised court infrastructure.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-250,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00245,EU_PSTW_202609_00245,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Tallinn University of Technology,R&D environmental protection.,"Monitoring sea-ice conditions in the Gulf of Riga is important for understanding winter dynamics, environmental conditions and maritime risks, yet consistent large-scale observation is difficult to achieve through field methods alone.",Statistical characterization of the sea ice extent in the Gulf of Riga (Baltic Sea) using optical remote-sensing imagery,Tallinn University of Technology; Academic-Research; R&D environmental protection.; Estonia,"This use case addressed the need for a more systematic way to characterise ice extent, ice occurrence probability and ice-season length across different winter scenarios in the Baltic Sea. The solution was implemented using optical remote-sensing data from the MODIS instrument at 250 m resolution, covering the period from 2002 to 2011.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Monitoring sea-ice conditions in the Gulf of Riga is important for understanding winter dynamics, environmental conditions and maritime risks, yet consistent large-scale observation is difficult to achieve through field methods alone. This use case addressed the need for a more systematic way to characterise ice extent, ice occurrence probability and ice-season length across different winter scenarios in the Baltic Sea. The stakeholders include researchers, environmental analysts and organisations concerned with marine observation, with the objective of generating a reliable statistical picture of sea-ice behaviour over time and across locations. The solution was implemented using optical remote-sensing data from the MODIS instrument at 250 m resolution, covering the period from 2002 to 2011. At a conceptual level, the method relied on bimodal histogram analysis of remote-sensing reflectance data to distinguish ice from water, then used the resulting classifications to calculate sea-ice extent, occurrence probability and season length. The outputs were compared with official ice charts and multi-sensor products, showing strong agreement with reference data, with R² values above 0.83. The study found large year-to-year variation: the ice season ranged from 68 to 146 days and maximum ice extent from 329 to 15,350 km², with higher ice occurrence in waters shallower than 15 m. More broadly, the work creates value by providing a robust analytical basis for regional ice monitoring and winter-scenario classification.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-251,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00246,EU_PSTW_202609_00246,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Social Insurance Board,Social protection n.e.c.,"Routine contact analysis can be difficult to perform manually at scale, especially when interactions take place across multiple channels and in more than one language.",Analysis of customer calls at the Estonian Social Insurance Board,Social Insurance Board; Central-Government; Social protection n.e.c.; Estonia,"This pilot was therefore designed to help the organisation detect patterns in customer appeals and use those insights to improve service design and communication. The stakeholders include the Social Insurance Board, service managers, call-centre staff and citizens using social-insurance services, with the objective of identifying the most common concerns more precisely and supporting more informed managerial decisions.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Estonian Social Insurance Board needed a better understanding of why customers were contacting its service channels and what recurring issues were driving calls and online chats. Routine contact analysis can be difficult to perform manually at scale, especially when interactions take place across multiple channels and in more than one language. This pilot was therefore designed to help the organisation detect patterns in customer appeals and use those insights to improve service design and communication. The stakeholders include the Social Insurance Board, service managers, call-centre staff and citizens using social-insurance services, with the objective of identifying the most common concerns more precisely and supporting more informed managerial decisions. The solution was implemented in cooperation with Feelingstream, whose application transcribed customer calls and chats over a three-month period from May to August 2020 in both Estonian and Russian. At a conceptual level, the system converted spoken and written interactions into analysable text and then examined these data to identify themes, recurring problems and patterns in customer needs. This provided a more detailed basis for service insight than high-level contact statistics alone. The impact includes better understanding of why citizens contact the organisation, improved potential to redesign problematic service areas and stronger evidence for decision-making. More broadly, the pilot creates value by turning everyday customer interactions into a usable source of operational intelligence and by supporting more responsive and efficient social-insurance services.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-252,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00247,EU_PSTW_202609_00247,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Estonian Fire Department,Fire-protection services,UNKNOWN,Identification of flammable dwellings,Estonian Fire Department; Central-Government; Fire-protection services; Estonia,"Because the system may concern private homes and potentially sensitive household data, its value depends on transparent criteria, appropriate legal basis, careful validation and use as a support tool rather than a fully automated enforcement mechanism.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Estonia’s flammable-dwellings project aims to support fire prevention by identifying residential buildings that may present elevated fire risk. The public-safety problem is that fire services need to prioritise prevention activities, inspections and awareness measures, but risk factors may be distributed across building characteristics, location, past incidents, socio-demographic indicators and other administrative records. The Estonian Fire Department is developing an AI-based solution to detect dwellings that are more likely to be flammable or vulnerable to fire-related incidents. The implementation is described as an AI project under development, likely combining risk indicators, building or housing data, historical fire information and classification models to support prioritisation. Conceptually, the system would produce risk signals for fire-safety professionals, helping them decide where preventive outreach or inspection should be directed. The expected impact is better targeting of prevention resources, earlier identification of risky dwellings, fewer fire incidents and improved protection of residents. Because the system may concern private homes and potentially sensitive household data, its value depends on transparent criteria, appropriate legal basis, careful validation and use as a support tool rather than a fully automated enforcement mechanism.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-253,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00248,EU_PSTW_202609_00248,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Ministry of Rural Affairs,"Agriculture, forestry, fishing and hunting","Estonia needed a more objective and scalable way to identify agricultural crops for official monitoring and statistics, as traditional methods can be labour-intensive and difficult to apply uniformly across the country. The operational challenge was to create a methodology suited specifically to Estonian conditions and capable of supporting both agricultural administration and the production of consistent national statistics.",Crop detection from Sentinel-1 and -2 time series using deep learning methods,"Ministry of Rural Affairs; Central-Government; Agriculture, forestry, fishing and hunting; Estonia","The stakeholders include Estonian universities, agricultural authorities such as PRIA and the wider state administration, with the objective of improving crop identification, strengthening the quality of agricultural statistics and providing a more standardised evidence base for public-sector use. The solution was implemented through a methodology that combines Sentinel-1 and Sentinel-2 time-series data with contextual variables including soil type, average daily air temperature and precipitation.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Estonia needed a more objective and scalable way to identify agricultural crops for official monitoring and statistics, as traditional methods can be labour-intensive and difficult to apply uniformly across the country. The operational challenge was to create a methodology suited specifically to Estonian conditions and capable of supporting both agricultural administration and the production of consistent national statistics. The stakeholders include Estonian universities, agricultural authorities such as PRIA and the wider state administration, with the objective of improving crop identification, strengthening the quality of agricultural statistics and providing a more standardised evidence base for public-sector use. The solution was implemented through a methodology that combines Sentinel-1 and Sentinel-2 time-series data with contextual variables including soil type, average daily air temperature and precipitation. At a conceptual level, deep-learning techniques were used to model crop classes from these multi-source inputs and distinguish between different agricultural patterns over time. The results showed that several crop categories could be identified with over 90% accuracy, including potatoes, field peas and beans, summer and winter rapeseed, maize, rye, winter wheat and barley, while berry and fruit plantations, as well as carrot and beetroot plantations, could not yet be classified with sufficient reliability. The impact is a more uniform and objective basis for agricultural statistics, better support for monitoring and a scalable method that can be integrated into PRIA and wider state use.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-254,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00249,EU_PSTW_202609_00249,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Taltech Marine Systems Institute,Environmental protection n.e.c.,"Authorities responsible for flood response and environmental monitoring need a dependable way to identify inundated areas quickly, yet flood mapping can be difficult because water detection varies depending on weather, vegetation cover and sensor type.",Mapping of flooded areas from satellite images,Taltech Marine Systems Institute; Academic-Research; Environmental protection n.e.c.; Estonia,"This use case focused on finding the most suitable operational methodology for mapping flooded areas under different conditions, with the objective of improving the reliability and timeliness of flood detection from satellite data. The solution was implemented through an evaluation of Sentinel-1 and Sentinel-2 imagery and the development of a prototype processing workflow.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Authorities responsible for flood response and environmental monitoring need a dependable way to identify inundated areas quickly, yet flood mapping can be difficult because water detection varies depending on weather, vegetation cover and sensor type. This use case focused on finding the most suitable operational methodology for mapping flooded areas under different conditions, with the objective of improving the reliability and timeliness of flood detection from satellite data. The stakeholders include environmental analysts, emergency-management actors and organisations working with Earth-observation data, all of whom benefit from more consistent flood mapping. The solution was implemented through an evaluation of Sentinel-1 and Sentinel-2 imagery and the development of a prototype processing workflow. At a conceptual level, the work compared the suitability of radar and optical satellite data, identified the necessary preprocessing steps and selected the most appropriate vegetation index for characterising open water. These elements were then combined into an operational prototype capable of processing Sentinel-1 and Sentinel-2 data for flood-area mapping. By structuring the method in this way, the project moved beyond ad hoc interpretation towards a repeatable analytical workflow. The impact includes a clearer methodological basis for flood detection, improved operational readiness for mapping inundation and better use of satellite resources under varying environmental conditions. More broadly, the prototype creates value by supporting faster, more systematic flood monitoring and by enabling scalable geospatial analysis for hazard assessment and response planning.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-255,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00250,EU_PSTW_202609_00250,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Estonian Internal Security Service,Police Services,"The operational challenge is that video streams and spatial data are often handled separately, which can limit the speed and effectiveness of intelligence, surveillance and reconnaissance work.",Kratt for Estonian Internal Security Service - Combining video and GIS to identify people,Estonian Internal Security Service; Central-Government; Police Services; Estonia,This use case was developed to strengthen situational awareness by combining these sources into a more usable operational picture. The solution is implemented as an ISR tool that integrates video streams with GIS data.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Internal security agencies need tools that help them interpret large volumes of surveillance information quickly and accurately, particularly in situations where location, movement and visual identification must be assessed together. The operational challenge is that video streams and spatial data are often handled separately, which can limit the speed and effectiveness of intelligence, surveillance and reconnaissance work. This use case was developed to strengthen situational awareness by combining these sources into a more usable operational picture. The stakeholders include Estonian internal security agencies and the analysts or officers responsible for surveillance and reconnaissance, with the objective of improving identification capability, increasing efficiency in analysis and supporting more informed operational decisions. The solution is implemented as an ISR tool that integrates video streams with GIS data. At a conceptual level, the system links visual input from live or recorded footage with geographic information so that people and movements can be analysed in relation to location, routes and surrounding spatial context. This creates a more structured and operationally useful interface than reviewing video and maps separately. Although the public description is brief, the expected impact lies in faster identification, better situational interpretation and improved coordination of surveillance analysis. More broadly, the project creates value by enhancing the operational effectiveness of security services through integrated spatial and visual intelligence support.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-256,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00251,EU_PSTW_202609_00251,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Police and Border Guard Board,Police Services,"Border authorities face the dual challenge of processing travellers quickly while also verifying identity and right of entry with a high level of reliability. Manual control remains essential, but alone it can slow throughput and leave less room for automated support in detecting document misuse.",Automatic border control - Facial Recognition at border controls,Police and Border Guard Board; Central-Government; Police Services; Estonia,The solution is implemented through biometric ABC gates that use facial-recognition-based identification algorithms alongside document verification.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Estonia introduced automated border-control gates to improve the efficiency and security of border crossing at busy entry points such as Tallinn Airport and the Narva road border crossing. Border authorities face the dual challenge of processing travellers quickly while also verifying identity and right of entry with a high level of reliability. Manual control remains essential, but alone it can slow throughput and leave less room for automated support in detecting document misuse. The stakeholders include border guards, travellers and the authorities responsible for border management, with the objective of speeding up crossings, reducing the risk of identity fraud and strengthening the overall reliability of entry checks. The solution is implemented through biometric ABC gates that use facial-recognition-based identification algorithms alongside document verification. At a conceptual level, the system compares the traveller’s biometric data with the information in the travel document and supports the border guard by automating core identity and eligibility checks before human intervention where needed. The reported performance is notable: initial testing showed that a person could pass through the gates in an average of 15 seconds. The impact includes faster border processing, improved operational efficiency and lower risk of crossing with the wrong document. More broadly, the system creates value by combining speed, security and scalability in border-control operations.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-257,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00252,EU_PSTW_202609_00252,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,National Library,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.","With around 14,000 question-related emails each year, the library faced a practical need to reduce repetitive manual work while maintaining a responsive service for users.",Chatbot Vesta - Estonian National Library,"National Library; Central-Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Estonia",The use case therefore aimed to improve the handling of first-level enquiries by sorting incoming questions more efficiently and automating answers where repetition made that feasible. The solution was implemented through a prototype chatbot application capable of classifying incoming questions by topic and responding automatically to repetitive first-level enquiries.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Estonian National Library receives a large number of daily questions and information requests, many of which concern recurring topics and consume significant staff time despite following recognisable patterns. With around 14,000 question-related emails each year, the library faced a practical need to reduce repetitive manual work while maintaining a responsive service for users. The use case therefore aimed to improve the handling of first-level enquiries by sorting incoming questions more efficiently and automating answers where repetition made that feasible. The stakeholders include library staff, users seeking information and the institution responsible for maintaining access to knowledge services, with the objective of improving efficiency, reducing routine workload and ensuring that common questions can be handled more quickly. The solution was implemented through a prototype chatbot application capable of classifying incoming questions by topic and responding automatically to repetitive first-level enquiries. At a conceptual level, the system analyses question content, identifies topical patterns and either routes or answers the request on the basis of recurring structures already known to the library. This allows staff attention to be concentrated on more complex requests that still require human expertise. The impact includes faster handling of standard enquiries, better sorting of incoming messages and reduced pressure on library staff. More broadly, Vesta creates value by modernising user support in a high-volume cultural institution and by providing a scalable approach to repetitive information requests in library services.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-258,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00253,EU_PSTW_202609_00253,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Heritage Protection Board,"Recreation, culture and religion n.e.c.","Museum collections require regular monitoring, but traditional inventory work can be labour-intensive, repetitive and difficult to prioritise when institutions hold large numbers of objects with different materials, conditions and preservation needs. The operational challenge is therefore not only to record what is in the collection, but also to identify which objects require attention and when, so that staff time can be used where it creates the greatest preservation value.",Kratt Sälli - Organising museum inventory,"Heritage Protection Board; Central-Government; Recreation, culture and religion n.e.c.; Estonia","The implementation is described as a prototype using a forecasting model to predict the ageing of museum objects. In the longer term, the use case can strengthen cultural-heritage management by helping institutions protect collections more systematically and efficiently.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Kratt Sälli was developed by Estonia’s National Heritage Board to support museums in managing preservation and inventory activities more efficiently. Museum collections require regular monitoring, but traditional inventory work can be labour-intensive, repetitive and difficult to prioritise when institutions hold large numbers of objects with different materials, conditions and preservation needs. The operational challenge is therefore not only to record what is in the collection, but also to identify which objects require attention and when, so that staff time can be used where it creates the greatest preservation value. The objective of Sälli is to make inventory processes faster, more convenient and more need-based. The implementation is described as a prototype using a forecasting model to predict the ageing of museum objects. By analysing available information on collection items and their preservation characteristics, the system can help estimate which objects may be more likely to deteriorate and should therefore be prioritised for inspection. This supports a shift from routine, calendar-based inventory towards targeted monitoring driven by risk and need. The expected benefits include savings in staff hours, better allocation of preservation resources, earlier identification of vulnerable objects and improved planning of museum inventory work. In the longer term, the use case can strengthen cultural-heritage management by helping institutions protect collections more systematically and efficiently.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-259,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00254,EU_PSTW_202609_00254,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Heritage Protection Board,"Recreation, culture and religion n.e.c.","The National Heritage Board sought to improve how digital objects are described within cultural collections, since manual description is time-consuming, can vary in quality and limits how quickly collections can be made accessible to the public. Heritage institutions increasingly manage large volumes of digital material, yet catalogue enrichment often remains labour-intensive and difficult to scale.",Kratt Folly - Recognition of museum objects,"Heritage Protection Board; Central-Government; Recreation, culture and religion n.e.c.; Estonia","The stakeholders include the National Heritage Board, memory-institution staff and users of cultural collections, with the objective of increasing data quality, using staff time more intelligently and improving public access to described digital objects. The solution is implemented as a prototype recognition system designed to identify and support description of digital collection items.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The National Heritage Board sought to improve how digital objects are described within cultural collections, since manual description is time-consuming, can vary in quality and limits how quickly collections can be made accessible to the public. Heritage institutions increasingly manage large volumes of digital material, yet catalogue enrichment often remains labour-intensive and difficult to scale. Kratt Folly was developed to address this challenge by moving closer to automated description of museum and heritage objects. The stakeholders include the National Heritage Board, memory-institution staff and users of cultural collections, with the objective of increasing data quality, using staff time more intelligently and improving public access to described digital objects. The solution is implemented as a prototype recognition system designed to identify and support description of digital collection items. At a conceptual level, the system applies image-recognition and object-description techniques to digital heritage material so that descriptive metadata can be generated or suggested automatically, reducing the need for fully manual catalogue creation. This creates a workflow in which automation supports curatorial work rather than replacing institutional expertise. The expected impact includes richer and more consistent object descriptions, more efficient use of staff capacity and better accessibility of collections for end users. More broadly, Kratt Folly creates value by supporting scalable digital heritage management and by helping cultural institutions make their collections more searchable, understandable and usable.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK|F06_HIGH_VOLUME_SCALABILITY_BOTTLENECK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-260,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00255,EU_PSTW_202609_00255,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Estonian Unemployment Insurance Fund,Unemployment.,UNKNOWN,Kratt for analysing customer conversations - Finding the root causes of customer inquiries,Estonian Unemployment Insurance Fund; Central-Government; Unemployment.; Estonia,"From April to August 2019, the organisation worked with Feelingstream on a pilot to identify the root causes of customer calls and use those insights to improve the overall service experience. The stakeholders include the Unemployment Insurance Fund, its customer-service teams and the people using its services, with the objective of reducing unnecessary calls, improving e-environments and addressing the issues most frequently driving customer contact.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Estonian Unemployment Insurance Fund needed a better understanding of why customers were contacting its service channels, particularly because recurring enquiry patterns were placing pressure on call volumes and indicated friction in digital services and grant-related communication. From April to August 2019, the organisation worked with Feelingstream on a pilot to identify the root causes of customer calls and use those insights to improve the overall service experience. The stakeholders include the Unemployment Insurance Fund, its customer-service teams and the people using its services, with the objective of reducing unnecessary calls, improving e-environments and addressing the issues most frequently driving customer contact. The solution was implemented through Feelingstream’s multilingual contact-analysis application, which transcribed calls and analysed them in order to detect patterns in customer concerns. At a conceptual level, the system used speech transcription and language-analysis techniques to convert conversations into structured insights, enabling the organisation to see not only what people were asking about, but also when and why certain topics became especially prominent. The pilot showed, for example, that enquiries linked to the receipt of grants rose significantly at the beginning of the month. The impact included actionable insight for service redesign, improvements to digital channels and a stronger basis for reducing avoidable demand, creating value through better customer experience and more efficient service management.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-261,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00256,EU_PSTW_202609_00256,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,University of Tartu,R&D education.,UNKNOWN,Text-to-speech synthesis in Estonian,University of Tartu; Academic-Research; R&D education.; Estonia,"This use case was developed to improve the quality of synthetic speech in Estonian and support applications that require clearer, more human-like spoken output. The solution was implemented by the University of Tartu language-technology research group as a neural-network-based speech-synthesis prototype trained on a corpus of Estonian news.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Estonian-language digital services need speech technologies that sound natural and handle linguistic details accurately, yet earlier synthesis methods often produced output that was less convincing in rhythm, intonation and pronunciation. This use case was developed to improve the quality of synthetic speech in Estonian and support applications that require clearer, more human-like spoken output. The stakeholders include language-technology researchers, developers and future users of Estonian speech services, with the objective of producing a more natural-sounding synthesis system that can better handle the specific features of the language. The solution was implemented by the University of Tartu language-technology research group as a neural-network-based speech-synthesis prototype trained on a corpus of Estonian news. At a conceptual level, the model learns speech patterns from recorded data and can currently imitate the voices of six different speakers, allowing it to generate spoken output with more realistic sound and prosody than previous methods. The project reports particular strengths in natural intonation, as well as pronunciation of numbers, symbols and abbreviations. Although the system remains under development and is not yet considered complete, it already marks a qualitative improvement over earlier synthesis approaches. The impact lies in more natural and intelligible Estonian speech generation, stronger technical capability for future voice services and a better basis for accessible digital applications. More broadly, the prototype creates value by advancing language technology for a smaller language community.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-262,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00257,EU_PSTW_202609_00257,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Tax and Customs Board,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.","The operational challenge is that tax authorities must decide where to focus limited inspection resources, and conventional review methods can make it difficult to distinguish higher-risk entities from the wider taxpaying population quickly and consistently.",Effective tax collection - Identifying companies or person in need for inspection,"Tax and Customs Board; Central-Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Estonia","The solution is implemented as an analytical model built on information already available in tax returns, such as turnover and related reporting patterns. At a conceptual level, the system uses predictive modelling to estimate the probability that a company is paying envelope salaries and to support the selection of suitable inspection targets.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Estonia’s Tax and Customs Board has explored how advanced analytical methods could improve tax collection by helping inspectors identify companies more likely to be engaged in undeclared wage payments, often referred to as envelope salaries. The operational challenge is that tax authorities must decide where to focus limited inspection resources, and conventional review methods can make it difficult to distinguish higher-risk entities from the wider taxpaying population quickly and consistently. The stakeholders include the Tax and Customs Board, tax inspectors and compliant businesses affected by the overall fairness of the system, with the objective of improving inspection targeting, increasing efficiency in tax enforcement and strengthening the board’s ability to detect hidden payroll practices. The solution is implemented as an analytical model built on information already available in tax returns, such as turnover and related reporting patterns. At a conceptual level, the system uses predictive modelling to estimate the probability that a company is paying envelope salaries and to support the selection of suitable inspection targets. This does not replace tax investigators’ judgement, but creates a structured risk signal that can be integrated into enforcement workflows. The expected impact is more focused inspections, better use of enforcement capacity and stronger support for fairer tax collection. More broadly, the project creates value by enabling more evidence-based compliance work and improving the efficiency of public revenue protection.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-263,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00258,EU_PSTW_202609_00258,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Ministry for Foreign Affairs of Finland,R&D general public services.,"Manual monitoring of such discussions is difficult to sustain at scale and may fail to provide timely insight for communications and policy support. The expected impact includes quicker access to public-discourse insight, reduced manual review effort and more consistent monitoring of high-volume digital communication.",Auwi SaaS - Real-time AI-run analysis from millions of message,Ministry for Foreign Affairs of Finland; Central-Government; R&D general public services.; Finland,"The use case therefore involved ministry teams responsible for analysis and public-information awareness, with the objective of turning high-volume online discussion into actionable intelligence that could support faster interpretation of emerging themes and sentiment. The solution was implemented through the Aiwo Digital platform, a software-as-a-service environment designed to process and analyse millions of messages in real time.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Finnish Ministry for Foreign Affairs needed a faster and more scalable way to understand public discussion during the coronavirus crisis, particularly on social media, where large volumes of posts can shape perceptions, reveal concerns and influence the wider information environment. Manual monitoring of such discussions is difficult to sustain at scale and may fail to provide timely insight for communications and policy support. The use case therefore involved ministry teams responsible for analysis and public-information awareness, with the objective of turning high-volume online discussion into actionable intelligence that could support faster interpretation of emerging themes and sentiment. The solution was implemented through the Aiwo Digital platform, a software-as-a-service environment designed to process and analyse millions of messages in real time. At a conceptual level, the platform applies automated language analysis and classification methods to large streams of social-media content, then presents the results through dashboards and structured findings that are intended to support decision-makers directly. In this case, the system was used to analyse coronavirus-related discussions on Twitter and convert dispersed online conversation into clearer analytical outputs. The expected impact includes quicker access to public-discourse insight, reduced manual review effort and more consistent monitoring of high-volume digital communication. More broadly, the use case creates value by improving situational awareness, supporting evidence-based communications and enabling public institutions to interpret online discussion at a scale that would be difficult to manage through conventional methods alone.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,NOT_IN_USE,미사용,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK|F06_HIGH_VOLUME_SCALABILITY_BOTTLENECK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-264,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00259,EU_PSTW_202609_00259,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Helen,Fuel and energy,UNKNOWN,Predicting Heat Energy Consumption,Helen; Private sector; Fuel and energy; Finland,"Helen introduced this use case in the City of Helsinki to improve the forecasting of district heat demand, a critical operational task for an energy provider serving an urban environment where consumption fluctuates according to weather, seasonality, building behaviour and customer needs. The objective of the use case is to support more precise short-term and medium-term planning by reducing the gap between expected and actual heat consumption.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Helen introduced this use case in the City of Helsinki to improve the forecasting of district heat demand, a critical operational task for an energy provider serving an urban environment where consumption fluctuates according to weather, seasonality, building behaviour and customer needs. Accurate heat-demand forecasting is essential for balancing supply and demand, planning production capacity, reducing operational inefficiencies and ensuring reliable service delivery to households, businesses and public facilities. The objective of the use case is to support more precise short-term and medium-term planning by reducing the gap between expected and actual heat consumption. The AI solution uses historical energy-consumption data together with local weather forecasts to predict future heat demand more accurately. At a conceptual level, the model identifies patterns between previous demand levels, meteorological conditions and consumption behaviour, then applies these relationships to forecast upcoming energy needs. This information can be integrated into Helen’s operational planning processes to support production scheduling and resource optimisation. The reported impact is significant: the solution has reduced errors between forecast and actual heat demand by one third. This improves reliability, supports more efficient use of energy-production assets, reduces unnecessary operational costs and strengthens Helsinki’s ability to manage urban energy services in a more data-driven and sustainable way.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-265,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00260,EU_PSTW_202609_00260,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Helsinki Region Environmental Services Authority HSY,Housing and community amenities n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,Sewage - Improving Sewage Pipe Network predicting sewer blockages,Helsinki Region Environmental Services Authority HSY; Local Government; Housing and community amenities n.e.c.; Finland,"Helsinki Region Environmental Services Authority HSY implemented this use case to improve the maintenance of a sewage network extending over more than 3,000 kilometres.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Helsinki Region Environmental Services Authority HSY implemented this use case to improve the maintenance of a sewage network extending over more than 3,000 kilometres. Sewer blockages create operational, environmental and service-continuity risks, as they can lead to local disruptions, emergency interventions, overflow incidents and higher maintenance costs. Traditional sewer cleaning often relies on fixed schedules or reactive responses after problems occur, which may not direct resources towards the sections most likely to fail. HSY therefore sought a more targeted approach to preventive maintenance, enabling cleaning teams to focus on drain ranges where the risk of blockage is highest. The AI solution, Smartvatten Neuro, predicts possible blockages by analysing sewage-network data and identifying pipe sections that show characteristics associated with blockage-prone areas. The system produces recommendations on where cleaning should be prioritised, allowing maintenance activity to be planned according to predicted risk rather than general assumptions alone. By combining operational data with predictive modelling, the solution supports earlier intervention and better allocation of maintenance capacity. The experience showed that the system can predict blockages effectively and that up to one third of annual sewer blockages could be avoided. The main benefits are reduced disruption, more efficient field operations, lower avoidable maintenance costs and improved reliability of wastewater services across the Helsinki region.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-266,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00261,EU_PSTW_202609_00261,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,General Directorate of Health,Health n.e.c.,"Processing these reports efficiently is difficult because staff must classify cases, identify the medically significant elements and spot unusual patterns, all while maintaining speed and consistency across a high-volume workflow. The expected impact is faster case handling, more reliable identification of significant signals and reduced manual burden on specialist teams.",Optimize the processing of reports of adverse health events,General Directorate of Health; Central-Government; Health n.e.c.; France,"This use case therefore addresses the operational need to strengthen pharmacovigilance and health-safety monitoring by helping analysts and case handlers prioritise their effort more effectively. The solution is implemented using the full body of adverse-event reports as a data source for automated classification, concept extraction and anomaly detection.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Health authorities receive large numbers of adverse health event reports, and each submission may contain a mixture of structured details, free-text descriptions and signals of varying relevance or urgency. Processing these reports efficiently is difficult because staff must classify cases, identify the medically significant elements and spot unusual patterns, all while maintaining speed and consistency across a high-volume workflow. This use case therefore addresses the operational need to strengthen pharmacovigilance and health-safety monitoring by helping analysts and case handlers prioritise their effort more effectively. The stakeholders include health-agency professionals, safety-monitoring teams and decision-makers responsible for the assessment of reported incidents, with the objective of improving the speed, quality and consistency of report handling while preserving human oversight of the final assessment. The solution is implemented using the full body of adverse-event reports as a data source for automated classification, concept extraction and anomaly detection. At a conceptual level, the system applies natural language processing and classification models to identify relevant business concepts within reports, categorise incoming cases and highlight submissions that deviate from expected patterns and may require closer attention. These outputs can then be integrated into existing review workflows to support prioritisation and triage. The expected impact is faster case handling, more reliable identification of significant signals and reduced manual burden on specialist teams. More broadly, the approach improves scalability, strengthens health surveillance and creates value through more timely and effective monitoring of adverse events.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK|F06_HIGH_VOLUME_SCALABILITY_BOTTLENECK,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-267,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00262,EU_PSTW_202609_00262,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Bordeaux University Hospital Center,Hospital services.,"Although the information exists within the record, it may be scattered across notes, reports and historical documentation, making it difficult to retrieve quickly in a busy clinical environment.",Facilitate access to patient information - Semantic search in patient records,Bordeaux University Hospital Center; Local Government; Hospital services.; France,"This use case addresses that practical problem by improving how healthcare staff access and visualise patient information, with the aim of reducing time spent on searching and allowing more attention to be devoted to care delivery. The stakeholders include nurses, physicians and other healthcare professionals who rely on the computerised patient record, with the objective of making clinically relevant information easier to find and use at the point of care.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"In hospitals and care settings, clinicians and nursing staff often lose valuable time searching through lengthy electronic patient records to locate relevant information needed for treatment, coordination or follow-up. Although the information exists within the record, it may be scattered across notes, reports and historical documentation, making it difficult to retrieve quickly in a busy clinical environment. This use case addresses that practical problem by improving how healthcare staff access and visualise patient information, with the aim of reducing time spent on searching and allowing more attention to be devoted to care delivery. The stakeholders include nurses, physicians and other healthcare professionals who rely on the computerised patient record, with the objective of making clinically relevant information easier to find and use at the point of care. The solution is implemented through a semantic-search prototype for electronic patient records. At a conceptual level, the system uses language-processing techniques to interpret the meaning of search queries and match them to relevant content across structured and unstructured patient data, rather than relying only on exact keyword matching. Results are then presented in a way that helps staff navigate the record more efficiently. The expected impact is quicker access to information, reduced administrative friction and improved workflow efficiency for clinical teams. In practical terms, the use case enhances the user experience, supports more reliable information retrieval and creates value by reducing wasted time in healthcare processes.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-268,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00263,EU_PSTW_202609_00263,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Institute for Radiation Protection and Nuclear Safety,R&D health.,"The challenge is not only to collect exposure data, but also to interpret it in a way that supports personalised follow-up and allows unusual patterns or potential anomalies to be detected early.",Ionising radiation exposure monitoring information system (SISERI),Institute for Radiation Protection and Nuclear Safety; Central-Government; R&D health.; France,"This use case therefore focuses on strengthening the SISERI monitoring system so that exposure oversight becomes more targeted, responsive and effective. The solution is implemented using data from the ionising radiation exposure monitoring information system itself.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Monitoring occupational exposure to ionising radiation is a critical public-health and workplace-safety responsibility, particularly for workers whose roles involve repeated or potentially high-risk exposure. The challenge is not only to collect exposure data, but also to interpret it in a way that supports personalised follow-up and allows unusual patterns or potential anomalies to be detected early. This use case therefore focuses on strengthening the SISERI monitoring system so that exposure oversight becomes more targeted, responsive and effective. The stakeholders include occupational-health professionals, radiation-protection specialists, regulatory bodies and exposed workers, with the objective of improving individual monitoring and supporting earlier identification of cases that may require investigation or intervention. The solution is implemented using data from the ionising radiation exposure monitoring information system itself. At a conceptual level, the system applies analytical models to historical and current exposure records in order to profile normal and abnormal patterns, support personalised monitoring of worker exposure and flag cases that diverge from expected ranges or trajectories. These outputs can then feed into occupational-health and regulatory workflows for review and follow-up. The expected impact is improved detection of anomalies, more tailored exposure monitoring and better use of specialist oversight capacity. More broadly, the approach strengthens safety governance, improves the reliability of monitoring processes and creates value by enabling a more scalable and preventive model of radiation-risk management.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-269,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00264,EU_PSTW_202609_00264,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,General Directorate of Local Authorities,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.","This creates a substantial operational burden, particularly where routine sorting and first-level screening absorb time that could otherwise be dedicated to the most sensitive or legally significant cases. The expected impact is reduced manual workload, faster processing of incoming acts and more focused allocation of expert attention.",Acts - Develop Artificial Intelligence in dematerialised legality control,"General Directorate of Local Authorities; Central-Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; France",The use case therefore addresses the need to make dematerialised legality control more efficient and better targeted. The solution is implemented using data from the @ctes system to automate sorting and highlight information requiring closer inspection.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Prefectures receive large volumes of administrative acts through the @ctes (Acts) application as part of the legality-control process, and agents must determine which acts are transmissible, which are not, and which elements merit priority review. This creates a substantial operational burden, particularly where routine sorting and first-level screening absorb time that could otherwise be dedicated to the most sensitive or legally significant cases. The use case therefore addresses the need to make dematerialised legality control more efficient and better targeted. The stakeholders include prefecture agents and supervisory authorities responsible for legality review, with the objective of simplifying intake, improving prioritisation and strengthening the consistency of initial case handling. The solution is implemented using data from the @ctes system to automate sorting and highlight information requiring closer inspection. At a conceptual level, the system combines classification techniques with information-extraction models to distinguish transmissible from non-transmissible acts and to identify the legal or administrative features that should be checked as a priority. These results are integrated into the agents’ workflow as decision-support outputs rather than replacing legal judgement. The expected impact is reduced manual workload, faster processing of incoming acts and more focused allocation of expert attention. In operational terms, the approach improves scalability, enhances reliability in triage and creates value by making legality control more efficient without weakening oversight.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-270,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00265,EU_PSTW_202609_00265,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,General Directorate of the National Gendarmerie,Police Services,UNKNOWN,Improve the online pre-complaint system - Detecting infringements automatically,General Directorate of the National Gendarmerie; Central-Government; Police Services; France,This use case addresses that inefficiency by improving the quality of online pre-complaints from the start. The solution is implemented through automated infringement detection and question-generation capabilities applied to online pre-complaint data.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Online pre-complaint services help citizens begin reporting offences before direct contact with law-enforcement officers, but these submissions are not always complete or sufficiently precise to be treated immediately as fully qualified complaints. As a result, complainants may need to repeat information and gendarmerie staff must spend additional time clarifying the nature of the infringement and collecting missing details. This use case addresses that inefficiency by improving the quality of online pre-complaints from the start. The stakeholders include complainants, gendarmerie officers and administrative staff, with the objective of transforming initial digital submissions into better-qualified complaints, reducing duplication and accelerating downstream case handling. The solution is implemented through automated infringement detection and question-generation capabilities applied to online pre-complaint data. At a conceptual level, the system analyses the content entered by the user, identifies the likely type of offence and determines which additional questions should be asked to complete the complaint in a structured way. By dynamically enriching the submission, the tool helps convert a preliminary account into a more usable case file before officer review. The expected impact is time savings for both citizens and law-enforcement personnel, better-quality complaint information and smoother intake processing. More broadly, the approach improves service responsiveness, enhances the user experience and creates operational value through more efficient digital complaint management.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-271,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00266,EU_PSTW_202609_00266,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,General Directorate of Customs and Indirect Duties,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.","The difficulty is that misleading or inaccurate descriptions can be used to place goods in a tax-advantaged category improperly, and manual detection of such cases is challenging across high volumes of declarations. The expected impact is more accurate detection of suspicious declarations, reduced manual screening effort and better targeting of enforcement resources.",Identify false customs declarations using text mining,"General Directorate of Customs and Indirect Duties; Central-Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; France",This use case therefore focuses on improving the fight against customs-duty fraud by helping inspectors identify potentially false declarations more efficiently. The solution is implemented by applying text-mining techniques to product descriptions contained in single administrative documents.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Customs authorities must review product descriptions in administrative documents to ensure that imported goods are declared under the correct nomenclature, particularly where some classifications attract more favourable tax treatment. The difficulty is that misleading or inaccurate descriptions can be used to place goods in a tax-advantaged category improperly, and manual detection of such cases is challenging across high volumes of declarations. This use case therefore focuses on improving the fight against customs-duty fraud by helping inspectors identify potentially false declarations more efficiently. The stakeholders include customs officers, fraud investigators and revenue authorities, with the objective of strengthening detection capability, improving prioritisation of controls and protecting public revenue. The solution is implemented by applying text-mining techniques to product descriptions contained in single administrative documents. At a conceptual level, the system analyses language patterns in the declared goods, compares them with expected nomenclature characteristics and flags cases where the textual description appears inconsistent with the claimed product category. These outputs can then support targeted inspection and further verification by customs staff. The expected impact is more accurate detection of suspicious declarations, reduced manual screening effort and better targeting of enforcement resources. In practical and financial terms, the approach enhances scalability, improves reliability in fraud detection and creates value by supporting more effective customs control and reducing losses linked to incorrect duty classification.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK|F06_HIGH_VOLUME_SCALABILITY_BOTTLENECK,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-272,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00267,EU_PSTW_202609_00267,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,National Institute of Statistics and Economic Studies,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.","However, declarations provided in census data may be incomplete, ambiguous or inconsistent with the official business register, creating a manual correction burden for agents and introducing inefficiencies into the production chain. More broadly, the approach enhances reliability, reduces manual effort and creates value by strengthening the quality and timeliness of official census-based statistics.",Improve the identification of the employing establishment in the population census,"National Institute of Statistics and Economic Studies; Central-Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; France",This use case addresses that problem by helping staff reconcile census declarations with the SIRENE database of companies and establishments more accurately and efficiently. The solution is implemented through a recommendation engine built on census declarations and the SIRENE register.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"In population-census processing, accurately identifying a respondent’s employing establishment is essential for producing reliable statistics on commuting and home-to-work patterns. However, declarations provided in census data may be incomplete, ambiguous or inconsistent with the official business register, creating a manual correction burden for agents and introducing inefficiencies into the production chain. This use case addresses that problem by helping staff reconcile census declarations with the SIRENE database of companies and establishments more accurately and efficiently. The stakeholders include census-processing agents, statistical-production teams and data users who depend on robust mobility and labour-market statistics, with the objective of improving data quality and streamlining correction work. The solution is implemented through a recommendation engine built on census declarations and the SIRENE register. At a conceptual level, the system compares the establishment information entered by respondents with relevant entities in the business database and proposes likely matches to support agent review and correction. This creates a decision-support layer that helps standardise identification work while preserving human validation of the final result. The expected impact is greater precision in the home-to-work statistics produced by INSEE and improved efficiency across the data-production process. More broadly, the approach enhances reliability, reduces manual effort and creates value by strengthening the quality and timeliness of official census-based statistics.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-273,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00268,EU_PSTW_202609_00268,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,New Aquitaine Chambers of Trades and Crafts,"General economic, commercial and labour affair","Choosing the right location is a decisive factor for the viability of a new craft business, yet artisans often have limited access to clear, evidence-based information on the characteristics of a territory before committing to an installation decision. Local demand, demographic structure and the existing economic fabric can all influence whether a craft activity is likely to thrive, but assessing these elements in a systematic way is difficult for individual entrepreneurs and for the public bodies that advise them.",Develop a tool to help the establishment of craft businesses,"New Aquitaine Chambers of Trades and Crafts; Regional Government; General economic, commercial and labour affair; France",This use case therefore addresses the need to support craftsmen more effectively when selecting where to establish their business. The solution is implemented by combining data on craft businesses with population data in order to estimate the likelihood of success for a given type of activity in a given area.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Choosing the right location is a decisive factor for the viability of a new craft business, yet artisans often have limited access to clear, evidence-based information on the characteristics of a territory before committing to an installation decision. Local demand, demographic structure and the existing economic fabric can all influence whether a craft activity is likely to thrive, but assessing these elements in a systematic way is difficult for individual entrepreneurs and for the public bodies that advise them. This use case therefore addresses the need to support craftsmen more effectively when selecting where to establish their business. The stakeholders include artisans, local economic-development actors and advisory services, with the objective of improving business-location decisions, reducing the risk of failure and strengthening the fit between craft activity and territorial opportunity. The solution is implemented by combining data on craft businesses with population data in order to estimate the likelihood of success for a given type of activity in a given area. At a conceptual level, the system uses predictive modelling and territorial analysis to relate business characteristics, local market conditions and demographic patterns, then translates these into decision-support outputs for advisers and business founders. The expected impact is better-informed establishment choices, improved quality of support to craftsmen and more efficient local economic guidance. More broadly, the approach can enhance business survival prospects, reduce wasted investment and create value through smarter alignment between entrepreneurship and territorial demand.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-274,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00269,EU_PSTW_202609_00269,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,National Institute for the Industrial Environment and Risks,Pollution abatement.,"In practice, chemical analyses generate complex spectral outputs whose similarities and differences can be difficult to assess consistently at scale, especially when many compounds or trace signatures must be compared across multiple samples.",AI to Identify molecules contaminating the environment and the sources of pollution,National Institute for the Industrial Environment and Risks; Central-Government; Pollution abatement.; France,This use case addresses that challenge by improving how analysts detect polluting molecules and interpret the relationships between molecular spectra. The solution is implemented by applying analytical models to the results of chemical analyses in order to compare spectra and objectify their proximity or divergence.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Environmental monitoring depends on the ability to identify contaminating substances accurately and to understand whether different samples point to the same pollutant source or to distinct pollution events. In practice, chemical analyses generate complex spectral outputs whose similarities and differences can be difficult to assess consistently at scale, especially when many compounds or trace signatures must be compared across multiple samples. This use case addresses that challenge by improving how analysts detect polluting molecules and interpret the relationships between molecular spectra. The stakeholders include environmental laboratories, pollution-monitoring authorities and investigators responsible for tracing contamination sources, with the objective of strengthening environmental surveillance and making the identification of pollutants more precise and operationally useful. The solution is implemented by applying analytical models to the results of chemical analyses in order to compare spectra and objectify their proximity or divergence. At a conceptual level, the system uses pattern-recognition techniques on molecular signatures to highlight recurring forms, distinguish substances more reliably and support the identification of contaminants present in environmental samples. This can also help investigators infer whether pollution may stem from similar or distinct origins. The expected impact is more accurate detection of polluting molecules, faster interpretation of analytical results and stronger support for source identification. In practical terms, the approach improves the reliability and scalability of environmental analysis and creates value by enabling more targeted pollution control and remediation efforts.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-275,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00270,EU_PSTW_202609_00270,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Directorate-General for Enterprises - Strategic Information and Economic Security Service,Economic affairs n.e.c,"This is a complex task because the relevant signals may be dispersed across investment files and external information sources, making manual assessment time-consuming and potentially uneven across cases.",AI to identify entities presenting a risk to economic interests,Directorate-General for Enterprises - Strategic Information and Economic Security Service; Central-Government; Economic affairs n.e.c; France,The use case therefore focuses on improving the processing of foreign investment dossiers and reinforcing economic intelligence capabilities. The solution is implemented by combining information from foreign-investment requests in France with external data sources to detect and characterise entities that may warrant closer examination.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Authorities responsible for reviewing foreign investment requests must assess not only the immediate transaction but also the broader profile of the entities involved, including whether they may present risks to national economic interests. This is a complex task because the relevant signals may be dispersed across investment files and external information sources, making manual assessment time-consuming and potentially uneven across cases. The use case therefore focuses on improving the processing of foreign investment dossiers and reinforcing economic intelligence capabilities. The stakeholders include public officials reviewing investments, economic-security specialists and decision-makers concerned with strategic assets and national interests, with the objective of identifying higher-risk entities earlier and supporting more consistent risk analysis in investment screening. The solution is implemented by combining information from foreign-investment requests in France with external data sources to detect and characterise entities that may warrant closer examination. At a conceptual level, the system uses entity analysis, cross-source matching and risk-scoring techniques to surface patterns, links or attributes associated with potential threats to France’s economic interests. These outputs are then used to support file processing and prioritisation rather than replace expert judgement. The expected impact is faster and more focused treatment of investment files, improved detection of strategic risks and better use of specialist review capacity. More broadly, the approach strengthens economic-security oversight, improves analytical consistency and creates public value through more informed handling of sensitive foreign investments.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-276,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00271,EU_PSTW_202609_00271,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Council of State,Law courts,UNKNOWN,Group disputes together with AI for a joint decision,Council of State; Central-Government; Law courts; France,This use case addresses that operational inefficiency by helping courts detect disputes that should be grouped together for joint handling or harmonised decision-making. The solution is implemented by analysing the body of requests submitted to administrative courts in order to identify recurring themes and detect case series automatically.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Administrative courts receive large numbers of claims, and many disputes may concern the same issue, legal basis or factual pattern even if they are filed separately. When these related cases are not identified early, judges and court staff may process them individually, increasing workload, delaying resolution and missing opportunities for more coherent treatment of series litigation. This use case addresses that operational inefficiency by helping courts detect disputes that should be grouped together for joint handling or harmonised decision-making. The stakeholders include administrative judges, court clerks and justice-system managers, with the objective of improving the efficiency of administrative justice while preserving consistency across similar cases. The solution is implemented by analysing the body of requests submitted to administrative courts in order to identify recurring themes and detect case series automatically. At a conceptual level, the system uses document comparison, clustering and thematic similarity techniques to reconcile claims dealing with the same matter and highlight groups of disputes that are likely to benefit from coordinated review. These groupings can then support judicial organisation and case-management decisions. The expected impact is faster processing of repetitive litigation, better consistency in decisions and more efficient allocation of judicial resources. In operational terms, the approach improves scalability in court administration, reduces duplicated effort and creates value by supporting a more streamlined and coherent handling of high-volume administrative disputes.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-277,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00272,EU_PSTW_202609_00272,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Directorate-General for Competition, Consumer Affairs and Fraud Control","General economic, commercial and labour affair",UNKNOWN,"AI for detecting mandatory information and unfair terms in contracts, quotes and invoices","Directorate-General for Competition, Consumer Affairs and Fraud Control; Central-Government; General economic, commercial and labour affair; France","The use case therefore addresses the need to strengthen consumer-market oversight while reducing the manual burden involved in document review. The solution is implemented by analysing reports and documents collected during controls together with the decisions, opinions and recommendations of the Unfair Terms Commission.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Consumer-protection authorities must examine contracts, quotations and invoices to verify whether mandatory information is present and whether contractual terms may be unfair, misleading or detrimental to consumers. This work is legally important but highly document-intensive, especially when investigators recover large numbers of business records during controls and must compare them with prior decisions, opinions and recommendations. The use case therefore addresses the need to strengthen consumer-market oversight while reducing the manual burden involved in document review. The stakeholders include DGCCRF investigators, legal analysts and market-regulation teams, with the objective of improving the detection of non-compliance, enhancing consumer protection and supporting fairer competition. The solution is implemented by analysing reports and documents collected during controls together with the decisions, opinions and recommendations of the Unfair Terms Commission. At a conceptual level, the system uses information extraction, clause classification and thematic analysis to detect mandatory information, identify the subject matter of contract clauses and flag terms that may be unfair according to existing regulatory and interpretative sources. These outputs support investigators in targeting the most problematic documents for closer review. The expected impact is quicker identification of unlawful or problematic contractual content, more consistent enforcement activity and better use of investigative capacity. More broadly, the approach improves reliability, increases scalability in market controls and creates value through stronger consumer protection and more effective competitive regulation.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-278,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00273,EU_PSTW_202609_00273,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,National Institute for Geographic and Forest Information,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.","Detecting these discontinuities is technically important because unrecognised shifts may distort downstream analyses, yet manual identification can be difficult across large volumes of satellite-derived positioning data. The expected impact is improved quality of reference-point data, more reliable maintenance of positioning systems and reduced manual effort in monitoring.",AI to identify breaks in antenna's positioning of terrestrial reference points from satellite data,"National Institute for Geographic and Forest Information; Central-Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; France",This use case therefore focuses on improving the monitoring of reference-point measurements so that anomalies can be recognised and incorporated into quality control more systematically. The solution is implemented by analysing satellite-based measurements from permanent antennas in order to identify breaks in the observed position series automatically.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Permanent GNSS antennas are essential to the quality of terrestrial reference systems, but interruptions or breaks in their position measurements can degrade the reliability of the data used for geodetic calculations and international reference frameworks. Detecting these discontinuities is technically important because unrecognised shifts may distort downstream analyses, yet manual identification can be difficult across large volumes of satellite-derived positioning data. This use case therefore focuses on improving the monitoring of reference-point measurements so that anomalies can be recognised and incorporated into quality control more systematically. The stakeholders include geodesy specialists, reference-system managers and institutions responsible for maintaining positioning infrastructure, with the objective of preserving the accuracy and consistency of the international terrestrial reference system. The solution is implemented by analysing satellite-based measurements from permanent antennas in order to identify breaks in the observed position series automatically. At a conceptual level, the system uses time-series analysis and anomaly-detection techniques to distinguish genuine discontinuities from normal variation and to flag measurement segments requiring adjustment or expert review. These results can then be integrated into geodetic processing workflows so that identified breaks are taken into account appropriately. The expected impact is improved quality of reference-point data, more reliable maintenance of positioning systems and reduced manual effort in monitoring. In broader terms, the approach enhances precision, supports scalability in geodetic operations and creates value by strengthening a foundational layer of spatial and navigation infrastructure.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK|F06_HIGH_VOLUME_SCALABILITY_BOTTLENECK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-279,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00274,EU_PSTW_202609_00274,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Court of Cassation,Law courts,Detecting these discrepancies early is difficult when case law volumes are high and legal reasoning is dispersed across multiple databases and courts.,Identify divergences in case law - Detection of divergent interpretations of the law,Court of Cassation; Central-Government; Law courts; France,This use case addresses that problem by helping identify decisions that deal with comparable legal issues but may reflect differing interpretations. The solution is implemented using case law data from the Court of Cassation’s Jurinet database and the Courts of Appeal’s JuriCA database.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Courts and legal institutions depend on a coherent interpretation of the law, yet divergences can arise when decisions on the same theme evolve differently across jurisdictions or judicial levels. Detecting these discrepancies early is difficult when case law volumes are high and legal reasoning is dispersed across multiple databases and courts. This use case addresses that problem by helping identify decisions that deal with comparable legal issues but may reflect differing interpretations. The stakeholders include magistrates, legal researchers and institutions responsible for ensuring consistency in jurisprudence, with the objective of improving visibility over divergent case law and supporting a more appropriate and harmonised interpretation of legal rules. The solution is implemented using case law data from the Court of Cassation’s Jurinet database and the Courts of Appeal’s JuriCA database. At a conceptual level, the system reconciles decisions dealing with the same subject through thematic comparison and legal-similarity analysis, then highlights areas where the reasoning or legal outcome appears to diverge. These outputs can support expert review, doctrinal analysis and judicial oversight rather than replacing legal interpretation itself. The expected impact is earlier identification of inconsistent jurisprudence, better support for legal harmonisation and more efficient navigation of large case law collections. More broadly, the approach improves reliability in legal analysis, strengthens institutional coherence and creates public value by supporting clearer and more consistent application of the law.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-280,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00275,EU_PSTW_202609_00275,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Hydrographic and Oceanographic Service of the Navy,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.","For the Hydrographic and Oceanographic Service of the Navy, this creates a significant operational burden, as agents must review large volumes of coastal measurements to ensure that reference data remain reliable for navigation, planning and environmental management. The stakeholders include hydrographic specialists, cartographic teams and public authorities that depend on precise coastal information, with the objective of improving production efficiency and reducing the manual effort involved in updating shoreline data.",Automating coastline correction work - Improve coastal mapping,"Hydrographic and Oceanographic Service of the Navy; Central-Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; France",The use case therefore addresses the need to speed up and standardise coastline correction work while preserving the quality of the French coastal reference base. The solution is implemented using laser remote-sensing measurements collected through lidar surveys along the coast.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Maintaining an accurate national coastline repository is a demanding task because shorelines change over time and raw observation data often require careful correction before they can be used in official mapping. For the Hydrographic and Oceanographic Service of the Navy, this creates a significant operational burden, as agents must review large volumes of coastal measurements to ensure that reference data remain reliable for navigation, planning and environmental management. The use case therefore addresses the need to speed up and standardise coastline correction work while preserving the quality of the French coastal reference base. The stakeholders include hydrographic specialists, cartographic teams and public authorities that depend on precise coastal information, with the objective of improving production efficiency and reducing the manual effort involved in updating shoreline data. The solution is implemented using laser remote-sensing measurements collected through lidar surveys along the coast. At a conceptual level, analytical models are applied to these geospatial point clouds to detect the relevant coastal contours and automate part of the correction work previously carried out by agents. The resulting outputs are then integrated into the production chain for the national coastline repository, where they support validation and refinement. The expected impact is faster updating of coastal maps, greater consistency in correction processes and better use of expert staff time. More broadly, the approach improves scalability, strengthens the reliability of coastal reference data and creates public value through more efficient management of a critical national geospatial asset.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK|F06_HIGH_VOLUME_SCALABILITY_BOTTLENECK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-281,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00276,EU_PSTW_202609_00276,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Monserrato,Police Services,Delays or incomplete information can affect the ability of public authorities and emergency services to intervene effectively.,Safe Spotter - AI platform to detect and transmit data of an accident,Municipality of Monserrato; Local Government; Police Services; Italy,"The use case responds to a critical road-safety challenge: when traffic accidents occur, emergency response depends on the rapid availability of reliable information on what happened, where it happened and which vehicles may be involved. The AI platform uses an advanced network of cameras and sound sensors installed on intelligent street lights.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Safe Spotter was developed for the Municipality of Monserrato as part of a broader effort to create an innovative and sustainable smart-city model focused on citizen protection and territorial management. The use case responds to a critical road-safety challenge: when traffic accidents occur, emergency response depends on the rapid availability of reliable information on what happened, where it happened and which vehicles may be involved. Delays or incomplete information can affect the ability of public authorities and emergency services to intervene effectively. The objective is therefore to strengthen the municipality’s capacity to govern road safety by detecting accidents automatically and transmitting essential data quickly. The AI platform uses an advanced network of cameras and sound sensors installed on intelligent street lights. The sound sensors are designed to recognise noises associated with traffic accidents and activate a directional camera when a relevant event is detected. The system then collects Minimum Safe Data, including images or videos, time, location, vehicle direction and registration number. This information can be transmitted to support situational awareness and response coordination. The expected impact is faster accident detection, improved evidence for emergency intervention, stronger monitoring of road-safety events and better protection of citizens. The use case also demonstrates how urban lighting infrastructure can be repurposed as a platform for smart public-safety services.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-282,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00277,EU_PSTW_202609_00277,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Central Agency of Social Security Bodies (ACOSS):,Social protection n.e.c.,"The Central Agency of Social Security Bodies developed the ACOSS voice-bot to answer questions from users of the Chèque Emploi Associatif, addressing a common service-delivery challenge in social-administration contexts: large volumes of routine enquiries can slow response times and place heavy demand on human support channels.",Voice-bot ACOSS - answering questions from users,Central Agency of Social Security Bodies (ACOSS):; Central-Government; Social protection n.e.c.; France,"The solution was implemented as a conversational voice agent capable of interacting with callers and supplying responses automatically. The voice-bot is no longer in use, but it remains a relevant example of how social-security institutions experimented with conversational voice technology to modernise user support.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Central Agency of Social Security Bodies developed the ACOSS voice-bot to answer questions from users of the Chèque Emploi Associatif, addressing a common service-delivery challenge in social-administration contexts: large volumes of routine enquiries can slow response times and place heavy demand on human support channels. The objective of the initiative was to provide faster, more accessible answers for users while easing pressure on administrative teams responsible for handling repeated questions. The stakeholders included ACOSS, users of the Chèque Emploi Associatif service and customer-support staff, with the intention of improving responsiveness and simplifying access to information by telephone. The solution was implemented as a conversational voice agent capable of interacting with callers and supplying responses automatically. At a conceptual level, the system used voice-based dialogue handling to recognise incoming questions, match them to relevant service information and deliver spoken answers within the call flow, creating an automated first-line support channel for the service. This allowed routine issues to be handled without immediate staff intervention and offered a more scalable way to provide information to users. While detailed performance figures are not provided in the source material, the expected benefits included faster access to answers, reduced administrative workload and more efficient handling of common queries. The voice-bot is no longer in use, but it remains a relevant example of how social-security institutions experimented with conversational voice technology to modernise user support.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,NOT_IN_USE,미사용,F06_HIGH_VOLUME_SCALABILITY_BOTTLENECK,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-283,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00278,EU_PSTW_202609_00278,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,French Biodiversity Agency (AFB):,Protection of biodiversity and landscape.,"Environmental police services must decide where to direct limited inspection resources across large territories and a wide variety of ecological risks, from habitat degradation to unlawful activities affecting protected areas.",Better guide environmental police checks,French Biodiversity Agency (AFB):; Central-Government; Protection of biodiversity and landscape.; France,This use case addresses that challenge by helping the French Biodiversity Agency improve how controls are planned and prioritised. The solution is implemented through analytical methods that help identify patterns of risk and guide control activity towards higher-priority locations or situations.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Environmental police services must decide where to direct limited inspection resources across large territories and a wide variety of ecological risks, from habitat degradation to unlawful activities affecting protected areas. Without strong targeting, inspections may be dispersed too broadly, reducing their deterrent effect and limiting the capacity of authorities to intervene where environmental harm is most likely or most severe. This use case addresses that challenge by helping the French Biodiversity Agency improve how controls are planned and prioritised. The stakeholders include environmental inspectors, biodiversity-protection teams and public authorities responsible for safeguarding natural ecosystems, with the objective of making inspections more efficient and strengthening the effectiveness of environmental enforcement. The solution is implemented through analytical methods that help identify patterns of risk and guide control activity towards higher-priority locations or situations. At a conceptual level, the system combines available environmental, territorial and operational information to support risk-based targeting of checks, enabling inspectors to focus attention where potential infringements or ecological pressures are more likely to occur. These outputs complement, rather than replace, field expertise and enforcement judgement. The expected impact is better allocation of inspection effort, more targeted interventions and stronger protection for vulnerable ecosystems. In operational terms, the approach improves efficiency, supports scalability in environmental policing and creates value by increasing the effectiveness of public controls without requiring a proportional increase in field resources.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-284,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00279,EU_PSTW_202609_00279,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Nuclear Safety Authority (ASN),UNKNOWN,"A major operational challenge was the large volume of information contained in around 20,000 inspection follow-up letters. These documents include observations, recommendations and signals that may be relevant for identifying recurring issues or emerging trends, but manual analysis at this scale is demanding and limits the ability of inspectors to detect patterns across time, sites and themes.",ASN: better use of the data contained in follow-up letter of inspections,Nuclear Safety Authority (ASN); Central-Government; France,"The French Nuclear Safety Authority, ASN, developed this use case to improve how inspection knowledge is exploited in the regulation of nuclear installations. The AI solution applies text-analysis techniques to inspection follow-up letters in order to detect weak signals, extract relevant information and identify statistical trends.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The French Nuclear Safety Authority, ASN, developed this use case to improve how inspection knowledge is exploited in the regulation of nuclear installations. Following the Fukushima accident of 11 March 2011, ASN requested operators on 5 May 2011 to undertake complementary safety assessments of their installations, reinforcing the importance of systematic oversight and evidence-based inspection practices. A major operational challenge was the large volume of information contained in around 20,000 inspection follow-up letters. These documents include observations, recommendations and signals that may be relevant for identifying recurring issues or emerging trends, but manual analysis at this scale is demanding and limits the ability of inspectors to detect patterns across time, sites and themes. The objective was to transform unstructured inspection correspondence into structured information that could support inspectors in their work. The AI solution applies text-analysis techniques to inspection follow-up letters in order to detect weak signals, extract relevant information and identify statistical trends. At a conceptual level, the tool processes regulatory documents, organises their content and makes recurring themes more visible to inspection teams. Its value lies in providing a ready-to-use decision-support capability, improving the exploitation of accumulated inspection evidence, reducing time spent on manual document review and strengthening ASN’s ability to identify cross-cutting safety issues across the nuclear sector.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK|F06_HIGH_VOLUME_SCALABILITY_BOTTLENECK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-285,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00280,EU_PSTW_202609_00280,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Departmental Directorate of Territories and the Sea (DDTM) of L'Hérault,Housing and community amenities n.e.c.,"Manual inspection alone is costly, slow and often unable to provide timely visibility of emerging problems.",Automatically detecting irregularities in occupancy soils,Departmental Directorate of Territories and the Sea (DDTM) of L'Hérault; Central-Government; Housing and community amenities n.e.c.; France,"Monitoring land use across a territory is a complex responsibility, particularly when authorities need to identify irregular developments such as illegal construction, unauthorised land occupation or waste dumping over large areas. This use case, developed by the Departmental Directorate of Territories and the Sea of Hérault, addresses that operational challenge by improving the detection of irregularities in land occupation and use.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Monitoring land use across a territory is a complex responsibility, particularly when authorities need to identify irregular developments such as illegal construction, unauthorised land occupation or waste dumping over large areas. Manual inspection alone is costly, slow and often unable to provide timely visibility of emerging problems. This use case, developed by the Departmental Directorate of Territories and the Sea of Hérault, addresses that operational challenge by improving the detection of irregularities in land occupation and use. The stakeholders include territorial authorities, environmental and planning officers and inspection teams, with the objective of strengthening oversight, accelerating detection and improving the protection of land and natural environments. The solution is implemented through the analysis of aerial and satellite imagery to identify patterns that may indicate unlawful or abnormal land-use activity. At a conceptual level, image-analysis and classification techniques are applied to large-scale geospatial data in order to detect changes or anomalies associated with illegal constructions, dumping or other irregular uses of land. These findings can then be routed into administrative and inspection workflows for verification and follow-up. The expected impact is quicker identification of suspect sites, reduced reliance on labour-intensive visual review and better prioritisation of field controls. More broadly, the approach improves scalability in territorial monitoring, enhances the reliability of detection and creates public value by supporting more effective environmental and land-use enforcement.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-286,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00281,EU_PSTW_202609_00281,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,General Directorate of Food (DGAL) of the Ministry of Agriculture and Food:,Other industries,"Food-safety inspection authorities must allocate limited inspection resources across a large and diverse population of establishments, where risks may vary depending on hygiene practices, customer experience, operational conditions and previous signals.",Targeting establishment inspection - refine targeting of establishments for inspections,General Directorate of Food (DGAL) of the Ministry of Agriculture and Food:; Central-Government; Other industries; France,"The General Directorate of Food of the French Ministry of Agriculture and Food developed this use case to improve the targeting of inspections in establishments that sell or deliver food directly to consumers, including restaurants, catering businesses and distributors. The objective of the use case is to refine the prioritisation of establishments to be inspected by using additional evidence from online consumer reviews.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The General Directorate of Food of the French Ministry of Agriculture and Food developed this use case to improve the targeting of inspections in establishments that sell or deliver food directly to consumers, including restaurants, catering businesses and distributors. Food-safety inspection authorities must allocate limited inspection resources across a large and diverse population of establishments, where risks may vary depending on hygiene practices, customer experience, operational conditions and previous signals. Traditional targeting methods may not fully capture emerging problems or recent consumer concerns, particularly where relevant information appears first in public feedback channels. The objective of the use case is to refine the prioritisation of establishments to be inspected by using additional evidence from online consumer reviews. The AI component analyses reviews published on online platforms to identify signals that may suggest possible food-safety or hygiene concerns. Natural language processing can be used to detect relevant terms, patterns of complaints or sentiment linked to establishments, which can then inform inspection planning alongside existing administrative data and regulatory criteria. The system is intended to support, not replace, professional judgement by inspectors. Its expected impact is a more risk-informed allocation of inspection capacity, earlier identification of establishments requiring attention, better use of publicly available feedback and improved protection of consumers through more targeted food-safety controls.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-287,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00282,EU_PSTW_202609_00282,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Ministry of Labor,Unemployment.,UNKNOWN,Intelligence Emploi,Ministry of Labor; Central-Government; Unemployment.; France,"The use case addresses a central labour-market challenge: employment services must support large numbers of jobseekers with different profiles, skills, barriers and career objectives, while also responding to employer demand and changing labour-market conditions. The AI implementation acts as a lever within employment-service processes, using available labour-market, jobseeker and vacancy-related data to support matching, guidance and decision-making.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Intelligence Emploi was introduced within Pôle emploi as a transformation initiative aimed at accelerating the return to sustainable employment. The use case addresses a central labour-market challenge: employment services must support large numbers of jobseekers with different profiles, skills, barriers and career objectives, while also responding to employer demand and changing labour-market conditions. Public employment counsellors need tools that help them identify relevant opportunities, personalise support and prioritise interventions without reducing the quality of human guidance. The objective is therefore to strengthen Pôle emploi’s capacity to help people move into stable work more quickly and effectively. The AI implementation acts as a lever within employment-service processes, using available labour-market, jobseeker and vacancy-related data to support matching, guidance and decision-making. At a conceptual level, models may analyse profiles, skills, employment histories, job offers and contextual indicators to identify suitable pathways or opportunities for users. These outputs can help advisers provide more tailored support and improve the relevance of recommendations made to jobseekers. The expected impact is faster return to sustainable employment, better targeting of support measures, improved efficiency for counsellors and greater personalisation of public employment services. For the administration, the use case supports a more data-driven service model while preserving the role of employment professionals in guiding complex individual transitions.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-288,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00283,EU_PSTW_202609_00283,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Ministry of Action and Public Accounts - DGFIP,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.","The difficulty lies in distinguishing the truly high-risk situations from the mass of ordinary declarations, especially where discrepancies may result from error, omission or deliberate fraud.",AI for targeting fraud and valuing requests (CFVR) to improve the efficiency of tax audits,"Ministry of Action and Public Accounts - DGFIP; Central-Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; France",This use case addresses that challenge by helping the Directorate General of Public Finances target tax audits more effectively and allocate investigative resources where they are most justified. The solution is implemented within the CFVR system through large-scale processing and cross-checking of data collected by different administrations.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Tax administrations must process very large volumes of information from multiple public sources in order to identify cases where individuals or companies may not be complying with their fiscal obligations. The difficulty lies in distinguishing the truly high-risk situations from the mass of ordinary declarations, especially where discrepancies may result from error, omission or deliberate fraud. This use case addresses that challenge by helping the Directorate General of Public Finances target tax audits more effectively and allocate investigative resources where they are most justified. The stakeholders include tax auditors, compliance teams and public-finance authorities, with the objective of improving the efficiency, fairness and consistency of fiscal controls. The solution is implemented within the CFVR system through large-scale processing and cross-checking of data collected by different administrations. At a conceptual level, the system analyses patterns such as abrupt changes in income, inconsistencies between declared situations and other indicators of risk in order to generate prioritised signals for tax-control teams. These signals support audit selection and case valuation while leaving the final judgement to tax professionals. The expected impact is more accurate targeting of audits, reduced time spent on low-yield controls and better recovery of irregular situations. In operational and public-value terms, the approach increases scalability, strengthens the credibility of tax enforcement and creates value through more efficient use of control resources and fairer treatment of compliant taxpayers.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-289,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00284,EU_PSTW_202609_00284,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Ministry of the Interior,R&D general public services.,"The operational challenge is to turn that information into structured insight that can support strategic analysis consistently across the ministry rather than in isolated, ad hoc exercises.",Datascience,Ministry of the Interior; Central-Government; R&D general public services.; France,"This use case focuses on expanding the activity of the scientific laboratory, or datalab, created in 2016 so that data-driven methods can be embedded more systematically across the organisation. The solution is implemented by enlarging the datalab’s role, capabilities and analytical reach, creating a shared environment for data exploitation and the emergence of more advanced modelling approaches.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Ministry of the Interior carries out a wide range of missions, from security and territorial administration to crisis management and public-policy coordination, all of which generate substantial volumes of data. The operational challenge is to turn that information into structured insight that can support strategic analysis consistently across the ministry rather than in isolated, ad hoc exercises. This use case focuses on expanding the activity of the scientific laboratory, or datalab, created in 2016 so that data-driven methods can be embedded more systematically across the organisation. The stakeholders include ministry analysts, operational departments and senior decision-makers, with the objective of strengthening strategic analysis, improving cross-cutting understanding of complex issues and enabling broader adoption of advanced analytical methods in support of ministerial missions. The solution is implemented by enlarging the datalab’s role, capabilities and analytical reach, creating a shared environment for data exploitation and the emergence of more advanced modelling approaches. At a conceptual level, this involves structuring data-science practices, developing reusable analytical workflows and promoting the use of predictive and exploratory techniques across multiple business areas of the ministry. The expected impact is better-informed decision-making, stronger analytical consistency and greater capacity to address diverse operational questions at scale. More broadly, the initiative improves institutional agility, supports innovation in public administration and creates value by making strategic analysis more systematic, reusable and relevant to the full range of the Ministry of the Interior’s responsibilities.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-290,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00285,EU_PSTW_202609_00285,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""social_nonprofit""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Ministry of Solidarity and Health - DREES,Health n.e.c.,"Health systems produce vast quantities of information, yet the value of these data is often constrained by fragmentation, inconsistent access and a shortage of shared tools and expertise to make them usable for research and operational decision-making.",Health data hub - Creation of a big data infrastructure in health,Ministry of Solidarity and Health - DREES; Central-Government; Health n.e.c.; France,"This use case addresses that structural problem by creating a health data infrastructure capable of supporting analysis at scale and improving how data are mobilised across the healthcare ecosystem. The solution is implemented through a big-data infrastructure in health that brings together datasets, analytical tools and specialist capabilities within a common framework.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Health systems produce vast quantities of information, yet the value of these data is often constrained by fragmentation, inconsistent access and a shortage of shared tools and expertise to make them usable for research and operational decision-making. This use case addresses that structural problem by creating a health data infrastructure capable of supporting analysis at scale and improving how data are mobilised across the healthcare ecosystem. Led by the Department of Research, Studies, Evaluation and Statistics of the Ministry of Solidarity and Health, the initiative involves researchers, policymakers, planners and healthcare stakeholders, with the objective of strengthening clinical research, improving support for resource-allocation decisions and enabling more predictive and effective forms of medicine. The solution is implemented through a big-data infrastructure in health that brings together datasets, analytical tools and specialist capabilities within a common framework. At a conceptual level, the platform is designed to facilitate secure data use, support large-scale analysis and provide the technical and organisational foundation needed for data-driven research and decision support. This creates an environment in which health information can be used more systematically to generate evidence and operational insight. The expected impact is improved efficiency in the health system, stronger support for clinical and policy analysis and better conditions for innovation in care and research. More broadly, the initiative enhances scalability, strengthens the value extracted from health data and creates public benefit through a more informed, coordinated and forward-looking healthcare system.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-291,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00286,EU_PSTW_202609_00286,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Ministry of Ecological and Inclusive Transition - Météo France,Environmental protection n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,Expert systems in meteorology and climate to better forecasting,Ministry of Ecological and Inclusive Transition - Météo France; Central-Government; Environmental protection n.e.c.; France,This use case addresses that dual challenge by modernising forecasting methods and supporting the organisation’s staff as their professional roles evolve alongside new technologies. The solution is implemented through expert systems and machine-learning-based approaches that enhance the modelling and interpretation of weather and climate data.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Weather and climate services must produce forecasts that are not only scientifically robust but also understandable, timely and useful for a wide range of users affected by meteorological risks. For Météo-France, this means continually improving predictive capability while also adapting communication tools and workforce practices to new operational realities. This use case addresses that dual challenge by modernising forecasting methods and supporting the organisation’s staff as their professional roles evolve alongside new technologies. The stakeholders include meteorologists, climate specialists, operational forecasters and end users of weather information, with the objective of improving the prediction of meteorological phenomena and their impacts while making forecast outputs more actionable and easier to communicate. The solution is implemented through expert systems and machine-learning-based approaches that enhance the modelling and interpretation of weather and climate data. At a conceptual level, these methods are used to refine forecasting processes, support impact-oriented analysis and integrate more effective communication tools into operational workflows. At the same time, the project supports staff in adapting to new modes of work and decision support. The expected impact is better forecasting performance, clearer communication of risks and stronger operational efficiency within the forecasting chain. More broadly, the initiative increases institutional resilience, improves the usefulness of meteorological services and creates value by combining technical innovation with organisational transformation.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION|S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-292,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00287,EU_PSTW_202609_00287,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Government’s Information and Press service,R&D general public services.,"The operational problem was the manual indexing of extensive photographic collections, a process described as laborious and time-consuming. The resulting metadata makes the collection easier to search and manage, while reducing the manual effort required from staff.",Indexing of Government photos for better identification on photos using facial recognition,Government’s Information and Press service; Central-Government; R&D general public services.; Luxembourg,The Government’s Information and Press Service of Luxembourg implemented this use case to improve the management of a large media library of political photographs belonging to the Luxembourg State. The AI project uses facial-recognition technology to identify political figures in images and generate enhanced metadata for the media library.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Government’s Information and Press Service of Luxembourg implemented this use case to improve the management of a large media library of political photographs belonging to the Luxembourg State. The operational problem was the manual indexing of extensive photographic collections, a process described as laborious and time-consuming. Without efficient metadata, the value of the media library is reduced, as photographs are harder to retrieve, identify and reuse by public institutions. The objective was to accelerate the identification of politicians appearing in government photographs and to improve the quality and completeness of metadata associated with the collection. The AI project uses facial-recognition technology to identify political figures in images and generate enhanced metadata for the media library. At a conceptual level, the system analyses visual features in photographs, compares them with authorised reference material and supports the tagging of individuals appearing in the images. The resulting metadata makes the collection easier to search and manage, while reducing the manual effort required from staff. The impact has been to facilitate and accelerate metadata production, improve the usability of the State’s photographic resources and enable better exploitation of the media library’s content across government. The use case delivers administrative efficiency, strengthens information management and supports more consistent reuse of public visual records.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-293,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00288,EU_PSTW_202609_00288,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Land Registry and Topography Administration,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.","This creates delays in keeping official spatial records current and can limit the quality and timeliness of services that depend on accurate geographic information. The stakeholders include mapping specialists, cadastral officials and public bodies that rely on up-to-date territorial information, with the objective of reducing manual workload, accelerating updates and improving the overall quality of topographical data.",Extraction of topographical objects - AI to extract topographical objects from aerial images,"Land Registry and Topography Administration; Central-Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Luxembourg",The use case therefore addresses the need to modernise how the Land Registry and Topography Administration detects changes in the built environment and maintains its databases. The solution is implemented through automated extraction of topographical objects from aerial images using computer-vision and object-detection techniques.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Updating national topographic and land-registry databases is labour-intensive, particularly when officials must identify newly built structures, new roads and emerging construction areas from large volumes of aerial imagery. This creates delays in keeping official spatial records current and can limit the quality and timeliness of services that depend on accurate geographic information. The use case therefore addresses the need to modernise how the Land Registry and Topography Administration detects changes in the built environment and maintains its databases. The stakeholders include mapping specialists, cadastral officials and public bodies that rely on up-to-date territorial information, with the objective of reducing manual workload, accelerating updates and improving the overall quality of topographical data. The solution is implemented through automated extraction of topographical objects from aerial images using computer-vision and object-detection techniques. At a conceptual level, the system analyses imagery to identify features such as buildings and roads, distinguishes new elements from the existing mapped environment and feeds these outputs into the database-update process. This creates a more efficient workflow for detecting construction areas and for enriching official geospatial records. The expected impact is faster database maintenance, improved data quality and lower administrative effort for State officials. More broadly, the initiative enhances scalability, supports the creation of new data and services, and strengthens the public value of official geospatial infrastructure through more timely and reliable mapping.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-294,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00289,EU_PSTW_202609_00289,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Luxembourg National archive,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.","Historical archives contain vast quantities of handwritten material that remain difficult to exploit because older manuscripts are often challenging to read, search and interpret at scale. The CLAVIS project addresses this documentary bottleneck by improving the legibility and usability of digitised manuscript collections.",CLAVIS project - Automatic recognition of old handwritten documents,"Luxembourg National archive; Central-Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Luxembourg","For the Luxembourg National Archives, this limits the accessibility of important seventeenth- and eighteenth-century sources and constrains the extent to which researchers can use them effectively. The solution is implemented by submitting digitised manuscript images to Transkribus, a platform for automated handwriting recognition, transcription and search in historic documents.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Historical archives contain vast quantities of handwritten material that remain difficult to exploit because older manuscripts are often challenging to read, search and interpret at scale. For the Luxembourg National Archives, this limits the accessibility of important seventeenth- and eighteenth-century sources and constrains the extent to which researchers can use them effectively. The CLAVIS project addresses this documentary bottleneck by improving the legibility and usability of digitised manuscript collections. The stakeholders include archivists, historians, researchers and cultural-heritage institutions, with the objective of making historic documents more accessible, searchable and useful for future generations while also improving archival description processes. The solution is implemented by submitting digitised manuscript images to Transkribus, a platform for automated handwriting recognition, transcription and search in historic documents. At a conceptual level, the system processes scanned archival pages, converts handwritten text into machine-readable transcriptions and enables downstream uses such as content search, structured retrieval and the automated generation of inventories or indexes of people and places. This transforms previously difficult-to-use archival sources into a more navigable digital resource. The expected impact is greatly improved accessibility to historical records, reduced manual transcription effort and stronger support for archival research and discovery. More broadly, the project improves the scalability of document processing, enriches cultural-heritage services and creates value by opening complex manuscript collections to much wider scholarly and public use.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-296,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00290,EU_PSTW_202609_00290,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Arnhem,Water supply.,"The operational issue is that sewer infrastructure is often located below ground in ways that may be difficult for construction teams to visualise accurately from the surface, creating a risk of costly damage, service disruption and safety hazards.",3D scan underground infrastructure - Prevention damage of critical infrastructure,Municipality of Arnhem; Local Government; Water supply.; Netherlands,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Arnhem’s 3D scan project focuses on preventing accidental damage to critical underground infrastructure during construction work. The operational issue is that sewer infrastructure is often located below ground in ways that may be difficult for construction teams to visualise accurately from the surface, creating a risk of costly damage, service disruption and safety hazards. The municipality’s approach combines a precise 3D scan of the main sewer with an application that visualises the sewer’s location above ground. The implementation therefore links geospatial surveying, three-dimensional infrastructure modelling and field-based visualisation, allowing users to understand the position of underground assets before or during works. Conceptually, the system turns hidden sewer infrastructure into a digital and spatially interpretable layer that can be consulted by staff or contractors. The expected impact is fewer accidental strikes, better planning of construction activities, reduced repair costs and improved protection of essential municipal infrastructure. By making underground assets more visible and usable in operational settings, the project supports safer, more data-driven public works management.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-297,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00291,EU_PSTW_202609_00291,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"8Ting, business office Work & Income of the municipality of Enschede",Secondary education.,UNKNOWN,8TING - Analysis of drivers to keep people in class,"8Ting, business office Work & Income of the municipality of Enschede; Local Government; Secondary education.; Netherlands","Its implementation uses profiles, behavioural data and contextual indicators to plan automatic or semi-automatic interventions, such as measurements, encouragement and targeted support activities intended to keep people engaged in class or training.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"8TING was used in Enschede to apply psychology and web technology to support people participating in municipal work-and-income or training pathways. The operational problem is that people may drop out of programmes when support is not aligned with their personal motivation, behaviour, context or barriers. The system seeks to make clear who needs what type of attention by monitoring and adjusting more than 80 factors related to internal drivers. Its implementation uses profiles, behavioural data and contextual indicators to plan automatic or semi-automatic interventions, such as measurements, encouragement and targeted support activities intended to keep people engaged in class or training. Conceptually, the tool combines behavioural profiling, motivation analysis, web-based interaction, programme planning and adaptive support. The expected impact is better retention in training, more personalised support, earlier identification of disengagement risks and improved effectiveness of municipal activation services. At the same time, because the tool analyses behavioural and psychological drivers, careful safeguards are needed around transparency, consent, proportionality and the avoidance of overly deterministic profiling of participants.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-298,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00292,EU_PSTW_202609_00292,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Dutch Food and Consumer Product Safety Authority (NVWA),"Agriculture, forestry, fishing and hunting",UNKNOWN,Aerial Inspection Drones,"Dutch Food and Consumer Product Safety Authority (NVWA); Central-Government; Agriculture, forestry, fishing and hunting; Netherlands",Aurea Imaging wants to develop image analysis technology for drones/Unmanned Aerial Vehicles (UAVs) to use them in the agricultural sector in detecting anomalies.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Aurea Imaging wants to develop image analysis technology for drones/Unmanned Aerial Vehicles (UAVs) to use them in the agricultural sector in detecting anomalies. These abnormalities may indicate harmful organisms. In a testing ground of the Dutch Food and Consumer Product Safety Authority (NVWA), we investigate the feasibility of this technology.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-299,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00293,EU_PSTW_202609_00293,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,ProRail,Transport,"ProRail’s agent system is designed to support train traffic controllers when disruptions or delays occur on the Dutch rail network. The operational challenge is that small delays can propagate quickly through interconnected railway services, requiring rapid decisions on routing, prioritisation and recovery measures.",Agent system,ProRail; Central-Government; Transport; Netherlands,"The expected impact is improved punctuality, better use of available rail capacity and more consistent support during disruption management.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"ProRail’s agent system is designed to support train traffic controllers when disruptions or delays occur on the Dutch rail network. The operational challenge is that small delays can propagate quickly through interconnected railway services, requiring rapid decisions on routing, prioritisation and recovery measures. Train traffic controllers must assess complex operational conditions under time pressure, balancing passenger disruption, infrastructure capacity and the need to prevent knock-on effects. The agent functions as an autonomous computer programme that operates with defined goals and associated actions, providing advice to controllers on how to respond to delays and reduce further disruption. At a conceptual level, the system can be understood as a decision-support agent that analyses traffic conditions, expected train movements, available infrastructure and possible operational choices, then proposes interventions for human operators to consider. The expected impact is improved punctuality, better use of available rail capacity and more consistent support during disruption management. By assisting controllers rather than replacing them, the system can help preserve human responsibility while improving the speed and quality of operational advice in complex traffic situations.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-300,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00294,EU_PSTW_202609_00294,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Dordrecht,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.",UNKNOWN,AI for public space notifications - Image recognition to analyse reports,"Municipality of Dordrecht; Local Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Netherlands","This use case, explored by the municipality of Dordrecht, addresses that operational friction by seeking to make reporting easier for residents and the follow-up process more efficient for municipal teams. The solution is implemented through image-recognition techniques applied to photographs submitted with defect reports.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Municipalities receive many citizen reports about defects and inconveniences in public space, ranging from loose paving stones and broken streetlights to potentially dangerous situations requiring urgent intervention. Although these reports are valuable, they can vary greatly in clarity and completeness, which means staff must spend time interpreting the issue before deciding how to respond. This use case, explored by the municipality of Dordrecht, addresses that operational friction by seeking to make reporting easier for residents and the follow-up process more efficient for municipal teams. The stakeholders include citizens, municipal service staff and teams responsible for public-space maintenance, with the objective of improving intake quality, speeding up case assessment and supporting quicker resolution of local problems. The solution is implemented through image-recognition techniques applied to photographs submitted with defect reports. At a conceptual level, the system analyses the uploaded image to identify the type of issue reported, extract relevant visual cues and support routing or prioritisation within the municipal workflow. This gives staff a clearer starting point for action and may reduce the need for repeated clarification. The expected impact is faster processing of public-space notifications, reduced administrative effort and a smoother user experience for residents. More broadly, the approach improves service responsiveness, enhances consistency in case handling and creates value by linking citizen reporting more directly to operational action.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-301,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00295,EU_PSTW_202609_00295,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Netherlands Enterprise Agency,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.","Agricultural entrepreneurs need accessible and consistent guidance when preparing or managing business transfers, as these processes involve administrative, financial and sector-specific questions that can be difficult to navigate through static information pages alone.",AI Chatbot Robin - Answer questions about business transfers,"Netherlands Enterprise Agency; Central-Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Netherlands",The implementation uses chatbot technology to interpret user questions and match them with prepared knowledge on agricultural business-transfer topics.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Agricultural entrepreneurs need accessible and consistent guidance when preparing or managing business transfers, as these processes involve administrative, financial and sector-specific questions that can be difficult to navigate through static information pages alone. AI Chatbot Robin addresses this need by providing a virtual agent for the Netherlands Enterprise Agency that answers questions on agricultural business transfers. The service builds on the earlier Pilot Watson and went live in April 2019, moving from an experimental conversational service towards a more operational support channel for users seeking information from the agency. Its purpose is to offer a first point of contact for recurring questions, helping business owners or prospective successors understand relevant procedures and obtain guidance without immediately relying on telephone or email support. The implementation uses chatbot technology to interpret user questions and match them with prepared knowledge on agricultural business-transfer topics. At a conceptual level, the system relies on natural language understanding, a structured knowledge base and conversational flows that guide users towards relevant answers. The main value is improved availability of information, more efficient handling of repetitive enquiries and a more user-friendly digital service for agricultural stakeholders. For the administration, Robin also supports channel shifting and allows staff to focus on more complex or case-specific requests.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-302,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00296,EU_PSTW_202609_00296,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Amsterdam Police,Police Services,"Police control rooms handle continuous streams of incident data and real-time reports, yet anticipating where risks may emerge remains a difficult task in fast-changing urban environments. The AICrimePrediction feasibility study, carried out by Amsterdam police and Ciphix, addresses the challenge of improving situational awareness and supporting earlier, better-informed deployment decisions in relation to crime and terrorism risks.",AICrimePrediction (AICP) - Prediction of the risk of crime and terrorism,Amsterdam Police; Local Government; Police Services; Netherlands,"Rather than focusing only on historical review, the use case seeks to inform live operational choices by connecting past patterns with current signals. The solution is implemented by combining historical control-room data, including type of incident, time and location, with real-time reporting inputs.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Police control rooms handle continuous streams of incident data and real-time reports, yet anticipating where risks may emerge remains a difficult task in fast-changing urban environments. The AICrimePrediction feasibility study, carried out by Amsterdam police and Ciphix, addresses the challenge of improving situational awareness and supporting earlier, better-informed deployment decisions in relation to crime and terrorism risks. The stakeholders include control-room staff, police planners and operational decision-makers, with the objective of understanding whether available data can be used to generate sufficiently accurate risk predictions to strengthen prevention and response. Rather than focusing only on historical review, the use case seeks to inform live operational choices by connecting past patterns with current signals. The solution is implemented by combining historical control-room data, including type of incident, time and location, with real-time reporting inputs. At a conceptual level, the system applies predictive modelling to spatio-temporal and incident-pattern data in order to estimate the likelihood of criminal or terrorism-related risk in particular contexts or locations. These predictions are intended to support operational awareness and resource allocation rather than replace police judgement. The expected impact is more targeted monitoring, improved readiness and better use of police capacity in high-risk situations. More broadly, the feasibility study explores whether data-driven forecasting can strengthen public-safety operations through greater precision, timeliness and coordination in risk assessment.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION|S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-303,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00297,EU_PSTW_202609_00297,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""social_nonprofit""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Education Implementation Service,R&D education.,"Educational and public-service organisations often hold large amounts of administrative and performance data, yet turning those data into useful learning insights for internal improvement can be slow and resource-intensive. In this context, the challenge is not simply to collect information, but to derive coherent reporting outputs that support reflection, oversight and service improvement from existing internal data.",AI machine learning supervised - Delivering learning reports,Education Implementation Service; Central-Government; R&D education.; Netherlands,"This use case, piloted internally by DUO, addresses the need to produce learning reports more efficiently and in a more structured way. The solution is implemented through a supervised machine-learning pilot designed to support the production of a learning report.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Educational and public-service organisations often hold large amounts of administrative and performance data, yet turning those data into useful learning insights for internal improvement can be slow and resource-intensive. This use case, piloted internally by DUO, addresses the need to produce learning reports more efficiently and in a more structured way. The stakeholders include internal analysts, programme managers and decision-makers within DUO, with the objective of improving how organisational learning is captured, reported and used to inform future action. In this context, the challenge is not simply to collect information, but to derive coherent reporting outputs that support reflection, oversight and service improvement from existing internal data. The solution is implemented through a supervised machine-learning pilot designed to support the production of a learning report. At a conceptual level, the system uses labelled data and supervised modelling techniques to identify relevant patterns, classify information or structure insights that can feed into report generation and internal evaluation processes. Rather than replacing expert interpretation, the tool is intended to support analysts in assembling evidence more systematically and efficiently. The expected impact is quicker preparation of learning reports, reduced manual processing and stronger consistency in how findings are compiled. More broadly, the pilot offers a foundation for scalable internal reporting, improved organisational learning and better use of data in steering, evaluation and continuous improvement activities within DUO.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-304,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00298,EU_PSTW_202609_00298,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,NVWA,Protection of biodiversity and landscape.,"Plant and tree health monitoring is essential for protecting agriculture, biodiversity and wider environmental quality, yet disease detection across large areas can be difficult, time-consuming and dependent on scarce specialist expertise. The NVWA pilot addresses this challenge by exploring a more efficient way to identify signs of disease in plants and trees before problems spread or become more costly to manage.",AI for detection of diseases in plants and trees,NVWA; Central-Government; Protection of biodiversity and landscape.; Netherlands,"The use case responds to the practical need for broader, more scalable monitoring in contexts where manual inspection alone may not provide sufficient coverage or speed. The solution is implemented through a pilot that applies image-based analysis to detect disease-related patterns in vegetation.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Plant and tree health monitoring is essential for protecting agriculture, biodiversity and wider environmental quality, yet disease detection across large areas can be difficult, time-consuming and dependent on scarce specialist expertise. The NVWA pilot addresses this challenge by exploring a more efficient way to identify signs of disease in plants and trees before problems spread or become more costly to manage. The stakeholders include inspectors, plant-health specialists and authorities responsible for biosecurity and environmental protection, with the objective of improving early detection, strengthening surveillance capacity and supporting more timely interventions. The use case responds to the practical need for broader, more scalable monitoring in contexts where manual inspection alone may not provide sufficient coverage or speed. The solution is implemented through a pilot that applies image-based analysis to detect disease-related patterns in vegetation. At a conceptual level, the system uses machine-learning techniques trained on visual indicators of plant or tree illness in order to distinguish healthy from potentially affected specimens and to support inspection prioritisation. These outputs can then be incorporated into plant-health workflows to guide follow-up checks and decisions. The expected impact is faster identification of potential disease outbreaks, better targeting of inspection resources and reduced reliance on purely manual screening. More broadly, the approach improves scalability, enhances the reliability of plant-health monitoring and creates value through stronger preventive action in environmental and agricultural protection.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-305,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00299,EU_PSTW_202609_00299,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,NVWA,"Agriculture, forestry, fishing and hunting","The challenge for inspectors is that suspected nests are not always easy to confirm quickly in the field, particularly across wide outdoor areas, and accurate species identification is essential to avoid unnecessary intervention.",AI for detecting Asian Hornbill,"NVWA; Central-Government; Agriculture, forestry, fishing and hunting; Netherlands","This use case, led by the NVWA, addresses that problem by improving how potential sightings and nest reports are verified. The solution is implemented through two complementary tools: drone surveys equipped with thermal-imaging cameras for suspected nest areas, and an image-recognition application that helps distinguish between the Asian hornet and the European hornet.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Detecting the presence of the Asian hornet is a growing operational priority because this invasive species can threaten biodiversity, disrupt ecosystems and create risks for beekeeping and public safety. The challenge for inspectors is that suspected nests are not always easy to confirm quickly in the field, particularly across wide outdoor areas, and accurate species identification is essential to avoid unnecessary intervention. This use case, led by the NVWA, addresses that problem by improving how potential sightings and nest reports are verified. The stakeholders include inspectors, environmental authorities and members of the public reporting possible nests, with the objective of accelerating detection, improving species recognition and supporting more targeted intervention against the invasive hornet. The solution is implemented through two complementary tools: drone surveys equipped with thermal-imaging cameras for suspected nest areas, and an image-recognition application that helps distinguish between the Asian hornet and the European hornet. At a conceptual level, the system combines aerial thermal sensing with visual classification models to support both field detection and species verification. These capabilities can then be integrated into the NVWA’s inspection workflow to prioritise action and reduce uncertainty. The expected impact is faster confirmation of nest locations, more reliable differentiation between species and better use of inspection resources. More broadly, the approach enhances scalability, supports environmental protection and creates public value through a more responsive and accurate invasive-species control process.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-306,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00300,EU_PSTW_202609_00300,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,NVWA,Environmental protection n.e.c.,"Inspection authorities face a recurring challenge when monitoring production and storage environments: potentially harmful anomalies may be difficult to identify quickly across large areas, yet delayed detection can weaken controls, increase operational costs and heighten risks to food safety.",AI for fisheries inspections - Detecting illegal barrels,NVWA; Central-Government; Environmental protection n.e.c.; Netherlands,"This use case, explored in a testing environment with the Netherlands Food and Consumer Product Safety Authority, focuses on improving inspection efficiency by identifying suspicious deviations that may indicate harmful organisms or other non-compliant conditions. The solution is implemented through image-analysis technology mounted on drones or other unmanned aerial vehicles, designed to detect anomalies from visual data gathered during inspections.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Inspection authorities face a recurring challenge when monitoring production and storage environments: potentially harmful anomalies may be difficult to identify quickly across large areas, yet delayed detection can weaken controls, increase operational costs and heighten risks to food safety. This use case, explored in a testing environment with the Netherlands Food and Consumer Product Safety Authority, focuses on improving inspection efficiency by identifying suspicious deviations that may indicate harmful organisms or other non-compliant conditions. The stakeholders include inspectors, laboratory teams and operational managers, with the objective of directing scarce inspection resources more effectively, strengthening control methods and improving the quality of oversight in high-risk contexts. The solution is implemented through image-analysis technology mounted on drones or other unmanned aerial vehicles, designed to detect anomalies from visual data gathered during inspections. At a conceptual level, the system combines aerial imaging, machine-learning-based pattern detection and inspection workflows so that unusual features can be highlighted and assessed for follow-up. These outputs can then support decisions on where to deploy staff, laboratory analysis or specialist equipment. The expected impact is more efficient use of inspection capacity, improved targeting of controls and stronger food-safety protection through earlier and more reliable anomaly detection. More broadly, the approach enhances scalability, reduces wasted effort and creates value by modernising field inspection methods in a resource-constrained regulatory environment.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-307,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00301,EU_PSTW_202609_00301,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Europol,R&D public order and safety.,"This creates an information paradox: there is abundant data, but not enough operational clarity, which can hinder decision-making, slow coordination and ultimately endanger lives.","AIDA (Artificial Intelligence Disaster Advisor), and Horizon 2020 project",Europol; European Institution/Agency; R&D public order and safety.; European Union,"AIDA was developed to address this challenge by helping emergency services identify and use the most relevant information during a crisis. The solution is implemented by combining big-data processing, data-science methods and streaming architectures to ingest and filter information from multiple public and non-public sources.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"During major incidents, control rooms are confronted with a flood of potentially relevant information arriving from social media, public channels and other data sources, yet it is rarely possible for emergency staff to collect, validate and assess all of it in real time. This creates an information paradox: there is abundant data, but not enough operational clarity, which can hinder decision-making, slow coordination and ultimately endanger lives. AIDA was developed to address this challenge by helping emergency services identify and use the most relevant information during a crisis. The stakeholders include control-room operators, emergency responders and coordinating authorities, with the objective of improving situational awareness and enabling faster, more informed action in high-pressure scenarios. The solution is implemented by combining big-data processing, data-science methods and streaming architectures to ingest and filter information from multiple public and non-public sources. At a conceptual level, the system analyses incoming data flows, evaluates relevance and rapidly distributes prioritised information to emergency services so that operational teams can focus on the most important signals. This supports live crisis management rather than retrospective analysis. The expected impact is better-informed emergency handling, faster access to critical intelligence and improved coordination across services. In practical terms, AIDA strengthens responsiveness, increases the usability of complex data in crisis settings and creates public value by supporting actions that can better save and protect human lives.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-308,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00302,EU_PSTW_202609_00302,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,University of Rotterdam and other 14 dutch universities,Education not definable by level.,"The operational challenge is to detect possible cheating in a digital setting where invigilators cannot physically observe candidates, but where fairness, equal treatment and the reliability of qualifications remain essential. The expected benefit for institutions is greater capacity to run remote examinations while maintaining oversight and reducing manual invigilation constraints.",Anti-cheat software for exams,University of Rotterdam and other 14 dutch universities; Academic-Research; Education not definable by level.; Netherlands,"Anti-cheat software for exams refers to the use of Proctorexam by several Dutch higher education institutions, including the University of Rotterdam and other universities, to monitor students during online tests. However, the use of such systems also raises important concerns around privacy, proportionality, transparency and student trust, meaning that its operational value depends strongly on governance safeguards, review procedures and clear communication with candidates.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Higher education institutions conducting remote examinations need to preserve the integrity of assessments while managing the practical limitations of supervising students outside traditional examination rooms. Anti-cheat software for exams refers to the use of Proctorexam by several Dutch higher education institutions, including the University of Rotterdam and other universities, to monitor students during online tests. The operational challenge is to detect possible cheating in a digital setting where invigilators cannot physically observe candidates, but where fairness, equal treatment and the reliability of qualifications remain essential. The software enables remote surveillance through a student’s webcam, microphone and web browser, allowing examination behaviour and the digital environment to be monitored during the assessment. Its technical implementation combines online proctoring functions with automated or semi-automated detection of behaviours that may be classified as suspicious, such as unusual movements, browser activity or environmental signals. The system can flag students for further review, rather than necessarily determining misconduct on its own. The expected benefit for institutions is greater capacity to run remote examinations while maintaining oversight and reducing manual invigilation constraints. However, the use of such systems also raises important concerns around privacy, proportionality, transparency and student trust, meaning that its operational value depends strongly on governance safeguards, review procedures and clear communication with candidates.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-309,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00303,EU_PSTW_202609_00303,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Rijkswaterstaat (RWS ),Transport,"Rijkswaterstaat needed a more efficient and affordable way to inspect national roads and waterways, since traditional inspection approaches often depend on expensive professional camera equipment and limited collection capacity. Routine infrastructure inspection is essential for maintaining safety and service quality, but manual processes and specialist hardware can restrict coverage and raise costs.",Artificial inspector - Self-learning system for the inspection of national roads and waterways,Rijkswaterstaat (RWS ); Central-Government; Transport; Netherlands,"The use case therefore focused on expanding inspection capability while lowering the barriers to large-scale visual data collection. The solution is implemented through standard Android smartphones mounted on cars and ships, each running an application that captures an image every second.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Rijkswaterstaat needed a more efficient and affordable way to inspect national roads and waterways, since traditional inspection approaches often depend on expensive professional camera equipment and limited collection capacity. Routine infrastructure inspection is essential for maintaining safety and service quality, but manual processes and specialist hardware can restrict coverage and raise costs. The use case therefore focused on expanding inspection capability while lowering the barriers to large-scale visual data collection. The stakeholders include Rijkswaterstaat, inspection teams and infrastructure managers, with the objective of automating a larger share of road and waterway inspection and improving the timeliness and consistency of asset monitoring. The solution is implemented through standard Android smartphones mounted on cars and ships, each running an application that captures an image every second. At a conceptual level, these images are combined with geographic data and used to train a cloud-based neural network that can recognise relevant infrastructure conditions and support automated inspection tasks. By using inexpensive, widely available devices instead of specialist cameras, the system creates a more scalable and flexible inspection model. The expected impact is lower equipment cost, broader inspection coverage and faster collection of infrastructure data. More broadly, the project creates value by modernising public-asset monitoring, improving operational efficiency and demonstrating how low-cost sensing combined with cloud-based analysis can reshape large-scale infrastructure inspection.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,NOT_IN_USE,미사용,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-310,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00304,EU_PSTW_202609_00304,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Province of North Holland, Municipality of Amersfoort, Bam infra, Province of Gelderland and other provinces",Transport,"Traditional inspection methods can be time-consuming and difficult to scale across large networks, particularly when both road-surface condition and associated roadside assets must be monitored regularly. The expected impact is earlier detection of deterioration, reduced reliance on manual inspection and better-targeted maintenance interventions.",Asphalt scan - Detection of damage to roads,"Province of North Holland, Municipality of Amersfoort, Bam infra, Province of Gelderland and other provinces; Local Government; Transport; Netherlands","This use case addresses that operational need by improving how defects in infrastructure are identified and prioritised. The solution is implemented through the Asphalt Scan, which gathers data from satellites and scanning vehicles and then applies analytical models to identify road damage and reductions in signage brightness.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Road authorities need reliable and up-to-date information on pavement damage and declining visibility of signage in order to plan maintenance before defects become more severe, more expensive and more disruptive for road users. Traditional inspection methods can be time-consuming and difficult to scale across large networks, particularly when both road-surface condition and associated roadside assets must be monitored regularly. This use case addresses that operational need by improving how defects in infrastructure are identified and prioritised. The stakeholders include road-maintenance teams, infrastructure managers and public authorities responsible for transport safety and asset performance, with the objective of detecting deterioration earlier and supporting more efficient maintenance planning. The solution is implemented through the Asphalt Scan, which gathers data from satellites and scanning vehicles and then applies analytical models to identify road damage and reductions in signage brightness. At a conceptual level, the system combines remote and mobile sensing with automated classification and anomaly-detection techniques to convert raw imagery and scan data into actionable maintenance signals. These outputs can then feed into infrastructure-management workflows and inspection planning. The expected impact is earlier detection of deterioration, reduced reliance on manual inspection and better-targeted maintenance interventions. More broadly, the approach improves scalability, enhances asset reliability and creates value through more efficient road-network management and safer travel conditions.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-311,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00305,EU_PSTW_202609_00305,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Netherlands Enterprise Agency (RVO),Police Services,"Authorities and online-safety stakeholders face the urgent challenge of identifying illegal child sexual abuse imagery on the internet quickly enough to support removal and limit further harm. The scale of online content makes manual review alone difficult, slow and distressing for staff, while delayed detection can allow illegal material to remain accessible for longer.",Combating Online Child Abuse,Netherlands Enterprise Agency (RVO); Central-Government; Police Services; Netherlands,"This use case addresses that problem by exploring whether a technical model can be created and deployed to recognise illegal images and support rapid action. At a conceptual level, the system uses image-classification techniques to analyse visual material, distinguish unlawful content from non-relevant imagery and generate outputs that can support review and takedown processes.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Authorities and online-safety stakeholders face the urgent challenge of identifying illegal child sexual abuse imagery on the internet quickly enough to support removal and limit further harm. The scale of online content makes manual review alone difficult, slow and distressing for staff, while delayed detection can allow illegal material to remain accessible for longer. This use case addresses that problem by exploring whether a technical model can be created and deployed to recognise illegal images and support rapid action. The stakeholders include enforcement and online-safety actors, specialist investigators and platform or service partners involved in the detection and removal process, with the objective of strengthening identification capability and improving the speed and consistency of intervention. The solution is being developed through an SBIR project in collaboration with PwC to assess the feasibility of creating and implementing a model that recognises illegal image content. At a conceptual level, the system uses image-classification techniques to analyse visual material, distinguish unlawful content from non-relevant imagery and generate outputs that can support review and takedown processes. These capabilities are intended to complement, not replace, human oversight in highly sensitive decision-making. The expected impact is faster detection of illegal material, reduced manual burden on specialist teams and more effective support for online removal. More broadly, the approach improves scalability, strengthens digital safeguarding and creates public value through better protection of children online.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-312,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00306,EU_PSTW_202609_00306,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Apeldoorn,Public order and safety n.e.c.,"Municipal authorities often face difficulty in deciding where to direct youth work, supervision and social intervention, especially when risks appear unevenly distributed across locations and groups. This created a data-driven view of behavioural risk across groups and places rather than depending solely on manual observation or isolated reports.",Prediction of young people engagement in undesirable behaviour (Pilot Apeldoorn Youth),Municipality of Apeldoorn; Local Government; Public order and safety n.e.c.; Netherlands,"The use case therefore involved local authorities and youth-related service actors, with the objective of detecting patterns earlier and supporting more targeted preventive measures rather than relying only on reactive responses once incidents had already occurred. The solution was implemented by linking multiple data sources in order to estimate which groups of young people were more likely to engage in undesirable behaviour and in which locations this was more likely to happen.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The municipality of Apeldoorn explored this pilot to address concerns about youth-related nuisance and other forms of undesirable behaviour in public space, with the aim of identifying where preventive action might be most needed. Municipal authorities often face difficulty in deciding where to direct youth work, supervision and social intervention, especially when risks appear unevenly distributed across locations and groups. The use case therefore involved local authorities and youth-related service actors, with the objective of detecting patterns earlier and supporting more targeted preventive measures rather than relying only on reactive responses once incidents had already occurred. The solution was implemented by linking multiple data sources in order to estimate which groups of young people were more likely to engage in undesirable behaviour and in which locations this was more likely to happen. At a conceptual level, the system used cross-dataset analysis and predictive modelling to generate risk patterns that could inform municipal decision-making about attention, intervention and local policy. This created a data-driven view of behavioural risk across groups and places rather than depending solely on manual observation or isolated reports. The expected impact was earlier visibility of emerging risks, more focused use of local preventive resources and stronger operational support for youth policy. More broadly, the pilot created value by exploring how linked data could help municipalities move towards more anticipatory public-order and youth-support strategies, though such approaches also raise important governance and proportionality questions.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,NOT_IN_USE,미사용,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-313,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00307,EU_PSTW_202609_00307,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Netherlands Enterprise Agency (RVO),Public order and safety n.e.c.,"In many cases, however, decision-makers must interpret large volumes of information under pressure, making it difficult to determine the optimal deployment strategy quickly and consistently.",Blue Operational Prevention Service,Netherlands Enterprise Agency (RVO); Central-Government; Public order and safety n.e.c.; Netherlands,This use case addresses that challenge by helping users prevent flooding through more precise and operationally relevant guidance. The solution is implemented through smart algorithms that combine live risk information with knowledge of the resources available.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Flood prevention requires timely decisions based on changing risk conditions, available response resources and the practical question of what action is most useful in a given moment. In many cases, however, decision-makers must interpret large volumes of information under pressure, making it difficult to determine the optimal deployment strategy quickly and consistently. This use case addresses that challenge by helping users prevent flooding through more precise and operationally relevant guidance. The stakeholders include flood-risk managers, emergency planners and operational users responsible for preventive action, with the objective of minimising risk through better real-time deployment choices and more tailored decision support. The solution is implemented through smart algorithms that combine live risk information with knowledge of the resources available. At a conceptual level, the system analyses evolving conditions, evaluates feasible response options and translates them into a user-specific action perspective, rather than producing a generic warning alone. This means the output is not simply descriptive, but directly linked to the operational choices that can still be made in time. The expected impact is more effective preventive deployment, quicker and better-targeted decisions and improved use of limited resources in flood-risk situations. More broadly, the approach enhances reliability, supports scalability in operational prevention and creates value by turning real-time information into practical, context-sensitive action for those managing water-related threats.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F06_HIGH_VOLUME_SCALABILITY_BOTTLENECK,S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-314,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00308,EU_PSTW_202609_00308,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Province of Gelderland, NEO, Wageningen Environmental Research",Environmental protection n.e.c.,"Regional authorities responsible for environmental protection need reliable methods to identify tree loss and possible illegal logging across large territories where field inspections alone may be slow, selective or reactive.",Tree Monitor - Detecting illegal logging,"Province of Gelderland, NEO, Wageningen Environmental Research; Regional Government; Environmental protection n.e.c.; Netherlands","The implementation uses Earth observation data, with satellite imagery analysed across time to identify changes in vegetation cover. It also demonstrates how satellite-based monitoring can support nature protection and land-use oversight at regional level.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Regional authorities responsible for environmental protection need reliable methods to identify tree loss and possible illegal logging across large territories where field inspections alone may be slow, selective or reactive. Tree Monitor gives the Province of Gelderland an additional monitoring tool for detecting potential illegal felling by comparing satellite images from different years. Operational since December 2017, the system helps authorities locate areas where trees appear to have disappeared and visualises these changes on a map. The objective is to improve the detection of irregular tree removal and support more targeted follow-up by enforcement or environmental-management teams. The implementation uses Earth observation data, with satellite imagery analysed across time to identify changes in vegetation cover. At a conceptual level, image-comparison techniques detect differences between historical and more recent satellite data, highlighting locations where tree cover has declined. These outputs can then be reviewed by officials and used to prioritise inspections or further investigation. The initiative involves the Province of Gelderland, NEO and Wageningen Environmental Research, combining public environmental responsibilities with specialist geospatial expertise. The main impact is broader territorial coverage, faster identification of potential illegal logging, better targeting of field checks and stronger evidence for environmental enforcement. It also demonstrates how satellite-based monitoring can support nature protection and land-use oversight at regional level.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-315,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00309,EU_PSTW_202609_00309,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Municipality of Enschede, Saxion, Winkelhart Enschede, University of Twente, Both Social, eLabbs, Marotura, Present Media, Squal Media, Think Public, University of Twente and Winkelhart Enschede",General public services n.e.c.,"Urban mobility in Enschede is affected by congestion, inefficient parking usage and limited coordination between private vehicles and public transport, creating delays, emissions and poor user experience for residents and visitors.",Brid.ge - Smart City Enschede,"Municipality of Enschede, Saxion, Winkelhart Enschede, University of Twente, Both Social, eLabbs, Marotura, Present Media, Squal Media, Think Public, University of Twente and Winkelhart Enschede; Local Government; General public services n.e.c.; Netherlands",UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Urban mobility in Enschede is affected by congestion, inefficient parking usage and limited coordination between private vehicles and public transport, creating delays, emissions and poor user experience for residents and visitors. Local authorities, transport operators and travellers require accurate, timely information to support better travel choices and reduce pressure on the city centre. The objective of this pilot is to improve accessibility and sustainability by guiding users to appropriate parking facilities or encouraging a shift towards public transport based on real-time conditions. The implementation integrates traffic sensors, parking occupancy data and public transport information into a single digital platform. Data analytics and predictive models evaluate congestion patterns and parking availability, while user-facing services deliver recommendations through navigation tools and digital signage. The platform connects with existing smart city and mobility systems to ensure interoperability. The pilot resulted in reduced time spent searching for parking, smoother traffic flows and increased uptake of alternative transport modes. Qualitative benefits include lower emissions, improved travel experience and enhanced decision support for urban planners, demonstrating a scalable model for smart mobility management.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-316,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00310,EU_PSTW_202609_00310,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Dutch Tax Authority,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.",UNKNOWN,Business Intelligence and Analytics (BI&A) - Indication of tax fraud risks based on previous declarations,"Dutch Tax Authority; Central-Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Netherlands","Tax administrations must identify fraud and recovery risks across very large volumes of declarations and receivables, while using enforcement capacity in a targeted and proportionate manner. Business Intelligence and Analytics (BI&A) supports the Dutch Tax Authority by using self-learning models to indicate where risks may exist, based on previously inspected declarations and feedback from inspection outcomes.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Tax administrations must identify fraud and recovery risks across very large volumes of declarations and receivables, while using enforcement capacity in a targeted and proportionate manner. Business Intelligence and Analytics (BI&A) supports the Dutch Tax Authority by using self-learning models to indicate where risks may exist, based on previously inspected declarations and feedback from inspection outcomes. The broader operational objective is to move from uniform or purely rule-based selection towards more refined risk identification, allowing the administration to focus attention where available data suggest a higher probability of non-compliance or recovery opportunities. One example is Dynamic Monitoring, which links outstanding receivables to possible sources of redress, such as wages, cars, real estate or other assets, and generates lists of claims where new recovery options may be available. The implementation combines machine-learning methods with administrative tax data, inspection histories and feedback loops from previous control results. Models are progressively refined as additional outcomes become available, improving their capacity to identify relevant risk patterns. The potential impact is more efficient allocation of inspection and recovery resources, faster identification of actionable cases and improved use of existing administrative data. At the same time, such systems require careful governance to ensure transparency, legality, non-discrimination and appropriate human oversight in tax decision-making.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-317,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00311,EU_PSTW_202609_00311,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Public Prosecution Service,Law courts,"Prosecutors and legal researchers face significant information-management challenges when preparing criminal cases, as relevant case law, procedural material and hidden connections may be dispersed across large and complex legal datasets. The impact is reduced time spent on manual research, better handling of information overload and improved ability to detect relevant or previously hidden information.",Case Law Engine,Public Prosecution Service; Central-Government; Law courts; Netherlands,"The tool, also described as a “Jurisprudentierobot”, supports prosecutors and researchers in identifying relevant case law, understanding patterns and making better use of information during preparation.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Prosecutors and legal researchers face significant information-management challenges when preparing criminal cases, as relevant case law, procedural material and hidden connections may be dispersed across large and complex legal datasets. Case Law Engine was developed by LexIQ for the Dutch Public Prosecution Service to reduce research time and improve the quality of case preparation. The Public Prosecution Service had explored how advanced digital tools could support daily working procedures, particularly where professionals need to search, analyse and draw insights from criminal-case information. The tool, also described as a “Jurisprudentierobot”, supports prosecutors and researchers in identifying relevant case law, understanding patterns and making better use of information during preparation. Its implementation combines machine-learning algorithms with intuitive search functions, data visualisations and analytical features that help users navigate large volumes of legal and case-related data. Rather than replacing legal judgement, the system provides indications, connections and possible outcome-related insights that professionals can assess within their own legal reasoning. The impact is reduced time spent on manual research, better handling of information overload and improved ability to detect relevant or previously hidden information. For the Public Prosecution Service, the tool offers business value by strengthening preparation quality, supporting consistency and enabling staff to approach criminal-case analysis in a more structured and evidence-informed way.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-318,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00312,EU_PSTW_202609_00312,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Amsterdam and AMS Institute,Public order and safety n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,CityFlows-Amsterdam,Municipality of Amsterdam and AMS Institute; Local Government; Public order and safety n.e.c.; Netherlands,The objective was to provide public authorities with a decision-support capability that helps them understand crowd dynamics and take timely measures to improve the use of public space.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Busy pedestrian areas in Amsterdam require reliable crowd-management capabilities to safeguard public safety, maintain comfort and support evidence-based operational decisions in high-density urban spaces. The CityFlows initiative, developed as an EIT Urban Mobility flagship project, brought together the Municipality of Amsterdam, AMS Institute and a wider consortium of partners to address the need for better monitoring and management of pedestrian flows, particularly in locations affected by congestion, events, tourism and changing mobility patterns. The objective was to provide public authorities with a decision-support capability that helps them understand crowd dynamics and take timely measures to improve the use of public space. The emerging technology implementation centres on the Crowd Management Decision-Support System, a software-based platform licensed through City Analytics to government authorities. At conceptual level, the system combines pedestrian-flow data, urban analytics and operational decision-support functions to help authorities assess crowding conditions, anticipate risks and select appropriate interventions. Its deployment supports integration between urban data sources, monitoring tools and municipal crowd-management processes. The main impact is improved situational awareness for public authorities, enabling safer, more comfortable and more responsive management of busy pedestrian spaces. The creation of City Analytics as a dedicated start-up also strengthens scalability and reuse, allowing the solution to be offered beyond the original project context and supporting cities seeking reliable tools for managing public space during normal operations and crisis situations.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-319,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00313,EU_PSTW_202609_00313,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Municipality of Rijswijk, Dynniq",Health n.e.c.,"The municipality of Rijswijk tested CrossWalk to address a concrete accessibility and road-safety challenge: standard pedestrian crossing times are often insufficient for older people, people with disabilities or citizens with reduced mobility. Its impact lies in improving pedestrian safety, accessibility and user experience for residents who walk more slowly.",CrossWalk in Smart City Rijswijk,"Municipality of Rijswijk, Dynniq; Local Government; Health n.e.c.; Netherlands","When a registered user with mobility difficulties approaches or waits at a crossing, the app uses location data to communicate with the traffic control software and signal the user’s presence.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The municipality of Rijswijk tested CrossWalk to address a concrete accessibility and road-safety challenge: standard pedestrian crossing times are often insufficient for older people, people with disabilities or citizens with reduced mobility. This can create unsafe situations at intersections and reduce the autonomy of residents who require more time to cross the road. The pilot involved the Municipality of Rijswijk and Dynniq, with the objective of making pedestrian infrastructure more responsive to the needs of vulnerable road users while avoiding permanent changes to signal timing for all pedestrians. The emerging technology implementation is based on a mobile application connected to traffic signal systems. When a registered user with mobility difficulties approaches or waits at a crossing, the app uses location data to communicate with the traffic control software and signal the user’s presence. The traffic light system can then automatically extend the green phase, allowing additional time for a safe crossing. At conceptual level, the solution integrates mobile positioning, traffic-light control logic and intersection management systems. Its impact lies in improving pedestrian safety, accessibility and user experience for residents who walk more slowly. It also demonstrates how smart-city infrastructure can provide targeted support without redesigning the entire traffic system, making the approach potentially scalable to other intersections and municipalities with similar accessibility objectives.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-320,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00314,EU_PSTW_202609_00314,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""social_nonprofit""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of The Hague and Phinion Data Engineering.,R&D general public services.,"Poor data quality can lead to delays, incorrect processing, duplicated work or reduced confidence in digital administration.",Improve data quality with AI - Machine learning to improve the data quality of registrations,Municipality of The Hague and Phinion Data Engineering.; Local Government; R&D general public services.; Netherlands,"The implementation uses Phinion’s Qbot product, which applies machine-learning techniques to discover patterns and deviations within and between registrations.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Municipal services depend on accurate and consistent data registrations, because errors, inconsistencies or missing information can directly affect service quality, administrative reliability and decisions made for residents. Improve data quality with AI was piloted by the Municipality of The Hague with Phinion Data Engineering to analyse, monitor and continuously improve the quality of municipal registrations. The problem addressed is not a single faulty dataset, but the broader need to maintain trustworthy records across the systems that underpin local public services. Poor data quality can lead to delays, incorrect processing, duplicated work or reduced confidence in digital administration. The implementation uses Phinion’s Qbot product, which applies machine-learning techniques to discover patterns and deviations within and between registrations. At a conceptual level, the system examines relationships in administrative data, identifies anomalies and highlights inconsistencies that may require correction or further investigation. This enables the municipality to move from ad hoc data-quality checks towards more continuous monitoring. The expected impact is higher-quality registrations, earlier detection of data problems, improved operational reliability and better support for service delivery. By helping staff identify structural issues in the data, the pilot also contributes to more efficient back-office processes and strengthens the foundation for future digital services that depend on accurate municipal information.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-321,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00315,EU_PSTW_202609_00315,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Dutch Police,Police Services,"The operational challenge is that valuable links between cases, suspects, events or pieces of evidence may remain hidden when data are fragmented across systems or too extensive to review manually.",The Refinery: AI in investigation support,Dutch Police; Local Government; Police Services; Netherlands,"The tool is intended to assist investigative judgement rather than automate final decisions. Its impact lies in faster analysis, improved situational awareness, better detection of links across cases and more effective use of available police data.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Police investigators need to process and interpret increasing volumes of information from multiple sources, including case files, operational records and digital evidence, without losing sight of relevant patterns or connections. The Refinery: AI in investigation support is a data-analysis facility developed by the Dutch National Police to enhance investigative capabilities and help officers work with complex information environments. The operational challenge is that valuable links between cases, suspects, events or pieces of evidence may remain hidden when data are fragmented across systems or too extensive to review manually. The platform enables investigators to access, interpret and visualise large volumes of police data drawn from diverse sources. Its implementation combines data integration, analytical tooling and AI-driven methods that support the detection of patterns, relationships and connections within investigative material. At a conceptual level, the system can help structure heterogeneous datasets, identify associations and present results through visual interfaces that make complex information easier to understand. The tool is intended to assist investigative judgement rather than automate final decisions. Its impact lies in faster analysis, improved situational awareness, better detection of links across cases and more effective use of available police data. For the Dutch National Police, The Refinery strengthens investigative capacity, supports evidence-led policing and provides a scalable analytical environment for complex criminal investigations.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-322,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00316,EU_PSTW_202609_00316,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Province of South Holland,Transport,"The Province of South Holland must manage the movement of both road traffic and shipping across a region where bridge openings can create delays, congestion and competing demands between transport modes.",Algorithm Impact monitor bridge openings,Province of South Holland; Regional Government; Transport; Netherlands,The smart bridge addresses this challenge by using digital monitoring and predictive analytics to support more informed bridge-operation decisions across an infrastructure portfolio that includes more than 100 bridges.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Province of South Holland must manage the movement of both road traffic and shipping across a region where bridge openings can create delays, congestion and competing demands between transport modes. The smart bridge addresses this challenge by using digital monitoring and predictive analytics to support more informed bridge-operation decisions across an infrastructure portfolio that includes more than 100 bridges. The initiative forms part of South Holland’s wider work on smart shipping and focuses on linking up-to-date road-traffic information with live shipping information, so that the impact of bridge openings can be better understood and anticipated. In the project “impact monitor bridge openings”, the province has taken an important step towards predicting traffic intensities around several key bridges. The technological implementation combines sensor-based data collection, traffic information and algorithmic analysis, including Long Short-Term Memory neural networks designed to forecast traffic conditions up to 21 minutes ahead. These models are suited to time-series prediction because they can learn from sequential patterns in traffic behaviour and bridge-operation data. The reported correlation-efficient accuracy of 60–80% indicates that the approach can provide useful short-term forecasts for operational planning. The main value is improved anticipation of congestion, better coordination between road and waterway mobility, more evidence-based bridge management and a stronger basis for reducing disruption while keeping both transport networks accessible and safe.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-323,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00317,EU_PSTW_202609_00317,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Netherlands Enterprise Agency (RVO),Public order and safety n.e.c.,"Traditional monitoring relies heavily on human operators observing multiple camera feeds, which can be demanding, inconsistent and slow.","Detection of aggression, panic and abnormal behaviour with smart cameras",Netherlands Enterprise Agency (RVO); Central-Government; Public order and safety n.e.c.; Netherlands,"The implementation uses video analytics and behaviour-recognition models to process visual information from cameras, identify movement or interaction patterns associated with abnormal events, and generate alerts for security or assistance personnel.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"This pilot investigates whether smart-camera analytics can support earlier recognition of aggression, panic and abnormal behaviour in crowded public environments. The operational problem is that incidents in stations, airports, city centres or entertainment districts can escalate quickly if security teams identify them too late. Traditional monitoring relies heavily on human operators observing multiple camera feeds, which can be demanding, inconsistent and slow. ViNotion develops technology that analyses the behaviour of individuals and detects signs of panic, aggression or deviant conduct at an early stage. The implementation uses video analytics and behaviour-recognition models to process visual information from cameras, identify movement or interaction patterns associated with abnormal events, and generate alerts for security or assistance personnel. Conceptually, the system combines computer vision, behavioural classification, anomaly detection and real-time alerting. The expected impact is faster deployment of staff, reduced harm to the public, more efficient incident response and better management of safety risks in large public areas. Its value depends on accuracy, proportionality and careful governance, because behavioural interpretation in public spaces can raise concerns around false positives, surveillance intensity and the treatment of individuals whose conduct is incorrectly flagged.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-324,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00318,EU_PSTW_202609_00318,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Amersfoort,Police Services,UNKNOWN,Firework nuisance detection system - AI to determine if bangs are firework,Municipality of Amersfoort; Local Government; Police Services; Netherlands,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Dutch municipalities face recurring public order and safety issues linked to illegal fireworks, particularly during evenings and festive periods, placing strain on enforcement capacity and generating resident complaints. The firework nuisance detection system was introduced to help municipalities such as Amersfoort identify when and where fireworks are being set off, enabling more targeted enforcement. Stakeholders include municipal authorities, enforcement officers, residents and public safety services. The objective is to distinguish fireworks from other loud noises, identify nuisance hotspots and optimise patrol routes and response times without relying solely on citizen reports. The system combines a distributed network of acoustic sensors placed in public space, which capture sound events and triangulate their location when multiple sensors detect the same bang. Signal-processing and classification techniques analyse acoustic signatures to distinguish fireworks from other impulsive sounds such as slamming doors or vehicle backfires. When a sound is classified as fireworks, alerts are automatically sent to municipal enforcers via mobile devices, alongside spatial and temporal data. The pilot has improved situational awareness and allowed more selective deployment of enforcement resources. Qualitatively, municipalities report faster response times, better evidence-based planning and improved transparency around nuisance patterns, while residents benefit from more visible and timely enforcement.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-325,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00319,EU_PSTW_202609_00319,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipalities (Boxmeer),Social protection n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,Diagnosis Plan and Control Tool - Assessing the risk of home fraud,Municipalities (Boxmeer); Local Government; Social protection n.e.c.; Netherlands,"In complex housing situations, case officers faced difficulties in deciding when a home visit was justified, leading to inconsistent practices and inefficient use of limited inspection capacity. The objective of this use case was to support more consistent, evidence-based prioritisation of cases where discrepancies between declared circumstances and observed living conditions suggested elevated risk, while preserving human decision-making authority.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Municipal social services must prevent benefit fraud while ensuring proportional enforcement and safeguarding citizens’ rights. In complex housing situations, case officers faced difficulties in deciding when a home visit was justified, leading to inconsistent practices and inefficient use of limited inspection capacity. The objective of this use case was to support more consistent, evidence-based prioritisation of cases where discrepancies between declared circumstances and observed living conditions suggested elevated risk, while preserving human decision-making authority. The tool aggregated administrative data, including household composition, housing arrangements, declared living costs and registered debts, and applied statistical risk-scoring techniques to generate structured risk profiles. These profiles were integrated into existing municipal case-management systems and presented as decision support indicators rather than automated outcomes. Municipalities reported more focused inspections and fewer unnecessary home visits, improving resource allocation and operational efficiency. However, concerns regarding transparency, proportionality and potential bias prompted increased regulatory scrutiny, and similar profiling tools have since been restricted or discontinued in several municipalities",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-326,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00320,EU_PSTW_202609_00320,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Ministry of Social Affairs and Employment,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.","The operational problem is that relevant information may be dispersed across documents, websites, policy files and other open sources, making manual retrieval slow and increasing the risk that important material is missed.",Digital assistant for answering Parliamentary questions,"Ministry of Social Affairs and Employment; Central-Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Netherlands","Depending on its configuration, such a tool may use natural language processing to understand questions, rank information by relevance and help structure the evidence base for a draft response. Its impact lies in reducing research time, improving consistency in source use, supporting more complete answers and allowing staff to focus more of their effort on analysis, drafting quality and institutional coordination.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Policy officials must often answer Parliamentary questions under time pressure, while ensuring that responses are accurate, well-evidenced and consistent with publicly available information. Digital assistant for answering Parliamentary questions supports the Ministry of Social Affairs and Employment by helping policy staff search public sources more efficiently during the preparation of answers. The operational problem is that relevant information may be dispersed across documents, websites, policy files and other open sources, making manual retrieval slow and increasing the risk that important material is missed. The assistant is intended to strengthen the preparatory phase of drafting by accelerating information discovery and supporting staff in identifying relevant public evidence. From a technological perspective, the system functions as a search and retrieval tool that can scan public sources, interpret user queries and return potentially relevant material for policy officers to review. Depending on its configuration, such a tool may use natural language processing to understand questions, rank information by relevance and help structure the evidence base for a draft response. It does not replace the judgement of officials or the political and legal checks required before an answer is submitted. Its impact lies in reducing research time, improving consistency in source use, supporting more complete answers and allowing staff to focus more of their effort on analysis, drafting quality and institutional coordination.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,S7_GENERATIVE_CONTENT_CREATION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-327,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00321,EU_PSTW_202609_00321,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Ministry of Justice and Security, Ministry of the Interior and Kingdom Relations",General services.,UNKNOWN,Digital identification and verification,"Ministry of Justice and Security, Ministry of the Interior and Kingdom Relations; Central-Government; General services.; Netherlands","This research initiative examined the potential use of biometric identifiers, such as facial recognition, within government contexts.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Public authorities in the Netherlands have explored digital identification methods to improve security, efficiency and user experience in public services. This research initiative examined the potential use of biometric identifiers, such as facial recognition, within government contexts. Stakeholders include government agencies, technology researchers, citizens and data protection authorities. The objective was to assess feasibility, risks and governance requirements rather than to deploy an operational system. The research analysed biometric recognition techniques, enrolment and verification processes, and possible integration with existing identity infrastructures. Particular attention was paid to data quality, accuracy, false positives, and compliance with privacy and data protection frameworks. Technical studies focused on how biometric templates could be securely stored, matched and audited, and under what conditions such systems might be appropriate. No operational rollout followed the research, but the findings informed policy discussions on digital identity, highlighting significant legal, ethical and societal constraints. The impact lies primarily in improved understanding of technological limitations and governance requirements, contributing to more cautious and rights-aware approaches to digital identity innovation in the public sector.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-328,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00322,EU_PSTW_202609_00322,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,National Archives and ICTU,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.","The National Archives of the Netherlands and ICTU explored E-Discovery to address a growing information-management challenge within public administration: the increasing volume of incoming and outgoing emails that must be classified, retained, searched and managed in accordance with archival and administrative requirements. Manual classification is time-consuming, inconsistent and difficult to scale, particularly for organisations that handle large volumes of official correspondence.",E-Discovery - automatic classifcation of incoming and outgoing emails,"National Archives and ICTU; Central-Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Netherlands",UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The National Archives of the Netherlands and ICTU explored E-Discovery to address a growing information-management challenge within public administration: the increasing volume of incoming and outgoing emails that must be classified, retained, searched and managed in accordance with archival and administrative requirements. Manual classification is time-consuming, inconsistent and difficult to scale, particularly for organisations that handle large volumes of official correspondence. In 2016, the Nationaal Archief launched a study into the potential of E-Discovery methods in information management, with the objective of testing whether automated support could improve the organisation, retrieval and governance of email records. The emerging technology implementation consisted of a prototype capable of classifying emails automatically. At conceptual level, the system used machine-learning techniques to analyse email content and metadata, identify relevant categories, and assign classifications that could support archival processes. The prototype was developed in cooperation with ICTU, linking information-management expertise with technical implementation capacity. The expected impact is a more efficient, consistent and scalable approach to managing public-sector email records. By reducing the burden of manual classification, the solution can support better compliance, faster retrieval of information and more reliable records management. Although described as a study and prototype, the case demonstrates the value of automated classification for public administrations facing rapidly growing volumes of digital correspondence.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK|F06_HIGH_VOLUME_SCALABILITY_BOTTLENECK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-329,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00323,EU_PSTW_202609_00323,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"ProRail, Schiphol and other organisations",Transport,"Organisations that generate residual materials often lack efficient mechanisms to identify potential reusers, assess the value of surplus flows and close material loops across sectors. Matching algorithms then identify possible links between suppliers and buyers, including combinations that may not be obvious through manual brokerage.",Excess Material Exchange (EME),"ProRail, Schiphol and other organisations; Central-Government; Transport; Netherlands",UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Organisations that generate residual materials often lack efficient mechanisms to identify potential reusers, assess the value of surplus flows and close material loops across sectors. Excess Material Exchange (EME) addresses this problem by creating a digital trading platform where supply and demand for excess materials can be matched at scale. The initiative involves organisations such as ProRail, Schiphol and other public and private actors seeking to reduce waste and improve circular-economy performance. The operational objective is to move residual materials away from disposal or low-value treatment and towards productive reuse by making information about available materials more visible, comparable and actionable. The technology implementation relies on artificial intelligence to read, interpret and structure data on material streams, including information on composition, availability, quality, location and potential applications. Matching algorithms then identify possible links between suppliers and buyers, including combinations that may not be obvious through manual brokerage. The platform’s intelligence lies not only in listing materials, but also in actively bringing together parties whose residual flows and needs may align. The expected impact is faster matching, greater scalability in circular-material exchange, reduced transaction costs and improved resource efficiency. For participating organisations, EME can support waste reduction, lower procurement or disposal costs, and contribute to broader sustainability and circular-economy objectives.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-330,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00324,EU_PSTW_202609_00324,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Technolution B.V.,Transport,"The system is intended to give authorities and transport planners a clearer picture of flows, routes and travel times without relying on manual observation or intrusive data collection.","FlowCube - a picture of the traffic of cyclists, vehicles and pedestrians",Technolution B.V.; Private sector; Transport; Netherlands,"FlowCube responds to this need by using compact camera-based modules to generate traffic intelligence for local mobility management. The expected impact is more accurate insight into active mobility, better evidence for infrastructure planning, improved monitoring of cycling and walking policies, and safer management of mixed-use streets.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Street managers need reliable information on how different road users move through public space, especially where cyclists, pedestrians and vehicles interact in ways that are not fully captured by traditional traffic counts. FlowCube responds to this need by using compact camera-based modules to generate traffic intelligence for local mobility management. The system is intended to give authorities and transport planners a clearer picture of flows, routes and travel times without relying on manual observation or intrusive data collection. Its implementation combines compact video sensors with image-recognition algorithms that distinguish cyclists, pedestrians and, where configured, other types of traffic. At a conceptual level, the modules detect objects, classify road-user categories, track local movement and aggregate observations into usable mobility data. Multiple FlowCubes can also combine data to infer commonly used cycling routes and corresponding travel times. The expected impact is more accurate insight into active mobility, better evidence for infrastructure planning, improved monitoring of cycling and walking policies, and safer management of mixed-use streets. Technolution presents FlowCube as an operational product and emphasises privacy compliance, because the system generates mobility indicators rather than functioning as an identity-based surveillance tool. This makes it relevant for municipalities seeking granular traffic intelligence while remaining within data-protection constraints.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-331,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00325,EU_PSTW_202609_00325,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Netherlands Forensic Institute (NFI),Police Services,"FIRE is an experimental forensic image-recognition system developed to reduce the manual burden on detection staff when analysing large volumes of visual material. If the approach succeeds, work that might otherwise require extensive manual review could be narrowed to a more manageable set of flagged images.",Forensic Image Recognition (FIRE) - Recognition of containers that have illegal cargo,Netherlands Forensic Institute (NFI); Central-Government; Police Services; Netherlands,"The implementation uses automatic image recognition, drawing on large datasets to train or test models capable of identifying relevant visual patterns. However, before broader use, the project must address dataset compilation, storage, lawful use and inter-agency sharing.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"FIRE is an experimental forensic image-recognition system developed to reduce the manual burden on detection staff when analysing large volumes of visual material. The operational problem is that forensic and investigative services may need to inspect extensive image datasets, including data carriers and other visual sources, to identify objects that could be relevant to criminal investigations. In this framed pilot area, the Netherlands Forensic Institute examines whether the system can learn to recognise sea containers that may contain illegal cargo. The implementation uses automatic image recognition, drawing on large datasets to train or test models capable of identifying relevant visual patterns. Conceptually, FIRE combines computer vision, dataset preparation, model training, image classification and explainability requirements, particularly because forensic applications must be legally robust and understandable. The expected impact is a substantial reduction in the time required for image-based screening, allowing staff to focus on higher-value forensic analysis. If the approach succeeds, work that might otherwise require extensive manual review could be narrowed to a more manageable set of flagged images. However, before broader use, the project must address dataset compilation, storage, lawful use and inter-agency sharing.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK|F06_HIGH_VOLUME_SCALABILITY_BOTTLENECK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-332,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00326,EU_PSTW_202609_00326,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Mental health institutions,Social protection n.e.c.,"Mental-health providers need accurate and current information on waiting times because delays affect access to care, referral choices, planning, accountability and communication with patients and commissioners. The practical problem is that waiting-time data can sit across electronic client records, intake processes, treatment-planning systems and reporting obligations, making it difficult to produce consistent insight without manual extraction and reconciliation.",Geniq - reports wait times,Mental health institutions; Local Government; Social protection n.e.c.; Netherlands,"The implementation uses data-processing and reporting functionality connected to the care-administration environment, allowing institutions to calculate waiting-time indicators, analyse patterns and generate reports for internal management or external accountability.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Mental-health providers need accurate and current information on waiting times because delays affect access to care, referral choices, planning, accountability and communication with patients and commissioners. Geniq was developed for Dutch mental-health institutions as a reporting and analytics capability that calculates, analyses and reports waiting times so that managers and operational teams can maintain an up-to-date view of service accessibility. The practical problem is that waiting-time data can sit across electronic client records, intake processes, treatment-planning systems and reporting obligations, making it difficult to produce consistent insight without manual extraction and reconciliation. The implementation uses data-processing and reporting functionality connected to the care-administration environment, allowing institutions to calculate waiting-time indicators, analyse patterns and generate reports for internal management or external accountability. At a conceptual level, the system transforms operational registration data into structured management information, helping organisations distinguish where delays arise, which pathways are under pressure and where additional capacity or process changes may be needed. The expected impact is better planning of clinical capacity, faster identification of bottlenecks, stronger compliance with waiting-time transparency requirements and more informed dialogue with referrers, insurers and patients. In the wider Dutch mental-health context, reliable waiting-time insight remains important because national regulators continue to emphasise the need for dependable data on the scale and development of GGZ waiting times.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,S7_GENERATIVE_CONTENT_CREATION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-333,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00327,EU_PSTW_202609_00327,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Province of Gelderland, Bolesian.ai",Pollution abatement.,"The GEO reference of documents project supports the Province of Gelderland in analysing large volumes of unstructured information related to nitrogen emissions and soil pollution. The policy and administrative challenge is that environmental files, permits, reports and technical documents may contain relevant evidence, but their unstructured format makes it difficult for officials to extract patterns, locations and relationships efficiently.",GEO reference of documents - Gaining more insight into data on nitrogen emissions and soil pollution,"Province of Gelderland, Bolesian.ai; Regional Government; Pollution abatement.; Netherlands","The implementation uses natural language processing, document analytics and geographic referencing to process unstructured text and connect relevant information to territorial or environmental contexts.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The GEO reference of documents project supports the Province of Gelderland in analysing large volumes of unstructured information related to nitrogen emissions and soil pollution. The policy and administrative challenge is that environmental files, permits, reports and technical documents may contain relevant evidence, but their unstructured format makes it difficult for officials to extract patterns, locations and relationships efficiently. Bolesian developed an application to help the province gain more insight from this dispersed information base. The implementation uses natural language processing, document analytics and geographic referencing to process unstructured text and connect relevant information to territorial or environmental contexts. Conceptually, the system can identify key entities, themes, locations and relationships within documents, then structure them so that policymakers and specialists can examine nitrogen and soil-pollution issues more systematically. The expected impact is improved environmental intelligence, faster review of complex documentation and stronger evidence for policy, permitting and enforcement decisions. By converting unstructured material into more usable data, the application can support more transparent and data-informed environmental governance in a policy field where decisions are often technically complex and politically sensitive.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F04_UNSTRUCTURED_INFORMATION_COMPLEXITY|F06_HIGH_VOLUME_SCALABILITY_BOTTLENECK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-334,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00328,EU_PSTW_202609_00328,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Netherlands Food and Consumer Product Safety Authority (NVWA),"Agriculture, forestry, fishing and hunting","The operational problem is that animal-distribution and abundance monitoring is traditionally carried out manually on foot or through aerial recording from manned aircraft, both of which can be costly, slow and labour-intensive. Conceptually, the system replaces broad manual observation with aerial data capture, image-processing pipelines and automated counting models, while still requiring quality checks and validation by competent staff.",Graasdierpremie - Use of drones in counting animals,"Netherlands Food and Consumer Product Safety Authority (NVWA); Central-Government; Agriculture, forestry, fishing and hunting; Netherlands","The implementation uses drone imagery to collect visual data on animals in the field, combined with computer-vision methods that detect, classify and count animals automatically.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Graasdierpremie pilot examines whether drones and automated image analysis can improve the counting and monitoring of grazing animals. The operational problem is that animal-distribution and abundance monitoring is traditionally carried out manually on foot or through aerial recording from manned aircraft, both of which can be costly, slow and labour-intensive. For public authorities such as the NVWA, more efficient monitoring can support nature conservation, agricultural control and subsidy-related verification. The implementation uses drone imagery to collect visual data on animals in the field, combined with computer-vision methods that detect, classify and count animals automatically. Conceptually, the system replaces broad manual observation with aerial data capture, image-processing pipelines and automated counting models, while still requiring quality checks and validation by competent staff. The expected impact is faster monitoring of animal populations, reduced inspection costs, improved geographical coverage and more consistent evidence for administrative or conservation decisions. If validated, the approach could make field verification more scalable and less dependent on resource-intensive manual methods. It may also support more timely insights into animal distribution, enabling better planning and more targeted follow-up inspections.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-335,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00329,EU_PSTW_202609_00329,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Netherlands Forensic Institute,Health n.e.c.,"Criminal investigations increasingly involve very large volumes of seized digital material, making it difficult for forensic experts and investigators to manually review every phone, computer, hard drive or storage device in time-sensitive cases. Hansken - Recognizing images of narcotics in digital seizure responds to this challenge by helping the Netherlands Forensic Institute and investigative authorities identify relevant visual or textual evidence more quickly within large datasets.",Hansken - Recognizing images of narcotics in digital seizure,Netherlands Forensic Institute; Central-Government; Health n.e.c.; Netherlands,"The technological implementation uses a self-learning algorithm integrated into the Hansken digital-forensics environment. Its impact is faster search across seized data, better use of investigative capacity, improved ability to find hidden or dispersed evidence and stronger support for serious-crime investigations where digital material is extensive and operational timelines are demanding.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Criminal investigations increasingly involve very large volumes of seized digital material, making it difficult for forensic experts and investigators to manually review every phone, computer, hard drive or storage device in time-sensitive cases. Hansken - Recognizing images of narcotics in digital seizure responds to this challenge by helping the Netherlands Forensic Institute and investigative authorities identify relevant visual or textual evidence more quickly within large datasets. The operational aim is to accelerate the discovery of incriminating or case-relevant material while reducing the burden of exhaustive manual review. The technological implementation uses a self-learning algorithm integrated into the Hansken digital-forensics environment. The system can be trained to recognise specific categories of images, such as weapons or drugs, and to detect text elements appearing in images, including licence plates or account numbers on stolen bank cards. At a conceptual level, image-recognition and pattern-detection techniques classify visual content and highlight items that may deserve closer examination by experts. The tool supports forensic prioritisation rather than replacing evidentiary assessment, which remains subject to professional and legal review. Its impact is faster search across seized data, better use of investigative capacity, improved ability to find hidden or dispersed evidence and stronger support for serious-crime investigations where digital material is extensive and operational timelines are demanding.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK|F06_HIGH_VOLUME_SCALABILITY_BOTTLENECK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-337,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00330,EU_PSTW_202609_00330,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,HKV Line in water B.V.,Environmental protection n.e.c.,"The operational challenge is that water authorities and infrastructure managers face expanding management areas while staffing capacity for inspection can be limited. Manual inspection of dikes, structures and water systems can be time-consuming, and early recognition of deterioration is essential for flood safety and maintenance planning.",HKV Eye - Line in Water,HKV Line in water B.V.; Private sector; Environmental protection n.e.c.; Netherlands,"HKV Lijn in Water developed HKVEYE to provide additional “eyes” on water infrastructure, using visual data to examine the condition of dikes and related assets in more detail. The implementation applies data-science techniques to interpret images and other visual data, supporting questions such as monitoring vegetation quality, assessing revetment condition, detecting sand-carrying boils and conducting difference analyses over time.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"HKVEYE supports the inspection and monitoring of dikes, water defences and water systems by adding data-driven visual analysis to water-management practice. The operational challenge is that water authorities and infrastructure managers face expanding management areas while staffing capacity for inspection can be limited. Manual inspection of dikes, structures and water systems can be time-consuming, and early recognition of deterioration is essential for flood safety and maintenance planning. HKV Lijn in Water developed HKVEYE to provide additional “eyes” on water infrastructure, using visual data to examine the condition of dikes and related assets in more detail. The implementation applies data-science techniques to interpret images and other visual data, supporting questions such as monitoring vegetation quality, assessing revetment condition, detecting sand-carrying boils and conducting difference analyses over time. Conceptually, the system combines visual monitoring, data interpretation, anomaly detection and inspection-support workflows. The expected impact is more precise, efficient and evidence-based inspection, helping managers focus attention on areas that require intervention. By extracting useful information from increasingly available visual data, HKVEYE can improve maintenance prioritisation, support flood-risk management and strengthen the reliability of water-safety monitoring.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,S3_DOCUMENT_INTELLIGENCE|S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-338,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00331,EU_PSTW_202609_00331,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Amsterdam,Social protection n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,Identifying people at risk of falling into large debts,Municipality of Amsterdam; Local Government; Social protection n.e.c.; Netherlands,"The Amsterdam use case concerns the use of predictive or data-driven methods to identify residents who may be at risk of accumulating large debts, so that support can be offered earlier. The intended value is not to automate sanctions, but to surface possible vulnerability so that human professionals can offer assistance, advice or referral to debt-support services.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Municipal debt-prevention services increasingly try to intervene before financial problems become unmanageable, because late intervention can lead to enforcement costs, housing insecurity, health impacts and long-term dependence on social support. The Amsterdam use case concerns the use of predictive or data-driven methods to identify residents who may be at risk of accumulating large debts, so that support can be offered earlier. The operational problem is that signs of debt distress may be distributed across different municipal or service-provider datasets, while front-line staff may only become aware of the situation once arrears have already escalated. At a conceptual level, the system would combine available administrative signals, risk indicators and historical patterns to prioritise cases for preventive outreach. The intended value is not to automate sanctions, but to surface possible vulnerability so that human professionals can offer assistance, advice or referral to debt-support services. The expected impact is earlier identification of residents in financial difficulty, better targeting of municipal support, reduced long-term debt escalation and more efficient use of social-service capacity. However, such systems require careful safeguards because financial-risk profiling can reproduce biases in historical data, affect vulnerable households and create opacity around why a person is flagged.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-339,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00332,EU_PSTW_202609_00332,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Public Prosecutor's Office, the National Police, the Tax Authorities, Customs, the FIOD and the Financial Intelligence Unit, the Social Affairs and Employment Inspectorate (Investigation Directorate), the Intelligence and Investigation Service of the Environment and Transport Inspectorate (ILT-IOD), the Intelligence and Investigation Service of the Dutch Food and Consumer Product Safety Authority (NVWA-IOD), the Media Commissioner",Police Services,"The operational problem is that individual agencies may each hold partial information, while criminal financial structures often exploit fragmentation between enforcement, tax, customs, labour and supervisory bodies.",Infobox criminal and unexplained assets (iCOV),"Public Prosecutor's Office, the National Police, the Tax Authorities, Customs, the FIOD and the Financial Intelligence Unit, the Social Affairs and Employment Inspectorate (Investigation Directorate), the Intelligence and Investigation Service of the Environment and Transport Inspectorate (ILT-IOD), the Intelligence and Investigation Service of the Dutch Food and Consumer Product Safety Authority (NVWA-IOD), the Media Commissioner; Central-Government; Police Services; Netherlands","Current public information describes iCOV as an active cooperation that helps partners through data-driven reports, including practical examples of use by investigation and prosecution services.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Financial crime investigations often require analysts to understand relationships between people, companies, assets, income, transactions and public records that are dispersed across different authorities. The Infobox Crimineel en Onverklaarbaar Vermogen, known as iCOV, provides Dutch public partners with data-driven reports that support the detection and disruption of money laundering, tax evasion, fraud and unexplained wealth. The operational problem is that individual agencies may each hold partial information, while criminal financial structures often exploit fragmentation between enforcement, tax, customs, labour and supervisory bodies. The implementation is a formal cooperation between public organisations, including the Public Prosecutor’s Office, National Police, Tax Authorities, Customs, FIOD, Financial Intelligence Unit and several inspection and investigative services. At a conceptual level, iCOV combines authorised data access, risk indicators, analytical models and reporting formats to generate insight into assets, income relationships, companies and suspicious patterns. The system is not a fully autonomous enforcement mechanism; its outputs support investigation, prosecution and asset recovery by giving professionals a richer picture of potential criminal or unexplained wealth. The expected impact is faster financial investigation, improved targeting of asset-confiscation work, stronger inter-agency cooperation and more effective action against organised financial crime. Current public information describes iCOV as an active cooperation that helps partners through data-driven reports, including practical examples of use by investigation and prosecution services.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-340,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00333,EU_PSTW_202609_00333,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Ministry of Instrastructures and market parties,Transport,"The bridge-inspection drone pilot explores how unmanned aerial vehicles can reduce reliance on manual inspection capacity for bridges and viaducts. The operational problem is that infrastructure inspections are often labour-intensive, require specialist access arrangements, and may expose inspectors to safety risks when structures are difficult to reach.",Inspection bridges with drones,Ministry of Instrastructures and market parties; Central-Government; Transport; Netherlands,"The implementation uses drones to capture visual data from bridges and viaducts, allowing inspection teams to review high-resolution imagery of structural elements that would otherwise require physical access.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The bridge-inspection drone pilot explores how unmanned aerial vehicles can reduce reliance on manual inspection capacity for bridges and viaducts. The operational problem is that infrastructure inspections are often labour-intensive, require specialist access arrangements, and may expose inspectors to safety risks when structures are difficult to reach. Public infrastructure managers need reliable ways to detect deterioration, document structural conditions and prioritise maintenance without closing assets unnecessarily or deploying extensive human resources for every inspection. The implementation uses drones to capture visual data from bridges and viaducts, allowing inspection teams to review high-resolution imagery of structural elements that would otherwise require physical access. At a conceptual level, the approach can combine UAV-based image capture, georeferenced inspection records, visual analytics and, where developed further, computer-vision models for recognising cracks, corrosion, deformation or other signs of damage. The expected impact is faster inspection, lower operational burden, improved safety for inspection staff and more consistent documentation of asset condition. If validated, the pilot could support more risk-based infrastructure maintenance by helping authorities identify which structures require closer expert review and which can be monitored remotely.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-341,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00334,EU_PSTW_202609_00334,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Province of Gelderland,Transport,UNKNOWN,Intelligent traffic control systems (I-VRI's) - Better traffic flow,Province of Gelderland; Local Government; Transport; Netherlands,The objective was to move beyond fixed or locally reactive signalling by using digital information from road users and connected systems. The implementation uses mobile data and vehicle-to-infrastructure communication so that road users and traffic-control installations can exchange information.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Urban traffic intersections must balance the needs of private vehicles, buses, emergency services, cyclists and pedestrians while reducing congestion and improving journey reliability. In Gelderland, existing traffic lights at eight intersections in Arnhem and Velp were replaced with intelligent traffic-control systems, or iVRIs, to support better traffic flow. The objective was to move beyond fixed or locally reactive signalling by using digital information from road users and connected systems. The implementation uses mobile data and vehicle-to-infrastructure communication so that road users and traffic-control installations can exchange information. Buses and emergency services can receive priority when approaching an intersection, while other road users may receive up-to-date information about routes and speed advice. At a conceptual level, iVRIs combine connected traffic-light controllers, data exchange standards, road-user messages, priority rules and adaptive signal control. This allows the intersection to respond more dynamically to current traffic conditions and to support policy priorities such as public transport reliability or emergency response. The expected impact is smoother traffic flow, reduced waiting times, better journey predictability and more efficient use of existing road infrastructure. Dutch public portals continue to provide national visibility on iVRI deployments, while Gelderland’s own public material refers to implementation and execution documents for the Arnhem and Velp iVRIs.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-342,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00335,EU_PSTW_202609_00335,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of The Hague,Housing and community amenities n.e.c.,"Municipal waste management services struggle with contamination of waste streams when incorrect materials are deposited in underground containers, leading to higher processing costs and reduced recycling rates. The pilots have improved operational efficiency by reducing manual inspections and enabling faster responses to misuse.",Use of scan cars for underground containers - Determining type of waste,Municipality of The Hague; Local Government; Housing and community amenities n.e.c.; Netherlands,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Municipal waste management services struggle with contamination of waste streams when incorrect materials are deposited in underground containers, leading to higher processing costs and reduced recycling rates. In The Hague and other cities, scan cars were introduced to monitor underground containers and improve waste separation. Stakeholders include municipal waste services, residents and environmental authorities. The objective is to detect incorrect waste disposal early and support targeted interventions. Vehicles equipped with cameras periodically capture images of open or overfilled containers. Image analysis techniques are applied to identify visible waste types, such as bulky items or mixed waste placed in recycling containers. The system integrates with municipal waste management platforms, allowing services to prioritise clean-up operations and identify recurring problem locations. The pilots have improved operational efficiency by reducing manual inspections and enabling faster responses to misuse. Municipalities report cleaner collection points, better data on citizen behaviour and more effective communication campaigns. While still limited in scope, the approach demonstrates scalable potential for data-driven waste management and supports broader circular economy objectives.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-343,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00336,EU_PSTW_202609_00336,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Dutch Police,Police Services,"Police investigations increasingly involve large volumes of online information, making manual monitoring of digital sources time-consuming and inconsistent. The project improved consistency and speed in early-stage investigations and reduced reliance on ad hoc manual searches.",iRN_iColumbo,Dutch Police; Central-Government; Police Services; Netherlands,"The service crawled publicly accessible online sources and forums using predefined search strategies, keywords and behavioural patterns.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Police investigations increasingly involve large volumes of online information, making manual monitoring of digital sources time-consuming and inconsistent. iColumbo was developed within the Dutch police’s Internet Research & Investigation Network (iRN) to support systematic online searches for suspicious material. Stakeholders included law enforcement investigators, digital forensics units and judicial authorities. The objective was to enhance investigative capacity by automating parts of open-source intelligence collection while preserving legal oversight. The service crawled publicly accessible online sources and forums using predefined search strategies, keywords and behavioural patterns. Collected material was indexed and presented to investigators for assessment, rather than triggering automatic enforcement actions. The project improved consistency and speed in early-stage investigations and reduced reliance on ad hoc manual searches. Although the specific iColumbo project has since been concluded, its concepts informed later police tooling for digital investigations. The main impact lies in professionalisation of online investigative practices and improved methodological consistency in handling internet-based evidence.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK|F06_HIGH_VOLUME_SCALABILITY_BOTTLENECK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-344,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00337,EU_PSTW_202609_00337,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,District Court of East Brabant,Law courts,"The operational challenge is that courts and judicial staff work with large volumes of case material, and finding relevant precedents or similar disputes can be time-consuming when searches rely only on keywords or manual legal knowledge.",Artificial Intelligence Knowledge System for the judiciary searches for similar lawsuits,District Court of East Brabant; Regional Government; Law courts; Netherlands,"Its value depends on the quality of the underlying case database, the transparency of similarity criteria and careful use by trained legal professionals.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Artificial Intelligence Knowledge System for the judiciary was developed to support legal professionals in identifying comparable lawsuits more efficiently. The operational challenge is that courts and judicial staff work with large volumes of case material, and finding relevant precedents or similar disputes can be time-consuming when searches rely only on keywords or manual legal knowledge. The District Court of East Brabant tested a system that allows users to enter text and receive the ten most similar lawsuits. The implementation is based on textual similarity search, likely combining natural language processing, document representation and ranking techniques to compare an input text with a database of past cases. Conceptually, the tool transforms legal documents into searchable representations, calculates similarity between the user’s text and available case records, and returns a ranked list of relevant results. The expected impact is faster legal research, more consistent retrieval of comparable cases and better support for judicial preparation. By assisting users in navigating case material, the system can improve internal efficiency without replacing judicial reasoning. Its value depends on the quality of the underlying case database, the transparency of similarity criteria and careful use by trained legal professionals.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK|F06_HIGH_VOLUME_SCALABILITY_BOTTLENECK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-345,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00338,EU_PSTW_202609_00338,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Ministry of Interior,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.",UNKNOWN,LAA - Address quality in Personal Records Database (BRP),"Ministry of Interior; Central-Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Netherlands","LAA - Address quality in Personal Records Database (BRP) supports the Dutch Ministry of Interior in improving address quality by using data analysis to recognise patterns that may indicate incorrect addressing. The technological implementation applies analytical methods to administrative data in order to detect combinations, deviations or patterns associated with possible address-registration problems.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Public authorities depend on accurate address records to deliver services, administer rights and obligations, and prevent misuse of public systems, but incorrect registrations in the Personal Records Database can undermine these functions. LAA - Address quality in Personal Records Database (BRP) supports the Dutch Ministry of Interior in improving address quality by using data analysis to recognise patterns that may indicate incorrect addressing. The underlying problem is that inaccurate address data can affect administrative decisions, public-service delivery and fraud prevention, particularly where residence information is used to determine eligibility, correspondence, enforcement or access to benefits. The initiative aims to help bring the BRP into better order by identifying records that may require verification or correction. The technological implementation applies analytical methods to administrative data in order to detect combinations, deviations or patterns associated with possible address-registration problems. These outputs can then guide further checks by competent authorities, who assess whether a registration is indeed incorrect and what follow-up is required. The system therefore functions as a risk-identification and prioritisation tool within a broader governance process. The expected impact is improved data quality, more targeted verification work, stronger reliability of the population register and better support for fraud prevention. Given the sensitivity of population data, the value of the system depends on clear legal safeguards, proportionality, transparency and appropriate human oversight.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-346,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00339,EU_PSTW_202609_00339,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Breda,Housing development.,"Housing policy can affect liveability through tenure mix, housing type, density, neighbourhood composition, public-space quality and access to services, but the relationship between physical interventions and social outcomes is difficult to assess with simple indicators.",Using smart algorithms to map whether building or demolishing certain homes improves liveability,Municipality of Breda; Local Government; Housing development.; Netherlands,"Breda’s use case concerns the application of machine-learning methods to investigate whether building or demolishing certain types of homes could improve neighbourhood liveability. The implementation concept uses historical and spatial data to detect patterns between housing stock, neighbourhood characteristics and liveability outcomes.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Housing policy can affect liveability through tenure mix, housing type, density, neighbourhood composition, public-space quality and access to services, but the relationship between physical interventions and social outcomes is difficult to assess with simple indicators. Breda’s use case concerns the application of machine-learning methods to investigate whether building or demolishing certain types of homes could improve neighbourhood liveability. The operational problem is that urban-planning decisions often involve long-term effects and many interacting variables, making it hard for decision-makers to isolate which housing interventions are likely to improve local conditions. The implementation concept uses historical and spatial data to detect patterns between housing stock, neighbourhood characteristics and liveability outcomes. At a conceptual level, the model would compare combinations of housing and contextual indicators, identify correlations with liveability measures and support planners in evaluating possible redevelopment scenarios. The expected impact is more evidence-based housing policy, better targeting of regeneration measures and stronger analytical support for decisions on demolition, new construction or housing mix. The system should be understood as a planning-support tool rather than a substitute for political, social and participatory decision-making. Its value depends on combining model outputs with local knowledge, resident engagement and policy judgement.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-347,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00340,EU_PSTW_202609_00340,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Milvum B.V.,Public order and safety n.e.c.,"Municipalities and safety-domain partners can lose valuable intelligence on subversive crime when suspicions are reported through fragmented channels or fail to reach the right professionals quickly. The operational challenge is to improve communication between municipalities and chain partners so that local safety information is not lost, delayed or handled inconsistently.",Tackling undermining using AI,Milvum B.V.; Private sector; Public order and safety n.e.c.; Netherlands,"Tackling undermining using AI builds on Milvum’s “Report a Suspicion” platform, which is already used by more than 300 professionals to share signals of undermining, such as illegal prostitution or drug production, directly with the relevant municipal actors.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Municipalities and safety-domain partners can lose valuable intelligence on subversive crime when suspicions are reported through fragmented channels or fail to reach the right professionals quickly. Tackling undermining using AI builds on Milvum’s “Report a Suspicion” platform, which is already used by more than 300 professionals to share signals of undermining, such as illegal prostitution or drug production, directly with the relevant municipal actors. The operational challenge is to improve communication between municipalities and chain partners so that local safety information is not lost, delayed or handled inconsistently. The existing platform combines a mobile app and web portal, enabling professionals to submit and route suspicions in a structured way. The planned or added AI functionality strengthens this model by applying big-data analysis to identify, categorise and prioritise signals of subversive crime more effectively. At a conceptual level, the system can analyse reports and related data to detect patterns, cluster similar cases, highlight urgent risks and support prioritisation for follow-up. The impact is improved information flow, faster routing of relevant signals, better coordination between municipal and safety partners, and more systematic handling of local crime intelligence. For public authorities, the platform offers operational value by turning dispersed observations into structured knowledge that can support earlier intervention and more targeted action against undermining.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-348,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00341,EU_PSTW_202609_00341,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Eindhoven,"General economic, commercial and labour affair",UNKNOWN,Detecting 'unknown' companies with environmental risk,"Municipality of Eindhoven; Local Government; General economic, commercial and labour affair; Netherlands",Eindhoven’s use case focused on mapping “unknown” companies that could pose a potential environmental risk by using data more intelligently. The implementation uses data matching and analytical screening to identify companies that may fall outside established inspection lists but show characteristics associated with environmental risk.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Environmental supervision depends on knowing which businesses operate in a municipality, what activities they carry out and whether those activities may create inspection or permitting risks. Eindhoven’s use case focused on mapping “unknown” companies that could pose a potential environmental risk by using data more intelligently. The operational problem is that companies may be invisible to inspectors because records are incomplete, activities change, addresses are inconsistent or information is distributed across multiple administrative sources. This creates the risk that environmentally relevant businesses are not prioritised for supervision or included in the appropriate regulatory process. The implementation uses data matching and analytical screening to identify companies that may fall outside established inspection lists but show characteristics associated with environmental risk. At a conceptual level, the system combines business-register information, location data, sector classifications, permit or enforcement records and risk indicators to generate candidate companies for further human review. The expected impact is better coverage of environmental supervision, earlier detection of potentially risky activities, more efficient targeting of inspections and improved data quality in municipal environmental registers. The system should be understood as a prioritisation and discovery tool: it does not determine guilt or non-compliance, but helps inspectors find entities that may require attention. Public case material records the project as an Eindhoven initiative to make otherwise hard-to-find companies visible through smarter data use.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-349,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00342,EU_PSTW_202609_00342,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Totta Datalab, municipality of Nissewaard, municipality of Brielle",Social protection n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,Detecting welfare fraud - Prediction of the likelihood of unlawful behavior on the House Market,"Totta Datalab, municipality of Nissewaard, municipality of Brielle; Local Government; Social protection n.e.c.; Netherlands",UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Municipalities administering welfare benefits face pressure to prevent fraud while ensuring fair and lawful treatment of beneficiaries. The Municipality of Nissewaard introduced a predictive model to identify cases with a higher likelihood of unlawful behaviour in the housing market, such as undeclared cohabitation or subletting. Stakeholders included social services, case officers and welfare recipients. The objective was to prioritise investigations and reduce random or unnecessary checks. The model analysed historical administrative data, identifying patterns associated with confirmed fraud cases and assigning risk scores to current clients. These scores informed decisions on whether further investigation was warranted, always requiring human review. While the system aimed to improve efficiency, concerns emerged around transparency, bias and legal safeguards. Following national scrutiny of similar systems and evolving legal standards, Nissewaard discontinued the model in 2021. The case significantly influenced Dutch policy discussions on automated risk profiling, reinforcing the need for strict governance, explainability and proportionality in data-driven public services.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,NOT_IN_USE,미사용,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-350,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00343,EU_PSTW_202609_00343,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Municipality of Deventer, Municipality of Goirle",Social protection n.e.c.,"The administrative problem was the need to target limited enforcement capacity towards cases considered more likely to involve irregularities, while managing public funds and ensuring compliance with welfare rules.",Detecting welfare fraud in the municipality of Deventer and Goirle (SyRI system),"Municipality of Deventer, Municipality of Goirle; Local Government; Social protection n.e.c.; Netherlands","At a conceptual level, such a system links administrative datasets, applies risk indicators or algorithmic scoring rules, and routes selected cases to additional human investigation.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Deventer and Goirle welfare-fraud detection pilot used risk profiling to identify welfare recipients whose files might require additional investigation. The administrative problem was the need to target limited enforcement capacity towards cases considered more likely to involve irregularities, while managing public funds and ensuring compliance with welfare rules. The stakeholders included the municipalities of Deventer and Goirle, welfare recipients, municipal social-service staff, enforcement teams and citizens affected by decisions based on risk scores. The implementation generated risk profiles for welfare recipients, with the resulting score used to decide whether a dossier should be examined more closely. At a conceptual level, such a system links administrative datasets, applies risk indicators or algorithmic scoring rules, and routes selected cases to additional human investigation. The intended impact was more efficient fraud detection, better prioritisation of municipal control resources and earlier identification of possible unlawful behaviour. However, because SyRI-style systems involve sensitive personal data and profiling of welfare recipients, they raise significant concerns around transparency, discrimination, proportionality and legal accountability. These concerns are particularly relevant given the broader Dutch SyRI judgment, where the system’s legal basis was found incompatible with privacy protections.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-351,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00344,EU_PSTW_202609_00344,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Leudal,Public order and safety n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,Detection of crime and subversion in the Municipality of Leudal (SyRI system),Municipality of Leudal; Local Government; Public order and safety n.e.c.; Netherlands,The Leudal use case concerns the reported use of algorithmic profiling to identify locations where paperwork or administrative signals may indicate possible criminal or irregular activity. The use case should be treated cautiously because “subversion” is a broad policy category and algorithmic indicators may produce false positives or reinforce biased inspection patterns.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Local authorities may seek to detect organised or “subversive” crime where administrative records suggest unusual patterns around buildings, businesses or permits. The Leudal use case concerns the reported use of algorithmic profiling to identify locations where paperwork or administrative signals may indicate possible criminal or irregular activity. The operational problem is that suspicious activity may not be visible through a single inspection file, but may emerge from combinations of property records, business information, permits, registrations, enforcement history or other municipal data. At a conceptual level, the system would combine multiple administrative indicators and generate risk signals for further investigation by officials. The intended impact is to help local government and enforcement partners prioritise inspections, detect hidden misuse of premises and support more coordinated action against organised crime. However, this type of system is high-risk because it can involve profiling of people, addresses or businesses without clear visibility for those affected. It therefore requires strict legal basis, transparency, proportionality, data-quality controls and human review. The use case should be treated cautiously because “subversion” is a broad policy category and algorithmic indicators may produce false positives or reinforce biased inspection patterns.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-352,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00345,EU_PSTW_202609_00345,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Dronten,Public order and safety n.e.c.,"The operational challenge was that forms of organised or undermining crime can be difficult for local authorities to detect because relevant signals may be dispersed across administrative records, enforcement data and local intelligence.",Detection of crime and subversion in the municipality of Dronten (SyRI system),Municipality of Dronten; Local Government; Public order and safety n.e.c.; Netherlands,"The implementation is described as an experiment using algorithms to detect subversive crime. The expected impact would be improved situational awareness for local enforcement actors, more targeted investigation and better use of limited municipal capacity.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Dronten’s pilot examined whether algorithms could help identify patterns associated with subversive crime at municipal level. The operational challenge was that forms of organised or undermining crime can be difficult for local authorities to detect because relevant signals may be dispersed across administrative records, enforcement data and local intelligence. The municipality therefore explored how algorithmic analysis could support earlier detection and prioritisation of cases requiring further attention. The implementation is described as an experiment using algorithms to detect subversive crime. Conceptually, the system may combine municipal data sources, risk indicators, pattern detection and case-selection workflows to flag locations, activities or dossiers that appear to warrant additional scrutiny. The expected impact would be improved situational awareness for local enforcement actors, more targeted investigation and better use of limited municipal capacity. At the same time, the use of predictive or profiling tools in public-order contexts requires strong safeguards. Risks include opaque criteria, inaccurate suspicion, disproportionate targeting of specific groups or areas, and weak routes for contesting decisions. The pilot therefore needs careful legal, ethical and operational governance if algorithmic signals are used to trigger further enforcement action.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-353,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00346,EU_PSTW_202609_00346,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Maastricht,Public order and safety n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,Detection of crime and subversion in the municipality of Maastricht (SyRI system),Municipality of Maastricht; Local Government; Public order and safety n.e.c.; Netherlands,"Municipal enforcement teams may use data analysis to identify properties or situations where administrative inconsistencies suggest possible criminal use of buildings, fraudulent activity or other forms of subversion. Maastricht was reported as one of the Dutch municipalities using algorithmic systems in this field.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Municipal enforcement teams may use data analysis to identify properties or situations where administrative inconsistencies suggest possible criminal use of buildings, fraudulent activity or other forms of subversion. Maastricht was reported as one of the Dutch municipalities using algorithmic systems in this field. The operational problem is that subversive crime may be hidden behind apparently ordinary businesses, rental arrangements or property uses, and manual inspection may not be able to cover all possible risk locations. At a conceptual level, the system would process administrative records and other available indicators to flag addresses or cases where the combination of data points appears unusual or suspicious. The expected impact is more targeted inspection, earlier detection of hidden criminal structures and better coordination between municipal services and enforcement partners. The system’s role should be framed as risk signalling for human review, not as automatic proof of wrongdoing. This distinction is important because the consequences of being flagged can be significant, including inspections, enforcement attention or reputational harm. The use case therefore raises strong requirements around explainability, proportionality, auditability and safeguards against discriminatory targeting of neighbourhoods, businesses or residents.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-354,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00347,EU_PSTW_202609_00347,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Nederweert,Public order and safety n.e.c.,"Smaller municipalities can face complex enforcement challenges when criminal or improper activity is hidden behind legal-looking administrative structures, property records or business registrations. A risk-based system can help identify buildings or cases where paperwork “smacks of suspicious cases”, allowing limited inspection capacity to be focused where further review appears justified.",Detection of crime and subversion in the municipality of Nederweert (SyRI system),Municipality of Nederweert; Local Government; Public order and safety n.e.c.; Netherlands,Nederweert was reported as using algorithmic methods to support detection of subversive crime.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Smaller municipalities can face complex enforcement challenges when criminal or improper activity is hidden behind legal-looking administrative structures, property records or business registrations. Nederweert was reported as using algorithmic methods to support detection of subversive crime. The operational problem is that municipal datasets may contain weak signals of irregularity, but individual records may not be meaningful unless combined with other information. A risk-based system can help identify buildings or cases where paperwork “smacks of suspicious cases”, allowing limited inspection capacity to be focused where further review appears justified. At a conceptual level, such a system would aggregate administrative indicators, score or classify cases and provide signals to officials for follow-up. The expected impact is more efficient local enforcement, earlier identification of hidden misuse of property and better support for multi-agency responses to organised crime. However, the system can also create legal and ethical risks if residents, owners or businesses are profiled through opaque combinations of data. The case therefore needs to be presented as an enforcement-support system requiring human verification, rather than an automated determination of criminal conduct. Transparency and safeguards are particularly important because flagged persons may not know that algorithmic profiling influenced official attention.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-355,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00348,EU_PSTW_202609_00348,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Municipality of Amsterdam (Parking, Team Analysis & Advice), the Hague, Rotterdam and Utrecht",Police Services,"The operational problem is that conventional on-foot inspection can be labour-intensive and difficult to scale across large numbers of parking spaces, particularly in cities with high vehicle turnover and complex parking regimes.",Parking control in various Dutch municipalities - Parking control by means of scan cars,"Municipality of Amsterdam (Parking, Team Analysis & Advice), the Hague, Rotterdam and Utrecht; Local Government; Police Services; Netherlands","Large municipalities need to enforce parking rules across dense urban areas efficiently, while limiting manual checks and maintaining consistent treatment of vehicles using paid or restricted parking spaces. The scan cars capture licence plates and use an image-recognition algorithm to identify registration numbers.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Large municipalities need to enforce parking rules across dense urban areas efficiently, while limiting manual checks and maintaining consistent treatment of vehicles using paid or restricted parking spaces. Parking control in various Dutch municipalities - Parking control by means of scan cars supports parking enforcement in Amsterdam, The Hague, Rotterdam and Utrecht through vehicles equipped with cameras that inspect parked cars while driving through city streets. The operational problem is that conventional on-foot inspection can be labour-intensive and difficult to scale across large numbers of parking spaces, particularly in cities with high vehicle turnover and complex parking regimes. The scan cars capture licence plates and use an image-recognition algorithm to identify registration numbers. These readings can then be checked against parking-payment or permit databases to determine whether a vehicle appears to be compliant. Cases that require further verification can be reviewed before enforcement action is taken, depending on local procedures. The technology therefore combines mobile sensing, automated number-plate recognition and integration with municipal parking-administration systems. Its impact is greater inspection coverage, faster detection of possible parking violations, more efficient deployment of enforcement staff and improved consistency in parking control. For municipalities, the approach can also generate better operational data on parking behaviour and support more responsive management of urban parking policy.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-356,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00349,EU_PSTW_202609_00349,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Intemo, Municipality of Nijmegen",Economic affairs n.e.c,"The objective is to generate continuous, objective data on footfall without relying on manual counts or surveys, enabling evidence-based decisions on zoning, infrastructure investments and city marketing.",Passer-by count,"Intemo, Municipality of Nijmegen; Local Government; Economic affairs n.e.c; Netherlands","The municipality of Nijmegen introduced passer-by counting to better understand how people move through the city centre, how busy specific areas are at different times, and how these patterns relate to retail performance and public space use. The system uses fixed sensors placed in public space to detect and count passers-by while avoiding direct identification of individuals.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"City centres increasingly require reliable insights into pedestrian flows to support economic development, urban planning and public safety. The municipality of Nijmegen introduced passer-by counting to better understand how people move through the city centre, how busy specific areas are at different times, and how these patterns relate to retail performance and public space use. Stakeholders include municipal planners, economic development teams, local businesses and event organisers. The objective is to generate continuous, objective data on footfall without relying on manual counts or surveys, enabling evidence-based decisions on zoning, infrastructure investments and city marketing. The system uses fixed sensors placed in public space to detect and count passers-by while avoiding direct identification of individuals. The sensors process visual or signal data locally to extract movement counts and directional flows, which are then aggregated and transmitted to municipal dashboards. Data is anonymised at source and integrated with other urban indicators, such as time of day, weather and events. The solution has improved the municipality’s ability to monitor urban vitality in near real time and to evaluate the impact of interventions such as street redesigns or events.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-357,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00350,EU_PSTW_202609_00350,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of The Hague,Unemployment.,"The operational problem was not only benefit administration, but also timely activation: municipalities need to recognise which people may benefit from rapid support before unemployment becomes prolonged or more difficult to address.",Analysing data to help people on welfare get back to work for Social Assistance in The Hague,Municipality of The Hague; Local Government; Unemployment.; Netherlands,"The value of the tool depends on using the analysis to offer assistance rather than punitive control, and on ensuring that data-driven categorisation remains transparent, fair and subject to professional judgement.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Hague’s social-assistance pilot used data analysis to identify welfare recipients who could be supported towards employment at an earlier stage. The operational problem was not only benefit administration, but also timely activation: municipalities need to recognise which people may benefit from rapid support before unemployment becomes prolonged or more difficult to address. The stakeholders included the Municipality of The Hague, social-assistance recipients, reintegration advisers, municipal employment services and residents whose welfare trajectory could be affected by early intervention. The implementation analyses administrative data to find people at an early stage so that they can be guided towards work more quickly. Conceptually, the system may combine historical welfare data, employment indicators, case characteristics and predictive or segmentation methods to identify groups requiring specific support. The expected impact is improved targeting of reintegration services, faster outreach to residents, more efficient allocation of municipal staff and potentially shorter welfare dependency where appropriate support is available. The value of the tool depends on using the analysis to offer assistance rather than punitive control, and on ensuring that data-driven categorisation remains transparent, fair and subject to professional judgement.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-358,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00351,EU_PSTW_202609_00351,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"National Police, Ziuz",Police Services,"AviaTor was developed to support law-enforcement authorities in processing referrals related to child sexual abuse material, a sensitive and resource-intensive operational area where delays can affect the identification of perpetrators and the protection of victims.",AviaTor - Pilot detection child abuse,"National Police, Ziuz; Central-Government; Police Services; Netherlands","Developed from research into a working prototype within 12 months, the tool uses expert insight from police interviews to structure workflows, prioritise reports and support the analysis of visual and contextual information.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"AviaTor was developed to support law-enforcement authorities in processing referrals related to child sexual abuse material, a sensitive and resource-intensive operational area where delays can affect the identification of perpetrators and the protection of victims. Police services receive large volumes of reports, including referrals from the National Center for Missing and Exploited Children, and need to assess them quickly, prioritise the most urgent cases and enrich available information for investigative follow-up. The project involved Dutch and Belgian police expertise, ZiuZ as solution developer, and funding from the European Union’s Internal Security Fund – Police. Its objective was to reduce the time and effort required to process reports while improving completeness and prioritisation. The emerging technology implementation combines augmented visual intelligence with targeted online research capabilities. Developed from research into a working prototype within 12 months, the tool uses expert insight from police interviews to structure workflows, prioritise reports and support the analysis of visual and contextual information. It also helps combine report data with relevant online sources at an investigative-support level. The impact is faster, more efficient and more complete processing of referrals, allowing law-enforcement officers to focus their attention on high-priority cases, victim identification and perpetrator detection. The case provides clear operational value by improving triage, reducing repetitive workload and supporting more targeted investigative action.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-359,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00352,EU_PSTW_202609_00352,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Municipality of Rotterdam, TNO and BZK",Social protection n.e.c.,"The policy challenge is that youth outcomes are shaped by multiple interacting variables across education, family context, neighbourhood conditions, health, welfare and social participation.",Pilot Youth Policy Rotterdam - Influencing factors socio-emotional development of young people,"Municipality of Rotterdam, TNO and BZK; Local Government; Social protection n.e.c.; Netherlands",The implementation links datasets and applies a combination of statistical analysis and machine learning to examine which factors are associated with socio-emotional development.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Rotterdam’s youth-policy pilot explored which factors influence the socio-emotional development of young people by combining linked datasets with statistical and machine-learning methods. The policy challenge is that youth outcomes are shaped by multiple interacting variables across education, family context, neighbourhood conditions, health, welfare and social participation. Local authorities need stronger evidence on these relationships to design interventions that are preventive, targeted and proportionate. The stakeholders included the Municipality of Rotterdam, TNO, the Dutch Ministry of the Interior and Kingdom Relations, young people, families, youth-service professionals and policymakers. The implementation links datasets and applies a combination of statistical analysis and machine learning to examine which factors are associated with socio-emotional development. Conceptually, the pilot functions as a data-driven policy lab: it integrates relevant administrative or research data, tests associations, identifies patterns and supports policy learning rather than producing a direct automated decision on individual young people. The expected impact is improved understanding of risk and protective factors, better targeting of youth policies and stronger evidence for preventive interventions. Its usefulness depends on data quality, careful interpretation, privacy protection and avoiding deterministic conclusions about individual young people based on statistical correlations.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-360,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00353,EU_PSTW_202609_00353,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"LexIQ, Public Prosecutor's Office",Law courts,"Public prosecutors and investigators face growing volumes of legal material and case law, making it increasingly difficult to identify relevant jurisprudence efficiently and consistently. To address this challenge, the Dutch Public Prosecution Service collaborated with the legal technology company LexIQ to develop the Jurisprudence Robot, a tool designed to support daily prosecutorial work by improving access to relevant judicial decisions.",Project Jurisprudence Robot - AI tool to support prosecutors and investigators,"LexIQ, Public Prosecutor's Office; Private sector; Law courts; Netherlands",The solution processes large collections of court rulings and legal texts and matches them to case-specific queries using automated text analysis techniques.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Public prosecutors and investigators face growing volumes of legal material and case law, making it increasingly difficult to identify relevant jurisprudence efficiently and consistently. To address this challenge, the Dutch Public Prosecution Service collaborated with the legal technology company LexIQ to develop the Jurisprudence Robot, a tool designed to support daily prosecutorial work by improving access to relevant judicial decisions. The main stakeholders include prosecutors, investigators, and judicial support staff, with the objective of reducing time spent on legal research while improving consistency in legal reasoning. The solution processes large collections of court rulings and legal texts and matches them to case-specific queries using automated text analysis techniques. The system structures legal documents, extracts relevant concepts, and presents ranked jurisprudence results to users through an interface integrated into existing prosecutorial workflows. Human decision-making remains central, with the tool acting strictly as an advisory aid rather than an automated decision-maker. According to publicly available judicial documentation, the Jurisprudence Robot has been tested in controlled environments and limited operational settings, with no evidence of nationwide deployment or discontinuation. Reported benefits include faster legal research, improved consistency in case preparation, and reduced administrative burden, while maintaining judicial accountability and professional autonomy.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,F06_HIGH_VOLUME_SCALABILITY_BOTTLENECK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-361,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00354,EU_PSTW_202609_00354,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Vialis B.V.,Transport,"The operational challenge is that incidents, congestion and disruptions can develop quickly in urban road networks, and traffic managers need early signals to intervene before delays escalate.",VIALIS Real-time incident detection,Vialis B.V.; Private sector; Transport; Netherlands,"The stakeholders include Vialis B.V., municipal or transport authorities using the system, road users, traffic-management centres and public bodies concerned with road safety and mobility performance. The implementation uses artificial intelligence to process urban traffic data and detect patterns that may indicate emerging incidents or traffic disruptions.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"VIALIS Real-time Incident Detection focuses on preventing traffic problems by analysing urban traffic data in real time. The operational challenge is that incidents, congestion and disruptions can develop quickly in urban road networks, and traffic managers need early signals to intervene before delays escalate. The stakeholders include Vialis B.V., municipal or transport authorities using the system, road users, traffic-management centres and public bodies concerned with road safety and mobility performance. The implementation uses artificial intelligence to process urban traffic data and detect patterns that may indicate emerging incidents or traffic disruptions. Conceptually, the system can combine sensor data, traffic-flow information, anomaly detection, predictive analytics and alerting functions to support operational decisions. The expected impact is earlier detection of incidents, faster traffic-management responses, reduced congestion and improved reliability for road users. By shifting from reactive monitoring to proactive analysis, the tool can help cities deploy interventions more efficiently, such as adjusting traffic signals, informing road users or dispatching response teams. Its added value depends on integration with existing traffic-control systems, the quality of real-time data streams and the accuracy of alerts produced by the model.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-362,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00355,EU_PSTW_202609_00355,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Municipality of Rotterdam, TUDelft",UNKNOWN,The operational challenge is that conventional weather information may be too coarse to support timely urban water-management decisions at neighbourhood or street level.,Rain radar Rijnmond - Prediction of rain to prevent flooding and damage,"Municipality of Rotterdam, TUDelft; Local Government; Netherlands",UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Rijnmond’s rain radar was developed to provide highly localised measurement and prediction of precipitation in the Rotterdam region, where intense rainfall can quickly create flooding risks and damage to public space, buildings and infrastructure. The operational challenge is that conventional weather information may be too coarse to support timely urban water-management decisions at neighbourhood or street level. Municipal services, emergency planners, water managers, residents, businesses and researchers therefore require more granular rainfall intelligence to anticipate localised disruption. The implementation combines radar-based precipitation measurement with predictive analytics to estimate rainfall patterns at a fine spatial scale. Conceptually, the system collects local meteorological data, processes it into short-term precipitation forecasts and supports decisions on drainage management, preparedness and response. Its value lies in moving from general weather awareness to actionable local prediction. The expected impact is improved prevention of flooding, faster operational response, reduced damage from heavy rainfall and stronger climate-resilience planning. By helping authorities understand where and when rain is likely to become problematic, the tool supports more targeted deployment of resources and better protection of vulnerable urban areas.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-363,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00356,EU_PSTW_202609_00356,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Province of Friesland, Ilionix",Environmental protection n.e.c.,"The environmental-management problem is that field-based monitoring of vegetation can be slow, costly and labour-intensive, while ecological conditions may change more rapidly than traditional inspection cycles can capture. The expected impact is more continuous insight into nature reserves, reduced dependence on manual fieldwork and improved evidence for conservation planning.",Remote sensing for monitoring nature reserves on the Frisian Wadden Islands,"Province of Friesland, Ilionix; Local Government; Environmental protection n.e.c.; Netherlands",The Frisian Wadden Islands remote-sensing pilot tested whether vegetation in protected nature reserves could be monitored more frequently and efficiently using satellite data. The implementation uses satellite imagery combined with machine-learning techniques to classify and monitor vegetation patterns.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Frisian Wadden Islands remote-sensing pilot tested whether vegetation in protected nature reserves could be monitored more frequently and efficiently using satellite data. The environmental-management problem is that field-based monitoring of vegetation can be slow, costly and labour-intensive, while ecological conditions may change more rapidly than traditional inspection cycles can capture. The Province of Friesland and Ilionix explored whether annual vegetation monitoring could be supported through data-driven models, giving nature managers a more regular view of habitat conditions. The implementation uses satellite imagery combined with machine-learning techniques to classify and monitor vegetation patterns. Conceptually, the models process remote-sensing data, identify vegetation characteristics and optimise classifications so that ecological changes can be assessed over time. This allows authorities to compare areas, detect developments in habitat composition and prioritise follow-up inspection or management action. The expected impact is more continuous insight into nature reserves, reduced dependence on manual fieldwork and improved evidence for conservation planning. By enabling annual monitoring, the pilot can support more responsive ecological management and help public authorities safeguard sensitive island habitats with more timely and consistent data.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-364,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00357,EU_PSTW_202609_00357,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Ministry of Infrastructure and Water Management,Transport,UNKNOWN,Rijkswaterstaat vital assets - Predictive maintenance on bridges,Ministry of Infrastructure and Water Management; Central-Government; Transport; Netherlands,"The Dutch Ministry of Infrastructure and Water Management needs to maintain bridges and hydraulic civil-engineering works in a way that prevents failures, reduces unnecessary interventions and uses maintenance budgets more efficiently. Rijkswaterstaat vital assets - Predictive maintenance on bridges supports this objective by using data to estimate when maintenance should be carried out on critical infrastructure assets.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Dutch Ministry of Infrastructure and Water Management needs to maintain bridges and hydraulic civil-engineering works in a way that prevents failures, reduces unnecessary interventions and uses maintenance budgets more efficiently. Rijkswaterstaat vital assets - Predictive maintenance on bridges supports this objective by using data to estimate when maintenance should be carried out on critical infrastructure assets. The operational challenge is that bridges and related water-management structures are subject to wear, environmental pressure and intensive use, while traditional maintenance planning can be either reactive or based on fixed schedules that do not always reflect the actual condition of the asset. The initiative, funded by the Dutch Ministry of Infrastructure and developed with Vitale Assets and the Water cluster of the CAMINO Smart Industry Field Lab, explores how predictive maintenance can be applied to hydraulic civil-engineering works. The technological implementation combines sensors, Internet of Things infrastructure, Big Data techniques and predictive modelling to collect and analyse information on asset condition, use patterns and potential deterioration. These data are used to estimate maintenance needs more accurately and to support planning before faults become critical. The expected impact is more efficient maintenance scheduling, fewer unnecessary inspections or interventions, improved reliability of vital infrastructure and reduced risk of disruption for road and waterway users. The approach also strengthens long-term asset management by helping authorities move from routine maintenance cycles towards condition-based decision-making.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-365,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00358,EU_PSTW_202609_00358,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Rotterdam,Public order and safety n.e.c.,"Manual enforcement alone could be difficult to scale across a large city, especially when conditions changed quickly and authorities needed timely information about where crowding or non-compliance might be occurring.",Scan car for checking group formation and complying with corona measures,Municipality of Rotterdam; Local Government; Public order and safety n.e.c.; Netherlands,"The use case therefore focused on supporting oversight of coronavirus measures in public areas, with the objective of improving situational awareness and helping the municipality respond more effectively to risky gatherings. The stakeholders include the municipality, enforcement and public-safety teams and people using public space, with the wider aim of reducing infection risk during the emergency period.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"During the COVID-19 pandemic, the municipality of Rotterdam needed ways to monitor compliance with public-health measures in urban space, particularly rules concerning group formation and distancing. Manual enforcement alone could be difficult to scale across a large city, especially when conditions changed quickly and authorities needed timely information about where crowding or non-compliance might be occurring. The use case therefore focused on supporting oversight of coronavirus measures in public areas, with the objective of improving situational awareness and helping the municipality respond more effectively to risky gatherings. The stakeholders include the municipality, enforcement and public-safety teams and people using public space, with the wider aim of reducing infection risk during the emergency period. The solution was implemented through a scan car used to observe public-space conditions and check for group formation linked to corona rules. At a conceptual level, the system combined mobile sensing and automated analysis to identify patterns of crowding or gathering while moving through the city, creating a more mobile form of monitoring than fixed observation points alone. This allowed the municipality to gather information on compliance across different locations and support operational responses where required. The expected impact was improved visibility of non-compliant situations, more efficient deployment of enforcement attention and stronger support for pandemic management. More broadly, the use case created value as an example of crisis-time urban monitoring, showing how mobile automated observation could support local public-health enforcement in exceptional circumstances.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,NOT_IN_USE,미사용,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-366,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00359,EU_PSTW_202609_00359,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Amsterdam,Waste management.,"The core operational problem is that urban maintenance and enforcement services depend on timely, structured and actionable information from the public, while fragmented reporting channels can make it harder to prioritise, route and resolve issues efficiently.",Sia - Online notification system of the municipality of Amsterdam,Municipality of Amsterdam; Local Government; Waste management.; Netherlands,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Municipality of Amsterdam must respond to a high volume of reports about public-space issues, ranging from street cleaning and repairs to unsafe traffic situations, nuisance and problems in parks or around restaurants. Sia provides a digital channel through which residents and other users can submit notifications when something in the public space needs attention. The core operational problem is that urban maintenance and enforcement services depend on timely, structured and actionable information from the public, while fragmented reporting channels can make it harder to prioritise, route and resolve issues efficiently. SIA enables reports to be made for several types of situations, including objects or areas that need to be repaired or cleaned, dangerous traffic conditions and nuisance caused by people or establishments. The technology implementation consists of an online reporting platform that captures user-submitted information and supports its classification and routing within municipal workflows. Where algorithmic support is used, incoming reports can be categorised, prioritised or assigned to the appropriate service teams based on the content and type of notification. The main impact is a more accessible reporting route for citizens, better structuring of municipal case handling, faster allocation of tasks to relevant departments and improved oversight of recurring public-space problems. For the municipality, SIA contributes to more responsive local service delivery and a stronger evidence base for managing urban maintenance and liveability.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-367,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00360,EU_PSTW_202609_00360,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Rijkswaterstaat, Innovation Centre and ViNotion",Transport,UNKNOWN,Smart Cameras - Analyzing road situations,"Rijkswaterstaat, Innovation Centre and ViNotion; Central-Government; Transport; Netherlands","The implementation uses camera images and computer-vision techniques to detect relevant traffic conditions, such as the presence of vehicles, congestion, incidents or lane availability. By enhancing the use of visual data, the solution strengthens real-time road management and supports a more efficient transport network.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Rijkswaterstaat needs to manage traffic flow on busy roads safely and efficiently, particularly when decisions such as opening or closing rush-hour lanes must be taken quickly and with reliable situational awareness. Smart Cameras support road operators by enabling cameras to interpret traffic conditions and assist with operational decisions on the road network. The problem is that conventional camera monitoring depends heavily on human observation, which can be demanding when many road sections must be supervised simultaneously and when traffic conditions change rapidly. The initiative, involving Rijkswaterstaat, the Innovation Centre and ViNotion, focuses on smart cameras that can “read” the road situation and thereby make the management of rush-hour lanes faster, easier and safer. The implementation uses camera images and computer-vision techniques to detect relevant traffic conditions, such as the presence of vehicles, congestion, incidents or lane availability. These outputs can be integrated into traffic-management processes to support decisions on whether a rush-hour lane can be opened or should be closed. The technology therefore adds an analytical layer to existing camera infrastructure, helping operators interpret visual information more consistently. The expected impact includes faster operational responses, improved safety when changing lane status, reduced workload for traffic-control staff and smoother traffic flow. By enhancing the use of visual data, the solution strengthens real-time road management and supports a more efficient transport network.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-368,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00361,EU_PSTW_202609_00361,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Public Prosecutor's Office and Rijkswaterstaat,Police Services,"The operational problem is that a red cross indicates that a lane must not be used, often because of incidents, roadworks or traffic-management measures, yet manual enforcement is difficult and dangerous on high-speed roads.",Smart enforcement cameras - Enforcement of drivers driving through red road crosses,Public Prosecutor's Office and Rijkswaterstaat; Central-Government; Police Services; Netherlands,The pilot uses smart cameras positioned along the motorway to identify vehicles that continue driving through lanes marked with a red cross.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Road authorities and enforcement bodies need to prevent drivers from ignoring red crosses above closed lanes, because such violations create serious safety risks for road workers, emergency responders and other road users. Smart enforcement cameras were tested by the Public Prosecutor’s Office and Rijkswaterstaat along the A28 motorway near Amersfoort to detect and reduce these violations. The operational problem is that a red cross indicates that a lane must not be used, often because of incidents, roadworks or traffic-management measures, yet manual enforcement is difficult and dangerous on high-speed roads. The pilot uses smart cameras positioned along the motorway to identify vehicles that continue driving through lanes marked with a red cross. Technically, the system combines camera-based monitoring with automated image analysis to capture relevant evidence, including vehicle passage in relation to the active lane-control signal. The data can then support enforcement processes, subject to verification and applicable legal safeguards. The purpose is not only to sanction non-compliance, but also to increase deterrence and promote safer behaviour around closed lanes. The expected impact is fewer red-cross violations, improved protection for road personnel and road users, better enforcement coverage on motorways and stronger compliance with traffic-control measures. The pilot also demonstrates how digital enforcement tools can complement existing road-safety policies where direct human monitoring is impractical.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-369,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00362,EU_PSTW_202609_00362,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,National Police Lab Artificial Intelligence,Police Services,"The operational challenge is that police capacity can be absorbed by reports that require review but do not meet the legal threshold for criminal investigation, while citizens still need clarity on what has happened and what they can do next.",Smart decision aid - Determining if a case is a scam or not,National Police Lab Artificial Intelligence; Central-Government; Police Services; Netherlands,"The system uses information entered by the declarant and applies decision-support logic, potentially combining rule-based criteria and data-driven indicators derived from previous reports, to assess whether the case fits known fraud patterns. It also supports more efficient use of investigative capacity for reports that are more likely to require police action.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The National Internet Reporting Centre receives around 42,000 reports of buying and selling fraud every year, yet in approximately 10,000 cases a police officer later determines that the reported situation does not constitute a criminal offence. Smart decision aid helps address this mismatch by supporting the initial assessment of online fraud reports and providing direct feedback to the person submitting the report. The operational challenge is that police capacity can be absorbed by reports that require review but do not meet the legal threshold for criminal investigation, while citizens still need clarity on what has happened and what they can do next. Developed within the National Police Lab Artificial Intelligence, the decision aid assists in determining whether a reported buying or selling incident is likely to be a scam. The system uses information entered by the declarant and applies decision-support logic, potentially combining rule-based criteria and data-driven indicators derived from previous reports, to assess whether the case fits known fraud patterns. When the situation does not appear to be a criminal offence, the tool can provide an alternative action perspective to the user. The impact is faster clarification for citizens, reduced unnecessary workload for police officers, better triage of internet-fraud reports and more consistent initial handling of cases. It also supports more efficient use of investigative capacity for reports that are more likely to require police action.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-370,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00363,EU_PSTW_202609_00363,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Rotterdam,Police Services,UNKNOWN,Smart lampposts for combating burglaries - Detection of suspicious movements and audio,Municipality of Rotterdam; Local Government; Police Services; Netherlands,"Conceptually, the lampposts function as distributed detection points in the neighbourhood, using environmental signals to support situational awareness. The pilot also illustrates the governance challenges of sensor-based urban safety systems, as microphones and movement detection in public or semi-public spaces require careful safeguards, transparency, proportionality and clear limits on data use.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Rotterdam’s smart lampposts pilot in the Lombardijen district was introduced to test whether street infrastructure could support earlier detection of burglary-related signals. The public-safety problem is that burglary attempts may occur before residents, police or municipal services become aware of suspicious activity, limiting the opportunity for rapid intervention. The Municipality of Rotterdam installed lampposts equipped with sensors able to record suspicious movements and microphones able to detect sounds such as tapping on a window. The implementation combines urban sensor infrastructure, motion detection, audio analysis and alerting logic to identify patterns that may suggest attempted burglary or abnormal activity. Conceptually, the lampposts function as distributed detection points in the neighbourhood, using environmental signals to support situational awareness. The expected impact is faster recognition of possible incidents, improved capacity to deploy security or police resources and stronger deterrence in areas affected by burglary risk. The pilot also illustrates the governance challenges of sensor-based urban safety systems, as microphones and movement detection in public or semi-public spaces require careful safeguards, transparency, proportionality and clear limits on data use.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-371,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00364,EU_PSTW_202609_00364,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Rotterdam,Transport,UNKNOWN,Smart sensor waste containers,Municipality of Rotterdam; Local Government; Transport; Netherlands,"This use case addresses this operational problem by installing sensors in waste containers to measure filling levels and support more efficient collection planning. The system uses “filling degree meters” that transmit the status of containers every hour, providing regular information on how full each bin is.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Municipality of Rotterdam needs to keep neighbourhood waste collection reliable and clean while avoiding both overflowing containers and unnecessary collection trips to bins that are not yet full. This use case addresses this operational problem by installing sensors in waste containers to measure filling levels and support more efficient collection planning. In dense urban areas, conventional waste collection based on fixed routes or schedules can result in two opposite inefficiencies: waste accumulating beside overcrowded containers when emptying is too late, and staff time and vehicle kilometres being wasted when containers are emptied too early. The system uses “filling degree meters” that transmit the status of containers every hour, providing regular information on how full each bin is. This data is made available to collection teams, and drivers can use a tablet to see which route should be taken next. The technological implementation combines sensor-based monitoring, data transmission and route-support tools that translate container-level information into operational guidance for waste-collection services. The expected impact is cleaner public space, fewer complaints about overflowing containers, more efficient use of collection vehicles and reduced unnecessary driving. For waste-management staff, the system improves day-to-day planning by showing where intervention is actually needed. For the municipality, it supports a more responsive and resource-efficient approach to urban waste services.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-372,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00365,EU_PSTW_202609_00365,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Aincient B.V., Picturae, Sioux Technologies, Islands of Meaning, Amsterdam City Archive.",General public services n.e.c.,"The operational challenge is that manual transcription of such large volumes would be extremely time-consuming, while image-only records limit the ability of researchers, citizens and archivists to search across complete texts. The impact is a major improvement in archival discovery, faster access to historical information, reduced dependence on manual indexing and greater reuse of cultural-heritage collections.",Amsterdam City Archive - Handwritten Text Recognition for eabling searches on old handwritten texts,"Aincient B.V., Picturae, Sioux Technologies, Islands of Meaning, Amsterdam City Archive.; Private sector; General public services n.e.c.; Netherlands",The implementation uses Handwritten Text Recognition to analyse scanned handwritten pages and generate machine-readable transcriptions.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Amsterdam City Archive holds millions of scans of historical handwritten documents that are valuable for research and public This initaitves aim to make these archival materials more discoverable by automatically converting handwritten scans into text transcriptions. The operational challenge is that manual transcription of such large volumes would be extremely time-consuming, while image-only records limit the ability of researchers, citizens and archivists to search across complete texts. The initiative involves Aincient B.V., Picturae, Sioux Technologies, Islands of Meaning and the Amsterdam City Archive, combining archival expertise with specialist technology providers. The implementation uses Handwritten Text Recognition to analyse scanned handwritten pages and generate machine-readable transcriptions. Natural language processing is then used to support further interpretation, structuring and searchability of the resulting text. At a conceptual level, the system identifies handwriting patterns, converts them into textual output and enables full-text search across materials that were previously far less accessible. The impact is a major improvement in archival discovery, faster access to historical information, reduced dependence on manual indexing and greater reuse of cultural-heritage collections. By making complete handwritten texts searchable, the project strengthens public access to Amsterdam’s documentary heritage and supports more scalable digital-archive services.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK|F06_HIGH_VOLUME_SCALABILITY_BOTTLENECK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-373,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00366,EU_PSTW_202609_00366,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,CentERdata,General services.,Smart Street Scan was developed to address this challenge by enabling faster identification and registration of litter so that waste-collection and street-cleaning operations can be organised more effectively.,Smart Street Scan really sets up municipal services differently,CentERdata; Private sector; General services.; Netherlands,"The stakeholders include municipal service providers, waste-collection teams and residents using public space, with the objective of improving cleanliness, reducing response time and optimising service deployment through better information. The solution is implemented by combining sensor and camera technology with image-recognition methods to scan public space automatically.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Municipalities need to keep public spaces clean, yet litter and other waste in streets and public areas are often detected only after complaints or routine inspections, which can delay action and make municipal services less efficient. Smart Street Scan was developed to address this challenge by enabling faster identification and registration of litter so that waste-collection and street-cleaning operations can be organised more effectively. The stakeholders include municipal service providers, waste-collection teams and residents using public space, with the objective of improving cleanliness, reducing response time and optimising service deployment through better information. The solution is implemented by combining sensor and camera technology with image-recognition methods to scan public space automatically. At a conceptual level, the system detects, classifies and registers litter in the street environment, creating a digital feed of observed waste conditions that can be linked to operational planning. This allows downstream services, such as sweeping and waste collection, to adapt routes and interventions on the basis of current conditions rather than relying solely on fixed schedules or resident reports. The expected impact is quicker detection of litter, more efficient route planning and improved street-cleaning performance. More broadly, the project creates value by changing how municipal services are organised around public-space maintenance, reducing unnecessary effort and supporting a more data-driven, responsive and scalable approach to keeping urban environments clean.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,NOT_IN_USE,미사용,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-374,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00367,EU_PSTW_202609_00367,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"UWV, SVB, the Tax And Customs Administration, DUO, Justid, CJIB and RDW","Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.",UNKNOWN,Enforcement of social policy laws with AI,"UWV, SVB, the Tax And Customs Administration, DUO, Justid, CJIB and RDW; Central-Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Netherlands","This national Dutch pilot concerns the use of data analysis to support enforcement of social-security legislation, particularly in relation to fraud detection and compliance with the duty to inform. Coordinated analysis can help authorities understand enforcement risks, identify inconsistencies and evaluate how social-security rules are implemented.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"This national Dutch pilot concerns the use of data analysis to support enforcement of social-security legislation, particularly in relation to fraud detection and compliance with the duty to inform. The administrative problem is that several public bodies are responsible for schemes, payments, registrations and enforcement processes that may involve overlapping information on citizens, benefits, education, taxation, vehicles and justice-related records. Coordinated analysis can help authorities understand enforcement risks, identify inconsistencies and evaluate how social-security rules are implemented. The implementation involves organisations including UWV, SVB, the Tax and Customs Administration, DUO, Justid, CJIB and RDW. Conceptually, the system can combine cross-agency data analysis, risk indicators, policy evaluation and compliance monitoring to support enforcement of social-policy laws. The expected impact is more structured insight into enforcement practice, better targeting of checks and improved understanding of where errors, omissions or potential fraud may occur. However, because the use case involves sensitive personal and administrative data across multiple bodies, it requires strong safeguards on legality, purpose limitation, transparency, proportionality and independent oversight. The business value for public administration depends on using analytics to improve fairness and efficiency without creating opaque or discriminatory risk profiling.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-375,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00368,EU_PSTW_202609_00368,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Dutch Police, with Speech lab B.V.",Police Services,"The operational issue is that investigations can involve lengthy recordings with multiple voices, requiring officers or analysts to listen manually to identify who is speaking and when. Its value depends on accuracy in difficult acoustic conditions, careful validation and appropriate human review, since mistaken speaker attribution could affect investigative interpretation.",Speaker Tracking in Time and Space,"Dutch Police, with Speech lab B.V.; Central-Government; Police Services; Netherlands","The implementation uses speech-processing techniques, likely including speaker diarisation, voice-separation, audio segmentation and speaker-labelling models.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Speaker Tracking in Time and Space supports police analysis of audio recordings by separating and labelling speech from different speakers. The operational issue is that investigations can involve lengthy recordings with multiple voices, requiring officers or analysts to listen manually to identify who is speaking and when. This is time-consuming and can also expose investigators to conversations unrelated to the subject of interest, creating unnecessary privacy intrusion. Spraaklab develops technology that can largely disassemble, distinguish and label speech from different speakers, allowing police teams to work more efficiently and with higher analytical quality. The implementation uses speech-processing techniques, likely including speaker diarisation, voice-separation, audio segmentation and speaker-labelling models. Conceptually, the tool structures raw audio into speaker-specific segments, helping analysts locate relevant parts of recordings without reviewing unrelated material repeatedly. The expected impact is faster audio review, improved investigative productivity, better-quality evidence handling and stronger privacy protection for people whose conversations are not relevant to the investigation. Its value depends on accuracy in difficult acoustic conditions, careful validation and appropriate human review, since mistaken speaker attribution could affect investigative interpretation.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-376,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00369,EU_PSTW_202609_00369,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of The Hague,Public order and safety n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,Road safety model The Hague - Providing a risk score for all road sections and intersections,Municipality of The Hague; Local Government; Public order and safety n.e.c.; Netherlands,"Each road segment and intersection is described using approximately 250 variables, enabling systematic comparison across the network.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Urban road authorities require proactive tools to reduce traffic accidents and prioritise safety interventions across complex road networks. The Municipality of The Hague developed a road safety model to assess and compare risk levels for all road sections and intersections within the city. Stakeholders include municipal traffic engineers, urban planners, and road safety policymakers. The objective is to move from reactive accident analysis to preventive, data-driven traffic safety management. The model combines historical accident records with a comprehensive dataset describing traffic conditions, infrastructure characteristics, environmental factors, and usage patterns. Each road segment and intersection is described using approximately 250 variables, enabling systematic comparison across the network. These datasets are processed to generate a relative risk score that highlights locations with a higher likelihood of future accidents. The results are visualised through analytical dashboards used by traffic experts to prioritise inspections, redesigns, and enforcement measures. According to official government documentation, the model is actively used as a decision-support instrument rather than an automated enforcement mechanism. The approach has improved the municipality’s ability to identify high-risk locations before accidents occur, allocate resources more effectively, and justify infrastructure investments transparently, contributing to a more systematic and preventive road safety strategy.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-377,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00370,EU_PSTW_202609_00370,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Municipality of Rotterdam, Welance.io,",Public order and safety n.e.c.,The Municipality of Rotterdam faces similar challenges to other large cities in managing road safety across a dense and diverse transport network.,Road safety model Rotterdam - Predictive model for road accidents,"Municipality of Rotterdam, Welance.io,; Local Government; Public order and safety n.e.c.; Netherlands","To support preventive traffic policy, Rotterdam implemented a road safety model that predicts accident risks by analysing the relationship between historical accidents and contextual road data. The model links accident records with data describing road geometry, traffic intensity, surrounding land use, weather conditions, and environmental characteristics within the influence area of each accident location.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Municipality of Rotterdam faces similar challenges to other large cities in managing road safety across a dense and diverse transport network. To support preventive traffic policy, Rotterdam implemented a road safety model that predicts accident risks by analysing the relationship between historical accidents and contextual road data. Key stakeholders include municipal traffic authorities, safety analysts, and infrastructure planners. The objective is to identify hazardous road locations proactively and guide targeted interventions. The model links accident records with data describing road geometry, traffic intensity, surrounding land use, weather conditions, and environmental characteristics within the influence area of each accident location. These combined datasets are analysed to identify patterns associated with elevated accident risks, allowing the municipality to generate predictive insights rather than relying solely on past accident counts. The results are used internally by experts to prioritise safety audits, redesigns, and policy measures. According to sector knowledge platform documentation, the model is operational as a decision-support tool and does not replace expert judgement. Its use has strengthened Rotterdam’s capacity to focus safety investments where they are most needed, improved transparency in traffic safety planning, and supported a shift towards data-informed preventive road management.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-378,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00371,EU_PSTW_202609_00371,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,File radar B.V.,Transport,"The operational problem is that traffic-control centres often react to current data, while non-recurrent congestion caused by incidents, sudden bottlenecks or unusual flows may require earlier warnings to deploy effective measures.",Movable Traffic Predictor,File radar B.V.; Private sector; Transport; Netherlands,"The implementation applies predictive analytics to traffic data to generate short-term forecasts in the range of approximately 20 to 60 minutes. The expected impact is earlier intervention, improved traffic flow, reduced delay and better use of transport-management resources.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Movable Traffic Predictor pilot was designed to support traffic-management decisions by forecasting traffic conditions before congestion becomes visible in real time. The operational problem is that traffic-control centres often react to current data, while non-recurrent congestion caused by incidents, sudden bottlenecks or unusual flows may require earlier warnings to deploy effective measures. File radar B.V. developed a new type of AI-based traffic forecaster capable of predicting traffic up to about one hour ahead, with testing carried out in collaboration with traffic-control centres, particularly the national VCNL centre near Utrecht. The implementation applies predictive analytics to traffic data to generate short-term forecasts in the range of approximately 20 to 60 minutes. Conceptually, the system analyses current and historical traffic conditions, identifies emerging disruptions and provides predictive information for operational decisions such as congestion detection, deployment of mobile assets or traffic-management measures. The expected impact is earlier intervention, improved traffic flow, reduced delay and better use of transport-management resources. By shifting traffic control from purely real-time monitoring to anticipatory decision support, the pilot can improve resilience to irregular and incident-driven congestion.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION|S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-379,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00372,EU_PSTW_202609_00372,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Financial Intelligence Unit Netherlands (FIU-Netherlands),Police Services,UNKNOWN,Reference Index Recherche Onderzoeken en Subjecten - Detection of unusual transactions,Financial Intelligence Unit Netherlands (FIU-Netherlands); Central-Government; Police Services; Netherlands,"Its use has improved investigative efficiency by enabling faster cross-referencing of financial intelligence, reducing manual searches, and supporting coordinated law enforcement action, while maintaining procedural safeguards and human oversight.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Law enforcement agencies must efficiently identify suspicious financial transactions that may be linked to ongoing criminal investigations while operating within strict legal frameworks. In the Netherlands, unusual transactions are partly assessed through comparison with the Reference Index Recherche Onderzoeken en Subjecten (VROS), a database managed by the National Police. Stakeholders include financial institutions, law enforcement bodies, and investigative authorities. The objective is to support criminal investigations by identifying whether reported unusual transactions are linked to existing police investigations. Transaction data flagged by reporting entities is compared through a partly automated process with entries in the VROS. A positive match indicates a potential connection between the transaction and an active investigation, triggering further human-led assessment. The system does not autonomously label transactions as criminal but acts as an investigative signal within a broader legal process. VROS is formally embedded in Dutch legislation and remains operational as part of the national framework for combating financial crime. Its use has improved investigative efficiency by enabling faster cross-referencing of financial intelligence, reducing manual searches, and supporting coordinated law enforcement action, while maintaining procedural safeguards and human oversight.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-380,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00373,EU_PSTW_202609_00373,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Dordrecht,Public order and safety n.e.c.,"The safety problem is that drownings or underwater incidents can be difficult to identify quickly, especially where visual monitoring from the shore is limited or delayed.",Viralcom Egg - accidents in the water,Municipality of Dordrecht; Private sector; Public order and safety n.e.c.; Netherlands,The implementation uses artificial intelligence and neural networks for pattern recognition and drowning detection.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Viralcom Egg is a prototype designed to detect accidents in water and communicate relevant alerts to rescue services. The safety problem is that drownings or underwater incidents can be difficult to identify quickly, especially where visual monitoring from the shore is limited or delayed. The Municipality of Dordrecht is linked to the pilot context, with Viralcom seeking to test the Egg remotely from the quay in Zwijndrecht to the opposite quay in Dordrecht. The implementation uses artificial intelligence and neural networks for pattern recognition and drowning detection. Conceptually, the system monitors activity in or around the water, analyses visual or sensor patterns that may indicate an accident, and sends information to emergency or rescue services when a potential incident is identified. The expected impact is faster detection of water accidents, shorter response times and improved public safety in areas where people may fall into or become distressed in water. The prototype’s value depends on reliable detection under varying environmental conditions, low false-alarm rates and effective integration with rescue-service workflows. If validated, it could strengthen prevention and emergency response in urban waterfront areas.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-381,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00374,EU_PSTW_202609_00374,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Twenterand,Social protection n.e.c.,"Residents experiencing debt, care needs or social vulnerability often interact with multiple services, but early warning signs may remain fragmented until problems become acute. The expected impact is earlier intervention, better coordination between social services, reduced crisis escalation and more efficient targeting of limited municipal capacity.",Predict which residents have a higher risk of care or debt problems in Twenterand (SyRI),Municipality of Twenterand; Local Government; Social protection n.e.c.; Netherlands,"Twenterand’s reported use case concerns profiling residents to map debt and care-related problems, with the aim of identifying people who may need help earlier. The use case must be handled carefully because it involves potentially sensitive social and financial data, and because predictive profiling can unintentionally stigmatise residents or neighbourhoods.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Residents experiencing debt, care needs or social vulnerability often interact with multiple services, but early warning signs may remain fragmented until problems become acute. Twenterand’s reported use case concerns profiling residents to map debt and care-related problems, with the aim of identifying people who may need help earlier. The operational problem is that social problems often develop gradually and may only become visible once enforcement, crisis care or intensive support is required. A predictive system can bring together administrative indicators and historical patterns to identify households or individuals whose situation resembles earlier cases of debt escalation or care need. At a conceptual level, the system would classify or prioritise residents according to risk signals, enabling municipal professionals to consider preventive outreach or support. The expected impact is earlier intervention, better coordination between social services, reduced crisis escalation and more efficient targeting of limited municipal capacity. The use case must be handled carefully because it involves potentially sensitive social and financial data, and because predictive profiling can unintentionally stigmatise residents or neighbourhoods. Its legitimacy depends on clear purpose limitation, strong human oversight, transparency to affected people and safeguards ensuring that the system is used to offer support rather than punishment.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-382,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00375,EU_PSTW_202609_00375,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Amersfoort and Dordrecht,Education n.e.c.,"However, the case also raises important governance issues because educational-risk profiling can affect young people’s opportunities and may be difficult to understand or contest.",Early school leavers in the municipality of Amersfoort - Estimation of students at risk,Municipality of Amersfoort and Dordrecht; Local Government; Education n.e.c.; Netherlands,"The Amersfoort and Dordrecht use case concerns the use of an algorithm to estimate which students may be at risk of becoming early school leavers. The expected impact is better targeting of support, lower dropout rates, more efficient use of guidance capacity and improved educational outcomes.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Preventing early school leaving requires timely identification of pupils whose educational trajectory suggests a higher risk of dropping out before completing their programme. The Amersfoort and Dordrecht use case concerns the use of an algorithm to estimate which students may be at risk of becoming early school leavers. The operational problem is that schools and municipalities may hold relevant signals, such as school changes, grade repetition or attendance-related patterns, but these signals may not be systematically combined for early intervention. At a conceptual level, the algorithm compares current pupils with patterns observed among previous early school leavers and assigns an indication of risk. Professionals can then decide whether additional guidance, counselling or follow-up is appropriate. The intended value is preventive: the model is meant to help education and youth-support services act before disengagement becomes irreversible. The expected impact is better targeting of support, lower dropout rates, more efficient use of guidance capacity and improved educational outcomes. However, the case also raises important governance issues because educational-risk profiling can affect young people’s opportunities and may be difficult to understand or contest. It should therefore be presented as decision support for human professionals, with transparent explanation of the indicators used and safeguards against discriminatory effects.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-383,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00376,EU_PSTW_202609_00376,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of The Hague,Environmental protection n.e.c.,"Permit procedures in this area can involve large volumes of documents and varied applicant questions, creating an administrative burden at the earliest stages of the process and slowing the movement of cases into proper review channels. At a conceptual level, the system analyses incoming textual information, identifies relevant content and helps structure or route permit applications so that initial handling requires less manual intervention.",Wabo assistant - Automated environmental permits,Municipality of The Hague; Local Government; Environmental protection n.e.c.; Netherlands,"The use case therefore focused on improving intake efficiency and reducing repetitive administrative work, while helping the municipality process environmental permits more consistently. The solution is implemented through a Wabo assistant that uses natural language processing to support permit-related tasks.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The municipality of The Hague needed a more efficient way to manage the intake of environmental-permit applications governed by the Environmental Law General Provisions Act, known as Wabo. Permit procedures in this area can involve large volumes of documents and varied applicant questions, creating an administrative burden at the earliest stages of the process and slowing the movement of cases into proper review channels. The use case therefore focused on improving intake efficiency and reducing repetitive administrative work, while helping the municipality process environmental permits more consistently. The stakeholders include municipal permit teams, applicants and environmental-administration staff, with the objective of automating part of the intake process and improving the speed and quality of early-stage handling. The solution is implemented through a Wabo assistant that uses natural language processing to support permit-related tasks. At a conceptual level, the system analyses incoming textual information, identifies relevant content and helps structure or route permit applications so that initial handling requires less manual intervention. This creates a more standardised intake workflow and allows staff to spend less time on repetitive front-end processing. The expected impact is faster handling of permit submissions, reduced clerical burden and improved efficiency in the administration of environmental permits. More broadly, the Wabo assistant creates value by modernising a document-heavy municipal process, supporting better service for applicants and enabling a more scalable model for managing regulatory intake under environmental law.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,NOT_IN_USE,미사용,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK|F06_HIGH_VOLUME_SCALABILITY_BOTTLENECK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-384,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00377,EU_PSTW_202609_00377,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Breda and I-SAGO,Waste water management.,UNKNOWN,Water fields - purification system for rainwater,Municipality of Breda and I-SAGO; Local Government; Waste water management.; Netherlands,"The City of Breda implemented “water fields”, a nature-based purification system that collects, buffers, and treats rainwater at district level. The objective is to optimise water use, reduce pressure on drinking water supplies, and improve climate resilience.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Cities increasingly face pressure to manage water sustainably while reducing reliance on potable water for non-drinking purposes. The City of Breda implemented “water fields”, a nature-based purification system that collects, buffers, and treats rainwater at district level. Stakeholders include municipal water managers, urban planners, and local residents. The objective is to optimise water use, reduce pressure on drinking water supplies, and improve climate resilience. Rainwater from the surrounding area is collected and filtered through a series of natural purification layers before being stored for reuse. Sensors continuously monitor system performance, rainfall volumes, and storage levels. Operational data is analysed to optimise purification processes and forecast water availability, supporting adaptive system management. According to reporting on the project, the water fields are fully implemented and integrated into Breda’s water infrastructure. The initiative has improved water efficiency, reduced operational costs, and enhanced resilience to drought and heavy rainfall. It also demonstrates how data-driven monitoring can support sustainable urban water systems while maintaining transparency and reliability.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-385,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00378,EU_PSTW_202609_00378,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Hoogheemraadschap de Stichtse Rijnlanden en Tauw en HAL24k,Protection of biodiversity and landscape.,"The Water management system supports Hoogheemraadschap De Stichtse Rijnlanden, Tauw and HAL24K in applying data science to complex water-management challenges.",Water management SR - Predicting of water levels,Hoogheemraadschap de Stichtse Rijnlanden en Tauw en HAL24k; Private sector; Protection of biodiversity and landscape.; Netherlands,"The technological implementation is based on HAL24K’s Dimension data-intelligence platform, demonstrated through the use of live sensor data. Predictive models can use these data to support earlier identification of risks and more timely operational decisions.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Water authorities need to manage treatment plants and water levels proactively, because delayed detection of operational issues can affect service reliability, maintenance planning and the ability to respond to changing environmental conditions. The Water management system supports Hoogheemraadschap De Stichtse Rijnlanden, Tauw and HAL24K in applying data science to complex water-management challenges. The case focuses on improving how real-time operational data can be used to understand conditions at water treatment plants and anticipate changes in water levels, rather than relying only on periodic checks or reactive maintenance. The technological implementation is based on HAL24K’s Dimension data-intelligence platform, demonstrated through the use of live sensor data. At a conceptual level, the platform collects and processes streams from sensors installed in water-management infrastructure, enabling the analysis of current conditions, anomalies and trends relevant to maintenance or level management. Predictive models can use these data to support earlier identification of risks and more timely operational decisions. The expected impact is more efficient maintenance, faster detection of emerging problems, better use of real-time information and improved capacity to manage water systems proactively. For the responsible organisations, the value lies in strengthening operational resilience, reducing avoidable interventions and creating a more data-driven basis for water-level control and treatment-plant management.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-386,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00379,EU_PSTW_202609_00379,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Municipality of Houten, Ijsselstein, Lopik, Nieuwegein and Vijfheerenland",Social protection n.e.c.,"The operational problem is that benefit-control processes are labour-intensive, and staff may need to identify which cases deserve additional review without checking every file manually.",WIL Leak Current - Discover abuse and improper use of benefits,"Municipality of Houten, Ijsselstein, Lopik, Nieuwegein and Vijfheerenland; Local Government; Social protection n.e.c.; Netherlands","Welfare administrations need to ensure that people who are entitled to benefits receive support while also detecting improper use or abuse of public funds. WIL Lekstroom, a cooperation of municipalities in the Utrecht region, was reported as using algorithms to support detection of abuse and improper use of social-assistance benefits.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Welfare administrations need to ensure that people who are entitled to benefits receive support while also detecting improper use or abuse of public funds. WIL Lekstroom, a cooperation of municipalities in the Utrecht region, was reported as using algorithms to support detection of abuse and improper use of social-assistance benefits. The operational problem is that benefit-control processes are labour-intensive, and staff may need to identify which cases deserve additional review without checking every file manually. At a conceptual level, the system would use historical benefit, case-management or administrative data to detect patterns associated with previous improper use, then generate signals or risk scores for officials. The expected impact is more targeted investigation, reduced unnecessary checks, improved administrative efficiency and better protection of public resources. However, welfare-fraud prediction is a high-risk area because historical data may reflect biased enforcement patterns and flagged residents may not know that an algorithm influenced additional scrutiny. The system should therefore be understood as decision support only, not as automatic sanctioning or benefit withdrawal. Any responsible use would require clear documentation, explainability, human review, appeal routes and monitoring for discriminatory impact, especially because the affected population may already be socio-economically vulnerable.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-387,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00380,EU_PSTW_202609_00380,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Brightlands Techruption, OU (CAROU), UM (BISS), Police NL (iLAB)",Police Services,"Officers often need to review recent incidents, local risks and operational context, but conventional briefing processes can be fragmented, time-consuming or dependent on manual searches across different systems.",Nexus - speech-operated expert system,"Brightlands Techruption, OU (CAROU), UM (BISS), Police NL (iLAB); Private sector; Police Services; Netherlands",The interface reduces the need for manual interaction and supports use in operational contexts where officers may benefit from hands-free access to information.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Nexus is designed to support community police officers who require timely, structured and locally relevant information before engaging with neighbourhoods. Officers often need to review recent incidents, local risks and operational context, but conventional briefing processes can be fragmented, time-consuming or dependent on manual searches across different systems. The case involves Brightlands Techruption, the Open University’s CAROU, Maastricht University’s BISS and the Dutch Police iLAB, with the objective of providing officers with an accessible expert system that delivers detailed neighbourhood briefings through speech interaction. The emerging technology implementation consists of a speech-operated expert system that allows officers to request and receive information verbally. At conceptual level, the system combines speech recognition, knowledge retrieval and expert-system logic to transform operational data on recent incidents into a concise briefing tailored to a specific neighbourhood. The interface reduces the need for manual interaction and supports use in operational contexts where officers may benefit from hands-free access to information. The impact is improved situational awareness, more efficient preparation and a better user experience for community police officers. By making local incident information easier to access and interpret, Nexus can support more informed decision-making, strengthen neighbourhood policing and reduce the time spent searching for relevant background information before field activities.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,S1_KNOWLEDGE_RETRIEVAL_RAG,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-388,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00381,EU_PSTW_202609_00381,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Municipality of Heerlen, CBS, VNG, CoTown, Heerlen Mijn Stad, Brightlands",General services.,"Service.Heerlen, or WESH, addresses the challenge of maintaining the public environment while promoting citizen participation and supporting local economic activity. Municipalities often face pressure to keep public spaces clean, safe and well maintained, while citizens may have limited structured opportunities to contribute to neighbourhood improvements.",W(e). Service.Heerlen (WESH),"Municipality of Heerlen, CBS, VNG, CoTown, Heerlen Mijn Stad, Brightlands; Local Government; General services.; Netherlands",UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"W(e). Service.Heerlen, or WESH, addresses the challenge of maintaining the public environment while promoting citizen participation and supporting local economic activity. Municipalities often face pressure to keep public spaces clean, safe and well maintained, while citizens may have limited structured opportunities to contribute to neighbourhood improvements. In Heerlen, the municipality and partners including CBS, VNG, CoTown, Heerlen Mijn Stad and Brightlands developed an application that enables citizens to take on maintenance tasks in the public environment. The objective is to create a participatory model in which residents can contribute directly to local upkeep and receive a financial reward that can be spent with local small and medium-sized enterprises. The emerging technology implementation is based on a digital platform that connects available municipal maintenance jobs with citizens willing to complete them. At conceptual level, the app supports task publication, citizen participation, completion confirmation and reward allocation, linking civic engagement with local economic circulation. The solution may also generate useful operational data on maintenance needs, participation patterns and service delivery. Its impact is both operational and social: the municipality can mobilise additional capacity for small-scale public-space maintenance, citizens gain a practical role in improving their environment, and local SMEs benefit from reward spending. The case demonstrates how digital public-service platforms can combine urban maintenance, participation and local value creation.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-389,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00382,EU_PSTW_202609_00382,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Brightlands Techruption, CBS, municipality of Heerlen, CZ, Maastricht University, TNO",Social protection n.e.c.,Early signs of financial vulnerability are often missed because relevant data is distributed across multiple public agencies that cannot easily share information without infringing privacy regulations.,Poverty prevention - Identification of poverty and debt early,"Brightlands Techruption, CBS, municipality of Heerlen, CZ, Maastricht University, TNO; Local Government; Social protection n.e.c.; Netherlands",The implementation applies privacy-preserving computation techniques that allow organisations to analyse combined indicators without revealing individual-level data. The project demonstrates that data-driven prevention policies can be effectively implemented without compromising confidentiality or individual rights.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Early signs of financial vulnerability are often missed because relevant data is distributed across multiple public agencies that cannot easily share information without infringing privacy regulations. Municipalities, social services and welfare organisations require new approaches to identify citizens at risk before situations escalate into severe poverty or debt. The objective of this pilot is to enable preventive social interventions while fully safeguarding personal data. The implementation applies privacy-preserving computation techniques that allow organisations to analyse combined indicators without revealing individual-level data. Secure algorithms process variables such as income changes, benefit dependency and arrears to identify risk patterns, and results are integrated into existing social support workflows. When risk thresholds are reached, case workers are alerted to initiate early assistance. The pilot improved the timeliness and targeting of interventions, reducing long-term social and financial costs. Qualitative impacts include stronger inter-agency collaboration, increased trust from citizens and full compliance with data protection requirements. The project demonstrates that data-driven prevention policies can be effectively implemented without compromising confidentiality or individual rights.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-390,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00383,EU_PSTW_202609_00383,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Gdansk,Transport,UNKNOWN,INZNAK Project - Intelligent road signs for adaptive vehicle traffic control,Gdansk; Local Government; Transport; Poland,"This multimodal approach allows the system to combine different detection methods, for example using lidar when standard cameras are less effective at night.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The INZNAK project responds to the need to improve road safety and traffic management by providing drivers with timely, location-specific warnings about hazards, disruptions and recommended speeds. Many highway collisions are linked to speed that is not adapted to current weather, road-surface or traffic conditions, particularly during sudden braking, congestion, fog, storms, blizzards or black ice. In Gdańsk, the project aims to develop a network of autonomous intelligent road signs capable of warning drivers earlier and supporting adaptive traffic control. The emerging technology implementation includes three types of signs: standing, hanging and mobile. Each sign has a display showing text and image messages, including suggested speeds and information on current disruptions. The signs communicate with each other through LoRaWAN and with the traffic control centre through LTE, creating a two-level communication architecture that improves resilience if telecommunications infrastructure fails. Each sign is equipped with sensors such as Doppler radar, lidar, acoustic probes, RGB cameras, Bluetooth receivers and a local weather station. This multimodal approach allows the system to combine different detection methods, for example using lidar when standard cameras are less effective at night. The expected impact is earlier hazard detection, more reliable traffic information and better adaptation of driver behaviour to real-time road conditions, with potential benefits for safety, traffic flow and operational resilience.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-391,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00384,EU_PSTW_202609_00384,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Chorzow,General public services n.e.c.,"The operational challenge is that citizens often need guidance outside office hours or may not know where to look for the correct administrative information, while staff can spend considerable time responding to repetitive enquiries.",Virtual Clerk - Assistance with search for information,Municipality of Chorzow; Local Government; General public services n.e.c.; Poland,The system uses artificial intelligence to support natural and user-friendly searches across institutional information.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Municipal offices that serve large numbers of residents and beneficiaries must provide clear procedural information quickly, even when enquiries concern many different services, administrative routes and eligibility conditions. Virtual Clerk helps the Municipality of Chorzów optimise service processes for hundreds of thousands of beneficiaries by offering an intuitive digital channel for finding information about the office and the procedures it manages. The operational challenge is that citizens often need guidance outside office hours or may not know where to look for the correct administrative information, while staff can spend considerable time responding to repetitive enquiries. The system uses artificial intelligence to support natural and user-friendly searches across institutional information. At a conceptual level, it interprets user questions, identifies the relevant procedural content and returns guidance in a form that is easier to access than navigating multiple static pages or contacting the office directly. The service is available 24 hours a day, seven days a week, improving accessibility for users who need information at different times. Its impact is faster access to public-service information, reduced friction for beneficiaries, lower pressure on front-office staff and more consistent communication on municipal procedures. For the municipality, Virtual Clerk contributes to a more modern, scalable and citizen-oriented model of administrative service delivery.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-392,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00385,EU_PSTW_202609_00385,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""social_nonprofit""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Płońsk,General public services n.e.c.,"County-office buildings require coordinated management of security, utilities, technical infrastructure and room equipment, yet these systems are often operated separately, making monitoring and optimisation more difficult. By integrating these functions, the administration can obtain a clearer view of how the building performs and respond more quickly to faults, security issues or inefficient consumption patterns.",Intelligent Management Systems,Municipality of Płońsk; Local Government; General public services n.e.c.; Poland,"Sensors, controllers and digital interfaces can be used to gather information on building conditions, resource use and system status, while management software supports supervision and operational decisions. The expected impact includes improved energy and water management, stronger security, more efficient use of office infrastructure, better continuity of building operations and reduced manual effort for technical staff.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"County-office buildings require coordinated management of security, utilities, technical infrastructure and room equipment, yet these systems are often operated separately, making monitoring and optimisation more difficult. This Intelligent Management Systems in the Municipality of Płońsk brings together a range of building-management functions within the County Office to improve control, efficiency and operational reliability. The case responds to the need for a more integrated approach to public-building management, covering access control, external roller shutters, monitoring, water and electricity consumption, PIAPs, IT networks, an intelligent conference room and the fire-protection system. The technological implementation consists of interconnected building-management systems that enable centralised monitoring and control of different technical installations. Sensors, controllers and digital interfaces can be used to gather information on building conditions, resource use and system status, while management software supports supervision and operational decisions. By integrating these functions, the administration can obtain a clearer view of how the building performs and respond more quickly to faults, security issues or inefficient consumption patterns. The expected impact includes improved energy and water management, stronger security, more efficient use of office infrastructure, better continuity of building operations and reduced manual effort for technical staff. In organisational terms, the project supports the modernisation of local-government facilities and provides a more resilient foundation for public administration services delivered from those buildings.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-393,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00386,EU_PSTW_202609_00386,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Warsaw,R&D general public services.,"A conventional planning model may struggle to capture these interactions, particularly where cooperation, conflict, negotiation and shared benefits influence outcomes.",The use of game theory in the process of modeling the development of smart city,Municipality of Warsaw; Local Government; R&D general public services.; Poland,"The implementation seeks to develop a participatory game model using artificial intelligence methods, with cooperative game theory providing the formal basis for representing smart-city processes.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Warsaw smart-city modelling pilot aims to represent the development and functioning of a smart city through a participatory game based on game-theory principles. The policy and planning problem is that urban development involves multiple actors with different objectives, resources and constraints, including city authorities, developers, environmental organisations and residents. A conventional planning model may struggle to capture these interactions, particularly where cooperation, conflict, negotiation and shared benefits influence outcomes. The implementation seeks to develop a participatory game model using artificial intelligence methods, with cooperative game theory providing the formal basis for representing smart-city processes. A key element is parameterisation: defining the number of players, assigning their roles and modelling how their decisions affect urban development scenarios. Conceptually, the system combines simulation, stakeholder modelling, strategic interaction and AI-supported analysis to illustrate how smart-city choices may evolve under different assumptions. The expected impact is better understanding of urban governance dynamics, stronger support for participatory planning and improved capacity to test policy options before implementation. By translating complex city-development processes into a structured game model, the pilot can help public authorities explore trade-offs, incentives and cooperation mechanisms in smart-city policy design.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-394,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00387,EU_PSTW_202609_00387,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Kartuzy,Water supply.,UNKNOWN,Monitoring of a Water Treatment Station,Municipality of Kartuzy; Local Government; Water supply.; Poland,This use case in the Municipality of Kartuzy addresses this need by introducing monitoring for the local water treatment plant and the associated water-supply system.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Municipal water operators need accurate and continuous information on flow, pressure, intake operations and distribution volumes to maintain reliable supply and detect losses within the network. This use case in the Municipality of Kartuzy addresses this need by introducing monitoring for the local water treatment plant and the associated water-supply system. The operational problem is that insufficient visibility over network parameters can make it harder to control the amount of water sent into the system, identify abnormal losses or regulate intake operations efficiently. The case focuses on measuring flow rate, monitoring network pressure, controlling and regulating intake activity, and balancing the volume of water distributed through the network. The technological implementation relies on sensors and monitoring equipment installed across key points of the treatment and distribution process. These devices collect operational measurements and transmit them to management systems that allow staff to observe conditions, compare distributed volumes and identify potential inefficiencies or losses. The resulting information supports more precise operational control and better evidence for maintenance or intervention decisions. The expected impact is improved reliability of water supply, faster identification of pressure or flow anomalies, better control of water intake and distribution, and stronger capacity to detect water losses. For the municipality, the system contributes to more efficient utility management and a more transparent view of essential water infrastructure.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-395,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00388,EU_PSTW_202609_00388,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Zywiec,Water supply.,"To address these challenges, an intelligent water and sewage network management system was developed to support real-time monitoring and optimisation of network operations.",Intelligent water and sewage network management system,Municipality of Zywiec; Local Government; Water supply.; Poland,"According to official innovation mapping documentation, the project aligns with international smart grid trends and is being implemented incrementally, combining pilot deployments with gradual scaling. Expected impacts include lower operational costs, improved service continuity, faster fault detection, and more efficient use of water resources, supporting long-term sustainability of public utility networks.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Water and sewage network operators face increasing complexity in managing ageing infrastructure, fluctuating demand, and rising maintenance costs. To address these challenges, an intelligent water and sewage network management system was developed to support real-time monitoring and optimisation of network operations. Stakeholders include utility operators, municipal authorities, and service recipients. The objective is to improve reliability, reduce losses, and optimise maintenance through better visibility of network performance. The system integrates hydraulic models with real-time sensor data collected across the network, including flow, pressure, and load information, alongside data on consumers and infrastructure assets. Analytical tools process this information to support dynamic resource allocation, predictive maintenance, and operational planning. According to official innovation mapping documentation, the project aligns with international smart grid trends and is being implemented incrementally, combining pilot deployments with gradual scaling. Expected impacts include lower operational costs, improved service continuity, faster fault detection, and more efficient use of water resources, supporting long-term sustainability of public utility networks.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION|S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-396,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00389,EU_PSTW_202609_00389,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Lupine,Street lighting.,The operational challenge is that conventional lighting systems are often switched according to fixed schedules and provide limited real-time information on individual lamp performance. Continuous monitoring also allows technical teams to detect failures more quickly and plan maintenance before problems become more costly.,Lupine Intelligent lighting system,Municipality of Lupine; Local Government; Street lighting.; Poland,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Municipalities must provide safe and reliable street lighting while limiting energy consumption, maintenance costs and response times when failures occur. Lupine Intelligent lighting system introduces centralised control of street lighting based on motion sensors and sensors that analyse weather conditions, allowing the municipality to adapt lighting more precisely to local needs. The operational challenge is that conventional lighting systems are often switched according to fixed schedules and provide limited real-time information on individual lamp performance. In this case, each lamp can be individually controlled and monitored, enabling different switching times and more flexible lighting patterns. The technological implementation combines sensor-based detection, central management software and remote monitoring of luminaire parameters. Motion and weather data inform when lighting should be activated or reduced, while lamps near pedestrian crossings can be switched on earlier and turned off later than others to improve visibility and safety. Continuous monitoring also allows technical teams to detect failures more quickly and plan maintenance before problems become more costly. The expected impact includes significant energy savings, increased safety at pedestrian crossings, fewer interruptions in lighting operation and shorter response times when faults occur. The availability of luminaire-performance data also supports earlier maintenance planning and cost minimisation, creating a more efficient and safety-oriented approach to local lighting management.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-397,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00390,EU_PSTW_202609_00390,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Tarnow,Public order and safety n.e.c.,"Existing monitoring mechanisms provide limited predictive capacity, making it difficult for crisis management services, water management authorities and municipalities to anticipate when and where flooding may occur, and with what intensity.",Development of the Electronic Flood Warning System,Municipality of Tarnow; Local Government; Public order and safety n.e.c.; Poland,"The solution combines sensor networks, weather radar data and hydrological stations with predictive models deployed within the existing warning infrastructure, enabling automated risk assessments and scenario simulations.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The project concerns the expansion of the Electronic Flood Warning System in the Biała Tarnowska River catchment area, where recurring floods and flash floods pose significant risks to residents, infrastructure, agriculture and local authorities. Existing monitoring mechanisms provide limited predictive capacity, making it difficult for crisis management services, water management authorities and municipalities to anticipate when and where flooding may occur, and with what intensity. The objective of the initiative is to strengthen early warning capabilities by delivering reliable, location-specific flood risk forecasts that support timely decision-making, resource allocation and public safety measures. The system integrates advanced data analytics and machine learning models to analyse correlations between historical and real-time meteorological and hydrological data. Supervised learning algorithms are trained on long-term precipitation records, river water level measurements and flood wave dynamics to model patterns leading to river overflow or flash floods. The solution combines sensor networks, weather radar data and hydrological stations with predictive models deployed within the existing warning infrastructure, enabling automated risk assessments and scenario simulations. The enhanced system improves forecasting accuracy and lead time, enabling earlier alerts and more targeted emergency responses. It strengthens operational reliability, reduces potential damage to property and infrastructure, and enhances public safety. Improved scalability and automation lower long-term monitoring costs while increasing resilience in the catchment area.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION|S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-398,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00391,EU_PSTW_202609_00391,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipalities of Gliwice & Poznan,Police Services,"Growing cities need to manage traffic, public transport, emergency movements, parking and traveller information in an integrated way, because fragmented systems can reduce punctuality, safety and network efficiency.",Gliwice & Poznan Integrated Public Transport Management System - ITS,Municipalities of Gliwice & Poznan; Local Government; Police Services; Poland,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Growing cities need to manage traffic, public transport, emergency movements, parking and traveller information in an integrated way, because fragmented systems can reduce punctuality, safety and network efficiency. Gliwice & Poznan Integrated Public Transport Management System brings together several intelligent transport functions to improve mobility management in the municipalities of Gliwice and Poznań. The case addresses multiple operational objectives: giving green-light priority to public transport, prioritising emergency-service vehicles at signalised intersections, informing drivers through road information systems, supporting road users through a mobile application and expanding traffic measurement points with vehicle identification. The technological implementation includes traffic-light priority systems, variable message signs showing journey times, video analytics for intersection monitoring, sensors or detection systems for free parking spaces, meteorological monitoring and pre-selection vehicle weighing at city inlets in Gliwice. Video analytics can automatically inform operators about dangerous road incidents, while traffic and weather data support better management of urban mobility conditions. The expected impact is improved punctuality of public transport, faster emergency-vehicle passage, better information for drivers, stronger incident detection, more efficient parking guidance and improved enforcement of vehicle-weight rules. Overall, the integrated system supports safer, more predictable and more data-informed urban transport management, while giving municipal authorities a broader operational picture of mobility across the city.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-399,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00392,EU_PSTW_202609_00392,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Cracow,General public services n.e.c.,"The administrative issue is that business users often need to navigate dispersed information on registration procedures, municipal services and entrepreneur support, which can slow down decision-making and increase demand for front-office assistance.",Cracow Virtual Clerk,Municipality of Cracow; Local Government; General public services n.e.c.; Poland,"The implementation uses a conversational interface supported by artificial intelligence, allowing users to search for information in the field of entrepreneur services through natural-language interaction rather than manual browsing.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Cracow’s Virtual Clerk was introduced on the new POP website to improve access to information for entrepreneurs and people interested in setting up a business. The administrative issue is that business users often need to navigate dispersed information on registration procedures, municipal services and entrepreneur support, which can slow down decision-making and increase demand for front-office assistance. The Municipality of Cracow therefore launched a virtual clerk to make information retrieval more intuitive, rapid and continuously available. The implementation uses a conversational interface supported by artificial intelligence, allowing users to search for information in the field of entrepreneur services through natural-language interaction rather than manual browsing. At a conceptual level, the tool combines question interpretation, information retrieval, guided assistance and digital service personalisation. Its 24/7 availability is particularly relevant for users who need support outside office hours or who prefer self-service channels. The expected impact is faster access to reliable municipal information, reduced pressure on staff handling repetitive enquiries and a more user-friendly experience for entrepreneurs. By simplifying the first stages of business-related interaction with the municipality, the pilot can contribute to more efficient local economic-service delivery and better support for people seeking to start or manage business activities.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-400,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00393,EU_PSTW_202609_00393,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Poznan,Transport,"The operational problem is that public transport schedules are often affected by traffic conditions, route characteristics, time of day and recurring delays, and manually adjusting timetables may not fully capture these patterns.",AI-based timetables,Municipality of Poznan; Local Government; Transport; Poland,AI-based timetables in the Municipality of Poznań supports the creation of more accurate timetables by using historical data collected from vehicles after completed services. Its technological implementation applies statistical analysis to vehicle data from completed journeys in order to determine optimal travel times for individual sections.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Public transport authorities need timetables that reflect real operating conditions, because planned travel times that do not match actual journey patterns can reduce punctuality and passenger confidence. AI-based timetables in the Municipality of Poznań supports the creation of more accurate timetables by using historical data collected from vehicles after completed services. The operational problem is that public transport schedules are often affected by traffic conditions, route characteristics, time of day and recurring delays, and manually adjusting timetables may not fully capture these patterns. The software enables historical trip data to be projected onto timetable planning, allowing planners to analyse how long vehicles actually take on specific route sections. Its technological implementation applies statistical analysis to vehicle data from completed journeys in order to determine optimal travel times for individual sections. By identifying recurring deviations between planned and actual performance, the system helps create schedules that are better aligned with observed conditions. The expected impact is improved punctuality, more realistic timetable planning, better use of vehicle and driver resources, and a more reliable passenger experience. For the municipality, the system provides a stronger evidence base for transport planning and helps shift timetable design from assumption-based scheduling towards continuous improvement based on operational data.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-401,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00394,EU_PSTW_202609_00394,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Poznan,Transport,"The transport-management problem is that electric buses require reliable scheduling and sufficient charging time at terminal loops, while delays caused by traffic signals can reduce operational efficiency and affect service regularity. By aligning traffic-control infrastructure with the needs of electric fleets, the pilot supports the transition to cleaner public transport while reducing the operational risks associated with battery charging and route delays.",Poznan: Improving the flow of electric bus traffic,Municipality of Poznan; Local Government; Transport; Poland,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Poznań’s electric-bus traffic-flow pilot was designed to support the introduction of electric buses on route No. 174 by improving bus priority at traffic lights. The transport-management problem is that electric buses require reliable scheduling and sufficient charging time at terminal loops, while delays caused by traffic signals can reduce operational efficiency and affect service regularity. The Municipality of Poznań therefore equipped traffic lights along the route with signal receivers from buses and adjusted signal programmes to prioritise bus movement. The implementation combines vehicle-to-infrastructure communication, traffic-signal priority and timetable-sensitive operational planning. Conceptually, buses transmit their presence or approach to the signal system, which can then adapt traffic-light phases to improve journey smoothness and reduce unnecessary waiting time. The objective is not simply to reduce travel time, but also to maximise the time available for vehicle charging at the end loop, which is critical for electric-bus reliability. The expected impact is smoother public transport operation, improved punctuality, better energy-management conditions and stronger passenger service quality. By aligning traffic-control infrastructure with the needs of electric fleets, the pilot supports the transition to cleaner public transport while reducing the operational risks associated with battery charging and route delays.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-402,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00395,EU_PSTW_202609_00395,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Digitization of KPRM,Street lighting.,UNKNOWN,Ioom Intelligent lighting system,Digitization of KPRM; Central-Government; Street lighting.; Poland,"Municipalities need street-lighting systems that reduce energy use and maintenance costs while still providing safe lighting conditions for pedestrians, drivers and public-space users. The wider public value is a more adaptive and safety-oriented lighting service that uses local conditions to guide infrastructure operation.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Municipalities need street-lighting systems that reduce energy use and maintenance costs while still providing safe lighting conditions for pedestrians, drivers and public-space users. Ioom Intelligent lighting system addresses this requirement through centralised street-lighting control based on motion sensors and sensors that analyse weather conditions. The service context is local infrastructure management, where lighting must respond to changing levels of movement, visibility and safety need rather than operating only according to fixed schedules. In the current model, each lamp can be individually controlled and monitored, allowing the responsible authority to define different switching times and lighting behaviours for specific locations. The implementation combines connected luminaires, sensor data, central management software and remote monitoring of lighting parameters. Lamps near pedestrian crossings can be activated earlier and switched off later than surrounding lights, increasing visibility in areas where road-safety risks are higher. Continuous technical monitoring also enables faster detection of failures and supports earlier planning of maintenance work. The expected results include significant energy savings, reduced interruptions in lighting operation, shorter response times when faults occur and lower maintenance costs through better planning. The wider public value is a more adaptive and safety-oriented lighting service that uses local conditions to guide infrastructure operation.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-403,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00396,EU_PSTW_202609_00396,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Wroclaw,Street lighting.,Smart lighting in urban space in Wrocław responds to this challenge through the installation of remotely controlled LED lighting in the WUWA 2 estate.,Smart lighting in urban space,Municipality of Wroclaw; Local Government; Street lighting.; Poland,"Urban residential areas require lighting that improves safety and liveability while limiting electricity costs and making better use of existing infrastructure. The implementation combines LED technology with a remote-control system and traffic-sensitive operation, enabling the lighting intensity to change when the system detects variations in use of the space.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Urban residential areas require lighting that improves safety and liveability while limiting electricity costs and making better use of existing infrastructure. Smart lighting in urban space in Wrocław responds to this challenge through the installation of remotely controlled LED lighting in the WUWA 2 estate. The case focuses on replacing conventional lighting with a more flexible system that can adjust to changes in movement and local conditions, rather than maintaining the same level of illumination throughout the night. The implementation combines LED technology with a remote-control system and traffic-sensitive operation, enabling the lighting intensity to change when the system detects variations in use of the space. This supports safer movement for residents, pedestrians and other users while reducing unnecessary electricity consumption during periods of lower activity. A further technical feature is the permanent power supply to street-lighting circuits, which creates opportunities to use lighting infrastructure for additional urban services. These may include electric-vehicle charging or internet provision, turning lighting poles into multifunctional public assets. The expected impact is lower energy expenditure, improved safety and comfort in the estate, and greater flexibility in how urban infrastructure is used. For the municipality, the project also demonstrates how lighting networks can become a platform for broader smart-city services.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-404,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00397,EU_PSTW_202609_00397,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Wroclaw,Street lighting.,"The operational problem is that traditional street-lighting infrastructure often represents a costly but underused urban asset, while authorities require better data on pedestrian, cyclist and vehicle flows to understand how public space is used.",Wroclaw Intelligent lighting system,Municipality of Wroclaw; Local Government; Street lighting.; Poland,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Cities need to modernise lighting poles so that they do more than illuminate streets, especially where existing infrastructure can support new data services for mobility, safety and urban planning. Wroclaw Intelligent lighting system is a research project aimed at combining the basic function of lighting poles with additional functions designed for residents and municipal management. The operational problem is that traditional street-lighting infrastructure often represents a costly but underused urban asset, while authorities require better data on pedestrian, cyclist and vehicle flows to understand how public space is used. The project therefore adds new sensing and analytical capabilities to lighting infrastructure. Devices installed on selected poles count pedestrians, cyclists and vehicles, including the identification of vehicle types. At the same time, the lighting itself has been replaced with LED luminaires that can adjust light intensity to external conditions. The technological implementation combines traffic-counting devices, adaptive LED lighting and infrastructure-level monitoring, creating a platform that supports both illumination and data collection. The expected impact is improved energy efficiency, better adjustment of lighting to real-world conditions and richer information on mobility patterns in the city. For Wrocław, the system provides a practical testbed for multifunctional urban infrastructure, helping the municipality explore how existing assets can support safer, more responsive and more evidence-based local services.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-405,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00398,EU_PSTW_202609_00398,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Wroclaw,Housing and community amenities n.e.c.,"Residents and visitors often lose time searching for parking spaces in busy urban areas, while municipalities lack precise and timely information on parking availability at street level.",Wroclaw Intelligent parking system,Municipality of Wroclaw; Local Government; Housing and community amenities n.e.c.; Poland,"The expected impact is a better user experience for drivers, reduced unnecessary circulation in search of parking, more efficient use of available spaces and potentially lower congestion and emissions in dense areas.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Residents and visitors often lose time searching for parking spaces in busy urban areas, while municipalities lack precise and timely information on parking availability at street level. Wroclaw Intelligent parking system addresses this problem by developing a prototype that collects data from multiple sources, including sensors and cameras, to estimate the likelihood of finding a free parking space in the area towards which a driver is travelling. The operational objective is to reduce uncertainty for road users and improve the management of scarce parking capacity in the city. The system focuses on recognising how many spaces are occupied or available in a given street area, transforming dispersed observations into information that can be used by residents. Its technological implementation combines physical sensors, camera-based detection and data-processing components that analyse occupancy signals and generate parking-availability estimates. As part of the project, a mobile application is planned to show users both the travel time to a car park and the availability of spaces. The expected impact is a better user experience for drivers, reduced unnecessary circulation in search of parking, more efficient use of available spaces and potentially lower congestion and emissions in dense areas. For the municipality, the system can also provide valuable data for parking policy, infrastructure planning and traffic-management decisions.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-406,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00399,EU_PSTW_202609_00399,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Wroclaw,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.","Residents searching for municipal information can struggle to find the correct answer on public administration websites when procedures, forms and official notices are dispersed across many pages.",Resident's Virtual Advisor,"Municipality of Wroclaw; Local Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Poland","The technological implementation belongs to the field of intelligent customer-interaction solutions and uses artificial-intelligence mechanisms integrated with sound and video functions. The expected impact is faster information retrieval, reduced pressure on municipal staff, more intuitive use of public information resources and greater consistency in how residents are directed to official answers.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Residents searching for municipal information can struggle to find the correct answer on public administration websites when procedures, forms and official notices are dispersed across many pages. Resident's Virtual Advisor helps the Municipality of Wrocław improve access to information on the Public Information Bulletin by guiding users towards the relevant content through a chat-based dialogue system. The service responds to the need to optimise customer-service processes for a large user base, including administrations and thousands of customers who require quick and reliable information. Rather than asking residents to navigate the website independently, the system simulates a conversation with a real person and leads the user to the appropriate answer. The technological implementation belongs to the field of intelligent customer-interaction solutions and uses artificial-intelligence mechanisms integrated with sound and video functions. At a conceptual level, the advisor interprets user queries, identifies the most relevant BIP content and presents guidance through a conversational interface. The service is available online, improving access beyond traditional office interactions. The expected impact is faster information retrieval, reduced pressure on municipal staff, more intuitive use of public information resources and greater consistency in how residents are directed to official answers. For Wrocław, the solution supports a more accessible and user-oriented model of digital municipal administration.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-407,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00400,EU_PSTW_202609_00400,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Katowice,Public order and safety n.e.c.,"Urban safety services need to monitor public spaces effectively and coordinate responses across different operational units, but conventional video surveillance can become difficult to manage when large numbers of cameras produce continuous streams of footage. Katowice Intelligent Monitoring and Analysis System (KISMiA) addresses this challenge by integrating video management, video analytics and operational support into a single monitoring environment for the Municipality of Katowice.",Katowice Intelligent Monitoring and Analysis System (KISMiA),Municipality of Katowice; Local Government; Public order and safety n.e.c.; Poland,"Its expected impact is faster operational awareness, improved coordination, more effective use of video data and stronger support for urban safety management.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Urban safety services need to monitor public spaces effectively and coordinate responses across different operational units, but conventional video surveillance can become difficult to manage when large numbers of cameras produce continuous streams of footage. Katowice Intelligent Monitoring and Analysis System (KISMiA) addresses this challenge by integrating video management, video analytics and operational support into a single monitoring environment for the Municipality of Katowice. The system is built around three components: a Video Materials Management System, a Video Analytics Component and an Operational Support Component. The video management system administers the video data supplied to the platform, while the analytics layer, based on IBM Intelligent Video Analytics, continuously analyses streams and generates descriptive information from each camera. The operational support component is designed to provide the right people with the right information, helping them coordinate actions and follow results. The main integration point is the IOC platform, which gathers all system elements and supports coordination with other integrated systems, including IVA and VMS. With 260 cameras currently in the system, KISMiA enhances the city’s ability to detect relevant events, manage situational information and support decision-making by public services. Its expected impact is faster operational awareness, improved coordination, more effective use of video data and stronger support for urban safety management.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-408,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00401,EU_PSTW_202609_00401,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Lublin,Police Services,"The operational challenge is to provide timely information and improve safety at stops and interchange points, particularly where passengers depend on accurate updates to plan journeys and where public transport infrastructure must be monitored continuously.",Integrated Public Transport Management System - ITS,Municipality of Lublin; Local Government; Police Services; Poland,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Public transport networks require reliable passenger information, safety monitoring and coordination with wider traffic-management systems to maintain service quality and user confidence. Integrated Public Transport Management System in Lublin complements the existing traffic-management system by adding functions that support travellers, operators and municipal transport management. The operational challenge is to provide timely information and improve safety at stops and interchange points, particularly where passengers depend on accurate updates to plan journeys and where public transport infrastructure must be monitored continuously. The system includes an information service for travellers, such as electronic information boards and online systems, as well as safety-monitoring systems installed at stops and transfer hubs. Technologically, it combines digital passenger-information displays, online data services, monitoring devices and integration with the city’s traffic-management environment. These components allow transport information to be communicated more clearly and enable responsible services to observe conditions at key public-transport locations. The expected impact is improved passenger experience, better access to real-time or updated transport information, enhanced safety at stops and hubs, and stronger operational coordination within the urban mobility system. For the municipality, the project supports a more integrated approach to public transport management and helps align passenger services with broader traffic-control capabilities.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-409,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00402,EU_PSTW_202609_00402,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Chojnice,General public services n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,Chojnice Virtual clerk,Municipality of Chojnice; Local Government; General public services n.e.c.; Poland,"The implementation uses a virtual assistant on the bizneschojnice.eu website to answer general questions connected with the poviat, including tourism and business-related information.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Chojnice’s Virtual Clerk was launched through the Biznes Chojnice portal as part of a wider project to promote the poviat as attractive, innovative and investment-friendly. The service problem is that residents, visitors, entrepreneurs and potential investors may require rapid access to information on local matters, tourism and business opportunities, while municipal staff can face repeated enquiries on standard topics. The chatbot is intended to optimise the servicing of large numbers of beneficiaries by providing a digital first point of contact. The implementation uses a virtual assistant on the bizneschojnice.eu website to answer general questions connected with the poviat, including tourism and business-related information. Conceptually, the system combines chatbot interaction, local information retrieval, structured knowledge content and self-service access to administrative or promotional material. The expected impact is faster access to information, greater availability of support, reduced repetitive workload for staff and improved visibility of the local business and tourism offer. By embedding the assistant in an investment and promotion portal, the municipality can also strengthen external communication with potential partners and visitors. The pilot’s value lies in making local government information easier to navigate while supporting economic-development objectives through a more accessible digital channel.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-410,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00403,EU_PSTW_202609_00403,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Rzeszow,Transport,"The mobility challenge is to improve first- and last-mile connectivity between local, long-distance, railway and air stations, where passengers often need convenient transfers across different transport modes.",Introduction of autonomous electric vehicles for urban transport,Municipality of Rzeszow; Local Government; Transport; Poland,"Conceptually, such a system uses sensors, navigation software, control systems and predefined operating routes to move passengers safely between nodes without a conventional driver.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Rzeszów’s autonomous electric vehicle pilot focuses on connecting different transport nodes through driverless public transport vehicles. The mobility challenge is to improve first- and last-mile connectivity between local, long-distance, railway and air stations, where passengers often need convenient transfers across different transport modes. The Municipality of Rzeszów and MPK are associated with the planned introduction of autonomous, driverless electric vehicles that would carry passengers between these transfer points. The implementation is based on autonomous electric shuttle technology, combining vehicle automation, electric propulsion, route planning, passenger-transfer management and integration with the wider urban transport network. Conceptually, such a system uses sensors, navigation software, control systems and predefined operating routes to move passengers safely between nodes without a conventional driver. The expected impact is improved intermodal connectivity, reduced dependence on private cars for short transfer journeys, lower local emissions and a more innovative public transport offer. If successfully tested, the pilot could provide evidence on the feasibility of autonomous shuttles in Polish urban mobility, including their operational reliability, passenger acceptance and integration with existing public transport services. Its long-term value would depend on safety performance, regulatory approval, infrastructure readiness and clear service integration.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-411,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00404,EU_PSTW_202609_00404,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Kielce Technology Park,Foreign economic aid.,UNKNOWN,Chatbot SAM - Virtual Community Assistant,Kielce Technology Park; Local Government; Foreign economic aid.; Poland,"Using Messenger, the virtual assistant is able to precisely locate its user, helping it find an event, office, or specific company, and even lead and navigate inside and outside the building. Using it, tenants have ongoing contact with guardians, and it is easier for them to find business partners.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Chatbot SAM is a solution that is being introduced to the Kielce Technology Park. It enables communication between tenants and guests with KPT employees. SAM runs mainly on the platforms that users know best, such as Facebook Messenger and Facebook. Using Messenger, the virtual assistant is able to precisely locate its user, helping it find an event, office, or specific company, and even lead and navigate inside and outside the building. Using it, tenants have ongoing contact with guardians, and it is easier for them to find business partners. Artificial Intelligence is being used here to present the offer and stimulate the activity of the KTP community.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-412,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00405,EU_PSTW_202609_00405,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Kąty Wrocławskie,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.","The underlying problem is that citizens often need to access information, submit requests, or follow administrative procedures through channels that can be fragmented, time-consuming, or dependent on office opening hours.",Kąty Wrocławskie Virtual clerk,"Municipality of Kąty Wrocławskie; Local Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Poland",UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Kąty Wrocławskie Virtual Clerk is designed to modernise interactions between the municipal administration and residents by expanding digital public services and making routine communication with the office more efficient. The underlying problem is that citizens often need to access information, submit requests, or follow administrative procedures through channels that can be fragmented, time-consuming, or dependent on office opening hours. For the municipality and commune administration, this creates workload pressures and can reduce the quality and timeliness of responses. The main stakeholders are inhabitants of the Kąty Wrocławskie commune, municipal employees, local decision-makers, and service providers supporting the eK@ty Wrocławskie mobile application. The objective is to improve and rationalise administrative work while giving residents a practical digital assistant for local public services. The implementation is based on a mobile application that acts as a citizen-facing service interface, combining digital forms, information access, service notifications, guided support, and communication features. Conceptually, the virtual clerk can be integrated with municipal back-office systems, case-management workflows, resident databases, and e-service portals, enabling users to interact with the administration through a single digital entry point. The expected results include faster handling of standard enquiries, reduced pressure on front-office staff, improved transparency for residents, and better continuity in communication between the office and the citizen. The initiative can also increase the accessibility of local services, support administrative standardisation, and improve satisfaction by making municipal procedures easier to navigate.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-413,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00406,EU_PSTW_202609_00406,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Secretary of State for Energy,Social protection n.e.c.,"The expected administrative benefit was faster processing, standardised eligibility checks and reduced burden on utility companies.",BOSCO - Distribution of financial aid,Secretary of State for Energy; Central-Government; Social protection n.e.c.; Spain,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Energy-price vulnerability requires public authorities to determine quickly and consistently whether households qualify for financial support with electricity bills. BOSCO was introduced in Spain by the Secretary of State for Energy to help electricity suppliers assess eligibility for the bono social eléctrico, a social tariff that reduces bills for eligible consumers. The stated rationale was to simplify the process for applicants and make eligibility checking more efficient for reference electricity suppliers. The implementation is an automated decision-support or rule-processing system that checks whether applicants meet legal and regulatory conditions for the social tariff. At a conceptual level, BOSCO translates eligibility rules into software logic, receives or verifies applicant information and returns an outcome that determines whether the supplier can apply the discount. The expected administrative benefit was faster processing, standardised eligibility checks and reduced burden on utility companies. However, the system became controversial after reports that eligible applicants were wrongly denied support. Civio investigated the problem and requested access to the source code to understand the causes. In 2025, Spain’s Supreme Court ordered the administration to provide access to the BOSCO code, making the case a major example of algorithmic transparency in public-service decision-making. Its significance therefore lies both in welfare automation and in the legal debate over accountability for automated public decisions.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-414,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00407,EU_PSTW_202609_00407,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Local police of Rivas-Vaciamadrid,Police Services,The Pred Crime pilot in Rivas-Vaciamadrid addressed the challenge of supporting local police in anticipating where and when common offences and misdemeanours were more likely to occur. The available information therefore points to limited operational impact in this specific municipality.,Pred Crime Pilot Rivas-Vaciamadrid,Local police of Rivas-Vaciamadrid; Local Government; Police Services; Spain,"The operational objective was to use historical policing information to support prevention-oriented deployment decisions, particularly in relation to offences such as robberies and traffic violations. The pilot was reportedly intended for full implementation during 2016; however, after nine months of testing, the municipality decided not to continue using the software.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Pred Crime pilot in Rivas-Vaciamadrid addressed the challenge of supporting local police in anticipating where and when common offences and misdemeanours were more likely to occur. In 2016, the local police of Rivas-Vaciamadrid, a municipality of around 86,000 inhabitants in the Madrid region, tested the Pred-Crime software developed by the Spanish company EuroCop. The operational objective was to use historical policing information to support prevention-oriented deployment decisions, particularly in relation to offences such as robberies and traffic violations. The emerging technology implementation relied on analytical software that processed historical crime and incident data to identify spatial and temporal patterns associated with different categories of offences. At conceptual level, the system generated predictions on likely locations and timeframes for future incidents, which could then inform police planning and patrol allocation. The pilot was reportedly intended for full implementation during 2016; however, after nine months of testing, the municipality decided not to continue using the software. The available information therefore points to limited operational impact in this specific municipality. While EuroCop states that it has numerous municipal customers across Spain, it is not clear whether those authorities use Pred-Crime or other non-predictive EuroCop tools. The case is therefore particularly relevant as an example of a tested but discontinued predictive policing application, highlighting issues of adoption, validation and long-term suitability.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-415,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00408,EU_PSTW_202609_00408,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Catalan Regional Government,General public services n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,Automated analysis of social media content,Catalan Regional Government; Local Government; General public services n.e.c.; Spain,"During the pilot, the system collected and analysed around 12,000 tweets concerning STEM disciplines in Catalonia, using machine-learning techniques to classify content and identify patterns in public attitudes.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The automated analysis of social media content in Catalonia was introduced to help public authorities better understand public reactions to policy initiatives and communication campaigns. In January 2018, the regional Government of Catalonia launched a pilot to assess the public impact of STEMcat, a policy initiative aimed at promoting scientific and technological vocations among young people. The objective was to move beyond traditional consultation and survey methods by analysing online conversations at scale, while supporting the SmartCAT strategy to position Catalonia as a smart region. The emerging technology implementation used Citibeats, a text-analysis software developed by Social Coin, a Barcelona-based start-up. During the pilot, the system collected and analysed around 12,000 tweets concerning STEM disciplines in Catalonia, using machine-learning techniques to classify content and identify patterns in public attitudes. Authorities reported insights such as different levels of responsiveness among women to messages on natural sciences compared with technology, which were then used to optimise strategy and propose further initiatives. Citibeats had also been used by Barcelona municipality in December 2017 to analyse around 30,000 comments from more than 15,000 people on public transport and mobility, and by Navarra from March 2019 to detect online hate speech across Facebook, Twitter and Instagram. The main reported value is faster, lower-cost analysis of large volumes of online content, although the representativeness of the samples, methodological transparency, oversight mechanisms and actual policy influence remain unclear.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-416,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00409,EU_PSTW_202609_00409,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Spanish Tax Authority,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.","Before the deployment, civil servants received a substantial volume of email enquiries related to VAT issues, creating repetitive workload and slowing down support for taxpayers. The operational context therefore involved a national tax administration seeking to provide more continuous, consistent, and scalable assistance, while reducing the burden on staff responsible for answering technical questions.",IBM Watson: Automated assistance in tax filing in Spain,"Spanish Tax Authority; Central-Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Spain","The Spanish Tax Authority’s use of IBM Watson responds to a specific administrative challenge in VAT filing, particularly for large companies dealing with complex reporting obligations. Reported results indicate a marked operational effect: between July 2017 and February 2018, weekly emails to civil servants on VAT matters fell from 900 to 165, while the assistant’s use rose from around 200 questions per week at launch in July 2017 to approximately 2,000 by November 2017.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Spanish Tax Authority’s use of IBM Watson responds to a specific administrative challenge in VAT filing, particularly for large companies dealing with complex reporting obligations. Before the deployment, civil servants received a substantial volume of email enquiries related to VAT issues, creating repetitive workload and slowing down support for taxpayers. The operational context therefore involved a national tax administration seeking to provide more continuous, consistent, and scalable assistance, while reducing the burden on staff responsible for answering technical questions. The objective was to offer automated support on a defined aspect of VAT filing, allowing companies to obtain guidance outside standard office hours and helping public servants concentrate on more complex cases. The implementation used IBM Watson as an automated assistance tool, applying natural language processing and question-answering capabilities to interpret written queries, retrieve relevant information, and provide responses linked to tax-filing requirements. At system level, the solution would rely on curated tax knowledge, structured guidance, conversational interfaces, and integration with the Tax Authority’s service channels. Public communications emphasised its 24/7 availability and its capacity to absorb repeated enquiries. Reported results indicate a marked operational effect: between July 2017 and February 2018, weekly emails to civil servants on VAT matters fell from 900 to 165, while the assistant’s use rose from around 200 questions per week at launch in July 2017 to approximately 2,000 by November 2017. These figures suggest improved scalability, lower administrative pressure, and greater availability of taxpayer support.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-417,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00410,EU_PSTW_202609_00410,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Logroño municipality and the La Rioja and Cantabria regional governments,Housing and community amenities n.e.c.,Its objective was to support more informed waste-management decisions by combining multiple sources of data rather than relying only on fixed collection schedules or fragmented local knowledge.,REACHOUT: Smart Cities: from smart waste to smart tourism,Logroño municipality and the La Rioja and Cantabria regional governments; Local Government; Housing and community amenities n.e.c.; Spain,"The REACHOUT-related Smart Waste example reflects the broader effort of Spanish local and regional authorities to use data-driven tools to improve municipal service planning. However, the available information does not explain the software’s internal functioning, the exact outputs it produces, or the concrete decisions adopted by authorities as a result of its use.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The REACHOUT-related Smart Waste example reflects the broader effort of Spanish local and regional authorities to use data-driven tools to improve municipal service planning. Waste management is a complex operational area in which authorities must decide where, when and how services are needed, while responding to changing urban patterns, citizen behaviour and resource constraints. The Smart Waste platform was first piloted in 2018 by Logroño municipality and the regional governments of La Rioja and Cantabria. Its objective was to support more informed waste-management decisions by combining multiple sources of data rather than relying only on fixed collection schedules or fragmented local knowledge. The emerging technology implementation was developed by The Circular Lab, the innovation centre of Ecoembes, with support from Minsait, a division of Indra. The platform gathers data from sensors installed in waste bins and collection trucks, and combines these with information from social media, surveys, census sources and satellite data. At conceptual level, the system supports automated analysis and decision-support outputs that help authorities estimate service needs by location and time. The platform is now described as available to local and regional authorities across Spain. Its potential impact includes improved targeting of waste services, better planning, more efficient resource allocation and increased scalability across municipalities. However, the available information does not explain the software’s internal functioning, the exact outputs it produces, or the concrete decisions adopted by authorities as a result of its use.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-418,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00411,EU_PSTW_202609_00411,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Local police in the cities of Kortrijk, Kuurne, and Lendelde (VLAS)",Police Services,"Traditional manual review of surveillance footage can be slow, resource-intensive, and inconsistent, especially where many cameras or long recordings are involved. The expected impact is faster investigation support, improved ability to identify relevant footage, and reduced time spent on manual review.",Briefcam - Smart video surveillance by the local police,"Local police in the cities of Kortrijk, Kuurne, and Lendelde (VLAS); Local Government; Police Services; Belgium","The Briefcam use case concerns the adoption of smart video surveillance by the local police serving Kortrijk, Kuurne, and Lendelede, known as VLAS, from 2019 onwards. The implementation uses Briefcam’s video analytics system, which stores objects detected in footage and classifies them into categories such as people, small vehicles, large vehicles, and animals.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Briefcam use case concerns the adoption of smart video surveillance by the local police serving Kortrijk, Kuurne, and Lendelede, known as VLAS, from 2019 onwards. The operational issue was the need to process large amounts of video footage more efficiently, particularly when police services had to search for people, vehicles, animals, or other objects appearing in recorded images. Traditional manual review of surveillance footage can be slow, resource-intensive, and inconsistent, especially where many cameras or long recordings are involved. The stakeholders include local police forces, municipal authorities, residents, people captured by surveillance systems, and the technology provider. The objective was to make video analysis faster and more searchable for policing and security purposes. The implementation uses Briefcam’s video analytics system, which stores objects detected in footage and classifies them into categories such as people, small vehicles, large vehicles, and animals. The system then performs additional categorisation: for people, it may distinguish between men, women, and children, and further identify visual attributes such as sleeve length, trousers, clothing colour, hats, handbags, or backpacks; comparable categorisation is applied to vehicles. At a conceptual level, the system combines object detection, image classification, metadata extraction, and searchable video indexing. The expected impact is faster investigation support, improved ability to identify relevant footage, and reduced time spent on manual review. However, the use case also raises important issues regarding proportionality, accuracy, transparency, and the monitoring of people in public spaces.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-419,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00412,EU_PSTW_202609_00412,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Leuven,Pre-primary and primary education.,UNKNOWN,Algorithmic school registrations,Municipality of Leuven; Local Government; Pre-primary and primary education.; Belgium,"For secondary schools, some cities use a different algorithm because distance can be discriminatory where not all municipalities have secondary schools; instead, priority students and indicator students are considered, with random allocation based on first choice across three rounds.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Algorithmic school registration systems in Belgian cities emerged in response to persistent problems in allocating school places fairly when demand exceeded capacity, particularly where parents sought admission to the same popular schools. In some cases, families spent nights in tents outside schools to secure early registration, which was widely seen as inequitable and administratively unsustainable. The stakeholders include children, parents, schools, municipalities, education authorities, and communities concerned with equal access to education. The objective was to replace first-come, first-served practices with centralised online registration procedures capable of allocating places according to defined criteria. In primary education, the Leuven system considers variables collected from parents, including the mother’s education level and whether the student receives a grant, to classify children as “indicator” or “non-indicator” students. Further allocation is based on distance between home and school and the stated school preference, with schools able to assign different weights to these two criteria, such as 30%, 50%, or 70%. In Antwerp, distance must receive at least 50% weight. For secondary schools, some cities use a different algorithm because distance can be discriminatory where not all municipalities have secondary schools; instead, priority students and indicator students are considered, with random allocation based on first choice across three rounds. The expected benefits include less queuing, greater procedural order, and more transparent allocation. Yet the system has also produced contested outcomes, such as pupils living near a school not receiving a place while others from farther away were admitted.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-420,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00413,EU_PSTW_202609_00413,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Agency for Upbringing (Agentschap Opgroeien),Health n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,Predictive ADM systems for healthcare and upbringing,Agency for Upbringing (Agentschap Opgroeien); Local Government; Health n.e.c.; Belgium,"This use case brings together two related applications of predictive automated decision-making in Belgium: health risk prediction and possible future support for child-related public services. The implementation described for atrial fibrillation involves a predictive model trained to identify patterns linked to the condition, using health-related data and machine-learning techniques to estimate risk.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"This use case brings together two related applications of predictive automated decision-making in Belgium: health risk prediction and possible future support for child-related public services. In healthcare, the operational problem concerns the early identification of atrial fibrillation, a heart rhythm disorder associated with serious cardiovascular events, including heart attacks. Earlier detection could support preventive care, clinical prioritisation, and better risk management. Stakeholders include patients, clinicians, researchers, healthcare providers, and technology developers such as the Brussels start-up Kantify and the AI Lab at Université Libre de Bruxelles, Iridia. The implementation described for atrial fibrillation involves a predictive model trained to identify patterns linked to the condition, using health-related data and machine-learning techniques to estimate risk. A second, less concrete strand concerns the Flemish Agency for Upbringing, where public discussion indicated interest in using predictive systems to anticipate whether problems may emerge for a particular child, based on existing information. However, the Agency stated that there were no concrete plans at that stage and that it was preparing a policy note. The potential impact in healthcare includes earlier detection, better targeting of interventions, and improved clinical efficiency. In child and family services, possible benefits could include earlier support and more proactive case management, but the absence of a defined implementation makes results uncertain. The use case therefore illustrates both the promise of predictive tools and the need for caution, legal clarity, transparency, and strong safeguards in sensitive domains.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-421,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00414,EU_PSTW_202609_00414,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Danish Tax Authority,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.","Denmark’s automated property valuation initiative addresses a long-standing administrative challenge in tax governance: how to assess property values consistently, efficiently, and at national scale. Property valuation is central to taxation, but manual or fragmented approaches can be slow, costly, uneven, and difficult to update across large numbers of properties.",Taxation and the automatization of property evaluation,"Danish Tax Authority; Central-Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Denmark","The Danish government has therefore sought for several years to centralise and automate parts of tax administration, with property assessment becoming one of the key initiatives. The legal basis is the Ejendomsvurderingslov, the 2017 law on property assessment, which mandates the use of an automated valuation system.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Denmark’s automated property valuation initiative addresses a long-standing administrative challenge in tax governance: how to assess property values consistently, efficiently, and at national scale. Property valuation is central to taxation, but manual or fragmented approaches can be slow, costly, uneven, and difficult to update across large numbers of properties. The Danish government has therefore sought for several years to centralise and automate parts of tax administration, with property assessment becoming one of the key initiatives. The relevant stakeholders include the Danish Tax Authority, property owners, taxpayers, legislators, administrative law experts, and system developers. The objective is to establish a more standardised valuation process capable of calculating property values through a national automated system. The legal basis is the Ejendomsvurderingslov, the 2017 law on property assessment, which mandates the use of an automated valuation system. The planned implementation is expected to use machine learning to develop a statistical predictive model, drawing on property characteristics, market information, geographic variables, and other administrative datasets to estimate values. At a system level, this requires integration with tax databases, cadastral information, valuation workflows, and channels for administrative review. The intended impact is greater scalability, more consistent assessments, lower long-term processing costs, and faster updates. However, the system has faced delays, rising cost concerns, doubts about accuracy, and questions over compatibility with Danish administrative law, meaning that the expected benefits remain dependent on technical reliability and legal robustness.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-422,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00415,EU_PSTW_202609_00415,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Danish Ministry of Education,Education not definable by level.,"Education authorities and institutions sought ways to identify plagiarism, unauthorised collaboration, or manipulation of exam conditions more efficiently than through manual review alone.",Exam control in schools: fighting fraud or profiling students?,Danish Ministry of Education; Central-Government; Education not definable by level.; Denmark,"This use case concerns the use of digital examination-monitoring tools in Denmark to detect suspected exam fraud in schools and universities. One strand involved The Digital Exam Monitor, while another example came from students at Copenhagen University, who developed a machine-learning system for plagiarism detection using around 130,000 student assignments.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"This use case concerns the use of digital examination-monitoring tools in Denmark to detect suspected exam fraud in schools and universities. The operational problem is the need to protect the integrity of assessments while managing large numbers of students and increasingly digital forms of examination. Education authorities and institutions sought ways to identify plagiarism, unauthorised collaboration, or manipulation of exam conditions more efficiently than through manual review alone. However, students, schools, universities, and public authorities became involved in a wider debate about proportionality, privacy, accuracy, and fairness. The implementation relied on digital monitoring and analytical tools designed to observe or assess student behaviour, device activity, and written submissions. One strand involved The Digital Exam Monitor, while another example came from students at Copenhagen University, who developed a machine-learning system for plagiarism detection using around 130,000 student assignments. Such systems can conceptually combine text comparison, behavioural monitoring, statistical similarity detection, and data-driven anomaly identification. The expected impact would be stronger fraud detection, more consistent enforcement, and reduced administrative effort in checking exams. Nevertheless, the use case generated substantial resistance because students considered the tools invasive and potentially discriminatory. Concerns also emerged around the unauthorised use of assignment data, the risk of false positives, and the profiling of particular groups of students.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-423,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00416,EU_PSTW_202609_00416,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Various municipalities in Estonia,Unemployment.,"Young people who are not in education, employment or training can be difficult for municipalities to identify early, especially when information about education, work, residence and social support is held in separate registers.",The Youth Guarantee Support System (YGSS) to identifying young people not in education or employment,Various municipalities in Estonia; Local Government; Unemployment.; Estonia,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Young people who are not in education, employment or training can be difficult for municipalities to identify early, especially when information about education, work, residence and social support is held in separate registers. Estonia’s Youth Guarantee Support System helps municipal case managers identify residents aged 16–26 who may need support to return to education or enter the labour market. The objective is to turn existing administrative data into an outreach mechanism for youth support, while keeping the intervention anchored in municipal casework. The implementation works through the STAR social-services and benefits information system. Twice a year, municipalities that have joined the programme can receive a list of young people registered in their locality who appear, based on data from multiple registers, to be in a NEET situation and potentially in need of help. At a conceptual level, the system combines register-based screening, local-authority access controls, case-manager review, youth contact and consent-based follow-up. Young people can restrict analysis, and where support proceeds, they are contacted and asked to complete a survey before individual assistance is planned. The expected impact is earlier identification of unsupported youth, more proactive municipal outreach, better targeting of employment and education services, and stronger prevention of long-term exclusion. Current European and Estonian policy sources continue to describe youth-guarantee support and NEET-targeted measures as active policy areas.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-424,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00417,EU_PSTW_202609_00417,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Estonian Health Insurance Fund,Health n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,Prediction model for the healthcare needs of patients with chronic illnesses,Estonian Health Insurance Fund; Central-Government; Health n.e.c.; Estonia,"This use case addresses the need to strengthen primary care in Estonia by helping family physicians identify patients with selected chronic conditions who are at higher risk of hospital admission or other adverse health outcomes. The operational challenge is that health systems often hold substantial administrative and billing data but may struggle to use it proactively to support prevention, care planning, and earlier intervention.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"This use case addresses the need to strengthen primary care in Estonia by helping family physicians identify patients with selected chronic conditions who are at higher risk of hospital admission or other adverse health outcomes. The operational challenge is that health systems often hold substantial administrative and billing data but may struggle to use it proactively to support prevention, care planning, and earlier intervention. Stakeholders include the Estonian Health Insurance Fund, family practitioners, patients with chronic illnesses, the World Bank, and international partners contributing comparative data. The objective is to provide a practical prediction model that can support physicians in prioritising patients who may require closer monitoring or tailored care. The implementation draws on medical invoice data held by the Estonian Health Insurance Fund to identify relevant diagnoses and patient characteristics. The pilot also involved Costa Rica, which provided access to its medical billing database to examine clinical and socio-economic data not included in EHIF datasets. At a conceptual level, the model applies predictive analytics to administrative health records, seeking the algorithm that best estimates the likelihood of hospital admission or further health problems. Training and reinforcement were supported through webinars for family practitioners, led by a local pilot coordinator acting as a World Bank consultant. The expected impact is better targeting of primary care, earlier identification of high-risk patients, and improved use of existing health data for prevention-oriented service planning.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-425,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00418,EU_PSTW_202609_00418,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Estonia Unemployment Insurance Fund (EUIF),Unemployment.,"Manual checks can be time-consuming, repetitive, and vulnerable to inconsistent handling, particularly where high volumes of applications are processed.",Checking of data Estonian Unemployment Insurance Fund,Estonia Unemployment Insurance Fund (EUIF); Central-Government; Unemployment.; Estonia,The implementation uses automated background checks across different databases once a citizen registers as unemployed through the Fund’s website.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The initiative was developed to address concerns on the automation of data verification during the registration of unemployed citizens in Estonia. The operational problem is that unemployment registration requires public authorities to validate applicant information against multiple administrative sources before granting formal status and determining the next procedural step. Manual checks can be time-consuming, repetitive, and vulnerable to inconsistent handling, particularly where high volumes of applications are processed. The key stakeholders are unemployed citizens, the Estonian Unemployment Insurance Fund, public databases holding relevant administrative records, and case officers responsible for employment services. The objective is to make registration faster and more standardised by allowing the system to verify the applicant’s data after online submission and determine which document should be sent to the person concerned. The implementation uses automated background checks across different databases once a citizen registers as unemployed through the Fund’s website. Conceptually, the solution combines data-matching, rule-based validation, automated decision workflows, and document-generation mechanisms. It checks whether submitted information is correct, processes the result, and initiates the appropriate communication with the applicant. The expected impact is a more efficient registration process, reduced administrative workload, quicker confirmation for eligible citizens, and more consistent handling of standard cases. By automating routine data checks, the Fund can allocate staff attention to more complex situations requiring human judgement or additional clarification.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK|F06_HIGH_VOLUME_SCALABILITY_BOTTLENECK,S6_WORKFLOW_PROCESS_AUTOMATION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-426,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00419,EU_PSTW_202609_00419,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Estonia Unemployment Insurance Fund (EUIF),Unemployment.,The objective is to accelerate and standardise the calculation of benefit rights while reducing manual workload in routine cases.,Checking entitlement of umemployment aid,Estonia Unemployment Insurance Fund (EUIF); Central-Government; Unemployment.; Estonia,"This use case concerns the use of automated decision-making by Estonia’s Unemployment Insurance Fund to determine what unemployed persons are entitled to receive, including the amount and duration of unemployment aid or unemployment insurance benefits. The implementation uses automated systems to assess entitlement on the basis of available applicant data and predefined legal or administrative criteria.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"This use case concerns the use of automated decision-making by Estonia’s Unemployment Insurance Fund to determine what unemployed persons are entitled to receive, including the amount and duration of unemployment aid or unemployment insurance benefits. The operational problem is that benefit entitlement assessment requires the processing of eligibility rules, personal employment histories, insurance records, and other administrative data, often under time pressure and with direct consequences for citizens’ income security. Stakeholders include unemployed applicants, the Estonian Unemployment Insurance Fund, public employment-service staff, and the wider social protection system. The objective is to accelerate and standardise the calculation of benefit rights while reducing manual workload in routine cases. The implementation uses automated systems to assess entitlement on the basis of available applicant data and predefined legal or administrative criteria. At a conceptual level, the system likely combines data retrieval from official registers, rule-based eligibility checks, automated calculation modules, and case-routing functions for decisions requiring further review. The information provided indicates that 50% of these decisions are made entirely through automated means. The expected impact includes faster processing, improved administrative scalability, and more uniform application of entitlement rules. At the same time, because the decisions affect access to financial support, the use case raises important requirements around explainability, contestability, accuracy, and human oversight, especially where applicants may need to challenge or correct the data used in the assessment.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-427,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00420,EU_PSTW_202609_00420,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Eksote,Health n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,Automatic identification of individual risk factors in social care and healthcare,Eksote; Local Government; Health n.e.c.; Finland,"Social exclusion among young adults can arise from interacting health, social, educational and economic factors, making it difficult for professionals to identify early patterns using service records in isolation. The operational problem was to understand which combinations of service-use patterns, health records or social-care indicators were associated with later adverse outcomes, so that welfare professionals could improve prevention.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Social exclusion among young adults can arise from interacting health, social, educational and economic factors, making it difficult for professionals to identify early patterns using service records in isolation. Eksote, the South Karelia social and healthcare district, worked with Fujitsu on an AI model to identify factors associated with social exclusion and related risks. The operational problem was to understand which combinations of service-use patterns, health records or social-care indicators were associated with later adverse outcomes, so that welfare professionals could improve prevention. The implementation used pseudonymised data from Eksote services and machine-learning methods to identify risk factors defined by professionals. At a conceptual level, the model analyses historical service data, identifies statistical relationships with social-exclusion outcomes and produces an overview of factors associated with increased risk. Its expected value is to support service planning, professional awareness and research on early intervention. Importantly, the system was constrained by Finnish rules on secondary and combined use of healthcare data: the model could not be used to identify individual young adults at risk without additional legal or consent arrangements. The case is therefore best framed as a modelling and risk-factor analysis project rather than a fully operational individual risk-identification system.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-428,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00421,EU_PSTW_202609_00421,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Eksote,Family and children.,"In South Karelia, Eksote developed a predictive welfare model to help social, health and education professionals identify children and young people who might need support before difficulties became more severe. The operational problem was that warning signs are often dispersed across maternity clinics, kindergartens, schools, healthcare, mental healthcare and social services, making it difficult for authorities to intervene early and coordinate support for families.",Predicting youth problems - Identifying youth problems and providing support,Eksote; Local Government; Family and children.; Finland,"However, the use case also raised serious questions about proportionality, data quality, family profiling and whether administrative indicators can reliably capture complex social and educational needs.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"In South Karelia, Eksote developed a predictive welfare model to help social, health and education professionals identify children and young people who might need support before difficulties became more severe. The operational problem was that warning signs are often dispersed across maternity clinics, kindergartens, schools, healthcare, mental healthcare and social services, making it difficult for authorities to intervene early and coordinate support for families. The objective was to detect emerging risks linked to school performance, attendance, behavioural issues, psychiatric care, custody decisions and substance abuse, so that preventive action could be taken sooner. The implementation used a model developed with the Finnish IT firm Avaintec, combining administrative data from several public-service systems and analysing predefined undesirable outcomes. It identified 1,340 risk factors, including examples such as dental problems, missed maternity-clinic appointments by parents and bullying behaviour among siblings. At a conceptual level, the system used cross-sector data integration, pattern detection and risk-factor modelling to follow indicators at individual level. The expected value was earlier identification of children requiring support, better coordination between services and more targeted preventive intervention. However, the use case also raised serious questions about proportionality, data quality, family profiling and whether administrative indicators can reliably capture complex social and educational needs.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-429,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00422,EU_PSTW_202609_00422,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Nice and Marseille,Education n.e.c.,"The intended objective was to replace or complement existing manual checks at the building entrance with a faster identity-verification process. The expected benefits included shorter queues, more consistent access control, reduced administrative burden for staff, and improved perceived security.",Face recognition at schools - Face recognition at the building’s entrance,Nice and Marseille; Local Government; Education n.e.c.; France,"The use case concerns a proposed access-control system for selected high schools in Nice and Marseille, where local authorities sought to manage entry to school buildings more efficiently while strengthening perimeter security and monitoring the flow of pupils. In conceptual terms, the system would have compared live facial images captured at entry points against enrolled biometric templates associated with authorised pupils, using image-processing models, biometric matching algorithms, controlled access infrastructure, and secure storage mechanisms.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The use case concerns a proposed access-control system for selected high schools in Nice and Marseille, where local authorities sought to manage entry to school buildings more efficiently while strengthening perimeter security and monitoring the flow of pupils. The operational issue was centred on verifying that only authorised students could enter school premises, with school administrations, municipal authorities, students, parents, and technology providers all affected by the initiative. The intended objective was to replace or complement existing manual checks at the building entrance with a faster identity-verification process. The proposed implementation relied on facial recognition technology installed at school entrances, under a programme to be funded and delivered by Cisco. In conceptual terms, the system would have compared live facial images captured at entry points against enrolled biometric templates associated with authorised pupils, using image-processing models, biometric matching algorithms, controlled access infrastructure, and secure storage mechanisms. Integration would likely have involved entry gates, local school databases, and identity-management workflows. The expected benefits included shorter queues, more consistent access control, reduced administrative burden for staff, and improved perceived security. However, the project was placed on hold after the French data protection authority considered it illegal, underlining that operational efficiency gains could not outweigh concerns around proportionality, consent, children’s rights, and compliance with data protection rules.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,NOT_IN_USE,미사용,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-430,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00423,EU_PSTW_202609_00423,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Bundeskriminalamt (Federal Crime Agency, BKA)",Police Services,UNKNOWN,RADAAR-iTE Predictive policing - risk assessment of militant Salafitsts,"Bundeskriminalamt (Federal Crime Agency, BKA); Central-Government; Police Services; Germany","The implementation is based on a standardised questionnaire completed by a caseworker using observable behaviour and data already gathered by the police, as well as information the police are legally authorised to access. However, the use case also requires careful safeguards due to its sensitivity, including legality, proportionality, non-discrimination, and accountability for decisions based on risk categorisation.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"RADAR-iTE is a German risk-assessment tool used to support police evaluation of persons already known to the authorities in relation to militant Salafist extremism. The operational problem is that security agencies must prioritise attention and resources across individuals who may present different levels of threat, while ensuring that assessments are structured, documented, and comparable. Stakeholders include the Bundeskriminalamt, caseworkers, other security authorities, assessed individuals, and the research partner involved in development. The objective is to classify known persons into threat levels that can inform subsequent operational decisions. The implementation is based on a standardised questionnaire completed by a caseworker using observable behaviour and data already gathered by the police, as well as information the police are legally authorised to access. The system then produces a categorisation into three levels: high, conspicuous, or moderate. Conceptually, RADAR-iTE functions as a structured professional judgement and risk-scoring tool, combining behavioural indicators, case information, and predefined assessment criteria rather than acting as a fully autonomous policing system. Once the threat level is generated, the responsible caseworker or department decides what action should follow. The expected impact is greater consistency in risk classification, clearer prioritisation of security resources, and more systematic documentation of assessments. However, the use case also requires careful safeguards due to its sensitivity, including legality, proportionality, non-discrimination, and accountability for decisions based on risk categorisation.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-432,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00424,EU_PSTW_202609_00424,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Baden Wurttemberg, Hesse, Berlin and North Rhine-Westphalia",Police Services,PRECOBS and related predictive policing systems in German administrations address the challenge of preventing burglary and other property crime through more targeted operational planning.,PRECOBS - Predictive policing in German administrations,"Baden Wurttemberg, Hesse, Berlin and North Rhine-Westphalia; Regional Government; Police Services; Germany","Several German Länder and cities have tested or deployed tools designed to identify locations where break-ins are more likely to occur, allowing police forces to incorporate forecasts into prevention and patrol strategies. Similar approaches have been adopted elsewhere: Hesse uses KLB-operativ, Berlin uses KrimPro, North Rhine-Westphalia developed SKALA based on IBM products, and Lower Saxony uses PreMAP.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"PRECOBS and related predictive policing systems in German administrations address the challenge of preventing burglary and other property crime through more targeted operational planning. Several German Länder and cities have tested or deployed tools designed to identify locations where break-ins are more likely to occur, allowing police forces to incorporate forecasts into prevention and patrol strategies. In Bavaria, PRECOBS, developed by the Institut für musterbasierte Prognosetechnik, has been used to calculate areas with higher predicted burglary risk. Similar approaches have been adopted elsewhere: Hesse uses KLB-operativ, Berlin uses KrimPro, North Rhine-Westphalia developed SKALA based on IBM products, and Lower Saxony uses PreMAP. The emerging technology implementation is based on analytical systems that process historical crime data and related contextual variables to identify recurring spatial and temporal patterns. These systems generate forecast areas or hot spots, which police authorities can use in action plans and resource deployment. The impact is intended to be more proactive burglary prevention, better allocation of police capacity and improved operational situational awareness. However, implementation outcomes vary across jurisdictions. In Baden-Württemberg, use of PRECOBS was discontinued in 2019 due to data-quality issues, showing that predictive policing tools depend heavily on the reliability, completeness and suitability of the underlying data. The case therefore illustrates both the operational promise and the governance limitations of algorithmic forecasting in police work.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-433,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00425,EU_PSTW_202609_00425,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"the Zentral- und Ansprechstelle Cybercrime (Contact Office for Cyber Crime, ZAC NRW)",Police Services,"Investigations into child sexual abuse material impose a severe workload and psychological burden on investigators, who may need to review large numbers of harmful images while identifying victims, suspects and links between cases. The operational objective was to reduce manual exposure to harmful material and accelerate the identification of relevant evidence, while complying with strict rules on data protection and child protection.",AI to combat child pornography online,"the Zentral- und Ansprechstelle Cybercrime (Contact Office for Cyber Crime, ZAC NRW); Regional Government; Police Services; Germany",Public material from North Rhine-Westphalia describes the work as a research cooperation at ZAC NRW using AI analysis methods to combat child sexual abuse material.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Investigations into child sexual abuse material impose a severe workload and psychological burden on investigators, who may need to review large numbers of harmful images while identifying victims, suspects and links between cases. North Rhine-Westphalia’s cybercrime contact office, ZAC NRW, collaborated with Microsoft and researchers to explore AI-supported analysis methods for this investigative context. The operational objective was to reduce manual exposure to harmful material and accelerate the identification of relevant evidence, while complying with strict rules on data protection and child protection. The implementation used software to anonymise or protect data before analysis, with cloud-based algorithms examining images, detecting illegal content indicators and supporting comparison with profiles or known material. At a conceptual level, the system combines content classification, face or feature matching, secure data handling and investigative review workflows. The technology is intended to support police and prosecutors by triaging and structuring evidence, not to replace human legal assessment or investigative responsibility. The expected impact is faster case analysis, reduced psychological strain for officers, better detection of recurring victims or perpetrators and more efficient allocation of specialist investigative resources. Public material from North Rhine-Westphalia describes the work as a research cooperation at ZAC NRW using AI analysis methods to combat child sexual abuse material.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-434,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00426,EU_PSTW_202609_00426,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Ministry of Justice of the State of North Rhine-Westphalia,Public order and safety n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,Suicide Prevention in Jail - Detecting of suspicious objects in a cell,Ministry of Justice of the State of North Rhine-Westphalia; Local Government; Public order and safety n.e.c.; Germany,The implementation uses video analytics and object-recognition techniques to monitor defined visual indicators associated with suicide risk.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"North Rhine-Westphalia’s prison suicide-prevention programme was launched to reduce suicide risk among detainees classified as medium to high risk. The operational problem is that conventional in-person checks at 15-minute intervals can be resource-intensive and may increase emotional strain on inmates, while prison staff still need reliable ways to detect dangerous objects or situations promptly. The Ministry of Justice of North Rhine-Westphalia commissioned FusionSystems GmbH to build a video surveillance system able to identify suspicious objects in a cell, such as a knotted rope or a firelighter, and alert officers on duty. The implementation uses video analytics and object-recognition techniques to monitor defined visual indicators associated with suicide risk. Conceptually, the system combines camera-based observation, automated object detection, risk-specific alerting and staff response workflows. The expected impact is earlier warning of potential self-harm situations, reduced need for intrusive repeated checks and better allocation of staff attention towards cases requiring intervention. However, because the system operates in a prison environment and concerns highly vulnerable individuals, it requires strict safeguards around privacy, necessity, accuracy, proportionality and human oversight. Its value depends on whether automated alerts can improve safety without creating excessive surveillance or false reassurance.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-435,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00427,EU_PSTW_202609_00427,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Federal Foreign Office,R&D public order and safety.,UNKNOWN,PRE-VIEW - Identification of international crisis,Federal Foreign Office; Central-Government; R&D public order and safety.; Germany,"Germany’s Federal Foreign Office uses PRE-VIEW, short for Prediction, Visualization, Early Warning, as a data-analytics tool to support crisis early warning and stabilisation planning.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Foreign ministries need early signals of instability so that diplomatic, stabilisation and humanitarian responses can be planned before crises escalate. Germany’s Federal Foreign Office uses PRE-VIEW, short for Prediction, Visualization, Early Warning, as a data-analytics tool to support crisis early warning and stabilisation planning. The operational problem is that political, social, economic and conflict-related developments produce large volumes of open data, but analysts need structured insight rather than unfiltered information. The implementation analyses publicly available data on current trends and conflicts, then produces maps, infographics and trend analyses for officials working on international stabilisation and crisis engagement. At a conceptual level, PRE-VIEW combines data collection, machine-learning or AI-supported processing, conflict indicators, geospatial visualisation and analyst-facing dashboards. The system helps civil servants understand how a conflict or political situation is evolving and what developments may require attention. Its outputs support, rather than determine, decisions about next steps in foreign-policy and stabilisation work. The expected impact is earlier identification of emerging risks, better situational awareness, more evidence-based crisis engagement and stronger prioritisation of preventive action. The case fits within the broader international field of AI-supported crisis early warning, where specialised models and integration into decision processes are increasingly emphasised.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-436,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00428,EU_PSTW_202609_00428,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Bundesamt für Migration und Flüchtlinge (Federal Office for Migration and Refugees, BAMF)",Social protection n.e.c.,"The implementation includes software capable of extracting and analysing data from mobile phones, tablets, or laptops when access is requested, presenting employees with a limited overview of device content and language patterns found in text.",Identity Check of Migrants - Analysis of content and language of phones,"Bundesamt für Migration und Flüchtlinge (Federal Office for Migration and Refugees, BAMF); Central-Government; Social protection n.e.c.; Germany","This use case concerns the use of automated text, speech, and device-analysis tools by Germany’s Federal Office for Migration and Refugees to support identity checks in asylum procedures. However, the use case involves significant risks relating to consent, privacy, evidential reliability, linguistic accuracy, and the potential consequences of automated indications in asylum decisions.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"This use case concerns the use of automated text, speech, and device-analysis tools by Germany’s Federal Office for Migration and Refugees to support identity checks in asylum procedures. The operational problem is that applicants may arrive without valid identity documents, requiring authorities to assess claims about country or region of origin through alternative evidence. Stakeholders include asylum seekers, BAMF employees, migration authorities, technology providers, legal representatives, and civil society organisations concerned with procedural rights and privacy. The objective is to provide officials with additional indications that may support verification of an applicant’s stated background. The implementation includes software capable of extracting and analysing data from mobile phones, tablets, or laptops when access is requested, presenting employees with a limited overview of device content and language patterns found in text. A separate tool analyses a two-minute voice recording in which the applicant describes a picture in their mother tongue, calculating how closely the speech matches a particular dialect. Conceptually, the system combines mobile forensics, text recognition, language detection, speech analysis, and probabilistic dialect classification. The expected administrative value is faster access to supporting information in cases where documentary evidence is missing. However, the use case involves significant risks relating to consent, privacy, evidential reliability, linguistic accuracy, and the potential consequences of automated indications in asylum decisions.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S3_DOCUMENT_INTELLIGENCE,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-437,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00429,EU_PSTW_202609_00429,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Agency for Labor, Social Affairs, Family and Integration",Family and children.,UNKNOWN,JUS-IT - Child Care Service,"Agency for Labor, Social Affairs, Family and Integration; Local Government; Family and children.; Germany","The objective is to support case administration and automate certain payment-related processes within the Agency for Labour, Social Affairs, Family and Integration.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"JUS-IT is a case-management and administrative software system used in Hamburg for the General Social Service, the Child Care Service, and the Economic Help for Youths Programme. The operational problem is that child and youth welfare services require the coordinated handling of sensitive cases, payments, records, and inter-agency information flows, often involving vulnerable children, families, social workers, police information, and health insurance data. The objective is to support case administration and automate certain payment-related processes within the Agency for Labour, Social Affairs, Family and Integration. The implementation is based on Cúram, a modular off-the-shelf IBM product that can be adapted to specific welfare-administration needs. At a conceptual level, the system combines case records, workflow management, payment automation, data interfaces, and administrative reporting functions. Its interfaces connect to police reports and health insurance funds, enabling caseworkers to draw on external information sources within welfare-related procedures. The expected benefits include more centralised case handling, reduced duplication of administrative tasks, improved continuity of records, and more efficient payment administration. Nevertheless, the system appears to have faced serious concerns regarding performance, suitability, or governance, since a major revision or complete shutdown was recommended in 2019. In a child welfare context, these issues are especially significant because system reliability, data quality, and clear accountability directly affect sensitive decisions and service delivery.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-438,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00430,EU_PSTW_202609_00430,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Federal Labor Agency,Unemployment.,UNKNOWN,ALLEGRO - administer unemployment benefit,Federal Labor Agency; Central-Government; Unemployment.; Germany,The Bundesagentur für Arbeit (Federal Labor Agency) uses an IT-system called ALLEGRO to administer unemployment benefit.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Bundesagentur für Arbeit (Federal Labor Agency) uses an IT-system called ALLEGRO to administer unemployment benefit. The agency workers input an applicants’ data and the system calculates the corresponding benefit levels. The system can connect to health insurance and pension funds and cooperate with customs and the Central Register of Foreign Nationals (Ausländerzentralregister) (Deutscher Bundestag, 2018). In 2012, ALLEGRO replaced the administration’s previous software, called A2LL, which was an error prone system developed by T-Systems. The new software was developed in-house at the Federal Labor Agency (Borchers, 2008).",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-439,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00431,EU_PSTW_202609_00431,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Cities of Singen and Weima,Social protection n.e.c.,"Its value lies in creating a more consistent approach to risk identification, particularly where fragmented information or subjective interpretation could otherwise affect decision-making.",DyRiAS threat levels of abusive male partners,Cities of Singen and Weima; Local Government; Social protection n.e.c.; Germany,"In Singen and Weimar, women’s protection centres use DyRiAS-Intimpartner to support the assessment of risks posed by abusive male partners. The implementation relies on a specialised risk-assessment tool for intimate partner violence, likely using standardised questions, behavioural indicators, case information, and scoring rules to generate a threat classification.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"In Singen and Weimar, women’s protection centres use DyRiAS-Intimpartner to support the assessment of risks posed by abusive male partners. The operational need arises in domestic violence contexts, where professionals must identify whether a perpetrator may represent an escalating threat and determine what protective measures may be required for the victim. The main stakeholders include women experiencing intimate partner violence, protection-centre staff, local authorities, police or support services that may act on the assessment, and the individuals being assessed. The objective is to provide a structured method for evaluating threat levels so that practitioners can prioritise cases, document risk factors, and coordinate appropriate responses. The implementation relies on a specialised risk-assessment tool for intimate partner violence, likely using standardised questions, behavioural indicators, case information, and scoring rules to generate a threat classification. Rather than replacing professional judgement, the system appears to support staff by organising relevant data and producing an assessment that can inform further action. Its value lies in creating a more consistent approach to risk identification, particularly where fragmented information or subjective interpretation could otherwise affect decision-making. The expected impact is earlier recognition of serious threats, better allocation of protective resources, and more systematic support for victims. At the same time, such a tool requires strong safeguards, because errors or over-reliance on automated risk categories could have serious consequences for safety, rights, and accountability.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-440,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00432,EU_PSTW_202609_00432,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Federal State Education Agency,Public order and safety n.e.c.,"The problem addressed is the difficulty of identifying credible warning signs in highly sensitive situations where schools and authorities must act quickly, but also avoid disproportionate or discriminatory responses.",DyRiAS potential to go on a school shooting rampage.,Federal State Education Agency; Central-Government; Public order and safety n.e.c.; Germany,"Within the Federal State Education Agency of Saxony-Anhalt, DyRiAS-Schule is deployed to help assess whether a student may present a serious threat of school violence, including the potential to carry out a shooting rampage. It can be understood as a structured risk-analysis instrument that organises case data, applies predefined criteria, and produces a threat-level assessment for professional review.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Within the Federal State Education Agency of Saxony-Anhalt, DyRiAS-Schule is deployed to help assess whether a student may present a serious threat of school violence, including the potential to carry out a shooting rampage. The problem addressed is the difficulty of identifying credible warning signs in highly sensitive situations where schools and authorities must act quickly, but also avoid disproportionate or discriminatory responses. Stakeholders include students, school staff, education authorities, families, psychological or security professionals, and the assessed individuals themselves. The purpose of the system is to support threat management by giving education authorities a structured framework for evaluating risk signals and deciding whether preventive intervention is required. Technically, the tool appears to operate through a standardised assessment process focused on observable behaviour, contextual information, and indicators associated with severe targeted violence. It can be understood as a structured risk-analysis instrument that organises case data, applies predefined criteria, and produces a threat-level assessment for professional review. The intended impact is to improve consistency in how schools and authorities interpret warning signs, reduce the likelihood of overlooked high-risk cases, and support earlier coordination of prevention measures. Its use may also help document why certain interventions were considered or initiated. However, because the system concerns minors and potentially life-altering assessments, its deployment requires careful governance, transparent procedures, proportionality, and safeguards against stigmatisation or inaccurate labelling.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-441,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00433,EU_PSTW_202609_00433,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Hellenic Ministry of Defense,Public order and safety n.e.c.,"The ROBORDER project was designed to address border-surveillance challenges that require persistent monitoring across complex land, sea and air environments. Its expected impact was to improve situational awareness, extend surveillance reach, reduce the need for continuous human presence in difficult environments and enhance the scalability of border-monitoring operations.",ROBORDER project,Hellenic Ministry of Defense; Central-Government; Public order and safety n.e.c.; Greece,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The ROBORDER project was designed to address border-surveillance challenges that require persistent monitoring across complex land, sea and air environments. The Hellenic Ministry of Defence participated in the consortium alongside the Centre for Research and Technology Hellas, the National and Kapodistrian University of Athens and partners from twelve other countries: Portugal, the United Kingdom, Romania, Germany, Italy, Switzerland, Bulgaria, Spain, Hungary, Belgium, Finland and Estonia. The objective was to deliver a fully functional autonomous border-surveillance system capable of supporting authorities in detecting and monitoring events across different border contexts. The emerging technology implementation consisted of an autonomous swarm of heterogeneous unmanned vehicles, including aerial, water-surface, underwater and ground robots. These vehicles were intended to operate both independently and collectively in coordinated swarms, combining multiple sensing platforms and mobility modes into a single surveillance architecture. At conceptual level, the system integrates robotics, autonomous navigation, sensor fusion and command-and-control functions to provide border authorities with wider coverage and more flexible monitoring capabilities. The project started in May 2017 and was scheduled to conclude in February 2021. Its expected impact was to improve situational awareness, extend surveillance reach, reduce the need for continuous human presence in difficult environments and enhance the scalability of border-monitoring operations. The case remains primarily a research and development initiative, with impact linked to prototype delivery and demonstration rather than confirmed routine operational deployment.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-442,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00434,EU_PSTW_202609_00434,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Trikala,Transport,The AVINT project in Trikala responded to the challenge of integrating automated mobility into an existing urban transport environment.,AVINT Trikala - Integration of a line of automated buses,City of Trikala; Local Government; Transport; Greece,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The AVINT project in Trikala responded to the challenge of integrating automated mobility into an existing urban transport environment. The City of Trikala participated in AVINT, Autonomous Vehicles Integrated within the Urban Context, to study how automated buses could operate safely and usefully within the city’s transportation network. The planned service consisted of three automated buses, each carrying up to 10 passengers, connecting Trikala city centre with the university campus. The objective was to assess the feasibility, safety and service value of autonomous public transport in a real urban setting, while exploring how such vehicles interact with passengers, road users, infrastructure and municipal transport planning. The emerging technology implementation centred on automated electric buses operating on a defined route within the city. At conceptual level, the system required integration between vehicle automation, route planning, traffic management, passenger service design and local operational supervision. The project started in January 2019 and was scheduled for completion in December 2021. Its expected impact included testing the reliability of automated buses in everyday urban conditions, improving knowledge of regulatory and operational requirements, and supporting future decisions on smart mobility deployment. For the municipality, the project offered a controlled environment to evaluate whether autonomous shuttles could complement existing public transport, provide a low-capacity link between key locations, and contribute to a broader transition towards innovative and sustainable urban mobility.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-443,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00435,EU_PSTW_202609_00435,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Hellenic Police,Police Services,"The operational challenge was to enable officers to check large volumes of moving vehicles more rapidly than would be possible through manual observation, particularly in situations where police cars need to operate across roads and public spaces.",ANPR Greece,Hellenic Police; Central-Government; Police Services; Greece,"The system can also reduce reliance on manual checks, though its use requires appropriate data-retention, accuracy, and oversight safeguards.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Greece introduced Automatic Number Plate Recognition cameras in 2019 to strengthen police capacity to identify vehicles linked to theft or criminal activity. The operational challenge was to enable officers to check large volumes of moving vehicles more rapidly than would be possible through manual observation, particularly in situations where police cars need to operate across roads and public spaces. The relevant stakeholders include the Hellenic Police, vehicle owners, road users, victims of vehicle-related crime, and public authorities responsible for law enforcement and traffic security. The objective is to provide mobile police units with a rapid detection capability that can compare observed licence plates against relevant records and alert officers when a match is found. The implementation consists of ANPR cameras installed on 50 Hellenic Police vehicles. These cameras capture licence plate images, convert them into readable alphanumeric data, and compare the results with databases of stolen vehicles or vehicles associated with criminal offences. The system is capable of recognising 15,000 licence plates per hour and can detect plates on vehicles travelling above 170 km per hour. Future functionality was expected to include identification of uninsured vehicles. The impact is increased operational reach for police patrols, faster detection of vehicles of interest, and improved capacity to act in real time. The system can also reduce reliance on manual checks, though its use requires appropriate data-retention, accuracy, and oversight safeguards.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK|F06_HIGH_VOLUME_SCALABILITY_BOTTLENECK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-444,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00436,EU_PSTW_202609_00436,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Hellenic Police and the Hellenic Ministry of Citizen Protection,Police Services,"The operational problem was that officers needed mobile access to identity, document and database-verification capabilities, while existing checks could require slower, less integrated procedures.",Smart policing software - Development an integrated information system for police,Hellenic Police and the Hellenic Ministry of Citizen Protection; Central-Government; Police Services; Greece,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Greece’s Smart Policing project was conceived as an integrated information system to support police checks in real time during stops and field operations. The operational problem was that officers needed mobile access to identity, document and database-verification capabilities, while existing checks could require slower, less integrated procedures. Under a €4 million contract signed in 2019, Intracom Telecom was to develop and supply a system for the Hellenic Police and the Ministry of Citizen Protection, including 1,000 portable devices and 500 external fingerprint scanners. The planned implementation combined facial recognition, automated fingerprint identification, document authentication, vehicle and object identification, and complex searches across existing databases. Conceptually, the system linked portable biometric devices, identity-verification workflows, document scanning, police databases and real-time mobile checks. The expected operational impact was faster field identification, more integrated access to law-enforcement information and greater efficiency during police stops. However, the project raised significant legality and data-protection concerns because it involved biometric processing in public policing contexts. In 2026, the Greek Data Protection Authority warned that activating the system’s biometric functions would constitute unlawful data processing, following an investigation initiated after a complaint by Homo Digitalis.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-445,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00437,EU_PSTW_202609_00437,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Hellenic Police,Police Services,"The SPIRIT project tackles the operational challenge faced by law-enforcement authorities when attempting to resolve identities and connect information across multiple investigative sources. The objective was to support intelligence analysis by enabling investigators to correlate fragmented information, including material from social media and other sources relevant to criminal investigations, while framing the project around privacy-preserving analysis.",SPIRIT predictive policing - privacy-preserving intelligence analysis for resolving identities,Hellenic Police; Central-Government; Police Services; Greece,"At the same time, the case raises important governance questions around personal data, transparency, oversight and safeguards in law-enforcement use of identity-resolution technologies.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The SPIRIT project tackles the operational challenge faced by law-enforcement authorities when attempting to resolve identities and connect information across multiple investigative sources. The Hellenic Police participated alongside the West Midlands Police, the Police and Crime Commissioner for Thames Valley, the Serbian Ministry of Interior and the Police Academy in Szczytno. The objective was to support intelligence analysis by enabling investigators to correlate fragmented information, including material from social media and other sources relevant to criminal investigations, while framing the project around privacy-preserving analysis. The emerging technology implementation included tools for face extraction and matching, social-media data correlation and complex associative searches across different information sources. At conceptual level, the system aimed to connect identity-related signals and support investigators in identifying relationships, matches or links that would be difficult to detect manually. Greek trials involved the Hellenic Police Border Unit and were scheduled between January and August 2020, with deliverables expected in April 2020 and January 2021. Two Greek private companies, Singular Logic and Nydor System Technologies, were members of the consortium. The project was due to end in July 2021. Its expected impact was to improve investigative efficiency, support cross-source intelligence analysis and reduce manual processing burdens. At the same time, the case raises important governance questions around personal data, transparency, oversight and safeguards in law-enforcement use of identity-resolution technologies.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-446,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00438,EU_PSTW_202609_00438,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Vimercate,Hospital services.,UNKNOWN,Predicting chronic pathologies and post-surgery complications,City of Vimercate; Local Government; Hospital services.; Italy,"The Vimercate hospital initiative applies predictive analytics to clinical and organisational decision support within the Lombardy public health system. The implementation uses algorithms and machine-learning analytics to develop predictive models for chronic diseases and post-operative complications, supporting the hospital’s broader precision-medicine objectives.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Vimercate hospital initiative applies predictive analytics to clinical and organisational decision support within the Lombardy public health system. The operational problem is that hospitals need to manage patients more efficiently, anticipate chronic disease risks and identify post-surgical complications early, while also reducing costs and improving logistics. ASST Vimercate adopted open-source cloud solutions developed by Almaviva, building on a long digitisation process of medical records. The implementation uses algorithms and machine-learning analytics to develop predictive models for chronic diseases and post-operative complications, supporting the hospital’s broader precision-medicine objectives. Conceptually, the system combines digitised clinical records, cloud infrastructure, predictive modelling, patient-risk stratification and decision-support tools for healthcare professionals. The expected impact is earlier identification of risk, more personalised treatment planning, improved allocation of hospital resources and stronger preventive care. Almaviva describes the wider platform as supporting prognoses in areas ranging from chronic diseases to post-operative infection risk and drug-therapy outcomes. The available sources present the initiative as a structured experimentation and adoption of predictive AI in a hospital setting, but not as a replacement for clinicians.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-447,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00439,EU_PSTW_202609_00439,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Tribunal of Pisa (and EMbeDS, KDD Lab)",Law courts,"The operational challenge is that courts and researchers handle large volumes of legal texts that require anonymisation, annotation, querying and analysis before they can support research, decision support or institutional knowledge management.",Predictive Jurisprudence - Tribunale di Pisa,"Tribunal of Pisa (and EMbeDS, KDD Lab); Local Government; Law courts; Italy",UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Predictive Jurisprudence is a long-term research and development initiative aimed at applying data science, natural language processing and legal analytics to judicial materials. The operational challenge is that courts and researchers handle large volumes of legal texts that require anonymisation, annotation, querying and analysis before they can support research, decision support or institutional knowledge management. The project started in 2019 at LIDER Lab, Scuola Superiore Sant’Anna, in collaboration with EMbeDS, KDD Lab and the Tribunal of Genoa, and was later followed by an agreement with the Tribunal of Pisa for anonymisation and pseudonymisation protocols of case-law materials. The implementation follows a modular, interdisciplinary approach, combining legal expertise, software pipelines, pseudonymisation tools, automatic annotation, data mining and predictive models. Conceptually, the project aims to build enabling technologies for legal analytics while remaining coherent with regulatory and ethical constraints. The expected impact is improved management of legal documents, better access to structured judicial knowledge, more efficient anonymisation of case law and stronger foundations for explainable legal analytics. The project’s own website still describes it as a commitment towards a fully automated data-driven platform for legal analytics.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,F06_HIGH_VOLUME_SCALABILITY_BOTTLENECK,S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-448,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00440,EU_PSTW_202609_00440,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Italian Tax Authority (Agenzia delle Entrate),"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.","However, the tool also raises questions about transparency, proportionality, data protection, and the risk that automated flags may place burdens on taxpayers without sufficient contextual explanation.",Evasometro Anonimizzato - Big data analysis of taxpayer behaviour,"Italian Tax Authority (Agenzia delle Entrate); Central-Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Italy","The implementation is described as using specific algorithms to cross-check databases held by the fiscal authorities and detect anomalies in consumption or financial operations. The expected impact is stronger targeting of tax controls, better use of existing administrative data, and potentially higher efficiency in identifying suspicious cases.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Italy’s Evasometro Anonimizzato was created to help the tax administration detect inconsistencies in taxpayer behaviour by analysing large volumes of fiscal and financial information. The underlying administrative problem is tax evasion, particularly cases where declared income, consumption patterns, or financial transactions appear misaligned. For the Agenzia delle Entrate, the objective is to acquire a broader evidence base for understanding taxpayers’ fiscal conduct and identifying situations that may merit further scrutiny. Stakeholders include the Italian Tax Authority, taxpayers, financial institutions, public bodies holding relevant records, and individuals whose data may be analysed. The implementation is described as using specific algorithms to cross-check databases held by the fiscal authorities and detect anomalies in consumption or financial operations. These “digital eyes” flag inconsistencies, after which tax officials may summon individuals for additional checks. The system is also intended to be combined with monitoring of open sources, including news articles, websites, and social media, to enrich the authority’s understanding of potentially relevant behaviour. Conceptually, the solution combines big-data analytics, anomaly detection, database matching, risk scoring, and open-source intelligence. The expected impact is stronger targeting of tax controls, better use of existing administrative data, and potentially higher efficiency in identifying suspicious cases. However, the tool also raises questions about transparency, proportionality, data protection, and the risk that automated flags may place burdens on taxpayers without sufficient contextual explanation.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-449,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00441,EU_PSTW_202609_00441,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Polizia di Stato - Questura di Milano,Police Services,"The policing problem is that serial crimes can appear as separate incidents when data are fragmented across reports, locations and investigative files, making it difficult to detect recurring patterns quickly.",KeyCrime - Crime Analysis for the next generation (Predictive Policing),Polizia di Stato - Questura di Milano; Local Government; Police Services; Italy,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"KeyCrime was developed from operational policing experience in Milan to support the analysis of thefts and robberies and help investigators identify offences that may have been committed by the same person or group. The policing problem is that serial crimes can appear as separate incidents when data are fragmented across reports, locations and investigative files, making it difficult to detect recurring patterns quickly. Stakeholders include the Polizia di Stato, the Milan Police Headquarters, investigators, victims of robbery, local communities and the KeyCrime company. The software, later associated with the company KeyCrime and the product Delia, analyses thousands of data points to compare different crimes and identify possible relationships between them. Conceptually, the system supports crime-linkage analysis by examining variables such as modus operandi, location, timing, target type, behavioural features and other investigative information. Its purpose is not only to support enforcement after offences occur, but also to assist prevention by helping police understand serial patterns and prioritise operational responses. The solution was tested in production in Italy by the State Police in Milan to combat robberies. The expected impact is faster recognition of crime series, more focused investigative work, better allocation of police resources and improved capacity to anticipate repeated offending. By transforming large volumes of case data into structured operational intelligence, the tool can strengthen investigative effectiveness, although its accuracy and governance arrangements require careful validation.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-450,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00442,EU_PSTW_202609_00442,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Department of Public Security Central Anti-Crime Directorate,Police Services,UNKNOWN,XLAW - Trends and pattern discovery in crimes,Department of Public Security Central Anti-Crime Directorate; Central-Government; Police Services; Italy,"The system was developed by a law-enforcement official and has been deployed in several cities, including Naples, Modena, Prato, Salerno, Livorno, Trieste, Trento, and Venice.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"XLAW is an Italian predictive policing system used to support the detection of crime trends and the anticipation of offences in urban areas. The system was developed by a law-enforcement official and has been deployed in several cities, including Naples, Modena, Prato, Salerno, Livorno, Trieste, Trento, and Venice. The operational problem is that police forces need to identify where and when certain offences are likely to occur, so that patrols can be positioned more effectively before crimes take place. Stakeholders include law enforcement agencies, the Department of Public Security’s Central Anti-Crime Directorate, local police services, residents, potential victims, and persons who may be affected by predictive policing practices. The implementation is described as a probabilistic, machine-learning-powered solution for discovering trends and patterns in crime. It relies on the analysis of criminal history in specific locations over time, reportedly drawing on 20 years of data and the developer’s field experience. The system can alert officers and support deployment before an offence is expected to happen, and it has been reported to provide details such as gender, height, citizenship, distinguishing features, and biometrics of a potential suspect. Reported accuracy figures include 87–93% in Naples, 92–93% in Venice, and 94% in Prato, while the Venice State Police stated that 84% of attempted or committed felonies had been foreseen. However, no independent public audit or fact-checking appears available for these trials, making external validation essential before treating these results as conclusive.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-451,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00443,EU_PSTW_202609_00443,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Dutch Police,Police Services,"The operational need is to compare images obtained during police work against an existing database of persons suspected of serious offences, thereby helping investigators establish possible identities more rapidly than through manual comparison alone.",Catch facial recognition software,Dutch Police; Central-Government; Police Services; Netherlands,"In 2017, 93 suspects were reportedly identified using the system.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Dutch police’s Catch system is a facial recognition tool used to support the identification of suspects in serious criminal investigations. The operational need is to compare images obtained during police work against an existing database of persons suspected of serious offences, thereby helping investigators establish possible identities more rapidly than through manual comparison alone. Stakeholders include the Dutch police, suspects whose images are stored or searched, victims of serious crime, investigators, oversight bodies, and the wider public concerned with biometric surveillance. The implementation relies on facial recognition software that compares submitted images against a database containing police-held facial images. An investigation published by VICE in July 2019 reported that Catch contained more than 2.2 million images relating to 1.3 million individuals who may have been suspected of committing serious criminal offences. In November 2019, the police confirmed that the facial recognition system had access to a complete database of people suspected of serious crimes. The national police also introduced a smartphone application allowing officers to send images to Catch. Conceptually, the system combines image capture, facial feature extraction, biometric matching, database search, and investigator review. Its expected impact is faster suspect identification, improved investigative capacity, and easier submission of images from operational settings. In 2017, 93 suspects were reportedly identified using the system. At the same time, the scale of the database and mobile access raise significant issues concerning legality, necessity, accuracy, retention, and safeguards against misuse.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-452,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00444,EU_PSTW_202609_00444,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Macau,Old age.,"Each year, approximately 11,000 retired Portuguese citizens abroad must demonstrate that they are alive in order to continue receiving their pension, a process that can be burdensome when it requires physical documentation or interaction with consular or administrative offices. The expected impact is reduced administrative burden for pensioners abroad, faster verification, fewer physical procedures and stronger continuity of pension payments.",Proof of life recognition - Audio or video recognition,City of Macau; Local Government; Old age.; Portugal,"The pilot began in Macau, where hundreds of people were already using the system. Because the process involves biometric data and elderly users, it requires careful safeguards for accessibility, consent, data security and alternative channels for those unable to use digital verification.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The proof-of-life recognition pilot aims to simplify pension verification for Portuguese retired citizens living abroad. Each year, approximately 11,000 retired Portuguese citizens abroad must demonstrate that they are alive in order to continue receiving their pension, a process that can be burdensome when it requires physical documentation or interaction with consular or administrative offices. Caixa Geral de Aposentações, the welfare institution for civil servants, launched a pilot to allow pensioners to provide proof of life through face or voice recognition. The pilot began in Macau, where hundreds of people were already using the system. The implementation relies on biometric verification through audio or video recognition, enabling remote confirmation of a pensioner’s identity and liveness. Conceptually, the system combines identity verification, facial or voice biometrics, secure submission and pension-administration workflows. The expected impact is reduced administrative burden for pensioners abroad, faster verification, fewer physical procedures and stronger continuity of pension payments. For the administration, it can reduce manual processing and improve assurance that benefits are paid only to eligible recipients. Because the process involves biometric data and elderly users, it requires careful safeguards for accessibility, consent, data security and alternative channels for those unable to use digital verification.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-453,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00445,EU_PSTW_202609_00445,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Ministry of Public Administration,Social protection n.e.c.,"The administrative challenge was to manage complex benefit applications more efficiently while supporting social workers in checking eligibility, reducing errors, and handling large volumes of interconnected information. Human intervention remains necessary for legal reasons, as social workers still evaluate, print, sign, and send applications, with manual actions recorded and analysed to improve the system.",AI in e-Sociala program - Optimization of the social benefits tranfers,Ministry of Public Administration; Central-Government; Social protection n.e.c.; Slovenia,"Citizens can use automated tools to understand possible social rights and estimate benefit payments before submitting an application, while certain application processes for social rights are fully automated.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Slovenia’s e-Sociala programme was introduced to improve the administration of social transfers across the welfare system, including social assistance, unemployment benefits, child benefits, subsidies, and related entitlements. The administrative challenge was to manage complex benefit applications more efficiently while supporting social workers in checking eligibility, reducing errors, and handling large volumes of interconnected information. Citizens, social workers, welfare authorities, and the Ministry of Public Administration are the main stakeholders, as the programme affects both service delivery and access to social rights. The system is built around multiple connected databases and has progressively incorporated automated decision-making, machine-learning and predictive capabilities. Citizens can use automated tools to understand possible social rights and estimate benefit payments before submitting an application, while certain application processes for social rights are fully automated. The platform can retrieve previous benefit decisions, generate informative decisions for specific applicants, identify people belonging to the same family, and access relevant financial data to simplify case handling. Its longer-term objective is to create a complete “social rights profile” for each applicant, showing eligibility, duration, and payment amounts. The expected impact is faster processing, fewer administrative mistakes, improved support for social workers, and greater predictability for citizens. Human intervention remains necessary for legal reasons, as social workers still evaluate, print, sign, and send applications, with manual actions recorded and analysed to improve the system.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK|F06_HIGH_VOLUME_SCALABILITY_BOTTLENECK|F08_ERROR_COMPLIANCE_RISK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-454,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00446,EU_PSTW_202609_00446,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Ministry of Finance,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.",UNKNOWN,Detecting tax evasion schemes - Machine learning to detect tax evasion,"Ministry of Finance; Central-Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Slovenia","In Slovenia, the financial administration under the Ministry of Finance applies machine-learning techniques to strengthen tax compliance and identify behaviour that may indicate evasion, fraud, or reporting errors. The policy problem is the difficulty of detecting irregular fiscal conduct across large volumes of tax declarations, transactions, and administrative records using only manual checks or traditional rule-based controls.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"In Slovenia, the financial administration under the Ministry of Finance applies machine-learning techniques to strengthen tax compliance and identify behaviour that may indicate evasion, fraud, or reporting errors. The policy problem is the difficulty of detecting irregular fiscal conduct across large volumes of tax declarations, transactions, and administrative records using only manual checks or traditional rule-based controls. The key stakeholders are the Ministry of Finance, the financial administration, taxpayers, auditors, and citizens whose fiscal data may be processed. The objective is to improve the authority’s ability to uncover tax evasion schemes, detect inaccurate tax reports, and prioritise cases that warrant closer examination. The implementation uses machine learning to analyse tax data and identify patterns associated with fraud or non-compliance. According to the information provided, the ministry also ranks citizens considered “risky” because they are thought to be more likely to become tax evaders. Conceptually, the system may combine anomaly detection, predictive risk scoring, historical compliance data, classification models, and automated case selection to support audit targeting. The expected benefits include more efficient allocation of inspection resources, faster detection of suspicious schemes, and improved consistency in identifying reporting anomalies. However, citizen risk-ranking raises important concerns around transparency, accuracy, contestability, and possible unfair treatment if individuals are prioritised for scrutiny on the basis of opaque or weakly explained statistical signals.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-455,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00447,EU_PSTW_202609_00447,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Ministry of Education, Science and Sport",Pre-primary and primary education.,"METIS was developed in Slovenia to test whether an intelligent educational support system could help identify early learning difficulties among primary-school pupils. The policy and pedagogical problem is that schools may need earlier warning of pupils who are struggling, but teachers require reliable, context-sensitive indicators rather than narrow administrative signals.",Detect early learning problems in primary schools (METS),"Ministry of Education, Science and Sport; Central-Government; Pre-primary and primary education.; Slovenia","Its implementation uses artificial intelligence and machine-learning methods based on indicators of educational success, with pupils assessed as being at increased risk of developing learning difficulties and then referred to an education professional for consultation and an action plan. The expected benefit is earlier intervention, more structured support for pupils and improved use of school data in educational planning.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"METIS was developed in Slovenia to test whether an intelligent educational support system could help identify early learning difficulties among primary-school pupils. The policy and pedagogical problem is that schools may need earlier warning of pupils who are struggling, but teachers require reliable, context-sensitive indicators rather than narrow administrative signals. The system was designed to monitor grades and absences, search for specific learning patterns and support teachers in identifying pupils who might need additional attention. Its implementation uses artificial intelligence and machine-learning methods based on indicators of educational success, with pupils assessed as being at increased risk of developing learning difficulties and then referred to an education professional for consultation and an action plan. The expected benefit is earlier intervention, more structured support for pupils and improved use of school data in educational planning. However, the project was criticised by educational experts who argued that grades and absences alone were not sufficient to detect learning problems reliably. Further concerns related to limited budget, interpolated data, methodological shortcuts and the role of a private commercial contractor holding substantial school data. These issues make the case important not only as an educational innovation, but also as an example of the governance risks of predictive systems in schools.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-456,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00448,EU_PSTW_202609_00448,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Public Employment Service,Unemployment.,The administrative challenge is to verify eligibility efficiently while processing applications in a consistent way and protecting public funds.,Checks for financial support - AI for checking companies requesting support,Public Employment Service; Central-Government; Unemployment.; Sweden,"The system rates whether a company can receive support using a traffic-light logic: green indicates eligibility, yellow means that an additional manual check is required, and red indicates non-eligibility. Its implementation uses automated checks against relevant company and eligibility data, translating administrative rules and risk indicators into a structured rating that guides case handling.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Sweden’s Public Employment Service has experimented with automated decision-making to assess companies applying for financial support linked to the employment of long-term unemployed people. The administrative challenge is to verify eligibility efficiently while processing applications in a consistent way and protecting public funds. The system rates whether a company can receive support using a traffic-light logic: green indicates eligibility, yellow means that an additional manual check is required, and red indicates non-eligibility. Its implementation uses automated checks against relevant company and eligibility data, translating administrative rules and risk indicators into a structured rating that guides case handling. Conceptually, the tool combines data validation, rule-based decision support and automated classification within the grant-assessment workflow. The expected impact is faster processing, reduced workload for caseworkers and more uniform treatment of standard applications. At the same time, the use case raises concerns because caseworkers reportedly have limited possibilities to challenge the automated decision in this area. This is particularly sensitive because access to employment-support funding can affect employers’ incentives to hire long-term unemployed people and can indirectly influence labour-market inclusion outcomes.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-457,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00449,EU_PSTW_202609_00449,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Nacka municipality,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.",UNKNOWN,Yasmine - Robot employee,"Nacka municipality; Local Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Sweden","Nacka municipality is part of Stockholm county, and it has developed and implemented a so-called robot employee called Yasmine.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Nacka municipality is part of Stockholm county, and it has developed and implemented a so-called robot employee called Yasmine. The robot is part of an RPA service platform that was provided by the Norwegian company Basefarm. So far, the administration of Nacka has automated three processes. Two of these are in the so-called Establishment Unit (Etablering), and the other is in the Work and Business Unit. The Establishment Unit is responsible for economic support, unaccompanied children and minors (refugees). The Work and Business Unit administers adult education, societal orientation, and labor market initiatives",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-458,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00450,EU_PSTW_202609_00450,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Swiss Execution of Penal Sentences and Justice,Police Services,UNKNOWN,Fall-Screening-Tool FaST - Assisting risk-oriented sanctioning,Swiss Execution of Penal Sentences and Justice; Central-Government; Police Services; Switzerland,"The implementation appears to use a screening instrument that organises case information and applies predefined assessment logic to support classification. At the same time, use in penal contexts requires particularly strong safeguards, because risk classifications can affect liberty, treatment pathways, and institutional decisions.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Switzerland’s Fall-Screening-Tool, known as FaST, supports the classification of cases within the framework of Risk-Oriented Sanctioning. The operational context is penal execution and justice administration, where authorities must assess cases systematically and determine how risks should influence sanctions, supervision, or intervention pathways. The stakeholders include the Swiss Execution of Penal Sentences and Justice, justice professionals, persons subject to penal measures, probation or enforcement services, and institutions involved in case management. The objective is to assist officials in classifying cases according to risk-related criteria, thereby supporting more consistent and structured decision-making. The implementation appears to use a screening instrument that organises case information and applies predefined assessment logic to support classification. At a conceptual level, such a tool may draw on offence characteristics, personal circumstances, prior records, behavioural indicators, and institutional information to distinguish cases requiring different levels of attention. It is best understood as a decision-support mechanism rather than a substitute for legal judgement, as risk-oriented sanctioning remains embedded in broader justice procedures. The expected impact is greater coherence in case categorisation, better targeting of supervision resources, and improved ability to identify situations requiring more intensive management. At the same time, use in penal contexts requires particularly strong safeguards, because risk classifications can affect liberty, treatment pathways, and institutional decisions. Transparency, reviewability, proportionality, and clear human accountability are therefore central to the system’s legitimacy.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-459,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00451,EU_PSTW_202609_00451,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,UK government,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.",UNKNOWN,Facial recognition Settled Status Applications - Checking identity,"UK government; Central-Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; United Kingdom","The expected impact was faster processing, greater accessibility for applicants able to use the digital route and reduced administrative pressure on physical checking channels. However, because the system relies on biometric comparison and affects legal residence status, it requires strong safeguards for accuracy, accessibility, alternative routes and remedies for applicants who cannot use or pass digital checks.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The UK’s EU Settlement Scheme introduced digital identity checking as part of the process for confirming the residency rights of EU, EEA and Swiss citizens and their eligible family members after Brexit. The administrative problem was the need to process a very large number of applications efficiently while verifying identity remotely and reducing reliance on in-person document checks. The registration system used an app that checked identity through facial matching, allowing applicants to submit identity evidence digitally as part of their application. The implementation combined mobile identity capture, facial comparison, document verification and online application workflows. Conceptually, the system compared a live or submitted facial image with identity-document data to support remote verification before the application moved through the settlement process. The expected impact was faster processing, greater accessibility for applicants able to use the digital route and reduced administrative pressure on physical checking channels. The system also formed part of a broader digital immigration infrastructure that has continued to evolve after the main 30 June 2021 application deadline. However, because the system relies on biometric comparison and affects legal residence status, it requires strong safeguards for accuracy, accessibility, alternative routes and remedies for applicants who cannot use or pass digital checks.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-460,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00452,EU_PSTW_202609_00452,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Police,Police Services,"The policing problem was the need to locate wanted individuals more rapidly across large crowds or busy public areas, where manual recognition by officers is limited.",Facial recognition for apprehension police - Apprehension of suspects,Police; Central-Government; Police Services; United Kingdom,"The expected operational impact is faster identification of wanted suspects, improved capacity to act in real time and more efficient use of police resources in crowded environments. The use case therefore sits at the intersection of public safety and civil liberties, with key concerns around accuracy, bias, proportionality, legal basis, public awareness and safeguards against mass surveillance.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"UK police forces trialled automatic facial recognition systems to identify people who should be apprehended as suspects, including deployments in public spaces such as major events. The policing problem was the need to locate wanted individuals more rapidly across large crowds or busy public areas, where manual recognition by officers is limited. The implementation used live or automatic facial recognition to compare faces captured by cameras against police watchlists and generate alerts when a possible match was detected. Conceptually, the system combines image capture, biometric template comparison, watchlist matching and officer review before enforcement action. The expected operational impact is faster identification of wanted suspects, improved capacity to act in real time and more efficient use of police resources in crowded environments. However, the trials were strongly contested by civil society organisations, including Big Brother Watch, which monitored facial recognition at Notting Hill Carnival and reported a high number of false positives. The use case therefore sits at the intersection of public safety and civil liberties, with key concerns around accuracy, bias, proportionality, legal basis, public awareness and safeguards against mass surveillance.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-461,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00453,EU_PSTW_202609_00453,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Nacka,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.","Nacka municipality introduced financial assistance automation to modernise social-service administration and reduce the administrative burden on caseworkers. The process reduced reliance on manual administration, improved transparency for applicants and strengthened municipal capacity to allocate staff time to advisory and support functions rather than routine processing.",Financial Assistance Automation,"Municipality of Nacka; Local Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Sweden",Nacka became the second municipality in Sweden to automate the financial assistance process.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Nacka municipality introduced financial assistance automation to modernise social-service administration and reduce the administrative burden on caseworkers. The objective was to create a more efficient and customer-oriented process for residents applying for financial assistance, while allowing municipal staff to focus more on individual support and pathways towards self-sufficiency. Work began in 2017 with the introduction of an e-service, and in December of the same year the processing workflow was automated. Nacka became the second municipality in Sweden to automate the financial assistance process. The emerging technology implementation combined a digital application channel with automated processing of defined steps in the case-management workflow. At conceptual level, the system enabled applicants to initiate a case online at a time of their choosing, submit required information digitally and follow the progress of their application. Automation supported the structured handling of applications where relevant information could be processed according to predefined rules, while staff remained available for assessment, support and more complex situations. The reported impact was an increase in quality for residents applying digitally, as well as a more efficient and user-centred service. The process reduced reliance on manual administration, improved transparency for applicants and strengthened municipal capacity to allocate staff time to advisory and support functions rather than routine processing. The case illustrates how automation can improve both internal efficiency and the citizen experience in welfare administration.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-462,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00454,EU_PSTW_202609_00454,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Uddevalla municipality,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.","The organisational problem was the presence of repetitive, rules-based tasks that absorbed staff time and limited the ability to respond to growing service volumes in areas such as childcare and home care. The impact is both operational and financial: automated processes can handle increased volumes without additional resources, reduce repetitive manual work, and allow freed-up capacity to be redirected towards childcare, home-care demand, and other social administration priorities.",Automated processes Uddevalla - AI to internal processes,"Uddevalla municipality; Local Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Sweden",UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Uddevalla municipality has pursued internal process automation to release administrative capacity within municipal services, particularly in the social administration. The organisational problem was the presence of repetitive, rules-based tasks that absorbed staff time and limited the ability to respond to growing service volumes in areas such as childcare and home care. Municipal employees, service managers, residents relying on welfare services, and the technology provider supporting automation are the principal stakeholders. The objective is to streamline back-office workflows and redirect staff capacity towards frontline and higher-value public service tasks. The implementation involves automated processes across several administrative areas, including compensation, sick pay costs, authorisation administration, IT group activities, planning systems, financial aid, automatic closing, accounting with Försäkringskassan, lunch boxes, absence, extra time, new decisions, income changes, and resource allocation. These workflows likely combine robotic process automation, rule-based processing, data extraction, system integration, and automated handovers between municipal applications. The municipality had 10 automated processes underway, corresponding to the annual work of 20 full-time positions. By 2021, further automation was expected to release capacity equivalent to another 20 full-time positions. The impact is both operational and financial: automated processes can handle increased volumes without additional resources, reduce repetitive manual work, and allow freed-up capacity to be redirected towards childcare, home-care demand, and other social administration priorities.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT|S6_WORKFLOW_PROCESS_AUTOMATION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-463,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00455,EU_PSTW_202609_00455,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Södertälje,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.","The Municipality of Södertälje faced inefficiencies and administrative bottlenecks in handling routine internal services such as absence reporting for hourly employees, termination of hourly contracts, travel service bookings and the issuance of employer certificates. These repetitive, high-volume processes required substantial manual effort from HR and administrative staff, leading to longer turnaround times, increased risk of human error and reduced capacity for strategic work.",RPA in Södertälje - Automation of internal services,"Municipality of Södertälje; Local Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Sweden","The municipality implemented Robotic Process Automation (RPA) technology to mimic human interactions with existing legacy and web-based administrative systems. Integration was achieved without altering core systems, using automation scripts connected to the municipal IT landscape and orchestrated by an RPA management platform that schedules, monitors and logs robot activities.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Municipality of Södertälje faced inefficiencies and administrative bottlenecks in handling routine internal services such as absence reporting for hourly employees, termination of hourly contracts, travel service bookings and the issuance of employer certificates. These repetitive, high-volume processes required substantial manual effort from HR and administrative staff, leading to longer turnaround times, increased risk of human error and reduced capacity for strategic work. Stakeholders included municipal HR teams, front-line administrators, IT services and employees who depended on timely processing of their HR requests. The objective was to streamline internal operations, improve service reliability and free up staff time for higher-value tasks by automating rule-based administrative workflows. The municipality implemented Robotic Process Automation (RPA) technology to mimic human interactions with existing legacy and web-based administrative systems. Software robots were configured to execute predefined sequences of tasks — such as retrieving absence records, inputting employee data into HR systems, processing terminations, managing travel service requests and generating employer certificates — based on business rules and triggers. Integration was achieved without altering core systems, using automation scripts connected to the municipal IT landscape and orchestrated by an RPA management platform that schedules, monitors and logs robot activities. The introduction of RPA significantly enhanced operational efficiency by reducing processing times, minimising errors associated with manual data entry and ensuring consistent execution of administrative tasks. Employees saw faster service delivery for routine HR matters, while HR and administrative staff reclaimed work hours previously spent on repetitive tasks. Overall organisational productivity improved, internal service reliability increased and administrative costs were reduced, enabling a shift in focus toward strategic projects and citizen-centric services.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK|F06_HIGH_VOLUME_SCALABILITY_BOTTLENECK,S6_WORKFLOW_PROCESS_AUTOMATION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-464,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00456,EU_PSTW_202609_00456,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Federal Statistical Office,R&D general public services.,UNKNOWN,Machine Learning SoSi (ML_SoSi),Federal Statistical Office; Central-Government; R&D general public services.; Switzerland,"The objective is analytical rather than case-management oriented: to group similar future pathways and estimate group membership using individual characteristics and retrospective progression data. The implementation applies machine-learning techniques to longitudinal administrative data, clustering typical trajectories and assessing how previous characteristics relate to later outcomes.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Social-security systems generate complex longitudinal data on benefit receipt, employment transitions and individual pathways, but statistical offices need methods that can summarise these patterns without reducing them to isolated indicators. The Swiss Federal Statistical Office developed ML_SoSi to identify typical prospective trajectory patterns in social security and gainful employment, especially among people entering unemployment insurance. The objective is analytical rather than case-management oriented: to group similar future pathways and estimate group membership using individual characteristics and retrospective progression data. The implementation applies machine-learning techniques to longitudinal administrative data, clustering typical trajectories and assessing how previous characteristics relate to later outcomes. At a conceptual level, the system combines sequence data, benefit-payment records, employment information and model-quality evaluation to produce interpretable groupings of social-security progression. This supports official statistics by revealing recurrent patterns that may not be visible through conventional tabulations. The expected impact is improved understanding of labour-market and social-security dynamics, stronger methodological capacity inside the statistical office, and better evidence for analysing policy-relevant transitions. The project also includes work on evaluating model quality and model drift, which is important for responsible use in official statistics. Public material presents ML_SoSi as an innovation project of the Swiss Federal Statistical Office, not as an automated decision system for individual benefit administration.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-465,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00457,EU_PSTW_202609_00457,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Canton Aargau,Health n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,MedicalGuide project,Canton Aargau; Local Government; Health n.e.c.; Switzerland,"Canton Aargau has been involved in the MedicalGuide project, which provides users with an initial assessment of disease by using a Chatbot",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Canton Aargau has been involved in the MedicalGuide project, which provides users with an initial assessment of disease by using a Chatbot",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,NOT_IN_USE,미사용,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-467,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00458,EU_PSTW_202609_00458,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Manchester City Council,Family and children.,"Families with complex support needs often interact with multiple public services, but professionals may lack a shared view of relevant information, making it harder to coordinate help and assess outcomes.",Manchester’s Research & Intelligence Database - Identifying troubled families,Manchester City Council; Local Government; Family and children.; United Kingdom,"The implementation was built around IBM iBase, with the council developing its own approach to data use, integration and operational practice.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Families with complex support needs often interact with multiple public services, but professionals may lack a shared view of relevant information, making it harder to coordinate help and assess outcomes. Manchester City Council developed the Manchester Research & Intelligence Database to support its work with families needing assistance, including those identified through the national Troubled Families programme. The objective was to help caseworkers access information more quickly, understand needs more holistically and analyse service impact. The implementation was built around IBM iBase, with the council developing its own approach to data use, integration and operational practice. At a conceptual level, the database links information legally available to lead professionals, allowing them to understand relationships between people, places, services and needs. It was framed by the council as a tool to empower professionals with better data rather than replace judgement with algorithmic decisions. The expected impact is improved case coordination, faster access to relevant records, better performance reporting and more informed service design. Future goals described in public material included decision-support tools, threshold and alerting tools, broader rollout and connection of additional datasets. The case is important because it shows how local government used data integration and intelligence tooling in social-policy work, while also raising governance questions about proportionality, consent, transparency and professional discretion.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-468,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00459,EU_PSTW_202609_00459,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Department of Hygiene and Epidemiology - University of Thessaly,Public health services.,"The reported impact was a more targeted testing approach, allowing Greece to prioritise higher-risk arrivals despite limited resources.",Efficient and targeted COVID-19 border testing via reinforcement learning,Department of Hygiene and Epidemiology - University of Thessaly; Central-Government; Public health services.; Greece,"Greece’s Eva system was deployed during the summer of 2020 to improve the allocation of limited COVID-19 tests at national borders and reduce the risk of asymptomatic infected travellers entering the country undetected. The public health challenge was that uniform or country-wide testing rules could not make optimal use of constrained testing capacity, particularly when traveller flows and infection prevalence changed rapidly.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Greece’s Eva system was deployed during the summer of 2020 to improve the allocation of limited COVID-19 tests at national borders and reduce the risk of asymptomatic infected travellers entering the country undetected. The public health challenge was that uniform or country-wide testing rules could not make optimal use of constrained testing capacity, particularly when traveller flows and infection prevalence changed rapidly. Border authorities, incoming travellers, public health officials, epidemiologists, and policy-makers were directly involved. The objective was to use available testing resources more strategically while generating real-time estimates of COVID-19 prevalence to inform border policy. The implementation relied on a reinforcement learning system that allocated tests using incoming travellers’ demographic information and results from previous travellers. Unlike static protocols, Eva updated its testing strategy as new evidence became available, learning which traveller profiles or routes were associated with higher positivity risk. Conceptually, the system combined sequential decision-making, adaptive risk estimation, epidemiological data, and border-testing operations into a single allocation mechanism. The reported impact was a more targeted testing approach, allowing Greece to prioritise higher-risk arrivals despite limited resources. The system also provided policy-relevant information on prevalence among travellers, supporting more responsive border management during the pandemic. Its value lay in improving public health surveillance, making testing more efficient, and enabling decisions to adjust as conditions evolved.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,NOT_IN_USE,미사용,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-469,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00460,EU_PSTW_202609_00460,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Aria Spa,Health n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,Predictive Healthcare Assistance DB (BDA) in Regione Lombardia,Aria Spa; Regional Government; Health n.e.c.; Italy,"The implementation builds on the existing Assisted Database, which has classified the Lombardy population according to healthcare consumption and 62 chronic pathologies, using variables such as tracer conditions, complexity level, territory, gender, age group, and type of assistance. The project operates under the scientific direction of the regional epidemiological observatory and applies predictive analytics to administrative and healthcare data.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Regione Lombardia’s predictive evolution of the Assisted Database focuses on improving population stratification and healthcare planning for chronic disease management. The health-system problem is that regional authorities need to understand the complexity, care needs, and service consumption of a large population affected by chronic conditions in order to plan assistance more effectively. Stakeholders include Regione Lombardia, ARIA SpA, the regional epidemiological observatory, healthcare providers, patients with chronic diseases, and policy-makers responsible for resource allocation. The objective is to move from descriptive stratification towards predictive assistance, enabling the region to anticipate needs and better organise care pathways. The implementation builds on the existing Assisted Database, which has classified the Lombardy population according to healthcare consumption and 62 chronic pathologies, using variables such as tracer conditions, complexity level, territory, gender, age group, and type of assistance. The project operates under the scientific direction of the regional epidemiological observatory and applies predictive analytics to administrative and healthcare data. At a conceptual level, the system may combine population stratification, risk modelling, longitudinal consumption data, and regional health-service information to identify groups requiring different levels of care. The expected impact is improved planning of chronic care, more accurate targeting of resources, and better understanding of territorial differences in healthcare needs. By strengthening predictive capacity, the database can support more proactive assistance models and improve the reliability of regional health policy decisions.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-470,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00461,EU_PSTW_202609_00461,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Aria Spa,Health n.e.c.,"The clinical and organisational challenge is that chronic patients often experience complex sequences of health events, service contacts, and care needs over time, making it difficult for healthcare planners and clinicians to anticipate the most appropriate subsequent intervention.","Finding the ‘next-best-clinical-action""",Aria Spa; Regional Government; Health n.e.c.; Italy,"The implementation uses data and process mining techniques to study the characteristics of the pathology and reconstruct the sequence of medical events, consumption patterns, and healthcare interactions among the chronic population affected by cognitive dementia.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The “next-best-clinical-action” project in Regione Lombardia focuses on improving the understanding of care pathways for patients affected by cognitive dementia. The clinical and organisational challenge is that chronic patients often experience complex sequences of health events, service contacts, and care needs over time, making it difficult for healthcare planners and clinicians to anticipate the most appropriate subsequent intervention. Stakeholders include Regione Lombardia, ARIA SpA, healthcare professionals, patients with cognitive dementia, carers, and institutions responsible for chronic care planning. The objective is to analyse and predict the patient journey following the onset of dementia, year by year, over a five-year period. The implementation uses data and process mining techniques to study the characteristics of the pathology and reconstruct the sequence of medical events, consumption patterns, and healthcare interactions among the chronic population affected by cognitive dementia. Conceptually, the project combines longitudinal health data, pathway modelling, event-sequence analysis, predictive analytics, and process mining to identify the likely next clinical step in a patient’s pathway. The expected impact is better anticipation of care needs, more informed clinical and organisational planning, and stronger evidence for designing pathways around real patient trajectories. The solution can support healthcare services in identifying recurrent patterns, improving continuity of care, and aligning resources with expected demand. Its broader value lies in moving from retrospective observation to more proactive management of chronic neurodegenerative conditions.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-471,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00462,EU_PSTW_202609_00462,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Aria Spa,Health n.e.c.,"The planning challenge is that health needs are not distributed evenly across the territory, and regional authorities require more precise ways to identify areas where patients share similar healthcare characteristics, service demand and clinical profiles.",Geo Machine Learning for Health,Aria Spa; Regional Government; Health n.e.c.; Italy,"Regione Lombardia’s Geo Machine Learning for Health project applies territorial analysis to respiratory care, with a focus on patients affected by respiratory insufficiency, chronic obstructive pulmonary disease and asthma. The implementation uses machine-learning techniques applied to health and territorial data, combining information on chronic respiratory conditions, patient characteristics, healthcare consumption and geographical distribution.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Regione Lombardia’s Geo Machine Learning for Health project applies territorial analysis to respiratory care, with a focus on patients affected by respiratory insufficiency, chronic obstructive pulmonary disease and asthma. The planning challenge is that health needs are not distributed evenly across the territory, and regional authorities require more precise ways to identify areas where patients share similar healthcare characteristics, service demand and clinical profiles. The main stakeholders include Regione Lombardia, ARIA SpA, the Regional Epidemiological Observatory, healthcare planners, providers and patients living with chronic respiratory diseases. The project aims to support more granular population analysis by defining what is described as the UMTA, or Minimum Unit Territorial Aggregation: a geographical area grouping assisted individuals with homogeneous health properties connected to the respiratory tracers identified by the regional observatory. The implementation uses machine-learning techniques applied to health and territorial data, combining information on chronic respiratory conditions, patient characteristics, healthcare consumption and geographical distribution. Conceptually, the model supports spatial clustering, population stratification and predictive territorial planning. The expected impact is a more accurate understanding of where respiratory care needs are concentrated, allowing the region to plan services, allocate resources and design interventions with greater territorial precision. By linking clinical indicators with geographical aggregation, the project can improve evidence-based planning, strengthen chronic disease management and support more targeted healthcare policies across Lombardy.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-472,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00463,EU_PSTW_202609_00463,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,TAUS,General services.,"Organisations developing translation services, public bodies, language service providers, technology companies, and data owners often face difficulties in finding trustworthy datasets that meet their linguistic, legal, and technical requirements.",CEF Data Marketplace,TAUS; Consortium; General services.; Netherlands,"The CEF Data Marketplace responds to a structural problem in the translation and language-technology sector: the fragmented availability of high-quality, domain-specific, and reusable language data across different languages, sectors, and use contexts. The objective of the use case is to create a broader and more sustainable market that serves the data needs of stakeholders operating in the global translation ecosystem, while supporting multiple language combinations and specialist domains.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The CEF Data Marketplace responds to a structural problem in the translation and language-technology sector: the fragmented availability of high-quality, domain-specific, and reusable language data across different languages, sectors, and use contexts. Organisations developing translation services, public bodies, language service providers, technology companies, and data owners often face difficulties in finding trustworthy datasets that meet their linguistic, legal, and technical requirements. The objective of the use case is to create a broader and more sustainable market that serves the data needs of stakeholders operating in the global translation ecosystem, while supporting multiple language combinations and specialist domains. The implementation consists of a universal and secure trading platform for language data, designed to facilitate the discovery, exchange, licensing, and controlled reuse of datasets for machine translation and other machine learning applications. At a conceptual level, the platform relies on data cataloguing, metadata standards, secure transaction mechanisms, access controls, quality assessment processes, and integration interfaces that allow suppliers and users to interact within a trusted digital environment. The expected impact is the creation of a continuous and long-term supply of language resources, reducing search and transaction costs while improving access to data for smaller and larger market participants alike. By strengthening data availability and interoperability, the marketplace can improve translation performance, accelerate innovation, and increase the economic value of language assets",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,NOT_IN_USE,미사용,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-473,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00464,EU_PSTW_202609_00464,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Cremona,Unemployment.,"The operational problem is that young people and jobseekers often need guidance on career planning, CV preparation, applications, interviews and access to vacancies, while local public services may lack scalable digital tools to provide personalised support.",ANCI Lombardia - CVqui-Jobiri,Municipality of Cremona; Local Government; Unemployment.; Italy,"Citizens can develop a professional project, consult job opportunities, create CVs using thousands of examples, access cover-letter templates, practise interviews through video simulations and improve their CVs through automated suggestions. Public institutions can use the ecosystem to expand employment services for citizens and businesses, while companies can publish vacancies, manage selection processes and collaborate with institutions to share employment trends.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The CVqui-Jobiri initiative was created to modernise youth information and employment-support services across parts of Lombardy, helping citizens, public institutions and companies interact within a more integrated labour-market ecosystem. The operational problem is that young people and jobseekers often need guidance on career planning, CV preparation, applications, interviews and access to vacancies, while local public services may lack scalable digital tools to provide personalised support. Employers, in turn, require more effective channels to reach candidates and communicate labour-market needs. The solution developed by Jobiri, in collaboration with the Informagiovani services of Cremona, Crema, Lodi, Novate Milanese and Bergamo, provides an intelligent career-service platform. Citizens can develop a professional project, consult job opportunities, create CVs using thousands of examples, access cover-letter templates, practise interviews through video simulations and improve their CVs through automated suggestions. The implementation combines digital self-service tools, recommendation functions, job-matching capabilities, automated CV analysis, interview-training simulations and employer-branding features. Public institutions can use the ecosystem to expand employment services for citizens and businesses, while companies can publish vacancies, manage selection processes and collaborate with institutions to share employment trends. The expected impact is broader access to employability support, improved quality of job applications, stronger matching between candidates and employers, and more efficient delivery of local employment services.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-474,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00465,EU_PSTW_202609_00465,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Nurogames GmbH and ATOS Spain,Education not definable by level.,UNKNOWN,MATHISIS H2020 - Italian Pilot (MIUR),Nurogames GmbH and ATOS Spain; Consortium; Education not definable by level.; Italy,"Using affective computing techniques, the platform analysed signals related to mood, movement and attention, and adjusted the difficulty of didactic exercises accordingly. Initial results indicated that use of the platform improved students’ learning paths by increasing concentration and attention levels.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Italian pilot of the MATHISIS H2020 project addressed the need for more personalised educational support for students with autism spectrum disorder, ADHD and multiple disabilities in public schools. The public stakeholders involved were Italian first- and second-degree schools, with the project aiming to improve learning pathways by adapting teaching exercises to students’ attention levels, emotional states and interaction patterns. The objective was to support teachers and learners through a platform able to respond dynamically to individual learning conditions, particularly for students who may require more tailored pedagogical approaches. The emerging technology implementation consisted of a teaching support platform connected to a webcam, movement-recognition equipment and a gyroscope. Using affective computing techniques, the platform analysed signals related to mood, movement and attention, and adjusted the difficulty of didactic exercises accordingly. At conceptual level, the system combined sensor-based interaction data with adaptive learning logic to increase or decrease task complexity in response to the learner’s condition. The pilot was tested in a sample of Italian public schools, involving just under 100 students. Initial results indicated that use of the platform improved students’ learning paths by increasing concentration and attention levels. The case demonstrates the potential of adaptive educational technologies to support inclusive education, improve engagement and provide teachers with additional tools for differentiated learning in complex classroom contexts.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-475,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00466,EU_PSTW_202609_00466,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Italy Minister of Defense,General services.,"The DANTE project, Detecting and Analysing Terrorist-related Online Contents and Financing Activities, addressed the challenge of supporting law-enforcement and defence authorities in identifying terrorist propaganda, training and financing activities across complex online environments. The operational problem concerned the difficulty of retrieving and analysing large volumes of heterogeneous, multilingual and unstructured online information, particularly across channels such as the open web, the deep web and the dark web.",DANTE H2020,Italy Minister of Defense; Consortium; General services.; Italy,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The DANTE project, Detecting and Analysing Terrorist-related Online Contents and Financing Activities, addressed the challenge of supporting law-enforcement and defence authorities in identifying terrorist propaganda, training and financing activities across complex online environments. Italy participated through the Ministry of Defence and the General Command of the Carabinieri, within a project involving 18 European countries. The operational problem concerned the difficulty of retrieving and analysing large volumes of heterogeneous, multilingual and unstructured online information, particularly across channels such as the open web, the deep web and the dark web. The emerging technology implementation consisted of an automated system for extracting and analysing data from unstructured sources. The system applied web-mining methods and advanced analytical techniques to identify, retrieve, collect and analyse online content linked to terrorist networks. At conceptual level, it combined multilingual information extraction, source monitoring, content analysis and investigative-support functions to help authorities trace financing, training and propaganda-related activities. The system was designed to process information from channels that are traditionally more difficult to investigate, thereby expanding the analytical reach of police and security authorities. Its expected impact was to improve the accuracy, speed and completeness of terrorism investigations by enabling investigators to handle larger volumes of online material and identify relevant patterns more effectively. The case provides an example of cross-border security research intended to strengthen investigative capabilities against online terrorist activity.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F04_UNSTRUCTURED_INFORMATION_COMPLEXITY|F06_HIGH_VOLUME_SCALABILITY_BOTTLENECK,S3_DOCUMENT_INTELLIGENCE,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-476,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00467,EU_PSTW_202609_00467,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Provincia autonoma di Trento - Trentino Trasporti (in house),Transport,"The operational challenge is that regional transport systems must coordinate tariffs, vehicles, passenger information and service planning across different routes and mobility needs.",Mobilità Integrata Trentino Trasporti (MITT),Provincia autonoma di Trento - Trentino Trasporti (in house); Local Government; Transport; Italy,"The project introduces smart cards for integrated and differentiated tariffs, with the possibility of extending their use in a multiservice logic.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"MITT was designed to improve the management of public transport in the Autonomous Province of Trento by making services more integrated, responsive and user-oriented. The operational challenge is that regional transport systems must coordinate tariffs, vehicles, passenger information and service planning across different routes and mobility needs. Citizens require reliable information on travel times, while transport operators need real-time visibility over vehicles and tools to support scheduling decisions. The main stakeholders include Provincia autonoma di Trento, Trentino Trasporti, passengers, transport planners and public-service managers. The project introduces smart cards for integrated and differentiated tariffs, with the possibility of extending their use in a multiservice logic. The technological implementation also includes real-time monitoring of local public transport vehicles, travel-time management and predictive support for bus scheduling. Positioning data from buses are detected and transmitted to provide passengers with information on journey times, while analytical tools can support planning and operational optimisation. At a conceptual level, the system combines smart-ticketing infrastructure, vehicle-location data, passenger information services, scheduling support and transport-management systems. The expected impact is a more efficient public transport network, better passenger experience, improved reliability of travel information and stronger capacity for transport planning. By linking fare integration with real-time operational intelligence, MITT can help the province deliver a more coordinated and effective mobility service.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-477,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00468,EU_PSTW_202609_00468,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,FCT (Fundação para a Ciência e Tecnologia),Social protection n.e.c.,The GADgET project addressed the public-policy and regulatory challenge of identifying risky behavioural patterns in online gambling.,GADgET: Detection of addition patterns in online game,FCT (Fundação para a Ciência e Tecnologia); Central-Government; Social protection n.e.c.; Portugal,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The GADgET project addressed the public-policy and regulatory challenge of identifying risky behavioural patterns in online gambling. Gambling behaviour is dynamic and time-sensitive, meaning that harmful patterns may emerge through sequences of actions rather than isolated events. The project, supported in Portugal through FCT, proposed a system capable of analysing gamblers’ behaviour over time in order to detect common patterns, identify actions associated with risk and enable continuous monitoring of online gambling activity. The emerging technology implementation was based on recurrent neural networks, with the architecture optimised through a neuroevolution algorithm. This approach was designed to compare time series associated with different gamblers’ behaviours, while explicitly taking account of the temporal dimension of the problem. At conceptual level, the system would process behavioural sequences generated during online play, identify similarities across users, detect indicators of risky conduct and operate in real time so that gambling activity could be continuously assessed. The intended impact was to improve the capacity of regulators, operators or public authorities to detect addiction-related patterns within an acceptable timeframe and intervene more effectively where risk indicators are present. By supporting real-time analysis, the solution aimed to move beyond retrospective assessment and enable more proactive monitoring of gambling-related harm. The case is particularly relevant for public administrations exploring data-driven approaches to consumer protection, behavioural risk detection and responsible gambling oversight.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-478,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00469,EU_PSTW_202609_00469,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,FCT (Fundação para a Ciência e Tecnologia),Social protection n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,AICE (Data Science and Over-Indebtedness: Use of AI algorithms in Credit and Debts),FCT (Fundação para a Ciência e Tecnologia); Central-Government; Social protection n.e.c.; Portugal,The emerging technology implementation proposed the use of machine-learning methods to develop descriptive and predictive models of over-indebtedness.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"AICE addressed the issue of over-indebtedness among Portuguese consumers, a problem strongly influenced by socio-economic conditions and financially relevant life events such as job loss. The project, supported by FCT, aimed to improve understanding of the factors that contribute to consumer vulnerability and to support better regulation and financial empowerment policies. Public authorities require more precise evidence on how different groups are affected by debt-related risk, but traditional analysis may not fully capture the interaction between socio-economic profiles, financial events and behavioural patterns. The emerging technology implementation proposed the use of machine-learning methods to develop descriptive and predictive models of over-indebtedness. In particular, unsupervised learning algorithms such as Self-Organising Maps and Agglomerative Hierarchical Clustering would be used to identify consumer clusters and reveal patterns within the data. At conceptual level, the system would process financial and socio-economic variables to group consumers according to similarities in debt vulnerability, enabling policymakers to better understand risk profiles and influencing factors. The expected impact was to produce evidence that could inform guidelines for over-indebtedness regulation, consumer financial empowerment and preventive policy design. By identifying clusters and risk factors, the project could support more targeted interventions, improve analytical capacity and contribute to fairer and more effective consumer-protection strategies in the credit and debt domain.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-479,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00470,EU_PSTW_202609_00470,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,AMA (Agência para a Modernização Administrativa),"General economic, commercial and labour affair","MapIntel addressed the challenge of supporting strategic decision-making through better analysis of large collections of business and policy-relevant documents. However, large text corpora are difficult to explore manually, particularly when documents are heterogeneous and relevant information is distributed across many sources.",MapIntel - Interactive Visual Analysis Platform for Competitive Intelligence,"AMA (Agência para a Modernização Administrativa); Central-Government; General economic, commercial and labour affair; Portugal",The emerging technology implementation was based on a neural network document-clustering system using Self-Organising Maps.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"MapIntel addressed the challenge of supporting strategic decision-making through better analysis of large collections of business and policy-relevant documents. Competitive intelligence requires gathering and analysing information on the business environment so that executives and decision-makers can identify trends, risks and opportunities. However, large text corpora are difficult to explore manually, particularly when documents are heterogeneous and relevant information is distributed across many sources. The project, involving Portugal’s Agency for Administrative Modernisation, proposed an interactive visual analysis platform to improve the organisation, retrieval and exploration of such information. The emerging technology implementation was based on a neural network document-clustering system using Self-Organising Maps. Documents were clustered and stored according to their content, creating a content-addressable memory system in which texts could be retrieved through semantic similarity rather than only by keywords. At conceptual level, documents were represented as points on a semantic map, arranged topologically so that proximity reflected similarity of content. This map then served as an interface for visual analytics, allowing users to explore the corpus through different but correlated views and iteratively select and examine documents. The expected impact was to improve the efficiency, transparency and usability of competitive-intelligence analysis. By turning complex text collections into navigable visual structures, MapIntel could support faster insight generation, better strategic planning and more informed decision-making within public administration and related policy contexts.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-480,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00471,EU_PSTW_202609_00471,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,AMA (Agência para a Modernização Administrativa),UNKNOWN,"The operational issue concerns the management of customer communication across text and voice channels, where high volumes of enquiries, inconsistent responses, and delays can negatively affect satisfaction and internal efficiency.",MILITARY CARE (improve customer satisfaction through cognitive models of text and voice),AMA (Agência para a Modernização Administrativa); Central-Government; Portugal,"The implementation uses cognitive models for text and voice, combining natural language processing, speech recognition, intent classification, sentiment analysis, and automated response-support capabilities.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"MILITARY CARE, also referred to as AIcare, focuses on improving the quality and responsiveness of interactions between public service users and the organisations responsible for handling their requests. The operational issue concerns the management of customer communication across text and voice channels, where high volumes of enquiries, inconsistent responses, and delays can negatively affect satisfaction and internal efficiency. In the Portuguese national context, the initiative is associated with AMA, the Agência para a Modernização Administrativa, and is relevant to public administrations, service agents, end users, and managers responsible for service performance. The objective is to enhance customer and business satisfaction by making service interactions more accurate, faster, and easier to manage. The implementation uses cognitive models for text and voice, combining natural language processing, speech recognition, intent classification, sentiment analysis, and automated response-support capabilities. These models can process written and spoken interactions, identify the subject and urgency of requests, route cases to appropriate channels, and support staff with suggested answers or structured information. Integration would take place at the level of contact-centre systems, knowledge bases, customer relationship management tools, and service dashboards. The expected results include shorter response times, improved consistency of information, better allocation of human operators, and a more seamless user experience. The business value lies in increasing service capacity without proportionally increasing staffing costs, while improving reliability and user satisfaction.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,NOT_IN_USE,미사용,F06_HIGH_VOLUME_SCALABILITY_BOTTLENECK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-481,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00472,EU_PSTW_202609_00472,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,AMA (Agência para a Modernização Administrativa),UNKNOWN,"Without an integrated situational-awareness capability, cybersecurity actors may struggle to coordinate responses, prioritise threats and support strategic decision-making. Its value lies in transforming fragmented cybersecurity information into usable operational intelligence.",Extended Situational and Operational Knowledge Management Service for National Cyberspace,AMA (Agência para a Modernização Administrativa); Central-Government; Portugal,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Portugal’s Extended Situational and Operational Knowledge Management Service for National Cyberspace forms part of a broader effort to strengthen national cybersecurity capacity and improve the country’s resilience against digital threats. The policy and operational problem is that public authorities require timely, structured and actionable knowledge about risks, incidents, vulnerabilities and trends affecting national cyberspace. Without an integrated situational-awareness capability, cybersecurity actors may struggle to coordinate responses, prioritise threats and support strategic decision-making. The main stakeholders include the National Cybersecurity Centre, AMA as the Portuguese Agency for Administrative Modernisation, public institutions, critical-service operators, businesses and citizens who depend on secure digital infrastructure. The implementation is centred on a knowledge-management service intended to support situational and operational awareness in cybersecurity. Conceptually, this may involve the collection, organisation and analysis of cybersecurity information, integration of threat data, dashboards for operational monitoring, incident-support workflows and mechanisms for sharing relevant knowledge among competent actors. The objective is to contribute to a safer, more prosperous and more resilient Portugal through innovative and inclusive action. The expected impact is improved capacity to monitor national cyberspace, support faster and better-informed responses to cyber risks, strengthen coordination among institutions and enhance trust in digital public services. Its value lies in transforming fragmented cybersecurity information into usable operational intelligence.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-482,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00473,EU_PSTW_202609_00473,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,AMA (Agência para a Modernização Administrativa),Economic affairs n.e.c,"Manual validation processes are resource-intensive and risk inconsistencies across operators. While no full production system has been publicly announced, the work has contributed to more robust regulatory data practices, reduced manual effort, and improved confidence in market oversight, laying the groundwork for scalable, data-driven regulation.",Methodologies for AI use in data analysis and statistical validation from markets regulated by ERSAR,AMA (Agência para a Modernização Administrativa); Central-Government; Economic affairs n.e.c; Portugal,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Public utility regulators require reliable, consistent, and timely market data to oversee service quality, pricing, and compliance. In Portugal, ERSAR regulates water, wastewater, and waste management markets, which generate large volumes of heterogeneous operational and financial data. Manual validation processes are resource-intensive and risk inconsistencies across operators. This initiative, coordinated by the Agency for Administrative Modernisation (AMA), aims to define, test, and adopt new methodologies to strengthen data analysis and statistical validation across ERSAR-regulated markets. Stakeholders include ERSAR, regulated utilities, policy analysts, and national statistical bodies. The technological approach focuses on advanced data-processing pipelines that ingest regulatory submissions, operational metrics, and historical datasets. Statistical validation techniques and pattern-recognition models are applied to detect anomalies, inconsistencies, and outliers, supporting automated pre-validation before human review. The methodologies are designed to integrate with existing regulatory information systems and reporting workflows. As a methodological programme rather than a single tool, the initiative emphasises repeatability, auditability, and governance. While no full production system has been publicly announced, the work has contributed to more robust regulatory data practices, reduced manual effort, and improved confidence in market oversight, laying the groundwork for scalable, data-driven regulation.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-483,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00474,EU_PSTW_202609_00474,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,AMA (Agência para a Modernização Administrativa),Social protection n.e.c.,"The policy problem concerns the persistence of unequal life chances and the difficulty of identifying where progress has occurred, where gaps remain and which factors may explain different outcomes for women and men.",CEOS - Knowledge of the Evolution of Opportunities between the Genders,AMA (Agência para a Modernização Administrativa); Central-Government; Social protection n.e.c.; Portugal,"At conceptual level, the project uses data-science methods to examine patterns across time, identify changes in access to key opportunities and reveal relationships between gender and socio-economic outcomes.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The CEOS project seeks to solve the need for public administrations to better understand how opportunities between genders have changed over time and how structural inequalities affect access to education, employment and social mobility. The policy problem concerns the persistence of unequal life chances and the difficulty of identifying where progress has occurred, where gaps remain and which factors may explain different outcomes for women and men. In Portugal, the project is linked to the Agência para a Modernização Administrativa and aims to provide evidence that can support more equitable public policies. The emerging technology implementation is based on the analysis of datasets covering education, labour-market participation and mobility trajectories. At conceptual level, the project uses data-science methods to examine patterns across time, identify changes in access to key opportunities and reveal relationships between gender and socio-economic outcomes. By structuring and analysing these data, the system can help transform dispersed administrative and social information into policy-relevant insights. The expected impact is improved analytical capacity for equality-oriented policymaking, allowing authorities to identify persistent barriers, monitor long-term developments and design more targeted interventions. The case supports evidence-based assessment of gender opportunities and can contribute to more transparent, data-informed strategies for reducing inequality and promoting fairer access to education, employment and social advancement.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-484,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00475,EU_PSTW_202609_00475,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,AMA (Agência para a Modernização Administrativa),General services.,"The operational challenge is to improve the interaction between ASAE, citizens and companies while ensuring a more effective response to inspection activities.",Autoridade de Segurança Alimentar e Económica (ASAE)- Intelligent Management and Operational Control Center - CIGESCOP,AMA (Agência para a Modernização Administrativa); Central-Government; General services.; Portugal,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The CIGESCOP project addresses the need to strengthen the institutional capacity of ASAE as both a criminal police body and an administrative authority responsible for food safety, consumer protection, economic inspection and the safeguarding of fair competition. The operational challenge is to improve the interaction between ASAE, citizens and companies while ensuring a more effective response to inspection activities. The project therefore aims to modernise internal processes, optimise operational control and support ASAE’s broader digital transformation. The emerging technology implementation combines process re-engineering with innovation models based on artificial intelligence and data science. At conceptual level, CIGESCOP is intended to support an intelligent management and operational control centre by integrating data-driven analysis, task automation and digital workflow optimisation. These capabilities can help organise inspection-related information, prioritise activities, streamline administrative procedures and support the allocation of human and technological resources. The expected impact is a more efficient and effective inspection authority, with improved capacity to respond to risks affecting public health, consumer rights and market fairness. By automating tasks and improving operational coordination, CIGESCOP can reduce administrative friction, increase the quality and timeliness of control activities, and generate business value for the public administration through more reliable, scalable and digitally enabled enforcement processes.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S6_WORKFLOW_PROCESS_AUTOMATION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-485,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00476,EU_PSTW_202609_00476,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,FCT (Fundação para a Ciência e Tecnologia),Secondary education.,UNKNOWN,Understanding the determinants of academic performance from Portuguese secondary education system,FCT (Fundação para a Ciência e Tecnologia); Central-Government; Secondary education.; Portugal,This project addresses the need to understand which factors influence student performance in Portuguese secondary education and how public authorities can use evidence to support better educational outcomes.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"This project addresses the need to understand which factors influence student performance in Portuguese secondary education and how public authorities can use evidence to support better educational outcomes. The work used an anonymous dataset from the 2014–15 school year provided by the Directorate-General for Statistics of Education and Science of the Portuguese Ministry of Education. The policy context concerns student achievement, educational inequality and the identification of variables that may help explain differences in high-school performance. The objective was to compare the predictive power of traditional statistical modelling with more advanced computational methods in order to assess which approaches can better support analysis of academic performance. The emerging technology implementation consisted of a comparison between a classic multilinear regression model and several machine-learning algorithms, including random forest, support vector machine, artificial neural network and an extreme gradient boosting machine stacking ensemble. At conceptual level, the models processed educational data to identify patterns and estimate student performance outcomes. The impact is mainly analytical and methodological: the project provides evidence on the usefulness of different modelling approaches for education policy and planning. By improving the understanding of performance determinants, it can support more targeted interventions, better monitoring of educational systems and more robust evidence for decision-makers seeking to improve student success in Portuguese secondary education.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-486,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00477,EU_PSTW_202609_00477,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,FCT (Fundação para a Ciência e Tecnologia),Secondary education.,The ModEst project addresses the challenge of estimating and forecasting how students move through the Portuguese education system across different regions and stages of learning.,Modeling student flow in the Portuguese education system,FCT (Fundação para a Ciência e Tecnologia); Central-Government; Secondary education.; Portugal,"It uses data from the Directorate-General for Statistics of Education and Science, Statistics Portugal, PorData and other public sources.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The ModEst project addresses the challenge of estimating and forecasting how students move through the Portuguese education system across different regions and stages of learning. Public authorities need reliable information on admissions, progression and drop-outs in order to plan resources, anticipate demographic and educational pressures, and support more effective policy decisions. The project focuses on NUTS3 regional granularity and covers the six stages of education, providing annual estimates of key variables related to student flow. It uses data from the Directorate-General for Statistics of Education and Science, Statistics Portugal, PorData and other public sources. The emerging technology implementation combines machine-learning and simulation approaches to develop predictive models of student movements. At conceptual level, these models integrate historical and administrative data to estimate flows across education stages and generate projections under different conditions. A user-centred application is also built so that the Directorate-General for Statistics of Education and Science can specify input variables, produce projections and run conditional “what-if” scenarios, while updating the models year by year. The expected impact is improved strategic planning, stronger capacity to anticipate regional education needs and better evidence for policy formulation. By enabling scenario analysis and regular model updates, ModEst supports more scalable, transparent and adaptive management of the education system.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-487,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00478,EU_PSTW_202609_00478,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,FCT (Fundação para a Ciência e Tecnologia),Secondary education.,UNKNOWN,HEALTHI.CO: Factors for promoting dialogue and healthy behaviors in online school communities.,FCT (Fundação para a Ciência e Tecnologia); Central-Government; Secondary education.; Portugal,The emerging technology implementation is based on the use of data-science methods to analyse online social conversation within school communities.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"HEALTHI.CO addresses the growing importance of online interaction within school communities and the need to better understand the dynamics of digital conversations among secondary school students and teachers. Schools increasingly rely on online spaces for communication, learning and social interaction, but these environments can also reflect or amplify exclusion, incivility and unhealthy behaviours. The project, supported in Portugal through FCT, seeks to generate evidence that can inform long-term educational programmes promoting empathy, inclusion, civility and healthy dialogue. The emerging technology implementation is based on the use of data-science methods to analyse online social conversation within school communities. At conceptual level, the project would examine patterns in digital communication, identify factors associated with constructive or harmful exchanges, and generate insights into how students and teachers interact in online environments. These findings could then be used to design or adjust educational interventions aimed at improving the quality of dialogue and strengthening positive behaviours. The expected impact is improved understanding of online school-community dynamics and stronger evidence for preventive and educational programmes. By helping schools and policymakers identify the conditions that foster respectful and inclusive communication, HEALTHI.CO can support healthier digital learning environments, improve student well-being and contribute to more effective long-term strategies for online citizenship education.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-488,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00479,EU_PSTW_202609_00479,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,AMA (Agência para a Modernização Administrativa),General services.,SARAI addresses the challenge faced by cybersecurity teams in obtaining accurate and timely information from large volumes of security-related data.,SARAI – Security Advanced Research with Artificial Intelligence,AMA (Agência para a Modernização Administrativa); Central-Government; General services.; Portugal,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"SARAI addresses the challenge faced by cybersecurity teams in obtaining accurate and timely information from large volumes of security-related data. Public organisations increasingly depend on digital infrastructures and must detect, correct and prevent the exploitation of computer and information-security flaws before they cause serious operational disruption. The objective of the project is to create a functional prototype capable of supporting cybersecurity teams in responding more effectively to threats and vulnerabilities. The emerging technology implementation explores artificial intelligence and data-science mechanisms for large-scale security analysis. At conceptual level, the prototype is intended to process high volumes of information produced by digital systems, identify relevant signals, support the detection of vulnerabilities or suspicious activity, and help teams prioritise corrective and preventive actions. The system therefore combines automated analysis with operational cybersecurity workflows, acting as a next-generation decision-support capability for security professionals. The expected impact is faster and more accurate identification of security weaknesses, improved prevention of exploitation, and better allocation of cybersecurity resources. By reducing the time needed to process large-scale information, SARAI can increase the reliability and resilience of public-sector digital systems. The case is particularly relevant for administrations seeking to strengthen cyber-defence capacity through scalable analytical tools that support, rather than replace, expert judgement.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F06_HIGH_VOLUME_SCALABILITY_BOTTLENECK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-489,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00480,EU_PSTW_202609_00480,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,AMA (Agência para a Modernização Administrativa),Education not definable by level.,CDAP@UP addresses the challenge of identifying students at risk of academic failure or dropout at the University of Porto early enough to provide effective support.,CDAP@UP (Predictive Analysis to Promote Student Success and Fight School Dropout),AMA (Agência para a Modernização Administrativa); Central-Government; Education not definable by level.; Portugal,"The project, implemented through the Data and Predictive Analytics Centre of the University of Porto, aims to support institutional decision-making by combining student data from different sources. The emerging technology implementation uses data analysis and artificial intelligence techniques to develop predictive models based on academic, sociodemographic and behavioural data.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"CDAP@UP addresses the challenge of identifying students at risk of academic failure or dropout at the University of Porto early enough to provide effective support. Universities need timely and reliable evidence to understand which students may require additional assistance and to design interventions that improve academic success and retention. The project, implemented through the Data and Predictive Analytics Centre of the University of Porto, aims to support institutional decision-making by combining student data from different sources. The emerging technology implementation uses data analysis and artificial intelligence techniques to develop predictive models based on academic, sociodemographic and behavioural data. At conceptual level, these models identify risk patterns associated with academic failure or dropout and generate early-warning information that can be used by university services. The outputs enable the university to implement personalised support actions, such as tutoring, psychological counselling, academic assistance or financial support, according to the nature of the identified risk. The expected impact is improved student success, reduced dropout rates and more efficient targeting of institutional support services. By enabling earlier intervention, CDAP@UP can enhance the student experience, strengthen educational outcomes and generate public value by helping the university allocate resources where they are most needed.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-490,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00481,EU_PSTW_202609_00481,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,AMA (Agência para a Modernização Administrativa),Secondary education.,"P.IA.ES tackles the challenge of reducing academic failure in higher education by enabling institutions to identify students at risk at an early stage of their academic path. Higher education institutions often need to intervene before poor performance becomes irreversible, but early warning is difficult when decisions must rely on limited information available at enrolment.",P.IA.ES - Modeling and prediction of failure and school dropout in Higher Education,AMA (Agência para a Modernização Administrativa); Central-Government; Secondary education.; Portugal,"This work uses a dataset from a higher education institution containing information known when students enrol, including academic trajectory, demographic characteristics and socio-economic factors. The project tests synthetic oversampling methods to promote class balancing and trains classification models using both standard machine-learning algorithms and state-of-the-art boosting algorithms.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"P.IA.ES tackles the challenge of reducing academic failure in higher education by enabling institutions to identify students at risk at an early stage of their academic path. Higher education institutions often need to intervene before poor performance becomes irreversible, but early warning is difficult when decisions must rely on limited information available at enrolment. This work uses a dataset from a higher education institution containing information known when students enrol, including academic trajectory, demographic characteristics and socio-economic factors. The objective is to support timely student-success strategies by predicting academic performance and identifying learners who may require additional support. The emerging technology implementation formulates the problem as a three-category classification task, complicated by strong class imbalance towards one of the categories. The project tests synthetic oversampling methods to promote class balancing and trains classification models using both standard machine-learning algorithms and state-of-the-art boosting algorithms. The results show that boosting algorithms perform better than standard methods for this specific task, although they still fall short in correctly identifying most cases in one minority class. The impact is mainly methodological and operational: the project demonstrates the potential of predictive modelling for early intervention, while also clarifying its limitations. Future improvements include adding first-year performance data, such as grades from initial semesters, to improve accuracy and better support targeted measures against failure and dropout.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-491,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00482,EU_PSTW_202609_00482,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,FCT (Fundação para a Ciência e Tecnologia),Hospital services.,UNKNOWN,ICDS4IM - Intelligent Support for Clinical Decision in Intensive Care Medicine,FCT (Fundação para a Ciência e Tecnologia); Central-Government; Hospital services.; Portugal,"By reducing fragmentation between monitoring devices and clinical decision processes, ICDS4IM can contribute to more reliable decision-making, more efficient use of clinical information and improved operational resilience in intensive medicine.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"ICDS4IM addresses the need for timely, reliable and high-quality information in intensive care medicine, where professionals must make decisions in fast-changing environments marked by complex and unpredictable events. Intensive care units generate continuous streams of clinical data from bedside devices, including vital-sign monitors, but the value of these data depends on their availability, accuracy and integration into clinical workflows. The project aims to support intensive medicine professionals by ensuring that relevant information is available in useful time and can contribute to better-supported decisions. The emerging technology implementation consists of an intelligent clinical decision-support architecture connected to bedside devices. At conceptual level, the system provides a communication layer between vital-sign monitors and decision-support components, allowing clinical information to be collected, structured and made available to professionals. The architecture is designed to improve information quality and availability, supporting the interpretation of real-time data in a mutable clinical environment. The expected impact is improved support for intensive care teams, better situational awareness and stronger capacity to respond to rapidly evolving patient conditions. By reducing fragmentation between monitoring devices and clinical decision processes, ICDS4IM can contribute to more reliable decision-making, more efficient use of clinical information and improved operational resilience in intensive medicine. The case is primarily focused on architectural design and prototype development for intelligent decision support in high-risk healthcare settings.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-492,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00483,EU_PSTW_202609_00483,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Universidade de Évora,Hospital services.,"SNS24 Scout.AI addresses the operational challenge of supporting nurses working in the Triage, Counselling and Referral Service of SNS24, the telephone service provided by the Contact Centre of Portugal’s National Health Service.","SNS24 Scout.AI - APPLICATION OF ARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE AND NATURAL LANGUAGE PROCESSING METHODOLOGIES IN THE TRIAGE, COUNSELLING AND REFERRAL SERVICE OF SNS24",Universidade de Évora; Academic-Research; Hospital services.; Portugal,The emerging technology implementation applies artificial intelligence and natural language processing methodologies to support the interpretation of user interactions and the selection of appropriate clinical pathways.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"SNS24 Scout.AI addresses the operational challenge of supporting nurses working in the Triage, Counselling and Referral Service of SNS24, the telephone service provided by the Contact Centre of Portugal’s National Health Service. In this context, nurses must select the most appropriate clinical algorithm and referral pathway during interactions with citizens, often under time pressure and with significant implications for access to care. The project, involving the University of Évora, aims to improve decision support in the triage process and to help the Directorate-General of Health optimise the design of clinical algorithms and their referrals. The emerging technology implementation applies artificial intelligence and natural language processing methodologies to support the interpretation of user interactions and the selection of appropriate clinical pathways. At conceptual level, the system is intended to analyse information exchanged during telephone contacts, assist the nurse in identifying the most relevant clinical algorithm, and generate evidence that can inform improvements to the structure and performance of triage algorithms. The expected impact is a more consistent, efficient and evidence-informed triage process, improving support for health professionals and potentially enhancing the user experience for citizens contacting SNS24. The project also creates value for health-system governance by helping DGS review and refine clinical algorithms on the basis of operational data and observed referral patterns.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-493,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00484,EU_PSTW_202609_00484,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Associação para a Inovação e Desenvolvimento da FCT (NOVA.ID.FCT),Public health services.,UNKNOWN,PrevOcupAI - AI applied to the prevention of work-related illnesses in Public Administration,Associação para a Inovação e Desenvolvimento da FCT (NOVA.ID.FCT); Academic-Research; Public health services.; Portugal,"The emerging technology implementation applies artificial intelligence and data-science methods to profile workers, analyse daily activities and identify clusters of work activities associated with exposure to risk factors.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"PrevOcupAI addresses the impact of work-related disorders on individual well-being, quality of life, productivity, absenteeism and the wider economy. Within public administration, occupational risks may vary across professional categories, daily activities and working environments, making it necessary to identify profiles, risk situations and indicators that can support prevention. The project, led by Associação para a Inovação e Desenvolvimento da FCT, aims to characterise potential work-related disorders in the Portuguese population and examine their relationship with professional categories and workplace risk situations. The emerging technology implementation applies artificial intelligence and data-science methods to profile workers, analyse daily activities and identify clusters of work activities associated with exposure to risk factors. At conceptual level, the system is intended to forecast the progression of occupational disorders, assess associated risk factors, and estimate socio-economic impacts linked to morbidity, mortality, reduced life expectancy and incapacity. It also supports the definition of national indicators to monitor and alert authorities to occupational risks in public administration. The expected impact is stronger preventive capacity, more targeted occupational-health policies and better evidence for managing workplace risks. By identifying risk clusters and forecasting disorder progression, PrevOcupAI can help public administrations reduce absenteeism, protect worker health and improve the sustainability and productivity of public-sector workplaces.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-494,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00485,EU_PSTW_202609_00485,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Universidade Nova de Lisboa,Public health services.,"FrailCare.AI addresses the challenge of supporting elderly citizens at risk of frailty through scalable, preventive and integrated public services.",FrailCare.AI - Intelligent trajectories of fragility for the Portuguese elderly population,Universidade Nova de Lisboa; Academic-Research; Public health services.; Portugal,"The emerging technology implementation uses intelligent analysis of frailty trajectories to support screening, follow-up and intervention planning.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"FrailCare.AI addresses the challenge of supporting elderly citizens at risk of frailty through scalable, preventive and integrated public services. As the population ages, public health systems must detect needs early, prevent adverse health events and coordinate support across health, social and safety dimensions. The proposal aims to support the national upscaling of a telecare screening and intervention service connected to SNS24 for older people with frailty risk. Its objectives include monitoring a group of elderly citizens through a telecare service, preventing health occurrences, detecting emerging needs early, promoting integrated care and contributing to healthy and active ageing. The emerging technology implementation uses intelligent analysis of frailty trajectories to support screening, follow-up and intervention planning. At conceptual level, the system would combine telecare data with risk assessment and predictive modelling to identify changes in frailty status and guide the prioritisation of support. Integration with SNS24 would allow the approach to be embedded in a national public-service context, supporting remote monitoring and coordinated responses. The expected impact is improved early detection of needs, better continuity of care and greater scalability of frailty-related public services. By enabling proactive follow-up rather than relying only on reactive care, FrailCare.AI can contribute to healthier ageing, reduced avoidable health incidents and more efficient allocation of health and social-care resources.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-495,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00486,EU_PSTW_202609_00486,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Instituto de Engenharia Mecânica (IDMEC),Public health services.,UNKNOWN,AI4Life - Artificial intelligence as a tool to reduce Premature Mortality,Instituto de Engenharia Mecânica (IDMEC); Academic-Research; Public health services.; Portugal,"The emerging technology implementation applies machine-learning techniques to discover patterns, relationships and knowledge from integrated datasets.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"AI4Life responds to the public-health objective of reducing premature mortality from non-communicable diseases, in line with the World Health Organization target of reducing such mortality by 25% by 2025. The project focuses on the infantile-juvenile population and responds to the need for a more complete understanding of the factors associated with health risks across several dimensions. Public-health authorities often hold relevant data in separate systems, limiting their ability to identify patterns and relationships that could support prevention. The project therefore aims to collect and integrate information from several data sources that had not previously been combined, in order to characterise young populations more comprehensively. The emerging technology implementation applies machine-learning techniques to discover patterns, relationships and knowledge from integrated datasets. At conceptual level, the system combines health and related contextual information to confirm existing assumptions, objectify known patterns and identify previously unknown associations that may be relevant to premature mortality. The expected impact is stronger analytical capacity for prevention-oriented public-health policy, allowing authorities to better understand risk profiles and design more targeted interventions. By integrating previously separate data sources, AI4Life can improve evidence generation, support early identification of relevant determinants and contribute to more effective strategies for reducing preventable mortality and improving population health outcomes.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-497,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00487,EU_PSTW_202609_00487,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Hospital Center of Baixo Vouga (HCBV),Hospital services.,UNKNOWN,Audit +: CHBV artificial intelligence engine and business intelligence system,Hospital Center of Baixo Vouga (HCBV); Central-Government; Hospital services.; Portugal,Its objective is to develop an artificial intelligence engine and business intelligence system that can help the hospital make better use of its data and strengthen evidence-informed governance.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Audit + addresses the need to improve information management, analytical capacity and decision support at the Hospital Centre of Baixo Vouga. Hospitals generate large volumes of clinical, administrative and operational data, but these data must be transformed into usable intelligence to support management, quality improvement and service planning. The project is led by the Hospital Centre of Baixo Vouga in partnership with the Faculty of Medicine of the University of Porto and CINTESIS. Its objective is to develop an artificial intelligence engine and business intelligence system that can help the hospital make better use of its data and strengthen evidence-informed governance. The emerging technology implementation combines advanced analytical components with business intelligence capabilities. At conceptual level, the system is intended to collect, organise and analyse hospital data, producing indicators, insights and decision-support outputs for managers and clinical or operational teams. The artificial intelligence engine can support the identification of patterns or anomalies, while the business intelligence layer makes results accessible through structured reporting and visualisation. The expected impact is improved hospital management, better monitoring of performance and greater capacity to support decisions with timely and reliable information. By integrating analytical intelligence into hospital governance, Audit + can contribute to more efficient processes, improved service quality and stronger institutional capacity for data-driven healthcare management.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-499,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00488,EU_PSTW_202609_00488,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"University Hospital Center of São João, EPE (UHCSJ)",Hospital services.,"CliKode tackles the administrative and clinical-documentation challenge of assigning accurate diagnostic and procedure codes to inpatient episodes. At conceptual level, the system analyses unstructured clinical documentation, identifies relevant medical concepts and maps them to standardised ICD-10 categories.",Inpatient AI Model Investigation for Procedure and Diagnostic Coding (CliKode),"University Hospital Center of São João, EPE (UHCSJ); Academic-Research; Hospital services.; Portugal",UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"CliKode tackles the administrative and clinical-documentation challenge of assigning accurate diagnostic and procedure codes to inpatient episodes. Hospital coding based on the ICD-10 classification system is essential for clinical reporting, financing, statistics and service management, but it requires substantial human effort and continuous monitoring to ensure consistency and completeness. The University Hospital Centre of São João, EPE, developed this project to improve the quality and efficiency of coding processes, particularly where coders must interpret large volumes of clinical text and keep coding practices aligned with evolving rules. The emerging technology implementation focuses on the automatic generation of ICD-10 diagnoses and procedure codes from clinical texts. At conceptual level, the system analyses unstructured clinical documentation, identifies relevant medical concepts and maps them to standardised ICD-10 categories. It is also designed to remain up to date over time, supporting the adaptation of coding outputs as classifications, clinical practices or documentation patterns evolve. The expected impact is higher coding quality, including an increase in the average number of coded diagnoses and procedures, and a reduction in the monitoring structure required for the codification process. By decreasing the time and number of activities dedicated to coding supervision, CliKode can reduce pressure on specialised human resources, improve hospital data quality and strengthen the reliability of administrative and clinical information.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F04_UNSTRUCTURED_INFORMATION_COMPLEXITY,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-500,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00489,EU_PSTW_202609_00489,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Instituto de Engenharia Mecânica,Hospital services.,UNKNOWN,IPOSCORE - Predict complications from surgical treatment and prognosis definition in cancer patients,Instituto de Engenharia Mecânica; Academic-Research; Hospital services.; Portugal,The case is particularly relevant for public healthcare settings seeking to improve oncology outcomes through data-driven risk stratification and more precise use of available clinical information.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"IPOSCORE responds to the need to improve risk assessment and prognosis definition for cancer patients undergoing surgical treatment. Surgical oncology involves complex decisions in which clinicians must consider patient profiles, disease characteristics and clinical data to estimate the likelihood of complications and anticipate disease progression. The project aims to support more informed clinical decision-making by identifying relevant links between patient characteristics and outcomes, including diagnostic and prognostic markers, progression rates and complication risks. The emerging technology implementation consists of a knowledge-discovery system integrating data-mining algorithms and predictive modelling techniques. At conceptual level, the system analyses patient profiles and clinical datasets to identify patterns associated with post-surgical complications in cancer patients. It also supports the exploration of relationships between disease markers, clinical trajectories and patient-specific risk factors. These models can help transform heterogeneous clinical information into structured evidence for prognosis and treatment planning. The expected impact is improved ability to anticipate complications, better definition of patient prognosis and stronger support for personalised clinical management. By identifying high-risk patients earlier, IPOSCORE can help clinicians plan follow-up, allocate resources and adapt interventions more effectively. The case is particularly relevant for public healthcare settings seeking to improve oncology outcomes through data-driven risk stratification and more precise use of available clinical information.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-501,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00490,EU_PSTW_202609_00490,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Instituto Português do Sangue e da Transplantação (IPST),Public health services.,LAIfeBlood addresses the operational challenge of improving the efficiency and resilience of Portugal’s blood supply chain.,LAIfeBlood - Artificial Intelligence for Blood Management,Instituto Português do Sangue e da Transplantação (IPST); Central-Government; Public health services.; Portugal,"Because blood is scarce and perishable, effective planning is essential to avoid shortages, minimise wastage and use collection teams and resources efficiently.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"LAIfeBlood addresses the operational challenge of improving the efficiency and resilience of Portugal’s blood supply chain. The Instituto Português do Sangue e da Transplantação is the public authority responsible for the collection and regulation of blood donation and transplantation at national level, including the quality and safety of donation, procurement, processing, storage, preservation and distribution of human blood, blood components, organs, tissues and human-origin cells. Because blood is scarce and perishable, effective planning is essential to avoid shortages, minimise wastage and use collection teams and resources efficiently. The emerging technology implementation focuses on extracting temporal and geographical patterns from historical collection and transfusion data, developing predictive models for expected collections and transfusions across different time periods and regions, and creating software tools to optimise national collection planning. At conceptual level, the system combines forecasting, optimisation and dynamic supply-chain management to help IPST plan collection events according to resource availability and demand expectations. The expected impact is improved blood-service effectiveness, reduced wastage and more efficient daily inventory rebalancing. The project also aims to generate implementable policies for IPST, strengthening national capacity to manage collections, allocate teams and respond to changing demand. By supporting evidence-based planning, LAIfeBlood can improve the reliability, scalability and sustainability of blood supply operations in Portugal.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-502,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00491,EU_PSTW_202609_00491,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Ministry of Justice,Law courts,"Judicial decision-making requires the analysis of previous court decisions, legal arguments and procedural documents, which can be time-consuming and may slow access to justice for citizens.","Modelling, Prediction and Decision in the Context of Jurisprudence",Ministry of Justice; Central-Government; Law courts; Portugal,The Ministry of Justice use case aims to provide magistrates with an additional analytical tool that helps them consult relevant jurisprudence and process incoming legal material more effectively. The emerging technology implementation uses past court decisions as a knowledge base for decision support.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"This pilot project addresses the need to support magistrates in managing legal information more efficiently and reaching conclusions more rapidly when handling inquiries or documents submitted by lawyers. Judicial decision-making requires the analysis of previous court decisions, legal arguments and procedural documents, which can be time-consuming and may slow access to justice for citizens. The Ministry of Justice use case aims to provide magistrates with an additional analytical tool that helps them consult relevant jurisprudence and process incoming legal material more effectively. The emerging technology implementation uses past court decisions as a knowledge base for decision support. At conceptual level, the system analyses judicial texts and identifies relevant patterns, precedents or similarities that may assist magistrates when examining new inquiries or documents. It is not intended to replace judicial discretion, but to improve access to structured information and reduce the time needed to locate and interpret comparable cases. The expected impact is faster handling of legal materials, more efficient judicial workflows and improved responsiveness of the justice system. By enabling magistrates to reach well-supported conclusions more quickly, the project can contribute to shorter processing times and better service to citizens. The case is relevant as an example of how data-driven tools may support legal professionals while preserving the central role of human legal judgement.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-504,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00492,EU_PSTW_202609_00492,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,AMA (Agência para a Modernização Administrativa),Cultural services.,"Cultural heritage sites face growing risks from environmental stress, structural degradation, and climate-related impacts, while heritage authorities operate with limited inspection resources.",SIAP - Artificial Intelligence System for detection and warning of risks on Heritage,AMA (Agência para a Modernização Administrativa); Central-Government; Cultural services.; Portugal,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Cultural heritage sites face growing risks from environmental stress, structural degradation, and climate-related impacts, while heritage authorities operate with limited inspection resources. The SIAP project was developed to strengthen preventive conservation by enabling earlier identification of risks affecting historical structures. Stakeholders include regional cultural heritage authorities, conservation professionals, research institutions, and local governments. The objective is to improve supervision efficiency, prioritise interventions, and reduce long-term restoration costs. The system combines sensor-based monitoring of physical and environmental parameters—such as vibration, humidity, and temperature—with advanced data-processing tools to analyse trends and detect early warning signals. Sensor data is continuously collected from monitored sites and processed through analytical models that identify deviations from normal behaviour. Alerts are generated when risk thresholds are exceeded, supporting targeted inspections and maintenance planning. The architecture is designed to integrate monitoring data with heritage management workflows and expert assessments. Public sources describe SIAP as a research-driven pilot demonstrated in collaboration between regional authorities and academic partners. While no evidence of nationwide deployment has been reported, the project has shown potential to enhance heritage protection, reduce emergency interventions, and support more sustainable conservation strategies through continuous, data-informed monitoring.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-505,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00493,EU_PSTW_202609_00493,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Portuguese government,Law courts,"Decoding the Legislation addresses the difficulty citizens face when trying to understand and consult legal texts. Legislation is often complex, technical and difficult to navigate, creating barriers to access to rights and obligations.",Decoding the Legislation,Portuguese government; Central-Government; Law courts; Portugal,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Decoding the Legislation addresses the difficulty citizens face when trying to understand and consult legal texts. Legislation is often complex, technical and difficult to navigate, creating barriers to access to rights and obligations. The project sought to facilitate citizen consultation of legal information by improving how legislation from the Diário da República Eletrónico can be searched and interpreted. The selected case studies were Consumer Rights Legislation and the Retirement Statute, both areas where citizens may need practical answers to specific legal questions. The emerging technology implementation involved the creation of repositories containing data sources and metadata from the Diário da República Eletrónico, combined with natural language processing tools. At conceptual level, legal attributes were transformed into code language, and a web application was developed to allow intelligent queries on a given law. A proof of concept demonstrated an intelligent search mechanism through which citizens could ask questions about selected legislative sets and receive automatic answers. The expected impact is improved accessibility of legislation, a better user experience for citizens and reduced friction in finding relevant legal information. By making legal consultation more intuitive, the project can support transparency, legal empowerment and more efficient interaction between citizens and public legal-information systems. It also provides a reusable model for transforming complex legal repositories into citizen-oriented digital services.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-506,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00494,EU_PSTW_202609_00494,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Supremo Tribunal de Justiça,Law courts,"IRIS addresses the challenge of analysing, summarising and disseminating court decisions within the Supreme Court of Justice. By reducing the burden of navigating lengthy and complex documents, IRIS can enhance access to legal knowledge, improve usability for different user groups and support a more transparent and efficient justice system.",IRIS – Application of Artificial Intelligence Techniques in the Supreme Court of Justice,Supremo Tribunal de Justiça; Central-Government; Law courts; Portugal,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"IRIS addresses the challenge of analysing, summarising and disseminating court decisions within the Supreme Court of Justice. Judicial decisions are complex documents that require careful study by legal professionals, but they are also increasingly relevant to citizens, researchers and other non-specialist users who need understandable access to legal information. The project has two main objectives: to develop automatic summarisation approaches for court decisions and to create a representation of judicial proceedings that can be browsed in a way that supports the decision process. The emerging technology implementation focuses on text-analysis and summarisation techniques applied to judicial documents. At conceptual level, the system is designed to generate both technical summaries, useful for legal study, processing and dissemination, and summaries that are more understandable for non-technical audiences. It also aims to represent the key elements of judicial proceedings in a structured, browsable format, making it easier to explore complex case materials. The expected impact is improved efficiency in analysing court decisions, better dissemination of jurisprudence and stronger support for judicial decision-making. By reducing the burden of navigating lengthy and complex documents, IRIS can enhance access to legal knowledge, improve usability for different user groups and support a more transparent and efficient justice system.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-507,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00495,EU_PSTW_202609_00495,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,AMA (Agência para a Modernização Administrativa),Law courts,UNKNOWN,BALCAT – Project on ballistics analysis,AMA (Agência para a Modernização Administrativa); Central-Government; Law courts; Portugal,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"BALCAT addresses the need for faster and more reliable access to information in serious-crime investigations involving firearms. In such cases, collected evidence and information about a weapon’s owner or registration status can be critical for investigative progress. Police authorities require rapid access to structured, accurate and searchable data so that forensic ballistics analysis can be connected with administrative information on weapons. The project, linked to the Agência para a Modernização Administrativa, aimed to create a database for ballistics forensic analysis that would make information on weapon registration and ownership more easily accessible to competent authorities. The emerging technology implementation consisted of a digital database and analytical tool supporting the identification of weapon-related information from available records. At conceptual level, the system links evidence from an investigation with stored data on weapons, owners and registration details, allowing police authorities to retrieve relevant information and analysis more quickly. The expected impact is a substantial reduction in the time needed to access ballistic and registration information, with authorities able to obtain relevant outputs almost immediately. This can improve investigative efficiency, support faster decision-making and strengthen the quality of forensic support in serious-crime cases. BALCAT therefore provides operational value by transforming dispersed weapon-related information into a more accessible and actionable resource for scientific police work.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-508,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00496,EU_PSTW_202609_00496,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,AMA (Agência para a Modernização Administrativa),General services.,"Given the limited public information available, the exact models, datasets and integration arrangements are not specified.",MEData - AI tools to define a strategy for predicting the behavior of Measurement Instruments,AMA (Agência para a Modernização Administrativa); Central-Government; General services.; Portugal,"The emerging technology implementation consists of analytical tools designed to model the behaviour of measurement instruments using available operational or historical data. At conceptual level, the system would use predictive modelling to identify patterns, estimate future behaviour and support decisions on maintenance, verification or monitoring priorities.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"MEData addresses the need to improve how public administration monitors and anticipates the behaviour of measurement instruments. Measurement instruments are relevant to regulatory, technical and inspection activities because their reliability can affect the quality of controls, certifications and administrative decisions. The available description indicates that the project, linked to the Agência para a Modernização Administrativa, aimed to develop artificial intelligence tools to define a strategy for predicting how such instruments behave over time. The operational problem is therefore the need to move from reactive monitoring or periodic checks towards a more anticipatory approach that can identify potential deviations, deterioration or performance issues before they affect service quality. The emerging technology implementation consists of analytical tools designed to model the behaviour of measurement instruments using available operational or historical data. At conceptual level, the system would use predictive modelling to identify patterns, estimate future behaviour and support decisions on maintenance, verification or monitoring priorities. Given the limited public information available, the exact models, datasets and integration arrangements are not specified. The expected impact is improved planning, more reliable measurement processes and better allocation of technical resources. By supporting earlier identification of possible issues, MEData can contribute to higher-quality administrative and regulatory operations, reduce the risk of failures in measurement-dependent processes and strengthen the evidence base for managing technical assets across the public sector.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-509,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00497,EU_PSTW_202609_00497,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,AMA (Agência para a Modernização Administrativa),General services.,"Artificial Intelligence Prototype-SAF appears to be an exploratory initiative linked to the Agência para a Modernização Administrativa, but the available information is limited to the project title and does not specify the public service area, institutional user group or operational process targeted. The business problem is therefore framed as the need to explore whether automation, data analysis or decision-support methods can improve an administrative process, reduce manual workload or strengthen evidence-based decision-making.",Artificial Intelligence Prototype-SAF,AMA (Agência para a Modernização Administrativa); Central-Government; General services.; Portugal,"On this basis, the use case can be described only cautiously as a prototype intended to test how advanced analytical tools could support a public-administration function associated with SAF.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Artificial Intelligence Prototype-SAF appears to be an exploratory initiative linked to the Agência para a Modernização Administrativa, but the available information is limited to the project title and does not specify the public service area, institutional user group or operational process targeted. On this basis, the use case can be described only cautiously as a prototype intended to test how advanced analytical tools could support a public-administration function associated with SAF. The business problem is therefore framed as the need to explore whether automation, data analysis or decision-support methods can improve an administrative process, reduce manual workload or strengthen evidence-based decision-making. The emerging technology implementation is identified only as an artificial intelligence prototype. At conceptual level, such a prototype would normally involve testing data-processing, pattern-recognition or decision-support capabilities on a defined administrative dataset, assessing whether outputs are accurate, useful and operationally feasible. However, the specific techniques, data sources, integration with existing systems and intended users are not available from the information provided. The impact and results should therefore be recorded as unconfirmed. The likely value of the prototype lies in experimentation, proof-of-concept development and assessment of whether the approach could be scaled or integrated into public-sector workflows. For observatory maintenance, this case should be flagged as requiring further validation, particularly to identify the meaning of SAF, the responsible body, the functional domain and any evidence of implementation beyond prototype stage.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-510,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00498,EU_PSTW_202609_00498,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,AMA (Agência para a Modernização Administrativa),General services.,PredictERPgDoc addresses the challenge of improving predictive decision-making by connecting structured enterprise resource planning data with document-management information at the Polytechnic Institute of Bragança.,PredictERPgDoc - Integration of ERP and IPB's docs management system for predictive decision making,AMA (Agência para a Modernização Administrativa); Central-Government; General services.; Portugal,"By reducing fragmentation between administrative systems, PredictERPgDoc can support more coherent planning, improve the traceability of decisions and enable public higher-education managers to use data more strategically in operational and institutional governance.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"PredictERPgDoc addresses the challenge of improving predictive decision-making by connecting structured enterprise resource planning data with document-management information at the Polytechnic Institute of Bragança. Public institutions often hold operational, financial, administrative and documentary data in separate systems, which limits their ability to generate integrated insights for management and planning. The project, linked to the Agência para a Modernização Administrativa, aims to integrate the ERP environment with IPB’s document management system so that institutional decisions can be supported by a more complete view of available information. The emerging technology implementation consists of system integration combined with predictive analytics. At conceptual level, the solution would connect transactional data from the ERP system with metadata and content from document-management workflows, enabling models to identify patterns, forecast needs or support decisions across administrative processes. Such integration can help transform dispersed institutional records into structured inputs for management intelligence. The exact algorithms, datasets and operational modules are not specified in the source description, so the technical implementation should be treated as high-level. The expected impact is improved information availability, stronger organisational intelligence and more timely decision-making. By reducing fragmentation between administrative systems, PredictERPgDoc can support more coherent planning, improve the traceability of decisions and enable public higher-education managers to use data more strategically in operational and institutional governance.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-511,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00499,EU_PSTW_202609_00499,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,AMA (Agência para a Modernização Administrativa),"General economic, commercial and labour affair","The expected impact is improved user experience, greater continuity of service and reduced pressure on administrative staff who would otherwise respond manually to recurring queries.",GeADAPTalks – Intel service,"AMA (Agência para a Modernização Administrativa); Central-Government; General economic, commercial and labour affair; Portugal",The available description indicates that the project applies artificial intelligence tools to improve the service and keep it continuously available in its various aspects for participants.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"GeADAPTalks addresses the need to improve the availability and quality of services supporting participants in evaluation processes. In public administration, evaluation activities often involve multiple stakeholders, recurring information requests and the need for timely guidance across different stages of a process. The available description indicates that the project applies artificial intelligence tools to improve the service and keep it continuously available in its various aspects for participants. The objective can therefore be understood as enhancing support, responsiveness and accessibility for users involved in evaluation procedures. The emerging technology implementation consists of an intelligent service layer that can assist participants by providing information, guidance or interaction capabilities linked to the evaluation process. At conceptual level, this may involve automated responses, knowledge retrieval, process guidance and service-support functions based on structured information about evaluation rules, procedures and user needs. However, the source does not specify the models used, the data sources, the public body responsible for the evaluation process or the degree of system integration, so these elements should remain unconfirmed. The expected impact is improved user experience, greater continuity of service and reduced pressure on administrative staff who would otherwise respond manually to recurring queries. By making service support more available and consistent, GeADAPTalks could contribute to more efficient evaluation processes, but further validation is required to confirm deployment status, users and measurable results.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,S1_KNOWLEDGE_RETRIEVAL_RAG,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-512,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00500,EU_PSTW_202609_00500,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,AMA (Agência para a Modernização Administrativa),Water supply.,"Water networks generate large volumes of operational data from meters, network assets, consumption records and maintenance activity, but this information is often fragmented across systems and difficult to translate into timely management decisions.",PAGIRAA - Analytical Platform for the Intelligent Management of Water Supply Networks,AMA (Agência para a Modernização Administrativa); Central-Government; Water supply.; Portugal,"PAGIRAA was conceived as an analytical platform for the intelligent management of water supply networks, supporting more informed decisions on system performance, resource use and operational anomalies. Although the available source provides limited operational detail, the case fits within a wider pattern of using data analytics to modernise essential infrastructure and support more proactive public-service management.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Water utilities and public administrations need reliable operational intelligence to manage supply networks, reduce losses and anticipate service problems across complex infrastructure. PAGIRAA was conceived as an analytical platform for the intelligent management of water supply networks, supporting more informed decisions on system performance, resource use and operational anomalies. The context was public-sector digital transformation in Portugal, with the aim of applying advanced analytics to a critical municipal and regional service. Water networks generate large volumes of operational data from meters, network assets, consumption records and maintenance activity, but this information is often fragmented across systems and difficult to translate into timely management decisions. The platform was designed to consolidate and analyse such data so that authorities and operators could better understand water-flow behaviour, identify irregularities and support planning. At a conceptual level, the solution would combine data integration, analytical modelling, visualisation and decision-support functionality for water-network management. Its expected value was improved efficiency, better detection of network problems, stronger evidence for investment and maintenance decisions, and more resilient management of a scarce public resource. Although the available source provides limited operational detail, the case fits within a wider pattern of using data analytics to modernise essential infrastructure and support more proactive public-service management.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,NOT_IN_USE,미사용,F06_HIGH_VOLUME_SCALABILITY_BOTTLENECK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-513,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00501,EU_PSTW_202609_00501,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,AMA (Agência para a Modernização Administrativa),Housing development.,"LAB BUPi seeks to resolve the long-standing challenge of identifying land structure and ownership in rural territories in Portugal. Incomplete or fragmented cadastral information can create difficulties for land registration, territorial planning, property management and interaction between citizens and public administration.",LAB BUPi - Creation of the Single Counter Laboratory Simplified Cadastral Information System,AMA (Agência para a Modernização Administrativa); Central-Government; Housing development.; Portugal,The emerging technology implementation centres on a digital platform using geo-referenced property information.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"LAB BUPi seeks to resolve the long-standing challenge of identifying land structure and ownership in rural territories in Portugal. Incomplete or fragmented cadastral information can create difficulties for land registration, territorial planning, property management and interaction between citizens and public administration. The reform consists of a pilot project for a simplified national cadastral information system, combined with the entry into operation of the Land Cover Monitoring System and the development of the BUPi digital land registry platform. The objective is to aggregate the information necessary for property registration and provide citizens with a physical and virtual counter that simplifies interaction with public services in the land-register domain. The emerging technology implementation centres on a digital platform using geo-referenced property information. At conceptual level, BUPi consolidates cadastral, registration and land-related data in a single service environment, allowing citizens and authorities to identify, record and manage rural properties more effectively. The reform also establishes the legal framework needed to operationalise the rural property registry and land-cover monitoring investment. The expected impact is improved accuracy and accessibility of property information, easier registration procedures, stronger territorial knowledge and better interaction between citizens and the administration. By combining digital services with geo-referenced data and a simplified counter model, LAB BUPi can improve scalability, reduce administrative barriers and support more coherent land-governance policies.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-514,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00502,EU_PSTW_202609_00502,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Universidade de Évora,Public order and safety n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,MOPREVIS - Modeling and prediction of traffic accidents in the district of Setúbal,Universidade de Évora; Academic-Research; Public order and safety n.e.c.; Portugal,"It includes a data-acquisition module that extracts information from accident documents using natural language processing and artificial intelligence techniques, as well as interfaces for automatic data collection from other systems, including traffic-intensity data in adjacent streets and climate data.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"MOPREVIS addresses the need to improve road-safety planning in the district of Setúbal by understanding where traffic accidents occur, why they happen and which locations are most likely to present future risks. The project aimed to create an information system combining several sources of information on accidents, traffic intensity and climatic conditions. The objective was to classify accident hotspots, identify determinant factors influencing the occurrence and severity of accidents, draw profiles of individuals involved, compare accident types such as collisions, pedestrian accidents and scraping incidents, and construct predictive models for both the number and severity of accidents. The emerging technology implementation consists of a digital platform with distributed architecture for storage, large-scale data processing and management. It includes a data-acquisition module that extracts information from accident documents using natural language processing and artificial intelligence techniques, as well as interfaces for automatic data collection from other systems, including traffic-intensity data in adjacent streets and climate data. An information-retrieval module supports access to the processed results. The expected impact is improved capacity for evidence-based traffic-safety interventions, more accurate identification of high-risk locations and better prioritisation of prevention measures. By integrating multiple data sources and predictive models, MOPREVIS can help authorities anticipate accident risks, allocate resources more effectively and design targeted actions to reduce accident frequency and severity.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S3_DOCUMENT_INTELLIGENCE,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-515,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00503,EU_PSTW_202609_00503,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Instituto de Engenharia de Sistemas e Computadores, Investigação e Desenvolvimento em Lisboa (INESC ID/INESC/IST/ULisboa)",Transport,UNKNOWN,iLU: Advanced Learning in Urban Data with Situational Context for Optimizing Mobility in Cities,"Instituto de Engenharia de Sistemas e Computadores, Investigação e Desenvolvimento em Lisboa (INESC ID/INESC/IST/ULisboa); Academic-Research; Transport; Portugal","The iLU project addresses the challenge of optimising urban mobility in Lisbon by making better use of heterogeneous data stored in the city’s Intelligent Management Platform. The expected impact is improved traffic anticipation, more responsive mobility management and better use of city infrastructure.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The iLU project addresses the challenge of optimising urban mobility in Lisbon by making better use of heterogeneous data stored in the city’s Intelligent Management Platform. Urban traffic management requires real-time understanding of mobility conditions, but relevant data are often distributed across different sources and may not be systematically connected with situational context. The project aims to consolidate multiple mobility data sources in the Plataforma de Gestão Inteligente de Lisboa, ensure their real-time updatability, discover actionable spatio-temporal patterns and anticipate congestion. The emerging technology implementation combines advanced predictive modelling with deep reinforcement learning. At conceptual level, the system analyses heterogeneous mobility data to detect non-trivial correlations between road traffic and contextual variables, forecast traffic congestion and support real-time mobility decisions. Deep reinforcement learning is intended to help condition city traffic positively by supporting interventions such as traffic-light control and road-message panels. The system therefore connects data integration, pattern discovery, prediction and operational decision support within an urban mobility-management environment. The expected impact is improved traffic anticipation, more responsive mobility management and better use of city infrastructure. By enabling real-time, data-driven interventions, iLU can contribute to reduced congestion, improved reliability of urban travel, more efficient traffic-control operations and stronger strategic capacity for mobility planning in Lisbon.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION|S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-516,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00504,EU_PSTW_202609_00504,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Universidade do Minho,Water supply.,"Inefficient planning can increase costs, reduce service reliability and limit the capacity of water systems to respond to stress events or long-term sustainability objectives.",PAMWater: Analytical and Predictive Models for Optimization of Multimunicipal Water Systems,Universidade do Minho; Academic-Research; Water supply.; Portugal,"The use of predictive models can support decisions on resource allocation, infrastructure management, demand anticipation and operational performance.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"PAMWater addresses the need to improve the efficiency, resilience and sustainability of multimunicipal water systems. Water utilities and public decision-makers must manage complex infrastructures where demand, supply, operational constraints and environmental pressures vary over time and across territories. Inefficient planning can increase costs, reduce service reliability and limit the capacity of water systems to respond to stress events or long-term sustainability objectives. The project, led by the University of Minho, aims to create a predictive and analytical platform that supports decision-makers by giving them access to a broad set of descriptive and predictive information. The emerging technology implementation consists of deep-learning models embedded in a decision-support platform for multimunicipal water systems. At conceptual level, the platform would process operational, temporal and system-level data to identify patterns, forecast relevant variables and provide analytical outputs for planning and optimisation. The use of predictive models can support decisions on resource allocation, infrastructure management, demand anticipation and operational performance. The expected impact is improved decision-making, stronger system resilience and more sustainable management of water resources. By consolidating descriptive and predictive information in a single platform, PAMWater can help public and utility stakeholders anticipate operational needs, reduce inefficiencies and improve the reliability and scalability of water-system management across multiple municipalities.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION|S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-517,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00505,EU_PSTW_202609_00505,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Universidade Nova de Lisboa,Environmental protection n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,MATISSE - Bivalve health: predictive and early warning system,Universidade Nova de Lisboa; Academic-Research; Environmental protection n.e.c.; Portugal,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"MATISSE addresses the environmental, public-health and economic risks created by harmful algal blooms and shellfish biotoxin contamination. Under favourable climate and oceanographic conditions, toxin-producing microalgae can proliferate, reach high concentrations in seawater, accumulate in shellfish and threaten seafood consumers. These events can also cause significant economic losses for shellfish farmers when harvesting or production must be restricted. The project, linked to Universidade Nova de Lisboa, responds to the need for more effective tools that help shellfish producers and public environmental authorities anticipate harmful algal bloom events and manage contamination risks. The emerging technology implementation focuses on statistical and machine-learning forecasting tools for predicting harmful algal blooms and shellfish biotoxin contamination. The reviewed approaches include autoregressive models, support vector machines, random forests, probabilistic graphical models and artificial neural networks, with more complex models increasingly supported by multi-source data. At conceptual level, these models analyse environmental, oceanographic and biological information to forecast bloom events or contamination risks and support early warning. The expected impact is improved decision-making for harvesting, production management and mitigation measures. By providing earlier and more accurate warnings, MATISSE can help reduce production losses, protect consumer health and strengthen the evidence base used by governmental agencies with environmental responsibilities.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-518,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00506,EU_PSTW_202609_00506,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Associação do Instituto Superior Técnico para a Investigação e o Desenvolvimento,"Agriculture, forestry, fishing and hunting","Manual verification can be costly, time-consuming and difficult to scale across extensive pasture areas. By automating parts of the monitoring process, GrassData can reduce administrative burden and support more credible environmental reporting.","GrassData - Identification, verification and quantification of CO2 sequestration in pastures","Associação do Instituto Superior Técnico para a Investigação e o Desenvolvimento; Academic-Research; Agriculture, forestry, fishing and hunting; Portugal","Public authorities and environmental programmes require reliable evidence to confirm whether declared practices, such as bush management or the existence of harrows, are actually being implemented and producing the expected environmental benefits.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"GrassData addresses the need to monitor and verify land-management practices that improve soil protection and carbon sequestration in pastures. Public authorities and environmental programmes require reliable evidence to confirm whether declared practices, such as bush management or the existence of harrows, are actually being implemented and producing the expected environmental benefits. Manual verification can be costly, time-consuming and difficult to scale across extensive pasture areas. The project, led by the Associação do Instituto Superior Técnico para a Investigação e o Desenvolvimento, aims to develop algorithms that support monitoring, verification and compliance assessment in relation to soil and carbon-related practices. The emerging technology implementation consists of automated algorithms designed to verify the results of pasture-management practices that contribute to carbon sequestration. At conceptual level, the system would analyse relevant data on land conditions, management activities and environmental indicators to detect whether required practices are present and to support quantification of CO2 sequestration outcomes. The exact datasets and model architecture are not specified in the source description, so the implementation should be understood as an algorithmic monitoring and verification capability. The expected impact is more efficient compliance control, stronger evidence for climate and land-management policies, and improved capacity to scale verification across pasture areas. By automating parts of the monitoring process, GrassData can reduce administrative burden and support more credible environmental reporting.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-519,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00507,EU_PSTW_202609_00507,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,NOVA.ID.FCT,Fire-protection services,AI-4-MUFF addresses the challenge of improving strategic decision-making for urban firefighting.,AI-4-MUFF - AI for the management of the degree of readiness in urban firefighting,NOVA.ID.FCT; Academic-Research; Fire-protection services; Portugal,"Decisions on preparedness and response capacity need to be based on empirical evidence and must balance multiple objectives, such as response time, coverage, resource use and operational resilience. The expected impact is improved evidence-based planning for urban firefighting, better readiness management and stronger capacity to evaluate strategic options before they are implemented.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"AI-4-MUFF addresses the challenge of improving strategic decision-making for urban firefighting. Fire services must maintain an appropriate degree of readiness while facing uncertainty regarding incident occurrence, resource availability, operational priorities and urban risk conditions. Decisions on preparedness and response capacity need to be based on empirical evidence and must balance multiple objectives, such as response time, coverage, resource use and operational resilience. The project, led by NOVA.ID.FCT, intends to develop a strategic decision-support system for combating urban fires. The emerging technology implementation combines machine-learning techniques, multi-objective optimisation models and an agent-based simulation environment. At conceptual level, machine-learning methods can be used to extract patterns from historical or operational fire-service data, while optimisation models support the allocation of resources across competing objectives. Agent-based simulation allows the modelling of interactions between units, incidents, urban environments and decision rules, enabling the testing of different preparedness scenarios. The expected impact is improved evidence-based planning for urban firefighting, better readiness management and stronger capacity to evaluate strategic options before they are implemented. By integrating prediction, optimisation and simulation, AI-4-MUFF can support more efficient resource deployment, increase service reliability and improve the ability of fire authorities to respond effectively to urban fire risks.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-520,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00508,EU_PSTW_202609_00508,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Associação do Instituto Superior Técnico para a Investigação e o Desenvolvimento,Public order and safety n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,RELIABLE - Building occupant risk warning panel during extreme weather events,Associação do Instituto Superior Técnico para a Investigação e o Desenvolvimento; Academic-Research; Public order and safety n.e.c.; Portugal,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"RELIABLE addresses the need to protect building occupants during extreme weather events, particularly heat and cold waves. Such events can create significant health risks, especially for vulnerable populations, and public authorities need timely, spatially detailed information to identify where risks are concentrated. Conventional warning systems may not provide sufficiently granular information on building-level or localised exposure, limiting the ability to target prevention and response measures. The project, led by the Associação do Instituto Superior Técnico para a Investigação e o Desenvolvimento, aims to develop a public geographic dashboard providing real-time information and high spatial resolution on the health risks faced by building occupants during extreme weather events. The emerging technology implementation consists of a geographic dashboard supported by real-time data processing and spatial risk modelling. At conceptual level, the system would combine environmental, geographic and building-related information to estimate occupant health risk during heat and cold waves and present it through an accessible public interface. The expected impact is improved situational awareness for authorities and citizens, more targeted risk communication and better prioritisation of preventive measures during extreme weather. By making high-resolution risk information available in real time, RELIABLE can support public-health preparedness, improve the user experience of warning services and strengthen resilience to climate-related hazards.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-521,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00509,EU_PSTW_202609_00509,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,AMA (Agência para a Modernização Administrativa),"Agriculture, forestry, fishing and hunting",UNKNOWN,IVDP DATA+ - AI models for processing relevant data for the economy of the Douro Wine Region.,"AMA (Agência para a Modernização Administrativa); Central-Government; Agriculture, forestry, fishing and hunting; Portugal",UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"IVDP DATA+ addresses the need to strengthen data-informed decision-making for the Douro Wine Region, an economically and culturally significant territory where public and sectoral authorities must manage complex information on production, markets and regional dynamics. The project is based on a new model of experimentation developed in close collaboration with the scientific community, with the aim of promoting knowledge transfer and adopting advanced tools and techniques from artificial intelligence and data science. The objective is to support decisions by processing existing data more effectively and transforming them into useful analytical insights for the region’s economy. The emerging technology implementation consists of artificial intelligence models and data-science methods applied to relevant datasets held or used in the Douro wine-sector context. At conceptual level, the models process existing information to identify patterns, generate forecasts or support scenario-based understanding of variables affecting the regional wine economy. The source description does not specify the exact datasets, models or operational modules, so the implementation should be recorded at a high level. The expected impact is improved analytical capacity, stronger collaboration between public institutions and scientific actors, and better evidence for strategic decisions affecting the Douro Wine Region. By creating a data-driven experimentation model, IVDP DATA+ can support more informed governance, improve planning and contribute to the competitiveness and resilience of the regional economy.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-522,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00510,EU_PSTW_202609_00510,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,AMA (Agência para a Modernização Administrativa),Environmental protection n.e.c.,"Ensuring equitable access to services of general interest—such as healthcare, education, transport, and administrative services—remains a major challenge in territories affected by demographic change and geographic disparities.",i-Territory – Intelligent Modeling of Territorial Access to Services of General Interest,AMA (Agência para a Modernização Administrativa); Central-Government; Environmental protection n.e.c.; Portugal,Public documentation positions i-Territory as an enabling framework and pilot rather than a fully deployed national system.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Ensuring equitable access to services of general interest—such as healthcare, education, transport, and administrative services—remains a major challenge in territories affected by demographic change and geographic disparities. The i-Territory initiative, promoted by AMA, seeks to improve how public authorities understand and plan territorial service accessibility. Stakeholders include central and local administrations, regional planners, and service providers. The objective is to support evidence-based territorial planning that reduces inequalities and improves service coverage. The initiative relies on the integration of geospatial data, demographic statistics, service location data, and mobility information into territorial analysis models. These models simulate accessibility scenarios, identify underserved areas, and assess the impact of policy options such as service relocation or new infrastructure. The system is designed to interoperate with public-sector data platforms and geographic information systems, enabling scenario analysis rather than operational decision automation. Public documentation positions i-Territory as an enabling framework and pilot rather than a fully deployed national system. Its value lies in improving analytical capacity, supporting more transparent planning decisions, and helping administrations anticipate territorial needs, even though large-scale operational adoption has not yet been publicly confirmed.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-523,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00511,EU_PSTW_202609_00511,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Genoa,General services.,"The administrative challenge is to ensure that reports from residents are received, assigned to the competent office, resolved and communicated back to the citizen in a transparent and traceable manner.",SegnalaCI - Transparent management of citizen reports about City status,Municipality of Genoa; Local Government; General services.; Italy,The objective is to improve the management of citizen feedback and use the resulting information to support better staff and resource planning.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"SegnalaCI provides the Municipality of Genoa with a digital channel for managing citizen reports on urban problems, such as road damage, waste collection issues or other maintenance needs affecting the city. The administrative challenge is to ensure that reports from residents are received, assigned to the competent office, resolved and communicated back to the citizen in a transparent and traceable manner. The stakeholders include residents, municipal departments, technical offices, maintenance teams, city administrators and service managers responsible for urban quality. The objective is to improve the management of citizen feedback and use the resulting information to support better staff and resource planning. The implementation allows the administration to take charge of a report, notify the relevant office, manage the intervention and inform the citizen of the outcome once the procedure is completed. Machine-learning techniques are used to process the data gathered through reports and provide a smart dashboard for city administrators. At a conceptual level, the system combines citizen-report intake, workflow routing, notification mechanisms, data classification, dashboard analytics and planning support. The expected impact is more transparent handling of local problems, quicker assignment of issues to responsible units, improved communication with residents and better evidence for allocating municipal resources. By analysing recurring reports and operational needs, the municipality can move from reactive case handling towards more informed urban-service management.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-524,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00512,EU_PSTW_202609_00512,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City Council of Sant Feliu de Llobregat,General services.,UNKNOWN,Brain4it : monitoring the SmartCity,City Council of Sant Feliu de Llobregat; Local Government; General services.; Spain,"City administrations need tools that can automate infrastructure control, support data governance and adapt to new use cases without relying on fragmented or closed technical environments. It uses a functional language designed to facilitate the implementation of expert systems and machine-learning applications.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Brain4it was developed by the City Council of Sant Feliu de Llobregat as an open-source expert-system platform for managing Smart City infrastructure and supporting Internet of Things applications. The operational problem is that connected urban infrastructures generate diverse data flows and require remote, flexible and secure management across multiple municipal systems. City administrations need tools that can automate infrastructure control, support data governance and adapt to new use cases without relying on fragmented or closed technical environments. The stakeholders include the City Council, municipal technical teams, Smart City operators, residents and developers working with urban IoT services. The platform runs as a network service and provides a REST API, enabling remote control and programming of the City-OS platform. It uses a functional language designed to facilitate the implementation of expert systems and machine-learning applications. Conceptually, Brain4it supports modular automation, rules-based reasoning, IoT integration, infrastructure monitoring, security management and adaptable service development. Its open-source character allows the municipality and wider developer community to reuse and extend the platform for different Smart City needs. The expected impact is more efficient infrastructure management, easier integration of IoT services, reduced dependence on proprietary solutions and greater flexibility in developing new municipal applications. By combining expert-system logic with programmable interfaces, Brain4it strengthens the city’s capacity to manage urban systems in a scalable and adaptable way.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-525,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00513,EU_PSTW_202609_00513,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""social_nonprofit""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,ISTI-CNT (National Research Center),General services.,"In practice, this approach is often inaccurate because it relies on only two variables and cannot capture complex relationships between the characteristics of places.",Deep Gravity: the algorithm that explains how people move,ISTI-CNT (National Research Center); Academic-Research; General services.; Italy,"The emerging technology implementation extends the gravitational model by incorporating multiple variables that describe points of interest in each location, such as restaurants, hotels, hospitals and streets, and by using deep learning to capture complex relationships among these variables.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Deep Gravity addresses the limitations of traditional mobility-flow prediction models used to estimate how people move between places, both on foot and by vehicle. The conventional gravitational model, inspired by Newton’s law of universal gravitation, estimates flows between two locations as proportional to population and inversely proportional to distance. In practice, this approach is often inaccurate because it relies on only two variables and cannot capture complex relationships between the characteristics of places. The Institute of Science and Information Technologies of the Italian National Research Council, together with the Bruno Kessler Foundation and Argonne National Laboratory, developed Deep Gravity to improve mobility prediction. The emerging technology implementation extends the gravitational model by incorporating multiple variables that describe points of interest in each location, such as restaurants, hotels, hospitals and streets, and by using deep learning to capture complex relationships among these variables. At conceptual level, the algorithm learns how the attractiveness and structure of places influence mobility flows, improving predictions beyond distance and population alone. The expected impact is more accurate mobility-flow estimation, which can support public authorities in transport planning, urban policy, infrastructure design and emergency management. By better explaining how people move, Deep Gravity can improve decision-making in mobility-related public services and provide a more scalable analytical basis for understanding urban and regional movement patterns.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-526,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00514,EU_PSTW_202609_00514,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Hospital G.U. Gregorio Marañón | Madrid, Spain",R&D health.,UNKNOWN,FAITH - monitoring mental health with Artificial Intelligence - SPAIN Pilot,"Hospital G.U. Gregorio Marañón | Madrid, Spain; Academic-Research; R&D health.; Spain","At conceptual level, the system uses patient monitoring data and related quality-of-life indicators to detect patterns that may signal psychological or cognitive disorders.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The FAITH Spain pilot addresses the need to improve aftercare for people who have undergone cancer treatment, with particular attention to mental health and quality of life. Cancer survivorship can involve depression, psychological distress and unmet aftercare needs that may not always be detected during routine clinical follow-up. The Spanish pilot, led by Hospital General Universitario Gregorio Marañón in Madrid, focuses on 100 breast-cancer survivors. Its objective is to monitor patients after treatment, evaluate their quality of life and identify possible care needs so that healthcare professionals can provide more personalised interventions. The emerging technology implementation consists of an artificial intelligence application designed to identify and analyse markers associated with depression in people treated for cancer. At conceptual level, the system uses patient monitoring data and related quality-of-life indicators to detect patterns that may signal psychological or cognitive disorders. These insights can support doctors and other healthcare professionals in defining protocols, standards of care and early-intervention pathways. The expected impact is earlier detection of aftercare needs, better personalisation of support and improved patient quality of life. By helping clinicians identify patients who may require additional follow-up, FAITH can strengthen survivorship care, improve continuity between clinical treatment and psychosocial support, and provide evidence for more systematic mental-health monitoring in oncology services.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-527,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00515,EU_PSTW_202609_00515,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Champalimaud Centre for the Unknown | Lisbon, Portugal",R&D health.,"The FAITH Portugal pilot addresses the challenge of supporting the mental health and quality of life of lung-cancer patients during and after oncological treatment. Psychological distress, cognitive changes and broader psychosocial needs can affect patients’ recovery and everyday functioning, but these needs may be difficult to identify consistently without structured follow-up.",FAITH - monitoring mental health with Artificial Intelligence - PORTUGAL Pilot,"Champalimaud Centre for the Unknown | Lisbon, Portugal; Academic-Research; R&D health.; Portugal","At conceptual level, the system uses these data to detect patterns that may indicate aftercare needs or risks to mental well-being.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The FAITH Portugal pilot addresses the challenge of supporting the mental health and quality of life of lung-cancer patients during and after oncological treatment. Psychological distress, cognitive changes and broader psychosocial needs can affect patients’ recovery and everyday functioning, but these needs may be difficult to identify consistently without structured follow-up. The Champalimaud Clinical Centre in Lisbon participates as a clinical partner and will involve 100 ambulatory, community-dwelling lung-cancer patients of any type with an indication for oncological treatment over a two-year period. The objective is to monitor patients for 12 months after their first appointment and offer intelligent post-cancer support. The emerging technology implementation consists of an artificial intelligence application that identifies and analyses depression markers in people who have undergone cancer treatment. In the Portuguese pilot, patients receive psychological and cognitive assessments through psychosocial questionnaires, neuropsychological testing and other relevant constructs. At conceptual level, the system uses these data to detect patterns that may indicate aftercare needs or risks to mental well-being. The expected impact is more proactive and personalised post-cancer support, improved early detection of psychological or cognitive difficulties, and better evidence for care planning. By embedding structured monitoring into oncology follow-up, FAITH can support clinicians in improving continuity of care and enhancing quality of life for lung-cancer patients.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-528,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00516,EU_PSTW_202609_00516,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"UPMC | Waterford, Ireland",R&D health.,UNKNOWN,FAITH - monitoring mental health with Artificial Intelligence - IRELAND Pilot,"UPMC | Waterford, Ireland; Academic-Research; R&D health.; Ireland","UPMC Waterford is the main Irish pilot test-trial site, focusing on breast-cancer patients as the selected use case and target group. The objective is to use FAITH to identify patients who would benefit from additional care and ensure that support needs are detected in a timely manner.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The FAITH Ireland pilot addresses the need to integrate mental-health and well-being monitoring into cancer care for patients undergoing treatment or who have completed treatment. UPMC Waterford is the main Irish pilot test-trial site, focusing on breast-cancer patients as the selected use case and target group. The clinical rationale is that patient well-being and mental health are as important as access to advanced oncology care, particularly when patients may require follow-up from oncologists, general practitioners or local cancer-support services. The objective is to use FAITH to identify patients who would benefit from additional care and ensure that support needs are detected in a timely manner. The emerging technology implementation consists of an artificial intelligence application that identifies and analyses depression markers in people treated for cancer. At conceptual level, the tool processes patient-related information collected through monitoring and assessment to identify signals that may indicate psychological distress or aftercare needs. These outputs can help clinicians decide whether a patient should receive follow-up from the oncology team, a GP or a local Cancer Support Centre. The expected impact is improved identification of patients requiring psychosocial or clinical follow-up, better integration of mental-health support into oncology pathways and a more personalised care experience. By supporting earlier referral decisions, FAITH can contribute to more holistic cancer survivorship care.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-529,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00517,EU_PSTW_202609_00517,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,CSI Piemonte (Inhouse ICT of Regione Piemonte),Fire-protection services,"SAFERS addresses the growing challenge of forest fires, whose impact has increased in connection with climate change and which damage ecosystems, communities, productive activities and air quality through greenhouse-gas and pollutant emissions.",SAFERS - Structured Approaches for Forest fire Emergencies in Resilient Societies,CSI Piemonte (Inhouse ICT of Regione Piemonte); Consortium; Fire-protection services; Italy,"The platform uses multiple data sources, including Copernicus Sentinel satellite data, ad hoc monitoring cameras, social media, and mobile-phone inputs from professional users, volunteers and citizens.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"SAFERS addresses the growing challenge of forest fires, whose impact has increased in connection with climate change and which damage ecosystems, communities, productive activities and air quality through greenhouse-gas and pollutant emissions. Public authorities, emergency services, volunteers and citizens require better information to prevent, monitor and manage forest-fire emergencies. The project, involving CSI Piemonte as the in-house ICT body of Regione Piemonte, aims to develop an innovative platform over three years to improve forest-fire management and reduce the impact of future events. The emerging technology implementation consists of a Big Data platform combined with information systems based on artificial intelligence. The platform uses multiple data sources, including Copernicus Sentinel satellite data, ad hoc monitoring cameras, social media, and mobile-phone inputs from professional users, volunteers and citizens. Citizens and field actors can report observed conditions through chatbots, enriching real-time situational awareness. At conceptual level, the system integrates earth observation, user-generated reports, sensor data and emergency-management workflows to support detection, assessment and response. The expected impact is improved forest-fire preparedness, faster access to operational information, better coordination among actors and stronger capacity to manage emergencies. By combining satellite monitoring with local observations and automated analysis, SAFERS can improve scalability, responsiveness and resilience in forest-fire governance.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI|S7_GENERATIVE_CONTENT_CREATION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-530,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00518,EU_PSTW_202609_00518,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,CSI Piemonte (Inhouse ICT of Regione Piemonte),General services.,"The operational problem was that emails arriving in public-service channels required manual reading, categorisation and forwarding, creating delays and repetitive workload. The expected impact is faster email handling, fewer manual sorting tasks, improved consistency of routing and greater capacity for staff to focus on exceptions or complex cases.",AI Autonomation - Automatic classification tools and integration with Robot Process Automation (RPA),CSI Piemonte (Inhouse ICT of Regione Piemonte); Regional Government; General services.; Italy,"CSI Piemonte developed an AI Automation prototype to combine automatic classification tools with Robotic Process Automation, using the regional car-tax management service as a concrete test case. The implementation uses text classification to sort incoming emails according to their content and direct them to the relevant offices.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Public administrations receive large volumes of email and document-based requests that must be routed to the correct office before any substantive work can begin. CSI Piemonte developed an AI Automation prototype to combine automatic classification tools with Robotic Process Automation, using the regional car-tax management service as a concrete test case. The operational problem was that emails arriving in public-service channels required manual reading, categorisation and forwarding, creating delays and repetitive workload. The implementation uses text classification to sort incoming emails according to their content and direct them to the relevant offices. Where the content and process conditions allow, the system can also trigger RPA workflows so that routine responses or administrative actions are handled automatically. At a conceptual level, the solution combines natural language processing, classification models, workflow routing and robotic process execution. The expected impact is faster email handling, fewer manual sorting tasks, improved consistency of routing and greater capacity for staff to focus on exceptions or complex cases. The use case is framed by CSI Piemonte as research and development work from 2019, centred on a prototype for the regional vehicle-tax service. It demonstrates how relatively narrow administrative processes can be automated by pairing AI classification with process automation rather than trying to replace entire services.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,S6_WORKFLOW_PROCESS_AUTOMATION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-531,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00519,EU_PSTW_202609_00519,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""social_nonprofit""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,CSI Piemonte (Inhouse ICT of Regione Piemonte),R&D health.,"Healthcare professionals, geneticists and researchers face challenges in rapidly interpreting whole-genome data to support personalised diagnosis and therapeutic decision-making, particularly in nephrology and other rare disease domains.",Genome representation and artificial intelligence,CSI Piemonte (Inhouse ICT of Regione Piemonte); Regional Government; R&D health.; Italy,"To address this need, CSI Piemonte, in collaboration with the University of Turin (Departments of Medical Sciences and Clinical and Biological Sciences), the ISI Foundation and GenomSys, launched a research initiative aimed at improving genomic data analysis for clinical use. The project leverages advanced genome representation techniques to encode large-scale sequencing data into structured, computationally efficient formats.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The diagnosis and treatment of rare diseases are often hindered by the complexity and volume of genomic data, which require specialised expertise and advanced analytical capabilities to translate raw sequencing results into clinically meaningful insights. Healthcare professionals, geneticists and researchers face challenges in rapidly interpreting whole-genome data to support personalised diagnosis and therapeutic decision-making, particularly in nephrology and other rare disease domains. To address this need, CSI Piemonte, in collaboration with the University of Turin (Departments of Medical Sciences and Clinical and Biological Sciences), the ISI Foundation and GenomSys, launched a research initiative aimed at improving genomic data analysis for clinical use. The objective is to enable faster, more accurate identification of genetic variants associated with rare diseases and to support the design of targeted diagnostic and treatment pathways. The project leverages advanced genome representation techniques to encode large-scale sequencing data into structured, computationally efficient formats. Machine learning models are developed to analyse genomic variants, detect patterns linked to specific pathologies and prioritise clinically relevant mutations. The system integrates genomic databases, clinical records and specialised bioinformatics pipelines, enabling automated variant interpretation and decision support within research and clinical environments. The initiative enhances the speed and reliability of genomic analysis, reducing the time required to reach a diagnosis and improving the precision of treatment planning. By supporting personalised medicine approaches, it contributes to better clinical outcomes for rare disease patients while increasing analytical scalability and fostering collaboration between research institutions and healthcare providers.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-532,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00520,EU_PSTW_202609_00520,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,CSI Piemonte (Inhouse ICT of Regione Piemonte),General services.,UNKNOWN,Citizen Conversational AI Platform,CSI Piemonte (Inhouse ICT of Regione Piemonte); Regional Government; General services.; Italy,"The objective was to engineer and automate bot production so that new services could be implemented quickly, while improving the effectiveness of conversations through context management and more fluid dialogue mechanisms. The emerging technology implementation consists of chatbot prototypes using conversational user-experience design, context management and artificial intelligence technologies.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Citizen Conversational AI Platform addresses the need to make digital public services more accessible through natural-language interaction. Citizens often need to navigate complex administrative information, and traditional interfaces may require users to understand service structures or search terms before finding the right answer. CSI Piemonte developed prototypes to explore how chatbots and conversational interfaces could support interaction with regional services in Piedmont. The objective was to engineer and automate bot production so that new services could be implemented quickly, while improving the effectiveness of conversations through context management and more fluid dialogue mechanisms. The emerging technology implementation consists of chatbot prototypes using conversational user-experience design, context management and artificial intelligence technologies. At conceptual level, the platform supports everyday natural-language requests, maintains elements of the current conversation, manages list references and provides refined responses. CSI Piemonte produced prototypes for searching general practitioners and paediatricians of the Piedmont Region through Alexa and Google Home, including search-refinement capabilities. The expected impact is improved user experience, faster access to information and increased scalability in the production of conversational public-service tools. By making services available through familiar voice assistants and conversational channels, the platform can reduce barriers for users, support more intuitive interaction with public administration and create reusable components for future chatbot-based services.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-533,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00521,EU_PSTW_202609_00521,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,THALES SIX GTS FRANCE SAS,General services.,"European researchers, innovators, businesses and public-sector users often operate across fragmented communities, which can limit reuse, collaboration and access to technical assets. The expected impact is increased European capacity in artificial intelligence, stronger collaboration across countries and sectors, and easier access to tools for organisations that may lack in-house capabilities.",AI4EU: Boosting Europe’s artificial intelligence capacity,THALES SIX GTS FRANCE SAS; Consortium; General services.; France,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"AI4EU addresses the strategic need to strengthen Europe’s artificial intelligence ecosystem by bringing together knowledge, algorithms, tools and resources in a common environment. European researchers, innovators, businesses and public-sector users often operate across fragmented communities, which can limit reuse, collaboration and access to technical assets. Launched in January 2019 as a €20 million European Union flagship project involving more than 70 partners across 21 countries, AI4EU aimed to unify Europe’s AI community and make capabilities more accessible to a broad range of users. The emerging technology implementation consists of a European platform designed to share expertise, knowledge, algorithms, tools and resources. At conceptual level, the platform provides an infrastructure for collaboration across research, innovation and business, enabling users to discover and reuse AI-related assets and contribute to collective work. The project was planned to run for three years, with the platform under development at the time described. The expected impact is increased European capacity in artificial intelligence, stronger collaboration across countries and sectors, and easier access to tools for organisations that may lack in-house capabilities. By consolidating resources and supporting a shared ecosystem, AI4EU can reduce duplication, promote innovation and strengthen Europe’s competitiveness and strategic autonomy in advanced digital technologies.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-534,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00522,EU_PSTW_202609_00522,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Ravenna,General services.,"DARE addresses the challenge of supporting urban regeneration in middle-sized cities through a citizen-centred and digitally enabled governance model. The policy challenge is not only technical, but also organisational: public administration must act as an enabler of collaboration between citizens, economic actors and strategic policy objectives.",DARE: Digital Environment for collaborative Alliances to Regenerate urban Ecosystems in middle-sized cities,Municipality of Ravenna; Local Government; General services.; Italy,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"DARE addresses the challenge of supporting urban regeneration in middle-sized cities through a citizen-centred and digitally enabled governance model. The project focuses on the Darsena area in Ravenna and aims to demonstrate an approach that can facilitate, support and accelerate the implementation and evaluation of the regeneration process, with potential replication in other European middle-sized cities. The policy challenge is not only technical, but also organisational: public administration must act as an enabler of collaboration between citizens, economic actors and strategic policy objectives. The emerging technology implementation consists of a three-layer digital environment composed of a data-management platform, an editorial content-management layer and a virtual-realm presentation layer. At conceptual level, this environment enables the collection, organisation and presentation of information needed to activate urban actors and support collaborative decision-making. The project also introduces a process-organisers team to connect strategy, economic opportunities and citizen needs, while promoting digital culture among residents so that they can become digitally aware city changers. The expected impact is improved coordination of urban-regeneration activities, stronger citizen participation and more transparent evaluation of interventions. By combining digital infrastructure with a revised public-administration role, DARE can increase the scalability and replicability of participatory regeneration models in other European urban contexts.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-535,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00523,EU_PSTW_202609_00523,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Lepida Spa (inhouse ICT of Regione Emilia Romagna),Environmental protection n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,RainBO (follow-up of BLUEAP Bologna Urban Environment Adaptation Plan for a resilient city),Lepida Spa (inhouse ICT of Regione Emilia Romagna); Regional Government; Environmental protection n.e.c.; Italy,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"RainBO addresses the need to improve Bologna’s resilience to severe rainfall and sudden flooding, particularly in relation to the hydrological response of small watercourses within the urban area. Extreme rainfall can create fast-moving local risks for citizens, infrastructure and municipal services, especially where watercourses react quickly to intense precipitation. As a follow-up to the BLUEAP Bologna Urban Environment Adaptation Plan, the project focuses on improving methods and tools that allow authorities to predict severe rainfall events and assess their likely impact. Lepida Spa, the in-house ICT body of Regione Emilia-Romagna, is identified as the public technology stakeholder. The emerging technology implementation consists of a monitoring and modelling system designed to forecast sudden flooding events. At conceptual level, the system combines rainfall prediction, hydrological modelling and local monitoring data to understand how small urban watercourses may respond to severe weather. These outputs can support early warning, preparedness and operational decision-making by public authorities. The expected impact is higher resilience in vulnerable urban watercourses, improved situational awareness before and during heavy rainfall, and stronger capacity to anticipate flood risks. By connecting monitoring with predictive modelling, RainBO can help authorities move from reactive emergency response towards more preventive and evidence-based management of rainfall-related urban hazards.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-536,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00524,EU_PSTW_202609_00524,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Inno-focus Businessconsulting GmbH,R&D general public services.,"Organisations seeking to apply advanced analytical tools in production contexts often face difficulties in finding appropriate partners, accessing relevant data environments and assembling reusable development components.",GAIA-X: AI-Marketplace: An Ecosystem For Artificial Intelligence,Inno-focus Businessconsulting GmbH; Consortium; R&D general public services.; Germany,"Inno-focus Businessconsulting GmbH is identified as the stakeholder associated with the use case. Its core functions include an intelligent partner agency for AI use cases in production development, a data room for production-development data and a construction kit for AI development.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The GAIA-X AI Marketplace addresses the need to support manufacturing companies, solution providers and artificial intelligence experts in developing innovative applications for product development. Organisations seeking to apply advanced analytical tools in production contexts often face difficulties in finding appropriate partners, accessing relevant data environments and assembling reusable development components. The project establishes a digital platform that brings together AI experts, solution providers and manufacturing companies to promote joint innovation and build on existing infrastructure. Inno-focus Businessconsulting GmbH is identified as the stakeholder associated with the use case. The emerging technology implementation consists of a platform ecosystem providing essential tools for the development of AI solutions. Its core functions include an intelligent partner agency for AI use cases in production development, a data room for production-development data and a construction kit for AI development. At conceptual level, the platform supports matchmaking, data access and reusable development components, enabling actors to design and test solutions more efficiently. The expected impact is faster innovation, stronger collaboration and more scalable development of AI applications in industrial settings. By creating a structured marketplace, the use case can reduce fragmentation, facilitate knowledge exchange and support the emergence of a trusted European data and innovation ecosystem aligned with GAIA-X principles.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-537,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00525,EU_PSTW_202609_00525,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,DataPort GmbH,R&D general public services.,The GAIA-X Data Science & Machine Learning Hub addresses the challenge of making public-administration data more accessible and usable for data-science and artificial intelligence applications while respecting data-protection requirements.,GAIA-X: Data Science & Machine Learning (DSML) Hub,DataPort GmbH; Consortium; R&D general public services.; Germany,"The DSML Hub, associated with DataPort GmbH, aims to create a sustainable approach to the use of data science and AI in the public sector and to support innovation and value creation across the wider ecosystem.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The GAIA-X Data Science & Machine Learning Hub addresses the challenge of making public-administration data more accessible and usable for data-science and artificial intelligence applications while respecting data-protection requirements. Public-sector data are often stored in organisational silos, limiting their reuse by administrations, companies, start-ups and research institutions. The DSML Hub, associated with DataPort GmbH, aims to create a sustainable approach to the use of data science and AI in the public sector and to support innovation and value creation across the wider ecosystem. The emerging technology implementation consists of five building blocks: unlocking administrative data silos and enabling access for third parties; creating infrastructure for data science and AI with modern hardware and common software, including a DSML platform; expanding an ecosystem of start-ups, public IT service providers, regional SMEs, administrations, politics and research institutions; synchronising with GAIA-X; and implementing ten selected innovative projects in administrations and companies. At conceptual level, the Hub combines data access, technical infrastructure and ecosystem governance. The expected impact is improved reuse of public data, stronger innovation capacity and better conditions for public-sector digital transformation. By providing shared infrastructure and connecting diverse actors, the DSML Hub can reduce duplication, improve scalability and enable more evidence-based administrative services and policy applications.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-538,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00526,EU_PSTW_202609_00526,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,DataPort GmbH,R&D general public services.,UNKNOWN,GAIA-X: Digital public administration – Chatbot,DataPort GmbH; Consortium; R&D general public services.; Germany,"The GAIA-X Digital Public Administration Chatbot use case addresses the need to develop and deploy digital assistants in public administration within a legally compliant and trustworthy cloud environment. Public bodies increasingly seek to use conversational services to guide citizens, answer questions and support administrative processes, but they must ensure that data storage, processing and software components comply with public-sector requirements.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The GAIA-X Digital Public Administration Chatbot use case addresses the need to develop and deploy digital assistants in public administration within a legally compliant and trustworthy cloud environment. Public bodies increasingly seek to use conversational services to guide citizens, answer questions and support administrative processes, but they must ensure that data storage, processing and software components comply with public-sector requirements. The project, associated with DataPort GmbH, aims to create the basis for a cloud environment in which digital assistants can be developed and used securely by administrations. The emerging technology implementation consists of modular functional AI building blocks offered as software-as-a-service components and linked with official infrastructure for data storage and processing. At conceptual level, the solution enables administrations to combine reusable chatbot capabilities, natural-language functions and compliant cloud infrastructure rather than building each assistant from scratch. This modularity can support faster deployment and more consistent service quality across public bodies. The expected impact is improved scalability of conversational public services, reduced technical barriers for administrations and a stronger compliance basis for cloud-based digital assistants. By connecting AI components with official infrastructure, the use case can support more accessible citizen services while preserving the governance, security and legal requirements expected in public administration.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-539,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00527,EU_PSTW_202609_00527,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,DataPort GmbH,R&D general public services.,"Road authorities must manage large infrastructure networks with limited resources, while poor road-surface quality can increase maintenance costs, congestion, downtime and costs for road users. The cloud-based approach is potentially reusable by other European public administrations facing similar road-maintenance challenges.",GAIA-X: Predictive maintenance for the road infrastructure in South Tyrol,DataPort GmbH; Consortium; R&D general public services.; Germany,This GAIA-X use case addresses the challenge of helping public administrations make better-founded decisions on road maintenance and budget planning.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"This GAIA-X use case addresses the challenge of helping public administrations make better-founded decisions on road maintenance and budget planning. Road authorities must manage large infrastructure networks with limited resources, while poor road-surface quality can increase maintenance costs, congestion, downtime and costs for road users. In South Tyrol, GAIA-X is described as a data lake and scalable infrastructure for storing and processing heterogeneous data sources relevant to road-surface quality and maintenance needs. DataPort GmbH is identified as the associated stakeholder. The emerging technology implementation aggregates data from different sensors and other sources useful for assessing road conditions and identifying potential maintenance issues. At conceptual level, the platform processes large volumes of heterogeneous information to generate evaluations within response times suitable for public-administration budget planning. Predictive maintenance methods can then support targeted interventions by estimating where maintenance is most needed and when it should be prioritised. The expected impact includes better management of public funds, more efficient and targeted maintenance, improved road-surface quality, reduced disruption from roadworks, lower agency costs for the administration and lower road-user costs for citizens and infrastructure users. The cloud-based approach is potentially reusable by other European public administrations facing similar road-maintenance challenges.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-540,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00528,EU_PSTW_202609_00528,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,AGID (Agenzia per Italia Digitale),General services.,"The operational issue addressed is the fragmentation of monitoring across different technical domains, where organisations may need separate tools for IT infrastructure, building management, environmental control, cloud systems, asset management and safety-related traceability.",ConsolONE: proactive monitoring and management system for real-time control of all company resources,AGID (Agenzia per Italia Digitale); Central-Government; General services.; Italy,"The stakeholders include public or private organisations using the platform, IT managers, facility managers, security and safety teams, asset-management staff and AgID as the catalogue authority. Its implementation covers monitoring and proactive management of IT infrastructure, BMS and home-automation systems, IoT sensors using IP, radio frequency, LoRaWAN and Narrowband technologies, environmental control systems, on-premise, cloud and hybrid-cloud data centres, and company assets such as PCs, servers, tablets and smartphones.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"ConsolONE is presented in the AgID data catalogue as a proactive monitoring and management system for real-time control of organisational resources, including technological infrastructure, buildings, IoT devices, environmental systems, data centres, assets, vehicles and people. The operational issue addressed is the fragmentation of monitoring across different technical domains, where organisations may need separate tools for IT infrastructure, building management, environmental control, cloud systems, asset management and safety-related traceability. The stakeholders include public or private organisations using the platform, IT managers, facility managers, security and safety teams, asset-management staff and AgID as the catalogue authority. The solution aims to provide a reliable, intuitive and integrated environment for supervising resources in real time. Its implementation covers monitoring and proactive management of IT infrastructure, BMS and home-automation systems, IoT sensors using IP, radio frequency, LoRaWAN and Narrowband technologies, environmental control systems, on-premise, cloud and hybrid-cloud data centres, and company assets such as PCs, servers, tablets and smartphones. It also supports traceability of vehicles and people within company premises for security and safety purposes. The expected impact is improved operational visibility, faster detection of anomalies, more coordinated infrastructure management and reduced complexity in controlling heterogeneous systems. By integrating multiple monitoring domains, ConsolONE can enhance reliability, support preventive maintenance and strengthen organisational control over critical resources.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-541,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00529,EU_PSTW_202609_00529,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,AGID (Agenzia per Italia Digitale),General services.,"Delivery through Akamai’s Content Delivery Network supports reliable digital distribution, while the SaaS model reduces infrastructure-management burdens and allows IBM to maintain updates, enrichment and security levels.",IBM Watson Content Hub,AGID (Agenzia per Italia Digitale); Central-Government; General services.; Italy,"Stakeholders include content editors, digital service teams, communications departments, IT administrators, end users accessing digital channels, and organisations using the service through the public cloud catalogue. A SOLR-based search engine supports asset and content discovery, while image rendition and sizing functions help adapt visual materials for omni-channel use.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"IBM Watson Content Hub provides a centralised environment for managing web content and digital assets across multiple digital channels. The operational need is to help organisations organise editorial materials, images, documents and other assets in a consistent way, while reducing duplication, improving searchability and supporting coherent publication across websites, applications and other online services. Stakeholders include content editors, digital service teams, communications departments, IT administrators, end users accessing digital channels, and organisations using the service through the public cloud catalogue. The implementation combines a content-management interface with IBM Watson services for automatic asset tagging, allowing digital materials to be categorised and retrieved more efficiently. A SOLR-based search engine supports asset and content discovery, while image rendition and sizing functions help adapt visual materials for omni-channel use. The system also offers REST APIs for editorial functions and content-model reuse, enabling integration with IBM and third-party solutions. Delivery through Akamai’s Content Delivery Network supports reliable digital distribution, while the SaaS model reduces infrastructure-management burdens and allows IBM to maintain updates, enrichment and security levels. The expected impact is lower operating cost, better content reuse, more consistent digital presence, faster asset discovery and improved editorial productivity. By combining centralised content governance, automated cataloguing and API-based distribution, the platform supports scalable management of digital communication services.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-542,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00530,EU_PSTW_202609_00530,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,AGID (Agenzia per Italia Digitale),General services.,UNKNOWN,Amazon Lex: service for creating communication interfaces via voice and text for any type of application,AGID (Agenzia per Italia Digitale); Central-Government; General services.; Italy,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Amazon Lex is a service for creating communication interfaces via voice and text for any type of application. Amazon Lex offers advanced deep learning capabilities for speech recognition and dictation, as well as natural language recognition and text comprehension, enabling the creation of engaging applications and lifelike conversations. With Amazon Lex, the same deep learning technologies that Amazon Alexa is based on are available to all developers, enabling the creation of sophisticated and natural chatbots quickly and easily. More information: https://aws.amazon.com/it/lex/",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-543,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00531,EU_PSTW_202609_00531,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,AGID (Agenzia per Italia Digitale),General services.,UNKNOWN,Amazon Polly: Text-to-Speech service,AGID (Agenzia per Italia Digitale); Central-Government; General services.; Italy,Amazon Polly is a Text-to-Speech service that uses advanced deep learning technologies to synthesize a human-like voice.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Amazon Polly is a service that turns text into real conversation, allowing you to build applications that speak and create entirely new categories of voice-enabled products. Amazon Polly is a Text-to-Speech service that uses advanced deep learning technologies to synthesize a human-like voice. With dozens of natural voice types in different languages, you can select the most suitable voice and distribute your applications with voice responses in different countries. More information: https://aws.amazon.com/it/polly/",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-544,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00532,EU_PSTW_202609_00532,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Turin,General services.,UNKNOWN,Alexacert: Voice application on Alexa for requesting a birth certificate,Municipality of Turin; Local Government; General services.; Italy,"The solution uses an Alexa voice application activated through the phrase “Alexa, speak with Torino certificates”. After recognition, the system uses the email address associated with the Amazon profile, asks the user to confirm the request and, once consent is given, retrieves the PDF certificate from the Registry or ANPR and sends it to the registered inbox.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Alexacert was developed by the Municipality of Turin as a proof of concept to test whether citizens could request registry certificates through a voice assistant rather than through traditional online portals or physical offices. The administrative problem is that even standard registry procedures can create friction for users, particularly when they require navigation across multiple interfaces, authentication steps and back-office systems. The prototype focuses on birth certificates, which are well suited to experimentation because they are exempt from stamp duty. The solution uses an Alexa voice application activated through the phrase “Alexa, speak with Torino certificates”. The user is identified once by linking the Amazon account with a recognised digital identity, such as SPID, and this authorisation remains valid until explicitly revoked. After recognition, the system uses the email address associated with the Amazon profile, asks the user to confirm the request and, once consent is given, retrieves the PDF certificate from the Registry or ANPR and sends it to the registered inbox. The expected value is a simpler, faster and more accessible interaction with municipal registry services. The prototype also demonstrates how voice interfaces, digital identity, consent management and public back-office integration can be combined in a citizen-facing service.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-545,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00533,EU_PSTW_202609_00533,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Regione Lombardia,General services.,"The expected impact is more targeted public communication, reduced fragmentation across channels, improved editorial efficiency and a better user experience for citizens receiving regional information.",LombardiaInforma: proactive Citizen Communication,Regione Lombardia; Regional Government; General services.; Italy,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"LombardiaInforma supports Regione Lombardia in managing personalised communications to citizens through a multi-channel and multi-service approach. The organisational need is to help editorial and communication teams disseminate information more effectively, while ensuring that citizens receive relevant content according to their interests and contact preferences. The stakeholders include Regione Lombardia, citizens, editorial staff, communication offices, service managers and data-protection functions responsible for GDPR-compliant profiling. The implementation is based on an omnichannel customer-experience architecture and a public portal, Lombardia Informa, which centralises citizens’ contact data and declared topics of interest. The platform enables the creation and management of personalised communication campaigns across different channels, harmonising data from heterogeneous systems and supporting a coherent browsing experience. It also allows the development of integrated communication tools such as landing pages and surveys, and supports enriched content through image slideshows, videos and PDF attachments. User management and profiling are designed to operate in line with GDPR requirements. The expected impact is more targeted public communication, reduced fragmentation across channels, improved editorial efficiency and a better user experience for citizens receiving regional information. By centralising contact management and campaign delivery, the solution strengthens the region’s capacity to communicate proactively, consistently and in a way that is more responsive to citizens’ interests.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-546,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00534,EU_PSTW_202609_00534,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Regione Piemonte,General services.,"The policy and operational problem is that organisations often hold or generate valuable data from sensors, social sources, public datasets and private systems, but lack a shared infrastructure for aggregation, selective sharing, real-time processing and advanced analysis.",YUCCA-Smart Data Platform,Regione Piemonte; Regional Government; General services.; Italy,"By providing end-to-end capabilities, the platform can reduce barriers to data innovation, support evidence-based decision-making and help public and private actors develop scalable solutions using shared digital infrastructure.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"YUCCA Smart Data Platform offers Regione Piemonte and other public or private entities an open, pre-competitive cloud environment for experimenting with data-driven solutions. The policy and operational problem is that organisations often hold or generate valuable data from sensors, social sources, public datasets and private systems, but lack a shared infrastructure for aggregation, selective sharing, real-time processing and advanced analysis. Stakeholders include Regione Piemonte, public administrations, private organisations, developers, data users, IoT operators and citizens who may benefit from new digital services. The platform aggregates information from the Internet of Things, including cameras, sensors and weather stations, from the Internet of People, such as tweets, and from public and private datasets. It supports selective data sharing, self-service real-time processing, advanced analytics and access to data and metadata through APIs. Conceptually, YUCCA functions as a data platform that enables ingestion, governance, processing, analysis, visualisation and reuse of heterogeneous information. The expected impact is the creation of value-added services across multiple policy areas, easier experimentation with innovative technologies, and better interoperability between data producers and service developers. By providing end-to-end capabilities, the platform can reduce barriers to data innovation, support evidence-based decision-making and help public and private actors develop scalable solutions using shared digital infrastructure.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-547,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00535,EU_PSTW_202609_00535,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Barcelona,Public order and safety n.e.c.,"High-density urban areas face operational challenges related to crowding, public-space management, mobility flows and emergency preparedness, particularly in locations affected by tourism, events and changing patterns of pedestrian movement.",CityFlows-Barcelona,Municipality of Barcelona; Local Government; Public order and safety n.e.c.; Spain,"At conceptual level, the platform uses urban analytics and pedestrian-flow information to support the monitoring, assessment and management of crowded spaces.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"CityFlows-Barcelona addresses the need to improve the safety and comfort of busy pedestrian spaces in Barcelona. High-density urban areas face operational challenges related to crowding, public-space management, mobility flows and emergency preparedness, particularly in locations affected by tourism, events and changing patterns of pedestrian movement. The CityFlows initiative, developed as an EIT Urban Mobility flagship project, brought together a diverse consortium of partners to create a Crowd Management Decision-Support System for public authorities and managers of pedestrian spaces. The Municipality of Barcelona is identified as the local public stakeholder. The emerging technology implementation centres on the CityFlows CM-DSS, a software solution later licensed through City Analytics, a start-up created to provide the system to government authorities. At conceptual level, the platform uses urban analytics and pedestrian-flow information to support the monitoring, assessment and management of crowded spaces. It can help authorities understand crowd conditions, assess potential risks and select appropriate operational measures. The expected impact is improved situational awareness, safer and more comfortable pedestrian environments, and stronger capacity to manage public spaces during normal operations and periods of disruption. The creation of City Analytics also supports scalability, enabling the solution to be reused by other authorities seeking reliable tools for evidence-based crowd management.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-548,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00536,EU_PSTW_202609_00536,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Milan,Public order and safety n.e.c.,"CityFlows-Milano addresses the challenge of managing busy pedestrian spaces in Milan by improving public authorities’ capacity to monitor crowding, assess risks and support operational decisions.",CityFlows-Milano,Municipality of Milan; Local Government; Public order and safety n.e.c.; Italy,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"CityFlows-Milano addresses the challenge of managing busy pedestrian spaces in Milan by improving public authorities’ capacity to monitor crowding, assess risks and support operational decisions. Dense urban areas require tools that can help maintain comfort and safety, especially in places affected by high footfall, public events, commuting flows and changing mobility patterns. CityFlows, an EIT Urban Mobility flagship project, brought together a diverse consortium of partners to launch a state-of-the-art Crowd Management Decision-Support System. The Municipality of Milan is identified as the local public stakeholder. The emerging technology implementation is based on the CityFlows CM-DSS, a decision-support software later licensed to government authorities through City Analytics, a dedicated start-up. At conceptual level, the system combines pedestrian-flow data, urban analytics and decision-support functions to help public authorities interpret crowd conditions and plan responses. It supports the connection between monitoring information and operational management of public space. The expected impact is better safety, comfort and responsiveness in pedestrian areas, with authorities able to rely on more structured information when managing crowd-related risks. The City Analytics licensing model also increases the potential for replication beyond the original project, allowing municipalities and public-space managers to adopt a scalable tool for crowd management, including during crisis situations or periods of unusual pressure on public space.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-549,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00537,EU_PSTW_202609_00537,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,LAAS-CNRS,R&D economic affairs,"Planning technologies can support decision-making where actors must sequence actions, optimise resources or coordinate complex processes, but they are often difficult for non-specialists to access and apply.",AI4EU-AIPlan4EU,LAAS-CNRS; Academic-Research; R&D economic affairs; France,"AIPlan4EU addresses the need to make automated planning technologies more accessible and usable for European innovators, practitioners and public or private organisations using the European AI On-Demand Platform. The expected impact is broader adoption of planning technologies, reduced barriers between research and application, and improved capacity for organisations to use advanced decision-support methods.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"AIPlan4EU addresses the need to make automated planning technologies more accessible and usable for European innovators, practitioners and public or private organisations using the European AI On-Demand Platform. Planning technologies can support decision-making where actors must sequence actions, optimise resources or coordinate complex processes, but they are often difficult for non-specialists to access and apply. The project, coordinated through LAAS-CNRS, aims to bring AI planning into the AI4EU platform as a first-class capability by creating a uniform, user-centred framework for accessing existing planning technologies. The emerging technology implementation focuses on integrating planning tools, methods and guidance into the European platform environment. At conceptual level, the framework provides common access routes to established planning technologies and supports users in selecting, understanding and applying these tools to concrete innovation problems. The project also develops practical guidelines for innovators and practitioners, helping translate research outputs into usable solutions. The expected impact is broader adoption of planning technologies, reduced barriers between research and application, and improved capacity for organisations to use advanced decision-support methods. By embedding planning capabilities in AI4EU, AIPlan4EU strengthens the platform’s value as a shared European resource and supports more scalable use of intelligent planning across sectors and use cases.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-550,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00538,EU_PSTW_202609_00538,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Centre for Research and Technology Hellas (CERTH),R&D economic affairs,"At conceptual level, the network brings together scientific expertise, technical tools and sector-specific challenges to support responsible innovation in media technologies.",AI4EU-AI4Media,Centre for Research and Technology Hellas (CERTH); Academic-Research; R&D economic affairs; Greece,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"AI4Media addresses the need to strengthen Europe’s research and innovation capacity in artificial intelligence for the media sector while ensuring that future deployments reflect European values of ethics, trustworthiness and responsibility. The media sector faces rapid technological change, including new forms of content production, distribution, verification and personalisation, requiring advanced tools and specialised expertise. Funded under the European Union’s Horizon 2020 research and innovation programme, AI4Media is structured as a Network of Research Excellence Centres and is associated with the Centre for Research and Technology Hellas. The objective is to become a Centre of Excellence engaging a wide network of researchers across Europe and beyond. The emerging technology implementation consists of research, training and development activities focused on the next generation of core artificial intelligence advances for media applications. At conceptual level, the network brings together scientific expertise, technical tools and sector-specific challenges to support responsible innovation in media technologies. It also emphasises the embedding of ethical and trustworthy AI principles in future deployments. The expected impact is improved European capacity to develop and apply advanced media technologies, stronger research collaboration and better alignment between innovation and public-interest values. By linking technical excellence with ethical governance, AI4Media supports a more responsible and competitive European media-technology ecosystem.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-551,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00539,EU_PSTW_202609_00539,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,National Center for Scientific Research Demokritos,R&D economic affairs,UNKNOWN,AI4EU-AICopernicus,National Center for Scientific Research Demokritos; Academic-Research; R&D economic affairs; Greece,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"AI4Copernicus addresses the need to connect artificial intelligence capabilities with Earth observation data, particularly data generated through the Copernicus programme. Researchers, innovators and public-sector users often face technical and organisational barriers when trying to combine satellite and geospatial data with advanced analytical methods. The project, associated with the National Center for Scientific Research Demokritos, aims to make the AI4EU AI-on-demand platform the digital environment of choice for users working with Copernicus data. The emerging technology implementation focuses on bridging the AI4EU platform with the Earth observation ecosystem. At conceptual level, AI4Copernicus supports access to tools, resources and services that enable users to apply artificial intelligence methods to Copernicus data for research and innovation purposes. This may include support for data discovery, model development, experimentation and reuse of analytical components in geospatial applications. The expected impact is stronger uptake of Earth observation data, improved innovation capacity and easier development of AI-enabled services based on Copernicus resources. By linking two major European digital assets, AI4Copernicus can support applications in areas such as environmental monitoring, climate services, land management and emergency response. The project contributes to a more integrated European data and AI ecosystem, making advanced analysis more accessible to both specialists and applied users.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-552,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00540,EU_PSTW_202609_00540,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Linköping University,R&D economic affairs,UNKNOWN,AI4EU-TAILOR,Linköping University; Academic-Research; R&D economic affairs; Sweden,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"TAILOR addresses the strategic objective of advancing trustworthy artificial intelligence in Europe by integrating reasoning, learning and optimisation. The project responds to the need for research and innovation that is not only technically advanced, but also human-centred, responsible and aligned with European values. Coordinated through a consortium including Linköping University, TAILOR is committed to demonstrating that expertise, cooperation and diversity can support the European vision of trustworthy AI and position Europe as a global role model for responsible development. The emerging technology implementation focuses on the foundations of trustworthy AI, particularly the integration of symbolic reasoning, machine learning and optimisation methods. At conceptual level, the project brings together research communities that address different aspects of intelligent systems, seeking approaches that are more robust, explainable, reliable and suitable for human-centred applications. Rather than a single operational tool, TAILOR functions as a research network and capability-building initiative that supports methods, knowledge exchange and innovation pathways. The expected impact is stronger European excellence in trustworthy AI, improved collaboration among researchers and better foundations for future applications in public and private sectors. By advancing methods that combine reasoning, learning and optimisation, TAILOR can contribute to systems that are more transparent, dependable and aligned with governance expectations for high-impact digital technologies.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-553,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00541,EU_PSTW_202609_00541,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Redoc (startup),Education not definable by level.,"REDOC, based on the Redooc learning platform, addresses the challenge of making mathematics and science education more engaging, accessible, and effective for students while also supporting teachers with structured digital resources. The problem it tackles is not only the difficulty many learners experience with STEM subjects, but also the need for educators to access exercises, explanations, simulations, and lesson materials in formats that are suitable for classroom and independent study.",REDOC - Digital Tutor to make easier learning the STEM subject,Redoc (startup); Private sector; Education not definable by level.; Italy,"The implementation uses a web-based educational platform combining video lessons, interactive exercises, tips, simulations, and game-like learning paths. Teachers can use the platform to complement classroom instruction, assign activities, and explain concepts through ready-made multimedia resources.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"REDOC, based on the Redooc learning platform, addresses the challenge of making mathematics and science education more engaging, accessible, and effective for students while also supporting teachers with structured digital resources. The problem it tackles is not only the difficulty many learners experience with STEM subjects, but also the need for educators to access exercises, explanations, simulations, and lesson materials in formats that are suitable for classroom and independent study. The stakeholders include students across different age groups, teachers, schools, families, and the start-up providing the service. The objective is to encourage learning through practice, motivation, and playful competition, helping students train their reasoning skills in a way comparable to regular physical exercise. The implementation uses a web-based educational platform combining video lessons, interactive exercises, tips, simulations, and game-like learning paths. Technology is applied through digital tutoring functions, adaptive content organisation, gamification mechanics, progress tracking, and online assessment features that allow learners to practise and receive structured feedback. Teachers can use the platform to complement classroom instruction, assign activities, and explain concepts through ready-made multimedia resources. The expected impact includes stronger learner engagement, greater continuity between school and home study, easier access to remedial materials, and improved support for teachers. By presenting STEM learning as an interactive and competitive experience, the platform can increase motivation and broaden participation in scientific and mathematical education.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-554,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00542,EU_PSTW_202609_00542,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,University Hospital Miguel Servet (HUMS),Public health services.,"At the University Hospital Miguel Servet (HUMS) in Zaragoza, outpatient pharmacy processes were fragmented, manual, and prone to dispensing and coordination errors.",SAMPA - Service of registration and promotion of the adherence to medications in elderly patients,University Hospital Miguel Servet (HUMS); Academic-Research; Public health services.; Spain,"The SAMPA project was implemented to improve treatment adherence, streamline medication management, and enhance patient experience. The project has been implemented at HUMS and has reduced dispensing errors, improved workflow efficiency, and enhanced accessibility for patients.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Medication non-adherence among elderly and chronic patients leads to poorer health outcomes and higher healthcare costs. At the University Hospital Miguel Servet (HUMS) in Zaragoza, outpatient pharmacy processes were fragmented, manual, and prone to dispensing and coordination errors. The SAMPA project was implemented to improve treatment adherence, streamline medication management, and enhance patient experience. Stakeholders include hospital pharmacists, clinicians, outpatient staff, and elderly patients. The solution involved a comprehensive digital transformation of the outpatient pharmacy unit. Core components include an integrated electronic prescription platform, automated storage and dispensing robots, full traceability across prescribing and dispensing stages, appointment scheduling, and adherence monitoring systems. These components are interconnected with hospital information systems, enabling end-to-end visibility of medication flows and patient interactions. Supporting tools such as a patient app, improved web services, and dedicated communication points facilitate engagement and follow-up. The project has been implemented at HUMS and has reduced dispensing errors, improved workflow efficiency, and enhanced accessibility for patients. SAMPA demonstrates how integrated digital systems can deliver measurable operational and quality improvements in hospital outpatient care.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK|F08_ERROR_COMPLIANCE_RISK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-555,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00543,EU_PSTW_202609_00543,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Tante Louise,Public health services.,UNKNOWN,Smart Dementia project,Tante Louise; Local Government; Public health services.; Netherlands,"The Smart Dementia project at Tante Louise uses eHealth technology to give people with Alzheimer’s disease more freedom inside and outside nursing-home settings. Its value lies in using technology as a supportive layer around professional judgement, not as a substitute for person-centred dementia care.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Dementia care requires balancing safety with personal freedom, because residents may benefit physically and mentally from moving around, while care teams must manage risks linked to disorientation or wandering. The Smart Dementia project at Tante Louise uses eHealth technology to give people with Alzheimer’s disease more freedom inside and outside nursing-home settings. The operational objective is to support professional risk assessment: care teams decide how much freedom each resident can safely have, and the technology helps them monitor whereabouts when additional safeguards are needed. The implementation combines a wristwatch, mobile app, sensor technology and GPS-based location tracking, allowing nurses and authorised staff to locate a resident if necessary. At a conceptual level, the device collects location data, communicates it to relevant caregivers and supports a personalised care arrangement rather than imposing uniform restrictions. The expected impact is greater autonomy for residents, improved confidence for staff and families, and longer preservation of physical and mental activity. The project also demonstrates how personal health data can support more precise and individualised care, while raising obvious requirements around consent, proportionality and data protection. Its value lies in using technology as a supportive layer around professional judgement, not as a substitute for person-centred dementia care.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-556,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00544,EU_PSTW_202609_00544,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Miguel Servet Hospital (Zaragoza, Spain)",Public health services.,"Track AI addresses the difficulty of assessing visual function in children and other non-collaborative patients who may be unable to complete conventional ophthalmological examinations. Reliable early detection of visual impairment is essential for timely intervention, but clinical assessment can be challenging when patients cannot follow instructions or communicate clearly.",Track AI,"Miguel Servet Hospital (Zaragoza, Spain); Local Government; Public health services.; Spain","The project, linked to Miguel Servet Hospital in Zaragoza, uses the DIVE medical device and aims to help physicians determine whether a patient has a visual impairment and, in some cases, estimate the probability of a specific pathology. At conceptual level, the system uses pattern comparison and probabilistic classification to support clinical interpretation of visual behaviour.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Track AI addresses the difficulty of assessing visual function in children and other non-collaborative patients who may be unable to complete conventional ophthalmological examinations. Reliable early detection of visual impairment is essential for timely intervention, but clinical assessment can be challenging when patients cannot follow instructions or communicate clearly. The project, linked to Miguel Servet Hospital in Zaragoza, uses the DIVE medical device and aims to help physicians determine whether a patient has a visual impairment and, in some cases, estimate the probability of a specific pathology. The emerging technology implementation integrates artificial intelligence into the DIVE device through a dedicated app running on a Huawei P30 mobile phone. The app analyses data from visual explorations, especially patient gaze patterns, and compares them with a database generated from previous diagnoses, available both online and offline. At conceptual level, the system uses pattern comparison and probabilistic classification to support clinical interpretation of visual behaviour. Although focused on non-collaborative patients, it can also be used with collaborative patients. The expected impact is improved support for clinicians, faster and more accessible screening, and better identification of patients who may require further assessment or treatment. By combining portable technology with diagnostic-support capabilities, Track AI can strengthen paediatric and inclusive eye-care services.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-557,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00545,EU_PSTW_202609_00545,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Osakidetza (Servicio Vasco de Salud/Basque Health System),Public health services.,"Access to psychological therapy can be constrained by limited professional capacity, distance, waiting times and the need for repeated face-to-face appointments.",MasterMind - Basque Country,Osakidetza (Servicio Vasco de Salud/Basque Health System); Local Government; Public health services.; Spain,"The project, involving Osakidetza within a broader European collaboration of 23 partners, aimed to use ICT to expand access while maintaining professional oversight. By combining structured digital therapy with clinical supervision, MasterMind can improve service accessibility, support efficient use of healthcare resources and provide patients with a flexible treatment pathway that complements conventional mental-health services in the Basque health system.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"MasterMind in the Basque Country addressed the need to make high-quality treatment for depression more widely available to adults with mild and moderate depression. Access to psychological therapy can be constrained by limited professional capacity, distance, waiting times and the need for repeated face-to-face appointments. The project, involving Osakidetza within a broader European collaboration of 23 partners, aimed to use ICT to expand access while maintaining professional oversight. The emerging technology implementation consisted of Computerised Cognitive Behaviour Therapy delivered through the web application “Overcome your depression”, accessible by computer, tablet or smartphone. At conceptual level, the application provides therapy through six modules combining text, videos, images, graphs and interactive tasks. Patients complete tests within the platform, and these results communicate the evolution of their condition to health professionals. Clinicians periodically review the outputs and receive alerts when a patient’s results indicate possible concern. The expected impact is wider availability of depression treatment, fewer required face-to-face appointments and better continuity of monitoring between patients and professionals. By combining structured digital therapy with clinical supervision, MasterMind can improve service accessibility, support efficient use of healthcare resources and provide patients with a flexible treatment pathway that complements conventional mental-health services in the Basque health system.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-558,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00546,EU_PSTW_202609_00546,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Region of Southern Denmark,Public health services.,UNKNOWN,MasterMind - Southern Denmark,Region of Southern Denmark; Regional Government; Public health services.; Denmark,"The application delivers therapy in six modules using text, videos, images, graphs and other structured materials. By enabling remote therapy while maintaining professional involvement, the solution can help mental-health services reach more patients, use clinical resources more effectively and provide a flexible pathway for people with mild or moderate depression.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"MasterMind in Southern Denmark addressed the same broader European objective of making high-quality depression treatment more widely available through ICT-enabled care. Adults with mild and moderate depression may face barriers to accessing regular therapy, including service availability, travel, stigma or scheduling constraints. The Region of Southern Denmark participated in the European MasterMind project, which involved 23 partners and focused on implementing Computerised Cognitive Behaviour Therapy as a scalable form of supported treatment. The emerging technology implementation centred on the web application “Overcome your depression”, which can be used through a computer, tablet or smartphone. The application delivers therapy in six modules using text, videos, images, graphs and other structured materials. Patients complete tests during the programme, allowing their progress and condition to be communicated to healthcare professionals. Clinicians review the results periodically and receive alarms when responses indicate a cause for concern. The expected impact is improved access to treatment, reduced reliance on face-to-face appointments and more efficient monitoring of patient progress. By enabling remote therapy while maintaining professional involvement, the solution can help mental-health services reach more patients, use clinical resources more effectively and provide a flexible pathway for people with mild or moderate depression. The case demonstrates how digital therapy can be integrated into regional healthcare provision.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-559,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00547,EU_PSTW_202609_00547,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""social_nonprofit""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,MPAI Association,R&D economic affairs,"The sectoral challenge is that media and data coding systems require interoperable standards, predictable licensing conditions and integration frameworks that allow different components to work together across products, services and markets. By combining technical specification work with transparent IPR frameworks, MPAI can support innovation while reducing fragmentation in media and data-coding ecosystems.","MPAI Community: Moving Picture, Audio and Data Coding by Artificial Intelligence (MPAI)",MPAI Association; Non-governmental; R&D economic affairs; Switzerland,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"MPAI is an international, unaffiliated, non-profit organisation established to develop technical specifications for moving picture, audio and data coding enabled by new digital technologies. The sectoral challenge is that media and data coding systems require interoperable standards, predictable licensing conditions and integration frameworks that allow different components to work together across products, services and markets. Stakeholders include technology developers, media companies, standardisation experts, rights holders, implementers, researchers and organisations seeking clarity on intellectual property arrangements. The initiative focuses on developing data-coding specifications with clear intellectual property rights licensing frameworks, particularly for moving picture, audio and data applications. Its implementation is not a single public service, but a standard-setting and specification-development process. Conceptually, this involves defining coding architectures, technical requirements, component interfaces, conformance expectations and licensing conditions that can support integration of coding components into broader systems. The expected impact is improved interoperability, reduced uncertainty for implementers, and stronger conditions for adoption of advanced coding technologies across international markets. By combining technical specification work with transparent IPR frameworks, MPAI can support innovation while reducing fragmentation in media and data-coding ecosystems. Its value lies in creating common reference points for industry and research actors developing or deploying advanced coding solutions.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-560,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00548,EU_PSTW_202609_00548,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Provincia Autonoma di Trento,Public health services.,"The service challenge is to guarantee citizens continuous, safe and integrated access to healthcare functions through one platform and one app, while maintaining the quality and reliability of care.",Trentino Salute 4.0: TREC+,Provincia Autonoma di Trento; Local Government; Public health services.; Italy,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"TreC+ brings together the digital health trials carried out in Trentino with citizens and health professionals, turning them into a single operational ecosystem for online health services. The service challenge is to guarantee citizens continuous, safe and integrated access to healthcare functions through one platform and one app, while maintaining the quality and reliability of care. The main stakeholders include the Autonomous Province of Trento, APSS, citizens, doctors, health professionals and the FBK research centre. The platform is designed around a constant exchange of data produced by patients and clinicians, with health information remaining under public control in APSS archives. Its implementation is based on open-source technologies and an open architectural model that allows proprietary tools and external solutions to be connected without moving health data away from the public system. The more advanced components, developed and experimented with FBK, explore virtual coaches, predictive models, chatbots and other intelligent applications. TreC+ can also integrate with existing healthcare-management systems, including booking services, electronic health records, payment systems and telemonitoring tools. The expected impact is broader access to digital health services, improved continuity of care, stronger data protection, and greater reusability in other regional, provincial, local health authority or hospital contexts. By combining public governance with modular integration, the ecosystem supports scalable digital healthcare while keeping citizens’ data central, protected and available.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-561,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00549,EU_PSTW_202609_00549,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Malpensa & Fiumicino Airports Management,Police Services,"The expected impact is smoother passenger circulation, shorter queues, reduced manual document checks and more consistent identity verification across airport touchpoints.",Seamless Flow: facial recognition of airport passengers,Malpensa & Fiumicino Airports Management; Local Government; Police Services; Italy,Seamless Flow supports automated passenger processing in airports by using biometric identification to manage travellers’ digital identities and reduce repeated document checks. The implementation uses IoT-enabled access gates equipped with cameras and facial recognition software.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Seamless Flow supports automated passenger processing in airports by using biometric identification to manage travellers’ digital identities and reduce repeated document checks. The operational issue is that airports must process large passenger flows while maintaining security, identity verification and efficient movement through controlled areas. Passengers, airport operators, border or security authorities, airlines and the technology provider Vision-Box are the central stakeholders. The solution is used in several airports, including Fiumicino, Malpensa and Linate, and aims to allow passengers to move through airport procedures without repeatedly presenting passports and tickets. The implementation uses IoT-enabled access gates equipped with cameras and facial recognition software. These devices capture the passenger’s face and associate it with passport and ticket data, creating a temporary digital identity that is maintained for the duration of the passenger’s stay at the airport. Conceptually, the system combines biometric enrolment, document-data verification, facial matching, passenger-flow management and integration with airport access-control infrastructure. The expected impact is smoother passenger circulation, shorter queues, reduced manual document checks and more consistent identity verification across airport touchpoints. For airport operators, it can improve throughput and service quality, while supporting security objectives. However, because the system relies on biometric data, its use requires strict safeguards on consent, retention, accuracy, access control and proportionality.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-563,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00550,EU_PSTW_202609_00550,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Istituzione Bologna Musei,Cultural services.,UNKNOWN,Shareart: measure the public's satisfaction in front of the works of art exhibited in museum,Istituzione Bologna Musei; Local Government; Cultural services.; Italy,The system uses a camera positioned near artworks to detect faces looking towards the piece and collect information about observation behaviour. Its implementation draws on big-data techniques and convolutional neural networks to correlate data on the use and enjoyment of artworks with characteristics inferred from visitors and their behaviour.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"ShareArt explores how museums can better understand visitors’ engagement with artworks by analysing behaviour during observation. The cultural-management problem is that museums often rely on indirect indicators, surveys or attendance numbers to assess how the public experiences exhibitions, whereas curators and administrators may need more granular evidence on attention, satisfaction and interaction with specific works. Stakeholders include ENEA’s Sustainability Department of Production and Territorial Systems, the Bologna Museums Institution, museum managers, visitors, curators and cultural-policy actors. The system uses a camera positioned near artworks to detect faces looking towards the piece and collect information about observation behaviour. Its implementation draws on big-data techniques and convolutional neural networks to correlate data on the use and enjoyment of artworks with characteristics inferred from visitors and their behaviour. Conceptually, the tool combines computer vision, behavioural analytics, image processing and data aggregation to infer patterns of public engagement. The expected impact is a more evidence-based understanding of museum experience, helping institutions assess which works attract attention, how visitors interact with exhibitions and how displays might be improved. It may support curatorial planning, audience development and cultural-service evaluation. At the same time, the use of cameras and inferred personal characteristics in a museum setting requires careful attention to transparency, proportionality, anonymisation and visitor privacy.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-564,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00551,EU_PSTW_202609_00551,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Multiple Italian Universities (Cattolica, Bocconi, Roma Tre etc.)",Education n.e.c.,"The educational challenge is to preserve exam integrity when assessments are conducted remotely, where universities cannot rely on traditional in-person invigilation.",Respondus: assessment software for remote management of students (university),"Multiple Italian Universities (Cattolica, Bocconi, Roma Tre etc.); Academic-Research; Education n.e.c.; Italy","The use of such tools therefore requires clear rules, proportionality, transparency, human oversight and accessible procedures for students to challenge contested findings.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Respondus is a remote proctoring solution used by several Italian universities to supervise online examinations and identify behaviour considered unusual or suspicious during tests. The educational challenge is to preserve exam integrity when assessments are conducted remotely, where universities cannot rely on traditional in-person invigilation. Stakeholders include universities, students, teaching staff, examination offices, technology providers and data-protection authorities or officers responsible for oversight. The software monitors the student’s device and collects visual data during the examination process. Its implementation includes video recording, facial image capture, periodic snapshots and algorithmic flagging of moments that may indicate abnormal conduct. These signals are intended to support decisions on whether a student may have copied, used unauthorised materials or violated examination rules. Conceptually, the system combines device monitoring, identity verification, behavioural analysis, video analytics and automated risk flagging, with human review normally needed to interpret the results. The expected benefits for universities are more scalable remote assessment, reduced need for live invigilators and stronger deterrence against fraud. However, the impact is contested because proctoring systems can affect privacy, create stress for students, and generate false or ambiguous alerts. The use of such tools therefore requires clear rules, proportionality, transparency, human oversight and accessible procedures for students to challenge contested findings.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-565,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00552,EU_PSTW_202609_00552,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Ministero dell'Interno,Police Services,UNKNOWN,SARI (Automatic Image Recognition System),Ministero dell'Interno; Central-Government; Police Services; Italy,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Facial images can be important investigative material when police need to compare a person shown in a photograph or video with people already recorded in law-enforcement databases. SARI, the Automatic Image Recognition System, was introduced in Italy for the Ministry of the Interior and the Central Anti-Crime Directorate of the State Police. The system is supplied by Parsec 3.26 S.r.l. and is structured around two components: SARI Enterprise and SARI Real-Time. SARI Enterprise supports retrospective identification by comparing a facial image already available to investigators with images in police databases of previously photo-recorded individuals. SARI Real-Time, by contrast, was designed to recognise people from a watch list through camera feeds. At a conceptual level, the technology combines biometric feature extraction, facial matching, image comparison and investigative review. Its operational value lies in helping officers narrow searches, identify possible matches and accelerate investigations where visual evidence is available. However, the two components raise different legal and ethical issues: retrospective matching for investigative purposes is different from real-time public-space recognition, which requires stronger safeguards because of its surveillance implications. The system should therefore be understood as an investigative-support tool whose outputs require human validation and legal control, not as an autonomous identification or enforcement mechanism. Current public material most clearly confirms knowledge of SARI Enterprise as the facial-recognition system used by Italian law enforcement for after-the-fact investigative identification.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-566,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00553,EU_PSTW_202609_00553,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,ISS- ITALY Istituto Superiore di Sanità,Public order and safety n.e.c.,"The policy problem is that vaccine debates unfold rapidly through social media and web platforms, making it difficult for health authorities to detect emerging concerns, influential actors and misinformation dynamics in real time.",EU-JAV European Joint Action on Vaccination,ISS- ITALY Istituto Superiore di Sanità; Central-Government; Public order and safety n.e.c.; Italy,The Data layer collected and cleaned raw information through platform APIs and keyword filters validated using recurrence and relevance criteria.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The EU-JAV vaccine conversation monitoring platform was developed to help public health actors understand how vaccination-related narratives circulate online across different countries and languages. The policy problem is that vaccine debates unfold rapidly through social media and web platforms, making it difficult for health authorities to detect emerging concerns, influential actors and misinformation dynamics in real time. Stakeholders include the Istituto Superiore di Sanità, EU-JAV partners, public health authorities, researchers, online communities and citizens exposed to vaccine information. In its initial phase, the platform operated in Italian, French and Spanish and was accessible via credentials. It monitored information flows from Twitter, Reddit, Wikipedia and Google Trends through a three-layer architecture: a Data layer, an Analysis layer and a Visualisation layer. The Data layer collected and cleaned raw information through platform APIs and keyword filters validated using recurrence and relevance criteria. The Analysis layer generated graphs, geolocated tweets and applied machine-learning processes for influencer analysis and early-signal detection, including the identification of active or influential users and emerging vaccine-related hashtags. The Visualisation layer presented results in an accessible way. The expected impact is stronger real-time monitoring of vaccine conversations, earlier detection of changing narratives, and better evidence for public communication strategies. By structuring online data into interpretable outputs, the platform supports more responsive vaccination policy and communication.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-567,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00554,EU_PSTW_202609_00554,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""social_nonprofit""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Politecnico of Turin,R&D education.,"This creates challenges for researchers, public administrations and digital service providers seeking to develop image–text retrieval, content moderation or assistive technologies tailored to Italian users.",CLIP (Contrastive Language–Image Pre-training) for the Italian Language,Politecnico of Turin; Academic-Research; R&D education.; Italy,"Using approximately 1.4 million paired image–text samples, the model is fine-tuned through contrastive learning techniques that optimise the similarity between matching image–text pairs while distinguishing non-matching pairs. By reducing dependency on English-centric models, it strengthens digital sovereignty, supports innovation in language-specific applications and provides a scalable resource for further research and public and private sector use cases.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Many advanced multimodal models have been primarily trained on English-language datasets, limiting their performance and applicability in languages with fewer annotated resources, such as Italian. This creates challenges for researchers, public administrations and digital service providers seeking to develop image–text retrieval, content moderation or assistive technologies tailored to Italian users. The project aims to bridge this gap by developing a multimodal model capable of understanding and linking Italian textual descriptions with visual content. The objective is to provide a robust foundation model that enables accurate alignment between images and Italian language inputs, supporting downstream applications in search, accessibility and digital services. The solution builds upon the Contrastive Language–Image Pre-training (CLIP) framework, combining a pre-trained Italian BERT language model (dbmdz) with a Vision Transformer architecture for image encoding. Using approximately 1.4 million paired image–text samples, the model is fine-tuned through contrastive learning techniques that optimise the similarity between matching image–text pairs while distinguishing non-matching pairs. The resulting architecture enables joint embedding of visual and textual data within a shared semantic space, accessible through an online demonstration environment. The Italian CLIP model improves multimodal understanding in a low-resource linguistic context, enhancing the accuracy of image–text retrieval and classification tasks in Italian. By reducing dependency on English-centric models, it strengthens digital sovereignty, supports innovation in language-specific applications and provides a scalable resource for further research and public and private sector use cases.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-568,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00555,EU_PSTW_202609_00555,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,European Dynamics,General services.,"iBorderCtrl, the Intelligent Portable Control System, was designed to address the operational challenge of conducting thorough yet faster border checks for third-country nationals crossing the land borders of EU Member States. The intended impact was to make border control more scalable, consistent, and efficient, particularly in land-border environments where fixed infrastructure may be limited.",iBorderCtrl: Virtual Policeman,European Dynamics; Consortium; General services.; Belgium,"The use case also illustrates the importance of safeguards, transparency, and proportionality in sensitive border-management technologies.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"iBorderCtrl, the Intelligent Portable Control System, was designed to address the operational challenge of conducting thorough yet faster border checks for third-country nationals crossing the land borders of EU Member States. Border authorities must balance security, legal compliance, traveller facilitation, and resource efficiency, particularly at road crossings, pedestrian routes, and train stations where flows can be complex and time-sensitive. The stakeholders include border guards, national authorities, EU-level border management actors, travellers, technology suppliers, and backend system operators. The objective was to create a comprehensive and mobile two-stage procedure capable of reducing the time and cost associated with checks while maintaining the depth of control required under Schengen border management. The implementation combined portable readers and scanners, automatic control subsystems, wireless networking, secure backend storage, and processing infrastructure. At a conceptual level, the system brought together hardware for document and biometric capture, software modules for verification and risk-related processing, mobile connectivity for field operations, and centralised data services for secure storage and decision support. The intended impact was to make border control more scalable, consistent, and efficient, particularly in land-border environments where fixed infrastructure may be limited. Expected results included reduced processing time per traveller, lower operational costs, improved mobility for border teams, and more reliable information flows between frontline officers and backend systems. The use case also illustrates the importance of safeguards, transparency, and proportionality in sensitive border-management technologies.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,NOT_IN_USE,미사용,,S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-569,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00556,EU_PSTW_202609_00556,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,National Research Center - Italy (CNR),Health n.e.c.,"Neurological disorders are often difficult for patients to understand due to the complexity of medical imaging and the abstract nature of diagnostic explanations. Clinicians face challenges in communicating disease progression, anatomical alterations and treatment implications in a clear and accessible manner, particularly when relying on two-dimensional scans and technical terminology.",NUMEL.AI - A communication tool for neurological disorders,National Research Center - Italy (CNR); Academic-Research; Health n.e.c.; Italy,NUMEL.AI addresses this communication gap by providing an interactive visualisation tool that enables doctors and patients to explore neurological conditions using the patient’s own medical imaging data. Computer vision techniques and body-tracking algorithms align these models with the patient’s body in real time using augmented reality rendering.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Neurological disorders are often difficult for patients to understand due to the complexity of medical imaging and the abstract nature of diagnostic explanations. Clinicians face challenges in communicating disease progression, anatomical alterations and treatment implications in a clear and accessible manner, particularly when relying on two-dimensional scans and technical terminology. NUMEL.AI addresses this communication gap by providing an interactive visualisation tool that enables doctors and patients to explore neurological conditions using the patient’s own medical imaging data. The objective is to enhance patient understanding, support shared decision-making and improve the quality of clinical consultations through immersive visual explanations grounded in real diagnostic data. The solution processes magnetic resonance (MR) images and other clinical imaging data imported directly from healthcare information systems to automatically generate personalised three-dimensional anatomical models. Computer vision techniques and body-tracking algorithms align these models with the patient’s body in real time using augmented reality rendering. Machine learning methods support image segmentation and the identification of relevant anatomical structures and disorder-related patterns. The system can operate locally or through a scalable, API-based cloud platform that allows secure data storage, retrieval and extensibility across healthcare environments. The tool improves the clarity and effectiveness of clinician–patient communication, enhances patient engagement and understanding of disease evolution, and supports more informed treatment discussions. Its scalable architecture facilitates integration with existing hospital systems, while automated model generation reduces preparation time for clinicians and increases the accessibility of advanced visualisation technologies in neurological care.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-570,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00557,EU_PSTW_202609_00557,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Estonian Ministry of Justice,Law courts,"The expected impact would be faster processing of routine small claims, lower administrative burden and improved access to justice for simple disputes. Later legal commentary suggests that Estonia has long used semi-automatic procedures for small claims, but public evidence of a fully operational autonomous AI judge remains limited.",AI Robort Judge for small claims in Estonia,Estonian Ministry of Justice; Central-Government; Law courts; Estonia,The use case should therefore be treated as an exploratory justice-automation initiative rather than a confirmed deployed judicial decision-maker.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Small-claims procedures can absorb judicial time even when the amounts involved are relatively low and the dispute is mainly documentary. Estonia’s proposed “robot judge” was intended to explore whether low-value civil claims, initially under €7,000 and focused on contract disputes, could be processed more efficiently through automated support. The objective was to reduce backlogs for judges and court clerks while preserving the possibility of appeal to a human judge. The implementation concept was that parties would upload documents and relevant evidence, after which the system would analyse the material and issue a decision or recommendation. At a conceptual level, such a tool would require structured digital submissions, document analysis, legal-rule modelling, case classification and clear procedural safeguards for review and appeal. The expected impact would be faster processing of routine small claims, lower administrative burden and improved access to justice for simple disputes. However, many details remained unresolved, including legal responsibility, procedural fairness, transparency and the appropriate boundary between automation and adjudication. Later legal commentary suggests that Estonia has long used semi-automatic procedures for small claims, but public evidence of a fully operational autonomous AI judge remains limited. The use case should therefore be treated as an exploratory justice-automation initiative rather than a confirmed deployed judicial decision-maker.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-571,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00558,EU_PSTW_202609_00558,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,University of Luxembourg,General services.,"The central problem is that legal materials are complex, interpretative and difficult to translate into structured representations that can support querying, compliance checking and decision support.",MIREL - Mining and reasoning with legal text,University of Luxembourg; Academic-Research; General services.; Luxembourg,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"MIREL was established to advance the mining and formal reasoning of legal texts by creating an international and inter-sectoral network across law, artificial intelligence and computational modelling. The central problem is that legal materials are complex, interpretative and difficult to translate into structured representations that can support querying, compliance checking and decision support. Researchers, legal experts, SMEs, industrial partners and academic institutions are therefore brought together to address both conceptual and computational barriers. The project develops a formal framework and tools for transforming legal texts into representations that machines can process, compare and reason over. Its implementation connects natural language processing, legal ontologies, computational argumentation, formal logic and reasoning methods. It also considers the role of legal interpretation, the handling of large legal datasets and the complexity of regulatory compliance. A distinctive feature is its effort to bridge communities that often work separately: those developing legal ontologies and NLP parsers, and those focused on formal reasoning and logic. Through mobility and staff exchange between SMEs and academia, MIREL aims to build an interdisciplinary and inter-continental knowledge base. Its expected impact is stronger legal technology research, improved foundations for compliance tools and future market-oriented services in legal reasoning.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-572,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00559,EU_PSTW_202609_00559,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Terna Spa,Fuel and energy,"Wind-power operators need to minimise unplanned downtime because failures in turbines can reduce energy production, increase maintenance costs and require costly intervention in remote or difficult-to-access sites.",Predictive maintenance for wind farms,Terna Spa; Private sector; Fuel and energy; Italy,"This predictive-maintenance service applies machine-learning techniques to support more efficient management of wind-power assets, with the objective of anticipating failures before they cause stoppages or major damage. The implementation uses a software system that processes machinery, sensor and production data to estimate component health, detect abnormal patterns and forecast the risk of specific failures.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Wind-power operators need to minimise unplanned downtime because failures in turbines can reduce energy production, increase maintenance costs and require costly intervention in remote or difficult-to-access sites. This predictive-maintenance service applies machine-learning techniques to support more efficient management of wind-power assets, with the objective of anticipating failures before they cause stoppages or major damage. The operational problem is that wind turbines generate large volumes of sensor and operational data, but maintenance decisions often depend on identifying weak signals before they become visible through conventional inspection. The implementation uses a software system that processes machinery, sensor and production data to estimate component health, detect abnormal patterns and forecast the risk of specific failures. At a conceptual level, the system combines IoT or machine data, historical failure records, predictive algorithms, alerts, maintenance planning and reporting. This allows maintenance teams to schedule targeted interventions, prioritise high-risk assets and avoid unnecessary preventive work where a component remains healthy. The expected impact is lower downtime, reduced maintenance cost, improved production efficiency and better quality of asset-management decisions. AIMonitoring’s current Predeep platform describes AI-supported predictive maintenance, integration with 4.0 machinery, IoT sensors and databases, automatic alerts, maintenance planning and reported average results such as reduced downtime and maintenance costs, although those performance figures are supplier-reported.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-573,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00560,EU_PSTW_202609_00560,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Lombardy Region,Public health services.,UNKNOWN,Lombardia Chatbot Coronavirus,Lombardy Region; Regional Government; Public health services.; Italy,"The implementation consisted of an automated information chatbot that led users through predefined interaction paths, using symptom selection, rule-based decision flows, natural language interaction elements, and curated guidance from the Ministry of Health and relevant international organisations.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Lombardia Coronavirus Chatbot was developed during the COVID-19 emergency to respond to the urgent need for clear, accessible, and structured public information on symptoms and appropriate behavioural measures. The operational problem was the rapid increase in citizen enquiries during a public health crisis, which placed pressure on regional information channels, healthcare services, and emergency communication systems. The stakeholders included residents of Lombardy, the regional administration, ARIA SpA as the Regional Company for Innovation and Purchasing, healthcare authorities, and public health bodies responsible for issuing guidance. The objective was to provide a guided self-orientation tool that helped users understand which actions to take based on their selected symptoms and official recommendations. The implementation consisted of an automated information chatbot that led users through predefined interaction paths, using symptom selection, rule-based decision flows, natural language interaction elements, and curated guidance from the Ministry of Health and relevant international organisations. The service was made available through a digital channel and integrated conceptually with regional communication infrastructure and authoritative public health content. The impact was immediate and visible, as the tool generated very high access numbers shortly after launch. Its main value was the ability to scale public communication rapidly, reduce repetitive enquiries to human operators, improve consistency of advice, and support citizens in navigating uncertainty during the emergency. The chatbot also strengthened the user experience by offering a simple, guided route to trusted information.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,NOT_IN_USE,미사용,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-575,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00561,EU_PSTW_202609_00561,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Barcelona,General services.,"The practical challenge was to connect diverse devices, applications and legacy systems in a way that was open, interoperable and scalable across city domains. This avoids fragmented one-purpose solutions and supports integration across environmental monitoring, mobility, public space, energy and other municipal services.",Sentilo BCN - Plataforma de Sensors i Actuadors de Barcelona,City of Barcelona; Local Government; General services.; Spain,"The expected impact is improved interoperability, lower integration cost, stronger reuse of urban data and a larger ecosystem of cities and companies using free software.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Smart-city services depend on the ability to collect, share and reuse information from many heterogeneous sensors and urban systems without creating isolated vertical platforms. Barcelona City Council developed Sentilo as an open-source sensor and actuator platform to support its smart-city strategy and provide a reusable infrastructure layer for real-time urban data. The practical challenge was to connect diverse devices, applications and legacy systems in a way that was open, interoperable and scalable across city domains. The implementation provides a cross-platform architecture through which sensors and actuators can publish and consume data, allowing different applications to reuse information from the same urban infrastructure. At a conceptual level, Sentilo acts as a middleware layer between physical devices and smart-city applications: sensors send data to the platform, applications subscribe to relevant streams, and the city can manage urban information through open standards and shared interfaces. This avoids fragmented one-purpose solutions and supports integration across environmental monitoring, mobility, public space, energy and other municipal services. The expected impact is improved interoperability, lower integration cost, stronger reuse of urban data and a larger ecosystem of cities and companies using free software. Current sources describe Sentilo BCN as providing real-time information on Barcelona and confirm that Sentilo remains available as open-source software with public documentation and code repositories.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-576,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00562,EU_PSTW_202609_00562,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""social_nonprofit"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Ministry of Public Administration,General services.,"The administrative problem is that ministries and public bodies often need better access to structured data, analytical tools and shared reporting capabilities to support evidence-based decisions, but such functions can be fragmented across institutions.",SKRINJA 2.0 - Business Intelligence in the civil service (BI),Ministry of Public Administration; Central-Government; General services.; Slovenia,"The main stakeholders include the Ministry of Public Administration, its ICT department, government bodies, civil servants, procurement officials and policy managers using data for planning or oversight. The system was implemented as a cross-government service to promote data-driven decision-making in the civil service.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"SKRINJA 2.0 provides Slovenia’s public administration with a horizontal data warehouse and business-intelligence service hosted on the Government Computer Cloud. The administrative problem is that ministries and public bodies often need better access to structured data, analytical tools and shared reporting capabilities to support evidence-based decisions, but such functions can be fragmented across institutions. The main stakeholders include the Ministry of Public Administration, its ICT department, government bodies, civil servants, procurement officials and policy managers using data for planning or oversight. The system was implemented as a cross-government service to promote data-driven decision-making in the civil service. Its implementation combines data warehousing, business-intelligence tools, cloud infrastructure and horizontal service configuration, enabling different parts of the administration to store, process, analyse and visualise data. Since the Ministry of Public Administration also handles national procurement activities, the system may also be used to support procurement procedures in the future. Conceptually, SKRINJA 2.0 provides common analytical infrastructure rather than a single sector-specific application, allowing public institutions to reuse data capabilities across policy areas. The expected impact is improved analytical capacity, more consistent reporting, reduced duplication of BI infrastructure and stronger support for strategic management in government. By centralising business-intelligence functions in the public cloud, the solution can enhance efficiency and create a foundation for broader data-driven governance.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-577,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00563,EU_PSTW_202609_00563,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,ProZorro,General services.,UNKNOWN,"ProZorro, Bid prediction",ProZorro; Central-Government; General services.; Ukraine,"ProZorro’s bid-prediction work applies artificial intelligence and machine-learning techniques to procurement data with the aim of estimating whether a tender is likely to take place successfully and whether a participant may leave the platform before completing the process. The implementation uses data from past tenders, participant behaviour, procurement categories and platform interactions to train predictive models.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Public procurement platforms need to understand tender behaviour in order to improve competition, reduce failed procedures and support better design of purchasing processes. ProZorro’s bid-prediction work applies artificial intelligence and machine-learning techniques to procurement data with the aim of estimating whether a tender is likely to take place successfully and whether a participant may leave the platform before completing the process. The operational problem is that public buyers, suppliers and platform administrators all face uncertainty: tenders may attract insufficient competition, bidders may drop out, and procurement processes may fail or require repetition. The implementation uses data from past tenders, participant behaviour, procurement categories and platform interactions to train predictive models. At a conceptual level, the system identifies patterns associated with successful or unsuccessful tenders and generates risk or probability signals that can help platform teams and procurement authorities focus attention on procedures that may require improvement. The expected impact is better procurement planning, fewer failed tenders, improved supplier experience and stronger evidence for platform design. Because ProZorro is a central national e-procurement system, even modest improvements in prediction and user support can have systemic value. Current public ProZorro services remain active, and recent Ukrainian procurement-analytics discussions continue to describe AI and analytics as relevant to the ProZorro ecosystem, but the exact operational status of this specific bid-prediction model is not clearly documented in current official sources.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-578,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00564,EU_PSTW_202609_00564,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,ProZorro,General services.,UNKNOWN,"ProZorro, CPV Prediction",ProZorro; Central-Government; General services.; Ukraine,"ProZorro developed an AI tool to predict the correct CPV codes and units of measurement for procurement items, with the objective of reducing misclassification and making user-facing procurement platforms easier to use. The implementation uses machine-learning methods trained on historical procurement descriptions, item names, categories and code assignments.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Correct procurement classification is essential because CPV codes affect searchability, competition, market analysis and reporting in public procurement systems. ProZorro developed an AI tool to predict the correct CPV codes and units of measurement for procurement items, with the objective of reducing misclassification and making user-facing procurement platforms easier to use. The operational problem is that buyers may choose incorrect codes or inconsistent measurement units, which can make tenders harder for suppliers to find, distort procurement statistics and reduce the usefulness of open procurement data. The implementation uses machine-learning methods trained on historical procurement descriptions, item names, categories and code assignments. At a conceptual level, a user enters or uploads procurement information, and the model proposes likely CPV codes and measurement units based on similarity to previous tenders and learned classification patterns. The expected impact is fewer classification errors, faster tender creation, improved supplier discovery, more accurate analytics and better-quality procurement data for monitoring and policy. Because ProZorro depends on open data and marketplace interoperability, standardised classification has value beyond individual tenders. However, the source information indicates that this tool had not yet been implemented at the time of documentation. Current ProZorro remains operational, but I found no current official source confirming that this specific CPV-prediction tool has since been deployed in production.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-579,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00565,EU_PSTW_202609_00565,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,ProZorro,General services.,"Public procurement oversight requires identifying high-risk tenders among very large numbers of procedures, a task that is difficult for experts and civil-society monitors to perform manually.",DoZorro AI tool,ProZorro; Central-Government; General services.; Ukraine,"DoZorro, linked to Ukraine’s ProZorro procurement ecosystem and Transparency International Ukraine, applies AI-supported risk analysis to help detect tenders that may involve corruption, collusion, unjustified disqualification or unfair selection of winners. The expected impact is better targeting of oversight resources, stronger anti-corruption monitoring, improved transparency and more efficient use of civil-society expertise.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Public procurement oversight requires identifying high-risk tenders among very large numbers of procedures, a task that is difficult for experts and civil-society monitors to perform manually. DoZorro, linked to Ukraine’s ProZorro procurement ecosystem and Transparency International Ukraine, applies AI-supported risk analysis to help detect tenders that may involve corruption, collusion, unjustified disqualification or unfair selection of winners. The platform already enables suppliers, buyers, control authorities and citizens to provide feedback on procurement processes. The AI tool strengthens that function by prioritising tenders with suspicious patterns, allowing experts to focus on cases with the highest probability of irregularity. At a conceptual level, the system combines open procurement data, complaint data, risk indicators, machine-learning models and expert review. It does not replace legal investigation or procurement-control decisions; rather, it narrows the search space and supports human monitors by highlighting tenders that deserve closer analysis. Reported beta-test results included 26% more cases of unfounded winner selection, 37% more cases of groundless disqualification and 298% more cases of participant conspiracy being identified. The expected impact is better targeting of oversight resources, stronger anti-corruption monitoring, improved transparency and more efficient use of civil-society expertise. Recent open-contracting material describes DoZorro analytics platforms as continuing to interact with ProZorro’s central database.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-580,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00566,EU_PSTW_202609_00566,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Primona,General services.,"However, procurement texts often contain complex legal and technical language, which can lead to misunderstandings, increased bidder risk, additional clarification rounds and, ultimately, delays in contract award and project execution. This leads to fewer clarification requests, faster procurement cycles, reduced administrative burden for contracting authorities and a lower risk of bid disputes.",Kommers (KAI) semantics check tool,Primona; Private sector; General services.; Sweden,"The tool leverages trained language models and domain-specific lexicons, combined with syntactic and semantic rule sets, to highlight problematic segments to the procurement author.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Public procurement processes in both public and private sector organisations require clear, unambiguous tender documentation to ensure fair competition and avoid misinterpretation by potential bidders. However, procurement texts often contain complex legal and technical language, which can lead to misunderstandings, increased bidder risk, additional clarification rounds and, ultimately, delays in contract award and project execution. Stakeholders in this context include procurement officers, legal advisors, potential suppliers and contracting authorities, all of whom benefit from improved clarity and reduced ambiguity in procurement materials. The objective of this initiative is to enhance the quality and precision of procurement documentation by identifying semantic inconsistencies, ambiguous phrases and unclear requirements before tender publication. The solution integrates an AI assistant into Primona’s Kommers e-procurement platform, extending its existing modules with natural language processing (NLP) capabilities. The AI component performs semantic analysis on requirement statements, specifications and other textual elements within procurement documents to detect unclear wording, potential contradictions and language that might be misinterpreted by bidders. The tool leverages trained language models and domain-specific lexicons, combined with syntactic and semantic rule sets, to highlight problematic segments to the procurement author. It is embedded within the Kommers workflow, allowing users to run checks directly within their document preparation interface and receive actionable feedback in real time. By automating semantic validation, the tool reduces the risk of ambiguous or poorly phrased procurement documents reaching the market, improving clarity and fairness in competitive tendering. This leads to fewer clarification requests, faster procurement cycles, reduced administrative burden for contracting authorities and a lower risk of bid disputes. Enhanced document quality contributes to better supplier engagement, more accurate tender responses and ultimately more efficient public and private sector procurement outcomes.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-581,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00567,EU_PSTW_202609_00567,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,State Treasury of Finland,General services.,"Despite Finland’s advanced adoption of e-invoicing, invoice processing still requires manual verification of key data such as agreement numbers, amounts, supplier information and accounting references, creating inefficiencies and limiting full automation. A significant challenge identified by the State Treasury is the inconsistent structuring and placement of invoice data by suppliers and IT service providers, which hinders seamless machine processing.",State Treasury e-invoicing plans,State Treasury of Finland; Central-Government; General services.; Finland,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Finnish State Treasury is responsible for overseeing and supporting financial management processes across central government entities, including the handling of a high volume of electronic invoices. Despite Finland’s advanced adoption of e-invoicing, invoice processing still requires manual verification of key data such as agreement numbers, amounts, supplier information and accounting references, creating inefficiencies and limiting full automation. A significant challenge identified by the State Treasury is the inconsistent structuring and placement of invoice data by suppliers and IT service providers, which hinders seamless machine processing. The objective of the initiative is to increase the level of end-to-end automation in public sector invoice handling, reduce manual workload and improve accuracy and processing speed across government financial operations. The planned solution combines artificial intelligence techniques and robotic process automation to analyse structured and semi-structured invoice data. Machine learning models are designed to automatically extract and validate relevant invoice fields, such as contract identifiers and payment amounts, while rule-based automation tools integrate with existing financial management systems to route, verify and post invoices. The system relies on standardised e-invoice formats and interoperability frameworks, and is embedded within the State Treasury’s digital financial infrastructure to enable scalable, centralised processing. The initiative aims to enable near real-time invoice validation, significantly reduce manual intervention, minimise processing errors and accelerate payment cycles. By improving data quality requirements and promoting standardisation among suppliers, the State Treasury strengthens the reliability and efficiency of public financial management, reduces administrative costs and enhances transparency and scalability in government accounting processes.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,S6_WORKFLOW_PROCESS_AUTOMATION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-583,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00568,EU_PSTW_202609_00568,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,YPO,General services.,"The operational problem is that procurement information can be spread across web pages, framework documentation and service categories, which makes self-service navigation difficult for schools, local authorities and other public organisations.","YPO, Procurement Information Provider",YPO; Central-Government; General services.; United Kingdom,"The implementation uses a conversational interface on YPO’s website, allowing users to ask questions or follow prompts rather than manually search through procurement content. The expected impact is faster access to procurement guidance, reduced friction for public buyers, fewer routine enquiries and better use of YPO’s framework catalogue.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Public-sector buyers often need help navigating procurement frameworks, services and specialist teams, especially when they must purchase quickly while complying with public procurement rules. YPO introduced the Procurement Information Provider, known as PIP, as a chatbot to help users find relevant frameworks, understand YPO services and reach the right procurement teams. The operational problem is that procurement information can be spread across web pages, framework documentation and service categories, which makes self-service navigation difficult for schools, local authorities and other public organisations. The implementation uses a conversational interface on YPO’s website, allowing users to ask questions or follow prompts rather than manually search through procurement content. At a conceptual level, the chatbot retrieves relevant information from the site, directs users to appropriate frameworks and provides hand-off to human procurement specialists where needed. The expected impact is faster access to procurement guidance, reduced friction for public buyers, fewer routine enquiries and better use of YPO’s framework catalogue. The chatbot’s role is advisory and navigational rather than transactional: it helps users identify procurement routes, but does not replace procurement judgement or formal tendering processes. UKAuthority reported the launch in 2018 as a chatbot built to help public-sector buyers, while YPO itself remains an active public-sector procurement organisation.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-584,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00569,EU_PSTW_202609_00569,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Hansel Ltd.,General services.,"The operational problem was that e-invoicing data from the state system was not originally categorised according to a standard procurement taxonomy, making it difficult to search, compare or analyse.",Dashboard to Explore state spending in Finland,Hansel Ltd.; Central-Government; General services.; Finland,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Open procurement data can strengthen public accountability only if citizens, companies and analysts can understand what the government is buying and from whom. Finland’s Explore State Spending service, Tutkihankintoja.fi, provides a public dashboard for examining government purchase-invoice data, including spending by procuring organisation, supplier and category of goods or services. The operational problem was that e-invoicing data from the state system was not originally categorised according to a standard procurement taxonomy, making it difficult to search, compare or analyse. Hansel piloted a machine-learning solution to automatically categorise state e-invoicing data according to the United Nations Standard Products and Services Code taxonomy. At a conceptual level, the system takes invoice-level procurement data, classifies spending into meaningful procurement categories and makes it searchable through a public web interface. This enables users to explore areas such as ICT procurement, administrative services, equipment or transport-related purchases. The expected impact is greater transparency, easier spending analysis, better procurement oversight and improved data quality for public buyers. Other AI-enabled analysis is also used internally for procurement authorities. Current sources confirm that the portal remains online and that Hansel continued expanding the service by adding new organisations in 2023.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-585,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00570,EU_PSTW_202609_00570,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Hansel Ltd.,General services.,"Finland’s eNest solution was designed to simplify supplier verification during public procurement by reducing the need for bidders to submit official records manually. This creates duplication, administrative burden and avoidable friction for both public buyers and service providers.",eNest solution,Hansel Ltd.; Central-Government; General services.; Finland,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Finland’s eNest solution was designed to simplify supplier verification during public procurement by reducing the need for bidders to submit official records manually. In tendering processes, contracting authorities must check that suppliers meet legal and administrative requirements, while companies often need to gather and attach documents that already exist in official databases. This creates duplication, administrative burden and avoidable friction for both public buyers and service providers. eNest aims to make the tendering process more efficient by automatically verifying information submitted by suppliers against official records. The implementation relies on the European Single Procurement Document as a standardised structure for supplier declarations, allowing the system to connect tender information with authoritative public registers. At a conceptual level, the solution combines procurement workflow integration, automated data checks, standardised supplier self-declarations and register-based verification. By replacing repeated document submission with automated confirmation, the system can improve reliability, reduce processing time and make participation easier for companies. The expected impact is lower administrative cost, fewer errors in tender documentation, faster assessment of suppliers and a better user experience for both contracting authorities and bidders. It also supports interoperability in public procurement by aligning national processes with European procurement standards.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-586,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00571,EU_PSTW_202609_00571,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Finnish Government Shared Services Centre for Finance and HR,General services.,UNKNOWN,Palkeet - RPA Project,Finnish Government Shared Services Centre for Finance and HR; Central-Government; General services.; Finland,"Palkeet, the Finnish Government Shared Services Centre for Finance and HR, introduced robotic process automation to automate routine business processes across public administration support services. This approach allowed Palkeet to automate without immediately replacing all underlying legacy systems.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Shared service centres handle large volumes of repetitive finance and HR tasks where accuracy, consistency and processing speed are essential. Palkeet, the Finnish Government Shared Services Centre for Finance and HR, introduced robotic process automation to automate routine business processes across public administration support services. The operational problem was that tasks such as purchase-invoice validation, supplier-register maintenance and other administrative checks required structured but repetitive work by staff. The implementation used UiPath software robots, alongside other tools where appropriate, to model and execute automated workflows. At a conceptual level, RPA bots log into existing systems, follow predefined process rules, validate data, move information between applications and flag exceptions for human review. This approach allowed Palkeet to automate without immediately replacing all underlying legacy systems. The expected impact is higher productivity, improved processing quality, fewer manual errors, faster throughput and better use of staff time for expert work. The project is recognised as one of the first major RPA implementations in the Finnish public sector and formed part of a wider government digitalisation agenda. UiPath’s case material states that Palkeet began considering RPA in spring 2015 and became one of the first Finnish organisations to foresee its benefits, while Palkeet remains an active shared-services provider in Finnish government.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S6_WORKFLOW_PROCESS_AUTOMATION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-587,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00572,EU_PSTW_202609_00572,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Ministry of Finance,General services.,"The operational problem was to move from fragmented or paper-heavy procurement processes towards a transparent digital system connecting public buyers, private platforms and a central data layer.",mTender in France,Ministry of Finance; Central-Government; General services.; Moldova,"The platform supports electronic open tenders, reverse auctions, aggregated procurement planning and online framework agreements, while using the Open Contracting Data Standard to make procurement information more accessible and reusable. The reported use of robotic process automation for tracking contract execution reflects the wider goal of automating repetitive controls and improving visibility across the procurement lifecycle.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Public procurement reform requires digital systems that increase transparency, reduce administrative friction and create structured data across the full procurement cycle. Moldova’s Ministry of Finance developed mTender with support from the European Bank for Reconstruction and Development as an open-source electronic procurement system. The platform supports electronic open tenders, reverse auctions, aggregated procurement planning and online framework agreements, while using the Open Contracting Data Standard to make procurement information more accessible and reusable. The operational problem was to move from fragmented or paper-heavy procurement processes towards a transparent digital system connecting public buyers, private platforms and a central data layer. The implementation consists of a public portal, an open-data central database and accredited private-sector platforms that support electronic procurement by public and private users. At a conceptual level, mTender digitises procurement planning, tender publication, bidding, data disclosure and contract-related monitoring. The reported use of robotic process automation for tracking contract execution reflects the wider goal of automating repetitive controls and improving visibility across the procurement lifecycle. The expected impact is more transparent public spending, greater competition, easier supplier participation, stronger data-driven monitoring and alignment with international open-contracting practice. The current MTender portal remains accessible and describes the system as supporting procurement from planning through payment, while recent Moldovan reporting indicates that a new e-Achiziții system has been approved as the next stage of modernisation.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S6_WORKFLOW_PROCESS_AUTOMATION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-588,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00573,EU_PSTW_202609_00573,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Estonian Government,Public health services.,"Its expected impact was faster access to public information, reduced administrative burden, greater consistency of answers and improved communication during a fast-moving crisis.",SUVE - Chatbot for questions about the emergency situation and the coronavirus,Estonian Government; Central-Government; Public health services.; Estonia,The case illustrates how conversational automation can be rapidly deployed for crisis communication when public demand is high and information needs are repetitive.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"During the COVID-19 emergency, citizens needed quick, consistent and accessible answers to frequently asked questions about restrictions, services and official guidance. Estonia’s SUVE chatbot was introduced across the websites of several government agencies to provide automated responses in Estonian and English, reducing pressure on public-service channels and helping users find reliable information from official sources. The operational problem was typical of the pandemic period: public rules changed rapidly, citizens repeatedly asked similar questions, and government agencies needed to maintain a single, trustworthy information layer while avoiding overload on call centres and staff. The implementation used an automated conversational interface able to answer frequently asked questions about general services and the emergency situation. At a conceptual level, the chatbot connected user queries to curated government information, allowing citizens to receive immediate guidance through agency websites without navigating multiple pages or waiting for human assistance. Its expected impact was faster access to public information, reduced administrative burden, greater consistency of answers and improved communication during a fast-moving crisis. Because SUVE relied on official sources and supported both Estonian and English, it also helped reach residents and foreign users who needed emergency information. The case illustrates how conversational automation can be rapidly deployed for crisis communication when public demand is high and information needs are repetitive.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,NOT_IN_USE,미사용,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-589,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00574,EU_PSTW_202609_00574,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Information System Authority,General services.,"If the system cannot determine whether an observed situation is erroneous or permitted, a user still decides how to interpret the signal.",KRATID - Detecting traffic anomalies and incidents of the Estonian data exchange layer (X-Road),Information System Authority; Central-Government; General services.; Estonia,"KRATID uses machine learning to monitor traffic patterns in the Estonian data exchange layer and detect anomalies or possible incidents. The operational problem is that very large numbers of exchanges take place through X-Road, making it difficult for human administrators to identify unusual behaviour, technical faults or unexpected changes in service use manually.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Digital government depends on reliable data exchange between public institutions, and Estonia’s X-Road is a critical infrastructure layer for secure communication between public and private-sector systems. KRATID uses machine learning to monitor traffic patterns in the Estonian data exchange layer and detect anomalies or possible incidents. The operational problem is that very large numbers of exchanges take place through X-Road, making it difficult for human administrators to identify unusual behaviour, technical faults or unexpected changes in service use manually. The implementation compares current data-exchange behaviour with previous periods and searches for differences that may indicate an error, abnormal usage or another event requiring attention. At a conceptual level, the system combines traffic monitoring, anomaly detection, historical pattern comparison and human review. If the system cannot determine whether an observed situation is erroneous or permitted, a user still decides how to interpret the signal. The expected impact is stronger operational awareness for the Information System Authority, faster detection of incidents, better guidance for X-Road users and more resilient digital public infrastructure. The use case should be separated from other Estonian AI examples sometimes listed in the same source, such as job matching, land-mowing detection or police traffic deployment; the core KRATID case concerns X-Road anomaly and incident detection.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-590,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00575,EU_PSTW_202609_00575,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,ITAINNOVA - Instituto Tecnológico de Aragón,Public health services.,UNKNOWN,COVID19 AragonBot,ITAINNOVA - Instituto Tecnológico de Aragón; Local Government; Public health services.; Spain,"The use case is strongly linked to the exceptional conditions of the pandemic, when speed of deployment, channel accessibility and operational relief were more important than building a long-term administrative assistant.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Regional emergency communication during the pandemic required fast responses to recurring citizen questions, particularly when telephone support lines were saturated. COVID19AragonBot was launched by the Government of Aragon with the support of ITAINNOVA to provide an automated response service through Telegram. The initiative emerged from a practical service-delivery problem: citizens repeatedly asked similar questions about COVID-19 rules, symptoms, services and procedures, while more complex or specific issues still required specialised human attention. The chatbot therefore acted as a first-level digital assistant, guiding users through common questions and helping redirect more complicated cases. The implementation used artificial intelligence and conversational automation within the Telegram messaging environment, making the service accessible through a familiar mobile channel. At a conceptual level, the bot classified user requests, matched them to prepared official information and provided immediate answers or orientation. Its expected impact was to reduce pressure on public helplines, accelerate responses, improve consistency of public information and allow human operators to focus on cases requiring judgement or escalation. The use case is strongly linked to the exceptional conditions of the pandemic, when speed of deployment, channel accessibility and operational relief were more important than building a long-term administrative assistant. It demonstrates how regional governments used lightweight conversational tools to manage sudden surges in public-service demand.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,NOT_IN_USE,미사용,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-591,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00576,EU_PSTW_202609_00576,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Pasteur Institute,Public health services.,UNKNOWN,Covid-bot - France,Pasteur Institute; Academic-Research; Public health services.; France,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Public-health authorities needed scalable tools to help people assess possible COVID-19 risk and obtain guidance without immediately contacting medical services. In France, the Pasteur Institute, Greater Paris University Hospitals and the chatbot start-up Clevy released an open-source COVID-19 auto-diagnosis chatbot in March 2020. The objective was to provide a structured questionnaire through which users could report symptoms and receive an indication of their possible risk of contamination or need for further action. The implementation relied on a conversational questionnaire rather than a free-form diagnostic system: users answered predefined questions, and the bot mapped responses to risk-assessment guidance based on the available public-health knowledge at the time. At a conceptual level, the service translated triage logic into an accessible digital interface, supporting early self-assessment, orientation and public communication. Its expected impact was to reduce unnecessary pressure on health services, help citizens understand their situation, and provide a rapid channel for standardised information during the first wave of the pandemic. The open-source character also allowed the tool to be reused or examined by other actors. The service should not be understood as a substitute for medical diagnosis; its role was to provide preliminary orientation during an emergency, when demand for health information was exceptionally high and official communication needed to scale quickly.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,NOT_IN_USE,미사용,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-594,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00577,EU_PSTW_202609_00577,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Amsterdam Parking Services,Transport,"Managing scarce parking space in a dense city requires regular enforcement, reliable permit checks and a process that can cover large numbers of parked vehicles without excessive manual patrol effort.",Automated parking control,Amsterdam Parking Services; Local Government; Transport; Netherlands,"Amsterdam uses automated parking control to determine whether parked vehicles have the right to occupy paid parking spaces, either because a fee has been paid through a parking meter or app, or because the vehicle has a valid parking permit. The implementation uses scan cars equipped with cameras and automatic number-plate recognition to identify licence plates and check them against relevant payment and permit systems.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Managing scarce parking space in a dense city requires regular enforcement, reliable permit checks and a process that can cover large numbers of parked vehicles without excessive manual patrol effort. Amsterdam uses automated parking control to determine whether parked vehicles have the right to occupy paid parking spaces, either because a fee has been paid through a parking meter or app, or because the vehicle has a valid parking permit. The implementation uses scan cars equipped with cameras and automatic number-plate recognition to identify licence plates and check them against relevant payment and permit systems. At a conceptual level, the scan car captures the plate, converts it into machine-readable data, checks the data against back-office records and flags cases where no valid parking right appears to exist. Human procedures can then verify and handle enforcement actions where needed. The expected impact is more efficient street-parking enforcement, better compliance with parking rules, reduced fraud and improved turnover of parking spaces. The system operates at large scale: Egis reports that Amsterdam’s on-street parking service uses ANPR to monitor more than six million vehicles per month.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-595,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00578,EU_PSTW_202609_00578,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Research, Information & Statistics (OIS)",General services.,"The earlier approach depended heavily on users choosing the right category from many drop-down options, which could lead to errors and delays because the municipality has many service departments and categories. This reduces the administrative burden on the person making the report and improves the chance that the case reaches the correct team quickly.",Reporting issues in public space,"Research, Information & Statistics (OIS); Local Government; General services.; Netherlands","The implementation uses an algorithm within the Signals reporting system to recognise relevant words and phrases in the user’s description, infer the likely category and support routing to the responsible department. At a conceptual level, the system applies natural-language processing or keyword-based text classification to citizen-submitted reports, then links the classification to municipal workflow allocation.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Cities receive large volumes of reports about waste, broken infrastructure, unsafe traffic situations, nuisance and maintenance issues, and the value of such reporting depends on routing each case to the correct municipal team. Amsterdam’s public-space reporting system allows residents to submit issues online, by phone or through external applications. The earlier approach depended heavily on users choosing the right category from many drop-down options, which could lead to errors and delays because the municipality has many service departments and categories. The implementation uses an algorithm within the Signals reporting system to recognise relevant words and phrases in the user’s description, infer the likely category and support routing to the responsible department. At a conceptual level, the system applies natural-language processing or keyword-based text classification to citizen-submitted reports, then links the classification to municipal workflow allocation. This reduces the administrative burden on the person making the report and improves the chance that the case reaches the correct team quickly. The expected impact is faster processing, fewer misrouted reports, less manual reclassification and better responsiveness in public-space management.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F08_ERROR_COMPLIANCE_RISK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-597,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00579,EU_PSTW_202609_00579,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Helsinki,Transport,"Parking rules can be difficult for residents and visitors to navigate because they differ by location, permit type, vehicle, time period and payment situation.",Parking chatbot,City of Helsinki; Local Government; Transport; Finland,The implementation uses a chatbot interface to answer common questions about parking services and guide users towards relevant information.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Parking rules can be difficult for residents and visitors to navigate because they differ by location, permit type, vehicle, time period and payment situation. Helsinki’s parking chatbot provides an automated customer-service channel for parking-related questions through the city’s parking website. The operational problem is that many parking enquiries are repetitive and informational, but still require users to interpret municipal rules or find the correct page. The implementation uses a chatbot interface to answer common questions about parking services and guide users towards relevant information. At a conceptual level, the system recognises user intent, retrieves or matches the appropriate parking guidance, and presents an answer in conversational form. It does not replace formal permit decisions or enforcement processes; its role is to improve access to parking information and reduce routine enquiries. The expected impact is better customer experience, quicker answers outside conventional service channels, reduced pressure on human customer-service staff and more consistent communication of parking rules. The chatbot is part of Helsinki’s public AI register, which documents AI and algorithmic systems used by the city to improve transparency, accountability and civic participation.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-598,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00580,EU_PSTW_202609_00580,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Helsinki City Hospital,Public health services.,UNKNOWN,Health center chatbot,Helsinki City Hospital; Local Government; Public health services.; Finland,"Health-service users often need basic guidance before deciding whether to use a digital service, contact a health station, seek dental or mental-health support, or use social-service counselling. The implementation uses natural-language processing to analyse users’ searches, identify their intended service need and match that need with reliable instructions.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Health-service users often need basic guidance before deciding whether to use a digital service, contact a health station, seek dental or mental-health support, or use social-service counselling. Helsinki’s health center chatbot provides anonymous, secure and round-the-clock advice for common health and illness-related questions. The operational objective is to improve access to information while directing users to the right digital health or social-service channel, such as Omaolo, Omakanta, social counselling chat or other city services. The implementation uses natural-language processing to analyse users’ searches, identify their intended service need and match that need with reliable instructions. At a conceptual level, the chatbot combines intent recognition, service navigation, evidence-based guidance and integration with digital health-service pathways. Clinical guidance is based on national practices, including evidence-based treatment, Current Care guidelines and emergency-care evaluation criteria. The expected impact is wider access to health-centre services, reduced queuing for routine guidance, improved consistency of advice, better user experience and a more efficient front door to digital health services. The service also supports staff by automating responses to frequently asked questions and collecting background information where appropriate.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-599,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00581,EU_PSTW_202609_00581,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Helsinki Central Library Oodi,Cultural services.,UNKNOWN,Oodi's book recommendation service Obotti,Helsinki Central Library Oodi; Local Government; Cultural services.; Finland,"At a conceptual level, the service uses contextual natural-language analysis to infer the user’s interests and connect them with available materials in Oodi’s collection at the time of recommendation. The expected impact is improved discovery of library resources, a more playful and personalised customer experience, greater use of the collection and broader experimentation with AI in cultural services.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Large public libraries hold collections that many visitors only partly discover, particularly when users do not know which titles, themes or authors match their interests. Obotti was developed for Helsinki Central Library Oodi as a recommendation chatbot that helps customers find books from Oodi’s collection. The objective is to make library material easier and more engaging to explore, especially by recommending works that users might not otherwise encounter through ordinary catalogue searches. The implementation is a mobile application for Android and iOS, divided into six thematic chatbot characters that recommend content according to different interests. Users can interact through predefined answer options or free search terms in text or speech. At a conceptual level, the service uses contextual natural-language analysis to infer the user’s interests and connect them with available materials in Oodi’s collection at the time of recommendation. The expected impact is improved discovery of library resources, a more playful and personalised customer experience, greater use of the collection and broader experimentation with AI in cultural services.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-600,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00582,EU_PSTW_202609_00582,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""social_nonprofit""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Bonn,General public services n.e.c.,"Municipal service information can be difficult for residents to locate when it is distributed across websites, offices and specialised service pages.",ChatBot Botty Bonn,City of Bonn; Local Government; General public services n.e.c.; Germany,"Recent reporting indicates that Bonn now uses a data-protection-compliant AI chatbot on the city website, developed by the GovTech start-up neuraflow, to expand online information on municipal services.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Municipal service information can be difficult for residents to locate when it is distributed across websites, offices and specialised service pages. Bonn’s chatbot Botty was developed to provide a conversational access point to municipal information and services, making the city’s website more intuitive for users seeking administrative guidance. The objective was to create an open community-oriented platform that could include municipal service information and earlier expert bots, while giving developers a live and development environment for further improvement. The implementation involved a technical foundation and server hosting financed and operated by the City of Bonn and GovBot, with municipal service information made available through the chatbot. At a conceptual level, the system recognises user questions, searches or maps them to municipal service descriptions, and responds in a chat-style interface. Its expected impact is easier access to public information, fewer routine enquiries, better digital availability outside office hours and a more collaborative ecosystem for chatbot development. Recent reporting indicates that Bonn now uses a data-protection-compliant AI chatbot on the city website, developed by the GovTech start-up neuraflow, to expand online information on municipal services.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-601,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00583,EU_PSTW_202609_00583,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Prefecture de l'Ile de France,General public services n.e.c.,"Entrepreneurs and start-ups often face fragmented administrative procedures, especially when they need to understand which obligations, forms or public services apply to their situation.",Noa - chatbot,Prefecture de l'Ile de France; Regional Government; General public services n.e.c.; France,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Entrepreneurs and start-ups often face fragmented administrative procedures, especially when they need to understand which obligations, forms or public services apply to their situation. Noa was designed as an online public-service chatbot to guide start-ups through administrative processes in the Île-de-France context. The operational objective was to simplify access to administrative guidance by offering a conversational entry point rather than requiring users to search across multiple institutional pages. The implementation used a chatbot interface to answer common questions, orient users through procedures and provide practical information on relevant public services. At a conceptual level, the system acted as a digital navigation layer over administrative content: users described their need, and the bot directed them towards the appropriate process or information. Its expected impact was reduced friction for start-ups, faster access to administrative guidance, lower demand for repetitive human support and a clearer user journey through public administration. The case reflects a broader shift towards user-centred government services, where chatbots are used not because the underlying procedures are simple, but because users need help navigating them. In this sense, Noa’s value lay in reducing uncertainty at the first point of contact and making public administration more approachable for new businesses.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,NOT_IN_USE,미사용,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-602,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00584,EU_PSTW_202609_00584,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Berlin,General public services n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,Bobbi - Berlin administration,City of Berlin; Local Government; General public services n.e.c.; Germany,"The use case also illustrates the role of municipal chatbots in large metropolitan administrations, where the volume and variety of citizen requests make navigation support valuable.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Citizens often need information from local administration outside normal service hours, and many requests concern recurring topics that can be answered through standardised guidance. Berlin’s chatbot Bobbi was introduced as an additional channel alongside the ServicePortal and the 115 citizen hotline, helping residents obtain quick answers to administrative questions at any time. The objective was not to replace existing service channels, but to provide a convenient first point of contact for common concerns, especially when users wanted immediate orientation rather than a telephone conversation. The implementation used a conversational interface connected to administrative information from Berlin’s public-service ecosystem. At a conceptual level, the chatbot interpreted user questions, matched them with relevant service descriptions or procedural information, and directed citizens towards the correct administrative pathway. Its expected impact was faster access to information, reduced pressure on human service channels, improved availability outside hotline hours and a more user-friendly digital front door for the city administration. The use case also illustrates the role of municipal chatbots in large metropolitan administrations, where the volume and variety of citizen requests make navigation support valuable. Bobbi was best understood as a supplementary digital assistant: useful for standard questions, but dependent on escalation or redirection where individual circumstances required human assessment.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,NOT_IN_USE,미사용,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-603,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00585,EU_PSTW_202609_00585,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Transport and Mobility Leuven,Transport,UNKNOWN,HoPE - Holistic Personal public Eco-mobility,Transport and Mobility Leuven; Consortium; Transport; Belgium,"HoPE was conceived as a user-centred mobility platform intended to encourage more conscious, convenient and sustainable use of public transport and eco-mobility services. Urban and regional travellers often face fragmented information across transport modes, ticketing systems, payment channels and journey-planning tools, which can make sustainable transport less attractive than private car use.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"HoPE was conceived as a user-centred mobility platform intended to encourage more conscious, convenient and sustainable use of public transport and eco-mobility services. Urban and regional travellers often face fragmented information across transport modes, ticketing systems, payment channels and journey-planning tools, which can make sustainable transport less attractive than private car use. The project therefore aimed to bring together mobility information and service functions in a single platform that could support greener travel choices while improving the user experience. The implementation combined info-mobility, trip planning, ticket reservation, fare calculation and mobile payment functions within a digital platform oriented around individual users. Conceptually, it integrated transport data, journey-planning algorithms, payment services, user-interface design and personalised mobility information to help people compare and access transport options more easily. The expected impact was greater awareness of public transport and eco-mobility, smoother journey planning, better access to multimodal services and increased attractiveness of low-emission travel. By combining information, reservation and payment capabilities, HoPE sought to reduce friction in everyday mobility decisions and create value for both users and transport providers. Its broader contribution lies in demonstrating how digital platforms can support behavioural change towards more sustainable transport systems.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-604,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00586,EU_PSTW_202609_00586,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Cyprus Research and Innovation Center,General services.,"FLOBOT addressed the difficulty of cleaning very large indoor surfaces in professional environments such as supermarkets, airports, trade fairs, hospitals and industrial or commercial premises. The expected impact was reduced manual effort, improved cleaning productivity, more consistent service quality and better scalability for cleaning operations across large buildings.",FLOBOT - Floor Washing Robot for Professional Users,Cyprus Research and Innovation Center; Consortium; General services.; Cyprus,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"FLOBOT addressed the difficulty of cleaning very large indoor surfaces in professional environments such as supermarkets, airports, trade fairs, hospitals and industrial or commercial premises. These facilities often require floor washing every day, and sometimes several times during the day, creating repetitive, physically demanding and time-consuming work for cleaning staff. Existing robotic solutions did not meet the standards expected by professional cleaning-service providers, particularly in relation to performance, safety, reliability and usability in busy environments. The project therefore developed and validated a professional robotic scrubber-dryer capable of washing and drying large floor areas. Its implementation combined robotics, autonomous navigation, floor-cleaning hardware, perception systems, safety controls and user interfaces suitable for professional operators. At a conceptual level, the robot needed to map and move through large premises, avoid obstacles, apply brushes and suction effectively, and operate in spaces shared with people and equipment. The expected impact was reduced manual effort, improved cleaning productivity, more consistent service quality and better scalability for cleaning operations across large buildings. By automating repetitive floor-maintenance tasks, FLOBOT could allow cleaning staff to focus on supervision, detailed tasks and service quality. The project also offered business value to cleaning providers by addressing labour intensity and operational efficiency.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-605,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00587,EU_PSTW_202609_00587,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,University of Wolverhampton,Public health services.,"FIRST was developed to improve reading accessibility for people with Autism Spectrum Disorders, who may experience difficulty understanding complex instructions, figurative language, uncommon words, ambiguous phrasing or imprecise documents.",FIRST - A Flexible Interactive Reading Support Tool,University of Wolverhampton; Consortium; Public health services.; United Kingdom,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"FIRST was developed to improve reading accessibility for people with Autism Spectrum Disorders, who may experience difficulty understanding complex instructions, figurative language, uncommon words, ambiguous phrasing or imprecise documents. The practical problem is that many everyday texts are written in ways that assume strong inferential and contextual reading skills, which can create barriers in education, employment, public services and independent living. The project aimed to give users a tool that could convert standard documents into personalised versions that are easier to understand. Its implementation used language technology to simplify text according to the needs of people with ASD. Conceptually, the system analysed source documents, identified linguistic features that may create comprehension barriers, and generated clearer alternatives through simplification, explanation, rephrasing or adaptation of wording. The expected impact was improved comprehension, greater autonomy, reduced reliance on intermediaries and better access to written information. For educators, carers, service providers and public bodies, such a tool could make communication more inclusive and reduce misunderstandings. By focusing on personalisation rather than one-size-fits-all simplification, FIRST recognised that accessibility depends on individual reading profiles and contexts. Its business and public value lie in making complex documents more usable for a group often underserved by mainstream text technologies.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-606,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00588,EU_PSTW_202609_00588,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Twente University,Public health services.,UNKNOWN,DE-ENIGMA (Multi-Modal Human-Robot Interaction for Teaching Social Imagination in Autistic Children),Twente University; Consortium; Public health services.; Netherlands,DE-ENIGMA focused on the use of robot-based interaction to support social imagination and social communication skills among autistic children.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"DE-ENIGMA focused on the use of robot-based interaction to support social imagination and social communication skills among autistic children. Many children with autism spectrum conditions may find social cues, imaginative interaction and reciprocal communication challenging, while therapists, educators and families need tools that can provide structured, engaging and repeatable support. The project aimed to create and evaluate technology that could help children practise social interaction in a controlled and motivating environment. The implementation used multi-modal human-robot interaction, combining robotic behaviour, sensors, affective computing, speech or gesture processing and adaptive interaction design. At a conceptual level, the robot acted as an interactive teaching partner, recognising child responses and supporting exercises designed to expand social understanding and imaginative engagement. The expected impact was more personalised therapeutic support, increased child engagement, improved consistency of social-skills training and new evidence on the role of robots in autism interventions. By combining robotics with behavioural and educational expertise, DE-ENIGMA sought to support professionals rather than replace them, offering an additional tool for structured learning. Its value lies in creating repeatable, measurable and potentially scalable interaction scenarios for children who may respond positively to predictable robotic partners.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-607,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00589,EU_PSTW_202609_00589,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Ulster University,Public health services.,"The operational challenge is that older people in rural and remote settings may have limited access to continuous rehabilitation support, mobility assessment and preventive monitoring, while health and care providers need better information on functional decline, wellbeing and safety. By allowing data to be gathered outside traditional clinical settings, SENDoc could reduce travel burdens, support preventive care and improve service reach.",Smart sENsor Devices fOr rehabilitation and Connected health - SENDoc,Ulster University; Consortium; Public health services.; United Kingdom,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"SENDoc introduced wearable sensor systems to support rehabilitation, independent living and connected health in ageing communities across remote areas of Northern Ireland and northern Scotland. The operational challenge is that older people in rural and remote settings may have limited access to continuous rehabilitation support, mobility assessment and preventive monitoring, while health and care providers need better information on functional decline, wellbeing and safety. The project therefore aimed to test how wearable technologies could measure mobility, strength, balance, exercise, wellbeing and safety in everyday contexts. Its implementation used smart sensor devices worn by users, collecting physiological or movement-related data that could inform rehabilitation and care decisions. Conceptually, the system combined wearable sensing, connected-health data flows, remote monitoring, user feedback and clinical or community-care interpretation. The expected impact was improved support for independent living, earlier identification of deterioration, more personalised rehabilitation and better continuity of care for older people living far from specialist services. By allowing data to be gathered outside traditional clinical settings, SENDoc could reduce travel burdens, support preventive care and improve service reach. Its broader value lies in demonstrating how connected health tools can strengthen ageing-in-place strategies and make rehabilitation more responsive in underserved rural communities.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-608,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00590,EU_PSTW_202609_00590,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Institute for Computer Science and Control,Transport,UNKNOWN,ADVANCE - Advanced predictive-analysis-based decision-support engine for logistics,Institute for Computer Science and Control; Consortium; Transport; Hungary,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"ADVANCE was designed to help logistics operators make better decisions in transport planning, dispatching and strategic network management. Logistics environments generate large volumes of data on routes, orders, partners, traffic conditions, capacity constraints and delivery performance, yet operational decisions are often made under uncertainty and with incomplete visibility. The project therefore sought to provide a decision-support platform capable of combining the most recent operational information with higher-level intelligence derived from longer-term data analysis. Its implementation used predictive analytics, data mining, machine learning and optimisation techniques, including heuristics, ant colony optimisation and evolutionary algorithms. The platform was intended to analyse massive datasets, including 50 million or more data elements, and to process new information rapidly for real-time or near-real-time decisions. Conceptually, it linked structured logistics data, forecasting models and optimisation routines to support tasks such as local dispatching, deadheading reduction, early detection of missed due dates and partnership planning. The expected impact was improved transport efficiency, more competitive logistics strategies, lower operational waste and better responsiveness to changing conditions. By making the software framework available as open source, the project also aimed to reduce initial investment barriers and encourage smaller logistics actors to exploit predictive decision-support tools.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION|S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-609,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00591,EU_PSTW_202609_00591,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Bournemouth University,General services.,PROTEUS addressed the challenge of analysing very large datasets and continuous data streams in situations where decisions must be made quickly and supported by interpretable visual information.,PROTEUS (Scalable online machine learning for predictive analytics and real-time visualisation),Bournemouth University; Consortium; General services.; United Kingdom,The project therefore aimed to develop ready-to-use scalable online machine-learning algorithms and interactive visualisation techniques for real-time predictive analytics.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"PROTEUS addressed the challenge of analysing very large datasets and continuous data streams in situations where decisions must be made quickly and supported by interpretable visual information. Organisations in sectors such as industry, energy, environment and knowledge-intensive services increasingly need predictive analytics that can operate at scale, but conventional batch-processing methods are not always suitable for fast-changing data. The project therefore aimed to develop ready-to-use scalable online machine-learning algorithms and interactive visualisation techniques for real-time predictive analytics. Its implementation focused on online learning methods, stream-processing architectures and visual analytics tools capable of handling extremely large datasets as they are generated. The developed algorithms and techniques were intended to form a library integrated into an enhanced version of Apache Flink, strengthening the capacity to process, model and visualise high-volume data streams. Conceptually, PROTEUS combined machine learning, distributed computing, real-time analytics and interactive user interfaces so that users could both forecast developments and understand emerging patterns. The expected impact was faster insight generation, more scalable predictive modelling, improved usability of complex analytics and stronger decision support in data-intensive environments. By integrating algorithms with visualisation, the project sought to make real-time predictive analytics more operationally usable rather than only technically feasible.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION|S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-610,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00592,EU_PSTW_202609_00592,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Technalia,General services.,"Good public services are central to quality of life, regional competitiveness and entrepreneurship, but digital uptake may be limited by lack of skills, fear of errors, low trust, service complexity or poor alignment with user needs. By examining not only technology but also trust, skills and usability, CITADEL treated digital transformation as an organisational and social challenge as much as a technical one.",CITADEL - Empowering Citizens to TrAnsform European PubLic Administrations,Technalia; Consortium; General services.; Spain,"CITADEL focused on helping public administrations understand why citizens do not always use digital public services and how those services can be redesigned to become more inclusive, efficient and citizen-centred. The project therefore aimed to provide public authorities with tools, recommendations and methods to identify barriers to use and bring citizens back into digital service channels.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"CITADEL focused on helping public administrations understand why citizens do not always use digital public services and how those services can be redesigned to become more inclusive, efficient and citizen-centred. Good public services are central to quality of life, regional competitiveness and entrepreneurship, but digital uptake may be limited by lack of skills, fear of errors, low trust, service complexity or poor alignment with user needs. The project therefore aimed to provide public authorities with tools, recommendations and methods to identify barriers to use and bring citizens back into digital service channels. Its implementation combined ICT-enabled service analysis, citizen engagement, public-sector transformation methods and evidence-based recommendations. CITADEL created an ecosystem of best practices and tools that allowed administrations to use existing knowledge and new data to shape services around what matters to users. Conceptually, it linked service-design insight, administrative data, stakeholder feedback and co-creation approaches to support better digital public services. The expected impact was improved digital-service adoption, more inclusive access, greater administrative responsiveness and better alignment between public services and citizen expectations. By examining not only technology but also trust, skills and usability, CITADEL treated digital transformation as an organisational and social challenge as much as a technical one.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F08_ERROR_COMPLIANCE_RISK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-611,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00593,EU_PSTW_202609_00593,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Istituto per la Ricerca Sociale,"General economic, commercial and labour affair","Local administrations often face complex energy-transition challenges that require public acceptance, technical expertise and coordinated action across stakeholders.",ENLARGE - ENergies for Local Administrations: Renovate Governance in Europe,"Istituto per la Ricerca Sociale; Consortium; General economic, commercial and labour affair; Italy",UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"ENLARGE examined how public authorities, experts and civil society can work together to design and deliver more effective public policies and services, with a particular focus on sustainable energy. Local administrations often face complex energy-transition challenges that require public acceptance, technical expertise and coordinated action across stakeholders. Traditional top-down policy design may not capture local knowledge or ensure long-term legitimacy, especially when energy measures affect daily life, costs and public-service delivery. The project therefore used intense policy dialogue to analyse existing models of co-design and co-production, drawing lessons on how collaborative governance can improve both sustainable-energy policies and broader public-administration performance. Its implementation relied on comparative analysis, case-study work, deliberative processes and structured engagement between policymakers, experts and civil-society actors. Conceptually, ENLARGE was less about a single digital tool and more about a governance methodology for identifying conditions under which co-production can increase policy quality, efficiency and public value. The expected impact was improved capacity of local administrations to involve stakeholders, stronger evidence on participatory energy governance and better guidance for designing public services that are both effective and legitimate. By focusing on co-design, the project supported administrative innovation in the energy-transition field.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-612,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00594,EU_PSTW_202609_00594,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,University of Lancaster,Old age.,UNKNOWN,Mobile-Age,University of Lancaster; Consortium; Old age.; United Kingdom,"The project therefore used a co-creation approach in which older people, public administrations, researchers and community actors worked together on services related to the production and use of open urban data.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Mobile-Age explored how open government data and mobile technologies could support public services for older people in European cities. Ageing populations create new demands for accessible, relevant and locally grounded services, particularly in relation to social isolation, safe mobility, inclusion and personal health information. Many older residents may also face digital barriers, meaning that services must be designed with them rather than simply delivered to them. The project therefore used a co-creation approach in which older people, public administrations, researchers and community actors worked together on services related to the production and use of open urban data. Its implementation combined mobile applications, open data, participatory design, local service scenarios and user-centred development. The project addressed themes including loneliness, social inclusion, safer and more accessible cities, data curation and personal health information. Conceptually, Mobile-Age linked civic data infrastructures with mobile interfaces and co-designed service models, ensuring that data-driven services responded to real needs in everyday urban life. The expected impact was improved accessibility of public services, stronger inclusion of older citizens in digital innovation, better reuse of open government data and more responsive local service delivery. By involving older people directly, the project strengthened both usability and democratic legitimacy.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-613,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00595,EU_PSTW_202609_00595,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,CERTH,General services.,"Public administrations hold substantial statistical information, but its value is often limited by fragmented formats, weak interoperability and insufficient mechanisms for external reuse.",OpenGovIntelligence (Fostering Innovation and Creativity in EU through Pub Administration Modernization),CERTH; Consortium; General services.; Greece,"OpenGovIntelligence sought to modernise public administrations by using Linked Open Statistical Data to co-produce innovative public services with citizens, businesses and public-sector actors. It followed an iterative approach, using development and evaluation cycles to refine the OpenGovIntelligence framework and associated tools.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"OpenGovIntelligence sought to modernise public administrations by using Linked Open Statistical Data to co-produce innovative public services with citizens, businesses and public-sector actors. Public administrations hold substantial statistical information, but its value is often limited by fragmented formats, weak interoperability and insufficient mechanisms for external reuse. The project therefore focused on new processes, policies and tools that would enable society and businesses to participate actively in data sharing and service co-production. Its implementation used Linked Open Statistical Data technologies and ran pilot studies in six countries, testing how structured, interoperable data could support new public-service models. Conceptually, the project combined linked data, semantic technologies, statistical datasets, co-creation methods and public-administration process redesign. It followed an iterative approach, using development and evaluation cycles to refine the OpenGovIntelligence framework and associated tools. The expected impact was greater transparency, more innovative public services, improved reuse of official statistics and stronger collaboration between administrations and external stakeholders. By treating data as a shared resource for service design, the project aimed to stimulate innovation and creativity while strengthening evidence-based administration.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-614,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00596,EU_PSTW_202609_00596,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,University of Ulm,"Agriculture, forestry, fishing and hunting","The expected impact was more efficient administrative control, lower monitoring burden, greater transparency for farmers and better targeting of inspections.",RECAP - Reliable Capacity Provisioning and Enhanced Remediation for Distributed Cloud Applications,"University of Ulm; Consortium; Agriculture, forestry, fishing and hunting; Germany","The project implemented a cloud-based Software-as-a-Service platform using open Earth Observation data, user-generated information and geospatial data to support compliance monitoring.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"RECAP supported the implementation of the Common Agricultural Policy by developing personalised public services for farmers, agricultural consultants and administrations responsible for monitoring compliance. CAP rules create complex obligations, particularly around cross-compliance, and public authorities need efficient ways to monitor whether requirements are being met across diverse agricultural contexts. Farmers and consultants also benefit from clearer, more transparent information on obligations and potential breaches. The project implemented a cloud-based Software-as-a-Service platform using open Earth Observation data, user-generated information and geospatial data to support compliance monitoring. Conceptually, the system extracted relevant features from satellite and other open data, correlated them with information available to public organisations and modelled potential breaches of CAP requirements. This enabled authorities and inspectors to focus attention on likely non-compliance while giving users more personalised support. The expected impact was more efficient administrative control, lower monitoring burden, greater transparency for farmers and better targeting of inspections. By combining Earth Observation with cloud services and user-generated data, RECAP demonstrated how agricultural public services can shift from document-heavy compliance checks towards more continuous, data-supported monitoring.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-615,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00597,EU_PSTW_202609_00597,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Trento,General services.,"The objective was to make digital services easier to understand, reduce the distance between users and administrations, and identify why online procedures remain difficult or underused. The expected impact was improved usability of online services, fewer errors during administrative procedures, better citizen satisfaction and stronger capacity for administrations to learn from recurring difficulties.",SIMPATICO - Platform for PS - Italian Usecase,Municipality of Trento; Consortium; General services.; Italy,"In the Italian use case, the Municipality of Trento provided a public-service context in which administrative procedures could be simplified and adapted through continuous feedback.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"SIMPATICO was developed to improve the way citizens and companies interact with online public services, addressing a common weakness in digital administration: many services are standardised replicas of offline procedures, designed mainly around internal organisational logic rather than the needs, language and behaviour of users. In the Italian use case, the Municipality of Trento provided a public-service context in which administrative procedures could be simplified and adapted through continuous feedback. The objective was to make digital services easier to understand, reduce the distance between users and administrations, and identify why online procedures remain difficult or underused. The implementation combined language-processing technologies, machine-learning techniques and community contributions. Public administrations supplied formal knowledge about procedures, while citizens, companies, civil servants and professionals contributed questions, doubts, explanations and improvement suggestions. At a conceptual level, the platform linked service interfaces, text simplification, workflow guidance, user feedback and collaborative knowledge creation, allowing interactions to be progressively refined. The expected impact was improved usability of online services, fewer errors during administrative procedures, better citizen satisfaction and stronger capacity for administrations to learn from recurring difficulties. By combining institutional knowledge with bottom-up input, SIMPATICO treated service improvement as a continuous, participatory process rather than a one-off redesign of digital forms.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F08_ERROR_COMPLIANCE_RISK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-616,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00598,EU_PSTW_202609_00598,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Empirica Gesellschaft für Kommunikations- & Technologieforschung mbH.,General services.,"WeGovNow explored how local public services and policy challenges could be addressed through collaborative digital participation, treating citizens as partners in public-service delivery rather than passive users or customers. Local authorities often need to respond to issues such as neighbourhood improvement, service quality, environmental concerns or community planning, but conventional consultation channels can be fragmented, episodic and disconnected from day-to-day civic action.",WeGovNow: Collective and participative approaches for addressing local policy challenges,Empirica Gesellschaft für Kommunikations- & Technologieforschung mbH.; Consortium; General services.; Germany,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"WeGovNow explored how local public services and policy challenges could be addressed through collaborative digital participation, treating citizens as partners in public-service delivery rather than passive users or customers. Local authorities often need to respond to issues such as neighbourhood improvement, service quality, environmental concerns or community planning, but conventional consultation channels can be fragmented, episodic and disconnected from day-to-day civic action. The project therefore aimed to provide public administrations, citizens, civil-society organisations and local businesses with digital tools for co-production, collective proposition development and shared problem-solving. Its implementation integrated emerging civic technologies into a pilot platform tested under day-to-day conditions in the London Borough of Southwark, the City of Turin and San Donà di Piave. Conceptually, the platform combined participatory mapping, issue reporting, discussion, collective proposal development and community coordination functions. The project also examined legal, ethical and organisational conditions to ensure democratic legitimacy and practical exploitability. The expected impact was stronger citizen engagement, more responsive local services and improved evidence on how digital co-production works in real administrative contexts. During the final project year, 9,976 pilot user accounts were registered across the three pilot municipalities, showing meaningful uptake in real-life settings.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-617,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00599,EU_PSTW_202609_00599,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,UNINETTUNO,Social protection n.e.c.,"The operational challenge was two-sided: newcomers often face fragmented information, language barriers, unfamiliar administrative systems and difficulties identifying relevant opportunities, while public authorities and support organisations need better tools to understand needs, coordinate services and evaluate impact.",REBUILD - ICT toolbox providing support to both migrants and refugees,UNINETTUNO; Consortium; Social protection n.e.c.; Italy,"Its implementation developed an ICT toolbox using user profiling, skills-and-needs matching, chatbot functions and accessible service interfaces.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"REBUILD aimed to improve how migrants and refugees access services in Europe while helping local service providers manage integration support more effectively. The operational challenge was two-sided: newcomers often face fragmented information, language barriers, unfamiliar administrative systems and difficulties identifying relevant opportunities, while public authorities and support organisations need better tools to understand needs, coordinate services and evaluate impact. The project adopted a user-centred and participatory design approach, involving migrants, refugees and service providers from the early stages so that ethical, cultural and practical requirements could shape the solution. Its implementation developed an ICT toolbox using user profiling, skills-and-needs matching, chatbot functions and accessible service interfaces. Conceptually, the ecosystem connected information about individuals’ needs, skills and preferences with local service offers, guidance pathways and administrative workflows. It also included mechanisms to monitor and validate socio-economic impact. The expected value was more personalised support, easier access to services, improved communication between users and administrations, and better evidence for integration policies. By combining digital tools with co-design, REBUILD sought to avoid imposing a generic integration platform and instead build services around the lived experience of migrants and refugees and the operational realities of local providers.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-618,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00600,EU_PSTW_202609_00600,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,SCRIPT & GO,Social protection n.e.c.,"Migration brings significant human capital to Europe, yet integration systems may struggle to match people’s abilities, career ambitions, educational requirements and social or cultural needs with real opportunities in host societies.",NADINE - platform to promote migrant inclusion,SCRIPT & GO; Consortium; Social protection n.e.c.; Greece,"The expected impact was more tailored assistance for migrants, better use of skills, improved decision support for professionals and stronger alignment between integration services and actual needs.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"NADINE was designed to support migrant inclusion by improving the way public authorities and guidance professionals understand skills, needs and available services. Migration brings significant human capital to Europe, yet integration systems may struggle to match people’s abilities, career ambitions, educational requirements and social or cultural needs with real opportunities in host societies. The project therefore aimed to build a platform that could support employability, career guidance and service matching on the basis of richer evidence about both migrants and local environments. Its implementation used open data, large-scale data repositories and analytical tools to create two main information banks: one concerning host societies and services, and another concerning migrants’ profiles, needs and skills. As users interacted with the platform, further data could be collected to refine the system and improve recommendations. Conceptually, NADINE combined skills assessment, labour-market intelligence, local-service mapping, guidance tools and personalised support interfaces. The expected impact was more tailored assistance for migrants, better use of skills, improved decision support for professionals and stronger alignment between integration services and actual needs. By linking social, educational, cultural and employment dimensions, NADINE approached inclusion as a multidimensional process rather than only a labour-market matching exercise.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-619,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00601,EU_PSTW_202609_00601,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Universidad Pompeu Fabra,Social protection n.e.c.,"Reception and integration processes often involve complex registration steps, orientation needs, language barriers, civic education, access to local services and social inclusion challenges, while administrations must provide guidance across diverse profiles and contexts.","WELCOME(Multiple Intelligent Conversation Agent Services for Reception, Management and Integration)",Universidad Pompeu Fabra; Consortium; Social protection n.e.c.; Germany,"The services were validated through pilot use cases in Catalonia, Spain, Germany and Greece.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"WELCOME focused on supporting the reception and integration of third-country nationals, migrants and refugees through personalised digital assistance for both newcomers and public administrations. Reception and integration processes often involve complex registration steps, orientation needs, language barriers, civic education, access to local services and social inclusion challenges, while administrations must provide guidance across diverse profiles and contexts. The project aimed to create a psychologically and socially competent support environment that could help users navigate these processes in a more accessible and personalised way. Its implementation developed intelligent multilingual conversational agents, including embodied assistants, designed for contexts such as registration, orientation, language learning, civic education and community integration. The system used personalisation techniques so that agent behaviour could adapt to the user’s profile and situation, and it also explored immersive virtual and augmented reality technologies to make interaction more realistic and engaging. Conceptually, WELCOME combined conversational interfaces, multilingual processing, user profiling, interaction adaptation and immersive service design. The expected impact was better access to integration guidance, reduced pressure on frontline administrations, more consistent information provision and improved user experience for migrants and refugees. The services were validated through pilot use cases in Catalonia, Spain, Germany and Greece.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-620,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00602,EU_PSTW_202609_00602,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Department of Public Expenditure and Reform,R&D economic affairs,"Public bodies often manage high-volume tasks that require staff to copy, check or transfer information between digital systems, even when the rules are clear and human judgement is limited. The expected impact was improved accuracy, fewer manual errors, faster processing and the release of staff time for higher-value work.",DPER Robotic Process Automation (RPA) Pilot,Department of Public Expenditure and Reform; Central-Government; R&D economic affairs; Ireland,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The DPER Robotic Process Automation pilot examined whether routine, stable and fully computer-based administrative processes in Irish central government could be automated to improve efficiency and service delivery. Public bodies often manage high-volume tasks that require staff to copy, check or transfer information between digital systems, even when the rules are clear and human judgement is limited. In 2017, the Department of Public Expenditure and Reform coordinated a pilot in which four government departments selected suitable processes to test the potential of robotic process automation. The implementation used software robots to operate existing applications at user-interface level, automating labour-intensive, rules-based tasks without requiring wholesale replacement of legacy systems. Conceptually, RPA was applied as a non-invasive automation layer that could log into systems, follow predefined steps, transfer data, validate entries and generate outputs consistently. The expected impact was improved accuracy, fewer manual errors, faster processing and the release of staff time for higher-value work. The pilot also helped the Irish public service understand which process characteristics make automation feasible and what governance, procurement and implementation arrangements would be needed for wider adoption. A later government RPA framework indicates that the pilot contributed to a broader national approach to automation services.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK|F06_HIGH_VOLUME_SCALABILITY_BOTTLENECK|F08_ERROR_COMPLIANCE_RISK,S6_WORKFLOW_PROCESS_AUTOMATION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-621,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00603,EU_PSTW_202609_00603,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Housing Assistance Payment Shared Service Centre,General services.,UNKNOWN,Housing Assistance Payment Robotics Pilot,Housing Assistance Payment Shared Service Centre; Local Government; General services.; Ireland,The use case therefore provided a practical basis for identifying further immediate automation opportunities within the HAP SSC.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Housing Assistance Payment Robotics Pilot addressed the operational pressure faced by the HAP Shared Service Centre, operated by Limerick City and County Council on behalf of the local government sector. The centre manages very large payment and tenancy volumes, including more than 47,000 tenancies, 27,000 landlords, over €400 million in payments and more than €100 million in rent collection, with projected growth to 80,000 tenancies, 55,000 landlords and €600 million in payments by 2021. These volumes create substantial administrative workload and a strong incentive to identify processes that can be automated safely. In 2018, Agilisys carried out a proof-of-concept pilot examining three micro-processes within the centre. The implementation used robotic process automation to test whether repeatable, rule-based activities could be executed by software robots across existing business systems. Conceptually, the pilot assessed process suitability, automation feasibility, savings potential and opportunities for wider business-system integration. The results demonstrated that efficiencies and savings could be achieved, and the subsequent business case suggested that replacing 10 complex processes with RPA could generate considerable savings while improving services for tenants, landlords and administrative stakeholders. The use case therefore provided a practical basis for identifying further immediate automation opportunities within the HAP SSC.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S6_WORKFLOW_PROCESS_AUTOMATION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-622,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00604,EU_PSTW_202609_00604,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Linköping University,Hospital services.,"The implementation used an artificial neural network trained on extensive gene-expression information, allowing the system to learn patterns without relying only on predefined disease categories or manually selected markers. Although the work remains research-oriented, its value lies in showing how neural-network methods can support biomedical knowledge discovery by extracting interpretable signals from datasets that exceed the capacity of conventional manual analysis.",Artificial intelligence finds disease-related genes,Linköping University; Academic-Research; Hospital services.; Sweden,"This use case addresses a central challenge in biomedical research: disease mechanisms are often distributed across large numbers of interacting genes, making it difficult for researchers to identify meaningful patterns in high-dimensional gene-expression data.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"This use case addresses a central challenge in biomedical research: disease mechanisms are often distributed across large numbers of interacting genes, making it difficult for researchers to identify meaningful patterns in high-dimensional gene-expression data. Scientists at Linköping University led a study exploring how these complex datasets could be used to discover groups of genes associated with disease and, in the longer term, support more precise diagnosis and treatment. The objective was not to replace biological interpretation, but to give researchers a stronger exploratory method for finding structures that may otherwise remain hidden in very large molecular datasets. The implementation used an artificial neural network trained on extensive gene-expression information, allowing the system to learn patterns without relying only on predefined disease categories or manually selected markers. At a conceptual level, the model processed expression profiles, detected relationships between genes and organised them into groups that could reveal disease-relevant biological signals. The expected impact is improved discovery of candidate genes, better understanding of disease pathways and stronger foundations for precision medicine and individualised treatment. Although the work remains research-oriented, its value lies in showing how neural-network methods can support biomedical knowledge discovery by extracting interpretable signals from datasets that exceed the capacity of conventional manual analysis.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-623,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00605,EU_PSTW_202609_00605,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Estonian Government,General services.,"The operational problem is that even highly digital government systems can remain fragmented from the user’s perspective, especially when a request requires cooperation between different agencies.",Estonia to give citizens Alexa-like access to public services,Estonian Government; Central-Government; General services.; Estonia,"Digital public services can still be difficult to use when citizens must navigate multiple portals, forms and institutional websites to complete a life event or obtain an answer.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Digital public services can still be difficult to use when citizens must navigate multiple portals, forms and institutional websites to complete a life event or obtain an answer. Estonia’s Bürokratt initiative aims to create a network of public-sector virtual assistants that allow people to interact with the state in natural language. The operational problem is that even highly digital government systems can remain fragmented from the user’s perspective, especially when a request requires cooperation between different agencies. The implementation concept connects chatbots across public-sector institutions into a shared assistant network. At a conceptual level, a citizen can ask a question or request a service through a conversational interface, while the system identifies the relevant institution, retrieves information and, where possible, supports service routing or administrative action. The longer-term vision is for personalised AI agents to cooperate across domains and help citizens solve problems without navigating complex portals or repeating information. The expected impact is simpler access to public services, better customer support, reduced administrative friction and more human-centred digital government. The initiative is significant because it moves beyond isolated chatbots towards an interoperable assistant layer for the public sector, building on Estonia’s wider digital-state infrastructure.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-624,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00606,EU_PSTW_202609_00606,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Policnico Universitario Campus Bio-Medico Roma,Hospital services.,UNKNOWN,"Artificial intelligence ""Wuhan model"" at the biomedical campus in Rome",Policnico Universitario Campus Bio-Medico Roma; Academic-Research; Hospital services.; Italy,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"During the early COVID-19 emergency, hospitals needed rapid tools to support diagnosis and monitoring of pneumonia from lung imaging, especially when clinical pressure was high and radiology teams faced urgent triage demands. Campus Bio-Medico University Hospital in Rome acquired an imaging system originally used in Wuhan to assist with early diagnosis and monitoring of COVID-19 pneumonia. The objective was to help radiologists interpret lung CT scans more quickly and consistently, while supporting clinical assessment rather than replacing specialist judgement. The implementation used an artificial-intelligence system trained to analyse chest CT images and identify patterns associated with COVID-19 pneumonia. Public reporting described a response reliability of 98.5% in around 20 seconds, and the system was tested successfully on anonymised patients by radiologists at the Rome hospital. At a conceptual level, the software processes CT image data, identifies relevant lung abnormalities and provides a rapid diagnostic support output for clinicians. The expected impact was faster radiological triage, improved monitoring of disease progression and better allocation of clinical resources during pandemic peaks. Its longer-term significance lies in demonstrating how emergency deployment of image-analysis systems can support hospital workflows, although public sources describe this primarily as a COVID-era installation and test rather than an enduring, general diagnostic platform.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-625,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00607,EU_PSTW_202609_00607,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Latina,Public health services.,"The early pandemic period generated intense demand for reliable information, while misinformation and rapidly changing rules made it difficult for citizens to know which guidance to follow. At a conceptual level, the system acted as a bridge between citizens and institutional information, reducing the need to search manually through government webpages and helping standardise the messages received by users.","Against the infection comes Minerva, a ""robot"" invented by an engineer from Noci (IT)",Municipality of Latina; Local Government; Public health services.; Italy,"Minerva was created as a chatbot intended to share information about coronavirus using data drawn from the Italian Ministry of Health website. Although described publicly as a “robot”, the use case was essentially a conversational information service rather than a physical robot or clinical diagnostic tool.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The early pandemic period generated intense demand for reliable information, while misinformation and rapidly changing rules made it difficult for citizens to know which guidance to follow. Minerva was created as a chatbot intended to share information about coronavirus using data drawn from the Italian Ministry of Health website. The objective was to provide a simple digital assistant that could answer recurring questions and direct users towards official public-health information. Although described publicly as a “robot”, the use case was essentially a conversational information service rather than a physical robot or clinical diagnostic tool. The implementation relied on an automated interface that processed user queries and returned relevant content based on official sources. At a conceptual level, the system acted as a bridge between citizens and institutional information, reducing the need to search manually through government webpages and helping standardise the messages received by users. Its expected impact was faster access to trusted information, improved public awareness and reduced confusion during a period of high uncertainty. The case was connected to a specific emergency context and should be interpreted as a lightweight crisis-communication tool rather than a long-term health-service platform. Its value lay in rapid deployment, official-source orientation and accessibility for users seeking immediate guidance.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,NOT_IN_USE,미사용,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-626,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00608,EU_PSTW_202609_00608,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Italian National Institute for Insurance against Accidents at Work (INAIL),Hospital services.,UNKNOWN,"E-health 4.0, chatbots at the service of diagnostic units",Italian National Institute for Insurance against Accidents at Work (INAIL); Central-Government; Hospital services.; Italy,"Through collaboration between Microsoft and INAIL, Italian healthcare organisations were able to use the Healthcare Bot solution free of charge to create digital assistants for self-assessment of coronavirus symptoms. The use case demonstrates how a reusable cloud-based bot platform could be rapidly adapted to emergency public-health needs.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Healthcare facilities faced extraordinary pressure during the COVID-19 emergency, especially where citizens needed preliminary symptom guidance and hospitals had to manage information demand at scale. Through collaboration between Microsoft and INAIL, Italian healthcare organisations were able to use the Healthcare Bot solution free of charge to create digital assistants for self-assessment of coronavirus symptoms. The initiative supported facilities such as Spallanzani, San Giovanni Addolorata Hospital and ASL Napoli 3 Sud, giving them a rapid way to deploy online symptom-checking and orientation tools. The implementation used a healthcare chatbot configured for COVID-19 self-assessment, allowing users to answer questions about symptoms and receive guidance through the healthcare provider’s portal. At a conceptual level, the service translated triage and public-health information into a conversational workflow, supporting preliminary risk orientation and directing users towards appropriate next steps. Its expected impact was reduced pressure on phone lines and diagnostic units, faster information access for citizens, and more consistent communication from healthcare organisations. The service did not replace medical evaluation; it provided a first layer of digital interaction at a time when health systems needed to reserve human capacity for urgent or complex cases. The use case demonstrates how a reusable cloud-based bot platform could be rapidly adapted to emergency public-health needs.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,NOT_IN_USE,미사용,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-627,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00609,EU_PSTW_202609_00609,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Hospital Gemelli di Roma,Hospital services.,"The practical problem was that many important data relating to COVID-19 patients were unstructured, particularly information contained in clinical notes and hospital documentation, while managers and clinicians needed timely intelligence on patient flows, complications and expected organisational impact. The implementation used SAS analytics, visual analytics and text-mining techniques to extract information from unstructured clinical data, support prediction of intensive-care demand, understand possible medical complications and plan hospital operations.",Management of the emergency at Hospital Gemelli - Rome,Hospital Gemelli di Roma; Local Government; Hospital services.; Italy,"SAS describes Gemelli as using its solutions to predict ICU admissions, understand medical complications, plan ward work and manage COVID-19 vaccine administration.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Major hospitals faced exceptional operational pressure during the COVID-19 emergency, requiring rapid decisions on intensive-care capacity, ward planning, patient pathways and resource allocation. Fondazione Policlinico Universitario Agostino Gemelli IRCCS used SAS analytics to support management of the emergency by transforming clinical and organisational data into actionable insight. The practical problem was that many important data relating to COVID-19 patients were unstructured, particularly information contained in clinical notes and hospital documentation, while managers and clinicians needed timely intelligence on patient flows, complications and expected organisational impact. The implementation used SAS analytics, visual analytics and text-mining techniques to extract information from unstructured clinical data, support prediction of intensive-care demand, understand possible medical complications and plan hospital operations. At a conceptual level, the system combines data integration, text mining, predictive analytics, dashboards and operational planning. Its expected impact was faster access to clinical information, better forecasting of admissions and hospitalisation needs, more efficient resource allocation and support for personalised patient pathways. SAS describes Gemelli as using its solutions to predict ICU admissions, understand medical complications, plan ward work and manage COVID-19 vaccine administration.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F04_UNSTRUCTURED_INFORMATION_COMPLEXITY,S3_DOCUMENT_INTELLIGENCE|S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION|S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-628,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00610,EU_PSTW_202609_00610,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,IIT and Aeroporto di Genova,Police Services,"COVID-19 restrictions severely limited in-person contact in hospitals, isolating patients from families and complicating communication between healthcare staff.",Artificial intelligence and video surveillance to control the distance at the airport,IIT and Aeroporto di Genova; Local Government; Police Services; Italy,"From 2020 to 2021, the pilot deployed Double Robotics Double3 mobile robots connected via a 5G network.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"COVID-19 restrictions severely limited in-person contact in hospitals, isolating patients from families and complicating communication between healthcare staff. Hospitals in Turin launched a pilot to explore how remote presence technologies could mitigate social isolation while maintaining safety protocols. Stakeholders included patients, relatives, healthcare professionals, hospital administrators, and telecom providers, with the objective of preserving human interaction and collaboration despite physical distancing requirements. From 2020 to 2021, the pilot deployed Double Robotics Double3 mobile robots connected via a 5G network. These remotely controlled robots enabled high-definition video communication and autonomous movement within hospital wards. Secure network connectivity ensured low latency and reliable audio-visual interaction, while simple user interfaces allowed staff and families to connect without specialised training. The robots integrated with hospital connectivity infrastructure and operated under strict data protection policies. The pilot improved patient well-being by restoring contact with families and supported collaboration among medical staff across wards. It reduced unnecessary physical movement within hospitals and demonstrated the value of high-bandwidth connectivity for healthcare environments. The solution proved reliable and scalable, highlighting potential long-term applications for telepresence in clinical care, training, and hospital operations beyond emergency contexts.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-629,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00611,EU_PSTW_202609_00611,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Swedish Land Registry,Housing development.,"Land-registry authorities manage large collections of historical legal documents, including handwritten records that can be difficult to search, interpret and reuse in modern digital workflows. The operational problem was that staff received many requests from citizens for information about properties, but older records were difficult to process because image quality and resolution were poor and handwriting recognition was challenging.",Natural language processing for Land Registry documentation in Sweden,Swedish Land Registry; Central-Government; Housing development.; Sweden,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Land-registry authorities manage large collections of historical legal documents, including handwritten records that can be difficult to search, interpret and reuse in modern digital workflows. The Swedish Land Registry used natural language processing to handle land-registry requests more efficiently by extracting and structuring information from low-quality handwritten documentation. The operational problem was that staff received many requests from citizens for information about properties, but older records were difficult to process because image quality and resolution were poor and handwriting recognition was challenging. The implementation first pre-processed handwritten documents to improve input quality, then used handwritten text recognition to extract text. A neural network then applied word corrections and associations to complete sentences where the recognition process missed words, after which an AI model highlighted key features such as locations, names and summaries. At a conceptual level, the system combines image pre-processing, handwritten text recognition, neural language correction and information extraction. The expected impact is faster handling of citizen requests, reduced manual search effort, better access to historical property information and more time for case handlers to focus on urgent decisions.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S3_DOCUMENT_INTELLIGENCE,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-631,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00612,EU_PSTW_202609_00612,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Ministry of Justice,Prisons.,"Prison oversight produces large volumes of inspection and monitoring reports, but staff may struggle to locate relevant patterns when information is buried in unstructured text. The expected impact is faster retrieval of relevant information, better trend detection, improved policy and operational analysis, and reduced staff time spent manually reading large numbers of reports.",How the Ministry of Justice used AI to compare prison reports,Ministry of Justice; Central-Government; Prisons.; United Kingdom,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Prison oversight produces large volumes of inspection and monitoring reports, but staff may struggle to locate relevant patterns when information is buried in unstructured text. The UK Ministry of Justice developed an intelligent search tool to compare prison reports and identify trends across more than 500 documents containing over 250,000 sentences. The source material included reports from HM Inspectorate of Prisons, the Independent Monitoring Board, the Prisons and Probation Ombudsman, and Ofsted reports on secure training centres. The implementation trained a neural network on prison-report text so that it could track how particular words are used in prison contexts and group words with similar meanings. At a conceptual level, this creates semantic search rather than simple keyword search: users can uncover related concepts, identify recurring themes and compare language across institutions or report types. New reports are automatically added to the tool’s library, helping the information base remain current. The expected impact is faster retrieval of relevant information, better trend detection, improved policy and operational analysis, and reduced staff time spent manually reading large numbers of reports. The tool’s value lies in surfacing evidence for human analysts, not in making prison-management decisions automatically.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK|F04_UNSTRUCTURED_INFORMATION_COMPLEXITY|F06_HIGH_VOLUME_SCALABILITY_BOTTLENECK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-632,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00613,EU_PSTW_202609_00613,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Hospital Centro Diagnostico Italiano di Milano,Public health services.,UNKNOWN,AI-for-Covid Predictive diagnostics made in Italy,Hospital Centro Diagnostico Italiano di Milano; Academic-Research; Public health services.; Italy,This use case addresses this challenge by supporting radiologists and hospital physicians with a decision-support service based on chest X-ray examinations performed at admission. The service is implemented through advanced machine learning techniques applied to diagnostic imaging.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"During the Covid-19 pandemic, hospitals faced significant pressure to rapidly assess newly admitted patients and prioritise clinical resources. Clinicians needed early, reliable indicators to identify which patients were at higher risk of respiratory deterioration and might require intensive care or mechanical ventilation. This use case addresses this challenge by supporting radiologists and hospital physicians with a decision-support service based on chest X-ray examinations performed at admission. The objective is to improve early risk stratification, optimise care pathways, and enable timely clinical interventions for patients with Covid-19–related pulmonary involvement. The service is implemented through advanced machine learning techniques applied to diagnostic imaging. Predictive models trained on large datasets of chest X-ray images and associated clinical outcomes analyse radiological patterns linked to disease progression. The system processes imaging data captured at hospital admission and generates a risk score indicating the likelihood of pulmonary worsening. The platform is integrated into existing radiology and hospital information systems, enabling seamless access for clinicians without disrupting established diagnostic workflows. The solution improves the timeliness and consistency of clinical decision-making by providing early insight into disease progression risk. This supports more effective allocation of intensive care resources, reduces uncertainty in patient triage, and enhances overall care quality. By enabling earlier interventions for high-risk patients, the platform contributes to better patient management, increased operational efficiency, and improved resilience of healthcare services during periods of high demand.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-633,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00614,EU_PSTW_202609_00614,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Government Digital Service,General public services n.e.c.,"GOV.UK faced a content-organisation problem because many pages were not tagged to the correct “sub-branches” of the site taxonomy, while manual classification at scale would have required substantial editorial effort. At a conceptual level, the system transformed unstructured text into features, learned from already-tagged pages and predicted the most appropriate content categories for untagged pages.",Using deep learning to structure GOV.UK's content,Government Digital Service; Central-Government; General public services n.e.c.; United Kingdom,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Large government websites contain thousands of pages that must be organised consistently so users can find information through topic structures, navigation and search. GOV.UK faced a content-organisation problem because many pages were not tagged to the correct “sub-branches” of the site taxonomy, while manual classification at scale would have required substantial editorial effort. The Government Digital Service used natural language processing to make page text machine-readable and combined this with metadata such as publication date and department to learn patterns associated with existing content tags. The implementation used a multilabel deep-learning model because some GOV.UK pages belong to more than one sub-branch. At a conceptual level, the system transformed unstructured text into features, learned from already-tagged pages and predicted the most appropriate content categories for untagged pages. Predictions were then used selectively: where training examples were too few or categories too diverse, the team did not rely on model outputs. The expected impact was better content structure, improved user journeys, reduced manual tagging work and more consistent organisation across GOV.UK. GDS reported that machine learning helped tag 96% of untagged GOV.UK pages, corresponding to roughly 100,000 pages, while user researchers and content designers reviewed the outputs before wider rollout.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK|F04_UNSTRUCTURED_INFORMATION_COMPLEXITY,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-634,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00615,EU_PSTW_202609_00615,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Driver and Vehicle Standards Agency (DVSA),Police Services,"Vehicle roadworthiness depends on MOT garages applying testing standards consistently, but inspection authorities cannot manually examine every garage and tester with the same intensity.",How the Department for Transport used AI to improve MOT testing,Driver and Vehicle Standards Agency (DVSA); Central-Government; Police Services; United Kingdom,"At a conceptual level, the system uses unsupervised learning to identify behavioural similarity, then enriches those groups with compliance history to prioritise inspection and enforcement resources. The expected impact is more effective targeting of garage visits, better identification of regional trends, stronger protection of road safety and improved use of examiner time.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Vehicle roadworthiness depends on MOT garages applying testing standards consistently, but inspection authorities cannot manually examine every garage and tester with the same intensity. The Driver and Vehicle Standards Agency used artificial intelligence to improve risk-based oversight of MOT testing across Great Britain. The operational problem was that the agency had very large volumes of garage and tester data, but no labelled dataset that directly identified all problematic behaviour. The implementation therefore applied a clustering model to garage test data from the previous three months, grouping MOT centres according to patterns in how they conducted tests. The agency then created a risk score by combining the clustering output with historical information on how often garages had been disciplined for failing to apply MOT standards correctly. At a conceptual level, the system uses unsupervised learning to identify behavioural similarity, then enriches those groups with compliance history to prioritise inspection and enforcement resources. The expected impact is more effective targeting of garage visits, better identification of regional trends, stronger protection of road safety and improved use of examiner time. DVSA updated garage test data every three months, balancing model freshness with stable inspection windows.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-635,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00616,EU_PSTW_202609_00616,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Department for International Development (DFID),General services.,"Population estimates are essential for census planning, vaccination campaigns, humanitarian response and service delivery, but countries affected by conflict, rapid growth or limited statistical capacity may lack accurate small-area data.",How DFID used satellite images to estimate populations,Department for International Development (DFID); Consortium; General services.; United Kingdom,"The objective was to help countries such as Nigeria, Zambia, Mozambique and the Democratic Republic of Congo plan censuses and public services using fine-grained gridded population data. The implementation uses remote sensing and geospatial modelling to identify settlements and produce population estimates for small, uniform grid cells, combining observed geographic features with available census or survey data.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Population estimates are essential for census planning, vaccination campaigns, humanitarian response and service delivery, but countries affected by conflict, rapid growth or limited statistical capacity may lack accurate small-area data. DFID supported GRID3, a geospatial programme that combines satellite imagery, settlement mapping, administrative boundaries and demographic modelling to estimate population distribution. The objective was to help countries such as Nigeria, Zambia, Mozambique and the Democratic Republic of Congo plan censuses and public services using fine-grained gridded population data. The implementation uses remote sensing and geospatial modelling to identify settlements and produce population estimates for small, uniform grid cells, combining observed geographic features with available census or survey data. At a conceptual level, the system transforms satellite-derived settlement and infrastructure information into spatial population layers that can support government and development planning. In conflict-affected or hard-to-reach areas, this can provide a safer and more practical basis for estimating population than traditional enumeration alone. The expected impact is better vaccination microplanning, improved census preparation, more accurate service-location decisions and stronger evidence for development investment.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-636,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00617,EU_PSTW_202609_00617,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Milan,Communication,UNKNOWN,Whatsapp InfoCenter City of Milan (020202),Municipality of Milan; Local Government; Communication; Italy,"This use case addresses the need to provide immediate, accessible guidance through a familiar digital channel, involving the municipal administration and citizens, with the objective of improving responsiveness and reducing pressure on frontline services. The service is implemented as a virtual assistant available via WhatsApp, guiding users through structured menus covering key topics such as citizenship services, economic activities, mobility rules, health information, parking, accessibility, and official decrees.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The City of Milan manages a very high volume of citizen enquiries, particularly during periods of regulatory change and public health or mobility restrictions, when residents need fast and reliable information across multiple service areas. Traditional channels such as phone lines and counters risk congestion and long waiting times. This use case addresses the need to provide immediate, accessible guidance through a familiar digital channel, involving the municipal administration and citizens, with the objective of improving responsiveness and reducing pressure on frontline services. The service is implemented as a virtual assistant available via WhatsApp, guiding users through structured menus covering key topics such as citizenship services, economic activities, mobility rules, health information, parking, accessibility, and official decrees. User selections trigger predefined conversational flows connected to up-to-date municipal content, integrated with existing information systems and communication processes. The solution improves accessibility by meeting citizens on a widely used platform and ensures consistency of official information. It delivers faster responses, reduces call volumes, and enhances user experience through simple, mobile-first interaction, strengthening the city’s capacity to communicate effectively at scale.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-637,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00618,EU_PSTW_202609_00618,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Ülemiste,Transport,UNKNOWN,A unique artificial intelligence system now directs Ülemiste City drivers to available parking spots,Municipality of Ülemiste; Local Government; Transport; Estonia,"Ülemiste City introduced an AI-powered smart parking system to guide drivers directly to available spaces and reduce the uncertainty of parking in a busy urban campus. The expected impact is shorter parking-search time, improved use of available capacity, lower unnecessary vehicle circulation and better experience for employees and visitors.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Large business districts can lose time, capacity and user satisfaction when drivers circulate looking for parking spaces without reliable real-time information. Ülemiste City introduced an AI-powered smart parking system to guide drivers directly to available spaces and reduce the uncertainty of parking in a busy urban campus. The implementation combines cameras, AI video analysis, signposts, LED displays and a web interface. Instead of relying on individual in-ground sensors for every bay, the system analyses camera feeds to estimate occupancy and communicate availability to drivers. At a conceptual level, computer-vision models detect vehicles and available spaces, aggregate occupancy information and distribute it through digital displays or interfaces so that drivers can decide where to go before entering or circulating through parking areas. The expected impact is shorter parking-search time, improved use of available capacity, lower unnecessary vehicle circulation and better experience for employees and visitors. The supplier, Pixevia, describes the underlying smart-parking technology as using precise mapping from security cameras, with one camera able to cover up to around 100 parking spots, and information on available places delivered to displays or applications.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-638,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00619,EU_PSTW_202609_00619,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Estonian Agricultural Registers and Information Board (ARIB),Environmental protection n.e.c.,"Agricultural payment agencies must verify whether farmers comply with land-management requirements, but manual on-the-spot checks are costly, time-consuming and difficult to scale nationally. The operational problem is that mowing is one of the conditions for receiving certain government grants, yet field inspections can only cover a limited sample of parcels.",Detection of mowing events on grasslands from Sentinel-data,Estonian Agricultural Registers and Information Board (ARIB); Central-Government; Environmental protection n.e.c.; Estonia,"Estonia’s Agricultural Registers and Information Board applied satellite-data analysis to automate detection of mowing events on grasslands, supporting compliance checks for agricultural subsidies. The implementation uses time series from Sentinel-1 radar and Sentinel-2 optical imagery, allowing the system to observe vegetation changes over the growing season even under frequent cloud cover.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Agricultural payment agencies must verify whether farmers comply with land-management requirements, but manual on-the-spot checks are costly, time-consuming and difficult to scale nationally. Estonia’s Agricultural Registers and Information Board applied satellite-data analysis to automate detection of mowing events on grasslands, supporting compliance checks for agricultural subsidies. The operational problem is that mowing is one of the conditions for receiving certain government grants, yet field inspections can only cover a limited sample of parcels. The implementation uses time series from Sentinel-1 radar and Sentinel-2 optical imagery, allowing the system to observe vegetation changes over the growing season even under frequent cloud cover. A deep-learning model with a reject option detects likely mowing events and can flag uncertain cases for further attention. At a conceptual level, the system converts satellite observations into a national monitoring layer for agricultural practice: changes in vegetation and surface characteristics indicate whether a field has probably been mown. The expected impact is fewer unnecessary field inspections, earlier reminders to applicants, better compliance monitoring and more efficient use of administrative resources. Public case records describe Estonia’s system as the first nationwide automated monitoring system for agricultural practices in the EU, operational from 2018, with reported mowing-detection accuracy of around 85%.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-639,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00620,EU_PSTW_202609_00620,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Hospitals of Regina Margherita and Sant’Anna (Turin),Hospital services.,"Job seekers often struggle to navigate complex information on occupations, training pathways, and reskilling opportunities, particularly in a rapidly changing labour market.",“Connected” robots in Turin hospitals,Hospitals of Regina Margherita and Sant’Anna (Turin); Local Government; Hospital services.; Italy,"The use case involves job seekers, students, career advisors, and AMS staff, with the objective of improving access to labour-market information and supporting lifelong learning. The solution is implemented as an interactive digital assistant that responds dynamically to user questions about professions, qualifications, and further education opportunities.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Job seekers often struggle to navigate complex information on occupations, training pathways, and reskilling opportunities, particularly in a rapidly changing labour market. Austria’s Public Employment Service (AMS) developed Berufs-Infomat to provide accessible, personalised career information to support informed decisions. The use case involves job seekers, students, career advisors, and AMS staff, with the objective of improving access to labour-market information and supporting lifelong learning. The solution is implemented as an interactive digital assistant that responds dynamically to user questions about professions, qualifications, and further education opportunities. It draws on structured labour-market data, occupational profiles, and training catalogues to generate tailored responses aligned with user interests and backgrounds. Integrated into AMS digital services, it complements human counselling rather than replacing it. The impact includes faster access to relevant career information, improved user engagement, and better alignment between individual aspirations and labour-market needs. AMS benefits from more scalable service delivery, while users gain clearer guidance and increased confidence in career planning, supporting employability and workforce adaptability.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-640,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00621,EU_PSTW_202609_00621,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,European Parliament,General services.,"European public administrations need to exchange information across many languages, but manual translation can be too slow or expensive for routine documents, datasets, forms and digital services.",eTranslation: Machine translation for public administrations,European Parliament; European Institution/Agency; General services.; European Union,The implementation uses neural machine translation built on the EU’s long history of professional translation and is available both through a web interface and machine-to-machine APIs.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"European public administrations need to exchange information across many languages, but manual translation can be too slow or expensive for routine documents, datasets, forms and digital services. eTranslation provides the European Commission’s machine-translation service for EU institutions, public administrations, academia, SMEs, NGOs, Digital Europe Programme projects and other eligible users. The objective is to make multilingual public-sector communication faster and more accessible while relying on infrastructure aligned with European institutional needs. The implementation uses neural machine translation built on the EU’s long history of professional translation and is available both through a web interface and machine-to-machine APIs. At a conceptual level, the service receives text, documents or integrated system requests, translates them across official EU languages and selected associated languages, and returns raw machine translation that can be used for understanding, publication support or professional refinement. Public systems can integrate the API directly into services, such as portals that need to translate metadata or user-facing content across multiple languages. The expected impact is reduced translation turnaround, greater multilingual accessibility, easier cross-border public-service delivery and more efficient reuse of public data. Current Commission material presents eTranslation under AI-based multilingual services in the Digital Europe framework and confirms that both web pages and APIs are available for machine-to-machine access.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK|F10_CREATIVE_PRODUCTION_CAPACITY_BOTTLENECK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-641,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00622,EU_PSTW_202609_00622,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Hamburg,General services.,UNKNOWN,Frag-den-Michel! Innovativer Online-Bürgerservice in Betrieb,City of Hamburg; Local Government; General services.; Germany,"This use case addresses the need to extend access to public information while complementing existing contact centres, involving city authorities and residents. Frag-den-Michel is implemented as a text-based dialogue system available online, designed to answer frequently asked questions using structured knowledge bases maintained by the city.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Hamburg authorities receive a wide range of general enquiries from citizens, many of which fall outside normal office hours and place pressure on telephone services. Citizens expect timely access to reliable information regardless of time or channel. This use case addresses the need to extend access to public information while complementing existing contact centres, involving city authorities and residents. The objective is to provide a continuous, digital entry point for common questions about administrative services. Frag-den-Michel is implemented as a text-based dialogue system available online, designed to answer frequently asked questions using structured knowledge bases maintained by the city. The chatbot operates alongside the central authority telephone number, offering an alternative self-service channel and integrating with official content repositories. The service improves availability of information beyond business hours, reduces waiting times on phone lines, and supports more efficient allocation of human resources. It enhances user satisfaction by offering immediate responses and strengthens the city’s multi-channel service strategy through a scalable and low-cost digital solution.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-642,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00623,EU_PSTW_202609_00623,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Ludwigsburg,R&D general public services.,"The expected impact is improved visitor experience, faster orientation, reduced routine guidance burden on staff and public familiarity with service robotics.",Municipality Welcome Manager (L2B2 Service Robot) in Ludwigsburg,City of Ludwigsburg; Local Government; R&D general public services.; Germany,"Ludwigsburg introduced L2B2 as a speaking service robot in the entrance area of its citizen office, making it the first German municipality to use this type of welcome robot in a city administration. The implementation uses a mobile robot that welcomes visitors, moves autonomously through corridors and accompanies them to relevant departments.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Visitors to municipal service offices often need orientation before reaching the correct desk, department or waiting area, especially in buildings where multiple citizen services are delivered. Ludwigsburg introduced L2B2 as a speaking service robot in the entrance area of its citizen office, making it the first German municipality to use this type of welcome robot in a city administration. The objective was to provide first information and guidance for citizens visiting the service department while demonstrating how robotics could support public-facing administration. The implementation uses a mobile robot that welcomes visitors, moves autonomously through corridors and accompanies them to relevant departments. At a conceptual level, the robot combines speech interaction, indoor navigation, visitor guidance and service information. It does not make administrative decisions or process applications; its role is to reduce uncertainty at the first point of contact and provide a visible, interactive guide within the municipal building. The expected impact is improved visitor experience, faster orientation, reduced routine guidance burden on staff and public familiarity with service robotics. Public material describes strong media and citizen interest, and the robot’s name was chosen through a public call, giving the project a participatory communication dimension as well as a technical one.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-643,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00624,EU_PSTW_202609_00624,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Upplands-Bro,Unemployment.,UNKNOWN,Swedish municipality deploys robots for safer recruitment,Municipality of Upplands-Bro; Local Government; Unemployment.; Sweden,"The implementation uses a conversational AI interview tool that conducts structured screening interviews, asking candidates identical or standardised questions and producing information for recruiters to review. At a conceptual level, the system separates initial candidate assessment from CV cues, applies consistent interview protocols and provides structured data that HR staff can use in later selection stages.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Recruitment processes can reproduce unconscious bias when candidates are screened through CVs, informal impressions or inconsistent interview questions. Upplands-Bro Municipality used Tengai’s AI interview system to support more structured and resume-free candidate screening, particularly during urgent recruitment needs in care and welfare roles during the COVID-19 period. The objective was to scale early-stage recruitment while reducing the influence of irrelevant background characteristics and ensuring that applicants were asked comparable questions. The implementation uses a conversational AI interview tool that conducts structured screening interviews, asking candidates identical or standardised questions and producing information for recruiters to review. At a conceptual level, the system separates initial candidate assessment from CV cues, applies consistent interview protocols and provides structured data that HR staff can use in later selection stages. The expected impact is faster screening of large applicant pools, reduced unconscious bias at the early interview stage, greater consistency across applicants and more time for HR staff to focus on qualitative follow-up and final hiring decisions.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-645,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00625,EU_PSTW_202609_00625,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of London Police,Public order and safety n.e.c.,"Traditional identification methods rely on post-event analysis or manual recognition, which can delay interventions and limit operational effectiveness.",London Police Facial Recognition System,City of London Police; Local Government; Public order and safety n.e.c.; United Kingdom,The system uses computer vision and deep learning models trained on large facial image datasets to extract biometric templates from live video feeds. These templates are compared against encrypted watch-list databases using pattern-matching algorithms.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Metropolitan Police Service in London seeks to enhance its capacity to identify individuals of interest in public spaces in order to prevent crime, safeguard vulnerable persons and support ongoing investigations. Traditional identification methods rely on post-event analysis or manual recognition, which can delay interventions and limit operational effectiveness. The deployment of live facial recognition aims to support frontline officers by automatically identifying individuals who appear on pre-defined police watch lists, such as wanted suspects or missing persons. Stakeholders include law enforcement officers, public authorities, oversight bodies and members of the public whose data may be processed in public settings. The objective is to enable rapid identification in real time while operating within the UK legal and regulatory framework. The system uses computer vision and deep learning models trained on large facial image datasets to extract biometric templates from live video feeds. These templates are compared against encrypted watch-list databases using pattern-matching algorithms. The solution is integrated with existing police camera infrastructure and operational command systems, generating alerts for officers when similarity thresholds are met. The technology supports high-speed processing of video streams in dynamic environments. The intended impact is improved response times, enhanced public safety and more efficient deployment of police resources. Operational benefits include automation of identity checks in crowded areas and support for targeted interventions, although the system also requires strict governance, oversight and accuracy monitoring to maintain reliability and public trust.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-647,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00626,EU_PSTW_202609_00626,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Oslo,General public services n.e.c.,"Municipal employees and citizens frequently require confirmations, certificates and standard information, which traditionally involve manual processing and significant administrative effort.",The Municipality of Oslo explores chatbots to respond to citizen requests,Municipality of Oslo; Local Government; General public services n.e.c.; Norway,"The objective was to test whether conversational interfaces could automate repetitive tasks while maintaining service quality and reliability. The solution uses natural language processing models to interpret user queries, classify intent and trigger backend workflows.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Municipality of Oslo sought to improve the efficiency and accessibility of internal and external administrative services by reducing response times to routine enquiries. Municipal employees and citizens frequently require confirmations, certificates and standard information, which traditionally involve manual processing and significant administrative effort. In collaboration with Capgemini, the municipality established an interdisciplinary team to develop proof-of-concept chatbot solutions capable of handling structured service requests. The objective was to test whether conversational interfaces could automate repetitive tasks while maintaining service quality and reliability. The initial pilot focused on an internal chatbot that allowed employees to request confirmation of employment through a chat interface. The solution uses natural language processing models to interpret user queries, classify intent and trigger backend workflows. When a request is validated, the chatbot interacts with existing automated document generation systems to produce confirmation documents and delivers status updates within the chat window. The system integrates with municipal HR and document management platforms through secure APIs. The pilot demonstrated the feasibility of conversational automation, reducing processing times and administrative workload. The approach improves user experience by providing immediate acknowledgement and faster turnaround, while offering scalability for broader deployment in citizen-facing services and other municipal processes.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-648,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00627,EU_PSTW_202609_00627,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Vienna,General public services n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,Chatbot - City of Vienna,City of Vienna; Local Government; General public services n.e.c.; Austria,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Residents and visitors often need quick answers to practical city questions while on the move, such as where to find a service, how long parking rules apply, when public transport departs or where the nearest public facility is located. Vienna developed WienBot as a digital assistant that complements the city website by providing concise answers through chat and voice. The objective is to make municipal information easier to consume in everyday situations, without requiring users to navigate long web pages or search-result lists. The implementation connects the chatbot to city information and open government data, including geospatial data, the city events database and real-time public-transport interfaces from Wiener Linien. At a conceptual level, the bot interprets a user’s question, retrieves relevant structured or editorial information and returns a direct answer, sometimes supported by map or route data. It can answer in standard language and has also been known for a local Viennese tone. The expected impact is faster access to municipal information, improved usability on mobile devices, greater accessibility through voice search and reduced friction for routine enquiries.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-649,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00628,EU_PSTW_202609_00628,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Transports for London,General public services n.e.c.,"The objective was to improve customer service by allowing users to ask for Tube, rail, tram and bus updates without navigating separate status pages or waiting for social-media staff to respond manually.",London TravelBot,Transports for London; Local Government; General public services n.e.c.; United Kingdom,"The implementation used an AI-powered chat function connected to TfL’s latest operational data. The expected impact was faster customer information, reduced pressure on manual social-media response teams, more convenient travel updates and better accessibility for users already using Messenger.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Transport users need immediate and reliable information about service status, disruptions and arrivals, particularly when travelling through a large multimodal network. Transport for London launched TravelBot as an automated Facebook Messenger service to provide quick journey information through a familiar social-media channel. The objective was to improve customer service by allowing users to ask for Tube, rail, tram and bus updates without navigating separate status pages or waiting for social-media staff to respond manually. The implementation used an AI-powered chat function connected to TfL’s latest operational data. It could answer questions about bus arrival times, service updates and Tube maps, and link users directly to a customer-service agent where escalation was required. At a conceptual level, the bot combined conversational intent recognition, transport-status data, route or line lookup and social-media messaging integration. The expected impact was faster customer information, reduced pressure on manual social-media response teams, more convenient travel updates and better accessibility for users already using Messenger. TfL’s 2017 launch described TravelBot as its first such social-media tool, and a 2018 London Assembly response reported that TfL had enhanced TravelBot’s technology to provide step-free access information more simply.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-650,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00629,EU_PSTW_202609_00629,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Policnico Universitario Campus Bio-Medico Roma,Hospital services.,"At a conceptual level, the system interprets user questions, matches them to a structured knowledge base and returns answers in natural language, reducing the need for users to search manually through multiple sources or wait for routine information. Its expected impact is easier access to medical guidance, stronger patient and caregiver support, reduced burden on staff for repetitive questions and a more responsive digital communication channel around chronic neurological care.",Virtual assistant equipped with artificial intelligence for Parkinson's patients and their families,Policnico Universitario Campus Bio-Medico Roma; Academic-Research; Hospital services.; Italy,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Patients living with Parkinson’s disease and their families often need practical information outside scheduled clinical appointments, especially when questions concern symptoms, therapies, daily management and the relationship between medical recommendations and everyday care. Campus Bio-Medico University Hospital in Rome and IBM developed “Parkinson bot” to provide a virtual assistant capable of answering Parkinson’s-related questions at any time. The service was designed to improve access to reliable information and strengthen continuity between specialist expertise and patients’ day-to-day needs. The implementation used IBM Watson Assistant, configuring a conversational interface around clinically relevant content prepared for people affected by Parkinson’s, caregivers and family members. At a conceptual level, the system interprets user questions, matches them to a structured knowledge base and returns answers in natural language, reducing the need for users to search manually through multiple sources or wait for routine information. Its expected impact is easier access to medical guidance, stronger patient and caregiver support, reduced burden on staff for repetitive questions and a more responsive digital communication channel around chronic neurological care. The case also illustrates how conversational AI can be used in hospital services when the subject matter requires a careful balance between accessibility and clinical reliability: the bot supports information delivery but does not replace medical evaluation or personalised decisions by clinicians.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-651,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00630,EU_PSTW_202609_00630,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Metropolia,Transport,UNKNOWN,Sohjoa - Baltic Sea Region transitioning into eco-friendly autonomous last mile public transport,Metropolia; Consortium; Transport; Finland,"This use case addresses these challenges by piloting eco-friendly autonomous minibuses on real urban routes, involving local authorities, transport operators, technology providers, and passengers, with the objective of evaluating the feasibility of autonomous last-mile public transport, supporting the transition to greener mobility, and generating evidence to inform future transport policies and deployments. The service is implemented through autonomous driving technologies that enable small electric buses to operate on predefined demonstration routes, using a combination of sensors such as cameras, lidar, and radar to continuously scan their surroundings, detect obstacles, and make driving decisions including slowing down or stopping when required.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Urban areas across the Baltic Sea region face growing pressure to provide sustainable, accessible, and efficient last-mile public transport. Cities need solutions that reduce emissions while maintaining safety, reliability, and public trust in new mobility services. This use case addresses these challenges by piloting eco-friendly autonomous minibuses on real urban routes, involving local authorities, transport operators, technology providers, and passengers, with the objective of evaluating the feasibility of autonomous last-mile public transport, supporting the transition to greener mobility, and generating evidence to inform future transport policies and deployments. The service is implemented through autonomous driving technologies that enable small electric buses to operate on predefined demonstration routes, using a combination of sensors such as cameras, lidar, and radar to continuously scan their surroundings, detect obstacles, and make driving decisions including slowing down or stopping when required. Control software processes sensor data in real time to support safe navigation in mixed traffic environments, while a trained operator remains on board during all pilot operations to supervise the system, intervene if necessary, and ensure compliance with safety regulations, with operational data collected to support system evaluation and refinement. The pilots provide valuable insights into the technical, operational, and regulatory aspects of autonomous public transport, demonstrating potential reductions in environmental impact, improved last-mile connectivity, and increased efficiency in urban transport networks, while the supervised deployment model helps build public confidence and supports the development of scalable, low-emission mobility solutions for cities planning future autonomous transport services.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-652,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00631,EU_PSTW_202609_00631,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Finnish Immigration Service,Social protection n.e.c.,"Citizens and applicants required timely, consistent information in both Finnish and English, while staff resources were limited.",Finland Migration Service Chatbot,Finnish Immigration Service; Central-Government; Social protection n.e.c.; Finland,"This use case addressed the need to improve access to basic immigration information and reduce pressure on customer service channels, involving the Finnish Immigration Service, applicants, and the general public. The solution was implemented through a rule-based and language-processing chatbot embedded across all website pages, designed to recognise common user queries and retrieve predefined responses from official information sources.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Finnish Immigration Service faced sustained pressure to respond to large volumes of repetitive public enquiries related to residence permits, application processing times, and contact information, particularly during periods of high migration demand. Citizens and applicants required timely, consistent information in both Finnish and English, while staff resources were limited. This use case addressed the need to improve access to basic immigration information and reduce pressure on customer service channels, involving the Finnish Immigration Service, applicants, and the general public. The objective was to provide a scalable, always-available information service integrated into the migri.fi website. The solution was implemented through a rule-based and language-processing chatbot embedded across all website pages, designed to recognise common user queries and retrieve predefined responses from official information sources. The chatbot supported bilingual interaction and was integrated with existing web content management systems. During its pilot phase, the service improved accessibility to information, reduced simple enquiries handled by staff, and enhanced user convenience. Although the chatbot has since been decommissioned, it demonstrated the value of automated digital front-line services in public administration and informed later approaches to online service design.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,NOT_IN_USE,미사용,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-654,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00632,EU_PSTW_202609_00632,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Office of the Revenue Commissioners,General services.,"The Revenue Commissioners of Ireland handle large volumes of telephone enquiries related to tax clearance certificates, creating operational pressure and delays for both staff and taxpayers.",Virtual Digital Assistant with the Revenue Commissioners of Ireland,Office of the Revenue Commissioners; Central-Government; General services.; Ireland,"This use case addresses the need to streamline telephone-based services while maintaining accuracy and trust, involving Revenue, taxpayers, and contact centre staff. The service is implemented as a voice-driven virtual assistant that recognises spoken queries, retrieves relevant information, and in some cases processes tax clearance certificate applications directly.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Revenue Commissioners of Ireland handle large volumes of telephone enquiries related to tax clearance certificates, creating operational pressure and delays for both staff and taxpayers. Many of these requests are repetitive and transactional in nature. This use case addresses the need to streamline telephone-based services while maintaining accuracy and trust, involving Revenue, taxpayers, and contact centre staff. The objective is to improve efficiency in handling general information requests and simple applications. The service is implemented as a voice-driven virtual assistant that recognises spoken queries, retrieves relevant information, and in some cases processes tax clearance certificate applications directly. It integrates with Revenue’s back-end systems to verify eligibility and status, operating within existing telephony infrastructure. The pilot demonstrates improved call handling efficiency, shorter response times, and reduced workload for human agents. It enhances accessibility for users who prefer voice interaction and supports more consistent service delivery, while freeing staff to focus on complex cases with higher added value.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F06_HIGH_VOLUME_SCALABILITY_BOTTLENECK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-655,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00633,EU_PSTW_202609_00633,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,University La Sapienza,General public services n.e.c.,"The solution improves convenience by enabling hands-free interaction, reduces queues and manual processing, and enhances the overall student experience.","University La Sapienza launches the first voice ""assistant"": Infostud-Watson",University La Sapienza; Academic-Research; General public services n.e.c.; Italy,"This use case involves the university administration, students, and technology partners, with the objective of improving usability and reducing administrative friction. Infostud-Watson is implemented as a mobile application that enables students to interact with university systems through voice commands and natural language dialogue.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Large universities manage complex administrative interactions with students, including exam bookings, certification requests, and general study-related enquiries, which can overwhelm traditional service desks. Sapienza University sought to simplify access to student services and modernise interaction channels. This use case involves the university administration, students, and technology partners, with the objective of improving usability and reducing administrative friction. Infostud-Watson is implemented as a mobile application that enables students to interact with university systems through voice commands and natural language dialogue. The assistant is connected to existing academic and administrative systems, allowing actions such as exam booking and certificate generation, and continuously adapts its responses based on usage patterns. The solution improves convenience by enabling hands-free interaction, reduces queues and manual processing, and enhances the overall student experience. It supports scalable service delivery across a large student population and strengthens digital engagement within the university ecosystem.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-656,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00634,EU_PSTW_202609_00634,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Regione Puglia,R&D general public services.,"Regional institutions face high volumes of requests, fragmented communication channels, and rising expectations for digital public services.","Project “Sibyl A.I. Virtual"" with Regione Puglia",Regione Puglia; Regional Government; R&D general public services.; Italy,"This use case addresses these challenges by creating a unified digital platform to support interactions between the Regione Puglia and citizens, involving public sector staff, local businesses, and end users, with the objective of improving access to information and services, streamlining communication processes, and enhancing the overall quality and responsiveness of institutional services. The platform is implemented through a virtual assistance system based on natural language processing and conversational interfaces, with language models trained on institutional content, public service information, and frequently asked questions to understand user requests and generate accurate, context-aware responses.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Public administrations increasingly need efficient and accessible channels to manage interactions with citizens, respond to enquiries, and deliver services in a timely manner. Regional institutions face high volumes of requests, fragmented communication channels, and rising expectations for digital public services. This use case addresses these challenges by creating a unified digital platform to support interactions between the Regione Puglia and citizens, involving public sector staff, local businesses, and end users, with the objective of improving access to information and services, streamlining communication processes, and enhancing the overall quality and responsiveness of institutional services. The platform is implemented through a virtual assistance system based on natural language processing and conversational interfaces, with language models trained on institutional content, public service information, and frequently asked questions to understand user requests and generate accurate, context-aware responses. The system integrates with existing public administration information systems and digital service portals, enabling automated handling of routine enquiries while allowing escalation to human operators when required, and using interaction data to continuously refine the knowledge base and improve response accuracy. The solution improves the efficiency and scalability of citizen support services by reducing response times and relieving administrative staff from repetitive tasks, while enhancing user experience through consistent, always-available access to information. For the administration, it supports cost-effective service delivery, greater transparency, and improved capacity to meet growing demand for digital public services, contributing to the modernisation of public sector operations in the region.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F06_HIGH_VOLUME_SCALABILITY_BOTTLENECK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-657,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00635,EU_PSTW_202609_00635,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Trieste,General services.,"Prior to the initiative, citizens relied heavily on in-person visits or phone calls, leading to queues, delays, and fragmented communication. During its initial operation in early 2020, the chatbot improved response times, increased transparency, and reduced manual workload for staff.",Artificial intelligence: high technology and innovation to make life easier for citizens,Municipality of Trieste; Local Government; General services.; Italy,"The use case involved the Municipality of Trieste, regional authorities, and citizens, with the objective of providing real-time information through the institutional website and improving service efficiency. The solution was implemented as a conversational chatbot developed by Insiel SpA and co-financed by the Friuli Venezia Giulia Region.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Municipality of Trieste sought to modernise citizen interaction with local administration by simplifying access to demographic services, such as registry information and administrative procedures. Prior to the initiative, citizens relied heavily on in-person visits or phone calls, leading to queues, delays, and fragmented communication. The use case involved the Municipality of Trieste, regional authorities, and citizens, with the objective of providing real-time information through the institutional website and improving service efficiency. The solution was implemented as a conversational chatbot developed by Insiel SpA and co-financed by the Friuli Venezia Giulia Region. It was connected to municipal information systems and structured knowledge bases covering demographic services, enabling automated responses to common questions. During its initial operation in early 2020, the chatbot improved response times, increased transparency, and reduced manual workload for staff. However, the service was later discontinued. Despite this, the project provided valuable insights into citizen expectations, system integration challenges, and the organisational changes required to sustain digital assistance services in local government.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,NOT_IN_USE,미사용,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-658,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00636,EU_PSTW_202609_00636,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,University of Bedfordshire,Old age.,"Loneliness and social isolation are persistent challenges in residential care homes, particularly among older adults with limited mobility or family contact. The pilot showed positive effects on resident engagement and mood, particularly for individuals with limited social contact.",Robots to be used in UK care homes to help reduce loneliness,University of Bedfordshire; Academic-Research; Old age.; United Kingdom,"Care staff reported that robots supplemented, rather than replaced, human interaction and could be deployed flexibly.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Loneliness and social isolation are persistent challenges in residential care homes, particularly among older adults with limited mobility or family contact. A UK pilot explored whether social robots could complement care staff by providing companionship and daily interaction for residents. Stakeholders included care home residents, carers, management, families, and technology providers, with the objective of improving emotional well-being without replacing human care. Conducted between 2019 and 2020, the pilot introduced “Pepper” wheeled humanoid robots into selected care homes. The robots were pre-programmed and gradually adapted to residents’ preferences, enabling simple conversations, music playback, reminders, and interactive activities. Sensors and speech interfaces supported autonomous navigation and engagement, while learning components allowed the robots to personalise interactions over time. The systems operated locally within care facilities and were supervised by staff. The pilot showed positive effects on resident engagement and mood, particularly for individuals with limited social contact. Care staff reported that robots supplemented, rather than replaced, human interaction and could be deployed flexibly. While not a substitute for personal care, the solution demonstrated potential to scale as a supportive tool to reduce loneliness and enhance quality of life in long-term care settings.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-659,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00637,EU_PSTW_202609_00637,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Siena,General public services n.e.c.,"The Municipality of Siena aimed to reduce administrative burdens on citizens and improve access to essential municipal services such as identity cards, change of residence, and electoral card renewals.","Caterina, the first (virtual) municipal employee for identity cards and changes of residence",Municipality of Siena; Local Government; General public services n.e.c.; Italy,"This use case involved the municipality, residents, and service providers, with the objective of enabling citizens to complete administrative procedures remotely. Caterina was implemented as a virtual assistant accessible online, designed to guide users through specific administrative workflows and provide procedural information.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Municipality of Siena aimed to reduce administrative burdens on citizens and improve access to essential municipal services such as identity cards, change of residence, and electoral card renewals. Long queues and office visits were a source of frustration, particularly for elderly residents. This use case involved the municipality, residents, and service providers, with the objective of enabling citizens to complete administrative procedures remotely. Caterina was implemented as a virtual assistant accessible online, designed to guide users through specific administrative workflows and provide procedural information. The system relied on structured process logic, predefined decision trees, and integration with municipal service information. While active, Caterina reduced unnecessary in-person visits, improved convenience, and demonstrated strong public interest in digital municipal services. The service has since been discontinued, but it highlighted the potential of virtual assistants to support administrative simplification and informed subsequent digitalisation efforts within Italian municipalities.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,NOT_IN_USE,미사용,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-660,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00638,EU_PSTW_202609_00638,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Norwegian University of Science and Technology (NTNU),General services.,UNKNOWN,"Anna, a bot for processing Expense Claim",Norwegian University of Science and Technology (NTNU); Academic-Research; General services.; Norway,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Norwegian University of Science and Technology (NTNU) and Visma has agreed to test letting a bot automatically process expense claims. The university is the first organisation to implement this functionality, financed by the NTNU digitalisation program. The functionality is not available anymore.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,NOT_IN_USE,미사용,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-661,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00639,EU_PSTW_202609_00639,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Madrid Metro (Department of Public Works, City Planning, and Transportation)",Environmental protection n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,Clean Air Optimisation in Madrid Metro,"Madrid Metro (Department of Public Works, City Planning, and Transportation); Local Government; Environmental protection n.e.c.; Spain","This use case involves the metro operator, passengers, and technology providers, with the objective of dynamically managing ventilation to maintain high air quality standards. The solution is implemented through a self-learning ventilation control system that processes environmental sensor data from stations to adjust airflow in real time.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Ensuring good air quality in underground transport systems is essential for passenger health and comfort, yet traditional ventilation systems often operate on static rules that do not respond efficiently to real conditions. Madrid Metro sought to improve air quality while optimising energy consumption. This use case involves the metro operator, passengers, and technology providers, with the objective of dynamically managing ventilation to maintain high air quality standards. The solution is implemented through a self-learning ventilation control system that processes environmental sensor data from stations to adjust airflow in real time. The system integrates with existing ventilation infrastructure and continuously adapts to changing conditions such as passenger density and external air quality. The result is improved air quality, more efficient energy use, and reduced operational costs. Passengers benefit from a healthier environment, while the operator gains a more sustainable and responsive infrastructure management approach.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-662,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00640,EU_PSTW_202609_00640,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Vienna AirportCity,Transport,UNKNOWN,Bebot – Vienna AirportCity Virtual Assistant,Vienna AirportCity; Local Government; Transport; Austria,"This use case involves airport operators, travellers, and businesses, with the objective of improving information access and reducing pressure on staff. Bebot is implemented as a web-based chatbot accessible via mobile and desktop through on-site Wi-Fi and the airport website.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Airports must provide clear, up-to-date information to diverse users, particularly during disruptions such as public health emergencies, while managing high volumes of enquiries. Vienna AirportCity introduced a digital assistant to support visitors navigating transport, facilities, and Covid-19-related requirements. This use case involves airport operators, travellers, and businesses, with the objective of improving information access and reducing pressure on staff. Bebot is implemented as a web-based chatbot accessible via mobile and desktop through on-site Wi-Fi and the airport website. It delivers structured responses on transport options, directions, facility services, and health regulations, integrated with airport information systems. The service enhances user experience by providing immediate guidance, improves operational efficiency by reducing routine enquiries, and supports consistent communication in a complex, high-traffic environment.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-663,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00641,EU_PSTW_202609_00641,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""social_nonprofit""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,NAVA,Cultural services.,"Hungary’s National Audiovisual Archive (NAVA) faced the challenge of organising and preserving millions of historical images and audiovisual records forming the country’s twentieth-century cultural memory. Manual classification was slow, costly, and unable to keep pace with digitisation efforts.",AI technology speeds up historical image handling at Hungary's National Archive,NAVA; Central-Government; Cultural services.; Hungary,"Between 2019 and 2021, a pilot deployed cloud-based image analysis services to classify large image collections automatically.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Hungary’s National Audiovisual Archive (NAVA) faced the challenge of organising and preserving millions of historical images and audiovisual records forming the country’s twentieth-century cultural memory. Manual classification was slow, costly, and unable to keep pace with digitisation efforts. Stakeholders included archivists, historians, public broadcasters, and citizens, with the objective of accelerating cataloguing while maintaining archival quality. Between 2019 and 2021, a pilot deployed cloud-based image analysis services to classify large image collections automatically. Visual recognition models were trained to detect objects, scenes, and contextual features, generating metadata to support indexing and retrieval. The system integrated with existing archive databases and digitisation workflows, enabling human archivists to validate and refine outputs rather than start from scratch. The pilot significantly reduced processing time for image classification, allowing millions of records to be indexed efficiently. It improved searchability and long-term preservation planning while lowering operational costs. Archivists retained editorial control, ensuring trust and reliability. The approach demonstrated strong scalability and laid the foundation for sustainable management of national audiovisual heritage.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-664,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00642,EU_PSTW_202609_00642,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,CitizenLab,Communication,UNKNOWN,Unlocking the potential of crowdsourcing for public decision-making with artificial intelligence,CitizenLab; Private sector; Communication; Belgium,"Public administrations increasingly use online participation platforms to collect citizen input, but processing thousands of contributions manually limits their impact on decision-making. This use case addresses the challenge of transforming large-scale citizen feedback into actionable insights, involving civil servants, policymakers, and citizens.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Public administrations increasingly use online participation platforms to collect citizen input, but processing thousands of contributions manually limits their impact on decision-making. This use case addresses the challenge of transforming large-scale citizen feedback into actionable insights, involving civil servants, policymakers, and citizens. The objective is to support evidence-based public decisions while maintaining inclusive participation. CitizenLab’s platform implements advanced text analysis techniques to automatically classify, cluster, and summarise citizen contributions by topic, location, and demographic traits. Dashboards present emerging trends and patterns, integrated into participation workflows used by public authorities. The solution significantly reduces analysis time, improves insight quality, and enables officials to respond more effectively to citizen priorities. It strengthens trust in participatory processes, enhances transparency, and scales citizen engagement without proportional increases in administrative effort.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-665,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00643,EU_PSTW_202609_00643,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Aarhus University,Cultural services.,"Researchers in history, computer science, and the social sciences face significant challenges when analysing large collections of historical photographs, as manual examination is time-consuming and limits the scale of inquiry.",Artificial intelligence dives into thousands of WWII photographs,Aarhus University; Academic-Research; Cultural services.; Denmark,"This use case addresses these challenges through an interdisciplinary study that examines thousands of Second World War photographs, involving academic researchers and cultural heritage institutions, with the objective of uncovering latent patterns in visual content and advancing new methods for large-scale historical image analysis. The study is implemented using computer vision and pattern recognition techniques applied to extensive datasets of digitised wartime photographs, with models trained to extract visual features related to composition, subject matter, and contextual elements.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Researchers in history, computer science, and the social sciences face significant challenges when analysing large collections of historical photographs, as manual examination is time-consuming and limits the scale of inquiry. Understanding patterns of authorship, perspective, and visual style in wartime imagery is important for historical interpretation, archival research, and cultural analysis. This use case addresses these challenges through an interdisciplinary study that examines thousands of Second World War photographs, involving academic researchers and cultural heritage institutions, with the objective of uncovering latent patterns in visual content and advancing new methods for large-scale historical image analysis. The study is implemented using computer vision and pattern recognition techniques applied to extensive datasets of digitised wartime photographs, with models trained to extract visual features related to composition, subject matter, and contextual elements. These models analyse similarities and recurring visual signatures across images, enabling the system to distinguish and attribute photographs to individual photographers based solely on image content, without relying on metadata. The results demonstrate that large-scale automated image analysis can reveal meaningful stylistic and contextual patterns in historical archives, including the ability to identify photographers with a high degree of accuracy. This approach significantly expands the analytical capacity of historical research, supports more systematic exploration of visual archives, and opens new opportunities for interdisciplinary collaboration and data-driven insights in the study of cultural heritage.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-666,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00644,EU_PSTW_202609_00644,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,U.K. Medicines and Healthcare Products Regulatory Agency (MHRA),R&D health.,"During the Covid-19 vaccination rollout, the Medicines and Healthcare Products Regulatory Agency (MHRA) faced the challenge of monitoring tens of thousands of reports within tight timeframes, far beyond what could be efficiently handled through manual analysis alone. The approach significantly improves the scalability and responsiveness of vaccine safety monitoring, enabling the MHRA to manage an expected volume of 50,000 to 100,000 reports of suspected side effects per 100 million doses administered over a six- to 12-month period, while reducing manual workload, accelerating the identification of potential risks, supporting faster regulatory decision-making, and strengthening the overall reliability of pharmacovigilance processes to deliver safer vaccination campaigns and improved public health outcomes.",U.K. to use AI to spot dangerous side effects in the COVID-19 vaccinations,U.K. Medicines and Healthcare Products Regulatory Agency (MHRA); Central-Government; R&D health.; United Kingdom,"This use case addresses the need for timely and reliable pharmacovigilance by supporting the MHRA in detecting early safety signals from reported side effects, involving regulators, healthcare providers, and the public, with the objective of ensuring vaccine safety while maintaining public confidence in the vaccination programme. The solution is implemented through a machine learning system developed under a $1.96 million contract awarded to the UK arm of Genpact, which ingests large volumes of reported side-effect data from multiple sources and applies statistical learning and pattern-recognition techniques to identify unusual trends, correlations, or clusters that may indicate potential safety concerns.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Large-scale vaccination programmes generate vast volumes of safety reports that regulators must review to identify potential adverse effects and protect public health. During the Covid-19 vaccination rollout, the Medicines and Healthcare Products Regulatory Agency (MHRA) faced the challenge of monitoring tens of thousands of reports within tight timeframes, far beyond what could be efficiently handled through manual analysis alone. This use case addresses the need for timely and reliable pharmacovigilance by supporting the MHRA in detecting early safety signals from reported side effects, involving regulators, healthcare providers, and the public, with the objective of ensuring vaccine safety while maintaining public confidence in the vaccination programme. The solution is implemented through a machine learning system developed under a $1.96 million contract awarded to the UK arm of Genpact, which ingests large volumes of reported side-effect data from multiple sources and applies statistical learning and pattern-recognition techniques to identify unusual trends, correlations, or clusters that may indicate potential safety concerns. Models are trained on historical vaccination and adverse-event data to distinguish normal background reporting patterns from signals requiring further investigation, and the platform integrates with existing regulatory reporting systems to enable continuous monitoring and prioritisation of cases for expert review. The approach significantly improves the scalability and responsiveness of vaccine safety monitoring, enabling the MHRA to manage an expected volume of 50,000 to 100,000 reports of suspected side effects per 100 million doses administered over a six- to 12-month period, while reducing manual workload, accelerating the identification of potential risks, supporting faster regulatory decision-making, and strengthening the overall reliability of pharmacovigilance processes to deliver safer vaccination campaigns and improved public health outcomes.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,S7_GENERATIVE_CONTENT_CREATION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-667,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00645,EU_PSTW_202609_00645,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Azienda USL Toscana Sud Est (Regional Health structure),Public health services.,UNKNOWN,"Aurora, the new virtual assistant of the South East Tuscany AUSL is online",Azienda USL Toscana Sud Est (Regional Health structure); Local Government; Public health services.; Italy,"This use case involved the health authority, technology providers, and residents, with the objective of delivering easy-to-use digital information services. Aurora was implemented as a 3D virtual avatar accessible via web and mobile devices, providing scripted responses to health service enquiries through a conversational interface.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Healthcare organisations face high demand for reliable public information, particularly across large geographic areas. The South East Tuscany Local Health Authority aimed to improve access to health-related information for citizens in Siena, Arezzo, and Grosseto. This use case involved the health authority, technology providers, and residents, with the objective of delivering easy-to-use digital information services. Aurora was implemented as a 3D virtual avatar accessible via web and mobile devices, providing scripted responses to health service enquiries through a conversational interface. The system integrated visual interaction with structured information repositories. During its pilot phase, Aurora improved accessibility and visibility of health information and showcased innovative digital communication methods. The service has since been discontinued, but it demonstrated early experimentation with virtual interfaces in healthcare communication and contributed to organisational learning on digital citizen engagement.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,NOT_IN_USE,미사용,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-668,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00646,EU_PSTW_202609_00646,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Gothenburg,Transport,UNKNOWN,Self-driving buses roll into Gothenburg,City of Gothenburg; Local Government; Transport; Sweden,"This use case focuses on the trial deployment of autonomous electric shuttles to provide public transport services within the Lindholmen Industrial and Science Park, involving the city, the transport operator Keolis, and passengers, with the objective of assessing the feasibility, safety, and operational value of self-driving buses in an urban setting. The service is implemented through autonomous electric shuttle vehicles operating on a dedicated 1.8-kilometre route, using onboard sensors, navigation systems, and control software to perceive their environment and drive without direct human control.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Cities seeking to modernise public transport must balance sustainability goals, service accessibility, and the safe introduction of new mobility technologies. In Gothenburg, local authorities and transport operators aimed to explore innovative solutions that could enhance connectivity within key employment and innovation districts while reducing environmental impact. This use case focuses on the trial deployment of autonomous electric shuttles to provide public transport services within the Lindholmen Industrial and Science Park, involving the city, the transport operator Keolis, and passengers, with the objective of assessing the feasibility, safety, and operational value of self-driving buses in an urban setting. The service is implemented through autonomous electric shuttle vehicles operating on a dedicated 1.8-kilometre route, using onboard sensors, navigation systems, and control software to perceive their environment and drive without direct human control. The shuttles operate under supervised conditions during a 4.5-month trial period that began on 18 January, allowing for real-world testing, data collection, and system validation while ensuring passenger safety and regulatory compliance. The trial provides insights into the performance, reliability, and user acceptance of autonomous public transport, demonstrating the potential for low-emission, flexible mobility services in urban areas. It supports evidence-based decision-making on future deployments, contributes to innovation in public transport provision, and helps position Gothenburg as a testbed for sustainable and scalable autonomous mobility solutions.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-669,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00647,EU_PSTW_202609_00647,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Agenzia delle Entrate,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.","Tax authorities face persistent challenges in identifying and preventing tax evasion, particularly as economic activities and financial relationships become increasingly complex. Traditional risk assessment methods often struggle to detect sophisticated patterns of non-compliance early enough to support effective enforcement and voluntary compliance.",Data-Driven Risk Analysis to Prevent Tax Evasion in Italy,"Agenzia delle Entrate; Central-Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Italy","This use case addresses these challenges by modernising tax evasion risk analysis for the Italian Revenue Agency, involving the Ministry of Finance, its in-house technology partner SOGEI, and European Union funding bodies, with the objective of strengthening preventive identification of taxpayers at high risk of non-compliance and improving the effectiveness of fiscal oversight. The initiative is implemented through a data-driven system that applies machine learning, network analysis, and advanced data visualisation techniques to large volumes of fiscal, administrative, and economic data.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Tax authorities face persistent challenges in identifying and preventing tax evasion, particularly as economic activities and financial relationships become increasingly complex. Traditional risk assessment methods often struggle to detect sophisticated patterns of non-compliance early enough to support effective enforcement and voluntary compliance. This use case addresses these challenges by modernising tax evasion risk analysis for the Italian Revenue Agency, involving the Ministry of Finance, its in-house technology partner SOGEI, and European Union funding bodies, with the objective of strengthening preventive identification of taxpayers at high risk of non-compliance and improving the effectiveness of fiscal oversight. The initiative is implemented through a data-driven system that applies machine learning, network analysis, and advanced data visualisation techniques to large volumes of fiscal, administrative, and economic data. Analytical models examine relationships between individuals, companies, and transactions to uncover hidden networks and anomalous behaviours associated with evasion risks, while visualisation tools support analysts in interpreting complex results and prioritising cases. The system integrates with existing tax administration processes and information infrastructures, enabling experimentation, validation, and gradual operational adoption. The project enhances the accuracy and timeliness of risk assessments, enabling more targeted controls and more efficient use of investigative resources. By shifting towards preventive and evidence-based analysis, it supports reduced tax evasion, improved compliance, and stronger public finances, while increasing the transparency, scalability, and analytical capacity of the Revenue Agency’s risk management processes.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-670,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00648,EU_PSTW_202609_00648,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""social_nonprofit""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Potsdam,Transport,The ASTRiD project addresses this challenge by developing a digital tram depot capable of supporting autonomous vehicle movements within controlled environments.,Autonomous Tram Depot (AStriD) – Digital Depot Operations in Potsdam,City of Potsdam; Local Government; Transport; Germany,"The objective is to automate routine depot manoeuvres, such as moving trams through washing facilities or into sidings, reducing manual labour and operational risk.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Urban transport operators face rising pressure to increase efficiency while maintaining safety and reliability in depot operations. The ASTRiD project addresses this challenge by developing a digital tram depot capable of supporting autonomous vehicle movements within controlled environments. Stakeholders include Verkehrsbetrieb Potsdam (ViP), Siemens Mobility, transport authorities, and national research funders. The objective is to automate routine depot manoeuvres, such as moving trams through washing facilities or into sidings, reducing manual labour and operational risk. The project integrates autonomous vehicle control systems, depot infrastructure sensors, digital signalling, and safety supervision mechanisms. Trams are equipped with perception and navigation systems that interpret depot layouts and execute predefined manoeuvres under supervision. The system interfaces with depot management software and safety controls to ensure compliance with operational constraints. According to public reporting, a key demonstration phase was successfully completed as part of the three-year research project funded under Germany’s mFund programme. While the project remains at pilot level, it has validated the technical feasibility of autonomous depot operations and provided a foundation for future scaling in urban rail systems.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-671,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00649,EU_PSTW_202609_00649,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Transport for Greater Manchester,Public order and safety n.e.c.,"The objective was to obtain continuous, anonymised data on active travel flows to support infrastructure planning, safety interventions, and evaluation of policy measures without relying on manual surveys.",Manchester uses artificial intelligence to gain more insight into active travel,Transport for Greater Manchester; Local Government; Public order and safety n.e.c.; United Kingdom,"Transport for Greater Manchester (TfGM) planned a regional monitoring initiative to gain better insight into how pedestrians and cyclists use segregated routes, junctions, and priority corridors.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"City regions face increasing pressure to promote walking and cycling while ensuring that investments in active travel infrastructure are evidence-based. Transport for Greater Manchester (TfGM) planned a regional monitoring initiative to gain better insight into how pedestrians and cyclists use segregated routes, junctions, and priority corridors. Stakeholders included TfGM, local authorities, urban planners, and residents. The objective was to obtain continuous, anonymised data on active travel flows to support infrastructure planning, safety interventions, and evaluation of policy measures without relying on manual surveys. The planned solution involved the installation of around 100 roadside sensors supplied by Vivacity Labs. These sensors combine optical and signal-based detection to count pedestrians, cyclists, and vehicles in real time while processing data locally to avoid personal identification. Aggregated flow data would be transmitted to central dashboards and combined with contextual information such as time of day and location. According to public reporting, elements of the sensor deployment were initiated, but the programme was framed as an exploratory pilot rather than a fully institutionalised system. The initiative demonstrated the potential for scalable, low-intrusion monitoring of active travel, supporting more responsive transport planning and contributing to wider smart mobility strategies, even though full regional rollout was not confirmed at the time.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PLANNED,계획,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-672,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00650,EU_PSTW_202609_00650,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Bjelovar,General services.,"It aims to replace fragmented service portals with a conversational assistant (similar to messaging apps), improving usability, accessibility, and awareness of available public services.",Creating an AI-driven mobile virtual assistant and common public services platform,City of Bjelovar; Local Government; General services.; Croatia,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Croatian pilot in the inGOV project focuses on the City of Bjelovar and demonstrates how digital public services can be made more accessible through an AI-driven mobile virtual assistant. The idea is to create a unified platform that allows citizens to access all local government services through a simple, mobile-first interface, reducing the complexity of navigating multiple disconnected systems. The pilot specifically targets around 32,000 citizens, with a strong emphasis on inclusivity, especially for older or less digitally experienced users. It aims to replace fragmented service portals with a conversational assistant (similar to messaging apps), improving usability, accessibility, and awareness of available public services. The solution also supports co-creation with stakeholders and is intended to reduce the need for helpdesk support by enabling more direct and intuitive interactions with government services.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-673,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00651,EU_PSTW_202609_00651,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,IRCCS San Raffaele Hospital,Health n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,"AI-SCORE, a project to calculate prognostic risk from Covid-19",IRCCS San Raffaele Hospital; Academic-Research; Health n.e.c.; Italy,"The platform uses machine learning to analyze a combination of clinical data, laboratory results, and medical imaging to estimate the probability that an infected patient will develop serious complications.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The AI-SCORE project (Artificial Intelligence – SARS-CoV-2 Risk Evaluation) is an Italian research initiative led by IRCCS San Raffaele Hospital in Milan, developed in collaboration with academic and industrial partners. Its goal is to build an AI-based system capable of predicting the risk of severe outcomes in COVID-19 patients. The platform uses machine learning to analyze a combination of clinical data, laboratory results, and medical imaging to estimate the probability that an infected patient will develop serious complications. This helps doctors identify high-risk patients early, improve triage decisions, and optimize hospital resource allocation during the pandemic. Beyond COVID-19, the project is designed as a broader model for predictive medicine, showing how artificial intelligence can support clinical decision-making in future infectious disease outbreaks and other high-risk health conditions.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-674,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00652,EU_PSTW_202609_00652,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Northampton,Transport,UNKNOWN,Computer vision e-scooter technology to be trialled in UK city,City of Northampton; Local Government; Transport; United Kingdom,"At the same time, it collects anonymised mobility data that cities can use to understand travel patterns, identify conflict zones, and improve infrastructure planning for pedestrians, cyclists, and scooter users.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The pilot is a UK trial of computer vision–enabled e-scooters designed to improve safety and urban mobility management in shared micromobility systems. The scooters are equipped with on-device AI and camera-based perception systems that can analyse the surrounding environment in real time. This allows them to detect where they are being ridden (for example, on roads, cycle lanes, or pedestrian areas) and whether they are being parked appropriately. When risky or non-compliant behaviour is identified, the system can trigger automated responses such as warnings or speed adjustments, helping encourage safer riding habits. At the same time, it collects anonymised mobility data that cities can use to understand travel patterns, identify conflict zones, and improve infrastructure planning for pedestrians, cyclists, and scooter users. Overall, the pilot explores how embedded AI in micromobility vehicles can support safer behaviour, better compliance with regulations, and smarter urban transport management.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-675,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00653,EU_PSTW_202609_00653,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Rome,Environmental protection n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,Rome conducts smart city trial of intelligent vision sensors,City of Rome; Local Government; Environmental protection n.e.c.; Italy,"Welfare Ticket – Blockchain-Based Social Security Platform for the Gig Economy (status: conceptual design, not implemented)",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Welfare Ticket – Blockchain-Based Social Security Platform for the Gig Economy (status: conceptual design, not implemented)",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-676,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00654,EU_PSTW_202609_00654,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Dublin City University,Public order and safety n.e.c.,"Dublin faces growing challenges in managing shared micro-mobility services, particularly e-scooters, as their rapid adoption raises concerns around road safety, pavement congestion, parking compliance, and integration with existing transport systems.",Dublin to host AI-powered e-scooter research project,Dublin City University; Academic-Research; Public order and safety n.e.c.; Ireland,"The objective of this use case is to improve how e-scooters are deployed, used, and regulated across the city by generating evidence-based insights that support safer operations, better infrastructure planning, and more effective policy decisions. The project applies advanced data analytics and machine learning techniques to data collected from e-scooters, including location, speed, braking events, trip duration, and interaction with the urban environment, combined with contextual data such as road layouts, traffic conditions, and weather.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Dublin faces growing challenges in managing shared micro-mobility services, particularly e-scooters, as their rapid adoption raises concerns around road safety, pavement congestion, parking compliance, and integration with existing transport systems. City authorities, transport planners, mobility operators, researchers, and citizens all have a stake in ensuring that e-scooters contribute positively to sustainable urban mobility rather than creating new risks or inefficiencies. The objective of this use case is to improve how e-scooters are deployed, used, and regulated across the city by generating evidence-based insights that support safer operations, better infrastructure planning, and more effective policy decisions. The project applies advanced data analytics and machine learning techniques to data collected from e-scooters, including location, speed, braking events, trip duration, and interaction with the urban environment, combined with contextual data such as road layouts, traffic conditions, and weather. Computer vision and predictive models are used to analyse rider behaviour, identify unsafe patterns, optimise parking zones, and simulate different regulatory or infrastructure scenarios. These models are integrated into a research platform that connects mobility operators, city systems, and analytical tools to enable continuous experimentation and evaluation. The initiative supports improved rider safety, reduced pavement clutter, and more efficient use of public space, while providing the city with robust evidence to guide regulation and investment. It enhances service reliability and user experience, enables scalable management of micro-mobility, and delivers long-term value by helping Dublin design safer, more sustainable urban transport systems.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-677,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00655,EU_PSTW_202609_00655,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Helsinki,General services.,"In Italy, a concept known as the “Welfare Ticket” was developed to address these challenges by enab",Helsinki trials AI and automation as part of its drive to digital services,City of Helsinki; Local Government; General services.; Finland,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Labour platforms and gig workers often operate outside traditional social security frameworks, creating gaps in insurance coverage and administrative complexity for short-term or task-based work. In Italy, a concept known as the “Welfare Ticket” was developed to address these challenges by enab",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-678,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00656,EU_PSTW_202609_00656,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Collegno Municipality,General services.,UNKNOWN,Chatbot Comune di Collegno (TO),Collegno Municipality; Local Government; General services.; Italy,The chatbot was implemented on the municipal website using predefined conversational flows linked to service-specific information.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Municipality of Collegno introduced a chatbot to support citizens seeking information on the Citizen Public Registry and separate waste collection services, aiming to reduce administrative workload and improve responsiveness. Stakeholders included municipal departments, residents, and service operators. The chatbot was implemented on the municipal website using predefined conversational flows linked to service-specific information. While the objective was to simplify access to information, the service faced usability and content limitations, leading to poor user satisfaction and eventual failure. The chatbot was later publicly acknowledged as unsuccessful and discontinued. Despite this outcome, the initiative highlighted critical success factors for digital assistants, including content quality, maintenance, and alignment with real user needs, offering valuable lessons for future public-sector digital services.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,NOT_IN_USE,미사용,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-679,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00657,EU_PSTW_202609_00657,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""social_nonprofit""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Foundation for Research and Technology Hellas,Police Services,"Urban authorities face growing challenges in ensuring public safety across busy urban environments, where large crowds, transport hubs, and shared public spaces can give rise to accidents, anti-social behaviour, and criminal activity.",MARVEL project in Trento: monitoring crowd in the city,Foundation for Research and Technology Hellas; Consortium; Police Services; Italy,"In Trento, local authorities and law enforcement needed tools to monitor multiple public areas simultaneously and respond rapidly to potentially dangerous situations while making efficient use of surveillance infrastructure. This use case addresses these needs by supporting the municipality and local police in monitoring public spaces such as streets, squares, subways, and parking areas, with the objective of identifying anomalous or risky situations, including fights, robberies, drug dealing, and traffic accidents, and enabling timely operational responses.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Urban authorities face growing challenges in ensuring public safety across busy urban environments, where large crowds, transport hubs, and shared public spaces can give rise to accidents, anti-social behaviour, and criminal activity. In Trento, local authorities and law enforcement needed tools to monitor multiple public areas simultaneously and respond rapidly to potentially dangerous situations while making efficient use of surveillance infrastructure. This use case addresses these needs by supporting the municipality and local police in monitoring public spaces such as streets, squares, subways, and parking areas, with the objective of identifying anomalous or risky situations, including fights, robberies, drug dealing, and traffic accidents, and enabling timely operational responses. The solution is implemented through a smart monitoring platform that analyses video and audio streams from distributed sensors and cameras across the city, applying advanced pattern recognition and anomaly detection techniques to identify events of interest within crowds and specific locations. Detected incidents trigger automated alerts to the police operational centre and generate a customised visual interface that highlights relevant camera feeds, supporting situational awareness and rapid decision-making. The system is tested across five trial cases, including crowd monitoring, detection of criminal or anti-social behaviour, subway monitoring, parking surveillance, and focused analysis of specific areas. The use case enhances public safety by enabling faster detection and response to incidents, improving coordination between monitoring systems and law enforcement, and increasing the effectiveness and scalability of urban surveillance, while supporting safer and more resilient city environments.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-680,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00658,EU_PSTW_202609_00658,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Autonomous Province of Trento - Health Agency,General services.,UNKNOWN,Chatbot Covibot Provincia Trento (TN),Autonomous Province of Trento - Health Agency; Local Government; General services.; Italy,"This use case involved the provincial health authority, citizens, and healthcare providers, with the objective of ensuring reliable information access at scale. Covibot was implemented as an informational chatbot connected to official health guidance and FAQs, accessible online to answer pandemic-related questions.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"During the Covid-19 pandemic, the Province of Trento needed to rapidly disseminate accurate and up-to-date information on testing, isolation rules, quarantine measures, and vaccination campaigns. Call centres and health services were under exceptional pressure. This use case involved the provincial health authority, citizens, and healthcare providers, with the objective of ensuring reliable information access at scale. Covibot was implemented as an informational chatbot connected to official health guidance and FAQs, accessible online to answer pandemic-related questions. The system enabled rapid updates as regulations changed and reduced pressure on human-operated channels. While no longer active, Covibot played a critical role during the emergency phase by improving information accessibility, reducing misinformation, and supporting public health communication under crisis conditions.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,NOT_IN_USE,미사용,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-681,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00659,EU_PSTW_202609_00659,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Genk,Public order and safety n.e.c.,"The system improves accuracy and objectivity in noise monitoring, reduces manual enforcement effort, and provides evidence to address resident concerns, supporting a healthier urban environment.",Belgian city uses artificial intelligence to tackle noise pollution,City of Genk; Local Government; Public order and safety n.e.c.; Belgium,"This use case involves city authorities, residents, and technology providers, with the objective of enabling data-driven noise management.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Excessive traffic noise poses health and safety risks for residents, yet traditional enforcement relies on sporadic checks and citizen complaints. Genk aimed to better quantify and address noise pollution along a busy street. This use case involves city authorities, residents, and technology providers, with the objective of enabling data-driven noise management. The solution deploys microphones and cameras as roadside sensors to measure sound levels and identify excessively noisy vehicles, combining acoustic thresholds with vehicle recognition. Data is transmitted to authorities to support informed enforcement and policy decisions. The system improves accuracy and objectivity in noise monitoring, reduces manual enforcement effort, and provides evidence to address resident concerns, supporting a healthier urban environment.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-682,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00660,EU_PSTW_202609_00660,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,National Institute for Social Security (INPS),General services.,"INPS receives millions of certified emails (PEC) each year, creating delays and inefficiencies in routing messages to the appropriate departments.",INPS project in the UNESCO World Top 10 for artificial intelligence,National Institute for Social Security (INPS); Central-Government; General services.; Italy,This use case addresses the need to streamline internal communication flows while improving response times for citizens and businesses. The system is implemented to automatically analyse the content of incoming PEC messages and route them to the most appropriate specialised operators within INPS.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"INPS receives millions of certified emails (PEC) each year, creating delays and inefficiencies in routing messages to the appropriate departments. This use case addresses the need to streamline internal communication flows while improving response times for citizens and businesses. It involves INPS, its staff, and service users, with the objective of optimising message handling and operator workload. The system is implemented to automatically analyse the content of incoming PEC messages and route them to the most appropriate specialised operators within INPS. Integrated with existing communication and case management systems, it enables faster triage and prioritisation. The solution significantly reduces processing times, improves consistency in responses, and allows staff to focus on higher-value activities. Its recognition by UNESCO reflects its impact on efficiency, scalability, and service quality in large-scale public administration.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-683,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00661,EU_PSTW_202609_00661,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""social_nonprofit""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Jawor,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.","Traditional paper-based or in-person voting methods are costly, slow, and limit participation, particularly for digitally active citizens.",iVoting in Poland - A cyberdemocracy tool,"Jawor; Local Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Poland","This use case addresses these challenges by enabling municipalities to conduct surveys, polls, civic budget voting, and statutory civic initiatives online, involving local administrations, elected bodies, and citizens, with the objective of strengthening participation, trust, and efficiency in local democracy. The solution is implemented through a secure digital voting platform that creates electronic citizen identities using advanced cryptography and distributed ledger technology.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Local governments increasingly seek secure, transparent, and inclusive ways to engage citizens in decision-making processes such as participatory budgeting, public consultations, petitions, and internal council voting. Traditional paper-based or in-person voting methods are costly, slow, and limit participation, particularly for digitally active citizens. This use case addresses these challenges by enabling municipalities to conduct surveys, polls, civic budget voting, and statutory civic initiatives online, involving local administrations, elected bodies, and citizens, with the objective of strengthening participation, trust, and efficiency in local democracy. The solution is implemented through a secure digital voting platform that creates electronic citizen identities using advanced cryptography and distributed ledger technology. Multi-level authentication allows citizens to vote via computers or smartphones, while decentralised notary servers validate and record voting events, ensuring transparency, auditability, and resistance to manipulation. Voting protocols support both secret and public ballots, depending on the use case, and integrate with municipal applications. The platform increases participation rates, reduces administrative costs, and accelerates decision-making processes. It enhances transparency and trust in civic processes, supports scalable deployment across municipalities, and provides a robust digital foundation for modern, participatory local governance.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-684,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00662,EU_PSTW_202609_00662,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,University of Trento,General services.,"The rapid spread of manipulated and deceptive images on social media has created significant challenges for journalists, platforms, institutions, and citizens seeking to assess the authenticity and provenance of digital content. Traditional verification methods struggle to keep pace with the volume and sophistication of falsified imagery, undermining trust in online information.",TrueBee (Digital forensics and blockchain technology against deceptive deepfake imaging in social media),University of Trento; Academic-Research; General services.; Italy,"This use case addresses these challenges by supporting the identification, classification, and long-term traceability of manipulated images, involving researchers, media professionals, and technology providers, with the objective of enabling reliable verification and increasing transparency in digital media ecosystems.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The rapid spread of manipulated and deceptive images on social media has created significant challenges for journalists, platforms, institutions, and citizens seeking to assess the authenticity and provenance of digital content. Traditional verification methods struggle to keep pace with the volume and sophistication of falsified imagery, undermining trust in online information. This use case addresses these challenges by supporting the identification, classification, and long-term traceability of manipulated images, involving researchers, media professionals, and technology providers, with the objective of enabling reliable verification and increasing transparency in digital media ecosystems. The project combines image analysis techniques for recognising and classifying falsified or manipulated images with a distributed ledger system that records classification results in an immutable register. Each analysed image is processed to detect signs of manipulation and assigned a verification outcome, which is then permanently stored to preserve the history, provenance, and processing chain of the multimedia content. The register is made accessible for consultation, allowing users to verify whether an image has been assessed and to review its authenticity status. The approach strengthens the reliability of digital forensics by ensuring traceability and tamper resistance, improves confidence in media verification processes, and supports the development of trustworthy tools to combat misinformation, contributing to greater transparency and accountability in online content sharing.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-685,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00663,EU_PSTW_202609_00663,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Cinisello Balsamo,Family and children.,"Access to early childhood education can be limited by administrative complexity and financial barriers, particularly for low-income families. The objective is to ensure equitable access to childcare services while reducing administrative burden and misuse of public funds.",Nidi Gratis - Public Service for Free Nursery,Municipality of Cinisello Balsamo; Local Government; Family and children.; Italy,"This use case addresses the challenge of managing free nursery provision by simplifying access to benefits and improving oversight, involving regional authorities, municipalities, service providers, and families. The service is implemented through a digital subsidy management system based on distributed ledger technology, where eligibility criteria, entitlements, and service usage are securely recorded.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Access to early childhood education can be limited by administrative complexity and financial barriers, particularly for low-income families. Public authorities must ensure that subsidies for nursery services are distributed fairly, efficiently, and transparently, while coordinating multiple stakeholders. This use case addresses the challenge of managing free nursery provision by simplifying access to benefits and improving oversight, involving regional authorities, municipalities, service providers, and families. The objective is to ensure equitable access to childcare services while reducing administrative burden and misuse of public funds. The service is implemented through a digital subsidy management system based on distributed ledger technology, where eligibility criteria, entitlements, and service usage are securely recorded. Transactions between families and authorised nursery providers are validated automatically, ensuring that public funding is used exclusively for eligible services. The system integrates with regional administrative platforms and service registries. The solution improves transparency and traceability of subsidies, reduces processing times, and limits errors or fraud. It enhances user experience for families and providers, supports scalable public service delivery, and strengthens trust in the management of social welfare programmes.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-686,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00664,EU_PSTW_202609_00664,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""social_nonprofit""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Regione Lombardia,"Agriculture, forestry, fishing and hunting","Regional authorities and industry stakeholders face difficulties in reliably tracking products across complex, multi-actor supply chains, particularly when data is fragmented or vulnerable to tampering.",Lombardia supply chain traceability system,"Regione Lombardia; Regional Government; Agriculture, forestry, fishing and hunting; Italy","This use case addresses these challenges by supporting the Lombardy Region’s strategic objective to modernise agri-food traceability systems, involving public authorities, producers, and supply chain operators, with the objective of improving traceability, accountability, and trust throughout the food lifecycle.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Food supply chains of animal origin require high levels of transparency and traceability to ensure food safety, regulatory compliance, and consumer trust. Regional authorities and industry stakeholders face difficulties in reliably tracking products across complex, multi-actor supply chains, particularly when data is fragmented or vulnerable to tampering. This use case addresses these challenges by supporting the Lombardy Region’s strategic objective to modernise agri-food traceability systems, involving public authorities, producers, and supply chain operators, with the objective of improving traceability, accountability, and trust throughout the food lifecycle. The experiment implements a digital traceability system based on distributed ledger technology to record and share information on the origin, processing, and movement of food products of animal origin. Data generated at different stages of the supply chain is securely recorded in a shared, tamper-resistant register, enabling authorised stakeholders to access consistent and verifiable information. The system is designed to integrate with existing supply chain and regulatory processes, supporting interoperability and gradual adoption. The initiative improves transparency and reliability in food traceability, reduces the risk of fraud and data manipulation, and strengthens consumer confidence, while providing a scalable foundation for innovation in agri-food systems and supporting the region’s broader strategy for research, innovation, and digital transformation.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-687,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00665,EU_PSTW_202609_00665,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,CSI Piemonte (Inhouse ICT of Regione Piemonte - Italy),"Agriculture, forestry, fishing and hunting",UNKNOWN,Project PININ – Blockchain-Based Traceability for Agri-Food Supply Chains,"CSI Piemonte (Inhouse ICT of Regione Piemonte - Italy); Regional Government; Agriculture, forestry, fishing and hunting; Italy",Users who are forced to use different sites or apps manually inputting the product lot code.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The project aims to build a distributed and decentralized infrastructure based on blockchain for agri-food products tracking throughout the whole supply chain. CSI is one of the partner, together with the University of Turin and Consoft Sistemi. Currently, the automated traceability data collection systems adopted by agri-food companies are different and not homogeneous. There are many applications that penalize the supply chain efficiency, the traced data accuracy and the individual user experience. Users who are forced to use different sites or apps manually inputting the product lot code.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-688,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00666,EU_PSTW_202609_00666,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,CSI Piemonte (Inhouse ICT of Regione Piemonte - Italy),General services.,"Migrants and refugees often interact with multiple public and private services during their integration journey, facing fragmented documentation processes, limited portability of credentials, and difficulties in managing personal records securely. These challenges can slow access to services, employment, housing, and social inclusion opportunities.",BLINC: BLockchain INclusive for digital Citizenships,CSI Piemonte (Inhouse ICT of Regione Piemonte - Italy); Regional Government; General services.; Italy,"This use case addresses these issues by supporting immigrants with a digital document wallet that brings together self-generated certificates and documents issued by public administrations and private service providers, involving migrants, public authorities, and service organisations, with the objective of simplifying document management, strengthening trust, and accelerating social inclusion. The solution is implemented through a mobile-first digital wallet based on distributed ledger technology, where credentials and documents are securely stored and linked to the individual.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Migrants and refugees often interact with multiple public and private services during their integration journey, facing fragmented documentation processes, limited portability of credentials, and difficulties in managing personal records securely. These challenges can slow access to services, employment, housing, and social inclusion opportunities. This use case addresses these issues by supporting immigrants with a digital document wallet that brings together self-generated certificates and documents issued by public administrations and private service providers, involving migrants, public authorities, and service organisations, with the objective of simplifying document management, strengthening trust, and accelerating social inclusion. The solution is implemented through a mobile-first digital wallet based on distributed ledger technology, where credentials and documents are securely stored and linked to the individual. The system supports granular privacy controls, enabling users to selectively share specific information with authorised parties, while recording verifiable traces of interactions and certifications that can support access to services or hospitality initiatives. The wallet is accessible from mobile devices and other connected platforms, ensuring flexibility and continuity of access. The approach improves usability and trust in digital identity management, reduces administrative friction, and empowers users with greater control over their personal data, contributing to faster integration pathways and more inclusive digital public services.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-689,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00667,EU_PSTW_202609_00667,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Ministry for Education and Employment,Education n.e.c.,"Verifying academic qualifications across institutions and borders is often slow, costly, and prone to fraud, creating challenges for graduates, employers, and education authorities. The service improves efficiency by eliminating manual verification processes, reduces fraud, and empowers citizens with ownership of their credentials.",Blockcerts academic credentials,Ministry for Education and Employment; Central-Government; Education n.e.c.; Malta,"This use case involves the Ministry for Education and Employment, educational institutions, students, and employers, with the objective of providing trustworthy, portable, and easily verifiable academic records. The solution is implemented using the Blockcerts open standard, which covers the full lifecycle of academic credentials, from issuance to verification.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Verifying academic qualifications across institutions and borders is often slow, costly, and prone to fraud, creating challenges for graduates, employers, and education authorities. The Maltese government sought to modernise credential verification by enabling citizens to securely prove their educational achievements. This use case involves the Ministry for Education and Employment, educational institutions, students, and employers, with the objective of providing trustworthy, portable, and easily verifiable academic records. The solution is implemented using the Blockcerts open standard, which covers the full lifecycle of academic credentials, from issuance to verification. Certificates are digitally issued by authorised institutions, anchored on a blockchain infrastructure, and stored under the control of the individual. Verification can be performed independently by third parties without contacting the issuing institution. The service improves efficiency by eliminating manual verification processes, reduces fraud, and empowers citizens with ownership of their credentials. It supports international recognition of qualifications, enhances labour market mobility, and delivers long-term value through a scalable, standards-based digital education infrastructure.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-690,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00668,EU_PSTW_202609_00668,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Mapping, Cadaster and Land Registration Authority",General services.,"Traditional real estate transaction processes are often slow, costly, and exposed to risks such as disputes over ownership and delays in settlement.",The Land Registry in the blockchain - testbed,"Mapping, Cadaster and Land Registration Authority; Central-Government; General services.; Sweden","This use case addresses these challenges by rethinking how property transfers and mortgage deeds are recorded and executed in Sweden, involving the national land registry, financial institutions, technology providers, and telecom operators, with the objective of increasing trust, reducing transaction times, and lowering operational risk. The testbed is implemented by recording property transactions and related documents on a distributed ledger shared among authorised stakeholders, enabling secure, tamper-resistant registration of ownership changes and mortgage agreements.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Property transactions involve high financial value and legal complexity, making security, transparency, and trust critical for buyers, sellers, banks, and public authorities. Traditional real estate transaction processes are often slow, costly, and exposed to risks such as disputes over ownership and delays in settlement. This use case addresses these challenges by rethinking how property transfers and mortgage deeds are recorded and executed in Sweden, involving the national land registry, financial institutions, technology providers, and telecom operators, with the objective of increasing trust, reducing transaction times, and lowering operational risk. The testbed is implemented by recording property transactions and related documents on a distributed ledger shared among authorised stakeholders, enabling secure, tamper-resistant registration of ownership changes and mortgage agreements. Smart contract mechanisms support the automation of transaction steps, while integration with existing digital land registry systems ensures legal validity and continuity with established processes. The initiative demonstrates the potential to significantly speed up settlements, reduce administrative costs, and minimise disputes, while improving transparency and reliability in real estate transactions and laying the groundwork for scalable digital transformation of land administration.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-691,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00669,EU_PSTW_202609_00669,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Zug,General services.,UNKNOWN,Veramo: decentralised identity,Municipality of Zug; Local Government; General services.; Switzerland,"This use case addresses these needs through the development of a decentralised digital identity framework initially adopted by the City of Zug, involving public authorities, citizens, and technology developers, with the objective of enabling trusted authentication for e-government services and selective data sharing under citizen control. The solution is implemented through a decentralised identity infrastructure built on distributed ledger technology, where individuals manage their own identifiers and credentials without reliance on a central authority.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Governments increasingly require secure and user-centric digital identity solutions to enable access to online public services while protecting personal data. Citizens also seek greater control over how their identity information is shared with public and private organisations. This use case addresses these needs through the development of a decentralised digital identity framework initially adopted by the City of Zug, involving public authorities, citizens, and technology developers, with the objective of enabling trusted authentication for e-government services and selective data sharing under citizen control. The solution is implemented through a decentralised identity infrastructure built on distributed ledger technology, where individuals manage their own identifiers and credentials without reliance on a central authority. The system enables selective disclosure, allowing users to share only specific attributes with selected organisations, while cryptographic mechanisms ensure integrity and authenticity. The evolution of the initiative into open-source projects such as Veramo supports broader interoperability and adoption across different services and platforms. The approach strengthens privacy, increases trust in digital interactions, and empowers citizens with ownership of their personal data, supporting more secure, flexible, and scalable digital identity ecosystems.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-692,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00670,EU_PSTW_202609_00670,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""social_nonprofit"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Infrachain,General services.,"Educational mobility and cross-border employment require high levels of trust in academic qualifications, yet verification processes remain fragmented across countries and institutions.",EBSILUX - Infrachain Project for digital academic diplomas in Luxembourg,Infrachain; Non-governmental; General services.; Luxembourg,"This use case involves schools, universities, students, employers, and public authorities, with the objective of delivering secure, cross-border digital academic certificates. The solution is implemented by integrating Luxembourg into the European Blockchain Services Infrastructure (EBSI) for the Diplomas use case.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Educational mobility and cross-border employment require high levels of trust in academic qualifications, yet verification processes remain fragmented across countries and institutions. Luxembourg aimed to align national diploma systems with European infrastructure to support transparency and interoperability. This use case involves schools, universities, students, employers, and public authorities, with the objective of delivering secure, cross-border digital academic certificates. The solution is implemented by integrating Luxembourg into the European Blockchain Services Infrastructure (EBSI) for the Diplomas use case. Academic credentials are issued and verified through a permissioned blockchain governed by Infrachain’s framework, which ensures privacy, cybersecurity, legal compliance, and business continuity. Independent nodes operate the infrastructure, separating network and service layers to support multiple public and private services. The system improves trust and efficiency in diploma verification, reduces administrative costs, and supports seamless cross-border recognition of qualifications. It strengthens European digital sovereignty, enhances labour mobility, and provides a robust, interoperable foundation for digital education services.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-693,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00671,EU_PSTW_202609_00671,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,APG (Algemene Pensioen Groep),Old age.,"Pension administration involves complex transactions, multiple stakeholders, and high operational costs, often lacking transparency for citizens.",Pension infrastructure,APG (Algemene Pensioen Groep); Central-Government; Old age.; Netherlands,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Pension administration involves complex transactions, multiple stakeholders, and high operational costs, often lacking transparency for citizens. A pilot pension infrastructure project explored whether a shared digital ledger could modernise pension administration. Stakeholders included pension funds, administrators, employers, and citizens, with the objective of increasing transparency while reducing costs. Conducted between 2016 and 2018, the pilot used blockchain technology to manage individual pension balances and transactions. Each participant held a personal account, with contributions and entitlements recorded as immutable transactions. Smart contracts governed rules for updates and transfers, integrating with existing pension systems. The pilot demonstrated improved transparency, traceability, and administrative efficiency. It reduced reconciliation efforts and highlighted potential long-term cost savings, while offering citizens clearer insight into their pension status.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-694,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00672,EU_PSTW_202609_00672,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Groningen,Social protection n.e.c.,"Municipalities must ensure that social benefits reach intended recipients and are spent on approved purposes, yet paper-based voucher systems are inefficient and difficult to control.",Stadjerspas smart vouchers,Municipality of Groningen; Local Government; Social protection n.e.c.; Netherlands,"This use case involves the municipality, beneficiaries, and authorised service providers, with the objective of improving targeting, transparency, and efficiency of public spending. The solution is implemented through a blockchain-based smart voucher system where eligibility criteria, spending limits, and authorised providers are encoded in smart contracts.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Municipalities must ensure that social benefits reach intended recipients and are spent on approved purposes, yet paper-based voucher systems are inefficient and difficult to control. The Municipality of Groningen sought to modernise its discount scheme for low-income citizens, known as Stadjerspas. This use case involves the municipality, beneficiaries, and authorised service providers, with the objective of improving targeting, transparency, and efficiency of public spending. The solution is implemented through a blockchain-based smart voucher system where eligibility criteria, spending limits, and authorised providers are encoded in smart contracts. Vouchers are issued digitally and can be redeemed for specific services such as sports, cultural activities, or energy-related investments. Transactions are automatically validated and recorded, ensuring compliance with programme rules. The system reduces administrative overhead, prevents misuse of funds, and provides real-time oversight for the municipality. It improves user experience for citizens and providers, enhances accountability, and delivers a scalable model for targeted social support.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-695,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00673,EU_PSTW_202609_00673,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Vienna,General services.,UNKNOWN,Blockchain pilot - Open Data notarization & change protocol,City of Vienna; Local Government; General services.; Austria,"This use case involves municipal authorities, data users, and federal partners, with the objective of guaranteeing integrity and traceability of public datasets. The solution is implemented through blockchain-based notarisation, where cryptographic fingerprints of open data resources are anchored on public blockchains.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Public administrations publish large volumes of open data that must remain accurate, authentic, and tamper-proof to maintain public trust and support reuse. The City of Vienna addressed the risk of data manipulation and version ambiguity by securing its open data infrastructure. This use case involves municipal authorities, data users, and federal partners, with the objective of guaranteeing integrity and traceability of public datasets. The solution is implemented through blockchain-based notarisation, where cryptographic fingerprints of open data resources are anchored on public blockchains. Nearly 4,000 datasets already include verifiable identifiers, and the approach is being extended nationally through the BLOCKSTEMPEL service in cooperation with the Austrian Federal Computing Centre. The system ensures that any alteration to published data can be detected and verified. It strengthens trust in open government data, reduces disputes over data authenticity, and supports scalable, reusable digital public services across Austria.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-696,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00674,EU_PSTW_202609_00674,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Vienna,Social protection n.e.c.,"Impact and Results: The pilot reduced manual processing, improved traceability, and demonstrated scalability for up to 20,000 employees, supporting future roll-out decisions.",Blockchain pilot - Digital food voucher,City of Vienna; Local Government; Social protection n.e.c.; Austria,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The City of Vienna sought to replace paper-based food vouchers for employees with a more efficient and transparent system. Stakeholders included city employees, participating vendors, and municipal administrators, with the objective of reducing administrative overhead and fraud. Emerging Technology Implementation: The 2017–2018 pilot issued digital food tokens to employees’ wallets, redeemable at authorised vendors. Transactions were recorded on a blockchain, enabling automatic settlement and auditability. Impact and Results: The pilot reduced manual processing, improved traceability, and demonstrated scalability for up to 20,000 employees, supporting future roll-out decisions.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-697,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00675,EU_PSTW_202609_00675,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Federal Government's Finance Agency (OeBFA),"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.",UNKNOWN,"Austrian Federal Government's Finance Agency, Oesterreichische Kontrollbank","Federal Government's Finance Agency (OeBFA); Central-Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Austria","This use case addresses these challenges by supporting the Austrian Federal Government’s Finance Agency in notarising a large-scale bond auction, involving the agency, the Oesterreichische Kontrollbank, and market participants, with the objective of strengthening transparency and reliability in sovereign debt issuance. The solution is implemented by recording key steps and outcomes of a bond auction worth approximately $1.3 billion on a distributed ledger, creating an immutable and verifiable record of the process.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Public debt auctions require high levels of transparency, integrity, and auditability to maintain market confidence and ensure trust in government financial operations. Traditional notarisation and record-keeping processes can be complex and rely on multiple intermediaries, creating potential inefficiencies and risks. This use case addresses these challenges by supporting the Austrian Federal Government’s Finance Agency in notarising a large-scale bond auction, involving the agency, the Oesterreichische Kontrollbank, and market participants, with the objective of strengthening transparency and reliability in sovereign debt issuance. The solution is implemented by recording key steps and outcomes of a bond auction worth approximately $1.3 billion on a distributed ledger, creating an immutable and verifiable record of the process. The system complements existing financial and settlement infrastructures, providing cryptographic proof of integrity and timing without disrupting established market operations. The initiative enhances trust and auditability in public financial management, demonstrates the feasibility of applying distributed ledger technology to capital markets, and supports more transparent and efficient government finance processes.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-698,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00676,EU_PSTW_202609_00676,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Smals Research,General services.,UNKNOWN,BeSure - Demonstrability Service,Smals Research; Central-Government; General services.; Belgium,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Belgian public administrations needed a reliable way to prove that digital documents had been sent and received via eBoxes without relying on central intermediaries. Stakeholders included citizens, public bodies, and legal authorities. Emerging Technology Implementation: From 2017 to 2019, a blockchain-based proof service generated immutable timestamps and receipt proofs linked to document exchanges. Impact and Results: The pilot enhanced legal certainty, security, and trust while meeting strict privacy requirements and reducing dependency on central systems.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-699,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00677,EU_PSTW_202609_00677,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Belfius Bank & Insurance,Public order and safety n.e.c.,FlowBikes addresses this challenge by encouraging active mobility among schoolchildren.,FlowBikes - Supporting cities and schools to promote sustainable mobility,Belfius Bank & Insurance; Local Government; Public order and safety n.e.c.; Belgium,"The use case involves local governments, schools, families, and private partners, with the objective of promoting walking and cycling to school while improving safety. The solution is implemented through a digital reward system that tracks children’s journeys along predefined safe routes and issues tokens for active travel.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Traffic congestion and road safety risks around schools are often driven by car-based commuting, affecting children’s safety and urban sustainability. FlowBikes addresses this challenge by encouraging active mobility among schoolchildren. The use case involves local governments, schools, families, and private partners, with the objective of promoting walking and cycling to school while improving safety. The solution is implemented through a digital reward system that tracks children’s journeys along predefined safe routes and issues tokens for active travel. These tokens can be redeemed at local shops or community events, creating positive reinforcement for sustainable behaviour. The system integrates route guidance, participation tracking, and reward management on a blockchain infrastructure. The initiative reduces car traffic near schools, improves safety, supports healthier lifestyles, and strengthens local economies. It provides a scalable model for behaviour-based mobility policies.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-700,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00678,EU_PSTW_202609_00678,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,E-Estonia,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.",Ensuring high participation and trust in democratic processes is a core challenge for modern societies.,i-Voting in Estonia,"E-Estonia; Central-Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Estonia","This use case involves the national electoral authorities, voters, and technology providers, with the objective of increasing accessibility and participation in elections. The system allows voters to cast ballots online during a pre-voting period using national digital identity tools.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Ensuring high participation and trust in democratic processes is a core challenge for modern societies. Estonia addressed this by enabling citizens to vote securely online. This use case involves the national electoral authorities, voters, and technology providers, with the objective of increasing accessibility and participation in elections. The system allows voters to cast ballots online during a pre-voting period using national digital identity tools. Voter identity is verified for eligibility, then cryptographically separated from the ballot to ensure anonymity before counting. The solution integrates with national electoral infrastructure and has been used in local, parliamentary, and European elections. It increases voter turnout, reduces logistical costs, and enables participation from anywhere in the world. Estonia’s i-Voting system demonstrates a mature, scalable model for secure digital democracy.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-703,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00679,EU_PSTW_202609_00679,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,E-Estonia,Public health services.,"Healthcare delivery requires timely access to accurate patient information across providers, yet fragmented systems hinder continuity of care.",e-Health Records in Estonia,E-Estonia; Central-Government; Public health services.; Estonia,"The use case involves healthcare providers, patients, and national authorities, with the objective of enabling secure, unified access to medical records. Integrity and access logs are secured using blockchain-based mechanisms to ensure records cannot be altered without detection.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Healthcare delivery requires timely access to accurate patient information across providers, yet fragmented systems hinder continuity of care. Estonia addressed this by creating a nationwide electronic health record system. The use case involves healthcare providers, patients, and national authorities, with the objective of enabling secure, unified access to medical records. The system aggregates data from multiple provider systems and presents it in a standardised format via a national patient portal. Integrity and access logs are secured using blockchain-based mechanisms to ensure records cannot be altered without detection. The solution improves clinical decision-making, reduces duplication of tests, and empowers patients with access to their data. It enhances efficiency, safety, and trust in national healthcare services.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-704,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00680,EU_PSTW_202609_00680,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,E-Estonia,General public services n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,Blockchain-based Keyless Signature Infrastructure (KSI),E-Estonia; Central-Government; General public services n.e.c.; Estonia,"This use case involves public institutions and infrastructure operators, with the objective of guaranteeing that digital records remain authentic and untampered. KSI uses a distributed ledger to cryptographically seal data and system logs, enabling mathematical proof of integrity without revealing content.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Governments must protect critical systems and data against both external attacks and internal manipulation. Estonia developed KSI to ensure data integrity across government networks. This use case involves public institutions and infrastructure operators, with the objective of guaranteeing that digital records remain authentic and untampered. KSI uses a distributed ledger to cryptographically seal data and system logs, enabling mathematical proof of integrity without revealing content. The system integrates with existing IT infrastructures and operates at national scale. It prevents undetected data manipulation, strengthens cybersecurity resilience, and provides long-term trust in digital government systems, delivering strong business and societal value through reliable, verifiable data integrity.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-705,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00681,EU_PSTW_202609_00681,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Federal office for Migration and Refugees,Social protection n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,FLORA – The federal asylum blockchain infrastructure,Federal office for Migration and Refugees; Central-Government; Social protection n.e.c.; Germany,The solution is implemented through a blockchain-based infrastructure where participating authorities deploy their process logic and share verified case data on a distributed ledger.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"European asylum procedures involve multiple national authorities, high volumes of documentation, and frequent data handovers, which can lead to long processing times, errors, and inconsistencies. Germany’s Federal Office for Migration and Refugees, together with research institutions and technology partners, sought to modernise asylum case management and improve cross-border coordination. The objective of FLORA is to support faster, more reliable asylum procedures by enabling secure information sharing across organisations and countries, while respecting national responsibilities. The solution is implemented through a blockchain-based infrastructure where participating authorities deploy their process logic and share verified case data on a distributed ledger. Each step of the asylum process is recorded immutably, enabling traceability, reducing duplication, and ensuring data integrity across organisational boundaries. The system is designed to be federal and nation-agnostic, supporting a European ecosystem rather than a single central database. FLORA reduces error rates, shortens processing times, and improves transparency between stakeholders. It strengthens trust, supports scalability across Member States, and delivers long-term value by enabling more efficient and coordinated asylum management in Europe.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-706,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00682,EU_PSTW_202609_00682,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""social_nonprofit""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Isle of Man,General services.,"The rapid growth of the Internet of Things has introduced challenges related to security, identity management, and trust between connected devices, particularly as systems scale across sectors and jurisdictions.",Securing the Internet of Things,Isle of Man; Regional Government; General services.; United Kingdom,"This use case addresses these challenges through a partnership between the Isle of Man government and a technology provider, involving public authorities, developers, and IoT stakeholders, with the objective of strengthening the security and manageability of connected devices. The solution is implemented using a distributed ledger to assign and manage unique digital identities for physical devices, enabling secure registration, authentication, and lifecycle tracking.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The rapid growth of the Internet of Things has introduced challenges related to security, identity management, and trust between connected devices, particularly as systems scale across sectors and jurisdictions. Physical devices require reliable, non-forgeable digital identities to ensure secure communication and interoperability. This use case addresses these challenges through a partnership between the Isle of Man government and a technology provider, involving public authorities, developers, and IoT stakeholders, with the objective of strengthening the security and manageability of connected devices. The solution is implemented using a distributed ledger to assign and manage unique digital identities for physical devices, enabling secure registration, authentication, and lifecycle tracking. The ledger maintains a trusted record of device identities and interactions, supporting scalable and interoperable IoT applications across different use cases. The approach improves trust, security, and resilience in IoT ecosystems, reduces the risk of identity spoofing and tampering, and provides a foundation for the safe deployment of large-scale connected services in both public and private sectors.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-707,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00683,EU_PSTW_202609_00683,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Isle of Man,General services.,The Isle of Man Government’s Department of Economic Development identified the rapid growth of digital currency businesses as both an opportunity and a regulatory challenge.,Digital Currency Business Register,Isle of Man; Regional Government; General services.; United Kingdom,"The register is designed using distributed ledger technology to ensure data integrity, immutability and traceability. Smart contract mechanisms can automate aspects of registration updates and compliance checks.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Isle of Man Government’s Department of Economic Development identified the rapid growth of digital currency businesses as both an opportunity and a regulatory challenge. To support innovation while ensuring transparency and oversight, the government proposed the creation of a dedicated register for island-based digital currency enterprises. The objective was to establish a structured, verifiable record of companies operating within the digital asset ecosystem, enhancing trust among investors, regulators and international partners. Stakeholders include digital currency service providers, financial regulators, investors and economic development authorities. The register is designed using distributed ledger technology to ensure data integrity, immutability and traceability. Blockchain protocols record registration details in a tamper-resistant ledger, enabling secure validation of business credentials and regulatory status. Smart contract mechanisms can automate aspects of registration updates and compliance checks. The system interfaces with existing corporate registry processes while maintaining decentralised verification features. The anticipated impact includes increased transparency, improved regulatory confidence and strengthened reputation of the jurisdiction as a secure and forward-looking fintech hub. By providing a reliable digital infrastructure for registration, the initiative supports innovation, reduces administrative friction and enhances international competitiveness in the digital currency sector.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-708,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00684,EU_PSTW_202609_00684,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Italian Istituto Poligrafico e Zecca dello Stato,"General economic, commercial and labour affair","Italian producers of PDO and PGI products face increasing challenges in protecting high-value agri-food supply chains from counterfeiting, fraud and reputational damage.",Traceability of Italian food products,"Italian Istituto Poligrafico e Zecca dello Stato; Central-Government; General economic, commercial and labour affair; Italy",UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Italian producers of PDO and PGI products face increasing challenges in protecting high-value agri-food supply chains from counterfeiting, fraud and reputational damage. Ensuring transparent traceability from production to distribution is essential to safeguard product authenticity and maintain consumer trust. To address this need, the Poligrafico e Zecca dello Stato and CSQA Certificazioni entered into a collaboration to strengthen traceability mechanisms and improve communication with consumers. The objective is to provide sector operators and citizens with reliable verification tools that certify origin, quality standards and compliance with protected designation frameworks. The initiative combines secure identification technologies with digital traceability systems. Unique product identifiers and secure labelling solutions are linked to digital records stored in interoperable databases, potentially supported by distributed ledger infrastructures to ensure tamper-resistant data recording. Consumers can access product provenance information through digital interfaces, while certification bodies update compliance data along the supply chain. Integration with quality assurance systems enables end-to-end tracking of production batches. The impact includes enhanced protection against fraud, increased transparency across supply chains and strengthened market value of certified Italian products. Producers benefit from improved brand protection, while consumers gain greater confidence in product authenticity and traceable origin.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-709,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00685,EU_PSTW_202609_00685,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Agenzia delle dogane e dei Monopoli,Police Services,UNKNOWN,Customs 4.0: innovation in the Customs Management,Agenzia delle dogane e dei Monopoli; Central-Government; Police Services; Italy,"This use case addresses these objectives through a pilot project focused on the production of Genepì, a traditional herbal liqueur from Piedmont, involving the Customs Agency, producers, consumers, and the technology partner Sogei, with the aim of demonstrating how innovative digital tools can enhance customs management, product traceability, and consumer information. The solution is implemented by digitally replicating all stages of the Genepì production and bottling process on a distributed ledger built on the Ethereum protocol, using open-source components.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Customs administrations face increasing pressure to manage complex supply chains, ensure regulatory compliance, and provide transparency to businesses and consumers while transitioning from paper-based processes to fully digital operations. In Italy, the Customs Agency (Agenzia delle Entrate) has pursued a progressive transformation towards an eCustoms and Customs 4.0 model, seeking to improve traceability, control, and trust across goods movements and production chains. This use case addresses these objectives through a pilot project focused on the production of Genepì, a traditional herbal liqueur from Piedmont, involving the Customs Agency, producers, consumers, and the technology partner Sogei, with the aim of demonstrating how innovative digital tools can enhance customs management, product traceability, and consumer information. The solution is implemented by digitally replicating all stages of the Genepì production and bottling process on a distributed ledger built on the Ethereum protocol, using open-source components. Data related to origin, processing, and bottling is securely recorded and made accessible through a QR code on the product, allowing consumers and authorities to verify information, while the architecture is designed to be extended to include transport and logistics phases in the future. The project improves transparency, data integrity, and trust along the supply chain, reduces administrative complexity, and strengthens the Customs Agency’s ability to monitor and certify goods, while offering consumers greater visibility into product provenance and providing a scalable model for innovation in customs and trade management.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-710,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00686,EU_PSTW_202609_00686,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Ministry of Agricultural, Food and Forestry Policies","General economic, commercial and labour affair",UNKNOWN,Food fraud and falsification for Made in Italy products,"Ministry of Agricultural, Food and Forestry Policies; Central-Government; General economic, commercial and labour affair; Italy","This use case addresses these challenges by protecting the integrity of Made in Italy wines, involving the Ministry of Agriculture, public agencies, producers, and consumers. The objective is to ensure traceability and prevent fraud using authoritative public data.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Food counterfeiting poses serious risks to consumers and causes significant economic losses for producers, particularly in high-value sectors such as Italian wine. Public authorities and industry stakeholders require reliable tools to guarantee product authenticity across complex supply chains. This use case addresses these challenges by protecting the integrity of Made in Italy wines, involving the Ministry of Agriculture, public agencies, producers, and consumers. The objective is to ensure traceability and prevent fraud using authoritative public data. The solution is implemented through a blockchain-based traceability platform developed by Almaviva, integrating official data from the National Agricultural Information System (SIAN). Supply chain events are recorded on a public blockchain and linked to physical products via technologies such as NFC tags, allowing consumers and authorities to verify provenance and history. By leveraging ministerial datasets already held by public administration, the system ensures accuracy and trustworthiness. The platform improves consumer confidence, reduces counterfeiting, and protects brand value. It enhances transparency, supports enforcement, and delivers economic benefits by safeguarding Italy’s agri-food excellence.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-711,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00687,EU_PSTW_202609_00687,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Ministry of Interior Affairs,General services.,UNKNOWN,Decentralised Digital Identity Proof of Concept,Ministry of Interior Affairs; Central-Government; General services.; Netherlands,"This use case addresses these challenges through a proof of concept for decentralised digital identity, involving citizens, retailers, and public authorities, with the objective of enabling selective disclosure of personal attributes while maintaining individual control over personal data. The solution is implemented through a digital identity framework supported by distributed ledger technology, where verifiable credentials are issued and managed by trusted parties and stored under the control of the individual.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Citizens increasingly require secure and privacy-preserving ways to prove specific personal attributes without disclosing unnecessary personal information, particularly in everyday transactions such as purchasing age-restricted goods. Traditional identity documents expose far more data than required, raising privacy and data protection concerns for individuals and businesses alike. This use case addresses these challenges through a proof of concept for decentralised digital identity, involving citizens, retailers, and public authorities, with the objective of enabling selective disclosure of personal attributes while maintaining individual control over personal data. The solution is implemented through a digital identity framework supported by distributed ledger technology, where verifiable credentials are issued and managed by trusted parties and stored under the control of the individual. The system supports context-specific disclosure, allowing users to prove, for example, that they are over 18 without revealing their full identity, while the ledger ensures integrity and trust in the verification process. The approach improves privacy protection, reduces data exposure for businesses, and enhances user trust, while demonstrating how digital identity solutions can support compliant, scalable, and citizen-centric service delivery.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-712,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00688,EU_PSTW_202609_00688,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Ministry of Justice,General services.,UNKNOWN,Execution of a judicial decision of juvenile court,Ministry of Justice; Central-Government; General services.; Netherlands,"This use case explores how the registration of completed labour could be improved, involving juvenile courts, enforcement organisations, and minors, with the objective of creating a trustworthy yet proportionate record of sentence completion. The proof of concept examines the use of a distributed ledger as a logging mechanism for completed labour activities, ensuring integrity and shared visibility across authorised parties.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Juvenile justice systems aim to balance accountability with rehabilitation, particularly for minor offences where long-term consequences such as permanent criminal records are explicitly avoided. In the Netherlands, the execution of HALT sentences requires reliable confirmation that assigned labour has been completed, while ensuring that records do not undermine the rehabilitative intent of the sentence. This use case explores how the registration of completed labour could be improved, involving juvenile courts, enforcement organisations, and minors, with the objective of creating a trustworthy yet proportionate record of sentence completion. The proof of concept examines the use of a distributed ledger as a logging mechanism for completed labour activities, ensuring integrity and shared visibility across authorised parties. At the same time, the project assesses legal and ethical constraints, particularly around data permanence and the right of minors to move on without a lasting record, and explores governance and design options to address these concerns. The work provides insight into how secure digital records could improve administrative reliability and coordination, while highlighting critical legal considerations that must be resolved before operational adoption in sensitive justice contexts.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-713,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00689,EU_PSTW_202609_00689,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Human Environment and Transport Inspectorate,Waste management.,"This complexity increases the risk of delays, errors, and reduced oversight. The approach demonstrates potential reductions in administrative burden, faster processing times, and improved traceability of hazardous waste movements, while strengthening regulatory oversight and cross-border coordination.",Toxic Waste Transport,Human Environment and Transport Inspectorate; Central-Government; Waste management.; Netherlands,"This use case addresses these challenges by rethinking the end-to-end process for toxic waste transport from the Netherlands to other EU countries, involving the Human Environment and Transport Inspectorate, foreign authorities, waste producers, transport companies, and disposal facilities, with the objective of improving efficiency, transparency, and regulatory compliance. The proof of concept is implemented through a digital workflow supported by distributed ledger technology, where notifications, approvals, and status updates are shared across stakeholders through interconnected applications.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The cross-border transport of toxic waste within the European Union involves complex regulatory procedures and coordination among multiple authorities and private actors, often relying on paper-based documentation and parallel administrative systems. This complexity increases the risk of delays, errors, and reduced oversight. This use case addresses these challenges by rethinking the end-to-end process for toxic waste transport from the Netherlands to other EU countries, involving the Human Environment and Transport Inspectorate, foreign authorities, waste producers, transport companies, and disposal facilities, with the objective of improving efficiency, transparency, and regulatory compliance. The proof of concept is implemented through a digital workflow supported by distributed ledger technology, where notifications, approvals, and status updates are shared across stakeholders through interconnected applications. Smart contract mechanisms are explored to automate approval decisions based on predefined regulatory conditions, reducing manual intervention and duplication of records. The approach demonstrates potential reductions in administrative burden, faster processing times, and improved traceability of hazardous waste movements, while strengthening regulatory oversight and cross-border coordination.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F08_ERROR_COMPLIANCE_RISK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-714,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00690,EU_PSTW_202609_00690,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,CAK,Public health services.,"Healthcare delivery often involves multiple providers who must coordinate care, particularly for elderly or vulnerable patients, yet authorisations and care arrangements are frequently informal, fragmented, or poorly documented.",Public Healthcare Benefits Subsidy,CAK; Central-Government; Public health services.; Netherlands,"This use case addresses these challenges by improving the management of authorisations within healthcare processes, involving patients, healthcare providers, and the Healthcare Institute, with the objective of ensuring that the right professionals have timely access to relevant information. The pilot is implemented through a prototype built on the Ethereum platform that maintains a shared overview of care-related authorisations, accessible to providers on a need-to-know basis.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Healthcare delivery often involves multiple providers who must coordinate care, particularly for elderly or vulnerable patients, yet authorisations and care arrangements are frequently informal, fragmented, or poorly documented. This can lead to uncertainty in emergency situations and inefficiencies in care coordination. This use case addresses these challenges by improving the management of authorisations within healthcare processes, involving patients, healthcare providers, and the Healthcare Institute, with the objective of ensuring that the right professionals have timely access to relevant information. The pilot is implemented through a prototype built on the Ethereum platform that maintains a shared overview of care-related authorisations, accessible to providers on a need-to-know basis. Patients retain control over their data through an application that allows them to grant or restrict access to additional information, while the ledger ensures consistency and real-time availability of authorisation status. The solution improves coordination and continuity of care, enhances patient empowerment and data control, and increases reliability in both routine and emergency healthcare situations, demonstrating the potential for more transparent and patient-centred care processes.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-715,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00691,EU_PSTW_202609_00691,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Healthcare Institute,Public health services.,UNKNOWN,Healthcare Process Authorization,Healthcare Institute; Central-Government; Public health services.; Netherlands,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Healthcare Institute focused on authorisations in the healthcare process. The pilot project evolved around a fictional elderly person, who required care from diferent healthcare providers. Who can make arrangements when this person ends up in a (temporary) emergency situation currently is arranged in an informal way or at best registered in a notebook. Healthcare providers don’t always know each other or aren’t aware of specific arrangements. The Healthcare Institute sees Blockchain as a technology that can create a clear overview of authorisations in the healthcare process. It has developed a prototype on Ethereum, which allows involved healthcare providers to get real time information on a need to know basis about their client/patient. The client/patient has more control over his data and can determine - via an application - which professional can get access to additional information.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-716,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00692,EU_PSTW_202609_00692,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Chamber of Commerce,General services.,"Healthcare delivery often involves multiple providers who must coordinate care, particularly for elderly or vulnerable patients, yet authorisations and care arrangements are frequently informal, fragmented, or poorly documented.",FlashCompany (Chamber of Commerce),Chamber of Commerce; Central-Government; General services.; Netherlands,"This use case addresses these challenges by improving the management of authorisations within healthcare processes, involving patients, healthcare providers, and the Healthcare Institute, with the objective of ensuring that the right professionals have timely access to relevant information. The pilot is implemented through a prototype built on the Ethereum platform that maintains a shared overview of care-related authorisations, accessible to providers on a need-to-know basis.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Healthcare delivery often involves multiple providers who must coordinate care, particularly for elderly or vulnerable patients, yet authorisations and care arrangements are frequently informal, fragmented, or poorly documented. This can lead to uncertainty in emergency situations and inefficiencies in care coordination. This use case addresses these challenges by improving the management of authorisations within healthcare processes, involving patients, healthcare providers, and the Healthcare Institute, with the objective of ensuring that the right professionals have timely access to relevant information. The pilot is implemented through a prototype built on the Ethereum platform that maintains a shared overview of care-related authorisations, accessible to providers on a need-to-know basis. Patients retain control over their data through an application that allows them to grant or restrict access to additional information, while the ledger ensures consistency and real-time availability of authorisation status. The solution improves coordination and continuity of care, enhances patient empowerment and data control, and increases reliability in both routine and emergency healthcare situations, demonstrating the potential for more transparent and patient-centred care processes.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-717,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00693,EU_PSTW_202609_00693,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""social_nonprofit""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Kadaster Gemeente Eindhoven,General services.,"In Eindhoven, the municipal department and the national land registry faced challenges in guaranteeing that users always accessed the most up-to-date datasets when completing processes such as environmental law applications.",Blockchain-based Land Registry,Kadaster Gemeente Eindhoven; Central-Government; General services.; Netherlands,"This use case addresses these issues by improving the reliability and traceability of land ownership and geo-information data, involving the municipality, the Kadaster, and data users, with the objective of ensuring data integrity, transparency, and trust across administrative processes. The solution is implemented by publishing open datasets on a distributed ledger with cryptographic timestamps that clearly identify the latest valid version of each dataset.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Public authorities responsible for land and geo-information management must ensure that datasets are accurate, current, and used in the correct legal and administrative context, as outdated or misapplied data can lead to regulatory errors and disputes. In Eindhoven, the municipal department and the national land registry faced challenges in guaranteeing that users always accessed the most up-to-date datasets when completing processes such as environmental law applications. This use case addresses these issues by improving the reliability and traceability of land ownership and geo-information data, involving the municipality, the Kadaster, and data users, with the objective of ensuring data integrity, transparency, and trust across administrative processes. The solution is implemented by publishing open datasets on a distributed ledger with cryptographic timestamps that clearly identify the latest valid version of each dataset. This enables all participants to verify that the data they are using is current and authoritative, while supporting reuse across multiple applications and services. The prototype serves as an initial, low-complexity implementation to explore the feasibility of distributed ledger technology in land administration, informed by international experiments. The approach reduces the risk of errors caused by outdated information, improves confidence in shared data, and provides a foundation for more advanced applications of trusted digital land registries.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-718,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00694,EU_PSTW_202609_00694,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Human Environment and Transport Inspectorate,Public order and safety n.e.c.,"Traditional tracking and reporting methods can be fragmented, prone to manipulation, and burdensome for drivers, employers, and regulators. Time-stamped entries create an immutable log that can be accessed by authorised parties, reducing disputes and manual reconciliation.",Truck Driver Tracking,Human Environment and Transport Inspectorate; Central-Government; Public order and safety n.e.c.; Netherlands,"This use case addresses these challenges by improving the transparency and reliability of driver hours registration, involving transport companies, drivers, and supervisory authorities, with the objective of ensuring compliance while reducing administrative friction. The system is implemented through a blockchain-based register that records driving and rest periods in a shared, tamper-resistant ledger.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Road transport regulation requires accurate monitoring of truck drivers’ working and resting hours to ensure safety, compliance with labour laws, and fair competition within the logistics sector. Traditional tracking and reporting methods can be fragmented, prone to manipulation, and burdensome for drivers, employers, and regulators. This use case addresses these challenges by improving the transparency and reliability of driver hours registration, involving transport companies, drivers, and supervisory authorities, with the objective of ensuring compliance while reducing administrative friction. The system is implemented through a blockchain-based register that records driving and rest periods in a shared, tamper-resistant ledger. Time-stamped entries create an immutable log that can be accessed by authorised parties, reducing disputes and manual reconciliation. The solution improves trust in reported data, simplifies compliance checks, and enhances road safety by supporting more consistent enforcement of working time regulations.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-719,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00695,EU_PSTW_202609_00695,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Ministry of Finance,Economic affairs n.e.c,"These processes are often slow and opaque, leading to delays and coordination challenges.",Financing New School Buildings,Ministry of Finance; Central-Government; Economic affairs n.e.c; Netherlands,"This use case addresses these issues by improving the way school construction projects are financed, involving public authorities and a national banking partner, with the objective of increasing transparency, efficiency, and coordination in public infrastructure financing. The prototype is implemented on the Ethereum platform, where financial commitments, approvals, and project milestones are recorded in a shared digital environment accessible to authorised stakeholders.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The development of new school buildings involves complex financial and administrative processes that span multiple governmental bodies, financial institutions, and project stakeholders. These processes are often slow and opaque, leading to delays and coordination challenges. This use case addresses these issues by improving the way school construction projects are financed, involving public authorities and a national banking partner, with the objective of increasing transparency, efficiency, and coordination in public infrastructure financing. The prototype is implemented on the Ethereum platform, where financial commitments, approvals, and project milestones are recorded in a shared digital environment accessible to authorised stakeholders. This enables clearer tracking of responsibilities and funds across institutions and supports automation of administrative steps. The initiative demonstrates the potential to reduce processing time, lower administrative costs, and improve accountability in the financing of public education infrastructure.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-720,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00696,EU_PSTW_202609_00696,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of the Hague,General services.,"Existing subsidy processes are often manual and administratively heavy, limiting scalability and increasing the risk of errors. Automation reduces manual intervention and enables consistent enforcement of subsidy rules.",Electric Vehicle Subsidy Registry,City of the Hague; Local Government; General services.; Netherlands,"This use case addresses these challenges by redesigning the regulation and administration of electric vehicle subsidies, involving public authorities, applicants, and service providers, with the objective of delivering a highly automated and reliable subsidy system. The solution is implemented through a digital registry based on distributed ledger technology, where eligibility checks, subsidy approvals, and payments are recorded and validated in a shared system.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Governments seeking to accelerate the adoption of electric vehicles must manage subsidy schemes that are efficient, transparent, and resistant to fraud, while handling high volumes of applications and payments. Existing subsidy processes are often manual and administratively heavy, limiting scalability and increasing the risk of errors. This use case addresses these challenges by redesigning the regulation and administration of electric vehicle subsidies, involving public authorities, applicants, and service providers, with the objective of delivering a highly automated and reliable subsidy system. The solution is implemented through a digital registry based on distributed ledger technology, where eligibility checks, subsidy approvals, and payments are recorded and validated in a shared system. Automation reduces manual intervention and enables consistent enforcement of subsidy rules. The result is a more scalable and transparent subsidy programme, with faster processing times, reduced administrative burden, and increased trust in the management of public funds.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK|F08_ERROR_COMPLIANCE_RISK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-721,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00697,EU_PSTW_202609_00697,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Ministry of Health, Welfare and Sport (CIBG)",Public health services.,UNKNOWN,Medical Instrument Registration,"Ministry of Health, Welfare and Sport (CIBG); Central-Government; Public health services.; Netherlands","This use case explores whether distributed ledger technology can add value to the registration of medical instruments and related data, involving the ministry, healthcare stakeholders, and regulatory bodies, with the objective of enhancing the robustness and transparency of health information management. The proposed approach examines the use of a blockchain-based register to record key registration events and updates in a tamper-resistant manner, complementing existing digital systems.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Ministry of Health, Welfare and Sport manages multiple national registers related to healthcare professionals and medical instruments, requiring high levels of accuracy, reliability, and public trust. Although these registers are largely digitised, questions remain about how to further strengthen data integrity, interoperability, and long-term trust in sensitive health-related information. This use case explores whether distributed ledger technology can add value to the registration of medical instruments and related data, involving the ministry, healthcare stakeholders, and regulatory bodies, with the objective of enhancing the robustness and transparency of health information management. The proposed approach examines the use of a blockchain-based register to record key registration events and updates in a tamper-resistant manner, complementing existing digital systems. By providing a shared and verifiable source of truth, the solution has the potential to improve auditability, reduce disputes over data accuracy, and reinforce confidence in national health registries, while supporting future innovation in healthcare data governance.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-722,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00698,EU_PSTW_202609_00698,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,The Inspectorate of the Ministry of Employment,General services.,"Fragmented information sharing limits the ability to identify identity fraud, labour exploitation, and organised crime within the work and income chain.",Employment and Identity Fraud,The Inspectorate of the Ministry of Employment; Central-Government; General services.; Netherlands,"This use case addresses these challenges by improving secure data sharing between governmental organisations involved in employment and social services, with the objective of strengthening fraud detection, protecting vulnerable workers, and improving enforcement effectiveness. The solution is implemented through a shared data-exchange framework supported by distributed ledger technology, where relevant events and verified data points from participating organisations are recorded and made accessible to authorised parties under agreed governance rules.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Public authorities responsible for employment, welfare, and income support must detect and prevent fraud while coordinating across multiple organisations that each hold partial and sensitive data. Fragmented information sharing limits the ability to identify identity fraud, labour exploitation, and organised crime within the work and income chain. This use case addresses these challenges by improving secure data sharing between governmental organisations involved in employment and social services, with the objective of strengthening fraud detection, protecting vulnerable workers, and improving enforcement effectiveness. The solution is implemented through a shared data-exchange framework supported by distributed ledger technology, where relevant events and verified data points from participating organisations are recorded and made accessible to authorised parties under agreed governance rules. The ledger provides a trusted, tamper-resistant reference that improves consistency across datasets while preserving institutional autonomy. The approach enhances the timeliness and accuracy of fraud detection, reduces duplication of investigations, and supports more coordinated and effective public-sector responses to complex employment-related crime.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-723,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00699,EU_PSTW_202609_00699,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Ministry of Foreign Affairs,General services.,"Complex public and private projects often involve financial arrangements between multiple stakeholders, creating challenges in coordination, transparency, and accountability. Disparate financial records and approval processes can lead to delays, misunderstandings, and increased administrative effort.",Multi-stakeholder Financial Arrangements,Ministry of Foreign Affairs; Central-Government; General services.; Netherlands,"This use case addresses these issues by improving the management of multi-stakeholder financial arrangements, involving public authorities, financial institutions, and other partners, with the objective of creating clearer oversight and more efficient financial coordination. The solution is implemented through a shared digital financial framework based on distributed ledger technology, where agreements, commitments, and transactions are recorded in a single, verifiable environment accessible to authorised stakeholders.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Complex public and private projects often involve financial arrangements between multiple stakeholders, creating challenges in coordination, transparency, and accountability. Disparate financial records and approval processes can lead to delays, misunderstandings, and increased administrative effort. This use case addresses these issues by improving the management of multi-stakeholder financial arrangements, involving public authorities, financial institutions, and other partners, with the objective of creating clearer oversight and more efficient financial coordination. The solution is implemented through a shared digital financial framework based on distributed ledger technology, where agreements, commitments, and transactions are recorded in a single, verifiable environment accessible to authorised stakeholders. Smart contract mechanisms can be used to automate predefined financial steps and conditions. The result is improved transparency, reduced administrative overhead, and greater trust between parties, supporting faster decision-making and more reliable execution of complex financial arrangements.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-724,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00700,EU_PSTW_202609_00700,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Municipalities of Barendrecht, Albrandswaard and Ridderkerk",General services.,"Municipalities are responsible for issuing permits for local events, but the process often requires consultation with multiple authorities such as police and fire services, resulting in lengthy procedures and high administrative burden. All involved agencies gain real-time visibility into each other’s input without relying on manual coordination by the municipality.",Large Event Permitting,"Municipalities of Barendrecht, Albrandswaard and Ridderkerk; Local Government; General services.; Netherlands","This use case addresses these inefficiencies by redesigning the event permitting process, involving municipalities and advisory authorities, with the objective of simplifying workflows and reducing processing time. The proof of concept models the permitting process on a blockchain, where applications, advice, and approvals are shared in a common environment and standard procedures are automated using smart contracts.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Municipalities are responsible for issuing permits for local events, but the process often requires consultation with multiple authorities such as police and fire services, resulting in lengthy procedures and high administrative burden. In the municipalities of Barendrecht, Albrandswaard, and Ridderkerk, even small-scale events such as street parties required complex coordination and repeated information exchange. This use case addresses these inefficiencies by redesigning the event permitting process, involving municipalities and advisory authorities, with the objective of simplifying workflows and reducing processing time. The proof of concept models the permitting process on a blockchain, where applications, advice, and approvals are shared in a common environment and standard procedures are automated using smart contracts. All involved agencies gain real-time visibility into each other’s input without relying on manual coordination by the municipality. The solution significantly reduces administrative workload, accelerates permit issuance, and makes the process more resilient by minimising manual communication and duplication.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-725,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00701,EU_PSTW_202609_00701,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Utrecht,Waste management.,This fragmentation increases the risk of discrepancies and reduces transparency across the waste processing chain.,Waste Sector Data Sharing,City of Utrecht; Local Government; Waste management.; Netherlands,"This use case addresses these challenges by improving data sharing and integrity in the waste sector in the City of Utrecht, with the objective of creating a reliable end-to-end overview of waste flows. The proof of concept is implemented through a blockchain-based shared administration that links waste weight data per container with information from transporters, intermediaries, and final processors.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Waste management involves multiple actors, including municipalities, transporters, intermediaries, and processors, each maintaining separate records that must align for regulatory compliance and billing. This fragmentation increases the risk of discrepancies and reduces transparency across the waste processing chain. This use case addresses these challenges by improving data sharing and integrity in the waste sector in the City of Utrecht, with the objective of creating a reliable end-to-end overview of waste flows. The proof of concept is implemented through a blockchain-based shared administration that links waste weight data per container with information from transporters, intermediaries, and final processors. Smart contracts automate selected control and verification steps, while each party retains its own operational systems. The approach improves trust in data accuracy, reduces reconciliation effort, and provides clear visibility from waste collection to final processing, supporting more efficient and accountable waste management.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-726,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00702,EU_PSTW_202609_00702,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,National Office for Identity Data (RvIG),General services.,UNKNOWN,Blockchain-based Digital Identity: Do-it-Yourself Marriage,National Office for Identity Data (RvIG); Central-Government; General services.; Netherlands,"This use case examines the concept of a “do-it-yourself” marriage, involving citizens and civil registration authorities, with the objective of understanding how identity verification and consent could be managed digitally. The concept is implemented through a blockchain-based digital identity framework in which the identities and intentions of both partners are verified and recorded, and smart contracts are used to formalise the agreement without the need for an intermediary registrar.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Civil status processes such as marriage traditionally rely on trusted third parties to verify identities and formalise agreements, which can limit flexibility and increase administrative involvement. The National Office for Identity Data explored whether emerging digital identity approaches could simplify such processes while maintaining legal certainty. This use case examines the concept of a “do-it-yourself” marriage, involving citizens and civil registration authorities, with the objective of understanding how identity verification and consent could be managed digitally. The concept is implemented through a blockchain-based digital identity framework in which the identities and intentions of both partners are verified and recorded, and smart contracts are used to formalise the agreement without the need for an intermediary registrar. While primarily exploratory, the use case illustrates how decentralised identity and automated trust mechanisms could reduce administrative steps, increase citizen autonomy, and inform future redesign of civil registry processes, subject to legal and regulatory considerations.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-727,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00703,EU_PSTW_202609_00703,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Zuidhorn,Family and children.,"In Zuidhorn, the implementation of the national Kindpakket programme, which provides financial support for children from low-income families, required a solution that reduced administrative burden, improved transparency, and involved local businesses and associations in a simple and trustworthy way. Payments are settled directly with entrepreneurs without manual intervention by the municipality, and the platform integrates with existing municipal administrative processes for eligibility and oversight.",Aid Subsidy Collection,Municipality of Zuidhorn; Local Government; Family and children.; Netherlands,"The objective of this use case is to enable fast, user-friendly subsidy collection for families, ensure correct spending of public funds, and simplify coordination between the municipality, beneficiaries, and local entrepreneurs. The solution is implemented through a digital subsidy platform based on distributed ledger technology, where entitlements are securely issued to eligible families and linked to specific spending purposes.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Municipalities are responsible for administering social support schemes efficiently while ensuring that public funds are used correctly and reach their intended beneficiaries. In Zuidhorn, the implementation of the national Kindpakket programme, which provides financial support for children from low-income families, required a solution that reduced administrative burden, improved transparency, and involved local businesses and associations in a simple and trustworthy way. Around 150 children in the municipality are eligible for the scheme, which supports participation in sports, cultural activities, and school-related initiatives. The objective of this use case is to enable fast, user-friendly subsidy collection for families, ensure correct spending of public funds, and simplify coordination between the municipality, beneficiaries, and local entrepreneurs. The solution is implemented through a digital subsidy platform based on distributed ledger technology, where entitlements are securely issued to eligible families and linked to specific spending purposes. Beneficiaries use a unique digital code at participating local businesses to pay for approved goods or services, such as a bicycle, while transaction records are validated and stored in a shared system. Payments are settled directly with entrepreneurs without manual intervention by the municipality, and the platform integrates with existing municipal administrative processes for eligibility and oversight. The approach significantly reduces administrative complexity and processing time, improves transparency and traceability of subsidy use, and enhances user experience for families and businesses. It also strengthens local economic participation, increases trust in subsidy management, and provides a scalable and cost-effective model for delivering targeted social support.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-728,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00704,EU_PSTW_202609_00704,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Ministry of Economy Tourism and Digital Agenda (MINETAD),General services.,"The management of digital assets after death or temporary incapacity presents legal and practical challenges, as traditional testamentary processes often do not adequately address online accounts, cryptocurrencies and other digital property.",TrustForWills,Ministry of Economy Tourism and Digital Agenda (MINETAD); Central-Government; General services.; Spain,The platform uses blockchain technology to record testamentary instructions in a tamper-resistant distributed ledger.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The management of digital assets after death or temporary incapacity presents legal and practical challenges, as traditional testamentary processes often do not adequately address online accounts, cryptocurrencies and other digital property. TrustForWills was developed as a government-supported initiative to facilitate the secure and automated execution of digital wills, ensuring that users’ digital assets are managed in accordance with their instructions. Stakeholders include individuals, legal professionals, public authorities and emerging digital service providers. The objective is to modernise inheritance management and stimulate new business models within the legal sector by addressing digital patrimony. The platform uses blockchain technology to record testamentary instructions in a tamper-resistant distributed ledger. Smart contracts are designed to execute predefined actions when specific conditions are met, such as verified notification of death or incapacity. Cryptographic mechanisms secure user identity and asset references, while integration with digital asset platforms enables controlled transfer or access. The system supports transparency, auditability and automated compliance with predefined rules. The expected impact includes greater legal certainty for digital asset succession, reduced administrative disputes and improved efficiency in estate management. By leveraging distributed ledger infrastructure, the initiative enhances trust, security and innovation in the management of digital legacies.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-729,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00705,EU_PSTW_202609_00705,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Valls City Council,General services.,"Open data initiatives aim to improve transparency and civic participation, but centralised portals can face challenges related to data integrity, persistence, and accountability of changes.",Distributed Open Data Portal,Valls City Council; Local Government; General services.; Spain,"This use case involves the municipality, developers, and citizens, with the objective of publishing open data in a secure, verifiable, and participatory way. The solution is implemented through a distributed open data portal where datasets are published both on a traditional web interface and on a decentralised storage network based on IPFS, ensuring long-term data persistence.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Open data initiatives aim to improve transparency and civic participation, but centralised portals can face challenges related to data integrity, persistence, and accountability of changes. The City of Valls sought to strengthen trust and resilience in its open data publication. This use case involves the municipality, developers, and citizens, with the objective of publishing open data in a secure, verifiable, and participatory way. The solution is implemented through a distributed open data portal where datasets are published both on a traditional web interface and on a decentralised storage network based on IPFS, ensuring long-term data persistence. All changes to datasets are logged on an immutable distributed ledger, enabling full traceability of updates. This hybrid architecture improves reliability and transparency of open data. It enhances citizen trust, supports reuse of data, and strengthens participation, while providing a scalable model for accountable open government data publishing.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-730,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00706,EU_PSTW_202609_00706,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Vattenfall - Business Area Markets,Fuel and energy,Municipal and national energy markets rely on centralised trading platforms that can be costly to operate and may limit flexibility and transparency for market participants.,Decentralised Energy Trading Marketplace,Vattenfall - Business Area Markets; Consortium; Fuel and energy; Sweden,"The objective of this use case is to assess whether a peer-to-peer trading model can match current trading platforms, reduce operational costs, and increase resilience by removing dependence on a single central intermediary. The pilot is implemented through a decentralised digital trading infrastructure based on distributed ledger technology, where participating firms exchange buy and sell orders directly via an encrypted, shared order book.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Municipal and national energy markets rely on centralised trading platforms that can be costly to operate and may limit flexibility and transparency for market participants. Vattenfall and 22 European energy trading firms sought to explore whether wholesale energy trading could be organised in a more efficient, decentralised way while maintaining the reliability of existing market operations. The objective of this use case is to assess whether a peer-to-peer trading model can match current trading platforms, reduce operational costs, and increase resilience by removing dependence on a single central intermediary. The pilot is implemented through a decentralised digital trading infrastructure based on distributed ledger technology, where participating firms exchange buy and sell orders directly via an encrypted, shared order book. Software provided by a specialised B2B solutions provider enables secure submission, matching, and settlement of trades without the need for a third-party exchange or broker, while integrating with participants’ existing trading and risk management systems. The trial allows real-time testing of decentralised order handling, transaction integrity, and system performance under market conditions. The project provides insight into the feasibility of decentralised wholesale energy trading, with potential benefits including lower transaction costs, reduced operational complexity, increased transparency, and improved scalability. It also supports innovation in energy markets by exploring alternative trading architectures that could enhance competition and efficiency in the long term.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-731,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00707,EU_PSTW_202609_00707,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""social_nonprofit""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Canton of Schaffhausen,General services.,UNKNOWN,PROCIVIS e-Government Pilot Project,Canton of Schaffhausen; Regional Government; General services.; Switzerland,"This use case involves cantonal authorities and residents, with the objective of enabling secure, user-friendly e-government services. The solution is implemented through the Schaffhauser eID+ mobile application, which allows citizens to install a verified electronic identity validated by the residents’ registration office.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Citizens increasingly expect secure and convenient access to public services via mobile devices, while governments seek to reduce reliance on multiple logins and paper documents. The Canton of Schaffhausen aimed to simplify digital access to public services through a trusted mobile identity. This use case involves cantonal authorities and residents, with the objective of enabling secure, user-friendly e-government services. The solution is implemented through the Schaffhauser eID+ mobile application, which allows citizens to install a verified electronic identity validated by the residents’ registration office. The app enables authentication for multiple public services and secure storage of digital documents on smartphones. It integrates with existing government systems while maintaining high security standards. The solution improves usability, reduces administrative friction, and increases uptake of digital services. It delivers operational efficiency, enhances citizen experience, and provides a scalable foundation for mobile-first e-government.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-732,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00708,EU_PSTW_202609_00708,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Zug,General public services n.e.c.,The platform operates alongside existing administrative systems and is limited in scope to enable controlled experimentation.,City of Zug Blockchain Voting Trial,City of Zug; Local Government; General public services n.e.c.; Switzerland,"This use case focuses on a municipal voting trial conducted between 25 June and 1 July 2018, involving residents and city authorities, with the objective of evaluating the feasibility and acceptance of digital voting at local level. The proof of concept is implemented through a blockchain-based e-voting platform that records voting transactions and voter credentials in a tamper-resistant system.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Cities are increasingly seeking secure and trustworthy digital channels to engage citizens in democratic processes, while ensuring transparency, data integrity, and voter confidence. The City of Zug aimed to test whether electronic voting could be delivered in a way that strengthens trust and protects voter identity, as part of its broader strategy to experiment with innovative digital public services. This use case focuses on a municipal voting trial conducted between 25 June and 1 July 2018, involving residents and city authorities, with the objective of evaluating the feasibility and acceptance of digital voting at local level. The proof of concept is implemented through a blockchain-based e-voting platform that records voting transactions and voter credentials in a tamper-resistant system. Resident identities are verified digitally, and votes are securely stored to ensure integrity, traceability, and protection against manipulation, while preserving voter confidentiality. The platform operates alongside existing administrative systems and is limited in scope to enable controlled experimentation. The trial demonstrates that digital voting can be carried out securely and transparently at municipal level, providing a positive user experience for participants. It offers valuable lessons on governance, security, and citizen trust, and supports informed decision-making on the future use of digital voting technologies in democratic processes.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-733,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00709,EU_PSTW_202609_00709,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Ministry of Finance,"General economic, commercial and labour affair","Privatisation and leasing of state assets are vulnerable to corruption, lack of transparency, and public mistrust.",e-Auction 3.0,"Ministry of Finance; Central-Government; General economic, commercial and labour affair; Ukraine","This use case involves public authorities, bidders, and oversight bodies, with the objective of ensuring fair, transparent asset auctions. The solution is implemented through a decentralised electronic auction platform based on blockchain technology, supporting the sale and lease of state and regional property, land, and enterprises.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Privatisation and leasing of state assets are vulnerable to corruption, lack of transparency, and public mistrust. Ukraine sought to reform these processes by introducing a more open and verifiable auction mechanism. This use case involves public authorities, bidders, and oversight bodies, with the objective of ensuring fair, transparent asset auctions. The solution is implemented through a decentralised electronic auction platform based on blockchain technology, supporting the sale and lease of state and regional property, land, and enterprises. The platform provides open APIs for monitoring and embeds transparency by design, making auction data publicly auditable. The system reduces opportunities for manipulation, increases competition, and improves public confidence. It accelerates privatisation processes, lowers administrative costs, and delivers strong governance value through transparency and accountability.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-734,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00710,EU_PSTW_202609_00710,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Financial Conduct Authority (FCA),"General economic, commercial and labour affair","Regulatory reporting of mortgage transactions involves complex data exchanges between financial institutions and supervisory authorities, often relying on fragmented reporting systems and manual reconciliation processes.",R3 Project Maison,"Financial Conduct Authority (FCA); Central-Government; General economic, commercial and labour affair; United Kingdom",Smart contract logic can standardise reporting formats and automate compliance checks.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Regulatory reporting of mortgage transactions involves complex data exchanges between financial institutions and supervisory authorities, often relying on fragmented reporting systems and manual reconciliation processes. These inefficiencies can increase operational risk, reporting errors and compliance costs for banks, while limiting real-time oversight capabilities for regulators. R3, in collaboration with the UK Financial Conduct Authority and major banks, launched Project Maison to explore how distributed ledger technology could improve mortgage reporting. The objective was to create a shared, secure infrastructure that streamlines data submission and enhances transparency in regulatory reporting workflows. The project utilises blockchain-based platforms to record mortgage transaction data in a shared ledger accessible to authorised participants. Cryptographic validation ensures data integrity, while consensus mechanisms synchronise updates across participating institutions. Smart contract logic can standardise reporting formats and automate compliance checks. The prototype integrates with banks’ internal systems through secure interfaces, enabling structured data exchange between institutions and regulators. The anticipated impact includes reduced duplication of reporting processes, improved data accuracy and enhanced regulatory visibility. By creating a common data layer, the initiative aims to lower compliance costs, strengthen trust between regulators and banks and support innovation in supervisory technology.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-735,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00711,EU_PSTW_202609_00711,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,HM Treasury (HMT) and the Department of Work and Pensions,Social protection n.e.c.,"Public welfare systems face ongoing challenges in distributing financial support efficiently while preventing fraud, reducing administrative costs, and ensuring that vulnerable citizens can access benefits easily.",GovCoin Proof of Concept,HM Treasury (HMT) and the Department of Work and Pensions; Central-Government; Social protection n.e.c.; United Kingdom,"The objective of this use case is to improve the registration and payment of benefits, strengthen control over public expenditure, and support broader policy goals such as financial inclusion and poverty reduction. The proof of concept is implemented through a digital payment and registration framework based on distributed ledger technology, often referred to as GovCoin.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Public welfare systems face ongoing challenges in distributing financial support efficiently while preventing fraud, reducing administrative costs, and ensuring that vulnerable citizens can access benefits easily. In the United Kingdom, HM Treasury and the Department for Work and Pensions sought to explore new payment models that could modernise the delivery of government grants and welfare support. The objective of this use case is to improve the registration and payment of benefits, strengthen control over public expenditure, and support broader policy goals such as financial inclusion and poverty reduction. The proof of concept is implemented through a digital payment and registration framework based on distributed ledger technology, often referred to as GovCoin. The system records benefit entitlements and transactions in a shared, secure ledger, enabling automated checks, traceability of payments, and reduced reliance on intermediaries. Integration with existing government systems supports eligibility verification and compliance, while digital wallets enable recipients to receive and use funds more efficiently. The approach demonstrates potential to reduce losses from fraud and error, lower administrative overhead, and increase transparency in welfare payments. It also improves user experience for beneficiaries by supporting faster and more reliable access to support, while offering better value for money and a more sustainable footing for public expenditure.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-736,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00712,EU_PSTW_202609_00712,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,The Royal Mint,"General economic, commercial and labour affair",UNKNOWN,RMGA The New Digital Gold Standard,"The Royal Mint; Central-Government; General economic, commercial and labour affair; United Kingdom","This use case involves institutional investors, traders, and market operators, with the objective of enabling secure, efficient gold trading and settlement. The solution is implemented by issuing digital records of ownership for gold stored in The Royal Mint’s vaults, with transactions executed and settled through integrated market infrastructure.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Trading and holding physical gold traditionally involves high costs, intermediaries, and operational complexity. The Royal Mint and CME Group sought to modernise gold markets by combining physical security with digital efficiency. This use case involves institutional investors, traders, and market operators, with the objective of enabling secure, efficient gold trading and settlement. The solution is implemented by issuing digital records of ownership for gold stored in The Royal Mint’s vaults, with transactions executed and settled through integrated market infrastructure. Each digital unit represents allocated physical gold, secured cryptographically and backed by trusted custody. The system reduces settlement risk, lowers costs, and improves market access. It enhances liquidity, transparency, and efficiency, delivering a modern alternative to traditional spot gold markets. However, the project has been discontinued in 2018.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,NOT_IN_USE,미사용,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-737,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00713,EU_PSTW_202609_00713,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Bank of England,"General economic, commercial and labour affair",UNKNOWN,Distributed Ledger-based Gross Settlement System,"Bank of England; Central-Government; General economic, commercial and labour affair; United Kingdom",UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Bank of England recognised the need to modernise its ageing Real-Time Gross Settlement (RTGS) system to ensure long-term financial stability and accommodate emerging financial technologies. As interbank settlement forms the backbone of the UK’s payment infrastructure, the central bank sought to design a replacement system capable of interfacing with innovative technologies without compromising resilience or security. Stakeholders include commercial banks, financial market infrastructures, fintech providers and regulatory authorities. The objective was to create a next-generation RTGS platform that safeguards monetary stability while enabling future integration with distributed ledger systems. The proposed architecture considers interoperability with distributed ledger technologies by enabling secure APIs and messaging interfaces that allow external platforms to connect with the central settlement core. While not replacing the RTGS with a blockchain system, the design incorporates modular components capable of interacting with tokenised assets or ledger-based platforms if these achieve industry maturity. Advanced encryption, real-time processing engines and high-availability infrastructure underpin the system. The impact is a more flexible, resilient and innovation-ready settlement infrastructure that maintains systemic stability while supporting future technological evolution. This approach enhances scalability, ensures regulatory compliance and positions the UK payment system to adapt to industry developments.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-738,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00714,EU_PSTW_202609_00714,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Food Standards Agency,"Agriculture, forestry, fishing and hunting","The Food Standards Agency (FSA), the UK’s food safety regulator, faces the ongoing challenge of ensuring transparency, traceability and regulatory oversight across complex meat supply chains, where fragmented records and limited data sharing can slow inspections and reduce trust.",Food Standards Agency,"Food Standards Agency; Central-Government; Agriculture, forestry, fishing and hunting; United Kingdom","Emerging Technology Implementation: The solution was implemented using a blockchain-based supply chain monitoring system, piloted through a proof of concept.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Food Standards Agency (FSA), the UK’s food safety regulator, faces the ongoing challenge of ensuring transparency, traceability and regulatory oversight across complex meat supply chains, where fragmented records and limited data sharing can slow inspections and reduce trust. In the context of meat production and inspection, the FSA worked with an unnamed cattle slaughterhouse to address the need for consistent, verifiable and timely access to production and inspection data. Key stakeholders included the regulator and the slaughterhouse operator, with the shared objective of improving end-to-end visibility of meat movements and compliance information while aligning industry practices with regulatory requirements. Emerging Technology Implementation: The solution was implemented using a blockchain-based supply chain monitoring system, piloted through a proof of concept. Standardised data relating to meat inspection and production events were recorded on a shared distributed ledger accessible to both the FSA and the slaughterhouse. The approach focused on developing common data standards in collaboration with industry, enabling secure, tamper-evident data sharing and integration with existing operational systems at a conceptual level. Impact and Results: The trial demonstrated that the technology functions effectively within this segment of the food industry, delivering improved transparency and shared visibility across the supply chain. It validated the feasibility of real-time data access for regulators, strengthened trust between industry and government, and highlighted opportunities to scale the approach. The results showed clear potential for more efficient inspections, improved compliance assurance, and a collaborative model that can be expanded across the wider food sector.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-739,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00715,EU_PSTW_202609_00715,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Aragon General Directorate of Contracting,General services.,"Public procurement transparency posed significant challenges for Spain's regional governments, with traditional vendor selection processes lacking auditability and creating opportunities for disputes over contract awards.","Aragon General Directorate of Contracting, Blockchain Project",Aragon General Directorate of Contracting; Regional Government; General services.; Spain,"The Government of Aragon implemented a pioneering hybrid blockchain solution combining Ethereum and Hyperledger Fabric technologies through the Alastria Consortium to automate and secure public tender evaluations. The system, developed in partnership with Oesia and Open Canarias, uses smart contracts to process vendor submissions whilst maintaining civil servant oversight for final decisions.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Public procurement transparency posed significant challenges for Spain's regional governments, with traditional vendor selection processes lacking auditability and creating opportunities for disputes over contract awards. The Government of Aragon implemented a pioneering hybrid blockchain solution combining Ethereum and Hyperledger Fabric technologies through the Alastria Consortium to automate and secure public tender evaluations. The system, developed in partnership with Oesia and Open Canarias, uses smart contracts to process vendor submissions whilst maintaining civil servant oversight for final decisions. Since launching in 2019, the platform has processed over 25 contracts, establishing Aragon as Spain's first regional government to deploy blockchain in public procurement. The initiative has demonstrated measurable improvements in processing times and transparency, with all evaluation criteria and scoring permanently recorded on the distributed ledger for public verification.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-740,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00716,EU_PSTW_202609_00716,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Bilbao,General services.,UNKNOWN,Basque Government Blockchain project,City of Bilbao; Local Government; General services.; Spain,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Bilbao's municipal administration required a secure infrastructure for inter-institutional data exchange that could integrate with the Basque government's existing digital systems whilst enabling innovative citizen services. The city launched a €150,000 tender to develop a public blockchain network built on JPMorgan's Quorum platform, designed for compatibility with the regional EJIE technological infrastructure. The resulting platform employs smart contracts to facilitate secure data sharing between government institutions, with initial applications including digital power of attorney services that allow citizens to authorise representatives online. The system operates as a permissioned network ensuring that sensitive administrative data remains accessible only to authorised public bodies. This initiative positioned Bilbao among Spain's early municipal adopters of distributed ledger technology for government operations.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-741,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00717,EU_PSTW_202609_00717,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Digipolis (IT agency for Antwerp),General services.,"Belgian public procurement processes faced challenges with document integrity verification and timestamp reliability for tender submissions, requiring a solution that could provide immutable evidence of submission times without compromising confidentiality.",V-ICT-OR Blockchain Project,Digipolis (IT agency for Antwerp); Local Government; General services.; Belgium,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Belgian public procurement processes faced challenges with document integrity verification and timestamp reliability for tender submissions, requiring a solution that could provide immutable evidence of submission times without compromising confidentiality. Digipolis Antwerp, the IT agency for the City of Antwerp, developed a blockchain-based procurement tool in partnership with BallistiX that timestamps tender metadata on a private distributed ledger. The system operates nodes at Digipolis, the Belgian federal agency BOSA, and the Port of Antwerp, creating a trusted network for procurement documentation. Integrated within the Antwerp City Platform as a Service (ACPaaS), the solution received recognition as Belgian ICT Project of the Year. The platform demonstrates how blockchain can enhance public procurement integrity without requiring fundamental changes to existing administrative workflows.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-742,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00718,EU_PSTW_202609_00718,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Digipolis (IT agency for Antwerp),General services.,"Traditional procurement systems can be vulnerable to data tampering and lack robust, tamper-evident records of submissions, which may undermine confidence among procurers and bidders and lead to disputes or compliance challenges.",Smart procurement tool in Antwerp,Digipolis (IT agency for Antwerp); Local Government; General services.; Belgium,"The blockchain component, using a proof-of-authority configuration hosted by Digipolis and the Belgian Federal Government Procurement Organization (BOSA), underpins the process alongside smart contracts that log key events such as contract award outcomes. The tool has been implemented as part of the BLING (Blockchain in Government) programme of proof-of-concept projects, and the early experience indicates the system is stable enough to support procurement workflows, offering enhanced transparency and tamper resistance compared to legacy systems while remaining compliant with public procurement law",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The City of Antwerp, through its municipal IT agency Digipolis, explored ways to enhance public procurement processes by introducing greater transparency, reliability and trust in how requests for quotations and vendor offers are exchanged and recorded. Traditional procurement systems can be vulnerable to data tampering and lack robust, tamper-evident records of submissions, which may undermine confidence among procurers and bidders and lead to disputes or compliance challenges. The tool was developed in the context of public procurement stakeholders including municipal procurement officers, IT administrators, and suppliers, with the objective of testing whether distributed ledger technology could strengthen the integrity of procurement transactions while respecting existing procurement regulations. The solution developed combines blockchain-based and conventional components: procurers publish requests for quotations via a user interface linked to a customer relationship management system, and metadata from published requests and vendor submissions are recorded on a permissioned blockchain to provide immutable time-stamped attestations that documents have not been altered. The blockchain component, using a proof-of-authority configuration hosted by Digipolis and the Belgian Federal Government Procurement Organization (BOSA), underpins the process alongside smart contracts that log key events such as contract award outcomes. Integration with the CRM system ensures award notifications and non-award communications can be automated following on-chain events. The tool has been implemented as part of the BLING (Blockchain in Government) programme of proof-of-concept projects, and the early experience indicates the system is stable enough to support procurement workflows, offering enhanced transparency and tamper resistance compared to legacy systems while remaining compliant with public procurement law",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-743,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00719,EU_PSTW_202609_00719,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Malta Information Technology Agency (MITA),General services.,UNKNOWN,"Malta Information Technology Agency (MITA), Smart Public Contracts",Malta Information Technology Agency (MITA); Central-Government; General services.; Malta,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"COVID-19 vaccine distribution required unprecedented cold chain monitoring, with Pfizer vaccines demanding storage at -70°C and any temperature deviation potentially compromising entire batches worth thousands of doses. NHS South Warwickshire partnered with Everyware Ltd and Hedera Hashgraph to deploy IoT sensors across Stratford Upon Avon and Warwick hospitals that continuously monitor refrigeration units and record encrypted temperature data on a distributed ledger. The blockchain-based audit trail provides tamper-proof evidence of compliance with storage requirements, supporting regulatory accountability and enabling rapid identification of any cold chain breaches. Following successful vaccine monitoring, the system has been expanded to track chemotherapy drugs, blood products, and other temperature-sensitive medical assets. Everyware was subsequently selected for the Digital Catapult 5G programme to further develop healthcare IoT applications.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-744,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00720,EU_PSTW_202609_00720,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Estonian Government,General services.,UNKNOWN,Data embassy - Estonian Blockchain Technology,Estonian Government; Central-Government; General services.; Estonia,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Estonia's comprehensive digital government services created an unprecedented national dependency on data infrastructure, raising concerns about continuity in the event of cyber attacks or physical disasters affecting domestic systems. The government established the world's first data embassy in Luxembourg, housing backup copies of critical national databases including land registry, population register, business register, and health records within a Tier 4 datacenter in Betzdorf. The facility operates under a bilateral agreement providing diplomatic immunity equivalent to a physical embassy, ensuring data sovereignty regardless of its physical location. Built with €2.2 million in funding (85% from EU ERDF), the infrastructure employs Guardtime's KSI Blockchain for data integrity verification alongside solutions from Cybernetica, Dell EMC, Ericsson, OpenNode, and Telia. The initiative has since inspired similar projects, with Monaco replicating the model in 2021.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-745,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00721,EU_PSTW_202609_00721,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Signaturit Solutions,General services.,"International credential recognition in Italy faced challenges with verification delays and document fraud, creating barriers for qualified professionals seeking employment or further education.",Signaturit - The smartest e-Signature solution to send and sign documents on the go,Signaturit Solutions; Consortium; General services.; Spain,"Italy became the first country to use blockchain technology specifically for credential evaluation processes, providing holders with a digital wallet containing their verified qualifications.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"International credential recognition in Italy faced challenges with verification delays and document fraud, creating barriers for qualified professionals seeking employment or further education. CIMEA, the Italian ENIC-NARIC centre responsible for foreign qualification recognition, developed Diplome as a private permissioned blockchain platform for automatic notarisation of Statements of Comparability and Verification. Italy became the first country to use blockchain technology specifically for credential evaluation processes, providing holders with a digital wallet containing their verified qualifications. In March 2024, CIMEA Diplome attestations were accepted as equivalent to the Declaration of Value for EU Blue Card applications, significantly streamlining immigration procedures for skilled workers. The CIMEA Diplome Chain infrastructure continues to expand, supporting European mobility initiatives and international recognition frameworks.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-746,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00722,EU_PSTW_202609_00722,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Centre Blockchain de Catalunya,Public health services.,"The system provides graduates with internationally recognisable proof of their qualifications, reducing verification delays and eliminating risks of credential fraud.",Obrir Girona - system of certifications of vaccinations and Covid19 tests,Centre Blockchain de Catalunya; Regional Government; Public health services.; Spain,"The government implemented blockchain technology to store all academic qualifications issued by Andorran higher education institutions, creating tamper-proof digital credentials that can be verified independently without contacting issuing institutions. The initiative demonstrates how smaller nations can leverage blockchain to enhance the international recognition of their educational systems.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Andorra's small higher education system required modernisation to ensure academic credentials would be readily verifiable by international employers and institutions, supporting graduate mobility in the European region. The government implemented blockchain technology to store all academic qualifications issued by Andorran higher education institutions, creating tamper-proof digital credentials that can be verified independently without contacting issuing institutions. This digital transformation aligned with broader national strategies to position Andorra as an innovative jurisdiction embracing emerging technologies. The system provides graduates with internationally recognisable proof of their qualifications, reducing verification delays and eliminating risks of credential fraud. The initiative demonstrates how smaller nations can leverage blockchain to enhance the international recognition of their educational systems.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-747,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00724,EU_PSTW_202609_00724,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""social_nonprofit""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Asure Foundation,R&D social protection.,"Academic and professional credential verification has traditionally relied on centralised institutions, creating administrative burdens for universities, graduates and employers who must repeatedly authenticate certificates and qualifications.",Decentralised Social Security System,Asure Foundation; Non-governmental; R&D social protection.; Germany,"To address inefficiencies in credential issuance and verification, the University of Nicosia pioneered the use of blockchain infrastructure to issue tamper-evident academic certificates that can be independently verified without requiring direct institutional confirmation. The initiative has been implemented and operational for several years, positioning the university as an early adopter of blockchain credentials.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Academic and professional credential verification has traditionally relied on centralised institutions, creating administrative burdens for universities, graduates and employers who must repeatedly authenticate certificates and qualifications. To address inefficiencies in credential issuance and verification, the University of Nicosia pioneered the use of blockchain infrastructure to issue tamper-evident academic certificates that can be independently verified without requiring direct institutional confirmation. The initiative sought to modernise trust mechanisms in academic certification while supporting innovation in digital currency and blockchain education. Stakeholders include students, employers, academic institutions and accreditation bodies. The system records cryptographic hashes of academic certificates on the Bitcoin blockchain, enabling third parties to validate authenticity through decentralised verification processes. Secure digital signatures and blockchain time-stamping ensure document integrity while preserving personal data off-chain. The solution is integrated into the university’s academic administration systems and supports issuance for large-scale online programmes, including the Digital Currency MOOC and specialised MSc degrees. The initiative has been implemented and operational for several years, positioning the university as an early adopter of blockchain credentials. It has improved efficiency in verification, reduced administrative overhead and enhanced global accessibility of academic records while strengthening trust in digital certification frameworks.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-749,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00725,EU_PSTW_202609_00725,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Hamm,General services.,"Malta's rental housing market suffered from widespread informal contracts and limited regulatory oversight, creating problems for tenants lacking legal protections and enabling tax evasion through unreported rental income.",Digital vehicle registration,City of Hamm; Local Government; General services.; Germany,"The system uses smart contracts to ensure documents cannot be tampered with after registration whilst maintaining controlled access for authorised parties including landlords, tenants, and regulatory authorities.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Malta's rental housing market suffered from widespread informal contracts and limited regulatory oversight, creating problems for tenants lacking legal protections and enabling tax evasion through unreported rental income. In June 2019, the Cabinet approved regulations requiring all property rental contracts to be registered on blockchain, making Malta the first country to mandate distributed ledger technology for residential tenancy agreements. The system uses smart contracts to ensure documents cannot be tampered with after registration whilst maintaining controlled access for authorised parties including landlords, tenants, and regulatory authorities. Ledger Projects, with developers David Schranz and Anthony Mamo, created a supporting application for property transfer management. The initiative aims to eliminate informal rental arrangements, protect tenant rights, and improve tax compliance in the housing sector.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-750,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00726,EU_PSTW_202609_00726,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Digital Europe for All (DE4A),General services.,"Italian universities faced increasing challenges with diploma verification requests and credential fraud, whilst graduates required faster proof of qualifications for job applications and further study. This approach enables independent verification by employers or other institutions without requiring contact with the university, reducing administrative burden whilst providing tamper-proof evidence of qualification authenticity.",Doing Business Abroad,Digital Europe for All (DE4A); Consortium; General services.; European Union,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Italian universities faced increasing challenges with diploma verification requests and credential fraud, whilst graduates required faster proof of qualifications for job applications and further study. The University of Cagliari became the first Italian university to issue blockchain-validated academic degrees, storing cryptographic hashes of diploma data on the public Ethereum network. This approach enables independent verification by employers or other institutions without requiring contact with the university, reducing administrative burden whilst providing tamper-proof evidence of qualification authenticity. The system supports European Higher Education Area mobility objectives by facilitating cross-border credential recognition. The initiative established a model subsequently adopted by other Italian institutions seeking to modernise their credential issuance processes.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-751,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00727,EU_PSTW_202609_00727,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,National Health Service (NHS),Public health services.,UNKNOWN,Cold Chain Monitoring of COVID-19 Vaccine,National Health Service (NHS); Central-Government; Public health services.; United Kingdom,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"COVID-19 vaccine distribution required unprecedented cold chain monitoring, with Pfizer vaccines demanding storage at -70°C and any temperature deviation potentially compromising entire batches worth thousands of doses. NHS South Warwickshire partnered with Everyware Ltd and Hedera Hashgraph to deploy IoT sensors across Stratford Upon Avon and Warwick hospitals that continuously monitor refrigeration units and record encrypted temperature data on a distributed ledger. The blockchain-based audit trail provides tamper-proof evidence of compliance with storage requirements, supporting regulatory accountability and enabling rapid identification of any cold chain breaches. Following successful vaccine monitoring, the system has been expanded to track chemotherapy drugs, blood products, and other temperature-sensitive medical assets. Everyware was subsequently selected for the Digital Catapult 5G programme to further develop healthcare IoT applications.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-752,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00728,EU_PSTW_202609_00728,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,The Open University,Education not definable by level.,UNKNOWN,Supporting Lifelong Learning,The Open University; Academic-Research; Education not definable by level.; United Kingdom,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Digital identity management in Luxembourg required solutions balancing strong authentication with GDPR-compliant privacy protection, particularly for the financial services sector where identity verification is fundamental. LuxTrust, a trust service provider two-thirds owned by the Luxembourg state, partnered with Cambridge Blockchain (Massachusetts) to develop IDKEEP, a blockchain-based identity platform enabling users to control their personal data whilst maintaining verified credentials. The platform launched in private beta in 2019, building on LuxTrust's existing subscriber base of over 500,000 users who rely on digital certificates for authentication. InfoCert (Italy) joined as an additional partner, whilst the project attracted investment from PayPal, Flourish Ventures, and Omidyar Network. The initiative demonstrates how public-private partnerships can advance self-sovereign identity concepts within regulated sectors.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-753,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00729,EU_PSTW_202609_00729,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Electrical & Computer Engineering (ECE) school of the National Technical University of Athens (NTUA),Post-secondary non-tertiary education.,"International credential recognition in Italy faced challenges with verification delays and document fraud, creating barriers for qualified professionals seeking employment or further education.",University Process Optimisation through Smart Curriculum Design and Student Accreditation,Electrical & Computer Engineering (ECE) school of the National Technical University of Athens (NTUA); Academic-Research; Post-secondary non-tertiary education.; Greece,"Italy became the first country to use blockchain technology specifically for credential evaluation processes, providing holders with a digital wallet containing their verified qualifications.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"International credential recognition in Italy faced challenges with verification delays and document fraud, creating barriers for qualified professionals seeking employment or further education. CIMEA, the Italian ENIC-NARIC centre responsible for foreign qualification recognition, developed Diplome as a private permissioned blockchain platform for automatic notarisation of Statements of Comparability and Verification. Italy became the first country to use blockchain technology specifically for credential evaluation processes, providing holders with a digital wallet containing their verified qualifications. In March 2024, CIMEA Diplome attestations were accepted as equivalent to the Declaration of Value for EU Blue Card applications, significantly streamlining immigration procedures for skilled workers. The CIMEA Diplome Chain infrastructure continues to expand, supporting European mobility initiatives and international recognition frameworks.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-754,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00730,EU_PSTW_202609_00730,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Supreme Council for Civil Personnel Selection (ASEP),General services.,"Citizens interacting with Antwerp's digital services lacked control over their personal data stored across multiple government databases, whilst GDPR requirements demanded new approaches to consent management and data portability.",Staffing the Public Sector – The Case of Greece,Supreme Council for Civil Personnel Selection (ASEP); Central-Government; General services.; Greece,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Citizens interacting with Antwerp's digital services lacked control over their personal data stored across multiple government databases, whilst GDPR requirements demanded new approaches to consent management and data portability. Digipolis Antwerp launched the Blockchain on the Move project in partnership with Jolocom (Berlin), V-ICT-OR, and the Flemish Government under the Programme for Innovation Procurement to develop Self-Sovereign Identity (SSI) infrastructure. The system enables citizens to manage their own identity data and control which government agencies can access specific information, with initial applications focusing on change of address processes. The initiative forms part of Digipolis's broader ACPaaS (Antwerp City Platform as a Service) ecosystem and includes GDPR management tools and a Business Rules Engine for access rights. By returning data control to citizens, the project demonstrates how blockchain technology can support privacy-by-design principles in public services.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-755,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00731,EU_PSTW_202609_00731,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""social_nonprofit""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Agência para a Modernização Administrativa (AMA),General services.,UNKNOWN,Staffing the Public Sector – The Case of Portugal,Agência para a Modernização Administrativa (AMA); Central-Government; General services.; Portugal,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Swiss institutions required secure and transparent methods for managing digital assets and credentials that met the country's high standards for financial services and data protection. Swiss Ledger was established as a private, institutional, and non-profit blockchain infrastructure providing distributed ledger services to Swiss organisations across sectors including finance, government, and academia. The platform enables secure credential management, asset tokenisation, and data integrity verification whilst maintaining compliance with Swiss regulatory requirements. Operating since 2021, Swiss Ledger provides a permissioned blockchain environment where participating organisations can issue and verify digital credentials, track assets, and maintain auditable records. The infrastructure supports Switzerland's broader digital transformation objectives whilst preserving the privacy and security standards that characterise Swiss financial and institutional services.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-756,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00732,EU_PSTW_202609_00732,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""social_nonprofit""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,KnowledegeBiz,General services.,"Traditional air quality monitoring relies on expensive governmental stations providing limited geographic coverage and delayed data publication, leaving many pollution hotspots undetected and populations without timely warnings.",Provision of Recruitment & Competency Management Services,KnowledegeBiz; Central-Government; General services.; Portugal,"PlanetWatch, a French startup and CERN spin-off, developed a global air quality monitoring network using the Algorand blockchain to create the first immutable ledger for environmental data. The platform incentivises individuals and organisations to deploy low-cost sensors through PLANET token rewards, with over 25,000 sensors deployed globally by late 2021.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Traditional air quality monitoring relies on expensive governmental stations providing limited geographic coverage and delayed data publication, leaving many pollution hotspots undetected and populations without timely warnings. PlanetWatch, a French startup and CERN spin-off, developed a global air quality monitoring network using the Algorand blockchain to create the first immutable ledger for environmental data. The platform incentivises individuals and organisations to deploy low-cost sensors through PLANET token rewards, with over 25,000 sensors deployed globally by late 2021. Initial deployments in Milan and Taranto, Italy, demonstrated the system's capability to provide dense, real-time monitoring complementing official stations at a fraction of the cost. Data collected undergoes validation through CERN's C2MON acquisition framework before blockchain recording, ensuring reliability. The Algorand Foundation supported the initiative through grants, recognising its environmental and public health potential.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-757,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00733,EU_PSTW_202609_00733,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Barcelona City Council,General services.,UNKNOWN,DECODE - Digital Democracy and Data Commons,Barcelona City Council; Local Government; General services.; Spain,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Barcelona Digital Democracy and Data Commons pilot explored how citizens could participate in online democratic processes while retaining greater control over their personal data. The operational problem was that digital participation platforms often require users to disclose personal information for authentication, even when the democratic action itself does not require full identity exposure. Barcelona City Council tested the pilot through Decidim, the city’s digital democracy platform, which had more than 60,000 users. The objective was to enable people to sign petitions anonymously while still meeting legitimate authentication requirements, such as proving place of residence. The implementation integrated a DECODE module into Decidim, supported by privacy-preserving data-sharing tools, the DECODE app, the BCNOW dashboard and a distributed ledger for transparency. Rather than relying on conventional identity checks, the system allowed users to disclose only the attributes needed for participation. The pilot also included a deliberative process on the politics and economics of data and on how Barcelona could develop a democratic city data commons. The expected impact was stronger trust in digital participation, more privacy-respecting civic engagement and a practical demonstration of how public authorities can enable democratic processes without unnecessary personal-data exposure.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-758,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00734,EU_PSTW_202609_00734,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Ideas for Change and FabLab Barcelona,General services.,"The urban challenge was that citizen-generated sensor data on issues such as pollution and noise can be valuable for local decision-making, but it also raises questions about privacy, ownership, consent and secure data sharing.",DECODE - Citizen Science Data Governance,Ideas for Change and FabLab Barcelona; Community led; General services.; Spain,"The pilot also included training and support sessions to help participants install sensors, gather data, analyse results and use the evidence to influence city-level decisions.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Barcelona Citizen Science Data Governance pilot focused on enabling residents to collect and share environmental data while maintaining control over how their information was used. The urban challenge was that citizen-generated sensor data on issues such as pollution and noise can be valuable for local decision-making, but it also raises questions about privacy, ownership, consent and secure data sharing. Residents used environmental sensors placed in their homes and neighbourhoods to record factors including noise levels and pollution. DECODE technology enabled them to share encrypted data anonymously with communities and public actors, according to conditions they could define. The implementation addressed both a technical and governance problem: how to collect and store continuous streams of citizen-sensed data while allowing individuals to decide what to share, with whom and under which terms. The pilot also included training and support sessions to help participants install sensors, gather data, analyse results and use the evidence to influence city-level decisions. Its expected impact was to strengthen citizen science, improve the evidence base for urban policy and demonstrate a privacy-preserving model for community data sharing. By linking environmental monitoring with citizen-controlled data governance, the pilot offered a practical route towards more democratic and accountable smart-city services.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-759,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00735,EU_PSTW_202609_00735,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Amsterdam Digital Register,General services.,UNKNOWN,DECODE - Amsterdam Digital Register - age check with municipal census data,Amsterdam Digital Register; Local Government; General services.; Netherlands,"The Amsterdam Digital Register pilot examined how citizens could use personal data held in municipal databases to prove specific attributes without revealing their full identity. Its specific use case was age verification, allowing citizens to prove that they were over 16 or 18 without disclosing broader personal information.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Amsterdam Digital Register pilot examined how citizens could use personal data held in municipal databases to prove specific attributes without revealing their full identity. The operational problem was that many public, civic or online interactions require proof of eligibility, such as age, but traditional verification often exposes more information than necessary, including name, full identity details or social security numbers. The pilot gave participants access to personal data stored in the municipal database and enabled them to reuse it in other contexts, both online and offline. Its specific use case was age verification, allowing citizens to prove that they were over 16 or 18 without disclosing broader personal information. The implementation used Attribute-Based Credentials, a data-minimising authentication mechanism that certifies only the required attribute. Conceptually, the system separated the proof of eligibility from the disclosure of identity, supporting selective and privacy-preserving data sharing. The expected impact was to demonstrate how municipalities can act as trusted custodians of citizen data while giving individuals more agency over its reuse. The pilot showed how public registers could support digital identity functions in a way that reduces unnecessary data exposure and strengthens privacy by design.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-760,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00736,EU_PSTW_202609_00736,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,GebiedOnline,General services.,UNKNOWN,DECODE - Gebiedonline (Neighbourhood Online),GebiedOnline; Community led; General services.; Netherlands,"This allowed communities using GebiedOnline to define which credentials they would accept, while giving users more granular control over the data they shared with the platform and with other members.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The GebiedOnline pilot in Amsterdam tested how a local neighbourhood social network could operate with stronger privacy protections and less dependence on commercial login systems. GebiedOnline was already used as a community platform, but access was managed through email and password credentials or Facebook login, creating security concerns and unwanted dependency on an external private platform. The pilot addressed the need for a more open, inclusive and privacy-preserving model for community interaction, where users could participate without unnecessarily sharing personal data. The implementation introduced Attribute-Based Credentials as an alternative login and access mechanism. This allowed communities using GebiedOnline to define which credentials they would accept, while giving users more granular control over the data they shared with the platform and with other members. Conceptually, the pilot combined decentralised identity, selective disclosure and community-governed access rules. The expected impact was to reduce platform dependency, improve security and strengthen trust in neighbourhood-level digital participation. By allowing each community to determine acceptable credentials, the pilot also supported local autonomy and flexibility. It demonstrated how small-scale civic platforms could adopt privacy-enhancing identity infrastructure while remaining inclusive, open-source oriented and aligned with the needs of community users.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-761,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00737,EU_PSTW_202609_00737,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Italian National Institute for Insurance against Accidents at Work (INAIL),General public services n.e.c.,"Catalonia's Open Administration Consortium (AOC) receives over 91,000 annual support requests, with citizens struggling to navigate complex e-government procedures for digital identification and certificate management.",Public administration employee skills cerification,Italian National Institute for Insurance against Accidents at Work (INAIL); Central-Government; General public services n.e.c.; Italy,"The AOC deployed La Paula (January 2021) and La Rita (April 2021) as AI-powered chatbot assistants supporting the VALid identification service and idCAT Certificate respectively, developed in partnership with 1MillionBot. The chatbots use natural language processing to interpret citizen queries and provide instant responses 24/7, achieving over 95% accuracy rate.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Catalonia's Open Administration Consortium (AOC) receives over 91,000 annual support requests, with citizens struggling to navigate complex e-government procedures for digital identification and certificate management. The AOC deployed La Paula (January 2021) and La Rita (April 2021) as AI-powered chatbot assistants supporting the VALid identification service and idCAT Certificate respectively, developed in partnership with 1MillionBot. The chatbots use natural language processing to interpret citizen queries and provide instant responses 24/7, achieving over 95% accuracy rate. In 2022, Paula supported 62,588 people with 127,454 messages whilst Rita assisted 43,520 people through 100,606 messages. The AOC has pioneered algorithmic transparency in Spain, publishing detailed documentation of chatbot operation, governance, and limitations. In May 2024, the AOC upgraded to generative AI chatbots, serving 11,575 monthly users with 88% success rate and 90% cost savings per query.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-762,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00738,EU_PSTW_202609_00738,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,HM Land Registry (HMLR),"General economic, commercial and labour affair","Italy loses over €99 billion annually to tax evasion, with €30 billion from VAT alone, whilst traditional enforcement methods struggled to identify non-compliant taxpayers among millions of returns.",Buying and selling property with blockchain,"HM Land Registry (HMLR); Central-Government; General economic, commercial and labour affair; United Kingdom","The Agenzia delle Entrate developed VeRa (Verifica dei Rapporti finanziari), an AI-powered algorithm that cross-references taxpayer filings with data from the Financial Relations Archive, property registries, bank accounts, and electronic payments to identify compliance risks.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Italy loses over €99 billion annually to tax evasion, with €30 billion from VAT alone, whilst traditional enforcement methods struggled to identify non-compliant taxpayers among millions of returns. The Agenzia delle Entrate developed VeRa (Verifica dei Rapporti finanziari), an AI-powered algorithm that cross-references taxpayer filings with data from the Financial Relations Archive, property registries, bank accounts, and electronic payments to identify compliance risks. In 2022, VeRa flagged one million high-risk cases and helped prevent over €6.8 billion in fraud. The system assigns risk scores enabling targeted audits rather than random checks, whilst EU-funded projects through Sogei introduce network analysis, machine learning, and data visualisation for complex evasion schemes. In 2023, the agency recovered €18.1 billion, increasing to projected €19.6 billion by 2025. A complementary AI chatbot launched in 2023 analyses VAT returns in real-time, flagging irregularities for immediate investigation.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-763,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00739,EU_PSTW_202609_00739,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,LuxTrust,General services.,UNKNOWN,Government-backed firm to launch Blockchain IDs,LuxTrust; Private sector; General services.; Luxembourg,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Digital identity management in Luxembourg required solutions balancing strong authentication with GDPR-compliant privacy protection, particularly for the financial services sector where identity verification is fundamental. LuxTrust, a trust service provider two-thirds owned by the Luxembourg state, partnered with Cambridge Blockchain (Massachusetts) to develop IDKEEP, a blockchain-based identity platform enabling users to control their personal data whilst maintaining verified credentials. The platform launched in private beta in 2019, building on LuxTrust's existing subscriber base of over 500,000 users who rely on digital certificates for authentication. InfoCert (Italy) joined as an additional partner, whilst the project attracted investment from PayPal, Flourish Ventures, and Omidyar Network. The initiative demonstrates how public-private partnerships can advance self-sovereign identity concepts within regulated sectors.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-764,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00740,EU_PSTW_202609_00740,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,European Comission,General services.,"Global mangrove mapping relied on infrequently updated datasets that could not capture the dynamic changes affecting these critical coastal ecosystems, limiting understanding of carbon sequestration, biodiversity, and natural coastal protection.",The European Blockchain Services Infrastructure (EBSI),European Comission; European Institution/Agency; General services.; European Union,"The UK Hydrographic Office (UKHO) developed the ADMIRALTY Global Mangrove Data Set using deep learning applied to European Space Agency Sentinel-2 satellite imagery, creating the world's highest resolution, most up-to-date global view of mangrove forests.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Global mangrove mapping relied on infrequently updated datasets that could not capture the dynamic changes affecting these critical coastal ecosystems, limiting understanding of carbon sequestration, biodiversity, and natural coastal protection. The UK Hydrographic Office (UKHO) developed the ADMIRALTY Global Mangrove Data Set using deep learning applied to European Space Agency Sentinel-2 satellite imagery, creating the world's highest resolution, most up-to-date global view of mangrove forests. The neural network was trained on over 7,000 coastal images and achieves geo-generalisability across diverse global locations through collaboration between UKHO's Data Science, Data Engineering, and Remote Sensing teams. The system automatically updates every six months, processing new cloud-free satellite imagery to detect mangrove extent changes. This serverless architecture enables scalable analysis supporting marine navigation, coastal sustainability planning, and climate change research on these vital carbon sinks that sequester 3-5 times more carbon than inland tropical forests.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-765,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00741,EU_PSTW_202609_00741,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Ukraine Central Election Commission,General services.,"Italy's education system faced the annual challenge of assigning approximately 100,000 teachers to schools across diverse regions, a complex optimization problem that had historically relied on manual processes prone to errors and delays.",Voting Trial Using NEM's Blockchain,Ukraine Central Election Commission; Central-Government; General services.; Ukraine,"The Ministry of Education implemented an algorithmic system to automatically assign teachers to schools based on preferences, qualifications, and regional needs, processing massive datasets to generate placements within compressed timeframes.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Italy's education system faced the annual challenge of assigning approximately 100,000 teachers to schools across diverse regions, a complex optimization problem that had historically relied on manual processes prone to errors and delays. The Ministry of Education implemented an algorithmic system to automatically assign teachers to schools based on preferences, qualifications, and regional needs, processing massive datasets to generate placements within compressed timeframes. However, the 2020 deployment generated significant controversy when thousands of teachers received assignments to schools far from their preferences or in regions they had not selected, with some relocated hundreds of kilometres from their homes. The algorithm's logic and decision criteria remained opaque, preventing affected teachers from understanding or effectively challenging their placements. The case became a prominent example in debates about algorithmic accountability in public administration, highlighting tensions between efficiency gains and transparency requirements in automated government decision-making.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK|F08_ERROR_COMPLIANCE_RISK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-767,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00742,EU_PSTW_202609_00742,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Ministry of Education,R&D education.,"International credential recognition in Italy faced challenges with verification delays and document fraud, creating barriers for qualified professionals seeking employment or further education.",Electronic qualification management system,Ministry of Education; Central-Government; R&D education.; Italy,"Italy became the first country to use blockchain technology specifically for credential evaluation processes, providing holders with a digital wallet containing their verified qualifications.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"International credential recognition in Italy faced challenges with verification delays and document fraud, creating barriers for qualified professionals seeking employment or further education. CIMEA, the Italian ENIC-NARIC centre responsible for foreign qualification recognition, developed Diplome as a private permissioned blockchain platform for automatic notarisation of Statements of Comparability and Verification. Italy became the first country to use blockchain technology specifically for credential evaluation processes, providing holders with a digital wallet containing their verified qualifications. In March 2024, CIMEA Diplome attestations were accepted as equivalent to the Declaration of Value for EU Blue Card applications, significantly streamlining immigration procedures for skilled workers. The CIMEA Diplome Chain infrastructure continues to expand, supporting European mobility initiatives and international recognition frameworks.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-768,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00743,EU_PSTW_202609_00743,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Istituto Superiore di Sanità,Public health services.,UNKNOWN,Study of therapies for viral hepatitis,Istituto Superiore di Sanità; Central-Government; Public health services.; Italy,"The Federal Customs Administration (Zoll) deployed LinA, an AI-powered chatbot that answers general inquiries about customs matters through natural language interaction available 24/7 on the authority's website.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Germany's customs authority receives high volumes of citizen inquiries about import duties, customs procedures, and international trade regulations, creating significant workload for staff and variable response times for users. The Federal Customs Administration (Zoll) deployed LinA, an AI-powered chatbot that answers general inquiries about customs matters through natural language interaction available 24/7 on the authority's website. The system handles routine questions about duty calculations, prohibited goods, travel allowances, and customs procedures, freeing human agents to address complex cases requiring specialist expertise. LinA is part of Germany's broader Bundesbots initiative developing standardised chatbot infrastructure for federal agencies, sharing technological approaches and ensuring consistent user experience across government services. The platform demonstrates how conversational AI can improve public service accessibility whilst managing increasing demand for digital interaction channels with government authorities.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-769,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00744,EU_PSTW_202609_00744,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Andorra's Government,Secondary education.,"The system provides graduates with internationally recognisable proof of their qualifications, reducing verification delays and eliminating risks of credential fraud.",Academic degrees storage,Andorra's Government; Central-Government; Secondary education.; Andorra,"The government implemented blockchain technology to store all academic qualifications issued by Andorran higher education institutions, creating tamper-proof digital credentials that can be verified independently without contacting issuing institutions. The initiative demonstrates how smaller nations can leverage blockchain to enhance the international recognition of their educational systems.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Andorra's small higher education system required modernisation to ensure academic credentials would be readily verifiable by international employers and institutions, supporting graduate mobility in the European region. The government implemented blockchain technology to store all academic qualifications issued by Andorran higher education institutions, creating tamper-proof digital credentials that can be verified independently without contacting issuing institutions. This digital transformation aligned with broader national strategies to position Andorra as an innovative jurisdiction embracing emerging technologies. The system provides graduates with internationally recognisable proof of their qualifications, reducing verification delays and eliminating risks of credential fraud. The initiative demonstrates how smaller nations can leverage blockchain to enhance the international recognition of their educational systems.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-770,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00746,EU_PSTW_202609_00746,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Repubblica di San Marino,Environmental protection n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,Sustainability: rewarding best practices,Repubblica di San Marino; Central-Government; Environmental protection n.e.c.; San Marino,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Consumers in Germany are increasingly purchasing food and cosmetics online, with a rising trend in online purchases of various items including kitchen utensils, toys, clothing, and tobacco products. The Federal Office of Consumer Protection and Food Safety (BVL) hosts a central unit called G@ZIELT, jointly managed by federal states, to regulate internet trade in products covered by the Food and Feed Code and tobacco. The G@ZIELT unit searches the internet to identify risky food offers, misleading labels, and unregistered businesses, reporting findings to appropriate authorities.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-774,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00747,EU_PSTW_202609_00747,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Ministero dell’Economia e delle Finanze,General services.,UNKNOWN,PoSeID-on - Protection and control of Secured Information by means of a privacy enhanced Dashboard,Ministero dell’Economia e delle Finanze; Consortium; General services.; Italy,"The Federal Police implemented EasyPASS, an automated border control system using biometric facial recognition developed by Bundesdruckerei, secunet, and Cognitec to verify travellers' identities against their e-passport chip photos. The system is deployed at Frankfurt, Munich, Hamburg, Hannover, Düsseldorf, Stuttgart, Berlin Brandenburg, and Cologne/Bonn airports, available to EU/EEA citizens aged 12+ and registered third-country nationals from the USA, Hong Kong, South Korea, Taiwan, and the UK.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"International airport border control in Germany faced growing passenger volumes requiring faster processing whilst maintaining security standards and compliance with Schengen external border regulations. The Federal Police implemented EasyPASS, an automated border control system using biometric facial recognition developed by Bundesdruckerei, secunet, and Cognitec to verify travellers' identities against their e-passport chip photos. The system is deployed at Frankfurt, Munich, Hamburg, Hannover, Düsseldorf, Stuttgart, Berlin Brandenburg, and Cologne/Bonn airports, available to EU/EEA citizens aged 12+ and registered third-country nationals from the USA, Hong Kong, South Korea, Taiwan, and the UK. Travellers place their e-passport on the document reader, then proceed through gates where cameras capture facial images for 1:1 comparison against stored biometric data, with information also checked against Schengen databases. Data is stored for 48 hours (extendable to 30 days for police investigations), and the system strictly limits facial recognition to identity verification purposes under the Passport Act.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-775,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00748,EU_PSTW_202609_00748,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Republic of Moldova,Police Services,UNKNOWN,Identity system to combat child trafficking,Republic of Moldova; Central-Government; Police Services; Moldova,"Using the chat bot, people can call up general information, arrange for callbacks, and determine time windows for contact purposes. It is also possible to use the chat bot to submit an initial application for accompanying assistance.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Office of Integration of the Ministry for Social Affairs, Health, Family, Youth, and Senior Citizens integrated a chat bot into its website in February 2020. This chat bot is being further developed in a follow-on project. Using the chat bot, people can call up general information, arrange for callbacks, and determine time windows for contact purposes. It is also possible to use the chat bot to submit an initial application for accompanying assistance.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-776,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00749,EU_PSTW_202609_00749,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,The University of Surrey,General services.,UNKNOWN,Safeguarding the integrity and accessibility of digital government records of national archives,The University of Surrey; Consortium; General services.; United Kingdom,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"School districts should be designed in such a way that schools have as even a capacity as possible and the distances to school for children are short. At the same time, it is important to achieve a social mix among the students. In order to achieve these goals, school office employees in the Berlin district of Tempelhof-Schoeneberg have invested a lot of time. Streets had to be walked through one by one, information was recorded in Excel spreadsheets. This work is now done by an algorithm that automatically outputs a first draft of the cut based on the available data from the State Statistical Office.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-777,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00750,EU_PSTW_202609_00750,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,The University of Nicosia,R&D education.,"Academic institutions worldwide lacked practical mechanisms for issuing credentials that could be independently verified without institutional involvement, creating inefficiencies for employers and graduates alike.",Blockchain Verified Certificates,The University of Nicosia; Academic-Research; R&D education.; Cyprus,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Academic institutions worldwide lacked practical mechanisms for issuing credentials that could be independently verified without institutional involvement, creating inefficiencies for employers and graduates alike. The University of Nicosia became the first university globally to issue academic certificates verified on the Bitcoin blockchain, pioneering a model subsequently adopted by institutions worldwide. The university launched the world's first Digital Currency MOOC in 2014, which has reached over 145,000 students across 120 countries, taught by leading experts including Andreas Antonopoulos and Professor George Giaglis. In 2024, marking the MOOC's 10th anniversary, the programme was restructured into two levels covering introductory and advanced blockchain concepts. The university also established the first MSc in Blockchain and Digital Currency and first MSc in Metaverse, whilst becoming the first accredited institution to accept Bitcoin for tuition payments.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-778,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00751,EU_PSTW_202609_00751,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Naples,General services.,"The purpose of this initiative is to free up the time that workers currently spend on manual, repetitive tasks that are of little added value, so they can devote their time to providing real value to the organisation and reduce the time it takes to respond to requests.",Electronic voting system,City of Naples; Local Government; General services.; Italy,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Robot Automation Plan has created a new service for robotic process automation. The aim behind the project is to provide the City Council with innovative solutions, such as increasing the quality of citizen help and information services and reducing human error in processes that are repetitive and with little added value, increasing the Council’s production capacity, for example in processing licences for café terraces, and increasing the guarantee and transparency of processes and ensuring coherency in the data processed. The purpose of this initiative is to free up the time that workers currently spend on manual, repetitive tasks that are of little added value, so they can devote their time to providing real value to the organisation and reduce the time it takes to respond to requests.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-780,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00752,EU_PSTW_202609_00752,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Government of Malta,Housing and community amenities n.e.c.,"Malta's rental housing market suffered from widespread informal contracts and limited regulatory oversight, creating problems for tenants lacking legal protections and enabling tax evasion through unreported rental income.",Registration of Property Rental Contracts,Government of Malta; Central-Government; Housing and community amenities n.e.c.; Malta,"The system uses smart contracts to ensure documents cannot be tampered with after registration whilst maintaining controlled access for authorised parties including landlords, tenants, and regulatory authorities.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Malta's rental housing market suffered from widespread informal contracts and limited regulatory oversight, creating problems for tenants lacking legal protections and enabling tax evasion through unreported rental income. In June 2019, the Cabinet approved regulations requiring all property rental contracts to be registered on blockchain, making Malta the first country to mandate distributed ledger technology for residential tenancy agreements. The system uses smart contracts to ensure documents cannot be tampered with after registration whilst maintaining controlled access for authorised parties including landlords, tenants, and regulatory authorities. Ledger Projects, with developers David Schranz and Anthony Mamo, created a supporting application for property transfer management. The initiative aims to eliminate informal rental arrangements, protect tenant rights, and improve tax compliance in the housing sector.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-781,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00753,EU_PSTW_202609_00753,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,UK government,Social protection n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,Solution for welfare payments in the UK,UK government; Central-Government; Social protection n.e.c.; United Kingdom,Nantes Métropole implemented an algorithmic system for solidarity transport pricing (Tarification Solidaire Transports) on its Naolib public transport network.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Public transport affordability is crucial for social inclusion, yet fixed-price ticketing can exclude low-income residents from mobility services essential for employment, healthcare access, and social participation. Nantes Métropole implemented an algorithmic system for solidarity transport pricing (Tarification Solidaire Transports) on its Naolib public transport network. The algorithm determines eligibility for free travel or discounts up to 50% on unlimited annual passes based on household income and family composition. All households with a family quotient of 900 or below qualify for solidarity pricing. Users apply at their local town hall, where agents collect supporting documents and enter information into Deltas, software developed by Nantes Métropole. The algorithm processes data in real time, immediately notifying applicants of their entitlement level. Benefits apply to all household members regardless of age. Since May 2023, the eligibility threshold was raised from 600 to 900 family quotient, significantly expanding coverage. The solidarity pricing programme extends beyond metropolitan residents to include commuters from surrounding areas who travel within Nantes Métropole. This ensures transport affordability for the most vulnerable populations whilst supporting labour market access and social cohesion across the metropolitan region.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-782,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00754,EU_PSTW_202609_00754,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Generalitat de Catalunya,General services.,UNKNOWN,Decentralised identity platform,Generalitat de Catalunya; Regional Government; General services.; Spain,The solution uses distributed ledger technology to establish a decentralised identity platform where the Catalan government acts as validator rather than data custodian.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Citizens across Europe increasingly interact with online public services that require secure identity verification, yet centralised identity systems raise concerns about data control, privacy and cross-border interoperability. The Government of Catalonia initiated the IdentiCAT pilot to give residents control over their own digital identities and enable secure engagement with online services without the government collecting personal data centrally. The objective is to align regional e-identification with EU eIDAS standards, enabling citizens to authenticate and transact with confidence not only within Catalonia but across other EU member states. The solution uses distributed ledger technology to establish a decentralised identity platform where the Catalan government acts as validator rather than data custodian. Personal identity claims and attestations are anchored on the ledger, ensuring transparency and immutability while supporting user-controlled consent for data disclosure. The platform integrates with public service portals and service providers to enable access, proof of identity, and electronic transactions under recognised standards. By decentralising identity management, IdentiCAT enhances privacy, reduces dependency on central databases, and supports cross-border recognition of digital identities. As a pilot, the project demonstrates scalable infrastructure for citizen empowerment, secure online interactions and compliance with EU digital identification frameworks.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-783,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00755,EU_PSTW_202609_00755,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Bari,Economic affairs n.e.c,"Public administrations traditionally manage surety policies through paper-based and fragmented procedures that are slow, error-prone and difficult to verify, exposing municipalities to fraud and administrative inefficiencies.",Digitallisation of surety policies' management process,Municipality of Bari; Local Government; Economic affairs n.e.c; Italy,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Public administrations traditionally manage surety policies through paper-based and fragmented procedures that are slow, error-prone and difficult to verify, exposing municipalities to fraud and administrative inefficiencies. The Municipality of Bari, together with banks, financial intermediaries, insurance companies and SIA as technology partner, launched this pilot to modernise the issuance and validation of surety guarantees required in public procurement and administrative processes. The objective is to simplify workflows, ensure authenticity and irrevocability of guarantees, and reduce processing times and disputes for all stakeholders. The solution implements a distributed ledger infrastructure where each surety policy is issued, certified and recorded as a unique digital record. Guarantees are registered once and become tamper-proof, with authorised issuers writing validated data to the ledger and the municipality accessing a single, trusted source of truth. Integration with existing administrative systems enables end-to-end digital handling without duplicating data. The pilot delivers faster issuance and verification, reduced administrative overhead, improved auditability and legal certainty, and lower fraud risk. It represents the first Italian public-sector experimentation of this kind and provides a scalable model for other municipalities seeking trusted digital guarantee management.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-784,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00756,EU_PSTW_202609_00756,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,University of Cagliari,Post-secondary non-tertiary education.,"Italian universities faced increasing challenges with diploma verification requests and credential fraud, whilst graduates required faster proof of qualifications for job applications and further study. This approach enables independent verification by employers or other institutions without requiring contact with the university, reducing administrative burden whilst providing tamper-proof evidence of qualification authenticity.",Ensuring the validity and integrity of European degree certificates,University of Cagliari; Academic-Research; Post-secondary non-tertiary education.; Italy,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Italian universities faced increasing challenges with diploma verification requests and credential fraud, whilst graduates required faster proof of qualifications for job applications and further study. The University of Cagliari became the first Italian university to issue blockchain-validated academic degrees, storing cryptographic hashes of diploma data on the public Ethereum network. This approach enables independent verification by employers or other institutions without requiring contact with the university, reducing administrative burden whilst providing tamper-proof evidence of qualification authenticity. The system supports European Higher Education Area mobility objectives by facilitating cross-border credential recognition. The initiative established a model subsequently adopted by other Italian institutions seeking to modernise their credential issuance processes.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-785,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00757,EU_PSTW_202609_00757,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipal Transport Company of Madrid (EMT),Transport,"Madrid’s public transport system involves multiple operators and services, leading to fragmented onboarding, duplicated user accounts and inconsistent payment experiences.",Payments on Madrid's buses with blockchain,Municipal Transport Company of Madrid (EMT); Local Government; Transport; Spain,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Madrid’s public transport system involves multiple operators and services, leading to fragmented onboarding, duplicated user accounts and inconsistent payment experiences. In partnership with Banco Santander and Vottun, the city sought to simplify access to public mobility while strengthening data security and interoperability across services. The objective of this pilot is to provide citizens with a single registration and payment interface covering buses, taxis and electric vehicle charging, reducing friction for users and operational complexity for providers. The solution is built around a blockchain-enabled certification layer that securely records user credentials and transactions, allowing a single digital identity and payment profile to be reused across transport services. Transaction records are cryptographically secured, while backend systems integrate with existing ticketing and payment infrastructures. The result is a unified mobility app that enables seamless payments and service access with enhanced data protection. Expected impacts include improved user experience, reduced onboarding costs, better interoperability between transport services and increased trust in digital payments, supporting Madrid’s transition towards integrated smart mobility services.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-786,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00758,EU_PSTW_202609_00758,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""social_nonprofit""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Groves Medical Group,Hospital services.,"Patients and healthcare providers often struggle with data silos, delayed access to records, and unclear consent management.",Enabling the transparent exchange of medical records,Groves Medical Group; Private sector; Hospital services.; United Kingdom,"This use case addresses the fragmentation and limited patient control of medical records within healthcare systems. The platform integrates video consultation services and enables payments using digital tokens, incentivising the use of telemedicine services.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"This use case addresses the fragmentation and limited patient control of medical records within healthcare systems. Patients and healthcare providers often struggle with data silos, delayed access to records, and unclear consent management. The Proof of Concept, conducted with Groves Medical Group in London, involves patients, doctors, and healthcare administrators. Its objective is to enable patients to securely manage, share, and monetise access to their medical records, while supporting flexible care delivery such as remote consultations. The solution is delivered through a blockchain-based health data platform where patients create digital wallets to manage permissions to their medical records. Health data references and access rights are recorded immutably, while clinical data remains securely stored off-chain. The platform integrates video consultation services and enables payments using digital tokens, incentivising the use of telemedicine services. The Proof of Concept enhances patient empowerment, data transparency, and trust between patients and clinicians. It improves access to care, supports new service models, and provides a scalable foundation for secure health data exchange, with positive implications for efficiency, user experience, and continuity of care.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F07_DATA_VISIBILITY_INTEGRATION_GAP,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-787,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00759,EU_PSTW_202609_00759,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,The Gendarmerie’s cybercrime division (C3N),Police Services,"The pilot has reduced administrative burden, improved financial traceability, and strengthened accountability while preserving operational secrecy.",Validation of judicial expenses incurred during investigations,The Gendarmerie’s cybercrime division (C3N); Central-Government; Police Services; France,The system uses smart contracts deployed on a public blockchain to manage authorised expense validation workflows.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Law enforcement agencies face complex administrative requirements when validating operational expenses, particularly in sensitive investigations where transparency must be balanced with confidentiality. The French Gendarmerie’s cybercrime division (C3N), together with defence and judicial authorities, launched this pilot to improve traceability and auditability of judicial expenses without exposing operational details. The objective is to simplify expense validation, ensure compliance, and enable secure oversight of funds used during investigations. The system uses smart contracts deployed on a public blockchain to manage authorised expense validation workflows. Only approved actors can interact with the contracts, which record transactions immutably and enable auditing of fund usage while abstracting sensitive operational information. Integration with internal financial systems allows expenses to be justified and reviewed efficiently. The pilot has reduced administrative burden, improved financial traceability, and strengthened accountability while preserving operational secrecy. It demonstrates how distributed ledger systems can support trusted financial governance in high-security public sector contexts.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-789,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00760,EU_PSTW_202609_00760,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""social_nonprofit""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,The Chamber of Industry and Commerce of Munich and Upper Bavaria,Education not definable by level.,"Employers and institutions often struggle to verify the authenticity of training certificates, while issuing bodies face increasing administrative workloads and fraud risks.",Blockchain-Based Training Certificates,The Chamber of Industry and Commerce of Munich and Upper Bavaria; Regional Government; Education not definable by level.; Germany,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Employers and institutions often struggle to verify the authenticity of training certificates, while issuing bodies face increasing administrative workloads and fraud risks. The Chamber of Industry and Commerce of Munich and Upper Bavaria, in cooperation with the Bavarian Digital Ministry, launched this pilot to modernise vocational certificate issuance and verification. The objective is to enable instant, reliable validation of qualifications while simplifying processes for graduates, employers and certifying authorities. Certificates are issued digitally, with cryptographic hash values anchored on a blockchain. Graduates share a PDF certificate and a verification key, which employers can upload to a web application to instantly confirm authenticity against the ledger. The solution integrates with existing certification workflows without exposing personal data. The pilot improves trust in credentials, reduces verification time from days to seconds, lowers administrative costs, and enhances labour market efficiency. It provides a scalable foundation for broader adoption of digital credentials across education and training systems.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-790,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00761,EU_PSTW_202609_00761,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Italian Society of Authors and Publishers (SIAE),Cultural services.,"Copyright management requires handling vast volumes of metadata, rights information and transactions, often across fragmented systems that lack transparency and efficiency.",Copyright Management,Italian Society of Authors and Publishers (SIAE); Central-Government; Cultural services.; Italy,The platform uses a public blockchain based on a pure Proof-of-Stake protocol to record rights metadata and transactions immutably.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Copyright management requires handling vast volumes of metadata, rights information and transactions, often across fragmented systems that lack transparency and efficiency. The Italian Society of Authors and Publishers (SIAE), together with Algorand, launched this pilot to modernise copyright registration, rights tracking and remuneration processes. The objective is to create a transparent, secure and scalable ecosystem that better serves creators, publishers and rights holders while reducing disputes and administrative costs. The platform uses a public blockchain based on a pure Proof-of-Stake protocol to record rights metadata and transactions immutably. Smart contracts manage ownership, licensing events and revenue distribution, while system integrations connect existing rights management databases to the ledger. The pilot enhances traceability, improves data integrity and supports high transaction throughput. Expected impacts include faster rights registration, improved transparency for creators, reduced reconciliation costs, and a future-proof infrastructure for digital copyright management at national scale.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-791,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00762,EU_PSTW_202609_00762,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Austrian Federal Ministry for Climate Action, Environment, Energy, Mobility, Innovation, and Technology",Environmental protection n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,Finding Waste Heat Spots,"Austrian Federal Ministry for Climate Action, Environment, Energy, Mobility, Innovation, and Technology; Central-Government; Environmental protection n.e.c.; Austria","The HotCity’s pilot, backed by the Austrian government and deployed in Vienna and Graz, addresses this inefficiency by engaging citizens in identifying waste heat sources.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Large amounts of industrial waste heat remain unused in cities, despite growing demand for sustainable district heating solutions. The HotCity’s pilot, backed by the Austrian government and deployed in Vienna and Graz, addresses this inefficiency by engaging citizens in identifying waste heat sources. Stakeholders include municipalities, energy providers, industrial operators and residents. The objective is to crowdsource reliable data on waste heat availability and integrate it into district heating planning, improving energy efficiency and reducing emissions. The pilot combines a digital platform with a blockchain-based incentive mechanism that records submissions and rewards contributors with tokens for validated data. Submitted location data is verified and aggregated to support urban energy planning. The approach improves data coverage at low cost while ensuring transparency and trust in contributions. The pilot demonstrates increased citizen engagement, better identification of usable waste heat, and potential reductions in CO₂ emissions by integrating industrial surplus heat into existing heating networks.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-792,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00763,EU_PSTW_202609_00763,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,TIXnGO,Public health services.,"Existing paper-based certificates were difficult to verify at scale, easy to falsify and slow to distribute. Health n Go, developed by Swiss company TIXnGO, repurposed an existing digital ticketing infrastructure to address this challenge by enabling the secure issuance and presentation of health certificates.",Health Certificates To Address Lockdowns,TIXnGO; Private sector; Public health services.; Switzerland,"The solution uses distributed ledger technology to issue each certificate with a unique, encrypted and traceable digital identity anchored on a blockchain.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"During the COVID-19 pandemic, governments, employers and event organisers required reliable ways to verify individuals’ health status in order to manage access to workplaces, transport and public spaces while limiting lockdowns. Existing paper-based certificates were difficult to verify at scale, easy to falsify and slow to distribute. Health n Go, developed by Swiss company TIXnGO, repurposed an existing digital ticketing infrastructure to address this challenge by enabling the secure issuance and presentation of health certificates. Stakeholders included healthcare providers, public authorities, organisations managing access control, and citizens needing proof of health status. The objective was to provide a scalable, secure and privacy-preserving mechanism for distributing trusted health certificates. The solution uses distributed ledger technology to issue each certificate with a unique, encrypted and traceable digital identity anchored on a blockchain. Certificates are stored in a mobile wallet on the user’s smartphone and can be presented via a QR code, which is scanned and verified against the ledger without revealing unnecessary personal data. The pilot enabled rapid deployment of digital health passes, reduced fraud risk, improved verification speed, and supported safer reopening strategies. It demonstrated the feasibility of decentralised digital certification in crisis response contexts.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-793,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00764,EU_PSTW_202609_00764,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,VeChain,Public health services.,"Healthcare providers face challenges in securely managing and sharing patient medical records while ensuring data integrity, privacy and accessibility. Fragmented systems and limited interoperability can delay treatment and reduce patient control over personal health information.",Medical Data Management,VeChain; Private sector; Public health services.; Cyprus,"To improve transparency and empower patients, VeChain and I-Dante co-developed a distributed ledger-based medical data management platform, deployed at the Mediterranean Hospital of Cyprus. The solution has been implemented in pilot deployment within the hospital environment.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Healthcare providers face challenges in securely managing and sharing patient medical records while ensuring data integrity, privacy and accessibility. Fragmented systems and limited interoperability can delay treatment and reduce patient control over personal health information. To improve transparency and empower patients, VeChain and I-Dante co-developed a distributed ledger-based medical data management platform, deployed at the Mediterranean Hospital of Cyprus. The objective is to provide secure, patient-centred record management while improving traceability and reliability of clinical data. The platform records encrypted medical data references on a blockchain infrastructure, ensuring tamper-resistant logging of transactions and access events. Patients access their records through mobile interfaces, while backend systems integrate with hospital information systems via secure APIs. Cryptographic validation mechanisms ensure that data integrity can be verified without exposing sensitive content on-chain. The first deployment phase in the emergency department enables identity verification, queue management and direct patient interaction with medical records. The solution has been implemented in pilot deployment within the hospital environment. It enhances transparency, strengthens data integrity, improves patient engagement and streamlines administrative processes while maintaining compliance with healthcare data protection standards.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-794,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00765,EU_PSTW_202609_00765,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Zermatt,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.",UNKNOWN,Tax Payments with bitcoins in the City of Zermatt,"City of Zermatt; Local Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Switzerland","Zermatt became the second Swiss municipality after Zug to accept Bitcoin for tax payments, allowing residents to settle obligations up to CHF 250 (approximately €230) using cryptocurrency.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Switzerland's canton system allows municipalities flexibility in payment methods, enabling innovative approaches to public finance that could position localities as attractive destinations for cryptocurrency businesses and residents. Zermatt became the second Swiss municipality after Zug to accept Bitcoin for tax payments, allowing residents to settle obligations up to CHF 250 (approximately €230) using cryptocurrency. The system operates through partnership with Bitcoin Suisse AG, which provides instant conversion to Swiss Francs, eliminating volatility risk for the municipal treasury whilst offering convenience for crypto-holding residents. All transactions maintain full regulatory compliance including anti-money laundering requirements. The initiative reflects Switzerland's broader approach of integrating cryptocurrency into mainstream financial services whilst maintaining regulatory oversight.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-795,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00766,EU_PSTW_202609_00766,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Fim Cisl,Religious and other community services.,"The blockchain-based union card initiative aims to address challenges related to membership verification, data integrity, and secure communication between trade unions and their members.",Blockchain-based union card,Fim Cisl; Non-governmental; Religious and other community services.; Italy,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The blockchain-based union card initiative aims to address challenges related to membership verification, data integrity, and secure communication between trade unions and their members. Traditional membership systems are vulnerable to duplication, outdated records, and privacy risks. The Italian trade union Fim Cisl, its members, and union administrators are the primary stakeholders. The objective is to ensure unique, tamper-proof membership registration while enabling trusted, ongoing digital engagement between the union and its members. The solution consists of a tokenised digital union card built on the Ethereum blockchain. Membership credentials are issued as digital tokens managed through mobile applications and wallets. Transactions and updates are recorded immutably, ensuring that each membership is uniquely registered and verifiable, while personal data is protected through cryptographic mechanisms. The initiative provides certified and transparent membership records, reduces the risk of fraud or data manipulation, and strengthens privacy protection. It also creates a secure digital channel for sharing information, announcements, and services, improving member engagement and enabling scalable, trusted union operations.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-796,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00767,EU_PSTW_202609_00767,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Regione Lombardia per la trasformazione digitale,"Agriculture, forestry, fishing and hunting",UNKNOWN,Traceability of meat and milk,"Regione Lombardia per la trasformazione digitale; Regional Government; Agriculture, forestry, fishing and hunting; Italy","Each product package includes a QR code that consumers can scan to access structured information pages containing official data from public authorities and companies, including inspection outcomes on animal health, drug use, hygiene conditions and third-party animal welfare controls.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Consumers increasingly demand transparency regarding the origin, safety and quality of agri-food products, while producers and public authorities require reliable mechanisms to demonstrate compliance with health and animal welfare standards. In Lombardy, the regional administration sought to strengthen trust across the agri-food supply chain by providing verifiable information on meat and milk products available in supermarkets. Stakeholders include consumers, farmers, processing plants, regional health inspectors, third-party certification bodies and food retailers. The objective is to enhance transparency, improve communication between companies and citizens, and reinforce food safety oversight through a trusted traceability framework. The solution is based on a blockchain infrastructure that records key supply chain events and inspection results in an immutable ledger. Each product package includes a QR code that consumers can scan to access structured information pages containing official data from public authorities and companies, including inspection outcomes on animal health, drug use, hygiene conditions and third-party animal welfare controls. The system integrates supply chain data inputs with public administration databases to ensure data integrity and accessibility. Expected impacts include increased consumer confidence, improved brand value for compliant producers, enhanced regulatory transparency and more efficient information sharing across the agri-food ecosystem.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-797,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00768,EU_PSTW_202609_00768,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""social_nonprofit""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Villa Flaminia international high schools,Secondary education.,"Paper-based diplomas are vulnerable to loss, forgery, and costly verification processes.",Digital baccalaureate diplomas,Villa Flaminia international high schools; Academic-Research; Secondary education.; Italy,"This use case addresses the need for secure, verifiable, and easily shareable educational credentials.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"This use case addresses the need for secure, verifiable, and easily shareable educational credentials. Paper-based diplomas are vulnerable to loss, forgery, and costly verification processes. The project involves Villa Flaminia International High Schools, students, and external stakeholders such as universities and employers. Its objective is to issue tamper-proof digital baccalaureate diplomas that certify both the final qualification and the student’s broader educational pathway. The solution issues digital diplomas certified on the public Ethereum platform. Key milestones of the student’s training process, including courses and subjects completed, are notarised and anchored to the ledger. Credentials can be shared digitally, with third parties able to verify authenticity without relying on intermediaries. The initiative improves trust and efficiency in credential verification, reduces administrative costs, and enhances student mobility. It delivers a better user experience for graduates and institutions while providing a scalable, reliable foundation for lifelong digital education records.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-798,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00769,EU_PSTW_202609_00769,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,The Finnish Immigration Service,Social protection n.e.c.,"Asylum seekers in Finland faced significant barriers accessing financial services, as traditional banking requires identity documentation that refugees often cannot provide, forcing reliance on cash and excluding them from the formal economy. MIT Technology Review recognised the initiative as a pioneering example of blockchain technology addressing humanitarian challenges.",Kickstarting the Financial Lives of Refugees in Finland,The Finnish Immigration Service; Central-Government; Social protection n.e.c.; Finland,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Asylum seekers in Finland faced significant barriers accessing financial services, as traditional banking requires identity documentation that refugees often cannot provide, forcing reliance on cash and excluding them from the formal economy. The Finnish Immigration Service partnered with MONI, a Finnish fintech startup, to provide asylum seekers with prepaid Mastercards linked to blockchain-based digital identities, replacing cash-based support systems. Several thousand participants received cards enabling participation in the formal economy whilst building transaction histories that could support future creditworthiness assessments. The blockchain component provides identity verification without requiring traditional documentation, enabling financial inclusion for individuals whose identity records may be incomplete or inaccessible. MIT Technology Review recognised the initiative as a pioneering example of blockchain technology addressing humanitarian challenges.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-799,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00770,EU_PSTW_202609_00770,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Ministry of Economy and Finance (MEF),Transfers of a general character between different levels of government.,"In Italy, the Ministry of Economy and Finance and the Ministry of Interior must cooperate to process salary information, yet fragmented private cloud infrastructures and rigid data-sharing mechanisms limit efficiency and transparency.",Online services for managing personnel salary accounts,Ministry of Economy and Finance (MEF); Central-Government; Transfers of a general character between different levels of government.; Italy,"The aim of this use case is to enable seamless inter-ministerial collaboration, optimise the use of existing IT resources, and ensure that sensitive data remains protected and under institutional control throughout its lifecycle.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Managing personnel salary accounts across public administrations requires the secure handling and exchange of highly sensitive personal and financial data, while complying with strict legal, privacy, and sovereignty constraints. In Italy, the Ministry of Economy and Finance and the Ministry of Interior must cooperate to process salary information, yet fragmented private cloud infrastructures and rigid data-sharing mechanisms limit efficiency and transparency. The aim of this use case is to enable seamless inter-ministerial collaboration, optimise the use of existing IT resources, and ensure that sensitive data remains protected and under institutional control throughout its lifecycle. The solution is delivered through the SUNFISH platform, which provides Federation-as-a-Service capabilities to securely interconnect heterogeneous private cloud environments. A blockchain-empowered service ledger governs service registration, access control, and accountability across the federation, while sensitive data processing is executed within authorised cloud domains. This approach enables secure resource sharing without centralising data. The demonstrator confirms improved infrastructure utilisation, reduced operational complexity, and secure cross-ministry data exchange, offering a scalable and compliant model for handling sensitive public sector information.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-800,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00771,EU_PSTW_202609_00771,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Ministry of Finance of Malta (MFIN),General services.,UNKNOWN,Use of Public Cloud PaaS to host SaaS and/or to integrate with commercial SaaS solutions,Ministry of Finance of Malta (MFIN); Central-Government; General services.; Malta,"The objective of this use case is to simplify interactions between citizens and government, improve service agility, and securely integrate governmental applications with external service providers.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Modernising fiscal services requires public administrations to exploit the scalability of public cloud platforms while preserving strict compliance with secrecy, privacy, and data protection regulations. In Malta, the Ministry for Finance and the Taxation Department must collect, validate, and process data from taxpayers, employers, banks, and SMEs, yet traditional systems constrain integration and responsiveness. The objective of this use case is to simplify interactions between citizens and government, improve service agility, and securely integrate governmental applications with external service providers. The solution relies on the SUNFISH platform to federate public cloud Platform-as-a-Service environments hosting government applications with commercial Software-as-a-Service solutions, such as payroll systems. Secure federation mechanisms enforce policies, control data flows, and ensure regulatory compliance across environments. The results include enhanced scalability, improved interoperability, reduced operational overhead, and faster deployment of digital services, demonstrating how public cloud adoption can strengthen efficiency and service quality in the public sector.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-801,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00772,EU_PSTW_202609_00772,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,South East Regional Cyber Crime Unit (SEROCU),Police Services,UNKNOWN,Organised Crime Unit: Secure Cloud Storage for Data,South East Regional Cyber Crime Unit (SEROCU); Regional Government; Police Services; United Kingdom,"The goal of this use case is to support secure, privacy-preserving cooperation without centralising or duplicating data.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Handling cybercrime evidence demands strict control over data locality, access rights, and confidentiality, while enabling effective collaboration across law enforcement agencies. The South East Regional Cyber Crime Unit must store sensitive investigation data on its own premises but also grant regulated access to other Regional Cyber Crime Units and national bodies during active cases. Conventional sharing models risk exposing sensitive information or breaching jurisdictional constraints. The goal of this use case is to support secure, privacy-preserving cooperation without centralising or duplicating data. The SUNFISH platform provides Secure Multiparty Computation services based on advanced cryptographic techniques, allowing authorised searches and operations to be performed on encrypted data without revealing its content. Data remains local, while access policies are enforced across federated cloud environments. The Proof of Concept demonstrates privacy-preserving cross-cloud data sharing, improved investigative collaboration, and stronger protection of sensitive evidence, establishing a robust and scalable framework for secure law enforcement operations.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-802,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00773,EU_PSTW_202609_00773,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,National Council of Clerks (NCC),Law courts,"French commercial court clerks faced delays and limited transparency when recording changes to companies’ legal status, including name changes, branch establishments and dissolutions.",Corporate Registry in commercial court,National Council of Clerks (NCC); Central-Government; Law courts; France,A distributed ledger platform jointly developed by IBM and the NCC was implemented using Hyperledger Fabric.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"French commercial court clerks faced delays and limited transparency when recording changes to companies’ legal status, including name changes, branch establishments and dissolutions. Multiple stakeholders – the National Council of Clerks (NCC), local court offices and regulatory bodies – required reliable, synchronised access to registry data to ensure legal certainty and efficient processing. The objective was to streamline updates to the corporate registry and improve data integrity across jurisdictions. A distributed ledger platform jointly developed by IBM and the NCC was implemented using Hyperledger Fabric. The permissioned blockchain records and shares regulatory information related to companies’ lifecycle events, ensuring tamper-evident entries, controlled access and real-time synchronisation between authorised participants through integrated registry systems. In proof-of-concept testing, registry update times were reduced from several days to one day, improving operational efficiency, transparency and trust while strengthening data reliability across the national registry ecosystem.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-804,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00774,EU_PSTW_202609_00774,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Link Campus University,Tertiary education.,UNKNOWN,Verifiable and tamper-proof university degree,Link Campus University; Academic-Research; Tertiary education.; Italy,"Link Campus University in Rome implemented blockchain-certified academic degrees, storing cryptographic hashes on a distributed ledger to enable instant verification by employers and other institutions worldwide.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Private universities in Italy sought differentiation through technological innovation in credential management, recognising that blockchain verification could provide competitive advantages in demonstrating institutional credibility to employers. Link Campus University in Rome implemented blockchain-certified academic degrees, storing cryptographic hashes on a distributed ledger to enable instant verification by employers and other institutions worldwide. The system allows graduates to share their credentials digitally with verifiable proof of authenticity, eliminating requirements for physical document copies or institutional confirmation letters. This digital sharing mechanism accelerates hiring processes whilst reducing administrative costs for both the university and credential verifiers. The initiative demonstrates private sector leadership in educational technology adoption within the Italian higher education system.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-805,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00775,EU_PSTW_202609_00775,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Bari,General services.,"Public administrations faced organisational and accountability challenges when adopting remote and flexible working arrangements, particularly in tracking activities, authorisations and compliance with internal rules. Smart contracts formalised working arrangements and ensured that records could not be altered unilaterally.",Smart Working management with blockchain in the Municipality of Bari,Municipality of Bari; Central-Government; General services.; Italy,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Public administrations faced organisational and accountability challenges when adopting remote and flexible working arrangements, particularly in tracking activities, authorisations and compliance with internal rules. The Municipality of Bari launched this pilot within the H2020 CITADEL project to explore new ways of managing smart working for municipal employees. Stakeholders included municipal managers, employees participating in agile working, and IT service providers. The objective was to test a transparent and trustworthy digital system to manage work authorisations, activities and reporting while supporting organisational flexibility. The solution involved developing a prototype based on distributed ledger technology to record work agreements, task assignments and activity logs in a tamper-resistant manner. Smart contracts formalised working arrangements and ensured that records could not be altered unilaterally. The system integrated with existing administrative tools while providing a shared, auditable record of smart working activities. The pilot improved transparency, reduced administrative disputes, and increased trust between employees and management. It also demonstrated how public administrations can adopt flexible working models while maintaining accountability, offering a replicable approach for digital transformation in public sector workforce management.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-806,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00776,EU_PSTW_202609_00776,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,The University of St. Gallen,Tertiary education.,Universities and employers face growing challenges in verifying academic qualifications due to increasing diploma fraud and time-consuming manual validation processes.,Verification of the authenticity of university degrees,The University of St. Gallen; Academic-Research; Tertiary education.; Switzerland,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Universities and employers face growing challenges in verifying academic qualifications due to increasing diploma fraud and time-consuming manual validation processes. The University of St. Gallen introduced a pilot to enable near-instant verification of degree authenticity, benefiting graduates, employers and academic institutions. The objective was to reduce verification times from days to seconds while strengthening trust in academic credentials. The solution was developed with Swiss startup BlockFactory and relies on issuing digital diplomas whose cryptographic fingerprints are anchored on a public blockchain. Graduates receive a digital certificate that can be shared electronically, while verifiers upload the certificate to a validation interface that checks its integrity against the blockchain record. The system integrates with the university’s degree management processes without exposing sensitive personal data on-chain. The pilot significantly reduced administrative workload, accelerated recruitment and admissions processes, and enhanced protection against fraudulent diplomas. It demonstrated a scalable and cost-effective approach to digital credential verification that supports international mobility and trust in higher education qualifications.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-807,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00777,EU_PSTW_202609_00777,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Agency for Energy and Sustainable Development (AESS),Fuel and energy,"In Bologna, citizens and companies participating in a local energy community face limited incentives to adapt energy usage patterns, despite growing pressure to reduce costs and emissions.",Flexible energy demand through blockchain,Agency for Energy and Sustainable Development (AESS); Central-Government; Fuel and energy; Italy,"The solution uses a blockchain-based infrastructure to record and validate energy production, consumption, and exchanges among participants, ensuring transparency and trust in shared data.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Creating more efficient and sustainable energy systems requires mechanisms that align consumption with local production while rewarding flexible demand. In Bologna, citizens and companies participating in a local energy community face limited incentives to adapt energy usage patterns, despite growing pressure to reduce costs and emissions. The GECO project brings together public bodies, research institutions, and local partners with the objective of enabling flexible energy demand and strengthening community-based energy models. The solution uses a blockchain-based infrastructure to record and validate energy production, consumption, and exchanges among participants, ensuring transparency and trust in shared data. Reduced tariffs and incentives are managed through trusted digital records accessible to community members. The pilot demonstrates improved coordination between producers and consumers, greater transparency in energy flows, lower energy costs, and a replicable model for smart, decentralised energy communities supporting sustainability objectives.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-808,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00778,EU_PSTW_202609_00778,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Marseille Fos port authority,Transport,"Intermodal freight transport along major logistics corridors involves numerous actors, documents and handovers, often resulting in data silos, delays and limited visibility across the supply chain.",Enhancing intermodal freight movements in Marseille,Marseille Fos port authority; Local Government; Transport; France,"The solution uses a blockchain-based data-sharing platform that allows authorised users to access protected logistics information, such as customs documentation, container status, weight, origin and destination, through controlled permissions.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Intermodal freight transport along major logistics corridors involves numerous actors, documents and handovers, often resulting in data silos, delays and limited visibility across the supply chain. The Marseille Fos Port Authority launched a proof of concept to improve coordination and data sharing along the Mediterranean–Rhône–Saône corridor. Stakeholders included port authorities, logistics operators, customs, shipping companies and inland transport providers. The objective was to enhance efficiency, transparency and trust in intermodal freight movements. The solution uses a blockchain-based data-sharing platform that allows authorised users to access protected logistics information, such as customs documentation, container status, weight, origin and destination, through controlled permissions. Data entries are recorded immutably, ensuring traceability across transport modes. The platform integrates with existing port and logistics systems, acting as a shared reference layer rather than replacing operational tools. The pilot improved information availability, reduced duplication of documents, and facilitated smoother coordination between actors. It demonstrated the potential for distributed ledgers to support resilient and efficient logistics ecosystems in complex port environments.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F07_DATA_VISIBILITY_INTEGRATION_GAP,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-809,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00779,EU_PSTW_202609_00779,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"University of Murcia, Polytechnic University of Cartagena (UPCT), and the San Antonio Catholic University",Tertiary education.,Spanish universities have faced increasing risks of academic credential falsification and administrative burdens in verifying qualifications for employers and international institutions.,University certificates verification in many Spanish Universities,"University of Murcia, Polytechnic University of Cartagena (UPCT), and the San Antonio Catholic University; Academic-Research; Tertiary education.; Spain",UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Spanish universities have faced increasing risks of academic credential falsification and administrative burdens in verifying qualifications for employers and international institutions. Three universities—the University of Murcia, the Polytechnic University of Cartagena and the San Antonio Catholic University—launched a joint pilot to test a shared digital standard for certificate verification. Stakeholders included universities, students, employers and accreditation bodies. The objective was to minimise diploma fraud while simplifying verification processes across institutions. The solution is based on a distributed ledger infrastructure where academic certificates are issued digitally and registered as immutable records. Graduates can share their credentials electronically, and verifiers can confirm authenticity against the ledger without contacting the issuing university. The system supports interoperability between institutions through a common certification standard. The pilot reduced verification times, lowered administrative costs, and strengthened trust in academic qualifications. It also demonstrated how multiple universities can collaborate on a shared digital infrastructure, paving the way for scalable national or regional credential verification systems.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-810,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00780,EU_PSTW_202609_00780,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Federal Ministry for Digital and Economic Affairs,R&D health.,"Ensuring the authenticity of health credentials became a critical challenge during the COVID-19 pandemic, as public authorities faced fraud, misinformation, and growing concerns over data privacy.",Verification of COVID-19 testing with blockchain in Austria,Federal Ministry for Digital and Economic Affairs; Central-Government; R&D health.; Austria,"The initiative involves public authorities and leverages the national digital identity framework to support trusted interactions. Verifiable records are anchored on the Ardor blockchain using the Ignis tokenisation system, ensuring integrity and tamper resistance.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Ensuring the authenticity of health credentials became a critical challenge during the COVID-19 pandemic, as public authorities faced fraud, misinformation, and growing concerns over data privacy. In Austria, the QualiSig project addresses these issues by enabling citizens to securely verify COVID-19 testing and related health information while retaining full control over their personal data. The initiative involves public authorities and leverages the national digital identity framework to support trusted interactions. The solution combines encrypted communications, decentralised data storage, and qualified digital signatures applied via mobile devices. Verifiable records are anchored on the Ardor blockchain using the Ignis tokenisation system, ensuring integrity and tamper resistance. The prototypes demonstrate improved fraud prevention, enhanced trust in digital health information, and strong privacy guarantees, delivering reliable, user-centric verification mechanisms for critical public health processes.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-811,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00781,EU_PSTW_202609_00781,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Direzione dei Sistemi Informativi e dell’Innovazione (DSII), Ministry of Economy and Finance (MEF)",R&D economic affairs,"These processes were slow, fragmented and prone to reconciliation errors. Requests, approvals and payroll withholdings are recorded on the ledger, enabling automated checks and reducing manual intervention.",Loans and insurance services requests simplification,"Direzione dei Sistemi Informativi e dell’Innovazione (DSII), Ministry of Economy and Finance (MEF); Central-Government; R&D economic affairs; Italy","The solution uses SIAChain, a blockchain network involving major Italian banks, to create a shared, trusted infrastructure for exchanging and validating loan and insurance data.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Public administration employees in Italy rely on complex processes to request loans and insurance services involving salary withholdings, requiring extensive coordination between the Ministry of Economy and Finance (MEF), financial institutions and employees. These processes were slow, fragmented and prone to reconciliation errors. CreditoNet 2.0 was launched to modernise and simplify these interactions. Stakeholders include MEF, public employees, banks, insurance companies and payroll service providers. The objective is to streamline requests, improve data security and accelerate reconciliation. The solution uses SIAChain, a blockchain network involving major Italian banks, to create a shared, trusted infrastructure for exchanging and validating loan and insurance data. Requests, approvals and payroll withholdings are recorded on the ledger, enabling automated checks and reducing manual intervention. Integration with existing MEF and financial systems ensures continuity of operations. The pilot improved processing speed, reduced errors and reconciliation times, enhanced data security, and enabled a more transparent and efficient service model. It illustrates how distributed ledger infrastructures can modernise complex financial workflows in public administration.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK|F08_ERROR_COMPLIANCE_RISK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-812,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00782,EU_PSTW_202609_00782,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""social_nonprofit"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Dutch Blockchain Coalition,R&D economic affairs,"Establishing a company entirely through digital channels requires multiple parties to trust the identity, authority, and credentials of founders and representatives without relying on fragmented paper-based checks.",Company Passport,Dutch Blockchain Coalition; Non-governmental; R&D economic affairs; Netherlands,"In the Netherlands, this use case emerged from collaboration between the Royal Dutch Association of Civil-Law Notaries, the Dutch Blockchain Coalition, chambers of commerce, financial institutions, and the Tax and Customs Administration to explore how the company-formation process could become more secure and efficient in a fully digital environment. At a conceptual level, it uses shared digital credentials and interoperable trust components to allow authorised actors such as notaries, banks, and public authorities to access and rely on the same verified information during the incorporation process.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Establishing a company entirely through digital channels requires multiple parties to trust the identity, authority, and credentials of founders and representatives without relying on fragmented paper-based checks. In the Netherlands, this use case emerged from collaboration between the Royal Dutch Association of Civil-Law Notaries, the Dutch Blockchain Coalition, chambers of commerce, financial institutions, and the Tax and Customs Administration to explore how the company-formation process could become more secure and efficient in a fully digital environment. The objective is to support digital company creation by creating a trusted framework through which relevant parties can rely on validated business and identity information. The implementation developed the concept of an enterprise or company passport as a trust framework rather than a single standalone application. At a conceptual level, it uses shared digital credentials and interoperable trust components to allow authorised actors such as notaries, banks, and public authorities to access and rely on the same verified information during the incorporation process. The model was shaped as a public-private collaboration involving legal, financial, and governmental stakeholders. The impact includes the potential to simplify digital incorporation, reduce duplication in verification steps, and improve trust across participating organisations. It also supports interoperability and creates strategic value by laying a common foundation for safer and more efficient digital business formation.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-813,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00783,EU_PSTW_202609_00783,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Employee Insurance Administration Agency,Secondary education.,"In the Netherlands, the Career Wallet concept addresses this challenge by enabling people to manage digitally validated diplomas, certificates, proofs of conduct, and related records in a way that supports mobility between education and work.",Carrière Wallet,Employee Insurance Administration Agency; Central-Government; Secondary education.; Netherlands,"At a conceptual level, the solution uses verifiable digital credentials and wallet infrastructure to allow holders to share selected records securely, including within European blockchain-related initiatives for trusted exchange.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Employers, public bodies, and education providers increasingly need secure ways to verify qualifications and professional records, while individuals want greater control over who can access their credentials and under what conditions. In the Netherlands, the Career Wallet concept addresses this challenge by enabling people to manage digitally validated diplomas, certificates, proofs of conduct, and related records in a way that supports mobility between education and work. The objective is to give individuals more control over their verified credentials while making it easier for employers and institutions to receive trusted information when needed. The implementation centres on wallet-based technology for storing and presenting digital proofs, supported by practical experiments involving large employers, financial and recruitment partners, the Employee Insurance Administration Agency, the Municipality of Rotterdam, and the Ministry of the Interior and Kingdom Relations. At a conceptual level, the solution uses verifiable digital credentials and wallet infrastructure to allow holders to share selected records securely, including within European blockchain-related initiatives for trusted exchange. The impact includes improved portability of qualifications, stronger privacy and user control, and more efficient verification of career-related information. It also supports scalability in labour-market and education processes and creates value by reducing friction in recruitment, onboarding, and skills recognition.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-814,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00784,EU_PSTW_202609_00784,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Dutch Blockchain Coalition,Public health services.,UNKNOWN,uNLock the world,Dutch Blockchain Coalition; Non-governmental; Public health services.; Netherlands,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The uNLock initiative was launched in 2020 by a consortium of public and private organisations in the Netherlands to address the need for secure and verifiable sharing of COVID-19 test results during the pandemic. The objective was to enable individuals to prove their health status in order to access controlled environments such as events or venues, thereby supporting safer reopening of society. The solution consists of a digital infrastructure that allows users to store and share certified test results in a secure and privacy-preserving manner. Distributed ledger technologies and cryptographic mechanisms are used to ensure the authenticity and integrity of the shared credentials, while giving users control over their personal data. The system enables verification of test results by authorised parties without centralised data storage. While the initiative demonstrated the feasibility of secure digital health credentials in controlled settings, its relevance declined as pandemic-related restrictions were lifted",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-815,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00785,EU_PSTW_202609_00785,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Dutch Blockchain Coalition,General services.,"Transferring pension rights between schemes is often administratively complex, slow, and difficult for individuals to assess, particularly when they need to compare accrued value across different providers and understand the implications of a transfer.",My pension rights transfer,Dutch Blockchain Coalition; Non-governmental; General services.; Netherlands,"In the Netherlands, this use case was developed to simplify pension-rights transfer by giving users a clearer and faster way to find existing pensions and review transfer proposals.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Transferring pension rights between schemes is often administratively complex, slow, and difficult for individuals to assess, particularly when they need to compare accrued value across different providers and understand the implications of a transfer. In the Netherlands, this use case was developed to simplify pension-rights transfer by giving users a clearer and faster way to find existing pensions and review transfer proposals. The objective is to make pension transfers more accessible, transparent, and secure, while reducing friction between pension administrators. The implementation resulted in the Mijnwaardeoverdracht.nl platform, developed with major pension providers and platform developers at APG under the Hyfen name. At a conceptual level, the platform connects the administrative systems of pension-sector organisations through a modular architecture that combines established technologies such as databases and cloud infrastructure with distributed ledger technology. The distributed ledger is used only where it adds value, specifically to create a shared understanding between participating parties, rather than as the whole solution. The impact includes quicker and simpler transfer processes, better comparability between pension arrangements, and improved coordination across providers. It also supports interoperability and scalability in the pension domain, while creating value for users by turning a traditionally cumbersome procedure into a more straightforward and digitally manageable service.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-816,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00786,EU_PSTW_202609_00786,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Port of Rotterdam,Transport,"Traditional paper-based bills of lading are inefficient, prone to delays, and vulnerable to fraud, creating bottlenecks in global supply chains.",Electronic bill of lading (eBL),Port of Rotterdam; Local Government; Transport; Netherlands,"The solution uses distributed ledger technologies and interoperable digital platforms to issue, transfer, and verify electronic bills of lading across different systems.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The electronic bill of lading initiative involves collaboration between Singapore and the Netherlands to modernise maritime trade documentation along the Europe–Far East trade lane. Traditional paper-based bills of lading are inefficient, prone to delays, and vulnerable to fraud, creating bottlenecks in global supply chains. The objective is to enable secure, interoperable, and legally recognised digital documentation for shipping transactions. The solution uses distributed ledger technologies and interoperable digital platforms to issue, transfer, and verify electronic bills of lading across different systems. The pilot demonstrated that documents generated on one platform could be validated and processed by another, supporting cross-platform interoperability. The system integrates digital identity, secure data exchange, and verification mechanisms to ensure trust and authenticity. While the pilot confirmed technical feasibility and potential efficiency gains, broader adoption depends on regulatory changes to recognise electronic documents as legally equivalent to paper-based versions.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-817,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00787,EU_PSTW_202609_00787,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Reiner Lemoine Institute,Fuel and energy,"Local electricity markets face increasing complexity due to decentralised renewable generation, fluctuating demand, and grid constraints that traditional market mechanisms struggle to manage in real time. In Germany, energy producers, consumers, grid operators, and market authorities require more flexible trading models to balance local surpluses and bottlenecks efficiently while maintaining system stability.",Energy trading platform,Reiner Lemoine Institute; Academic-Research; Fuel and energy; Germany,"The solution is implemented through a blockchain-based electricity market provider system (SMBS) that records transactions, matches local buyers and sellers, and manages market rules in a decentralised manner. The project demonstrates improved market transparency, faster settlement, and more efficient use of local energy resources.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Local electricity markets face increasing complexity due to decentralised renewable generation, fluctuating demand, and grid constraints that traditional market mechanisms struggle to manage in real time. In Germany, energy producers, consumers, grid operators, and market authorities require more flexible trading models to balance local surpluses and bottlenecks efficiently while maintaining system stability. The BEST project, funded by the Federal Ministry for Economic Affairs and Energy and led by the Reiner Lemoine Institute, aims to design a decentralised electricity market framework that enables local energy trading and optimised grid usage. The objective is to support real-time balancing of supply and demand at local level, reducing congestion and improving the integration of renewable energy sources. The solution is implemented through a blockchain-based electricity market provider system (SMBS) that records transactions, matches local buyers and sellers, and manages market rules in a decentralised manner. Smart contracts support automated settlement and coordination between participants, while grid and metering data feeds enable near real-time operation. The project demonstrates improved market transparency, faster settlement, and more efficient use of local energy resources. Expected impacts include reduced grid congestion, lower balancing costs, enhanced renewable integration, and a scalable model for future decentralised energy markets.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-818,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00788,EU_PSTW_202609_00788,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Antwerp,R&D general public services.,UNKNOWN,Privacy engine,City of Antwerp; Local Government; R&D general public services.; Belgium,"Citizens using Antwerp’s digital services lacked meaningful control over personal data distributed across multiple municipal systems, while compliance with the General Data Protection Regulation required transparent consent management and data portability. The initial use case focused on change-of-address procedures, enabling residents to manage which public bodies could access specific attributes.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Citizens using Antwerp’s digital services lacked meaningful control over personal data distributed across multiple municipal systems, while compliance with the General Data Protection Regulation required transparent consent management and data portability. Digipolis Antwerp, together with Jolocom, V-ICT-OR and the Flemish Government, launched the Blockchain on the Move initiative to create a Self-Sovereign Identity infrastructure embedded in the Antwerp City Platform as a Service ecosystem. The initial use case focused on change-of-address procedures, enabling residents to manage which public bodies could access specific attributes. The solution relies on distributed ledger technology to anchor decentralised identifiers and verifiable credentials, combined with cryptographic key pairs and a business rules engine governing permissions. Personal data remain under citizen control, while hashed proofs and consent records are registered on-chain and connected to municipal back-office systems through secure APIs. The architecture supports selective disclosure of attributes and auditable access logs. The project improved transparency, strengthened privacy-by-design compliance and reduced administrative friction in data exchange between agencies. It demonstrated a scalable model for citizen-centric identity management within local government digital platforms.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-819,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00789,EU_PSTW_202609_00789,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""social_nonprofit""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Antwerp,R&D education.,"Children’s talents and personal interests are often recorded informally and remain fragmented across schools, youth organisations and associations, limiting the ability of educators and supervisors to nurture strengths consistently over time. The City of Antwerp introduced the Digital Talent Profile to address this challenge by creating a trusted way to collect, represent and share children’s talents beyond organisational boundaries.",Digital talent profile,City of Antwerp; Local Government; R&D education.; Belgium,The solution is implemented using a decentralised digital identity approach based on distributed ledger technology.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Children’s talents and personal interests are often recorded informally and remain fragmented across schools, youth organisations and associations, limiting the ability of educators and supervisors to nurture strengths consistently over time. The City of Antwerp introduced the Digital Talent Profile to address this challenge by creating a trusted way to collect, represent and share children’s talents beyond organisational boundaries. Stakeholders include children, parents, educators, youth organisations and municipal services. The objective is to empower children by giving them ownership over how their talents are documented and shared, while enabling supervisors to better support personal development through a holistic and portable profile. The solution is implemented using a decentralised digital identity approach based on distributed ledger technology. Individual talent statements are captured as claims, supported by proofs and attestations issued by trusted actors, each recorded with a unique and immutable timestamp. These records are managed through digital wallets that allow children, with appropriate guardianship, to control consent, scope and duration of data sharing. The pilot improves trust, data portability and privacy, reduces duplication of assessments, and enhances collaboration across youth services. It delivers qualitative benefits in empowerment and personalisation and provides a scalable foundation for citizen-centric identity services.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-820,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00790,EU_PSTW_202609_00790,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,European Blockchain Services Infrastructure (EBSI),General services.,"Cross-border mobility within the European Union creates significant challenges for the verification of social security coverage, as citizens moving between Member States must rely on fragmented, paper-based, or slow administrative procedures. Public authorities, employers, and mobile citizens face delays, errors, and uncertainty in proving entitlement to social security rights.",European Social Security Pass,European Blockchain Services Infrastructure (EBSI); European Institution/Agency; General services.; European Union,"The solution is developed using the European Blockchain Services Infrastructure (EBSI), the EU-wide platform designed to support trusted cross-border public services.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Cross-border mobility within the European Union creates significant challenges for the verification of social security coverage, as citizens moving between Member States must rely on fragmented, paper-based, or slow administrative procedures. Public authorities, employers, and mobile citizens face delays, errors, and uncertainty in proving entitlement to social security rights. The European Social Security Pass pilot addresses these issues by exploring a digital solution for secure cross-border verification of social security status and identity. Launched in 2021, the initiative involves around a dozen Member States and aims to assess technical, organisational, and legal feasibility ahead of possible large-scale deployment. The solution is developed using the European Blockchain Services Infrastructure (EBSI), the EU-wide platform designed to support trusted cross-border public services. Social security credentials and identity attributes are issued and verified through decentralised mechanisms, enabling citizens to manage their data while authorities can reliably validate entitlements across borders. Interoperability with national systems and compliance with EU data protection rules are built into the design. The pilot demonstrates faster verification processes, reduced administrative burden, improved fraud prevention, and greater user control. It lays the groundwork for a more efficient, secure, and citizen-centric approach to social security coordination across Europe.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F08_ERROR_COMPLIANCE_RISK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-821,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00791,EU_PSTW_202609_00791,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Italferr,Transport,UNKNOWN,BIMChain: the blockchain application for managing BIM files,Italferr; Private sector; Transport; Italy,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Building Information Modelling projects involve multiple actors exchanging complex digital files throughout design, construction and operation phases, often leading to disputes over file ownership, versioning and responsibilities. Italferr developed BIMChain to address transparency and trust issues in collaborative BIM environments. Stakeholders include infrastructure designers, engineers, contractors, clients and asset managers. The objective is to ensure traceability, accountability and trust in the lifecycle management of BIM files while supporting collaborative workflows. BIMChain integrates distributed ledger technology into BIM processes to create a shared environment governed by smart contracts. Each BIM file and modification is associated with a verifiable digital identity, recording who performed which action, with what role and at what time. Smart contracts regulate access rights, responsibilities and exchange conditions between parties, while metadata and transaction logs are anchored immutably to the ledger. The solution integrates with existing BIM tools without duplicating large files on-chain. The pilot improves collaboration efficiency, reduces disputes, enhances auditability and supports compliance in complex infrastructure projects. It demonstrates how blockchain can strengthen governance and trust in digital construction ecosystems.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-822,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00792,EU_PSTW_202609_00792,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,ICE - Agenzia per la promozione all'estero e l'internazionalizzazione delle imprese italiane,Tertiary education.,UNKNOWN,Diploma Notarisation,ICE - Agenzia per la promozione all'estero e l'internazionalizzazione delle imprese italiane; Central-Government; Tertiary education.; Italy,The solution uses distributed ledger technology to notarise diploma data by anchoring cryptographic representations of certificates on a shared ledger.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Educational institutions and employers require reliable mechanisms to verify academic diplomas, while issuing bodies must protect against falsification and disputes over authenticity. ICE, the Italian Trade Agency, launched a pilot to certify diplomas issued for the 52nd CORCE “Fausto De Franceschi” Master programme in internationalisation. Stakeholders include graduates, ICE, academic partners and employers. The objective is to guarantee authenticity, date certainty and immutability of issued diplomas, while simplifying verification processes. The solution uses distributed ledger technology to notarise diploma data by anchoring cryptographic representations of certificates on a shared ledger. Each notarised record provides proof of existence, authorship and integrity without exposing personal data publicly. Verification can be performed independently by authorised parties by comparing diploma data against the ledger record. The pilot reduces verification time, strengthens trust in qualifications, and lowers administrative overhead for issuing and verifying entities. It also provides graduates with portable, verifiable credentials, demonstrating the potential of blockchain-based notarisation for education and professional certification.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-823,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00793,EU_PSTW_202609_00793,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Federazione Lavoratori Pubblici e Funzioni Pubbliche,General services.,"Public administrations and trade unions often rely on paper-based or fragmented digital procedures for approvals, negotiations and certifications, limiting transparency and slowing decision-making. The pilot improves procedural transparency, reduces delays, enables remote participation and strengthens trust among parties.",A blockchain platform for public administration,Federazione Lavoratori Pubblici e Funzioni Pubbliche; Non-governmental; General services.; Italy,"The platform applies distributed ledger technology to notarise decisions and procedural steps, including budget approvals, remote trade union conciliation, supplementary collective agreements and diploma notarisation.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Public administrations and trade unions often rely on paper-based or fragmented digital procedures for approvals, negotiations and certifications, limiting transparency and slowing decision-making. Blockchain Italia developed a blockchain-based platform for the Federazione Lavoratori Pubblici e Funzioni Pubbliche (FLP) to support secure and auditable administrative processes. Stakeholders include trade unions, public employees, employers and administrative bodies. The objective is to enable legally robust, transparent and remote execution of administrative and contractual procedures. The platform applies distributed ledger technology to notarise decisions and procedural steps, including budget approvals, remote trade union conciliation, supplementary collective agreements and diploma notarisation. Each action is recorded immutably, creating a shared and verifiable process history accessible to authorised parties. Integration with existing organisational workflows allows gradual adoption without disrupting operations. The pilot improves procedural transparency, reduces delays, enables remote participation and strengthens trust among parties. It illustrates how blockchain platforms can modernise governance processes in public administration and labour relations.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-824,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00794,EU_PSTW_202609_00794,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""social_nonprofit"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,German Federal Ministry for Economic Affairs and Energy,General services.,"Digital services across sectors require secure and interoperable identity solutions, yet fragmented identity systems create barriers to cross-sector and cross-border services.",Secure Digital Identities,German Federal Ministry for Economic Affairs and Energy; Central-Government; General services.; Germany,The solution is based on self-sovereign identity principles using distributed ledger infrastructure to anchor decentralised identifiers and verifiable credentials.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Digital services across sectors require secure and interoperable identity solutions, yet fragmented identity systems create barriers to cross-sector and cross-border services. The IDunion cooperative was established to develop a shared self-sovereign identity ecosystem in Germany, supported by the Federal Ministry for Economic Affairs and Energy. Stakeholders include technology providers, public authorities, businesses and citizens. The objective is to establish a production-ready identity network supporting people, organisations and devices across multiple domains such as education, mobility, e-health and industry. The solution is based on self-sovereign identity principles using distributed ledger infrastructure to anchor decentralised identifiers and verifiable credentials. Identity wallets allow users to store and present credentials issued by trusted parties, while a test network supports interoperability and governance across partners. The pilot improves identity security, user control and interoperability, reduces dependency on central identity providers and enables new digital services. With a functioning test network and multiple applications, IDunion provides a scalable foundation for trusted digital identity ecosystems.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-825,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00795,EU_PSTW_202609_00795,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""social_nonprofit""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Lugano,R&D general public services.,UNKNOWN,"Swiss Ledger: Private, institutional and non-profit blockchain network",Municipality of Lugano; Local Government; R&D general public services.; Switzerland,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Swiss institutions required secure and transparent methods for managing digital assets and credentials that met the country's high standards for financial services and data protection. Swiss Ledger was established as a private, institutional, and non-profit blockchain infrastructure providing distributed ledger services to Swiss organisations across sectors including finance, government, and academia. The platform enables secure credential management, asset tokenisation, and data integrity verification whilst maintaining compliance with Swiss regulatory requirements. Operating since 2021, Swiss Ledger provides a permissioned blockchain environment where participating organisations can issue and verify digital credentials, track assets, and maintain auditable records. The infrastructure supports Switzerland's broader digital transformation objectives whilst preserving the privacy and security standards that characterise Swiss financial and institutional services.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-826,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00796,EU_PSTW_202609_00796,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,San Marino Innovation S.p.A.,Public health services.,UNKNOWN,National Green Pass,San Marino Innovation S.p.A.; Central-Government; Public health services.; San Marino,"The San Marino Digital Covid Certificate, using a public key for issuance and validation, has been set up as a national infrastructure at San Marino Innovation S.p.A.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The San Marino Digital Covid Certificate covers vaccination against Sars-Cov2, past infection or negative test result. The certificate issued has a validity of 1 week starting 15 days after the first vaccination dose or 9 months from the completed vaccination cycle. The certificate is issued upon personal request, in a paper or digital format, by the ISS or facilities authorized by the San Marino health authorities. The San Marino Digital Covid Certificate, using a public key for issuance and validation, has been set up as a national infrastructure at San Marino Innovation S.p.A. This ensures the integrity, authenticity and control of the data contained therein. The entity temporarily performs the functions of Country Signing Certification Authority (CSCA) and is in charge of issuing the Root Certificate for the generation of public and private keys necessary for signing the digital certificates. According to the Ministry of Health and Social Security, the extremely efficient development is owed fully to the multi-layered, fruitful collaboration between San Marino’s public entities and private partners DNV and VeChain.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,NOT_IN_USE,미사용,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-827,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00797,EU_PSTW_202609_00797,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""social_nonprofit""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,PlanetWatch,Environmental protection n.e.c.,"Traditional air quality monitoring relies on expensive governmental stations providing limited geographic coverage and delayed data publication, leaving many pollution hotspots undetected and populations without timely warnings.",PlanetWatch: monitoring air quality,PlanetWatch; Private sector; Environmental protection n.e.c.; Italy,"PlanetWatch, a French startup and CERN spin-off, developed a global air quality monitoring network using the Algorand blockchain to create the first immutable ledger for environmental data. The platform incentivises individuals and organisations to deploy low-cost sensors through PLANET token rewards, with over 25,000 sensors deployed globally by late 2021.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Traditional air quality monitoring relies on expensive governmental stations providing limited geographic coverage and delayed data publication, leaving many pollution hotspots undetected and populations without timely warnings. PlanetWatch, a French startup and CERN spin-off, developed a global air quality monitoring network using the Algorand blockchain to create the first immutable ledger for environmental data. The platform incentivises individuals and organisations to deploy low-cost sensors through PLANET token rewards, with over 25,000 sensors deployed globally by late 2021. Initial deployments in Milan and Taranto, Italy, demonstrated the system's capability to provide dense, real-time monitoring complementing official stations at a fraction of the cost. Data collected undergoes validation through CERN's C2MON acquisition framework before blockchain recording, ensuring reliability. The Algorand Foundation supported the initiative through grants, recognising its environmental and public health potential.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-828,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00798,EU_PSTW_202609_00798,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Agency for Digital Italy (AGiD),General services.,"Italy lacked a national blockchain infrastructure for public services, limiting the country's ability to participate in European initiatives and deploy distributed ledger solutions for citizen services at scale.",Blockchain service infrastructure,Agency for Digital Italy (AGiD); Central-Government; General services.; Italy,"The project aims to develop applications supporting digital certificates, Made in Italy supply chain traceability, and sustainable energy models using Distributed Ledger Technology.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Italy lacked a national blockchain infrastructure for public services, limiting the country's ability to participate in European initiatives and deploy distributed ledger solutions for citizen services at scale. IBSI (Italian Blockchain Service Infrastructure) was launched in January 2021 by a consortium including AgID, Poste Italiane, INAIL, INPS, Sogei, Politecnico di Milano, University of Cagliari, and others to create Italy's first national blockchain network for public services. The project aims to develop applications supporting digital certificates, Made in Italy supply chain traceability, and sustainable energy models using Distributed Ledger Technology. IBSI operates in synergy with the European Blockchain Services Infrastructure (EBSI), with Italy receiving €149,928 through the Blockchain4Italy project for EBSI node deployment. The infrastructure implements the Once Only principle, enabling citizens and businesses to provide data once for reuse across public administrations.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-829,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00799,EU_PSTW_202609_00799,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""social_nonprofit""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Innovative Medicines Initiative (IMI) and the European Federation of Pharmaceutical Industries and Associations (EFPIA),R&D health.,"The healthcare sector faces persistent challenges related to fragmented data, complex supply chains, lengthy drug development processes, and limited trust between stakeholders.",Pharmaledger: blockchain enabled healthcare,Innovative Medicines Initiative (IMI) and the European Federation of Pharmaceutical Industries and Associations (EFPIA); European Institution/Agency; R&D health.; European Union,"The platform is implemented using distributed ledger technologies to ensure data integrity, traceability, and controlled access among authorised participants.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The healthcare sector faces persistent challenges related to fragmented data, complex supply chains, lengthy drug development processes, and limited trust between stakeholders. Pharmaceutical companies, regulators, healthcare providers, and patients require secure and interoperable systems to exchange information while ensuring transparency, compliance, and data protection. PharmaLedger addresses these needs through a large-scale collaborative initiative funded under the Innovative Medicines Initiative, bringing together pharmaceutical industry leaders, public bodies, research institutions, SMEs, and patient organisations. The project aims to enable a trusted digital platform supporting healthcare processes across three core areas: pharmaceutical supply chains, clinical trials, and health data management. The platform is implemented using distributed ledger technologies to ensure data integrity, traceability, and controlled access among authorised participants. Smart contracts and decentralised governance models support shared processes, while sensitive data is managed in compliance with regulatory and ethical requirements. PharmaLedger demonstrates how shared digital infrastructure can reduce inefficiencies, improve collaboration, and shorten development timelines. The expected impact includes reduced healthcare costs, improved drug traceability, faster and more transparent clinical trials, and enhanced trust across the healthcare ecosystem, providing a scalable foundation for digital transformation in the sector.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F07_DATA_VISIBILITY_INTEGRATION_GAP,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-831,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00800,EU_PSTW_202609_00800,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Associazione Nazionale Società Organismi di Attestazione - Unionsoa,General services.,"Public procurement processes are often characterised by complex documentation, fragmented qualification checks and slow administrative procedures, increasing costs and delaying projects. The pilot reduces processing times, lowers administrative burden, improves auditability and supports faster, more reliable procurement procedures.",Public procurement: streamlined and faster digital procedures,Associazione Nazionale Società Organismi di Attestazione - Unionsoa; Central-Government; General services.; Italy,"The solution uses a blockchain platform deployed on IBM Cloud, structured around notarisation and notification services.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Public procurement processes are often characterised by complex documentation, fragmented qualification checks and slow administrative procedures, increasing costs and delaying projects. This pilot, proposed by UnionSOA and IBM, aims to fully digitise qualification and documentation management in public procurement. Stakeholders include contracting authorities, suppliers, qualification bodies and oversight institutions. The objective is to simplify administrative processes, ensure legal certainty and improve transparency across the procurement chain. The solution uses a blockchain platform deployed on IBM Cloud, structured around notarisation and notification services. Qualification documents and dossiers are notarised on the ledger, providing proof of existence, originality and provenance, while each procedural step is recorded immutably to ensure traceability. Authorised actors can access validated information without repeated submissions. The pilot reduces processing times, lowers administrative burden, improves auditability and supports faster, more reliable procurement procedures. It demonstrates how blockchain can enable end-to-end digital transformation in public procurement systems.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-832,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00801,EU_PSTW_202609_00801,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Affidaty,R&D environmental protection.,UNKNOWN,Ecocoin: encouraging virtuous behavior,Affidaty; Private sector; R&D environmental protection.; Italy,"Municipalities often struggle to track these activities transparently and at scale, while citizens lack incentives that are trusted and easy to use.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Encouraging citizens to adopt sustainable behaviours such as recycling and low-emission mobility requires mechanisms that can reliably measure actions and translate them into tangible rewards. Municipalities often struggle to track these activities transparently and at scale, while citizens lack incentives that are trusted and easy to use. The Ecocoin pilot was created to address this gap by assigning concrete value to virtuous environmental actions within a local circular economy. Stakeholders include citizens, municipal waste collectors, local authorities and affiliated commercial partners. The objective is to promote sustainable behaviour while strengthening local economic circuits. The solution combines interconnected sensors used by waste collectors to measure recycling activities with a blockchain-based ledger that records verified actions immutably. Citizens create a digital profile with an associated wallet; when waste is delivered and measured, tokens are generated automatically and credited to the wallet. These tokens can then be spent within participating local networks. The pilot improves transparency, prevents manipulation of data, and builds trust in incentive mechanisms. It delivers qualitative benefits in citizen engagement, supports measurable environmental outcomes, and offers a scalable model for municipalities seeking to reward sustainability while reinforcing local economies.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-833,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00802,EU_PSTW_202609_00802,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Olsztyn,Public health services.,"Emergency services require rapid, secure access to physical spaces to save lives and protect property, yet traditional key management and access authorisation processes often cause delays.",Blockchain for emergency services,City of Olsztyn; Local Government; Public health services.; Poland,The objective of this use case is to improve response times and operational effectiveness by enabling authorised emergency crews to access buildings without manual key handovers or administrative delays. The city conducted a pilot using a blockchain-based access management solution known as SmartKey.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Emergency services require rapid, secure access to physical spaces to save lives and protect property, yet traditional key management and access authorisation processes often cause delays. In the Polish city of Olsztyn, police, fire, and ambulance services faced operational inefficiencies when responding to incidents requiring immediate building access. The objective of this use case is to improve response times and operational effectiveness by enabling authorised emergency crews to access buildings without manual key handovers or administrative delays. The city conducted a pilot using a blockchain-based access management solution known as SmartKey. The system connects smart contracts deployed on the Ethereum network with physical IoT devices installed in buildings and emergency vehicles. Access rights are managed digitally and activated automatically during emergencies, allowing authorised responders to unlock doors securely and auditable. The trial demonstrates faster emergency response, reduced operational friction, and improved coordination between services. Qualitative benefits include enhanced safety for citizens and responders, greater accountability through tamper-proof access logs, and a scalable model that can be extended to other cities and critical infrastructure environments.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-834,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00803,EU_PSTW_202609_00803,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,The Court of Cremona,Law courts,"Judicial systems rely on secure, reliable, and timely registration of legal documents, yet many courts still depend on paper-based or fragmented digital processes that hinder efficiency and continuity, particularly during emergencies. At the Court of Cremona, the Gip-Gup section faced the risk of operational disruption during the COVID-19 lockdown, threatening delays in hearings and case backlogs.",Blockchain-based service to register digital acts at the Court of Cremona,The Court of Cremona; Local Government; Law courts; Italy,"The objective of this use case was to ensure continuity of judicial activities while strengthening the integrity and traceability of digital legal documents. In collaboration with Stampiz, the court implemented a service to register digital acts such as judgments, files, copies, and certificates.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Judicial systems rely on secure, reliable, and timely registration of legal documents, yet many courts still depend on paper-based or fragmented digital processes that hinder efficiency and continuity, particularly during emergencies. At the Court of Cremona, the Gip-Gup section faced the risk of operational disruption during the COVID-19 lockdown, threatening delays in hearings and case backlogs. The objective of this use case was to ensure continuity of judicial activities while strengthening the integrity and traceability of digital legal documents. In collaboration with Stampiz, the court implemented a service to register digital acts such as judgments, files, copies, and certificates. The solution anchors cryptographic proofs of documents on a blockchain while keeping the files stored securely on the court’s internal servers, ensuring anonymity and preventing unauthorised access. Registration is fully digital and does not require moving files outside the court’s infrastructure. The initiative enabled uninterrupted hearings during the pandemic, accelerated recovery of postponed cases, and improved reliability of registry operations. It demonstrates increased efficiency, stronger evidential integrity, and a robust, scalable approach to digital justice administration.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-835,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00804,EU_PSTW_202609_00804,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,University of Lille,R&D education.,France's largest public university faced substantial administrative burden from diploma verification requests whilst graduates experienced delays obtaining proof of qualifications for employment or further study.,Digital credentials for graduates at University of Lille,University of Lille; Academic-Research; R&D education.; France,The system uses the Avalanche blockchain with an estimated energy footprint of just 0.025g CO2 per credential.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"France's largest public university faced substantial administrative burden from diploma verification requests whilst graduates experienced delays obtaining proof of qualifications for employment or further study. The University of Lille partnered with BCdiploma to implement blockchain-based digital credentials through the Dem-Attest project, issuing bilingual (French/English) certificates that are tamper-proof and valid for life. Since July 2021, the university has issued over 80,000 blockchain certificates covering 700 diploma types at approximately 20,000 per year. The system uses the Avalanche blockchain with an estimated energy footprint of just 0.025g CO2 per credential. The University of Lille leads the Fr.EBSI project integrating with the European Blockchain Services Infrastructure and participates in EBSI-VECTOR for W3C Verifiable Credentials deployment. Online verification has been accessed over 132,000 times, demonstrating strong adoption by employers and institutions.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-836,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00805,EU_PSTW_202609_00805,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Emmen,Environmental protection n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,"EnergieKnip, Energy Wallet",Municipality of Emmen; Local Government; Environmental protection n.e.c.; Netherlands,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"European student mobility programmes like Erasmus faced administrative friction from credential verification requirements, with universities unable to efficiently validate transcripts and qualifications from partner institutions across borders. The EBSI multi-University pilot, launched through the Early Adopters Programme in 2021, brought together 11 universities from 11 countries and 2 European university alliances to test cross-border Verifiable Credentials for education. Six scenarios were developed including Erasmus transcript validation, cross-border PhD applications, European student card verification, and diploma recognition for employment. The pilot demonstrated end-to-end exchange of W3C-standard credentials via EBSI-compliant digital wallets, with institutions including KU Leuven and Italian universities testing the Belgium-Italy corridor. The EBSI-VECTOR project (2023-2025) expanded these pilots across 52 partners in 20 countries, advancing toward production deployment of decentralised educational credentials.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,NOT_IN_USE,미사용,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-837,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00806,EU_PSTW_202609_00806,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Santander,General services.,"Smart cities generate vast volumes of data through IoT platforms, yet much of this data remains underused due to limited trust, unclear ownership, and fragmented access mechanisms.",Data Valorisation Services,Municipality of Santander; Local Government; General services.; Spain,"This use case, developed within the EU-funded TOKEN project, aims to unlock the economic and social value of municipal data while ensuring transparency, accountability, and data governance.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Smart cities generate vast volumes of data through IoT platforms, yet much of this data remains underused due to limited trust, unclear ownership, and fragmented access mechanisms. In Santander, municipal datasets produced by smart city infrastructures offer significant potential value for public administrations, businesses, and citizens, but require a trusted framework to enable secure sharing and reuse. This use case, developed within the EU-funded TOKEN project, aims to unlock the economic and social value of municipal data while ensuring transparency, accountability, and data governance. Stakeholders include the municipality, data providers, service developers, and data consumers, all seeking reliable access to high-quality urban data. The solution introduces a blockchain-based data marketplace where datasets generated by IoT platforms are registered, described, and exchanged under clear usage conditions. Distributed ledgers record data transactions, access rights, and usage policies, while smart contracts govern permissions and potential monetisation schemes. Data itself remains off-chain, ensuring scalability and compliance. The results include improved trust in data sharing, new opportunities for data-driven services, and more efficient reuse of public data assets. The initiative demonstrates how cities can foster innovation ecosystems while maintaining control, transparency, and public value creation.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-838,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00807,EU_PSTW_202609_00807,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"The municipalities of Amsterdam, The Hague and Eindhoven",R&D social protection.,UNKNOWN,The red button: debt pause button,"The municipalities of Amsterdam, The Hague and Eindhoven; Local Government; R&D social protection.; Netherlands",UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Individuals facing problem debt often experience escalating stress due to continuous collection actions, while municipalities require time to assess and implement sustainable debt restructuring solutions. In Amsterdam, the “Red Button” pilot was introduced as an innovative instrument in debt counselling to temporarily pause debt collection, giving debtors space to stabilise their situation. Stakeholders include indebted citizens, municipal debt counselling services, courts and collection agencies such as the Central Judicial Collection Agency. The objective is to reduce pressure on vulnerable individuals while enabling more effective and durable financial solutions. The mechanism allows a temporary, legally recognised suspension of collection activities during counselling periods. Digital coordination between municipal services and enforcement bodies ensures that the pause is applied consistently and transparently. The pilot demonstrated improved cooperation between institutions, reduced stress for debtors, and better engagement in counselling processes. Qualitative results suggest higher chances of successful debt restructuring and more humane public debt management. The approach offers a replicable policy tool for municipalities aiming to balance enforcement with social support.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-839,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00808,EU_PSTW_202609_00808,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""social_nonprofit""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Blockchainlab Drenthe,General services.,The Self-Sovereign Attendance pilot addresses this operational and compliance challenge by rethinking how attendance is recorded and verified.,Self Sovereign Attendancy,Blockchainlab Drenthe; Local Government; General services.; Netherlands,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Attendance registration at events, classes and conferences is commonly handled through paper lists that expose personal data, are vulnerable to manipulation and fail to meet data protection requirements. The Self-Sovereign Attendance pilot addresses this operational and compliance challenge by rethinking how attendance is recorded and verified. Stakeholders include event organisers, educational institutions, attendees and auditing bodies. The objective is to provide a secure, privacy-preserving and fraud-resistant alternative to traditional attendance lists. The solution enables participants to check in through a decentralised application built on a distributed ledger infrastructure. Attendance records are cryptographically secured, immutable and accessible only to authorised parties, ensuring that personal data is protected while proof of attendance remains verifiable. The proof of concept demonstrated reliable check-in, resistance to data tampering and improved compliance with data protection rules. The pilot enhances trust and fairness for both organisers and participants and provides a scalable foundation for attendance management across education and events.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-840,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00809,EU_PSTW_202609_00809,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,University of Oldenburg,Public health services.,"In Oldenburg, health organisations faced challenges with fake or invalid registration certificates required under the German Prostitutes Protection Act.",Generating Health Certificates,University of Oldenburg; Local Government; Public health services.; Germany,"The solution uses a distributed ledger to issue and verify certificates, ensuring that each record is traceable to its source and cannot be altered.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Local authorities are required to verify compliance with health regulations while protecting sensitive personal information, particularly in contexts involving vulnerable groups. In Oldenburg, health organisations faced challenges with fake or invalid registration certificates required under the German Prostitutes Protection Act. The pilot aimed to ensure authenticity and integrity of health certificates while preserving privacy. Stakeholders include municipal health authorities, registered individuals, and regulatory bodies. The objective was to provide a reliable way to prove that health certificates are valid and up to date without exposing personal data. The solution uses a distributed ledger to issue and verify certificates, ensuring that each record is traceable to its source and cannot be altered. A privacy-preserving design allows verification of certificate validity without revealing underlying personal details. The pilot improved trust in certificates, reduced fraud risk and simplified verification procedures. It demonstrated a scalable and secure approach to regulatory compliance in sensitive public health contexts.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-841,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00810,EU_PSTW_202609_00810,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Roeselare,Public health services.,UNKNOWN,Healthy on the Blockchain,Municipality of Roeselare; Local Government; Public health services.; Belgium,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Employers seeking to promote healthier lifestyles among staff often face resistance due to concerns about data privacy and misuse of personal health information. The municipality of Roeselare and HOWEST University launched this pilot to encourage preventive health and physical activity among municipal employees while safeguarding trust. Stakeholders include employees, health coaches, the municipality and academic partners. The objective is to stimulate healthy behaviour without granting employers access to sensitive personal data. Wearable devices track physical activity, and progress is visualised through an application that connects trainees with personal coaches. Blockchain technology is used to secure health data and control access, ensuring that employees retain ownership of their information. The pilot increased participation in wellness programmes, improved trust in data handling, and reduced privacy concerns. It delivers qualitative benefits in employee wellbeing and organisational culture and provides a replicable model for privacy-preserving workplace health initiatives.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-842,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00811,EU_PSTW_202609_00811,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Deinze,Transport,"Business parks often lack efficient transport connections, limiting access for students and job seekers and increasing reliance on private cars. The Mobiliti pilot was developed to address accessibility challenges in the Deinze business park by enabling trusted ride sharing.",Blockchain-based Mobiliti App,City of Deinze; Local Government; Transport; Belgium,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Business parks often lack efficient transport connections, limiting access for students and job seekers and increasing reliance on private cars. The Mobiliti pilot was developed to address accessibility challenges in the Deinze business park by enabling trusted ride sharing. Stakeholders include students, employers, local authorities and employment agencies. The objective is to improve accessibility while promoting sustainable mobility. The application provides a ride-sharing platform where participants can coordinate trips securely. Blockchain technology underpins identity and transaction trust, ensuring that participants can share rides with confidence while protecting personal data. The pilot improved mobility options, reduced barriers to employment and education, and supported more sustainable commuting. It demonstrates how decentralised trust mechanisms can enable collaborative mobility services in local economic zones.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-843,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00812,EU_PSTW_202609_00812,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,University of Aalborg,Transport,"Maritime ports involve complex logistics chains where fragmented information systems hinder efficiency, transparency and coordination between authorities and businesses.",Blockchain for Maritime Ports,University of Aalborg; Academic-Research; Transport; Denmark,This pilot investigates the feasibility of using blockchain technology to improve freight transport and government-to-business services in ports.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Maritime ports involve complex logistics chains where fragmented information systems hinder efficiency, transparency and coordination between authorities and businesses. This pilot investigates the feasibility of using blockchain technology to improve freight transport and government-to-business services in ports. Stakeholders include port authorities, customs, logistics operators and researchers. The objective is to assess whether distributed ledgers can enhance data sharing and service quality in port logistics. The study explores shared ledgers for tracking freight documentation and logistics events, enabling authorised access to consistent and tamper-proof data. Results indicate potential improvements in transparency, reduced administrative friction and better coordination across stakeholders. The pilot provides a knowledge base for future operational deployments in smart port ecosystems.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-844,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00813,EU_PSTW_202609_00813,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Centre for Research and Technology-Hellas (CERTH),General services.,"In the municipality of Katerini, traditional procurement and budgeting processes limited visibility into public spending and constrained meaningful civic engagement.",Transparent management of public accounts,Centre for Research and Technology-Hellas (CERTH); Academic-Research; General services.; Greece,"This use case, part of the EU-funded TOKEN project, seeks to enhance trust between citizens and local government by making public accounts more transparent and participatory. The use case demonstrates how transparent digital infrastructures can strengthen democratic processes, improve governance quality, and create more accountable and inclusive local administrations.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Public administrations face growing pressure to ensure transparency, accountability, and citizen participation in the management of public finances. In the municipality of Katerini, traditional procurement and budgeting processes limited visibility into public spending and constrained meaningful civic engagement. This use case, part of the EU-funded TOKEN project, seeks to enhance trust between citizens and local government by making public accounts more transparent and participatory. The objective is to provide clear insight into procurement processes and enable citizens to actively contribute to decisions on public spending. The solution integrates blockchain technology into existing smart city initiatives to create a tamper-proof system for recording procurement activities and managing participatory budgeting through electronic voting. Transactions related to public spending and voting outcomes are immutably recorded, ensuring traceability and auditability. Citizen participation is supported through secure digital identities and transparent voting mechanisms. The impact includes increased trust in public institutions, reduced risk of fraud or manipulation, and improved civic engagement. The use case demonstrates how transparent digital infrastructures can strengthen democratic processes, improve governance quality, and create more accountable and inclusive local administrations.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-845,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00814,EU_PSTW_202609_00814,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Bundesdruckerei GmbH,General services.,"The ONCE project addresses these challenges by enabling citizens to manage their digital identity data directly on their smartphones. The project demonstrates reduced administrative burden, faster onboarding to digital services, and enhanced privacy protection.",ONCE project: Sovereign Data and Self Sovereign Identity,Bundesdruckerei GmbH; Community led; General services.; Germany,"Initially deployed in the German state of Hesse and selected regions in Bavaria and North Rhine-Westphalia, the initiative involves public authorities, municipalities, and private sector partners. It provides a scalable and interoperable model for secure digital identities, supporting cross-sector use and reinforcing citizen-centric data governance.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Digital public services often require citizens to repeatedly register and share personal data across different administrations and sectors, leading to inefficiencies and loss of user control. The ONCE project addresses these challenges by enabling citizens to manage their digital identity data directly on their smartphones. Initially deployed in the German state of Hesse and selected regions in Bavaria and North Rhine-Westphalia, the initiative involves public authorities, municipalities, and private sector partners. Its objective is to simplify online registration and authentication across domains such as e-government, mobility, and tourism, while strengthening data sovereignty and trust. The solution combines self-sovereign identity principles with existing identity infrastructures, building on OPTIMOS 2.0 and mobile identity wallets. Identity credentials are issued, stored, and shared under user control, with verification supported by decentralised trust mechanisms. The system integrates with public and private services without centralised identity databases. The project demonstrates reduced administrative burden, faster onboarding to digital services, and enhanced privacy protection. It provides a scalable and interoperable model for secure digital identities, supporting cross-sector use and reinforcing citizen-centric data governance.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-846,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00815,EU_PSTW_202609_00815,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Una Europa, ERUA, and FilmEU",Tertiary education.,UNKNOWN,Cross-border erasmus exchange scenario,"Una Europa, ERUA, and FilmEU; Consortium; Tertiary education.; European Union",UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"European student mobility programmes like Erasmus faced administrative friction from credential verification requirements, with universities unable to efficiently validate transcripts and qualifications from partner institutions across borders. The EBSI multi-University pilot, launched through the Early Adopters Programme in 2021, brought together 11 universities from 11 countries and 2 European university alliances to test cross-border Verifiable Credentials for education. Six scenarios were developed including Erasmus transcript validation, cross-border PhD applications, European student card verification, and diploma recognition for employment. The pilot demonstrated end-to-end exchange of W3C-standard credentials via EBSI-compliant digital wallets, with institutions including KU Leuven and Italian universities testing the Belgium-Italy corridor. The EBSI-VECTOR project (2023-2025) expanded these pilots across 52 partners in 20 countries, advancing toward production deployment of decentralised educational credentials.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-847,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00816,EU_PSTW_202609_00816,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Open Government of Catalonia (AOC),General services.,Students across Europe face fragmented systems for proving their status and accessing benefits when interacting with public services in different regions.,European Student eCard with blockchain,Open Government of Catalonia (AOC); Consortium; General services.; European Union,"The objective is to enable students to access municipal discounts using a trusted digital identity. The solution leverages a blockchain-based digital identity wallet aligned with European infrastructure standards, allowing students to present verified credentials securely.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Students across Europe face fragmented systems for proving their status and accessing benefits when interacting with public services in different regions. This pilot, led by the Open Government of Catalonia in collaboration with municipalities, universities and technology providers, aims to improve interoperability of student credentials. Stakeholders include students, city councils, universities and service providers. The objective is to enable students to access municipal discounts using a trusted digital identity. The solution leverages a blockchain-based digital identity wallet aligned with European infrastructure standards, allowing students to present verified credentials securely. The pilot enabled students to obtain discounts in multiple Catalan municipalities while maintaining control over their data. It demonstrated interoperability, compliance with European identity frameworks and improved user experience, laying the groundwork for scalable cross-border student services.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-848,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00817,EU_PSTW_202609_00817,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,The Council of Europe,Social protection n.e.c.,"Refugees often face significant barriers to education and employment due to the lack of formal documentation proving their qualifications. The European Qualification Passport for Refugees (EQPR), developed by the Council of Europe, addresses this challenge by providing a structured and trusted assessment of refugees’ educational background, even when evidence is incomplete.",EQPR - CoE Refugee Passport,The Council of Europe; European Institution/Agency; Social protection n.e.c.; European Union,"Implemented since 2017 in 15 countries, the EQPR involves refugees, higher education institutions, employers, and public authorities. The digital pilot leverages the European Blockchain Services Infrastructure to issue and verify EQPR credentials securely.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Refugees often face significant barriers to education and employment due to the lack of formal documentation proving their qualifications. The European Qualification Passport for Refugees (EQPR), developed by the Council of Europe, addresses this challenge by providing a structured and trusted assessment of refugees’ educational background, even when evidence is incomplete. The objective is to support further studies, labour market access, and social integration across Europe. Implemented since 2017 in 15 countries, the EQPR involves refugees, higher education institutions, employers, and public authorities. The digital pilot leverages the European Blockchain Services Infrastructure to issue and verify EQPR credentials securely. The entire workflow has been digitalised, with qualification assessments issued as verifiable credentials through a blockchain-based diploma platform. Holders can store and share their EQPR via a digital wallet, enabling instant verification by universities and employers across borders. The initiative significantly improves accessibility, trust, and efficiency in qualification recognition. It reduces administrative delays, empowers refugees with portable credentials, and supports inclusive education and employment pathways across Europe.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-849,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00818,EU_PSTW_202609_00818,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,The European Consortium of Innovative Universities (ECIU),Tertiary education.,"Student mobility within Europe is hindered by the lack of interoperable systems for recognising short learning experiences such as micro-credentials. Universities often rely on manual processes, and achievements obtained abroad are difficult to transfer or verify.",Cross-border micro-credentials,The European Consortium of Innovative Universities (ECIU); Consortium; Tertiary education.; European Union,"The objective of this use case is to enable trusted, transferable, and learner-controlled micro-credentials across borders. The solution is implemented through the EBSI Early Adopters pilot, using self-sovereign identity and verifiable credentials.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Student mobility within Europe is hindered by the lack of interoperable systems for recognising short learning experiences such as micro-credentials. Universities often rely on manual processes, and achievements obtained abroad are difficult to transfer or verify. Tampere University and Kaunas University of Technology face these challenges within the ECIU university network, particularly for micro-modules and challenge-based learning. The objective of this use case is to enable trusted, transferable, and learner-controlled micro-credentials across borders. The solution is implemented through the EBSI Early Adopters pilot, using self-sovereign identity and verifiable credentials. Universities issue micro-credentials aligned with shared semantic standards, while students store them in digital wallets and share them as needed. The EBSI infrastructure ensures authenticity, legal validity, and compliance with GDPR and eIDAS. The impact includes reduced administrative workload, improved student mobility, and better recognition of skills and achievements. The use case demonstrates a scalable European model for micro-credential exchange, supporting lifelong learning and flexible education pathways.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-850,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00819,EU_PSTW_202609_00819,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""social_nonprofit""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"EBSI4RO, Eledger and fr.EBSI",Tertiary education.,"Cross-border recognition of higher education diplomas remains complex, slowing student mobility and increasing administrative costs for institutions and graduates.",Bachelor/Master Diploma Credential,"EBSI4RO, Eledger and fr.EBSI; Academic-Research; Tertiary education.; European Union","This use case aims to simplify the issuance and verification of Bachelor, Master, and PhD diplomas across Europe. The solution leverages the European Blockchain Services Infrastructure to issue digital diplomas as verifiable credentials.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Cross-border recognition of higher education diplomas remains complex, slowing student mobility and increasing administrative costs for institutions and graduates. This use case aims to simplify the issuance and verification of Bachelor, Master, and PhD diplomas across Europe. Involving Romania, Greece, and France, the initiative supports the issuance of Europass diploma credentials that are valid and verifiable across borders. The objective is to provide students, universities, and employers with a trusted and efficient mechanism to exchange academic qualifications. The solution leverages the European Blockchain Services Infrastructure to issue digital diplomas as verifiable credentials. Academic institutions anchor diploma proofs on the shared European infrastructure, while graduates receive credentials they can store and share through digital wallets. Verification can be performed instantly by authorised parties without intermediaries. The results include faster verification processes, reduced fraud risk, and improved interoperability between national education systems. This use case strengthens trust in academic credentials, enhances student mobility, and lays the foundation for a harmonised European digital education ecosystem.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-851,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00820,EU_PSTW_202609_00820,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,University of Tartu,R&D general public services.,UNKNOWN,Neurokõne - provide more accessible e-services,University of Tartu; Academic-Research; R&D general public services.; Estonia,"Estonian-language e-services remained inaccessible to visually impaired citizens and others who rely on screen readers, as existing text-to-speech solutions for Estonian lacked natural-sounding voices and adequate quality for extended use.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Estonian-language e-services remained inaccessible to visually impaired citizens and others who rely on screen readers, as existing text-to-speech solutions for Estonian lacked natural-sounding voices and adequate quality for extended use. The University of Tartu's Natural Language Processing research group developed Neurokõne, a neural network-based text-to-speech synthesis system trained on 92.4 hours of speech data from six speakers. Released as open-source software (MIT licence), the system integrates with Android and iOS accessibility features, enabling Estonian speech synthesis across government e-services and applications. The solution significantly outperforms existing alternatives including Google's Estonian speech synthesis in evaluation tests. Neurokõne forms part of Estonia's national language technology programme ensuring digital services remain accessible to all citizens regardless of ability, supporting the country's digital-first government approach.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-852,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00821,EU_PSTW_202609_00821,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,HI IBERIA INGENIERIA Y PROYECTOS SL and Fondazione Bruno Kessler,R&D health.,"Supporting citizens in maintaining long-term physical, cognitive, mental, and social wellbeing is a growing societal challenge, particularly in ageing populations where lifestyle-related risks accumulate over time. Individuals often lack personalised guidance and sustained motivation to adopt healthier behaviours, while health and social care systems face increasing pressure to deliver preventive, user-centred support at scale.",WellCo - Wellbeing and health virtual coach,HI IBERIA INGENIERIA Y PROYECTOS SL and Fondazione Bruno Kessler; Academic-Research; R&D health.; Spain,"The solution is implemented through a digital platform featuring a virtual coaching system that delivers tailored interventions based on individual profiles, preferences, and behavioural patterns.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Supporting citizens in maintaining long-term physical, cognitive, mental, and social wellbeing is a growing societal challenge, particularly in ageing populations where lifestyle-related risks accumulate over time. Individuals often lack personalised guidance and sustained motivation to adopt healthier behaviours, while health and social care systems face increasing pressure to deliver preventive, user-centred support at scale. The WellCo project, funded under Horizon 2020, addresses this challenge by empowering users to make informed and sustainable lifestyle choices across seven wellbeing domains, including physical activity, nutrition, cognitive stimulation, and social participation. Stakeholders include citizens, healthcare and wellbeing service providers, and public authorities seeking preventive, cost-effective interventions that adapt to individual needs. The objective is to provide continuous, personalised guidance and follow-up that supports behavioural change and long-term wellbeing. The solution is implemented through a digital platform featuring a virtual coaching system that delivers tailored interventions based on individual profiles, preferences, and behavioural patterns. Personalisation is achieved through data-driven models that analyse user interactions and contextual information to recommend goals, activities, and social engagement opportunities. The system integrates mobile interfaces, social networking features, and health-related data sources. The project demonstrates improved user engagement, more effective behaviour change support, and scalable delivery of personalised wellbeing services, contributing to better quality of life and reduced long-term pressure on health and care systems.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-855,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00822,EU_PSTW_202609_00822,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Wuppertal Institute for Climate,Waste management.,"Municipal waste management systems face persistent challenges in increasing recycling rates, improving material purity, and reducing the volume of waste sent to landfill. Manual sorting processes are labour-intensive, costly, and expose workers to health and safety risks, while also limiting the efficiency of material recovery.",Artificial Intelligence in the Sorting of Municipal Waste as an Enabler of the Circular Economy,Wuppertal Institute for Climate; Academic-Research; Waste management.; Germany,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Municipal waste management systems face persistent challenges in increasing recycling rates, improving material purity, and reducing the volume of waste sent to landfill. Manual sorting processes are labour-intensive, costly, and expose workers to health and safety risks, while also limiting the efficiency of material recovery. The “ZRR for municipal waste” research project addresses these challenges by modernising waste sorting operations to better support circular economy objectives. Stakeholders include municipal authorities, waste management operators, workers, and recycling industries seeking higher-quality secondary raw materials. The objective is to increase recovery rates, capture valuable materials from rejected waste streams, and improve working conditions within treatment plants. The solution introduces robotic sorting systems equipped with advanced perception and decision-making capabilities, integrated into existing waste treatment facilities. These systems analyse waste streams in real time to identify and separate materials with high precision, complementing or replacing manual sorting. Operational data is used to optimise performance under real plant conditions. Results from pilot deployments, including tests in Barcelona, show higher recycling yields, improved purity of recovered materials, and reduced landfill disposal. The initiative delivers environmental benefits, operational efficiency gains, and safer working environments, demonstrating the potential for scalable, technology-enabled circular waste management.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-857,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00823,EU_PSTW_202609_00823,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Leiden University Medical Centre,R&D health.,"Many patients lack accessible tools for continuous monitoring of foot health outside clinical settings, resulting in delayed intervention and increased healthcare costs.",Prevention of Diabetic Foot Syndrome,Leiden University Medical Centre; Academic-Research; R&D health.; Netherlands,"The proposed system uses machine learning models trained on clinical image datasets to identify visual indicators of inflammation, ulcers or tissue deterioration.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Diabetic Foot Syndrome represents a major public health concern, frequently leading to hospitalisation and lower-limb amputation if not detected at an early stage. Many patients lack accessible tools for continuous monitoring of foot health outside clinical settings, resulting in delayed intervention and increased healthcare costs. Team FAIDEN from Leiden University proposed a user-friendly software solution to support at-home monitoring of early warning signs associated with diabetic complications. The objective is to empower patients to detect anomalies promptly and seek timely medical advice, thereby reducing severe outcomes. The proposed system uses machine learning models trained on clinical image datasets to identify visual indicators of inflammation, ulcers or tissue deterioration. Through image capture via smartphone or dedicated devices, computer vision algorithms analyse patterns and classify risk levels. The application provides automated feedback and may connect to healthcare providers for further assessment. The project is currently at the concept and development stage, presented within innovation and regional collaboration frameworks. Its potential impact includes earlier detection, reduced hospital admissions, lower amputation rates and improved patient autonomy, contributing to cost savings and improved quality of life.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-858,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00824,EU_PSTW_202609_00824,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Vojkovice,Public order and safety n.e.c.,"Manual enforcement mechanisms proved insufficient, limiting the municipality’s ability to monitor violations and impose penalties effectively.",Smart enforcement: automated detection and handling of prohibited transit in Vojkovice,Municipality of Vojkovice; Local Government; Public order and safety n.e.c.; Czechia,The municipality of Vojkovice has experienced increased safety risks and infrastructure damage due to heavy trucks using roads where transit is prohibited. The project has been implemented as a smart enforcement solution within the municipality.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The municipality of Vojkovice has experienced increased safety risks and infrastructure damage due to heavy trucks using roads where transit is prohibited. Manual enforcement mechanisms proved insufficient, limiting the municipality’s ability to monitor violations and impose penalties effectively. The objective of the project is to strengthen traffic law enforcement, protect infrastructure and improve quality of life for residents by introducing automated monitoring and sanctioning mechanisms. The system employs camera-based monitoring infrastructure combined with automated number plate recognition technology to detect unauthorised transit in real time. Image processing algorithms extract vehicle identifiers, while rule-based validation systems compare detected vehicles against authorised transit databases. Integration with municipal enforcement systems enables automated generation of violation records and supports administrative follow-up procedures. The project has been implemented as a smart enforcement solution within the municipality. It improves detection accuracy, accelerates penalisation procedures, enhances road safety and reduces infrastructure damage, while optimising resource allocation within local law enforcement services.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-861,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00825,EU_PSTW_202609_00825,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,AOC Consorci Administració Oberta de Catalunya,General public services n.e.c.,"Catalonia's Open Administration Consortium (AOC) receives over 91,000 annual support requests, with citizens struggling to navigate complex e-government procedures for digital identification and certificate management.",La Paula i la Rita - the chatbot assistants in the authentication processes in Catalunya,AOC Consorci Administració Oberta de Catalunya; Regional Government; General public services n.e.c.; Spain,"The AOC deployed La Paula (January 2021) and La Rita (April 2021) as AI-powered chatbot assistants supporting the VALid identification service and idCAT Certificate respectively, developed in partnership with 1MillionBot. The chatbots use natural language processing to interpret citizen queries and provide instant responses 24/7, achieving over 95% accuracy rate.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Catalonia's Open Administration Consortium (AOC) receives over 91,000 annual support requests, with citizens struggling to navigate complex e-government procedures for digital identification and certificate management. The AOC deployed La Paula (January 2021) and La Rita (April 2021) as AI-powered chatbot assistants supporting the VALid identification service and idCAT Certificate respectively, developed in partnership with 1MillionBot. The chatbots use natural language processing to interpret citizen queries and provide instant responses 24/7, achieving over 95% accuracy rate. In 2022, Paula supported 62,588 people with 127,454 messages whilst Rita assisted 43,520 people through 100,606 messages. The AOC has pioneered algorithmic transparency in Spain, publishing detailed documentation of chatbot operation, governance, and limitations. In May 2024, the AOC upgraded to generative AI chatbots, serving 11,575 monthly users with 88% success rate and 90% cost savings per query.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-878,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00826,EU_PSTW_202609_00826,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Diputació de Girona,General public services n.e.c.,"At the Diputació de Girona, the user support service managed by the AOC faced challenges in understanding process variability and identifying improvement opportunities based on traditional reporting methods. By reconstructing real process flows from operational data, the approach reveals hidden process variants, bottlenecks, and rework loops.",Process Mining pilot for the support service and case management of the Diputació de Girona,Diputació de Girona; Local Government; General public services n.e.c.; Spain,The solution applies process mining techniques to system event logs generated by support and records management platforms.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Public sector support and case management services often appear straightforward but can hide significant operational complexity that affects efficiency, response times, and resource allocation. At the Diputació de Girona, the user support service managed by the AOC faced challenges in understanding process variability and identifying improvement opportunities based on traditional reporting methods. The objective of this pilot was to gain a clear, evidence-based view of how support processes actually function in practice and to identify optimisation scenarios that could improve service delivery. Stakeholders include support staff, service managers, and public sector users relying on timely and effective assistance. The solution applies process mining techniques to system event logs generated by support and records management platforms. By reconstructing real process flows from operational data, the approach reveals hidden process variants, bottlenecks, and rework loops. The pilot uncovered more than 3,400 distinct process variants within workflows previously assumed to be linear. Simulation capabilities allow decision-makers to test “what-if” scenarios, such as changing escalation paths between support levels. The results indicate potential savings of up to 16,000 processing hours per year, alongside improved transparency and data-driven decision-making. The pilot demonstrates how process intelligence can significantly enhance efficiency, service quality, and organisational learning in public administration.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-879,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00827,EU_PSTW_202609_00827,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,AOC Consorci Administració Oberta de Catalunya,General public services n.e.c.,"Public administrations receive large volumes of citizen requests through digital channels, often submitted as generic applications that require manual review and routing. This leads to delays, inconsistent handling, and increased administrative workload.",Automatic classification of citizens' issues or solecituds,AOC Consorci Administració Oberta de Catalunya; Regional Government; General public services n.e.c.; Spain,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Public administrations receive large volumes of citizen requests through digital channels, often submitted as generic applications that require manual review and routing. This leads to delays, inconsistent handling, and increased administrative workload. The AOC initiative addresses the need to streamline the intake and management of citizen submissions received via platforms such as EACAT and e-TRAM. Stakeholders include civil servants responsible for case handling, administrative managers, and citizens expecting timely responses. The objective is to improve efficiency and consistency by automatically classifying incoming requests and directing them to the appropriate services. The solution implements predictive classification models trained on historical application data to analyse the content and metadata of submissions. Incoming requests are automatically categorised based on learned patterns, enabling faster routing and prioritisation. The system integrates with existing document and case management platforms, supporting human oversight and continuous improvement. The pilot demonstrates reduced manual effort, faster response times, and more consistent case handling. It also improves scalability as request volumes grow, while freeing staff to focus on higher-value tasks. Overall, the initiative enhances administrative efficiency and user experience, supporting more responsive and effective digital public services.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK|F06_HIGH_VOLUME_SCALABILITY_BOTTLENECK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-880,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00828,EU_PSTW_202609_00828,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Panevėžys Development Agency,Unemployment.,"Regional authorities often lack timely, evidence-based insights into how labour market demand evolves, making it difficult to plan vocational education, reskilling programmes and economic development policies. In Lithuania, the Panevėžys Development Agency initiated this pilot to address fragmentation of labour market data and the absence of long-term, region-specific forecasts.",Lithuanian solution for systematised forecast of the regional labour market demand,Panevėžys Development Agency; Local Government; Unemployment.; Lithuania,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Regional authorities often lack timely, evidence-based insights into how labour market demand evolves, making it difficult to plan vocational education, reskilling programmes and economic development policies. In Lithuania, the Panevėžys Development Agency initiated this pilot to address fragmentation of labour market data and the absence of long-term, region-specific forecasts. Stakeholders include regional development agencies, vocational training providers, educational institutions, employers and job seekers. The objective is to provide a comprehensive and forward-looking view of labour market dynamics to better align skills supply with future demand. The solution automatically collects and aggregates publicly available data sources related to employment, education, demographics and economic activity. Advanced forecasting models are applied to identify trends, skill shortages and emerging occupations over extended time horizons. The system integrates these outputs into a regional labour market model that supports scenario analysis and policy planning. The pilot was co-created with Neurotechnology, a Lithuanian technology company acting as a startup partner. The impact includes improved strategic planning for vocational guidance, more targeted training and retraining services, and better-informed regional economic policies. Qualitatively, the solution reduces manual analytical effort, improves forecast accuracy and provides a scalable decision-support tool that can be replicated in other regions.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-883,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00829,EU_PSTW_202609_00829,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Vilnius City Municipality,Protection of biodiversity and landscape.,"Urban greenery plays a critical role in environmental quality and citizens’ wellbeing, yet monitoring vegetation health across an entire city is resource-intensive and often relies on manual inspections. The City of Vilnius launched this pilot to improve oversight of urban green areas and enable early identification of issues such as drought stress, lack of maintenance or illegal felling.",Lithuania Solution for greening the city of Vilnius using satellite data and AI,Vilnius City Municipality; Local Government; Protection of biodiversity and landscape.; Lithuania,The prototype continuously monitors the city’s green infrastructure using a combination of radar and optical satellite imagery.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Urban greenery plays a critical role in environmental quality and citizens’ wellbeing, yet monitoring vegetation health across an entire city is resource-intensive and often relies on manual inspections. The City of Vilnius launched this pilot to improve oversight of urban green areas and enable early identification of issues such as drought stress, lack of maintenance or illegal felling. Stakeholders include municipal environmental departments, urban planners, maintenance contractors and citizens. The objective is to reduce administrative burden while improving the accuracy and timeliness of greenery management decisions. The prototype continuously monitors the city’s green infrastructure using a combination of radar and optical satellite imagery. Analytical algorithms process time-series data to detect changes in vegetation condition, identify problem areas and flag anomalies requiring intervention. Outputs are visualised through a monitoring interface that supports planning of watering, mowing, replacement and enforcement activities. The pilot significantly reduces the need for on-site inspections, improves detection of illegal or unauthorised actions, and enables proactive maintenance. It delivers qualitative gains in efficiency, environmental protection and decision-making quality, while providing a scalable model for data-driven urban environmental management that can be extended to other cities.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-884,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00830,EU_PSTW_202609_00830,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Lithuanian Hydrometeorological Service,Environmental protection n.e.c.,"Extreme weather events and hazardous natural phenomena pose increasing risks to populations, infrastructure and economic activity, yet information about their impact is often fragmented across agencies and data sources.",Lithuania Innovative solution for the assessment of the impact and damage of hydrometeorological phenomena,Lithuanian Hydrometeorological Service; Central-Government; Environmental protection n.e.c.; Lithuania,The approach is designed to scale nationally and to evolve with additional data sources and use cases.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Extreme weather events and hazardous natural phenomena pose increasing risks to populations, infrastructure and economic activity, yet information about their impact is often fragmented across agencies and data sources. This Lithuanian pilot was initiated to strengthen national capabilities in monitoring, forecasting and impact assessment of hydrometeorological events. Stakeholders include public authorities, emergency services, infrastructure operators, businesses and citizens. The objective is to improve situational awareness and deliver more accurate and timely alerts to support preparedness and response. The solution integrates data from multiple sources, including meteorological observations, environmental sensors and historical records, into a unified processing pipeline. Predictive and analytical models are applied to monitor conditions, forecast hazardous events and assess potential impacts and damages across sectors. The system supports the generation of targeted alerts and risk assessments tailored to specific regions and user groups. The pilot improves the performance of climate monitoring and forecasting, enhances coordination between institutions, and supports more effective risk communication. Qualitative impacts include faster response times, better-informed decision-making and increased resilience of communities and economic actors. The approach is designed to scale nationally and to evolve with additional data sources and use cases.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-885,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00831,EU_PSTW_202609_00831,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Public Service Ministry,General public services n.e.c.,"Citizens often need quick answers to routine questions about public services, but traditional customer-service models in government can be costly to scale and may deliver inconsistent response times across ministries.",Servizz.Gov Chatbox in Malta,Public Service Ministry; Central-Government; General public services n.e.c.; Malta,"The implementation uses a chatbot architecture based initially on a rule-based mechanism and FAQ content, with machine-learning elements added incrementally as user intents are captured and used to refine the model through iteration and feedback.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Citizens often need quick answers to routine questions about public services, but traditional customer-service models in government can be costly to scale and may deliver inconsistent response times across ministries. In Malta, the Servizz.Gov chatbot was introduced to improve front-line digital interaction and create a shared support layer that could serve multiple ministries more efficiently. The objective is to provide more immediate access to information, achieve economies of scale through horizontal collaboration, and gradually develop a more capable citizen chatbot integrated with wider government services. The implementation uses a chatbot architecture based initially on a rule-based mechanism and FAQ content, with machine-learning elements added incrementally as user intents are captured and used to refine the model through iteration and feedback. At a conceptual level, the solution combines a central knowledge base, conversational handling in Maltese and English, and a training loop that improves responses over time. The longer-term design also envisages integration with third-party systems so that the chatbot can move beyond information provision towards more connected service support. The impact includes reduced waiting time for routine enquiries, broader 24/7 digital access, and more consistent handling of common questions. It also improves scalability across government and creates operational value by consolidating support functions into a shared conversational channel.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-886,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00832,EU_PSTW_202609_00832,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,General Directorate of Public Finance,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.","Property-tax compliance is difficult to enforce comprehensively when local authorities depend on declarations that may omit features affecting taxable value, such as swimming pools and other built structures. The objective is to detect non-declared taxable features, increase the accuracy of property records, and improve revenue collection without relying solely on manual inspection.",AI tool to spot undeclared swimming pools in France,"General Directorate of Public Finance; Central-Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; France","In France, this use case was developed to help the tax administration identify undeclared swimming pools more efficiently and strengthen property-tax enforcement using large-scale image analysis. The implementation uses a computer-vision system developed with Capgemini, working with aerial photographs and comparing detected features against real-estate and tax databases.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Property-tax compliance is difficult to enforce comprehensively when local authorities depend on declarations that may omit features affecting taxable value, such as swimming pools and other built structures. In France, this use case was developed to help the tax administration identify undeclared swimming pools more efficiently and strengthen property-tax enforcement using large-scale image analysis. The objective is to detect non-declared taxable features, increase the accuracy of property records, and improve revenue collection without relying solely on manual inspection. The implementation uses a computer-vision system developed with Capgemini, working with aerial photographs and comparing detected features against real-estate and tax databases. At a conceptual level, the system scans images for pool-like visual patterns, links identified locations to property records, and flags cases where a relevant address appears not to have declared a pool. This creates a targeted workflow for further tax compliance action. Reported results show that 20,356 previously undeclared residential swimming pools were identified, generating about EUR 10 million in additional tax revenue in the earlier reported phase, while later reporting noted wider fiscal gains and plans to extend checks to features such as annexes and verandas. The impact includes stronger compliance detection, more efficient tax control, and improved scalability in property-record verification.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-887,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00833,EU_PSTW_202609_00833,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Ministry of Finance,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.","Italy loses over €99 billion annually to tax evasion, with €30 billion from VAT alone, whilst traditional enforcement methods struggled to identify non-compliant taxpayers among millions of returns.","VeRa 1.0, the artificial intelligence that tracks tax evaders","Ministry of Finance; Central-Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Italy","The Agenzia delle Entrate developed VeRa (Verifica dei Rapporti finanziari), an AI-powered algorithm that cross-references taxpayer filings with data from the Financial Relations Archive, property registries, bank accounts, and electronic payments to identify compliance risks.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Italy loses over €99 billion annually to tax evasion, with €30 billion from VAT alone, whilst traditional enforcement methods struggled to identify non-compliant taxpayers among millions of returns. The Agenzia delle Entrate developed VeRa (Verifica dei Rapporti finanziari), an AI-powered algorithm that cross-references taxpayer filings with data from the Financial Relations Archive, property registries, bank accounts, and electronic payments to identify compliance risks. In 2022, VeRa flagged one million high-risk cases and helped prevent over €6.8 billion in fraud. The system assigns risk scores enabling targeted audits rather than random checks, whilst EU-funded projects through Sogei introduce network analysis, machine learning, and data visualisation for complex evasion schemes. In 2023, the agency recovered €18.1 billion, increasing to projected €19.6 billion by 2025. A complementary AI chatbot launched in 2023 analyses VAT returns in real-time, flagging irregularities for immediate investigation.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-888,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00834,EU_PSTW_202609_00834,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Ministry of Economic Affairs and Communications (MKM),Cultural services.,Estonia identified the limited availability of high-quality speech datasets as a barrier to the development of advanced language technologies in a small-language context.,"""Donate a Speech"": Estonia crowdsources speech data for the preservation of the Estonian language",Ministry of Economic Affairs and Communications (MKM); Central-Government; Cultural services.; Estonia,The initiative has been implemented and successfully collected thousands of hours of speech data.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Estonia identified the limited availability of high-quality speech datasets as a barrier to the development of advanced language technologies in a small-language context. To preserve the Estonian language and enhance digital public services, the Ministry of Economic Affairs and Communications and the State Information System Agency launched the “Donate a Speech” campaign. The objective is to create an open, large-scale spoken language corpus to support the development of speech-based digital services across the public and private sectors. Citizens voluntarily contribute voice recordings, which are anonymised and compiled into a national speech database. The dataset supports the training of machine learning models for speech recognition, text-to-speech and real-time subtitling applications. The infrastructure integrates data collection platforms, secure storage systems and research access frameworks. The initiative has been implemented and successfully collected thousands of hours of speech data. It strengthens Estonia’s digital sovereignty, enhances accessibility of public services, supports innovation in language technologies and improves inclusivity through better speech-enabled services.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-889,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00835,EU_PSTW_202609_00835,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,European Patent Office,"General economic, commercial and labour affair","Patent examination at the European Patent Office involves assessing millions of applications against complex technical and legal criteria, with AI-related inventions presenting particular challenges due to rapidly evolving technology and novel patentability questions.",Applying AI to increase quality in the patent grant process at the European Patent Office,"European Patent Office; European Institution/Agency; General economic, commercial and labour affair; European Union","The EPO has implemented AI and machine learning tools to enhance patent examination quality, assist examiners with prior art searches, and improve consistency in assessing technical character and inventive step for computer-implemented inventions.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Patent examination at the European Patent Office involves assessing millions of applications against complex technical and legal criteria, with AI-related inventions presenting particular challenges due to rapidly evolving technology and novel patentability questions. The EPO has implemented AI and machine learning tools to enhance patent examination quality, assist examiners with prior art searches, and improve consistency in assessing technical character and inventive step for computer-implemented inventions. The 2024 Guidelines for Examination introduced specific provisions for AI inventions, requiring disclosure of training datasets when necessary to reproduce claimed technical effects and establishing standards for plausibility of AI-related claims. These updates followed decisions from the Boards of Appeal (including T 0161/18) addressing sufficiency of disclosure for machine learning inventions. The system supports examiners whilst maintaining human decision-making authority, processing applications across all 39 member states of the European Patent Organisation.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-896,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00836,EU_PSTW_202609_00836,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""social_nonprofit""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,National Land Survey of Finland,"General economic, commercial and labour affair","Traditional map updating methods are time-consuming and struggle to keep pace with rapid changes in the built and natural environment. In Finland, the National Land Survey sought to improve the accuracy and timeliness of spatial data while reducing manual effort.",Advanced Technology for National Topographic Map Updating - Increasing spatial data accuracy with AI in Finland,"National Land Survey of Finland; Central-Government; General economic, commercial and labour affair; Finland","The solution applies advanced image analysis techniques to aerial and satellite imagery, using deep neural models trained for object detection and change recognition tasks.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Maintaining accurate and up-to-date national topographic maps is essential for land management, infrastructure planning, environmental monitoring, and public safety. Traditional map updating methods are time-consuming and struggle to keep pace with rapid changes in the built and natural environment. In Finland, the National Land Survey sought to improve the accuracy and timeliness of spatial data while reducing manual effort. The objective of the ATMU project was to enhance object detection and change recognition for buildings, roads, and watercourses at national scale. Stakeholders include mapping authorities, planners, and public agencies relying on high-quality geospatial data. The solution applies advanced image analysis techniques to aerial and satellite imagery, using deep neural models trained for object detection and change recognition tasks. Different model architectures are applied to specific features, supported by transfer and multitask learning approaches. By the end of the project, building detection achieved an accuracy of 97.9%, and 96% of building and road changes were correctly identified. The project delivers significant improvements in data accuracy, update frequency, and operational efficiency, providing a scalable foundation for modern, data-driven national mapping services.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-897,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00837,EU_PSTW_202609_00837,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,UK Hydrographic Office,Environmental protection n.e.c.,"Global mangrove mapping relied on infrequently updated datasets that could not capture the dynamic changes affecting these critical coastal ecosystems, limiting understanding of carbon sequestration, biodiversity, and natural coastal protection.",Mapping mangroves with Serverless and Deep Learning,UK Hydrographic Office; Central-Government; Environmental protection n.e.c.; United Kingdom,"The UK Hydrographic Office (UKHO) developed the ADMIRALTY Global Mangrove Data Set using deep learning applied to European Space Agency Sentinel-2 satellite imagery, creating the world's highest resolution, most up-to-date global view of mangrove forests.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Global mangrove mapping relied on infrequently updated datasets that could not capture the dynamic changes affecting these critical coastal ecosystems, limiting understanding of carbon sequestration, biodiversity, and natural coastal protection. The UK Hydrographic Office (UKHO) developed the ADMIRALTY Global Mangrove Data Set using deep learning applied to European Space Agency Sentinel-2 satellite imagery, creating the world's highest resolution, most up-to-date global view of mangrove forests. The neural network was trained on over 7,000 coastal images and achieves geo-generalisability across diverse global locations through collaboration between UKHO's Data Science, Data Engineering, and Remote Sensing teams. The system automatically updates every six months, processing new cloud-free satellite imagery to detect mangrove extent changes. This serverless architecture enables scalable analysis supporting marine navigation, coastal sustainability planning, and climate change research on these vital carbon sinks that sequester 3-5 times more carbon than inland tropical forests.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-900,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00838,EU_PSTW_202609_00838,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Estonian Investment Agency,UNKNOWN,"Military intelligence operations struggle with data overload from multiple sources including UAVs, cyber events, open-source intelligence, and human reports, requiring rapid integration and analysis to support tactical decision-making.","SensusQ: transforming information into knowledge with the help of automation, integration, and interoperability",Estonian Investment Agency; Central-Government; Estonia,"SensusQ, an Estonian DefenceTech startup founded by combat veterans Marko Kaseleht and Villiko Nurmoja, developed an AI-powered intelligence management platform that fuses data from diverse sensors and sources into a unified operational picture. The system has been deployed by NATO forces and Ukraine, where it has demonstrated 200% improvement in response times and significant increases in data accuracy.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Military intelligence operations struggle with data overload from multiple sources including UAVs, cyber events, open-source intelligence, and human reports, requiring rapid integration and analysis to support tactical decision-making. SensusQ, an Estonian DefenceTech startup founded by combat veterans Marko Kaseleht and Villiko Nurmoja, developed an AI-powered intelligence management platform that fuses data from diverse sensors and sources into a unified operational picture. The system has been deployed by NATO forces and Ukraine, where it has demonstrated 200% improvement in response times and significant increases in data accuracy. In 2024, SensusQ secured €3.8M funding from US-based New North Ventures and in 2025 contributed to the British Army's Project ASGARD digital targeting initiative. The platform supports both the Estonian Defence League and allied nations, featuring NATO-standard military symbols, cross-data search, and standardised reporting whilst maintaining on-premise deployment to ensure sensitive data remains under client control.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-902,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00839,EU_PSTW_202609_00839,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Ministry of Education,Education not definable by level.,"Italy's education system faced the annual challenge of assigning approximately 100,000 teachers to schools across diverse regions, a complex optimization problem that had historically relied on manual processes prone to errors and delays.",The AI algorithm that blew up Italy’s school system,Ministry of Education; Central-Government; Education not definable by level.; Italy,"The Ministry of Education implemented an algorithmic system to automatically assign teachers to schools based on preferences, qualifications, and regional needs, processing massive datasets to generate placements within compressed timeframes.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Italy's education system faced the annual challenge of assigning approximately 100,000 teachers to schools across diverse regions, a complex optimization problem that had historically relied on manual processes prone to errors and delays. The Ministry of Education implemented an algorithmic system to automatically assign teachers to schools based on preferences, qualifications, and regional needs, processing massive datasets to generate placements within compressed timeframes. However, the 2020 deployment generated significant controversy when thousands of teachers received assignments to schools far from their preferences or in regions they had not selected, with some relocated hundreds of kilometres from their homes. The algorithm's logic and decision criteria remained opaque, preventing affected teachers from understanding or effectively challenging their placements. The case became a prominent example in debates about algorithmic accountability in public administration, highlighting tensions between efficiency gains and transparency requirements in automated government decision-making.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK|F08_ERROR_COMPLIANCE_RISK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-911,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00840,EU_PSTW_202609_00840,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Robert Koch Institut (RKI),Public health services.,"The system processes data from Germany's mandatory notification system, comparing current case counts against historical patterns whilst adjusting for seasonal variations and reporting delays.",Signals - Detection of statistically significant clusters in reported infectious diseases,Robert Koch Institut (RKI); Central-Government; Public health services.; Germany,The Robert Koch Institute (RKI) developed the Signals system using statistical algorithms to automatically detect statistically significant changes in reported disease incidence that warrant epidemiological investigation.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Germany's disease surveillance system required methods to identify unusual patterns in reported infections that might indicate emerging outbreaks, requiring analysis of large volumes of routine surveillance data against expected baselines. The Robert Koch Institute (RKI) developed the Signals system using statistical algorithms to automatically detect statistically significant changes in reported disease incidence that warrant epidemiological investigation. The system processes data from Germany's mandatory notification system, comparing current case counts against historical patterns whilst adjusting for seasonal variations and reporting delays. When anomalies are detected, automated alerts trigger specialist review to determine whether observed increases represent genuine outbreaks requiring public health response. The platform has operated since 2015, providing early warning capabilities that complement traditional epidemiological surveillance methods and supporting Germany's capacity to detect and respond to infectious disease threats.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-912,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00841,EU_PSTW_202609_00841,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,General Directorate of Customs (GZD),"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.","Customs authorities face growing challenges in detecting counterfeit goods within increasing volumes of international trade. Manual inspection processes are resource-intensive and may not consistently identify sophisticated counterfeits, undermining trademark protection and fair competition.",AI to spot counterfeit products at customs,"General Directorate of Customs (GZD); Central-Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Germany",The system uses image recognition algorithms trained on extensive datasets of authentic and counterfeit product images. The solution has been implemented within customs advisory frameworks.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Customs authorities face growing challenges in detecting counterfeit goods within increasing volumes of international trade. Manual inspection processes are resource-intensive and may not consistently identify sophisticated counterfeits, undermining trademark protection and fair competition. The German Federal Information Technology Centre (ITZBund), advising the Central Office for Commercial Legal Protection and the General Directorate of Customs, developed an AI-supported solution to enhance counterfeit detection. The objective is to improve efficiency and accuracy in identifying infringing goods while supporting customs officers in decision-making. The system uses image recognition algorithms trained on extensive datasets of authentic and counterfeit product images. Visual data captured during inspections are processed through convolutional neural networks that classify brand attributes and estimate the probability of counterfeiting. The system continuously updates its training dataset to improve performance. Integration with customs databases enables officers to access risk assessments in real time, while final decisions remain under human authority. The solution has been implemented within customs advisory frameworks. It increases detection accuracy, accelerates inspections, reduces manual workload and strengthens protection of intellectual property rights.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-921,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00842,EU_PSTW_202609_00842,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,General Customs Directorate,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.",UNKNOWN,Chatbot LinA: the chatbot that answers general inquiries in the field of motor vehicle tax and customs law,"General Customs Directorate; Central-Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Germany","The Federal Customs Administration (Zoll) deployed LinA, an AI-powered chatbot that answers general inquiries about customs matters through natural language interaction available 24/7 on the authority's website.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Germany's customs authority receives high volumes of citizen inquiries about import duties, customs procedures, and international trade regulations, creating significant workload for staff and variable response times for users. The Federal Customs Administration (Zoll) deployed LinA, an AI-powered chatbot that answers general inquiries about customs matters through natural language interaction available 24/7 on the authority's website. The system handles routine questions about duty calculations, prohibited goods, travel allowances, and customs procedures, freeing human agents to address complex cases requiring specialist expertise. LinA is part of Germany's broader Bundesbots initiative developing standardised chatbot infrastructure for federal agencies, sharing technological approaches and ensuring consistent user experience across government services. The platform demonstrates how conversational AI can improve public service accessibility whilst managing increasing demand for digital interaction channels with government authorities.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-922,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00843,EU_PSTW_202609_00843,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,GZD/ Financial Intelligence Unit (FIU),"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.",The increasing volume and complexity of financial data make manual analysis inefficient and time-consuming.,Assessment of money laundering activities through AI,"GZD/ Financial Intelligence Unit (FIU); Central-Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Germany","The objective of this use case is to enhance risk analysis capabilities and improve the efficiency of processing suspicious activity reports. The solution automates parts of the analytical workflow by applying machine learning techniques to identify patterns, correlations, and anomalies within large datasets of financial transactions and reports.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The German Financial Intelligence Unit (FIU) is responsible for analysing suspicious transaction reports to detect and prevent money laundering and terrorist financing. The increasing volume and complexity of financial data make manual analysis inefficient and time-consuming. The objective of this use case is to enhance risk analysis capabilities and improve the efficiency of processing suspicious activity reports. The solution automates parts of the analytical workflow by applying machine learning techniques to identify patterns, correlations, and anomalies within large datasets of financial transactions and reports. The system continuously incorporates new data, enabling iterative re-evaluation of cases and improving detection accuracy over time. It integrates with existing FIU systems to support analysts in prioritising high-risk cases. As an implemented solution, it contributes to faster processing times, improved risk assessment, and more effective allocation of investigative resources, strengthening the overall capacity to combat financial crime.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-925,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00844,EU_PSTW_202609_00844,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Federal Central Tax Office (BZSt),"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.","With 14,000 daily consultations and 95,000 telephone calls, managing routine enquiries whilst ensuring staff availability for complex cases presents a significant operational challenge.",Chatbot ViOlA for the homepage of the german Federal Central Tax Office,"Federal Central Tax Office (BZSt); Central-Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Germany","The Bundesagentur für Arbeit deployed ViOlA (Virtual Online Assistant), an AI-powered chatbot on its website to handle common enquiries about job searches, unemployment benefits, vocational training, and family allowances. The chatbot uses natural language processing to understand user questions and provide relevant information or direct users to appropriate resources.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Germany's Federal Employment Agency (Bundesagentur für Arbeit) serves millions of citizens seeking employment services, training, and benefits. With 14,000 daily consultations and 95,000 telephone calls, managing routine enquiries whilst ensuring staff availability for complex cases presents a significant operational challenge. The Bundesagentur für Arbeit deployed ViOlA (Virtual Online Assistant), an AI-powered chatbot on its website to handle common enquiries about job searches, unemployment benefits, vocational training, and family allowances. The chatbot uses natural language processing to understand user questions and provide relevant information or direct users to appropriate resources. ViOlA operates alongside other digital services including video consultations (Mein Videotermin) that connect over 1,000 locations. The agency's digital transformation runs on a containerised infrastructure with approximately 600 services and 24,000 containers managed through Rancher Prime and Kubernetes, migrated at the end of 2021. The chatbot reduces pressure on telephone services and local offices by addressing frequently asked questions automatically, allowing the agency's 100,000 employees across 156 employment agencies and 302 job centres to focus on personalised advice and complex benefit determinations. The approach supports the agency's mission to remain close to customers whilst managing increasing service demands.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-929,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00845,EU_PSTW_202609_00845,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Federal Central Tax Office (BZSt),"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.","Tax authorities face growing challenges detecting undeclared economic activity as commerce increasingly moves online. Traditional audit methods cannot efficiently monitor the vast volume of internet commercial activity, creating opportunities for tax evasion through e-commerce platforms and social media.",X-PIDER - an electonic investigative tool in the field of e-commerce used by the German tax authorities,"Federal Central Tax Office (BZSt); Central-Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Germany","The German Federal Central Tax Office (Bundeszentralamt für Steuern) deployed X-PIDER, an AI-powered web crawling and scraping system to automatically identify taxable business activity online. The system can be deployed anywhere on the internet and autonomously navigates web content to build comprehensive profiles of online commercial presence.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Tax authorities face growing challenges detecting undeclared economic activity as commerce increasingly moves online. Traditional audit methods cannot efficiently monitor the vast volume of internet commercial activity, creating opportunities for tax evasion through e-commerce platforms and social media. The German Federal Central Tax Office (Bundeszentralamt für Steuern) deployed X-PIDER, an AI-powered web crawling and scraping system to automatically identify taxable business activity online. The system operates as an internet crawler connected to a knowledge management component, systematically following links across websites, e-commerce platforms, and social media to detect indicators of commercial activity. X-PIDER can identify internet pages suggesting business transactions and collects taxpayer-related data for cross-referencing with tax records. The system can be deployed anywhere on the internet and autonomously navigates web content to build comprehensive profiles of online commercial presence. X-PIDER supports the regional tax authorities by providing centralised intelligence on potential tax non-compliance. The system complements other AI initiatives in German tax administration including the Steuerchatbot virtual assistant (extended nationally and to English and French in 2024), nudging tools that adapt communication based on taxpayer risk profiles, and automated verification systems for processing tax returns. These tools collectively strengthen enforcement whilst reducing manual investigative workload.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-930,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00846,EU_PSTW_202609_00846,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,German Environment Agency (UBA),R&D environmental protection.,"The initiative improves analytical depth, reduces manual effort in data preparation, and enhances the usability of environmental data for decision-making.",Environmental monitoring with AI at the German Environment Agency,German Environment Agency (UBA); Central-Government; R&D environmental protection.; Germany,"Environmental authorities face growing challenges in analysing large volumes of heterogeneous data across climate, air quality, energy and resource use, often stored in fragmented and hard-to-access systems. The German Environment Agency (UBA) established its AI Application Lab to improve the operational use of complex environmental data and support evidence-based environmental policy.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Environmental authorities face growing challenges in analysing large volumes of heterogeneous data across climate, air quality, energy and resource use, often stored in fragmented and hard-to-access systems. The German Environment Agency (UBA) established its AI Application Lab to improve the operational use of complex environmental data and support evidence-based environmental policy. Stakeholders include UBA departments, federal and regional authorities, researchers and policy makers. The objective is to enable cross-disciplinary and cross-scale analysis of environmental data while ensuring that digital solutions themselves are energy- and resource-efficient. The Lab develops and evaluates data-driven applications that integrate diverse datasets such as environmental measurements, administrative records and scientific data. Advanced analytical pipelines and modelling approaches are used to structure, process and visualise complex information, making it accessible across domains. Particular emphasis is placed on sustainable system design, including efficient computing architectures and responsible data practices. The initiative improves analytical depth, reduces manual effort in data preparation, and enhances the usability of environmental data for decision-making. Qualitatively, it strengthens institutional capacity, improves transparency and supports scalable, reproducible analysis across environmental policy areas.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-937,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00847,EU_PSTW_202609_00847,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Federal Office for Economic Affairs and Export Control (BAFA),"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.",UNKNOWN,AI warns german businesses that the documents uploaded to apply for import tax exemptions might be wrong,"Federal Office for Economic Affairs and Export Control (BAFA); Central-Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Germany",UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"German businesses, in the case of an application to limit the Renewable Energy Sources (EEG) surcharge as part of the special equalization regulation, must submit all relevant documents before the material deadline. If this deadline is missed, the electricity-intensive companies face the irremediable rejection of the application and thus the payment of the full EEG surcharge. In order to minimize this risk for companies, BAFA has fundamentally modernized the application process this year: With the help of artificial intelligence, the uploaded application documents are automatically searched for errors. If it is determined with a certain probability that the wrong documents may have been uploaded, the application portal issues a warning message.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-939,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00848,EU_PSTW_202609_00848,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Federal Institute for Geosciences and Natural Resources (BGR),R&D environmental protection.,UNKNOWN,Artificial intelligence helps predict groundwater levels,Federal Institute for Geosciences and Natural Resources (BGR); Central-Government; R&D environmental protection.; Germany,"Public authorities and planners require accurate forecasts to support drinking water supply, agricultural irrigation, infrastructure protection, and land-use planning. The solution uses artificial neural network models trained on historical groundwater measurements, meteorological data, and hydrological variables.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Climate change and increasing water demand are placing growing pressure on groundwater resources, making reliable prediction of groundwater levels critical for sustainable water management. Public authorities and planners require accurate forecasts to support drinking water supply, agricultural irrigation, infrastructure protection, and land-use planning. The Federal Institute for Geosciences and Natural Resources and the Karlsruhe Institute of Technology address this need through a joint initiative focused on nationwide groundwater level prediction. Stakeholders include water authorities, municipalities, farmers, and infrastructure operators. The objective is to improve decision-making by providing timely and reliable groundwater forecasts that support risk assessment and adaptation strategies. The solution uses artificial neural network models trained on historical groundwater measurements, meteorological data, and hydrological variables. These models capture complex, non-linear relationships influencing groundwater behaviour and generate predictive insights at regional and national scales. The results support more accurate identification of drought-prone areas, zones at risk of subsidence, and regions vulnerable to high groundwater levels. The initiative enhances preparedness, supports sustainable resource management, and enables evidence-based adaptation to climate impacts, delivering significant long-term environmental and socio-economic benefits.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-941,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00849,EU_PSTW_202609_00849,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Federal Institute for Geosciences and Natural Resources (BGR),R&D environmental protection.,"Traditionally, analysing pore frequency, size, shape, and distribution from microscopic images has been a time-consuming and manual task, limiting consistency and scalability. The ITERATOR project addresses this challenge by supporting geoscientists in the systematic analysis of rock microstructures.",ITERATOR – Automated Detection of Pores in Rocks,Federal Institute for Geosciences and Natural Resources (BGR); Central-Government; R&D environmental protection.; Germany,The solution applies advanced image processing techniques to scanning electron microscopy images to semi-automatically detect and characterise pores.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Understanding the physical properties of rocks is essential for applications such as geothermal energy, fluid storage, and geological disposal, where porosity plays a decisive role. Traditionally, analysing pore frequency, size, shape, and distribution from microscopic images has been a time-consuming and manual task, limiting consistency and scalability. The ITERATOR project addresses this challenge by supporting geoscientists in the systematic analysis of rock microstructures. Stakeholders include geological research institutions and applied geoscience bodies that require reliable parameters to assess heat flow, fluid flow, and mechanical behaviour of rocks. The objective is to improve the accuracy, reproducibility, and efficiency of porosity analysis while enabling deeper insights into the relationships between mineral phases, stress regimes, and pore orientation. The solution applies advanced image processing techniques to scanning electron microscopy images to semi-automatically detect and characterise pores. Algorithms identify pore boundaries and compute quantitative descriptors, including size distribution and shape metrics, which are then linked to geological and petrophysical models. The results significantly reduce manual effort and increase analytical consistency. The project enables more detailed and data-driven interpretation of rock properties, supporting improved modelling, better parameter estimation, and more robust decision-making in geoscientific applications.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-942,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00850,EU_PSTW_202609_00850,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""social_nonprofit""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Federal Institute for Geosciences and Natural Resources (BGR),R&D environmental protection.,"Hydrogeological assessments often rely on data collected at different spatial scales, making it difficult to derive coherent regional models that support water management and planning. Local measurements provide high accuracy but limited coverage, while regional datasets lack detail and consistency.",MACRO – Cross-Scale Regionalisation of Hydrogeological Information,Federal Institute for Geosciences and Natural Resources (BGR); Central-Government; R&D environmental protection.; Germany,The project develops and tests data-driven modelling approaches using selected hydrogeological use cases.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Hydrogeological assessments often rely on data collected at different spatial scales, making it difficult to derive coherent regional models that support water management and planning. Local measurements provide high accuracy but limited coverage, while regional datasets lack detail and consistency. The MACRO project addresses this gap by developing methods to integrate and regionalise hydrogeological information across scales. Stakeholders include geological surveys, water authorities, and planners who require harmonised, spatially consistent groundwater information for sustainable resource management. The objective is to establish a methodological foundation that allows reliable upscaling of hydrogeological parameters while preserving local characteristics. The project develops and tests data-driven modelling approaches using selected hydrogeological use cases. Diverse datasets, such as geological maps, monitoring data, and environmental variables, are combined to generate regionally consistent representations of subsurface properties. These methods are designed to support cross-scale learning and validation, ensuring robustness across different spatial resolutions. The project delivers improved regional groundwater models, reduced uncertainty in parameter estimation, and enhanced transferability of local knowledge to larger areas. This contributes to more informed decision-making, better water resource planning, and a scalable framework for nationwide hydrogeological assessments.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-945,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00851,EU_PSTW_202609_00851,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""social_nonprofit""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Federal Ministry of Economics and Climate Protection (BMWi),R&D environmental protection.,"Traditional satellite imagery captures only a limited portion of the electromagnetic spectrum, restricting scientists' ability to detect subtle environmental changes in vegetation health, water quality, soil composition, and mineral deposits essential for climate monitoring and resource management.",EnMAP - Environmental Mapping and Analysis Program,Federal Ministry of Economics and Climate Protection (BMWi); Central-Government; R&D environmental protection.; Germany,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Traditional satellite imagery captures only a limited portion of the electromagnetic spectrum, restricting scientists' ability to detect subtle environmental changes in vegetation health, water quality, soil composition, and mineral deposits essential for climate monitoring and resource management. EnMAP (Environmental Mapping and Analysis Program) is Germany's first hyperspectral satellite mission, launched in April 2022 aboard a SpaceX Falcon 9. The satellite carries a dual-spectrometer instrument capturing 230 spectral bands from 420 to 2450 nanometres at 30-metre ground resolution, enabling detection of biochemical and geochemical signatures invisible to conventional sensors. Operating in sun-synchronous orbit at 653 kilometres altitude, EnMAP records up to 5,000 kilometres of imagery daily with 4-day revisit capability. The German Research Centre for Geosciences (GFZ) leads scientific coordination whilst DLR manages operations. In 2024, the SpectralEarth dataset was released, comprising 538,974 image patches for training AI foundation models in hyperspectral analysis. EnMAP provides unprecedented environmental monitoring capability for agriculture, forestry, water management, and geology applications. The mission supports Copernicus services and contributes to climate protection initiatives by enabling precise characterisation of ecosystem changes. Data is freely available to the global scientific community through DLR's ground segment.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-946,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00852,EU_PSTW_202609_00852,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Federal Office of Consumer Protection and Food Safety (BVL),"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.",UNKNOWN,G@ZIELT: Safe shopping on the internet,"Federal Office of Consumer Protection and Food Safety (BVL); Central-Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Germany",UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Consumers in Germany are increasingly purchasing food and cosmetics online, with a rising trend in online purchases of various items including kitchen utensils, toys, clothing, and tobacco products. The Federal Office of Consumer Protection and Food Safety (BVL) hosts a central unit called G@ZIELT, jointly managed by federal states, to regulate internet trade in products covered by the Food and Feed Code and tobacco. The G@ZIELT unit searches the internet to identify risky food offers, misleading labels, and unregistered businesses, reporting findings to appropriate authorities.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-953,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00853,EU_PSTW_202609_00853,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Thünen-Institut,"Agriculture, forestry, fishing and hunting","Manual identification of wood species is labour-intensive, requires specialist expertise, and is difficult to apply at scale for inspections and compliance checks.",Automated image recognition for determining the type of wood,"Thünen-Institut; Central-Government; Agriculture, forestry, fishing and hunting; Germany",This use case addresses the need for efficient and reliable verification methods that support regulatory enforcement.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Ensuring the legal origin of timber products is a key requirement under the European Timber Trade Regulation, which obliges market participants to accurately document the wood species used in traded goods. Manual identification of wood species is labour-intensive, requires specialist expertise, and is difficult to apply at scale for inspections and compliance checks. This use case addresses the need for efficient and reliable verification methods that support regulatory enforcement. Stakeholders include regulatory authorities, inspection bodies, and timber traders responsible for due diligence. The objective is to enable large-scale verification of declared wood species and reduce the risk of illegal timber entering the market. The solution involves the development of automated image recognition systems that analyse macroscopic and microscopic wood images. By learning distinguishing visual patterns across species, the system supports consistent classification and comparison against declared information. Integrated into inspection workflows, it enables rapid screening of products and supports expert decision-making. The results include faster and more scalable compliance checks, reduced dependence on scarce specialist resources, and improved detection of mislabelled timber. The initiative strengthens enforcement of timber regulations, supports sustainable forestry, and enhances transparency in the wood supply chain.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-959,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00854,EU_PSTW_202609_00854,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Thünen-Institut,R&D environmental protection.,Traditional field surveys are accurate but costly and limited in spatial coverage.,Monitoring biological diversity in agricultural landscapes with remote sensing,Thünen-Institut; Central-Government; R&D environmental protection.; Germany,"Policymakers and environmental agencies require consistent, large-scale monitoring tools to assess habitat quality, land-use intensity, and landscape structure over time. The solution uses Earth observation data from the Sentinel-1 and Sentinel-2 satellites within the Copernicus programme to model habitat features and land-use intensity at large scales.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Maintaining and enhancing biodiversity in agricultural landscapes is a central objective of agri-environment-climate measures under the EU Common Agricultural Policy. Policymakers and environmental agencies require consistent, large-scale monitoring tools to assess habitat quality, land-use intensity, and landscape structure over time. Traditional field surveys are accurate but costly and limited in spatial coverage. This project addresses the need for a nationwide, spatially explicit monitoring approach that captures biodiversity-relevant habitat features across agricultural landscapes. Stakeholders include agricultural authorities, environmental agencies, and policymakers responsible for evaluating and designing biodiversity measures. The objective is to establish a robust indicator framework that reflects landscape and field-level characteristics relevant to different taxonomic groups. The solution uses Earth observation data from the Sentinel-1 and Sentinel-2 satellites within the Copernicus programme to model habitat features and land-use intensity at large scales. Spatial indicators are derived to describe landscape structure, elements, and management intensity in cropland and grassland areas. The results provide consistent, repeatable monitoring of biodiversity-related patterns at national level. The project supports evidence-based policy evaluation, improved targeting of agri-environment measures, and long-term tracking of biodiversity trends.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-962,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00855,EU_PSTW_202609_00855,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Federal Language Office,General services.,"Public administrations operating in international contexts require fast and reliable translation capabilities, yet traditional human-only workflows struggle to keep pace with growing volumes of multilingual content.",Language skills in the digital age,Federal Language Office; Central-Government; General services.; Germany,The objective is to improve efficiency while maintaining acceptable quality standards for official use. The pilot applies automated translation systems trained on extensive existing linguistic corpora held by the institution.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Public administrations operating in international contexts require fast and reliable translation capabilities, yet traditional human-only workflows struggle to keep pace with growing volumes of multilingual content. The Federal Language Office initiated this pilot to modernise language services and support staff working with foreign-language materials. Stakeholders include translators, analysts, defence and diplomatic personnel, and administrative units relying on timely translations. The objective is to improve efficiency while maintaining acceptable quality standards for official use. The pilot applies automated translation systems trained on extensive existing linguistic corpora held by the institution. These systems learn from previously translated documents and continuously improve translation accuracy across relevant language pairs. The solution is integrated into existing translation workflows, allowing human experts to review, correct and refine outputs rather than translating from scratch. The impact includes faster turnaround times, reduced workload for routine translations and better prioritisation of expert human resources. Qualitatively, the project improves operational responsiveness and supports knowledge sharing in multilingual environments, while laying the groundwork for scalable language services across government.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F06_HIGH_VOLUME_SCALABILITY_BOTTLENECK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-968,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00856,EU_PSTW_202609_00856,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,German Weather Service (DWD),R&D environmental protection.,UNKNOWN,AI in numerical weather and climate forecasting,German Weather Service (DWD); Central-Government; R&D environmental protection.; Germany,The system uses deep neural networks trained on historical meteorological datasets to extract relevant atmospheric patterns from satellite images and translate them into parameters usable by forecasting models. The approach has been implemented within operational research environments at DWD.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Numerical weather prediction relies on complex physical models and vast quantities of observational data, including satellite imagery. Processing and integrating this data efficiently remains computationally demanding and may limit forecasting precision, particularly in extreme weather scenarios. The German Weather Service (DWD), in collaboration with Ludwig Maximilian University of Munich, developed an AI-supported neural network approach to enhance the processing of satellite imagery within forecasting workflows. The objective is to improve predictive accuracy and optimise the integration of observational data into numerical models. The system uses deep neural networks trained on historical meteorological datasets to extract relevant atmospheric patterns from satellite images and translate them into parameters usable by forecasting models. The AI component operates alongside traditional physics-based simulations, providing complementary inputs that refine model outputs. The approach has been implemented within operational research environments at DWD. It contributes to more accurate short-term forecasts, improved detection of extreme weather events and enhanced computational efficiency, supporting better-informed public warnings and climate resilience planning.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-974,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00857,EU_PSTW_202609_00857,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,German Federal Office for Migration and Refugees (BAMF),Police Services,"Manual processes increase the risk of data inconsistencies, duplicate records, and security vulnerabilities.","AI-based Integrated identity management for asylum procedures, improving plausability, data quality and security aspects",German Federal Office for Migration and Refugees (BAMF); Central-Government; Police Services; Germany,"This use case supports the Federal Office responsible for asylum procedures by improving the quality, plausibility, and security of identity data.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Asylum procedures require accurate registration and verification of personal identity under challenging conditions, often involving missing documents, language barriers, and high case volumes. Manual processes increase the risk of data inconsistencies, duplicate records, and security vulnerabilities. This use case supports the Federal Office responsible for asylum procedures by improving the quality, plausibility, and security of identity data. Stakeholders include caseworkers, asylum authorities, and applicants whose cases depend on accurate and fair identity assessment. The objective is to streamline registration processes while strengthening data integrity and preventing fraud. The solution integrates multiple automated support tools into a single identity management system. These include automated transcription of names from non-Latin scripts, dialect analysis to support origin verification, biometric image checks to prevent duplicate files, and extraction of relevant information from mobile devices when documents are unavailable. The system supports caseworkers with structured, evidence-based inputs while maintaining oversight and control. The results include improved data quality, reduced duplication, enhanced security, and more efficient case handling. This leads to faster, fairer asylum procedures and increased trust in administrative processes.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,S3_DOCUMENT_INTELLIGENCE,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-981,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00858,EU_PSTW_202609_00858,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Federal Agency for Civic Education (BPB),General services.,"Traditional information channels can be difficult to navigate or overwhelming, especially for first-time voters.",Chatbot for the 2021 Federal Elections,Federal Agency for Civic Education (BPB); Central-Government; General services.; Germany,"The objective of this use case was to provide accessible, structured, and neutral information about the election process and related political topics.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Elections require well-informed citizens who understand voting procedures, the electoral system, and key democratic principles. During the 2021 Federal Elections, the Federal Agency for Civic Education sought to improve access to reliable information and support voter engagement, particularly among digitally active audiences. Traditional information channels can be difficult to navigate or overwhelming, especially for first-time voters. The objective of this use case was to provide accessible, structured, and neutral information about the election process and related political topics. The solution involved deploying an interactive chatbot that delivered curated content on voting procedures, electoral system structure, and practical considerations before election day. Information was organised into thematic dialogues, complemented by quizzes that allowed users to test their knowledge. The chatbot was accessible through digital channels commonly used by citizens, enabling on-demand interaction. The initiative improved accessibility to civic information, encouraged active learning, and supported informed participation in the electoral process. Qualitative results include increased user engagement, clearer understanding of democratic procedures, and a scalable communication tool for future civic education initiatives.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-983,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00859,EU_PSTW_202609_00859,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""social_nonprofit""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Federal Statistical Office (StBA),General services.,"Traditional register maintenance relies heavily on administrative updates and manual checks, which can lead to inconsistencies, delays, and incomplete coverage. The Sat4GWR_IF-Bund innovation project addresses these challenges by supporting the German register census with independent, area-wide verification of building data.",Sat4GWR_IF-Bund – Remote sensing & AI for the register census,Federal Statistical Office (StBA); Central-Government; General services.; Germany,The solution applies neural network–based image analysis to satellite imagery and aerial photographs to automatically detect buildings and derive characteristics such as building size and usage.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Maintaining an accurate and up-to-date register of buildings and dwellings is essential for population statistics, spatial planning, and evidence-based policymaking. Traditional register maintenance relies heavily on administrative updates and manual checks, which can lead to inconsistencies, delays, and incomplete coverage. The Sat4GWR_IF-Bund innovation project addresses these challenges by supporting the German register census with independent, area-wide verification of building data. Stakeholders include the Federal Statistical Office, public administrations, and policymakers who depend on reliable building and housing information. The objective is to enhance data quality assurance, complement register-based census methods, and support the long-term maintenance of building registers. The solution applies neural network–based image analysis to satellite imagery and aerial photographs to automatically detect buildings and derive characteristics such as building size and usage. These outputs are compared with existing register data to identify discrepancies and support validation workflows. Remote sensing data is used as an auxiliary, non-intrusive source that scales nationwide. The project improves completeness, consistency, and reliability of census data, reduces manual verification effort, and establishes a scalable approach to register maintenance, strengthening the statistical foundation for public decision-making.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-987,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00860,EU_PSTW_202609_00860,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Paul Ehrlich Institut,R&D health.,UNKNOWN,Machine learning increases biotechnological protein production,Paul Ehrlich Institut; Central-Government; R&D health.; Germany,"The results include improved yield forecasts, reduced trial-and-error experimentation and broader applicability across different biotechnological use cases.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Biotechnological production of proteins, such as those used in vaccines and therapeutics, is complex and sensitive to numerous biological and process variables, making yield optimisation challenging. Researchers at the Paul Ehrlich Institute initiated this project to improve predictability and efficiency in protein synthesis within host organisms. Stakeholders include biomedical researchers, biomanufacturers and public health institutions. The objective is to enhance production yields and process reliability to support biomedical innovation. The project develops mathematical and computational models that integrate experimental data from biotechnological production processes. These models capture relationships between process parameters, biological responses and output yields, enabling more precise predictions and optimisation strategies. By embedding these models into research and development workflows, researchers can test scenarios digitally before applying changes in laboratory or production settings. The results include improved yield forecasts, reduced trial-and-error experimentation and broader applicability across different biotechnological use cases. Qualitatively, the project accelerates research cycles, reduces costs and supports scalable protein production critical for vaccines and advanced therapies.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-990,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00861,EU_PSTW_202609_00861,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""social_nonprofit""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Federal Criminal Police Office (BKA),Police Services,"Online hate crime poses growing societal risks, yet early detection and prevention raise complex legal, ethical and operational challenges for security authorities.",KISTRA (use of artificial intelligence for the early detection of criminal offenses) - research project,Federal Criminal Police Office (BKA); Central-Government; Police Services; Germany,"The objective is not operational deployment, but the development of scientifically grounded insights, safeguards and prototypes for responsible use. Analytical methods are applied to publicly available online content to explore patterns associated with hate crime, while governance frameworks define acceptable use conditions.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Online hate crime poses growing societal risks, yet early detection and prevention raise complex legal, ethical and operational challenges for security authorities. The KISTRA research project was initiated to explore how digital tools could support earlier identification of hate-related offences while respecting fundamental rights. Stakeholders include law enforcement agencies, legal experts, social scientists, technology researchers and civil society. The objective is not operational deployment, but the development of scientifically grounded insights, safeguards and prototypes for responsible use. The project combines socio-scientific, legal and ethical analysis with the development of technical demonstrators. Analytical methods are applied to publicly available online content to explore patterns associated with hate crime, while governance frameworks define acceptable use conditions. The results include software demonstrators, ethical guidelines and legal assessments that inform future policy choices. The impact lies in improved understanding of risks and opportunities, clearer boundary conditions for technology use, and a foundation for transparent, accountable approaches to digital crime prevention.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-992,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00862,EU_PSTW_202609_00862,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Federal Police,Public order and safety n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,Automated border control via Easypass Registered Traveler Program - biometric face recognition system,Federal Police; Central-Government; Public order and safety n.e.c.; Germany,"The Federal Police implemented EasyPASS, an automated border control system using biometric facial recognition developed by Bundesdruckerei, secunet, and Cognitec to verify travellers' identities against their e-passport chip photos. The system is deployed at Frankfurt, Munich, Hamburg, Hannover, Düsseldorf, Stuttgart, Berlin Brandenburg, and Cologne/Bonn airports, available to EU/EEA citizens aged 12+ and registered third-country nationals from the USA, Hong Kong, South Korea, Taiwan, and the UK.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"International airport border control in Germany faced growing passenger volumes requiring faster processing whilst maintaining security standards and compliance with Schengen external border regulations. The Federal Police implemented EasyPASS, an automated border control system using biometric facial recognition developed by Bundesdruckerei, secunet, and Cognitec to verify travellers' identities against their e-passport chip photos. The system is deployed at Frankfurt, Munich, Hamburg, Hannover, Düsseldorf, Stuttgart, Berlin Brandenburg, and Cologne/Bonn airports, available to EU/EEA citizens aged 12+ and registered third-country nationals from the USA, Hong Kong, South Korea, Taiwan, and the UK. Travellers place their e-passport on the document reader, then proceed through gates where cameras capture facial images for 1:1 comparison against stored biometric data, with information also checked against Schengen databases. Data is stored for 48 hours (extendable to 30 days for police investigations), and the system strictly limits facial recognition to identity verification purposes under the Passport Act.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-994,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00863,EU_PSTW_202609_00863,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Office of Integration of the Ministry for Social Affairs, Health, Family, Youth, and Senior Citizens of Schleswig-Holstein",Social protection n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,InA– The Chatbot at the Office of Integration,"Office of Integration of the Ministry for Social Affairs, Health, Family, Youth, and Senior Citizens of Schleswig-Holstein; Regional Government; Social protection n.e.c.; Germany","Using the chat bot, people can call up general information, arrange for callbacks, and determine time windows for contact purposes. It is also possible to use the chat bot to submit an initial application for accompanying assistance.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Office of Integration of the Ministry for Social Affairs, Health, Family, Youth, and Senior Citizens integrated a chat bot into its website in February 2020. This chat bot is being further developed in a follow-on project. Using the chat bot, people can call up general information, arrange for callbacks, and determine time windows for contact purposes. It is also possible to use the chat bot to submit an initial application for accompanying assistance.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1003,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00864,EU_PSTW_202609_00864,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Municipalities of Bielefeld, Gütersloh, Lemgo, Neuss, Rheine and Soest",Transport,"Optimising traffic signal systems is a critical lever for improving traffic flow in dense city environments, yet conventional control strategies struggle to adapt to dynamic traffic conditions. The KI4LSA project addresses this challenge by exploring adaptive control of traffic lights in inner-city areas.",KI4LSA – Artificial Intelligence for Adaptive Traffic Signal Control,"Municipalities of Bielefeld, Gütersloh, Lemgo, Neuss, Rheine and Soest; Local Government; Transport; Germany","The solution uses reinforcement learning, a data-driven control approach trained in detailed traffic simulations.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Increasing urban traffic congestion leads to longer travel times, higher fuel consumption, and increased noise and air pollution. Optimising traffic signal systems is a critical lever for improving traffic flow in dense city environments, yet conventional control strategies struggle to adapt to dynamic traffic conditions. The KI4LSA project addresses this challenge by exploring adaptive control of traffic lights in inner-city areas. Stakeholders include transport authorities, municipalities, and road users seeking smoother traffic flow and reduced environmental impact. The objective is to optimise traffic signal behaviour in real time to minimise congestion and unnecessary stopping. The solution uses reinforcement learning, a data-driven control approach trained in detailed traffic simulations. Traffic signal controllers learn optimal strategies by observing traffic states and evaluating outcomes such as queue length and waiting time. After training, the learned control policies are evaluated against conventional approaches. The project demonstrates improved traffic flow, reduced stop-and-go behaviour, and lower emissions resulting from fewer accelerations and idling periods. The results highlight the potential of adaptive signal control to improve urban mobility efficiency and environmental performance, while providing a transferable framework for future smart traffic management systems.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1015,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00865,EU_PSTW_202609_00865,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Heilbronn,General services.,"Municipal archives often hold vast photographic collections that are difficult to index and search due to limited metadata and manual cataloguing processes. The impact includes significantly reduced manual indexing effort, richer search functionality and improved public access to cultural heritage materials.",Pilot project to support the future development of photos with AI,City of Heilbronn; Local Government; General services.; Germany,"The pilot trains image analysis systems to recognise approximately 1,500 individuals and around 200 distinctive buildings using existing labelled photographs.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Municipal archives often hold vast photographic collections that are difficult to index and search due to limited metadata and manual cataloguing processes. The Heilbronn City Archive launched this pilot to improve access to its digital photo collections and enrich historical documentation. Stakeholders include archivists, researchers, local historians and the general public. The objective is to increase indexing depth and accelerate the availability of detailed information about archived images. The pilot trains image analysis systems to recognise approximately 1,500 individuals and around 200 distinctive buildings using existing labelled photographs. The resulting metadata is integrated into the HEUSS online archive database, enabling faster and more detailed tagging of images. The impact includes significantly reduced manual indexing effort, richer search functionality and improved public access to cultural heritage materials. Qualitatively, the project enhances the long-term value of archival assets and demonstrates scalable approaches to digital heritage management.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1016,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00866,EU_PSTW_202609_00866,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Berlin District of Tempelhof-Schöneberg,Education n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,Intelligent layout of areas for optimal school districts design,Berlin District of Tempelhof-Schöneberg; Local Government; Education n.e.c.; Germany,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"School districts should be designed in such a way that schools have as even a capacity as possible and the distances to school for children are short. At the same time, it is important to achieve a social mix among the students. In order to achieve these goals, school office employees in the Berlin district of Tempelhof-Schoeneberg have invested a lot of time. Streets had to be walked through one by one, information was recorded in Excel spreadsheets. This work is now done by an algorithm that automatically outputs a first draft of the cut based on the available data from the State Statistical Office.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1018,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00867,EU_PSTW_202609_00867,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Calella Municipality,R&D public order and safety.,UNKNOWN,AI Beach Capacity Monitoring,Calella Municipality; Local Government; R&D public order and safety.; Spain,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The City Council has contracted the services of the company DatActions to implement the DasEyes application, whose function is to sectorize the beaches in multiple virtual areas in order to detect the number of people in each sector. On weekends, when there is more confluence and traffic on the beach, municipal agents are provided with tools to monitor capacity; these agents patrol to make sure there are no problems and ensure order in the areas. In addition, to make it easier for them, Artificial Intelligence methods make it possible to predict when the beaches will be filled. Depending on the data, the agents open and close the different sections to the public in such a way that there will always be a safe capacity in all areas.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1022,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00868,EU_PSTW_202609_00868,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Barcelona City Council,Social protection n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,AI to support decision-making processes in social services,Barcelona City Council; Local Government; Social protection n.e.c.; Spain,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The municipal social services deal with an average of 50,000 initial visits every year ranging through a variety of issues (from financial or addiction problems to gender-based violence) and are handled by a team of over 700 professionals. An algorithm provides the staff with digital support in the decision-making process. Having been trained with a corpus of 300,000 interviews, it suggests the appropriate resources for each case. It's a recommendation algorithm, hence, the algorithm is an aid for the decision-making process, the decision is ultimately made by the professional handling the case. The algorithm was audited by an external third party.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1023,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00869,EU_PSTW_202609_00869,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Barcelona City Council,General public services n.e.c.,"The purpose of this initiative is to free up the time that workers currently spend on manual, repetitive tasks that are of little added value, so they can devote their time to providing real value to the organisation and reduce the time it takes to respond to requests.",Robotic Automation Plan,Barcelona City Council; Local Government; General public services n.e.c.; Spain,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Robot Automation Plan has created a new service for robotic process automation. The aim behind the project is to provide the City Council with innovative solutions, such as increasing the quality of citizen help and information services and reducing human error in processes that are repetitive and with little added value, increasing the Council’s production capacity, for example in processing licences for café terraces, and increasing the guarantee and transparency of processes and ensuring coherency in the data processed. The purpose of this initiative is to free up the time that workers currently spend on manual, repetitive tasks that are of little added value, so they can devote their time to providing real value to the organisation and reduce the time it takes to respond to requests.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,S6_WORKFLOW_PROCESS_AUTOMATION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1024,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00870,EU_PSTW_202609_00870,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""social_nonprofit""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Florence Metropolitan City,Police Services,UNKNOWN,ELASTIC – Autonomous Transport Network for Urban Tramways,Florence Metropolitan City; Local Government; Police Services; Italy,"The pilot, deployed on Florence’s tramway network, involved transport operators, city authorities, and technology providers. Sensor data is processed in real time using perception and data fusion techniques to extract information on tram positioning, obstacle detection, and movement of vehicles and pedestrians.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Urban public transport systems require precise situational awareness to ensure safety, reliability, and efficient operation, particularly as cities move towards higher levels of automation. The ELASTIC project addresses this need by developing a software architecture for advanced mobility systems, with a focus on autonomous and assisted transport networks. The pilot, deployed on Florence’s tramway network, involved transport operators, city authorities, and technology providers. The objective was to enhance real-time monitoring of tram operations, infrastructure, and surrounding environments without compromising passenger privacy. The solution integrates multiple onboard and roadside sensors, including cameras, radar, lidar, and inertial measurement units, installed on tram vehicles and at stops. Sensor data is processed in real time using perception and data fusion techniques to extract information on tram positioning, obstacle detection, and movement of vehicles and pedestrians. Visual data is analysed on the fly and immediately discarded, ensuring that no personal data is retained. The pilot demonstrates improved operational awareness, enhanced safety, and better incident prevention. It provides a scalable and privacy-preserving foundation for future autonomous public transport systems in urban environments.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1026,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00871,EU_PSTW_202609_00871,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Nantes Métropole & Ville,Social protection n.e.c.,"Access to affordable water is a fundamental right, yet low-income households often struggle with utility costs.",Nantes Social Water Pricing,Nantes Métropole & Ville; Local Government; Social protection n.e.c.; France,The algorithm calculates eligibility based on household income and family composition using data from the Caisse d'Allocations Familiales (CAF).,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Access to affordable water is a fundamental right, yet low-income households often struggle with utility costs. Traditional means-tested assistance programmes require complex application processes and frequently miss eligible beneficiaries who are unaware of available support. Nantes Métropole developed an open-source algorithm to determine eligibility for its social water tariff (Tarification Solidaire de l'Eau), guaranteeing that no household spends more than 3% of income on water for reasonable consumption of 30 cubic metres per person annually. The algorithm calculates eligibility based on household income and family composition using data from the Caisse d'Allocations Familiales (CAF). For CAF beneficiaries with family quotient below the threshold, assistance is granted automatically. Other residents can apply at their town hall, where agents enter information into Deltas software developed in-house by Nantes Métropole. The algorithm processes data in real time, providing immediate eligibility determination. The source code is published under the CeCILL-B open-source licence. In 2024, 5,866 households benefited from the social water tariff, receiving €407,153 in total assistance. Since January 2025, reformed Agence de l'Eau charges have been integrated into the pricing structure. The open-source approach supports transparency in public decision-making and enables other municipalities to adapt the algorithm for their own social pricing programmes.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1027,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00872,EU_PSTW_202609_00872,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Nantes Métropole & Ville,Social protection n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,Nantes Solidarity Transport Pricing,Nantes Métropole & Ville; Local Government; Social protection n.e.c.; France,Nantes Métropole implemented an algorithmic system for solidarity transport pricing (Tarification Solidaire Transports) on its Naolib public transport network.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Public transport affordability is crucial for social inclusion, yet fixed-price ticketing can exclude low-income residents from mobility services essential for employment, healthcare access, and social participation. Nantes Métropole implemented an algorithmic system for solidarity transport pricing (Tarification Solidaire Transports) on its Naolib public transport network. The algorithm determines eligibility for free travel or discounts up to 50% on unlimited annual passes based on household income and family composition. All households with a family quotient of 900 or below qualify for solidarity pricing. Users apply at their local town hall, where agents collect supporting documents and enter information into Deltas, software developed by Nantes Métropole. The algorithm processes data in real time, immediately notifying applicants of their entitlement level. Benefits apply to all household members regardless of age. Since May 2023, the eligibility threshold was raised from 600 to 900 family quotient, significantly expanding coverage. The solidarity pricing programme extends beyond metropolitan residents to include commuters from surrounding areas who travel within Nantes Métropole. This ensures transport affordability for the most vulnerable populations whilst supporting labour market access and social cohesion across the metropolitan region.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1028,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00873,EU_PSTW_202609_00873,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Greater Paris,Pollution abatement.,"In Paris, fragmented data sources and limited temporal resolution make it difficult to link emissions to concrete activities and evaluate the impact of climate policies.",Carbon Intelligent City - Paris,Greater Paris; Local Government; Pollution abatement.; France,"The solution combines diverse datasets within a unified platform, using advanced analytics to extract activity signals from defined areas of interest and infer associated CO₂ emissions.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Cities face increasing pressure to accurately measure, understand, and reduce greenhouse gas emissions across all sectors, from buildings and transport to industry and energy production. In Paris, fragmented data sources and limited temporal resolution make it difficult to link emissions to concrete activities and evaluate the impact of climate policies. The Carbon Intelligent City pilot addresses this gap by providing a comprehensive, multi-scale emissions monitoring system. Stakeholders include city managers, policymakers, businesses, NGOs, and citizens seeking transparent and actionable climate information. The objective is to support evidence-based climate action through detailed, science-based emissions tracking at city, district, and street level. The solution combines diverse datasets within a unified platform, using advanced analytics to extract activity signals from defined areas of interest and infer associated CO₂ emissions. Satellite imagery and other data sources are analysed to detect operational activity, such as industrial site usage, and translate these signals into emissions estimates. The pilot improves transparency, enables continuous monitoring, and supports scenario analysis and impact assessment. It strengthens the city’s capacity to manage emissions strategically and engage stakeholders in collective climate action.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F07_DATA_VISIBILITY_INTEGRATION_GAP,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1030,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00874,EU_PSTW_202609_00874,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Copenhagen,Pollution abatement.,UNKNOWN,Carbon Intelligent City - Copenhagen,City of Copenhagen; Local Government; Pollution abatement.; Denmark,This pilot within the AI4Cities project applies the Carbon Intelligent City approach to the Danish context.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Achieving climate neutrality requires cities to move beyond aggregated emissions inventories towards dynamic, activity-based monitoring of greenhouse gas emissions. In Copenhagen, the need for precise, spatially resolved emissions data is critical to guide urban planning and track progress towards ambitious climate targets. This pilot within the AI4Cities project applies the Carbon Intelligent City approach to the Danish context. Stakeholders include municipal authorities, urban planners, businesses, and civil society organisations. The objective is to provide a reliable, transparent emissions information system that supports informed decision-making at multiple urban scales. The solution integrates heterogeneous datasets into a single analytical platform, where automated methods extract activity patterns from areas of interest and infer corresponding emissions. By linking observed activity to emissions factors, the system generates timely and comparable emissions estimates across sectors such as transport, buildings, and industry. The pilot improves the granularity and responsiveness of emissions monitoring, supports evaluation of policy interventions, and enhances public transparency. It demonstrates how data-driven emissions intelligence can strengthen climate governance and accelerate urban decarbonisation strategies.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1031,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00875,EU_PSTW_202609_00875,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Greater Paris,Street lighting.,UNKNOWN,IX3 - Mobility Pilot Project in Paris,Greater Paris; Local Government; Street lighting.; France,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Urban traffic inefficiencies contribute significantly to greenhouse gas emissions, particularly through frequent stopping and re-acceleration at traffic lights. The IX3 mobility pilot in Paris addresses this issue by optimising traffic signal management to smooth vehicle flows. Stakeholders include city transport authorities, road users, and residents affected by congestion and air pollution. The objective is to reduce unnecessary stops, improve driving continuity, and lower traffic-related emissions at city scale. The solution combines real-time machine vision sensors with traffic light controllers to observe traffic conditions and dynamically adjust signal logic. Visual data is processed locally to extract anonymous traffic metrics, such as vehicle counts and flow patterns, without storing personal identifiers or retaining footage beyond quality assurance. The system continuously adapts signal timing to actual traffic demand. Pilots and earlier deployments show that city-wide adoption can reduce traffic-related emissions by around 2%, alongside improved travel times and driving comfort. The project demonstrates a privacy-preserving, scalable approach to intelligent traffic management with measurable environmental and mobility benefits.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1032,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00876,EU_PSTW_202609_00876,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Helsinki,Street lighting.,UNKNOWN,IX3 - Mobility Pilot Project in Helsinki,City of Helsinki; Local Government; Street lighting.; Finland,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Urban traffic congestion in Helsinki contributes to unnecessary fuel consumption, emissions, and reduced travel comfort, particularly due to frequent stopping and waiting at red lights. Traditional traffic signal control systems are often rule-based and insufficiently responsive to real-time traffic conditions. This pilot, part of the AI4Cities project, addresses the need for more adaptive and efficient traffic management that supports the city’s environmental and mobility goals. Stakeholders include the City of Helsinki, transport authorities, technology providers, and road users. The objective is to reduce stop-and-go traffic, improve flow continuity, and lower traffic-related emissions without requiring major infrastructure changes. The solution integrates real-time machine vision sensors with existing traffic light controllers, enabling continuous observation of traffic conditions such as vehicle presence and flow intensity. These data feed optimisation logic that dynamically adjusts signal timing to actual demand. The system is developed in collaboration with MarshallAI and Dynniq, combining expertise in computer vision and traffic management. Only anonymous traffic data is processed, and video footage is not retained beyond quality assurance. The pilot builds on earlier deployments showing that city-wide adoption can reduce traffic-related emissions by around 2%, while also improving travel times, driving comfort, and overall traffic efficiency in a scalable and privacy-preserving manner.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1033,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00877,EU_PSTW_202609_00877,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""social_nonprofit""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Berlin,Police Services,UNKNOWN,BerDiBa Project: Berlin Digital Railway Operation,City of Berlin; Local Government; Police Services; Germany,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"BerDiBa Project addresses the need to modernise urban rail transport in Berlin by increasing automation, safety and operational reliability under real traffic conditions. As part of the Siemensstadt innovation initiative, a consortium of twelve partners is collaborating over four years to research and validate technologies that enable automated train driving while ensuring passenger safety and infrastructure integrity. The project seeks to enhance rail efficiency, reduce human error and support future digital railway operations within Berlin’s public transport system. The solution is based on high-performance sensor systems installed on and within trains to provide continuous environmental perception. Machine learning models process visual and sensor data to detect track obstacles, identify hazards affecting passengers and monitor changes in rail infrastructure. The system integrates perception modules with train control and operational management platforms, enabling automated decision support and adaptive responses during live operations. Funded under Berlin’s ProFIT programme, the initiative supports application-oriented research and large-scale testing. Expected impacts include improved safety, reduced operational disruptions, enhanced monitoring accuracy and the foundation for scalable automated rail travel in metropolitan environments.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1034,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00878,EU_PSTW_202609_00878,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Amsterdam and other dutch municipalities,General services.,Manual triage is time-consuming and can delay resolution.,Signalen - AI Categorisation of Public Space Reports,City of Amsterdam and other dutch municipalities; Local Government; General services.; Netherlands,"It uses language-based classification models trained on historical report data, relying primarily on keyword recognition and probabilistic categorisation.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Municipalities receive large volumes of reports from residents concerning issues in public space, such as waste, infrastructure damage or nuisance, which must be routed quickly to the correct department. Manual triage is time-consuming and can delay resolution. Signalen was developed as a shared, open-source system by and for Dutch municipalities to streamline the intake and handling of such reports. Stakeholders include local authorities, civil servants responsible for service delivery and residents submitting reports. The objective is to improve response times, reduce administrative workload and ensure consistent handling across municipalities. The system automatically categorises incoming reports based on their content and routes them to the appropriate municipal unit. It uses language-based classification models trained on historical report data, relying primarily on keyword recognition and probabilistic categorisation. The platform is API-driven and integrates with existing municipal case management systems. Personal data is optional and excluded from automated processing, ensuring privacy by design. Signalen is currently used by municipalities serving over two million residents and processes more than 600,000 reports annually. The system has improved operational efficiency, reduced misrouting, and increased transparency for citizens, while its open-source nature supports scalability, reuse and long-term sustainability across the Netherlands.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1035,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00879,EU_PSTW_202609_00879,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Copenhagen,Fuel and energy,UNKNOWN,Energy Optimisation of Buildings,City of Copenhagen; Local Government; Fuel and energy; Denmark,"Municipal buildings represent a major share of urban energy consumption, yet optimising energy use while maintaining indoor comfort is operationally complex. Predictive optimisation tools generate operational recommendations for heating, ventilation and electricity use, accounting for fluctuating demand and supply constraints.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Municipal buildings represent a major share of urban energy consumption, yet optimising energy use while maintaining indoor comfort is operationally complex. Copenhagen Solutions Lab and Copenhagen City Properties initiated this pilot to reduce energy consumption, emissions and costs across municipal buildings. Stakeholders include facility managers, city authorities, energy providers and building occupants. The objective is to support energy-efficient operations without compromising indoor climate quality. The project integrates building management system data, energy consumption records, indoor climate measurements and external data such as weather and grid conditions. Predictive optimisation tools generate operational recommendations for heating, ventilation and electricity use, accounting for fluctuating demand and supply constraints. The impact includes reduced heating and electricity bills, lower CO₂ emissions and improved operational insight for facility managers. Qualitatively, the pilot enhances comfort, supports climate targets and provides a replicable model for data-driven energy management at city scale.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1036,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00880,EU_PSTW_202609_00880,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""social_nonprofit"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Copenhagen,Fuel and energy,"Cities seeking to accelerate the energy transition face challenges in efficiently exchanging locally produced renewable energy and rewarding prosumers for their contributions. In many urban contexts, surplus solar energy is underutilised due to fragmented systems and limited incentives.",HOLONI – Blockchain-Based Local Green Energy Exchange,City of Copenhagen; Local Government; Fuel and energy; Denmark,The objective is to automate incentive mechanisms and improve redistribution of locally generated energy surpluses. The solution uses distributed ledger technologies to record energy exchanges and automatically execute result-based reward schemes.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Cities seeking to accelerate the energy transition face challenges in efficiently exchanging locally produced renewable energy and rewarding prosumers for their contributions. In many urban contexts, surplus solar energy is underutilised due to fragmented systems and limited incentives. The HOLONI project, part of AI4Cities, addresses this challenge by enabling reliable and transparent exchange of local green energy within urban communities. Stakeholders include municipalities, solar prosumers, energy retailers, and local businesses aiming to increase renewable self-consumption and community-based energy sharing. The objective is to automate incentive mechanisms and improve redistribution of locally generated energy surpluses. The solution uses distributed ledger technologies to record energy exchanges and automatically execute result-based reward schemes. Transactions related to energy production, consumption, and incentives are immutably logged, ensuring transparency, traceability, and trust among participants. Smart contracts enable automated settlement and reward distribution based on predefined sustainability criteria. The pilot demonstrates improved efficiency in local energy exchange, increased engagement of prosumers, and fair redistribution of benefits within the community. It provides a scalable and trustworthy foundation for decentralised energy markets, supporting urban decarbonisation and strengthening local energy ecosystems.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1037,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00881,EU_PSTW_202609_00881,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Copenhagen,Fuel and energy,"Unlocking the full potential of urban solar energy requires accurate assessment of production capacity and surplus at city scale, yet municipalities often lack tools to analyse this potential dynamically.",HOLONI – AI for Urban Solar Potential Assessment,City of Copenhagen; Local Government; Fuel and energy; Denmark,"The objective is to predict solar generation and surplus across the urban fabric and use these insights to support local energy exchange and new community-based business models. The solution applies predictive analytics to diverse urban datasets, including building characteristics, solar exposure, and consumption patterns, to estimate current and future solar surplus potential.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Unlocking the full potential of urban solar energy requires accurate assessment of production capacity and surplus at city scale, yet municipalities often lack tools to analyse this potential dynamically. This HOLONI pilot within AI4Cities focuses on enabling data-driven planning and coordination of local solar energy generation. Stakeholders include city administrations, sustainable brands, energy retailers, and prosumers interested in collective climate impact. The objective is to predict solar generation and surplus across the urban fabric and use these insights to support local energy exchange and new community-based business models. The solution applies predictive analytics to diverse urban datasets, including building characteristics, solar exposure, and consumption patterns, to estimate current and future solar surplus potential. These insights support planning of incentive schemes, energy sharing strategies, and commercial relationships between stakeholders. The system integrates with local energy platforms to align predictions with operational decision-making. The pilot enables municipalities to better understand city-wide renewable potential, supports efficient matching of supply and demand, and strengthens community engagement around sustainability goals. It demonstrates how predictive intelligence can drive more effective local energy systems and accelerate the transition to clean, collective urban energy models.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1038,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00882,EU_PSTW_202609_00882,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Copenhagen,Fuel and energy,The SPIKE pilot in Copenhagen addresses this challenge by enabling real-time optimisation of building energy consumption while maintaining occupant comfort.,SPIKE – Energy Pilot Project in Copenhagen,City of Copenhagen; Local Government; Fuel and energy; Denmark,"Buildings account for a significant share of urban energy consumption, yet many existing facilities lack the flexibility to optimise energy use without costly renovations. The objective is to reduce operational energy use and integrate buildings into flexible, community-based energy systems.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Buildings account for a significant share of urban energy consumption, yet many existing facilities lack the flexibility to optimise energy use without costly renovations. The SPIKE pilot in Copenhagen addresses this challenge by enabling real-time optimisation of building energy consumption while maintaining occupant comfort. Stakeholders include building owners, occupants, energy communities, and city authorities pursuing energy efficiency and decarbonisation targets. The objective is to reduce operational energy use and integrate buildings into flexible, community-based energy systems. The solution deploys a scalable plug-and-play kit of sensors and controllers that interfaces with existing HVAC systems. Real-time data is used to fine-tune energy usage dynamically, without structural changes. An integrated platform orchestrates energy loads and renewable production under an “Energy as a Service” model, enabling coordination at community level. The pilot achieves on average 20% operational energy savings and over 90% in-comfort time for occupants. It also demonstrates the potential to aggregate flexible buildings into a virtual power plant at urban scale, supporting grid stability, renewable integration, and cost-effective energy management.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1039,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00883,EU_PSTW_202609_00883,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Amsterdam,Fuel and energy,Many buildings operate inefficiently due to static control systems and limited integration with renewable energy sources.,Spike - Energy Pilot Project in Amsterdam,City of Amsterdam; Local Government; Fuel and energy; Netherlands,"The objective is to optimise energy use in real time, improve comfort, and support local energy balancing without major retrofits. The solution uses a plug-and-play hardware kit combined with a unified digital platform that continuously adjusts HVAC operation based on real-time conditions.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"In Amsterdam, achieving climate goals requires scalable solutions that reduce building energy consumption while enabling participation in local energy communities. Many buildings operate inefficiently due to static control systems and limited integration with renewable energy sources. This SPIKE pilot, part of AI4Cities, aims to demonstrate how existing buildings can become active components of a flexible urban energy system. Stakeholders include municipalities, building managers, occupants, and energy providers. The objective is to optimise energy use in real time, improve comfort, and support local energy balancing without major retrofits. The solution uses a plug-and-play hardware kit combined with a unified digital platform that continuously adjusts HVAC operation based on real-time conditions. The platform supports “Energy as a Service” by orchestrating demand and renewable production across multiple buildings. Results mirror those achieved in other pilots, with average energy savings of around 20% and high comfort levels for occupants. The project shows how buildings can be aggregated into virtual power plants, enabling scalable energy flexibility, reduced emissions, and new value chains within urban energy communities.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1040,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00884,EU_PSTW_202609_00884,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Helsingborg Municipality,Pre-primary and primary education.,UNKNOWN,LAIban - A chatbot for children,Helsingborg Municipality; Local Government; Pre-primary and primary education.; Sweden,LAIban operates as a conversational assistant deployed across all 80 public preschools in Helsingborg. It responds to common questions using structured content and language models trained on predefined educational and administrative data.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Children in municipal preschools frequently ask practical questions about daily routines, activities and schedules, which can place additional demands on staff. The City of Helsingborg introduced LAIban to support children’s independence while complementing the role of educators. Stakeholders include children, preschool staff, municipal administrators and parents. The objective is to provide accessible, age-appropriate information while fostering children’s confidence and inclusion. LAIban operates as a conversational assistant deployed across all 80 public preschools in Helsingborg. It responds to common questions using structured content and language models trained on predefined educational and administrative data. The system supports multiple languages, reflecting the demographic diversity of its users, and adapts responses accordingly. Children were actively involved in the design and iterative improvement of the assistant, influencing both content and functionality. LAIban also explains its own role and limitations to children, supporting transparency and digital literacy. The solution remains in active use and has improved children’s autonomy, reduced repetitive questions for staff and enhanced inclusiveness through multilingual support. Its participatory design approach has strengthened trust and usability, while its controlled scope ensures reliability and age-appropriate interaction.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1043,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00885,EU_PSTW_202609_00885,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Stockholm,R&D public order and safety.,"The AI Safety Perception Patterns project, developed by Senseable Stockholm Lab in collaboration with the City of Stockholm, addresses this challenge by creating a reproducible, data-driven approach to mapping perceived safety.",AI Safety Perception Patterns,City of Stockholm; Local Government; R&D public order and safety.; Sweden,"Perceived safety is a critical factor influencing how citizens use and experience urban spaces, yet it is difficult to measure systematically over time. The approach is designed to be fully replicable, using the same code and methods year after year, enabling longitudinal analysis.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Perceived safety is a critical factor influencing how citizens use and experience urban spaces, yet it is difficult to measure systematically over time. The AI Safety Perception Patterns project, developed by Senseable Stockholm Lab in collaboration with the City of Stockholm, addresses this challenge by creating a reproducible, data-driven approach to mapping perceived safety. Stakeholders include urban planners, architects, policymakers, and residents concerned with inclusive and secure public environments. The objective is to support long-term monitoring of perceived safety and evaluate the impact of urban interventions. The solution combines multiple data sources, including web-based surveys and urban data, to model safety perception across the city. Analytical methods identify patterns and generate spatial representations that are integrated into the city’s GIS platform. The approach is designed to be fully replicable, using the same code and methods year after year, enabling longitudinal analysis. The results provide planners with actionable insights into where and why safety perceptions vary, supporting evidence-based urban design. The project delivers a scalable framework for monitoring social sustainability and assessing the effectiveness of policy and design interventions over time.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1044,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00886,EU_PSTW_202609_00886,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Tallinn Municipality,Pollution abatement.,"Urban authorities increasingly need to align shared mobility policies with ambitious greenhouse gas reduction targets, yet lack tools to quantify the climate impact of different regulatory strategies. In Tallinn, policymakers face complex choices around fleet caps, pricing, and incentives for shared mobility services, with limited visibility of their emissions outcomes.",AVENUE – Mobility Pilot Project in Tallinn,Tallinn Municipality; Local Government; Pollution abatement.; Estonia,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Urban authorities increasingly need to align shared mobility policies with ambitious greenhouse gas reduction targets, yet lack tools to quantify the climate impact of different regulatory strategies. In Tallinn, policymakers face complex choices around fleet caps, pricing, and incentives for shared mobility services, with limited visibility of their emissions outcomes. The AVENUE pilot addresses this challenge by providing a decision support capability to assess and monitor the carbon footprint effects of shared mobility policies. Stakeholders include city leaders, transport authorities, and mobility operators. The objective is to enable data-driven, timely decisions that support climate mitigation while maintaining effective mobility systems. The solution combines large-scale geolocated mobility data from anonymised mobile network records and operator-shared datasets with urban data sources. Demand prediction models simulate modal choice behaviour and assess how regulatory measures influence shared mobility usage. Scenario analysis evaluates resulting emissions impacts. The pilot improves policymakers’ ability to compare strategies, anticipate outcomes, and track progress over time. It supports more effective regulation of shared mobility, enhanced accountability of climate policies, and measurable reductions in transport-related emissions at city scale.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1045,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00887,EU_PSTW_202609_00887,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Stavanger,Pollution abatement.,Designing shared mobility policies that effectively contribute to greenhouse gas reduction is a growing challenge for medium-sized European cities.,AVENUE – Mobility Pilot Project in Stavanger,City of Stavanger; Local Government; Pollution abatement.; Norway,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Designing shared mobility policies that effectively contribute to greenhouse gas reduction is a growing challenge for medium-sized European cities. In Stavanger, authorities require robust evidence to understand how regulatory choices affect travel behaviour and emissions. The AVENUE pilot responds by offering a data-driven framework to analyse and monitor the environmental impact of shared mobility strategies. Stakeholders include municipal decision-makers, transport planners, and mobility providers. The objective is to support climate-aligned policy design through continuous measurement and scenario-based evaluation. The solution integrates anonymised geolocated mobility data and operator-provided information to model demand and modal shift patterns. Predictive models simulate how changes in policy parameters influence the uptake of shared mobility services and the resulting emissions footprint. These insights enable comparison of alternative regulatory approaches and ongoing monitoring of their effectiveness. The pilot strengthens strategic planning, reduces uncertainty in policy decisions, and enhances the city’s capacity to implement evidence-based mobility measures that contribute to decarbonisation and sustainable urban transport systems.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1046,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00888,EU_PSTW_202609_00888,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Tallinn Municipality,Pollution abatement.,"Cities aiming to decarbonise transport must identify where shared micro-mobility can most effectively replace car trips, yet traditional planning tools offer limited insight into spatial and temporal dynamics.",MPAT – Mobility Policy Auto Tuner Pilot in Tallinn,Tallinn Municipality; Local Government; Pollution abatement.; Estonia,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Cities aiming to decarbonise transport must identify where shared micro-mobility can most effectively replace car trips, yet traditional planning tools offer limited insight into spatial and temporal dynamics. The MPAT pilot in Tallinn addresses this gap by enabling targeted optimisation of mobility policies to maximise emissions reductions. Stakeholders include city transport authorities and policymakers seeking to accelerate low-carbon mobility adoption. The objective is to identify priority areas for policy interventions such as subsidies or fleet adjustments and to quantify their climate impact. The solution analyses travel patterns to determine where shared electric bicycles, scooters, or mopeds are most likely to displace internal combustion vehicle trips. Forecasting models anticipate growth in micro-mobility demand and evolving mode shift patterns. Policy scenarios are evaluated based on their projected CO₂ savings and monitored over time. The pilot demonstrates improved precision in policy targeting, greater transparency in emissions outcomes, and enhanced ability to adapt measures dynamically. It provides a practical tool for embedding climate objectives directly into mobility policy design and evaluation.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1047,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00889,EU_PSTW_202609_00889,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Amsterdam,Pollution abatement.,UNKNOWN,MPAT – Mobility Policy Auto Tuner Pilot in Amsterdam,City of Amsterdam; Local Government; Pollution abatement.; Netherlands,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Amsterdam’s ambition to reduce transport emissions requires sophisticated tools to understand and influence modal shift towards low-carbon mobility. The MPAT pilot supports this goal by providing a data-driven engine to optimise shared micro-mobility policies. Stakeholders include municipal authorities and urban planners responsible for balancing accessibility, sustainability, and public space management. The objective is to maximise the emissions reduction potential of shared electric mobility while anticipating future demand. The solution combines spatial analysis of travel behaviour with predictive modelling to identify locations where micro-mobility can most effectively replace car journeys. Policy levers such as incentives, parking regulations, and fleet size adjustments are evaluated based on their projected climate benefits. Continuous monitoring allows policymakers to track performance and refine interventions. The pilot enhances strategic planning, supports transparent assessment of policy impacts, and contributes to measurable CO₂ reductions. It demonstrates how predictive analytics can guide more effective and adaptive urban mobility policies aligned with climate targets.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1048,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00890,EU_PSTW_202609_00890,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Helsinki University Hospital,Public health services.,"Adolescents often face barriers to accessing mental health information and support, including stigma, lack of anonymity and limited service availability.","Milli, AI Chatbot for Mental Health",Helsinki University Hospital; Local Government; Public health services.; Finland,"Identifying information is automatically deleted, and users can request explanations about data use and system functioning.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Adolescents often face barriers to accessing mental health information and support, including stigma, lack of anonymity and limited service availability. Milli was developed in Finland to provide a confidential entry point for young people seeking information about mental health concerns. Stakeholders include adolescents, healthcare providers, psychologists and public health institutions. The objective is to improve early access to reliable information and guide users towards appropriate support when needed. Milli operates as a conversational assistant that responds to anonymous user queries and provides tailored information based on the user’s age group. It processes text inputs to identify themes and risk indicators, enabling differentiated responses and escalation in urgent situations. If signs of self-harm or crisis are detected, the system directs users to emergency services or trusted adults. Identifying information is automatically deleted, and users can request explanations about data use and system functioning. Milli currently handles between 500 and 1,200 queries per week. Reported impacts include improved accessibility to mental health information, reduced barriers to help-seeking and strengthened trust through transparency, privacy safeguards and user-centred design. Ongoing development focuses on fairness, accessibility and inclusive language.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1049,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00891,EU_PSTW_202609_00891,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Espoo Municipality,Social protection n.e.c.,"The City of Espoo sought to improve preventive social, healthcare and education services by better understanding citizens’ needs before problems escalated into costly or irreversible social exclusion.",Intelligent Wellness Platform Experiment,Espoo Municipality; Local Government; Social protection n.e.c.; Finland,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The City of Espoo sought to improve preventive social, healthcare and education services by better understanding citizens’ needs before problems escalated into costly or irreversible social exclusion. Traditional service planning relied on siloed data and reactive interventions, limiting the city’s ability to act early and in a targeted way. In collaboration with Tieto (now Tietoevry), Espoo launched an experiment to test whether large-scale data analysis could support more timely, citizen-centric welfare services. Stakeholders included municipal social and healthcare departments, education services, policymakers and the city’s residents. The objective was to assess whether predictive insights could inform service planning and resource allocation while respecting strict data protection requirements. The solution integrated longitudinal data from social services, healthcare and education covering the years 2002–2016, representing around 520,000 residents and over 37 million service interactions. Data were anonymised at source, encrypted in transit and processed in a secure analytical environment. Predictive models were used to identify risk patterns associated with social exclusion and future service needs, with results fed into planning processes rather than individual-level decisions. The experiment demonstrated that data-driven insights could support earlier interventions, more efficient service planning and new preventive service pathways. Qualitatively, it showed strong potential for improving wellbeing outcomes while reducing long-term social and healthcare costs.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1050,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00892,EU_PSTW_202609_00892,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Dublin City Council,Cultural services.,UNKNOWN,Gallery Visitor's AI Analysis,Dublin City Council; Local Government; Cultural services.; Ireland,"The implemented solution uses a network of connected cameras combined with edge processing technology developed with Arm to capture anonymised visual data. The approach enhances operational efficiency, improves visitor experience and demonstrates how advanced analytics can be deployed responsibly within cultural institutions.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Gallery Visitor’s AI Analysis responds to the Hugh Lane Gallery’s need to obtain accurate, non-intrusive insights into visitor numbers and movement patterns in order to improve exhibition planning, optimise spatial design and strengthen health and safety management. Working with Dublin City Council’s Smart City team, the Gallery sought a privacy-conscious technological approach capable of delivering reliable analytics without compromising visitors’ rights. The objective was to better understand how audiences engage with exhibitions and facilities while maintaining full compliance with data protection principles. The implemented solution uses a network of connected cameras combined with edge processing technology developed with Arm to capture anonymised visual data. Computer vision techniques analyse movement flows without identifying individuals, and aggregated outputs are visualised through heat mapping to highlight high-traffic zones and dwell times. Data processing is designed according to privacy-by-design principles, ensuring no personally identifiable information is stored. The insights generated support curatorial decision-making, space utilisation planning and visitor flow management. The approach enhances operational efficiency, improves visitor experience and demonstrates how advanced analytics can be deployed responsibly within cultural institutions.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1051,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00893,EU_PSTW_202609_00893,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Greater Manchester,R&D economic affairs,"In Greater Manchester, traditional signal control struggled to adapt to changing travel patterns, particularly during increased active travel uptake.",AI-controlled Smart Traffic Junctions in Manchester,Greater Manchester; Local Government; R&D economic affairs; United Kingdom,Transport for Greater Manchester implemented AI-controlled smart junctions to address this challenge. The solution uses sensors with embedded intelligence to anonymously detect and classify road users in real time.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The rapid growth of walking and cycling requires traffic management systems that can dynamically prioritise different road users while maintaining safety and efficiency. In Greater Manchester, traditional signal control struggled to adapt to changing travel patterns, particularly during increased active travel uptake. Transport for Greater Manchester implemented AI-controlled smart junctions to address this challenge. Stakeholders include transport authorities, pedestrians, cyclists, and motorists. The objective is to improve junction performance, support active travel, and optimise traffic flow. The solution uses sensors with embedded intelligence to anonymously detect and classify road users in real time. Traffic signals are dynamically adjusted to prioritise pedestrians or cyclists when demand increases. Deployed initially at selected sites and later scaled to 20 junctions, the system operates without collecting personal data. The project improves safety, reduces waiting times for active modes, and enhances network efficiency. It demonstrates a scalable approach to adaptive traffic management that supports sustainable mobility and more inclusive urban streets.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1052,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00894,EU_PSTW_202609_00894,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Greater Manchester,Police Services,"During the COVID-19 pandemic, transport authorities faced unprecedented challenges in ensuring passenger safety while maintaining essential mobility services. Transport for Greater Manchester (TfGM) needed timely insights into how travellers moved through interchanges and where social distancing was difficult to maintain.",Behaviour Analytics to Measure Impact of Social Distancing in Transport,Greater Manchester; Local Government; Police Services; United Kingdom,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"During the COVID-19 pandemic, transport authorities faced unprecedented challenges in ensuring passenger safety while maintaining essential mobility services. Transport for Greater Manchester (TfGM) needed timely insights into how travellers moved through interchanges and where social distancing was difficult to maintain. Existing CCTV systems provided visual coverage but limited analytical value. TfGM partnered with startup Humanising Autonomy to pilot a behaviour analytics solution at key transport hubs, including Bolton and Wythenshawe interchanges and Wigan bus station. The objective was to support rapid, evidence-based decisions on temporary infrastructure changes and operational measures. The implementation analysed live and recorded CCTV footage through a cloud-based platform that extracted anonymised behavioural patterns, such as crowd density, group formation and directional movement. The system generated dashboards highlighting hotspots where distancing compliance was challenging, without identifying individuals. Insights were integrated into TfGM’s operational decision-making processes. The pilot enabled faster identification of problem areas, informed layout adjustments and supported safer passenger flows. Qualitatively, it improved situational awareness, reduced reliance on manual observation and demonstrated how existing infrastructure could be leveraged to enhance public safety during emergencies.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1053,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00895,EU_PSTW_202609_00895,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Gava Municipality,Social protection n.e.c.,"Municipal social services often struggle to provide timely, clear, and personalised information on social benefits, leading to underutilisation of available aid and administrative overload. Reported outcomes include improved accessibility to social benefits, reduced administrative burden, and faster processing times, demonstrating the feasibility of conversational digital services in local welfare administration.",Gavius Virtual Assistant,Gava Municipality; Local Government; Social protection n.e.c.; Spain,"The solution was implemented as a virtual assistant accessible via mobile devices, designed to guide users through available social aids, eligibility criteria, application processes, and follow-up. The system integrates municipal databases, service workflows, and rule-based decision logic to provide tailored responses and automate parts of case handling.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Municipal social services often struggle to provide timely, clear, and personalised information on social benefits, leading to underutilisation of available aid and administrative overload. The City Council of Gavà led the Gavius project to improve citizens’ access to social assistance by simplifying how benefits are discovered, requested, and managed. Stakeholders included social services staff, municipal managers, citizens—particularly vulnerable groups—and seven public and private partners. The objective was to reduce bureaucratic barriers, accelerate benefit processing, and improve the quality of interaction between the municipality and residents. The solution was implemented as a virtual assistant accessible via mobile devices, designed to guide users through available social aids, eligibility criteria, application processes, and follow-up. The system integrates municipal databases, service workflows, and rule-based decision logic to provide tailored responses and automate parts of case handling. In addition to the citizen-facing assistant, separate virtual assistants were developed for municipal staff and managers to support internal processes. The project was funded through ERDF under the Urban Innovative Actions programme and formally concluded in February 2023. Reported outcomes include improved accessibility to social benefits, reduced administrative burden, and faster processing times, demonstrating the feasibility of conversational digital services in local welfare administration.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1054,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00896,EU_PSTW_202609_00896,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Rimini Municipality,General public services n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,Rimini Chatbot,Rimini Municipality; Local Government; General public services n.e.c.; Italy,"The solution remains in active use and has improved response times, reduced routine enquiries handled by staff and enhanced user satisfaction.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Citizens seeking information about municipal services often rely on email, phone calls or in-person visits, creating pressure on administrative staff and limiting accessibility outside office hours. The City of Rimini introduced a digital assistant to provide continuous support to residents navigating municipal services. Stakeholders include citizens, municipal departments and customer service staff. The objective is to improve access to information, increase citizen autonomy and reduce the workload on traditional service channels. The chatbot is embedded in the city’s institutional portal and interacts with users through natural language queries. It is trained on administrative service descriptions, procedures and FAQs, and continuously improves its responses based on user interactions. The system integrates with municipal content management systems to ensure up-to-date information. The assistant is available 24/7 and complements existing communication channels rather than replacing them. The solution remains in active use and has improved response times, reduced routine enquiries handled by staff and enhanced user satisfaction. Its conversational design supports intuitive interaction, while continuous learning contributes to scalability and long-term service quality.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1055,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00897,EU_PSTW_202609_00897,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Polish Ministry of Labour,Unemployment.,UNKNOWN,AI Algorithm used toclassify unemployed people in Poland,Polish Ministry of Labour; Central-Government; Unemployment.; Poland,"The Polish government implemented an AI algorithm aimed at profiling individuals without jobs and establishing an automated system to assist in determining the support they could receive, utilizing their personal data and responses from job center interviews.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Polish government implemented an AI algorithm aimed at profiling individuals without jobs and establishing an automated system to assist in determining the support they could receive, utilizing their personal data and responses from job center interviews. However, this initiative was abandoned in December 2019 due to its deficiency in transparency and violations of data protection rights.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,NOT_IN_USE,미사용,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1056,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00898,EU_PSTW_202609_00898,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,France's Ministry of Istruction,Education not definable by level.,"Young people often struggle to navigate complex education and career choices, particularly when information is fragmented or difficult to personalise.",Hello Charly - AI chatbot assisting students making decisions,France's Ministry of Istruction; Central-Government; Education not definable by level.; France,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Young people often struggle to navigate complex education and career choices, particularly when information is fragmented or difficult to personalise. Hello Charly was developed to provide accessible guidance to students aged 14–24 through familiar digital channels. Stakeholders include students, educational institutions, career advisors and public authorities. The objective is to support informed decision-making while ensuring anonymity and ease of access. The service engages users through conversational interactions, asking structured questions to understand interests, preferences and aspirations. It draws on a large, curated database covering more than 850 professions and 110,000 training pathways. Users can explore options, compare alternatives and build personalised scorecards without registering or sharing personal data. Hello Charly is available via web and popular social media platforms. Since its launch in 2016, it has been used by over 220,000 people in France. Reported impacts include improved engagement with guidance services, greater clarity for students and reduced reliance on traditional counselling resources. Its anonymity-first design and broad coverage have supported scalability and sustained uptake.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1057,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00899,EU_PSTW_202609_00899,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,France's Ministry of Istruction,Education not definable by level.,"The system addresses the challenge of matching student preferences with available study programmes at national scale. Parcoursup has faced criticism regarding transparency and perceived fairness, particularly due to limited disclosure of sorting logic.",PARCOURSUP | Screening of university applicants in France,France's Ministry of Istruction; Central-Government; Education not definable by level.; France,"Parcoursup collects student preferences, academic records and programme criteria, and applies algorithmic sorting to prioritise applications. Despite ongoing public debate, the system remains in use nationwide.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"France introduced Parcoursup to manage the allocation of university places following reforms that allowed institutions to select applicants. The system addresses the challenge of matching student preferences with available study programmes at national scale. Stakeholders include prospective students, universities, the Ministry of Higher Education and admissions staff. The objective is to coordinate applications, manage capacity constraints and standardise admissions procedures. Parcoursup collects student preferences, academic records and programme criteria, and applies algorithmic sorting to prioritise applications. While human decision-making remains central at institutional level, the system supports ranking and matching processes. Parcoursup has faced criticism regarding transparency and perceived fairness, particularly due to limited disclosure of sorting logic. Despite ongoing public debate, the system remains in use nationwide. Impacts include faster processing of large application volumes, centralised coordination and reduced administrative fragmentation, alongside continued scrutiny concerning accountability and explainability.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1058,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00900,EU_PSTW_202609_00900,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Milan,Police Services,Traditional policing methods struggled to identify patterns across large volumes of crime data over time.,DELIA | Algorithm to predict location and perpetrator of crime,City of Milan; Local Government; Police Services; Italy,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Law enforcement agencies in Milan sought more effective ways to prevent and investigate serial crimes, particularly commercial and bank robberies, which had significant social and economic impacts. Traditional policing methods struggled to identify patterns across large volumes of crime data over time. The City of Milan’s Questura began long-term field trials of predictive crime analysis tools developed by startup KeyCrime, with the objective of supporting investigative work and targeted prevention strategies. Stakeholders included police analysts, operational units, judicial authorities and academic evaluators. The solution analysed historical crime records, spatial and temporal patterns, and offence characteristics to identify links between incidents and likely future scenarios. Over successive trial phases, the system evolved into the DELIA platform, improving usability, analytical performance and pattern-linking capabilities. Results were reviewed by the Italian Ministry of Justice and independently evaluated by the University of Essex to ensure objectivity. The impact was significant: between 2008 and 2019, commercial robberies in Milan decreased by around 58%, while bank robberies in the wider province fell by approximately 89% between 2009 and 2019. The project demonstrated strong operational value in crime prevention and investigation.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F06_HIGH_VOLUME_SCALABILITY_BOTTLENECK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1059,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00901,EU_PSTW_202609_00901,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Devon County Council,General public services n.e.c.,"At a conceptual level, the system combines assessment data, defined cost assumptions, and policy rules covering matters such as social isolation, replacement care, and shared care, while leaving room for review, challenge, and revision during support planning.",Resource Allocation System,Devon County Council; Local Government; General public services n.e.c.; United Kingdom,"The implementation uses a rule-based budgeting framework linked to assessment information, applying published principles, standard rates, and eligibility-related rules to generate an estimated budget. The impact lies in greater transparency, more consistent budget estimation, and better use of practitioner time.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Adult social care authorities must translate assessed needs into indicative budgets in a way that is fair, transparent, and flexible enough to reflect individual circumstances, while still allowing professional judgement and support planning to shape the final outcome. In Devon, the Resource Allocation System is used to generate an estimated personal budget for community care, giving practitioners, the person being assessed, and their representatives a structured starting point for planning how identified needs might be met. The objective is to support consistent budgeting, provide early visibility of the likely scale of support, and guide discussion without turning the estimate into a fixed entitlement or a substitute for professional judgement. The implementation uses a rule-based budgeting framework linked to assessment information, applying published principles, standard rates, and eligibility-related rules to generate an estimated budget. At a conceptual level, the system combines assessment data, defined cost assumptions, and policy rules covering matters such as social isolation, replacement care, and shared care, while leaving room for review, challenge, and revision during support planning. The impact lies in greater transparency, more consistent budget estimation, and better use of practitioner time. It also supports a strengths-based approach by treating the budget as guidance within a broader planning process rather than as an automatic final decision.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1061,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00902,EU_PSTW_202609_00902,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,UK Municipalities,Social protection n.e.c.,"To manage limited investigative resources, around 80 councils introduced automated risk screening tools to flag claims for further review.",80 councils in the UK use AI to identify possible frauds in welfare claims,UK Municipalities; Local Government; Social protection n.e.c.; United Kingdom,"As a result, the use of such systems has come under legal, regulatory and public scrutiny.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Local authorities in the UK are responsible for administering welfare benefits while preventing fraud and error. To manage limited investigative resources, around 80 councils introduced automated risk screening tools to flag claims for further review. Stakeholders include councils, welfare claimants, investigators and technology providers. The objective is to prioritise checks and reduce erroneous payments. The system analyses administrative data associated with claims and assigns risk indicators that inform follow-up actions by caseworkers. Human review remains mandatory before any decision is taken. Investigations revealed that certain variables, including age, influenced risk assessments, raising concerns about bias and proportionality. As a result, the use of such systems has come under legal, regulatory and public scrutiny. While tools continue to be used, councils have been required to review practices, improve governance and ensure compliance with equality and data protection standards. Impacts include efficiency gains in case selection, alongside heightened awareness of the need for transparency, oversight and safeguards in automated welfare administration.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1062,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00903,EU_PSTW_202609_00903,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Bundeskriminalamt,Police Services,UNKNOWN,RADAR-rechts | Assessing the risk of violence by far-right extremists,Bundeskriminalamt; Central-Government; Police Services; Germany,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Following a series of fatal right-wing extremist attacks in Germany in 2019 and 2020, federal and state law enforcement authorities faced increasing pressure to strengthen the prevention of politically motivated violent crime. To support a more systematic and person-centred approach, the Federal Criminal Police Office (BKA) developed RADAR-rechts, a structured risk assessment instrument designed to prioritise individuals known from the right-wing extremist spectrum who may pose an acute threat of committing life-threatening violence. The tool supports police forces nationwide by enabling consistent prioritisation of so-called dangerous and relevant persons and identifying cases requiring more detailed individual risk analysis. RADAR-rechts is based on a scientifically grounded, rule-based analytical framework that evaluates standardised behavioural, situational and criminological indicators drawn from police data and professional assessments. The system integrates with existing police workflows while preserving human responsibility for final decisions. Since becoming operational in May 2022, RADAR-rechts has improved the consistency and transparency of risk management across Germany, allowing law enforcement agencies to allocate preventive and investigative resources more effectively. Its introduction represents a significant step towards standardised, evidence-informed approaches in the person-oriented prevention of far-right extremist violence.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1063,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00904,EU_PSTW_202609_00904,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Basque Government Informatic Society,Cultural services.,UNKNOWN,VicomTTS - Quality synthetic voices,Basque Government Informatic Society; Regional Government; Cultural services.; Spain,"VicomTTS was developed to address the limited availability of high-quality digital services in the Basque language, which can hinder its everyday use in public, educational and personal contexts. Built as a digital public service, VicomTTS uses neural speech synthesis models implemented in Python and executed via the PyTorch framework, trained on Basque-language datasets to ensure accurate pronunciation and intelligible prosody.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"VicomTTS was developed to address the limited availability of high-quality digital services in the Basque language, which can hinder its everyday use in public, educational and personal contexts. The service provides citizens, learners and institutions with a reliable tool for converting written Basque text into clear, natural-sounding speech, supporting linguistic inclusion and accessibility. Built as a digital public service, VicomTTS uses neural speech synthesis models implemented in Python and executed via the PyTorch framework, trained on Basque-language datasets to ensure accurate pronunciation and intelligible prosody. The system is deployed as an online service designed for low-latency performance and high reliability. While initial uptake was lower than anticipated, the service has steadily gained popularity and is now processing approximately 5,000 requests per day. Performance metrics indicate that around 50% of requests are handled in under one second, with a reported success rate of 99.7%. VicomTTS demonstrates how advanced speech technologies can contribute to the preservation and digital normalisation of minority languages while delivering a scalable and dependable public-facing service.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1068,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00905,EU_PSTW_202609_00905,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Generalitat de Catalunya,Public order and safety n.e.c.,These infractions contribute significantly to road accidents and increase the burden on enforcement authorities. Public administrations face challenges in detecting violations efficiently and processing sanctions in a timely manner.,Enhancing road safety through automatic identification of traffic violations,Generalitat de Catalunya; Regional Government; Public order and safety n.e.c.; Spain,Enhancing Road Safety through Automatic Identification of Traffic Violations addresses persistent risks caused by behaviours such as driving without seat belts and using mobile phones while driving. The solution employs computer vision models deployed on roadside or mobile camera systems to automatically detect seat belt non-use and handheld phone usage.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Enhancing Road Safety through Automatic Identification of Traffic Violations addresses persistent risks caused by behaviours such as driving without seat belts and using mobile phones while driving. These infractions contribute significantly to road accidents and increase the burden on enforcement authorities. Public administrations face challenges in detecting violations efficiently and processing sanctions in a timely manner. The objective is to strengthen deterrence and improve compliance through automated monitoring mechanisms that complement traditional policing. The solution employs computer vision models deployed on roadside or mobile camera systems to automatically detect seat belt non-use and handheld phone usage. Image recognition algorithms analyse video streams, classify violations and extract relevant evidence, which is then transmitted to enforcement platforms for validation and sanction processing. The system integrates with existing traffic management and administrative databases to streamline case handling. By automating detection and optimising the sanction workflow, authorities can increase enforcement consistency and reduce manual workload. Expected impacts include improved road safety outcomes, faster processing of offences, more efficient allocation of enforcement resources and a reduction in human error in violation assessment.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1069,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00906,EU_PSTW_202609_00906,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Government of Flanders,Family and children.,UNKNOWN,Fraud detection in family allowances (Groeipakket),Government of Flanders; Regional Government; Family and children.; Belgium,The implemented solution uses data analytics and predictive modelling techniques to detect patterns indicative of fraud across administrative and income datasets.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Fraud Detection in Family Allowances (Groeipakket) addresses the need to safeguard the Flemish Government’s family benefits system, which distributes approximately €4 billion annually to 1.6 million children residing in Flanders. The scheme operates through automated rights assignment, granting customised financial support based on household circumstances and parental income. Detected fraud exceeding €500,000 in 2022 highlighted vulnerabilities requiring enhanced monitoring capabilities. The objective is to support file managers and social inspectors by identifying irregularities at an early stage and preventing financial losses. The implemented solution uses data analytics and predictive modelling techniques to detect patterns indicative of fraud across administrative and income datasets. Machine learning models analyse historical case data to generate risk scores and flag suspicious claims for further investigation. The system integrates with existing benefit management platforms, providing alerts to grant outsourcers while maintaining procedural oversight by human officers. Anticipated results include improved detection accuracy, more efficient allocation of inspection resources and reduced financial leakage. By strengthening control mechanisms, the initiative contributes to financial sustainability and may support a budget surplus for the agency.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1070,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00907,EU_PSTW_202609_00907,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Generalitat de Catalunya,General public services n.e.c.,"Public administrations often place a heavy administrative burden on citizens, requiring repeated data entry and proactive applications for benefits, which disadvantages those with limited bureaucratic capacity. The impact to date has been limited due to low uptake and insufficient communication, but the pilot demonstrates potential reductions in administrative workload, fewer missed application deadlines and fairer access to benefits.",Proactive administrative procedures,Generalitat de Catalunya; Regional Government; General public services n.e.c.; Spain,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Public administrations often place a heavy administrative burden on citizens, requiring repeated data entry and proactive applications for benefits, which disadvantages those with limited bureaucratic capacity. Spanish authorities explored proactive administrative procedures to ensure that public resources reach those most in need, focusing initially on university scholarships. Stakeholders included students, universities, regional administrations and social services. The objective was to reduce administrative friction while complying with legal requirements that still require explicit citizen consent for procedures. The pilot integrated existing administrative datasets to pre-fill applications and notify eligible students of potential entitlements, minimising the steps required from citizens. Rather than fully automated decision-making, the system supported a “near-proactive” approach, prompting users to confirm intent and data accuracy. The impact to date has been limited due to low uptake and insufficient communication, but the pilot demonstrates potential reductions in administrative workload, fewer missed application deadlines and fairer access to benefits. Lessons learned are informing future iterations and broader diffusion strategies.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1071,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00908,EU_PSTW_202609_00908,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Helsinki,Waste management.,"Maintaining cleanliness in large, complex environments such as airport parking areas and service zones is labour-intensive and costly, particularly in Nordic climates with year-round maintenance needs. The results showed improved cleaning efficiency, reduced manual labour requirements and suitability for continuous, year-round operations.",Trombia Free experiment in Helsinki,City of Helsinki; Local Government; Waste management.; Finland,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Maintaining cleanliness in large, complex environments such as airport parking areas and service zones is labour-intensive and costly, particularly in Nordic climates with year-round maintenance needs. Helsinki Airport tested the Trombia Free autonomous street sweeper to assess whether automated cleaning could improve efficiency and reliability. Stakeholders included airport operators, facility managers and technology provider startup Trombia Technologies. The objective was to evaluate performance in semi-closed environments combining narrow indoor spaces and busy outdoor areas. The vehicle used sensor-based navigation and Trombia’s patented air-knife cleaning technology, enabling effective dust removal without water, even in sub-zero temperatures. During the pilot, Trombia Free covered between 5,000 and 15,000 m² per hour, outperforming traditional suction sweepers. The results showed improved cleaning efficiency, reduced manual labour requirements and suitability for continuous, year-round operations. Qualitatively, the pilot demonstrated how autonomous maintenance vehicles could lower operational costs and improve service quality in large infrastructure environments.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1082,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00909,EU_PSTW_202609_00909,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Swedish Police Authority,Police Services,UNKNOWN,Swedish police used face recognition until 2019 at the Skavsta airport,Swedish Police Authority; Central-Government; Police Services; Sweden,"In 2021, the Swedish Authority for Privacy Protection ruled that the use of the system violated the Swedish Criminal Data Act, citing unlawful processing of personal data.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"As part of Operation Rimfrost, aimed at combating organised crime, the Swedish Police tested facial recognition tools to identify suspects across images from crime scenes and CCTV footage. The objective was to enhance investigative capabilities by quickly identifying recurring individuals across large image datasets. Stakeholders included law enforcement units, oversight authorities and the public. The police used the Clearview AI application during pilot deployments, including at Skavsta Airport. The system compared images against a large external database to suggest potential matches for investigative follow-up. In 2021, the Swedish Authority for Privacy Protection ruled that the use of the system violated the Swedish Criminal Data Act, citing unlawful processing of personal data. As a result, the pilot was terminated and all personal data were required to be deleted. The case had significant impact by clarifying legal boundaries for biometric technologies, reinforcing data protection obligations and influencing future governance of digital tools in law enforcement.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1083,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00910,EU_PSTW_202609_00910,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Police department,Police Services,UNKNOWN,Use of smart video surveillance by the local police,Police department; Local Government; Police Services; Belgium,"The deployment has improved investigative efficiency and situational awareness, while also raising ongoing discussions about proportionality, oversight and data protection in the use of advanced surveillance technologies by law enforcement.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Since 2019, local police forces in the Belgian cities of Kortrijk, Kuurne and Lendelede have used smart video surveillance systems to support investigations, public safety and policy analysis. The system assists officers in efficiently reviewing large volumes of video footage, for example by enabling the search for individuals based on attributes such as clothing or movement patterns, reconstructing escape routes, or locating missing persons. It also supports live alerting in predefined situations, such as detecting prohibited vehicle movements near schools, and produces aggregated statistical insights for policing and urban policy. The system stores and categorises objects appearing in video footage, allowing investigators to filter and analyse recordings more effectively. Facial recognition capabilities exist but are subject to strict legal constraints and may only be used in exceptional cases with judicial authorisation. The deployment has improved investigative efficiency and situational awareness, while also raising ongoing discussions about proportionality, oversight and data protection in the use of advanced surveillance technologies by law enforcement.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1088,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00911,EU_PSTW_202609_00911,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,South Wales Police,Police Services,UNKNOWN,Automated Facial Recognition technology within the UK's South Wales Police Department,South Wales Police; Regional Government; Police Services; United Kingdom,"Over the course of 2020, the UK witnessed the first high profile application of the Law Enforcement Directive 15 concerning the use of face recognition technologies in public spaces by the police. The court initially upheld the use of Automated Facial Recognition technology (“AFR”) by South Wales Police, declaring it lawful and proportionate.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Over the course of 2020, the UK witnessed the first high profile application of the Law Enforcement Directive 15 concerning the use of face recognition technologies in public spaces by the police. Seen as an important precedent on a hotly debated topic, the verdict was greeted with a great deal of attention from civil society actors and legal scholars all over Europe and beyond. The case was brought to court by Ed Bridges, a 37 years old man from Cardiff, who claimed his face was scanned without his consent both during Christmas shopping in 2017, and at a peaceful anti-arms protest a year later. The court initially upheld the use of Automated Facial Recognition technology (“AFR”) by South Wales Police, declaring it lawful and proportionate. But the decision was appealed by Liberty, a civil rights group, and the Court of Appeal of England and Wales decided to overturn the High Court’s dismissal and ruled it unlawful on August 11, 2020.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,NOT_IN_USE,미사용,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1089,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00912,EU_PSTW_202609_00912,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Slovenian Police,Police Services,UNKNOWN,Face recognition tool for the Slovenian police,Slovenian Police; Central-Government; Police Services; Slovenia,"The Slovenian police began using facial comparison software as early as 2014 to support criminal investigations involving images from open sources such as social media and online platforms. However, for several years the use of this technology lacked a clear legal basis and was only formally regulated in 2019, five years after its initial deployment.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Slovenian police began using facial comparison software as early as 2014 to support criminal investigations involving images from open sources such as social media and online platforms. The tool, known as Face Trace, was used to assist investigators in identifying suspects by comparing facial features across image datasets. However, for several years the use of this technology lacked a clear legal basis and was only formally regulated in 2019, five years after its initial deployment. The system is intended to generate investigative leads rather than definitive identifications, with results requiring verification through traditional investigative methods. Following legal scrutiny and public debate, the use of facial recognition by the Slovenian police has since been significantly constrained, with stricter requirements for authorisation, documentation and oversight. This case has become emblematic of broader European concerns regarding the lawful, transparent and proportionate use of biometric technologies in policing.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1090,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00913,EU_PSTW_202609_00913,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Brussels Airport Checks,Police Services,UNKNOWN,Face recognition system at Brussels Airport,Brussels Airport Checks; Central-Government; Police Services; Belgium,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"In July 2015, Brussels Airport installed ‘e-gates’ with face recognition technology that checks the identity of EU-citizens from outside the Schengen zone. It electronically checks to see if identity papers are real and if a passenger is flagged. In addition, the face recognition software compares the picture on the passport chip in the identity card with the digital picture the software renders at the e-gate (De Morgen, 2019). In early 2020, the airport scrapped the e-gates, which cost €2.4 million, because they were constantly defective, according to the police (The Bulletin, 2020).",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,NOT_IN_USE,미사용,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1093,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00914,EU_PSTW_202609_00914,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Hellenic Police,Police Services,SPIRIT – Scalable Privacy Preserving Intelligence Analysis for Resolving Identities aims to enhance criminal investigations by enabling authorities to correlate fragmented information across multiple digital sources while addressing privacy considerations. Law enforcement agencies face growing volumes of social media content and multimedia data that may contain relevant evidence but are difficult to analyse manually.,SPIRIT - Scalable privacy preserving intelligence analysis for resolving identities,Hellenic Police; Central-Government; Police Services; Greece,"The system applies facial extraction and matching techniques to images and videos, combined with data mining methods to correlate information from social media and other investigation-relevant sources.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"SPIRIT – Scalable Privacy Preserving Intelligence Analysis for Resolving Identities aims to enhance criminal investigations by enabling authorities to correlate fragmented information across multiple digital sources while addressing privacy considerations. Law enforcement agencies face growing volumes of social media content and multimedia data that may contain relevant evidence but are difficult to analyse manually. The project seeks to improve identity resolution processes and investigative efficiency through scalable analytical tools, with trial deployments including in Greece. The system applies facial extraction and matching techniques to images and videos, combined with data mining methods to correlate information from social media and other investigation-relevant sources. Advanced search algorithms perform continuous associative queries across heterogeneous datasets, identifying potential links between individuals and activities. Privacy-preserving mechanisms are embedded to manage data access and processing safeguards. By automating complex cross-source analysis, the project is expected to accelerate investigations, enhance the identification of suspects and improve the capacity of authorities to process large-scale digital evidence while maintaining structured oversight.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK|F06_HIGH_VOLUME_SCALABILITY_BOTTLENECK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1094,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00915,EU_PSTW_202609_00915,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Saint-Étienne,Police Services,UNKNOWN,Automated surveillance through audio processing,City of Saint-Étienne; Local Government; Police Services; France,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Automated Surveillance through Audio Processing concerns a planned public safety initiative in Saint-Étienne, a city of approximately 175,000 inhabitants, aiming to strengthen urban security monitoring in a disadvantaged neighbourhood. Municipal authorities proposed deploying a network of microphones capable of detecting suspicious sounds and coordinating responses with existing CCTV infrastructure and an autonomous camera-equipped drone. The objective was to enable faster identification of potentially dangerous situations, such as gunshots, and support law enforcement intervention. The proposed system relied on audio signal processing models trained to classify predefined sound categories, including gunshots, drills, sprays and whistles. Detected events would trigger alerts and potentially activate linked visual surveillance systems. Integration with municipal control centres was intended to enable real-time monitoring and response coordination. However, the project was placed on hold after the French data protection authority determined that the deployment did not comply with applicable legal requirements. The case highlights both the potential of automated acoustic monitoring to enhance situational awareness and the critical importance of ensuring legal and data protection compliance in public sector technology deployments.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1096,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00916,EU_PSTW_202609_00916,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Government of France,General public services n.e.c.,"Traditionally, many procedures require individuals to appear physically at public offices to confirm their identity, creating administrative burdens and limiting accessibility.",ALICEM - face recognition for administrative procedures,Government of France; Central-Government; General public services n.e.c.; France,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"ALICEM – Face Recognition for Administrative Procedures is a French government initiative designed to enable citizens to complete administrative processes remotely by verifying their identity through a mobile application. Traditionally, many procedures require individuals to appear physically at public offices to confirm their identity, creating administrative burdens and limiting accessibility. The objective of ALICEM is to streamline digital public services by providing secure remote authentication linked to official identity documents. The system allows users to register their biometric facial characteristics in a smartphone application, which captures and verifies facial data against official identity credentials. Facial recognition algorithms compare live images with stored biometric references to confirm identity before granting access to online administrative services. The application integrates with government digital service platforms to enable secure transactions. Following public debate and media scrutiny in 2019, the government postponed the launch, and no official release date has been publicly confirmed. The initiative illustrates efforts to modernise e-government services while raising important considerations regarding biometric data protection, public trust and regulatory oversight.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1097,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00917,EU_PSTW_202609_00917,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Portuguese Immigration and Border Service,Police Services,"Long queues, manual document checks and fragmented procedures can negatively affect both traveller experience and border operations. While limited in scope and duration, BOM provided valuable insights into operational performance, user acceptance and data protection considerations, informing future EU-level discussions on biometric border management.",BOM - Biometrics on the Move,Portuguese Immigration and Border Service; Central-Government; Police Services; Portugal,"The pilot relied on biometric enrolment of participating passengers, who consented to the use of their facial image and fingerprints.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"European border authorities face constant pressure to improve passenger flow efficiency while maintaining high security standards at external borders. Long queues, manual document checks and fragmented procedures can negatively affect both traveller experience and border operations. To explore alternatives, Frontex, together with the Portuguese Immigration and Border Service and Lisbon Airport Authority (ANA), launched the voluntary “Biometrics on the Move” (BOM) pilot at Humberto Delgado International Airport in October 2019. The objective was to test whether travellers could cross borders more smoothly without repeatedly presenting physical documents, while still complying with border control requirements. The pilot relied on biometric enrolment of participating passengers, who consented to the use of their facial image and fingerprints. These biometric identifiers were captured once and then used at subsequent checkpoints, where automated gates matched live biometric scans against enrolled templates. The system integrated with existing border control infrastructure and operated alongside traditional procedures. The pilot demonstrated the technical feasibility of near-seamless border crossing, significantly reducing interaction time at checkpoints and improving passenger experience. While limited in scope and duration, BOM provided valuable insights into operational performance, user acceptance and data protection considerations, informing future EU-level discussions on biometric border management.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1099,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00918,EU_PSTW_202609_00918,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Public Transport and Traffic Management Department,Police Services,"While the pilot was limited in scale, it demonstrated the feasibility of continuous, automated traffic monitoring and provided city authorities with more detailed and timely insights than manual counting methods.",Machine vision AI solution for better traffic management in Tallinn,Public Transport and Traffic Management Department; Local Government; Police Services; Estonia,"To improve evidence-based transport planning, the City of Tallinn launched a pilot project in 2018 to monitor traffic flows using automated video analysis at selected intersections. The system was trained using locally collected data to reflect real traffic conditions.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"To improve evidence-based transport planning, the City of Tallinn launched a pilot project in 2018 to monitor traffic flows using automated video analysis at selected intersections. The initiative, developed in cooperation with the private company Sifr OÜ, aimed to provide accurate data on the number and types of vehicles entering and leaving the city, supporting decisions on parking management, road construction and congestion mitigation. Cameras installed at three intersections capture traffic footage, which is analysed to count and classify vehicles such as cars, buses, trucks and motorcycles. The system was trained using locally collected data to reflect real traffic conditions. While the pilot was limited in scale, it demonstrated the feasibility of continuous, automated traffic monitoring and provided city authorities with more detailed and timely insights than manual counting methods. The project also laid the groundwork for potential future extensions, including pedestrian counting, although no large-scale expansion has been documented.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1102,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00919,EU_PSTW_202609_00919,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Office of Qualifications and Examinations Regulation (Ofqual),Secondary education.,UNKNOWN,System replacing final examinations in a pattern following previous years,Office of Qualifications and Examinations Regulation (Ofqual); Central-Government; Secondary education.; United Kingdom,Automated decision-making system deployed by the Office of Qualifications and Examinations Regulation (Ofqual) on the basis of an data-based assessment to replace A-level examinations as wells as General Certificates of Secondary Education i 2020 and replacing International Baccalaureate (IB) final examinations in 2020 in such a way that “the distribution of grades follows a similar pattern to that in other years”.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Automated decision-making system deployed by the Office of Qualifications and Examinations Regulation (Ofqual) on the basis of an data-based assessment to replace A-level examinations as wells as General Certificates of Secondary Education i 2020 and replacing International Baccalaureate (IB) final examinations in 2020 in such a way that “the distribution of grades follows a similar pattern to that in other years”. As exams did not take place this summer, students’ grades were instead based on evidence of their likely performance in the exams had they gone ahead. In the absence of any formal assessments delivered by exam boards, evidence relating to students’ expected performance in qualifications this year was required. The algorithm was scrapped after students' protests.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,NOT_IN_USE,미사용,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1113,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00920,EU_PSTW_202609_00920,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Tartu,Education not definable by level.,"The system addresses the administrative complexity of allocating limited places fairly while taking into account parental preferences, proximity and eligibility rules. Developed by the Estonian company OÜ Piksel, the system has become a core component of Tartu’s education administration, reducing manual workload and improving clarity for families.",System called ARNO for submitting and processing application in Estonia,City of Tartu; Local Government; Education not definable by level.; Estonia,"The City of Tartu uses the ARNO system to manage applications and placements for municipal nursery schools, kindergartens and general education schools in a centralised and transparent manner. Parents apply for childcare and school places through a single digital interface, while municipal education authorities use the system to manage queues and generate annual placement decisions.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The City of Tartu uses the ARNO system to manage applications and placements for municipal nursery schools, kindergartens and general education schools in a centralised and transparent manner. The system addresses the administrative complexity of allocating limited places fairly while taking into account parental preferences, proximity and eligibility rules. Parents apply for childcare and school places through a single digital interface, while municipal education authorities use the system to manage queues and generate annual placement decisions. ARNO integrates population registry data, address information and parental input to automatically determine placements, with options for families to request changes if necessary. Developed by the Estonian company OÜ Piksel, the system has become a core component of Tartu’s education administration, reducing manual workload and improving clarity for families. By standardising decision rules and making the process traceable, ARNO has increased trust in allocation outcomes and improved service efficiency.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1114,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00921,EU_PSTW_202609_00921,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,European Commission - DG HOME,Police Services,"The Schengen Area faces the challenge of facilitating legitimate travel while managing security, migration, and public health risks associated with visa-exempt travellers. Risk rules and screening criteria are applied to identify applications requiring further manual assessment by national authorities.",ETIAS (EU Travel Information and Authorisation System),European Commission - DG HOME; European Institution/Agency; Police Services; European Union,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Schengen Area faces the challenge of facilitating legitimate travel while managing security, migration, and public health risks associated with visa-exempt travellers. ETIAS was designed to address this by introducing a pre-travel authorisation requirement for non-EU nationals who do not need a visa to enter Schengen States. Stakeholders include EU border authorities, national security agencies, travellers, and the EU Agency eu-LISA, which is responsible for system development and operation. The objective is to strengthen external border management while reducing refusals at border crossings and improving traveller predictability. ETIAS operates as a centralised IT system that collects traveller application data and automatically checks it against multiple EU information systems, including security, migration, and health-related databases. Risk rules and screening criteria are applied to identify applications requiring further manual assessment by national authorities. Most applications are expected to be processed within minutes, while a minority may take up to 30 days. Although initially planned for earlier deployment, ETIAS has been postponed and is now expected to become operational in 2026, with transitional measures foreseen. Once active, ETIAS is expected to streamline border checks, enhance security cooperation, and improve the overall efficiency and reliability of Schengen border management.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1116,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00922,EU_PSTW_202609_00922,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,European Commission - DG HOME,Police Services,"Cross-border crime within the European Union creates challenges for judicial authorities who must determine whether a third-country national has previous convictions in other Member States. Prior to ECRIS-TCN, criminal record checks for non-EU nationals were fragmented and time-consuming, potentially delaying judicial proceedings and undermining the principle of mutual recognition of judicial decisions.",European Criminal Records Information System – Third Country Nationals (ECRIS-TCN),European Commission - DG HOME; European Institution/Agency; Police Services; European Union,The system has been implemented at EU level and is operational as part of the broader ECRIS infrastructure.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Cross-border crime within the European Union creates challenges for judicial authorities who must determine whether a third-country national has previous convictions in other Member States. Prior to ECRIS-TCN, criminal record checks for non-EU nationals were fragmented and time-consuming, potentially delaying judicial proceedings and undermining the principle of mutual recognition of judicial decisions. The system supports judges, prosecutors and competent authorities by enabling efficient identification of Member States holding criminal records of third-country nationals, thereby strengthening cooperation in the Schengen area and enhancing public security. The objective is to facilitate faster and more reliable exchange of criminal record information across borders. The system operates as a centralised EU-wide index managed by eu-LISA, containing identity data and biometric identifiers such as fingerprints. National authorities query the system to identify which Member State holds relevant criminal records, after which detailed information is exchanged through existing ECRIS channels. The platform is integrated within the EU interoperability framework for large-scale IT systems in justice and home affairs. The system has been implemented at EU level and is operational as part of the broader ECRIS infrastructure. It enhances judicial efficiency, reduces processing time for cross-border checks, strengthens legal certainty and improves the fight against transnational crime.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1117,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00923,EU_PSTW_202609_00923,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Ministry of Migration and Asylum,Police Services,"Reception and identification centres for asylum seekers in Greece face operational challenges related to security management, overcrowding and incident prevention.",CENTAUR - Greek camps for asylum seekers surveillance systems,Ministry of Migration and Asylum; Central-Government; Police Services; Greece,Video and sensor inputs are processed using automated analytics algorithms capable of detecting movement patterns and anomalies. The infrastructure has been implemented and deployed in several camps.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Reception and identification centres for asylum seekers in Greece face operational challenges related to security management, overcrowding and incident prevention. The Greek Ministry of Migration and Asylum introduced surveillance infrastructure to enhance safety within camps and monitor potential unlawful activities. The objective is to improve situational awareness, prevent violence or illegal actions, and support camp administration in maintaining order. Stakeholders include camp authorities, law enforcement bodies, asylum seekers and oversight institutions. The CENTAUR system integrates drones, thermal cameras, motion sensors and perimeter monitoring tools to collect real-time environmental data. Video and sensor inputs are processed using automated analytics algorithms capable of detecting movement patterns and anomalies. Alerts are generated for authorities when predefined thresholds are triggered. The system is connected to central monitoring centres that coordinate response actions. The infrastructure has been implemented and deployed in several camps. While it enhances monitoring capacity and rapid response capabilities, it has raised concerns regarding transparency, proportionality and data protection, making governance and oversight central to its continued operation.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1118,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00924,EU_PSTW_202609_00924,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Frontex,Police Services,"Customs and border authorities require fast, reliable tools to detect illicit drugs, precursors and cutting agents during inspections, often under time pressure and with limited laboratory access. Existing methods can be slow, costly or prone to false positives, creating operational inefficiencies.",BorderSens - Border detection of illicit drugs and precursors by highly accurate electrosensors,Frontex; European Institution/Agency; Police Services; European Union,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Customs and border authorities require fast, reliable tools to detect illicit drugs, precursors and cutting agents during inspections, often under time pressure and with limited laboratory access. Existing methods can be slow, costly or prone to false positives, creating operational inefficiencies. The EU-funded BorderSens project aimed to address this challenge by developing a portable, low-cost and user-friendly device for on-site drug detection. Stakeholders included border guards, customs officers, forensic experts and research institutions. The objective was to enable accurate identification of a wide range of substances within seconds, supporting frontline decision-making. The solution combined electrochemical sensing with selective nano-Molecularly Imprinted Polymers (nanoMIPs) and advanced nanomaterials to capture and enrich chemical fingerprints. These signals were processed through analytical models to distinguish drugs, precursors and adulterants with high precision. The prototype targeted results within 45 seconds, with reduced rates of false positives and negatives. The project demonstrated strong potential for improving border inspection efficiency, lowering operational costs and enhancing reliability compared to traditional field tests, while supporting safer and more effective enforcement activities.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1120,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00925,EU_PSTW_202609_00925,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Portuguese Immigration and Border Service,Police Services,UNKNOWN,Project RAPID - Automatic Identification of Passengers Holding Traveling Documents,Portuguese Immigration and Border Service; Central-Government; Police Services; Portugal,"The system integrates electronic passport authentication, chip reading compliant with ICAO standards and biometric verification to automate border control procedures.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Portugal’s Immigration and Border Service sought to increase efficiency and security at Schengen external borders in response to rising passenger volumes and evolving document fraud risks. In 2019, EUR 4.2 million plus VAT was invested to upgrade Project RAPID and deploy automated eGates at Lisbon, Porto, Faro and Madeira airports. The system integrates electronic passport authentication, chip reading compliant with ICAO standards and biometric verification to automate border control procedures. It reads data stored in the passport chip via secure protocols, verifies document authenticity, and performs facial comparison between the stored image and a live capture at the gate. Biometric matching algorithms generate a similarity score that determines whether the gate opens automatically or refers the traveller to an officer. The system is connected to national and European border management databases for additional checks. Implementation increased passenger throughput, reduced waiting times and standardised verification procedures while maintaining security controls. Automation allowed border officers to focus on high-risk cases and improved the overall traveller experience through faster, self-service processing.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1122,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00927,EU_PSTW_202609_00927,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,UK Home Office,Police Services,UNKNOWN,EU Exit: ID document check - applying for settled status in the UK after Brexit,UK Home Office; Central-Government; Police Services; United Kingdom,"The application extracts biographical and biometric data from the chip using secure NFC communication, then performs facial matching between the chip image and the live capture.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Following the United Kingdom’s withdrawal from the European Union, millions of EU citizens needed to apply for settled status through a streamlined and scalable digital process. The Home Office introduced the “EU Exit: ID Document Check” smartphone application to enable remote identity verification and reduce reliance on in-person appointments. Applicants capture an image of their passport or identity card, scan the embedded NFC chip, record a facial video and photograph, and complete an online questionnaire. The application extracts biographical and biometric data from the chip using secure NFC communication, then performs facial matching between the chip image and the live capture. Where applicants provide a National Insurance number, automated data-matching routines cross-check residence evidence against HM Revenue and Customs and Department for Work and Pensions records through secure system integration. This reduces the need for additional documentation when records confirm eligibility. The service processed millions of applications, significantly accelerating decision times and lowering administrative costs compared with paper-based systems, while raising concerns about digital exclusion and error resolution mechanisms.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S3_DOCUMENT_INTELLIGENCE,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1128,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00928,EU_PSTW_202609_00928,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Dutch police,Police Services,The operational problem was to prioritise limited resources and identify cases requiring attention within the youth justice system.,Pro-Kid used for risk assessment by the Dutch police,Dutch police; Central-Government; Police Services; Netherlands,"It applies actuarial scoring techniques that assign weighted values to predefined variables, producing risk categories intended to indicate the likelihood of future police registration rather than proven criminal behaviour.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Since 2011, the Dutch police have used ProKid to support early identification of children and young people considered at risk of reoffending, informing preventive interventions by police and youth care services. The operational problem was to prioritise limited resources and identify cases requiring attention within the youth justice system. ProKid draws on police registration data, including criminal fact and criminal opinion databases, to generate structured risk classifications. It applies actuarial scoring techniques that assign weighted values to predefined variables, producing risk categories intended to indicate the likelihood of future police registration rather than proven criminal behaviour. The output can influence referrals to youth services and protective interventions, with significant implications for children and families. An evaluation by the Ministry of Security and Justice found that around one-third of assessed cases were misclassified due to data errors or inclusion of irrelevant incidents, raising concerns about reliability and proportionality. While the tool aimed to standardise screening and support preventive action, criticism has focused on data quality, transparency and the potential for unjustified stigmatisation.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1130,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00929,EU_PSTW_202609_00929,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Amsterdam,Police Services,The initiatives illustrate how data integration can support targeted crime prevention strategies while highlighting governance and rights-related challenges in automated profiling.,“Top 600” and “Top400” used for automated risk modelling,City of Amsterdam; Local Government; Police Services; Netherlands,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Amsterdam authorities introduced the Top 600 and later Top 400 programmes to reduce high-impact crime by targeting individuals assessed as posing significant risk. The Top 600 focuses on young adults with prior arrests or convictions for serious offences, while the Top 400 addresses minors under 16 to enable early preventive intervention. The programmes aggregate police, municipal and social service data to produce ranked lists based on predefined criteria such as arrest history, judicial records and, for minors, broader social indicators. Statistical scoring and profiling methods prioritise individuals for coordinated multi-agency action plans. Those listed may face intensified supervision, frequent checks and tailored enforcement or support measures. Authorities report reductions in repeat high-impact offences among targeted groups and improved coordination between agencies. However, civil society organisations have raised concerns about due process, transparency and the impact on employment or residence status. The initiatives illustrate how data integration can support targeted crime prevention strategies while highlighting governance and rights-related challenges in automated profiling.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1131,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00930,EU_PSTW_202609_00930,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Roermond police,Police Services,UNKNOWN,The Sensing Project for risk assessment and profiling by the Roermond police,Roermond police; Local Government; Police Services; Netherlands,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Roermond police developed the Sensing Project to address recurring pickpocketing and shoplifting in a shopping district, focusing on patterns described as “mobile banditry”. The operational objective was to identify vehicles and groups considered likely to commit offences and deploy patrols more efficiently. The locally developed system analyses travel-related variables, including vehicle type, number of passengers, routes taken, licence plate information and links to rental vehicles previously associated with criminal incidents. Rule-based scoring mechanisms combine these factors to produce a composite risk score. When predefined thresholds are exceeded, the system generates an alert and records vehicle details in police databases, influencing subsequent risk calculations. Officers retain discretion in responding to alerts. Critics argued that the approach risked discriminatory profiling and created feedback loops, as prior registration increased future risk scores regardless of new evidence. The project sought to improve situational awareness and allocate policing resources more effectively, but it raised significant legal and ethical concerns regarding data protection and equal treatment.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1132,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00931,EU_PSTW_202609_00931,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Ministry of Justice,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.","While Cassandra has streamlined report preparation and introduced greater standardisation, limited transparency exists regarding the underlying data, weighting logic and validation.",Cassandra – Automated risk assessment system used by the Ukraine Ministry of Justice,"Ministry of Justice; Central-Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Ukraine",UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Judicial systems often seek tools to support consistent and efficient pre-trial and pre-sentence decision-making, particularly in assessing the likelihood of re-offending. In Ukraine, the Ministry of Justice introduced Cassandra, an automated system used by probation services to support the preparation of reports for judges. Stakeholders include probation officers, judges, defendants and justice authorities. The objective is to provide a structured assessment of re-offending risk to inform sentencing decisions, such as custodial versus non-custodial measures. Probation officers input information about an individual offender through a structured questionnaire covering personal history, social factors and prior behaviour. The system assigns weighted scores to responses and aggregates them into a numerical risk estimate ranging from 0 to 97. These outputs are included in reports submitted to courts. While Cassandra has streamlined report preparation and introduced greater standardisation, limited transparency exists regarding the underlying data, weighting logic and validation. Concerns have been raised about accountability, explainability and potential bias. The case highlights both the efficiency gains of automated assessment tools and the importance of governance, oversight and safeguards in justice contexts.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1133,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00932,EU_PSTW_202609_00932,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Swiss Execution of Penal Sentences and Justice,Police Services,UNKNOWN,AI tool to predict recidivism in prisoners in Switzerland,Swiss Execution of Penal Sentences and Justice; Central-Government; Police Services; Switzerland,Swiss penal authorities implemented the Risk-Oriented Sanctioning framework to structure sentence execution and reduce reoffending through systematic case management. The tool processes criminal record data and predefined statistical risk factors using rule-based scoring and classification techniques.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Swiss penal authorities implemented the Risk-Oriented Sanctioning framework to structure sentence execution and reduce reoffending through systematic case management. The framework divides offender management into triage, assessment, planning and progress stages. During triage, the Fall-Screening-Tool classifies cases into categories A, B or C to determine the need for further professional assessment. The tool processes criminal record data and predefined statistical risk factors using rule-based scoring and classification techniques. Category C indicates potential violent or sexual delinquency risk, category B reflects general recidivism risk, and category A requires no additional assessment. The output guides allocation of resources and informs development of an offender’s problem profile for sentence planning. FaST does not replace expert evaluation and is used exclusively at the initial screening stage. The framework aims to standardise early risk identification, improve consistency across cantons and support evidence-based decision-making in corrections. It enhances transparency in triage decisions while leaving subsequent assessments to human professionals.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1137,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00933,EU_PSTW_202609_00933,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Metropolitan Police Service,Police Services,UNKNOWN,Gangs Violence Matrix (GVM) to predict crime in London,Metropolitan Police Service; Regional Government; Police Services; United Kingdom,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Urban law enforcement agencies seek to identify individuals at risk of involvement in serious violence to inform prevention and safeguarding strategies. Since 2012, the Metropolitan Police has used the Gangs Violence Matrix to risk-assess individuals linked to gang activity. Stakeholders include police services, local authorities, affected individuals and civil society organisations. The objective is to support targeted interventions by categorising individuals according to the level of harm they pose or risk they face. The system compiles police intelligence and recorded offences, applying a scoring framework to classify individuals as red, amber or green based on evidence of violence, weapons offences or gang association. Data are stored in a central database used to inform operational and safeguarding decisions. Expected impacts include focused allocation of policing resources, improved identification of high-risk individuals and enhanced coordination of violence prevention efforts; however, significant concerns have been raised regarding transparency, proportionality and potential discriminatory impacts.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,NOT_IN_USE,미사용,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1140,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00934,EU_PSTW_202609_00934,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Durham Constabulary,Police Services,UNKNOWN,Harm Assessment Risk Tool (HART) in Durham,Durham Constabulary; Local Government; Police Services; United Kingdom,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Preliminary research into a two-year pilot programme run by Durham Constabulary, found the Harm Assessment Risk Tool (Hart) was slightly better overall at predicting reoffending rates than human custody sergeants. As part of the study, overseen by Cambridge University affiliate Dr Geoffrey Barnes, both the machine-learning program and human custody officers assessed whether offenders brought into custody were at high, medium or low risk of reoffending. Hart successfully predicted that 89.8% of those who were actually brought back into custody for a serious offence within two years had been at high risk of reoffending, compared to an 81.2% success rate for custody officers. Of those predicted to be at low risk of reoffending, Hart got it right 78.8% of the time, compared to 66.5% accurate forecasting by humans. The study was based on individuals who were brought into custody in County Durham and Darlington between September 2016 and October 2017, then followed up whether they reoffended and returned to custody within two years.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,NOT_IN_USE,미사용,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1141,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00935,EU_PSTW_202609_00935,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,National Revenue Administration,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.",UNKNOWN,STIR - an algorithm to fight VAT fraud in Poland,"National Revenue Administration; Central-Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Poland",UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Poland introduced the STIR system to combat large-scale VAT carousel fraud and organised tax evasion that significantly reduced public revenues. Operated by the National Revenue Administration under the State Clearing Chamber, STIR collects and analyses financial transaction data transmitted by banks and credit institutions to identify suspicious economic activity and high-risk entrepreneurs. The objective is to provide tax authorities with early risk indicators enabling rapid intervention, including temporary account freezes, without prior notification to the bank or account holder. The system processes transaction patterns, account behaviour and links to jurisdictions associated with VAT fraud, applying undisclosed risk-scoring algorithms and automated decision-support mechanisms. Where risk thresholds are met, authorities may block an account for up to 72 hours, extendable to three months. Legal provisions prohibit disclosure of the algorithmic criteria, with criminal penalties for unauthorised access or publication. STIR has strengthened investigative capacity, accelerated fraud detection and enabled preventative asset freezes, contributing to improved VAT collection performance in Poland, though concerns have been raised regarding transparency, due process and proportionality safeguards.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1142,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00936,EU_PSTW_202609_00936,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Gladsaxe,Social protection n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,Gladsaxe Model - profiling families for early detection of vulnerable children,Municipality of Gladsaxe; Local Government; Social protection n.e.c.; Denmark,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Municipal child welfare authorities aim to identify children at risk of neglect or social vulnerability at an early stage to enable preventive intervention. The Gladsaxe municipality developed a points-based profiling system designed to aggregate administrative indicators such as parental unemployment, mental health status, missed medical appointments and family structure changes. The objective was to support social workers in prioritising cases requiring attention and to improve early intervention outcomes. The model combined data from municipal databases and applied statistical scoring techniques to calculate risk levels. Outputs generated alerts for caseworkers to review flagged households. The system was initially piloted but faced significant criticism regarding proportionality, fairness and data protection. After review by data protection authorities, permission to continue was denied and the system was discontinued. Although subsequent research projects explored similar concepts in academic contexts, the original Gladsaxe model was terminated and never moved into full operational deployment.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1143,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00937,EU_PSTW_202609_00937,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Ministry of Justice,Police Services,UNKNOWN,Offender Assessment System (OASys),Ministry of Justice; Central-Government; Police Services; United Kingdom,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Offender Assessment System was developed by the Ministry of Justice to standardise risk and needs assessment across probation services and prisons in England and Wales. Its purpose is to evaluate the likelihood of reoffending, assess risks of serious harm and identify criminogenic needs to inform sentencing, supervision and rehabilitation planning. Initially paper-based, OASys evolved into a digital platform incorporating structured assessment tools and data-driven scoring components managed in coordination with the Ministry of Justice Data Science Hub. Practitioners input information on criminal history, personal circumstances, behavioural factors and previous supervision outcomes. Embedded scoring matrices and statistical weightings generate structured risk classifications and support sentence planning, parole decisions and review processes. Assessments are conducted at pre-sentence stage, at the start of custody or community supervision, during reviews and at key decision points. OASys improves consistency, documentation quality and information sharing across agencies, supporting evidence-based offender management. However, scrutiny has focused on transparency of scoring logic and the implications of risk categorisation for liberty and progression within the justice system.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1146,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00938,EU_PSTW_202609_00938,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,UK Home Office & others,Police Services,UNKNOWN,National Data Analytics Solution (NDAS),UK Home Office & others; Central-Government; Police Services; United Kingdom,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The National Data Analytics Solution was developed by West Midlands Police in collaboration with the UK Home Office and other forces to enhance early identification of individuals considered at risk of serious violence. The operational objective was to support preventative policing by analysing patterns across intelligence and operational datasets. NDAS integrates police intelligence reports, custody records, stop and search data, drug offence information and event-based records into a unified analytical environment. Machine-learning classification models and predictive analytics techniques generate individual risk scores indicating the statistical likelihood of involvement in serious violence. These outputs are intended to inform safeguarding referrals and targeted interventions rather than direct enforcement decisions. The system enables cross-force data integration and structured behavioural analysis at scale. Authorities reported improved analytical capacity and more focused preventative strategies. Nevertheless, concerns have been raised regarding data quality, potential bias linked to stop and search disparities and the transparency of predictive modelling in policing contexts.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1148,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00939,EU_PSTW_202609_00939,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Ministry of Finance,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.","The proposal faced criticism from the French data protection authority regarding proportionality and privacy safeguards, but the Constitutional Council upheld its legality following parliamentary challenge.",Machine-learning to detect tax fraud in France,"Ministry of Finance; Central-Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; France",UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"To strengthen tax compliance and address undeclared income, France amended its 2020 Budget Law to permit the Ministry of Finance to collect publicly accessible data from social networks, online marketplaces and classified advertising platforms. The objective was to detect discrepancies between declared income and visible indicators of lifestyle or commercial activity. The tax authority deploys automated data-scraping tools and machine-learning classification systems to analyse publicly available posts, images and transaction listings, comparing patterns with tax return information stored in administrative databases. Anomaly detection techniques flag cases for further human investigation, rather than issuing automatic sanctions. The framework includes data retention limits and oversight mechanisms. The proposal faced criticism from the French data protection authority regarding proportionality and privacy safeguards, but the Constitutional Council upheld its legality following parliamentary challenge. The system enhances investigative efficiency and expands evidentiary sources for detecting hidden economic activity, while raising ongoing debate about surveillance boundaries and data protection compliance.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1153,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00940,EU_PSTW_202609_00940,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Copenhagen,Unemployment.,The operational challenge was to identify those at higher risk of long-term unemployment and tailor activation measures accordingly.,Profiling system for the unemployed,City of Copenhagen; Local Government; Unemployment.; Denmark,"Upon registration, a digital profile is automatically generated using variables such as age, education, employment history, occupation, health-related indicators and labour market background.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Austria introduced a nationwide profiling tool to support employment services in allocating assistance to newly unemployed individuals claiming benefits. The operational challenge was to identify those at higher risk of long-term unemployment and tailor activation measures accordingly. Upon registration, a digital profile is automatically generated using variables such as age, education, employment history, occupation, health-related indicators and labour market background. Statistical modelling techniques estimate the probability of prolonged unemployment and assign individuals to categories reflecting expected reintegration prospects. The output provides decision support to caseworkers in determining training, counselling or intensive support pathways; it does not automatically determine benefit eligibility or sanctions. Integration with national employment databases allows consistent application across regions. Authorities argued that the tool improves resource allocation and early intervention efficiency. However, public debate has focused on fairness, potential discrimination linked to origin or gender variables and the transparency of modelling assumptions in labour market governance.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1154,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00941,EU_PSTW_202609_00941,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Estonian Social Insurance Board (SKA),Family and children.,"Previously, parents were required to submit applications, creating administrative burden and risk of delayed payments.",Automatic proactive social service,Estonian Social Insurance Board (SKA); Central-Government; Family and children.; Estonia,The objective was to deliver benefits automatically using existing population registry data.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Estonia’s Social Insurance Board introduced an event-based proactive service in 2019 to simplify access to family benefits following the birth of a child. Previously, parents were required to submit applications, creating administrative burden and risk of delayed payments. The objective was to deliver benefits automatically using existing population registry data. Once a birth is registered and the child named, interoperable government databases exchange verified data through Estonia’s X-Road infrastructure. An automated rule-based system confirms eligibility conditions and triggers benefit activation without requiring a separate application. Parents receive electronic notification and, upon confirmation, payments commence. The solution, developed with Nortal, integrates civil registry, social insurance and payment systems, relying on deterministic decision rules rather than predictive modelling. The reform streamlined internal workflows, reduced processing time and minimised administrative effort for families and staff. It marked Estonia’s first fully event-based public service, reinforcing its digital-by-default governance model.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1155,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00942,EU_PSTW_202609_00942,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Ministry of Finance,Recreational and sporting services.,"Citizens often struggle to navigate complex public services during major life events, such as moving, retirement, or job loss, leading to unmet needs and administrative inefficiencies. AuroraAI addresses this challenge by rethinking service delivery around life events rather than institutional structures.",AuroraAI – Life-Event Management for Public Services,Ministry of Finance; Central-Government; Recreational and sporting services.; Finland,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Citizens often struggle to navigate complex public services during major life events, such as moving, retirement, or job loss, leading to unmet needs and administrative inefficiencies. AuroraAI addresses this challenge by rethinking service delivery around life events rather than institutional structures. Stakeholders include citizens, public authorities, and service providers across sectors. The objective is to proactively guide individuals towards relevant services based on their personal circumstances, reducing friction and improving outcomes. The solution integrates data from multiple public sector domains to identify patterns associated with life events and anticipate service needs. Personalised recommendations are generated to suggest appropriate public and private services at the right time. The system supports proactive outreach while respecting governance and oversight requirements. The initiative demonstrates improved accessibility of services, more coordinated public support, and enhanced user experience. It provides a vision for citizen-centric service ecosystems, where timely guidance and personalised pathways help individuals navigate life transitions more effectively.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1156,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00943,EU_PSTW_202609_00943,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Social Insurance Institution,Social protection n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,Automated benefit processes at the Social Insurance Institution of Finland,Social Insurance Institution; Central-Government; Social protection n.e.c.; Finland,"Kela has implemented automated decision processes to handle routine benefit cases where eligibility can be determined through clear statutory criteria and verified data. Automation applies rule-based logic embedded in information systems, using data from national registers and applicant submissions to determine entitlement, calculate benefit rates or adjust payments.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Kela has implemented automated decision processes to handle routine benefit cases where eligibility can be determined through clear statutory criteria and verified data. The operational objective is to improve processing speed and allocate staff resources to complex cases requiring individual assessment. Automation applies rule-based logic embedded in information systems, using data from national registers and applicant submissions to determine entitlement, calculate benefit rates or adjust payments. No machine-learning or statistical prediction models are used in decision-making. Automated decisions are issued only when conditions are unambiguous and are clearly labelled as such, including information sources and appeal instructions. Applications include student financial aid awards, benefit adjustments, government guarantees for student loans and certain cancellations based on updated information or non-response. The approach has accelerated processing times, improved consistency and enhanced service efficiency, enabling Kela to focus human expertise on advisory services and exceptional cases while maintaining legal accountability and transparency.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1160,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00944,EU_PSTW_202609_00944,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Agency for the digitalisation (Ministry of Finance),Social protection n.e.c.,"Welfare administrations responsible for pensions, housing, family, disability, and maternity-related benefits face a difficult balance between protecting public funds and respecting the rights of citizens, especially when fraud controls operate across very large populations.",System to control social welfare recipients,Agency for the digitalisation (Ministry of Finance); Central-Government; Social protection n.e.c.; Denmark,"In Denmark, Udbetaling Danmark uses automated controls to analyse whether payments are lawful by bringing together data from multiple public registers, including tax, housing, and civil records, as well as information relating to family members and housemates. At the same time, the use case illustrates the trade-offs associated with extensive public-sector data analysis, particularly where large volumes of personal data are processed in ways that raise concerns about privacy, proportionality, and discrimination.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Welfare administrations responsible for pensions, housing, family, disability, and maternity-related benefits face a difficult balance between protecting public funds and respecting the rights of citizens, especially when fraud controls operate across very large populations. In Denmark, Udbetaling Danmark uses automated controls to analyse whether payments are lawful by bringing together data from multiple public registers, including tax, housing, and civil records, as well as information relating to family members and housemates. The objective is to detect irregularities, identify potentially unlawful payments, and support centralised control of welfare disbursement at scale. The implementation combines broad data integration with evolving analytical criteria, including modular checks whose partial results are combined into an overall conclusion on whether further scrutiny is warranted. At a conceptual level, the system functions as a large-scale risk and compliance engine embedded within the administration of welfare benefits, supporting investigators and decision-makers by flagging cases of possible concern. The impact is greater control capacity, faster identification of suspicious cases, and improved scalability in payment oversight. At the same time, the use case illustrates the trade-offs associated with extensive public-sector data analysis, particularly where large volumes of personal data are processed in ways that raise concerns about privacy, proportionality, and discrimination.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1161,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00945,EU_PSTW_202609_00945,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Caixa Geral de Aposentações,Old age.,"This creates administrative burdens for both pensioners and public authorities, particularly for elderly individuals with mobility constraints. However, feedback also highlighted usability challenges for some elderly participants unfamiliar with digital tools.",Pensioners prove they are alive via face or voice recognition,Caixa Geral de Aposentações; Central-Government; Old age.; Portugal,"Caixa Geral de Aposentações launched a pilot to simplify this process using remote identity verification, initially in Macau. The system allows pensioners to submit proof of life remotely using biometric verification, based on facial or voice recognition through a digital interface.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Each year, thousands of Portuguese pensioners living abroad must prove they are alive to continue receiving benefits, traditionally requiring in-person visits to consulates or paper-based processes. This creates administrative burdens for both pensioners and public authorities, particularly for elderly individuals with mobility constraints. Caixa Geral de Aposentações launched a pilot to simplify this process using remote identity verification, initially in Macau. Stakeholders include pensioners abroad, welfare administrators and consular services. The objective was to reduce costs, improve convenience and maintain trust in pension disbursement. The system allows pensioners to submit proof of life remotely using biometric verification, based on facial or voice recognition through a digital interface. Captured biometric samples are matched against previously registered references to confirm identity, after which confirmation is transmitted securely to the pension authority. The pilot showed clear efficiency gains and was positively received by digitally confident users. However, feedback also highlighted usability challenges for some elderly participants unfamiliar with digital tools. The project demonstrated both the administrative benefits of remote verification and the need for inclusive design and alternative channels.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1162,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00946,EU_PSTW_202609_00946,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Royal Norwegian Ministry of Trade, Industry and Fisheries",Fuel and energy,UNKNOWN,PHM Hydro – Performance and Health Monitoring for Hydropower Plants,"Royal Norwegian Ministry of Trade, Industry and Fisheries; Central-Government; Fuel and energy; Norway","Algorithms are deployed within a cloud and fog computing architecture, enabling both local processing and centralised analysis.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Hydropower operators depend on the reliable performance of turbines, generators, and auxiliary equipment to ensure safe, continuous, and profitable electricity production. Unplanned downtime or undetected efficiency losses can lead to significant economic impacts and operational risks. The PHM Hydro initiative addresses the need for systematic, scalable monitoring of equipment condition and performance across multiple components and power plants. Stakeholders include hydropower plant operators, maintenance teams, and energy companies seeking to optimise asset management and extend equipment lifetime. The objective is to establish a unified architecture that supports early fault detection, performance optimisation, and predictive maintenance. The solution combines physics-based modelling with data-driven analytics to analyse sensor data from critical components. Algorithms are deployed within a cloud and fog computing architecture, enabling both local processing and centralised analysis. This flexible design supports scalability across plants and assets while integrating new analytical methods over time. The approach improves visibility into equipment health, reduces unplanned outages, and supports condition-based maintenance strategies. The project delivers increased operational efficiency, lower maintenance costs, and enhanced reliability of hydropower generation, contributing to a more resilient and sustainable energy system.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1163,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00947,EU_PSTW_202609_00947,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Akershus University Hospital,R&D health.,"Heart failure remains a major cause of morbidity and mortality, often diagnosed at a late stage when intervention options are limited.",Heart failure risk prediction by Ahus,Akershus University Hospital; Academic-Research; R&D health.; Norway,The objective is to strengthen preventive care and clinical decision-making using routinely collected medical data. The system is designed to analyse electrocardiogram (ECG) data alongside other clinical indicators using machine learning models trained on historical patient datasets.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Heart failure remains a major cause of morbidity and mortality, often diagnosed at a late stage when intervention options are limited. Akershus University Hospital (Ahus) aims to improve early detection by developing predictive tools that assist clinicians in identifying patients at elevated risk. The objective is to strengthen preventive care and clinical decision-making using routinely collected medical data. Stakeholders include cardiologists, general practitioners, patients and healthcare administrators. The system is designed to analyse electrocardiogram (ECG) data alongside other clinical indicators using machine learning models trained on historical patient datasets. Predictive algorithms generate risk scores that can support clinical assessments. The tool is developed within Norway’s regulatory sandbox framework to ensure compliance with data protection requirements. The project remains in development and testing phases, with evaluation occurring in controlled clinical environments. Expected impacts include earlier diagnosis, improved patient outcomes and more efficient allocation of healthcare resources.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1165,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00948,EU_PSTW_202609_00948,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Ministry of Transport,Transport,"Manual packing of luggage into trolleys is labour-intensive, ergonomically challenging and difficult to scale. Expected impacts include reduced manual labour, improved safety, more predictable operations and scalability to other airports, supporting long-term efficiency gains in baggage handling.",Robotised baggage handling (Roba) in airports project,Ministry of Transport; Central-Government; Transport; Norway,Results from the research phase are being implemented in a pilot facility.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Airports face increasing operational pressure to handle growing passenger volumes while maintaining safety, efficiency and cost control in baggage handling operations. Manual packing of luggage into trolleys is labour-intensive, ergonomically challenging and difficult to scale. Avinor initiated the RoBa (Robotised Baggage Handling) project to research and pilot automated methods for packing luggage and managing trolley transport at Oslo Gardermoen Airport. Stakeholders include airport operators, ground handling services, technology providers and passengers. The objective is to improve robustness, throughput and working conditions. The project explores robotic manipulation systems capable of handling luggage of varying sizes and weights, integrated with automated transport and intermediate storage systems. Sensor-based perception and control systems enable robots to place baggage efficiently into trolleys while adapting to operational variability. Results from the research phase are being implemented in a pilot facility. Expected impacts include reduced manual labour, improved safety, more predictable operations and scalability to other airports, supporting long-term efficiency gains in baggage handling.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1166,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00949,EU_PSTW_202609_00949,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Ministry of Transport,Transport,"Airport aprons are complex, high-risk environments where aircraft, vehicles, and personnel operate in close proximity, leading to frequent safety incidents and costly damage. Despite the scale of the risk, safety reporting in many airports remains manual, time-consuming, and prone to underreporting.",FLAIT – AI-Assisted Airport Safety Reporting through Human–AI Teaming,Ministry of Transport; Central-Government; Transport; Norway,"The system automates parts of the reporting workflow, structures incident information, and supports consistency, while keeping humans in control of validation and decision-making.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Airport aprons are complex, high-risk environments where aircraft, vehicles, and personnel operate in close proximity, leading to frequent safety incidents and costly damage. Despite the scale of the risk, safety reporting in many airports remains manual, time-consuming, and prone to underreporting. The FLAIT project addresses this challenge by supporting safety reporting through human–technology collaboration. Stakeholders include airport operators, ground handling staff, and safety managers responsible for reducing incidents and improving situational awareness. The objective is to increase the volume, quality, and timeliness of safety reports without adding burden to personnel. The solution introduces the FLAIT Assistant, a decision support system that supports apron staff in documenting incidents more efficiently. The system automates parts of the reporting workflow, structures incident information, and supports consistency, while keeping humans in control of validation and decision-making. By lowering reporting barriers, the approach encourages more comprehensive incident capture. The project improves safety data availability, strengthens risk analysis, and supports proactive safety management. It contributes to reduced ground damage, improved operational safety, and a stronger safety culture at airports through effective human–technology teaming.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1167,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00950,EU_PSTW_202609_00950,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Ministry of Transport and Communication,Transport,"Traditional manual inspections are labour-intensive, costly, and often require track closures, disrupting services and limiting inspection frequency. The IARI project addresses these challenges by modernising inspection processes to improve efficiency and coverage.",IARI - AI-based image recognition for railway infrastructure inspection,Ministry of Transport and Communication; Central-Government; Transport; Norway,"The objective is to automate selected inspection tasks while improving understanding of infrastructure condition. The solution uses image recognition techniques applied to data captured by cameras mounted on trains and drones, complemented by infrared and depth sensors.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Ensuring the safety and reliability of railway infrastructure requires frequent inspections of tracks, overhead lines, and supporting structures. Traditional manual inspections are labour-intensive, costly, and often require track closures, disrupting services and limiting inspection frequency. The IARI project addresses these challenges by modernising inspection processes to improve efficiency and coverage. Stakeholders include infrastructure managers, maintenance teams, and railway operators seeking safer and more cost-effective inspection methods. The objective is to automate selected inspection tasks while improving understanding of infrastructure condition. The solution uses image recognition techniques applied to data captured by cameras mounted on trains and drones, complemented by infrared and depth sensors. Algorithms are trained on datasets of known defects to identify faults such as wear, cracks, or misalignments. Image-based analysis is combined with classical methods to improve robustness and interpretability. The system supports continuous data collection without service disruption. The project reduces the need for manual inspections, improves safety for inspection personnel, and enables earlier detection of defects. It delivers cost savings, improved asset management, and more resilient railway operations through data-driven infrastructure monitoring.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1169,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00951,EU_PSTW_202609_00951,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Haukeland University Hospital (Helse Bergen),R&D health.,UNKNOWN,AI decision support in medical emergency calls,Haukeland University Hospital (Helse Bergen); Regional Government; R&D health.; Norway,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Emergency Medical Communication Centres in Norway must rapidly identify stroke cases during 113 calls in order to dispatch appropriate ambulances and alert hospital stroke teams, yet symptom descriptions are often inconsistent and assessments depend on dispatcher experience, creating risks of delayed diagnosis and treatment. Stakeholders include patients, call handlers, hospitals and regional health authorities, with the objective of improving early stroke detection before hospital admission and strengthening pre-hospital triage. The project combined structured data from the Norwegian Patient Register with historical emergency call recordings to train supervised predictive models capable of analysing speech patterns and reported symptoms. Natural language processing techniques and statistical classification models were validated on retrospective datasets and integrated into the AMK Bergen call-handling platform to generate real-time risk scores during live calls. The solution is being evaluated against existing triage procedures to measure improvements in diagnostic accuracy and response prioritisation. Expected results include faster dispatch decisions, more precise allocation of resources and improved patient outcomes, while supporting scalable, data-driven decision support across emergency services.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1174,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00952,EU_PSTW_202609_00952,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Haukeland University Hospital (Helse Bergen),R&D health.,UNKNOWN,AI for improved diagnostics in endometrial cancer,Haukeland University Hospital (Helse Bergen); Regional Government; R&D health.; Norway,"It contributes to earlier and more accurate diagnosis, improved treatment outcomes, and more efficient use of healthcare resources in gynaecological oncology.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Accurate diagnosis and personalised treatment of endometrial cancer require deeper integration of imaging, molecular, and clinical information. While medical imaging reveals structural and functional patterns associated with disease progression, these biomarkers are not yet fully embedded in clinical decision-making. This initiative addresses the need to translate imaging-derived insights into actionable diagnostic and therapeutic strategies. Stakeholders include clinicians, researchers, and patients seeking more precise diagnostics and tailored treatments. The objective is to improve diagnostic accuracy and identify molecular targets by linking imaging phenotypes to underlying disease mechanisms. The solution analyses imaging data to extract quantitative biomarkers associated with tumour characteristics and progression. These biomarkers are correlated with molecular and clinical data to support stratification of patients and inform treatment pathways. The approach supports integration into clinical workflows and research settings. The project improves understanding of disease heterogeneity, supports more informed treatment selection, and lays the groundwork for personalised oncology. It contributes to earlier and more accurate diagnosis, improved treatment outcomes, and more efficient use of healthcare resources in gynaecological oncology.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1175,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00953,EU_PSTW_202609_00953,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Central Norway Regional Health Authority,R&D health.,"Sepsis is a life-threatening condition requiring rapid detection and intervention, yet early signs are often subtle and distributed across unstructured clinical notes and structured patient data. Clinicians face challenges in continuously monitoring complex patient trajectories in real time.",CoSeM - Computational sepsis mining and modelling,Central Norway Regional Health Authority; Regional Government; R&D health.; Norway,"CoSeM delivers improved patient safety, better clinical outcomes, and enhanced use of healthcare data for real-time decision support.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Sepsis is a life-threatening condition requiring rapid detection and intervention, yet early signs are often subtle and distributed across unstructured clinical notes and structured patient data. Clinicians face challenges in continuously monitoring complex patient trajectories in real time. The CoSeM project addresses this by enhancing clinical decision support for infection and sepsis management. Stakeholders include healthcare professionals, hospitals, and patients in acute care settings. The objective is to support early detection, personalised risk assessment, and timely intervention. The solution integrates structured clinical data with temporal interpretation of free-text clinical notes to reconstruct individual patient histories. These data feed models that monitor infection progression and sepsis risk in real time. Outputs are presented through a clinical dashboard that extends existing electronic health records with process monitoring and alerts. The project improves situational awareness, supports individualised care, and reduces delayed interventions. Indirectly, it contributes to more appropriate treatment decisions and reduced antimicrobial resistance. CoSeM delivers improved patient safety, better clinical outcomes, and enhanced use of healthcare data for real-time decision support.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F04_UNSTRUCTURED_INFORMATION_COMPLEXITY,S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1178,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00954,EU_PSTW_202609_00954,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Central Norway Regional Health Authority,R&D health.,"Limited visibility and anatomical complexity result in low diagnostic success rates for non-visible tumours, increasing the need for repeat procedures or more invasive diagnostics. The Fraxinus project addresses this challenge by improving navigation support during bronchoscopy.","Fraxinus - An open source, software only, navigation system for bronchoscopy",Central Norway Regional Health Authority; Regional Government; R&D health.; Norway,"The solution provides a software-only, open-source navigation system that guides clinicians through the airway tree using preoperative imaging and real-time procedural data. It supports wider adoption of navigated bronchoscopy, leading to earlier diagnosis, improved patient outcomes, and more efficient use of clinical resources.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Bronchoscopy is a critical procedure for diagnosing lung cancer, yet navigating the complex airway structure to reach small peripheral lesions remains challenging. Limited visibility and anatomical complexity result in low diagnostic success rates for non-visible tumours, increasing the need for repeat procedures or more invasive diagnostics. The Fraxinus project addresses this challenge by improving navigation support during bronchoscopy. Stakeholders include pulmonologists, hospitals, and patients requiring accurate and minimally invasive diagnostics. The objective is to increase biopsy success rates and reduce procedure complexity. The solution provides a software-only, open-source navigation system that guides clinicians through the airway tree using preoperative imaging and real-time procedural data. By correlating planned pathways with live bronchoscope positioning, the system supports more precise targeting of lesions. The approach integrates into existing clinical workflows without requiring proprietary hardware. Fraxinus improves diagnostic accuracy, reduces procedure time, and increases confidence in sampling difficult-to-reach lesions. It supports wider adoption of navigated bronchoscopy, leading to earlier diagnosis, improved patient outcomes, and more efficient use of clinical resources.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1179,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00955,EU_PSTW_202609_00955,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Central Norway Regional Health Authority,R&D health.,"Clinicians face challenges in providing consistent, evidence-based care tailored to each patient’s evolving needs, particularly across prevention, diagnosis, treatment, and follow-up. The expected impact includes improved diagnostic accuracy, more targeted interventions, better continuity of care, and reduced burden on families and services.",IDDEAS - Individual Digital Decision Assist System for Child and Adolescent Mental Health,Central Norway Regional Health Authority; Regional Government; R&D health.; Norway,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Children and adolescents with attention deficit hyperactivity disorder represent a significant proportion of mental health cases in Norway, with long-term consequences if diagnosis and treatment are delayed or inadequate. Clinicians face challenges in providing consistent, evidence-based care tailored to each patient’s evolving needs, particularly across prevention, diagnosis, treatment, and follow-up. The IDDEAS project addresses this gap by supporting specialist healthcare services with structured, personalised decision support. Stakeholders include healthcare professionals, patients, families, and the wider health system. The objective is to improve clinical outcomes and service quality through early intervention, continuous monitoring, and optimised treatment pathways. The solution is a knowledge-based decision support system that adapts clinical data, guidelines, and patient-reported information to individual needs. It provides clinicians with evidence-based recommendations and supports personalised assessment and follow-up workflows. The system is designed to integrate into existing healthcare processes and support prevention and early diagnosis as well as long-term management. The expected impact includes improved diagnostic accuracy, more targeted interventions, better continuity of care, and reduced burden on families and services. IDDEAS contributes to more effective, equitable, and sustainable mental healthcare for young people.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1180,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00956,EU_PSTW_202609_00956,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Central Norway Regional Health Authority,R&D health.,"Manual interpretation of thoracic CT scans is complex and time-consuming, particularly in the mediastinal region where structures are densely packed. The objective is to improve detection and localisation of potentially malignant lymph nodes while reducing manual workload.",Segmenting and characterising lymph nodes from thoracic CT scans using machine learning,Central Norway Regional Health Authority; Regional Government; R&D health.; Norway,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Accurate staging of lung cancer depends on reliably identifying whether the disease has spread to nearby lymph nodes and understanding their anatomical relationships. Manual interpretation of thoracic CT scans is complex and time-consuming, particularly in the mediastinal region where structures are densely packed. This project addresses the need for precise, reproducible tools to support clinicians in cancer diagnosis and treatment planning. Stakeholders include radiologists, oncologists, and patients requiring accurate assessment. The objective is to improve detection and localisation of potentially malignant lymph nodes while reducing manual workload. The solution introduces a newly annotated dataset covering fifteen mediastinal anatomical structures and lymph nodes of varying sizes. A two-dimensional semantic segmentation and instance detection pipeline is developed to automatically identify and delineate these structures in CT images. The approach enables clear spatial understanding of lymph nodes in relation to airways and surrounding anatomy. The results improve diagnostic confidence, support more accurate staging, and facilitate treatment planning. By enhancing consistency and efficiency, the project contributes to better clinical decision-making and more personalised lung cancer care.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1181,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00957,EU_PSTW_202609_00957,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Central Norway Regional Health Authority,R&D health.,"Radiotherapy planning requires precise delineation of tumours and surrounding organs at risk, a process that traditionally involves extensive manual work by oncologists. The objective is to reduce manual workload and enable clinicians to focus on treatment quality and patient care.",AI for cancer treatment,Central Norway Regional Health Authority; Regional Government; R&D health.; Norway,This use case addresses the need to streamline treatment planning while maintaining accuracy and safety. The solution automates the identification and outlining of organs such as the heart and lungs within radiotherapy planning systems.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Radiotherapy planning requires precise delineation of tumours and surrounding organs at risk, a process that traditionally involves extensive manual work by oncologists. Drawing anatomical structures across hundreds of CT slices is time-consuming and can limit the time available for clinical decision-making and patient interaction. This use case addresses the need to streamline treatment planning while maintaining accuracy and safety. Stakeholders include oncologists, hospitals, and cancer patients. The objective is to reduce manual workload and enable clinicians to focus on treatment quality and patient care. The solution automates the identification and outlining of organs such as the heart and lungs within radiotherapy planning systems. Automated segmentation supports faster preparation of treatment plans and reduces variability between clinicians. The impact includes significant time savings, increased capacity to treat more patients, and improved consistency in treatment planning. Clinicians can dedicate more time to evaluating individual cases and refining therapeutic strategies. The initiative enhances efficiency in oncology departments and supports high-quality, patient-centred cancer treatment.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1182,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00958,EU_PSTW_202609_00958,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Central Norway Regional Health Authority,R&D health.,UNKNOWN,AI in image-guided neurosurgery,Central Norway Regional Health Authority; Regional Government; R&D health.; Norway,"It contributes to more informed treatment choices, improved patient outcomes, and better use of clinical data in neurosurgical practice.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Brain tumour treatment requires careful consideration of tumour characteristics, progression, and response to therapy, yet predicting outcomes remains complex. Clinicians must interpret large volumes of imaging and clinical data to guide personalised treatment decisions. This project addresses the need to integrate imaging biomarkers with clinical knowledge to support prognosis and therapy planning. Stakeholders include neurosurgeons, oncologists, researchers, and patients. The objective is to improve understanding of disease progression and enable more personalised treatment strategies. The solution develops predictive models trained on imaging, clinical, and outcome data from the Central Norwegian Brain Tumour Registry and Biobank, complemented by national and international datasets. Automated image analysis extracts relevant features, which are combined with historical patient data to predict survival and treatment response. The project improves insight into tumour behaviour, supports individualised decision-making, and advances research into personalised neuro-oncology. It contributes to more informed treatment choices, improved patient outcomes, and better use of clinical data in neurosurgical practice.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1183,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00959,EU_PSTW_202609_00959,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Northern Norway Regional Health Authority,R&D health.,"Chronic pain affects a diverse patient population with varying causes, responses to treatment, and side effects, making it difficult to identify optimal therapies.",Personalised decision support for patients with chronic pain,Northern Norway Regional Health Authority; Regional Government; R&D health.; Norway,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Chronic pain affects a diverse patient population with varying causes, responses to treatment, and side effects, making it difficult to identify optimal therapies. Existing decision support tools are largely generic and fail to account for individual patient differences. This project addresses the need for personalised, evidence-based treatment guidance for chronic pain management. Stakeholders include patients, clinicians, and healthcare institutions. The objective is to support shared decision-making by providing tailored treatment options with transparent probabilities of outcomes and side effects. The solution collects patient-reported data via a mobile application and combines it with electronic health record data from multiple institutions. Privacy is ensured through secret sharing and distributed computing techniques, allowing data to contribute to analysis without being centrally shared. Personalised decision trees are generated in real time to support clinical consultations. The impact includes more informed treatment choices, improved patient engagement, and better alignment between therapies and individual needs. The project supports more effective and patient-centred chronic pain management.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1184,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00960,EU_PSTW_202609_00960,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Northern Norway Regional Health Authority,R&D health.,"Clinicians face time constraints and administrative burden, which can compromise coding quality. The ClinCode project addresses this challenge by supporting clinicians with computer-assisted coding.",ClinCode: Computerised clinical ICD-10 coding,Northern Norway Regional Health Authority; Regional Government; R&D health.; Norway,"Using natural language processing and deep learning techniques, the system suggests relevant ICD-10 codes during clinical documentation. ClinCode enhances efficiency, reduces administrative burden, and supports more reliable use of clinical data across healthcare systems.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Accurate ICD-10 coding is essential for clinical documentation, billing, and healthcare planning, yet the complexity of the coding system often leads to incomplete or incorrect entries. Clinicians face time constraints and administrative burden, which can compromise coding quality. The ClinCode project addresses this challenge by supporting clinicians with computer-assisted coding. Stakeholders include healthcare professionals, hospital administrators, and health authorities. The objective is to improve coding accuracy and completeness without increasing clinicians’ workload. The solution analyses existing electronic patient records, including free-text notes and structured data, to learn coding patterns from previously coded cases. Using natural language processing and deep learning techniques, the system suggests relevant ICD-10 codes during clinical documentation. The impact includes higher coding quality, improved billing accuracy, and better data for planning and research. ClinCode enhances efficiency, reduces administrative burden, and supports more reliable use of clinical data across healthcare systems.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1185,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00961,EU_PSTW_202609_00961,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Northern Norway Regional Health Authority,R&D health.,"In Northern Norway, healthcare providers face the challenge of coordinating hospital and municipal services for complex patient groups with diverse and evolving needs.",IM-PACT - Identification of multimorbid patients with modifiable risk profiles using artificial intelligence,Northern Norway Regional Health Authority; Regional Government; R&D health.; Norway,"Patients with multiple chronic conditions often experience fragmented care, delayed interventions, and limited involvement in decision-making, leading to poorer outcomes and inefficient use of health resources.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Patients with multiple chronic conditions often experience fragmented care, delayed interventions, and limited involvement in decision-making, leading to poorer outcomes and inefficient use of health resources. In Northern Norway, healthcare providers face the challenge of coordinating hospital and municipal services for complex patient groups with diverse and evolving needs. The IM-PACT project addresses this gap by supporting a patient-centred, integrated care model that identifies individuals with multimorbidity and modifiable risk profiles. Stakeholders include patients, carers, clinicians, and healthcare organisations involved in the PAtient Centered-Team (PACT) model. The objective is to enable proactive, timely, and personalised care by identifying patients who can benefit most from targeted interventions. The solution analyses large-scale datasets from hospitals and municipal care services to identify patterns of risk and opportunity for intervention. Data-driven models support stratification of patients and provide insights to multidisciplinary care teams, while incorporating patient and carer feedback into care planning. The project improves coordination across care levels, supports shared decision-making, and enables earlier, more tailored interventions. IM-PACT contributes to improved clinical outcomes, better patient experiences, and more sustainable delivery of integrated healthcare services.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1186,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00962,EU_PSTW_202609_00962,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Northern Norway Regional Health Authority,R&D health.,"The main goal is to develop an AI-based tool that can indicate the probability of a postoperative wound infection occurring in the individual patient, so that risk-reducing measures can be taken and the burden of disease for the patient is reduced.",Predicting the risk of postoperative wound infections with AI,Northern Norway Regional Health Authority; Regional Government; R&D health.; Norway,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The main goal is to develop an AI-based tool that can indicate the probability of a postoperative wound infection occurring in the individual patient, so that risk-reducing measures can be taken and the burden of disease for the patient is reduced. The project's team will evaluate the validity of four non-AI-based risk tools to see which best predict the risk of postoperative wound infection. The variables, such as previous illnesses and age, will be used to develop algorithms for an AI-based risk tool, which we assume will predict the risk of postoperative wound infection better than the non-AI-based risk tools.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1189,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00963,EU_PSTW_202609_00963,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Northern Norway Regional Health Authority,R&D health.,"Surgeons and oncologists face difficult decisions about how much tissue to remove and how to plan subsequent treatment, given the risks of neurological damage.",Prediction of relapse in glioblastoma,Northern Norway Regional Health Authority; Regional Government; R&D health.; Norway,The objective is to identify areas at high risk of tumour regrowth using post-operative imaging.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Glioblastoma is an aggressive brain tumour with a poor prognosis, and recurrence after surgery is common, often occurring in tissue surrounding the original tumour site. Surgeons and oncologists face difficult decisions about how much tissue to remove and how to plan subsequent treatment, given the risks of neurological damage. This project addresses the need for better tools to anticipate tumour relapse and guide personalised treatment strategies. Stakeholders include neurosurgeons, oncologists, radiologists, and patients with glioblastoma. The objective is to identify areas at high risk of tumour regrowth using post-operative imaging. The solution analyses magnetic resonance and positron emission tomography images of peripheral brain regions surrounding the resection site. Predictive models identify patterns associated with future tumour formation, enabling risk mapping of surrounding tissue. The results support more informed surgical planning and treatment decisions, potentially improving local tumour control. The project contributes to personalised neuro-oncology, with the potential to improve survival outcomes and optimise treatment strategies for patients with glioblastoma.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1190,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00964,EU_PSTW_202609_00964,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,National Center for e-Health Research,R&D health.,"Preventing chronic diseases requires early identification of risk factors and sustained support for lifestyle change, yet individuals often lack timely, personalised feedback about their health risks. The WARIFA project addresses this challenge by empowering citizens and patients with tools for early risk detection and prevention.",WARIFA - Watching the risk factors to prevent chronic conditions,National Center for e-Health Research; Academic-Research; R&D health.; European Union,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Preventing chronic diseases requires early identification of risk factors and sustained support for lifestyle change, yet individuals often lack timely, personalised feedback about their health risks. The WARIFA project addresses this challenge by empowering citizens and patients with tools for early risk detection and prevention. Stakeholders include individuals, healthcare providers, and public health authorities across Europe. The objective is to support healthier lifestyles and early intervention through personalised risk notification and guidance. The solution combines user-generated data with additional health-related information to assess individual risk profiles. A mobile application delivers tailored risk alerts and personalised recommendations for lifestyle changes and health education, aligned with each individual’s risk level. Users at increased risk are encouraged to engage with healthcare services for further assessment. The project motivates preventive behaviour, supports early detection of chronic conditions, and reduces long-term disease burden. WARIFA demonstrates how digital prevention tools can strengthen public health, improve individual awareness, and support more sustainable healthcare systems.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1191,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00965,EU_PSTW_202609_00965,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,South-Eastern Norway Regional Health Authority,R&D health.,The approach supports consistent documentation and reduces administrative burden.,Phenotyping for Genetic Analysis Requisitioning,South-Eastern Norway Regional Health Authority; Regional Government; R&D health.; Norway,This use case addresses the need to simplify and improve genetic test requests while increasing diagnostic precision. It contributes to improved patient outcomes and more efficient use of genetic testing resources.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Accurate requisitioning of genetic tests for hereditary conditions depends on clear and structured descriptions of patient phenotypes, yet clinicians often document symptoms in free text, making the process time-consuming and error-prone. This use case addresses the need to simplify and improve genetic test requests while increasing diagnostic precision. Stakeholders include clinicians, genetic laboratories, and patients undergoing genetic evaluation. The objective is to support faster, more accurate selection of genetic analyses. The solution interprets clinical text and converts it into structured phenotype descriptions aligned with the Human Phenotype Ontology. By assisting clinicians in selecting appropriate phenotype terms during requisition, the system improves form completion and data quality. The approach supports consistent documentation and reduces administrative burden. Developed as a proposal for a national cloud-based service, the solution enhances diagnostic workflows, increases the likelihood of selecting the correct genetic test, and accelerates time to diagnosis. It contributes to improved patient outcomes and more efficient use of genetic testing resources.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1193,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00966,EU_PSTW_202609_00966,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Western Norway Regional Health Authority,R&D health.,"The incidence of malignant melanoma has risen sharply in recent decades, placing growing pressure on pathology departments to examine increasing numbers of skin biopsies with limited specialist resources. Traditional diagnosis relies on expert pathologists manually reviewing whole-slide histological images to identify subtle morphological features, a process that is time-consuming and subject to inter-observer variability.",Automatic Classification of Skin Lesions in Digital Pathology,Western Norway Regional Health Authority; Regional Government; R&D health.; Norway,"This use case, developed within the Western Norway Health Region’s centre for applied digitisation, aims to support pathologists in delivering faster, more consistent, and more accurate diagnoses. Digital slides are processed using image analysis techniques to highlight patterns associated with malignancy and prioritise cases for expert review.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The incidence of malignant melanoma has risen sharply in recent decades, placing growing pressure on pathology departments to examine increasing numbers of skin biopsies with limited specialist resources. Traditional diagnosis relies on expert pathologists manually reviewing whole-slide histological images to identify subtle morphological features, a process that is time-consuming and subject to inter-observer variability. The central challenge is efficiently identifying rare malignant melanomas among a large volume of benign or less aggressive lesions. This use case, developed within the Western Norway Health Region’s centre for applied digitisation, aims to support pathologists in delivering faster, more consistent, and more accurate diagnoses. The solution introduces a computer-aided diagnostic system for whole-slide images that automatically analyses tissue morphology and cytology to assist in lesion classification. Digital slides are processed using image analysis techniques to highlight patterns associated with malignancy and prioritise cases for expert review. The system is designed to integrate into existing pathology workflows. The expected impact includes reduced examination time per case, lower diagnostic variability, and improved detection accuracy for malignant melanoma. This supports better patient outcomes, improved use of specialist expertise, and more sustainable pathology services under increasing diagnostic demand.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1195,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00967,EU_PSTW_202609_00967,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Western Norway Regional Health Authority,R&D health.,"Digital pathology offers major opportunities to improve diagnostic quality and accessibility, yet widespread adoption is limited by the complexity of image interpretation and the need for scalable infrastructure. The CLARIFY programme addresses these challenges by bridging engineering and medicine to advance automated digital diagnostics in pathology.",CLARIFY – Cloud Artificial Intelligence for Pathology,Western Norway Regional Health Authority; Regional Government; R&D health.; European Union,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Digital pathology offers major opportunities to improve diagnostic quality and accessibility, yet widespread adoption is limited by the complexity of image interpretation and the need for scalable infrastructure. The CLARIFY programme addresses these challenges by bridging engineering and medicine to advance automated digital diagnostics in pathology. The initiative brings together academic, clinical, and industrial partners to strengthen expertise at the intersection of clinical pathology, cloud computing, and advanced analytics. Its objective is to create a robust, automated digital diagnostic environment that supports pathologists in interpreting whole-slide images regardless of location. The solution focuses on developing cloud-based platforms and algorithms that enable large-scale storage, processing, and analysis of high-resolution pathology images. Automated analysis supports tasks such as tissue characterisation and diagnostic decision support, while cloud-oriented architectures ensure accessibility, scalability, and collaboration across institutions. Alongside technical development, the programme trains early-stage researchers to ensure long-term capacity building. The impact includes improved diagnostic efficiency, wider access to specialist expertise, and accelerated adoption of digital pathology. CLARIFY contributes to more consistent, scalable, and accessible pathology services, while strengthening Europe’s research and skills base in digital health technologies.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1196,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00968,EU_PSTW_202609_00968,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Western Norway Regional Health Authority,R&D health.,"Markers such as Ki-67 and mitotic counts are widely used but lack standardised, reproducible assessment across laboratories, leading to potential over- or undertreatment. Integration into pathology workflows reduces manual counting and variability.",Automated Measurement of Proliferation in Breast Cancer,Western Norway Regional Health Authority; Regional Government; R&D health.; Norway,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Accurate assessment of tumour proliferation is critical for prognosis and treatment decisions in breast cancer, yet current methods are either subjective or labour-intensive. Markers such as Ki-67 and mitotic counts are widely used but lack standardised, reproducible assessment across laboratories, leading to potential over- or undertreatment. This project addresses the need for objective, validated, and efficient measurement of proliferation indicators in breast cancer pathology. Stakeholders include pathologists, oncologists, and patients whose treatment decisions depend on reliable prognostic information. The objective is to improve consistency and accuracy in assessing proliferation indices such as the Mitotic Activity Index, Ki-67, and PHH3. The solution develops and validates a digital image analysis system that automatically identifies and quantifies relevant cellular features in histological slides. By training models on annotated data, the system supports standardised calculations aligned with clinical guidelines. Integration into pathology workflows reduces manual counting and variability. The project improves diagnostic precision, reduces workload for pathologists, and supports more informed therapeutic decisions. It contributes to higher-quality breast cancer care and more consistent application of prognostic biomarkers in clinical practice.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1197,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00969,EU_PSTW_202609_00969,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Western Norway Regional Health Authority,R&D health.,"Although quantitative morphometric methods such as the D-score provide reproducible risk assessment, their adoption has been limited due to cost and time constraints, leading many laboratories to rely on less precise diagnostic criteria.",Automated Quantitative Analysis of Endometrioid Intraepithelial Neoplasia (EIN),Western Norway Regional Health Authority; Regional Government; R&D health.; Norway,"The objective is to enable widespread use of quantitative, evidence-based EIN scoring.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Endometrioid intraepithelial neoplasia is a premalignant condition with a significant risk of progression to endometrial cancer, making accurate diagnosis and risk stratification increasingly important. Although quantitative morphometric methods such as the D-score provide reproducible risk assessment, their adoption has been limited due to cost and time constraints, leading many laboratories to rely on less precise diagnostic criteria. This project addresses the need for faster and more standardised evaluation of EIN. Stakeholders include pathologists and clinicians managing patients at risk of endometrial cancer. The objective is to enable widespread use of quantitative, evidence-based EIN scoring. The solution develops an automated application that performs morphometric analysis on digital pathology images to calculate EIN scores rapidly and reproducibly. The system standardises assessment across laboratories and aligns diagnostics with contemporary classification systems. The impact includes reduced analysis time, improved diagnostic consistency, and better identification of high-risk patients. By supporting accurate and efficient risk assessment, the project enhances clinical decision-making, facilitates earlier intervention, and contributes to improved prevention of endometrial cancer.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1198,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00970,EU_PSTW_202609_00970,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Western Norway Regional Health Authority,R&D health.,Inefficient workflows risk longer turnaround times and increased strain on personnel.,Pathology services in the Western Norway Health Region – a center for applied digitization: Optimization of the pathology workflow,Western Norway Regional Health Authority; Regional Government; R&D health.; Norway,"This use case addresses the need for systematic optimisation of pathology operations across the Western Norway Health Region. The solution leverages detailed operational data from the Unilab-700 system, which captures granular information about each step in the pathology process.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Pathology departments face growing workloads due to increasing sample volumes and analytical complexity, while staffing levels remain relatively stable. Inefficient workflows risk longer turnaround times and increased strain on personnel. This use case addresses the need for systematic optimisation of pathology operations across the Western Norway Health Region. Stakeholders include pathology managers, laboratory staff, and healthcare providers dependent on timely diagnostic results. The objective is to improve efficiency and resilience by optimising resource allocation and workflow planning. The solution leverages detailed operational data from the Unilab-700 system, which captures granular information about each step in the pathology process. An optimisation model is developed to generate both long-term capacity plans and short-term, adaptive schedules for daily operations. The system supports scenario analysis and informed decision-making across departments. The impact includes improved turnaround times, better utilisation of staff and equipment, and increased transparency in workload management. This initiative supports sustainable pathology services by enabling data-driven operational planning and continuous improvement.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1200,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00971,EU_PSTW_202609_00971,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Western Norway Regional Health Authority,R&D health.,"Ki-67 is a critical biomarker for grading and treatment planning in neuroendocrine tumours of the gastrointestinal tract, yet accurate assessment requires counting a large number of cells, making manual evaluation impractical in routine settings. The impact includes improved grading accuracy, reduced observer variability, and lower manual workload.",Ki-67 in neuroendocrine tumours of the gastrointestinal tract,Western Norway Regional Health Authority; Regional Government; R&D health.; Norway,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Ki-67 is a critical biomarker for grading and treatment planning in neuroendocrine tumours of the gastrointestinal tract, yet accurate assessment requires counting a large number of cells, making manual evaluation impractical in routine settings. As a result, visual estimates or reduced counts are often used, compromising precision and reproducibility. This project addresses the need for accurate, standardised Ki-67 measurement in gastrointestinal neuroendocrine tumours. Stakeholders include pathologists, oncologists, and patients whose treatment decisions depend on reliable tumour grading. The objective is to improve accuracy and consistency in Ki-67 index calculation. The solution develops a digital image analysis system that automatically identifies tumour cells and calculates the Ki-67 index based on large cell populations in histological slides. The system is trained and validated on representative datasets and designed for integration into clinical workflows. The impact includes improved grading accuracy, reduced observer variability, and lower manual workload. This supports better treatment stratification, more reliable prognostic assessment, and improved quality of care for patients with neuroendocrine tumours.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1201,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00972,EU_PSTW_202609_00972,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Cancer Registry Norway,R&D health.,UNKNOWN,MIM - Machine learning in the Mammography programme,Cancer Registry Norway; Regional Government; R&D health.; Norway,"The expected impact includes reduced reading workload, improved use of specialist expertise, and increased screening capacity.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Breast cancer screening programmes generate very large volumes of mammograms, the majority of which show no signs of disease, yet all images must be carefully reviewed by specialists. This creates significant workload pressure and limits the capacity of screening services. The MIM project addresses the need to improve efficiency and effectiveness in mammography screening while maintaining high diagnostic quality. Stakeholders include radiologists, screening programme operators, and women participating in national screening initiatives. The objective is to support early detection of breast cancer by enabling more efficient triage of screening images and allowing clinicians to focus attention on higher-risk cases. The solution develops deep learning models trained on large datasets combining mammograms, screening metadata, and confirmed breast cancer diagnoses. By learning complex visual patterns associated with disease and normal findings, the system can identify mammograms that are highly likely to be free of cancer. These predictions can be used to prioritise or streamline review processes within screening workflows. The expected impact includes reduced reading workload, improved use of specialist expertise, and increased screening capacity. Ultimately, the project supports more sustainable screening services while maintaining early detection and patient safety.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1205,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00973,EU_PSTW_202609_00973,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Norwegian Environmental Agency,Environmental protection n.e.c.,"The solution improves monitoring efficiency, enables early identification of encroachments and strengthens evidence-based environmental governance while reducing reliance on manual inspection.",Naturinngrep i inngrepsfri natur,Norwegian Environmental Agency; Central-Government; Environmental protection n.e.c.; Norway,"To support systematic surveillance, the Norwegian Computing Center developed an automated method to identify construction and forest roads using Sentinel-2 satellite imagery with 10-metre spatial resolution. The approach applies a deep convolutional neural network based on the U-Net architecture, trained on labelled examples from wind power development sites and known road datasets.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Norwegian Environment Agency is responsible for maintaining a national map of intervention-free nature, defined as areas located at least one kilometre from the nearest technical infrastructure or human intervention. Monitoring these areas is challenging due to Norway’s vast territory and the need to detect new roads or construction linked to energy and forestry developments. To support systematic surveillance, the Norwegian Computing Center developed an automated method to identify construction and forest roads using Sentinel-2 satellite imagery with 10-metre spatial resolution. The approach applies a deep convolutional neural network based on the U-Net architecture, trained on labelled examples from wind power development sites and known road datasets. The model performs pixel-level segmentation to distinguish road structures from surrounding terrain. Testing in Åfjord municipality successfully identified all wind power development areas, several of which reduced intervention-free zones. Additional training enabled detection of an illegally constructed road in Melhus. The solution improves monitoring efficiency, enables early identification of encroachments and strengthens evidence-based environmental governance while reducing reliance on manual inspection.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1208,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00974,EU_PSTW_202609_00974,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,NAV IT,Social protection n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,Chatbot Frida,NAV IT; Central-Government; Social protection n.e.c.; Norway,It uses natural language processing models trained on historical enquiry data and curated question–answer pairs developed by subject matter experts.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"NAV introduced Chatbot Frida to improve responsiveness to citizen enquiries and reduce pressure on call centres and service desks. The objective was to provide continuous digital assistance for frequently asked questions related to welfare benefits, employment services and administrative procedures. The chatbot, delivered by boost.ai, is integrated into NAV’s digital service platform and website. It uses natural language processing models trained on historical enquiry data and curated question–answer pairs developed by subject matter experts. The system classifies user intent, retrieves relevant information from knowledge bases and guides users through structured dialogue flows. Escalation mechanisms redirect complex cases to human advisers when required. Frida operates continuously, handling large volumes of routine enquiries simultaneously and standardising responses across regions. The deployment has reduced waiting times, improved accessibility outside office hours and enabled staff to focus on complex cases requiring individual assessment. The system contributes to service efficiency and consistent communication while maintaining oversight through supervised content management.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1213,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00975,EU_PSTW_202609_00975,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""social_nonprofit""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,NORCE,R&D health.,The RE-SIKTET project addresses these challenges by supporting operators during live medical calls.,RE-SIKTET- Conversation and documentation quality in medical telephone assessment assisted by artificial intelligence,NORCE; Academic-Research; R&D health.; Norway,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Emergency medical call centres play a critical role in patient safety, yet telephone assessments are complex, time-pressured, and highly dependent on operator experience. Important questions may be missed, documentation quality can vary, and subtle indicators of serious conditions may be overlooked. The RE-SIKTET project addresses these challenges by supporting operators during live medical calls. Stakeholders include emergency call centre staff, healthcare services, and patients relying on accurate triage. The objective is to improve conversation quality, documentation, and clinical assessment without disrupting the natural flow of dialogue. The solution provides an interactive web interface that dynamically suggests relevant questions based on the conversation context, predicted severity, and known medical patterns. Selected questions and responses are automatically documented, creating structured and standardised records. Over time, the system generates large, high-quality datasets describing medical assessment interviews. The project evaluates the effect of this support on call quality and documentation. The impact includes more consistent assessments, reduced risk of misinterpretation, improved documentation, and better decision support for operators. It also creates a strong foundation for further research into communication, triage, and patient safety.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1214,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00976,EU_PSTW_202609_00976,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,NORCE,Fuel and energy,Offshore wind energy has significant potential but faces operational challenges that limit efficiency and profitability.,CONWIND: Forskning på smarte driftskontrollteknologier for havvindparker,NORCE; Academic-Research; Fuel and energy; Norway,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Offshore wind energy has significant potential but faces operational challenges that limit efficiency and profitability. Larger turbines reduce costs per unit of energy, yet they also create complex wake effects and turbulent wind fields that increase mechanical stress and reduce energy output. The CONWIND project addresses the need for smarter operational control of offshore wind farms. Stakeholders include energy producers, turbine operators, and grid operators seeking to maximise output and reduce maintenance costs. The objective is to improve wind farm performance through more accurate prediction and control of wind conditions under uncertainty. The solution develops advanced control algorithms that combine wind field modelling, sensor data, and predictive methods to optimise turbine operation across the farm. These control strategies account for wake interactions and uncertainty in environmental conditions. Even modest improvements in control performance can yield significant gains at scale. The project demonstrates that a control system increasing profitability by just over 2% would have major economic impact. CONWIND contributes to lower operating costs, higher energy production, and increased competitiveness of offshore wind, supporting the transition to renewable energy.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1215,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00977,EU_PSTW_202609_00977,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Norwegian Research Council,R&D economic affairs,"Manual handling is time-consuming and risks inconsistencies, particularly as interdisciplinary research grows.",Research grant for case management AI,Norwegian Research Council; Academic-Research; R&D economic affairs; Norway,"The solution applies thematic modelling to analyse submitted proposals and predict characteristics such as academic disciplines, research topics and sector relevance.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Research Council of Norway (RCN) processes large volumes of grant applications, requiring efficient classification and routing to appropriate expert panels. Manual handling is time-consuming and risks inconsistencies, particularly as interdisciplinary research grows. To address this, RCN explored digital support tools to optimise case management while keeping human oversight central. Stakeholders include case officers, expert evaluators, applicants and research institutions. The objective was to reduce administrative workload and improve consistency in thematic classification. The solution applies thematic modelling to analyse submitted proposals and predict characteristics such as academic disciplines, research topics and sector relevance. These predicted tags are presented to case officers, who review and decide on final classifications. The system integrates with existing grant management workflows and supports, rather than replaces, human judgement. Early results indicate improved efficiency, faster processing times and more consistent tagging across applications. The pilot demonstrates how decision support tools can enhance public sector workflows while preserving accountability and professional control.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1216,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00978,EU_PSTW_202609_00978,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Norsk Hydro ASA,"Mining, manufacturing and construction.",The AdaptAl project addresses this challenge by extending digital process optimisation into real-time adaptive control.,AdaptAl - Adaptive control of aluminium production,"Norsk Hydro ASA; Central-Government; Mining, manufacturing and construction.; Norway",UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Aluminium production involves complex, tightly coupled processes that are sensitive to raw material quality, chemical variations, and operational disturbances. Without adaptive control, these variations can exceed tolerance limits, leading to increased costs, waste, and quality issues. The AdaptAl project addresses this challenge by extending digital process optimisation into real-time adaptive control. Stakeholders include aluminium producers, industrial operators, and downstream manufacturing sectors such as automotive and furniture. The objective is to stabilise and optimise production across the aluminium value chain under varying conditions. The solution builds on Hydro’s PRO3 digital twin, which simulates and optimises aluminium production processes. AdaptAl integrates sensor data, virtual models, and optimisation software to enable adaptive control that continuously adjusts process parameters in response to observed deviations. The system is demonstrated in extrusion and forming processes across multiple industrial markets. The impact includes improved process stability, reduced production costs, higher product quality, and increased flexibility. AdaptAl strengthens industrial competitiveness and demonstrates how digital twins and adaptive control can deliver measurable value in energy-intensive manufacturing.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1219,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00979,EU_PSTW_202609_00979,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Norsk Hydro ASA,"Mining, manufacturing and construction.",UNKNOWN,Norsk Hydro's AI tool to detect hackers,"Norsk Hydro ASA; Central-Government; Mining, manufacturing and construction.; Norway","The objective of this use case is to continuously identify vulnerabilities and early warning signs of malicious activity across industrial control systems, reducing the likelihood of large-scale operational disruption. It applies machine learning–based anomaly detection techniques trained on historical operational data to identify subtle changes that may indicate cyber intrusion or system misuse.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Large industrial operators face significant risks from cyberattacks targeting operational technology and production infrastructure, as demonstrated by the 2019 ransomware attack against Norsk Hydro. As a partly state-owned aluminium producer operating complex, globally distributed industrial systems, Hydro needed a way to strengthen cyber resilience beyond traditional perimeter security. The objective of this use case is to continuously identify vulnerabilities and early warning signs of malicious activity across industrial control systems, reducing the likelihood of large-scale operational disruption. The solution monitors industrial equipment and network behaviour to detect deviations from normal operating patterns over time. It applies machine learning–based anomaly detection techniques trained on historical operational data to identify subtle changes that may indicate cyber intrusion or system misuse. The tool integrates with Hydro’s existing industrial monitoring and security infrastructure, enabling continuous assessment without interrupting production processes. The pilot improved Hydro’s ability to detect security weaknesses earlier and prioritise mitigation actions, strengthening situational awareness across plants. Qualitatively, it reduced reliance on reactive incident response and improved confidence in industrial cybersecurity governance. The project demonstrates the business value of predictive security monitoring in critical infrastructure, particularly for large industrial actors with high availability requirements.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1220,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00980,EU_PSTW_202609_00980,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Norwegian Customs (Tolletaten),"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.","Norwegian customs authorities faced analytical challenges because foreign operators in customs declarations were recorded as free-text entries, resulting in inconsistent spellings and fragmented data that limited statistical analysis and risk assessment. The approach reduces manual cleaning efforts, improves accuracy in identifying economic actors and strengthens the analytical basis for customs controls.",Unsupervised learning to detect customs declarations,"Norwegian Customs (Tolletaten); Central-Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Norway","The system constructs similarity networks based on textual features and string distance measures, then applies cluster analysis and cluster-splitting techniques to identify likely matches without pre-labelled training data.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Norwegian customs authorities faced analytical challenges because foreign operators in customs declarations were recorded as free-text entries, resulting in inconsistent spellings and fragmented data that limited statistical analysis and risk assessment. To improve data quality and support enforcement activities, the data analytics department developed an unsupervised clustering solution to group similar operator names automatically. The system constructs similarity networks based on textual features and string distance measures, then applies cluster analysis and cluster-splitting techniques to identify likely matches without pre-labelled training data. By grouping variant spellings and related entities, the tool enhances data harmonisation and supports downstream analytical tasks, including risk profiling and trade pattern analysis. The approach reduces manual cleaning efforts, improves accuracy in identifying economic actors and strengthens the analytical basis for customs controls. The solution increases scalability in processing large declaration datasets and enhances operational insight while preserving human oversight in enforcement decisions.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK|F07_DATA_VISIBILITY_INTEGRATION_GAP,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1221,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00981,EU_PSTW_202609_00981,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Norwegian Maritime Authority,"General economic, commercial and labour affair",Manual case handling can be resource-intensive and prone to inconsistencies in documentation management.,Automatisert saksbehandling med arkivmessige hensyn for kunstig intelligens,"Norwegian Maritime Authority; Central-Government; General economic, commercial and labour affair; Norway",The objective is to automate administrative workflows while maintaining legally compliant record-keeping practices.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Norwegian Maritime Authority seeks to modernise certificate issuance and case processing through automation while ensuring proper archival compliance and long-term document preservation. Manual case handling can be resource-intensive and prone to inconsistencies in documentation management. The objective is to automate administrative workflows while maintaining legally compliant record-keeping practices. Stakeholders include maritime authorities, certification applicants, the National Archives and the Directorate of Digitisation. The new solution integrates automated decision rules into certificate issuance processes and embeds metadata capture, documentation logging and archival structuring within the workflow. Automated systems collect, process and store data while ensuring traceability and accessibility in accordance with public sector information management standards. The project is being developed within a regulatory sandbox and organisational transformation programme. It is currently in development and phased implementation, with testing focused on ensuring archival integrity and lawful automation.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,S6_WORKFLOW_PROCESS_AUTOMATION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1222,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00982,EU_PSTW_202609_00982,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Norwegian Water Resources and Energy Directorate,Fuel and energy,UNKNOWN,NVE Copernicus 2,Norwegian Water Resources and Energy Directorate; Central-Government; Fuel and energy; Norway,The initiative has been implemented within NVE’s operational forecasting and monitoring framework.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Norway faces significant risks from floods and avalanches, requiring timely monitoring of snow and water conditions. The Norwegian Water Resources and Energy Directorate (NVE), in collaboration with Norsk Regnesentral, aims to enhance emergency warning systems by improving environmental data analysis. The objective is to strengthen early warning capabilities and disaster preparedness. The project develops algorithms that process satellite imagery from the Copernicus programme alongside hydrological and meteorological data. Machine learning techniques extract indicators related to snow cover, water levels and terrain conditions, supporting predictive modelling for hazard warnings. The system integrates with NVE’s emergency alert infrastructure to provide decision support to authorities. The initiative has been implemented within NVE’s operational forecasting and monitoring framework. It enhances forecasting accuracy, improves response times and strengthens resilience against natural hazards.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION|S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1223,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00983,EU_PSTW_202609_00983,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Oslo University Hospital,R&D health.,"Dementia places a growing burden on healthcare systems, and early identification of individuals at risk remains a critical unmet need. The AI-Mind project addresses this challenge by focusing on patients with mild cognitive impairment, a group in which approximately half may progress to dementia.",AI-Mind,Oslo University Hospital; Academic-Research; R&D health.; Norway,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Dementia places a growing burden on healthcare systems, and early identification of individuals at risk remains a critical unmet need. The AI-Mind project addresses this challenge by focusing on patients with mild cognitive impairment, a group in which approximately half may progress to dementia. Stakeholders include clinical researchers, neurologists, patients, healthcare providers, and academic institutions coordinated by Oslo Metropolitan University. The objective is to enable earlier and more reliable risk stratification, supporting timely intervention and improved care planning. The project centres on the analysis of electrophysiological brain signals collected through EEG examinations, which can reveal disturbances in brain networks associated with early synaptic dysfunction. The AI-Mind Connector processes EEG data to detect network-level patterns indicative of elevated dementia risk, integrating clinical, demographic, and neurophysiological information into a unified assessment framework. The system is designed to support clinicians rather than replace diagnostic judgement. Funded with approximately EUR 14 million and subject to European ethical review, the project remains in the research and validation phase. Expected impacts include improved diagnostic accuracy, better targeting of preventive measures, and reduced long-term healthcare costs through earlier intervention.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1224,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00984,EU_PSTW_202609_00984,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,National Archives of Norway,General services.,"The project enhanced processing speed, reduced manual cataloguing effort and expanded digital accessibility of archival collections, supporting research and administrative transparency.",Optical Character Recognition (OCR),National Archives of Norway; Central-Government; General services.; Norway,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The National Archives of Norway sought to enhance access to digitised historical documents by automating transcription and metadata extraction processes. Large volumes of scanned archival material required efficient processing to enable searchability and identification of sensitive content. The initiative combined advanced optical character recognition with systematic preprocessing of scanned files to improve text recognition accuracy. Machine-trained recognition models were applied to detect structured metadata elements such as letterheads, sender and recipient names, dates and document identifiers. Additional classification models were trained to categorise concepts including locations, individuals and organisations, and to flag case files potentially containing sensitive information based on patterns learned from annotated examples. The integration of OCR outputs with archival management systems improved indexing, retrieval and compliance with access restrictions. The project enhanced processing speed, reduced manual cataloguing effort and expanded digital accessibility of archival collections, supporting research and administrative transparency.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1243,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00985,EU_PSTW_202609_00985,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,The Norwegian Health Economics Administration,Public health services.,"The objective is to detect erroneous claims earlier, reduce manual audits, and simplify compliance for legitimate providers.",AI to detect incorrect health reimbursement payments,The Norwegian Health Economics Administration; Central-Government; Public health services.; Norway,"In Norway, Helfo initiated a collaborative innovation project to improve the accuracy of health reimbursement payments and ensure responsible use of public funds. Anticipated benefits include reduced incorrect payments, more efficient use of inspection resources, and increased trust among healthcare providers by focusing controls where risks are highest.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Public health reimbursement systems must balance timely payments with robust controls to prevent incorrect or fraudulent claims. In Norway, Helfo initiated a collaborative innovation project to improve the accuracy of health reimbursement payments and ensure responsible use of public funds. Stakeholders include Helfo, healthcare providers, researchers from Skatteforsk at the Norwegian University of Life Sciences, the Norwegian Institute of Public Health, and the University of Agder. The objective is to detect erroneous claims earlier, reduce manual audits, and simplify compliance for legitimate providers. The project develops data-driven control systems that analyse large volumes of reimbursement claims, historical payment data, and provider behaviour patterns. These systems generate risk indicators that support pre-payment checks and post-payment reviews, while remaining integrated with existing reimbursement workflows. Emphasis is placed on uncovering blind spots in current controls and improving reporting efficiency. The initiative is still under development, with pilots used to test models and operational integration. Anticipated benefits include reduced incorrect payments, more efficient use of inspection resources, and increased trust among healthcare providers by focusing controls where risks are highest.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1265,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00986,EU_PSTW_202609_00986,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Statnett,Fuel and energy,The objective is to enable early detection of degradation and prevent costly supply interruptions.,SSmart On-Line health assessment of cable terminations,Statnett; Central-Government; Fuel and energy; Norway,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Failures in high-voltage cable terminations pose a significant risk to electricity supply security, particularly in transmission networks. The SmartACT project addresses this issue by developing a continuous, non-intrusive monitoring system for 420 kV oil-filled cable terminations, which are among the most failure-prone components. Stakeholders include the Norwegian transmission system operator Statnett, industrial partners, and research institutions such as SINTEF and NTNU. The objective is to enable early detection of degradation and prevent costly supply interruptions. The project develops long-life sensors installed inside cable terminations to continuously measure temperature, humidity, pressure, and partial discharges. Sensor data is analysed through a digital assessment framework that combines physical modelling with data-driven evaluation methods. The monitoring system is designed to integrate with grid operation and maintenance systems, enabling condition-based maintenance strategies. SmartACT remains in the research and testing phase under the ENERGIX programme. Expected impacts include improved grid reliability, reduced unplanned outages, lower maintenance costs, and enhanced decision-making for critical energy infrastructure.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1275,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00987,EU_PSTW_202609_00987,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Universitetet i Stavanger,R&D health.,"Accurate documentation of events during newborn resuscitation and early care is essential for clinical quality, training, and patient safety, yet manual recording is often incomplete or imprecise.",NewbornTime,Universitetet i Stavanger; Academic-Research; R&D health.; Norway,"Using advanced modelling techniques, the system estimates the precise time of birth from thermal signals and recognises key clinical activities from video streams.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Accurate documentation of events during newborn resuscitation and early care is essential for clinical quality, training, and patient safety, yet manual recording is often incomplete or imprecise. The NewbornTime project aims to generate an objective and detailed timeline of events surrounding newborn care immediately after birth. Stakeholders include clinicians, neonatal researchers, hospitals, and healthcare quality organisations. The objective is to improve clinical documentation, support performance analysis, and enhance learning in neonatal care. The project combines infrared thermal video and optical video recordings captured in delivery rooms and resuscitation areas. Using advanced modelling techniques, the system estimates the precise time of birth from thermal signals and recognises key clinical activities from video streams. These events are automatically assembled into a structured timeline that can be reviewed by clinicians and researchers. The system is designed to integrate with hospital workflows while respecting strict data protection and ethical requirements. NewbornTime remains in the research phase, with validation studies ongoing. Potential impacts include more accurate clinical records, improved training and audit capabilities, and better insights into time-critical newborn interventions.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1284,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00988,EU_PSTW_202609_00988,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Directorate of Immigration,Police Services,"Norway’s Directorate of Immigration (UDI) faced growing backlogs in citizenship and family immigration applications, driven by rising volumes and fully manual case handling. Structured data from application forms and supporting documents is analysed and routed accordingly, while complex or ambiguous cases are flagged for manual review.",Automated case management of family immigration with a professional background,Directorate of Immigration; Central-Government; Police Services; Norway,"The objective of this use case was to streamline case management by automating routine assessments, while preserving legal certainty and human oversight. The solution uses rule-based automation combined with data-driven classification models to pre-process applications, extract relevant information and assess whether cases meet predefined conditions for simplified handling.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Norway’s Directorate of Immigration (UDI) faced growing backlogs in citizenship and family immigration applications, driven by rising volumes and fully manual case handling. Processing times regularly exceeded one year, placing strain on applicants, caseworkers and administrative resources. The objective of this use case was to streamline case management by automating routine assessments, while preserving legal certainty and human oversight. The system focuses on applications from individuals with professional backgrounds where documentation and eligibility criteria are relatively standardised. The solution uses rule-based automation combined with data-driven classification models to pre-process applications, extract relevant information and assess whether cases meet predefined conditions for simplified handling. Structured data from application forms and supporting documents is analysed and routed accordingly, while complex or ambiguous cases are flagged for manual review. The system integrates with UDI’s existing case management platforms and national registers. The impact has been a substantial reduction in average processing time for eligible cases, freeing case officers to focus on more complex applications. Administrative efficiency improved, backlogs decreased and applicants benefited from faster, more predictable outcomes, demonstrating how automation can enhance public service delivery at scale.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1285,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00989,EU_PSTW_202609_00989,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Directorate of Immigration,Police Services,"By standardising routine assessments and data validation, Ada reduces manual workload and processing times.",Ada-bot helps process applications for family reunification,Directorate of Immigration; Central-Government; Police Services; Norway,"Ada processes application data, verifies submitted documentation and applies rule-based logic derived from immigration legislation and administrative guidelines.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Norwegian Directorate of Immigration introduced the automated system “Ada” to improve efficiency in processing family reunification applications, which involve high case volumes and legally structured eligibility criteria. The objective was to streamline document collection and support consistent application of statutory rules. Ada processes application data, verifies submitted documentation and applies rule-based logic derived from immigration legislation and administrative guidelines. The system cross-checks information against internal registries and predefined criteria, identifying complete cases suitable for automated decision pathways while routing complex or ambiguous cases to human caseworkers. By standardising routine assessments and data validation, Ada reduces manual workload and processing times. Authorities reported that certain cases can be handled up to five times faster compared with traditional processing. The implementation enhances consistency, shortens waiting periods for applicants and allows staff to focus on legally complex cases requiring discretionary judgement, while maintaining legal accountability and audit trails.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1286,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00990,EU_PSTW_202609_00990,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Kristiansand Municipality,Housing development.,Manual processing of construction cases and maintenance of cadastral and mapping databases is time-consuming and prone to inconsistencies.,KartAi - Automate and streamline case management in construction cases,Kristiansand Municipality; Local Government; Housing development.; Norway,"Accurate and up-to-date property and land-use data are essential for urban planning, construction permitting, and public administration. The objective is to automate and support case management while improving the quality of the property register and the Central Common Map Database.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Accurate and up-to-date property and land-use data are essential for urban planning, construction permitting, and public administration. Manual processing of construction cases and maintenance of cadastral and mapping databases is time-consuming and prone to inconsistencies. KartAi addresses the need to improve data quality and streamline administrative workflows related to construction and property management. Stakeholders include municipalities, planning authorities, surveyors, and citizens interacting with land administration systems. The objective is to automate and support case management while improving the quality of the property register and the Central Common Map Database. The solution applies machine learning to analyse geospatial data, documents, and historical records to detect inconsistencies, support classification, and assist decision-making in construction cases. Automated analysis supports faster updates to cadastral records and improved alignment between maps and legal property information. The impact includes reduced processing time, higher data accuracy, and more transparent case handling. KartAi contributes to more efficient public administration, better land-use decisions, and improved trust in core spatial data infrastructures.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1288,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00991,EU_PSTW_202609_00991,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Barcelona,General services.,"The system processes structured and unstructured inputs recorded during consultations and provides recommendations to social workers, who retain full decision-making authority.",AI in Social Services,City of Barcelona; Local Government; General services.; Spain,The solution uses natural language processing and machine learning models trained on a large corpus of historical case records to analyse interview data and suggest relevant interventions and services.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The City of Barcelona’s social services manage a high volume of complex cases involving vulnerable individuals, requiring timely and consistent decision-making by professionals. Social workers must assess diverse situations and determine appropriate interventions, often under time constraints and with varying levels of information. The objective is to support professionals in delivering more consistent and informed service allocation. The solution uses natural language processing and machine learning models trained on a large corpus of historical case records to analyse interview data and suggest relevant interventions and services. The system processes structured and unstructured inputs recorded during consultations and provides recommendations to social workers, who retain full decision-making authority. It is integrated into existing case management workflows as a decision-support tool. The implementation enhances efficiency, supports consistency in case handling, and improves access to relevant resources, while maintaining human oversight and ethical safeguards in sensitive decision-making contexts.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F04_UNSTRUCTURED_INFORMATION_COMPLEXITY,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1290,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00992,EU_PSTW_202609_00992,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Helsinki,General services.,UNKNOWN,Helsinki chatbot,City of Helsinki; Local Government; General services.; Finland,"The City of Helsinki implemented a 24-hour chatbot service to improve accessibility of municipal information and enhance user experience on its public website. Initially deployed within Sports and Recreation pages, the objective was to provide residents with rapid, tailored answers without requiring manual searches or direct contact with staff.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The City of Helsinki implemented a 24-hour chatbot service to improve accessibility of municipal information and enhance user experience on its public website. Initially deployed within Sports and Recreation pages, the objective was to provide residents with rapid, tailored answers without requiring manual searches or direct contact with staff. The chatbot operates using structured rule-based dialogue paths and curated question–answer pairs developed by Helsinki City Information specialists and thematic experts. Natural language understanding components interpret user queries and match them to predefined intents, guiding users through conversational flows that provide location details, service descriptions and practical instructions. The system integrates with the city’s content management environment to ensure updated information delivery. The deployment reduced routine enquiries to customer service channels, improved response speed and enabled continuous availability beyond office hours. It supports scalable digital interaction while preserving human oversight in content design and maintenance.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1291,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00993,EU_PSTW_202609_00993,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Financial Administration of the Republic of Slovenia,Police Services,UNKNOWN,E-vignette: toll road payment controlled by AI,Financial Administration of the Republic of Slovenia; Central-Government; Police Services; Slovenia,"Motorists purchase access online using a vehicle registration number and email address, eliminating the need for physical stickers.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Slovenia replaced its physical motorway vignette with a fully digital e-vignette system to simplify toll payment and improve enforcement efficiency. Motorists purchase access online using a vehicle registration number and email address, eliminating the need for physical stickers. Compliance is monitored by the Motorway Company of the Republic of Slovenia (DARS), the Financial Administration and the Police through roadside cameras, control points and mobile enforcement vehicles. The system captures vehicle registration numbers and compares them in real time with the central e-vignette database. Automated image recognition and database matching confirm whether a valid vignette has been purchased. If compliance is verified, vehicle data are deleted immediately; if not, records are retained for minor offence proceedings. The digital infrastructure improves enforcement coverage, reduces administrative costs and enhances convenience for drivers, while incorporating data minimisation safeguards. The system enables scalable motorway toll management and streamlined cross-agency cooperation.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1292,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00994,EU_PSTW_202609_00994,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"City of Amsterdam, Barcelona, Brussels, Eindhoven, Mannheim, Rotterdam and Sofia","Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.","Cities lacked a shared, practical framework for explaining how such systems are used, for what purpose, and with what safeguards.",Algorithmic Transparency Standard,"City of Amsterdam, Barcelona, Brussels, Eindhoven, Mannheim, Rotterdam and Sofia; Local Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; European Union","The Algorithmic Transparency Standard was developed to address this gap by providing a common structure for documenting algorithmic use across local governments. Cities using the standard benefit from clearer governance processes and reduced duplication of effort, while citizens gain accessible insight into algorithmic decision-making.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Public administrations increasingly rely on algorithmic systems, creating a growing need for transparency, accountability and public understanding. Cities lacked a shared, practical framework for explaining how such systems are used, for what purpose, and with what safeguards. The Algorithmic Transparency Standard was developed to address this gap by providing a common structure for documenting algorithmic use across local governments. The initiative involves municipalities, civic technology organisations and researchers, with the objective of enabling meaningful transparency for citizens while supporting internal governance. The standard defines a shared set of information categories, such as system purpose, data sources, decision impacts, oversight mechanisms and risks. These are published in open, machine-readable formats (CSV and Excel), allowing comparison across systems and jurisdictions. The standard can be integrated into existing registers or disclosure portals without requiring new technical infrastructure. Its impact lies in improved comparability, easier public scrutiny and stronger internal documentation practices. Cities using the standard benefit from clearer governance processes and reduced duplication of effort, while citizens gain accessible insight into algorithmic decision-making. The initiative supports trust, accountability and responsible digital transformation in public administration.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1294,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00995,EU_PSTW_202609_00995,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Metropolia University of Applied Sciences,Fuel and energy,The BEE pilot addresses the challenge of reducing emissions while maintaining comfort for building occupants.,BEE Pilot - Building Energy Efficiency,Metropolia University of Applied Sciences; Academic-Research; Fuel and energy; Finland,"The objective is to use buildings as flexible energy resources that shift consumption towards periods of high renewable availability. The solution integrates with existing building management systems to collect data on energy use and indoor conditions, combined with external data such as weather forecasts and grid information.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Buildings play a crucial role in the energy transition, both as major energy consumers and as flexible assets that can support renewable integration. The BEE pilot addresses the challenge of reducing emissions while maintaining comfort for building occupants. Stakeholders include building owners, facility managers, energy providers, and city authorities. The objective is to use buildings as flexible energy resources that shift consumption towards periods of high renewable availability. The solution integrates with existing building management systems to collect data on energy use and indoor conditions, combined with external data such as weather forecasts and grid information. Daily forecasts of grid mix, building usage, and flexibility potential are used to generate optimised control schedules for heating, cooling, ventilation, water heating, and electric vehicle charging. These schedules proactively minimise energy use and maximise renewable consumption while preserving comfort. The pilot demonstrates reduced emissions, improved energy efficiency, and better alignment between buildings and the energy system. BEE shows how intelligent building control can support decarbonisation at city scale while delivering operational and comfort benefits.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION|S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1295,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00996,EU_PSTW_202609_00996,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Amsterdam,General services.,UNKNOWN,Public Eye: AI crowd monitoring system in Amsterdam,City of Amsterdam; Local Government; General services.; Netherlands,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The City of Amsterdam developed Public Eye to support crowd management in busy public spaces such as Arena Boulevard, Marineterrein and Dam Square, where high visitor numbers can create safety risks and congestion. The objective is to provide real-time insight into crowd density to inform operational decisions and increase transparency for residents. Cameras installed at selected locations transmit video streams to municipal servers, where computer vision algorithms analyse images to estimate the number of people present and, in some areas, the occupancy of bicycle parking facilities. The system extracts numerical counts and density indicators without storing or displaying identifiable images. Aggregated data are published on a public platform for citizen access and simultaneously shared with municipal staff to support traffic regulation and event management. By delivering near real-time occupancy data, Public Eye enhances situational awareness, enables proactive crowd control measures and improves public communication. The approach supports scalable deployment across additional sites while embedding data minimisation principles in its technical architecture.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1298,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00997,EU_PSTW_202609_00997,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Amsterdam,Social exclusion n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,AI for poverty detection,City of Amsterdam; Local Government; Social exclusion n.e.c.; Netherlands,"Amsterdam implemented an early intervention system to identify households at risk of financial hardship based on payment arrears reported by health insurers, housing associations, energy suppliers and Waternet.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Amsterdam implemented an early intervention system to identify households at risk of financial hardship based on payment arrears reported by health insurers, housing associations, energy suppliers and Waternet. The operational goal is to prevent escalation into problematic debt by initiating timely social support. When a creditor submits a report, the system links it with municipal poverty reduction databases through an automated matching process initiated by authorised staff. The case is then forwarded to a social worker in the relevant district, who must contact the resident within 14 days and complete an initial assessment within 28 days. After this point, the process relies on human case management rather than automated decision-making. Data integration tools support case allocation and tracking, while professionals determine appropriate support measures and agreements with creditors. The system improves early detection of financial vulnerability, strengthens coordination between creditors and social services and promotes preventive intervention, while maintaining human oversight in all substantive decisions.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1299,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00998,EU_PSTW_202609_00998,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Amsterdam,General services.,"Amsterdam’s dense canal network presents both opportunities and challenges for urban mobility, logistics and environmental monitoring.",Autonomous Boats Deployment,City of Amsterdam; Local Government; General services.; Netherlands,"The pilots demonstrated safe autonomous navigation, modular vessel design and flexible deployment across use cases.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Amsterdam’s dense canal network presents both opportunities and challenges for urban mobility, logistics and environmental monitoring. Traditional boats require human operators, limiting scalability and increasing operational costs. The Roboat project was launched by MIT and the AMS Institute, together with the City of Amsterdam and Waternet, to explore autonomous vessels as a new urban infrastructure layer. The objective is to deploy self-driving boats capable of performing multiple functions, including passenger transport, waste collection and water quality monitoring. Roboats are equipped with sensor suites such as cameras, lidar and GPS, enabling perception, navigation and collision avoidance in complex canal environments. Onboard systems process real-time data to plan routes and coordinate with other vessels, while shore-based platforms monitor fleet operations. The pilots demonstrated safe autonomous navigation, modular vessel design and flexible deployment across use cases. Impacts include reduced labour requirements, improved efficiency in waste collection and new data streams for environmental management. The project highlights the potential of autonomous waterborne transport to complement urban mobility systems, reduce congestion and support sustainable city operations.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1300,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_00999,EU_PSTW_202609_00999,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Amsterdam,Sickness and disability.,"Historically, such data was fragmented, outdated or manually collected. The impact includes improved visibility of accessibility issues, better prioritisation of infrastructure investments and reduced reliance on manual surveys.",Using AI to measure accessibility,City of Amsterdam; Local Government; Sickness and disability.; Netherlands,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Ensuring equal access to public space for people with disabilities requires detailed, up-to-date information on urban accessibility. Historically, such data was fragmented, outdated or manually collected. The City of Amsterdam launched the “Amsterdam for All” initiative to systematically measure accessibility barriers across the city and support evidence-based interventions. Stakeholders include city planners, accessibility experts and residents with mobility impairments. The objective is to identify obstacles such as blocked pavements, missing curb ramps or inaccessible crossings. The project analyses street-level imagery and spatial data to detect urban features relevant to pedestrian accessibility. Predictive models identify the presence and condition of sidewalks, crossings and obstacles, producing city-wide accessibility maps. These outputs integrate into municipal planning tools and dashboards used by city officials. The impact includes improved visibility of accessibility issues, better prioritisation of infrastructure investments and reduced reliance on manual surveys. Qualitatively, the project strengthens inclusive urban design and supports Amsterdam’s commitment to universal accessibility through data-driven decision-making.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1301,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01000,EU_PSTW_202609_01000,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Amsterdam,Sickness and disability.,UNKNOWN,Automatically Assessing and Improving the Readability of Municipal Communication,City of Amsterdam; Local Government; Sickness and disability.; Netherlands,The project applies natural language processing techniques to analyse municipal texts and measure readability. The project has been implemented as an operational support tool within the municipality.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Low literacy levels present barriers to civic participation, particularly in accessing municipal services such as taxation, voting and social benefits. The City of Amsterdam, through its “Amsterdam for All” initiative, aims to improve the accessibility of public communication by ensuring that official texts are understandable to citizens with varying literacy levels. The objective is to support inclusive public services and reduce administrative exclusion. The project applies natural language processing techniques to analyse municipal texts and measure readability. Algorithms evaluate lexical complexity and syntactic structure, and contextual lexical simplification methods suggest alternative wording for complex terms while preserving meaning. The system integrates into content creation workflows to assist communication officers in drafting clearer documents. The project has been implemented as an operational support tool within the municipality. It improves clarity of communication, enhances citizen access to services and promotes inclusive governance through data-driven language optimisation.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1302,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01001,EU_PSTW_202609_01001,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Amsterdam,R&D environmental protection.,UNKNOWN,AI for green roof management,City of Amsterdam; Local Government; R&D environmental protection.; Netherlands,"To address this, a proof of concept was developed using Very High Resolution satellite imagery with a spatial resolution of approximately 30 cm, enabling detailed observation of roof composition.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The City of Amsterdam required reliable data on green roof coverage to support climate adaptation, biodiversity and urban planning policies, as previous mapping methods classified entire roofs as green when only partial vegetation was present, leading to significant overestimation and unreliable statistics. Municipal authorities, environmental planners and property owners needed precise measurements of actual vegetated surface across the city. To address this, a proof of concept was developed using Very High Resolution satellite imagery with a spatial resolution of approximately 30 cm, enabling detailed observation of roof composition. Geospatial image analysis techniques were applied to classify pixels according to material type and vegetation cover, allowing roof areas to be quantified accurately at pixel level. The processed data were integrated into municipal geographic information systems to update spatial datasets and support policy analysis. The approach substantially improves data granularity and reliability compared with previous estimates, enabling better targeting of incentives, monitoring of environmental performance and evidence-based investment in green infrastructure, while offering a scalable method for continuous urban monitoring.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1303,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01002,EU_PSTW_202609_01002,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Amsterdam,Basic research.,Traditional data collection methods are labour-intensive and may lack precision or timeliness.,3D Point Cloud,City of Amsterdam; Local Government; Basic research.; Netherlands,"The objective is to automate the detection and classification of urban assets to support planning, maintenance, and operational efficiency. The solution uses mobile LiDAR scanners to generate detailed three-dimensional representations of the urban environment in the form of point clouds.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The City of Amsterdam aims to improve urban asset management by maintaining accurate and up-to-date information about public infrastructure such as trees, street lighting, and traffic signs. Traditional data collection methods are labour-intensive and may lack precision or timeliness. The objective is to automate the detection and classification of urban assets to support planning, maintenance, and operational efficiency. The solution uses mobile LiDAR scanners to generate detailed three-dimensional representations of the urban environment in the form of point clouds. Machine learning and computer vision techniques are applied to process these datasets, identifying and classifying objects based on their geometric and spatial characteristics. The system integrates with municipal data platforms to update asset inventories. As an implemented solution, it improves data accuracy, reduces manual surveying efforts, and enables more efficient urban management and planning.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1304,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01003,EU_PSTW_202609_01003,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of The Hague,R&D general public services.,"Urban planners face challenges in monitoring real-time conditions and anticipating congestion, especially during peak periods.",The Hague Digital Twin,Municipality of The Hague; Local Government; R&D general public services.; Netherlands,"As a pilot initiative, it demonstrates the potential to improve traffic management, optimise infrastructure use, and enhance urban planning, although full-scale deployment has not yet been confirmed.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Municipality of The Hague is developing a digital twin within the Scheveningen Living Lab to better understand and manage urban dynamics, particularly in high-traffic coastal areas. Urban planners face challenges in monitoring real-time conditions and anticipating congestion, especially during peak periods. The objective is to support data-driven urban management and planning through integrated visualisation and analysis tools. The solution consists of a digital twin platform that aggregates and visualises real-time and static data, including pedestrian and bicycle flows, public transport movements, and infrastructure availability. Advanced analytics are applied to model mobility patterns and support scenario analysis. The system integrates multiple data sources into a unified dashboard accessible to decision-makers. As a pilot initiative, it demonstrates the potential to improve traffic management, optimise infrastructure use, and enhance urban planning, although full-scale deployment has not yet been confirmed.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1305,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01004,EU_PSTW_202609_01004,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Port of Rotterdam,Transport,"Port operations involve complex coordination of vessel movements, infrastructure, and environmental conditions, where inefficiencies can lead to delays and increased costs.",Digital Twin Technology to Improve Operations,Port of Rotterdam; Local Government; Transport; Netherlands,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Port of Rotterdam initiated the development of a digital twin to enhance operational efficiency and optimise maritime logistics. Port operations involve complex coordination of vessel movements, infrastructure, and environmental conditions, where inefficiencies can lead to delays and increased costs. The objective is to improve planning and utilisation of port resources by providing real-time insights and predictive capabilities. The solution is based on an Internet of Things-enabled cloud platform that collects data from sensors measuring hydrological and meteorological conditions such as water levels, currents, wind, and visibility. Advanced analytics and predictive models are applied to simulate port operations and determine optimal timing for mooring, loading, and departure. The platform integrates data from multiple stakeholders to support coordinated decision-making. As a pilot, it demonstrates potential to reduce waiting times, improve capacity utilisation, and enhance operational reliability, though full deployment status remains unconfirmed.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1306,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01005,EU_PSTW_202609_01005,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Helsinki,Housing and community amenities n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,Chatbot for rental apartment search,City of Helsinki; Local Government; Housing and community amenities n.e.c.; Finland,The system automates responses to common enquiries while escalating complex cases to human advisers when necessary.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The City of Helsinki introduced a rental apartment search chatbot to improve accessibility and efficiency of housing services. Prospective tenants frequently required guidance on eligibility criteria, application procedures and housing availability, placing pressure on customer service channels. The chatbot provides a continuous digital interface integrated into the city’s housing services website. It operates through structured rule-based dialogue paths and curated question–answer pairs defined by housing service experts. Natural language understanding components interpret user queries and map them to predefined intents, enabling tailored responses related to rental applications, waiting lists and housing policies. The system automates responses to common enquiries while escalating complex cases to human advisers when necessary. By providing immediate answers, the chatbot reduces response times, increases service availability outside office hours and improves user experience. It enhances operational efficiency by handling high volumes of routine questions and standardising information delivery across the housing service.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1307,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01006,EU_PSTW_202609_01006,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Swedish Companies Registration Office,"General economic, commercial and labour affair","The Swedish Companies Registration Office identified inefficiencies in how entrepreneurs, customers and business partners access and share verified company information, as validation often relies on fragmented registers, manual document exchange and repeated checks. The objective was to enable secure, rapid and trustworthy exchange of certified corporate data while reducing administrative burden and strengthening confidence in digital interactions.",Proof of Business,"Swedish Companies Registration Office; Central-Government; General economic, commercial and labour affair; Sweden","A proof of concept digital wallet was developed using Distributed Ledger Technology to collect, store and manage verified company credentials issued by the authority.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Swedish Companies Registration Office identified inefficiencies in how entrepreneurs, customers and business partners access and share verified company information, as validation often relies on fragmented registers, manual document exchange and repeated checks. The objective was to enable secure, rapid and trustworthy exchange of certified corporate data while reducing administrative burden and strengthening confidence in digital interactions. A proof of concept digital wallet was developed using Distributed Ledger Technology to collect, store and manage verified company credentials issued by the authority. Blockchain infrastructure ensures immutability and traceability of records, while cryptographic mechanisms allow authorised parties to share tamper-evident certificates through secure interfaces. The system demonstrates how verified information can be accessed and exchanged in a controlled, interoperable environment without duplicating data across institutions. The pilot highlights potential reductions in processing time and administrative costs, faster onboarding of business relationships and improved transparency in commercial transactions, supporting scalable digital trust services for both public and private sector stakeholders.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1309,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01007,EU_PSTW_202609_01007,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,AMA (Agência para a Modernização Administrativa),"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.",The challenge addressed was fragmented citizen engagement mechanisms and limited transparency in consultation and participatory budgeting initiatives.,Participa.gov – Digital Public Participation Platform,"AMA (Agência para a Modernização Administrativa); Central-Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Portugal","Citizens authenticate using secure electronic identification mechanisms, and distributed ledger technology records transactions to ensure auditability and integrity of votes and proposals. The system supports diverse use cases, including national participatory budgeting, sector-specific consultations and restricted processes for defined groups.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Participa.gov was launched under Portugal’s Simplex modernisation programme to provide a unified national platform for participatory processes across public entities. The challenge addressed was fragmented citizen engagement mechanisms and limited transparency in consultation and participatory budgeting initiatives. Developed by the Administrative Modernisation Agency, the platform enables institutions to launch structured participatory processes, define eligibility criteria and manage proposal submission and voting through a standardised interface. Citizens authenticate using secure electronic identification mechanisms, and distributed ledger technology records transactions to ensure auditability and integrity of votes and proposals. Cryptographic safeguards protect data while allowing transparent verification of outcomes. The system supports diverse use cases, including national participatory budgeting, sector-specific consultations and restricted processes for defined groups. A consistent front-end design enhances usability, while configurable back-office tools allow institutional customisation. Participa.gov increases transparency, broadens civic engagement and strengthens trust in digital democratic processes through secure, standardised participation infrastructure.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1310,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01008,EU_PSTW_202609_01008,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,HM Revenue & Customs,"General economic, commercial and labour affair","Importers and exporters trading with the United Kingdom must submit multiple declarations to authorities such as HM Revenue & Customs and the Food Standards Agency, leading to duplication of information, administrative burden and delays at the border.",Reducing Friction in Trade (RFIT) in the UK,"HM Revenue & Customs; Central-Government; General economic, commercial and labour affair; United Kingdom",UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Importers and exporters trading with the United Kingdom must submit multiple declarations to authorities such as HM Revenue & Customs and the Food Standards Agency, leading to duplication of information, administrative burden and delays at the border. The RFIT project was launched as a proof of concept to explore how supply chain data could be shared directly with public authorities to ensure timeliness, provenance and compliance without repetitive filings. In collaboration with Chainvine’s “Intelligent Wine” application, upstream data from wine producers relevant to customs and Port Health Authorities were captured digitally and recorded on a blockchain-based platform. Distributed ledger mechanisms ensured data integrity, traceability and controlled access for authorised stakeholders through secure system integration. The pilot demonstrated how trusted shared datasets can streamline regulatory processes, reduce the need for discrete declarations and lower the cost of compliance. Anticipated benefits include faster clearance times, improved transparency across the supply chain and enhanced coordination between businesses and authorities, supporting more efficient and reliable cross-border trade.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1311,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01009,EU_PSTW_202609_01009,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,European Commission,Other industries,"Global cotton value chains are complex and fragmented, making it difficult for governments and companies to ensure traceability, compliance, and sustainability from production to retail. Risks such as forced labour, environmental harm, and lack of transparency undermine due diligence efforts and consumer trust.",Harnessing the potential of blockchain technology for due diligence and sustainability in cotton value chains,European Commission; European Institution/Agency; Other industries; European Union,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Global cotton value chains are complex and fragmented, making it difficult for governments and companies to ensure traceability, compliance, and sustainability from production to retail. Risks such as forced labour, environmental harm, and lack of transparency undermine due diligence efforts and consumer trust. The UNECE pilot project addresses these challenges by supporting traceability and accountability across the cotton supply chain, involving farmers, processors, manufacturers, brands, regulators, and consumers. The objective is to provide practical tools that enable reliable verification of origin, production practices, and sustainability claims, aligned with international policy and trade frameworks. The solution implements a distributed ledger–based traceability system that records key events and data points along the value chain, from field to shelf. Standardised data models developed under the UN/CEFACT framework ensure interoperability and consistency, while secure digital records enable verifiable information sharing among authorised actors. The pilot improves transparency, supports regulatory compliance, and strengthens due diligence processes. It enables more responsible sourcing, reduces information asymmetries, and enhances trust between stakeholders. Overall, the initiative contributes to more sustainable cotton value chains and provides a scalable reference model for traceability in the garment and footwear sector.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1312,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01010,EU_PSTW_202609_01010,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Bank of Lithuania,"General economic, commercial and labour affair",Start-ups and technology firms often face challenges in testing novel financial services within existing supervisory frameworks and accessing regulatory guidance during early development stages.,LBChain - Blockchain sandbox,"Bank of Lithuania; Central-Government; General economic, commercial and labour affair; Lithuania",UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Bank of Lithuania, as central bank and financial market supervisor, seeks to promote innovation in the fintech sector while ensuring compliance with regulatory requirements and maintaining financial stability. Start-ups and technology firms often face challenges in testing novel financial services within existing supervisory frameworks and accessing regulatory guidance during early development stages. LBChain was established as a technological and regulatory sandbox to provide a controlled environment where innovative solutions can be developed and assessed. The platform is based on blockchain architecture and combines distributed ledger infrastructure with supervisory support, enabling participants to test smart contracts, payment solutions and other ledger-based services under defined conditions. Secure nodes and predefined testing scenarios allow experimentation without full market exposure, while regulators observe performance and compliance aspects. The initiative fosters collaboration between innovators and authorities, accelerates product development cycles and reduces regulatory uncertainty. As a result, it strengthens the competitiveness of the national fintech ecosystem and supports responsible deployment of emerging financial technologies.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1313,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01011,EU_PSTW_202609_01011,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Nis,"General economic, commercial and labour affair","Traditional remittance channels often involve high fees and limited traceability, constraining economic benefits for recipient communities.",Transforming International Remittance for Serbian Diaspora using Blockchain and Digital Identity,"City of Nis; Local Government; General economic, commercial and labour affair; Serbia",UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"To reduce remittance costs and increase transparency in cross-border transfers, UNDP Serbia partnered with AID:Tech to pilot a blockchain-based remittance solution targeting the Serbian diaspora. Traditional remittance channels often involve high fees and limited traceability, constraining economic benefits for recipient communities. The system tokenises remittance value into blockchain-based digital assets linked to specific entitlements such as utilities or goods. Transactions are recorded on a distributed ledger, enabling traceability while preserving user privacy through cryptographic mechanisms. Digital identity components authenticate senders and recipients, ensuring that funds or entitlements reach intended beneficiaries. The pilot, launched in 2018 in the City of Niš, covered the full remittance journey from diaspora sender to local service redemption. The solution aimed to lower transaction costs, improve accountability and stimulate local economic activity. By combining distributed ledger infrastructure with digital identity verification, the initiative demonstrated an alternative model for secure, transparent remittance delivery.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1314,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01012,EU_PSTW_202609_01012,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Agence Nationale des Fréquences,Communication,UNKNOWN,Blockchain of Frequencies,Agence Nationale des Fréquences; Central-Government; Communication; France,A blockchain-based booking platform was developed to register and manage frequency reservations using distributed ledger technology.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The rapid growth of wireless devices and digital services has increased demand for radio spectrum, a finite public resource that must be carefully managed to avoid interference and ensure service quality. During major cultural, political and sporting events, numerous wireless microphones and devices operate simultaneously, creating risks of signal conflicts in Programme Making and Special Events bands. The French National Frequency Agency sought to improve transparency and coordination in frequency allocation within general authorisation bands. A blockchain-based booking platform was developed to register and manage frequency reservations using distributed ledger technology. The system records allocations in an immutable and transparent manner, enabling authorised users to reserve frequencies while preventing double bookings and ensuring traceability. Smart contract functionalities manage permissions and updates across stakeholders involved in event organisation and spectrum management. The solution enhances reliability and coordination, reduces interference risks and improves overall quality of service during high-profile events, supporting more efficient and accountable spectrum governance.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1315,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01013,EU_PSTW_202609_01013,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""social_nonprofit""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Technical University of Munich,Tertiary education.,"Paper-based certificates and fragmented digital solutions create administrative burdens, slow verification, and limit cross-border recognition. The DiBiHo project addresses this challenge by exploring a trusted digital infrastructure for academic credentials that can be used nationally and internationally.",DiBiHo - Digital Credentials for Higher Education Institutions,Technical University of Munich; Academic-Research; Tertiary education.; Germany,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Higher education institutions face growing demand for secure, interoperable, and internationally recognised academic credentials, as student mobility and digital service delivery increase. Paper-based certificates and fragmented digital solutions create administrative burdens, slow verification, and limit cross-border recognition. The DiBiHo project addresses this challenge by exploring a trusted digital infrastructure for academic credentials that can be used nationally and internationally. Stakeholders include German higher education institutions, students, employers, and credential evaluators. The objective is to define a vendor-independent, interoperable standard for issuing, storing, presenting, and verifying digital academic credentials. The solution investigates distributed infrastructure approaches that allow institutions to issue tamper-resistant credentials while enabling learners to control and share them securely. Common standards and governance principles are defined to ensure long-term interoperability, legal validity, and alignment with European and international frameworks. The project improves efficiency in credential management, reduces verification costs, and enhances trust in academic records. It supports student mobility, lifelong learning, and digital transformation in higher education, while providing a foundation for scalable, future-proof credential ecosystems independent of specific technology providers.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1316,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01014,EU_PSTW_202609_01014,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Carlos III University of Madrid,Tertiary education.,"Universities issuing digital certificates for online courses must ensure authenticity, prevent fraud and enable straightforward verification by employers and other institutions, as conventional digital documents can be copied or altered and often require manual validation.",Competency certificates in Small Private Online Courses (SPOCs),Carlos III University of Madrid; Academic-Research; Tertiary education.; Spain,"A blockchain-based mechanism was implemented to anchor each certificate to a distributed ledger by storing a cryptographic hash of the credential on-chain, creating a tamper-evident and time-stamped record linked to the learner’s achievement.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Universities issuing digital certificates for online courses must ensure authenticity, prevent fraud and enable straightforward verification by employers and other institutions, as conventional digital documents can be copied or altered and often require manual validation. The project aimed to strengthen trust in competency accreditations generated through the OPEN edX Learning Management System and formally issued by the university. A blockchain-based mechanism was implemented to anchor each certificate to a distributed ledger by storing a cryptographic hash of the credential on-chain, creating a tamper-evident and time-stamped record linked to the learner’s achievement. The certificates remain accessible through the LMS, while external stakeholders can verify their validity by comparing the document with the ledger entry, without relying on intermediaries. The solution reduces administrative workload and verification time, enhances transparency and portability of qualifications, and increases confidence in digital credentials across academic and professional contexts.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1320,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01015,EU_PSTW_202609_01015,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Digital Agency of Andalusia,Social protection n.e.c.,"The volume and complexity of aid schemes made manual processing inefficient, error-prone, and difficult to scale.",Intelligent management of Aid to Citizens and companies,Digital Agency of Andalusia; Regional Government; Social protection n.e.c.; Spain,"This use case addresses the need to accelerate aid delivery while ensuring compliance, transparency, and accountability. The objective is to automate complex administrative workflows to process aid applications quickly and reliably.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"During the COVID-19 pandemic, the Andalusian administration was required to rapidly distribute large-scale financial aid to citizens and businesses to protect employment and economic stability. The volume and complexity of aid schemes made manual processing inefficient, error-prone, and difficult to scale. This use case addresses the need to accelerate aid delivery while ensuring compliance, transparency, and accountability. Stakeholders include public administrators, beneficiaries, and oversight bodies. The objective is to automate complex administrative workflows to process aid applications quickly and reliably. The solution applies robotic process automation to handle repetitive, rule-based tasks such as data validation, eligibility checks, and application processing. Automated workflows integrate with existing administrative systems, while human oversight is retained for exception handling and decision-making. Since implementation, the system has processed more than 300,000 aid applications, significantly reducing processing times and administrative workload. The initiative improves service delivery, ensures timely support to citizens and companies, and strengthens institutional resilience during crises. It demonstrates how intelligent automation can enhance public sector efficiency, scalability, and responsiveness in high-pressure policy contexts.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,S6_WORKFLOW_PROCESS_AUTOMATION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1321,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01016,EU_PSTW_202609_01016,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Flemish Ministry of Mobility and Public Works (MOW),Transport,UNKNOWN,Monitoring structure health of bridges with InSAR,Flemish Ministry of Mobility and Public Works (MOW); Regional Government; Transport; Belgium,"Using Interferometric Synthetic Aperture Radar (InSAR), phase differences between successive radar satellite images are processed to calculate surface deformation over time.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Public authorities responsible for bridge management must ensure structural safety while optimising inspection budgets and minimising traffic disruption. Conventional monitoring relies on periodic visual inspections and targeted topographic surveys, which may fail to detect gradual deformations at an early stage. The project explores whether satellite-based interferometric analysis can support daily bridge management by identifying millimetre-level displacements and prioritising on-site investigations. Using Interferometric Synthetic Aperture Radar (InSAR), phase differences between successive radar satellite images are processed to calculate surface deformation over time. Different satellite data sources and processing techniques are assessed to determine their suitability for bridge monitoring, with algorithms designed to filter noise and automatically flag anomalous displacement patterns. The results are compared with ground-based measurements to validate accuracy and reliability. The approach aims to enable earlier detection of structural issues, more efficient allocation of inspection resources and reduced monitoring costs. By supporting risk-based maintenance planning and continuous remote observation, the solution enhances infrastructure safety, scalability of monitoring operations and evidence-based asset management.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1322,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01017,EU_PSTW_202609_01017,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Environment Agency,Public order and safety n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,Hello Lamp Post,Environment Agency; Central-Government; Public order and safety n.e.c.; United Kingdom,"The system uses natural language processing to interpret questions and deliver contextualised information, drawing on curated flood safety guidance and local data.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Environment Agency is responsible for protecting communities from flood and coastal risks, yet engaging diverse local audiences and providing timely, location-specific safety information has been challenging through traditional communication channels. The objective was to create an accessible, continuous engagement mechanism in high-risk areas in the South West of England and Newcastle, improving public awareness and preparedness. Hello Lamp Post introduced an interactive 24/7 conversational platform accessible via mobile devices, allowing residents to interact with physical infrastructure such as flood defences through text-based messaging. The system uses natural language processing to interpret questions and deliver contextualised information, drawing on curated flood safety guidance and local data. Conversations are geo-referenced to specific assets, ensuring relevance and enabling authorities to analyse recurring concerns. The initiative increased public interaction with flood risk information, improved accessibility outside office hours and generated insights into community awareness gaps. This supports more responsive communication strategies, strengthens trust between authorities and citizens and enhances resilience through scalable, place-based digital engagement.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1324,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01018,EU_PSTW_202609_01018,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Uppsala University,Tertiary education.,UNKNOWN,ViEWS - The Political Violence Early-Warning System,Uppsala University; Academic-Research; Tertiary education.; Sweden,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Violence Early-Warning System was launched in 2017 by Uppsala University and the Peace Research Institute Oslo to support policymakers and humanitarian actors in anticipating organised political violence. The objective is to provide transparent, evidence-based forecasts of conflict fatalities up to 36 months ahead across Africa and the Middle East. ViEWS integrates historical conflict event data, socioeconomic indicators, political variables and spatial-temporal datasets into statistical forecasting models. Ensemble modelling techniques and machine-learning algorithms generate monthly probabilistic predictions at fine geographic resolution. Results are published through an open online platform, enabling users to explore forecast scenarios and underlying data. The system enhances anticipatory action planning, resource allocation and early intervention strategies. By making methodology and forecasts publicly accessible, ViEWS strengthens transparency and reproducibility in conflict forecasting. Its structured modelling framework supports continuous updates and methodological refinement as new data become available.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION|S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1325,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01019,EU_PSTW_202609_01019,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Barcelona,General services.,"Governments and public organisations often struggle to interpret large volumes of unstructured citizen feedback from social media, surveys and open data sources, limiting their ability to detect emerging social concerns and respond proactively. By automating large-scale text analysis, it reduces manual workload, enhances analytical capacity and supports scalable, data-driven governance grounded in citizen perspectives.",Citibeats – Ethical AI for Social Understanding,City of Barcelona; Local Government; General services.; Spain,"The platform applies natural language processing and machine learning techniques to analyse large-scale text datasets, identifying themes, sentiment patterns and leading indicators of social trends. Algorithms classify content into policy-relevant categories and generate dashboards that visualise geographic and temporal patterns, using anonymised and aggregated data to protect privacy.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Governments and public organisations often struggle to interpret large volumes of unstructured citizen feedback from social media, surveys and open data sources, limiting their ability to detect emerging social concerns and respond proactively. The objective is to transform dispersed textual data into structured insights that inform policy design and service delivery while ensuring ethical data practices. The platform applies natural language processing and machine learning techniques to analyse large-scale text datasets, identifying themes, sentiment patterns and leading indicators of social trends. Algorithms classify content into policy-relevant categories and generate dashboards that visualise geographic and temporal patterns, using anonymised and aggregated data to protect privacy. The system integrates open data streams and institutional datasets to provide near real-time monitoring of public discourse. The solution enables earlier identification of societal risks, evidence-based prioritisation and more inclusive decision-making. By automating large-scale text analysis, it reduces manual workload, enhances analytical capacity and supports scalable, data-driven governance grounded in citizen perspectives.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK|F04_UNSTRUCTURED_INFORMATION_COMPLEXITY|F06_HIGH_VOLUME_SCALABILITY_BOTTLENECK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1326,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01020,EU_PSTW_202609_01020,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Empresa Municipal de Transported del Ayuntamiento de Madrid,Transport,"Urban commuters often face fragmented information across public transport, carsharing and bicycle services.",Madrid Mobility 360 (multimodal planner for urban commuting),Empresa Municipal de Transported del Ayuntamiento de Madrid; Local Government; Transport; Spain,"The route planner uses optimisation algorithms to estimate current and projected journey times, incorporating live traffic data and vehicle capacity information for selected bus routes.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Madrid Mobility 360 was developed to provide residents and visitors with an integrated digital tool for planning journeys across multiple transport modes in the city. Urban commuters often face fragmented information across public transport, carsharing and bicycle services. The application aggregates data from transport operators and mobility providers into a unified platform offering route calculation, real-time updates and integrated payment options. The route planner uses optimisation algorithms to estimate current and projected journey times, incorporating live traffic data and vehicle capacity information for selected bus routes. Users can compare transport options, purchase tickets and view estimated carbon dioxide emissions associated with each trip. The system supports multimodal planning by integrating public transport schedules, shared mobility availability and geolocation services. By centralising mobility information, the app improves travel efficiency, enhances user experience and supports sustainable transport choices. It contributes to smart city mobility management and data-driven urban planning.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1327,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01021,EU_PSTW_202609_01021,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Faculty of Medicine, University of Belgrade",R&D health.,"Healthcare systems often face challenges in providing access to specialised expertise, particularly in complex cases requiring multidisciplinary input or remote consultation.",Transforming Public Healthcare and Education Using AI-powered Mixed Reality Technology,"Faculty of Medicine, University of Belgrade; Academic-Research; R&D health.; Serbia","The Government of Serbia, in collaboration with Microsoft, explored the use of mixed reality technologies to enhance healthcare delivery and medical education. While the initiative demonstrates potential to improve efficiency, reduce the need for physical presence, and enhance learning experiences, no recent evidence confirms large-scale deployment or continued operational use.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Government of Serbia, in collaboration with Microsoft, explored the use of mixed reality technologies to enhance healthcare delivery and medical education. Healthcare systems often face challenges in providing access to specialised expertise, particularly in complex cases requiring multidisciplinary input or remote consultation. The objective of this initiative is to enable remote collaboration among healthcare professionals and improve training opportunities for medical students. The solution combines mixed reality environments with data-driven analytical tools to allow clinicians to visualise patient data and medical scenarios in immersive formats. It supports real-time collaboration, enabling experts to contribute to diagnosis and treatment planning regardless of location. The system also facilitates remote education by simulating clinical scenarios for training purposes. While the initiative demonstrates potential to improve efficiency, reduce the need for physical presence, and enhance learning experiences, no recent evidence confirms large-scale deployment or continued operational use.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1328,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01022,EU_PSTW_202609_01022,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Government of Serbia,Unemployment.,"Young graduates often face a difficult transition from education to work, particularly when they lack practical experience and employers are hesitant to recruit first-time entrants during uncertain labour-market conditions.",My First Salary - a project to address youth unemployment,Government of Serbia; Central-Government; Unemployment.; Serbia,"The implementation is built around a fully digital platform that automates application handling, approval, eligibility checks, and candidate-employer matching.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Young graduates often face a difficult transition from education to work, particularly when they lack practical experience and employers are hesitant to recruit first-time entrants during uncertain labour-market conditions. In Serbia, the My First Salary programme was designed to address this gap by helping young people under 30 obtain their first work opportunity while supporting employers in filling roles with subsidised early-career talent. The objective is to reduce youth unemployment, shorten the waiting time for a first job, and make the application and recruitment process far less bureaucratic than conventional state-support schemes. The implementation is built around a fully digital platform that automates application handling, approval, eligibility checks, and candidate-employer matching. At a conceptual level, the system combines digital identity and certificate-based registration, automated verification of company and candidate conditions, and a recommendation algorithm that helps match participants to opportunities. The programme also includes dashboards and visualisation tools to support administration of the scheme. The impact includes a simpler zero-paper process, faster access to support for both employers and young applicants, and more efficient matching between labour demand and available candidates. It also improves scalability and transparency in programme delivery while creating public value through a more accessible route into first employment.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1329,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01023,EU_PSTW_202609_01023,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Helsinki,Environmental protection n.e.c.,"Urban air quality monitoring traditionally relies on a limited number of fixed stations, providing only coarse-grained insights into citizens’ actual exposure to pollution.",HOPE - Healthy Outdoor Premises for Everyone,City of Helsinki; Local Government; Environmental protection n.e.c.; Finland,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Urban air quality monitoring traditionally relies on a limited number of fixed stations, providing only coarse-grained insights into citizens’ actual exposure to pollution. In the Helsinki region, this limits the ability of individuals and policymakers to understand air quality variations at neighbourhood or street level. The HOPE project addresses this gap by enabling hyper-local air quality monitoring through a dense, participatory sensing network. Stakeholders include citizens, city authorities, researchers, and public health organisations. The objective is to provide accessible, location-specific information on air pollution exposure in daily life. The solution combines data from fixed monitoring stations with crowdsourced measurements collected via portable sensors carried by citizens. These data are aggregated and visualised on digital maps, providing near real-time insights into air quality across the city. The project enhances spatial coverage, raises public awareness, and supports evidence-based urban planning and health interventions. HOPE empowers citizens with actionable information, improves understanding of pollution dynamics, and supports targeted measures to improve urban environmental health and quality of life.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1330,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01024,EU_PSTW_202609_01024,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Irish Naval Service,UNKNOWN,"Maritime authorities require wide-area surveillance to address security threats, coordinate search and rescue operations and monitor environmental risks, yet conventional ship-based sensors have limited range constrained by low elevation.","Aeolus – Mid-altitude maritime monitoring platform for security, search & rescue and environmental monitoring",Irish Naval Service; Central-Government; Ireland,It can be deployed using different lifting mechanisms and remain operational for extended periods.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Maritime authorities require wide-area surveillance to address security threats, coordinate search and rescue operations and monitor environmental risks, yet conventional ship-based sensors have limited range constrained by low elevation. The Aeolus project aims to extend maritime situational awareness by elevating monitoring systems to mid-altitude, thereby expanding coverage without deploying additional vessels. The platform consists of an airborne sensor system operating at approximately 450 metres above a host ship, increasing the observable area by more than eleven times compared with standard configurations. It can be deployed using different lifting mechanisms and remain operational for extended periods. Equipped with optical and radar sensors, the system transmits real-time data to onboard command centres, where information is processed and integrated with existing surveillance systems. The solution enhances detection capabilities for vessels, persons in distress and environmental anomalies, while improving operational efficiency. By increasing coverage and persistence at relatively low cost, the platform strengthens maritime safety, supports faster response times and enables scalable monitoring across diverse missions.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1331,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01025,EU_PSTW_202609_01025,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,University of Nottingham,R&D education.,UNKNOWN,Monitoring slavery from space,University of Nottingham; Academic-Research; R&D education.; United Kingdom,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Brick kilns across the South Asian “Brick Belt” are associated with high prevalence of forced labour, yet the absence of reliable data on their number and distribution has hindered effective intervention by governments and non-governmental organisations. The project sought to generate an evidence base to estimate the scale of kiln operations and, by proxy, potential exposure to modern slavery. High-resolution satellite imagery was analysed to identify and map kiln structures across regions of Pakistan, India, Bangladesh and Nepal. Image recognition techniques and spatial analysis were used to detect characteristic kiln shapes and validate findings against ground-truth information. The resulting geospatial dataset enabled a more credible estimate of kiln numbers and their geographic concentration. By providing transparent, large-scale evidence, the initiative supports targeted inspections, policy prioritisation and resource allocation. It enhances situational awareness for anti-slavery actors and demonstrates how satellite data can improve accountability, scalability of monitoring and informed action against labour exploitation.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1332,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01026,EU_PSTW_202609_01026,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Saint Denis Innovation Lab,Sickness and disability.,UNKNOWN,R2D3: Active-waiting robot for the disabled people’s waiting room,Saint Denis Innovation Lab; Local Government; Sickness and disability.; France,"It delivers information on administrative procedures and services through conversational interaction, using natural language processing and pre-programmed knowledge bases.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Seine-Saint-Denis Innovation Lab developed the R2D3 robot to improve accessibility and user experience for individuals visiting the Departmental House for Disabled Persons. Waiting times in administrative services can be challenging, particularly for users with diverse needs and varying levels of digital literacy. The objective is to provide an inclusive and engaging way to deliver information and support users during waiting periods. The solution consists of an interactive robot equipped with speech recognition and multilingual capabilities, enabling it to communicate with users in a simple and accessible manner. It delivers information on administrative procedures and services through conversational interaction, using natural language processing and pre-programmed knowledge bases. The system is designed to be user-oriented and adaptable to different user profiles. As a pilot initiative, it demonstrates the potential to reduce the digital divide, improve access to information, and enhance user experience, although no evidence confirms broader deployment.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1333,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01027,EU_PSTW_202609_01027,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Aylesbury Vale District Council,General public services n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,Voice assistant services,Aylesbury Vale District Council; Local Government; General public services n.e.c.; United Kingdom,The service integrates structured council data and frequently asked questions into Amazon’s voice interface using predefined intents and dialogue flows. It demonstrates how local authorities can leverage commercial voice platforms to extend service delivery channels efficiently.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Aylesbury Vale District Council sought to expand digital access to local government information by offering residents an alternative to web-based and telephone enquiries. The objective was to improve convenience and accessibility for routine questions relating to council services, payments and waste management. The council became the first UK local authority to launch an Amazon Alexa skill, enabling residents to obtain information through voice commands. The service integrates structured council data and frequently asked questions into Amazon’s voice interface using predefined intents and dialogue flows. Users can ask about political leadership, council tax payment options, recycling guidance and book assisted waste collections. Requests are processed through Amazon’s voice recognition infrastructure and linked to council information systems via secure APIs. The solution increases out-of-hours access, reduces pressure on contact centres and broadens digital inclusion for users preferring voice interaction. It demonstrates how local authorities can leverage commercial voice platforms to extend service delivery channels efficiently.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1334,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01028,EU_PSTW_202609_01028,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Driver and Vehicle Licensing Agency (DVLA),General public services n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,Alexa skill - vehicle tax and MOT,Driver and Vehicle Licensing Agency (DVLA); Central-Government; General public services n.e.c.; United Kingdom,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Driver and Vehicle Licensing Agency introduced an Alexa skill to simplify access to information about vehicle tax and MOT expiry dates. Vehicle owners often needed quick confirmation of compliance deadlines without navigating government websites. The objective was to provide a convenient, hands-free information channel. Users can ask Alexa when a vehicle’s tax or MOT is due by stating the registration number. The service connects Amazon’s voice recognition system with DVLA databases through secure application interfaces, retrieving real-time status information. Structured query handling ensures that only publicly accessible vehicle compliance data are returned, without disclosing personal details. The implementation reduces friction in accessing regulatory information and encourages timely renewals, supporting road safety and compliance. By leveraging a commercial voice platform, DVLA broadened service accessibility while maintaining existing digital channels. The initiative illustrates integration of public registers with consumer voice technologies for citizen-facing information services.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1335,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01029,EU_PSTW_202609_01029,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Department for Work and Pensions,Old age.,"By offering voice-based access, DWP aimed to enhance accessibility for users with limited digital literacy or visual impairments.",Alexa skill and Google home app - checking state pension age,Department for Work and Pensions; Central-Government; Old age.; United Kingdom,The service uses predefined conversational flows and structured eligibility rules based on date of birth.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Department for Work and Pensions developed a prototype voice service enabling citizens to check their state pension age through Amazon Alexa and Google Home devices. Many users sought quick clarification of eligibility timelines without navigating detailed guidance pages. The objective was to provide simple, accessible answers via voice interaction. The service uses predefined conversational flows and structured eligibility rules based on date of birth. Users provide relevant information verbally, which is processed through the respective voice assistant platforms and matched against pension age legislation encoded in the application logic. The system returns an estimated pension age without storing personal data beyond the session. By offering voice-based access, DWP aimed to enhance accessibility for users with limited digital literacy or visual impairments. The prototype demonstrated the feasibility of integrating statutory rules into consumer voice platforms while maintaining privacy safeguards and limiting responses to informational guidance rather than personalised entitlement decisions.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1336,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01030,EU_PSTW_202609_01030,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Her Majesty’s Revenue & Customers,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.",UNKNOWN,Alexa skill - enquiring about tax credits,"Her Majesty’s Revenue & Customers; Central-Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; United Kingdom",The service integrates structured knowledge content into Amazon’s voice assistant using defined intents and scripted dialogue paths. It illustrates the use of voice platforms for scalable dissemination of public information in high-demand administrative processes.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"HM Revenue and Customs launched an Alexa skill to support over three million tax credit customers during renewal periods, when contact volumes are typically high. The objective was to provide accessible guidance on renewal deadlines, required information and general eligibility queries. The service integrates structured knowledge content into Amazon’s voice assistant using defined intents and scripted dialogue paths. Users can ask about renewal dates, documentation requirements and payment information. The system retrieves up-to-date guidance from HMRC information sources without enabling transactional account access or disclosure of sensitive personal data. By automating responses to common enquiries, the skill reduces call centre demand and improves response times during peak periods. The initiative broadens digital engagement channels while preserving existing secure online and telephone services for account-specific matters. It illustrates the use of voice platforms for scalable dissemination of public information in high-demand administrative processes.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1337,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01031,EU_PSTW_202609_01031,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Met Office,"General economic, commercial and labour affair",UNKNOWN,Alexa skill - personalised weather report and forecast,"Met Office; Central-Government; General economic, commercial and labour affair; United Kingdom",UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The UK Met Office introduced an Alexa “Flash Briefing” skill to provide personalised weather forecasts and updates through voice interaction. The operational goal was to extend access to official meteorological information beyond traditional broadcast and web channels. Users can request weather updates for selected regions, receiving spoken forecasts tailored to their preferences. The service integrates the Met Office’s forecasting datasets with Amazon’s voice recognition and delivery platform. Structured content feeds and location-based parameters generate region-specific briefings, while forecast data are updated regularly from meteorological models and observation systems. The solution enhances accessibility, enabling hands-free access to authoritative weather information in homes and workplaces. It supports public safety and daily planning by delivering timely updates through widely adopted consumer devices. The deployment demonstrates integration of national forecasting infrastructure with commercial voice ecosystems to expand reach and user convenience.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1338,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01032,EU_PSTW_202609_01032,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Ministry of Public Administration,General services.,"The Ministry of Public Administration sought to improve organisational efficiency, workforce planning and employee satisfaction, but faced fragmented human resources data and limited analytical capacity to extract strategic insights.",Big Data Analysis for HR efficiency improvement,Ministry of Public Administration; Central-Government; General services.; Slovenia,The pilot project aimed to assess how a big data tool deployed on the Governmental State Cloud Infrastructure could support advanced analysis of HR datasets.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Ministry of Public Administration sought to improve organisational efficiency, workforce planning and employee satisfaction, but faced fragmented human resources data and limited analytical capacity to extract strategic insights. The pilot project aimed to assess how a big data tool deployed on the Governmental State Cloud Infrastructure could support advanced analysis of HR datasets. The solution integrated personnel records, performance indicators and administrative data into a centralised analytics environment, applying data mining and statistical techniques to identify patterns related to staffing levels, absenteeism and organisational performance. Dashboards and reporting tools enabled managers to explore trends and simulate scenarios for workforce optimisation. By consolidating data sources and automating analysis, the project enhanced evidence-based decision-making in HR management. Anticipated benefits include improved allocation of human resources, increased organisational effectiveness and better-informed strategies to support staff development and satisfaction, while demonstrating the scalability of cloud-based analytics within public administration.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1339,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01033,EU_PSTW_202609_01033,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Heidenheim,General public services n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,Kora - virtual assistant,Municipality of Heidenheim; Local Government; General public services n.e.c.; Germany,The system uses natural language processing to interpret user questions and match them to a structured knowledge base of predefined answers.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Municipality of Heidenheim introduced Kora, a virtual assistant designed to provide round-the-clock responses to common citizen enquiries. The objective was to improve accessibility to municipal information while reducing administrative workload. Kora operates as a self-learning chatbot integrated into the municipal website, handling approximately 70 enquiries per day with a reported success rate of 99 per cent at launch. The system uses natural language processing to interpret user questions and match them to a structured knowledge base of predefined answers. Machine-learning components refine intent recognition over time based on interaction patterns and supervised updates. Initial high-demand topics included weather, local events and coronavirus-related guidance. The chatbot standardises responses, shortens waiting times and ensures consistent information delivery outside office hours. By automating routine enquiries, the municipality can allocate staff resources to more complex cases requiring individual attention, enhancing service efficiency and digital engagement.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1340,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01034,EU_PSTW_202609_01034,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Munich,General public services n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,Chatbot Munich,Municipality of Munich; Local Government; General public services n.e.c.; Germany,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The IT-Department of the municipality of Munich is developing a chatbot for Munich which is supposed to complete the current services like the common emergency number 115, the service portal and mobile apps.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1341,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01035,EU_PSTW_202609_01035,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Karlsruhe,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.","Tax administrations receive large numbers of routine questions about declarations, deadlines, and procedures, and handling these enquiries manually can consume staff time that could otherwise be used for more complex taxpayer support.",Steuerchatbot,"Municipality of Karlsruhe; Local Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Germany",The implementation uses a conversational chatbot provided through the Baden-Württemberg tax administration’s digital service environment.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Tax administrations receive large numbers of routine questions about declarations, deadlines, and procedures, and handling these enquiries manually can consume staff time that could otherwise be used for more complex taxpayer support. In Baden-Württemberg, the Karlsruhe finance administration introduced a tax chatbot to answer simple questions related to tax declarations and make basic guidance available through a digital self-service channel. The objective is to improve accessibility to standard tax information, reduce waiting times for routine enquiries, and offer citizens a more convenient entry point into tax-related guidance. The implementation uses a conversational chatbot provided through the Baden-Württemberg tax administration’s digital service environment. At a conceptual level, the solution combines a question-and-answer interface with a structured knowledge base on tax topics, enabling users to ask common questions and receive immediate responses without having to contact the tax office directly. The chatbot is positioned as a front-line support tool for straightforward matters rather than for complex or case-specific advisory work. The impact includes quicker access to tax information, reduced pressure on telephone and counter services, and a more scalable way to provide standard guidance. It also improves the user experience by making tax support available through a simple digital interface that can handle repetitive enquiries consistently and at any time.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1342,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01036,EU_PSTW_202609_01036,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Deutsche Bahn,Transport,"The operational challenge was to manage high volumes of candidate enquiries and simplify application procedures. By automating responses to frequently asked questions and enabling conversational job matching, DB Smile accelerates the recruitment journey and reduces administrative burden.",DB Smile - the national railway service chatbot for job seekers,Deutsche Bahn; Central-Government; Transport; Germany,The system uses natural language processing to interpret queries and match candidates with suitable vacancies based on role preferences and qualifications.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Deutsche Bahn introduced DB Smile to streamline recruitment processes and support job seekers navigating employment opportunities within the national railway company. The operational challenge was to manage high volumes of candidate enquiries and simplify application procedures. DB Smile functions as an interactive chatbot on the company’s recruitment platform, guiding users through job searches, answering application-related questions and facilitating submission of applications via chat. The system uses natural language processing to interpret queries and match candidates with suitable vacancies based on role preferences and qualifications. Integrated with Deutsche Bahn’s recruitment database, the chatbot provides real-time information on openings and application status. By automating responses to frequently asked questions and enabling conversational job matching, DB Smile accelerates the recruitment journey and reduces administrative burden. The solution improves candidate experience, shortens response times and supports efficient talent acquisition within a large public transport organisation.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F06_HIGH_VOLUME_SCALABILITY_BOTTLENECK,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1343,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01037,EU_PSTW_202609_01037,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Deutsche Bahn,Transport,UNKNOWN,SEMMI - Avatar,Deutsche Bahn; Central-Government; Transport; Germany,"The system can be deployed on existing or new display hardware, indoors or outdoors, with or without touchscreen functionality, and adapts responsively to different screen sizes and formats.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Deutsche Bahn developed SEMMI Avatar to enhance customer service interactions in stations and other service environments by providing an engaging digital interface capable of handling routine enquiries. The operational objective is to complement human staff, improve accessibility and deliver consistent information across locations with varying infrastructure constraints. SEMMI Avatar is built on the SEMMI Conversational AI platform and combines speech recognition, natural language understanding and animated visual representation to create a multimodal human–machine interface. The system can be deployed on existing or new display hardware, indoors or outdoors, with or without touchscreen functionality, and adapts responsively to different screen sizes and formats. User queries are processed through conversational models that interpret intent and retrieve relevant information from integrated service databases. The avatar delivers responses through synchronised speech and visual cues, creating an interactive experience. The solution improves service availability, reduces waiting times for routine questions and supports scalable deployment across transport hubs and public-facing environments.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1344,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01038,EU_PSTW_202609_01038,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Deutsche Bahn,Transport,"Customer-facing organisations often struggle to provide multilingual, consistent and accessible front-office support, particularly in high-traffic environments such as transport hubs or service centres.",SEMMI - Robot,Deutsche Bahn; Central-Government; Transport; Germany,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Customer-facing organisations often struggle to provide multilingual, consistent and accessible front-office support, particularly in high-traffic environments such as transport hubs or service centres. SEMMI Robot was designed to enhance on-site customer interaction by answering routine enquiries in visitors’ native languages, reducing waiting times and improving service accessibility. Stakeholders include service providers, customer service staff and end users. The robot combines speech recognition, natural language understanding and multilingual conversational models to interpret user queries, retrieve relevant information from integrated knowledge bases and generate context-appropriate responses through speech synthesis and on-screen displays. The system connects to backend information systems, such as timetables or service databases, enabling real-time responses. Early deployments indicate improved user engagement, reduced pressure on frontline staff and enhanced customer experience, particularly for international users. The solution remains in progressive development and deployment phases across selected sites, with no confirmed large-scale standardisation across all potential service locations.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1345,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01039,EU_PSTW_202609_01039,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Deutsche Bahn,Transport,UNKNOWN,SEMMI - Hotline,Deutsche Bahn; Central-Government; Transport; Germany,"The system uses automatic speech recognition, intent classification models and dialogue management frameworks to conduct natural conversations, access integrated customer databases and provide real-time responses.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"High call volumes and repetitive enquiries place significant strain on customer service centres, leading to long waiting times and rising staffing costs. SEMMI Hotline was conceived as an advanced interactive voice response solution capable of handling routine enquiries autonomously while escalating complex cases to human agents. Stakeholders include contact centre operators, service agents and callers. The system uses automatic speech recognition, intent classification models and dialogue management frameworks to conduct natural conversations, access integrated customer databases and provide real-time responses. Cloud-based telephony integration allows rapid scaling to handle simultaneous calls without proportional increases in staffing. Reported benefits include reduced agent workload, shorter waiting times and improved scalability during peak demand. The solution continues to evolve, with ongoing enhancements to conversational capabilities; no definitive information indicates full replacement of traditional call centre models at scale.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1346,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01040,EU_PSTW_202609_01040,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Bad Hersfeld,General public services n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,Urban Cockpit - a digital twin of Bad Hersfeld,Municipality of Bad Hersfeld; Local Government; General public services n.e.c.; Germany,"The platform allows residents to monitor service availability and urban conditions, while municipal staff use the same data to support operational planning.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The municipality of Bad Hersfeld developed the Urban Cockpit as a publicly accessible digital twin to increase transparency and improve urban management. The objective was to aggregate heterogeneous municipal data into a single visual platform supporting informed decision-making and citizen engagement. The system integrates open-source datasets and sensor data into a geospatial dashboard that visualises real-time and static information. Data layers include parking occupancy, waste bin fill levels and locations of electric vehicle charging stations. Geographic information systems and data integration tools harmonise inputs from different municipal departments, enabling dynamic visualisation on an interactive map. The platform allows residents to monitor service availability and urban conditions, while municipal staff use the same data to support operational planning. By centralising urban indicators, the Urban Cockpit enhances situational awareness, promotes transparency and supports data-driven governance. The digital twin approach enables scalable expansion to additional datasets and smart city applications.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1347,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01041,EU_PSTW_202609_01041,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City Hall of Lisbon,Police Services,The operational challenge was to collect and interpret diverse mobility and contextual data to support coordinated transport planning and congestion mitigation.,Traffic flux representation with AI in Lisbon,City Hall of Lisbon; Local Government; Police Services; Portugal,"By transforming raw mobility data into actionable insights, Lisbon strengthens its capacity to manage traffic flux proactively, optimise infrastructure use and improve travel conditions for residents and visitors.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Lisbon City Council partnered with the National Civil Engineering Laboratory and Instituto Superior Técnico to improve integrated traffic management in a densely populated urban environment. The operational challenge was to collect and interpret diverse mobility and contextual data to support coordinated transport planning and congestion mitigation. The initiative deploys data analytics systems that gather information from traffic sensors, mobility services and contextual urban datasets. Machine-learning models classify traffic patterns and estimate flow dynamics across the road network, generating predictive and descriptive indicators for decision-makers. Integrated dashboards visualise congestion levels and support scenario analysis for traffic regulation measures. The system enhances coordination between agencies and enables evidence-based planning of mobility interventions. By transforming raw mobility data into actionable insights, Lisbon strengthens its capacity to manage traffic flux proactively, optimise infrastructure use and improve travel conditions for residents and visitors.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1348,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01042,EU_PSTW_202609_01042,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Copenhagen Airport,Transport,Managing baggage flows requires precise coordination of staff and conveyor systems to minimise delays and queue times. Optimisation algorithms recommend resource allocation to reduce bottlenecks and improve turnaround times.,Baggage handling at the Copenhagen Airport,Copenhagen Airport; Central-Government; Transport; Denmark,"The airport implemented predictive analytics models that analyse operational data, flight schedules and historical handling patterns to forecast where staff deployment would most effectively empty aircraft and load baggage onto conveyor belts.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Copenhagen Airport introduced data-driven optimisation in 2016 to improve baggage handling efficiency and passenger experience. Managing baggage flows requires precise coordination of staff and conveyor systems to minimise delays and queue times. The airport implemented predictive analytics models that analyse operational data, flight schedules and historical handling patterns to forecast where staff deployment would most effectively empty aircraft and load baggage onto conveyor belts. Optimisation algorithms recommend resource allocation to reduce bottlenecks and improve turnaround times. The system integrates with airport operational management platforms, providing decision support to supervisors. By anticipating workload peaks and adjusting staffing dynamically, the solution shortens waiting times, enhances punctuality and improves passenger satisfaction. The initiative demonstrates how advanced analytics can optimise complex logistics environments, increasing operational efficiency while maintaining safety and reliability in airport ground handling processes.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION|S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1349,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01043,EU_PSTW_202609_01043,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Rwth Aachen University,Cultural services.,Cultural heritage sites often face challenges in conveying their original scale and significance due to the loss or deterioration of structures.,"Cluny Abbey using AR and 3D recordings to visualise ""what has today disappeared""",Rwth Aachen University; Academic-Research; Cultural services.; Germany,"The town of Cluny implemented a digital heritage initiative to enhance visitor engagement by reconstructing historical environments that are no longer physically present. The solution uses augmented reality and three-dimensional modelling to recreate the abbey and its surroundings, based on historical documents, architectural plans, and visual records.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The town of Cluny implemented a digital heritage initiative to enhance visitor engagement by reconstructing historical environments that are no longer physically present. Cultural heritage sites often face challenges in conveying their original scale and significance due to the loss or deterioration of structures. The objective is to provide visitors with an immersive understanding of the abbey’s historical context. The solution uses augmented reality and three-dimensional modelling to recreate the abbey and its surroundings, based on historical documents, architectural plans, and visual records. Visitors can access digital reconstructions through AR interfaces installed on-site, enabling them to visualise past structures in their original locations. The system integrates a comprehensive database of 2D and 3D assets for both public engagement and scientific use. As an implemented solution, it enhances cultural interpretation, improves visitor experience, and supports research and preservation efforts.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1350,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01044,EU_PSTW_202609_01044,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Government of Flanders - Ghent University,Police Services,UNKNOWN,Fietsbarometer - barometer for cycleways safety,Government of Flanders - Ghent University; Regional Government; Police Services; Belgium,Municipal planners use these insights to inform infrastructure improvements and traffic management measures.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Province of Antwerp developed the Fietsbarometer as a web-based participatory platform to assess and improve cycling safety, particularly on home-to-school routes. The objective was to gather detailed, location-specific insights from adolescents regarding perceived and observed safety conditions in school neighbourhoods. Participants digitally map their daily cycling routes and evaluate factors such as infrastructure quality, traffic intensity and hazardous points. The platform aggregates geolocated inputs into spatial datasets, which are visualised through interactive mapping tools. Data analytics functions identify recurring safety concerns and prioritise areas requiring intervention. Municipal planners use these insights to inform infrastructure improvements and traffic management measures. By combining citizen-generated data with geographic information systems, the Fietsbarometer strengthens evidence-based policy-making and youth engagement in mobility planning. The approach enhances transparency and supports targeted investment in safer cycling environments across Flemish municipalities.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1351,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01045,EU_PSTW_202609_01045,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Berlin Public Transport Authority,Transport,Urban travellers often face fragmented booking and payment systems across transport providers.,Jelbi - the mobility app for Berlin's public and sharing services,Berlin Public Transport Authority; Local Government; Transport; Germany,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Jelbi was launched by Berlin’s public transport authority BVG to integrate public transport and shared mobility services into a single digital platform. Urban travellers often face fragmented booking and payment systems across transport providers. Jelbi aggregates buses, trains, carsharing, bike sharing and e-scooter services within one application, enabling route planning, booking and payment through a unified interface. The platform integrates real-time transport data, availability feeds from mobility partners and secure payment processing systems. Route optimisation algorithms propose multimodal journeys tailored to user preferences and current conditions. Physical Jelbi stations across Berlin provide co-located access to shared vehicles, reinforcing the digital–physical integration. The app enhances user convenience, encourages multimodal travel and supports sustainable urban mobility by reducing reliance on private car ownership. By centralising mobility services, Jelbi strengthens cooperation between public and private providers and contributes to Berlin’s smart city transport strategy.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1352,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01046,EU_PSTW_202609_01046,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Nordic Council of Ministers,Transport,Travellers often encounter fragmented booking systems and limited integration between national transport providers.,NOMAD - the travel-app to travel anywhere in the Nordics,Nordic Council of Ministers; Central-Government; Transport; Denmark,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Nordic Open Mobility and Digitalisation initiative aims to enable seamless cross-border travel across Nordic countries by promoting interoperable Mobility as a Service solutions. Travellers often encounter fragmented booking systems and limited integration between national transport providers. NOMAD seeks to establish a market-enabler framework combining technological standards and business practices that support MaaS roaming. The system architecture allows users to plan, book and manage multimodal journeys across borders through a single interface. Interoperability protocols connect transport operators, payment systems and journey planning services, facilitating integrated ticketing and administration of international trips. Data exchange standards ensure compatibility between participating platforms while respecting national regulatory requirements. By fostering cross-border cooperation and digital integration, NOMAD enhances user convenience, supports sustainable mobility choices and strengthens regional transport connectivity. The initiative contributes to harmonising smart mobility ecosystems across the Nordic region.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1353,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01047,EU_PSTW_202609_01047,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Associação Porto Digital,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.","Urban tourists often face fragmented sources of information covering points of interest, transport routes and local services.",Explore.Porto - discover and explore the city through a digital platform,"Associação Porto Digital; Local Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Portugal",UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Municipality of Porto developed Explore.Porto to provide visitors and residents with an integrated digital tool for navigating the city and accessing up-to-date information on attractions and mobility options. Urban tourists often face fragmented sources of information covering points of interest, transport routes and local services. The objective was to centralise these datasets into a single web-based and mobile application offering intuitive and real-time guidance. The platform aggregates geolocated information from municipal databases and authorised partners who can create and update points of interest on an interactive map. Route optimisation functions analyse available mobility options, suggesting the most efficient paths between selected locations and highlighting nearby attractions. The system integrates tourism content, transport data and spatial analytics within a unified interface, enabling personalised exploration of the city. By consolidating dispersed information streams, Explore.Porto enhances user experience, supports sustainable mobility choices and strengthens digital engagement with the city’s cultural and commercial ecosystem.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1355,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01048,EU_PSTW_202609_01048,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Ca' Foscari University of Venice,Cultural services.,"Archaeological artefacts are often discovered in thousands of fragments, making physical reconstruction a painstaking and time-consuming task that requires scarce expert skills. The RePAIR project addresses this challenge by supporting archaeologists and conservators in reconstructing fragmented cultural heritage.",RePAIR – Artificial Intelligence and Robotics for Cultural Heritage Reconstruction,Ca' Foscari University of Venice; Academic-Research; Cultural services.; Italy,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Archaeological artefacts are often discovered in thousands of fragments, making physical reconstruction a painstaking and time-consuming task that requires scarce expert skills. Many damaged artworks remain in storage for decades due to the effort required to restore them. The RePAIR project addresses this challenge by supporting archaeologists and conservators in reconstructing fragmented cultural heritage. Stakeholders include museums, researchers, conservation experts, and the public. The objective is to dramatically reduce the time and effort needed to restore large, shattered artefacts while preserving scientific and cultural integrity. The solution develops an intelligent robotic system capable of digitally analysing fragments, identifying matching pieces, and physically assembling artefacts autonomously. Advanced pattern recognition and shape-matching techniques guide robotic manipulation, enabling precise reconstruction. The system is tested on complex frescoes from the UNESCO World Heritage Site of Pompeii. RePAIR accelerates restoration timelines, reduces manual labour, and enables the recovery of artefacts previously considered too complex to reconstruct. It opens new possibilities for preserving and showcasing cultural heritage through scalable, technology-enabled conservation.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1356,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01049,EU_PSTW_202609_01049,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Federal Employment Agency,Unemployment.,UNKNOWN,Chatbot - German Federal Employment Agency,Federal Employment Agency; Central-Government; Unemployment.; Germany,It uses natural language processing to interpret user questions and match them with curated responses drawn from official guidance content. The system automates responses to routine enquiries while redirecting complex or case-specific matters to human advisers.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Federal Employment Agency introduced a chatbot to improve accessibility to information on employment services and social benefits. Citizens frequently seek guidance on unemployment benefits, short-time allowance, child supplement, training opportunities and related administrative procedures, creating high enquiry volumes. The chatbot provides a digital self-service channel available at all times through the Agency’s website. It uses natural language processing to interpret user questions and match them with curated responses drawn from official guidance content. Structured dialogue flows guide users to relevant pages, forms and contact points. The system automates responses to routine enquiries while redirecting complex or case-specific matters to human advisers. By standardising information delivery and reducing response times, the chatbot alleviates pressure on call centres and improves user convenience. The deployment supports scalable digital service provision while maintaining human oversight for personalised assistance and legal determinations.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1357,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01050,EU_PSTW_202609_01050,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,State Government of North Rhine-Westphalia,"General economic, commercial and labour affair","By automating responses to frequently asked questions, the system reduces administrative burden on service desks and improves accessibility for businesses seeking to register or modify operations.",Chatbot Guido,"State Government of North Rhine-Westphalia; Regional Government; General economic, commercial and labour affair; Germany",The State of North Rhine-Westphalia implemented Chatbot Guido to simplify access to information regarding online business registration procedures. Guido operates using predefined question–answer pairs and natural language processing components that classify user intent and deliver relevant guidance.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The State of North Rhine-Westphalia implemented Chatbot Guido to simplify access to information regarding online business registration procedures. Entrepreneurs and business owners often require clear guidance on administrative steps, documentation requirements and digital submission processes. The objective was to provide immediate, structured answers through a conversational interface integrated into the state’s business service portal. Guido operates using predefined question–answer pairs and natural language processing components that classify user intent and deliver relevant guidance. The chatbot directs users to appropriate online forms, explains procedural steps and clarifies regulatory requirements. By automating responses to frequently asked questions, the system reduces administrative burden on service desks and improves accessibility for businesses seeking to register or modify operations. The solution enhances efficiency in public service delivery and supports digitalisation of business administration processes across the state.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1358,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01051,EU_PSTW_202609_01051,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Integration Office of the State Government of Schleswig-Holstein,General public services n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,Digitale Assistenz Ina,Integration Office of the State Government of Schleswig-Holstein; Regional Government; General public services n.e.c.; Germany,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The state of Schleswig-Holstein introduced the digital assistant Ina to provide information related to the Integration Office, particularly on matters concerning disability and workplace inclusion. Citizens and employers often require guidance on rights, benefits and administrative procedures, leading to frequent general enquiries. Ina offers a conversational interface embedded in the state administration website, delivering responses based on curated knowledge content and structured dialogue paths. Natural language processing components interpret user input and map questions to predefined topics such as disability support, financial assistance and legal frameworks. The system provides general information and directs users to relevant forms or contact points, while complex or individual cases remain handled by human staff. By offering continuous digital assistance, Ina improves accessibility, reduces waiting times and supports consistent information dissemination. The initiative contributes to inclusive digital public services while preserving professional oversight for case-specific decisions.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1359,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01052,EU_PSTW_202609_01052,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Essen,Transport,"Managing Germany’s extensive road network requires continuous assessment of pavement condition and infrastructure assets, yet the volume and complexity of data make manual evaluation inefficient and costly.",TWIN4ROAD@Essen: AI-based analysis and forecast,City of Essen; Local Government; Transport; Germany,"Data are collected using ground penetrating radar, mobile laser scanning, 3D measurements and high-resolution imagery to create a detailed inventory of road surfaces and subsurface structures.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Managing Germany’s extensive road network requires continuous assessment of pavement condition and infrastructure assets, yet the volume and complexity of data make manual evaluation inefficient and costly. The City of Essen launched TWIN4ROAD to build a comprehensive digital representation of its road infrastructure to support maintenance planning and investment decisions. Data are collected using ground penetrating radar, mobile laser scanning, 3D measurements and high-resolution imagery to create a detailed inventory of road surfaces and subsurface structures. Advanced data analytics and machine learning models process these datasets to assess current damage, identify structural weaknesses and forecast potential pothole development. The resulting digital twin integrates condition data with spatial information systems, enabling automated evaluations and scenario analyses. The approach improves accuracy and timeliness of maintenance planning, reduces inspection costs and supports preventive interventions. By providing a reusable infrastructure inventory, the system also benefits other municipal services operating in public road space, enhancing cross-departmental coordination and long-term asset management.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1360,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01053,EU_PSTW_202609_01053,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,State Government of Schleswig-Holstein,R&D environmental protection.,UNKNOWN,KI4Forst - Monitoring the Forest with Satellite Data and AI,State Government of Schleswig-Holstein; Regional Government; R&D environmental protection.; Germany,The solution involves developing an automated processing chain that applies deep learning techniques to satellite imagery.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Schleswig-Holstein State Office for Surveying and Geoinformation aims to improve forest monitoring and management by leveraging satellite data. Traditional methods for assessing forest conditions are resource-intensive and may not provide timely or comprehensive insights into environmental changes such as drought damage or pest infestations. The objective is to enable automated, large-scale monitoring of forest areas and their condition. The solution involves developing an automated processing chain that applies deep learning techniques to satellite imagery. Image classification models, based on artificial neural networks, are trained to detect forest areas and distinguish between different forest types, such as deciduous and coniferous. The system also identifies changes in vegetation health and potential damage. Outputs are integrated into geospatial information systems for visualisation and decision support.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1361,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01054,EU_PSTW_202609_01054,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Flensburg,General public services n.e.c.,Residents often encounter difficulty identifying the correct department or procedure for specific requests.,Chatbot Govii,City of Flensburg; Local Government; General public services n.e.c.; Germany,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The City of Flensburg piloted Chatbot Govii in 2021 to streamline access to municipal administrative services and contact information. Residents often encounter difficulty identifying the correct department or procedure for specific requests. Govii provides a conversational interface on the city’s website, enabling users to ask questions in natural language and receive structured guidance. The system relies on predefined knowledge bases and intent recognition models to classify enquiries and retrieve relevant service descriptions, contact details and application links. By automating responses to routine queries, the chatbot reduces the need for direct phone or in-person enquiries and improves navigation of municipal services. The deployment enhances service efficiency, shortens response times and supports digital-first interaction with the administration. It also allows municipal staff to focus on complex cases requiring personalised attention.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1366,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01055,EU_PSTW_202609_01055,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Immigration Service, Patent and Registration Office and the Tax Administration",General public services n.e.c.,"Starting a business as a foreign entrepreneur can require interaction with multiple agencies covering immigration, registration, patents, and tax, and this fragmentation often creates confusion, duplicated effort, and avoidable delays.",Starting up smoothly: chatbot service (Virtual Agent Network) for foreign entrepreneurs in Finland,"Immigration Service, Patent and Registration Office and the Tax Administration; Central-Government; General public services n.e.c.; Finland","The implementation linked three virtual agents—Kamu, PatRek, and VeroBot—through a shared conversational network, using natural language understanding to detect when a user’s question fell outside one bot’s scope and hand over the conversation within the same chat window to the most relevant agency bot.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Starting a business as a foreign entrepreneur can require interaction with multiple agencies covering immigration, registration, patents, and tax, and this fragmentation often creates confusion, duplicated effort, and avoidable delays. In Finland, the “Starting up Smoothly” initiative was designed to reduce these barriers by connecting the conversational services of the Immigration Service, the Patent and Registration Office, and the Tax Administration into a more seamless user journey. The objective was to break down agency silos and give entrepreneurs a more coherent route through immigration, business-registration, and taxation questions. The implementation linked three virtual agents—Kamu, PatRek, and VeroBot—through a shared conversational network, using natural language understanding to detect when a user’s question fell outside one bot’s scope and hand over the conversation within the same chat window to the most relevant agency bot. However, Finnish official material from the start of the project described the inter-agency cooperation as a trial ending in June 2019, and Migri later announced that Kamu itself was discontinued after 31 October 2024, even though PRH still operates its Trade Register chatbot. The impact of the original project was to demonstrate smoother inter-agency digital support, but its current status is best described as partly absorbed into successor services rather than still running unchanged.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1367,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01056,EU_PSTW_202609_01056,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,State Government of Schleswig-Holstein,Pre-primary and primary education.,"Primary schools face a persistent literacy challenge, as a substantial share of pupils do not reach fluent reading speed by the end of primary education and often struggle to recover later.",120 Words per Minute - a Training App for Basal Automated Reading,State Government of Schleswig-Holstein; Regional Government; Pre-primary and primary education.; Germany,"The objective is to improve reading fluency, make diagnosis and support easier to use in everyday teaching, and enable children to practise independently or in pairs while giving teachers a more continuous way to monitor progress. The implementation centres on an app that digitises the phases of the existing reading-support programme and uses speech-to-text methods to automate the level check that children would otherwise complete manually.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Primary schools face a persistent literacy challenge, as a substantial share of pupils do not reach fluent reading speed by the end of primary education and often struggle to recover later. In Schleswig-Holstein, the “120 Words per Minute” project was conceived to support the “Lesen macht stark” programme by converting a printed diagnostic and training approach into a more flexible app-based tool for schools. The objective is to improve reading fluency, make diagnosis and support easier to use in everyday teaching, and enable children to practise independently or in pairs while giving teachers a more continuous way to monitor progress. The implementation centres on an app that digitises the phases of the existing reading-support programme and uses speech-to-text methods to automate the level check that children would otherwise complete manually. Public descriptions of the project present it as a digital education solution intended to support classroom use from the earliest training stages and to simplify the practical application of the methodology in school settings. The expected impact includes improved reading fluency, more practical use of diagnostic support at school level, and a more scalable method for early literacy intervention, especially for children at risk of falling behind.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,S3_DOCUMENT_INTELLIGENCE,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1368,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01057,EU_PSTW_202609_01057,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Schleswig-Holstein State Office for Surveying and Geo-Information,General services.,"Many specialised databases contain location references in unstructured textual form, making it difficult to integrate them into standardised geospatial systems.",KI4GeoSeDa – AI-Based Georeferencing of Specialist Data Pools for Semantic Data Integration,Schleswig-Holstein State Office for Surveying and Geo-Information; Regional Government; General services.; Germany,"The solution uses natural language processing and machine learning techniques to analyse address data and linguistic spatial references, converting them into precise geospatial coordinates. As a pilot project, its focused on algorithm design and validation includes with expected benefits including improved data integration, enhanced decision-making, and more efficient use of geospatial information.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Schleswig-Holstein State Office for Surveying and Geoinformation, in collaboration with the Karlsruhe Institute of Technology, aims to improve the integration of heterogeneous spatial datasets used in public administration. Many specialised databases contain location references in unstructured textual form, making it difficult to integrate them into standardised geospatial systems. The objective is to enable automated georeferencing and semantic integration of such data. The solution uses natural language processing and machine learning techniques to analyse address data and linguistic spatial references, converting them into precise geospatial coordinates. These outputs are then integrated into existing spatial data infrastructures, enabling unified access and analysis. The system supports semantic enrichment and interoperability across datasets. As a pilot project, its focused on algorithm design and validation includes with expected benefits including improved data integration, enhanced decision-making, and more efficient use of geospatial information.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F04_UNSTRUCTURED_INFORMATION_COMPLEXITY,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1369,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01058,EU_PSTW_202609_01058,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Westküste University of Applied Sciences,Environmental protection n.e.c.,"Monitoring weeds, crop health, and forest conditions typically requires significant manual effort and may lack precision at scale.",AI-Supported unmanned aeriasl vehicles (UAVs) for Ecological Agriculture and Forestry,Westküste University of Applied Sciences; Central-Government; Environmental protection n.e.c.; Germany,This project explores the use of unmanned aerial vehicles to support ecological agriculture and forestry management.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"This project explores the use of unmanned aerial vehicles to support ecological agriculture and forestry management. Monitoring weeds, crop health, and forest conditions typically requires significant manual effort and may lack precision at scale. The objective is to enable automated and efficient environmental monitoring and intervention. The solution involves adapting an existing AI-based weed detection system to operate on low-power embedded hardware mounted on drones. Machine learning models process visual data captured by onboard sensors to identify weeds and assess vegetation conditions in real time. The project also investigates the transferability of these models to forestry applications. Integration with drone platforms allows flexible and targeted data collection across large areas. As a research and development initiative, the system is still being tested and validated, with expected benefits including reduced labour requirements, improved precision, and more sustainable land management practices",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1370,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01059,EU_PSTW_202609_01059,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,University of Applied Sciences (FH) Kiel,Unemployment.,"Employment services often face challenges in matching individuals with suitable job opportunities and training pathways, particularly when dealing with diverse profiles and labour market dynamics.",Support the integration of job seekers with artificial intelligence,University of Applied Sciences (FH) Kiel; Academic-Research; Unemployment.; Germany,"The solution uses machine learning techniques, including self-organising maps and reinforcement learning, to analyse anonymised data and cluster job seekers based on skills, experience, and labour market conditions.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The University of Applied Sciences Kiel, in collaboration with Jobcenter Kiel and the Institute for Labour Market and Vocational Research, is developing tools to improve the integration of job seekers into the labour market. Employment services often face challenges in matching individuals with suitable job opportunities and training pathways, particularly when dealing with diverse profiles and labour market dynamics. The objective is to enhance counselling services through data-driven recommendations. The solution uses machine learning techniques, including self-organising maps and reinforcement learning, to analyse anonymised data and cluster job seekers based on skills, experience, and labour market conditions. These clusters are used to generate personalised recommendations for employment, retraining, and further education opportunities. The system is designed to complement the expertise of job centre staff and support decision-making processes. The project aims to improve matching efficiency and service effectiveness, although no evidence confirms full operational deployment.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1371,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01060,EU_PSTW_202609_01060,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,State Government of Schleswig-Holstein,Environmental protection n.e.c.,"Farmers and wildlife-protection volunteers have only a short time to locate hidden fawns in fields before mowing begins, and manual searches with thermal drones can still be labour-intensive, weather-sensitive, and dependent on multiple people.",Autonomous Fawn Tracking System - AROS,State Government of Schleswig-Holstein; Central-Government; Environmental protection n.e.c.; Germany,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Farmers and wildlife-protection volunteers have only a short time to locate hidden fawns in fields before mowing begins, and manual searches with thermal drones can still be labour-intensive, weather-sensitive, and dependent on multiple people. AROS was designed in Schleswig-Holstein to address this by automating more of the detection and localisation process so that fawns can be found and rescued more efficiently. The objective is to protect wildlife, reduce dependence on scarce human resources, and enable more reliable pre-harvest scanning of fields across different conditions. The implementation combines autonomous drone flight, thermal and visual sensing, computer-vision models based on convolutional neural networks, and geolocation functions that send the detected animal’s GPS position and aerial view to the farmer and hunter for intervention. Technical reporting describes an autonomous fawn-tracking system with high positioning accuracy, while project material presents the solution as a collaboration between nAIture and public actors in Schleswig-Holstein. The expected impact is faster rescue, better field coverage, and a more scalable wildlife-protection workflow during mowing season.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1372,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01061,EU_PSTW_202609_01061,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Ministry of Social Affairs, Youth, Family, Senior Citizens, Integration and Equality of Schleswig-Holstein",Public health services.,"Individuals and families often struggle to identify regional counselling centres, specialist doctors, care options and support services.",“Demenzwegweiser-SH” (Schleswig-Holstein Guide to Dementia),"Ministry of Social Affairs, Youth, Family, Senior Citizens, Integration and Equality of Schleswig-Holstein; Regional Government; Public health services.; Germany",UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Demenzwegweiser-SH was created to centralise and maintain up-to-date information on dementia-related services across Schleswig-Holstein. Individuals and families often struggle to identify regional counselling centres, specialist doctors, care options and support services. The platform functions as a searchable database providing structured information on medical, social and legal aspects of dementia, including nursing care insurance, legal guardianship and non-pharmacological therapies. Automated data processing techniques support continuous updating and categorisation of entries, ensuring that regional service information remains current. Information extraction and classification tools organise content into thematic categories and geographic locations. By consolidating dispersed information into a single digital guide, the platform enhances accessibility for patients, caregivers and professionals. It improves transparency of available support networks and facilitates informed decision-making regarding care pathways.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1373,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01062,EU_PSTW_202609_01062,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,University of Lübec,Fuel and energy,UNKNOWN,MASIRI – Multi-Agent Simulation for Integrated Energy and Mobility Systems,University of Lübec; Academic-Research; Fuel and energy; Germany,The objective is to understand and simulate interactions between human behaviour and intelligent energy systems to optimise resource use.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The integration of energy and mobility systems, such as vehicle-to-grid solutions, depends not only on technical optimisation but also on human behaviour and decision-making. Traditional system models often neglect how users’ experiences, preferences, and habits influence energy and mobility usage. The MASIRI project addresses this gap by exploring how behavioural insights can improve the design and regulation of integrated energy and mobility systems. Stakeholders include researchers, system designers, policymakers, and energy providers. The objective is to understand and simulate interactions between human behaviour and intelligent energy systems to optimise resource use. The solution develops a multi-agent simulation framework in which intelligent agents represent energy users with psychologically informed behaviour models. These agents interact within simulated vehicle-to-grid environments, allowing analysis of how different incentives, regulations, and system designs influence outcomes. The project improves understanding of behavioural dynamics, supports more realistic system modelling, and informs better policy and infrastructure design. MASIRI contributes to more efficient, user-centred, and resilient energy and mobility systems that align technical optimisation with real-world human behaviour.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1374,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01063,EU_PSTW_202609_01063,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Republic of Cyprus,Subsidiary services to education.,"The issuance and verification of professional licences for graduates in regulated professions, such as engineering or medicine, is often a manual and time-consuming process, particularly in cross-border contexts. In Cyprus and Greece, where many students study abroad, delays, administrative burden, and risks of fraud undermine efficiency and trust.",Licence to practice,Republic of Cyprus; Central-Government; Subsidiary services to education.; Cyprus,"The objective of this use case is to streamline the issuance, sharing, and verification of professional credentials across borders. The solution leverages the European Blockchain Services Infrastructure (EBSI) to enable secure, verifiable digital credentials.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The issuance and verification of professional licences for graduates in regulated professions, such as engineering or medicine, is often a manual and time-consuming process, particularly in cross-border contexts. In Cyprus and Greece, where many students study abroad, delays, administrative burden, and risks of fraud undermine efficiency and trust. The objective of this use case is to streamline the issuance, sharing, and verification of professional credentials across borders. The solution leverages the European Blockchain Services Infrastructure (EBSI) to enable secure, verifiable digital credentials. Academic and professional authorities issue licences in a digital format that can be shared and validated across jurisdictions using distributed ledger technology. The system ensures authenticity, integrity, and traceability of credentials while reducing reliance on paper-based processes. As a pilot initiative involving stakeholders such as GUnet, the Cypriot Deputy Ministry, and ETEK, it demonstrates potential to reduce processing times, administrative costs, and fraud risks, while improving cross-border mobility and trust in professional qualifications.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1375,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01064,EU_PSTW_202609_01064,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Helsinki,General public services n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,Helsinki Map Service,City of Helsinki; Local Government; General public services n.e.c.; Finland,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Helsinki Map Service is a browser-based geospatial platform providing comprehensive mapping and real-time urban data to residents, businesses and visitors. The operational objective is to centralise spatial information about city infrastructure, services and environmental conditions into an accessible digital interface. The system integrates geographic information systems with datasets covering traffic conditions, public transport, real estate, buildings, outdoor lighting, cycling and walking routes and municipal services. Real-time feeds update traffic and mobility layers, while thematic map tools allow users to visualise specific categories of interest. The platform supports interactive exploration, search functions and layered data visualisation through a web-based map viewer. By consolidating diverse geospatial datasets, the service enhances transparency, supports urban planning and enables residents to make informed mobility and property decisions. The initiative strengthens Helsinki’s data-driven governance and open data ecosystem.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1376,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01065,EU_PSTW_202609_01065,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Helsinki,General public services n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,Talbotti - Helsinki's financial management service chatbot,City of Helsinki; Local Government; General public services n.e.c.; Finland,The chatbot uses natural language processing to interpret questions and retrieve information from curated knowledge bases and financial service databases.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The City of Helsinki introduced Talbotti as a digital contact channel for its financial management services to improve accessibility and reduce pressure on traditional customer service channels. Residents and city employees frequently require guidance on invoicing, payments and financial procedures, often outside standard office hours. Talbotti provides round-the-clock responses to common enquiries and, during opening hours, allows seamless transfer to a live chat with a customer adviser. The chatbot uses natural language processing to interpret questions and retrieve information from curated knowledge bases and financial service databases. It offers self-service guidance, up-to-date information and direct links to relevant webpages, streamlining access to forms and instructions. Interaction data are analysed to refine responses and improve intent recognition over time. By automating routine queries, Talbotti shortens response times, enhances user experience and enables staff to focus on complex cases requiring individual assessment. The solution strengthens service continuity and operational efficiency within municipal financial administration.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1377,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01066,EU_PSTW_202609_01066,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Helsinki,General public services n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,Sotebotti hester,City of Helsinki; Local Government; General public services n.e.c.; Finland,Hester operates using predefined dialogue paths and natural language understanding components that classify user intent and retrieve content from official service databases.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Helsinki’s Social Services, Health Care and Rescue Services Division introduced Sotebotti Hester to improve access to information across a broad range of welfare services. Citizens often seek guidance on maternity clinics, child health services and other social and health-related provisions. The chatbot provides a digital interface available at all times, delivering structured information and directing users to relevant services. Hester operates using predefined dialogue paths and natural language understanding components that classify user intent and retrieve content from official service databases. It guides users through service descriptions, contact information and procedural steps, while complex or sensitive matters remain handled by professionals. By automating responses to frequently asked questions, the system reduces call volumes and enhances accessibility outside office hours. The solution supports consistent information delivery and facilitates navigation of health and social care services, contributing to improved user experience and more efficient allocation of administrative resources.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1378,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01067,EU_PSTW_202609_01067,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Logistics Community Brabant (LCB),Transport,"Coordinated by Logistics Community Brabant in collaboration with academic partners and Argaleo, the initiative addresses the need for integrated analysis of mobility bottlenecks and opportunities in urban environments.",Dutch cycling intelligence,Logistics Community Brabant (LCB); Academic-Research; Transport; Netherlands,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Dutch Cycling Intelligence programme developed the Digital Twin “Atlas of the Accessible City” to provide data-driven insights into urban accessibility and cycling infrastructure. Coordinated by Logistics Community Brabant in collaboration with academic partners and Argaleo, the initiative addresses the need for integrated analysis of mobility bottlenecks and opportunities in urban environments. The Atlas aggregates publicly available datasets and, where applicable, private data sources into a three-dimensional geospatial visualisation platform. Geographic information systems and data analytics tools map traffic flows, cycling routes and accessibility indicators, enabling stakeholders to identify congestion points and infrastructural gaps at a glance. The digital twin translates policy questions into measurable spatial insights, supporting planning decisions and infrastructure investment. By centralising and visualising complex datasets, the system enhances collaboration between public authorities and research institutions, strengthens evidence-based mobility policy and improves strategic planning for accessible and sustainable urban transport networks.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1380,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01068,EU_PSTW_202609_01068,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Utrecht,Transport,"Geospatial modelling and traffic data processing tools visualise cycling flows, detect bottlenecks such as traffic light delays and evaluate accessibility scenarios.",Atlas Cyclable Utrecht,City of Utrecht; Local Government; Transport; Netherlands,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Commissioned by the Province of Utrecht, Atlas Fietsbaar Utrecht was developed within a digital twin framework to assess cycling accessibility, particularly in relation to educational institutions. Policymakers required detailed insights into route quality, infrastructure performance and optimal locations for bicycle tunnels. The platform integrates publicly available transport data with advanced bicycle usage analyses into a centralised dashboard. Geospatial modelling and traffic data processing tools visualise cycling flows, detect bottlenecks such as traffic light delays and evaluate accessibility scenarios. The dashboard enables users to explore route efficiency and identify priority areas for infrastructure development. By translating mobility data into actionable policy insights, the Atlas supports targeted investment in cycling infrastructure and promotes sustainable transport. The system enhances transparency and decision-making efficiency by offering user-friendly access to consolidated, data-driven indicators relevant to regional planning and educational accessibility strategies.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1381,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01069,EU_PSTW_202609_01069,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Logistics Community Brabant (LCB),Transport,UNKNOWN,Atlas Multimodal Brabant – Digital Twin for Freight Corridors (Netherlands),Logistics Community Brabant (LCB); Academic-Research; Transport; Netherlands,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Atlas Multimodal Brabant was developed by Argaleo for Logistics Community Brabant to support coordination of multimodal freight transport corridors. Transport operators require clear visibility of shared routes, transhipment terminals and environmental performance indicators to optimise logistics planning. The digital twin aggregates data on transport corridors, terminal locations and CO₂ reduction targets into an interactive geospatial platform. Users can identify accessible terminals from their location, evaluate route options and contact operators directly through integrated communication functions. Data integration and visual analytics tools provide insights into corridor usage and sustainability performance, enabling evidence-based decisions on modal shifts and collaborative transport solutions. By centralising freight information and environmental metrics, the Atlas enhances transparency, supports emission reduction goals and improves efficiency in regional logistics networks. The platform strengthens cooperation between public authorities, academic institutions and private transport actors within Brabant’s multimodal strategy.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1382,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01070,EU_PSTW_202609_01070,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of 's-Hertogenbosch,Police Services,UNKNOWN,Crowd management dashboard in 's-Hertogenbosch,City of 's-Hertogenbosch; Local Government; Police Services; Netherlands,"Following the COVID-19 pandemic, the municipality of ’s-Hertogenbosch implemented a digital dashboard to manage pedestrian flows safely during reopening phases.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Following the COVID-19 pandemic, the municipality of ’s-Hertogenbosch implemented a digital dashboard to manage pedestrian flows safely during reopening phases. The operational objective was to monitor visitor density and movement patterns in real time to prevent overcrowding. Developed by Argaleo, the dashboard integrates pedestrian sensor data and historical mobility datasets into a unified visual interface. Data analytics tools process current flow information and analyse trends, answering questions such as peak entry times and predominant directions of movement. The system provides municipal staff with situational awareness to support timely crowd control measures and communication strategies. By combining real-time monitoring with historical analysis, the dashboard enables data-driven decisions that balance public health considerations with economic activity. The initiative enhances urban resilience and supports safe city centre management through digital twin technologies and mobility analytics.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1383,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01071,EU_PSTW_202609_01071,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Technical University of Delft,Police Services,UNKNOWN,Outdoor Mobility Digital Twin at TU Delft,Technical University of Delft; Academic-Research; Police Services; Netherlands,"These data are visualised in a three-dimensional digital twin dashboard, using heat maps to indicate crowd density and movement patterns.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Technical University of Delft developed a digital twin of its campus environment to analyse and manage pedestrian and cyclist flows during the COVID-19 pandemic. Managing crowd density in public spaces became critical to ensure safety and compliance with health measures. The objective is to provide real-time insights into mobility patterns and support data-driven decision-making for crowd management. The solution integrates data from sensors that convert visual inputs into privacy-preserving representations, combined with external data sources such as public transport locations and infrastructure status. These data are visualised in a three-dimensional digital twin dashboard, using heat maps to indicate crowd density and movement patterns. Advanced analytics are applied to identify congestion and support scenario analysis. As a pilot, the system demonstrates the potential to improve situational awareness, enhance safety, and optimise space utilisation, although no evidence confirms long-term operational deployment.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1384,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01072,EU_PSTW_202609_01072,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Senigallia,General public services n.e.c.,"The objective is to improve responsiveness, make common information easier to obtain, and reduce the burden on municipal staff dealing with repetitive requests.",Chatbot - Municipality of Senigallia,Municipality of Senigallia; Local Government; General public services n.e.c.; Italy,"In Senigallia, this use case centres on a chatbot introduced on the municipality’s official website to respond to general citizen enquiries and provide a more accessible first point of contact. The implementation uses a website-based conversational interface embedded in the municipality’s digital environment.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Municipal administrations often receive a high volume of general enquiries from citizens, many of which concern routine information that can be handled more efficiently through digital self-service than through direct staff intervention. In Senigallia, this use case centres on a chatbot introduced on the municipality’s official website to respond to general citizen enquiries and provide a more accessible first point of contact. The objective is to improve responsiveness, make common information easier to obtain, and reduce the burden on municipal staff dealing with repetitive requests. The implementation uses a website-based conversational interface embedded in the municipality’s digital environment. At a conceptual level, the chatbot serves as a front-end guidance tool that interprets user questions and returns prepared answers or directions to the relevant information, helping citizens navigate services without needing to identify the correct office in advance. The solution is intended for general enquiries rather than personalised case handling, making it suitable for high-volume, low-complexity interactions. The impact includes faster access to municipal information, improved user convenience, and a more efficient allocation of staff effort. It also supports the wider digitalisation of citizen services by creating a scalable channel for routine communication and by improving the municipality’s ability to handle enquiries outside traditional contact methods. The official municipal site confirms the broader digital service context in which this type of web-based support is delivered.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1385,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01073,EU_PSTW_202609_01073,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Treviso,General public services n.e.c.,"Citizens interacting with municipal services often need quick answers to straightforward questions, yet websites and administrative structures can be difficult to navigate without prior knowledge of which office is responsible. The objective is to offer a more immediate and accessible way to obtain basic information, reduce dependence on manual front-office responses, and simplify the first step in accessing public services.",Chatbot - Municipality of Treviso,Municipality of Treviso; Local Government; General public services n.e.c.; Italy,"In Treviso, this use case involves a chatbot introduced on the municipality’s official website to respond to general enquiries and improve day-to-day communication with residents. The implementation uses a web-based chatbot integrated into the municipality’s digital environment.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Citizens interacting with municipal services often need quick answers to straightforward questions, yet websites and administrative structures can be difficult to navigate without prior knowledge of which office is responsible. In Treviso, this use case involves a chatbot introduced on the municipality’s official website to respond to general enquiries and improve day-to-day communication with residents. The objective is to offer a more immediate and accessible way to obtain basic information, reduce dependence on manual front-office responses, and simplify the first step in accessing public services. The implementation uses a web-based chatbot integrated into the municipality’s digital environment. At a conceptual level, the solution provides a conversational entry point through which users can ask general questions and receive guidance towards the relevant information or service area, rather than navigating the full structure of the administration unaided. As with many municipal chatbots, its role is likely focused on standard information and routine support rather than on complex individual casework. The impact includes faster access to general municipal information, improved convenience for users, and more efficient handling of repetitive enquiries. It also supports service scalability by extending the municipality’s digital front door and strengthening the consistency of first-line communication across online channels. The municipality’s current digital portal and service structure show the wider administrative context into which this kind of citizen-support functionality fits.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1386,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01074,EU_PSTW_202609_01074,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,University of Helsinki,General public services n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,Hubot – University of Helsinki Student Advisory Chatbot,University of Helsinki; Academic-Research; General public services n.e.c.; Finland,"The University of Helsinki implemented Hubot to support student advisory services and improve access to information regarding applications and studies. Hubot operates as a conversational interface on the university’s digital platforms, using natural language processing to interpret queries and match them to curated guidance content.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The University of Helsinki implemented Hubot to support student advisory services and improve access to information regarding applications and studies. Prospective and current students frequently require guidance on admission procedures, course selection and academic regulations. Hubot operates as a conversational interface on the university’s digital platforms, using natural language processing to interpret queries and match them to curated guidance content. The chatbot retrieves instructions from official knowledge bases and directs users to relevant forms and webpages. If unable to resolve an enquiry, Hubot generates a service request to student advisers or transfers the user to a live chat when available. By automating responses to common questions, the system reduces response times and supports continuous access to essential academic information. The deployment enhances efficiency in student services while preserving human interaction for complex or individual cases requiring personalised support.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1387,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01075,EU_PSTW_202609_01075,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Healthcare Service of Valle d'Aosta,General public services n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,Chatbot - Healthcare Unit of Aosta Valley,Healthcare Service of Valle d'Aosta; Regional Government; General public services n.e.c.; Italy,The chatbot uses natural language processing to interpret user questions and retrieve validated responses from structured health information databases and frequently asked questions curated by the healthcare authority.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Healthcare Unit of Aosta Valley introduced a chatbot on its official website to manage high volumes of citizen enquiries during the COVID-19 pandemic. Residents required timely information regarding vaccination campaigns, health certifications and related administrative procedures, creating significant demand on telephone and in-person services. The objective was to provide continuous, reliable guidance while reducing pressure on frontline staff. The chatbot uses natural language processing to interpret user questions and retrieve validated responses from structured health information databases and frequently asked questions curated by the healthcare authority. It delivers guidance on vaccination booking, certification requirements and procedural updates, redirecting complex medical or personal cases to appropriate health professionals. By automating responses to recurring enquiries, the system improves accessibility outside office hours and ensures consistent communication. The deployment enhanced service efficiency, reduced waiting times and supported transparent dissemination of public health information during a period of rapidly evolving regulations.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1388,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01076,EU_PSTW_202609_01076,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Training, Guidance and Employment Agency Monza and Brianza",Unemployment.,UNKNOWN,"Chatbot - Training, Guidance and Employment Agency Monza and Brianza","Training, Guidance and Employment Agency Monza and Brianza; Local Government; Unemployment.; Italy","The Training, Guidance and Employment Agency of Monza and Brianza implemented a chatbot to streamline access to information on employment services, training programmes and administrative procedures. It operates using predefined knowledge bases and natural language understanding components that classify user intent and match queries with relevant information.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Training, Guidance and Employment Agency of Monza and Brianza implemented a chatbot to streamline access to information on employment services, training programmes and administrative procedures. Citizens and job seekers frequently require guidance on registration, vocational courses and support measures, often generating repetitive enquiries. The chatbot provides a digital self-service channel integrated into the agency’s website, offering continuous responses to general questions. It operates using predefined knowledge bases and natural language understanding components that classify user intent and match queries with relevant information. The system directs users to appropriate forms, deadlines and contact details, while more complex or individual cases are referred to staff. By automating routine information delivery, the chatbot reduces response times and administrative workload. The solution enhances accessibility to employment services and supports digital transformation within local labour market administration.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1389,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01077,EU_PSTW_202609_01077,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Chamber of Commerce of Reggio Emilia,"General economic, commercial and labour affair",UNKNOWN,Chatbot - Chamber of Commerce of Reggio Emilia,"Chamber of Commerce of Reggio Emilia; Local Government; General economic, commercial and labour affair; Italy","Using natural language processing and curated question–answer datasets, it interprets user input and retrieves relevant procedural information and links to official documentation.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Chamber of Commerce of Reggio Emilia introduced a chatbot on its official website to improve access to information for citizens and businesses. Companies frequently seek guidance on registration procedures, certifications, regulatory obligations and digital services, generating substantial demand on administrative staff. The chatbot provides structured responses to general enquiries through a conversational interface available at all times. Using natural language processing and curated question–answer datasets, it interprets user input and retrieves relevant procedural information and links to official documentation. The system supports navigation of digital services, application processes and contact channels, while personalised or legally complex matters remain handled by human advisers. By standardising answers and automating routine interactions, the chatbot enhances service efficiency, shortens response times and improves user experience. The initiative contributes to the Chamber’s digital service modernisation and supports business-friendly administrative access.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1390,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01078,EU_PSTW_202609_01078,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Ministry of Foreign Affairs,Foreign economic aid.,"Aid disbursement in fragile or corruption-prone environments raises a longstanding public-administration challenge: how to ensure that funds reach the intended beneficiaries securely, transparently, and with reduced leakage.",Blockchain to increase security in foreign aid disbursement in Demnark,Ministry of Foreign Affairs; Central-Government; Foreign economic aid.; Denmark,"At a conceptual level, the proposed implementation would use blockchain-based transaction records and digital-payment mechanisms to track disbursements and potentially route aid more directly to end recipients.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Aid disbursement in fragile or corruption-prone environments raises a longstanding public-administration challenge: how to ensure that funds reach the intended beneficiaries securely, transparently, and with reduced leakage. The Danish Ministry of Foreign Affairs explored this issue in a Danida report that considered whether blockchain and cryptocurrency-based approaches could improve traceability and integrity in development assistance. The objective was to test whether distributed-ledger models might reduce corruption risks, strengthen beneficiary access to funds, and modernise aid-delivery mechanisms. At a conceptual level, the proposed implementation would use blockchain-based transaction records and digital-payment mechanisms to track disbursements and potentially route aid more directly to end recipients. The case is therefore framed as an innovation concept for development cooperation rather than a conventional administrative service. The expected impact lies in stronger transparency, improved traceability of transfers, and greater confidence that funds can reach intended beneficiaries safely, while also contributing to wider debate on how emerging financial technologies might support more accountable aid delivery.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1392,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01079,EU_PSTW_202609_01079,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Publications Office of the European Union,General public services n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,Chatbot Publio - EU Publication Office,Publications Office of the European Union; European Institution/Agency; General public services n.e.c.; European Union,The assistant uses natural language processing to interpret user queries and match them with indexed content across the Publications Office website.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Publio is the virtual assistant of the Publications Office of the European Union, designed to help users navigate extensive digital repositories of EU documents and institutional information. Researchers, professionals and citizens often require support in locating specific publications, organisations or officials within complex databases. Publio provides a multilingual conversational interface available in English, French and Spanish. The assistant uses natural language processing to interpret user queries and match them with indexed content across the Publications Office website. It retrieves document references, organisational details and relevant links, guiding users through search results without requiring advanced search skills. By facilitating structured interaction with large document repositories, Publio improves discoverability, reduces search time and enhances accessibility to EU information resources. The solution supports transparency and public access to official documentation through scalable digital assistance.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1393,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01080,EU_PSTW_202609_01080,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Federal Ministry of Justice,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.","Investigators face challenges in extracting relevant information from heterogeneous datasets, including documents, communication records and transactional data.",AI for analysing investigative data in Austria,"Federal Ministry of Justice; Central-Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Austria","Austrian authorities implemented advanced analytics tools to process and analyse large volumes of investigative data in complex cases involving fraud and financial crime. The solution applies machine-learning techniques for classification, metadata extraction and relationship detection across structured and unstructured data sources.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Austrian authorities implemented advanced analytics tools to process and analyse large volumes of investigative data in complex cases involving fraud and financial crime. Investigators face challenges in extracting relevant information from heterogeneous datasets, including documents, communication records and transactional data. The solution applies machine-learning techniques for classification, metadata extraction and relationship detection across structured and unstructured data sources. Natural language processing and pattern recognition models identify semantic correlations, trace information origins and map communication flows. Visual analytics components generate network graphs and fraud pattern visualisations without requiring custom coding for each case. The system integrates into investigative workflows, supporting analysts in identifying hidden links and prioritising lines of enquiry. By automating data processing and pattern detection, the solution accelerates investigations, improves analytical depth and enhances evidence-based decision-making while maintaining human oversight in prosecutorial actions.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S3_DOCUMENT_INTELLIGENCE,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1394,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01081,EU_PSTW_202609_01081,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Court of Ravenna,Law courts,UNKNOWN,Court of Ravenna - digital signature,Court of Ravenna; Local Government; Law courts; Italy,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Courts increasingly need secure, traceable ways to authenticate documents and preserve confidence in judicial records as procedures become more digital. In Ravenna, this project was described as an enhancement to the court’s existing IT system through the addition of a digital-signature solution and data storage on a private or consortium permissioned blockchain. The objective is to improve the integrity, authenticity, and traceability of judicial documents while fitting within an existing court information environment rather than replacing it. The implementation, as described in European public-sector blockchain case documentation, combines digital-signature workflows with permissioned distributed-ledger storage so that document events and validation information can be recorded in a controlled blockchain environment. At a conceptual level, the solution is aimed at strengthening evidential trust and process reliability in a judicial setting where records must remain tamper-evident and attributable. The anticipated impact is stronger document security, better traceability, and a more robust digital workflow for judicial administration, especially where authenticity and auditability are essential to procedural trust.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1395,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01082,EU_PSTW_202609_01082,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Department of Justice,Law courts,"Malta sought to modernise the management of lawyers’ warrants by replacing fragmented and paper-based records with a transparent, reliable and accessible digital register.",Lawyers' blockchain-based register in Malta,Department of Justice; Central-Government; Law courts; Malta,A blockchain-based architecture was implemented to store and synchronise records in an immutable and decentralised ledger.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Malta sought to modernise the management of lawyers’ warrants by replacing fragmented and paper-based records with a transparent, reliable and accessible digital register. Previously, verifying whether a lawyer was duly authorised required consultation of administrative records that were not fully interoperable or publicly searchable, limiting transparency for citizens and institutions. The pilot project aimed to create the first electronic register of lawyers in Malta, enabling government, legal professionals and the public to access consistent and up-to-date warrant information. A blockchain-based architecture was implemented to store and synchronise records in an immutable and decentralised ledger. The system was initially developed as a private blockchain to ensure controlled participation, with a design allowing future migration to a public blockchain for non-personal data such as names and warrant status. Cryptographic mechanisms ensure data integrity and traceability of updates, while authorised entities can validate entries in real time. The initiative enhances trust in legal services, reduces administrative verification time and strengthens transparency, providing a scalable digital infrastructure for professional registers.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1396,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01083,EU_PSTW_202609_01083,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Ministry of Justice and Security,Law courts,"Government approval workflows often involve many actors, documents, and hand-offs, making it difficult to ensure transparency, trust, and efficient coordination across the full chain of transactions and authorisations. At a conceptual level, the approach seeks to create a shared, tamper-evident process layer across approvals, rather than relying only on fragmented back-office records.",DigiAkkoord - blockchain-based approval processes for government activities,Ministry of Justice and Security; Central-Government; Law courts; Netherlands,"DigiAkkoord was presented as a project using a permissioned blockchain to support workflow, transaction, and document approval processes in government. The implementation, as described in European public-sector blockchain material, uses a permissioned distributed ledger to record approval events and workflow progression in a controlled environment suitable for public administration.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Government approval workflows often involve many actors, documents, and hand-offs, making it difficult to ensure transparency, trust, and efficient coordination across the full chain of transactions and authorisations. DigiAkkoord was presented as a project using a permissioned blockchain to support workflow, transaction, and document approval processes in government. The objective is to increase trust, improve traceability, and streamline approval procedures where multiple parties need a shared record of validated process steps. The implementation, as described in European public-sector blockchain material, uses a permissioned distributed ledger to record approval events and workflow progression in a controlled environment suitable for public administration. At a conceptual level, the approach seeks to create a shared, tamper-evident process layer across approvals, rather than relying only on fragmented back-office records. The expected impact is greater transparency, stronger process integrity, and better efficiency in document approval and inter-organisational workflow handling, particularly where several public actors need a common, trusted view of procedural progress.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1397,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01084,EU_PSTW_202609_01084,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Ministry of Justice and Security,Police Services,"International travel requires repeated identity checks across airlines, airports, border agencies, and other actors, and this fragmented process can create friction, redundancy, and security inefficiencies. The objective was to test a traveller-centric identity model that could support smoother airport passage, reduce repeated manual checks, and improve trust between public and private travel actors.",Known Traveller Digital Identity Pilot Project (KTDI),Ministry of Justice and Security; Central-Government; Police Services; Netherlands,"In the pilot model, travellers would upload information in advance, and the system would use biometric recognition, including facial-image matching, to support functions such as automated check-in and airport gate passage. The impact of the project lies in demonstrating a possible future travel architecture and informing best practices for digital identity in cross-border mobility, with potential benefits in speed, convenience, interoperability, and more efficient use of identity data across the travel ecosystem.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"International travel requires repeated identity checks across airlines, airports, border agencies, and other actors, and this fragmented process can create friction, redundancy, and security inefficiencies. KTDI was developed to explore whether a trusted, decentralised digital identity could simplify this end-to-end journey by allowing traveller data to be shared and reused in a more secure and interoperable way. The objective was to test a traveller-centric identity model that could support smoother airport passage, reduce repeated manual checks, and improve trust between public and private travel actors. The implementation combined decentralised identity concepts with technologies such as biometrics, blockchain, mobile devices, and cryptography. In the pilot model, travellers would upload information in advance, and the system would use biometric recognition, including facial-image matching, to support functions such as automated check-in and airport gate passage. The impact of the project lies in demonstrating a possible future travel architecture and informing best practices for digital identity in cross-border mobility, with potential benefits in speed, convenience, interoperability, and more efficient use of identity data across the travel ecosystem.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1399,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01085,EU_PSTW_202609_01085,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Swedish Tax Agency,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.","The Swedish Tax Agency identified inefficiencies in accounting, auditing and taxation processes linked to paper-based and fragmented receipt management, which increases administrative burden for businesses and limits real-time oversight. Digital receipts are standardised, stored electronically and linked to accounting systems, reducing manual input and reconciliation tasks.",Digital receipt processing at the Swedish Tax Agency,"Swedish Tax Agency; Central-Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Sweden",UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Swedish Tax Agency identified inefficiencies in accounting, auditing and taxation processes linked to paper-based and fragmented receipt management, which increases administrative burden for businesses and limits real-time oversight. The project aimed to explore digital solutions capable of streamlining receipt handling and improving data reliability for tax purposes. Following an initial phase examining blockchain-inspired approaches to accounting and auditing, the second phase focused on end-to-end digital receipt processing for companies. The solution tested distributed ledger concepts and secure data-sharing mechanisms to record transaction data in a tamper-resistant format, enabling authorised stakeholders to access verified information directly. Digital receipts are standardised, stored electronically and linked to accounting systems, reducing manual input and reconciliation tasks. The approach supports automated validation and traceability of transactions, facilitating more efficient audits and compliance checks. Expected benefits include reduced administrative costs for businesses, improved accuracy of tax reporting and enhanced transparency in financial flows, contributing to a more efficient and trustworthy taxation ecosystem.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1401,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01086,EU_PSTW_202609_01086,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Swedish Tax Agency,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.",UNKNOWN,Chatbot Skatti - Swedish Tax Agency,"Swedish Tax Agency; Central-Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Sweden",UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Swedish Tax Agency introduced Chatbot Skatti to provide accessible guidance on tax-related matters through its official website. Citizens frequently seek clarification on filing procedures, deadlines, deductions and digital services, creating recurring enquiries. Skatti offers a conversational interface available around the clock, delivering responses to general questions based on curated tax guidance content. The system employs natural language processing to interpret user input and match it with predefined intents and information sources. It directs users to relevant forms, explanatory pages and digital services while refraining from handling personalised account data. By automating answers to routine enquiries, Skatti reduces call centre workload, improves response times and enhances user convenience. The chatbot supports consistent dissemination of tax information and contributes to the Agency’s broader digital service strategy.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1402,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01087,EU_PSTW_202609_01087,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Fraunhofer Heinrich Hertz Institute (HHI),R&D education.,The FAKE-ID project addresses this challenge by supporting the identification of falsified and manipulated identities in video material.,FAKE-ID - Video analysis using AI to detect false and manipulated identities,Fraunhofer Heinrich Hertz Institute (HHI); Academic-Research; R&D education.; Germany,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The rapid spread of manipulated video content poses growing risks to trust, security, and democratic processes, as falsified or synthetic identities can be used for fraud, disinformation, and impersonation. Public authorities, media organisations, and digital platforms require reliable methods to detect such content at scale. The FAKE-ID project addresses this challenge by supporting the identification of falsified and manipulated identities in video material. Stakeholders include security agencies, technology providers, and institutions responsible for safeguarding digital integrity. The objective is to improve the detection of deep-fake videos and manipulated identity representations to strengthen trust in digital media. The solution develops advanced video analysis methods that examine visual and temporal inconsistencies in facial movements, expressions, and image artefacts. Trained models analyse video streams to identify signs of synthetic manipulation under varying recording conditions. The project establishes robust evaluation frameworks and datasets to ensure reliability. The impact includes improved detection accuracy of manipulated videos, reduced risk of identity fraud, and enhanced resilience against disinformation. FAKE-ID contributes to safer digital environments and supports the development of trustworthy media verification technologies.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1403,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01088,EU_PSTW_202609_01088,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Fraunhofer Institute for Computer Graphics Research IGD,"Agriculture, forestry, fishing and hunting","Monitoring fish populations such as sea trout is essential for environmental conservation and fisheries management, but traditional counting methods based on manual observation are labour-intensive and prone to error.",Environmental monitoring of sea trouts with AI,"Fraunhofer Institute for Computer Graphics Research IGD; Central-Government; Agriculture, forestry, fishing and hunting; Germany","The objective of this use case is to automate the monitoring process and improve accuracy in species identification and counting. The solution uses underwater cameras to capture video sequences, which are analysed using deep learning models trained to distinguish sea trout from other objects such as leaves or different fish species.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Monitoring fish populations such as sea trout is essential for environmental conservation and fisheries management, but traditional counting methods based on manual observation are labour-intensive and prone to error. The objective of this use case is to automate the monitoring process and improve accuracy in species identification and counting. The solution uses underwater cameras to capture video sequences, which are analysed using deep learning models trained to distinguish sea trout from other objects such as leaves or different fish species. The system processes visual data to detect and count individuals in real time or through batch analysis. This approach enables scalable and consistent monitoring across different locations. The system demonstrates potential to improve efficiency and data quality in environmental monitoring.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1406,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01089,EU_PSTW_202609_01089,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Earth Observation Center (EOC),R&D environmental protection.,"Traditional methods rely on manual interpretation of satellite imagery, which is time-consuming and limited in scalability. The project demonstrates potential to improve detection accuracy and operational efficiency, although full deployment details remain limited.",Differentiation of ship types on satellite radar images,Earth Observation Center (EOC); Academic-Research; R&D environmental protection.; Germany,"The objective is to automate the detection and classification of vessels using remote sensing data. The solution applies deep learning techniques to satellite radar images, enabling the identification and differentiation of ship types based on their radar signatures.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Monitoring maritime activities over large ocean areas is critical for environmental protection, security, and enforcement against illegal activities such as unregulated fishing. Traditional methods rely on manual interpretation of satellite imagery, which is time-consuming and limited in scalability. The objective is to automate the detection and classification of vessels using remote sensing data. The solution applies deep learning techniques to satellite radar images, enabling the identification and differentiation of ship types based on their radar signatures. These models are trained on labelled datasets and integrated into earth observation systems used by authorities and organisations. The system supports applications such as maritime surveillance, environmental monitoring, and enforcement. The project demonstrates potential to improve detection accuracy and operational efficiency, although full deployment details remain limited.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1407,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01090,EU_PSTW_202609_01090,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Helmholtz Center for Ocean Research Kiel (GEOMAR),R&D environmental protection.,The CONMAR project addresses the environmental and safety challenges posed by marine munitions and contaminated sites in coastal and offshore areas. These hazards are difficult to monitor due to limited accessibility and fragmented data across institutions.,CONMAR - studying contaminated sites with underwater drones,Helmholtz Center for Ocean Research Kiel (GEOMAR); Academic-Research; R&D environmental protection.; Germany,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The CONMAR project addresses the environmental and safety challenges posed by marine munitions and contaminated sites in coastal and offshore areas. These hazards are difficult to monitor due to limited accessibility and fragmented data across institutions. The objective is to improve understanding, monitoring, and management of such sites through integrated data and advanced technologies. The solution combines underwater drones with data integration and analysis tools to collect and process information on contaminated areas. Machine learning techniques are applied to analyse sensor and imaging data, supporting detection and assessment of hazards. The project also integrates existing datasets from research organisations, government agencies, and industry to create a comprehensive knowledge base. As a research and development initiative, the project is ongoing, with expected outcomes including improved environmental monitoring, informed policy decisions, and enhanced remediation strategies.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F07_DATA_VISIBILITY_INTEGRATION_GAP,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1408,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01091,EU_PSTW_202609_01091,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Peter L. Reichertz Institute for Medical Informatics,R&D health.,"Caring relatives often face complex daily coordination tasks, emotional strain and limited access to tailored nursing knowledge while supporting family members in need of care.",MoCaB - Reliable mobile support for caring relatives,Peter L. Reichertz Institute for Medical Informatics; Academic-Research; R&D health.; Germany,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Caring relatives often face complex daily coordination tasks, emotional strain and limited access to tailored nursing knowledge while supporting family members in need of care. The MoCaB project aims to develop a mobile assistance system that helps organise care routines, provides situation-specific guidance and promotes carers’ physical and mental wellbeing. A knowledge-based infrastructure, known as the MoCaB world, underpins the system and enables a mobile software agent to deliver context-aware support. Sensors capture relevant data from everyday activities to recognise situations and trigger appropriate recommendations, while maintaining an overview of the condition of the person receiving care. Intelligent algorithms process contextual information to provide timely prompts, educational resources and links to support services. Data protection and privacy safeguards are integrated into the architecture to ensure secure handling of sensitive health information. The solution seeks to reduce stress, improve care quality and strengthen carers’ resilience, offering scalable digital support that complements existing health and social care services.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1409,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01092,EU_PSTW_202609_01092,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,L3S Research Center,R&D economic affairs,Cities often lack tools that translate large volumes of heterogeneous mobility data into actionable insights for planning and operations.,Data4UrbanMobility - Development of application-driven tools for mobility information,L3S Research Center; Academic-Research; R&D economic affairs; Germany,"The solution integrates regional and historical mobility data from multiple sources and applies data extraction, integration, and analytical methods to create event-based views of mobility systems. Predictive models generate forecasts of traffic behaviour and system development, while tailored analytics tools support specific use cases.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Urban mobility planning increasingly depends on integrating diverse data sources to understand traffic dynamics, user behaviour, and emerging mobility services. Cities often lack tools that translate large volumes of heterogeneous mobility data into actionable insights for planning and operations. Data4UrbanMobility addresses this gap by developing application-driven tools that support data-informed mobility decisions. Stakeholders include urban administrations, mobility service providers, and citizens. The objective is to enable evidence-based planning, development, and operation of innovative mobility services. The solution integrates regional and historical mobility data from multiple sources and applies data extraction, integration, and analytical methods to create event-based views of mobility systems. Predictive models generate forecasts of traffic behaviour and system development, while tailored analytics tools support specific use cases. All components are brought together in a unified platform. The project improves situational awareness, supports proactive planning, and enables more efficient mobility services. It contributes to better-coordinated transport systems, improved user experience, and data-driven urban mobility governance.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F06_HIGH_VOLUME_SCALABILITY_BOTTLENECK,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1410,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01093,EU_PSTW_202609_01093,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Julius Kühn Institute Federal Research Institute for Cultivated Plants (JKI),Protection of biodiversity and landscape.,"Traditional methods of analysing insect trap data are labour-intensive and slow, limiting the ability to respond quickly to emerging threats.",AI^2 - (Aphid identification through artificial intelligence),Julius Kühn Institute Federal Research Institute for Cultivated Plants (JKI); Academic-Research; Protection of biodiversity and landscape.; Germany,"The objective is to automate identification and counting of insect species in monitoring systems. The solution uses machine learning and deep learning techniques to analyse images from airborne insect traps, identifying and classifying species such as aphids.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The AI² project focuses on improving the monitoring of harmful insects in agriculture, where timely detection is critical to prevent crop damage and ensure food security. Traditional methods of analysing insect trap data are labour-intensive and slow, limiting the ability to respond quickly to emerging threats. The objective is to automate identification and counting of insect species in monitoring systems. The solution uses machine learning and deep learning techniques to analyse images from airborne insect traps, identifying and classifying species such as aphids. Neural networks are trained on labelled datasets to recognise visual patterns and quantify insect populations. The system enables rapid and standardised analysis across multiple locations. As an ongoing development project, it demonstrates potential to significantly reduce processing time, improve consistency of results, and support early detection and management of invasive pests.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1411,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01094,EU_PSTW_202609_01094,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,German Research Center for Artificial Intelligence GmbH (DFKI),"Agriculture, forestry, fishing and hunting","Current sensor systems often struggle to deliver consistent and certifiable outputs under varying conditions, limiting deployment in safety-critical contexts. AI-TEST-FIELD addresses this challenge by developing the foundations for robust, trustworthy sensor systems.",AI-TEST-FIELD – Semantic Environment Perception for Intelligent Sensor Systems,"German Research Center for Artificial Intelligence GmbH (DFKI); Academic-Research; Agriculture, forestry, fishing and hunting; Germany",The solution focuses on semantic environment perception using learning-based approaches that fuse data from multiple sensor modalities.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Safe operation of autonomous and assisted systems requires reliable perception of complex and changing environments. Current sensor systems often struggle to deliver consistent and certifiable outputs under varying conditions, limiting deployment in safety-critical contexts. AI-TEST-FIELD addresses this challenge by developing the foundations for robust, trustworthy sensor systems. Stakeholders include industrial developers, certification bodies, and operators of automated systems. The objective is to enable sensor technologies that provide stable and interpretable guidance for action across diverse environmental scenarios. The solution focuses on semantic environment perception using learning-based approaches that fuse data from multiple sensor modalities. Models are trained and tested in controlled environments to ensure robustness and consistency of outputs. Emphasis is placed on reproducibility, validation, and suitability for certification. The project delivers reference architectures, datasets, and testing methodologies. The impact includes increased reliability of intelligent sensor systems, improved safety assurance, and accelerated industrial adoption. AI-TEST-FIELD supports the deployment of trustworthy perception technologies in domains such as automated transport and industrial automation.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1412,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01095,EU_PSTW_202609_01095,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Julius Kühn Institute Federal Research Institute for Cultivated Plants (JKI),"Agriculture, forestry, fishing and hunting",UNKNOWN,BETTER-WEEDS - AI to recognise weed species,"Julius Kühn Institute Federal Research Institute for Cultivated Plants (JKI); Academic-Research; Agriculture, forestry, fishing and hunting; Germany",High-resolution field imagery and sensor data are analysed using machine learning-based image recognition models trained to distinguish between competitive and non-competitive weed species.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Farmers must balance effective weed control to protect crop yields with growing societal and regulatory expectations for environmentally sustainable practices and biodiversity preservation. Conventional blanket herbicide application can harm beneficial plant species and increase ecological pressure. The BETTER-WEEDS project aims to develop cost-effective and time-efficient tools to record weed species and densities at field level, enabling differentiated management strategies. High-resolution field imagery and sensor data are analysed using machine learning-based image recognition models trained to distinguish between competitive and non-competitive weed species. The system generates area-specific maps indicating species distribution, density and ecological traits, which are then used to design tailored management plans. By identifying competitively weak or rare species with ecosystem benefits, the approach supports selective interventions rather than uniform treatment. The solution enhances operational efficiency, reduces input costs and promotes biodiversity-friendly farming practices. It contributes to improved sustainability and ecological performance while maintaining agricultural productivity through data-driven, site-specific decision support.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1413,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01096,EU_PSTW_202609_01096,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,FZI Research Center for Computer Science,"Agriculture, forestry, fishing and hunting","Assessing plant health, growth, and pest infestation is essential for sustainable agriculture, yet manual inspection is labour-intensive, costly, and dependent on expert judgement. Sampling-based methods provide limited resolution and may miss early signs of stress or disease.",BoniKI - plant-specific assessment by autonomous systems,"FZI Research Center for Computer Science; Academic-Research; Agriculture, forestry, fishing and hunting; Germany","The solution uses unmanned aerial systems to capture high-resolution imagery of agricultural fields. Data processing pipelines separate crops from weeds and background elements, followed by automated assessment of plant condition using trained analytical models.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Assessing plant health, growth, and pest infestation is essential for sustainable agriculture, yet manual inspection is labour-intensive, costly, and dependent on expert judgement. Sampling-based methods provide limited resolution and may miss early signs of stress or disease. The BoniKI project addresses this challenge by enabling automated, plant-level assessment at scale. Stakeholders include farmers, agronomists, and agricultural service providers. The objective is to increase efficiency, transparency, and sustainability in crop monitoring and decision-making. The solution uses unmanned aerial systems to capture high-resolution imagery of agricultural fields. Data processing pipelines separate crops from weeds and background elements, followed by automated assessment of plant condition using trained analytical models. The system derives relevant scoring parameters and generates actionable insights for field management. The project aims to deliver an integrated, user-friendly solution for routine agricultural use. The impact includes reduced monitoring costs, earlier detection of stress or infestation, and more targeted interventions. BoniKI supports precision agriculture and contributes to more resource-efficient and resilient farming practices.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1414,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01097,EU_PSTW_202609_01097,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Julius Kühn Institute Federal Research Institute for Cultivated Plants (JKI),"Agriculture, forestry, fishing and hunting","Extreme weather events increasingly threaten agricultural productivity, yet timely and region-specific assessment tools are often lacking.",DynAWI - Predict extreme weather situations reliably and region-specific,"Julius Kühn Institute Federal Research Institute for Cultivated Plants (JKI); Academic-Research; Agriculture, forestry, fishing and hunting; Germany",The solution integrates geospatial datasets within a scalable data infrastructure and applies analytical models to derive dynamic agricultural weather indicators.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Extreme weather events increasingly threaten agricultural productivity, yet timely and region-specific assessment tools are often lacking. Farmers and public authorities need reliable indicators to anticipate yield impacts and support risk management decisions. DynAWI addresses this need by developing a process chain for monitoring and analysing extreme weather conditions in agriculture. Stakeholders include agricultural agencies, researchers, and private sector users of environmental data. The objective is to provide scalable, regionally differentiated indicators that link weather extremes to crop yield outcomes. The solution integrates geospatial datasets within a scalable data infrastructure and applies analytical models to derive dynamic agricultural weather indicators. These indicators capture the intensity, duration, and spatial extent of extreme conditions relevant to crop development. Outputs are made available through standardised interfaces for integration into national and European data spaces. The project improves early warning capabilities, supports impact assessment, and enhances planning under climate uncertainty. DynAWI contributes to more resilient agricultural systems and informed adaptation strategies in the face of climate change.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1415,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01098,EU_PSTW_202609_01098,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Deggendorf University of Technology,"Agriculture, forestry, fishing and hunting","Quality assessment of fruit and vegetables is critical for regional agriculture and efficient food supply chains, yet traditional inspection methods are often subjective, labour-intensive, and limited in predictive power. FreshRegio addresses this challenge by enabling objective, data-driven evaluation of product quality and shelf life.",FreshRegio – making the quality of fruit and vegetables measurable,"Deggendorf University of Technology; Academic-Research; Agriculture, forestry, fishing and hunting; Germany",UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Quality assessment of fruit and vegetables is critical for regional agriculture and efficient food supply chains, yet traditional inspection methods are often subjective, labour-intensive, and limited in predictive power. FreshRegio addresses this challenge by enabling objective, data-driven evaluation of product quality and shelf life. Stakeholders include farmers, distributors, retailers, and consumers. The objective is to strengthen regional food systems by improving transparency and reducing waste. The solution combines near-infrared sensor technology with analytical models to assess internal quality attributes of produce non-destructively. Measurements are processed to predict freshness and remaining shelf life, supporting informed decisions along the supply chain. The system simplifies quality control processes and enables consistent evaluation across batches. The impact includes reduced food waste, improved product differentiation, and stronger trust between producers and buyers. FreshRegio contributes to more sustainable food systems by aligning quality measurement with regional agricultural value creation.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1417,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01099,EU_PSTW_202609_01099,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Martin Luther University Halle-Wittenberg, Institute for Agricultural and Nutritional Sciences","Agriculture, forestry, fishing and hunting","Barley breeders need to introduce useful traits from wild or diverse accessions, but selecting which genetic material should be crossed and tested can be expensive, slow and uncertain.",HEB KI– Artificial intelligence searches to improve barley variety,"Martin Luther University Halle-Wittenberg, Institute for Agricultural and Nutritional Sciences; Academic-Research; Agriculture, forestry, fishing and hunting; Germany","By reducing trial-and-error, HEB KI can help breeders use limited experimental capacity more effectively while strengthening the resilience and adaptability of future barley varieties.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"HEB KI focuses on improving plant-breeding decisions by increasing genetic diversity in barley while reducing the resources required to identify promising crosses. Barley breeders need to introduce useful traits from wild or diverse accessions, but selecting which genetic material should be crossed and tested can be expensive, slow and uncertain. The project therefore aims to expand the HEB-25 barley population towards HEX-35 by selecting suitable accessions from a wild barley population in a more targeted way. The implementation further develops and adapts the XSeedScore algorithm for barley, combining genome and phenome datasets to prioritise breeding material with the highest expected value. Conceptually, the system links genetic information, observed plant traits and predictive scoring so that breeders can focus field trials and controlled experiments on the most promising candidates. This enables a more systematic approach to introducing novel alleles into barley breeding. The expected impact is faster and more resource-efficient variety development, improved breeding diversity and better selection of lines with agronomically useful traits. By reducing trial-and-error, HEB KI can help breeders use limited experimental capacity more effectively while strengthening the resilience and adaptability of future barley varieties.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1418,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01100,EU_PSTW_202609_01100,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Leibniz Institute for Agricultural Engineering and Bioeconomy,"Agriculture, forestry, fishing and hunting",UNKNOWN,KAMI – Dairy cow respiratory rate monitoring,"Leibniz Institute for Agricultural Engineering and Bioeconomy; Academic-Research; Agriculture, forestry, fishing and hunting; Germany","The expected impact is improved animal welfare, earlier response to health or stress events, reduced medication use, better use of performance potential and lower economic losses for farms.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"KAMI responds to the need for earlier and less intrusive detection of illness and stress in dairy herds. Respiratory rate is a sensitive indicator of animal condition, reflecting heat stress, fear, pain and possible disease processes, but conventional observation requires visual counting of flank movements, which is labour-intensive, intermittent and may disturb the animals. Farmers therefore need a practical monitoring approach that can identify individual changes before welfare problems or financial losses escalate. The KAMI project develops a prototype for automated, image-based and contact-free detection of respiration in dairy cows. Its implementation combines camera-based monitoring with data-driven analysis and links breathing information with other parameters such as milk quality, movement, feeding and rumination behaviour. Conceptually, the system captures visual signals, estimates individual respiratory patterns and translates deviations into actionable warnings. The expected impact is improved animal welfare, earlier response to health or stress events, reduced medication use, better use of performance potential and lower economic losses for farms. By enabling continuous and objective monitoring without attaching devices to animals, KAMI offers a more scalable route to precision livestock management and more informed herd-care decisions.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1419,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01101,EU_PSTW_202609_01101,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Leibniz Institute for Plant Genetics and Crop Plant Research (IPK),"Agriculture, forestry, fishing and hunting",KIBREED addresses the difficulty of breeding crop varieties that remain productive under local environmental conditions and future climate pressures.,KIBREED – using AI to grow site-adapted plants,"Leibniz Institute for Plant Genetics and Crop Plant Research (IPK); Academic-Research; Agriculture, forestry, fishing and hunting; Germany","The project uses wheat as a model crop because of its global importance and the availability of rich breeding datasets. By supporting the development of resistant and locally adapted crops, the project can contribute to food security, healthier nutrition and more ecological land use.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"KIBREED addresses the difficulty of breeding crop varieties that remain productive under local environmental conditions and future climate pressures. Farmers require varieties that combine yield stability, resistance and regional adaptation, but plant-breeding decisions depend on many interacting factors, including genetic background, plant traits and environmental conditions. The project uses wheat as a model crop because of its global importance and the availability of rich breeding datasets. Its objective is to make advanced algorithms usable for selecting locally adapted varieties and strengthening agricultural resilience. The implementation integrates genotyping, phenotyping and envirotyping data through deep-learning procedures, allowing models to analyse genetic information, observed plant characteristics and environmental context together rather than separately. Conceptually, KIBREED combines multi-source biological and environmental data to improve prediction of which varieties are likely to perform well in specific locations. The expected impact is better variety selection, improved yield security, more efficient breeding processes and stronger sustainability in agriculture. By supporting the development of resistant and locally adapted crops, the project can contribute to food security, healthier nutrition and more ecological land use. Its value lies in connecting molecular, field and environmental evidence in a single predictive breeding framework.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1420,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01102,EU_PSTW_202609_01102,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Deggendorf University of Technology,"Agriculture, forestry, fishing and hunting",UNKNOWN,KIdetect - Reliable detection of weed growth centers,"Deggendorf University of Technology; Academic-Research; Agriculture, forestry, fishing and hunting; Germany",The implementation uses image processing supported by artificial intelligence together with multispectral camera systems in the visible and short-wave infrared ranges.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"KIdetect tackles a practical limitation in mechanical weed control: as weed infestation becomes denser, it becomes harder for camera-guided hoeing systems to identify where tools should act precisely. Farmers and agricultural-technology providers need methods that can target weeds selectively without damaging crops and without relying excessively on herbicides. The project therefore aims to detect weeds reliably and in real time, with particular attention to the spatial location of their growth centres, which may be hidden by leaves or neighbouring plants. The implementation uses image processing supported by artificial intelligence together with multispectral camera systems in the visible and short-wave infrared ranges. It also creates 3D reconstructions of weed areas so that growth centres can be located even under heavy infestation. Testing takes place in a controlled vertical-farming stand, where weed image data can be generated under adjustable lighting, wavelength and camera conditions before being processed with specialised methods. The expected impact is more accurate activation of hoeing devices, improved selective weed removal, lower chemical input and more reliable smart-farming equipment. By linking multispectral sensing, 3D reconstruction and real-time interpretation, KIdetect strengthens precision agriculture’s ability to manage weeds sustainably.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1421,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01103,EU_PSTW_202609_01103,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Julius Kühn Institute Federal Research Institute for Cultivated Plants (JKI),"Agriculture, forestry, fishing and hunting",AI-iREPro addresses the difficulty of forecasting grape yield accurately at vineyard level.,AI-iREPro - using AI to manage viticulture more efficiently,"Julius Kühn Institute Federal Research Institute for Cultivated Plants (JKI); Academic-Research; Agriculture, forestry, fishing and hunting; Germany","Winegrowers and wineries must plan labour, harvest logistics, transport capacity, cellar use and quality management before the final yield is known, while weather conditions, variety characteristics and annual growth patterns can cause large fluctuations. Its implementation combines modern sensor technology with intelligent forecast models, using historical yield data, environmental information, field observations and sensor-derived indicators.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"AI-iREPro addresses the difficulty of forecasting grape yield accurately at vineyard level. Winegrowers and wineries must plan labour, harvest logistics, transport capacity, cellar use and quality management before the final yield is known, while weather conditions, variety characteristics and annual growth patterns can cause large fluctuations. The project therefore develops digital tools for vineyard-specific yield prediction, helping producers make earlier and better-founded operational decisions. Its implementation combines modern sensor technology with intelligent forecast models, using historical yield data, environmental information, field observations and sensor-derived indicators. At a conceptual level, the system extracts features from vineyard data, models yield-related relationships and generates forecasts that can support both vineyard management and winery planning. It also contributes to grapevine breeding by increasing the efficiency and objectivity of yield assessment. The expected impact includes improved harvest planning, reduced waiting times during grape delivery, better utilisation of presses and fermentation tanks, stronger quality management and lower financial risk for winegrowers. The project produced high-performance prototypes and digital methods that can underpin new services for vineyard and winery management. Its broader value lies in making viticulture more predictable, data-supported and resilient to annual variability.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1422,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01104,EU_PSTW_202609_01104,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"University of Hohenheim, Department of Artificial Intelligence in Agricultural Engineering","Agriculture, forestry, fishing and hunting",UNKNOWN,KINERA – Optimizing production processes in agriculture,"University of Hohenheim, Department of Artificial Intelligence in Agricultural Engineering; Academic-Research; Agriculture, forestry, fishing and hunting; Germany","Modern high-tech farm equipment can be difficult to operate at its full potential, creating a performance gap between technical capability and practical field use. The implementation integrates agricultural machinery into operational edge infrastructure and cross-company cloud infrastructure, allowing machines to use local data, communicate with other machines and draw on shared information.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"KINERA focuses on improving the efficiency and resilience of agricultural production processes that rely on complex machinery. Modern high-tech farm equipment can be difficult to operate at its full potential, creating a performance gap between technical capability and practical field use. Farmers also face disruptions from weather, logistics, machine availability and other external factors, making operational reliability increasingly important. KINERA therefore aims to support both conventional tractor-implement combinations and autonomous field robots, especially in crop establishment, through better process coordination and adaptive decision support. The implementation integrates agricultural machinery into operational edge infrastructure and cross-company cloud infrastructure, allowing machines to use local data, communicate with other machines and draw on shared information. At a conceptual level, process data are analysed to provide setting support, self-adaptation and decision recommendations for farmers. The expected impact is higher machine utilisation, improved area performance, more efficient logistics and greater resilience of digitalised agricultural businesses under disruptive conditions. By combining field experiments, simulation and connected infrastructure, KINERA seeks to make advanced farm technology easier to operate and more reliable. Its business value lies in turning complex machines into coordinated, data-supported production systems that can sustain productivity while reducing inefficiency.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1423,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01105,EU_PSTW_202609_01105,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Justus Liebig University Giessen, Professorship for Plant Breeding","Agriculture, forestry, fishing and hunting",UNKNOWN,AI-Breeding – find the highest yielding strains quickly and accurately,"Justus Liebig University Giessen, Professorship for Plant Breeding; Academic-Research; Agriculture, forestry, fishing and hunting; Germany","Its implementation develops and applies machine-learning methods for pattern recognition in highly complex genome, phenome and environmental datasets. The expected impact is faster breeding progress, improved yield security and better use of large-scale biological datasets in practical breeding programmes.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"AI-Breeding addresses the need to accelerate plant breeding by identifying high-performing lines and hybrids more quickly, accurately and resource-efficiently. Breeders working on important crops must combine yield potential, environmental adaptation and genetic diversity, but conventional selection can require long cycles of field testing and complex interpretation of genome, phenotype and environmental information. The project brings together practical plant breeding, breeding theory, quantitative genetics, genome analysis and advanced prediction methods to improve selection success across different breeding stages. Its implementation develops and applies machine-learning methods for pattern recognition in highly complex genome, phenome and environmental datasets. These methods are used to predict performance-related and environment-dependent genetic structures in breeding populations, extracting data attributes that can support better decisions on combination ability, line selection and hybrid performance. Conceptually, the approach links genomic markers, phenotypic observations, environmental conditions and predictive modelling to support “big data”-based breeding. The expected impact is faster breeding progress, improved yield security and better use of large-scale biological datasets in practical breeding programmes. Rather than relying only on traditional trial-and-error selection, the project aims to make breeding decisions more evidence-driven and scalable, helping to develop crop varieties that remain productive under changing environmental conditions.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1429,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01106,EU_PSTW_202609_01106,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"University of Lübeck, Institute for Robotics and Cognitive Systems","Agriculture, forestry, fishing and hunting",UNKNOWN,KRIBL - affordable robotics also for small businesses,"University of Lübeck, Institute for Robotics and Cognitive Systems; Academic-Research; Agriculture, forestry, fishing and hunting; Germany",UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"KRIBL focuses on making agricultural robotics accessible to smaller farms, which may otherwise be excluded from advanced automation because current systems are often large, specialised and expensive. Organic and biological farming businesses need practical tools for monitoring, plant care and field operations, but the economics of highly automated machines tend to favour very large agricultural enterprises. The project therefore aims to develop compact, affordable and flexible robot systems that can be used in everyday agricultural settings without requiring excessive investment. Its implementation combines small mobile robots, sensor technology, field robotics, modular software and intelligent data processing to support precision tasks in biological agriculture. Conceptually, KRIBL explores how lightweight robotic platforms can perceive their environment, support crop and weed-related operations, and operate as part of a broader digital farming workflow. Recent project material also links KRIBL to containerised AI deployment and reproducible weed-detection workflows on mobile agricultural robots, showing how software modularity can improve maintainability and transferability. The expected impact is broader access to automation, reduced labour intensity, more precise field operations and improved competitiveness for smaller farms. By lowering the economic and technical threshold for robotics, KRIBL contributes to a more inclusive model of agricultural innovation rather than concentrating advanced technology only in large-scale operations.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1430,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01107,EU_PSTW_202609_01107,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Justus Liebig University Giessen, Institute for Animal Breeding and Domestic Animal Genetics","Agriculture, forestry, fishing and hunting","Livestock owners need effective protection, but conventional electric fences and deterrent systems may lose effectiveness when predators become habituated, and remote grazing sites often lack permanent infrastructure.",mAInZaun – the intelligent fence that recognizes animal species,"Justus Liebig University Giessen, Institute for Animal Breeding and Domestic Animal Genetics; Academic-Research; Agriculture, forestry, fishing and hunting; Germany",UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"mAInZaun was created to improve herd protection in areas where grazing animals are exposed to predators, especially wolves. Livestock owners need effective protection, but conventional electric fences and deterrent systems may lose effectiveness when predators become habituated, and remote grazing sites often lack permanent infrastructure. The project therefore developed a modular, autonomous and intelligent protective fence able to recognise approaching animals and trigger appropriate deterrence measures. Its implementation combines sensors, local power supply, animal-species recognition and dynamic deterrent responses, including stimuli such as ultrasonic signals. The system is designed not only for integration into existing fences, but also for independent deployment in temporary safety zones, for example near roads, settlements, railways, hiking trails, tourist areas or landscape-conservation projects. Conceptually, mAInZaun links edge sensing, classification of approaching animals, automated alerting and context-specific deterrence. It can also integrate police, road-maintenance departments or railway authorities into the alert system where relevant. The expected impact is stronger protection of grazing animals, reduced economic losses for farmers, improved coexistence between livestock farming and wildlife conservation, and more flexible management of temporary risk areas. By reacting dynamically rather than relying on static barriers, the system seeks to maintain deterrent effectiveness over time.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1431,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01108,EU_PSTW_202609_01108,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,DIL German Institute for Food Technologye,"Agriculture, forestry, fishing and hunting","Movi-Q addresses a persistent quality-control problem in the food industry: fruit, vegetables, meat and other natural products vary widely in appearance, while defects are often detected manually by visual inspection. The expected impact is more reliable quality assurance, lower reject rates, reduced manual sorting effort and better resource efficiency.",Movi-Q – optimize visual quality controls intelligently,"DIL German Institute for Food Technologye; Academic-Research; Agriculture, forestry, fishing and hunting; Germany",UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Movi-Q addresses a persistent quality-control problem in the food industry: fruit, vegetables, meat and other natural products vary widely in appearance, while defects are often detected manually by visual inspection. This makes sorting resource-intensive, inconsistent and prone to human error, and it can contribute to unnecessary rejection of usable products or failure to remove defective material. The project, led through the DIL German Institute of Food Technologies in Quakenbrück, aimed to develop a mobile and modular prototype for visual quality detection that could be used by companies in different food-processing contexts. Its implementation combines image acquisition hardware, machine-vision methods, artificial intelligence and software modules able to identify defects and trigger downstream processes such as sorting. A key component is the creation of high-quality training data through a mobile image-capture unit that can be adapted to different products and application scenarios. Conceptually, Movi-Q links visual sensing, defect classification, process integration and practical guidelines for food companies. The expected impact is more reliable quality assurance, lower reject rates, reduced manual sorting effort and better resource efficiency. By demonstrating transferable application scenarios, the project helps food businesses assess where automated visual inspection can improve productivity, quality standards and sustainability.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1432,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01109,EU_PSTW_202609_01109,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,German Research Center for Artificial Intelligence,"Agriculture, forestry, fishing and hunting",UNKNOWN,RoLand - how to achieve low-cost automation in agriculture,"German Research Center for Artificial Intelligence; Academic-Research; Agriculture, forestry, fishing and hunting; Germany",UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"RoLand develops a low-cost robotic harvesting system for fruit production, with particular attention to open-field strawberry harvesting. Fruit farms face labour shortages, rising production costs and time-sensitive harvesting windows, while robotic harvesting solutions have historically been concentrated in greenhouse environments or designed at cost levels unsuitable for smaller farms. The project therefore aims to create a semi-autonomous mobile system that can harvest fruit independently under real outdoor conditions, including changing weather, uneven terrain and variable crop presentation. Its implementation combines a mobile robotic platform, fruit-recognition systems, an end-effector for harvesting, autonomous navigation and perception technologies. DFKI’s SHIVAA robot, developed in the project, is designed to identify ripe fruit and pick it in open fields, with the possibility of scaling work output through multiple compact units. Conceptually, the system integrates visual detection, motion planning, manipulation and field mobility in a platform intended to remain economically viable for smaller producers. The expected impact is reduced dependence on seasonal labour, improved supply security, more flexible harvesting by time of day and stronger competitiveness for regional fruit production. Public demonstrations indicate that RoLand has moved from design towards field-tested prototype capability, including public strawberry-field testing.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1438,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01110,EU_PSTW_202609_01110,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Rheinische Friedrich-Wilhelms-Universität Bonn, Faculty of Agriculture, Institute for Agricultural Engineering","Agriculture, forestry, fishing and hunting","This slows innovation and makes it harder to compare tools, field conditions and weed-management strategies.",WeedAI – promoting non-chemical weed control in sugar beet cultivation,"Rheinische Friedrich-Wilhelms-Universität Bonn, Faculty of Agriculture, Institute for Agricultural Engineering; Academic-Research; Agriculture, forestry, fishing and hunting; Germany","Mechanical and robotic weeding methods are increasingly important as agriculture seeks to reduce herbicide use, but assessing their effectiveness usually requires extensive field trials and labour-intensive manual ground-truthing. Its implementation is based on deep-learning methods for plant recognition, using image data to detect and distinguish sugar beet plants and weeds, estimate their position and size, and evaluate treatment outcomes.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"WeedAI responds to the growing need for reliable evaluation of non-chemical weed-control technologies, especially in sugar beet cultivation. Mechanical and robotic weeding methods are increasingly important as agriculture seeks to reduce herbicide use, but assessing their effectiveness usually requires extensive field trials and labour-intensive manual ground-truthing. This slows innovation and makes it harder to compare tools, field conditions and weed-management strategies. The project therefore develops automated methods to assess how well weeding operations remove weeds while preserving crops. Its implementation is based on deep-learning methods for plant recognition, using image data to detect and distinguish sugar beet plants and weeds, estimate their position and size, and evaluate treatment outcomes. Conceptually, WeedAI links field imagery, crop-weed segmentation, object recognition and performance assessment into a system that can quantify both crop and weed responses after intervention. The expected impact is faster evaluation of weeding systems, lower trial costs, better evidence for non-chemical crop protection and more rapid development of sustainable weed-control technologies. Project outputs indicate that the system can make precise predictions on individual sugar beet and weed positions, even in fields unknown to the model, provided that high-quality drone and camera data are available.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1443,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01111,EU_PSTW_202609_01111,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Lithuanian National Paying Agency (NPA),"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.","Agricultural paying agencies must verify compliance with Common Agricultural Policy requirements across very large areas, but manual field inspections can be slow, expensive and disruptive for both administrations and farmers.",AI-based tool for monitoring cultivated land in Lithuania using Copernicus Sentinel data,"Lithuanian National Paying Agency (NPA); Central-Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Lithuania",Lithuania’s National Paying Agency worked with Geomatrix UAB and iTree Lietuva to use Copernicus Sentinel data and machine-learning methods for monitoring farm activities and land use during the agricultural season.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Agricultural paying agencies must verify compliance with Common Agricultural Policy requirements across very large areas, but manual field inspections can be slow, expensive and disruptive for both administrations and farmers. Lithuania’s National Paying Agency worked with Geomatrix UAB and iTree Lietuva to use Copernicus Sentinel data and machine-learning methods for monitoring farm activities and land use during the agricultural season. The objective was to assess subsidy-related conditions more quickly and accurately, giving the agency an evidence base for checking claims and supporting more efficient distribution of CAP funds. The implementation developed SAGRIS, a Sentinel-based agriculture information service, and CAPCON, a CAP control service. These tools process Sentinel-1 and other satellite data to detect and classify land-cover and agricultural activity patterns relevant to subsidy control. At a conceptual level, the service combines Earth-observation imagery, pre-processing workflows, machine-learning classification, parcel-level monitoring and integration with paying-agency information systems. This allows checks to be carried out during the farming season rather than relying only on retrospective or sample-based inspections. The expected impact is faster compliance assessment, reduced need for on-site control, improved administrative efficiency and better targeting of follow-up checks. The case demonstrates how Copernicus data can be operationalised inside national agricultural administration, turning satellite observation into a practical tool for public funding governance.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1447,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01112,EU_PSTW_202609_01112,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Federal Ministry of Justice,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.","Courts receive large volumes of incoming documents through electronic communication channels, scanned files and structured or unstructured submissions, creating a substantial administrative burden before a case can even be processed. The expected impact is faster handling of incoming judicial documents, fewer manual routing steps, improved consistency in document registration and reduced administrative workload in court offices.",AI for analysing incoming mail,"Federal Ministry of Justice; Central-Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Austria","Austria’s Federal Ministry of Justice developed AI-supported incoming-mail analysis to automate routing and classification tasks that would otherwise require court staff to read, interpret and manually allocate documents.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Courts receive large volumes of incoming documents through electronic communication channels, scanned files and structured or unstructured submissions, creating a substantial administrative burden before a case can even be processed. Austria’s Federal Ministry of Justice developed AI-supported incoming-mail analysis to automate routing and classification tasks that would otherwise require court staff to read, interpret and manually allocate documents. The implementation analyses input documents, extracts metadata, identifies file numbers or other identifiers and recognises procedure types where no file number is available. It can also support intelligent allocation, file creation, assignment to the appropriate case or organisational unit, categorisation of scanned documents and preparation of proposed document descriptions or names. At a conceptual level, the system combines document classification, metadata extraction, entity or identifier recognition and workflow-routing rules. Its role is administrative: it turns incoming material into structured, actionable information for court systems and staff. The expected impact is faster handling of incoming judicial documents, fewer manual routing steps, improved consistency in document registration and reduced administrative workload in court offices. This type of tool is particularly useful in digital justice environments because delays or errors at the intake stage can affect all later case-management processes. The Austrian justice framework presents such AI applications as support functions that do not replace or influence judicial decisions.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK|F04_UNSTRUCTURED_INFORMATION_COMPLEXITY|F06_HIGH_VOLUME_SCALABILITY_BOTTLENECK,S3_DOCUMENT_INTELLIGENCE,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1448,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01113,EU_PSTW_202609_01113,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Federal Ministry of Justice,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.","Austria’s Federal Ministry of Justice developed AI-supported digital file management to ensure that unstructured documents are processed uniformly and that metadata can be adopted automatically. The expected impact is more consistent electronic files, lower manual effort, easier navigation for judges and court staff, and stronger quality in the transition from paper-based to digital workflows.",AI for digital file management,"Federal Ministry of Justice; Central-Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Austria",UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Digital court files require consistent structure, accurate metadata and reliable separation of documents, particularly when legacy paper files are scanned in bulk or when incoming documents arrive in different formats. Austria’s Federal Ministry of Justice developed AI-supported digital file management to ensure that unstructured documents are processed uniformly and that metadata can be adopted automatically. The operational problem is that court staff must often separate individual documents from scanned batches, identify first pages, classify attachments and maintain a coherent digital file structure. The implementation supports detection of first pages after bulk scanning, separation of submissions and supplements, document description, classification, harmonisation of document titles and intelligent creation of file structures, including tables of contents. It can also support object-oriented views, such as grouping documents related to a particular party. At a conceptual level, the system combines document segmentation, classification, metadata extraction, title harmonisation and digital-case-file structuring. The expected impact is more consistent electronic files, lower manual effort, easier navigation for judges and court staff, and stronger quality in the transition from paper-based to digital workflows. In legal administration, this matters because document organisation directly affects searchability, procedural efficiency and the ability of professionals to understand the case file quickly. The system is best understood as a support layer for digital file management, not as a tool that interprets legal merits or influences case outcomes.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK|F04_UNSTRUCTURED_INFORMATION_COMPLEXITY,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1449,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01114,EU_PSTW_202609_01114,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Federal Ministry of Justice,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.","The operational challenge is that manual anonymisation is time-consuming and must preserve the comprehensibility of the case while removing or masking information that could identify individuals or organisations. Its expected impact is reduced manual workload, faster publication of case law, more consistent anonymisation and wider access to legally relevant decisions.",AI for anonymisation of court decisions,"Federal Ministry of Justice; Central-Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Austria","OECD material describes Austria’s Federal Ministry of Justice as implementing a comprehensive project to automate anonymisation of court decisions, with goals including efficiency, reduced manual effort and broader publication of anonymised decisions.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Publishing court decisions improves transparency, legal certainty and access to case law, but it requires careful protection of personal data, addresses, parties and other identifying information. Austria’s Federal Ministry of Justice developed AI-supported anonymisation to prepare court decisions for publication in the Legal Information System. The operational challenge is that manual anonymisation is time-consuming and must preserve the comprehensibility of the case while removing or masking information that could identify individuals or organisations. The implementation analyses decision documents, extracts metadata, recognises natural and legal persons, detects addresses and identifies the roles of actors within the case. It then supports anonymisation of personal data while aiming to maintain the legal and factual intelligibility of the decision. At a conceptual level, the system combines named-entity recognition, role detection, document analysis, metadata extraction and rule-guided anonymisation. Its expected impact is reduced manual workload, faster publication of case law, more consistent anonymisation and wider access to legally relevant decisions. The tool is especially valuable where courts produce large volumes of decisions and where publication depends on balancing transparency with privacy obligations. OECD material describes Austria’s Federal Ministry of Justice as implementing a comprehensive project to automate anonymisation of court decisions, with goals including efficiency, reduced manual effort and broader publication of anonymised decisions.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1450,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01115,EU_PSTW_202609_01115,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Government of Romania,General services.,UNKNOWN,Ion - the world's first government advisor,Government of Romania; Central-Government; General services.; Romania,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Governments increasingly seek tools to understand public concerns in real time, but turning large volumes of citizen expression into reliable policy insight raises questions about transparency, representativeness and accountability. Romania’s ION was launched in March 2023 as an artificial-intelligence government adviser, presented as a system that could collect and synthesise citizens’ opinions and concerns for public officials. The objective was to enhance citizen-government communication by receiving direct submissions through an official platform and monitoring public online content to identify issues that mattered to the population. The implementation combined an AI-branded public interface with sentiment or topic-analysis ambitions, aiming to generate a real-time snapshot of public mood and communicate it to government decision-makers. At a conceptual level, the system was intended to aggregate citizen messages, detect recurrent topics, summarise concerns and support public officials with a non-traditional channel of feedback. Its expected impact was more responsive government, broader participation and faster awareness of social priorities. However, the project soon faced criticism over transparency, data collection and unclear evidence of meaningful functionality. Later reporting indicated that ION had been reduced to a simple informational chatbot, and subsequent accounts suggested that it was removed from government premises after administrative change. The case is therefore best understood as an ambitious but short-lived experiment in AI-mediated civic listening.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,NOT_IN_USE,미사용,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1451,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01116,EU_PSTW_202609_01116,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Madrid,General public services n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,Chatbot - LineaMadrid,Municipality of Madrid; Local Government; General public services n.e.c.; Spain,"The implementation uses a chatbot or virtual-assistant interface that supports navigation, answers common questions and can connect users with personalised assistance where appropriate.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Large city administrations receive many routine questions from residents and visitors about municipal services, procedures, appointments, local taxes, transport, events and city information. Línea Madrid’s virtual assistant provides a conversational support channel on Madrid City Council’s institutional website, helping users find answers without needing to call, visit a service office or navigate complex web pages. The operational objective is to improve access to municipal information by offering immediate guidance through an online chat available across the council’s web environment. The implementation uses a chatbot or virtual-assistant interface that supports navigation, answers common questions and can connect users with personalised assistance where appropriate. At a conceptual level, the assistant interprets the user’s question, retrieves relevant municipal information and directs the user towards the appropriate service page, procedure or support channel. The expected impact is faster access to public information, reduced pressure on physical and telephone help centres, improved service availability outside office-based interaction and a more user-friendly digital front door for Madrid’s municipal administration. The value of the system lies in navigation and orientation: it helps citizens locate the right process or information but does not replace formal administrative decisions or case-specific assessment. The City Council describes the service as a 24/7 online chat available from all pages of the institutional website.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1452,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01117,EU_PSTW_202609_01117,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Helsinki,"Recreation, culture and religion n.e.c.",UNKNOWN,Virtual helsinki - digital twin of the city of Helsinki,"City of Helsinki; Local Government; Recreation, culture and religion n.e.c.; Finland","Cities increasingly use three-dimensional models to communicate urban change, support planning, visualise public assets and make local environments accessible in new ways. Virtual Helsinki applies this logic to a digital twin of the Finnish capital, creating an interactive 3D environment that can support tourism, shopping, training, simulations, concerts, public engagement and virtual participation.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Cities increasingly use three-dimensional models to communicate urban change, support planning, visualise public assets and make local environments accessible in new ways. Virtual Helsinki applies this logic to a digital twin of the Finnish capital, creating an interactive 3D environment that can support tourism, shopping, training, simulations, concerts, public engagement and virtual participation. The initiative transforms selected parts of Helsinki into immersive spaces where users can visit places remotely and experience the city through a virtual interface. The implementation combines 3D city modelling, game-engine environments, virtual-reality design and digital storytelling. At a conceptual level, real urban locations are reconstructed as navigable digital scenes, enabling users to interact with city spaces in ways that are not possible through maps or photographs alone. The expected impact is broader access to Helsinki’s urban and cultural environment, new formats for tourism and events, improved engagement with urban development and experimentation with virtual citizenship concepts. The use case is significant because it connects the technical digital-twin agenda with public-facing experience design: a 3D model is not only an analytical planning tool, but also a platform for services, participation and communication. Helsinki’s broader 3D city models are part of the city’s digital-twin infrastructure, combining open data, information technology and updating urban information.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1453,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01118,EU_PSTW_202609_01118,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,National Institute for Social Security (INPS),Social protection n.e.c.,"The volume and sensitivity of these messages create a major operational burden because staff must read each message and any attachments, understand the subject, identify the relevant service area and forward the case to the appropriate office. At a conceptual level, the model analyses unstructured citizen communications, assigns them to relevant welfare-service categories and supports routing to the correct territorial or functional unit.",Classification and automatic sorting of CEMs at the Italian National Institute for Social Security (INPS),National Institute for Social Security (INPS); Central-Government; Social protection n.e.c.; Italy,"The implementation initially experimented with open-source GPT-2 and then moved to BERT, using transformer-based natural language processing to classify email content and attachments.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Certified email is one of the main channels through which Italian citizens communicate with INPS, including for pensions, disability, unemployment and other welfare matters. The volume and sensitivity of these messages create a major operational burden because staff must read each message and any attachments, understand the subject, identify the relevant service area and forward the case to the appropriate office. INPS developed an in-house AI system to classify and sort certified emails automatically while keeping data within INPS data centres to meet confidentiality and GDPR requirements. The implementation initially experimented with open-source GPT-2 and then moved to BERT, using transformer-based natural language processing to classify email content and attachments. At a conceptual level, the model analyses unstructured citizen communications, assigns them to relevant welfare-service categories and supports routing to the correct territorial or functional unit. Its expected impact is faster communication handling, reduced manual triage, shorter response times and major savings in administrative working days. The system also demonstrates the value of keeping sensitive public-service AI inside institutional infrastructure when the data include personal and welfare-related information. INPS reports that the project was included in IRCAI’s global Top 10 AI projects supporting the Sustainable Development Goals, and public technology-watch material describes the growth in certified emails from 3 million in 2019 to more than 6 million in 2023.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F04_UNSTRUCTURED_INFORMATION_COMPLEXITY,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1454,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01119,EU_PSTW_202609_01119,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,National Institute for Social Security (INPS),Social protection n.e.c.,"Digital customer-service channels can become inefficient when many requests must be handled twice: first by a front-line operator and then again by a specialist office. The operational challenge is to distinguish simple requests that can be handled at first level from complex ones that require second-level intervention, thereby reducing unnecessary handovers and response delays.","""INPS risponde"" - Optimised management of web requests to Customer Service",National Institute for Social Security (INPS); Central-Government; Social protection n.e.c.; Italy,"The expected impact is faster response time for citizens, fewer duplicate handling steps, improved workload distribution and more efficient use of expert staff. The use case is closely related to INPS’s broader strategy of applying natural language processing to high-volume citizen communication channels while preserving human responsibility for substantive responses.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Digital customer-service channels can become inefficient when many requests must be handled twice: first by a front-line operator and then again by a specialist office. INPS Risponde receives large numbers of online requests from citizens seeking clarification on services, rules and individual procedures. The operational challenge is to distinguish simple requests that can be handled at first level from complex ones that require second-level intervention, thereby reducing unnecessary handovers and response delays. INPS developed an AI decision engine upstream of the customer-service process to assess the complexity of incoming web requests. At a conceptual level, the system analyses the text of a citizen’s request, identifies topic and complexity signals, and recommends whether the request can be treated by first-level support or should be routed to a specialised operator. The expected impact is faster response time for citizens, fewer duplicate handling steps, improved workload distribution and more efficient use of expert staff. The use case is closely related to INPS’s broader strategy of applying natural language processing to high-volume citizen communication channels while preserving human responsibility for substantive responses. The online INPS Risponde service itself remains a key channel for submitting and monitoring requests for information, clarifications and case updates.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1455,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01120,EU_PSTW_202609_01120,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,National Institute for Social Security (INPS),Social protection n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,Virtual Assistant based on generative AI at INPS,National Institute for Social Security (INPS); Central-Government; Social protection n.e.c.; Italy,"Citizens using social-security services often struggle to find the correct information among many pages, circulars, administrative messages and service sheets, especially for complex benefits such as pension schemes. The implementation uses a large language model through Microsoft Azure and OpenAI technologies, integrated into the INPS digital environment.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Citizens using social-security services often struggle to find the correct information among many pages, circulars, administrative messages and service sheets, especially for complex benefits such as pension schemes. INPS began experimenting with a generative-AI virtual assistant to improve search and orientation on its website. The objective is to allow users to ask follow-up questions conversationally after searching for a service, receiving clearer explanations and guidance without having to interpret multiple documents independently. The implementation uses a large language model through Microsoft Azure and OpenAI technologies, integrated into the INPS digital environment. In the first experimental area, the assistant supported questions on the “Opzione Donna” pension measure, drawing from service pages, circulars, messages and internal acts. At a conceptual level, the system combines retrieval of institutional knowledge sources with conversational generation, maintaining dialogue context so that users can ask additional questions and receive more precise answers. The expected impact is improved accessibility of social-security information, more intuitive navigation of INPS services, reduced friction for citizens and better use of institutional documentation. The assistant is designed as an information and orientation tool, not as a decision-maker on eligibility or benefits. Its significance lies in the movement from traditional keyword search towards conversational access to complex public-administration knowledge.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S1_KNOWLEDGE_RETRIEVAL_RAG,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1456,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01121,EU_PSTW_202609_01121,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,National Institute for Social Security (INPS),Social protection n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,Talk with Arianna - the chatbot of the Italian National Institute for Social Security,National Institute for Social Security (INPS); Central-Government; Social protection n.e.c.; Italy,Citizens using INPS digital services often need immediate help with access credentials before they can complete personal procedures online. The implementation uses a conversational chatbot interface that allows citizens to interact through a guided dialogue rather than searching manually across support pages.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Citizens using INPS digital services often need immediate help with access credentials before they can complete personal procedures online. Arianna was designed as a virtual assistant dedicated to questions about the INPS PIN, a credential historically used to access services on the institute’s website. The operational problem was that users frequently needed guidance on how to request, manage, recover or understand their PIN, and these recurring enquiries could generate avoidable pressure on help desks and service channels. The implementation uses a conversational chatbot interface that allows citizens to interact through a guided dialogue rather than searching manually across support pages. At a conceptual level, the assistant recognises the user’s question, connects it to the relevant support content and provides a direct answer or orientation on the next step. The expected impact is faster access to credential-related information, reduced repetitive support requests, improved usability for citizens and a more efficient first level of digital assistance. Although the use case is narrow, it is significant because identity and access management are preconditions for many welfare and social-security services. By making support available continuously and in a conversational format, Arianna helped reduce friction at a critical entry point to INPS online services.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1457,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01122,EU_PSTW_202609_01122,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Department for Science, Innovation and Technology",R&D economic affairs,UNKNOWN,Quantum Catalyst Fund in the UK,"Department for Science, Innovation and Technology; Central-Government; R&D economic affairs; United Kingdom","The use case was developed to accelerate the use of quantum technologies across the public sector and help government departments understand where quantum solutions can create practical value. Quantum technologies can offer capabilities beyond conventional computing, sensing and measurement systems, but public-sector adoption requires testing, evidence and clear use cases rather than abstract technological promise.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The use case was developed to accelerate the use of quantum technologies across the public sector and help government departments understand where quantum solutions can create practical value. Quantum technologies can offer capabilities beyond conventional computing, sensing and measurement systems, but public-sector adoption requires testing, evidence and clear use cases rather than abstract technological promise. The fund therefore supports government-facing projects in policy areas such as transport, health, space, energy and net zero, including applications from drug discovery and dementia diagnosis to navigation, infrastructure monitoring and grid optimisation. Its implementation operates through a £15 million competition funded and delivered by the Department for Science, Innovation and Technology and Innovate UK. Phase 1 selected 30 projects to explore the benefits of quantum technologies for government, and later funding rounds supported projects progressing towards applied demonstrations. Conceptually, the programme acts as an innovation-procurement and adoption mechanism: it links public-sector problems with companies and researchers able to test quantum sensing, quantum computing or quantum-enabled algorithms. The expected impact is faster learning inside government, reduced adoption barriers, stronger UK quantum supply chains and earlier identification of high-value public-sector applications. It also supports the UK ambition to build a quantum-enabled economy by 2033.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1458,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01123,EU_PSTW_202609_01123,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""social_nonprofit""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Technology Foundation Berlin and the Berlin University of Applied Sciences (HTW),R&D education.,UNKNOWN,COMo - CO2 monitoring in publicly accessible indoor spaces,Technology Foundation Berlin and the Berlin University of Applied Sciences (HTW); Academic-Research; R&D education.; Germany,"Facility managers could use the information to decide when to ventilate, while visitors could consult published data before entering a space.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Publicly accessible indoor spaces such as cultural venues, educational buildings and hospitality settings needed practical ways to manage air quality during the pandemic, when ventilation was closely linked to infection-risk reduction. COMo used carbon dioxide concentration as a proxy indicator for indoor air quality and aerosol accumulation, helping operators understand when rooms needed ventilation. The initiative built on the earlier Clair Berlin work and focused on connected sensors that could collect, process and publish CO₂ readings from indoor environments. The implementation combined networked CO₂ sensors, an Internet of Things platform, data processing and public visualisation. At a conceptual level, sensors measured indoor air conditions, transmitted readings to a digital platform, and converted them into actionable information for operators and visitors. Facility managers could use the information to decide when to ventilate, while visitors could consult published data before entering a space. The expected impact was more objective management of indoor air, improved confidence in public venues, better infection-prevention behaviour and a replicable model for low-cost environmental monitoring. The value of the case lies in translating an invisible risk factor into visible, operational information that could support everyday decisions without requiring specialist technical knowledge from building users.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1459,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01124,EU_PSTW_202609_01124,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Greater London Authority,General public services n.e.c.,"The operational challenge is that charging-point data can be fragmented across operators and boroughs, while investment decisions require evidence on utilisation, location, coverage and demand. The dashboard also demonstrates how data collaboration can make a fragmented smart-city service area more manageable, particularly where delivery depends on many organisations rather than a single municipal provider.",London Electric Vehicle Charge Points Dashboard,Greater London Authority; Local Government; General public services n.e.c.; United Kingdom,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Electric-vehicle charging infrastructure in London is delivered by many public and private actors, creating a coordination problem for boroughs, the Greater London Authority and infrastructure providers. The London Electric Vehicle Charge Points Dashboard was developed to provide a shared view of what charging infrastructure exists, how it is used and where future installations may be needed. The operational challenge is that charging-point data can be fragmented across operators and boroughs, while investment decisions require evidence on utilisation, location, coverage and demand. The implementation brings together data on charge-point locations and usage into a dashboard that supports planning and analysis. At a conceptual level, the system aggregates infrastructure and utilisation data, presents it spatially and analytically, and helps public authorities compare provision across places. This enables better strategic deployment of new EV charging infrastructure rather than relying on isolated local datasets or assumptions. The expected impact is more coordinated infrastructure planning, improved understanding of charging behaviour, better targeting of investment and stronger support for London’s transition to cleaner transport. The dashboard also demonstrates how data collaboration can make a fragmented smart-city service area more manageable, particularly where delivery depends on many organisations rather than a single municipal provider.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1460,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01125,EU_PSTW_202609_01125,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Skopje,Environmental protection n.e.c.,"Severe urban air pollution in Skopje has posed persistent public health and environmental challenges, with limited large-scale institutional interventions.",Air-purifying billboard in Skopje,City of Skopje; Local Government; Environmental protection n.e.c.; North Macedonia,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Severe urban air pollution in Skopje has posed persistent public health and environmental challenges, with limited large-scale institutional interventions. The air-purifying billboard was introduced as a pilot civil society initiative to demonstrate a visible, scalable mitigation concept and stimulate broader institutional engagement. Stakeholders include local communities, environmental groups and public authorities. The billboard incorporates filtration materials and airflow mechanisms designed to capture particulate matter from surrounding air. In a second prototype phase, air quality sensors and Internet of Things components are integrated to collect real-time environmental data, enabling data analytics, monitoring dashboards and predictive modelling of pollution trends. Although still at prototype and testing stages, the initiative has raised public awareness and demonstrated the feasibility of modular, low-cost air purification installations. No evidence suggests large-scale municipal deployment to date; the project remains developmental and exploratory.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1462,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01126,EU_PSTW_202609_01126,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""social_nonprofit""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Paris,Street lighting.,The operational problem was not limited to lighting: the city needed a networked urban grid that could minimise environmental impact while also enabling new functions across the public realm.,Use of Public Lighting System to Deploy a Comprehensive Smart City Platform,City of Paris; Local Government; Street lighting.; France,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Public lighting is one of the most extensive forms of urban infrastructure, making it a strong foundation for smart-city services when it is connected, centrally managed and technically scalable. Paris used its public-lighting system as the basis for a citywide smart-city platform, with the aim of reducing energy consumption, improving public safety and creating infrastructure that could support future digital services. The operational problem was not limited to lighting: the city needed a networked urban grid that could minimise environmental impact while also enabling new functions across the public realm. The implementation modernised and connected the lighting grid, transforming it from a standalone utility into a smart-city-ready infrastructure layer. At a conceptual level, connected lighting points can provide power, location, communication and mounting capacity for additional sensors, actuators or urban services. This makes it easier to scale future applications without rebuilding infrastructure from scratch. The expected impact is lower electricity consumption, better maintenance, improved user experience, enhanced public-space safety and a reusable platform for other smart services. The case is important because it treats lighting not simply as an energy-efficiency target, but as a strategic physical and digital backbone for urban innovation.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1463,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01127,EU_PSTW_202609_01127,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Ministry of Finance,"General economic, commercial and labour affair",UNKNOWN,Blockchain to distribute government saving bonds in Poland,"Ministry of Finance; Central-Government; General economic, commercial and labour affair; Poland","Poland’s Ministry of Finance, together with PKO Bank Polski and the National Clearing House, implemented a blockchain-based reporting system for treasury savings bonds. The implementation uses an enterprise blockchain architecture, based on Hyperledger Fabric, to record relevant transaction lifecycle information and allow authorised parties to access consistent data.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Retail government bonds are distributed through intermediaries, which means several actors need consistent data on sales, transactions and reporting. Poland’s Ministry of Finance, together with PKO Bank Polski and the National Clearing House, implemented a blockchain-based reporting system for treasury savings bonds. The objective was to improve the collection and reporting of transaction data related to the sale and distribution of government savings bonds to consumers. The implementation uses an enterprise blockchain architecture, based on Hyperledger Fabric, to record relevant transaction lifecycle information and allow authorised parties to access consistent data. At a conceptual level, the platform creates a shared ledger for bond-distribution reporting, reducing reconciliation problems between the Ministry, banks and intermediaries. Rather than functioning as a retail cryptocurrency or public token market, the system supports administrative and reporting processes around conventional government savings bonds. The expected impact is better transparency between institutions, more reliable reporting, reduced duplication of records and improved operational oversight of bond distribution. The use case is notable because it applies blockchain to a public-finance reporting problem where shared data integrity and multi-party access are more relevant than speculative digital assets. It demonstrates a pragmatic form of distributed ledger adoption in the public sector, focused on back-office coordination.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1464,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01128,EU_PSTW_202609_01128,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Banque de France,"General economic, commercial and labour affair",UNKNOWN,Experimenting settlement of French government bonds in Central Bank Digital Currency with blockchain technology,"Banque de France; Central-Government; General economic, commercial and labour affair; France","In France, Banque de France commissioned a consortium led by Euroclear to test whether French government bonds could be settled using central bank digital currency on a blockchain platform.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Post-trade financial markets depend on secure settlement, liquidity management and reliable interoperability between institutions that operate under strict regulatory and operational constraints. In France, Banque de France commissioned a consortium led by Euroclear to test whether French government bonds could be settled using central bank digital currency on a blockchain platform. The experiment involved Agence France Trésor, BNP Paribas CIB, Crédit Agricole CIB, HSBC, Société Générale and IBM, and its objective was to assess whether a broad range of securities-settlement operations could be executed on distributed ledger infrastructure from a user perspective. The implementation used wholesale CBDC in a test blockchain environment to process securities issuance, primary-market and secondary-market trades, repo-based liquidity optimisation and interest payments. At a conceptual level, the platform linked tokenised securities, central bank money, privacy-preserving tokens, hybrid cloud capabilities and interfaces with existing market infrastructure. This allowed the consortium to test not only individual transactions, but the feasibility of an end-to-end securities lifecycle on blockchain. The experiment demonstrated that distributed ledger systems could coexist and interoperate with conventional market infrastructure while supporting several core post-trade functions. Its main value was evidential rather than operational: it provided Banque de France and market participants with practical insight into the technical, functional and institutional conditions under which CBDC-based settlement might become viable.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1465,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01129,EU_PSTW_202609_01129,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Government of Germany,"General economic, commercial and labour affair","Green-bond markets rely on investor confidence that proceeds are genuinely linked to environmental objectives, but impact claims can be difficult to verify when reporting formats differ and supporting evidence is dispersed. The initiative aimed to increase both the supply of credible green investment opportunities and demand from investors seeking transparent climate-aligned assets, especially in markets where verification capacity may be limited.",Green Assets Wallet - tracking the impact of green bonds,"Government of Germany; Central-Government; General economic, commercial and labour affair; Germany",UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Green-bond markets rely on investor confidence that proceeds are genuinely linked to environmental objectives, but impact claims can be difficult to verify when reporting formats differ and supporting evidence is dispersed. The Green Assets Wallet initiative was developed to strengthen trust in sustainable finance by creating a shared digital environment for issuers, investors and validators to record, review and communicate the environmental impact of green debt instruments. The initiative aimed to increase both the supply of credible green investment opportunities and demand from investors seeking transparent climate-aligned assets, especially in markets where verification capacity may be limited. Its implementation used blockchain to create an immutable record of green-bond information and impact data. At a conceptual level, the platform combined distributed ledger technology, impact validation workflows, reporting tools and access for market participants, allowing green-investment claims to be checked against traceable evidence. The system was launched by Stockholm Green Digital Finance and brought together climate-finance and digital-technology expertise, including ChromaWay, CICERO, Öhman and SEB, with support connected to Swedish and German public actors. The expected impact was greater transparency, reduced verification friction, improved investor confidence and stronger scalability for credible green finance. By making impact data more auditable, Green Assets Wallet sought to reduce greenwashing risks and support capital allocation aligned with the Sustainable Development Goals and the Paris Agreement.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1466,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01130,EU_PSTW_202609_01130,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Fondazione IRCCS Ca' Granda Ospedale Maggiore Policlinico Hospital and the University of Milan,R&D health.,UNKNOWN,AI-based device for parkinson patients,Fondazione IRCCS Ca' Granda Ospedale Maggiore Policlinico Hospital and the University of Milan; Academic-Research; R&D health.; Italy,The system uses implanted electrodes to record brain signals and applies machine learning models to detect pathological patterns associated with motor symptoms.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Parkinson’s disease patients undergoing deep brain stimulation often receive continuous stimulation at fixed parameters, which may not optimally respond to fluctuating symptoms and can affect quality of life. The objective of this project is to develop an adaptive neurostimulation device capable of adjusting stimulation in response to real-time neural activity, involving patients, clinicians and medical device manufacturers. The system uses implanted electrodes to record brain signals and applies machine learning models to detect pathological patterns associated with motor symptoms. Control algorithms dynamically adjust stimulation parameters, creating a closed-loop system integrated with clinical programming tools. Clinical studies indicate improved symptom control, reduced side effects and more personalised therapy compared with conventional constant stimulation. The device remains under clinical development and regulatory processes in several markets, with ongoing trials and staged commercialisation rather than universal availability.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1467,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01131,EU_PSTW_202609_01131,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Rural Support Service of the Republic of Latvia,"Agriculture, forestry, fishing and hunting",UNKNOWN,Chatbot - Lad Va,"Rural Support Service of the Republic of Latvia; Central-Government; Agriculture, forestry, fishing and hunting; Latvia","The implementation uses an AI-powered chatbot that answers general enquiries and helps users navigate support programmes, rural development measures, funding opportunities and administrative procedures.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Farmers, rural businesses and applicants for agricultural support often need guidance on funding schemes, application procedures, permits, registers and deadlines outside ordinary office hours. The Rural Support Service of Latvia introduced the LAD virtual assistant to make information about agricultural and rural support services easier to access online. The operational problem is that the agency provides a large number of services, while its users often work seasonally and may need answers when consultants or regional offices are not available. The implementation uses an AI-powered chatbot that answers general enquiries and helps users navigate support programmes, rural development measures, funding opportunities and administrative procedures. At a conceptual level, the assistant interprets a user’s question, maps it to the relevant institutional information and provides guidance in conversational form. It reduces the need for users to search across many pages or contact staff for routine questions. The expected impact is quicker access to information, reduced workload for agency employees, better support for applicants and improved accessibility of rural-support administration. The chatbot also helps standardise answers in a service area where rules, deadlines and funding conditions can be complex. Its role is informational and navigational, supporting users before formal application, assessment or decision-making processes take place.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1472,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01132,EU_PSTW_202609_01132,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,General Directorate of Financial Controls (GDDE),"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.","Public financial oversight bodies face limited resources and cannot audit all government entities annually, increasing the risk of inefficiency, fraud, and corruption. The system increases audit coverage, supports targeted interventions, and saves at least 20% of staff time previously spent on manual checks.",AI to increase the efficiency of fiscal audit at the Greek General Directorate of Financial Controls (GDDE),"General Directorate of Financial Controls (GDDE); Central-Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Greece","This use case involves central government bodies and auditors, with the objective of automating fiscal assessment and enabling targeted controls. The solution is implemented through a digital financial control system that consolidates audit data into a central database and applies risk analysis to prioritise audits.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Public financial oversight bodies face limited resources and cannot audit all government entities annually, increasing the risk of inefficiency, fraud, and corruption. Greece’s General Directorate of Financial Controls sought to improve audit coverage and effectiveness. This use case involves central government bodies and auditors, with the objective of automating fiscal assessment and enabling targeted controls. The solution is implemented through a digital financial control system that consolidates audit data into a central database and applies risk analysis to prioritise audits. Automated evaluation supports online assessments and policy analysis per institution. The system increases audit coverage, supports targeted interventions, and saves at least 20% of staff time previously spent on manual checks. It improves efficiency, transparency, and governance, delivering measurable public sector value.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1474,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01133,EU_PSTW_202609_01133,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Palaio Faliro,"General economic, commercial and labour affair","Municipalities increasingly need continuous visibility over how buildings, facilities and urban assets consume energy and other resources, rather than relying on fragmented readings or periodic manual checks.",Municipality of Palaio Faliro: Development of IOT Platform - Energy Upgrade,"Municipality of Palaio Faliro; Local Government; General economic, commercial and labour affair; Greece","The implementation uses the SenseOne IoT platform, connecting sensors, meters and infrastructure data sources so that information can be collected, processed and displayed through dashboards.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Municipalities increasingly need continuous visibility over how buildings, facilities and urban assets consume energy and other resources, rather than relying on fragmented readings or periodic manual checks. In Palaio Faliro, the administration developed a centralised IoT management platform to monitor, control, record and analyse consumption data across municipal infrastructure. The local context includes a diverse estate of municipal buildings, schools, social facilities, sports facilities and street lighting, with further potential sources of data across urban services. The objective is to give the municipality a single operational layer for understanding its energy footprint and progressively improving the performance of public assets. The implementation uses the SenseOne IoT platform, connecting sensors, meters and infrastructure data sources so that information can be collected, processed and displayed through dashboards. At a conceptual level, the platform can extend beyond electricity monitoring to environmental indicators, waste bins, parking spaces, gas meters, smart water meters, tanks, wells and electric-vehicle chargers. This makes the system a scalable smart-city architecture rather than a narrow energy-accounting tool. The expected impact is improved decision-making, lower consumption, earlier detection of anomalies and better prioritisation of energy-efficiency interventions. By consolidating operational data, Palaio Faliro can shift from reactive infrastructure management to continuous, evidence-based monitoring of its municipal footprint.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1476,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01134,EU_PSTW_202609_01134,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Palaio Faliro,General public services n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,Chatbot Poseidon - Municipality of Palaio Faliro,Municipality of Palaio Faliro; Local Government; General public services n.e.c.; Greece,"The operational problem is common in local government: useful information may exist online, but users do not always know where to find it, which department is responsible or which procedure applies to their situation. The implementation uses a chatbot interface embedded in the municipal website, allowing users to ask questions and receive automated guidance rather than navigating only through static pages.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Residents and visitors to a municipality often need quick answers about local services, procedures, facilities, events and community resources. Poseidon was introduced as a virtual assistant on the Municipality of Palaio Faliro’s digital portal to make municipal information more accessible through an automated conversational channel. The operational problem is common in local government: useful information may exist online, but users do not always know where to find it, which department is responsible or which procedure applies to their situation. The implementation uses a chatbot interface embedded in the municipal website, allowing users to ask questions and receive automated guidance rather than navigating only through static pages. At a conceptual level, the system recognises common municipal-service queries, retrieves or maps the relevant information and directs users towards appropriate content or service pathways. The expected impact is faster access to municipal information, fewer routine enquiries for staff, better availability outside office hours and improved user experience for people interacting with the municipality online. The chatbot’s function is primarily informational and navigational: it does not replace formal applications, permits or administrative decisions, but provides a more convenient first point of contact. The case fits into Palaio Faliro’s wider digital-service approach, where municipal online channels are used to improve citizen support and access to local services.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1477,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01135,EU_PSTW_202609_01135,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Tallinn Strategy Center,Housing development.,"Urban-planning projects can be difficult for residents to imagine when future public spaces exist only as drawings, plans or technical descriptions.",Pollinator Highway Augmented Reality Application - Avalinn AR,Tallinn Strategy Center; Local Government; Housing development.; Estonia,"The case is significant because it uses AR not as a novelty, but as a participatory planning tool that connects local art, environmental strategy and spatial imagination.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Urban-planning projects can be difficult for residents to imagine when future public spaces exist only as drawings, plans or technical descriptions. AvaLinn AR was created in Tallinn to support citizen engagement around the Pollinator Highway, a green corridor and public-space project in North Tallinn. The objective is to help people experience proposed landscape-architecture solutions through augmented reality, making the future intervention more understandable and inviting. The implementation combines mobile augmented reality, murals, map-based interaction and visualisations of future public space. Users download the application and scan wall paintings along the Pollinator Highway, each linked to themes such as species richness, sustainable mobility, reuse of garages and leisure activities. At a conceptual level, physical murals become digital entry points: the app overlays additional information, future visions and interactive content onto the urban environment. The expected impact is more accessible planning communication, stronger citizen participation, better understanding of biodiversity and mobility goals, and increased engagement with a major green-infrastructure project. The case is significant because it uses AR not as a novelty, but as a participatory planning tool that connects local art, environmental strategy and spatial imagination. It allows residents to experience potential future changes in the place where those changes would occur.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1480,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01136,EU_PSTW_202609_01136,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Helsinki,Community development.,"Municipalities need up-to-date information on the condition of roads, cycle lanes, public infrastructure and environmental assets, but comprehensive field surveys can be costly and slow.",Crowdsorsa - City of Helsinki,City of Helsinki; Local Government; Community development.; Finland,"Crowdsorsa applies gamification and crowdsourcing to urban data collection, allowing citizens to complete location-based missions through a mobile app while collecting GPS-tagged photos or videos.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Municipalities need up-to-date information on the condition of roads, cycle lanes, public infrastructure and environmental assets, but comprehensive field surveys can be costly and slow. Crowdsorsa applies gamification and crowdsourcing to urban data collection, allowing citizens to complete location-based missions through a mobile app while collecting GPS-tagged photos or videos. In Helsinki, the approach has been used for tasks such as surveying road and cycle-lane conditions, mapping infrastructure assets and supporting environmental missions. The implementation turns data collection into a game: participants move through assigned areas, collect virtual objects, record evidence and upload material for analysis. At a conceptual level, the system combines citizen participation, location tracking, smartphone sensing, AI-based asset analysis and municipal asset-management workflows. Data gathered by participants can be evaluated by AI and converted into condition assessments or maintenance intelligence. The expected impact is faster infrastructure inventories, lower survey costs, improved coverage of physical assets and more participatory maintenance of the urban environment. The model also creates a direct incentive for residents to contribute, because missions can include monetary rewards. Its value lies in combining human mobility, local knowledge and automated analysis to update infrastructure data at a scale that would otherwise require dedicated survey teams.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1482,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01137,EU_PSTW_202609_01137,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Bordeaux metropole,R&D environmental protection.,"It also incorporates subjective perceptions, recognising that physical measurements alone cannot capture vulnerability, behavioural adaptation or citizen satisfaction.","Tackling urban heat through data, paying attention to our citizens' perception",Bordeaux metropole; Local Government; R&D environmental protection.; France,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Rising urban temperatures require local authorities to understand not only where heat is physically concentrated, but also how inhabitants experience discomfort, exposure and relief in everyday public spaces. Bordeaux Métropole developed this initiative to support climate-adaptation policies with indicators that combine environmental measurement and citizens’ perceived thermal comfort. Technical services need evidence on the conditions under which trees, fountains, urban design or surface materials reduce heat-island effects, while elected officials and planners also need to know whether residents actually feel improvements in liveability. The project began with a data-collection phase across eight cities in the metropolitan area, installing sensors in places with varied topographical and urban characteristics, including public squares, alleys, dense districts and wooded areas. The implementation combines environmental sensing, spatial analysis and typologies of urban spaces to compare temperature differences and evaluate the effects of local interventions. It also incorporates subjective perceptions, recognising that physical measurements alone cannot capture vulnerability, behavioural adaptation or citizen satisfaction. The expected impact is better targeted climate-adaptation planning, stronger evidence on which measures work, and greater legitimacy for public investment in cooling strategies. By connecting measurable heat conditions with lived experience, the initiative helps Bordeaux Métropole design responses that are both technically informed and socially meaningful, with possible replication across the wider metropolitan territory.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1484,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01138,EU_PSTW_202609_01138,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Fedeal Ministry of Justice,Law courts,"Justice services can be difficult for citizens, companies and legal professionals to navigate because procedures, forms, court contacts and digital submissions are distributed across many legal-administrative pathways.",The Chatbot of the Austrian Federal Ministry of Justice,Fedeal Ministry of Justice; Central-Government; Law courts; Austria,"The implementation uses a conversational interface to guide users through simple legal-administrative questions and direct them to relevant digital services on JustizOnline. It supports transparency and accessibility by making the justice portal easier to use, particularly for users who are unfamiliar with legal terminology or administrative structures.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Justice services can be difficult for citizens, companies and legal professionals to navigate because procedures, forms, court contacts and digital submissions are distributed across many legal-administrative pathways. The Austrian Ministry of Justice’s JustizOnline portal provides a digital access point for court services, case information, electronic submissions and justice-related documents, and the chatbot functions as an interactive assistant within this environment. The operational objective is to help users identify the correct procedure, form or information source before engaging with a court or public prosecutor’s office. The implementation uses a conversational interface to guide users through simple legal-administrative questions and direct them to relevant digital services on JustizOnline. At a conceptual level, the chatbot interprets user intent, links questions to justice-service content and provides orientation for self-service tasks. It supports transparency and accessibility by making the justice portal easier to use, particularly for users who are unfamiliar with legal terminology or administrative structures. The expected impact is quicker navigation, fewer misdirected enquiries, improved access to online justice services and better availability outside office hours. The tool remains informational and procedural: it does not provide binding legal advice or make decisions in judicial proceedings, but helps users reach the appropriate official channel.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1485,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01139,EU_PSTW_202609_01139,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Ministry of Justice,Law courts,"The operational challenge is that courts produce large volumes of decisions, and manual anonymisation can be slow, inconsistent and resource-intensive.",AI for anonymisation of court decisions in Croatia,Ministry of Justice; Central-Government; Law courts; Croatia,"The project therefore uses AI-powered tools to support automatic pre-anonymisation, helping identify personal names, addresses and other sensitive elements before decisions are reviewed and published.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Publishing court decisions is essential for transparency, legal certainty and consistency of case law, but it requires strong safeguards for personal data and confidential information. Croatia’s AI anonymisation initiative addresses this problem by preparing judicial decisions for online publication while protecting the identities of parties and other individuals mentioned in the text. The operational challenge is that courts produce large volumes of decisions, and manual anonymisation can be slow, inconsistent and resource-intensive. The project therefore uses AI-powered tools to support automatic pre-anonymisation, helping identify personal names, addresses and other sensitive elements before decisions are reviewed and published. At a conceptual level, the system combines document analysis, named-entity recognition, anonymisation rules, search functionality and publication workflows. Its purpose is not only to remove personal data, but also to preserve the legal meaning of the decision so that judges, lawyers, researchers, businesses and citizens can search and understand case law. The expected impact is greater transparency, faster publication of judgments, improved consistency in anonymisation and stronger public access to judicial information. The initiative also sits within a wider digital-justice agenda, including e-communication between courts, prosecutors, litigants and other justice stakeholders.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK|F06_HIGH_VOLUME_SCALABILITY_BOTTLENECK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1488,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01140,EU_PSTW_202609_01140,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Ministry of Justice,Law courts,"Manual anonymisation of court rulings is time-consuming, resource-intensive and prone to inconsistencies, particularly given the volume and complexity of decisions issued by public courts. Expected impacts include significant reductions in manual workload, faster publication cycles, improved consistency in anonymisation practices and strengthened compliance with data protection legislation, thereby enhancing trust in digital justice services.",Judicial Anonymisation Tool,Ministry of Justice; Central-Government; Law courts; Czechia,"The objective of this use case is to streamline and standardise the anonymisation process prior to publication, ensuring legal compliance while preserving the substantive content and readability of decisions.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Courts must publish judicial decisions to ensure transparency and legal certainty, while strictly complying with personal data protection requirements. Manual anonymisation of court rulings is time-consuming, resource-intensive and prone to inconsistencies, particularly given the volume and complexity of decisions issued by public courts. Stakeholders include judicial authorities, court clerks, data protection officers, legal professionals and citizens accessing published rulings. The objective of this use case is to streamline and standardise the anonymisation process prior to publication, ensuring legal compliance while preserving the substantive content and readability of decisions. The solution combines rule-based expert systems with natural language processing techniques to automatically detect and classify personal data elements such as names, addresses and identifiers within judicial texts. Predefined legal rules guide masking, redaction or pseudonymisation, and the tool integrates with court document management and publication platforms to support human validation workflows. Expected impacts include significant reductions in manual workload, faster publication cycles, improved consistency in anonymisation practices and strengthened compliance with data protection legislation, thereby enhancing trust in digital justice services.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1489,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01141,EU_PSTW_202609_01141,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Court administration (Domstolsstyrelsen),Law courts,UNKNOWN,AI to anonymise judicial courts in Denmark,Court administration (Domstolsstyrelsen); Central-Government; Law courts; Denmark,"Denmark’s court judgment anonymisation project supports publication of decisions in Domsdatabasen, the Danish courts’ judgment database, by using machine learning to pseudonymise judgments before they are made public. The implementation uses a machine-learning system to pre-screen documents and suggest text passages that may need anonymisation or pseudonymisation.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Judicial transparency depends on publishing court judgments in a form that is accessible to the public, while privacy law requires that parties and other individuals are protected from unnecessary identification. Denmark’s court judgment anonymisation project supports publication of decisions in Domsdatabasen, the Danish courts’ judgment database, by using machine learning to pseudonymise judgments before they are made public. The operational problem is that judgments contain names, pronouns, descriptions, contextual details and other linguistic elements that can reveal identity directly or indirectly. The implementation uses a machine-learning system to pre-screen documents and suggest text passages that may need anonymisation or pseudonymisation. At a conceptual level, the solution combines named-entity recognition, privacy-risk detection, text marking and human quality control. Each judgment is reviewed by human participants after the software has identified potentially sensitive content, creating a hybrid workflow in which AI accelerates the process but does not make publication decisions alone. The expected impact is faster preparation of judgments, more consistent protection of personal data, broader access to case law and better public understanding of judicial practice. The system is particularly important because anonymisation quality affects both privacy and the usefulness of judgments for legal analysis.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1490,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01142,EU_PSTW_202609_01142,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Court administration (Domstolsstyrelsen),Law courts,"The administrative problem is that meeting organisation can consume staff time, generate inefficient schedules and make it difficult to distinguish between urgent or high-priority meetings and less critical coordination tasks.","AI to plan meeting dates, times and locations for prosecutors",Court administration (Domstolsstyrelsen); Central-Government; Law courts; Denmark,"Denmark’s meeting-planning AI use case focused on allocating meeting dates, times and locations for prosecutors according to profile and logistical constraints. The implementation concept uses profile-based allocation, prioritisation and scheduling logic to reduce manual planning effort.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Court and prosecution services depend on effective scheduling, because hearings, meetings and procedural events must bring together busy professionals, appropriate locations and case-specific priorities. Denmark’s meeting-planning AI use case focused on allocating meeting dates, times and locations for prosecutors according to profile and logistical constraints. The administrative problem is that meeting organisation can consume staff time, generate inefficient schedules and make it difficult to distinguish between urgent or high-priority meetings and less critical coordination tasks. The implementation concept uses profile-based allocation, prioritisation and scheduling logic to reduce manual planning effort. At a conceptual level, the system would combine information about prosecutors’ roles, calendars, availability, meeting importance, location constraints and procedural requirements. It could then propose suitable meeting slots and venues, helping administrative staff organise work more efficiently. The expected impact is reduced clerical burden, fewer scheduling conflicts, better use of court and prosecution resources, and more consistent prioritisation of important meetings. The use case is administrative rather than adjudicative: it does not assess legal merits or influence prosecutorial decisions, but supports the logistics around justice work. This distinction is important because automation in scheduling can improve efficiency while leaving professional judgement and procedural responsibility with human officials.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1491,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01143,EU_PSTW_202609_01143,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Ministry of Justice,Law courts,"In Finland, the Ministry of Justice led the Anoppi project to address this challenge for court decisions and other official decisions issued by public authorities. By reducing the burden on courts and authorities, Anoppi strengthens digital justice services while maintaining data-protection requirements.",Automatic anonymisation and content description of documents containing personal data,Ministry of Justice; Central-Government; Law courts; Finland,"Manual anonymisation can be slow, costly and inconsistent, particularly when large collections of documents must be prepared for publication, research use or legal-information services. The implementation uses language-technology and semantic-computing methods to support two functions: automatic or semi-automatic anonymisation and pseudonymisation of personal data, and content description or annotation of legal documents.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Judicial and administrative documents contain information that is valuable for transparency, research and decision-making, but wider electronic availability is constrained by the need to protect personal data. In Finland, the Ministry of Justice led the Anoppi project to address this challenge for court decisions and other official decisions issued by public authorities. Manual anonymisation can be slow, costly and inconsistent, particularly when large collections of documents must be prepared for publication, research use or legal-information services. The objective was therefore to provide tools that help authorities make documents more accessible while preserving privacy and legal safeguards. The implementation uses language-technology and semantic-computing methods to support two functions: automatic or semi-automatic anonymisation and pseudonymisation of personal data, and content description or annotation of legal documents. At a conceptual level, the system applies natural language processing, named-entity recognition, semantic annotation and document-structuring techniques to identify sensitive elements and generate metadata that improves searchability and reuse. The expected impact is faster document processing, broader access to legal information, improved support for research and more efficient publication workflows. By reducing the burden on courts and authorities, Anoppi strengthens digital justice services while maintaining data-protection requirements. It also supports the wider development of open, anonymised legal data for intelligent legal applications and the Semantic Finlex environment.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1493,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01144,EU_PSTW_202609_01144,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Ministry of Justice,Law courts,"Finland’s Ministry of Justice applied robotic process automation in the enforcement-of-fines area to improve administrative efficiency and reduce manual handling. The expected impact is faster processing, fewer manual errors, better handling of overpayments, more consistent financial administration and reduced staff time spent on routine clerical activity.",Robot process automation (RPA) in Finnish judicial system,Ministry of Justice; Central-Government; Law courts; Finland,"The implementation uses software robots to carry out structured actions such as sending enquiries by email to banks and the Finnish Population Centre, reviewing data to identify erroneous overpayments and supporting the return or allocation of payments to citizens. The use case shows how justice-system digitalisation is not limited to courtrooms or judicial decisions: substantial value can come from automating back-office processes that support the enforcement and correction of financial sanctions.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Judicial administrations handle many repetitive financial and register-based tasks that require accuracy but do not necessarily require complex human judgement. Finland’s Ministry of Justice applied robotic process automation in the enforcement-of-fines area to improve administrative efficiency and reduce manual handling. The operational problem concerns recurring enquiries, overpayment checks and payment allocation tasks linked to fines and other financial sanctions. The implementation uses software robots to carry out structured actions such as sending enquiries by email to banks and the Finnish Population Centre, reviewing data to identify erroneous overpayments and supporting the return or allocation of payments to citizens. At a conceptual level, RPA interacts with existing administrative systems by following predefined rules, retrieving information, comparing records, triggering messages and flagging cases that require human attention. The expected impact is faster processing, fewer manual errors, better handling of overpayments, more consistent financial administration and reduced staff time spent on routine clerical activity. The use case shows how justice-system digitalisation is not limited to courtrooms or judicial decisions: substantial value can come from automating back-office processes that support the enforcement and correction of financial sanctions.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK|F08_ERROR_COMPLIANCE_RISK,S6_WORKFLOW_PROCESS_AUTOMATION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1494,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01145,EU_PSTW_202609_01145,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Ministry of Justice,Law courts,"The project ultimately did not reach operational deployment, showing the difficulty of applying algorithmic tools in domains where individual harm, legal nuance and institutional legitimacy are closely intertwined.",DataJust,Ministry of Justice; Central-Government; Law courts; France,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Compensation for bodily injury is a legally sensitive field because victims, parties, lawyers and judges need reliable information on comparable cases while preserving the independence and discretion of judicial decision-making. In France, the Ministry of Justice launched DataJust to explore whether past civil and administrative liability decisions could support a more consistent understanding of compensation levels. The project aimed to carry out retrospective and prospective evaluations of public policies, develop an indicative framework for bodily-injury compensation, inform parties during amicable settlement discussions and provide documentation for judges ruling on damages claims. The implementation was authorised by Decree No. 2020-356 of 27 March 2020, following CNIL review, and involved automated processing of personal data from relevant judicial decisions. At a conceptual level, the system was intended to extract compensation-related information, structure legal and factual variables, conduct statistical analysis and generate decision-support or information outputs. The expected value was greater transparency for parties, better-informed settlement negotiations and more documented policy evaluation in civil liability. However, the initiative raised concerns because it involved sensitive personal and judicial data and could influence perceptions of standardisation in damages assessment. The project ultimately did not reach operational deployment, showing the difficulty of applying algorithmic tools in domains where individual harm, legal nuance and institutional legitimacy are closely intertwined.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1495,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01146,EU_PSTW_202609_01146,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Commission for information technology in the judiciary,Law courts,"In Germany, this project component focused on automating the analysis of existing PDF files containing land-register information so that their contents could be fragmented, interpreted and assigned to appropriate database fields. The operational problem is that land-register material may exist in document formats that are difficult to search, integrate or process electronically, creating manual workload for justice administrations and limiting interoperability between systems.",Land register analysis component in the project Development of a federal database land register,Commission for information technology in the judiciary; Central-Government; Law courts; Germany,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Digitising land-register information requires converting legally important legacy documents into structured, accurate and reusable data without weakening the certainty attached to property records. In Germany, this project component focused on automating the analysis of existing PDF files containing land-register information so that their contents could be fragmented, interpreted and assigned to appropriate database fields. The operational problem is that land-register material may exist in document formats that are difficult to search, integrate or process electronically, creating manual workload for justice administrations and limiting interoperability between systems. The objective was to support the development of a federal land-register database by transforming unstructured or semi-structured documents into structured records. The implementation is based on expert systems and rule-based techniques, reflecting the formalised structure of land-register documents and the need for deterministic interpretation rather than open-ended prediction. At a conceptual level, the tool analyses document content and layout, separates relevant sections, recognises values according to predefined rules and maps them into corresponding database fields. The expected impact is reduced manual transcription, improved consistency of digitised records, faster migration of legacy information and stronger support for electronic workflows involving courts, land-registry authorities, notaries and other authorised users. The case demonstrates that rule-based automation remains valuable where legal formats are stable and accuracy requirements are high.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1496,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01147,EU_PSTW_202609_01147,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Federal Criminal Police Office (BKA),Police Services,"Online hate crime creates a difficult enforcement problem because potentially criminal content circulates at high volume, changes quickly and may be embedded in ambiguous linguistic, social or political contexts.",Application of AI for early recognition of criminal offences that relate to hate crime,Federal Criminal Police Office (BKA); Central-Government; Police Services; Germany,"In Germany, the Federal Criminal Police Office participated in a research project examining how police organisations could use automated support to detect, prevent and prosecute hate crime while respecting legal, ethical and organisational constraints.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Online hate crime creates a difficult enforcement problem because potentially criminal content circulates at high volume, changes quickly and may be embedded in ambiguous linguistic, social or political contexts. In Germany, the Federal Criminal Police Office participated in a research project examining how police organisations could use automated support to detect, prevent and prosecute hate crime while respecting legal, ethical and organisational constraints. The objective was not only to classify online statements, but to define the conditions under which such tools could be responsibly adopted by law-enforcement bodies. The implementation investigated technical approaches for classifying hate speech according to criminal-law categories, hate-speech phenomena and thematic content. At a conceptual level, the project combined natural language processing, supervised classification, domain-specific annotation, organisational analysis and legal-ethical assessment. This holistic design was intended to ensure that any prototype would be evaluated not only for technical accuracy, but also for its suitability within police workflows and fundamental-rights safeguards. The expected impact was faster identification of potentially criminal hate messages, better prioritisation of police review, and stronger evidence on how automated tools can support, rather than replace, human legal judgement. FRA records the output as reports and a technical prototype to be evaluated by police, while later project reporting indicates that a tool recognising criminal hate messages from social media posts was developed through KISTRA.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F06_HIGH_VOLUME_SCALABILITY_BOTTLENECK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1497,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01148,EU_PSTW_202609_01148,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Central Cybercrime Department of North-Rhine-Westphalia,Law courts,"Criminal proceedings increasingly rely on digital evidence, remote participation, audio-visual material and complex case records, while many courtrooms remain organised around paper-based or manually documented hearings.",Future criminal court rooms,Central Cybercrime Department of North-Rhine-Westphalia; Regional Government; Law courts; Germany,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Criminal proceedings increasingly rely on digital evidence, remote participation, audio-visual material and complex case records, while many courtrooms remain organised around paper-based or manually documented hearings. In North Rhine-Westphalia, the Central Cybercrime Department developed a pilot project aimed at modernising criminal courtrooms through technologies that could reduce the time and effort required to record, review and present proceedings. The objective was to create a courtroom environment in which hearings could be videotaped, speech could be converted into written transcripts, and participants could access both the transcription and embedded audio material. The implementation concept combined audio recording, video recording, speech-to-text recognition and digital document production inside the courtroom workflow. A further element under consideration was the 3D projection of crime scenes, which could support the presentation of spatially complex evidence in cybercrime or other criminal cases. At a conceptual level, the system would connect hearing capture, automated transcription, evidentiary presentation and case-file integration, allowing judges, prosecutors, lawyers and other authorised participants to work with richer digital records. The expected impact was reduced administrative effort, faster review of hearings, more accurate documentation and broader modernisation of criminal justice infrastructure. The available European Commission study records the project as a pilot within the wider mapping of innovative technologies in justice, but does not confirm full operational roll-out.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,S3_DOCUMENT_INTELLIGENCE,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1498,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01149,EU_PSTW_202609_01149,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,National Office for the Judiciary,Law courts,"Court proceedings generate spoken information that must be transformed into written minutes, decisions and procedural records, but manual transcription can be time-consuming and places a heavy burden on clerical staff. The operational challenge is that hearings and procedural events contain substantial spoken content, and delays in putting decisions or minutes into writing can affect court efficiency.",Speech recognition and transcription,National Office for the Judiciary; Central-Government; Law courts; Hungary,"Hungary’s National Office for the Judiciary examined the use of speech-recognition and transcription software in courts to accelerate the production of written judicial documents. The implementation applies automatic speech recognition to convert audio from proceedings into text, creating a draft transcript or written basis for further review.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Court proceedings generate spoken information that must be transformed into written minutes, decisions and procedural records, but manual transcription can be time-consuming and places a heavy burden on clerical staff. Hungary’s National Office for the Judiciary examined the use of speech-recognition and transcription software in courts to accelerate the production of written judicial documents. The operational challenge is that hearings and procedural events contain substantial spoken content, and delays in putting decisions or minutes into writing can affect court efficiency. The implementation applies automatic speech recognition to convert audio from proceedings into text, creating a draft transcript or written basis for further review. At a conceptual level, the system combines audio capture, speech-to-text processing, language modelling, transcription correction and integration into court workflows. It is intended to assist court staff rather than replace the official validation of minutes or decisions. The expected impact is reduced transcription time, better compliance with procedural deadlines, more efficient use of clerical capacity and faster availability of written records. For courts, the quality of such tools depends heavily on legal vocabulary, acoustic conditions, speaker variation and the ability to correct outputs reliably. The use case therefore illustrates both the potential and the implementation challenges of applying speech technology to judicial administration.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,S3_DOCUMENT_INTELLIGENCE,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1499,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01150,EU_PSTW_202609_01150,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Department of Justice and Equality,Law courts,"Road-policing and law-enforcement operations often require rapid identification of vehicles without manual reading or recording of registration plates. The expected impact is faster identification of vehicles, more efficient enforcement, reduced manual input, and stronger operational capacity in roads policing.",Automatic Number Plate Recognition (ANPR),Department of Justice and Equality; Central-Government; Law courts; Ireland,"Ireland’s Automatic Number Plate Recognition use case concerns deployment of ANPR technology by justice and policing authorities to support traffic and law-enforcement operations. The implementation uses optical character recognition, later capable of being augmented by artificial-intelligence techniques, to read and record vehicle registration plates automatically from camera feeds.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Road-policing and law-enforcement operations often require rapid identification of vehicles without manual reading or recording of registration plates. Ireland’s Automatic Number Plate Recognition use case concerns deployment of ANPR technology by justice and policing authorities to support traffic and law-enforcement operations. The operational problem is that officers may need to identify vehicles of interest, verify registration information or process large numbers of passing vehicles quickly and accurately. The implementation uses optical character recognition, later capable of being augmented by artificial-intelligence techniques, to read and record vehicle registration plates automatically from camera feeds. At a conceptual level, the system captures an image of a vehicle plate, converts it into text, compares it with relevant records or alert lists, and produces information for policing or administrative follow-up. The expected impact is faster identification of vehicles, more efficient enforcement, reduced manual input, and stronger operational capacity in roads policing. The technology also raises privacy and governance questions because vehicle movements can reveal sensitive information about individuals’ behaviour. For that reason, legal authorisation, retention rules, access controls and oversight are important elements of responsible deployment. The tool should be understood as an investigative and traffic-management support capability rather than an autonomous sanctioning system.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1500,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01151,EU_PSTW_202609_01151,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Department of Justice and Equality,Police Services,"Law-enforcement and justice authorities face recurring challenges in identifying persons of interest, comparing image material and supporting intelligence processes, but facial-matching technologies raise significant questions about accuracy, proportionality, bias and fundamental rights.",Evaluate the potential of facial matching technologies as an aid to the intelligence gathering process,Department of Justice and Equality; Central-Government; Police Services; Ireland,"The objective was exploratory: to assess potential use rather than to present a fully deployed policing system. However, the use case also sits in a high-risk area, because biometric matching in justice and policing contexts requires a clear legal basis, strict necessity and proportionality assessment, data-protection safeguards and transparency.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Law-enforcement and justice authorities face recurring challenges in identifying persons of interest, comparing image material and supporting intelligence processes, but facial-matching technologies raise significant questions about accuracy, proportionality, bias and fundamental rights. In Ireland, the Department of Justice and Equality developed a 2019 project to evaluate whether facial matching could assist intelligence gathering as part of a broader European mapping of innovative technologies in justice. The objective was exploratory: to assess potential use rather than to present a fully deployed policing system. The implementation concerned facial-matching technologies, which compare facial images to identify possible similarities or candidate matches. At a conceptual level, such a tool would require image inputs, biometric feature extraction, similarity scoring, human review and governance controls to ensure that any result remains investigative support rather than automatic identification or evidentiary determination. The expected value would be faster screening of image material, better support for intelligence workflows and improved prioritisation where large visual datasets are involved. However, the use case also sits in a high-risk area, because biometric matching in justice and policing contexts requires a clear legal basis, strict necessity and proportionality assessment, data-protection safeguards and transparency. The European Commission study records the Irish project as an evaluation initiative, and I found no evidence that it progressed to full operational deployment.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1501,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01152,EU_PSTW_202609_01152,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Brescia's Court of Appeal,Law courts,UNKNOWN,Predictive justice - a database to provide predictable guidelines and timing in particular areas,Brescia's Court of Appeal; Regional Government; Law courts; Italy,"The implementation uses a structured jurisprudence database supported by natural language processing and rule-based or expert-system logic. At a conceptual level, users choose a legal area and follow a guided path that progressively identifies the judicial situation most similar to their own issue, using essential linguistic and graphical presentation to make the case-law material easier to consult.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Judicial users, lawyers and citizens often need to understand how similar cases have been decided and how long proceedings may take, especially in areas where case-law orientation can affect litigation strategy and settlement decisions. In Brescia, the Court of Appeal developed a predictive justice initiative to organise selected decisions from the Brescia Court of Appeal and Ordinary Court into a navigable database. The objective was to provide predictable guidelines, relevant precedents and procedural orientation in particular legal areas, while supporting legal professionals rather than automating judicial decisions. The implementation uses a structured jurisprudence database supported by natural language processing and rule-based or expert-system logic. At a conceptual level, users choose a legal area and follow a guided path that progressively identifies the judicial situation most similar to their own issue, using essential linguistic and graphical presentation to make the case-law material easier to consult. The platform focuses on legal similarity, orientation and knowledge access rather than on generating binding outcomes. The expected impact is improved transparency of local case-law, faster retrieval of relevant decisions, better preparation by lawyers and parties, and greater consistency in understanding judicial tendencies. Current online material shows that the Brescia predictive justice platform remains publicly accessible and presents selected decisions grouped by thematic areas, notably labour and business law.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1502,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01153,EU_PSTW_202609_01153,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""social_nonprofit""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Milan's Court of Appeal,Law courts,"Private antitrust enforcement depends on access to specialised case law, but judgments in this field can be difficult to locate, compare and analyse across courts.",ITA.CA (Italian Case-Law On Private Antitrust Enforcement ),Milan's Court of Appeal; Regional Government; Law courts; Italy,"The implementation uses a structured online database hosted by the Eurojus scientific journal, collecting judgments and organising them so that users can search and analyse relevant material.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Private antitrust enforcement depends on access to specialised case law, but judgments in this field can be difficult to locate, compare and analyse across courts. ITA.CA was developed to collect and systematise Italian case law on private enforcement of competition law, initially around the Milan courts and later with broader contributions. The operational problem is that practitioners, judges, scholars and public stakeholders need a coherent view of decisions relating to damages actions, competition infringements and related civil litigation. The implementation uses a structured online database hosted by the Eurojus scientific journal, collecting judgments and organising them so that users can search and analyse relevant material. At a conceptual level, the system combines legal information retrieval, classification, expert curation and rule-based structuring of case-law content. It is not an autonomous legal decision-maker; rather, it supports legal knowledge management by making decisions easier to find and compare. The expected impact is improved legal transparency, better access to antitrust jurisprudence, stronger doctrinal analysis and more consistent understanding of private enforcement across Italy. The case is relevant to digital justice because curated and searchable databases are a foundation for more advanced legal analytics, even when the underlying technology is primarily rule-based or expert-system oriented.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1503,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01154,EU_PSTW_202609_01154,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Procesutore General's Office,Law courts,UNKNOWN,Voice recognition in court administration,Procesutore General's Office; Central-Government; Law courts; Latvia,"Latvia’s voice-recognition use case for the Prosecutor’s Office applied AI-based speech technology to support court-administration or prosecution-office workflows. The implementation uses voice-recognition technology to transform speech into text, reducing the need for purely manual typing and helping legal-administrative staff prepare records more efficiently.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Court and prosecution services generate large volumes of spoken material, including hearings, interviews, procedural acts and internal administrative communication. Latvia’s voice-recognition use case for the Prosecutor’s Office applied AI-based speech technology to support court-administration or prosecution-office workflows. The operational problem is that transcription and document preparation can be labour-intensive, especially where staff must convert spoken material into structured written records. The implementation uses voice-recognition technology to transform speech into text, reducing the need for purely manual typing and helping legal-administrative staff prepare records more efficiently. At a conceptual level, the tool combines audio capture, automatic speech recognition, language processing and human correction or validation. Its expected impact is faster production of written records, reduced clerical burden, more efficient use of prosecution staff and better standardisation of documentation. In justice settings, such systems must handle legal vocabulary, speaker variation, Latvian-language recognition requirements and confidentiality constraints. Human review remains essential, because transcript errors can have procedural consequences. The use case should therefore be understood as an administrative support tool: it can improve efficiency in preparing and managing documents, but it does not replace prosecutorial judgement or court decision-making.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1504,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01155,EU_PSTW_202609_01155,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Ministry of Justice,General public services n.e.c.,"Land-register services can be difficult for citizens and professionals to navigate because users must understand property identifiers, cadastral information, service categories and procedural steps.",Just - virtual assistant of the State Unitary Computerized Land Book,Ministry of Justice; Central-Government; General public services n.e.c.; Latvia,"The implementation uses a virtual assistant on the land-register portal, allowing users to ask questions or obtain orientation without relying only on static navigation menus.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Land-register services can be difficult for citizens and professionals to navigate because users must understand property identifiers, cadastral information, service categories and procedural steps. Latvia’s Court Administration introduced the virtual assistant Justs into the Land Register portal to help users find their way around the services of the State Unified Computerised Land Book. The objective was to provide a conversational guidance layer for people seeking land-register information or digital services. The implementation uses a virtual assistant on the land-register portal, allowing users to ask questions or obtain orientation without relying only on static navigation menus. At a conceptual level, the assistant maps user questions to service information, helps identify relevant procedures and supports navigation through the portal. Its expected impact is easier access to property-information services, fewer routine support enquiries, better digital self-service and improved user experience for citizens, businesses and legal professionals. Because land-register data are legally significant, the assistant’s role is informational: it supports navigation and service access, while formal registry records, rights and procedural acts remain governed by official systems and staff. The case is a useful example of conversational support in a specialised justice-administration domain where users may not know the correct terminology or route to obtain information.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1505,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01156,EU_PSTW_202609_01156,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,University of Alicante,R&D education.,"Maritime surveillance, coastal monitoring and aerial reconnaissance generate large volumes of visible-spectrum imagery in which ships or small unidentified floating objects may be difficult to identify quickly and consistently. Traditional approaches to ship detection have often relied on manually designed image-processing features, followed by conventional supervised classifiers, which can be sensitive to changing image conditions and may not capture complex visual patterns.",Automatic Ship Classification from Optical Aerial Images with Convolutional Neural Networks,University of Alicante; Academic-Research; R&D education.; Spain,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Maritime surveillance, coastal monitoring and aerial reconnaissance generate large volumes of visible-spectrum imagery in which ships or small unidentified floating objects may be difficult to identify quickly and consistently. Traditional approaches to ship detection have often relied on manually designed image-processing features, followed by conventional supervised classifiers, which can be sensitive to changing image conditions and may not capture complex visual patterns. Researchers at the University of Alicante addressed this challenge by developing a method to determine whether an optical aerial image contains a ship. The implementation used a convolutional neural network architecture adapted to visible-spectrum aerial imagery. At a conceptual level, the model extracted neural visual codes from images and combined them with a k-nearest neighbour method to improve classification performance. This approach moved feature extraction away from manually engineered descriptors and towards representations learned from image data. The expected impact is faster and more accurate screening of aerial image collections, reduced manual inspection effort and stronger support for maritime monitoring applications such as traffic surveillance, search and rescue, environmental control or security tasks. The work is research-oriented, but it demonstrates how deep-learning methods can improve remote-sensing image interpretation when the operational question is binary and clearly framed: whether a ship is present or absent in the observed scene.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK|F06_HIGH_VOLUME_SCALABILITY_BOTTLENECK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1506,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01157,EU_PSTW_202609_01157,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,EWZ - Service Division of the Department of Public Utilities and Transport of the City of Zurich,Transport,UNKNOWN,LoRaWAN - The IoT network for Zurich,EWZ - Service Division of the Department of Public Utilities and Transport of the City of Zurich; Local Government; Transport; Switzerland,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Chronic-disease care requires continuity across hospitals, community services, clinicians, patients and caregivers, especially where repeated outpatient visits or avoidable hospitalisations can reduce quality of life and increase pressure on health systems. COReHealth was developed in Puglia as a regional telemedicine operations centre for chronic diseases and clinical networks, with a particular role in turning the Apulian Oncological Network into a digitally enabled care ecosystem. The operational problem is that patients with chronic conditions often need monitoring, therapeutic adherence support, clinical follow-up and timely intervention without necessarily being physically present in hospital. The implementation provides a telemedicine platform through which patients can be cared for from home, send measurements, monitor their therapeutic plan, receive support for self-management and access remote-care services. At a conceptual level, the system connects patients, clinical networks and regional operational coordination through digital tools, enabling data collection, monitoring, communication and escalation where intervention is needed. The expected impact is reduced hospitalisation, fewer outpatient visits, better continuity of care, improved patient comfort and more proactive disease management. COReHealth is also significant because it positions telemedicine as a regional operating model rather than a single disease-specific application, creating infrastructure that can support chronic-care pathways and clinical networks across multiple territories.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1507,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01158,EU_PSTW_202609_01158,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,DigiFarm AS,"Agriculture, forestry, fishing and hunting",UNKNOWN,Sen4Weeds: automatic detection in-field weeds,"DigiFarm AS; Consortium; Agriculture, forestry, fishing and hunting; Norway","Farmers need timely and reliable information on weed pressure, because uncontrolled weeds reduce yields, increase inspection workload and often lead to broad herbicide use even when infestation is spatially uneven. Sen4Weeds was developed within AI4Copernicus to create an automated system for detecting and mapping weed patches at large scale, using Earth-observation data rather than relying only on field scouting.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Farmers need timely and reliable information on weed pressure, because uncontrolled weeds reduce yields, increase inspection workload and often lead to broad herbicide use even when infestation is spatially uneven. Sen4Weeds was developed within AI4Copernicus to create an automated system for detecting and mapping weed patches at large scale, using Earth-observation data rather than relying only on field scouting. The objective is to support earlier, more targeted interventions that improve productivity while reducing environmental impact and input costs. The implementation combines high-resolution and super-resolved Sentinel-2 imagery, automated field delineation, weed classification and AI-based spectral unmixing of multi-spectral satellite data. At a conceptual level, the system identifies field boundaries, enhances satellite-image detail, detects spectral patterns associated with weeds and produces maps showing where weed pressure is likely to be concentrated. These outputs can then help farmers, advisers or agri-tech platforms decide where treatment is needed. The expected impact is more efficient crop monitoring, reduced manual inspection, lower herbicide use and improved scalability across different crops, weed species and regions. By linking Copernicus data with machine-learning models, Sen4Weeds demonstrates how satellite-based precision agriculture can move from general vegetation monitoring towards actionable, within-field weed-management intelligence.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1508,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01159,EU_PSTW_202609_01159,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,ENCORE LAB,"Agriculture, forestry, fishing and hunting","The operational problem is not the absence of satellite data, but the difficulty of translating seasonal vegetation signals into practical decisions for field management and harvest planning.",Scaviho: scalable vegetation index and harvesting forecaster,"ENCORE LAB; Consortium; Agriculture, forestry, fishing and hunting; Spain","Precision agriculture already uses NDVI widely to monitor crop condition, but intermediate NDVI values during the growth stage can be difficult for farmers to interpret despite containing important information on crop development. The implementation uses historical Sentinel-2 NDVI data, crop phenological information and artificial-intelligence methods to broaden the interpretation of intermediate vegetation-index values.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Precision agriculture already uses NDVI widely to monitor crop condition, but intermediate NDVI values during the growth stage can be difficult for farmers to interpret despite containing important information on crop development. In Spain, Scaviho was developed to make vegetation-index information more actionable for vineyards and other agricultural contexts, helping users assess growth evolution, anticipate hidden problems and forecast harvesting dates. The operational problem is not the absence of satellite data, but the difficulty of translating seasonal vegetation signals into practical decisions for field management and harvest planning. The implementation uses historical Sentinel-2 NDVI data, crop phenological information and artificial-intelligence methods to broaden the interpretation of intermediate vegetation-index values. At a conceptual level, the solution builds a scale that links NDVI patterns to crop-growth stages and uses past satellite observations to identify deviations or signals relevant to expected harvest timing. The project involves ENCORE LAB and Castillo de Maetierra, combining digital-agriculture capabilities with vineyard operational experience. The expected impact is better crop monitoring, earlier detection of hidden stress, improved harvest planning and more precise use of remote-sensing information in everyday farm decisions. AI4Copernicus and the AI-on-demand platform describe Scaviho as an Open Call success story whose results have been developed in a consortium already using satellite images in daily agricultural operations.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1509,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01160,EU_PSTW_202609_01160,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Örebro Universit,"Mining, manufacturing and construction.",UNKNOWN,Planning for flexible manufacturing with robots,"Örebro Universit; Academic-Research; Mining, manufacturing and construction.; Sweden","Heavy-vehicle axle production involves many machining stages, machine constraints, stock dependencies and planning decisions that are difficult to automate when they rely heavily on human expertise. In this Swedish use case, the challenge is to support flexible manufacturing of front axles by transforming expert production knowledge into a form that automated planning and scheduling systems can use.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Heavy-vehicle axle production involves many machining stages, machine constraints, stock dependencies and planning decisions that are difficult to automate when they rely heavily on human expertise. In this Swedish use case, the challenge is to support flexible manufacturing of front axles by transforming expert production knowledge into a form that automated planning and scheduling systems can use. Human planners understand which operations are possible, which machines can perform them, how available stock affects production routes and how orders should be sequenced, but this knowledge is often implicit and hard to scale. The implementation combines planning and scheduling techniques to generate machining plans from three main inputs: expert knowledge extracted from human planners, incoming order requirements and available stock. At a conceptual level, the system formalises production rules, links them to machine capabilities and uses scheduling-based reasoning to identify feasible and efficient machining sequences. This allows planning decisions to be produced more systematically while remaining grounded in operational know-how. The expected impact is faster production planning, better use of machinery, reduced dependence on individual planners and improved ability to respond to changing orders or stock availability. By combining knowledge representation with automated scheduling, the use case demonstrates how industrial planning systems can support flexible, small-batch or variable manufacturing without requiring a complete redesign of production infrastructure. (aiod.eu",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1510,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01161,EU_PSTW_202609_01161,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,German Research Center for Artificial Intelligence,"Mining, manufacturing and construction.","The practical challenge is that a robot may need to find boxes or other objects placed on tables, move through the environment, decide which actions to perform and adapt when observations differ from the initial model.",Planning and execution for a mobile indoor robot,"German Research Center for Artificial Intelligence; Academic-Research; Mining, manufacturing and construction.; Germany","This German use case explores how task planning and execution can control a mobile robot performing manipulation tasks in such settings. The use case is valuable because it demonstrates not only plan generation, but also the operational challenge of making plans executable on real robots.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Indoor mobile manipulation requires a robot to act in environments where object positions, task conditions and execution outcomes may not be fully known in advance. This German use case explores how task planning and execution can control a mobile robot performing manipulation tasks in such settings. The practical challenge is that a robot may need to find boxes or other objects placed on tables, move through the environment, decide which actions to perform and adapt when observations differ from the initial model. The implementation combines task-planning methods with execution control on a mobile robotic platform. In the demonstration scenario, the robot starts without knowing where objects are located and must plan, perceive, move, manipulate and update its knowledge as the task progresses. At a conceptual level, symbolic planning represents goals, actions and preconditions, while execution modules dispatch actions to the robot, monitor results and trigger replanning if expected conditions fail. This creates a loop between high-level reasoning and low-level robotic control. The expected impact is more robust service robotics, improved ability to operate in uncertain indoor environments and a clearer path from planning research to physical deployment. The use case is valuable because it demonstrates not only plan generation, but also the operational challenge of making plans executable on real robots. (research.aiodp.eu",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1512,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01162,EU_PSTW_202609_01162,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Kirklees Council traffic authority,Public order and safety n.e.c.,"The operational problem is that urban networks experience congestion, emissions, delays and safety risks, while traffic-control systems often act through limited instruments such as traffic-light timing.",AI planning for integrated urban traffic control,Kirklees Council traffic authority; Local Government; Public order and safety n.e.c.; United Kingdom,"In Kirklees and related research settings, this use case examines how urban traffic control could combine conventional signal management with route guidance for connected vehicles. The implementation uses planning techniques to coordinate traffic-light decisions and connected-vehicle routing so that the two mechanisms work together rather than separately.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Connected and autonomous vehicles create new possibilities for traffic management because they can exchange information with infrastructure rather than only reacting independently to road conditions. In Kirklees and related research settings, this use case examines how urban traffic control could combine conventional signal management with route guidance for connected vehicles. The operational problem is that urban networks experience congestion, emissions, delays and safety risks, while traffic-control systems often act through limited instruments such as traffic-light timing. Vehicle-to-infrastructure communication adds a further control channel: vehicles can provide information about traffic conditions and receive guidance about preferable routes. The implementation uses planning techniques to coordinate traffic-light decisions and connected-vehicle routing so that the two mechanisms work together rather than separately. At a conceptual level, the system represents the traffic network, vehicle movements, signal states and possible interventions, then generates control strategies able to respond to expected or unusual conditions. The expected impact is reduced congestion, lower emissions, time savings, improved accident prevention and more integrated management of urban mobility. Research linked to this area shows that model-based planning can address both normal patterns and unexpected traffic conditions in real time, making it relevant for future traffic-control systems as connected vehicles become more common. (aiod.eu",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1514,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01163,EU_PSTW_202609_01163,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Fundación Asturiana de la Energía (FAEN),Fuel and energy,UNKNOWN,AI for predicting the climate change impact in RES and energy demand at regiona and local level,Fundación Asturiana de la Energía (FAEN); Academic-Research; Fuel and energy; Spain,"The implementation uses artificial intelligence to connect broad climate projections with in situ data, producing finer-scale estimates of local climate impacts and their consequences for renewable energy and demand. By translating climate projections into actionable local energy intelligence, the use case supports public authorities and energy agencies in aligning climate adaptation, renewable deployment and demand management.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Regional energy planners need more precise information on how climate change will affect renewable-resource availability and local energy demand, because high-level climate projections are often too coarse for operational decisions. In Asturias, FAEN led a pilot focused on downscaling climate information to support regional and local energy planning. The problem is that changing weather patterns can alter heating and cooling demand, solar and wind generation potential, and the reliability of renewable-energy planning assumptions. Existing climate models may describe long-term trends, but their spatial resolution is often insufficient for local infrastructure, investment or policy decisions. The implementation uses artificial intelligence to connect broad climate projections with in situ data, producing finer-scale estimates of local climate impacts and their consequences for renewable energy and demand. At a conceptual level, the system combines climate-model outputs, local measurements, energy-demand data and renewable-generation indicators to generate geographically relevant forecasts. The expected impact is better regional energy planning, improved assessment of renewable potential, stronger adaptation strategies and more resilient investment decisions. By translating climate projections into actionable local energy intelligence, the use case supports public authorities and energy agencies in aligning climate adaptation, renewable deployment and demand management. (research.aiodp.eu",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1516,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01164,EU_PSTW_202609_01164,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Ministry of Economic Affairs and Communication,R&D economic affairs,"Small and medium-sized enterprises and regional development actors often lack timely, data-driven insights to assess business resilience, market positioning and risk exposure. Fragmented data sources and delayed financial indicators can hinder proactive decision-making, increasing the likelihood of business failure.",Business Viability Index: AI support for business planning and avoiding failures,Ministry of Economic Affairs and Communication; Central-Government; R&D economic affairs; Estonia,"The solution aggregates heterogeneous data sources, including market trends, financial performance indicators and operational metrics, and applies supervised and unsupervised machine learning models alongside deep neural networks to detect behavioural patterns and correlations.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Small and medium-sized enterprises and regional development actors often lack timely, data-driven insights to assess business resilience, market positioning and risk exposure. Fragmented data sources and delayed financial indicators can hinder proactive decision-making, increasing the likelihood of business failure. Stakeholders include entrepreneurs, investors, financial institutions and regional authorities. The objective is to provide an integrated, real-time assessment framework that supports strategic planning and early risk detection. The solution aggregates heterogeneous data sources, including market trends, financial performance indicators and operational metrics, and applies supervised and unsupervised machine learning models alongside deep neural networks to detect behavioural patterns and correlations. Feature engineering and predictive analytics generate a composite viability index, delivered through dashboards and decision-support interfaces integrated with enterprise management systems. Anticipated impacts include improved strategic foresight, earlier identification of financial distress signals, more informed investment decisions and strengthened regional economic resilience, with scalability across sectors due to modular data integration and cloud-based deployment.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,F07_DATA_VISIBILITY_INTEGRATION_GAP,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1524,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01165,EU_PSTW_202609_01165,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,AReSS Puglia,Public health services.,COReHealth (Regional telemedicine operations centre for chronic diseases and clinical networks) addresses the challenges posed by the COVID-19 emergency and aims to transform the Apulian Oncological Network into a digitally-enabled ecosystem.,COReHealth – operating centre of telemedicine for chronic conditions and clinical networks,AReSS Puglia; Regional Government; Public health services.; Italy,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"COReHealth (Regional telemedicine operations centre for chronic diseases and clinical networks) addresses the challenges posed by the COVID-19 emergency and aims to transform the Apulian Oncological Network into a digitally-enabled ecosystem. Thanks to COReHealth, people with chronic diseases can be treated at home, i.e. in a comfortable environment, thus reducing hospitalisations and visits to outpatient clinics. It enables self-management of one's illness and the possibility of being constantly followed, monitored and treated even at a distance or with timely interventions.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1525,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01166,EU_PSTW_202609_01166,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Federal Agency for Technical Relief (THW),R&D public order and safety.,It is particularly useful where personnel need to rehearse high-risk movements regularly but cannot reproduce dangerous field conditions safely.,VR4SAW | RESCUESAW VR - Virtual exercise for disaster response,Federal Agency for Technical Relief (THW); Central-Government; R&D public order and safety.; Germany,"Forestry workers, firefighters and rescue-service personnel face serious risks when using chainsaws or rescue saws, particularly because recoil, cutting errors and unstable working conditions can cause severe injuries. The implementation uses virtual reality to simulate chainsaw handling, cutting techniques and work steps in a controlled environment.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Forestry workers, firefighters and rescue-service personnel face serious risks when using chainsaws or rescue saws, particularly because recoil, cutting errors and unstable working conditions can cause severe injuries. VR4SAW, also presented as CHAIN SAW VR or RESCUESAW VR, addresses the need for frequent, safe and cost-effective training without fuel consumption, equipment wear or exposure to dangerous practice conditions. The implementation uses virtual reality to simulate chainsaw handling, cutting techniques and work steps in a controlled environment. Trainees wear a VR headset and interact with a secured saw-like device, allowing them to practise procedures and understand hazards before operating in real emergencies. At a conceptual level, the simulator combines immersive visual scenarios, tool handling, procedural instruction and risk-feedback mechanisms. The expected impact is safer chainsaw use, more repeatable training, reduced material cost and better preparation for rescue or forestry operations. It is particularly useful where personnel need to rehearse high-risk movements regularly but cannot reproduce dangerous field conditions safely. Public product material and reporting present the solution as a VR training product for foresters and emergency services, rather than only a research prototype.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1532,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01167,EU_PSTW_202609_01167,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Karlsruhe Institute of Technology (KIT),R&D environmental protection.,UNKNOWN,Digital assistant for forest management,Karlsruhe Institute of Technology (KIT); Academic-Research; R&D environmental protection.; Germany,"The implementation develops a digital assistant that uses district and inventory-related data together with medium-term and regional climate forecasts to support harvesting decisions in real time and in the field. The expected impact is more resilient harvesting planning, better adaptation to climate risks, improved use of local forest information and stronger support for less experienced decision-makers.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Forest managers must plan harvesting under conditions that are becoming more uncertain because of climate change, biodiversity requirements, market pressures and the loss of experienced local knowledge. In Germany, the EDE 4.0 project focuses on logging planning as a central task of forest management, covering the type, quantity and quality of wood to be harvested. District foresters and forest enterprises need recommendations that reflect inventory data, stand conditions, regional climate forecasts and operational constraints, but established decision-making routines may be too static for rapidly changing forests. The implementation develops a digital assistant that uses district and inventory-related data together with medium-term and regional climate forecasts to support harvesting decisions in real time and in the field. At a conceptual level, the system combines forest data, growth modelling, predictive analytics and mobile decision support, extending established processes with data-based recommendations rather than replacing professional judgement. The expected impact is more resilient harvesting planning, better adaptation to climate risks, improved use of local forest information and stronger support for less experienced decision-makers. Project reporting indicates that EDE 4.0 integrates multiple data sources and forest-growth modelling to create predictive recommendations for dynamic logging planning. (www.fnr.de",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1536,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01168,EU_PSTW_202609_01168,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,University of Bonn,"Agriculture, forestry, fishing and hunting","PhenoRob, the Cluster of Excellence led by the University of Bonn with Forschungszentrum Jülich, addresses this challenge by combining robotics, phenotyping, digitalisation and machine learning for sustainable crop production.",PhenoRob proejct - Fertilise crops more specifically,"University of Bonn; Academic-Research; Agriculture, forestry, fishing and hunting; Germany","The objective is to understand plant growth, nutrient use and water efficiency more precisely so that fertiliser and crop-protection measures can be applied only where and when needed. The implementation uses ground robots, drones, sensor-based phenotyping, field monitoring and large-scale data analysis.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Crop production must become more resource-efficient while maintaining yields under climate, biodiversity and environmental pressures. PhenoRob, the Cluster of Excellence led by the University of Bonn with Forschungszentrum Jülich, addresses this challenge by combining robotics, phenotyping, digitalisation and machine learning for sustainable crop production. The objective is to understand plant growth, nutrient use and water efficiency more precisely so that fertiliser and crop-protection measures can be applied only where and when needed. The implementation uses ground robots, drones, sensor-based phenotyping, field monitoring and large-scale data analysis. At a conceptual level, robotic platforms collect high-resolution data on crop growth, soil, canopy structure and field conditions, while machine-learning models transform these observations into knowledge about plant performance and resource needs. This supports more targeted agronomic interventions and helps researchers explore crop adaptability to climate conditions and biodiversity-preserving practices. The expected impact is lower input use, reduced environmental burden, better yield stability and more robust evidence for sustainable farming systems. PhenoRob is not a single deployed product but a major interdisciplinary research cluster whose work feeds methods, prototypes and scientific knowledge into future agricultural practice. In 2025, the cluster received further funding for a second phase extending to 2032, confirming continued institutional support.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1542,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01169,EU_PSTW_202609_01169,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,German Research Center for Artificial Intelligence (DFKI),"Mining, manufacturing and construction.",UNKNOWN,FlatFish - Autonomous inspections in the deep sea,"German Research Center for Artificial Intelligence (DFKI); Academic-Research; Mining, manufacturing and construction.; Germany",FlatFish was developed to simplify repeated subsea inspection by using an autonomous underwater vehicle capable of operating for extended periods around underwater structures.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Inspection of offshore oil and gas infrastructure is expensive, technically complex and risky when it depends on remotely operated vehicles, support vessels and specialist divers. FlatFish was developed to simplify repeated subsea inspection by using an autonomous underwater vehicle capable of operating for extended periods around underwater structures. The objective is to reduce inspection cost and risk while improving the frequency and quality of monitoring for industrial assets. The implementation combines autonomous navigation, acoustic and optical sensors, subsea docking concepts and robotic inspection capabilities. At a conceptual level, the vehicle maps and localises itself underwater, follows inspection missions, collects visual and sensor data, and performs tasks such as structure and pipeline inspection without continuous tethered control. Original research involved DFKI, the Brazilian Institute of Robotics and Shell, while later industrial development connected FlatFish to Saipem’s Hydrone underwater robotics programme. The expected impact is lower reliance on maintenance ships, reduced exposure of personnel to hazardous offshore work, better repeatability of inspections and more efficient asset monitoring. Recent public information indicates that FlatFish has advanced beyond laboratory research: Saipem reported successful functional testing with Petrobras in 2026, including autonomous inspection missions, cathodic-protection measurements and wall-thickness assessments.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1544,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01170,EU_PSTW_202609_01170,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Technical University Ilmenau,Protection of biodiversity and landscape.,"Biodiversity awareness depends on people being able to recognise the plants around them, but botanical identification can be difficult without specialist knowledge.",Flora Incognita app – recognise wild plants using an app,Technical University Ilmenau; Academic-Research; Protection of biodiversity and landscape.; Germany,"Flora Incognita addresses this gap through a free mobile application that uses artificial intelligence to identify wild plants from images taken with a smartphone or tablet. The implementation applies deep-learning models to photographs of plant parts such as flowers, leaves, bark or fruit.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Biodiversity awareness depends on people being able to recognise the plants around them, but botanical identification can be difficult without specialist knowledge. Flora Incognita addresses this gap through a free mobile application that uses artificial intelligence to identify wild plants from images taken with a smartphone or tablet. The objective is both educational and scientific: users receive immediate help identifying species, while the observations generated through the app can contribute to research on plant distribution, biodiversity change and environmental monitoring. The implementation applies deep-learning models to photographs of plant parts such as flowers, leaves, bark or fruit. At a conceptual level, the app analyses the visual characteristics of the plant, compares them with learned species patterns and returns a likely identification together with supporting information on characteristics, distribution and protection status. Its expected impact is wider public engagement with nature, easier access to botanical knowledge, increased participation in citizen science and better data for conservation research. The app is especially useful for schools, universities, nature-protection initiatives and individual users who want to learn more about local flora. By combining image recognition with educational content and observation recording, Flora Incognita turns everyday encounters with plants into structured biodiversity data and public learning opportunities.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1546,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01171,EU_PSTW_202609_01171,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Karlsruhe Institute of Technology (KIT),R&D health.,UNKNOWN,HoloMed – precise puncturing during brain operations,Karlsruhe Institute of Technology (KIT); Academic-Research; R&D health.; Germany,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Neurosurgical ventricular punctures require extremely precise placement of a needle or catheter into deep brain structures that are not directly visible during the operation. Freehand procedures can be challenging because the target lies inside the brain, the permitted angle may have only a few degrees of tolerance and every minute can matter in acute cases such as brain haemorrhage or stroke. HoloMed was developed to support surgeons by making patient-specific anatomical information visible in their field of view. The implementation creates a three-dimensional model of the patient’s brain from CT or MRI images and displays relevant guidance through augmented-reality glasses. At a conceptual level, the system combines medical imaging, model generation, spatial registration, augmented-reality visualisation and surgical navigation. Graphic elements help the medical team identify the puncture point and insertion angle, allowing hidden structures to be mapped onto the patient during the procedure. KIT notes that only about 60% of freehand punctures reach an optimal position, which explains the clinical rationale for navigation support. The expected impact is higher first-attempt accuracy, improved safety, reduced cognitive load and more efficient surgical workflows. Prototype testing at Günzburg hospital was reported positively, although the available sources present HoloMed as a research and prototype system rather than routine clinical deployment. (kit.edu",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1548,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01172,EU_PSTW_202609_01172,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,DFKI Bremen,R&D health.,"Stroke and other neurological conditions can leave patients with severe movement limitations, creating a need for intensive, repetitive and adaptive rehabilitation that is difficult to provide continuously through therapist-led sessions alone.",Wearable exoskeleton for stroke patients,DFKI Bremen; Academic-Research; R&D health.; Germany,"The implementation uses a metal exoskeleton structure with modular actuators, sensor systems and control software.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Stroke and other neurological conditions can leave patients with severe movement limitations, creating a need for intensive, repetitive and adaptive rehabilitation that is difficult to provide continuously through therapist-led sessions alone. In Bremen, DFKI’s Robotics Innovation Center developed Recupera REHA as a mobile whole-body exoskeleton and modular assistance system for robot-supported rehabilitation. The aim was to expand therapy options by helping patients practise gripping, arm movement, standing or walking with robotic support adapted to their impairment. The implementation uses a metal exoskeleton structure with modular actuators, sensor systems and control software. Individual actuator modules can support the body directly or be reconfigured as gripper arms attached to a wheelchair, enabling assistance for different mobility needs. At a conceptual level, the system combines rehabilitation robotics, biosignal analysis, sensor fusion and adaptive “assist-as-needed” control so that support can match the patient’s capability and therapy goals. The expected impact is more intensive rehabilitation, greater patient autonomy, reduced dependence on constant therapist assistance and new possibilities for home or near-home training. The project ran from 2014 to 2017, and later research continued to advance the Recupera-Reha exoskeleton’s modelling and control for actions such as sitting, standing and static walking. (dfki.de",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1549,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01173,EU_PSTW_202609_01173,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Georg-August University of Göttingen (UGOE) and Julius Kühn Institute (JKI,"Agriculture, forestry, fishing and hunting",UNKNOWN,NOcsPS (Agriculture 4.0 without chemical-synthetic crop protection),"Georg-August University of Göttingen (UGOE) and Julius Kühn Institute (JKI; Academic-Research; Agriculture, forestry, fishing and hunting; Germany","NOcsPS develops a cultivation model that sits between conventional and organic farming: it avoids chemical-synthetic crop protection while retaining mineral fertilisation and using new cultivation measures, data-driven monitoring and agronomic optimisation.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Pressure to reduce chemical-synthetic plant protection products is reshaping agricultural research, because farmers still need systems that protect yields, manage weeds and diseases, and remain economically viable. NOcsPS develops a cultivation model that sits between conventional and organic farming: it avoids chemical-synthetic crop protection while retaining mineral fertilisation and using new cultivation measures, data-driven monitoring and agronomic optimisation. The initiative brings together the University of Hohenheim, the University of Göttingen and the Julius Kühn Institute to test whether more sustainable cropping systems can be designed without seriously impairing yield performance. The implementation combines field trials, crop-rotation design, soil and fertilisation strategies, digital monitoring and Agriculture 4.0 methods. At a conceptual level, data from experimental plots, crop growth, management practices and environmental conditions are used to compare production systems and support decisions on how to control pests, weeds and diseases without conventional pesticides. Initial research indicates that systems without chemical-synthetic plant protection generally achieve lower yields than conventional systems, but considerably higher yields than organic systems, suggesting a potential middle path for sustainable intensification. The expected impact is reduced pesticide dependency, improved evidence for future farming systems, and better support for farmers facing regulatory and environmental pressure. NOcsPS has moved into a second phase, with 18 scientific consortium partners working across cropping systems, sustainability and transformation.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1550,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01174,EU_PSTW_202609_01174,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Karlsruhe Institute of Technology (KIT),"Mining, manufacturing and construction.","ARMAR-6 was developed by Karlsruhe Institute of Technology as a humanoid assistance robot able to support people in industrial contexts, especially where tasks cannot be fully anticipated at programming time.",ARMAR-6 – Cooperative robot in industrial environments,"Karlsruhe Institute of Technology (KIT); Academic-Research; Mining, manufacturing and construction.; Germany","Industrial maintenance often requires workers to perform varied physical tasks in environments where tool use, unexpected situations and human cooperation are central. The objective is to create a robot that can recognise when a human partner needs help, use human-designed tools and perform or assist with maintenance activities.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Industrial maintenance often requires workers to perform varied physical tasks in environments where tool use, unexpected situations and human cooperation are central. ARMAR-6 was developed by Karlsruhe Institute of Technology as a humanoid assistance robot able to support people in industrial contexts, especially where tasks cannot be fully anticipated at programming time. The objective is to create a robot that can recognise when a human partner needs help, use human-designed tools and perform or assist with maintenance activities. The implementation combines a human-like upper body, bimanual manipulation, mobile movement, perception, action planning and interaction capabilities. At a conceptual level, the robot observes the work situation, interprets human activity, identifies opportunities to assist and executes tasks such as handing tools, holding components or operating equipment. Its human-like morphology allows it to interact with objects and tools designed for people, such as drills or hammers. The expected impact is safer and more flexible industrial support, reduced physical burden on workers and better handling of tasks that are difficult to automate with fixed machinery. ARMAR-6 remains primarily a research platform for collaborative humanoid robotics, but it is important because it demonstrates how industrial assistance can move from pre-programmed automation towards proactive, context-aware cooperation with human workers.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1551,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01175,EU_PSTW_202609_01175,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Fingal County Council,Community development.,"Public consultation on urban regeneration can be difficult when residents are asked to interpret technical plans, drawings or planning documents that do not clearly show how a place will feel once works are completed.",Dublin's Balbriggan district 3D Model to visualise in VR,Fingal County Council; Local Government; Community development.; Ireland,"The implementation uses aerial survey photography and ground-level photo surveys to create a digital 3D map, or digital twin, of Balbriggan.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Public consultation on urban regeneration can be difficult when residents are asked to interpret technical plans, drawings or planning documents that do not clearly show how a place will feel once works are completed. Fingal County Council developed the Balbriggan 3D Model to help citizens visualise the planned transformations in the Our Balbriggan 2019–2025 Town Rejuvenation Plan, particularly around Quay Street and the Harbour. The objective is to make proposed public-realm changes more understandable, tangible and accessible, enabling local people to engage with the future of the town through an intuitive visual environment. The implementation uses aerial survey photography and ground-level photo surveys to create a digital 3D map, or digital twin, of Balbriggan. This model can be explored through a web viewer and virtual-reality experience, allowing users to see planned changes to streets, the harbour and public spaces in a spatially realistic way. At a conceptual level, the system connects geospatial capture, three-dimensional modelling, visualisation and citizen-engagement workflows. The expected impact is better-informed public consultation, higher-quality feedback, improved trust in regeneration planning and stronger community participation in place-making. Fingal County Council now presents Balbriggan as part of a wider digital-twin portfolio covering public-realm projects, while the Quay Street and Harbour works have progressed as a €15 million transformation scheme.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1554,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01176,EU_PSTW_202609_01176,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Bristol City Council,Public health services.,"Care staff supporting people with dementia need to understand how sensory, visual and motor challenges shape everyday tasks, but classroom training may not fully convey what dementia can feel like from the resident’s perspective. The objective was to improve person-centred care by helping staff experience simulated challenges that residents may encounter when completing basic activities.",Bristol city council virtual reality program to train caregivers,Bristol City Council; Local Government; Public health services.; United Kingdom,"The implementation uses VR headsets to immerse trainees in scenarios that replicate aspects of dementia-related perception and movement difficulty. At a conceptual level, the training converts experiential simulation into practical reflection: staff observe how tasks become confusing or physically difficult, then use this insight to design better care plans and support environments that set residents up for success.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Care staff supporting people with dementia need to understand how sensory, visual and motor challenges shape everyday tasks, but classroom training may not fully convey what dementia can feel like from the resident’s perspective. Bristol City Council funded a virtual-reality dementia training programme at Redfield Lodge, its council-owned care home specialising in dementia care. The objective was to improve person-centred care by helping staff experience simulated challenges that residents may encounter when completing basic activities. The implementation uses VR headsets to immerse trainees in scenarios that replicate aspects of dementia-related perception and movement difficulty. At a conceptual level, the training converts experiential simulation into practical reflection: staff observe how tasks become confusing or physically difficult, then use this insight to design better care plans and support environments that set residents up for success. The expected impact is greater empathy, improved staff awareness, more appropriate care planning and better daily support for residents. The programme began with around 30 caregivers and was expanded to managers and supervisors, indicating organisational uptake beyond a single training session. Public reporting describes the initiative as complete, with the council funding VR training to improve dementia-care practice.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1555,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01177,EU_PSTW_202609_01177,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Ravenna,General public services n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,Use of IoT technologies to gather data from the City of Ferrara,City of Ravenna; Local Government; General public services n.e.c.; Italy,"Cities that manage tourism, air quality, shared mobility and public-space use need timely information from many urban systems, but these data are often fragmented across departments, platforms and service providers. Ravenna’s smart-city work has explored the use of IoT technologies to monitor sensitive urban indicators, including air quality, tourist flows and mobility-sharing patterns.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Cities that manage tourism, air quality, shared mobility and public-space use need timely information from many urban systems, but these data are often fragmented across departments, platforms and service providers. Ravenna’s smart-city work has explored the use of IoT technologies to monitor sensitive urban indicators, including air quality, tourist flows and mobility-sharing patterns. The operational objective is to help the municipality understand how people move through the city, how tourism affects local areas and where environmental or mobility pressures require intervention. The implementation is based on connected sensors, data collection from urban infrastructure and analytical platforms that support monitoring and planning. At a conceptual level, IoT devices and digital services collect local observations, which can then be integrated into dashboards or planning tools to inform mobility, tourism and environmental policies. This approach is consistent with Ravenna’s wider digital transition trajectory, including the DARE programme and the more recent FOOTPRINTS initiative, which focuses on sustainable tourism, accessibility, mobility, digital facilities and flow management. The expected impact is improved situational awareness, better management of tourist pressure, more evidence-based mobility planning and stronger capacity to reduce the ecological footprint of visitors. Current public information suggests that Ravenna continues to develop smart mobility and sustainable destination tools, although the precise status of the individual IoT pilots described in the original source is not fully specified.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1556,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01178,EU_PSTW_202609_01178,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Schiphol Airport Amsterdam,Transport,"The expected impact is faster response to bottlenecks, improved staffing and resource allocation, more reliable passenger information and better long-term planning of terminal infrastructure.",Veovo BlipTrack Guest Predictability solution in Amsterdam Airport Schiphol,Schiphol Airport Amsterdam; Central-Government; Transport; Netherlands,"The implementation uses sensors and analytics to detect passenger movement and estimate queue length, dwell time and flow across terminal processes.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Large airports need to manage passenger flows through security, border control, gates and terminal areas while avoiding congestion and maintaining predictable journey times. Amsterdam Airport Schiphol adopted Veovo’s BlipTrack / Passenger Predictability technology to measure queues and passenger movement automatically, beginning with security-process wait-time measurement. The operational problem was that airport staff needed real-time information on where congestion was forming and how long passengers were waiting, especially during irregular operations or periods of high demand. The implementation uses sensors and analytics to detect passenger movement and estimate queue length, dwell time and flow across terminal processes. At a conceptual level, the system collects movement signals, converts them into operational indicators and provides airport teams with dashboards or alerts that support proactive capacity management. The expected impact is faster response to bottlenecks, improved staffing and resource allocation, more reliable passenger information and better long-term planning of terminal infrastructure. The technology also supports a shift from reactive airport management to predictive flow management, where data from multiple stages of the passenger journey helps identify pressure points before they become severe. Its value lies in combining real-time operational awareness with evidence for future process and facility improvements.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1561,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01179,EU_PSTW_202609_01179,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Valencia,Environmental protection n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,Smart monitoring system of tourism footprint,City of Valencia; Local Government; Environmental protection n.e.c.; Spain,"The implementation uses smart labels or smart tags assigned to different tourism activities, breaking each activity into a calculation scheme that estimates its carbon footprint. The use case is relevant because it applies blockchain to environmental verification rather than financial speculation, focusing on trust, auditability and shared sustainability data across a destination ecosystem.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Tourism destinations seeking carbon neutrality need reliable information about the environmental impact of visitor activity, but tourism emissions are distributed across accommodation, mobility, restaurants, attractions, events and other services. Visit Valencia adopted GO2, a blockchain-based tool, to calculate and manage the carbon footprint of tourism activity. The objective is to support data-driven decision-making and make sustainability performance more visible for destination managers and tourism actors. The implementation uses smart labels or smart tags assigned to different tourism activities, breaking each activity into a calculation scheme that estimates its carbon footprint. At a conceptual level, data from tourism organisations and activities are processed through a digital platform, with blockchain technology used to provide traceability, verification and integrity for the information. This makes it possible to monitor emissions more systematically and to connect individual activities with the destination’s broader sustainability strategy. The expected impact is better measurement of tourism-related emissions, stronger accountability for climate commitments, more informed mitigation measures and improved communication with visitors and businesses. The use case is relevant because it applies blockchain to environmental verification rather than financial speculation, focusing on trust, auditability and shared sustainability data across a destination ecosystem.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1562,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01180,EU_PSTW_202609_01180,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,German National Tourist Board,"General economic, commercial and labour affair",UNKNOWN,Germany analyses big data with AI to manage tourist trends,"German National Tourist Board; Central-Government; General economic, commercial and labour affair; Germany","The German National Tourist Board developed a data-driven analytics solution to monitor visitor trends and assess tourism-market dynamics using large datasets and machine-learning techniques. The use case also shows how AI can support destination management indirectly: tourists may not interact with the system, but its insights help shape campaigns, services and strategic decisions that affect the visitor experience.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"National tourism organisations need to understand how marketing campaigns, origin markets, travel behaviour and external events influence visitor demand. The German National Tourist Board developed a data-driven analytics solution to monitor visitor trends and assess tourism-market dynamics using large datasets and machine-learning techniques. The operational problem was that tourism demand is influenced by many variables, including flight reservations, campaign exposure, traveller preferences, seasonality and source-market conditions. The implementation combined data from Global Distribution Systems and other travel-data sources with business-intelligence tools and trend-prediction algorithms. A specialised data-analytics provider supported the analysis by supplying near-real-time market data, reports and visualisation capabilities. At a conceptual level, the solution integrates large-scale booking and campaign datasets, identifies patterns before and after marketing actions, and generates insights on visitor origin, travel routes, popular cities and segment-specific preferences. The expected impact is better allocation of marketing investment, more targeted destination promotion, improved forecasting of demand and more tailored tourism offers for visitors. The use case also shows how AI can support destination management indirectly: tourists may not interact with the system, but its insights help shape campaigns, services and strategic decisions that affect the visitor experience.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1565,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01181,EU_PSTW_202609_01181,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Amsterdam,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.","Cultural institutions and destination-marketing organisations often struggle to reach younger audiences with relevant information, especially when cultural offers are spread across many venues, events and platforms.",Goochem - a pioneering interactive chatbot created as a marketing tool to bring culture closer to young people,"City of Amsterdam; Local Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Netherlands","Rather than presenting a static calendar, the chatbot asks questions and uses the user’s responses to filter and prioritise cultural suggestions.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Cultural institutions and destination-marketing organisations often struggle to reach younger audiences with relevant information, especially when cultural offers are spread across many venues, events and platforms. Goochem was created by Amsterdam Marketing and the City of Amsterdam as an interactive chatbot to bring culture closer to young people through personalised recommendations. The chatbot was available through Facebook Messenger and used conversational interaction to learn about a user’s interests, build a simple profile and suggest relevant cultural activities. The implementation was developed by digital technology companies Raft and Axendo after an open call for innovative pitches. At a conceptual level, Goochem combines dialogue-based profiling, recommendation logic and cultural-event information to guide users towards activities that match their preferences. Rather than presenting a static calendar, the chatbot asks questions and uses the user’s responses to filter and prioritise cultural suggestions. The expected impact is easier access to cultural information, stronger engagement with younger audiences, better promotion of Amsterdam’s cultural offer and more personalised destination marketing. The case is significant because it treats a chatbot not only as a customer-service tool, but as an active marketing and cultural-discovery interface that can make a city’s cultural ecosystem feel more accessible and relevant.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1566,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01182,EU_PSTW_202609_01182,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Dubrovnik,"General economic, commercial and labour affair","Historic city centres can become difficult to manage when visitor flows peak unpredictably, especially where tourism pressure affects residents, local businesses, transport and heritage conservation.",AI application developed for Dubrovnik visitors predicting the number of visitors in the Old Town,"City of Dubrovnik; Local Government; General economic, commercial and labour affair; Croatia","The objective is to help tourists, citizens and businesses plan their activities using clearer information about expected crowd levels. The implementation uses machine learning to estimate the number of people in the historic core on a selected day, drawing on parameters associated with tourism flows and urban demand.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Historic city centres can become difficult to manage when visitor flows peak unpredictably, especially where tourism pressure affects residents, local businesses, transport and heritage conservation. Dubrovnik developed an application to predict the number of visitors in the Old Town and surrounding contact zones as part of the wider “Respect the City” measures. The objective is to help tourists, citizens and businesses plan their activities using clearer information about expected crowd levels. The implementation uses machine learning to estimate the number of people in the historic core on a selected day, drawing on parameters associated with tourism flows and urban demand. At a conceptual level, the application translates historical and contextual data into visitor-number predictions, making crowd pressure more visible before people decide when to visit. The expected impact is better distribution of visitors across time, reduced pressure on the UNESCO-listed Old Town, improved planning for local businesses and more informed daily choices by residents. The system is also relevant for urban mobility and destination management, because crowd forecasts can support operational decisions beyond individual visitor planning. Its value lies in shifting from reactive crowd management to anticipatory visitor-flow intelligence in a city where tourism intensity is a central policy issue.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1570,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01183,EU_PSTW_202609_01183,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Dubrovnik,General public services n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,Smart parking in Dubrovnik with IoT,City of Dubrovnik; Local Government; General public services n.e.c.; Croatia,"Dubrovnik’s Smart Parking initiative uses Internet of Things technology to provide real-time information on parking availability across the city. The expected impact is reduced search traffic, improved use of parking capacity, lower congestion, easier payment and better data for city mobility management.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Parking pressure in tourist cities can generate congestion, emissions and frustration, particularly when visitors circulate near historic centres searching for available spaces. Dubrovnik’s Smart Parking initiative uses Internet of Things technology to provide real-time information on parking availability across the city. The operational problem is acute because the city receives large visitor numbers and has constrained access around the old town. The implementation connects sensors and digital systems to parking spaces and car parks, providing information on the number of free spaces, prices, opening hours and distance from the user’s location. Integration with Google Maps helps route drivers to available parking, while payment functionality supports quicker transactions and reduces queues at machines. At a conceptual level, NB-IoT sensors detect occupancy, transmit parking-space status to a central platform and make the information available through a mobile or map-based interface. The expected impact is reduced search traffic, improved use of parking capacity, lower congestion, easier payment and better data for city mobility management. The project also contributes to broader smart-city strategy by showing how connected infrastructure can address a very practical urban-management problem in a high-pressure destination.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1571,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01184,EU_PSTW_202609_01184,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Konjice Secondary School,Secondary education.,"Education systems and companies increasingly need staff who can understand, design and apply extended-reality technologies, but many schools lack curricula, equipment and trained mentors for augmented and virtual reality.",Augmented Reality Technologies Enter Slovenian Schools and Companies - THINK XR Project,Konjice Secondary School; Academic-Research; Secondary education.; Slovenia,"THINK XR was created in Slovenia to introduce new teaching modules and practical training in this field, linking formal education, vocational training and industrial use cases. The implementation includes the establishment of an XR Academy, training for future XR trainers, development of 3D content and use of tools such as Blender and Unity.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Education systems and companies increasingly need staff who can understand, design and apply extended-reality technologies, but many schools lack curricula, equipment and trained mentors for augmented and virtual reality. THINK XR was created in Slovenia to introduce new teaching modules and practical training in this field, linking formal education, vocational training and industrial use cases. The initiative involves Konjice Secondary School and Slovenian education and research partners, with the objective of making immersive technologies more accessible for students, teachers and companies. The implementation includes the establishment of an XR Academy, training for future XR trainers, development of 3D content and use of tools such as Blender and Unity. At a conceptual level, the project combines curriculum design, teacher training, immersive content development, specialised equipment and cooperation between education and industry. Its purpose is not only to demonstrate augmented reality, but to embed it into learning pathways and practical skills development. The expected impact is improved student engagement, better understanding of complex subjects, stronger digital competences among teachers and more direct links between schools and companies using XR. The first Slovenian XR trainer course took place in spring 2023, while the national and final conference in Ljubljana in April 2024 reported strong participation and results that exceeded expectations.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1573,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01185,EU_PSTW_202609_01185,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Gallerie dell'Accademia di Firenze,Cultural services.,UNKNOWN,"""Chatta col David"" - Chatbot on the Galleria dell'Accademia di Firenze website",Gallerie dell'Accademia di Firenze; Local Government; Cultural services.; Italy,"The implementation uses automated chatbot technology and deep-learning-based interaction developed with Querlo, allowing users to ask questions and receive responses about the artwork, its context and its symbolism. The use case is notable because it turns a single iconic object into a conversational interface, using AI not to replace curatorial interpretation but to make it more discoverable, playful and personalised for digital visitors.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Museums increasingly need digital tools that make collections more accessible, engaging and conversational, especially for audiences who may approach cultural heritage through informal online channels. “Chatta col David” was created by the Galleria dell’Accademia di Firenze as an AI chatbot that allows users to interact with a digital alter ego of Michelangelo’s David. The objective is to communicate historical, artistic, religious and anecdotal information about the statue in a more immediate and engaging form than a conventional web page. The implementation uses automated chatbot technology and deep-learning-based interaction developed with Querlo, allowing users to ask questions and receive responses about the artwork, its context and its symbolism. At a conceptual level, the system transforms museum interpretation into a dialogue: rather than reading a fixed description, users can explore their curiosity through questions and answers. The expected impact is broader public engagement, a more accessible entry point to Renaissance art, improved digital museum experience and increased interaction with the museum’s online presence. The use case is notable because it turns a single iconic object into a conversational interface, using AI not to replace curatorial interpretation but to make it more discoverable, playful and personalised for digital visitors.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1575,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01186,EU_PSTW_202609_01186,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Metropolitan city of Venice,Public order and safety n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,Venice smart monitoring systems for tourists,Metropolitan city of Venice; Local Government; Public order and safety n.e.c.; Italy,"This use case involves the city administration, residents, and visitors, with the objective of monitoring and managing tourist density.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Overtourism threatens the liveability and heritage of Venice, requiring precise understanding of visitor flows to support management decisions. This use case involves the city administration, residents, and visitors, with the objective of monitoring and managing tourist density. The solution combines data from pedestrian sensors, cameras, and anonymised mobile phone information to analyse real-time and historical flows. Integrated monitoring enables modelling of crowd dynamics and supports timely interventions. The system improves situational awareness, supports sustainable tourism policies, and enhances safety. It delivers data-driven management of a fragile urban environment while balancing tourism and resident needs.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1578,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01187,EU_PSTW_202609_01187,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,SEGITTUR,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.","Spain lacked a unified, open platform to consolidate tourism intelligence.",DATAESTUR to understand tourism in Spain via big data,"SEGITTUR; Central-Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Spain",UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Tourism policy requires timely, comprehensive data, particularly during crises such as the Covid-19 pandemic. Spain lacked a unified, open platform to consolidate tourism intelligence. DATAESTUR addresses this gap by involving public agencies and private data providers, with the objective of improving decision-making and transparency. The platform aggregates national tourism data from multiple authoritative sources and publishes them openly across structured categories. It improves accessibility to insights on demand, economics, transport, and accommodation. DATAESTUR supports evidence-based policy, industry analysis, and research, delivering long-term value through openness, coordination, and data-driven governance.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1579,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01188,EU_PSTW_202609_01188,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Helsinki,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.",UNKNOWN,MyHelsinki Open API,"City of Helsinki; Local Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Finland","Although not an AI use case in itself, it was an important example of data-driven public-service enablement and reuse.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Tourism, event promotion and city discovery services depend on high-quality local data that can be reused by developers, businesses and public-sector actors. The MyHelsinki Open API was created to provide structured access to information about places, events and activities in Helsinki and the surrounding region. The objective was to make city-curated content available as open data, encouraging reuse in digital services while maintaining quality standards and multilingual accessibility. The implementation exposed three databases through an application programming interface, allowing users to query and filter content by location, category, time and other criteria. At a conceptual level, the API turned Helsinki Marketing’s curated destination information into machine-readable resources that third-party applications could integrate into websites, mobile apps or digital services. Content was maintained by experts, updated regularly and made available in Finnish, Swedish, English and Chinese, improving both local and international reuse. The expected impact was stronger tourism innovation, easier development of city-service applications, improved visibility of Helsinki’s events and attractions, and reduced duplication of content maintenance across public and private platforms. The case illustrates how open APIs can serve as public digital infrastructure, enabling an ecosystem of services around trusted municipal data. Although not an AI use case in itself, it was an important example of data-driven public-service enablement and reuse.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,NOT_IN_USE,미사용,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1580,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01189,EU_PSTW_202609_01189,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Helsinki,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.",UNKNOWN,Helinki Tourist info chatbot,"City of Helsinki; Local Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Finland",The implementation uses a chatbot that answers questions through a conversational interface and guides users towards relevant information from Helsinki’s tourism ecosystem.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Visitors to a city often need immediate practical information about attractions, transport, events, services and local recommendations, but traditional tourist-information channels may not be available at all hours or in the user’s preferred format. Helsinki’s virtual tourist-information assistant, Lokki, provides a chatbot interface on the MyHelsinki website to help travellers and other users find relevant city information. The operational problem is that tourism information is broad and situational: users may ask about what to see, how to move around, where to get help, what events are taking place or how to plan a visit. The implementation uses a chatbot that answers questions through a conversational interface and guides users towards relevant information from Helsinki’s tourism ecosystem. At a conceptual level, Lokki interprets user intent, retrieves destination information and provides concise answers or navigation support without requiring users to browse multiple pages. The expected impact is quicker tourist assistance, better 24-hour accessibility, reduced pressure on staff for routine questions and a more convenient digital front door to Helsinki. The service complements telephone, email, physical tourist information and seasonal street-based support, making it part of a multi-channel visitor-service model rather than a replacement for human advice.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1581,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01190,EU_PSTW_202609_01190,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Amsterdam,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.",UNKNOWN,iBeacon Living Lab,"City of Amsterdam; Local Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Netherlands","The implementation used beacons and long-range sensor-beacon infrastructure deployed in the city, with Glimworm and KPN playing key roles alongside the City of Amsterdam.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Urban Internet of Things ecosystems require open test environments where developers, citizens, businesses and public authorities can experiment with sensors and location-based services in real streets. Amsterdam’s iBeacon Living Lab, including the Beacon Mile, was created as a public demonstrator for beacon and LoRa-enabled sensor applications. The initiative gave tourists and other users location-based information during city walks, while also serving as a testbed for Amsterdam’s broader IoT economy. The implementation used beacons and long-range sensor-beacon infrastructure deployed in the city, with Glimworm and KPN playing key roles alongside the City of Amsterdam. At a conceptual level, beacons transmit proximity signals to nearby mobile devices, enabling context-specific information delivery, while LoRa-enabled components support wider sensor-network experimentation. The expected impact was to stimulate IoT innovation, provide real-life conditions for developers, improve visitor information and demonstrate how public urban infrastructure can host digital services. The project also helped connect beacon experimentation with smart-city policy discussions, open innovation and sensor-network deployment. Available public evidence points to a pioneering mid-2010s living-lab initiative rather than a currently active citywide service. Its lasting value is therefore as an early urban IoT test environment that helped familiarise Amsterdam with beacon-guided services and public sensor experimentation.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1582,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01191,EU_PSTW_202609_01191,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Go:teborg&co,Recreational and sporting services.,"Cities and event organisers need reliable tools to assess the economic, environmental, and social impacts of events, yet such analysis is often complex and costly.",Event Impact Calculator in Gothenburg,Go:teborg&co; Local Government; Recreational and sporting services.; Sweden,"This use case involves organisers, municipalities, tourism stakeholders, and researchers, with the objective of enabling informed decision-making.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Cities and event organisers need reliable tools to assess the economic, environmental, and social impacts of events, yet such analysis is often complex and costly. Gothenburg developed the Event Impact Calculator to support sustainable event planning. This use case involves organisers, municipalities, tourism stakeholders, and researchers, with the objective of enabling informed decision-making. The tool allows users to input key event parameters and generates impact reports based on established models. It improves professionalism, supports negotiations, grant applications, and sponsorships, and embeds sustainability considerations into event planning. The calculator enhances transparency, reduces analysis costs, and delivers value by supporting more sustainable and data-informed events.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S7_GENERATIVE_CONTENT_CREATION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1583,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01192,EU_PSTW_202609_01192,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Florence,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.",UNKNOWN,Silfi Smart City Control Room in Florence,"City of Florence; Local Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Italy","Some dashboards are restricted because they include sensitive operational information, while other outputs can support public-facing services, such as parking-availability forecasts using machine-learning techniques. The expected impact is more integrated urban governance, faster situational awareness, improved coordination between service actors and better use of real-time data in daily city operations.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Urban management in a city such as Florence requires coordination across mobility, public safety, parking, public transport, utilities, environmental monitoring and tourism-related pressures. The Smart City Control Room was developed as a physical and digital hub where municipal authorities, service operators and service providers can share data and coordinate the management of the city. The operational problem is that many actors influence daily urban functioning, but their data and decisions are often distributed across separate systems. The implementation brings together information from city authorities, police, traffic and parking operators, public transport, water, waste, energy utilities, sharing-mobility providers, taxi companies and tourist-bus operators. At a conceptual level, the control room aggregates urban data, generates indicators, supports forecasts and simulations, and displays city status through operational dashboards. The underlying digital environment is based on Snap4City, which manages IoT data, big-data analytics, visualisation and decision-support functions. Some dashboards are restricted because they include sensitive operational information, while other outputs can support public-facing services, such as parking-availability forecasts using machine-learning techniques. The expected impact is more integrated urban governance, faster situational awareness, improved coordination between service actors and better use of real-time data in daily city operations.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1584,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01193,EU_PSTW_202609_01193,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Helsinki,Public order and safety n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,Heatmap in Helsinki,City of Helsinki; Local Government; Public order and safety n.e.c.; Finland,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Crowded urban spaces can affect visitor experience, resident comfort and perceived safety, particularly in tourism areas, events and popular public places. Helsinki’s Heatmap service was designed to show the congestion status of key tourism-related areas, sights and services through a colour-coded map. The operational objective was to help tourists and local residents make better decisions about when and where to visit, especially when avoiding crowding was important for safety and comfort. The implementation used real-time or near-real-time data to represent the busyness of selected areas on a public map interface. At a conceptual level, the system converts urban activity data into an accessible visual indicator: users do not need to interpret raw counts or technical mobility data, but can simply see whether an area is relatively quiet, moderate or crowded. The expected impact is smoother distribution of visitors, safer and more pleasant experiences, better support for tourism recovery and useful operational data for the city and tourism stakeholders. The case is also significant because it made crowding information openly available rather than limiting it to internal city managers, turning urban analytics into a public-facing service for everyday decision-making.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1587,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01194,EU_PSTW_202609_01194,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Region of Lombardy,General public services n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,Webapp L15 - digital ecosystem of Lombardy Region,Region of Lombardy; Regional Government; General public services n.e.c.; Italy,"using Open 2.0, the open-source application made available by Regione Lombardia for collaborative tools.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Regional digital ecosystems can help public and private actors expose services through APIs, creating reusable building blocks for transport, tourism, healthcare, mobility and other domains. Lombardy’s E015 digital ecosystem was designed as a platform where public and private providers could publish APIs and combine them into user-facing services. The Webapp E015, also referred to as L15 in some catalogues, demonstrated the potential of this ecosystem by bringing together multiple services in a single digital environment, including information such as healthcare-unit availability in regional hospitals and parking availability. The implementation was based on a customisation developed by ARIA S.p.A. using Open 2.0, the open-source application made available by Regione Lombardia for collaborative tools. At a conceptual level, the platform operated as an API aggregation and service-composition environment: providers exposed data or functions, while applications could reuse them to create integrated services for citizens, businesses or institutions. Its expected impact was to demonstrate interoperability, stimulate reuse of regional digital assets, support service innovation and show how a mature API ecosystem could generate practical applications. The webapp was ultimately discontinued in January 2024 because it had completed its demonstrative role. The wider E015 initiative was considered mature, with multiple success stories showing that the ecosystem model had been validated beyond the original demonstrator.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,NOT_IN_USE,미사용,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1588,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01195,EU_PSTW_202609_01195,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,visitBerlin,"Recreation, culture and religion n.e.c.",UNKNOWN,Visit Berlin - Digital tourism,"visitBerlin; Local Government; Recreation, culture and religion n.e.c.; Germany","Visitors exploring a city often want location-specific historical and cultural information without joining a scheduled tour or using a conventional guidebook. The use case is relevant because it uses simple digital infrastructure to create an augmented cultural route through the city, offering interpretation at the exact location where historical meaning is embedded.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Visitors exploring a city often want location-specific historical and cultural information without joining a scheduled tour or using a conventional guidebook. Berlin’s digital tourism initiative used QR codes placed near cultural resources, buildings, paving stones and tiles to connect physical urban locations with multimedia content. The operational goal was to make the city’s history more accessible through a low-friction digital layer that tourists could activate with their own devices. The implementation relied on QR-code technology linked to descriptive, audio or visual content, including information about historical transformations in the city. An interactive map helped users locate the codes and follow a thematic route, functioning as a decentralised open-air museum. At a conceptual level, the system connects urban heritage interpretation with mobile self-guided discovery: a street, façade or public-space marker becomes a gateway to curated historical material. The expected impact is richer cultural tourism, more flexible visitor experiences, improved visibility of dispersed heritage assets and reduced dependence on physical signage. The use case is relevant because it uses simple digital infrastructure to create an augmented cultural route through the city, offering interpretation at the exact location where historical meaning is embedded.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1589,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01196,EU_PSTW_202609_01196,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Brasov,Cultural services.,"Traditional signage and guidebooks offered limited storytelling capacity and failed to convey the intangible dimensions of heritage, such as legends, social context, and historical evolution.",Brasov virtual tour,Municipality of Brasov; Local Government; Cultural services.; Romania,"This use case involves local authorities, cultural experts, residents, and tourists, with the objective of revitalising the historical centre through immersive and educational experiences. The “Virtual Brașov” application is implemented as a handheld augmented reality solution that recognises monuments and key locations in the city using computer vision and geolocation techniques.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The city of Brașov sought to enhance access to its cultural heritage and improve visitor engagement, particularly in cases where historical assets have been altered or demolished. Traditional signage and guidebooks offered limited storytelling capacity and failed to convey the intangible dimensions of heritage, such as legends, social context, and historical evolution. This use case involves local authorities, cultural experts, residents, and tourists, with the objective of revitalising the historical centre through immersive and educational experiences. The “Virtual Brașov” application is implemented as a handheld augmented reality solution that recognises monuments and key locations in the city using computer vision and geolocation techniques. Once a site is identified, the application overlays digital reconstructions, historical images, and curated multimedia content onto the physical environment. Informative narratives are developed collaboratively by historians, architects, and cultural specialists, with a strong focus on storytelling and intangible heritage. The solution enriches cultural discovery, improves visitor understanding of Brașov’s history, and broadens access to heritage beyond physical constraints. It strengthens cultural tourism, supports education, and provides a scalable digital tool for heritage preservation and promotion, enhancing the city’s attractiveness and cultural value.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1590,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01197,EU_PSTW_202609_01197,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Antwerp,Public order and safety n.e.c.,Traditional manual counting and static sensors provided limited coverage and delayed insights.,Crowd monitoring in Antwerp,City of Antwerp; Local Government; Public order and safety n.e.c.; Belgium,"This use case involves the city administration, security services, transport planners, and mobile network operators, with the objective of understanding population flows while respecting privacy. The solution is implemented through anonymised mobile network positioning data provided by Orange Belgium, derived from the interaction of devices with 2G, 3G, and 4G base stations and timestamp analysis.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The City of Antwerp required accurate, real-time insights into crowd movements to support public safety, mobility management, and city marketing, particularly during large events and peak activity periods. Traditional manual counting and static sensors provided limited coverage and delayed insights. This use case involves the city administration, security services, transport planners, and mobile network operators, with the objective of understanding population flows while respecting privacy. The solution is implemented through anonymised mobile network positioning data provided by Orange Belgium, derived from the interaction of devices with 2G, 3G, and 4G base stations and timestamp analysis. Each device is assigned a non-identifiable unique ID, and aggregated data is visualised through a dedicated crowd monitoring platform developed with Cropland. The system provides real-time and historical views of crowd density, movement patterns, and dwell times across defined urban areas. The solution improves situational awareness, supports proactive crowd management, and informs transport and security planning. It enhances decision-making for events and urban operations, reduces risk, and delivers value through scalable, privacy-preserving urban analytics.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1591,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01198,EU_PSTW_202609_01198,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Province of Gipuzkoa,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.","Decision-makers lacked integrated, data-driven insights into visitor behaviour, consumption, and movement across the territory.",BIG DATA TO IMPROVE TOURISM MANAGEMENT IN GIPUZKOA UNDER THE BEGIKA MODEL,"Province of Gipuzkoa; Local Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Spain","This use case involves the provincial government, tourism authorities, businesses, and researchers, with the objective of supporting sustainable and efficient tourism management. The HODEIAn platform is implemented as a big data analytics tool that aggregates information from multiple public and private sources, including mobility data, consumption indicators, and accommodation statistics.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Gipuzkoa faced growing pressure to manage tourism sustainably while balancing economic benefits with resident wellbeing and environmental impact. Decision-makers lacked integrated, data-driven insights into visitor behaviour, consumption, and movement across the territory. This use case involves the provincial government, tourism authorities, businesses, and researchers, with the objective of supporting sustainable and efficient tourism management. The HODEIAn platform is implemented as a big data analytics tool that aggregates information from multiple public and private sources, including mobility data, consumption indicators, and accommodation statistics. Indicators are contextualised according to their source and methodology, enabling nuanced interpretation rather than simplistic rankings. The platform analyses visitor origin, spending patterns, and spatial movement to support evidence-based planning. The solution improves the quality of tourism intelligence, supports targeted policies, and enables more balanced distribution of visitors. It enhances strategic decision-making, strengthens sustainability outcomes, and provides long-term value by aligning tourism development with territorial capacity and local needs.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1592,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01199,EU_PSTW_202609_01199,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Province of Gipuzkoa,"Recreation, culture and religion n.e.c.",UNKNOWN,Arantxat virtual tourist guide,"Province of Gipuzkoa; Local Government; Recreation, culture and religion n.e.c.; Spain","This use case involves tourism authorities, visitors, and local service providers, with the objective of improving information accessibility while optimising operational efficiency. Arantxat is implemented as a virtual tourist guide delivered through a chatbot available on WhatsApp, built on the most common questions received by physical tourist offices.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Tourist offices in Gipuzkoa receive high volumes of repetitive enquiries, limiting staff capacity for personalised support and strategic promotion. Visitors increasingly expect instant, mobile access to reliable information. This use case involves tourism authorities, visitors, and local service providers, with the objective of improving information accessibility while optimising operational efficiency. Arantxat is implemented as a virtual tourist guide delivered through a chatbot available on WhatsApp, built on the most common questions received by physical tourist offices. The system integrates curated information flows covering attractions, services, events, and practical travel guidance. It provides automated, conversational responses in a familiar messaging environment, accessible before and during visits. The solution reduces pressure on in-person and telephone services, improves response speed, and ensures consistent information delivery. It enhances visitor experience, supports digital tourism promotion, and offers a scalable, low-cost channel for destination management organisations.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1593,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01200,EU_PSTW_202609_01200,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,European High-Performance Computing Joint Undertaking (EuroHPC JU),R&D education.,"European researchers, public institutions and companies need access to quantum-computing capacity in order to explore problems that are difficult for conventional computing alone, including optimisation, materials, health, energy and climate-related modelling. The operational challenge is to build a distributed European quantum-computing capability that can be accessed by users across the continent rather than remaining concentrated in isolated laboratories.",EU deploys first quantum technology in six sites across Europe,European High-Performance Computing Joint Undertaking (EuroHPC JU); Academic-Research; R&D education.; European Union,"The use case is important because it treats quantum computers as shared public research infrastructure, embedded in the European supercomputing ecosystem rather than developed only as standalone experimental machines.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"European researchers, public institutions and companies need access to quantum-computing capacity in order to explore problems that are difficult for conventional computing alone, including optimisation, materials, health, energy and climate-related modelling. The EuroHPC Joint Undertaking selected six European sites to host the first EuroHPC quantum computers, integrating them with existing high-performance computing infrastructures in Czechia, Germany, Spain, France, Italy and Poland. The operational challenge is to build a distributed European quantum-computing capability that can be accessed by users across the continent rather than remaining concentrated in isolated laboratories. The implementation combines quantum processors with supercomputing centres, allowing hybrid workflows where classical HPC resources and quantum devices can be used together. At a conceptual level, researchers and industry users can submit appropriate problems to quantum-HPC environments, testing whether quantum methods can accelerate or improve specific classes of computation. The expected impact is stronger European technological sovereignty, broader access to emerging quantum resources, support for advanced scientific research and early experimentation by industry. The use case is important because it treats quantum computers as shared public research infrastructure, embedded in the European supercomputing ecosystem rather than developed only as standalone experimental machines.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1594,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01201,EU_PSTW_202609_01201,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Italian Trade Agency,"General economic, commercial and labour affair",UNKNOWN,TrackIT - Blockchain for trade export,"Italian Trade Agency; Central-Government; General economic, commercial and labour affair; Italy","The implementation uses blockchain to record relevant supply-chain data in a secure and immutable way, creating a direct-to-consumer communication channel that links physical products to digital evidence. Current official material shows that TrackIT is active beyond the original 2023–2025 pilot framing: the Italian Trade Agency states that participation is open until 31 August 2026 and presents a showcase of companies already using the blockchain traceability project.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Italian exporters, particularly small and medium-sized enterprises, often need to prove product origin, production history and supply-chain quality in international markets where counterfeiting and “Italian sounding” can weaken consumer trust. TrackIT was launched by the Italian Trade Agency to help companies communicate the value of Made in Italy products through blockchain-based traceability. The initiative enables participating firms to attach QR codes or smart tags to goods so that buyers and consumers can access information on origin, production stages and supply-chain events. The implementation uses blockchain to record relevant supply-chain data in a secure and immutable way, creating a direct-to-consumer communication channel that links physical products to digital evidence. At a conceptual level, the platform combines product identification, event recording, distributed ledger technology, consumer-facing interfaces and export-promotion services. During the pilot period, the tool was made available free of charge to encourage participation, particularly among SMEs. The expected impact is greater transparency, stronger anti-counterfeiting protection, improved reputation for Italian products and better international competitiveness. Current official material shows that TrackIT is active beyond the original 2023–2025 pilot framing: the Italian Trade Agency states that participation is open until 31 August 2026 and presents a showcase of companies already using the blockchain traceability project.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1595,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01202,EU_PSTW_202609_01202,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Albanian national agency of information technology (AKSHI),General public services n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,e-Albania Platform Chatbot,Albanian national agency of information technology (AKSHI); Central-Government; General public services n.e.c.; Albania,"Citizens using digital public services often need help identifying the right procedure, understanding required documents and completing applications through online portals. The implementation uses an AI-based assistant that can guide users through service information, answer common questions and support interaction with digital public-service workflows.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Citizens using digital public services often need help identifying the right procedure, understanding required documents and completing applications through online portals. The e-Albania chatbot was developed as a virtual assistant for the national e-Albania platform, Albania’s central digital gateway for public services. The operational objective is to make online public administration easier to navigate by providing conversational support directly within the portal. The implementation uses an AI-based assistant that can guide users through service information, answer common questions and support interaction with digital public-service workflows. At a conceptual level, the assistant interprets citizen requests, retrieves information from the e-Albania service environment and provides step-by-step orientation in a more accessible format than static pages alone. The expected impact is faster access to services, reduced reliance on human help desks, improved user experience and greater uptake of digital government channels. The case is notable because it places conversational AI at the front door of a national e-government platform, where the assistant can support many different services rather than a single departmental process. It also reflects a broader shift towards more automated, personalised and always-available interaction between citizens and public administration.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1597,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01203,EU_PSTW_202609_01203,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Fondazione Bruno Kessler,Public health services.,"The operational problem is that information on appointments, pregnancy monitoring, child-care support and maternal-health guidance can be fragmented across services and channels.",TreC Mamma - Italian app for new mothers,Fondazione Bruno Kessler; Academic-Research; Public health services.; Italy,The use case shows how conversational tools can be embedded in a broader public-health application rather than offered as a standalone chatbot.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Pregnancy and early childhood involve many health, administrative and practical questions, and families benefit from reliable, localised information that connects them with the health services available in their territory. TreC Mamma was developed in Trentino as a digital application supporting women during pregnancy and through the first thousand days of a child’s life. The operational problem is that information on appointments, pregnancy monitoring, child-care support and maternal-health guidance can be fragmented across services and channels. The implementation provides a secure app integrated with the Trentino digital-health ecosystem, allowing users to access validated content, personal tools, appointment information, videos, podcasts, a digital diary and educational digital assistants. At a conceptual level, the app combines health-service integration, personalised information, chatbot-based education and digital self-management. Its chatbots can support topics such as pregnancy, childcare and nutrition, guiding women through structured microlearning or personalised coaching pathways. The expected impact is improved maternal-health literacy, easier access to local support services, better continuity between health professionals and families, and more personalised digital support during a sensitive life stage. The use case shows how conversational tools can be embedded in a broader public-health application rather than offered as a standalone chatbot.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1599,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01204,EU_PSTW_202609_01204,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,London Borough of Hillingdon,General public services n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,Hillingdon Council praised for innovative approach to safeguarding children,London Borough of Hillingdon; Local Government; General public services n.e.c.; United Kingdom,"Hillingdon Council deployed an automated voice and web-chat solution to improve access to council services and reduce pressure on its contact centre. The use case is relevant because it shows conversational AI being deployed at scale in a council setting, not simply as an experimental website chatbot but as part of a wider transformation of resident interaction.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Local authorities must handle large volumes of resident contacts while also protecting capacity for complex and sensitive cases, including safeguarding-related needs. Hillingdon Council deployed an automated voice and web-chat solution to improve access to council services and reduce pressure on its contact centre. The operational problem was that high call volumes were absorbing staff time, while many enquiries could be resolved through structured guidance, signposting or transaction support. The implementation used conversational AI across voice and web channels, supported by PwC and Amazon Web Services, to understand why residents were contacting the council and provide an answer, link or next step where possible. At a conceptual level, the system combines automated call handling, intent recognition, web-chat interaction and service routing. It can support residents with routine enquiries while preserving human capacity for situations requiring judgement, empathy or statutory intervention. The expected impact is better service availability, shorter waiting times, more efficient handling of high-volume enquiries and greater resilience in local-authority customer contact. The use case is relevant because it shows conversational AI being deployed at scale in a council setting, not simply as an experimental website chatbot but as part of a wider transformation of resident interaction.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1600,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01205,EU_PSTW_202609_01205,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,AIT Austrian Institute of Technology,Police Services,"Border surveillance in forested, remote or extreme-climate areas is difficult because people and vehicles can move under dense vegetation where conventional sensors often produce unreliable detections or high false-alarm rates. The expected impact is improved detection in foliated areas, reduced false alarms, better allocation of border-guard resources and enhanced safety in difficult environments.","FOLDOUT - Through-foliage detection, including in the outermost regions of the EU",AIT Austrian Institute of Technology; Academic-Research; Police Services; Austria,"At a conceptual level, the platform combines data from several sensing modalities, applies detection and classification models, and presents near-real-time information to operators. The project produced research outputs on hyperspectral surveillance, sensor fusion and object tracking, and its own platform material describes demonstrations at end-user sites using an intelligent and robust detection system.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Border surveillance in forested, remote or extreme-climate areas is difficult because people and vehicles can move under dense vegetation where conventional sensors often produce unreliable detections or high false-alarm rates. FOLDOUT was developed as a Horizon 2020 project to improve through-foliage detection in both inner and outermost EU regions, supporting border guards with faster and more reliable situational awareness. The objective was to detect, locate, track and classify people, vehicles and other objects in challenging terrain while reducing operational workload and improving border-management efficiency. The implementation integrates ground, aerial and satellite data with specialised sensors, machine-learning algorithms and multi-sensor fusion. At a conceptual level, the platform combines data from several sensing modalities, applies detection and classification models, and presents near-real-time information to operators. FOLDOUT also developed performance-evaluation approaches to assess how well the system works under different environmental and weather conditions. The expected impact is improved detection in foliated areas, reduced false alarms, better allocation of border-guard resources and enhanced safety in difficult environments. The project produced research outputs on hyperspectral surveillance, sensor fusion and object tracking, and its own platform material describes demonstrations at end-user sites using an intelligent and robust detection system.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1602,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01206,EU_PSTW_202609_01206,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Federation Wallonie-Bruxelles,Secondary education.,"Allocating pupils to secondary schools is a sensitive administrative process because families express preferences, schools have limited capacity and authorities must apply common rules fairly.",School choice preferences algorithm in the Federation Wallonie-Bruxelles,Federation Wallonie-Bruxelles; Regional Government; Secondary education.; Belgium,"The Federation Wallonie-Bruxelles uses an automatic allocation algorithm in the secondary-school enrolment procedure to rank applications and assign available places. The implementation uses a preference-optimisation procedure based on deferred acceptance, where pupils list preferred schools, schools rank applicants according to the applicable rules, and the algorithm iteratively assigns places while respecting pupil preferences as far as possible.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Allocating pupils to secondary schools is a sensitive administrative process because families express preferences, schools have limited capacity and authorities must apply common rules fairly. The Federation Wallonie-Bruxelles uses an automatic allocation algorithm in the secondary-school enrolment procedure to rank applications and assign available places. The operational problem is that many pupils apply to multiple schools, and each school may receive more applications than available seats. The implementation uses a preference-optimisation procedure based on deferred acceptance, where pupils list preferred schools, schools rank applicants according to the applicable rules, and the algorithm iteratively assigns places while respecting pupil preferences as far as possible. At a conceptual level, the system creates ordered applicant lists for schools, allocates provisional seats, removes duplicate allocations where pupils receive a place in more than one school, and reassigns freed places to the next pupils on waiting lists. The expected impact is a more transparent, rule-based and consistent enrolment process, reducing ad hoc allocation and making the treatment of preferences more systematic. The algorithmic nature of the procedure also makes it important that families can understand the ranking process, the role of preferences and the avenues for appeal or correction.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1604,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01207,EU_PSTW_202609_01207,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Vrije Universiteit Brussels,Other industries,"Food manufacturers face a difficult product-development problem when they try to make bakery and patisserie products healthier without compromising taste, texture, functionality or consumer acceptance.",Healthy Food production with AI,Vrije Universiteit Brussels; Academic-Research; Other industries; Belgium,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Food manufacturers face a difficult product-development problem when they try to make bakery and patisserie products healthier without compromising taste, texture, functionality or consumer acceptance. The Healthy Food R&D project brings Puratos together with the Artificial Intelligence Lab at Vrije Universiteit Brussel to support recipe development with data-driven prediction rather than relying only on repeated trial-and-error experimentation. The objective is to identify healthier ingredients, understand how they behave during and after baking, and recommend recipes with a more balanced nutritional profile while maintaining the product characteristics expected by consumers and industrial processes. The implementation develops a multi-objective predictive model that can evaluate several goals simultaneously, such as nutritional improvement, ingredient behaviour, sensory properties and technical performance. At a conceptual level, the system links ingredient data, processing conditions, product characteristics and predictive modelling to generate recommendations for recipe development. This makes it possible to explore trade-offs between health, quality and manufacturability before committing to extensive physical testing. The expected impact is faster R&D cycles, lower development cost, more systematic formulation of healthier products and improved capacity to innovate in bakery and patisserie. Although the public VUB material describes the project clearly as an R&D collaboration, I found no evidence that the specific model has been released as a standalone commercial tool.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1605,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01208,EU_PSTW_202609_01208,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Vrije Universiteit Brussels,Tertiary education.,UNKNOWN,EPD: Employability in programme development,Vrije Universiteit Brussels; Academic-Research; Tertiary education.; Belgium,"The implementation uses artificial intelligence and natural language processing to analyse labour-market data, identify skills signals and support the development of a prototype dashboard for higher education staff.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Higher education institutions increasingly need reliable labour-market intelligence to ensure that courses and programmes equip students with skills that employers actually demand. The EPD project addresses this gap by helping participating universities understand what information on skills demand is available, what programme designers and employability practitioners need, and how this evidence can be incorporated into curriculum development. The objective is to establish a feedback loop between the labour market and higher education, so that programme design is informed not only by academic expertise but also by evolving employment patterns and employer expectations. The implementation uses artificial intelligence and natural language processing to analyse labour-market data, identify skills signals and support the development of a prototype dashboard for higher education staff. Secondary data sources are complemented by employer input, while the project also curates European best practices in employability through case studies. At a conceptual level, the system combines skills extraction, labour-market intelligence, dashboard visualisation and stakeholder feedback to make employability evidence usable for institutional decision-making. The expected impact is better alignment between curricula and labour-market needs, more evidence-based course design, and improved graduate employability. By translating complex labour-market information into a practical dashboard, EPD helps universities move from general employability ambitions to concrete, data-supported programme-development choices. The project was funded under Erasmus+ for 2021–2023 with a grant of €433,771.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1606,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01209,EU_PSTW_202609_01209,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Vrije Universiteit Brussels,R&D public order and safety.,"Cybersecurity teams operate in environments where attacks evolve quickly, alerts are numerous and purely rule-based systems may struggle to distinguish meaningful threats from routine activity. CSAI responds to this challenge by exploring how more intelligent models and techniques can support cybersecurity technology beyond automated analytics alone.",CSAI: Cyber Security Artificial Intelligence,Vrije Universiteit Brussels; Academic-Research; R&D public order and safety.; Belgium,"The implementation combines symbolic and sub-symbolic approaches, using domain-specific cybersecurity knowledge, mechanisms to acquire new knowledge and techniques that can apply that knowledge to operational tasks.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Cybersecurity teams operate in environments where attacks evolve quickly, alerts are numerous and purely rule-based systems may struggle to distinguish meaningful threats from routine activity. CSAI responds to this challenge by exploring how more intelligent models and techniques can support cybersecurity technology beyond automated analytics alone. The project, funded by Flanders’ innovation and entrepreneurship agency, brings research and industry actors together to investigate methods that can improve detection, reasoning and response across cybersecurity contexts, from devices and networks to compliance. The implementation combines symbolic and sub-symbolic approaches, using domain-specific cybersecurity knowledge, mechanisms to acquire new knowledge and techniques that can apply that knowledge to operational tasks. At a conceptual level, CSAI seeks to connect machine-learning models, knowledge representation, threat intelligence and security workflows so that systems can reason more effectively about risks and incidents. The expected impact is stronger cyber-threat detection, more robust decision support for analysts, and improved ability to apply research advances in practical security products and services. By focusing on hybrid intelligence rather than only statistical detection, the project aims to make cybersecurity tools more explainable, adaptable and aligned with professional expertise. Its public value lies in strengthening digital resilience in a domain where both technical sophistication and trustworthiness are essential.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1608,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01210,EU_PSTW_202609_01210,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Vrije Universiteit Brussels,Transport,"The rapid electrification of vehicle fleets creates a practical energy-management problem: charging many vehicles at the same time can overload local capacity, increase costs and make operations difficult to coordinate, especially in unpredictable real environments. By testing the approach in real environments, the project emphasises robustness and practical viability, which are critical for fleet operators that cannot rely on perfectly predictable charging behaviour.",EV Charging Optimiser,Vrije Universiteit Brussels; Academic-Research; Transport; Belgium,"The implementation applies reinforcement learning to charging scheduling, allowing the system to learn policies that balance fleet requirements, grid constraints and operational uncertainty. The expected impact is more efficient fleet charging, lower peak demand, improved use of charging infrastructure and better integration of electric mobility into energy systems.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The rapid electrification of vehicle fleets creates a practical energy-management problem: charging many vehicles at the same time can overload local capacity, increase costs and make operations difficult to coordinate, especially in unpredictable real environments. VUB and Energis developed the EV Charging Optimiser to test how an entire fleet of electric vehicles could be charged in a coordinated and controlled way, both in laboratory settings and in operational contexts. The objective is to move beyond static charging schedules and create a controller that remains robust when vehicles arrive late, leave early, require different charging levels or face changing electricity and infrastructure constraints. The implementation applies reinforcement learning to charging scheduling, allowing the system to learn policies that balance fleet requirements, grid constraints and operational uncertainty. At a conceptual level, the controller observes charging demand, vehicle availability, battery states and environmental constraints, then selects charging actions that improve long-term outcomes rather than optimising only the immediate moment. The expected impact is more efficient fleet charging, lower peak demand, improved use of charging infrastructure and better integration of electric mobility into energy systems. By testing the approach in real environments, the project emphasises robustness and practical viability, which are critical for fleet operators that cannot rely on perfectly predictable charging behaviour.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1609,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01211,EU_PSTW_202609_01211,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Vrije Universiteit Brussels,R&D economic affairs,"Data governance often remains distant from the everyday contexts in which business users create, interpret and reuse data, leading to weak adoption, unclear ownership and limited trust in datasets.",Driving collective data governance through smart engagement platforms (COLLIBRA),Vrije Universiteit Brussels; Academic-Research; R&D economic affairs; Belgium,"The implementation developed a platform that uses artificial intelligence to learn bottom-up data-usage context and provide reliable, machine-accurate recommendations for the next best action inside core business applications. This allows governance guidance to appear at the point of use rather than as a separate administrative obligation.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Data governance often remains distant from the everyday contexts in which business users create, interpret and reuse data, leading to weak adoption, unclear ownership and limited trust in datasets. This project sought to bring governance closer to actual workplaces by embedding support into the systems of engagement where decisions are already discussed, such as boardrooms, design workshops, Confluence, Slack and other collaboration tools. The objective was to help users make better low-level decisions when interacting with data, while generating richer contextual information on how datasets are used. The implementation developed a platform that uses artificial intelligence to learn bottom-up data-usage context and provide reliable, machine-accurate recommendations for the next best action inside core business applications. At a conceptual level, the system combines data-governance metadata, engagement traces, workflow context, recommendation logic and integration with collaboration environments. This allows governance guidance to appear at the point of use rather than as a separate administrative obligation. The expected impact is better data stewardship, stronger trust in datasets, more efficient governance decisions and more time for users to focus on substantive business problems. The project also supports dataset promotion by helping users build credibility around data generated through collaborative engagements, making governance more participatory and embedded in practice.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1610,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01212,EU_PSTW_202609_01212,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Flemish Employment and Vocational Training Service (VDAB),Unemployment.,Public employment services often struggle to match jobseekers and vacancies accurately due to vague job descriptions and inconsistent skill profiling. This leads to inefficient labour market matching and prolonged unemployment.,Competence based matching - a success story from Flanders to Malta,Flemish Employment and Vocational Training Service (VDAB); Regional Government; Unemployment.; Belgium,"This use case involves the Flemish employment service (VDAB), employers, jobseekers, and later the Maltese Public Employment Service, with the objective of improving match quality and labour market efficiency. The system is implemented through structured competency frameworks that require employers to define vacancies in detailed, standardised skill terms, while jobseekers build equally comprehensive competency profiles.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Public employment services often struggle to match jobseekers and vacancies accurately due to vague job descriptions and inconsistent skill profiling. This leads to inefficient labour market matching and prolonged unemployment. This use case involves the Flemish employment service (VDAB), employers, jobseekers, and later the Maltese Public Employment Service, with the objective of improving match quality and labour market efficiency. The system is implemented through structured competency frameworks that require employers to define vacancies in detailed, standardised skill terms, while jobseekers build equally comprehensive competency profiles. Matching algorithms compare these profiles to generate higher-quality matches based on skills rather than job titles alone. The concept was successfully transferred from Flanders to Malta in 2016, demonstrating cross-border applicability. The solution improves placement accuracy, reduces mismatch, and shortens recruitment times. It supports mobility of best practices across Europe and delivers measurable value through more effective employment services and better labour market outcomes.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1611,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01213,EU_PSTW_202609_01213,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Flemish Employment and Vocational Training Service (VDAB),Unemployment.,"Public employment services face challenges in efficiently matching jobseekers with suitable vacancies, particularly as labour markets become more dynamic and digitally mediated.",Managing vacancies - Recommender: the amazon of the labour market,Flemish Employment and Vocational Training Service (VDAB); Regional Government; Unemployment.; Belgium,"The system leverages big data analytics to process vacancy descriptions, candidate profiles and historical placement data.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Public employment services face challenges in efficiently matching jobseekers with suitable vacancies, particularly as labour markets become more dynamic and digitally mediated. Traditional keyword-based search functions often fail to capture skills proximity, transferable competences and evolving job profiles. Stakeholders include jobseekers, employers and public employment agencies. The objective is to enhance matching quality and labour market efficiency through personalised vacancy recommendations. The system leverages big data analytics to process vacancy descriptions, candidate profiles and historical placement data. Collaborative filtering, content-based recommendation algorithms and skills ontology mapping are used to infer relevance scores and suggest vacancies aligned with a jobseeker’s competences and preferences. Integration with the public vacancy platform enables real-time recommendations upon user login, supported by feedback loops to continuously refine model accuracy. Expected benefits include improved placement rates, reduced job search duration, better utilisation of transferable skills and enhanced user experience comparable to leading e-commerce recommendation systems.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1612,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01214,EU_PSTW_202609_01214,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Wien - Municipal Directorate of Strategic ICT,"Mining, manufacturing and construction.","Building-permit procedures are often slow, document-heavy and difficult for applicants and administrations to navigate, especially in a growing city that receives high volumes of construction applications. The City of Vienna’s objective is to make permit procedures faster, more transparent and less costly, while creating a model that can later be transferred to other fields of public administration.",A DIGITAL BUILDING PERMIT SYSTEM FOR THE SMART CITY in Wien,"City of Wien - Municipal Directorate of Strategic ICT; Local Government; Mining, manufacturing and construction.; Austria","Instead of submitting 2D plans, applicants use digital 3D building models that can be checked against relevant building regulations.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Building-permit procedures are often slow, document-heavy and difficult for applicants and administrations to navigate, especially in a growing city that receives high volumes of construction applications. Vienna’s BRISE project rethinks this process by replacing paper-based, two-dimensional submissions with a more integrated digital workflow for construction approvals. The City of Vienna’s objective is to make permit procedures faster, more transparent and less costly, while creating a model that can later be transferred to other fields of public administration. The implementation combines Building Information Modelling, artificial intelligence, augmented reality and robotic process automation. Instead of submitting 2D plans, applicants use digital 3D building models that can be checked against relevant building regulations. At a conceptual level, the system connects BIM data, automated rule checking, administrative workflows, AR-supported inspection and digital collaboration between applicants, experts and public officials. This creates an end-to-end basis for managing a construction project from planning and submission to site inspection and acceptance of the completed structure. The expected impact is faster processing, improved project quality, greater transparency and reduced administrative effort. OECD OPSI reports 50% time savings, while Vienna’s project material notes approximately €4.8 million in EU Urban Innovative Actions funding and implementation by March 2023.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F06_HIGH_VOLUME_SCALABILITY_BOTTLENECK,S6_WORKFLOW_PROCESS_AUTOMATION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1615,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01215,EU_PSTW_202609_01215,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Direzione dei Sistemi Informativi e dell'Innovazione of the Minitry of the Economics and Finances,Transfers of a general character between different levels of government.,Traditional support channels faced congestion and delays.,Facebook chatbot connected to NoiPA services,Direzione dei Sistemi Informativi e dell'Innovazione of the Minitry of the Economics and Finances; Central-Government; Transfers of a general character between different levels of government.; Italy,"This use case involves the Ministry of Economy and Finance, public employees, and service administrators, with the objective of improving communication and assistance. The solution is implemented as a chatbot integrated into Facebook Messenger, providing automated responses to frequently asked questions about NoiPA services.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"NoiPA manages payroll and administrative services for a large number of Italian public employees, generating frequent enquiries related to payslips, allowances, and administrative procedures. Traditional support channels faced congestion and delays. This use case involves the Ministry of Economy and Finance, public employees, and service administrators, with the objective of improving communication and assistance. The solution is implemented as a chatbot integrated into Facebook Messenger, providing automated responses to frequently asked questions about NoiPA services. The chatbot draws from official knowledge bases and guides users through common issues in a conversational format. It operates alongside existing support channels, offering immediate assistance through a widely used social platform. The service improves accessibility, reduces call centre workload, and speeds up resolution of simple enquiries. It enhances user satisfaction and demonstrates effective use of social messaging channels for public sector service delivery.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1617,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01216,EU_PSTW_202609_01216,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Sovrintendenza ai Beni Culturali di Roma Capitale,Cultural services.,"Archaeological sites can be difficult for visitors to interpret when only partial remains are visible and the original urban context has disappeared beneath modern layers of the city. At a conceptual level, XR technology overlays or recreates missing architectural elements, allowing users to move from fragmentary remains to a fuller understanding of the original structure and its urban setting.",Stadio di Domiziano - visita con realtà aumentata,Sovrintendenza ai Beni Culturali di Roma Capitale; Local Government; Cultural services.; Italy,The Domiziano Virtual Experience uses virtual and augmented reality to help visitors understand the ancient Stadium of Domitian beneath Piazza Navona in Rome. The use case demonstrates how immersive technologies can support cultural management by making conservation-compatible interpretation possible without physically reconstructing the monument.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Archaeological sites can be difficult for visitors to interpret when only partial remains are visible and the original urban context has disappeared beneath modern layers of the city. The Domiziano Virtual Experience uses virtual and augmented reality to help visitors understand the ancient Stadium of Domitian beneath Piazza Navona in Rome. The operational objective is to make the site’s historical significance more accessible by reconstructing the stadium’s original appearance, spatial organisation and role in Roman public life. The implementation provides an immersive digital reconstruction that can show the ancient monument as it may have appeared, supporting interpretation through visualisation rather than text panels alone. At a conceptual level, XR technology overlays or recreates missing architectural elements, allowing users to move from fragmentary remains to a fuller understanding of the original structure and its urban setting. The expected impact is improved heritage interpretation, broader educational outreach, greater accessibility for users who struggle to imagine lost spaces and stronger public engagement with protected archaeological assets. The use case demonstrates how immersive technologies can support cultural management by making conservation-compatible interpretation possible without physically reconstructing the monument.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1622,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01217,EU_PSTW_202609_01217,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Duomo di Milano,Cultural services.,"Historic monuments often contain stories, materials and construction processes that visitors cannot easily access through a normal tour, especially when key places such as quarries or technical workspaces are physically distant or restricted.",Duomo di Milano 4.0,Duomo di Milano; Local Government; Cultural services.; Italy,"Duomo di Milano 4.0 uses virtual reality and augmented reality to reveal the cathedral’s history, material origins and construction techniques. The programme includes a VR journey to the Candoglia Quarry, where the marble used for the cathedral was extracted, and AR-guided experiences that use smart glasses to enrich the visitor route with reconstructions, images and videos.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Historic monuments often contain stories, materials and construction processes that visitors cannot easily access through a normal tour, especially when key places such as quarries or technical workspaces are physically distant or restricted. Duomo di Milano 4.0 uses virtual reality and augmented reality to reveal the cathedral’s history, material origins and construction techniques. The programme includes a VR journey to the Candoglia Quarry, where the marble used for the cathedral was extracted, and AR-guided experiences that use smart glasses to enrich the visitor route with reconstructions, images and videos. At a conceptual level, the technology creates an immersive interpretative layer: visitors can visualise quarry environments, historical stages and sculptural processes that would otherwise remain abstract. The expected impact is deeper public understanding of the cathedral’s long construction history, more inclusive and engaging heritage education, and a stronger connection between the monument, its material supply chain and the craftsmanship behind it. The use case is particularly relevant because it applies XR to a major cultural site not only for spectacle, but also to explain geological origin, artistic labour, conservation and historical continuity. It supports cultural-management objectives around accessibility, learning and innovation in heritage interpretation.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1623,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01218,EU_PSTW_202609_01218,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Duomo di Milano,Cultural services.,UNKNOWN,Chatbot Duomo di Milano official website,Duomo di Milano; Local Government; Cultural services.; Italy,"The implementation uses an integrated chatbot interface to help users navigate site content and find practical information more quickly than by browsing all pages manually. The use case is part of a broader digital-service ecosystem around the cathedral, which also includes official apps, video guides and immersive visitor experiences.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Visitors planning a visit to a major cultural and religious monument often need fast answers about tickets, opening hours, guided tours, accessibility, services and institutional information. The Duomo di Milano website chatbot supports this need by providing a conversational assistance layer within the official digital environment of the Veneranda Fabbrica del Duomo. The operational problem is that users may arrive with very different needs: some are tourists planning a route through the Cathedral, terraces and museum, while others are researchers, worshippers or users looking for information about the institution, events or visitor rules. The implementation uses an integrated chatbot interface to help users navigate site content and find practical information more quickly than by browsing all pages manually. At a conceptual level, the assistant interprets the user’s question, maps it to available visitor-service content and directs the user towards the relevant page, ticketing option or information area. The expected impact is improved digital visitor experience, reduced friction in planning a visit, fewer repetitive enquiries for staff and better online accessibility of the Duomo’s cultural and administrative information. The use case is part of a broader digital-service ecosystem around the cathedral, which also includes official apps, video guides and immersive visitor experiences.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1624,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01219,EU_PSTW_202609_01219,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Ministry of Culture (MiBACT) - Parco Archeologico del Colosseo,Cultural services.,"Archaeological remains can be difficult for visitors to interpret when the surviving structures only partially convey the scale, decoration and spatial logic of the original monument. The expected impact is stronger public understanding of the Domus Aurea, better interpretation of fragmentary remains, increased cultural participation and more sustainable heritage presentation because digital reconstruction can communicate lost elements without physical rebuilding.",Colosseum Archeologic Park - Domus Area Virtual Experience,Ministry of Culture (MiBACT) - Parco Archeologico del Colosseo; Central-Government; Cultural services.; Italy,The Domus Aurea Virtual Experience addresses this challenge by using immersive virtual-reality technology to reconstruct Nero’s palace and help visitors understand its architectural and decorative richness. The use case is particularly relevant for conservation because it enhances visitor experience while respecting the vulnerability of the archaeological site.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Archaeological remains can be difficult for visitors to interpret when the surviving structures only partially convey the scale, decoration and spatial logic of the original monument. The Domus Aurea Virtual Experience addresses this challenge by using immersive virtual-reality technology to reconstruct Nero’s palace and help visitors understand its architectural and decorative richness. The operational objective is to translate complex archaeological knowledge into an accessible visitor experience within a fragile heritage environment. The implementation combines an educational guided tour with VR content that reconstructs lost spaces, decorations and spatial relationships that are no longer visible in the physical remains. At a conceptual level, the system overlays scholarly interpretation onto the site through immersive visualisation, helping users move between the surviving archaeological fabric and a scientifically informed reconstruction of the original palace. The expected impact is stronger public understanding of the Domus Aurea, better interpretation of fragmentary remains, increased cultural participation and more sustainable heritage presentation because digital reconstruction can communicate lost elements without physical rebuilding. The use case is particularly relevant for conservation because it enhances visitor experience while respecting the vulnerability of the archaeological site. It demonstrates how XR can support both education and preservation in high-value cultural assets.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1625,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01220,EU_PSTW_202609_01220,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Ministry of Culture (MiBACT) - Parco Archeologico del Colosseo,Cultural services.,UNKNOWN,"Colosseum Archeological Park - ""Speak with Nerone"" chatbot",Ministry of Culture (MiBACT) - Parco Archeologico del Colosseo; Central-Government; Cultural services.; Italy,"The implementation uses chatbot technology to simulate dialogue with Nero, enabling users to ask questions and receive answers through a conversational interface.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Cultural institutions increasingly need digital tools that help visitors access historical interpretation and practical information in a more engaging format than static web pages. The “Speak with Nerone” chatbot was introduced by the Colosseum Archaeological Park as an AI-based conversational assistant embodied as Emperor Nero. The operational objective is to help users obtain information about opening hours, monuments and the park’s historical context while also creating a more memorable and playful interaction with cultural heritage. The implementation uses chatbot technology to simulate dialogue with Nero, enabling users to ask questions and receive answers through a conversational interface. At a conceptual level, the tool combines visitor assistance, public-history storytelling and automated information retrieval: a historical figure becomes the interface through which users navigate content about the Colosseum Archaeological Park and related sites. The expected impact is improved online engagement, better access to practical visitor information, stronger appeal for younger audiences and broader cultural literacy around the park’s monuments. The system does not replace curatorial or guide-led interpretation; rather, it provides an additional digital channel that makes introductory information more accessible and interactive. Its value lies in combining service support with historical characterisation, turning an administrative chatbot into a cultural-education tool.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1626,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01221,EU_PSTW_202609_01221,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,National and Kapodistrian University of Athens (NKUoA),Cultural services.,"Access to major archaeological sites is often limited by distance, mobility, cost, crowding or conservation constraints, while educational users may need detailed visual material that goes beyond conventional photographs.",Virtual tour of the Acropolisin Athens,National and Kapodistrian University of Athens (NKUoA); Academic-Research; Cultural services.; Greece,"The implementation uses panoramic high-resolution imagery, virtual navigation and descriptive historical content linked to selected monuments and points of interest. The expected impact is broader public access, stronger educational use, better support for users unable to visit physically and enhanced understanding of the Acropolis’ historical and architectural value.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Access to major archaeological sites is often limited by distance, mobility, cost, crowding or conservation constraints, while educational users may need detailed visual material that goes beyond conventional photographs. The Acropolis Virtual Tour provides an immersive digital environment through which users can explore the Acropolis and its principal monuments, including the Parthenon, Propylaia, Erechtheion and Temple of Athena Nike. The operational objective is to widen access to one of Greece’s most important cultural-heritage sites and support learning through interactive 360-degree exploration. The implementation uses panoramic high-resolution imagery, virtual navigation and descriptive historical content linked to selected monuments and points of interest. At a conceptual level, the system converts the archaeological site into a structured digital experience: users can move between viewpoints, examine architectural details and read contextual information that explains the significance of the monuments. The expected impact is broader public access, stronger educational use, better support for users unable to visit physically and enhanced understanding of the Acropolis’ historical and architectural value. The use case is important because it demonstrates how digital heritage platforms can complement physical visitation, providing a conservation-friendly channel for interpretation and remote learning.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1627,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01222,EU_PSTW_202609_01222,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,National Centre of Audiovisual Media and Communication,Broadcasting and publishing services.,"The operational problem is that audiovisual production incentives, education programmes, archive initiatives and administrative processes can be difficult to navigate for users with different levels of expertise.",EKOME Greece - chatbot,National Centre of Audiovisual Media and Communication; Central-Government; Broadcasting and publishing services.; Greece,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Audiovisual-sector organisations must guide producers, educators, researchers and citizens through incentives, administrative procedures, digital-media initiatives and institutional services. The EKOME chatbot was designed as a digital assistant on the website of Greece’s National Centre of Audiovisual Media and Communication, supporting users seeking immediate guidance on the organisation’s activities. The operational problem is that audiovisual production incentives, education programmes, archive initiatives and administrative processes can be difficult to navigate for users with different levels of expertise. The implementation provides a conversational interface that can answer general enquiries and direct users to relevant information areas. At a conceptual level, the chatbot recognises user intent, retrieves institutional service information and presents concise responses or pathways through the website. The expected impact is faster access to public information, reduced pressure on staff for repetitive enquiries, improved service accessibility and better user experience for the audiovisual sector. The case fits within a broader public-service trend in which specialised agencies add automated assistants to help users navigate complex policy and funding environments. It also reflects the role of digital service design in creative-industry support, where efficient access to information can affect production planning and participation in public programmes.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1634,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01223,EU_PSTW_202609_01223,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Agenzia delle dogane e dei Monopoli (ADM),"General economic, commercial and labour affair","International freight documentation remains heavily dependent on paper, repeated signatures and fragmented exchanges between exporters, freight forwarders, customs authorities, ports and importers.",ACCUDIRE - export tracking based on blockchain,"Agenzia delle dogane e dei Monopoli (ADM); Central-Government; General economic, commercial and labour affair; Italy",UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"International freight documentation remains heavily dependent on paper, repeated signatures and fragmented exchanges between exporters, freight forwarders, customs authorities, ports and importers. ACCUDIRE addresses this problem by digitising logistics documentation and creating trusted interoperability between public and private actors in export processes. The July 2021 pilot concerned a shipment from Italy to Turkey and involved Benetton Group, the Port of Trieste, the Italian Customs and Monopolies Agency, a global freight forwarder and a Turkish importing company. The implementation used the ACCUDIRE platform to create an electronic CMR consignment note, verify the identities of participants through two-factor authentication, geolocate signatures and store relevant information on a permissioned Hyperledger Fabric blockchain. The platform interoperated with AIDA, the Italian Customs Agency’s system, and transmitted pre-arrival goods information to the Port Community System. At a conceptual level, the solution combines blockchain-based immutability, digital identity, e-signature, customs integration and logistics-document workflows. The expected impact is lower paperwork, faster export procedures, better traceability, stronger anti-counterfeiting protection and improved coordination across the supply chain. Subsequent material shows that ACCUDIRE’s ExAc platform continues to support e-CMR and supply-chain document digitalisation, including further pilots connected to eFTI and customs interoperability.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1635,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01224,EU_PSTW_202609_01224,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Agenzia per l'Italia Digitale (AGID),"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.",UNKNOWN,Giotto OnChain blockchain-based Notarisation Service for digital assets,"Agenzia per l'Italia Digitale (AGID); Central-Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Italy","The expected impact is stronger protection against fraudulent use, clearer evidentiary value for documents, easier verification by third parties and a reusable trust layer for digital communications.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Public bodies, companies and organisations increasingly need to prove the integrity, authorship and existence of digital documents, especially where fraud, counterfeiting or later disputes could undermine trust. Giotto OnChain is a blockchain-based notarisation service developed by Almaviva to certify digital assets such as documents, letterheads, presentations and press releases. The operational problem is that digital content can be copied, altered or misrepresented, making it useful to create an independent proof that a given asset existed at a certain time and has not been changed. The implementation generates a digital fingerprint of the asset and records notarisation evidence on a public blockchain, while also supporting ownership certification through electronic signature. At a conceptual level, the service does not store the full document on-chain; instead, it anchors cryptographic evidence that can later be used to verify integrity and authenticity. The expected impact is stronger protection against fraudulent use, clearer evidentiary value for documents, easier verification by third parties and a reusable trust layer for digital communications. The service is also available in SaaS mode, making it suitable for organisations that need blockchain-based certification without building distributed-ledger infrastructure themselves.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1636,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01225,EU_PSTW_202609_01225,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Government of Gibraltar,Police Services,"Regulators and law-enforcement agencies face growing difficulty tracing illicit finance when crypto assets move quickly across wallets, exchanges and decentralised services.",Gibraltar Regulatory Agencies Trial Anti-money Laundering Platform for Crypto Asset Investigations,Government of Gibraltar; Regional Government; Police Services; United Kingdom,"The implementation uses blockchain-native analytics capable of monitoring high-risk transactions, tracing misappropriated funds and visualising transaction pathways. Public statements described the 2021 initiative as the first use of such technology to support Gibraltar’s anti-money-laundering work.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Regulators and law-enforcement agencies face growing difficulty tracing illicit finance when crypto assets move quickly across wallets, exchanges and decentralised services. Gibraltar entered into an agreement with Coinfirm to trial an AML risk-management platform for crypto and blockchain assets, aiming to strengthen its ability to investigate money laundering and terrorist-financing risks in digital-asset markets. The implementation uses blockchain-native analytics capable of monitoring high-risk transactions, tracing misappropriated funds and visualising transaction pathways. At a conceptual level, the platform analyses source and destination of funds, wallet ownership indicators, transaction fingerprints, clustering patterns and e-discovery data to support investigative workflows. This allows regulators and enforcement agencies to move beyond isolated wallet checks towards network-based analysis of suspicious activity. The expected impact is improved detection of high-risk crypto transactions, better investigative intelligence, stronger supervisory capacity and support for Gibraltar’s positioning as a regulated digital-asset jurisdiction. Public statements described the 2021 initiative as the first use of such technology to support Gibraltar’s anti-money-laundering work. Because the source frames the initiative as a trial, its main value is best understood as controlled evaluation of RegTech capabilities rather than confirmed permanent deployment across all Gibraltar agencies.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1637,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01226,EU_PSTW_202609_01226,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Commissariat à l'énergie atomique,R&D environmental protection.,UNKNOWN,Green Blockchain,Commissariat à l'énergie atomique; Academic-Research; R&D environmental protection.; France,"The use case involves sensors deployed in urban areas, which send raw climatic data to a laboratory responsible for processing it into time series of CO₂ concentrations. The implementation uses blockchain anchoring rather than storing all raw data on-chain: hashes of treated data or processing outputs are written to smart contracts, allowing other laboratories or auditors to reproduce the treatment process and compare results with the recorded hashes.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Climate-data trust is becoming increasingly important as cities, laboratories and environmental actors rely on low-cost sensors to monitor CO₂ concentrations and other indicators. Green Blockchain addresses this issue by notarising the processing of climatic data so that the public and third parties can verify that treatment steps have not been altered. The use case involves sensors deployed in urban areas, which send raw climatic data to a laboratory responsible for processing it into time series of CO₂ concentrations. The implementation uses blockchain anchoring rather than storing all raw data on-chain: hashes of treated data or processing outputs are written to smart contracts, allowing other laboratories or auditors to reproduce the treatment process and compare results with the recorded hashes. At a conceptual level, the system combines urban sensing, climate-data processing, web dashboards and low-energy blockchain approaches such as proof-of-stake or Byzantine fault-tolerant mechanisms. The expected impact is greater public trust in environmental data, stronger auditability between laboratories, and new trusted services for institutional or industrial environmental actors without requiring a central control body. Its participation in the European Blockchain Sandbox indicates regulatory and technical interest in the model. The use case remains particularly relevant because it connects blockchain not to speculative assets, but to data integrity and climate-monitoring accountability.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1638,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01227,EU_PSTW_202609_01227,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,European Commission,General public services n.e.c.,"At a conceptual level, educational institutions issue tamper-resistant digital credentials; learners store them in wallets; and receiving organisations verify their authenticity through decentralised trust registries rather than repeated manual checks. The expected impact is faster cross-border recognition of qualifications, less administrative burden for universities and employers, stronger user control over personal credentials and more reliable verification of academic records.",EBSI-VECTOR - EBSI enabled VErifiable Credentials & Trusted Organisations Registries - Education,European Commission; European Institution/Agency; General public services n.e.c.; European Union,"EBSI-VECTOR addresses this problem by applying the European Blockchain Services Infrastructure to verifiable credentials and trusted registries, with education as a major use case. The implementation builds on the EBSI diploma use-case specifications and the wider EBSI Verifiable Credentials Framework, using W3C Verifiable Credentials, digital wallets and blockchain-based trust mechanisms.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Cross-border education and employment processes still require people and institutions to verify diplomas, qualifications and organisational status through complex, document-heavy procedures. EBSI-VECTOR addresses this problem by applying the European Blockchain Services Infrastructure to verifiable credentials and trusted registries, with education as a major use case. The objective is to simplify how students, universities, employers and public authorities issue, hold, share and verify educational credentials across Europe. The implementation builds on the EBSI diploma use-case specifications and the wider EBSI Verifiable Credentials Framework, using W3C Verifiable Credentials, digital wallets and blockchain-based trust mechanisms. At a conceptual level, educational institutions issue tamper-resistant digital credentials; learners store them in wallets; and receiving organisations verify their authenticity through decentralised trust registries rather than repeated manual checks. EBSI-VECTOR extends existing EBSI capabilities by adding further functions such as trusted organisation registries, legal-person identity and revocation mechanisms. The expected impact is faster cross-border recognition of qualifications, less administrative burden for universities and employers, stronger user control over personal credentials and more reliable verification of academic records. The project involves 52 partners from 20 countries and is designed to deploy several cross-border journeys for education and social-security capabilities.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1639,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01228,EU_PSTW_202609_01228,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,European Commission,General public services n.e.c.,"Cross-border social security procedures in Europe still require workers, employers and public administrations to exchange and verify documents across different national systems, which can slow down labour mobility and create administrative friction. At a conceptual level, a competent social security institution issues a credential, the citizen or worker stores it in a wallet, and another authority or relying party verifies authenticity through decentralised trust mechanisms rather than manual document checks.",EBSI-VECTOR - EBSI enabled VErifiable Credentials & Trusted Organisations Registries - Social security,European Commission; European Institution/Agency; General public services n.e.c.; European Union,The EBSI-VECTOR social security use case addresses this problem by applying decentralised digital identity and verifiable credentials to two key documents: the Portable Document A1 for posted workers and the European Health Insurance Card.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Cross-border social security procedures in Europe still require workers, employers and public administrations to exchange and verify documents across different national systems, which can slow down labour mobility and create administrative friction. The EBSI-VECTOR social security use case addresses this problem by applying decentralised digital identity and verifiable credentials to two key documents: the Portable Document A1 for posted workers and the European Health Insurance Card. The objective is to introduce EBSI-compliant infrastructure into social security processes so that citizens and institutions can issue, hold, present and verify trusted documents more efficiently across borders. The implementation builds on the European Blockchain Services Infrastructure, digital wallets, W3C-style verifiable credentials and trusted registries for issuers and organisations. At a conceptual level, a competent social security institution issues a credential, the citizen or worker stores it in a wallet, and another authority or relying party verifies authenticity through decentralised trust mechanisms rather than manual document checks. The expected impact is faster verification, less paperwork, stronger fraud prevention, better user control over credentials and more interoperable social security administration. The project is linked to ESSPASS, whose first phase focused on cross-border digital verification of the PDA1, and EBSI-VECTOR aims to bridge pre-production capabilities towards real-life adoption in education and social security.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1640,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01229,EU_PSTW_202609_01229,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,European Commission,General public services n.e.c.,"The expected impact is faster cross-border onboarding, stronger legal-entity verification, reduced administrative burden and more secure business-to-government or business-to-business digital transactions.",EBSI-VECTOR - EBSI enabled VErifiable Credentials & Trusted Organisations Registries - Business registries,European Commission; European Institution/Agency; General public services n.e.c.; European Union,"The EBSI-VECTOR business registries use case focuses on these frictions by exploring how digital wallets, national business registries and legal-entity verification systems can work together.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Companies operating across borders often need to prove legal identity, authority, registration status or organisational credentials repeatedly to banks, public administrations, trading partners and digital-service providers. The EBSI-VECTOR business registries use case focuses on these frictions by exploring how digital wallets, national business registries and legal-entity verification systems can work together. The operational objective is to create a more trustworthy and interoperable way of recognising organisations across countries and sectors, reducing duplicated checks while improving confidence in business transactions. The implementation is based on EBSI-compliant verifiable credentials, trusted organisation registries and decentralised identity concepts for legal persons. In this pilot, digital-identity experts from 12 EU Member States collaborate to examine how national registries and wallet-based credentials can interact. At a conceptual level, a business registry or authorised issuer provides verifiable credentials for a legal entity, while relying parties verify that credential through EBSI trust frameworks and registry endpoints. The expected impact is faster cross-border onboarding, stronger legal-entity verification, reduced administrative burden and more secure business-to-government or business-to-business digital transactions. The wider EBSI framework also defines legal entity credential registry standards for publishing legal-entity verifiable credentials in an open and interoperable manner.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1641,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01230,EU_PSTW_202609_01230,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Heidelberg University of Education,R&D education.,UNKNOWN,VR4CLIMATE - Virtual Reality (VR) in education for sustainable development,Heidelberg University of Education; Academic-Research; R&D education.; Germany,"The implementation uses immersive VR scenarios to make abstract energy-system transformations visible and experiential. The solution is being further developed into “Green Experience Packages” that combine VR hardware, software and educational content for use in schools, digital hubs, innovation labs and similar settings.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Energy-transition concepts such as Power-to-X, hydrogen production and sector coupling are technically complex, yet public acceptance and workforce preparation depend on making their risks and opportunities understandable. VR4CLIMATE was developed from a virtual-reality experience commissioned by WWF Germany within the Kopernikus P2X project, with the aim of explaining grey, green and blue hydrogen worlds to a broad audience, especially young adults. The implementation uses immersive VR scenarios to make abstract energy-system transformations visible and experiential. At a conceptual level, learners enter a simulated hydrogen and Power-to-X environment, encounter different production pathways and explore how renewable electricity can be converted into gases, fuels, chemicals or other products. The solution is being further developed into “Green Experience Packages” that combine VR hardware, software and educational content for use in schools, digital hubs, innovation labs and similar settings. The expected impact is improved understanding of sustainable energy technologies, stronger engagement in climate education and more accessible communication of scientific findings from the Kopernikus P2X research programme. Its value lies in translating technical research into an educational format that can reach non-specialist audiences and support curricula in vocational and general education. Public Kopernikus and WWF material confirms the broader P2X education and outreach context, while product material frames VR4CLIMATE as a deployable learning package.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1642,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01231,EU_PSTW_202609_01231,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Ghent,Police Services,UNKNOWN,Ghent turns downtown traffic data into predictions during three-month pilot,Municipality of Ghent; Local Government; Police Services; Belgium,"At a conceptual level, the system combined urban sensing, data anonymisation, time-series analysis and predictive modelling, using several months of observations to forecast likely pressure points in the city centre. Ghent has since continued to experiment with data-driven mobility tools, including EIT Urban Mobility-supported work using AI and drones for road-safety analysis, indicating an ongoing smart-mobility trajectory beyond the initial pilot.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Busy city centres need better information on how pedestrians, cyclists and vehicles move through streets, especially when congestion affects shops, public space, safety and accessibility. Ghent’s three-month pilot tested whether anonymised traffic data from downtown streets could be converted into predictions about when and where crowding or congestion would occur. The municipality’s objective was both analytical and practical: to understand high-traffic areas better, provide useful intelligence for city officials and businesses, and identify which sensing technology would be most suitable for possible wider deployment. The implementation tested four types of cameras and sensors, each collecting anonymised counts or movement data on pedestrians, cyclists and vehicles. At a conceptual level, the system combined urban sensing, data anonymisation, time-series analysis and predictive modelling, using several months of observations to forecast likely pressure points in the city centre. These insights could support decisions on mobility management, public-space planning and retail-area operations. The expected impact was earlier anticipation of congestion, more evidence-based traffic and crowd management, and better understanding of sensor performance before scale-up. Ghent has since continued to experiment with data-driven mobility tools, including EIT Urban Mobility-supported work using AI and drones for road-safety analysis, indicating an ongoing smart-mobility trajectory beyond the initial pilot.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1643,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01232,EU_PSTW_202609_01232,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Aarhus Municipality,"General economic, commercial and labour affair",UNKNOWN,Aarhus Municipality creates an AI-enabled analytical tool for sustainable procurement decisions,"Aarhus Municipality; Local Government; General economic, commercial and labour affair; Denmark","The objective was to move sustainable procurement from broad policy ambition to concrete decision support, using municipal expenditure data to identify where emissions reductions might be achieved.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Public procurement has a major climate footprint, because everyday purchases of goods and services generate indirect emissions across large supplier chains. Aarhus Municipality worked with the local company Konsido to make these impacts more visible and actionable, helping public buyers understand how purchasing patterns could be redirected towards more sustainable options. The objective was to move sustainable procurement from broad policy ambition to concrete decision support, using municipal expenditure data to identify where emissions reductions might be achieved. The implementation developed Konsido CPO, an AI-based analytical tool that processes purchasing behaviour, supplier expenditure and climate-emissions information. GovLaunch reports that the tool divided municipal purchases into around 1,000 categories based on invoice analysis, while Aarhus describes the project as further developing Konsido CO2-focused Procurement Optimisation for automated Scope 3 climate accounting. At a conceptual level, the system links e-invoices, financial systems, supplier registers, category structures and emissions estimates to produce procurement intelligence. The expected impact is better prioritisation of green purchasing measures, more continuous follow-up on whether interventions work, and stronger ability to align procurement with climate-reduction targets. Konsido now presents itself as providing AI-based consumption and climate-emission analytics for public authorities and private companies, suggesting that the solution has moved beyond an isolated municipal experiment.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1644,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01233,EU_PSTW_202609_01233,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Koge,Public health services.,"Municipal eldercare services need to identify health deterioration early enough to prevent avoidable hospital admissions, while also allocating limited staff and resources where they are most needed.",AI-enabled platform for reduced hospitalizations among senior residents,Municipality of Koge; Local Government; Public health services.; Denmark,"Køge Municipality’s planned AI-enabled platform uses information from municipal caregiver notes to support earlier disease detection among senior residents. The Danish Digitisation Agency funded the project and intended to use the Køge platform as a model for other municipalities, showing its role as a public-sector AI signature initiative.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Municipal eldercare services need to identify health deterioration early enough to prevent avoidable hospital admissions, while also allocating limited staff and resources where they are most needed. Køge Municipality’s planned AI-enabled platform uses information from municipal caregiver notes to support earlier disease detection among senior residents. The objective is to complement staff training in early diagnosis and patient communication with a data-supported tool that can detect warning signs in everyday care documentation. The implementation analyses text notes and care observations, looking for patterns that may indicate emerging illness or increased risk. At a conceptual level, the system processes caregiver documentation, identifies clinically relevant signals, and provides recommendations on local resource deployment so that officials and care managers can respond earlier. The expected impact is fewer preventable hospitalisations, better targeting of municipal care resources, improved continuity for older citizens and stronger evidence for preventive eldercare. The Danish Digitisation Agency funded the project and intended to use the Køge platform as a model for other municipalities, showing its role as a public-sector AI signature initiative. However, available public sources describe it as planned or project-based rather than as a fully embedded operational service, so the current maturity should be treated cautiously.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1645,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01234,EU_PSTW_202609_01234,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Sønderborg,Public health services.,"The expected impact is faster case handling, fewer delays caused by missing documents, better allocation of caseworker time and a more reliable application pathway for citizens needing assistive technology.",AI-enabled system for assistive technology requests,Municipality of Sønderborg; Local Government; Public health services.; Denmark,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Municipal welfare services must process large volumes of applications for assistive technologies while ensuring that urgent cases receive attention quickly and that documentation is complete. Sønderborg Municipality handles around 5,000 annual requests for body-worn aids, including items such as corsets, diabetes materials and orthopaedic footwear, with 15 to 20 applications arriving each day. The operational objective is to reduce processing time and improve prioritisation without weakening the individual assessment that each citizen’s situation requires. The implementation tests an AI-enabled system that reviews whether the necessary medical or administrative documentation is present and sorts applications according to urgency and condition. At a conceptual level, the system analyses application content, checks completeness against case requirements, identifies missing information and supports triage so that caseworkers can focus on the most urgent or complex files first. The expected impact is faster case handling, fewer delays caused by missing documents, better allocation of caseworker time and a more reliable application pathway for citizens needing assistive technology. GovLaunch reports that the municipality received a DKK 3.2 million grant for a pilot of AI-supported application review, indicating that the initiative was designed as a funded test rather than a fully mature service.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1646,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01235,EU_PSTW_202609_01235,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Aalborg, Viborg and Skive Municipalities",Public health services.,"Dementia care often involves residents who experience loneliness, high arousal, withdrawal or difficulty sustaining social interaction, while care staff must balance emotional support with limited time and resources.",The LOVOT social AI robot in nursing homes to help people with dementia,"Aalborg, Viborg and Skive Municipalities; Local Government; Public health services.; Denmark","The implementation uses LOVOT, a small social robot designed to respond to touch, speech and eye contact through sensors, movement, warmth, sounds, facial expression and body language. Ethical attention remains necessary, especially because lifelike robots can blur emotional boundaries for people with cognitive impairment, making staff assessment and transparent use essential.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Dementia care often involves residents who experience loneliness, high arousal, withdrawal or difficulty sustaining social interaction, while care staff must balance emotional support with limited time and resources. Aalborg, Viborg and Skive municipalities piloted LOVOT in Danish nursing homes to investigate whether a social robot could contribute to quality of life for people with dementia. The target group includes residents in care centres who may benefit from comforting interaction, sensory stimulation or a non-demanding social presence. The implementation uses LOVOT, a small social robot designed to respond to touch, speech and eye contact through sensors, movement, warmth, sounds, facial expression and body language. At a conceptual level, the robot detects social cues, reacts in ways that resemble a living companion, and develops interaction patterns that may encourage residents to engage, calm down or seek contact. The technology does not replace professional care or relational work by staff; rather, it provides an additional welfare-technology tool that can be introduced where a resident’s needs and preferences make it appropriate. The expected impact is improved wellbeing, reduced loneliness or agitation, and richer activity options in dementia care. Ethical attention remains necessary, especially because lifelike robots can blur emotional boundaries for people with cognitive impairment, making staff assessment and transparent use essential.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1647,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01236,EU_PSTW_202609_01236,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Tampere,General public services n.e.c.,"The operational problem is that footfall depends on multiple variables, including regular mobility patterns, weather, public holidays, events and local venue programming, making manual forecasting unreliable.","The city of Tampere, in Finland, predicts future foot traffic in the city center with its Tamperen Pulssi tool",Municipality of Tampere; Local Government; General public services n.e.c.; Finland,Tampereen Pulssi was developed by the City of Tampere and Wapice as an AI-powered tool that predicts visitor flows in the city centre.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"City-centre businesses, event organisers and municipal services need to anticipate pedestrian flows in order to plan staffing, maintenance, opening hours and operational resources. Tampereen Pulssi was developed by the City of Tampere and Wapice as an AI-powered tool that predicts visitor flows in the city centre. The operational problem is that footfall depends on multiple variables, including regular mobility patterns, weather, public holidays, events and local venue programming, making manual forecasting unreliable. The implementation collects anonymised data from downtown traffic cameras and combines it with weather forecasts, the Tampere event calendar and ticketing-related information. At a conceptual level, the system transforms mobility observations and contextual variables into short- and medium-term predictions of pedestrian activity. The results are shown through the Tampere.Finland app and a map-based interface, helping local businesses and agencies understand where and when higher flows are expected. The expected impact is better preparation for busy periods, more efficient retail and restaurant staffing, improved planning of street maintenance and stronger data-driven support for city-centre vitality. The case is significant because it turns smart-city sensing into a practical economic-development service for businesses, rather than keeping mobility analytics only inside municipal operations.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1649,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01237,EU_PSTW_202609_01237,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Kuusamo Municipality,General services.,"The operational problem was that many website visitors searched for recurring topics, and municipal staff could not offer continuous live support across all channels. The expected impact is better after-hours service, faster answers for residents and visitors, reduced repetitive staff workload and evidence on what topics users most often struggle to find.",Kuusamo Municipality experiments with Kuusamo-Kati chatbot for tourism and health questions,Kuusamo Municipality; Local Government; General services.; Finland,"The implementation began by training or programming the bot with knowledge about the most frequently searched website topics, then using real user conversations and feedback forms to refine future development.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Municipal websites often serve multiple audiences, including residents, seasonal visitors and tourists, who may need guidance outside office hours on services, tourism, local events and health-related information. Kuusamo-Kati was introduced as an AI-enabled chatbot to provide automated support when live help was unavailable, especially during evenings and weekends. The operational problem was that many website visitors searched for recurring topics, and municipal staff could not offer continuous live support across all channels. The implementation began by training or programming the bot with knowledge about the most frequently searched website topics, then using real user conversations and feedback forms to refine future development. At a conceptual level, the system interprets a question, matches it to existing municipal content and returns an answer or direction to the relevant web page. The expected impact is better after-hours service, faster answers for residents and visitors, reduced repetitive staff workload and evidence on what topics users most often struggle to find. The case is also relevant because it combines tourism and municipal-service information in a single assistant, reflecting the needs of a destination municipality where visitors interact with local public services as well as tourism providers.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1651,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01238,EU_PSTW_202609_01238,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Järvenpää Municipality,General services.,The City of Järvenpää sought to modernise customer service by providing residents with faster access to information while managing limited staff resources.,Järvenpää is piloting Kunta-Kati the AI chatbot as its new customer service representative,Järvenpää Municipality; Local Government; General services.; Finland,"This use case involves the city administration, residents, and technology partners, with the objective of improving service availability and efficiency. Kunta-Kati is implemented as a digital customer service representative embedded on the city’s website, capable of answering questions about municipal services and local issues.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The City of Järvenpää sought to modernise customer service by providing residents with faster access to information while managing limited staff resources. Citizens often had questions outside office hours or on topics spanning multiple departments. This use case involves the city administration, residents, and technology partners, with the objective of improving service availability and efficiency. Kunta-Kati is implemented as a digital customer service representative embedded on the city’s website, capable of answering questions about municipal services and local issues. When unable to resolve a query, it seamlessly redirects users to human customer service staff. The solution improves first-contact resolution, extends service availability, and reduces routine workload for employees. It enhances citizen experience through clear, immediate guidance and supports a hybrid service model combining automation and human expertise.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1652,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01239,EU_PSTW_202609_01239,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Porvoo Municipality,General services.,"The solution improves website usability, reduces administrative burden, and strengthens digital service delivery.",Porvoo Municipality integrated the Kunta-Kai AI virtual customer advisor into the city's website,Porvoo Municipality; Local Government; General services.; Finland,"This use case involves municipal services, residents, and visitors, with the objective of providing more intuitive digital support.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Porvoo Municipality aimed to improve navigation and information access on its website while reducing repetitive enquiries to city departments. This use case involves municipal services, residents, and visitors, with the objective of providing more intuitive digital support. The Kunta-Kati virtual advisor is integrated into the city’s website to handle frequently asked questions and support search functionality. It connects users to relevant information and, when necessary, directs them to the appropriate department. The city is exploring extensions such as voice interaction to further enhance accessibility. The solution improves website usability, reduces administrative burden, and strengthens digital service delivery. It delivers scalable customer support and supports continuous improvement of municipal digital channels.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1653,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01240,EU_PSTW_202609_01240,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Turku Municipality,General services.,"The City of Turku faced urgent communication challenges during the COVID-19 pandemic and later during the Ukrainian refugee crisis, requiring rapid dissemination of reliable information across language barriers.",Turku Municipality breaks down language barriers to COVID-19 outreach and later Ukrainian refugee crisis with Åbot chatbot,Turku Municipality; Local Government; General services.; Finland,"This use case involved city authorities, residents, refugees, and volunteers, with the objective of ensuring inclusive access to public information. Åbot was implemented as a multilingual conversational service capable of responding in over one hundred languages.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The City of Turku faced urgent communication challenges during the COVID-19 pandemic and later during the Ukrainian refugee crisis, requiring rapid dissemination of reliable information across language barriers. This use case involved city authorities, residents, refugees, and volunteers, with the objective of ensuring inclusive access to public information. Åbot was implemented as a multilingual conversational service capable of responding in over one hundred languages. It provided up-to-date guidance on health measures, city services, and support for Ukrainian refugees and those wishing to help them. Integrated with official information sources, the service ensured accuracy and consistency. Åbot improved accessibility, reduced misinformation, and supported social inclusion during crises. It strengthened community resilience and demonstrated the value of multilingual digital services in emergency and integration contexts.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,NOT_IN_USE,미사용,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1654,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01241,EU_PSTW_202609_01241,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Bordeaux metropole,Transport,"Shared e-scooter services can improve short urban trips, but they also create safety, parking and sidewalk-riding challenges for municipalities.",Bordeaux partners with Superpedestrian on micro-mobility service featuring AI-enabled scooters,Bordeaux metropole; Local Government; Transport; France,"The operational objective was to offer residents a low-emission mobility option while using embedded technology to improve compliance and vehicle safety. The system can slow or stop vehicles when they are used on sidewalks or outside permitted areas, while operational teams use electric vans for lower-impact maintenance and collection.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Shared e-scooter services can improve short urban trips, but they also create safety, parking and sidewalk-riding challenges for municipalities. Bordeaux partnered with Superpedestrian to deploy LINK e-scooters as part of a regulated micro-mobility service. The operational objective was to offer residents a low-emission mobility option while using embedded technology to improve compliance and vehicle safety. The implementation used Superpedestrian’s Vehicle Intelligence system, which continuously evaluates component status and can respond when scooters operate in restricted ways. At a conceptual level, the scooters combine sensors, microprocessors, diagnostics, geofencing and real-time control logic. The system can slow or stop vehicles when they are used on sidewalks or outside permitted areas, while operational teams use electric vans for lower-impact maintenance and collection. The expected impact is safer shared micro-mobility, better management of public-space rules, reduced maintenance risk and improved confidence for the city in permitting e-scooter operations. The use case is important because it shows how AI-enabled vehicle intelligence can be embedded directly into mobility devices, shifting enforcement from only after-the-fact regulation towards real-time operational controls that support local policy.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1655,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01242,EU_PSTW_202609_01242,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Cologne,Waste water management.,"The operational problem was that earlier methods relied heavily on manual inspections and property-owner self-reporting, which made it difficult to detect changes in paved surfaces consistently across the city. The expected impact is fairer taxation, lower manual workload, improved data quality and better environmental planning.",Municipality of Cologne improves the accuracy of its wastewater taxation system with a geospatial AI system,Municipality of Cologne; Local Government; Waste water management.; Germany,"Cologne’s municipal wastewater authority, Stadtentwässerungsbetriebe Köln, worked with Hexagon to improve the accuracy and efficiency of this process using a GeoAI solution. The implementation uses aerial imagery, LiDAR mapping and AI-based classification to identify sealed and unsealed surfaces on private land.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Wastewater taxation often depends on the amount of impervious surface on private property, because paved or sealed areas increase rainwater runoff into the drainage system. Cologne’s municipal wastewater authority, Stadtentwässerungsbetriebe Köln, worked with Hexagon to improve the accuracy and efficiency of this process using a GeoAI solution. The operational problem was that earlier methods relied heavily on manual inspections and property-owner self-reporting, which made it difficult to detect changes in paved surfaces consistently across the city. The implementation uses aerial imagery, LiDAR mapping and AI-based classification to identify sealed and unsealed surfaces on private land. At a conceptual level, the platform analyses high-resolution geospatial data, detects changes since previous surveys and classifies surfaces that affect drainage-fee calculations. This provides a more accurate basis for wastewater-tax assessment and can also support simulations related to climate adaptation, such as renaturalisation or de-sealing of paved areas. The expected impact is fairer taxation, lower manual workload, improved data quality and better environmental planning. The case shows how GeoAI can support municipal finance and climate resilience at the same time: a taxation dataset becomes useful not only for billing, but also for understanding urban runoff and planning greener surfaces.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1656,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01243,EU_PSTW_202609_01243,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""social_nonprofit""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Rüsselsheim am Main,Pollution abatement.,Air quality and traffic-management measures can be difficult to enforce when they depend on distinguishing specific vehicle types without creating intrusive surveillance. The operational problem was to identify vehicles subject to the ban at roads entering and exiting the city while protecting privacy and avoiding manual monitoring.,Rüsselsheim am Main enforces truck ban with AI to abate pollution,City of Rüsselsheim am Main; Local Government; Pollution abatement.; Germany,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Air quality and traffic-management measures can be difficult to enforce when they depend on distinguishing specific vehicle types without creating intrusive surveillance. Rüsselsheim am Main introduced an AI-enabled intelligent-infrastructure solution to support enforcement of a ban on trucks weighing 7.5 tonnes or more when they originate outside city limits. The operational problem was to identify vehicles subject to the ban at roads entering and exiting the city while protecting privacy and avoiding manual monitoring. The implementation installed sixteen Velodyne Ultra Puck lidar sensors on street lights along relevant routes. These sensors collect anonymised three-dimensional traffic data that allow an AI-enabled platform to distinguish heavy trucks from other vehicles. At a conceptual level, the system analyses vehicle shape, size and movement patterns rather than relying on facial recognition or licence-plate identification. This enables local officials to understand whether restricted vehicles are entering the city and to generate evidence for traffic-control and environmental-management decisions. The expected impact is improved enforcement of the truck restriction, reduced pollution and noise, better data on freight movement and a foundation for wider traffic and air-quality analytics. The same infrastructure can also support prediction-based traffic management and environmental monitoring, making it a broader smart-city tool rather than a single-purpose enforcement device.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1657,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01244,EU_PSTW_202609_01244,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Bad Hersfeld,Street lighting.,"Street lighting is essential for safety, accessibility and public confidence, but conventional lighting systems often waste energy and provide limited flexibility for environmental or situational needs. The operational challenge was to modernise lighting while balancing energy efficiency, safety, traffic conditions, weather, noise, temperature and ecological concerns such as wildlife-sensitive light colour and brightness.",Urban Intelligence-as-a-Service model for smart lighting upgrade,City of Bad Hersfeld; Local Government; Street lighting.; Germany,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Street lighting is essential for safety, accessibility and public confidence, but conventional lighting systems often waste energy and provide limited flexibility for environmental or situational needs. Bad Hersfeld used an Urban Intelligence-as-a-Service model to upgrade its public lighting and integrate smart technologies into a unified urban-management system. The operational challenge was to modernise lighting while balancing energy efficiency, safety, traffic conditions, weather, noise, temperature and ecological concerns such as wildlife-sensitive light colour and brightness. The implementation connects new LED lighting fixtures to sensors and digital controls that gather urban data and adjust lighting behaviour. At a conceptual level, the system links lighting infrastructure, real-time traffic information, weather data, environmental sensors and a central interface. This allows lighting levels, light distribution and colour temperature to be adapted according to conditions rather than remaining fixed. Residents can also request lighting changes through a mobile app, adding a participatory service layer to the infrastructure. The expected impact is reduced energy consumption, improved safety and visibility, better environmental compatibility and a reusable platform for future smart-city services. The case is significant because it treats lighting as both a utility and a data-enabled urban platform, where operational intelligence can be delivered as a service rather than through isolated infrastructure projects.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1658,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01245,EU_PSTW_202609_01245,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Messina,Waste management.,"Waste-management services affect every household, yet residents often struggle to understand collection schedules, sweeping activities, transport responsibilities, washing services and waste-fee information.",Messina online transparency platform for waste management services,Municipality of Messina; Local Government; Waste management.; Italy,"Messina adopted an online transparency platform to make the city’s waste-management service more visible and easier to use. At a conceptual level, the system turns structured service information into both web-based and voice-based interaction: citizens can ask what waste is collected, update their calendar, select or change their collection zone, request information about responsible entities and use a “rifiutologo” function to understand where a specific item should be disposed of.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Waste-management services affect every household, yet residents often struggle to understand collection schedules, sweeping activities, transport responsibilities, washing services and waste-fee information. Messina adopted an online transparency platform to make the city’s waste-management service more visible and easier to use. The operational problem was to provide clear, accessible and regularly updated information about collection, transport, sweeping, washing and the application of the TARI waste-management service. The implementation makes this information available through a digital transparency platform and extends access through a voice-assistant skill for Amazon Alexa. At a conceptual level, the system turns structured service information into both web-based and voice-based interaction: citizens can ask what waste is collected, update their calendar, select or change their collection zone, request information about responsible entities and use a “rifiutologo” function to understand where a specific item should be disposed of. The expected impact is better resident compliance with collection rules, fewer information gaps, improved transparency of waste-service responsibilities and more convenient access for people who prefer voice interaction. The use case is relevant because it connects open municipal-service information with conversational AI, making waste-management transparency available through everyday domestic devices.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1660,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01246,EU_PSTW_202609_01246,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of The Hague,Transport,"Fixed-route public transport in low-density neighbourhoods often suffers from low occupancy rates and limited flexibility, resulting in operational inefficiencies and reduced service attractiveness.",The Hague and The Routing Company develop on-demand public transit service,Municipality of The Hague; Local Government; Transport; Netherlands,"The service uses a digital platform accessible via passenger and driver applications, where ride requests are processed using optimisation algorithms and AI-enabled routing models that account for real-time traffic conditions, vehicle capacity and stop locations.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Fixed-route public transport in low-density neighbourhoods often suffers from low occupancy rates and limited flexibility, resulting in operational inefficiencies and reduced service attractiveness. In The Hague’s Benoordenhout area, local transit operator HTM sought to replace underused fixed routes with a flexible, demand-responsive alternative. Stakeholders include passengers, municipal authorities, HTM and technology providers. The objective is to improve accessibility and service efficiency through dynamic routing. The service uses a digital platform accessible via passenger and driver applications, where ride requests are processed using optimisation algorithms and AI-enabled routing models that account for real-time traffic conditions, vehicle capacity and stop locations. The system dynamically assigns vehicles and calculates efficient shared routes between designated stops, integrating with existing ticketing and operational systems. Anticipated impacts include higher vehicle utilisation, reduced operational costs, shorter waiting times and improved user satisfaction, alongside data-driven service planning capabilities.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1664,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01247,EU_PSTW_202609_01247,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Rotterdam,Community development.,"Urban planning in Rotterdam requires balancing growth, sustainability, and resilience, yet fragmented data limited predictive analysis and cross-domain optimisation.",Rotterdam created digital twin to improve urban planning,City of Rotterdam; Local Government; Community development.; Netherlands,"This use case involves the city administration, planners, infrastructure managers, and technology partners, with the objective of improving evidence-based urban planning. Rotterdam’s digital twin is implemented by integrating existing city maps, LiDAR-based 3D models, engineering blueprints, and real-time sensor data into a unified virtual representation of the city.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Urban planning in Rotterdam requires balancing growth, sustainability, and resilience, yet fragmented data limited predictive analysis and cross-domain optimisation. This use case involves the city administration, planners, infrastructure managers, and technology partners, with the objective of improving evidence-based urban planning. Rotterdam’s digital twin is implemented by integrating existing city maps, LiDAR-based 3D models, engineering blueprints, and real-time sensor data into a unified virtual representation of the city. The platform supports simulation and analysis across domains such as energy consumption, mobility, and subsurface infrastructure management. The solution improves planning accuracy, supports scenario testing, and enhances coordination across departments. It reduces risk, supports sustainability goals, and delivers long-term value by enabling smarter, data-driven urban development.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F07_DATA_VISIBILITY_INTEGRATION_GAP,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1665,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01248,EU_PSTW_202609_01248,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Borås Municipality,General public services n.e.c.,"Borås Municipality faces a high volume of routine enquiries from residents seeking information on municipal services, which can place pressure on customer service teams and slow response times.",Borås Municipality assesses AI chatbot improvements to customer service during pilot,Borås Municipality; Local Government; General public services n.e.c.; Sweden,"The use case involves municipal departments, customer service staff, and citizens, with the objective of providing faster answers to common questions and ensuring smooth escalation to human agents when needed. The solution is implemented through a conversational chatbot embedded on the municipal website, initially populated with frequently asked questions identified by staff.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Borås Municipality faces a high volume of routine enquiries from residents seeking information on municipal services, which can place pressure on customer service teams and slow response times. To address this, the municipality launched a six-month pilot to assess whether a virtual assistant could improve accessibility and service quality while supporting staff rather than replacing them. The use case involves municipal departments, customer service staff, and citizens, with the objective of providing faster answers to common questions and ensuring smooth escalation to human agents when needed. The solution is implemented through a conversational chatbot embedded on the municipal website, initially populated with frequently asked questions identified by staff. The system continuously expands its knowledge base by learning from user interactions and feedback, and it is integrated with existing customer service workflows to redirect unresolved queries to human operators. During the pilot, the chatbot increased first-contact resolution for routine enquiries and improved availability outside office hours. The municipality is evaluating user satisfaction, accuracy of responses, and operational impact. Early results indicate reduced workload for staff, faster access to information for residents, and valuable insights into citizen needs, supporting evidence-based decisions on future digital service delivery.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F06_HIGH_VOLUME_SCALABILITY_BOTTLENECK,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1670,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01249,EU_PSTW_202609_01249,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Birmingham City Council,Police Services,"When a suspected incident is detected, the system sends an alert to council officers with relevant imagery, reducing the need to manually review hours of footage. The expected impact is faster officer response, improved investigation efficiency, better evidence for enforcement and reduced street-cleansing burden.",Birmingham City Council takes aim at illegal dumping with its AI-enabled CCTV camera pilot,Birmingham City Council; Local Government; Police Services; United Kingdom,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Illegal dumping creates environmental, financial and public-safety problems for local authorities, particularly when waste is left repeatedly in known hotspots and enforcement teams must review footage or respond after the fact. Birmingham City Council introduced an AI-enabled CCTV pilot to strengthen its response to fly-tipping in Aston, Erdington and North Edgbaston. The operational objective is to detect suspected dumped waste more quickly, support timely investigations and deter environmental offences alongside signage on penalties and online reporting channels. The implementation places AI-enhanced cameras near fly-tipping hotspots, where they are trained to identify waste placed anywhere other than public bins. When a suspected incident is detected, the system sends an alert to council officers with relevant imagery, reducing the need to manually review hours of footage. At a conceptual level, the system combines object detection, event-triggered notification, CCTV evidence capture and enforcement workflow support. The expected impact is faster officer response, improved investigation efficiency, better evidence for enforcement and reduced street-cleansing burden. The pilot was supported by a £45,000 national grant, and Birmingham’s own announcement described three AI CCTV cameras as an additional tool in the city’s effort to tackle environmental criminals. Its value lies in targeted hotspot monitoring rather than generalised citywide surveillance.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1673,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01250,EU_PSTW_202609_01250,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Derby City Council,General public services n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,Derby City Council connects residents to city services with AI-powered digital assistants,Derby City Council; Local Government; General public services n.e.c.; United Kingdom,"Derby City Council introduced AI-powered digital assistants, Darcie for council customer services and Ali for Derby Homes housing enquiries, to create a more accessible digital front door for residents. The implementation uses conversational AI across website and phone interactions, replacing or augmenting traditional switchboard and interactive voice-response functions.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Residents contacting a council often need help with multiple service areas through web and telephone channels, but routine enquiries can absorb staff capacity that would be better directed to complex cases. Derby City Council introduced AI-powered digital assistants, Darcie for council customer services and Ali for Derby Homes housing enquiries, to create a more accessible digital front door for residents. The operational problem was to allow people to access services in one place, receive immediate assistance and avoid unnecessary transfer between channels or teams. The implementation uses conversational AI across website and phone interactions, replacing or augmenting traditional switchboard and interactive voice-response functions. At a conceptual level, the assistants interpret what the resident is asking, provide answers, route the query to the right service or escalate to staff where human support is needed. The expected impact is quicker access to information, reduced waiting times, lower handling cost and more staff capacity for issues that require professional judgement or personalised service. The case is also important because it applies AI to mainstream local-government customer contact, rather than limiting it to a specialist back-office process. It demonstrates how conversational tools can become part of an authority-wide operating model for resident interaction.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1674,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01251,EU_PSTW_202609_01251,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,London Borough of Harrow,Fuel and energy,"Housing decarbonisation is particularly difficult in social housing because retrofit measures must reduce emissions and energy costs while remaining practical, affordable and acceptable for residents.",Project-A.I.R (Artificial Intelligence Guided Retrofit),London Borough of Harrow; Local Government; Fuel and energy; United Kingdom,"GovLaunch reported a potential annual bill reduction of up to 45% if implemented, while NewFrame reports that the three-kilowatt solar-glass array could deliver up to 68% of individual household energy bills throughout the year.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Housing decarbonisation is particularly difficult in social housing because retrofit measures must reduce emissions and energy costs while remaining practical, affordable and acceptable for residents. In Harrow, Project-A.I.R explored a solar-glass structure concept intended to support household energy transition and reduce fuel poverty. The concept centres on a standalone transparent garden structure or pergola-canopy in which photovoltaic glass serves both as construction material and power-generating surface. The implementation brings together retrofit analysis, solar PV glass, a three-kilowatt peak array and the wider transition from fabric-first improvements to electric-powered homes. Public descriptions frame the initiative as AI-guided because the concept was identified and assessed through an approach designed to target efficient decarbonisation measures in housing. At a conceptual level, the solution combines household retrofit intelligence, semi-transparent solar generation, structural design and social-housing energy planning. The expected impact is reduced household energy bills, lower carbon emissions and a visible demonstration of renewable technology integrated into domestic space. GovLaunch reported a potential annual bill reduction of up to 45% if implemented, while NewFrame reports that the three-kilowatt solar-glass array could deliver up to 68% of individual household energy bills throughout the year. The project was presented publicly at Grand Designs Live in 2023.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1675,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01252,EU_PSTW_202609_01252,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Dudley Metropolitan Borough Council,Transport,UNKNOWN,Dudley Metropolitan Borough Council creates smart intersection with AI-powered road sensors,Dudley Metropolitan Borough Council; Local Government; Transport; United Kingdom,"Dudley Metropolitan Borough Council deployed AI-powered road sensors with VivaCity to support smarter junction management and improve traffic flow in the Brierley Hill and Merry Hill area. The operational problem was to identify where congestion and active-travel demand were greatest, then use data to select and improve a specific junction.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Traffic congestion and road-safety problems often require detailed information about how different users move through junctions, including cars, buses, cyclists and pedestrians. Dudley Metropolitan Borough Council deployed AI-powered road sensors with VivaCity to support smarter junction management and improve traffic flow in the Brierley Hill and Merry Hill area. The operational problem was to identify where congestion and active-travel demand were greatest, then use data to select and improve a specific junction. The implementation installed a network of sixty-five VivaCity sensors, with a cluster supporting a Smart Junction at Venture Way and Mill Street. At a conceptual level, the sensors use computer vision to classify and count road users, measure demand and journey times, and feed multimodal traffic data into a signal-control platform. This allows traffic lights to respond more dynamically to current motor-vehicle, cycling and pedestrian flows. The expected impact is reduced delay, better traffic flow, improved air quality, stronger support for active travel and more evidence-based local transport interventions. The case is significant because it combines monitoring and control: the same sensor data that helps diagnose congestion also supports real-time or near-real-time optimisation of traffic signals.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1676,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01253,EU_PSTW_202609_01253,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Cambridgeshire County Council,Public order and safety n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,"Cambridgeshire County Council, GB pilots AI-powered traffic sensors at East Road in Cambridge",Cambridgeshire County Council; Local Government; Public order and safety n.e.c.; United Kingdom,"At East Road in Cambridge, AI-powered traffic sensors were trialled to reduce waiting times for pedestrians and other road users while making crossings safer and more responsive. The use case demonstrates how computer vision can improve local traffic infrastructure by making crossings more adaptive to actual street conditions.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Signalised crossings must balance pedestrian safety and traffic flow, especially in busy urban locations where road users arrive in groups and demand changes throughout the day. At East Road in Cambridge, AI-powered traffic sensors were trialled to reduce waiting times for pedestrians and other road users while making crossings safer and more responsive. The operational problem was that conventional crossing systems do not always understand the number, type or behaviour of users waiting to cross. The implementation used Starling Technologies’ camera-based AI sensors, installed through collaboration between the Greater Cambridge Partnership and Cambridgeshire County Council’s traffic signals team. At a conceptual level, the system analyses road users in real time, identifies different types of movement, and learns when people are likely to want to cross. Linked signals can then adjust timings based on pedestrian and traffic conditions. The expected impact is safer crossings, shorter pedestrian waits, smoother traffic movement and improved support for active travel. Privacy is also relevant because the technology is designed to analyse movement patterns rather than identify individuals. The use case demonstrates how computer vision can improve local traffic infrastructure by making crossings more adaptive to actual street conditions.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1677,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01254,EU_PSTW_202609_01254,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Leeds City Council,Transport,"The operational problem was that traditional detection infrastructure, such as induction loops, provides limited information and can be costly to maintain, while modern transport policy requires multimodal prioritisation.",Leeds City Council pursues smart traffic management with AI for Detection technology,Leeds City Council; Local Government; Transport; United Kingdom,"Leeds City Council deployed VivaCity’s AI for Detection technology across a network of junctions to improve smart traffic management and tackle congestion. At a conceptual level, the sensors use computer vision to classify road users, detect buses, cyclists, pedestrians and other vehicles, and communicate with existing traffic-signal controllers.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Busy urban junctions require accurate real-time detection of different transport modes if traffic signals are to support buses, cyclists, pedestrians and general traffic effectively. Leeds City Council deployed VivaCity’s AI for Detection technology across a network of junctions to improve smart traffic management and tackle congestion. The operational problem was that traditional detection infrastructure, such as induction loops, provides limited information and can be costly to maintain, while modern transport policy requires multimodal prioritisation. The implementation installed more than 180 AI for Detection sensors across twenty-five sites. At a conceptual level, the sensors use computer vision to classify road users, detect buses, cyclists, pedestrians and other vehicles, and communicate with existing traffic-signal controllers. This enables more dynamic signal responses, potentially including bus priority or active-travel support at critical times of day. The expected impact is reduced stop-start traffic, improved bus reliability, better cycling and pedestrian movement, and richer traffic datasets for future road projects. The case is important because it embeds AI into existing urban traffic-control infrastructure, showing how cities can upgrade junction intelligence without replacing the entire signal network.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1678,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01255,EU_PSTW_202609_01255,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Westminster City Council,Police Services,"Illegal dumping creates costs for councils, damages the public realm and can become concentrated in specific hotspots where conventional enforcement is difficult. Its value lies in increasing enforcement visibility and reducing the manual burden of watching footage continuously.",Westminster City Council deploys AI-supported cameras in campaign against illegal dumping,Westminster City Council; Local Government; Police Services; United Kingdom,"Westminster City Council deployed AI-supported CCTV cameras as part of a campaign against fly-tipping and wider anti-social behaviour. When a suspected incident occurs, the system captures relevant footage and nearby licence plates, after which council or enforcement staff review the material and may pursue fines or further action using Driver and Vehicle Licensing Agency data.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Illegal dumping creates costs for councils, damages the public realm and can become concentrated in specific hotspots where conventional enforcement is difficult. Westminster City Council deployed AI-supported CCTV cameras as part of a campaign against fly-tipping and wider anti-social behaviour. The operational problem was to detect suspected dumping incidents more systematically at locations where waste was repeatedly left and to support enforcement with usable evidence. The implementation placed cameras linked to an AI-enabled platform at three hotspots. When a suspected incident occurs, the system captures relevant footage and nearby licence plates, after which council or enforcement staff review the material and may pursue fines or further action using Driver and Vehicle Licensing Agency data. At a conceptual level, the system combines event detection, CCTV evidence capture, vehicle-record linkage and human enforcement review. The expected impact is faster identification of suspected offenders, deterrence in problem areas, more targeted clean-street operations and better coordination under local anti-social-behaviour strategies. The technology does not issue penalties autonomously; it supports investigation by surfacing incidents and evidence for human review. Its value lies in increasing enforcement visibility and reducing the manual burden of watching footage continuously.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1679,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01256,EU_PSTW_202609_01256,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Powys County Council,Social protection n.e.c.,The operational problem was to process high volumes of £200 support-payment applications without creating excessive manual workload or delaying assistance to eligible households.,Powys County Council builds AI-enabled system for expedited Fuel Support Scheme reviews,Powys County Council; Local Government; Social protection n.e.c.; United Kingdom,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Emergency household-support schemes require fast payment, fraud control and simple application processes, particularly when residents face acute energy-cost pressures. Powys County Council built an AI-enabled platform to accelerate reviews of applications for the Welsh Government Fuel Support Scheme. The operational problem was to process high volumes of £200 support-payment applications without creating excessive manual workload or delaying assistance to eligible households. The implementation allowed applicants meeting all scheme criteria to receive automatic approvals, while duplicate applications were filtered out and potentially fraudulent or unclear applications were routed for council review. At a conceptual level, the system combines eligibility checking, duplicate detection, workflow triage and exception handling. This means automation handles straightforward applications, while human officers focus on cases where judgement or further investigation is required. The expected impact is quicker payment to residents, reduced administrative cost, better fraud prevention and a more user-friendly support process. The case shows how AI can support welfare administration when rules are structured and urgency is high, provided that human review remains available for flagged applications. It is best understood as a narrowly scoped administrative tool for a defined support scheme rather than a general welfare decision-making system.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK|F06_HIGH_VOLUME_SCALABILITY_BOTTLENECK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1680,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01257,EU_PSTW_202609_01257,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Bournemouth, Christchurch & Poole Council",Pollution abatement.,"The expected impact is lower monitoring cost, reduced reliance on slow laboratory testing, earlier warning of deterioration and a reusable model for other councils managing coastal water quality.","Bournemouth, Christchurch & Poole Council monitors coastal water quality with AI sensors","Bournemouth, Christchurch & Poole Council; Local Government; Pollution abatement.; United Kingdom","The implementation deployed low-cost wireless sensors on buoys and connected them to UnifAI’s analytical platform. At a conceptual level, the system collects raw water-quality measurements, transmits them remotely, and applies AI-enabled modelling to turn sensor readings into indicators, alerts and analysis of pollutant or discharge conditions.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Protected coastal waters require continuous monitoring because pollution, nutrients, industrial discharges and changing environmental conditions can affect bathing water, shellfish beds and sensitive ecosystems. Bournemouth, Christchurch and Poole Council worked with Poole Harbour Commissioners and UnifAI Technology to develop a more timely and affordable alternative to traditional laboratory-based monitoring. The objective was to provide real-time insight into water-quality changes so that authorities could identify risks earlier and target mitigation or clean-up activity more effectively. The implementation deployed low-cost wireless sensors on buoys and connected them to UnifAI’s analytical platform. At a conceptual level, the system collects raw water-quality measurements, transmits them remotely, and applies AI-enabled modelling to turn sensor readings into indicators, alerts and analysis of pollutant or discharge conditions. BCP Council described the first phase as live in October 2020 and as the first deployment of a low-cost remote and continuous sensor network using AI to monitor a protected water ecosystem in real time. A later council update states that BCP had partnered with UnifAI two years earlier to develop low-cost wireless sensors with AI for more timely monitoring. The expected impact is lower monitoring cost, reduced reliance on slow laboratory testing, earlier warning of deterioration and a reusable model for other councils managing coastal water quality.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1683,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01258,EU_PSTW_202609_01258,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Southampton City Council,Education not definable by level.,UNKNOWN,Southampton City Council trains residents for the Future of Work with its Bodyswaps pilot,Southampton City Council; Local Government; Education not definable by level.; United Kingdom,"The implementation uses virtual reality and AI-enabled simulations in which learners practise communication scenarios, receive feedback and reflect on their performance. The case is significant because it connects immersive technology with local economic-development policy, using VR not as entertainment but as a practical tool for employability and workforce preparation.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Employment support increasingly needs to help residents build soft skills, confidence and communication abilities, not only provide job listings or formal training. Southampton City Council selected Bodyswaps as part of its Future of Work programme to help residents prepare for interviews, teamwork, leadership and workplace communication through immersive learning. The operational problem was to support people seeking jobs, apprenticeships or other work-related outcomes in a labour market shaped by digitisation and changing employer expectations. The implementation uses virtual reality and AI-enabled simulations in which learners practise communication scenarios, receive feedback and reflect on their performance. At a conceptual level, Bodyswaps creates a safe practice environment where users can experience realistic conversations, observe themselves from different perspectives and build confidence through repeated attempts. Employment-service providers host the technology, and employability professionals support learners alongside the digital training. The expected impact is improved interview performance, stronger soft skills, increased confidence and better access to innovative training for residents who may benefit from experiential learning. The case is significant because it connects immersive technology with local economic-development policy, using VR not as entertainment but as a practical tool for employability and workforce preparation.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1686,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01259,EU_PSTW_202609_01259,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Leicester City Council,Public health services.,UNKNOWN,Leicester City Council partners with university researchers on H2A: Lifestyle initiative,Leicester City Council; Local Government; Public health services.; United Kingdom,"The implementation uses artificial intelligence and machine learning to analyse multiple variables, including demographic, social, environmental and activity-related information. The initiative also reflects a broader Local+ partnership between Leicester City Council and the university, using academic research to inform public-policy interventions.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Obesity prevention requires more than generic health messaging, because lifestyle choices are shaped by local environment, housing, employment, activity patterns, demographics and access to support. Leicester City Council partnered with De Montfort University on the H2A: Lifestyle initiative to develop more personalised weight-loss and health-improvement support for residents. The objective is to help practitioners and public-health officials understand which factors contribute to obesity risk in different parts of the city and to design interventions that respond to individual and neighbourhood conditions. The implementation uses artificial intelligence and machine learning to analyse multiple variables, including demographic, social, environmental and activity-related information. The planned tool recommends small, realistic lifestyle changes based on individual activity data and wider city health data, while dashboards can help officials target wellness campaigns by postcode. At a conceptual level, the system links personalisation, population-health analytics, behavioural insight and local policy planning. The expected impact is more relevant support for residents, better targeting of obesity-prevention campaigns, and stronger evidence for local strategies that reduce barriers to healthy living. The initiative also reflects a broader Local+ partnership between Leicester City Council and the university, using academic research to inform public-policy interventions. Public sources describe it as a research-led programme rather than a fully mature citywide health service.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1687,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01260,EU_PSTW_202609_01260,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Monmouthshire County Council,Public order and safety n.e.c.,"The operational problem was to understand whether the new arrangement improved cycling conditions and affected vehicle behaviour, rather than relying only on anecdotal feedback or occasional manual counts.",Monmouthshire County Council monitors the success of new bike lanes with Vivacity Labs platform,Monmouthshire County Council; Local Government; Public order and safety n.e.c.; United Kingdom,"At a conceptual level, the sensors use AI-enabled computer vision to distinguish different road-user types, measure flows and produce indicators such as cyclist counts and average vehicle speed. The use case is significant because it links policy evaluation directly to live street data, helping a local authority understand the real-world effects of infrastructure investment.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Active-travel infrastructure needs reliable evidence on how streets are used before and after design changes, especially where new cycle lanes alter traffic flow or reduce carriageway space for motor vehicles. Monmouthshire County Council used VivaCity sensors to evaluate protected contraflow cycling lanes introduced on one-way streets in Abergavenny. The operational problem was to understand whether the new arrangement improved cycling conditions and affected vehicle behaviour, rather than relying only on anecdotal feedback or occasional manual counts. The implementation placed sensors at three points on Lion Street to gather anonymised multimodal data on traffic density, user classification, paths and speeds. At a conceptual level, the sensors use AI-enabled computer vision to distinguish different road-user types, measure flows and produce indicators such as cyclist counts and average vehicle speed. This allows the council to monitor whether cycling becomes more popular and whether vehicle speeds change as a result of the new layout. The expected impact is more evidence-based active-travel planning, improved safety for cyclists and pedestrians, and better assessment of whether similar interventions should be used elsewhere. The use case is significant because it links policy evaluation directly to live street data, helping a local authority understand the real-world effects of infrastructure investment.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1688,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01261,EU_PSTW_202609_01261,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Edinburgh Council,Public order and safety n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,The City of Edinburgh Council continues smart city evolution with CCTV Control Centre,City of Edinburgh Council; Local Government; Public order and safety n.e.c.; United Kingdom,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Large urban centres need coordinated situational awareness across public safety, transport, major events and city operations. Edinburgh’s smart CCTV Control Centre was developed to modernise the city’s security and surveillance infrastructure while integrating it with traffic and transportation systems. The operational problem was that older CCTV and remote security systems were not fully connected, limiting rapid retrieval of footage, coordinated response and citywide operational insight. The implementation included high-definition CCTV cameras, expanded security coverage, advanced analytics and artificial intelligence capable of supporting situational awareness and alerts about emerging threats. North was selected to deliver the system and integrate the security environment with wider city platforms. At a conceptual level, the control centre consolidates camera feeds, analytics, transport data and operational workflows into a single environment where authorised staff can identify incidents, retrieve evidence and coordinate responses. The expected impact is improved public safety, faster issue resolution, better traffic and city-operations management, and richer understanding of how people move through the urban environment by foot, bicycle or vehicle. The case demonstrates how CCTV modernisation can become part of a wider smart-city operating model when video infrastructure is connected to urban analytics and service coordination.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1689,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01262,EU_PSTW_202609_01262,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Sunderland City Council,Public order and safety n.e.c.,"Local authorities hold large volumes of data across services such as anti-social behaviour, social care, taxation, health and environmental incidents, but fragmented datasets can prevent early intervention and coordinated action.",Sunderland City Council converts data into improved services with AI-powered platform,Sunderland City Council; Local Government; Public order and safety n.e.c.; United Kingdom,"The implementation uses artificial intelligence and machine learning to analyse raw datasets, identify patterns and suggest potential courses of action. At a conceptual level, the platform aggregates diverse operational data, applies analytical models, presents insights through dashboards and supports multi-agency decision-making.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Local authorities hold large volumes of data across services such as anti-social behaviour, social care, taxation, health and environmental incidents, but fragmented datasets can prevent early intervention and coordinated action. Sunderland City Council adopted ConnexinOS as an integrated smart-city platform to pool council and third-party data and support more responsive public services. The operational problem was to move from isolated information systems towards a shared data environment that could reveal trends, risks and service demands across departments. The implementation uses artificial intelligence and machine learning to analyse raw datasets, identify patterns and suggest potential courses of action. At a conceptual level, the platform aggregates diverse operational data, applies analytical models, presents insights through dashboards and supports multi-agency decision-making. This can help staff detect issues such as vandalism patterns, environmental incidents or residents who may need support before problems escalate. The expected impact is earlier intervention, better coordination across agencies, improved service targeting and stronger evidence for local policy decisions. The case is important because it treats AI not as a single service chatbot or automation script, but as a cross-cutting urban intelligence layer that can inform multiple municipal service areas.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F06_HIGH_VOLUME_SCALABILITY_BOTTLENECK|F07_DATA_VISIBILITY_INTEGRATION_GAP,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1690,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01263,EU_PSTW_202609_01263,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Wandsworth Borough Council,Pollution abatement.,UNKNOWN,Wandsworth Borough Council tracks air quality and traffic patterns with Eco Census cameras,Wandsworth Borough Council; Local Government; Pollution abatement.; United Kingdom,"The use case shows how environmental monitoring and mobility analytics can be joined in one street-level system, helping a borough understand not only air quality outcomes but also the traffic conditions behind them.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Air-quality policy depends on understanding both pollution levels and the local traffic behaviours that contribute to them. Wandsworth Borough Council used Eco Census cameras to combine traffic monitoring with real-time air-quality measurement in Putney, supporting its wider Air Quality Action Plan. The operational problem was that the council needed better evidence on pollutants such as nitrogen dioxide and particulate matter, including PM2.5, while also understanding how pedestrians, cyclists and motorists used the monitored streets. The implementation installed AI-enabled cameras and environmental monitoring equipment at key locations, producing data on traffic patterns and pollutant concentrations through a dedicated dashboard. At a conceptual level, the system combines computer vision, road-user classification, pollutant sensing and place-based analytics. This allows officials to evaluate long-term trends, assess policy interventions, understand road-layout impacts and support measures to reduce pollution exposure. The expected impact is better air-quality management, more evidence-based street design, improved safety for pedestrians and cyclists, and clearer progress tracking against local and national objectives. The use case shows how environmental monitoring and mobility analytics can be joined in one street-level system, helping a borough understand not only air quality outcomes but also the traffic conditions behind them.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1691,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01264,EU_PSTW_202609_01264,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,London Borough of Redbridge,General public services n.e.c.,"The objective is to streamline enquiries, reduce delays, and support faster planning decisions.",London Borough of Redbridge modernizes planning requests and services with Planbot,London Borough of Redbridge; Local Government; General public services n.e.c.; United Kingdom,"This use case addresses the need to simplify access to planning information and improve service efficiency, involving the borough council, customer support teams, applicants, and residents. Planbot is implemented as a mobile-friendly conversational assistant covering around 200 planning topics, connected to official guidance, forms, and documents.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Planning services in the London Borough of Redbridge handle complex regulations and high volumes of enquiries, often diverting specialist staff from higher-value work. Residents and developers require clear guidance on planning rules, applications, and documentation. This use case addresses the need to simplify access to planning information and improve service efficiency, involving the borough council, customer support teams, applicants, and residents. The objective is to streamline enquiries, reduce delays, and support faster planning decisions. Planbot is implemented as a mobile-friendly conversational assistant covering around 200 planning topics, connected to official guidance, forms, and documents. It supports both self-service use by the public and assisted use by customer support staff handling phone calls, ensuring consistent and accurate responses. The bot integrates with planning information systems and digital content repositories. As a result, routine questions are resolved more quickly, calls are handled more efficiently, and planning officers can focus on complex applications and strategic work. The solution improves user experience, shortens response times, and delivers operational savings, contributing to a more responsive and effective local planning service.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1692,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01265,EU_PSTW_202609_01265,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Dorset Council,"Agriculture, forestry, fishing and hunting","Aquaculture businesses need continuous insight into marine growing conditions, yet coastal environments can be difficult to monitor because connectivity is limited and underwater data collection is technically demanding. The expected impact is better monitoring of stock and growing conditions, reduced need for manual inspection, improved responsiveness to environmental changes and stronger evidence for low-carbon aquaculture development.",Dorset Council leverages 5G connectivity along the coast for Aquaculture Monitoring System,"Dorset Council; Local Government; Agriculture, forestry, fishing and hunting; United Kingdom",The implementation uses 5G-connected buoys made from sustainable materials and underwater cameras that can stream live 4K video from marine sites.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Aquaculture businesses need continuous insight into marine growing conditions, yet coastal environments can be difficult to monitor because connectivity is limited and underwater data collection is technically demanding. Dorset Council’s 5G RuralDorset programme supported an Aquaculture Monitoring System for Jurassic Sea Farms in Portland Harbour, developed with JET Engineering System Solutions. The objective is to help a growing seaweed and shellfish sector improve productivity, sustainability and operational decisions through real-time environmental intelligence. The implementation uses 5G-connected buoys made from sustainable materials and underwater cameras that can stream live 4K video from marine sites. Real-time data are then fed into an AI-enabled platform that can support recommendations on how to maximise cultivation conditions for seaweed and shellfish. At a conceptual level, the system combines coastal connectivity, sensor buoys, underwater visual monitoring, data transmission and analytics for aquaculture decision support. The expected impact is better monitoring of stock and growing conditions, reduced need for manual inspection, improved responsiveness to environmental changes and stronger evidence for low-carbon aquaculture development.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1693,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01266,EU_PSTW_202609_01266,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Bournemouth, Christchurch & Poole Councils",Waste management.,UNKNOWN,"Bournemouth, Christchurch & Poole Councils turn litter maps into localized engagement","Bournemouth, Christchurch & Poole Councils; Local Government; Waste management.; United Kingdom","Bournemouth, Christchurch and Poole Council worked with Ellipsis Earth and campaign partners to use AI-enabled litter mapping as the basis for localised engagement. The expected impact is more targeted litter reduction, improved use of council resources, stronger environmental protection and better engagement with residents, visitors and businesses.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Coastal destinations face intense litter pressure during visitor peaks, but effective interventions require knowing exactly what is being littered, where it accumulates and when behaviour changes. Bournemouth, Christchurch and Poole Council worked with Ellipsis Earth and campaign partners to use AI-enabled litter mapping as the basis for localised engagement. The operational problem was to move beyond general anti-litter messaging towards evidence-based action tailored to specific places and waste types. The implementation used drone and camera-based surveys analysed by AI to identify and classify litter items, generating detailed maps of plastic, cigarette butts, tissues and other waste patterns. At a conceptual level, the system converts images into geolocated litter intelligence, showing hotspots, item types and temporal patterns. This data then informs practical interventions such as bin placement, street-cleaning routes, signage and behaviour-change campaigns. The expected impact is more targeted litter reduction, improved use of council resources, stronger environmental protection and better engagement with residents, visitors and businesses. The case is significant because it links advanced image analysis with public participation: the data does not remain a technical map, but becomes the basis for local action and communication designed to change behaviour.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1694,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01267,EU_PSTW_202609_01267,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Derby City Council,General public services n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,Derby City Council embarks on next phase of using AI to streamline services,Derby City Council; Local Government; General public services n.e.c.; United Kingdom,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Local authorities face rising demand, financial pressure and complex service backlogs, especially in adult social care and customer contact. Derby City Council entered a four-year AI transformation partnership with ICS.AI to expand digital assistants and staff copilots across council services. The objective is to support staff in day-to-day work while keeping human officers responsible for decisions that require professional judgement. In adult social care, copilots are intended to help review care packages and assess whether residents receiving support at home have the right level of care. In customer services, Derby’s Darcie and Ali digital assistants are being upgraded and expanded to handle web-based and telephone enquiries, while AI tools also support email triage and response. At a conceptual level, the system combines conversational AI, generative assistants, human-in-the-loop workflow support, knowledge retrieval and service-specific process integration. The expected impact is faster responses, reduced call transfers, more staff time for complex cases and improved service resilience.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S1_KNOWLEDGE_RETRIEVAL_RAG,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1697,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01268,EU_PSTW_202609_01268,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Ministry of Labour and Social Policies,Social protection n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,Ministry of Labour and Social Policies chatbot,Ministry of Labour and Social Policies; Central-Government; Social protection n.e.c.; Italy,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The chatbot replies to general enquiries from citizens in relation to social policies, such as the ""bonus trasporti"" (Italian citizens could apply for a cash benefit of maximum 60 euros to buy transportation monthly passes).",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1700,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01269,EU_PSTW_202609_01269,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Aarhus Municipality,Waste management.,"Harbours and urban waterways collect floating waste that is difficult to remove continuously through manual cleaning alone. The expected impact is cleaner harbour water, reduced manual cleaning effort, greater public visibility of marine litter problems and a testbed for autonomous environmental robotics.",City Shark - Keeping Aarhus harbor clean with a robot,Aarhus Municipality; Local Government; Waste management.; Denmark,The use case demonstrates how relatively small autonomous systems can support municipal environmental services in constrained urban-water settings.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Harbours and urban waterways collect floating waste that is difficult to remove continuously through manual cleaning alone. Aarhus Municipality developed the City Shark project to test robotic and drone-based methods for keeping the harbour cleaner. The operational problem was to collect waste from the water surface efficiently while also exploring how autonomous technology could support environmental management. The core solution was a WasteShark, a sailing drone designed primarily to collect floating rubbish. The project was organised in phases by Aarhus Municipality’s ITK department in collaboration with the Agency for Data Supply and Efficiency, the Port of Aarhus, the City of Aarhus and external suppliers. At a conceptual level, the water drone navigates the harbour, collects waste from the surface and can be combined with other sensor or drone systems to detect pollution such as oil or petrol from the air and guide the water drone to the affected area. The expected impact is cleaner harbour water, reduced manual cleaning effort, greater public visibility of marine litter problems and a testbed for autonomous environmental robotics. The use case demonstrates how relatively small autonomous systems can support municipal environmental services in constrained urban-water settings.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1702,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01270,EU_PSTW_202609_01270,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Italian Central Bank,"General economic, commercial and labour affair",UNKNOWN,Italian central bank backs Decentralised Finance tokenisation project,"Italian Central Bank; Central-Government; General economic, commercial and labour affair; Italy",Its value is primarily exploratory: it gives public authorities and market participants a controlled setting to test opportunities and limits before any possible production-market use.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Institutional financial markets are exploring tokenisation, but regulated actors need evidence that digital-asset infrastructures can operate within existing supervisory, security and operational requirements. In Italy, the Bank of Italy’s Milano Hub selected an Institutional DeFi for Security Token ecosystem project promoted by Cetif Advisory, with partners including Fireblocks, Polygon Labs, Reply, Linklaters and DVRS. The initiative focuses on security tokens, understood as digital representations of ownership or rights over real-world assets, and examines how institutional players could access DeFi-like services while mitigating risks that would be unacceptable in retail or unregulated environments. The implementation is experimental and has no commercialisation purpose. At a conceptual level, it combines tokenised financial instruments, blockchain infrastructure, custody and security technology, legal structuring, compliance analysis and secondary-market workflows. The project extends analysis of how security tokens could circulate in institutional environments, including how regulated intermediaries might interact with decentralised infrastructures without losing control over identity, governance, investor protection and operational resilience. The expected impact is improved understanding of tokenised securities, clearer regulatory and technical requirements, and stronger Italian capacity to evaluate institutional digital-finance models. Its value is primarily exploratory: it gives public authorities and market participants a controlled setting to test opportunities and limits before any possible production-market use.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1703,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01271,EU_PSTW_202609_01271,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,ICI Bucharest (National Institute for Research and Development in Informatics),R&D education.,UNKNOWN,Romania plugs into Web3 with national NFT marketplace,ICI Bucharest (National Institute for Research and Development in Informatics); Central-Government; R&D education.; Romania,"While the platform’s long-term public value depends on sustained institutional use and careful governance, its launch marked a concrete move from policy interest to operational Web3 experimentation.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Public institutions are beginning to experiment with Web3 tools as a way to create new forms of digital interaction, cultural promotion and trusted asset management. Romania’s National Institute for Research and Development in Informatics, ICI Bucharest, launched ICI D|SERVICES as an institutional platform for managing and trading digital assets, with an initial focus on non-fungible tokens. The objective is to connect public institutions, private actors and users through a government-backed digital marketplace that can support minting, management and trading of NFTs. The implementation operates on decentralised blockchain and Web3 technologies and was presented as the first European institutional platform for trading digital assets. At a conceptual level, the platform combines NFT issuance, digital-asset management, blockchain-based ownership records, user accounts and institutional collections, enabling organisations to publish digital assets while users can verify provenance and ownership. Its expected impact is to promote Web3 adoption in Romania, support new models of cultural and institutional engagement, and test how public-sector organisations can participate in digital-asset ecosystems. The initiative also provides an experimental bridge between national research infrastructure, government-backed digital transformation and private-sector blockchain communities. While the platform’s long-term public value depends on sustained institutional use and careful governance, its launch marked a concrete move from policy interest to operational Web3 experimentation.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1704,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01272,EU_PSTW_202609_01272,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Agencia Tributaria,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.",UNKNOWN,Multiple chatbots on the spanish Agenzia Tributaria website,"Agencia Tributaria; Central-Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Spain","The use case is significant because it combines several forms of automated assistance, from natural-language virtual assistants to rule-based calculators, within a single tax-administration support environment.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Tax systems are procedurally complex, and citizens, companies and advisers often need interactive guidance on declarations, VAT, census obligations, collection, payment deadlines and classification of economic activities. Spain’s Agencia Tributaria provides a suite of virtual-assistance tools to help users answer common tax questions and complete or understand procedures. The operational problem is that many enquiries require structured interpretation of tax rules, but can still be handled through guided questions, calculators or virtual assistants before a user needs personalised support. The implementation includes virtual assistants, informants, economic-activity search tools, locators for goods and service transactions, real-estate transaction qualifiers, payment-term calculators, invoicing calculators, late-payment interest calculators and tools supporting VAT forms 303 and 390. At a conceptual level, these tools translate tax logic into interactive workflows: users provide relevant facts and the system returns guidance, classification or calculation results. The expected impact is improved taxpayer self-service, fewer avoidable errors, reduced demand for human assistance and greater consistency in first-level answers. The use case is significant because it combines several forms of automated assistance, from natural-language virtual assistants to rule-based calculators, within a single tax-administration support environment.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1705,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01273,EU_PSTW_202609_01273,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""social_nonprofit""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Rosealare,General public services n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,Chatbot - City of Rosealare,City of Rosealare; Local Government; General public services n.e.c.; Belgium,"This use case involves the municipal administration and citizens, with the objective of improving access to information and reducing pressure on staff. The solution is implemented as a general-purpose chatbot on the city’s website, designed to answer common questions using structured municipal information and predefined conversational flows.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The City of Roeselare receives frequent general enquiries from residents and visitors covering a wide range of municipal topics, from administrative services to local facilities. Managing these enquiries through traditional channels can be resource-intensive and limit availability outside office hours. This use case involves the municipal administration and citizens, with the objective of improving access to information and reducing pressure on staff. The solution is implemented as a general-purpose chatbot on the city’s website, designed to answer common questions using structured municipal information and predefined conversational flows. The chatbot is integrated with official content sources to ensure consistency and accuracy and acts as a first point of contact, guiding users to relevant information or services. The system improves responsiveness, provides immediate answers at any time, and reduces repetitive enquiries handled by staff. It enhances the overall digital experience for residents, supports more efficient use of municipal resources, and offers a scalable foundation for expanding digital citizen services over time.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1706,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01274,EU_PSTW_202609_01274,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Ministerium der Justiz und für Europa (JUM),Law courts,"In the German justice context, the project responds to the recurring need for rapid work translations across many languages, where in-house expertise may not be available and manual translation can be too slow or costly for initial case assessment.",Machine translation service of the justice system - Developing translation memory,Ministerium der Justiz und für Europa (JUM); Central-Government; Law courts; Germany,The implementation uses neural machine translation trained on translated texts relevant to the judicial environment.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Judicial authorities often need to understand foreign-language documents quickly, but legal translation is sensitive, time-critical and unsuitable for generic public translation platforms when quality, confidentiality and data protection are required. In the German justice context, the project responds to the recurring need for rapid work translations across many languages, where in-house expertise may not be available and manual translation can be too slow or costly for initial case assessment. The objective is to help justice staff search and understand foreign-language material more effectively, while preserving control over legal data. The implementation uses neural machine translation trained on translated texts relevant to the judicial environment. At a conceptual level, foreign-language documents are processed through a secure translation system that produces immediate work translations, enabling users to obtain an overview, search documents more precisely and decide whether certified or expert translation is needed. A further development goal is translation memory: texts already processed as input or output are recognised and reused, improving consistency and efficiency over time. In regular operation, the system is intended to shift towards a central translation platform. The expected impact is faster access to multilingual legal material, lower thresholds for preliminary translations, reduced dependence on external platforms and better support for time-critical judicial workflows. Because legal meaning is context-dependent, the service remains a support tool rather than a substitute for authoritative legal translation.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK|F10_CREATIVE_PRODUCTION_CAPACITY_BOTTLENECK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1708,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01275,EU_PSTW_202609_01275,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Information Flanders,Housing development.,"Identifying buildings with asbestos-containing roofing materials is essential for public health, environmental protection and safe renovation planning, yet manual inspections across large territories are costly and time-consuming.",Asbestos suspect roofs from aerial imagery,Information Flanders; Regional Government; Housing development.; Belgium,"The objective is to detect and map roofs likely to contain asbestos using remote sensing data. High-resolution aerial imagery is processed using convolutional neural networks trained on labelled roof samples to classify material types based on texture, colour and spectral characteristics.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Identifying buildings with asbestos-containing roofing materials is essential for public health, environmental protection and safe renovation planning, yet manual inspections across large territories are costly and time-consuming. Public authorities require scalable methods to prioritise inspections and remediation efforts. Stakeholders include environmental agencies, municipal authorities and property owners. The objective is to detect and map roofs likely to contain asbestos using remote sensing data. High-resolution aerial imagery is processed using convolutional neural networks trained on labelled roof samples to classify material types based on texture, colour and spectral characteristics. Geospatial analysis techniques integrate detection outputs into geographic information systems, generating risk maps and inspection priority lists. The approach enhances coverage, reduces field survey costs and supports evidence-based environmental policy, enabling proactive risk management and more efficient allocation of inspection resources.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1709,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01276,EU_PSTW_202609_01276,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,ISSeP,Public order and safety n.e.c.,Manual monitoring through CCTV systems can be labour-intensive and reactive.,Smart Airports: Object detection and pose estimation for airport activity monitoring,ISSeP; Academic-Research; Public order and safety n.e.c.; France,"The system processes video streams using deep learning-based object detection models to identify aircraft, service vehicles and ground equipment, combined with pose estimation algorithms to interpret movement patterns and spatial relationships.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Airports manage complex ground operations involving aircraft, vehicles, equipment and personnel, where situational awareness and safety are critical. Manual monitoring through CCTV systems can be labour-intensive and reactive. Stakeholders include airport operators, ground handling services and safety authorities. The objective is to enhance operational efficiency and safety through automated activity monitoring. The system processes video streams using deep learning-based object detection models to identify aircraft, service vehicles and ground equipment, combined with pose estimation algorithms to interpret movement patterns and spatial relationships. Detected events are integrated into airport operational dashboards and alert systems, supporting real-time decision-making and post-event analysis. Expected impacts include improved safety compliance, faster incident detection, optimisation of ground handling processes and data-driven performance management across airport operations.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1710,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01277,EU_PSTW_202609_01277,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,ETH ZURICH,Social protection n.e.c.,Conventional census-based methods may lack timeliness or granularity.,Mapping Vulnerable Populations with AI,ETH ZURICH; Academic-Research; Social protection n.e.c.; Switzerland,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Policymakers require accurate, up-to-date insights into the spatial distribution of vulnerable populations to design targeted social, health and disaster response interventions. Conventional census-based methods may lack timeliness or granularity. Stakeholders include public authorities, social services and humanitarian organisations. The objective is to generate high-resolution vulnerability maps to support evidence-based policy and resource allocation. The approach integrates satellite imagery, demographic data and socio-economic indicators, applying machine learning models such as supervised classifiers and clustering algorithms to infer vulnerability proxies at fine spatial scales. Outputs are incorporated into geographic information systems and policy dashboards, enabling scenario analysis and targeted intervention planning. Anticipated benefits include improved targeting of public services, enhanced disaster preparedness and more equitable allocation of resources, supported by scalable data processing pipelines.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1711,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01278,EU_PSTW_202609_01278,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,KULeuven,Protection of biodiversity and landscape.,Traditional field surveys are resource-intensive and limited in spatial coverage.,Deep learning-based mapping of tree density and species diversity in Flemish forests,KULeuven; Academic-Research; Protection of biodiversity and landscape.; Belgium,The solution processes multispectral and hyperspectral Earth observation imagery using convolutional neural networks trained on labelled forest plots to estimate tree density and classify species composition.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Forest managers require accurate, large-scale and up-to-date information on tree density and species diversity to support biodiversity conservation, climate adaptation strategies and sustainable timber management. Traditional field surveys are resource-intensive and limited in spatial coverage. Stakeholders include regional environmental authorities, forest agencies, researchers and land managers. The objective is to generate high-resolution forest structure and species diversity maps across Flemish territories to inform policy and operational planning. The solution processes multispectral and hyperspectral Earth observation imagery using convolutional neural networks trained on labelled forest plots to estimate tree density and classify species composition. Feature extraction techniques capture spectral, textural and structural patterns, while geospatial processing pipelines integrate outputs into geographic information systems for analysis and reporting. Expected impacts include broader spatial coverage than field surveys, reduced monitoring costs, improved ecological modelling and more precise targeting of conservation measures, supporting evidence-based forest governance.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1712,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01279,EU_PSTW_202609_01279,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Universite Liège,Protection of biodiversity and landscape.,"Detecting objects and land features in satellite imagery often requires extensive labelled datasets, which are costly and time-consuming to produce. Limited annotated data can constrain model performance and scalability.",Semi-Supervised Training to Improve Detection for Satellite Images,Universite Liège; Academic-Research; Protection of biodiversity and landscape.; Belgium,"The approach combines supervised learning on annotated satellite images with semi-supervised techniques that leverage large volumes of unlabelled data. Anticipated benefits include improved detection performance, reduced labelling costs and faster deployment of monitoring applications across domains such as land use, infrastructure and environmental change.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Detecting objects and land features in satellite imagery often requires extensive labelled datasets, which are costly and time-consuming to produce. Limited annotated data can constrain model performance and scalability. Stakeholders include Earth observation analysts, public authorities and environmental monitoring agencies. The objective is to enhance detection accuracy while reducing dependency on fully labelled datasets. The approach combines supervised learning on annotated satellite images with semi-supervised techniques that leverage large volumes of unlabelled data. Methods such as pseudo-labelling, consistency regularisation and iterative retraining are applied to improve model generalisation. Detection models, typically based on deep convolutional architectures, are integrated into Earth observation processing workflows and evaluated against benchmark datasets. Anticipated benefits include improved detection performance, reduced labelling costs and faster deployment of monitoring applications across domains such as land use, infrastructure and environmental change.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1713,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01280,EU_PSTW_202609_01280,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,VITO,R&D environmental protection.,UNKNOWN,AI-based compression on Sentinel 2 data,VITO; Academic-Research; R&D environmental protection.; Netherlands,"The solution applies machine learning-based compression models, including autoencoder architectures, to learn compact feature representations of Sentinel-2 imagery.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The increasing volume of high-resolution satellite imagery places pressure on onboard storage, downlink bandwidth and ground processing infrastructure. Conventional image compression techniques may reduce file size but can limit downstream analytical value. Stakeholders include space agencies, data service providers and Earth observation users. The objective is to develop efficient onboard data reduction methods that preserve information relevant for analysis while reducing transmission requirements. The solution applies machine learning-based compression models, including autoencoder architectures, to learn compact feature representations of Sentinel-2 imagery. Additional onboard inference capabilities, such as cloud detection and information extraction, further reduce unnecessary data transmission. The compressed representations retain meaningful features suitable for lightweight downstream applications with reduced training data requirements. Expected impacts include lower data transmission costs, improved onboard processing efficiency and enhanced scalability of satellite missions without compromising analytical performance.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1714,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01281,EU_PSTW_202609_01281,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Spacebel,Protection of biodiversity and landscape.,"Monitoring forest ecosystems at individual tree level is essential for biodiversity assessment, carbon accounting and precision forestry, yet conventional surveys lack scalability.",IADAS: project to identify individual tree and tree species based on EO and AI,Spacebel; Academic-Research; Protection of biodiversity and landscape.; Belgium,The objective is to identify and classify individual trees and their species using Earth observation data.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Monitoring forest ecosystems at individual tree level is essential for biodiversity assessment, carbon accounting and precision forestry, yet conventional surveys lack scalability. Stakeholders include environmental authorities, forestry services and research institutions. The objective is to identify and classify individual trees and their species using Earth observation data. The system integrates high-resolution satellite and aerial imagery with deep learning models for object detection and classification. Instance segmentation techniques isolate individual tree crowns, while classification networks assign species labels based on spectral and structural features. Outputs are georeferenced and incorporated into forest management systems, enabling detailed inventory mapping and change detection. Anticipated benefits include more granular forest inventories, improved carbon stock estimation and enhanced monitoring of ecosystem health, supporting data-driven environmental policy and sustainable land management.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1715,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01282,EU_PSTW_202609_01282,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,LIST,Public order and safety n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,Floodwater detection in urban areas powered by AI and SAR data,LIST; Academic-Research; Public order and safety n.e.c.; Luxembourg,"The objective is to detect and map floodwater in near real time using synthetic aperture radar (SAR) data, which is resilient to weather conditions.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Rapid and accurate detection of urban flooding is critical for emergency response, infrastructure protection and disaster risk management. Optical imagery is often hindered by cloud cover during extreme weather events. Stakeholders include civil protection authorities, municipalities and urban planners. The objective is to detect and map floodwater in near real time using synthetic aperture radar (SAR) data, which is resilient to weather conditions. The approach employs deep learning segmentation models trained on labelled SAR datasets to distinguish floodwater from urban surfaces, incorporating backscatter intensity and spatial texture features. Post-processing techniques refine boundaries and integrate outputs into geospatial platforms for emergency coordination. Expected impacts include faster situational awareness, improved targeting of response resources and enhanced resilience planning in flood-prone urban areas.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1716,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01283,EU_PSTW_202609_01283,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Ministero Economia e Finanze,"General economic, commercial and labour affair",UNKNOWN,AI for Ethically Responsible Big Open Data (ETAPAS Case 1),"Ministero Economia e Finanze; Central-Government; General economic, commercial and labour affair; Italy","The ETAPAS NoiPA use case examines these risks in relation to Italy’s NoiPA platform, which provides HR shared services to around 100 central and local administrations and is used by approximately 2.2 million civil servants. The implementation applies the ETAPAS responsible-disruptive-technology framework to NoiPA open-data publication processes, focusing on privacy, fairness, transparency and risk management.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Publishing open datasets from public administration can create transparency and research value, but it can also expose privacy, fairness and re-identification risks when datasets are sensitive or can be combined with other information. The ETAPAS NoiPA use case examines these risks in relation to Italy’s NoiPA platform, which provides HR shared services to around 100 central and local administrations and is used by approximately 2.2 million civil servants. The objective is to identify ethical issues, legal risks and possible negative impacts linked to the analysis and publication of anonymised open datasets, then define mitigation actions consistent with the GDPR and public-sector accountability. The implementation applies the ETAPAS responsible-disruptive-technology framework to NoiPA open-data publication processes, focusing on privacy, fairness, transparency and risk management. At a conceptual level, the use case analyses how dataset combinations, proxy variables, biased sampling or apparently anonymised aggregates could reveal personal information or discriminatory patterns. The expected impact is a more robust procedure for publishing open data, stronger safeguards against re-identification, and better institutional awareness of ethical risks in public-data analytics. The use case is particularly relevant because it treats open-data governance not as a purely technical anonymisation task, but as an organisational process requiring continuous assessment, mitigation and documentation.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1717,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01284,EU_PSTW_202609_01284,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,University of Trento,General services.,"Synthetic and manipulated images can support fraud, harassment, disinformation and other harms, especially when they circulate rapidly on social platforms and users lack tools to assess provenance.",TrueBee (Digital forensics and blockchain tech against deceptive deepfake imaging in social media),University of Trento; Consortium; General services.; Italy,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Synthetic and manipulated images can support fraud, harassment, disinformation and other harms, especially when they circulate rapidly on social platforms and users lack tools to assess provenance. TrueBees was created to help distinguish trustworthy images from deceptive deepfake or AI-generated visual content by combining forensic analysis with blockchain-based traceability. The project originated in 2022 through collaboration between U-Hopper and researchers from the University of Trento, with support from TruBlo, an EU-funded Next Generation Internet initiative. The implementation combines an AI module based on image-forensics methods with a blockchain registry. Users can upload an image through a web interface, receive a TrueBees score indicating trustworthiness, and have the image hash and corresponding score stored on a blockchain. At a conceptual level, the system links media-forensic classification, provenance recording, immutable timestamping and consultation of prior assessments. The expected impact is improved detection of deceptive images, stronger evidential trails for journalists or fact-checkers, and more trust in media verification workflows. The project white paper reports a prototype architecture and cites high classification performance, including a 97.99% figure in the publicly indexed summary, while the current TrueBees website presents the work as moving from research into a practical solution for AI-generated image detection.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S7_GENERATIVE_CONTENT_CREATION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1718,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01285,EU_PSTW_202609_01285,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Forensic Science Centre of Lithuania,Law courts,Manual analysis can delay investigations and obscure network structures.,"ROXANNE – Real-Time Network, Text and Speaker Analytics for Combating Organised Crime",Forensic Science Centre of Lithuania; Central-Government; Law courts; Lithuania,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Law enforcement agencies investigating organised and cross-border crime face large volumes of multilingual communications, multimedia content and complex relational data. Manual analysis can delay investigations and obscure network structures. Stakeholders include law enforcement authorities, judicial bodies and security agencies. The objective is to support the investigation and mitigation of organised crime through integrated analytical capabilities. The platform combines speech recognition, speaker identification, natural language processing and social network analysis to extract entities, detect relationships and generate visual maps of criminal networks. Interoperability mechanisms enable integration with existing investigative tools and databases. Expected impacts include accelerated investigations, reduced workload per case, improved identification of cross-border networks and enhanced analytical consistency within a defined legal framework.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1719,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01286,EU_PSTW_202609_01286,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,EUIPO,Communication,The operational problem is that manual image comparison and Vienna Classification searches can be time-consuming and may miss visually similar marks where keywords or classifications are incomplete.,EUIPO eSearch Plus,EUIPO; Central-Government; Communication; Spain,"The implementation uses AI-based image-recognition technology to analyse uploaded or selected images and return visually similar trade marks or designs, which users can combine with criteria such as Nice and Vienna classifications.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Trade mark clearance depends on identifying earlier similar marks, including figurative marks whose similarity may arise from shapes, colours, textures and visual composition rather than text alone. EUIPO’s eSearch Plus image-search functionality supports users searching for similar-looking trade marks and designs in the European Union Intellectual Property Office’s databases. The operational problem is that manual image comparison and Vienna Classification searches can be time-consuming and may miss visually similar marks where keywords or classifications are incomplete. The implementation uses AI-based image-recognition technology to analyse uploaded or selected images and return visually similar trade marks or designs, which users can combine with criteria such as Nice and Vienna classifications. At a conceptual level, the system extracts visual features from an image, compares them with stored intellectual-property images and ranks results by similarity. The expected impact is more efficient trade mark clearance, better support for applicants and representatives, improved monitoring of potentially conflicting marks and stronger quality in IP-search workflows. The tool does not make legal decisions about likelihood of confusion or registrability; it supports human users by surfacing relevant prior rights and visual similarities for further legal assessment.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1720,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01287,EU_PSTW_202609_01287,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Digital Europe Programme (DIGITAL),"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.","The operational objective is to help drafters avoid avoidable errors, produce clearer legislation and reduce the administrative burden associated with post-publication corrections.",LEOS Case Study 1: Learning from examining corrigenda,"Digital Europe Programme (DIGITAL); Central-Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Belgium","The implementation uses legal-text analysis and hybrid AI methods applied to corpora of EU legislation and corrigenda, with particular attention to pattern detection and visualisation. The case is part of a wider European Commission study on smart functionalities for LEOS, which piloted four use cases including corrigenda analysis, transposition, derogations and digital readiness.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Legislative corrigenda reveal recurring drafting, formatting or reference problems that can undermine legal clarity and create costs when acts must be corrected and republished. This LEOS case study explored whether patterns in corrigenda could be identified and used to improve future legal drafting. The operational objective is to help drafters avoid avoidable errors, produce clearer legislation and reduce the administrative burden associated with post-publication corrections. The implementation uses legal-text analysis and hybrid AI methods applied to corpora of EU legislation and corrigenda, with particular attention to pattern detection and visualisation. At a conceptual level, the system clusters corrigenda, identifies recurring error types and translates those findings into possible smart functionalities for the LEOS legal drafting editor. These functionalities could warn drafters about risky structures, problematic references or repeated drafting patterns before publication. The expected impact is better regulation, clearer legislative texts, lower republication costs and faster availability of updated legislation. The case is part of a wider European Commission study on smart functionalities for LEOS, which piloted four use cases including corrigenda analysis, transposition, derogations and digital readiness. Public material describes the work as proof-of-concept research rather than a fully integrated production feature available across all EU legal drafting workflows.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F08_ERROR_COMPLIANCE_RISK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1721,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01288,EU_PSTW_202609_01288,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Digital Europe Programme (DIGITAL),"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.",UNKNOWN,LEOS Case 2: Transposition of EU directives,"Digital Europe Programme (DIGITAL); Central-Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Belgium","The implementation uses legal-text processing, similarity analysis, document comparison and visualisation tools within the broader LEOS smart-functionality framework.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Transposing EU directives into national law is legally complex because Member States must ensure that domestic provisions correctly reflect EU obligations while adapting them to national legal structures. This LEOS case study examines how AI-supported analysis could document and compare transposition more systematically. The pilot focuses on comparing selected directives with Italian domestic legislation to identify relationships between articles and detect where texts converge, diverge or omit relevant obligations. The implementation uses legal-text processing, similarity analysis, document comparison and visualisation tools within the broader LEOS smart-functionality framework. At a conceptual level, the system represents EU and national provisions, measures semantic or structural relationships between them, and highlights possible correspondences or gaps for legal experts to review. The expected impact is faster transposition analysis, improved documentation of legal implementation, better support for consolidation work and reduced risk that obligations are overlooked. Such functionality would not replace legal assessment, since transposition depends on legal interpretation, institutional context and national drafting conventions. Its value is to make comparison more systematic and navigable, helping experts focus on legally meaningful divergences. The European Commission’s LEOS smart-functionality study presents this case as one of four pilots exploring how hybrid AI can enhance legislative drafting and analysis.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1722,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01289,EU_PSTW_202609_01289,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Digital Europe Programme (DIGITAL),"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.",The operational problem is that drafters and users may struggle to trace the relationship between a general rule and exceptions that appear elsewhere in a legal act or in related legislation.,LEOS Case 3: Derogations and transitory provisions,"Digital Europe Programme (DIGITAL); Central-Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Belgium","Derogations and transitional provisions are essential in legislation, but they can make legal obligations difficult to understand because they modify when, where or under what conditions a rule applies. The implementation uses AI classification, legal annotation and structured analysis of normative text.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Derogations and transitional provisions are essential in legislation, but they can make legal obligations difficult to understand because they modify when, where or under what conditions a rule applies. This LEOS case study explores how classification and legal-text analysis can help identify derogations, connect them to the obligations they modify, and extract temporal or conditional parameters. The operational problem is that drafters and users may struggle to trace the relationship between a general rule and exceptions that appear elsewhere in a legal act or in related legislation. The implementation uses AI classification, legal annotation and structured analysis of normative text. At a conceptual level, the system detects clauses that create derogations or transitional regimes, identifies the initial obligations affected, and extracts information such as dates, durations, conditions and applicability criteria. These outputs can then support drafting review, legal navigation and clearer presentation of exceptions. The expected impact is improved legal clarity, fewer interpretative errors, better traceability of temporal rules and stronger support for digital legal services. For policy and drafting teams, the value is not automatic legal interpretation but earlier visibility of how exceptions interact with obligations. The use case was included in the European Commission’s LEOS smart-functionality study alongside pilots on corrigenda, transposition and digital readiness.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1723,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01290,EU_PSTW_202609_01290,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Digital Europe Programme (DIGITAL),"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.","The proof of concept has three linked goals: calculate a digital-readiness index for a document, assess how normative citations and definitions propagate through the legal system, and develop a predictive model that estimates the index of new text fragments during drafting.",LEOS Case 4: Checking for digital readiness,"Digital Europe Programme (DIGITAL); Central-Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Belgium","The implementation uses legal-text analysis, citation analysis, modelling of definitions and predictive scoring.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Digital-ready legislation is increasingly important because laws and policies must be implementable through digital-by-default public services, data exchange and automated administrative processes. This LEOS case study investigates how smart drafting support could assess whether a legal act enables smooth digital implementation. The operational problem is that digital readiness is often assessed late or inconsistently, even though legislative wording can determine whether services can be automated, data can be reused and citizens can interact digitally with administrations. The proof of concept has three linked goals: calculate a digital-readiness index for a document, assess how normative citations and definitions propagate through the legal system, and develop a predictive model that estimates the index of new text fragments during drafting. The implementation uses legal-text analysis, citation analysis, modelling of definitions and predictive scoring. At a conceptual level, the system examines how a draft refers to other norms, how definitions affect implementation, and whether textual features support or hinder digital administration. The expected impact is earlier identification of provisions that may block digital-by-default services, better alignment between legal drafting and digital policy implementation, and more actionable feedback for drafters. The European Commission’s later LEOS work frames these cases as part of a broader move towards AI-based microservices that assist lawyers and policy developers while preserving human legal control.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1724,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01291,EU_PSTW_202609_01291,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,AOC Consorci Administració Oberta de Catalunya,General services.,"Digital public services require reliable remote identification, but traditional identity enrolment can depend on face-to-face registration that is inconvenient for citizens and difficult to scale. The expected impact is easier access to digital identity credentials, reduced need for physical registration, improved inclusion for users who cannot attend offices and stronger uptake of digital public services.",Facial and official document recognition to deliver digital identities,AOC Consorci Administració Oberta de Catalunya; Regional Government; General services.; Spain,"The Open Administration of Catalonia developed video-identification capabilities for obtaining digital identity credentials such as idCAT, using facial recognition and official-document recognition. The case is relevant because it applies biometric matching in a controlled enrolment process, rather than in open-ended public-space surveillance.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Digital public services require reliable remote identification, but traditional identity enrolment can depend on face-to-face registration that is inconvenient for citizens and difficult to scale. The Open Administration of Catalonia developed video-identification capabilities for obtaining digital identity credentials such as idCAT, using facial recognition and official-document recognition. The operational problem is to verify that the person applying remotely is the legitimate holder of the identity document and that the document is genuine, while maintaining security and privacy guarantees equivalent to in-person processes. The implementation asks the user to show both sides of an identity document, performs document checks, captures a selfie and compares the facial image with the image extracted from the document. The process also includes mobile-phone verification and later review by a trained operator. At a conceptual level, the system combines document image analysis, facial comparison, liveness or video evidence, consent capture, audit recording and human validation. The expected impact is easier access to digital identity credentials, reduced need for physical registration, improved inclusion for users who cannot attend offices and stronger uptake of digital public services. The case is relevant because it applies biometric matching in a controlled enrolment process, rather than in open-ended public-space surveillance.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1725,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01292,EU_PSTW_202609_01292,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,AOC Consorci Administració Oberta de Catalunya,Social protection n.e.c.,"This legal safeguard is essential for vulnerable citizens, but the administrative process is burdensome: utility companies send monthly lists in different formats, and municipal staff must manually check whether each person or household is at social or economic risk. The expected impact is shorter processing time, fewer manual checks, more consistent treatment of households and better prevention of unlawful supply cuts.",Automating the energy poverty report,AOC Consorci Administració Oberta de Catalunya; Regional Government; Social protection n.e.c.; Spain,"AOC developed a pilot cloud service to standardise and automate the preparation of energy-vulnerability reports, improving interoperability between suppliers, municipalities and other administrations.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Energy-poverty protection in Catalonia requires municipalities to determine whether households with unpaid utility bills are socially vulnerable before suppliers can cut off gas, water or electricity. This legal safeguard is essential for vulnerable citizens, but the administrative process is burdensome: utility companies send monthly lists in different formats, and municipal staff must manually check whether each person or household is at social or economic risk. AOC developed a pilot cloud service to standardise and automate the preparation of energy-vulnerability reports, improving interoperability between suppliers, municipalities and other administrations. The implementation validates and cross-checks data, manages requests through a single point and helps municipal social services process supplier consultations more efficiently. At a conceptual level, the system combines data reception, identity or case matching, rule-based verification, workflow automation and administrative interoperability to produce or support the required report. The expected impact is shorter processing time, fewer manual checks, more consistent treatment of households and better prevention of unlawful supply cuts. AOC reports that the pilot began in 2023 in the social-services areas of El Prat de Llobregat, Granollers and Amposta and validated technical and organisational feasibility in a real environment. The initiative’s public value is significant because it converts a rights-protection obligation into a more scalable and less error-prone administrative process.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,S6_WORKFLOW_PROCESS_AUTOMATION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1726,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01293,EU_PSTW_202609_01293,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Ministry of Justice Austria,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.",UNKNOWN,JustizOnline,"Ministry of Justice Austria; Central-Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Austria",UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Access to justice can be hindered when citizens and organisations must physically attend court offices or navigate several separate portals to find information, forms and register services. JustizOnline was created as Austria’s digital gateway to the judiciary, bringing together online information, procedural services and access to registers in a single service environment. The operational objective is to reduce unnecessary court visits and help users find relevant functions more quickly, whether they need information on proceedings, court services, forms, the Companies Register, the Land Register or judicial announcements. The implementation includes the chatbot “Justitia”, which guides users to the relevant information and can answer simple justice-related questions. At a conceptual level, the platform combines digital self-service, secure authentication, linked e-government interfaces, register access and conversational navigation. Integration with other Austrian e-government portals reduces duplication by allowing representation and authorisation information to be managed consistently across connected services. The expected impact is easier access to justice, faster completion of routine interactions, fewer administrative barriers and more efficient communication between the judiciary and the public. JustizOnline is important because it treats digital justice as a service ecosystem rather than a single online form, supporting both information needs and transactional access to judicial services.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1727,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01294,EU_PSTW_202609_01294,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Provincia Autonoma di Trento,Public order and safety n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,AI for Weather Forecast in Trentino,Provincia Autonoma di Trento; Regional Government; Public order and safety n.e.c.; Italy,"In Trentino, the weather-forecasting AI use case focused on supporting MeteoTrentino and the Autonomous Province of Trento by improving the validation and use of weather-station data. The use case is particularly relevant for mountain territories, where storms can develop rapidly and localised data quality is essential for timely action.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Local authorities need reliable warning systems for storms and fast-developing weather events because short lead times can affect emergency management, road safety, civil protection and public communication. In Trentino, the weather-forecasting AI use case focused on supporting MeteoTrentino and the Autonomous Province of Trento by improving the validation and use of weather-station data. The operational problem is that meteorological networks collect large volumes of real-time data from stations across the territory, but missing values, anomalous measurements and inconsistencies between nearby or similar-altitude stations can reduce the quality of warnings and forecasts. The implementation approach used artificial intelligence to identify errors or anomalies in weather data and improve the quality of archived and operational datasets. At a conceptual level, the system compares real-time station values with climatology, neighbouring stations and expected physical patterns, flagging values that may require correction or review before being used in alerts. The expected impact is more reliable weather warnings, better support for local administrators, improved emergency notifications and stronger data quality for climate and weather services. The use case is particularly relevant for mountain territories, where storms can develop rapidly and localised data quality is essential for timely action.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1728,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01295,EU_PSTW_202609_01295,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Provincia Autonoma di Trento,Protection of biodiversity and landscape.,"In Trentino, the Public AI Challenge applied artificial intelligence to the Careser Glacier in the Dolomites to reconstruct and forecast water flows towards reservoirs located downhill. The case is also relevant because it shows how an open-innovation challenge can introduce AI methods into provincial administration without requiring a full internal AI development team.",AI for Monitoring Glacier water flows,Provincia Autonoma di Trento; Regional Government; Protection of biodiversity and landscape.; Italy,"The expected impact is improved continuity of hydrological monitoring, better evidence for water-resource planning and stronger public-sector capacity to use existing environmental datasets.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Mountain water management depends on understanding how glacier melt contributes to downstream reservoirs, especially as climate change alters snow, ice and seasonal runoff dynamics. In Trentino, the Public AI Challenge applied artificial intelligence to the Careser Glacier in the Dolomites to reconstruct and forecast water flows towards reservoirs located downhill. The operational problem is that environmental monitoring datasets can contain gaps, missing measurements or discontinuities, making it harder for public authorities to understand hydrological conditions and anticipate changes in water availability. The implementation used dense neural-network techniques to estimate and reconstruct missing flow-rate data by learning relationships between available observations and expected water-flow patterns. At a conceptual level, the model supports prediction-measurement comparison: where sensor or historical data are incomplete, the neural network can infer plausible values and help reconstruct the hydrological series needed for environmental assessment. This creates a decision-support layer for monitoring glacier-fed systems, reservoir inflows and broader environmental status. The expected impact is improved continuity of hydrological monitoring, better evidence for water-resource planning and stronger public-sector capacity to use existing environmental datasets. The case is also relevant because it shows how an open-innovation challenge can introduce AI methods into provincial administration without requiring a full internal AI development team. Trentino Innovation describes the Public AI Challenge as a mechanism through which students develop predictive models and intelligent systems for public-administration needs.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1729,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01296,EU_PSTW_202609_01296,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Provincia Autonoma di Trento,Waste water management.,"The operational problem is that treatment plants consume significant energy, and rainfall-driven changes in inflow can create operational peaks that are expensive or inefficient if not anticipated.",AI for Water in Garda Lake area,Provincia Autonoma di Trento; Regional Government; Waste water management.; Italy,"The Trentino / Garda-area AI for Water use case involved NIRIS and AI Challenge participants working on data from public water-treatment infrastructure to forecast incoming water variations using historical plant data and meteorological information. At a conceptual level, the system learns from past inflow, weather and operational patterns to forecast future water loads, enabling plant managers to prepare equipment and optimise energy use.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Wastewater-treatment and water-infrastructure operators need to manage variable inflows while controlling energy consumption, maintenance risks and service continuity. The Trentino / Garda-area AI for Water use case involved NIRIS and AI Challenge participants working on data from public water-treatment infrastructure to forecast incoming water variations using historical plant data and meteorological information. The operational problem is that treatment plants consume significant energy, and rainfall-driven changes in inflow can create operational peaks that are expensive or inefficient if not anticipated. The implementation used historical purifier data, weather data from the Stenico area and machine-learning methods to identify correlations between meteorological conditions, anthropic impact and plant behaviour. At a conceptual level, the system learns from past inflow, weather and operational patterns to forecast future water loads, enabling plant managers to prepare equipment and optimise energy use. The expected impact is lower energy consumption, better remote management, improved predictive maintenance and more resilient operation of depuration infrastructure. The use case is notable because it applies AI to a highly practical utility-management problem, where small percentage savings can matter because water-treatment systems operate continuously and at high energy intensity across many plants.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1730,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01297,EU_PSTW_202609_01297,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Provincia Autonoma di Trento,Cultural services.,"Large cultural collections can become difficult to preserve and make accessible when the volume of material exceeds the capacity of manual cataloguing teams. The administrative challenge is to count, catalogue, classify, preserve, assess image quality and prepare selected materials for public exposure without imposing unsustainable labour requirements on cultural staff.",Fondo Caproni Project (AI for Culture),Provincia Autonoma di Trento; Regional Government; Cultural services.; Italy,"The implementation explored the use of artificial intelligence to automate parts of the catalogue-building process, with particular emphasis on image-quality assessment and primary thematic classification. Provincial material also confirms that the wider Caproni archive is being digitalised using PNRR funding, making the AI challenge part of a larger cultural-heritage digitisation trajectory.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Large cultural collections can become difficult to preserve and make accessible when the volume of material exceeds the capacity of manual cataloguing teams. The Caproni Collection contains approximately 200,000 historic photographs and aviation-related assets, including valuable images, memorabilia and documentation linked to Italian aeronautical history. The administrative challenge is to count, catalogue, classify, preserve, assess image quality and prepare selected materials for public exposure without imposing unsustainable labour requirements on cultural staff. The implementation explored the use of artificial intelligence to automate parts of the catalogue-building process, with particular emphasis on image-quality assessment and primary thematic classification. At a conceptual level, computer-vision models can identify damaged, blurred or low-quality photographs, group images into broad themes, support metadata generation and prioritise items requiring human review or conservation attention. This does not remove curatorial responsibility, but it can shift expert effort towards validation, interpretation and public presentation. The expected impact is faster digitisation, improved quality control, better searchability and broader public access to the Caproni heritage. Provincial material also confirms that the wider Caproni archive is being digitalised using PNRR funding, making the AI challenge part of a larger cultural-heritage digitisation trajectory. The case demonstrates how AI can support public cultural institutions when archive scale makes purely manual processes impractical.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1731,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01298,EU_PSTW_202609_01298,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Lambda Automata,UNKNOWN,"Remote island monitoring requires persistent situational awareness across land, sea and air, but continuous human surveillance can be costly, difficult and operationally limited. The operational problem is to detect and track diverse possible threats, including people, drones, vessels, aircraft, vehicles and smoke columns, across locations where infrastructure and personnel may be limited.",‘Outpost’ autonomous towers to monitor Greek islands for threats,Lambda Automata; Private sector; Greece,"The implementation uses autonomous towers equipped with sensors and artificial-intelligence software for geolocation, classification and tracking. The use case is security-focused and therefore distinct from civilian smart-city monitoring, but it illustrates a broader pattern in public-sector AI: distributed sensor infrastructure combined with automated classification to provide decision support in environments where rapid detection matters.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Remote island monitoring requires persistent situational awareness across land, sea and air, but continuous human surveillance can be costly, difficult and operationally limited. “Outpost” is an autonomous surveillance tower concept developed by Lambda Automata for monitoring Greek islands and other remote or sensitive areas. The operational problem is to detect and track diverse possible threats, including people, drones, vessels, aircraft, vehicles and smoke columns, across locations where infrastructure and personnel may be limited. The implementation uses autonomous towers equipped with sensors and artificial-intelligence software for geolocation, classification and tracking. At a conceptual level, the system observes the surrounding environment, processes sensor inputs, classifies detected objects or events and supports operators with alerts or situational information. The expected impact is stronger border and island surveillance, earlier detection of threats, reduced reliance on continuous manual observation and improved response coordination. The use case is security-focused and therefore distinct from civilian smart-city monitoring, but it illustrates a broader pattern in public-sector AI: distributed sensor infrastructure combined with automated classification to provide decision support in environments where rapid detection matters. It also raises typical governance issues around surveillance, oversight, proportionality and the human role in interpreting alerts.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1732,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01299,EU_PSTW_202609_01299,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Italian Department of Public Security,Public order and safety n.e.c.,"Traditional investigative tools struggle to cope with this complexity, leading to lengthy investigations and fragmented insights.",I-CAN Police Crime Predictive System against Ndrangheta Network Crime,Italian Department of Public Security; Central-Government; Public order and safety n.e.c.; Italy,"I-CAN is operational as an INTERPOL cooperation framework, with tools progressively deployed to support investigations.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Investigating organised and transnational criminal networks such as the ’Ndrangheta requires law enforcement agencies to analyse vast volumes of heterogeneous data, including communications, financial records, multimedia material and relational information. Traditional investigative tools struggle to cope with this complexity, leading to lengthy investigations and fragmented insights. INTERPOL launched the I-CAN initiative to strengthen the analytical capacity of police forces involved in combating Italian mafia-type organisations, supporting national and cross-border investigations. Stakeholders include INTERPOL, national police forces, judicial authorities and specialised organised crime units. The objective is to accelerate investigations, improve detection of hidden criminal relationships and enhance international cooperation. The system builds on technologies developed in EU-funded research projects such as ROXANNE, integrating network analysis, multilingual text and speech processing, and video analysis into a single interoperable platform. Data from multiple investigative sources are correlated to generate visual network graphs, behavioural patterns and relationship mappings, while remaining compatible with existing law enforcement databases and legal safeguards. I-CAN is operational as an INTERPOL cooperation framework, with tools progressively deployed to support investigations. Reported impacts include faster case analysis, improved situational awareness, reduced investigative workload per case and stronger cross-border intelligence sharing, contributing to more effective disruption of organised crime networks.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1733,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01300,EU_PSTW_202609_01300,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Venezia Municipality,Public order and safety n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,Venice Smart Control Room,Venezia Municipality; Local Government; Public order and safety n.e.c.; Italy,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Managing Venice requires coordination across pedestrian flows, water traffic, road access, tourism pressure, public transport, weather, tides, security and parking. The Venice Smart Control Room was developed as an integrated urban “control tower” to aggregate information from sensors, video feeds, municipal systems and service operators. The operational problem is that Venice’s urban mobility is unusually complex: boats, pedestrians, road transport and tourist flows interact with weather and tidal risks in a fragile historic environment. The implementation, developed by the Municipality of Venice with TIM and local public-safety stakeholders, brings together data on people present in the city, types of boats in canals, water and road transport movement, tourist flows, forecasts and parking. At a conceptual level, the platform combines sensor networks, camera feeds, telecommunications data, urban analytics and predictive modelling to support monitoring and decision-making. It can help detect mobility issues, identify congestion, support public safety and provide evidence for planning. The expected impact is improved city management, faster operational coordination, better regulation of water and land mobility, and more informed decisions during high-pressure events. The case is significant because it combines heritage-city governance with smart-city operations in a territory where urban management has both everyday and exceptional-risk dimensions.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1734,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01301,EU_PSTW_202609_01301,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Accademia Belle Arti - Roma,Education not definable by level.,"Schools and higher-education institutions face a pedagogical challenge when generative AI can produce coherent written answers, summaries and draft texts in response to ordinary prompts.",ChatGPT as a tool for Education,Accademia Belle Arti - Roma; Academic-Research; Education not definable by level.; Italy,"This use case concerns the educational use of ChatGPT not as an autonomous teaching system, but as a tool for critical collaboration between students, teachers and AI-generated text. The operational issue is that students may use the system to produce assignments mechanically, while educators can instead redesign activities so that learners interrogate, verify, contextualise and improve AI outputs.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Schools and higher-education institutions face a pedagogical challenge when generative AI can produce coherent written answers, summaries and draft texts in response to ordinary prompts. This use case concerns the educational use of ChatGPT not as an autonomous teaching system, but as a tool for critical collaboration between students, teachers and AI-generated text. The operational issue is that students may use the system to produce assignments mechanically, while educators can instead redesign activities so that learners interrogate, verify, contextualise and improve AI outputs. The implementation is therefore mainly pedagogical: ChatGPT can be used to generate examples, draft outlines, propose questions, create exercises, provide writing models, support translation, or produce material that students then analyse against sources and disciplinary knowledge. At a conceptual level, the tool offers syntactically coherent and semantically connected responses, but it does not independently establish a situated, critical or interpretative perspective. The expected impact is a shift from banning AI to teaching students how to question it, evaluate its limitations and use it to accelerate constructive thinking. The case is particularly relevant in arts and humanities education, where human contextualisation, semiotic reading and critical interpretation remain central to the learning process.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PLANNED,계획,,S7_GENERATIVE_CONTENT_CREATION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1735,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01302,EU_PSTW_202609_01302,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Lithuania Innovation Agency,"General economic, commercial and labour affair","The operational problem is that data on publications, graduates, joint projects and institutional knowledge-transfer activities can be dispersed, delayed or difficult to compare across sectors.",Evaluate science and business cooperation in terms of knowledge usage efficiency,"Lithuania Innovation Agency; Central-Government; General economic, commercial and labour affair; Lithuania","In Lithuania, GovTech Lab supported a challenge to create an AI-enabled tool for evaluating science-business cooperation and knowledge-use efficiency.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Public innovation agencies need to understand whether scientific knowledge generated by universities and research institutions is effectively used by businesses and economic sectors. In Lithuania, GovTech Lab supported a challenge to create an AI-enabled tool for evaluating science-business cooperation and knowledge-use efficiency. The operational problem is that data on publications, graduates, joint projects and institutional knowledge-transfer activities can be dispersed, delayed or difficult to compare across sectors. This limits the ability of public authorities to assess which forms of cooperation produce value and where additional policy support is needed. The implementation develops an innovative, user-friendly tool to collect, process, classify and systematise relevant data from institutions of science and studies. At a conceptual level, the solution combines data integration, automated classification, interactive visualisation and analytical summaries, allowing users either to compare information quickly through diagrams or conduct deeper cross-sectional analysis. The expected impact is more transparent monitoring of knowledge-transfer effectiveness, better evidence for science and innovation policy, and improved capacity to identify sectors where scientific knowledge is being underused.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1736,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01303,EU_PSTW_202609_01303,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Hamburg Port Authority,Transport,"The expected impact is reduced delays, lower greenhouse-gas and air-pollutant emissions, fewer unnecessary vehicle kilometres, better reliability for logistics operators and stronger environmental performance.",Intelligent traffic control in the port of Hamburg - various applications,Hamburg Port Authority; Local Government; Transport; Germany,"Hamburg Port Authority’s smartPORT approach applies digital intelligence to improve the efficiency and sustainability of port operations. The implementation uses sensor technology, analysis, forecasting and information systems to support intelligent traffic and goods management.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Ports are complex logistics ecosystems in which road, rail, vessel, terminal and warehouse operations must be synchronised to avoid congestion, emissions and wasted capacity. Hamburg Port Authority’s smartPORT approach applies digital intelligence to improve the efficiency and sustainability of port operations. The operational problem is that freight movement and traffic flows depend on many actors and real-time conditions, including vessel arrivals, truck movements, parking availability, road capacity, environmental constraints and terminal operations. The implementation uses sensor technology, analysis, forecasting and information systems to support intelligent traffic and goods management. At a conceptual level, data from infrastructure, vehicles, logistics systems and operational platforms are integrated to produce traffic information, forecasts and decision support for port users and managers. This can improve routing, parking, congestion management and the utilisation of existing infrastructure. The expected impact is reduced delays, lower greenhouse-gas and air-pollutant emissions, fewer unnecessary vehicle kilometres, better reliability for logistics operators and stronger environmental performance. The case is important because it treats port digitalisation as a system-level optimisation problem: AI and analytics do not only improve one process, but help coordinate the port as an urban-industrial infrastructure.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION|S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1738,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01304,EU_PSTW_202609_01304,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""social_nonprofit""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Port of Duisburg,Transport,UNKNOWN,5G test field planned in the Port of Duisburg,Port of Duisburg; Local Government; Transport; Germany,"The expected impact is higher container-handling capacity, more flexible port operations, better congestion prevention and a foundation for future AI- and IoT-enabled logistics use cases.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Inland ports need low-latency, high-capacity connectivity to support automation, traffic control and real-time logistics coordination. The Port of Duisburg 5G test field was designed to explore how private or local 5G infrastructure can improve port operations, especially the partially automated control of container cranes and future smart logistics applications. The operational problem is that conventional connectivity may not provide the responsiveness, reliability or flexibility needed for semi-automated equipment, sensor networks, drones and real-time traffic-management systems. The implementation established a 5G test environment in Europe’s largest inland port, with support from North Rhine-Westphalia’s 5G.NRW funding framework and technology partners including Deutsche Telekom. At a conceptual level, cameras, sensors, industrial equipment and control systems communicate over the 5G network, enabling data to be collected and interpreted rapidly for operational decisions. The expected impact is higher container-handling capacity, more flexible port operations, better congestion prevention and a foundation for future AI- and IoT-enabled logistics use cases. The project illustrates how next-generation connectivity can be a prerequisite for more advanced automation: AI-driven prediction or control depends on timely data transmission from distributed port assets.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1739,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01305,EU_PSTW_202609_01305,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""social_nonprofit""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,European Publication Office,General services.,UNKNOWN,Procurement data quality and CPV improvement for TED data,European Publication Office; Central-Government; General services.; Belgium,"The TED data-quality and CPV improvement use case focuses on using AI and natural language processing to improve the quality of Tenders Electronic Daily data and generate more reliable procurement statistics. The use case is important because high-quality procurement data is a foundation for accountability, competition analysis and anti-corruption monitoring across the EU.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"European public procurement transparency depends on high-quality, standardised data, but procurement notices can contain missing, inconsistent or incorrectly classified information. The TED data-quality and CPV improvement use case focuses on using AI and natural language processing to improve the quality of Tenders Electronic Daily data and generate more reliable procurement statistics. The operational problem is that Common Procurement Vocabulary codes and notice metadata are essential for search, monitoring, analytics and expenditure analysis, but they may be incomplete or inaccurate when submitted. The implementation concept includes extracting or validating CPV codes from procurement documents, detecting inconsistencies, deriving missing fields, harmonising data and producing improved statistics on expenditure, contractors and contracting authorities. At a conceptual level, NLP models read tender text and metadata, infer likely classifications or corrections, and support data-quality auditing. More advanced analytical objectives include identifying patterns in awards and forecasting likely bidders or awardees under certain tender characteristics. The expected impact is better procurement transparency, more accurate public spending analysis, improved searchability of tenders and stronger evidence for policy, audit and market-intelligence work. The use case is important because high-quality procurement data is a foundation for accountability, competition analysis and anti-corruption monitoring across the EU.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1740,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01306,EU_PSTW_202609_01306,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Finland Tax Agency,"General economic, commercial and labour affair",UNKNOWN,Finland Tax Agency AI support,"Finland Tax Agency; Central-Government; General economic, commercial and labour affair; Finland","The Finnish Tax Administration use case concerns the controlled use of artificial intelligence in taxation, within the legal framework governing tax assessment and administrative procedure. The operational problem is that taxation requires both efficiency and legal certainty: AI can support risk selection, classification, service delivery or internal analysis, but the consequences for taxpayers mean that each use must be assessed separately.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Tax administrations process large volumes of returns, declarations, payments, risk signals and taxpayer interactions, making them natural candidates for automation and data-driven decision support. The Finnish Tax Administration use case concerns the controlled use of artificial intelligence in taxation, within the legal framework governing tax assessment and administrative procedure. The operational problem is that taxation requires both efficiency and legal certainty: AI can support risk selection, classification, service delivery or internal analysis, but the consequences for taxpayers mean that each use must be assessed separately. The implementation approach is therefore governance-led rather than based on one single model. Different AI solutions may be used for different functions, and the degree of automation depends on the purpose, legal basis and procedural safeguards of each system. At a conceptual level, AI can either generate recommendations for tax officials or, where legally permitted, support more automated processing. The expected impact is more efficient tax administration, improved targeting of checks, better consistency in routine processing and faster taxpayer service. The case is significant because it emphasises that AI in taxation is not merely a technical matter: it must be aligned with administrative-law principles, taxpayer rights, transparency, accountability and the distinction between decision support and independent automated decision-making.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1741,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01307,EU_PSTW_202609_01307,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Basque Country,Law courts,"In the Basque Country, Transkit was developed to reduce the burden of manually reviewing court-session videos and preparing transcripts. The operational problem is that legal staff may need to locate exactly where a phrase was said, who said it, or which named entities were mentioned, but manual video review is slow and does not create searchable text.","Speeding up judicial proceedings with Transkit, an AI-based tool that transcribes trials",Basque Country; Regional Government; Law courts; Spain,"The implementation uses neural-network-based speech-to-text technology capable of transcribing Spanish, Basque and bilingual Spanish-Basque audio or video content.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Court proceedings generate large volumes of spoken material that may later need to be searched, quoted, reviewed or used in higher-instance appeals. In the Basque Country, Transkit was developed to reduce the burden of manually reviewing court-session videos and preparing transcripts. The operational problem is that legal staff may need to locate exactly where a phrase was said, who said it, or which named entities were mentioned, but manual video review is slow and does not create searchable text. The implementation uses neural-network-based speech-to-text technology capable of transcribing Spanish, Basque and bilingual Spanish-Basque audio or video content. At a conceptual level, the system converts recorded proceedings into timestamped text, links speech with searchable terms and allows staff to focus on correction, validation and completion rather than full manual transcription. The expected impact is faster judicial administration, improved productivity for clerks and court staff, easier review of appeal material, better searchability of proceedings and more efficient preparation of judgments or records. The tool does not replace official legal validation of transcripts; it provides a draft and search layer that supports human professionals in managing recorded procedural content more efficiently.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,S3_DOCUMENT_INTELLIGENCE,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1743,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01308,EU_PSTW_202609_01308,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,EU DG COMP,"General economic, commercial and labour affair","Public administrations frequently handle large volumes of unstructured documents and emails that must be reviewed within strict deadlines, particularly in investigative and compliance contexts. Manual classification is labour-intensive and may lead to inconsistencies.",ISA2 Action 1.7 – AI Pilot for Document Classification,"EU DG COMP; Central-Government; General economic, commercial and labour affair; Belgium",UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Public administrations frequently handle large volumes of unstructured documents and emails that must be reviewed within strict deadlines, particularly in investigative and compliance contexts. Manual classification is labour-intensive and may lead to inconsistencies. Stakeholders include administrative bodies, investigative units and IT departments. The objective is to assess the added value of machine learning for document review and classification within public administrations. The pilot evaluates multiple machine learning algorithms and modelling approaches, including supervised text classification models based on natural language processing techniques. The system is designed to integrate with existing document repositories and review platforms, addressing interoperability, organisational and legal considerations. Anticipated impacts include faster document triage, improved consistency in classification decisions, reduced administrative burden and clearer understanding of governance implications associated with automated document analysis.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK|F04_UNSTRUCTURED_INFORMATION_COMPLEXITY|F06_HIGH_VOLUME_SCALABILITY_BOTTLENECK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1744,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01309,EU_PSTW_202609_01309,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Ministry of Justice,Law courts,Citizens and businesses in Portugal often face difficulties understanding legal procedures due to complex language and fragmented information sources.,Portugal Ministry of Justice – GPJ Digital Assistant,Ministry of Justice; Central-Government; Law courts; Portugal,"This use case involves the Ministry, citizens, companies, and legal professionals, with the objective of simplifying access to information about legal proceedings. The GPJ digital assistant is implemented as a conversational system trained on official justice content and written in plain language.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Citizens and businesses in Portugal often face difficulties understanding legal procedures due to complex language and fragmented information sources. The Ministry of Justice sought to improve access to justice by making procedural information clearer, more accessible, and easier to navigate. This use case involves the Ministry, citizens, companies, and legal professionals, with the objective of simplifying access to information about legal proceedings. The GPJ digital assistant is implemented as a conversational system trained on official justice content and written in plain language. It responds to user questions through natural dialogue, guiding them through procedures, requirements, and next steps. The system is integrated with Ministry information sources and designed to evolve as regulations change. The solution improves comprehension, reduces the need for in-person or phone enquiries, and supports more equal access to justice information. It enhances user experience by lowering language barriers, improves efficiency within the justice system, and delivers public value by making legal information more transparent and accessible.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1745,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01310,EU_PSTW_202609_01310,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,University of Palermo,Fuel and energy,"Small islands face distinctive energy challenges because they often rely on isolated grids, imported fuels and limited flexibility, while renewable generation requires coordination between producers, consumers and local distributors.",Blockchain to certify energy services delivery,University of Palermo; Academic-Research; Fuel and energy; Italy,"The implementation uses a blockchain-like platform for secure data transfer between users, developed with the University of Palermo and industrial partners including Exalto Energy & Innovation and Regalgrid.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Small islands face distinctive energy challenges because they often rely on isolated grids, imported fuels and limited flexibility, while renewable generation requires coordination between producers, consumers and local distributors. BloRin was developed for Sicilian islands such as Favignana and Lampedusa to test whether blockchain-based systems could support decentralised renewable-energy communities and transparent energy-service delivery. The objective is to manage exchanges between prosumers, aggregate distributed resources, support balancing services and coordinate energy flows without relying on opaque or intermediary-heavy processes. The implementation uses a blockchain-like platform for secure data transfer between users, developed with the University of Palermo and industrial partners including Exalto Energy & Innovation and Regalgrid. Tests are linked to Mediterranean island settings and the University of Palermo campus. At a conceptual level, the system records energy production, consumption, exchange and service-delivery information in a tamper-resistant environment, helping coordinate microgrid operations and interactions with electricity distributors. It can also support electric-vehicle charging infrastructure in vehicle-to-grid mode. The expected impact is greater transparency in renewable-energy management, stronger participation by local prosumers, better integration of distributed generation and a replicable model for sustainable small-island energy communities. Public sources continue to describe BloRin as a project being developed and tested, rather than a mature commercial utility platform.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1746,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01311,EU_PSTW_202609_01311,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,ENGINEERING - INGEGNERIA INFORMATICA SPA,Police Services,UNKNOWN,Artificial Intelligence and advanced Data Analytics for Law Enforcement Agencies,ENGINEERING - INGEGNERIA INFORMATICA SPA; Consortium; Police Services; Italy,"The tools were intended to be made available to law-enforcement agencies through a secure sandbox environment using real data, raising the technological readiness level towards TRL 7. The project also produced work on safeguards and citizen perspectives, reflecting the sensitivity of AI use in policing.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Law-enforcement agencies investigating cybercrime and terrorism must process large, heterogeneous and fast-moving datasets, including online content, digital traces, multilingual material, network information and intelligence records. AIDA was created to provide European law-enforcement bodies with a modular platform for descriptive and predictive analytics that could support investigation and proactive prevention while operating in a secure environment. The objective was to improve the full analytical workflow, from content acquisition and information extraction to fusion, knowledge management, enrichment, visual analytics and operational decision support. The implementation combines big-data processing, machine learning, deep learning, predictive analytics, multilingual scene-text recognition and task-specific analytical tools for cybercrime and terrorism investigations. At a conceptual level, AIDA integrates data ingestion, automated mining, entity and relationship extraction, knowledge representation and visual interfaces so that analysts can identify patterns, links and risks across large datasets. The tools were intended to be made available to law-enforcement agencies through a secure sandbox environment using real data, raising the technological readiness level towards TRL 7. The expected impact is faster intelligence analysis, more efficient investigations, improved situational awareness and stronger ability to detect complex criminal activities. The project also produced work on safeguards and citizen perspectives, reflecting the sensitivity of AI use in policing.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1748,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01312,EU_PSTW_202609_01312,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of The Hague,Police Services,"Urban enforcement teams in busy nightlife and tourist areas must respond to nuisance quickly, but reports may arrive too late or lack precise location information.",The Hague uses AI to reduce laughing gas nuisance,Municipality of The Hague; Local Government; Police Services; Netherlands,"The expected impact is quicker enforcement response, better targeting of patrols and reduced nuisance linked to laughing gas use, without creating a general-purpose surveillance stream.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Urban enforcement teams in busy nightlife and tourist areas must respond to nuisance quickly, but reports may arrive too late or lack precise location information. In Scheveningen, The Hague tested whether smart cameras could help detect nitrous oxide, or laughing gas, abuse around the boulevard, where nuisance was linked to parking problems, traffic violations, noise and other undesirable behaviour. The objective was to give municipal enforcement staff timely information while avoiding unnecessary collection of identifiable footage. The implementation used AI-enabled cameras from Avutec trained to recognise nitrous oxide balloons and related paraphernalia, with Monotch’s TLEX platform forwarding detection data to the enforcement organisation. At a conceptual level, the system combines video analytics, object recognition, privacy-preserving event reporting and real-time notification workflows. Smart Cities World reports that no footage was sent because of privacy considerations; only detection data were forwarded. The pilot assessed algorithm functionality, camera accuracy, notification speed and privacy protection during a test day on the Scheveningen boulevard. The expected impact is quicker enforcement response, better targeting of patrols and reduced nuisance linked to laughing gas use, without creating a general-purpose surveillance stream. The test confirmed the algorithm’s functionality and gave a “green light” for refinement, but public sources describe it as a trial rather than full deployment.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1749,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01313,EU_PSTW_202609_01313,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,HM Government of Gibraltar,General public services n.e.c.,"The operational problem is that financial institutions and regulated digital-asset firms must verify customers reliably, but repeated checks across different services can be inefficient and burdensome. At a conceptual level, government-issued or institutionally verified credentials can be stored or referenced in a secure digital environment, allowing authorised parties to validate identity attributes without relying entirely on repeated manual document checks.",Digital identity verification for financial services in Gibraltar,HM Government of Gibraltar; Local Government; General public services n.e.c.; United Kingdom,Gibraltar’s blockchain-related digital-identity use case concerns the use of distributed-ledger and secure credential technologies to streamline identity verification and strengthen the integrity of financial-service interactions. The use case aligns with Gibraltar’s wider strategy of positioning itself as a regulated fintech and distributed-ledger jurisdiction.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Financial services require robust identity verification to prevent fraud, meet compliance obligations and support trust in digital transactions. Gibraltar’s blockchain-related digital-identity use case concerns the use of distributed-ledger and secure credential technologies to streamline identity verification and strengthen the integrity of financial-service interactions. The operational problem is that financial institutions and regulated digital-asset firms must verify customers reliably, but repeated checks across different services can be inefficient and burdensome. A blockchain-enabled approach can support certified credentials, tamper-evident records and shared assurance mechanisms while preserving appropriate access controls. At a conceptual level, government-issued or institutionally verified credentials can be stored or referenced in a secure digital environment, allowing authorised parties to validate identity attributes without relying entirely on repeated manual document checks. The expected impact is reduced fraud, faster onboarding, improved regulatory confidence and greater efficiency for Gibraltar’s fintech and digital-asset ecosystem. The use case aligns with Gibraltar’s wider strategy of positioning itself as a regulated fintech and distributed-ledger jurisdiction. Its success depends on governance, compliance, data protection and regulatory supervision, because digital identity in financial services involves sensitive personal and transactional information.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1750,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01314,EU_PSTW_202609_01314,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,HM Government of Gibraltar,General public services n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,Blockchain-enabled land registry in Gibraltar,HM Government of Gibraltar; Local Government; General public services n.e.c.; United Kingdom,"Gibraltar’s blockchain-enabled land-registry use case concerns the integration of distributed-ledger technology into government systems to support trusted digital records and more efficient public-service delivery. The use case is best understood within Gibraltar’s wider effort to apply blockchain to legacy government systems, rather than as a standalone cryptocurrency project.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Property markets depend on reliable land records, because ownership, transfers and encumbrances must be trusted by buyers, sellers, lenders, lawyers and public authorities. Gibraltar’s blockchain-enabled land-registry use case concerns the integration of distributed-ledger technology into government systems to support trusted digital records and more efficient public-service delivery. The operational problem is that land-related transactions can involve multiple documents, institutions and verification steps, creating time, cost and administrative friction. A blockchain-based approach can create tamper-evident records or certificates that help verify the authenticity and integrity of government-held information. At a conceptual level, the system links official land or property records with cryptographic proofs, enabling authorised users to confirm that a document or register entry has not been altered after certification. The expected impact is faster property transactions, lower administrative cost, stronger trust in land-title records and greater attractiveness for investors operating in Gibraltar’s real-estate market. The use case is best understood within Gibraltar’s wider effort to apply blockchain to legacy government systems, rather than as a standalone cryptocurrency project. Its value lies in using distributed-ledger features such as immutability, auditability and shared verification to strengthen conventional public administration.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1751,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01315,EU_PSTW_202609_01315,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Republic of Georgia’s National Agency of Public Registry (NAPR),General public services n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,Blockchain-enabled land registry in Georgia,Republic of Georgia’s National Agency of Public Registry (NAPR); Central-Government; General public services n.e.c.; Georgia,"Citizens can receive digital certificates linked to their property assets, and officials can use the system to reinforce confidence in registry data. The project is significant because it was widely presented as one of the first government blockchain land-registry systems, applying distributed-ledger technology to a concrete public-administration function rather than a speculative digital-asset use case.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Secure land registration is central to property rights, investor confidence and citizens’ trust in government, particularly where land-title disputes or weak records can undermine economic development. Georgia’s National Agency of Public Registry partnered with Bitfury to introduce blockchain technology into the land-title registry. The operational objective was to strengthen the security and integrity of property records by anchoring land-title information to a cryptographic proof. The implementation generates a hash of relevant land-registry data and records that proof on a blockchain, allowing future verification that the record has not been altered. At a conceptual level, the authoritative register remains a government land-registry system, while the blockchain layer provides an additional tamper-evident certification mechanism. Citizens can receive digital certificates linked to their property assets, and officials can use the system to reinforce confidence in registry data. The expected impact is stronger protection of ownership rights, better trust in public records, reduced risk of manipulation and a more modern land-administration service. The project is significant because it was widely presented as one of the first government blockchain land-registry systems, applying distributed-ledger technology to a concrete public-administration function rather than a speculative digital-asset use case.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1752,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01316,EU_PSTW_202609_01316,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,HM Government of Gibraltar,General public services n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,E-residency program in Gibraltar,HM Government of Gibraltar; Local Government; General public services n.e.c.; United Kingdom,"The use case should be treated carefully because the source confirms a blockchain pilot for government-issued credentials, while the e-residency component appears more as a strategic direction than a fully documented operational programme.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Digital entrepreneurs and internationally mobile business owners increasingly seek remote access to legally recognised business environments, but establishing a trusted online presence across borders requires secure identity, credentials and public-service infrastructure. Gibraltar’s e-residency idea is linked to its broader blockchain-enabled government-services programme, which aims to modernise legacy public systems and improve interaction between government departments, individuals and organisations. The operational problem is that remote business users need verifiable credentials, trusted access to services and a clear legal environment without necessarily being physically present in the territory. The implementation concept builds on blockchain and distributed-ledger technology to support government-issued certified credentials and improve interconnection between public systems. At a conceptual level, an e-residency model would allow eligible users to establish a recognised digital relationship with Gibraltar, authenticate themselves securely and access administrative or business services online. The expected impact is greater attractiveness for digital entrepreneurs, easier company formation or service access, stronger digital trust and a more competitive fintech and digital-business ecosystem. The use case should be treated carefully because the source confirms a blockchain pilot for government-issued credentials, while the e-residency component appears more as a strategic direction than a fully documented operational programme.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PLANNED,계획,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1753,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01317,EU_PSTW_202609_01317,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,World Health Organisation Europe,Public health services.,"International ports and ships play a frontline role in preventing, detecting and responding to public-health events linked to maritime travel and trade, but high-quality ship sanitation inspection training can be difficult to deliver consistently across countries.",WHO Europe launched an innovative virtual reality training tool on ship sanitation inspection,World Health Organisation Europe; Central-Government; Public health services.; European Union,"The implementation uses immersive virtual reality to familiarise trainees with realistic ship environments, technical areas and inspection procedures, allowing them to practise end-to-end sanitation inspection, risk assessment and interaction with captains and crews.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"International ports and ships play a frontline role in preventing, detecting and responding to public-health events linked to maritime travel and trade, but high-quality ship sanitation inspection training can be difficult to deliver consistently across countries. WHO/Europe introduced a virtual reality training tool to strengthen the capacity of port health officers and International Health Regulations focal points, especially after the COVID-19 pandemic showed how vulnerable maritime inspection systems can be when travel restrictions and port closures disrupt normal practice. The tool was first tested during a four-day training in Istanbul with 13 professionals from Azerbaijan, Kazakhstan and Turkmenistan. The implementation uses immersive virtual reality to familiarise trainees with realistic ship environments, technical areas and inspection procedures, allowing them to practise end-to-end sanitation inspection, risk assessment and interaction with captains and crews. At a conceptual level, the system converts inspection guidance into scenario-based simulation, combining visual immersion, procedural learning and public-health decision-making. The expected impact is more standardised training, improved inspection readiness and stronger public-health capacity across more than 230 ports in the WHO European Region and 41 IHR State Parties with authorised ports for issuing ship sanitation certificates. Once fully launched, the tool should help scale specialist training without requiring constant access to vessels or in-person inspection settings.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1754,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01318,EU_PSTW_202609_01318,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Region of Central Macedonia,Public health services.,"The operational problem is that older people may need rapid help after a fall, health incident or emergency, but cannot always rely on family members or nearby carers.",Assisted Living and Safe Aging Services in the Region of Central Macedonia (Greece),Region of Central Macedonia; Regional Government; Public health services.; Greece,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Ageing populations create increasing demand for social-care models that help people remain independent at home while reducing isolation and improving safety. The Region of Central Macedonia introduced Assisted Living and Safe Aging Services as a telecare model for elderly people who live alone, have chronic conditions, face health risks or are socially isolated. The operational problem is that older people may need rapid help after a fall, health incident or emergency, but cannot always rely on family members or nearby carers. The implementation combines a 24-hour call centre with trained social workers and psychologists, home monitoring devices, fall-detection systems, panic buttons, outdoor tracking and locator devices. At a conceptual level, the service links personal devices, monitoring infrastructure and human support teams so that alerts can be assessed and responded to quickly. The expected impact is safer independent living, reduced loneliness, faster response to emergencies and greater confidence for elderly people and their families. The service is also important as a preventive social-care intervention: rather than waiting for institutionalisation or crisis, it provides continuous, personalised support that can help people remain in familiar home environments while still being connected to public care services.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1758,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01319,EU_PSTW_202609_01319,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Brønnøysund Register Centre under the Ministry of Trade, Industry and Fisheries","General economic, commercial and labour affair","The operational objective was to provide rapid liquidity to eligible firms without creating a slow, manually administered grant system. This design reduced the need for extensive post-payment verification because key checks were performed before disbursement, lowering both administrative burden and misconduct risk.",Automated disbursement of the Covid-19 Business Compensation in Norway,"Brønnøysund Register Centre under the Ministry of Trade, Industry and Fisheries; Central-Government; General economic, commercial and labour affair; Norway","The expected impact was fast crisis support, protection of business continuity, transparent use of public money and reusable capacity for future emergency schemes.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The economic shock of the COVID-19 pandemic required governments to deliver financial support quickly while still preventing fraud, error and misuse of public funds. Norway’s digital compensation scheme was created to help businesses withstand pandemic-related revenue losses through a highly automated application, verification and payment process. The operational objective was to provide rapid liquidity to eligible firms without creating a slow, manually administered grant system. Applications were approved by an auditor and then automatically processed through checks against multiple official sources and registers. At a conceptual level, the system combined digital application intake, pre-disbursement validation, register-based verification, automated decision support, transparency mechanisms and rapid payment workflows. Information about grants was published every hour, applicants could receive a reply within minutes, and payments were made within 72 hours. This design reduced the need for extensive post-payment verification because key checks were performed before disbursement, lowering both administrative burden and misconduct risk. The expected impact was fast crisis support, protection of business continuity, transparent use of public money and reusable capacity for future emergency schemes. The Norwegian case is a strong example of digital-era public administration: automation was used not merely for efficiency, but to combine speed, control, auditability and public transparency during an exceptional economic crisis.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,NOT_IN_USE,미사용,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1759,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01320,EU_PSTW_202609_01320,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Swedish National Financial Management Authority (ESV),Economic affairs n.e.c,"Macroeconomic forecasting is essential for public financial management, but machine-learning models can be difficult for officials to trust if their predictions are not interpretable.",Forecasting Sweeish GDP with Explainable AI,Swedish National Financial Management Authority (ESV); Central-Government; Economic affairs n.e.c; Sweden,"The expected impact is more accountable use of AI in public financial management, better integration of machine learning into economic forecasting and stronger confidence among officials who must interpret forecasts for policy and budgetary purposes. The use case is significant because it focuses on explainability as a core requirement for government AI, rather than treating predictive accuracy alone as sufficient.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Macroeconomic forecasting is essential for public financial management, but machine-learning models can be difficult for officials to trust if their predictions are not interpretable. The Swedish National Financial Management Authority developed an explainable-AI application to improve GDP forecasting and help analysts understand the behaviour of black-box models. The operational problem was not only to produce accurate forecasts, but also to make visible which variables influenced a prediction and how their importance changed over time. The implementation used machine-learning forecasting models alongside explainability tools developed in collaboration with a scholar from KTH Royal Institute of Technology. At a conceptual level, the web application analyses the contribution of individual variables to GDP predictions, showing both temporal variation and aggregated effects over longer periods. This allows economists to inspect model behaviour, compare it with domain knowledge and improve forecasting practice. The expected impact is more accountable use of AI in public financial management, better integration of machine learning into economic forecasting and stronger confidence among officials who must interpret forecasts for policy and budgetary purposes. The use case is significant because it focuses on explainability as a core requirement for government AI, rather than treating predictive accuracy alone as sufficient.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1760,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01321,EU_PSTW_202609_01321,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,National Health Service (NHS),Public health services.,UNKNOWN,NHS Flu and COVID-19 Vaccines Chatbot,National Health Service (NHS); Central-Government; Public health services.; United Kingdom,"The use case is notable because it applies conversational AI to a public-health communication problem where trust, clarity and behavioural nudges are central.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Seasonal vaccination programmes require clear public information because citizens may have many questions about eligibility, timing, safety, booking and the relationship between different vaccines. The NHS Winter Vaccines Virtual Helper was developed to turn vaccine search into a conversational experience, giving users a single place to ask questions about flu and COVID-19 vaccination. The operational problem was that search results often returned only one answer even though users’ questions were complex or sequential, while misinformation and uncertainty could reduce vaccination uptake. The implementation created a “one stop vaccination shop” that answered common vaccine questions and directed users towards booking their jab. At a conceptual level, the chatbot interprets vaccination-related questions, retrieves trusted NHS information and supports the user journey from uncertainty to action. The expected impact is improved access to authoritative public-health information, reduced confusion, more efficient digital guidance and stronger encouragement for eligible people to book vaccination appointments. The use case is notable because it applies conversational AI to a public-health communication problem where trust, clarity and behavioural nudges are central. Rather than replacing clinical advice, it provides a scalable front-line information channel aligned with NHS messaging during seasonal vaccination campaigns.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1761,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01322,EU_PSTW_202609_01322,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Ministry of Public Administration,General services.,UNKNOWN,Semantic Analyser – Smart Text Search Engine,Ministry of Public Administration; Central-Government; General services.; Slovenia,"The implementation uses natural-language processing to extract characteristic concepts from documents, identify concepts that co-occur, group texts with related content and visualise semantic relationships. The use case is important because it shows how AI can support everyday knowledge work in government, especially in smaller-language contexts where off-the-shelf NLP tools may be less mature.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Public administrations work with large collections of legal, policy and administrative texts, but conventional keyword search may miss related documents where different terminology is used. Slovenia’s Semantic Analyser was developed as an open-source smart text-search prototype for Slovenian-language documents. The operational problem is to help officials analyse collections of laws, proposals, vocabularies and other public-administration texts by identifying key concepts and relationships between them. The implementation uses natural-language processing to extract characteristic concepts from documents, identify concepts that co-occur, group texts with related content and visualise semantic relationships. At a conceptual level, the tool turns terms into links between documents, enabling users to find related materials, identify missing concepts in vocabularies and compare texts across collections. Users can also search by a set of terms and discover documents connected by synonyms, broader terms or related concepts. The expected impact is more efficient document analysis, better management of legal and administrative knowledge, improved development of public-sector vocabularies and stronger reuse of textual data. The use case is important because it shows how AI can support everyday knowledge work in government, especially in smaller-language contexts where off-the-shelf NLP tools may be less mature.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1763,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01323,EU_PSTW_202609_01323,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Gotenburg,General public services n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,Goteverse - Expanding Gothenburg's digital twin within the metaverse,City of Gotenburg; Local Government; General public services n.e.c.; Sweden,"Goteverse extends this digital twin into more immersive and public-facing virtual experiences, allowing audiences to visit representations of Gothenburg and explore how future cities can use virtual technologies for services, communication and participation. The implementation uses game-engine and metaverse techniques to transform geographic information and collected city imagery into interactive digital environments.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Urban development increasingly depends on the ability to communicate future plans visually, coordinate stakeholders and make city information accessible to wider audiences. Gothenburg’s digital twin, Virtual Gothenburg, provides a three-dimensional model of the city that can be used to visualise development projects, support analysis and improve shared understanding of urban change. Goteverse extends this digital twin into more immersive and public-facing virtual experiences, allowing audiences to visit representations of Gothenburg and explore how future cities can use virtual technologies for services, communication and participation. The implementation uses game-engine and metaverse techniques to transform geographic information and collected city imagery into interactive digital environments. The city’s own material describes Virtual Gothenburg as an 800 km² 3D model based on municipal geographic information, designed to support visualisation, simulation and more informed urban decisions. At a conceptual level, Goteverse connects urban digital-twin data, immersive storytelling, virtual access and digital placemaking. The expected impact is better communication of future projects, stronger public engagement, more accessible city experiences and new opportunities for tourism, planning and co-creation. Gothenburg & Co also describes Goteverse as a hub for shared exploration and notes related virtual initiatives such as Gothenburg Arena in Fortnite, showing that the digital-twin strategy has expanded beyond technical planning into public digital experience.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1765,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01324,EU_PSTW_202609_01324,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Pisa University,R&D health.,UNKNOWN,AI-assisted diagnostic and rehabilitative tools for personalised support of children with cerebral palsy,Pisa University; Academic-Research; R&D health.; Italy,"The operational problem is that conventional care can rely on fragmented clinical observations, periodic assessments and limited real-time information on how children use their upper limb outside the clinic. The implementation uses a multimodal approach that combines clinical phenotyping, advanced brain imaging and real-time monitoring of upper-limb function.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Children with unilateral cerebral palsy require highly personalised assessment and rehabilitation because motor impairments, upper-limb function and treatment response vary substantially across individuals. The AInCP project, led by the University of Pisa, addresses this need by developing clinical decision-support tools that can help clinicians provide functional diagnosis, prognosis and tailored home-based intervention for children with unilateral cerebral palsy. The operational problem is that conventional care can rely on fragmented clinical observations, periodic assessments and limited real-time information on how children use their upper limb outside the clinic. The implementation uses a multimodal approach that combines clinical phenotyping, advanced brain imaging and real-time monitoring of upper-limb function. Artificial intelligence methods are used to integrate these sources into personalised assessment and rehabilitation tools, supporting both clinical interpretation and home-based treatment planning. At a conceptual level, the system links diagnostic data, imaging features, movement monitoring and intervention recommendations in an evidence-based decision-support framework. The expected impact is more precise functional profiling, better prediction of rehabilitation needs, improved personalisation of therapy and stronger continuity between clinical and home settings. The project also aims to validate the tools through observational and rehabilitation studies, making clinical reliability and trustworthiness central to its development. CORDIS lists AInCP as running from 1 June 2022 to 31 May 2027.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1767,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01325,EU_PSTW_202609_01325,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Viborg Municipality,Outpatient services.,UNKNOWN,VR in wound care in Denmark,Viborg Municipality; Local Government; Outpatient services.; Denmark,"Viborg Municipality tested the use of virtual reality glasses during wound care in its home-care and everyday-rehabilitation services, with the aim of distracting citizens from pain and improving the experience of treatment. The implementation uses VR headsets as a sensory distraction technology during wound treatment.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Wound treatment can be painful and stressful for citizens, while nurses need practical ways to reduce discomfort without making procedures more complex or resource-intensive. Viborg Municipality tested the use of virtual reality glasses during wound care in its home-care and everyday-rehabilitation services, with the aim of distracting citizens from pain and improving the experience of treatment. The context is municipal healthcare, where wound care may take place repeatedly in health centres, nursing clinics or citizens’ homes, and where small improvements in comfort can affect cooperation, anxiety and staff workload. The implementation uses VR headsets as a sensory distraction technology during wound treatment. At a conceptual level, the citizen is immersed in a visual and auditory environment that draws attention away from the procedure, while nurses continue their ordinary clinical tasks. The project does not replace wound-care expertise; it adds a non-pharmacological support layer to reduce perceived pain and make treatment more tolerable. The expected impact is improved citizen experience, easier completion of wound procedures and better working conditions for staff managing painful treatments. Viborg Municipality reports that the project confirmed the pain-distraction effect indicated by research and produced good results for both citizens and employees. As a result, VR glasses are now being used further in home nursing and nursing clinics.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1768,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01326,EU_PSTW_202609_01326,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Aarhus University, Municipality of Aalborg and DigiRehab",Outpatient services.,"Municipal rehabilitation for older citizens requires caseworkers to assess training needs, assistive aids, fall-prevention measures and expected self-sufficiency, often under pressure from ageing populations and limited care resources.",AI-enabled Rehabilitation in danish municipalities,"Aarhus University, Municipality of Aalborg and DigiRehab; Academic-Research; Outpatient services.; Denmark","The implementation uses models and statistical analysis based on municipal rehabilitation data, including information on granted assistive devices and completed training pathways, to support decisions about which rehabilitation measures may be most appropriate for older citizens. The expected impact is more consistent rehabilitation planning, better use of assistive aids, earlier fall-prevention attention and improved targeting of training resources.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Municipal rehabilitation for older citizens requires caseworkers to assess training needs, assistive aids, fall-prevention measures and expected self-sufficiency, often under pressure from ageing populations and limited care resources. The AIR project was designed to support these professional assessments with data-driven decision support rather than replace municipal judgement. With Aalborg Municipality as project owner, Aarhus University, DigiRehab, Syddjurs Municipality and Viborg Municipality collaborated on one of Denmark’s municipal AI signature projects. The implementation uses models and statistical analysis based on municipal rehabilitation data, including information on granted assistive devices and completed training pathways, to support decisions about which rehabilitation measures may be most appropriate for older citizens. At a conceptual level, the system analyses patterns in previous rehabilitation cases and translates them into recommendations or decision support for case managers, with performance measured through improved self-sufficiency and reduced reliance on home help. The expected impact is more consistent rehabilitation planning, better use of assistive aids, earlier fall-prevention attention and improved targeting of training resources. A proof-of-concept was conducted in 2018–2019, while the AIR signature project ran from 2020 to 2022 and delivered a prototype by the end of the project period.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1769,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01327,EU_PSTW_202609_01327,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Messina,Police Services,"Urban areas face growing challenges in preventing crime and ensuring public safety, particularly where surveillance systems and data sources are fragmented.",Me@Gis - AI-powered webcams in the Municipality of Messina to enhahnce public security,Municipality of Messina; Local Government; Police Services; Italy,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Urban areas face growing challenges in preventing crime and ensuring public safety, particularly where surveillance systems and data sources are fragmented. In Messina, local authorities needed a more coordinated approach to monitoring public spaces and supporting police operations. The ME@GIS project addresses this need by creating an integrated urban security infrastructure that enhances situational awareness and investigative capacity. Stakeholders include the municipal administration, local police forces, and citizens. The objective is to reduce crime risk, improve response times, and modernise public security governance through a scalable digital model. The solution integrates a network of webcams, sensors, and data sources into a unified platform that enables real-time monitoring and analysis of urban environments. Advanced analytics support the detection of suspicious patterns and events, while shared dashboards improve coordination between operational units. The pilot nature of the project allows solutions to be tested and refined before replication in other cities. The impact includes improved detection and prevention of criminal activity, more efficient investigations, and strengthened trust in public safety services. ME@GIS demonstrates how integrated digital infrastructures can enhance urban resilience and deliver measurable public security benefits.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1770,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01328,EU_PSTW_202609_01328,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Vaxjo Kommun,General services.,UNKNOWN,Vaxjo Kommun chatbot,Vaxjo Kommun; Local Government; General services.; Sweden,Växjö Municipality’s chatbot use case concerns the use of conversational support to help users navigate municipal information and reach the right service more quickly.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Municipal websites contain information for residents, businesses and visitors across many service areas, from permits and schools to care, traffic, culture and local democracy. Växjö Municipality’s chatbot use case concerns the use of conversational support to help users navigate municipal information and reach the right service more quickly. The operational problem is that users may not know which department, page or e-service corresponds to their question, particularly when municipal terminology differs from ordinary language. The implementation places a chatbot or AI-supported contact layer within the municipality’s digital service environment, allowing users to ask questions and receive guidance in a more accessible form than browsing menus alone. At a conceptual level, the assistant interprets a user’s request, searches or maps it against municipal content and returns relevant links, answers or next steps. The expected impact is improved accessibility of local-government information, reduced pressure on the contact centre for routine enquiries, better user experience and clearer pathways to e-services. Such systems are most useful where they remain tightly connected to official content and where complex or sensitive cases can still be escalated to human staff. The chatbot is therefore best understood as a navigation and first-line information tool, not as a replacement for municipal decision-making.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1771,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01329,EU_PSTW_202609_01329,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Syddjurs,General services.,"Employment services often involve complex citizen journeys across unemployment benefits, municipal departments, job-search support and follow-up activities, making it difficult for caseworkers and managers to see which pathways are effective. Syddjurs Municipality and four partner municipalities used AI-based process mining to analyse how citizens move through employment-service systems and where delays, detours or inefficient steps occur.","Syddjurs, DK and neighboring cities map employment service processes with AI-powered platform",Municipality of Syddjurs; Local Government; General services.; Denmark,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Employment services often involve complex citizen journeys across unemployment benefits, municipal departments, job-search support and follow-up activities, making it difficult for caseworkers and managers to see which pathways are effective. Syddjurs Municipality and four partner municipalities used AI-based process mining to analyse how citizens move through employment-service systems and where delays, detours or inefficient steps occur. The objective was to create a more holistic and effective employment effort by turning administrative process data into maps of real user experiences and predictive insights. The implementation combines municipal data on unemployment benefits, service contacts and departmental interactions with process-mining and predictive-monitoring techniques. At a conceptual level, the platform reconstructs actual citizen pathways, identifies patterns in service sequences and recommends the most suitable route for each resident’s employment search. This allows municipalities to understand not only which services are provided, but how citizens experience the sequence of interventions. The expected impact is better prioritisation of employment support, improved coordination between municipal teams, faster identification of bottlenecks and more personalised guidance for jobseekers. The participating municipalities received DKK 4.1 million from a national innovation fund, and the dedicated project site remains active with resources on process mining, data strategy and evaluation.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1772,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01330,EU_PSTW_202609_01330,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Hertfordshire County Council,Police Services,"Traffic surveys are essential for highways planning, junction design and transport policy, but manual traffic counts can be slow, inconsistent and labour-intensive. Twelve-hour video segments can be categorised much faster than manual review, and the interface is designed to be usable without specialist training.",Hertfordshire County Council implemented a AI-powered traffic monitoring platform,Hertfordshire County Council; Local Government; Police Services; United Kingdom,"Hertfordshire County Council developed an AI-powered traffic monitoring platform with AWS to automate and standardise the analysis of local traffic-camera footage. The expected impact is faster traffic surveys, lower administrative burden, better standardisation, improved evidence for transport planning and more scalable monitoring of road use across the county.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Traffic surveys are essential for highways planning, junction design and transport policy, but manual traffic counts can be slow, inconsistent and labour-intensive. Hertfordshire County Council developed an AI-powered traffic monitoring platform with AWS to automate and standardise the analysis of local traffic-camera footage. The operational problem was to reduce the staff time required to classify vehicles, directions and speeds while producing more consistent data for decision-making. The implementation sends video from local traffic cameras through an application built on AWS Amplify to Amazon Rekognition for automated analysis. At a conceptual level, the system processes video, identifies traffic types, direction and speed, records the relevant survey data and then deletes imagery to reduce data-retention risks. Twelve-hour video segments can be categorised much faster than manual review, and the interface is designed to be usable without specialist training. The expected impact is faster traffic surveys, lower administrative burden, better standardisation, improved evidence for transport planning and more scalable monitoring of road use across the county. The case demonstrates how cloud-based computer vision can automate a narrow but high-volume local-government task when privacy and deletion rules are built into the workflow.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1773,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01331,EU_PSTW_202609_01331,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Aalborg and Municipality of Rødovre,Hospital services.,UNKNOWN,AI-powered platform for hospital rehabilitation recommendations in two danish municipalities,Municipality of Aalborg and Municipality of Rødovre; Local Government; Hospital services.; Denmark,"Aalborg and Rødovre developed an AI-powered platform to support recommendations for rehabilitation pathways. The implementation uses data from around 18,000 previous patients to generate insights on how comparable future patients might be treated.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Municipal rehabilitation planning after hospital admission requires clinicians to assess each patient’s needs, available services and likely recovery pathway, but this can consume substantial staff time and vary across cases. Aalborg and Rødovre developed an AI-powered platform to support recommendations for rehabilitation pathways. The operational problem was that municipal staff spent thousands of hours each year planning rehabilitation for patients discharged from hospitals, while historical patient data contained patterns that could help inform future treatment plans. The implementation uses data from around 18,000 previous patients to generate insights on how comparable future patients might be treated. At a conceptual level, the platform analyses patient characteristics, prior rehabilitation trajectories and outcomes to suggest possible treatment pathways. Licensed clinicians then review the recommendations and develop final plans in collaboration with the system, ensuring that professional judgement remains central. The expected impact is reduced administrative planning time, more consistent rehabilitation recommendations, better use of historical data and improved support for patients moving from hospital to municipal rehabilitation services. The case is important because it applies AI to a care-planning workflow where recommendations are decision-support outputs, not automated treatment decisions. Human clinicians remain responsible for adapting pathways to individual clinical and social circumstances.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1774,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01332,EU_PSTW_202609_01332,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Graz,Public order and safety n.e.c.,"The operational problem was that conventional sensor systems can struggle to distinguish between cars, mopeds, bicycles, scooters and other users, limiting the usefulness of traffic data for active-travel policy.",Graz outfits intersection with smart sensors to improve cyclist commute,Municipality of Graz; Local Government; Public order and safety n.e.c.; Austria,"The implementation uses light-mounted smart sensors and a GDPR-compliant video analytics platform that applies AI algorithms at the edge to classify traffic and detect behaviours such as bus-lane use by passenger vehicles, illegal turns and red-light violations. The case is relevant because it shows how AI-enabled sensing can be deployed quickly at existing street infrastructure to support urban mobility and climate-policy goals.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Cycling policy depends on accurate evidence about how different road users behave at intersections, especially where safety, illegal manoeuvres and modal shift are priorities. Graz trialled LMT’s smart traffic monitoring platform at a busy central intersection to collect real-time data and support improvements for cyclists. The operational problem was that conventional sensor systems can struggle to distinguish between cars, mopeds, bicycles, scooters and other users, limiting the usefulness of traffic data for active-travel policy. The implementation uses light-mounted smart sensors and a GDPR-compliant video analytics platform that applies AI algorithms at the edge to classify traffic and detect behaviours such as bus-lane use by passenger vehicles, illegal turns and red-light violations. At a conceptual level, the system analyses video streams, categorises road users, measures traffic patterns and produces data that can inform future junction design or enforcement priorities. The expected impact is better safety analysis, more evidence-based cycling infrastructure, improved understanding of violations and a lower-cost alternative to infrastructure-heavy monitoring systems. The case is relevant because it shows how AI-enabled sensing can be deployed quickly at existing street infrastructure to support urban mobility and climate-policy goals.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1775,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01333,EU_PSTW_202609_01333,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Staffordshire County Council,Transport,"Road-condition assessment is a core highways function, but traditional surveys can be slow, resource-intensive and dependent on manual inspection or resident reports. The operational problem was that inspectors previously relied heavily on manual checks and ReportIt submissions, which could make it harder to build a complete and comparable picture of defects across a large road network.",Staffordshire County Council implemented AI technology for road assessments,Staffordshire County Council; Local Government; Transport; United Kingdom,"The implementation allows highway inspectors to capture road video using mobile devices while carrying out their normal work. At a conceptual level, the tool applies computer-vision analysis to ordinary inspection footage and turns it into geospatial intelligence for maintenance planning.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Road-condition assessment is a core highways function, but traditional surveys can be slow, resource-intensive and dependent on manual inspection or resident reports. Staffordshire County Council introduced Vaisala RoadAI to give its highways team a more consistent and scalable way to assess carriageway condition. The operational problem was that inspectors previously relied heavily on manual checks and ReportIt submissions, which could make it harder to build a complete and comparable picture of defects across a large road network. The implementation allows highway inspectors to capture road video using mobile devices while carrying out their normal work. The RoadAI system then processes the footage and converts it into mapped road-condition data, identifying defects such as potholes, surface deterioration, pavement settlement, standing water, road-marking issues and traffic-sign problems. At a conceptual level, the tool applies computer-vision analysis to ordinary inspection footage and turns it into geospatial intelligence for maintenance planning. The expected impact is faster defect detection, more accurate prioritisation of repairs, improved asset-management data and stronger long-term planning for highways investment. The use case is significant because it embeds AI into routine field inspection rather than requiring a separate specialist survey process, helping road teams update condition evidence more frequently and consistently.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1776,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01334,EU_PSTW_202609_01334,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Meath County Council,Pollution abatement.,UNKNOWN,Air quality sensors in Trim (Ireland),Meath County Council; Local Government; Pollution abatement.; Ireland,The use case is relevant because it makes environmental data directly available to residents rather than keeping it only inside council systems.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Local air quality can vary sharply by street, traffic pattern, weather condition and time of day, but many towns have historically relied on sparse monitoring data that does not reflect neighbourhood-level exposure. Meath County Council’s Trim air-quality project installed sensors in the town to generate hyperlocal environmental information and make it visible through a public dashboard. The operational problem was to provide residents and decision-makers with more granular evidence on pollutants, traffic-related exposure and the health implications of air quality. The implementation combines air-quality sensors, the Meath Environment Platform and Trilateral Research’s STRIAD:AIR platform. At a conceptual level, sensors collect local readings, the platform stores and displays the data, and analytical tools help identify trends and potential health impacts. The expected impact is better public understanding of local air quality, stronger evidence for environmental policy, support for behaviour change and improved ability to assess whether local interventions are working. The use case is relevant because it makes environmental data directly available to residents rather than keeping it only inside council systems. It also supports a more participatory model of climate and air-quality governance, where local evidence can inform both public debate and operational decisions.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1777,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01335,EU_PSTW_202609_01335,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Sheffield City Council,Pollution abatement.,UNKNOWN,Sheffield City Council works with VivaCity on AI-powered monitoring of its Clean Air Zone,Sheffield City Council; Local Government; Pollution abatement.; United Kingdom,"Sheffield City Council worked with VivaCity to monitor the impact of its Clean Air Zone using AI-enabled traffic sensors. The implementation uses seventeen VivaCity sensors along the A61 and associated areas to collect traffic-flow data, classify road users and track changes in movement patterns.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Clean Air Zones require careful evaluation because their effects depend on how traffic patterns, vehicle types and emissions change after implementation. Sheffield City Council worked with VivaCity to monitor the impact of its Clean Air Zone using AI-enabled traffic sensors. The operational problem was to understand how traffic on key routes changed during peak and off-peak periods, and how those changes related to air-quality outcomes. The implementation uses seventeen VivaCity sensors along the A61 and associated areas to collect traffic-flow data, classify road users and track changes in movement patterns. This data is then considered alongside readings from existing air-quality sensors. At a conceptual level, computer vision classifies vehicles and movement flows, while environmental monitoring provides evidence on pollutant levels. Combining these datasets allows the council to evaluate whether policy measures are changing traffic behaviour and contributing to community-health objectives. The expected impact is more evidence-based Clean Air Zone management, better understanding of displacement or peak-period effects, stronger air-quality evaluation and improved transparency around policy outcomes. The case demonstrates how AI-enabled sensing can support environmental regulation by connecting mobility behaviour with public-health evidence.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1778,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01336,EU_PSTW_202609_01336,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Great Manchester Combined Authority(GMCA),Education not definable by level.,The operational problem is that traditional training can struggle to convey the lived experience and psychological dynamics of coercive control.,Greater Manchester adopts virtual reality for training on gender based violence,Great Manchester Combined Authority(GMCA); Local Government; Education not definable by level.; United Kingdom,"The implementation uses VR headsets to immerse learners in scenarios based on survivor accounts, helping them experience situations from the perspective of people affected by abuse. The use case is significant because it applies VR to safeguarding and social policy, where emotional understanding and behavioural recognition are central to service quality.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Professionals who support people affected by gender-based violence need to understand coercive control, trauma, survivor experience and the behaviour patterns that may not be immediately visible in standard casework. Greater Manchester funded the Unbroken virtual-reality training programme to improve awareness and response across relevant professional and support sectors. The operational problem is that traditional training can struggle to convey the lived experience and psychological dynamics of coercive control. The implementation uses VR headsets to immerse learners in scenarios based on survivor accounts, helping them experience situations from the perspective of people affected by abuse. The programme includes different learning tracks, including “Insight” for professional awareness and “Flourish” for support contexts. At a conceptual level, immersive simulation creates experiential learning: users do not simply read guidance, but encounter a structured scenario that prompts empathy, reflection and better recognition of abusive patterns. The expected impact is improved professional understanding, stronger support for survivors, better multi-agency awareness and more effective local responses to gender-based violence. The use case is significant because it applies VR to safeguarding and social policy, where emotional understanding and behavioural recognition are central to service quality.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1779,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01337,EU_PSTW_202609_01337,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Suffolk County Council,Public health services.,"People living with dementia can benefit from familiar, calming and stimulating experiences, but access to meaningful places, memories or environments may become restricted by mobility, care setting or cognitive challenges.",Virtual reality (VR) application for people living with dementia in Suffolk,Suffolk County Council; Local Government; Public health services.; United Kingdom,"It is not a general entertainment device placed in care settings without mediation; the value lies in structured, supported use by carers or professionals who understand the person’s preferences and needs. Suffolk County Council announced the launch of Reverie in February 2025, confirming that the project progressed from procurement to an implemented VR product.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"People living with dementia can benefit from familiar, calming and stimulating experiences, but access to meaningful places, memories or environments may become restricted by mobility, care setting or cognitive challenges. Suffolk County Council developed a specialist virtual-reality application to support wellbeing, cognition and communication among residents with dementia. The council’s Digital Care Team based the proposal on evidence that supported immersive experiences can improve quality of life when they are designed carefully and co-produced with users. After a competitive tender, Spark Emerging Technologies was awarded an £80,000 contract to build the platform with people living with dementia in Suffolk. The implementation resulted in Reverie, a VR solution that offers supported immersive experiences intended to help people reconnect with memories, engage cognitively and communicate in a safe environment. At a conceptual level, the system combines VR content, user-centred design, care support and reminiscence-style engagement. It is not a general entertainment device placed in care settings without mediation; the value lies in structured, supported use by carers or professionals who understand the person’s preferences and needs. The expected impact is improved wellbeing, enhanced communication, more personalised care activities and new tools for social care teams. Suffolk County Council announced the launch of Reverie in February 2025, confirming that the project progressed from procurement to an implemented VR product.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1780,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01338,EU_PSTW_202609_01338,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,South Dublin County Council,Education not definable by level.,UNKNOWN,South Dublin County Council's Think Big Space stimulates STEM learning among community members,South Dublin County Council; Local Government; Education not definable by level.; Ireland,"The use case is relevant because it treats emerging technology as a civic-learning asset, not only as an operational tool.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Communities need accessible routes into science, technology, engineering, arts and mathematics, particularly where young people and adult learners may not otherwise encounter advanced digital tools such as cloud computing, robotics or virtual reality. South Dublin County Council and Amazon Web Services created AWS Think Big Space in Tallaght as a dedicated educational lab for STEAM learning. The operational objective is to provide a place beyond the classroom where students, teachers and community members can explore technologies that are increasingly relevant to future work and public-service innovation. The implementation offers free workshops, monthly events, virtual-reality and robotics sessions, cloud-computing courses and AWS certification pathways for adult learners. At a conceptual level, the facility combines physical learning space, digital tools, structured workshops and community outreach. The expected impact is stronger digital literacy, increased interest in STEAM careers, improved access to high-quality technology resources and broader community participation in future-skills development. The use case is relevant because it treats emerging technology as a civic-learning asset, not only as an operational tool. By locating advanced learning resources in a public-library and community context, the initiative links digital inclusion, workforce development and local innovation capacity.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1781,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01339,EU_PSTW_202609_01339,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Cornwall Council,Housing development.,"Public consultation on town-centre regeneration can be limited when residents must judge proposed changes from drawings, written plans or static visualisations.",Cornwall Council brings Newquay Futures development project to life with a VR experience,Cornwall Council; Local Government; Housing development.; United Kingdom,"The implementation created a VR version of Newquay using immersive visualisation, 360-degree images, environmental sound and a cyclist’s perspective to show how proposed improvements might feel at street level.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Public consultation on town-centre regeneration can be limited when residents must judge proposed changes from drawings, written plans or static visualisations. Cornwall Council used virtual reality to make the Newquay Futures project more tangible, helping residents experience proposals for active travel infrastructure and new community spaces before they are built. The initiative supported public engagement around the future of the coastal town and its investment plan, working with Newquay Business Improvement District, Newquay Town Council, Newquay Town Team and Digital Urban. The implementation created a VR version of Newquay using immersive visualisation, 360-degree images, environmental sound and a cyclist’s perspective to show how proposed improvements might feel at street level. At a conceptual level, the system transforms planning proposals into experiential consultation, allowing citizens to see, hear and navigate potential changes rather than only read about them. After experiencing the VR environment at community events, residents could provide feedback that informed local decision-making. The expected impact is more inclusive consultation, better understanding of spatial change, richer feedback from residents and stronger legitimacy for regeneration choices. Cornwall Council described the approach as giving residents a real insight into the difference proposed changes could make, while Digital Urban framed the experience as a way to make future-place consultation more engaging and accessible.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1782,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01340,EU_PSTW_202609_01340,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Eskilstuna Kommun,Secondary education.,"Young people making education and career choices often have limited direct experience of workplaces, particularly in sectors with labour shortages or unfamiliar technical roles.",Eskilstuna Kommun develoeped virtual reality training tool for its Jobbcirkus youth employment program,Eskilstuna Kommun; Local Government; Secondary education.; Sweden,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Young people making education and career choices often have limited direct experience of workplaces, particularly in sectors with labour shortages or unfamiliar technical roles. Eskilstuna’s Jobbcirkus programme developed a VR tool to make career guidance more experiential and less stressful for students in grade 9 and other residents facing career decisions. The objective is to help users explore industries, post-secondary options and work environments before making choices that can affect education, training and employment pathways. The implementation was developed with Combitech AB and Reality Labs as a 360-degree VR programme, allowing users to simulate career and education decisions and experience work settings virtually. At a conceptual level, the application combines immersive workplace visits, guided career reflection and information about sectors with talent shortages. It can be used by study and career advisers, schools and other organisations supporting young people or adults in transition. The expected impact is better-informed career choices, stronger motivation to explore shortage occupations, and more equal access to workplace insight for students who may not otherwise visit such environments. Jobbcirkus VR app is available for download on the Meta Quest Store, indicating that the project advanced towards implementation.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1783,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01341,EU_PSTW_202609_01341,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Greater Manchester’s Violence Reduction Unit (VRU),Public order and safety n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,Virtual reality technology programme used to tackle youth violence in Greater Manchester,Greater Manchester’s Violence Reduction Unit (VRU); Local Government; Public order and safety n.e.c.; United Kingdom,"Greater Manchester’s Violence Reduction Unit worked with Greater Manchester Magistrates’ Association and Round Midnight to deliver Virtual_Decisions, a prevention programme using virtual reality as part of a wider educational curriculum. The implementation places students inside an immersive simulated situation with multiple possible outcomes, then uses facilitated discussion and a 12-week curriculum to explore decision points, consequences, empathy and alternatives to violence.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Youth violence prevention requires interventions that help children understand consequences, peer pressure and decision-making before harmful behaviour escalates. Greater Manchester’s Violence Reduction Unit worked with Greater Manchester Magistrates’ Association and Round Midnight to deliver Virtual_Decisions, a prevention programme using virtual reality as part of a wider educational curriculum. The project began with a pilot across 31 schools, involving 1,395 pupils, and focused on helping young people examine the roots of violent behaviour and make safer choices. The implementation places students inside an immersive simulated situation with multiple possible outcomes, then uses facilitated discussion and a 12-week curriculum to explore decision points, consequences, empathy and alternatives to violence. At a conceptual level, the technology creates an emotionally engaging scenario, while the educational programme converts that experience into reflection and behavioural learning. The expected impact is improved awareness of knife crime and serious violence, better decision-making among pupils and stronger prevention work in schools. Following the pilot, the programme was rolled out across Greater Manchester for pupils from Year 5 to Year 8. Later youth-justice resources continue to describe it as a 12-week virtual programme to help children avoid serious violence and knife crime.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1784,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01342,EU_PSTW_202609_01342,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Härnösand,Education n.e.c.,"Practical training for social interaction, conflict management, workplace behaviour and difficult scenarios can be hard to deliver safely through conventional classroom methods. The operational problem is that some skills require embodied experience, feedback and repetition, but real-world practice may be costly, risky or difficult to stage.",Municipality of Härnösand is building a training center that utilizes realistic virtual reality environments,Municipality of Härnösand; Local Government; Education n.e.c.; Sweden,"The use case demonstrates how a municipality can treat VR as part of adult learning and workforce capability-building, rather than as a standalone demonstration technology.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Practical training for social interaction, conflict management, workplace behaviour and difficult scenarios can be hard to deliver safely through conventional classroom methods. Härnösand’s VR studio was developed as a virtual-reality training environment where learners can practise in realistic simulated settings. The operational problem is that some skills require embodied experience, feedback and repetition, but real-world practice may be costly, risky or difficult to stage. The implementation is based in Technichus Science Center and is linked to Härnösand Municipality’s Learning Centre, creating a shared environment for training, education and development. The VR studio can be used to create scenarios for specific educational needs, allowing participants to interact with digital environments and practise situations such as social encounters, conflict handling or demanding work tasks. At a conceptual level, virtual reality provides a controlled simulation space where behaviour, movement and responses can be observed and reflected upon. The expected impact is more engaging learning, safer practice of challenging situations, better preparation for real-world interactions and a local platform for developing digital education. The use case demonstrates how a municipality can treat VR as part of adult learning and workforce capability-building, rather than as a standalone demonstration technology.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1785,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01343,EU_PSTW_202609_01343,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Horn,Cultural services.,"This supports trip planning, cultural promotion and digital access for users who cannot visit immediately.",Municipality of Horn created the “360° Horn” website for visitors to experience the city in virtual reality,Municipality of Horn; Local Government; Cultural services.; Austria,"The implementation uses high-quality 360-degree imagery that can be viewed on a computer, smartphone or VR headset. The use case is significant because it shows how virtual-reality-style content can support local economic development and cultural communication even without highly complex interactive modelling.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Smaller municipalities often need cost-effective ways to promote local heritage, cultural venues and tourism assets to people who may not yet be physically present. Horn’s 360° virtual experience was created to allow visitors to explore the city and associated cultural locations through panoramic digital media. The operational objective is to make the city more visible and attractive as a destination by offering a remote, immersive preview of places such as Kunsthaus Horn and the surrounding urban environment. The implementation uses high-quality 360-degree imagery that can be viewed on a computer, smartphone or VR headset. At a conceptual level, the website converts physical locations into navigable panoramic scenes, allowing users to look around as if standing in the space. This supports trip planning, cultural promotion and digital access for users who cannot visit immediately. The expected impact is stronger destination marketing, improved online visibility for local attractions, wider access to cultural spaces and a more engaging digital presence for the municipality. The use case is significant because it shows how virtual-reality-style content can support local economic development and cultural communication even without highly complex interactive modelling.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1786,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01344,EU_PSTW_202609_01344,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Turku Municipality,Cultural services.,"Local history can be difficult to communicate to younger audiences or visitors when it is presented only through plaques, static museum text or traditional guided tours.",Turku Municipality produces Save Turku augmented reality game for resident engagement with local history,Turku Municipality; Local Government; Cultural services.; Finland,"The implementation was developed with CTRL Reality Oy and uses AR to place actors and narrative elements into the real environment when users reach specific locations. The case is relevant because it uses AR as a civic storytelling tool, making local heritage experiential and participatory rather than purely informational.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Local history can be difficult to communicate to younger audiences or visitors when it is presented only through plaques, static museum text or traditional guided tours. Save Turku was created as an augmented-reality mobile game that turns Turku’s historic centre into an interactive adventure. The operational objective was to celebrate the city’s long history and encourage residents and visitors to engage actively with historical sites around the Old Great Square and Cathedral Park area. The implementation was developed with CTRL Reality Oy and uses AR to place actors and narrative elements into the real environment when users reach specific locations. At a conceptual level, the game combines geolocation, augmented video content, historical storytelling and puzzle-based exploration. Users follow clues, observe monuments and urban spaces carefully, and try to recover the city’s founding document with help from local characters. The expected impact is stronger public engagement with cultural heritage, more attractive history education, increased footfall in historic areas and a playful way to connect residents with place identity. The case is relevant because it uses AR as a civic storytelling tool, making local heritage experiential and participatory rather than purely informational.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1788,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01345,EU_PSTW_202609_01345,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,London Borough of Redbridge,Education not definable by level.,"Addressing domestic violence requires behavioural change interventions that help perpetrators understand the impact of their actions, yet traditional programmes often struggle to foster empathy and reflection.",London Borough of Redbridge addresses root causes of domestic violence with VR training,London Borough of Redbridge; Local Government; Education not definable by level.; United Kingdom,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Addressing domestic violence requires behavioural change interventions that help perpetrators understand the impact of their actions, yet traditional programmes often struggle to foster empathy and reflection. In Redbridge, local authorities sought innovative approaches to strengthen existing intervention programmes. The objective of this pilot is to enhance offender awareness by enabling participants to experience domestic violence scenarios from a child’s perspective. The implementation introduces immersive virtual reality training capsules integrated into an eight-week behavioural programme. Participants are exposed to realistic scenarios designed to evoke emotional understanding and reflection, supported by structured facilitation. Academic researchers assess participant responses and attitudinal changes. Expected impacts include deeper emotional engagement, improved programme effectiveness and stronger evidence for behaviour change. Qualitative results suggest increased empathy, improved self-reflection and potential reductions in repeat offending, supporting future scaling of immersive interventions.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1789,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01346,EU_PSTW_202609_01346,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Karlskrona Municipality,Community development.,"In Karlskrona, fragmented datasets limited the ability to simulate future development scenarios.",Karlskrona generates digital twin to show the city's present and future in three dimensions,Karlskrona Municipality; Local Government; Community development.; Sweden,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Municipal planning increasingly requires integrated spatial analysis and effective citizen engagement to manage complex urban environments. In Karlskrona, fragmented datasets limited the ability to simulate future development scenarios. The objective of this project is to create a comprehensive digital representation of the city to support planning, infrastructure management and public engagement. The implementation combines aerial imagery, building plans, drone data, IoT inputs and departmental datasets into a three-dimensional digital twin. The model supports internal analysis, such as infrastructure planning, and immersive engagement through AR and VR events for residents. Expected impacts include more informed decision-making, improved coordination across departments and enhanced transparency. Qualitative outcomes include stronger public participation, better scenario testing and streamlined municipal processes.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F07_DATA_VISIBILITY_INTEGRATION_GAP,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1790,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01347,EU_PSTW_202609_01347,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Skanderborg Municipality,Education not definable by level.,People with anxiety often struggle to engage with traditional therapeutic approaches due to fear of real-world exposure.,Skanderborg Municipality uses virtual reality to help people with anxiety,Skanderborg Municipality; Local Government; Education not definable by level.; Denmark,"The implementation uses virtual reality to recreate social situations that users can experience progressively, guided by professionals.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"People with anxiety often struggle to engage with traditional therapeutic approaches due to fear of real-world exposure. In Skanderborg, social and employment services explored new welfare technologies to support gradual treatment. The objective of this pilot is to provide a safe, controlled environment for exposure-based therapy. The implementation uses virtual reality to recreate social situations that users can experience progressively, guided by professionals. The approach allows customisation to individual needs and integration into existing care pathways. Expected impacts include improved treatment engagement, reduced anxiety symptoms and enhanced therapeutic outcomes. Qualitative benefits include increased confidence, better coping strategies and a scalable model for mental health support.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1791,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01348,EU_PSTW_202609_01348,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Skovde,Education not definable by level.,UNKNOWN,Municipality of Skövde provides funds to purchase driving simulators to improve training and reduce emissions,Municipality of Skovde; Local Government; Education not definable by level.; Sweden,"Expected impacts include increased training capacity, reduced fuel use and emissions, lower wear-and-tear costs on vehicles and improved safety through risk-free scenario practice.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Vocational education programmes in transport, construction and heavy vehicle operation require extensive practical driving hours, which can limit student access, increase fuel consumption and generate avoidable emissions. In Skövde, vocational teachers identified capacity constraints and environmental impacts linked to traditional vehicle-based instruction. Stakeholders include the municipality, vocational schools, teachers and students in vehicle transport and construction programmes. The objective is to expand access to practical training while reducing environmental footprint and operational costs. The solution involves the deployment of eight advanced driving simulators that replicate vehicle, truck and machinery operation scenarios through immersive software environments. Simulation platforms integrate realistic physics engines, scenario-based training modules and performance analytics dashboards to track learner progress. These systems complement, rather than fully replace, on-road instruction and are embedded within the existing curriculum. Expected impacts include increased training capacity, reduced fuel use and emissions, lower wear-and-tear costs on vehicles and improved safety through risk-free scenario practice.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1793,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01349,EU_PSTW_202609_01349,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Helsinki,Public health services.,UNKNOWN,City of Helsinki evaluates sensory room and virtual reality therapy at Kustaankartano Senior Centre,City of Helsinki; Local Government; Public health services.; Finland,"The pilot demonstrates reduced stress levels among residents, lower staff workload, and decreased use of antidepressant medication.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Older adults with memory disorders often experience anxiety, stress, and reduced quality of life, placing pressure on care staff and increasing reliance on medication. The City of Helsinki sought innovative, non-pharmacological approaches to improve wellbeing in residential care settings. This pilot, developed at the Kustaankartano Senior Centre, addresses the need for calming, engaging therapeutic environments for residents with cognitive impairment. Stakeholders include residents, care staff, healthcare providers, and city authorities. The objective is to reduce stress, improve mood, and support care delivery through immersive and sensory-based interventions. The solution combines a sensory room featuring natural elements with interactive digital installations and virtual reality experiences. Residents can engage with calming nature simulations or creative activities that stimulate senses and memories. These interventions are integrated into daily care routines and tailored to individual needs. The pilot demonstrates reduced stress levels among residents, lower staff workload, and decreased use of antidepressant medication. It improves quality of life, supports staff efficiency, and provides evidence for scalable, technology-enabled therapies in elder care.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1794,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01350,EU_PSTW_202609_01350,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Greater London Authority,Recreational and sporting services.,UNKNOWN,Greater London Authority places city planning into virtual reality with Square Mile VR project,Greater London Authority; Local Government; Recreational and sporting services.; United Kingdom,"The objective of this use case is to improve planning decisions, pre-application discussions and public consultations by providing accurate, city-wide visual context for proposed developments. Over 80% of London boroughs use the platform, reporting improved transparency, reduced planning friction and better understanding of impacts, delivering time and cost savings while increasing confidence in the city’s long-term development strategy.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Urban planning in London involves balancing growth, density and heritage across multiple boroughs, while ensuring transparency and public trust in decision-making. The Greater London Authority (GLA), borough planning departments, developers and citizens require clear ways to understand how new developments affect their surroundings. The objective of this use case is to improve planning decisions, pre-application discussions and public consultations by providing accurate, city-wide visual context for proposed developments. The implementation relies on a highly detailed three-dimensional digital model of London, covering the full 1,619 km² of the capital with accuracy down to 15 cm. The platform integrates geospatial data, building information and planning datasets, allowing users to visualise massing, height, density and sightlines in an interactive environment, including immersive virtual reality. It integrates with planning workflows used by boroughs and developers. The results include faster and more informed planning decisions, improved quality of submissions and clearer communication with citizens. Over 80% of London boroughs use the platform, reporting improved transparency, reduced planning friction and better understanding of impacts, delivering time and cost savings while increasing confidence in the city’s long-term development strategy.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1795,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01351,EU_PSTW_202609_01351,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Trafford Council,Community development.,UNKNOWN,Trafford Council mixes virtual reality with online engagement for Urmston Active Neighbourhood,Trafford Council; Local Government; Community development.; United Kingdom,"Residents now use the council's virtual room to find data, suggested solutions, and maps in a format similar to the in-person session.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Trafford Council started with a pre-COVID listening session that generated more than 3,400 comments on walking and cycling plans for Urmston. Residents now use the council's virtual room to find data, suggested solutions, and maps in a format similar to the in-person session. Project leaders conduct webchats with up to 20 participants in each session for additional feedback. Engaged residents can help those without computers understand the planning process as project ambassadors.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,NOT_IN_USE,미사용,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1796,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01352,EU_PSTW_202609_01352,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Fingal County Council,Community development.,"Urban regeneration projects often struggle with public engagement, as long-term plans can be difficult for residents to visualise. Fingal County Council addressed this challenge in Balbriggan by creating a 3D virtual reality experience showcasing six years of planned development.","Fingal County Council, IE shows Balbriggan residents the future with a 3D Virtual Reality plan",Fingal County Council; Local Government; Community development.; Ireland,"Users explore the town’s future layout using VR headsets maintained at the Our Balbriggan Hub, with staff available to answer questions.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Urban regeneration projects often struggle with public engagement, as long-term plans can be difficult for residents to visualise. Fingal County Council addressed this challenge in Balbriggan by creating a 3D virtual reality experience showcasing six years of planned development. Stakeholders include local residents, planners and county operations staff. The objective is to improve transparency, community involvement and understanding of how regeneration initiatives will affect daily life. The VR environment presents planned infrastructure such as smart public furniture, maker spaces, micro-mobility hubs and harbour upgrades. Users explore the town’s future layout using VR headsets maintained at the Our Balbriggan Hub, with staff available to answer questions. The system integrates urban planning data and architectural models into an immersive experience. The impact has been improved public understanding, more informed feedback and stronger community buy-in. The project demonstrates how immersive technologies can enhance participatory planning, reduce consultation barriers and support smarter, citizen-centred urban development.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1797,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01353,EU_PSTW_202609_01353,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Fingal County Council and Cork county council,Public order and safety n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,"Fingal County Council and Cork county council partnered with AVIVA Driving School to launch ""Your Life - Your Choice"" VR",Fingal County Council and Cork county council; Local Government; Public order and safety n.e.c.; Ireland,"The programme uses immersive virtual reality headsets to simulate a car journey from the passenger’s perspective, culminating in a realistic traffic accident scenario.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Fingal County Council and Cork County Council partnered with AVIVA Driving School to deliver the “Your Life – Your Choice” virtual reality road safety experience targeting young drivers aged 16 to 25. Road accidents involving distraction and speeding remain a significant public safety concern among this age group. The programme uses immersive virtual reality headsets to simulate a car journey from the passenger’s perspective, culminating in a realistic traffic accident scenario. The simulation reconstructs contributory factors such as mobile phone distraction and excessive speed, alongside the serious consequences of collisions. Interactive audiovisual elements create an experiential learning environment designed to influence attitudes and risk perception. By placing participants in a controlled yet immersive scenario, the initiative aims to strengthen awareness of dangerous driving behaviours. The programme supports preventive education efforts and enhances engagement compared with traditional classroom-based road safety campaigns.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1798,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01354,EU_PSTW_202609_01354,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Hertfordshire County Council,Housing and community amenities n.e.c.,"Local highway authorities face persistent challenges from potholes, which damage vehicles, increase accident risk and generate high maintenance costs through labour-intensive repair processes. The implementation deploys a robotic system equipped with sensors, cameras and automated repair mechanisms capable of identifying surface depressions, assessing their size and filling them without manual intervention.",Pothole ROBOT hits the roads in Hertfordshire,Hertfordshire County Council; Local Government; Housing and community amenities n.e.c.; United Kingdom,The objective of this use case is to modernise road maintenance by automating pothole detection and repair.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Local highway authorities face persistent challenges from potholes, which damage vehicles, increase accident risk and generate high maintenance costs through labour-intensive repair processes. Hertfordshire County Council sought a solution to improve repair speed, consistency and worker safety while minimising disruption to road users. The objective of this use case is to modernise road maintenance by automating pothole detection and repair. The implementation deploys a robotic system equipped with sensors, cameras and automated repair mechanisms capable of identifying surface depressions, assessing their size and filling them without manual intervention. The system combines computer vision, depth measurement and automated material application, integrating with maintenance planning processes to log repairs and prioritise intervention areas. The reported impact shows that repairs can be completed up to 70% faster than traditional methods, with fewer road closures and reduced exposure of workers to live traffic. Qualitative outcomes include improved road condition reliability, lower operational costs over time, enhanced worker safety and increased public satisfaction with road maintenance services.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1801,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01355,EU_PSTW_202609_01355,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Suma Gestión Tributaria. Diputación de Alicante,General public services n.e.c.,"Municipal administrations routinely handle large volumes of repetitive citizen enquiries related to local services, opening hours and administrative procedures, which places pressure on staff and slows response times.",Chatbot alicante,Suma Gestión Tributaria. Diputación de Alicante; Local Government; General public services n.e.c.; Spain,"The objective of this use case is to provide fast, consistent answers to general enquiries through digital channels.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Municipal administrations routinely handle large volumes of repetitive citizen enquiries related to local services, opening hours and administrative procedures, which places pressure on staff and slows response times. The City of Alicante aimed to improve accessibility to information while optimising internal resources. The objective of this use case is to provide fast, consistent answers to general enquiries through digital channels. The implementation introduces a conversational interface embedded in the municipal website, connected to curated knowledge bases containing validated service information. The system retrieves relevant answers and directs users to appropriate online resources or offices when needed, integrating with existing content management systems. The impact includes reduced call centre workload, improved response times and increased availability of information outside office hours. Qualitative benefits include enhanced citizen experience, greater service consistency and more efficient allocation of municipal staff to complex cases.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK|F06_HIGH_VOLUME_SCALABILITY_BOTTLENECK,S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1802,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01356,EU_PSTW_202609_01356,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Suma Gestión Tributaria. Diputación de Alicante,General public services n.e.c.,"Accessing municipal services can be difficult for citizens facing mobility constraints, limited opening hours or low familiarity with traditional digital portals.",Office in the metaverse through mobile phone,Suma Gestión Tributaria. Diputación de Alicante; Local Government; General public services n.e.c.; Spain,"The objective of this use case is to provide an immersive, user-friendly digital environment for interacting with public services.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Accessing municipal services can be difficult for citizens facing mobility constraints, limited opening hours or low familiarity with traditional digital portals. Alicante sought to explore new interaction models that improve accessibility and engagement. The objective of this use case is to provide an immersive, user-friendly digital environment for interacting with public services. The implementation delivers a virtual office accessible via mobile devices, where citizens create avatars and navigate a three-dimensional municipal space to consult services and obtain guidance. The environment integrates service information, interaction tools and navigation support within a simulated public office setting. The impact includes improved accessibility, increased citizen engagement and a more inclusive service channel. Qualitative results suggest higher user interest, improved understanding of available services and a novel complement to traditional e-government platforms.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1803,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01357,EU_PSTW_202609_01357,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Suma Gestión Tributaria. Diputación de Alicante,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.",UNKNOWN,Innovation in Local Government: digital transformation and GeoAI for Data Management.,"Suma Gestión Tributaria. Diputación de Alicante; Local Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Spain",The objective of this use case is to identify buildings that are not correctly registered for taxation.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Local governments often face revenue losses due to incomplete or inconsistent property data spread across multiple administrative databases. Alicante sought to improve tax fairness and revenue collection. The objective of this use case is to identify buildings that are not correctly registered for taxation. The implementation integrates geospatial analysis with data-sharing across cadastral, planning and utility databases. By cross-referencing spatial footprints with administrative records, discrepancies are detected and flagged for review. The impact includes increased tax revenue, improved data accuracy and more efficient enforcement processes. Qualitative benefits include fairer taxation, improved inter-agency cooperation and stronger financial sustainability for local government.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1804,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01358,EU_PSTW_202609_01358,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Flanders Investment and Trade,"General economic, commercial and labour affair","Previously, case handling relied on Microsoft Dynamics for customer relationship management and SharePoint for document storage, requiring manual searches through past files and reports.",AI within the Flanders Investment and Trade organisation,"Flanders Investment and Trade; Regional Government; General economic, commercial and labour affair; Belgium","To improve efficiency and knowledge reuse, FIT implemented a natural language processing solution designed to partially automate responses to routine enquiries.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Flanders Investment and Trade (FIT), the regional trade promotion organisation, manages numerous ‘trade cases’ submitted by Flemish companies seeking information on foreign markets, regulations, partners or opportunities. Previously, case handling relied on Microsoft Dynamics for customer relationship management and SharePoint for document storage, requiring manual searches through past files and reports. To improve efficiency and knowledge reuse, FIT implemented a natural language processing solution designed to partially automate responses to routine enquiries. Trained on a large dataset of historical trade questions and answers, the model functions as a semantic search engine that interprets the full context of incoming questions rather than matching isolated keywords. The system retrieves relevant prior answers and presents suggestions directly within the CRM interface through an additional tab. Staff review and adapt these suggestions before responding. The solution reduces search time, accelerates handling of frequently asked questions and strengthens organisational knowledge management while maintaining human oversight for complex or strategic cases.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1805,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01359,EU_PSTW_202609_01359,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Federal Public Service Policy ans Support (FPS BOSA),General services.,"Following the EU Web Accessibility Directive requiring public sector websites to meet accessibility standards, Belgium’s Federal Public Service Policy and Support (BOSA) sought to improve consistency and reduce manual errors in compliance assessments.",AI-enable accessibility check tool to make EU-compliant federal websites,Federal Public Service Policy ans Support (FPS BOSA); Central-Government; General services.; Belgium,BOSA integrated an AI-driven open-source module into its accessibility checking tool to automate detection of common accessibility issues. The tool is available for use by federal agencies to support continuous monitoring and corrective action.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Following the EU Web Accessibility Directive requiring public sector websites to meet accessibility standards, Belgium’s Federal Public Service Policy and Support (BOSA) sought to improve consistency and reduce manual errors in compliance assessments. Traditional accessibility checks relied heavily on human evaluation, which could be time-consuming and inconsistent. BOSA integrated an AI-driven open-source module into its accessibility checking tool to automate detection of common accessibility issues. The system analyses website code and content, identifying potential non-compliance with accessibility guidelines such as missing alternative text, improper structure or contrast issues. Automated classification algorithms flag problematic elements and generate structured reports for public organisations. The tool is available for use by federal agencies to support continuous monitoring and corrective action. By combining automated scanning with human review, the solution increases efficiency, standardises evaluation processes and supports EU-compliant digital services across federal institutions.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK|F08_ERROR_COMPLIANCE_RISK,S7_GENERATIVE_CONTENT_CREATION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1806,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01360,EU_PSTW_202609_01360,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Flemish public employment service (VDAB),Unemployment.,Traditional standardised tests offered limited adaptability to individual profiles and labour market trends.,The Flemish public employment service reinvents orientation tests with AI,Flemish public employment service (VDAB); Regional Government; Unemployment.; Belgium,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"VDAB, the Flemish public employment service, modernised its career orientation testing system to provide more personalised guidance for job seekers. Traditional standardised tests offered limited adaptability to individual profiles and labour market trends. In collaboration with Radix, VDAB developed Orient 2.0, a digital orientation tool incorporating machine-learning techniques to tailor assessments dynamically. The system analyses user responses and behavioural patterns to predict professional interests and suggest relevant career paths. Models are trained on historical test data and employment outcomes, enabling adaptive question sequencing and personalised feedback. The tool integrates into VDAB’s digital platform, supporting counsellors in advising job seekers while preserving professional oversight. By delivering customised assessments at scale, Orient 2.0 enhances user engagement, improves relevance of career recommendations and increases efficiency in employment guidance services. The approach strengthens data-driven labour market orientation while maintaining human involvement in final counselling decisions.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1807,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01361,EU_PSTW_202609_01361,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Brussels Airport,Transport,UNKNOWN,Brussels Airport Company – Check-in Forecasting Project (Belgium),Brussels Airport; Central-Government; Transport; Belgium,"In collaboration with Radix, predictive models were developed using historical passenger survey data and flight characteristics.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"As part of its Airport Operations Plan, Brussels Airport Company sought to optimise workforce allocation by forecasting passenger arrivals at check-in counters in 30-minute intervals. Fluctuating passenger flows can create congestion or underutilised staff capacity. In collaboration with Radix, predictive models were developed using historical passenger survey data and flight characteristics. Machine-learning algorithms analyse variables such as flight type, destination and traveller behaviour patterns to estimate arrival volumes at specific times. Forecast outputs are integrated into operational dashboards used by airport managers, who share insights with partners to align staffing levels. By anticipating peak loads more accurately, the system improves passenger experience, reduces queues and enhances cost efficiency. The data-driven approach supports coordinated planning across stakeholders and strengthens real-time operational decision-making within the airport environment.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1808,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01362,EU_PSTW_202609_01362,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Brussels Airport,Transport,UNKNOWN,Brussels Airport Company – Passenger Reduced Mobility (PRM) Forecasting (Belgium),Brussels Airport; Central-Government; Transport; Belgium,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Brussels Airport Company aimed to improve planning for assistance services provided to passengers with reduced mobility (PRMs), a resource-intensive yet essential service. Daily fluctuations in PRM numbers require accurate staffing forecasts to maintain service quality without incurring excessive costs. As part of the Airport Operations Plan, predictive analytics models were developed to estimate PRM demand based on flight data, historical assistance records and seasonal trends. Machine-learning techniques generate forecasts that inform workforce scheduling and coordination among service partners. Outputs are integrated into operational dashboards, enabling proactive capacity planning. By improving demand prediction, the airport enhances service reliability for PRMs, reduces waiting times and optimises staff allocation. The initiative balances inclusivity and operational efficiency, ensuring high-quality assistance while maintaining financial sustainability.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1809,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01363,EU_PSTW_202609_01363,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Brussels Airport,Transport,"Integrated into operational dashboards, the predictions enable airport managers to anticipate peak loads and mitigate delays.",Brussels Airport Company – Baggage Forecasting Project (Belgium),Brussels Airport; Central-Government; Transport; Belgium,"To prevent bottlenecks in baggage handling, Brussels Airport Company implemented predictive analytics to forecast the number of bags per flight, with particular focus on time-critical transfer baggage.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"To prevent bottlenecks in baggage handling, Brussels Airport Company implemented predictive analytics to forecast the number of bags per flight, with particular focus on time-critical transfer baggage. With an average of 17,150 departing bags daily in January 2023, including 5,796 transfer bags, accurate forecasting is essential for smooth operations. Machine-learning models analyse historical baggage volumes, flight schedules and transfer patterns to estimate expected bag counts at granular intervals. The forecasts support allocation of staff and conveyor resources within the baggage handling system. Integrated into operational dashboards, the predictions enable airport managers to anticipate peak loads and mitigate delays. The approach improves processing efficiency, reduces congestion risks and enhances overall airport performance. By leveraging data-driven forecasting, the airport strengthens coordination among operational partners and supports a reliable passenger experience.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1810,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01364,EU_PSTW_202609_01364,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Statistics Flanders,General services.,UNKNOWN,AI-Based Public Sentiment Analysis – Statistics Flanders (Belgium),Statistics Flanders; Regional Government; General services.; Belgium,"Statistics Flanders explored the use of publicly available social media data to complement official statistics with timely insights into public sentiment. In collaboration with a technology partner, the agency developed an AI-powered dashboard analysing up to 50,000 Flemish tweets per month.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Statistics Flanders explored the use of publicly available social media data to complement official statistics with timely insights into public sentiment. The objective was to assess societal reactions to major events such as the COVID-19 pandemic and the war in Ukraine. In collaboration with a technology partner, the agency developed an AI-powered dashboard analysing up to 50,000 Flemish tweets per month. Natural language processing and sentiment analysis models classify posts by topic and emotional tone, generating aggregate indicators displayed in interactive visualisations. The system enables trend analysis over time and comparison across themes. While not replacing traditional survey methods, the tool provides experimental, complementary insights into societal perceptions. The pilot enhances methodological innovation, expands data sources and supports evidence-based policy discussions while maintaining transparency regarding data limitations and representativeness.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1811,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01365,EU_PSTW_202609_01365,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Prague,Police Services,UNKNOWN,AI-Powered Speed Measurement – Municipal Police of Prague,Municipality of Prague; Local Government; Police Services; Czechia,"The system comprises over 60 devices across the city, including more than 40 section-control installations that calculate average speed between two points using induction loops embedded in the road surface, and around 20 radar-based cameras measuring instantaneous speed.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Municipal Police of Prague operate an automated speed enforcement system to improve road safety and ensure compliance with traffic regulations. The system comprises over 60 devices across the city, including more than 40 section-control installations that calculate average speed between two points using induction loops embedded in the road surface, and around 20 radar-based cameras measuring instantaneous speed. Captured data include vehicle images and licence plate numbers, with automatic number plate recognition applied in designated subsystems. The technology processes speed measurements and generates violation records, producing approximately 300,000 fines annually. While detection and data processing are automated, all sanction decisions are reviewed by a human officer before issuance, ensuring procedural oversight. The system enhances enforcement coverage, increases consistency in speed control and supports deterrence of traffic offences. By combining automated measurement with human validation, Prague strengthens road safety management while maintaining administrative accountability.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1812,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01366,EU_PSTW_202609_01366,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Ministry of Interior,General services.,UNKNOWN,Algorithm based quality control of presidential candidates,Ministry of Interior; Central-Government; General services.; Czechia,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"To ensure compliance with legal requirements for presidential candidacy, Czech authorities apply statistical verification to nomination signatures submitted by candidates. Candidates may qualify either through parliamentary support or by collecting 50,000 voter signatures. In the latter case, the Ministry of Interior conducts a statistical quality control process to verify that signatories are eligible voters. An algorithm randomly selects two batches of 8,500 signatures for detailed validation against population registers. Error rates identified in the samples are extrapolated to estimate the overall validity of submitted signatures. The process resembles statistical quality control methods used in auditing, enabling efficient review of large datasets without checking every entry individually. While the approach accelerates verification and standardises assessment, it has generated debate regarding sampling design and accuracy. In the 2013 election cycle, two candidates were excluded following application of this procedure. The system aims to balance administrative feasibility with electoral integrity safeguards.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1813,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01367,EU_PSTW_202609_01367,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""social_nonprofit""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,National Tax Authority,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.",Tax administrations face increasing challenges in detecting complex and evolving forms of tax fraud while ensuring compliance with strict data protection and transparency requirements.,Algorithm to detect fraud at the National Tax Authority in Hungary,"National Tax Authority; Central-Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Hungary",The objective of this use case is to improve the effectiveness of fraud prevention and risk analysis while safeguarding taxpayer rights and data confidentiality. The implementation focuses on developing a semantic data asset methodology that enables structured use of anonymised tax data for advanced analytical research.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Tax administrations face increasing challenges in detecting complex and evolving forms of tax fraud while ensuring compliance with strict data protection and transparency requirements. The Hungarian National Tax Authority (NAV) manages extensive datasets related to declarations, transactions and compliance behaviour, yet traditional analytical approaches limit the ability to uncover hidden patterns at scale. To address this, NAV established a dedicated working group in April 2022 to explore advanced, data-driven approaches to fraud detection within a clearly defined legal and organisational framework. The objective of this use case is to improve the effectiveness of fraud prevention and risk analysis while safeguarding taxpayer rights and data confidentiality. The implementation focuses on developing a semantic data asset methodology that enables structured use of anonymised tax data for advanced analytical research. This includes the design of algorithms for pattern detection, as well as robust validation and testing methodologies to assess reliability and bias. Strong governance mechanisms ensure that data protection rules define the operational boundaries of the work. The expected impact includes improved identification of fraudulent behaviour, more targeted inspections and better allocation of investigative resources. Qualitative outcomes include enhanced analytical capacity, stronger evidence-based decision-making and a scalable methodological foundation for future tax compliance initiatives.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1814,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01368,EU_PSTW_202609_01368,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,National Institute for Social Security (INPS),Sickness and disability.,UNKNOWN,Blockchain-enhanced INPS disability card,National Institute for Social Security (INPS); Central-Government; Sickness and disability.; Italy,"The objective of this use case is to improve reliability, transparency and accessibility of disability certification.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"People with disabilities often face repeated verification processes when accessing services, while authorities must ensure eligibility, prevent fraud and protect personal data. INPS aimed to strengthen the European Disability Card system. The objective of this use case is to improve reliability, transparency and accessibility of disability certification. The implementation supports the physical card with a secure distributed digital infrastructure that records eligibility attributes and access rights in a tamper-resistant manner. Service providers can verify entitlements quickly without accessing unnecessary personal data. The impact includes reduced fraud risk, faster service access and improved user experience. Qualitative benefits include greater trust, better interoperability across services and improved protection of sensitive information.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PLANNED,계획,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1817,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01369,EU_PSTW_202609_01369,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Cassa Depositi e Prestiti,R&D general public services.,UNKNOWN,"Blockchain-enabled ""building bonus"" credits in Italy",Cassa Depositi e Prestiti; Central-Government; R&D general public services.; Italy,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Italy’s “110% superbonus” policy generated complex administrative flows for managing construction tax credits, creating risks of fraud and inefficiency. The objective of this proof of concept is to improve transparency and traceability of bonus payments to construction firms. The implementation tokenises building credits on a distributed ledger, enabling secure tracking of issuance, transfer and redemption. The impact includes improved oversight, reduced fraud risk and more efficient credit circulation. Qualitative outcomes include stronger confidence in public incentive schemes and improved administrative control.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PLANNED,계획,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1820,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01370,EU_PSTW_202609_01370,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,National Institute for Social Security (INPS),Social protection n.e.c.,"In Italy, a concept known as the “Welfare Ticket” was developed to address these challenges by enabling temporary, portable social security coverage for gig economy workers.",Welfare ticket - a blockchian-based platform for the gig economy environment,National Institute for Social Security (INPS); Central-Government; Social protection n.e.c.; Italy,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Labour platforms and gig workers often operate outside traditional social security frameworks, creating gaps in insurance coverage and administrative complexity for short-term or task-based work. In Italy, a concept known as the “Welfare Ticket” was developed to address these challenges by enabling temporary, portable social security coverage for gig economy workers. Stakeholders included employers, platform workers, and social security institutions, with the objective of simplifying compliance while ensuring that workers remained insured during the execution of specific tasks. The proposed solution centred on a shared digital register where employers could issue a time-bound social security ticket linked to a specific job. The platform design relied on distributed ledger technology to record the issuance, validity period, and redemption of welfare tickets in an immutable and auditable manner. Each transaction would be visible to authorised parties, reducing disputes and administrative reconciliation. Although the concept was developed through an enterprise design thinking process and documented by INPS, it did not progress to operational deployment. Nevertheless, the initiative provided a structured blueprint for how decentralised registers could support more flexible labour markets, influencing later discussions on portable benefits and digital welfare infrastructure.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PLANNED,계획,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1822,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01371,EU_PSTW_202609_01371,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Discovergy GmbH,Fuel and energy,Traditional energy markets offer limited support for local energy exchange and self-consumption. The BloGPV project addresses this challenge by enabling local trading of solar electricity among neighbours through a virtual storage concept.,Blockchain-based virtual large-scale storage for photovoltaics system operators,Discovergy GmbH; Consortium; Fuel and energy; Germany,The objective is to maintain the economic viability of solar installations and strengthen local renewable energy use.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"As feed-in tariffs are phased out, photovoltaic system operators face declining revenues, while consumers seek affordable green electricity. Traditional energy markets offer limited support for local energy exchange and self-consumption. The BloGPV project addresses this challenge by enabling local trading of solar electricity among neighbours through a virtual storage concept. Stakeholders include PV system operators, electricity consumers, and local energy communities. The objective is to maintain the economic viability of solar installations and strengthen local renewable energy use. The solution creates a distributed virtual storage network that connects producers and consumers, allowing surplus electricity to be traded locally. Automated transaction mechanisms record energy exchanges and settlements within the network, ensuring transparency and reliability. By coordinating production and consumption, the system reduces reliance on centralised grids and supports local balancing. The impact includes improved returns for solar producers, more attractive green electricity for consumers, and increased local self-sufficiency. BloGPV contributes to decentralised energy systems and offers a replicable model for community-based renewable energy trading.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1836,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01372,EU_PSTW_202609_01372,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Norway’s central register and Norway's tax authority,General public services n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,Norway steps into the metaverse with decentraland tax office,Norway’s central register and Norway's tax authority; Central-Government; General public services n.e.c.; Norway,The objective of this use case is to explore whether immersive virtual spaces can complement traditional service channels for providing tax-related information and guidance.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Public administrations increasingly seek new ways to reach citizens and businesses, particularly younger and digitally native audiences, while improving accessibility to information and services. In Norway, authorities identified emerging digital environments as potential new channels for public engagement. The objective of this use case is to explore whether immersive virtual spaces can complement traditional service channels for providing tax-related information and guidance. The implementation involves establishing a virtual tax office within Decentraland, a decentralised digital platform, through a partnership between Brønnøysund Register Centre, the national tax authority Skatteetaten and external advisors. The virtual office replicates elements of a physical service point, allowing users to access information, attend events and interact with guidance resources in a three-dimensional environment. The pilot focuses on experimentation, user experience and organisational learning rather than transactional services. Expected impacts include insights into citizen engagement in virtual environments, expanded outreach to new user groups and lessons on integrating public services into emerging digital ecosystems. Qualitative benefits include increased visibility of public institutions, innovation capacity building and evidence to inform future digital service strategies.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1837,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01373,EU_PSTW_202609_01373,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Helsinki,R&D general public services.,UNKNOWN,The City of Helsinki is investigating the benefits of generative artificial intelligence in various work tasks,City of Helsinki; Local Government; R&D general public services.; Finland,"The objective of this use case is to assess how everyday work processes can be streamlined and supported in a controlled and responsible manner, while maintaining public sector values such as reliability, transparency and staff wellbeing. Usage is monitored to understand task suitability, workflow integration and organisational implications rather than to automate decisions.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Municipal administrations face increasing pressure to deliver high-quality services while managing growing volumes of documents, correspondence and internal coordination tasks. In the City of Helsinki, employees across departments spend significant time on routine knowledge work, limiting their capacity for strategic analysis, collaboration and citizen-facing activities. The objective of this use case is to assess how everyday work processes can be streamlined and supported in a controlled and responsible manner, while maintaining public sector values such as reliability, transparency and staff wellbeing. The implementation consists of a structured experimentation phase within the existing Microsoft 365 work environment, where selected employees test integrated digital assistance functions. These include support for drafting and reviewing documents, summarising lengthy materials, managing email overload and organising information across files and meetings. Usage is monitored to understand task suitability, workflow integration and organisational implications rather than to automate decisions. The expected impact includes reduced administrative workload, time savings in routine tasks and improved consistency in document handling. Qualitative results focus on improved employee experience, better focus on high-value work and evidence-based insights to guide future adoption decisions, ensuring that productivity gains align with ethical, organisational and operational requirements.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1838,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01374,EU_PSTW_202609_01374,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Agenzia delle Dogane e dei Monopoli (ADM),"General economic, commercial and labour affair",Certification processes are often fragmented across authorities and perceived as administrative burdens rather than value drivers.,Traceability Platform for Alcoholic Production Certification,"Agenzia delle Dogane e dei Monopoli (ADM); Central-Government; General economic, commercial and labour affair; Italy",UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Producers of high-quality regional spirits face increasing regulatory and market demands for traceability, authenticity, and proof of origin. Certification processes are often fragmented across authorities and perceived as administrative burdens rather than value drivers. This project addresses the need to transform certification into a strategic asset for “Made in Italy” products, starting with Genepì del Piemonte. Stakeholders include producers, certification bodies, public administrations, and consumers. The objective is to ensure transparent tracking of production batches while enhancing product value and consumer trust. The solution implements a platform-based certification system that records production and certification data across the supply chain. Information is securely shared among authorised administrations, creating a one-stop framework for compliance and verification. Batch-level traceability enables producers to demonstrate authenticity and quality to markets and regulators. The impact includes reduced administrative complexity, improved transparency, and stronger brand protection for regional products. The initiative supports innovation in agri-food certification and provides a scalable model for traceability across other protected productions.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1839,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01375,EU_PSTW_202609_01375,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Bicocca and Padua University,Tertiary education.,Universities face risks of document falsification and administrative burdens in responding to verification requests from employers.,BLOCKCERTS: Blockchain-based university degrees at Bicocca and Padua University in Italy,Bicocca and Padua University; Academic-Research; Tertiary education.; Italy,"The University of Milan Bicocca, in collaboration with the Cineca inter-university consortium, implemented a blockchain-based certification system to safeguard the authenticity of academic degrees and official documents.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The University of Milan Bicocca, in collaboration with the Cineca inter-university consortium, implemented a blockchain-based certification system to safeguard the authenticity of academic degrees and official documents. Universities face risks of document falsification and administrative burdens in responding to verification requests from employers. The solution issues certified digital diplomas anchored to a blockchain infrastructure, ensuring immutability and verifiable integrity. Cryptographic hashes of degree documents are recorded on the distributed ledger, allowing third parties to validate authenticity without accessing personal data or contacting the issuing institution. The system is based on open standards for blockchain certification, enabling secure, tamper-evident verification through a simple digital check. By digitising credential validation, the initiative reduces administrative workload, accelerates employer verification processes and strengthens trust in academic qualifications. The approach enhances transparency and international portability of degrees while preserving data protection safeguards.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1842,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01376,EU_PSTW_202609_01376,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Automobile Club d'Italia,Transport,"Vehicle ownership history and documentation verification traditionally require manual administrative checks. Cryptographic notarisation ensures that stored records cannot be altered retroactively, providing tamper-evident documentation of key vehicle events.",App ACI Space - blockchain-based notarisation,Automobile Club d'Italia; Central-Government; Transport; Italy,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Automobile Club of Italy (ACI), in cooperation with EY, developed the ACI Space application to enable digital certification of vehicle data and lifecycle records. Vehicle ownership history and documentation verification traditionally require manual administrative checks. The app allows users to enter a vehicle registration number and access certified information recorded on a private blockchain platform integrated with Ethereum technology. Cryptographic notarisation ensures that stored records cannot be altered retroactively, providing tamper-evident documentation of key vehicle events. Smart contract components manage validation processes and secure data anchoring. The application enhances transparency in used-car transactions by enabling buyers, sellers and businesses to verify vehicle history quickly through a smartphone interface. By digitising notarisation processes, ACI improves data integrity, reduces fraud risks and streamlines administrative procedures related to vehicle documentation.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1843,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01377,EU_PSTW_202609_01377,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Hamburg,R&D general public services.,UNKNOWN,"""LLMoin"" pilot project to provide insight into Hamburg's medium-term LLM strategy",City of Hamburg; Local Government; R&D general public services.; Germany,"The pilot uses the Luminous language model developed and hosted in Germany by startup Aleph Alpha, with a custom front-end and data processing layer built specifically for Hamburg.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Public administrations face growing demands for efficiency in text-heavy tasks such as summarising documents, drafting responses and conducting research. Hamburg’s local government launched the LLMoin pilot to explore how large language models could support administrative work while meeting strict data protection and organisational requirements. Around 100 employees across departments participate, with the objective of assessing acceptance, usefulness and governance needs. The pilot uses the Luminous language model developed and hosted in Germany by startup Aleph Alpha, with a custom front-end and data processing layer built specifically for Hamburg. The system supports tasks such as text summarisation, drafting and information retrieval, operating within controlled environments. Outputs are reviewed by users, ensuring human responsibility for decisions. Early results show productivity gains for participating staff and valuable insights into training needs, technical integration and ethical considerations. The pilot informs Hamburg’s medium-term strategy for language technologies in government, highlighting both efficiency potential and the importance of sovereign, transparent deployment models in the public sector.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S7_GENERATIVE_CONTENT_CREATION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1850,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01378,EU_PSTW_202609_01378,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Dublin City council,R&D public order and safety.,"Local authorities increasingly recognise the potential of unmanned aerial vehicles to support public services, yet fragmented regulation, limited operational experience and lack of shared standards have slowed adoption.",Dublin City Council accelerates the potential of drones across Ireland,Dublin City council; Local Government; R&D public order and safety.; Ireland,"The programme establishes a cross-agency network to identify priority use cases, assess operational constraints and share best practices observed internationally. Its impact lies in improved readiness for emergency scenarios, more efficient data collection and a clearer regulatory and governance framework for scaling drone use nationwide.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Local authorities increasingly recognise the potential of unmanned aerial vehicles to support public services, yet fragmented regulation, limited operational experience and lack of shared standards have slowed adoption. Dublin City Council (DCC), through its Smart Dublin programme, launched a coordinated initiative to explore how drones could be safely and effectively used across Ireland for civil defence, emergency response, public safety and environmental monitoring. Stakeholders include local and national government agencies, emergency services, regulators and technology providers. The objective is to build institutional knowledge, test real-world applications and inform policymaking through evidence rather than isolated trials. The programme establishes a cross-agency network to identify priority use cases, assess operational constraints and share best practices observed internationally. Drone deployments are tested in controlled pilots, integrating aerial data collection with existing emergency management and environmental monitoring systems. Outputs include geospatial imagery, situational awareness data and operational insights rather than standalone services. The initiative has accelerated learning across the public sector, reduced duplication of effort and positioned Ireland to adopt drones more strategically. Its impact lies in improved readiness for emergency scenarios, more efficient data collection and a clearer regulatory and governance framework for scaling drone use nationwide.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1851,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01379,EU_PSTW_202609_01379,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Italian Senate,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.","The system reduces manual sorting effort, accelerates legislative processing and enhances organisational efficiency during plenary sessions.",AI-powered “Gestore Emendamenti” (GEM) at the Italian Senate,"Italian Senate; Central-Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Italy","GEM applies text clustering algorithms to group amendments based on semantic similarity, enabling simultaneous discussion and voting where appropriate.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Italian Senate introduced the “Gestore Emendamenti” (GEM) system to manage large volumes of legislative amendments more efficiently. Parliamentary debates often involve numerous amendments with similar wording, creating administrative complexity in scheduling votes. GEM applies text clustering algorithms to group amendments based on semantic similarity, enabling simultaneous discussion and voting where appropriate. Natural language processing techniques analyse amendment text and generate clusters within seconds. Parliamentary staff review, adjust and validate the machine-generated groupings before integration into the legislative workflow, ensuring that final procedural decisions remain under human control. The system reduces manual sorting effort, accelerates legislative processing and enhances organisational efficiency during plenary sessions. By combining automated text analysis with expert oversight, GEM supports transparent and accountable parliamentary procedures while maintaining institutional authority over decision-making.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1852,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01380,EU_PSTW_202609_01380,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Federal Ministry for Digital and Transport (BMDV),Transport,Fragmented mobility data and limited interoperability between transport services hinder environmentally oriented and multimodal traffic management across cities and regions.,Artificial Intelligence and Mobility - AIAMO,Federal Ministry for Digital and Transport (BMDV); Consortium; Transport; Germany,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Fragmented mobility data and limited interoperability between transport services hinder environmentally oriented and multimodal traffic management across cities and regions. The AIAMO project seeks to strengthen data sovereignty in transport by networking mobility services and simplifying access to high-quality data for innovative applications. Coordinated by ITS Germany, the initiative establishes a networked integration zone where mobility data from different providers can be securely shared and processed. Trusted data infrastructures and interoperable interfaces enable the development of advanced analytics and decision-support applications, facilitating research results and market-ready services. The platform supports small and medium-sized enterprises by lowering barriers to entry and enabling scalable deployment of digital mobility solutions. By improving data availability and integration, the project enhances traffic management, supports multimodal planning and fosters innovation ecosystems across cities. The initiative also promotes European cooperation through outreach to national ITS organisations, strengthening cross-border interoperability and sustainable mobility development.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1853,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01381,EU_PSTW_202609_01381,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""social_nonprofit""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,University of Applied Sciences Energy Technology Center (TZE),Transport,UNKNOWN,Open Mobility Electrical Infrastructure - OMEI,University of Applied Sciences Energy Technology Center (TZE); Consortium; Transport; Germany,"Fragmented data and incompatible systems currently limit optimisation of charging networks and energy use. This use case addresses these challenges by developing an open data and service framework to support planning, operation, and optimisation of fast and bidirectional charging infrastructure across Europe.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The expansion of electromobility requires coordinated planning of charging infrastructure, integration of renewable energy, and efficient management of energy flows across regions. Fragmented data and incompatible systems currently limit optimisation of charging networks and energy use. This use case addresses these challenges by developing an open data and service framework to support planning, operation, and optimisation of fast and bidirectional charging infrastructure across Europe. The project involves public authorities, infrastructure operators, energy providers, and mobility stakeholders, with the objective of enabling a decarbonised and economically viable energy supply for electric mobility. The solution is implemented through a digital platform that collects and integrates charging, user, energy, and traffic data from demonstration sites, including fast-charging stations with hybrid energy storage and bidirectional charging capabilities. Advanced analytics and simulation models are used to optimise operating strategies, economic performance, and vehicle-to-home and vehicle-to-grid concepts, while distributed ledger technology is examined for secure and transparent energy trading and billing. The project supports improved infrastructure planning, better utilisation of renewable energy, and scalable data sharing across borders. It contributes to reduced emissions, more efficient charging operations, and a robust foundation for future European mobility and energy data spaces.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1854,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01382,EU_PSTW_202609_01382,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,acatech - German Academy of Engineering Sciences,Economic affairs n.e.c,Fragmented data ecosystems and inconsistent standards can hinder cross-sector collaboration and scalable digital services.,National Initiative for AI-based Transformation in the Data Economy - NITD,acatech - German Academy of Engineering Sciences; Consortium; Economic affairs n.e.c; Germany,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Germany seeks to strengthen its position in the European data economy by improving interoperability, governance and trust in data-driven innovation. Fragmented data ecosystems and inconsistent standards can hinder cross-sector collaboration and scalable digital services. Stakeholders include federal ministries, industry partners, research institutions and public interest organisations. The objective is to create technical, organisational and funding foundations that enable marketable and socially beneficial data-driven applications. The initiative develops interoperable data spaces, harmonised quality and testing standards, and a quality and innovation centre to validate and certify applications. It also designs funding mechanisms that complement existing innovation schemes and accelerate implementation. Technical components include shared architectures for secure data exchange, governance frameworks and evaluation methodologies for compliance and performance. Anticipated impacts include improved data sharing across sectors, enhanced trust through transparent standards, accelerated innovation uptake and strengthened competitiveness of European digital ecosystems.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F07_DATA_VISIBILITY_INTEGRATION_GAP,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1855,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01383,EU_PSTW_202609_01383,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Free and Hanseatic City of Hamburg, Authority for Transport and Mobility Transition (FHH)",Transport,"Urban mobility providers face operational and regulatory challenges when integrating on-demand transport services and emerging autonomous vehicle technologies into existing public transport systems. The AHOI project in Hamburg aims to analyse, design and implement operational concepts for a mixed fleet of manually driven and autonomous vehicles within an on-demand service model.",Automation of Hamburg's on-demand offering with integration into public transport - AHOI project,"Free and Hanseatic City of Hamburg, Authority for Transport and Mobility Transition (FHH); Consortium; Transport; Germany",UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Urban mobility providers face operational and regulatory challenges when integrating on-demand transport services and emerging autonomous vehicle technologies into existing public transport systems. The AHOI project in Hamburg aims to analyse, design and implement operational concepts for a mixed fleet of manually driven and autonomous vehicles within an on-demand service model. A dedicated control centre is developed to coordinate fleet management, dispatching and data integration, ensuring accessibility across the entire travel chain. The project progresses through three phases: operation of a manually driven on-demand fleet with passengers; testing integration of autonomous vehicles under technical supervision without passengers; and deployment of a mixed fleet with passengers and integrated data ecosystems. Advanced fleet management algorithms and data analytics support routing, demand forecasting and system optimisation. The initiative evaluates user behaviour, accessibility impacts and regulatory implications, generating evidence for large-scale deployment. Expected outcomes include improved service efficiency, enhanced user experience and a scalable framework for integrating automated mobility into public transport networks.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1856,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01384,EU_PSTW_202609_01384,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Droniq GmbH,R&D public order and safety.,UNKNOWN,Establishment of a U-Space real laboratory in Hamburg - U-Space real laboratory,Droniq GmbH; Consortium; R&D public order and safety.; European Union,"The increasing use of drones for commercial and public services requires their safe and equitable integration into controlled airspace alongside traditional aviation. Digital traffic management systems, communication interfaces and identification services were deployed to enable real-time data exchange between airspace users and supervisory entities.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The increasing use of drones for commercial and public services requires their safe and equitable integration into controlled airspace alongside traditional aviation. To support implementation of the European U-space regulatory framework, Hamburg established a real-world laboratory to test operational, technical and procedural arrangements for unmanned aircraft systems. The project brought together aviation authorities, drone operators, air navigation service providers and technology developers to simulate and demonstrate coordinated airspace management in a controlled environment. Digital traffic management systems, communication interfaces and identification services were deployed to enable real-time data exchange between airspace users and supervisory entities. Operational scenarios tested flight authorisation, tracking, conflict detection and emergency procedures under realistic conditions. The laboratory also enabled participation of third parties, fostering interoperability and stakeholder collaboration. The initiative demonstrated practical pathways for compliant drone integration, reduced regulatory uncertainty and provided a replicable model for other German and European regions. It strengthens safety, transparency and scalability in the emerging low-altitude drone ecosystem.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1857,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01385,EU_PSTW_202609_01385,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Institute for Internet Security,R&D economic affairs,"Manufacturing companies increasingly rely on data-driven solutions and shared data spaces, yet potential users often lack transparent criteria to assess organisational reliability and the robustness of digital solutions.",Trustworthiness platform for AI solutions and data rooms - TrustKI,Institute for Internet Security; Academic-Research; R&D economic affairs; Germany,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Manufacturing companies increasingly rely on data-driven solutions and shared data spaces, yet potential users often lack transparent criteria to assess organisational reliability and the robustness of digital solutions. The TrustKI project aims to establish a platform that enables structured evaluation and documentation of trustworthiness in manufacturing organisations, their data rooms and related digital applications. The platform develops a trust index and reputation system based on defined assessment criteria, incorporating technical, organisational and governance indicators agreed upon with industry stakeholders. Companies can document compliance, security standards and quality assurance measures within a cooperative, sector-specific framework. Data are aggregated into a transparent scoring mechanism accessible to users and partners, facilitating informed decisions. By providing a structured and comparable basis for evaluating digital offerings, the initiative reduces uncertainty, promotes responsible deployment of advanced technologies and supports broader adoption of trusted industrial data ecosystems. It enhances transparency, strengthens collaboration and contributes to a competitive and reliable digital manufacturing landscape.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1858,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01386,EU_PSTW_202609_01386,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Oberaudorf Municipality, Municipality of Kiefersfelden",Transport,"Mountain regions face complex mobility challenges due to terrain, weather variability, cross-border traffic, and safety requirements.",Alpine mobility data room Inntal - ALMODA,"Oberaudorf Municipality, Municipality of Kiefersfelden; Consortium; Transport; Germany","This use case addresses these needs by establishing a cross-border data space that supports real-time communication, navigation, and surveillance services for mobility users and authorities. The solution is implemented through a multimodal data platform that aggregates high-precision digital elevation models, static and dynamic geographic protection zones, navigation and communication data, and high-resolution local weather information.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Mountain regions face complex mobility challenges due to terrain, weather variability, cross-border traffic, and safety requirements. Local communities in the Alpine Inntal region require accurate, real-time information to support safe, efficient, and sustainable mobility. This use case addresses these needs by establishing a cross-border data space that supports real-time communication, navigation, and surveillance services for mobility users and authorities. The project involves regional administrations, mobility service providers, and infrastructure operators, with the objective of enabling Mobility 4.0 through a shared and trusted digital situation picture. The solution is implemented through a multimodal data platform that aggregates high-precision digital elevation models, static and dynamic geographic protection zones, navigation and communication data, and high-resolution local weather information. Data is authenticated, validated, and combined using advanced analytical methods to create real-time situational awareness, which is then distributed securely to users and systems across borders. The platform supports multiple mobility use cases tailored to local needs, including safety, routing, and traffic management. The project improves reliability and safety of mobility in complex Alpine environments, enhances cross-border coordination, and enables scalable data-driven services. It delivers clear value by supporting informed decision-making, reducing risk, and laying the groundwork for integrated, resilient mobility services in sensitive regions.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1860,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01387,EU_PSTW_202609_01387,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Fraunhofer Institute for Material Flow and Logistics IML,Transport,"Air freight transport involves numerous actors, including airlines, freight forwarders, ground handlers and customs authorities, whose fragmented information systems limit efficiency and transparency across the logistics chain.",Digital test field Air Cargo - DTAC,Fraunhofer Institute for Material Flow and Logistics IML; Consortium; Transport; Germany,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Air freight transport involves numerous actors, including airlines, freight forwarders, ground handlers and customs authorities, whose fragmented information systems limit efficiency and transparency across the logistics chain. The Digital Test Field Air Cargo (DTAC) aims to enhance performance by increasing networking and digitalisation of air cargo processes in a neutral, cross-site environment. Stakeholders collaborate to improve data exchange standards and interoperability across the transport chain. The project develops shared digital platforms and interfaces to enable real-time information flow, while testing automation of dispatching, handling and transport operations. Advanced analytics and predictive models are applied to optimise capacity utilisation, forecast demand and streamline workflows. Solutions are piloted under operational conditions to validate scalability and integration with existing infrastructure. Expected outcomes include reduced processing times, improved coordination among actors and more reliable cargo flows. The initiative strengthens competitiveness of the air freight sector by enabling data-driven optimisation, increased transparency and higher overall system efficiency.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1862,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01388,EU_PSTW_202609_01388,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Kombiverkehr German Society for Combined Freight Transport mbH & Co. KG,Transport,UNKNOWN,AI and discrete loading optimization models to increase utilization in KV - KiBa,Kombiverkehr German Society for Combined Freight Transport mbH & Co. KG; Consortium; Transport; Germany,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Combined transport terminals must assign loading units to wagon sets efficiently, yet placement decisions often need to be made before complete information on incoming shipments is available. This creates operational uncertainty and can reduce utilisation of rail capacity. The KiBa project addresses this assignment problem by developing IT-based optimisation methods that generate rapid and efficient loading proposals under dynamic conditions. Discrete mathematical optimisation models are combined with machine learning techniques to calculate optimal placement of loading units on available wagons, considering route, weight and temporal constraints. The system processes partial and evolving data, updating recommendations as new information becomes available. By integrating predictive algorithms into terminal management systems, the solution supports near real-time decision-making. The approach improves wagon utilisation, reduces empty capacity and enhances planning reliability in combined transport operations. It contributes to more efficient rail logistics, lower operational costs and increased competitiveness of intermodal freight transport.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1863,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01389,EU_PSTW_202609_01389,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""social_nonprofit""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Blickfeld GmbH,Public order and safety n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,Networked LiDAR bus for safe autonomous use in shared spaces - LiDARshared,Blickfeld GmbH; Consortium; Public order and safety n.e.c.; Germany,"The collected data are processed using advanced spatial analytics to identify congestion points, risk zones and behavioural patterns.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Urban authorities require detailed and up-to-date traffic data to manage complex shared spaces safely, particularly in dense city centres where interactions between vehicles, cyclists and pedestrians are frequent. The LiDARshared project equips public buses with high-performance LiDAR sensors to collect comprehensive three-dimensional traffic data during regular operations. In cooperation with stakeholders in Kiel, sensor-equipped buses capture detailed information on traffic flows, road geometry and movement patterns along main arteries and busy intersections. The collected data are processed using advanced spatial analytics to identify congestion points, risk zones and behavioural patterns. Results can inform adjustments to traffic light phases, speed limits, one-way regulations and targeted safety interventions for vulnerable road users. By leveraging existing public transport fleets as mobile sensing platforms, the project enables cost-efficient, large-scale data acquisition. The initiative supports smarter traffic management, enhances road safety and provides a scalable data foundation for future autonomous mobility applications.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1866,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01390,EU_PSTW_202609_01390,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Erfurt University of Applied Sciences, Faculty of Economics, Logistics, Transport",Transport,"Overcrowding at truck rest areas along heavily used federal highways leads to safety risks, inefficient parking searches and uneven utilisation of available capacity.",Feasibility study for the harmonisation of truck parking space occupancy through networked model-predictive control systems based on V2X technologies - PAHA,"Erfurt University of Applied Sciences, Faculty of Economics, Logistics, Transport; Academic-Research; Transport; Germany","Based on reference scenarios and interface analyses, a forecasting model was developed to predict time- and location-specific occupancy rates using available traffic and infrastructure data.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Overcrowding at truck rest areas along heavily used federal highways leads to safety risks, inefficient parking searches and uneven utilisation of available capacity. Despite infrastructure investments, demand peaks in the evening and at night continue to strain facilities. The PAHA project examined how predictive and networked control systems could harmonise occupancy across rest areas. Based on reference scenarios and interface analyses, a forecasting model was developed to predict time- and location-specific occupancy rates using available traffic and infrastructure data. Model-predictive control approaches generate recommendations for optimal rest areas tailored to individual vehicles, incorporating static and dynamic variables such as current occupancy and travel time. The concept integrates Vehicle-to-Everything (V2X) communication to transmit guidance directly to drivers. Requirements for upgrading existing large-scale occupancy detection systems were identified to enable integration of forecasting and control functions. The approach promises improved utilisation of parking capacity, reduced congestion and enhanced safety and efficiency in long-distance freight transport.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1867,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01391,EU_PSTW_202609_01391,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""social_nonprofit""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,DroidDrive GmbH,Transport,UNKNOWN,Smart Urban Logistics through Electrification Collaboration and Automation - SULEICA,DroidDrive GmbH; Consortium; Transport; Germany,"Pilot operations were conducted using partially automated, electrically powered logistics vehicles capable of communicating and interacting with pedestrians and other road users. Acceptance studies assessed public perception and identified factors influencing user confidence, such as train length and infrastructure use.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Urban freight transport contributes to congestion, emissions and reduced quality of life, prompting cities to seek cleaner and more efficient logistics models. The SULEICA project aimed to design, implement and validate an emission-free and traffic-reducing urban logistics concept that integrates electrification and automation while actively involving citizens. Pilot operations were conducted using partially automated, electrically powered logistics vehicles capable of communicating and interacting with pedestrians and other road users. A key outcome was the development and road approval of the modular “Ducktrain” vehicle, designed for flexible goods transport in urban areas. The system was integrated into existing transport infrastructure, and operational data were analysed to refine vehicle design and coordination mechanisms. Acceptance studies assessed public perception and identified factors influencing user confidence, such as train length and infrastructure use. The project demonstrated potential improvements in traffic flow and ecological performance for logistics operators. Results include a roadmap for future vehicle registration under increasing automation levels and a foundation for scaling emission-free, cooperative urban logistics solutions.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1869,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01392,EU_PSTW_202609_01392,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"State of Berlin, represented by the Senate Department for the Environment, Transport and Climate Protection",R&D public order and safety.,"Urban authorities face challenges in integrating highly automated vehicles into complex city traffic while ensuring safety, interoperability and public acceptance.",Autonomous shuttles & Co in the digital test field for city traffic - Shuttles &Co,"State of Berlin, represented by the Senate Department for the Environment, Transport and Climate Protection; Local Government; R&D public order and safety.; Germany",UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Urban authorities face challenges in integrating highly automated vehicles into complex city traffic while ensuring safety, interoperability and public acceptance. The Shuttles &Co project established a digital test field in Berlin to trial automated fleet vehicles under real traffic conditions and evaluate requirements for future regular operation. Tests revealed technological limits but generated valuable operational insights, particularly regarding perception, localisation and prediction in dense environments. Cooperative methods based on Vehicle-to-Everything (V2X) communication enabled data exchange between vehicles and infrastructure, improving situational awareness and road safety. Enhanced traffic control and information services were demonstrated to public stakeholders, supported by newly integrated data and exchange platforms. The project also advanced automated recognition and updating of map objects, contributing to the self-updating digital map managed by the Berlin administration. Outcomes include improved cooperative traffic management methods, strengthened data interoperability and practical knowledge to support safe deployment of automated fleets in urban environments.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1870,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01393,EU_PSTW_202609_01393,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Erfurt University of Applied Sciences,R&D public order and safety.,UNKNOWN,AI and M2M -based optimization of safety and comfort for people with mobility restrictions in non-motorized individual transport in the ERFURTS location,Erfurt University of Applied Sciences; Academic-Research; R&D public order and safety.; Germany,Sensor systems and machine-to-machine communication technologies were deployed to automatically detect individuals requiring assistance at traffic light-controlled junctions and transition points to public transport.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"People with mobility restrictions often encounter safety risks and accessibility barriers at signalised intersections and public transport stops, particularly where pedestrian flows intersect with motorised traffic. The project aimed to enhance safety and comfort for vulnerable road users by improving infrastructure responsiveness and information exchange. Sensor systems and machine-to-machine communication technologies were deployed to automatically detect individuals requiring assistance at traffic light-controlled junctions and transition points to public transport. Intelligent control mechanisms dynamically adjusted traffic signal phases and transmitted relevant information to approaching vehicles and public transport operators. Vehicle-to-Everything (V2X) interfaces were designed to enable future communication with automated vehicles. Data-driven algorithms supported real-time decision-making while respecting privacy requirements. The initiative contributes to inclusive mobility by reducing conflict situations and waiting times, improving perceived safety and facilitating smoother transfers between transport modes. It advances the development of a connected and automated transport ecosystem that better accommodates diverse user needs.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1871,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01394,EU_PSTW_202609_01394,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Hamburg University of Technology, Hamburg",Transport,UNKNOWN,Smart control center for automated transport robots and buses in the city of Lauenburg/Elbe - TaBuLa-LOGplus,"Hamburg University of Technology, Hamburg; Academic-Research; Transport; Germany",UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Integrating passenger and goods transport through automated systems requires coordinated fleet management and reliable operational oversight. The TaBuLa-LOGplus project aimed to implement a prototypical control centre to enable efficient combined transport services in Lauenburg/Elbe. Building on previous test tracks and self-developed transport robots, the project connected autonomous logistics units with a conventional scheduled bus service. The control centre used advanced coordination algorithms to optimise routing, scheduling and resource allocation across passenger and freight flows. Increased automation levels in the transport robots were achieved through technical enhancements in navigation and system integration. Real-time data exchange enabled synchronisation between vehicles and infrastructure, supporting seamless multimodal operations. The demonstrator showed how centralised supervision can improve utilisation rates and operational reliability. The project provides a blueprint for scalable urban and regional mobility systems that integrate automated freight and passenger services, enhancing efficiency and sustainability.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1873,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01395,EU_PSTW_202609_01395,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Valeo Switches and Sensors GmbH,Transport,"In Upper Franconia, authorities and operators sought to modernise shuttle services by reducing dependence on onboard staff, improving passenger assistance, and enabling safe operation even in difficult weather, while complying with national regulations on autonomous driving.",Shuttle model region Upper Franconia II - SMO-II,Valeo Switches and Sensors GmbH; Consortium; Transport; Germany,"This use case focuses on the SMO-II project, involving regional transport authorities, operators, and control centres, with the objective of holistically automating the mobility chain and enabling demand-responsive shuttle services. The solution is implemented through a highly automated transport system that integrates vehicle automation, sensor technology, passenger assistance systems, and remote operations.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Public transport providers in rural and semi-rural regions face growing pressure to offer flexible, reliable, and cost-efficient mobility services despite low passenger density and challenging operating conditions. In Upper Franconia, authorities and operators sought to modernise shuttle services by reducing dependence on onboard staff, improving passenger assistance, and enabling safe operation even in difficult weather, while complying with national regulations on autonomous driving. This use case focuses on the SMO-II project, involving regional transport authorities, operators, and control centres, with the objective of holistically automating the mobility chain and enabling demand-responsive shuttle services. The solution is implemented through a highly automated transport system that integrates vehicle automation, sensor technology, passenger assistance systems, and remote operations. Shuttles are designed to operate at Level 4 autonomy in defined environments, with real-time monitoring and manoeuvre release managed from an existing control centre. Digital systems cover vehicle interiors, door areas, and passenger support functions, while advanced perception and control technologies enable safe operation without accompanying personnel. The project improves operational efficiency by reducing staffing requirements, increases service availability through on-demand operation, and enhances safety and reliability under varied conditions. It supports scalable deployment of autonomous shuttles in non-urban regions and contributes to more inclusive, sustainable public transport offerings.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1874,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01396,EU_PSTW_202609_01396,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"State of Berlin, represented by the Senate Department for the Environment, Mobility, Consumer and Climate Protection",Transport,UNKNOWN,AI-based system for connected mobility - KIS'M,"State of Berlin, represented by the Senate Department for the Environment, Mobility, Consumer and Climate Protection; Local Government; Transport; Germany",UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Rapidly growing residential areas in Berlin require affordable and reliable public transport solutions capable of operating safely in mixed traffic environments. The KIS'M project explores driverless on-demand transport integrated into the existing public transport network, aiming to develop robust processes for technical supervision at virtual stops and within vehicles. Automated vehicles are networked with traffic management systems to enhance coordination and safety. Data collected from operations are analysed to generate updated map and traffic information, improving navigation accuracy and situational awareness. Cooperative communication interfaces enable interaction between vehicles and infrastructure, supporting realistic autonomous driving manoeuvres in mixed traffic. The project also evaluates governance, safety and operational frameworks necessary for large-scale deployment. Results will inform a strategic roadmap for introducing driverless vehicles in Berlin. Expected benefits include improved service reliability, enhanced traffic safety and scalable integration of automated mobility into urban public transport systems.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1875,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01397,EU_PSTW_202609_01397,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""social_nonprofit""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Kelheim district,Public order and safety n.e.c.,"The approach provides a foundation for evidence-based planning, allowing authorities to identify bottlenecks and optimise station locations or staffing patterns.",AI -supported data analysis and simulation of rescue services - AI-Rescue,Kelheim district; Academic-Research; Public order and safety n.e.c.; Germany,The model replicated dispatch and resource allocation decisions using adaptive algorithmic strategies to test alternative deployment scenarios.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Rescue services in Germany operate within heterogeneous organisational structures and varying levels of digitalisation, limiting data-driven optimisation of emergency response. The AI-Rescue feasibility study aimed to develop a simulation demonstrator to assess how advanced analytics could support operational decision-making. Real operational data on mission frequency, duration and spatial distribution were collected and used to parameterise a spatially referenced simulation model. The model replicated dispatch and resource allocation decisions using adaptive algorithmic strategies to test alternative deployment scenarios. By modelling dynamic and static influencing factors within the rescue chain, the system enabled evaluation of response times and resource utilisation under different conditions. The approach provides a foundation for evidence-based planning, allowing authorities to identify bottlenecks and optimise station locations or staffing patterns. Anticipated impacts include improved response efficiency, better allocation of emergency resources and strengthened resilience of rescue services through data-driven scenario analysis.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1876,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01398,EU_PSTW_202609_01398,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,EasyMile GmbH,Transport,"Rural and semi-urban districts such as Kelheim face challenges in providing reliable and affordable public transport, particularly under adverse weather conditions that affect automated vehicle performance.",Weather-independent and highly automated ridesharing service in Kelheim (Germany),EasyMile GmbH; Consortium; Transport; Germany,"Sensor fusion techniques, enhanced perception algorithms and resilient control systems are implemented to ensure safe navigation year-round.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Rural and semi-urban districts such as Kelheim face challenges in providing reliable and affordable public transport, particularly under adverse weather conditions that affect automated vehicle performance. The KelRide project aims to establish a highly automated ride-sharing service integrated into public transport, operating with a mixed fleet of automated and conventional shuttles. A central objective is to develop a robust overall system capable of maintaining reliable operation in rain, snow and other challenging weather scenarios. Sensor fusion techniques, enhanced perception algorithms and resilient control systems are implemented to ensure safe navigation year-round. The service model combines demand-responsive routing with integration into existing transport networks, supported by digital booking and fleet management platforms. Operational trials will assess technical reliability, user acceptance and system efficiency. The project seeks to provide a transferable blueprint for other municipalities, demonstrating scalable, weather-resilient automated mobility services that enhance accessibility and sustainability.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1877,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01399,EU_PSTW_202609_01399,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"FORWISS, University of Passau",Transport,UNKNOWN,Adaptive maps and AI -based infrastructure monitoring - AK-KII,"FORWISS, University of Passau; Academic-Research; Transport; Germany",UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Highly automated mobility depends on precise and continuously updated digital maps reflecting the condition of road infrastructure. Vehicles can only operate safely and efficiently if they have reliable, location-specific information about road geometry, surface condition and structural integrity. The AK-KII preliminary study aimed to establish an interdisciplinary consortium to advance infrastructure monitoring methods capable of updating high-definition maps in near real time. The project analysed the state of the art in sensor technologies, including camera, radar and LiDAR systems, to determine how infrastructure condition data can be captured and processed. Approaches to sensor data fusion were examined to combine heterogeneous data sources across different road types, with particular attention to bridge condition monitoring and automated documentation of algorithmic decision processes. Evaluation criteria for data relevance and quality were defined to support scalable deployment. The study lays the groundwork for integrating automated infrastructure assessment into adaptive map layers, improving reliability of automated driving systems, enabling faster reflection of infrastructure changes and supporting safer, more sustainable mobility.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1878,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01400,EU_PSTW_202609_01400,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,University of Passau,R&D general public services.,"Regions at the interface between urban and rural areas often suffer from fragmented mobility systems, inefficient logistics, and limited transport alternatives, leading to higher emissions and reduced accessibility.",AI -based cross-type mobility optimization in non-urban regions - KIMoNo,University of Passau; Academic-Research; R&D general public services.; Germany,"This use case addresses these challenges by developing cross-type mobility optimisation strategies that improve sustainability and performance in non-urban regions, involving local authorities, residents, logistics providers, and researchers. The project is implemented through data-driven analysis of mobility flows across logistics and individual transport, supported by advanced modelling and optimisation techniques.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Regions at the interface between urban and rural areas often suffer from fragmented mobility systems, inefficient logistics, and limited transport alternatives, leading to higher emissions and reduced accessibility. In the Passau district, stakeholders sought to better understand and optimise mobility patterns across different transport modes, taking into account the specific needs of rural populations. This use case addresses these challenges by developing cross-type mobility optimisation strategies that improve sustainability and performance in non-urban regions, involving local authorities, residents, logistics providers, and researchers. The objective is to design mobility solutions that are technically viable, socially accepted, and tailored to local demand. The project is implemented through data-driven analysis of mobility flows across logistics and individual transport, supported by advanced modelling and optimisation techniques. Decentralised infrastructure concepts are evaluated, and digital solutions are developed to coordinate different modes more efficiently. The initiative also establishes the necessary technological infrastructure and actively engages citizens through targeted science communication, public events, and interactive exhibitions to assess acceptance and usability. The project supports more efficient and sustainable mobility patterns, improves alignment between infrastructure and real demand, and strengthens public acceptance of new mobility services, creating long-term value for regional transport planning.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1879,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01401,EU_PSTW_202609_01401,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Ingolstadt,Public order and safety n.e.c.,"Modern transport systems often lack a complete and up-to-date view of all road users, particularly pedestrians and cyclists, which makes it harder to optimise traffic control and improve safety in mixed urban environments.",Artificial intelligence in Ingolstadt's transport system - KIVI,City of Ingolstadt; Consortium; Public order and safety n.e.c.; Germany,"In Ingolstadt, the KIVI project was developed to address this problem by building a more comprehensive, multimodal picture of traffic conditions across selected network nodes and using that information to support smarter control and warning functions. The implementation combines local high-definition stationary sensors with mobile sensors from cars, buses, bicycles, and pedestrians equipped with 5G-enabled devices, and fuses these data using data-driven methods into a current digital representation of the traffic situation.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Modern transport systems often lack a complete and up-to-date view of all road users, particularly pedestrians and cyclists, which makes it harder to optimise traffic control and improve safety in mixed urban environments. In Ingolstadt, the KIVI project was developed to address this problem by building a more comprehensive, multimodal picture of traffic conditions across selected network nodes and using that information to support smarter control and warning functions. The objective is to improve traffic efficiency and safety by recognising all relevant road users, forecasting network conditions, and adapting traffic measures more precisely to real demand. The implementation combines local high-definition stationary sensors with mobile sensors from cars, buses, bicycles, and pedestrians equipped with 5G-enabled devices, and fuses these data using data-driven methods into a current digital representation of the traffic situation. At a conceptual level, the system supports network-wide multimodal forecasting, demand-based green-time allocation for different road users, a traffic-light phase assistant, and warning functions that recognise behaviour and intent and provide visual or acoustic alerts in dangerous situations. The impact includes better situational awareness, more balanced traffic control, and stronger protection for vulnerable road users. It also supports smarter urban mobility management by improving scalability, responsiveness, and the quality of data available for operational traffic decisions.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1880,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01402,EU_PSTW_202609_01402,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Fraunhofer Institute for Material Flow and Logistics IML, Dortmund","Mining, manufacturing and construction.",Fragmented digital systems and limited interoperability hinder efficiency and innovation in logistics and supply chain management.,Silicon Economy Logistics Ecosystem - SELE,"Fraunhofer Institute for Material Flow and Logistics IML, Dortmund; Consortium; Mining, manufacturing and construction.; Germany","Advanced analytics and intelligent optimisation algorithms were implemented to enhance planning, coordination and resource allocation, while new communication technologies were integrated to strengthen connectivity across platforms.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Fragmented digital systems and limited interoperability hinder efficiency and innovation in logistics and supply chain management. The SELE project sought to establish an open-source infrastructure and shared technical components to support a platform-based logistics ecosystem. The objective was to facilitate cross-company collaboration, digitisation and automation of supply chain processes through interoperable digital services. The initiative developed modular software components and open standards that enable secure data exchange and integration across logistics actors. Advanced analytics and intelligent optimisation algorithms were implemented to enhance planning, coordination and resource allocation, while new communication technologies were integrated to strengthen connectivity across platforms. The ecosystem approach promotes reusable services and common digital building blocks, enabling companies to develop and deploy innovative applications more rapidly. By improving interoperability and reducing duplication of effort, the project enhances operational efficiency, transparency and resilience within supply chains. It supports scalable digital transformation of the logistics sector and contributes to more sustainable and competitive transport systems.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1881,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01403,EU_PSTW_202609_01403,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Institute of Shipping Economics and Logistics (ISL),Transport,UNKNOWN,Intelligent information technologies for process optimization and automation in inland ports - Binntelligent,Institute of Shipping Economics and Logistics (ISL); Consortium; Transport; Germany,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Inland waterways offer underutilised capacity for freight transport, yet coordination gaps within multimodal logistics chains limit the performance of inland ports. The Binntelligent project aimed to strengthen inland port operations by improving data-driven coordination among transport stakeholders. Intelligent information technologies were designed and tested to collect, process and share decision-relevant data across shipping companies, port operators and logistics providers. Real-world operational data were used to develop predictive models for arrival times, handling processes and resource allocation. Data integration mechanisms enabled improved forecasting of transport flows and alignment with road and rail networks. The system supports adaptive planning and real-time optimisation of port processes, enhancing transparency and collaboration. By increasing predictability and reducing idle times, the solution improves utilisation of inland waterway capacity and strengthens integration within the broader freight transport system. The project contributes to more efficient, reliable and sustainable multimodal logistics operations.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION|S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1882,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01404,EU_PSTW_202609_01404,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Cologne University of Applied Sciences,Transport,"The approach enhances occupational safety by limiting manual intervention, improves asphalt durability through precise application and minimises traffic disruption during works.",Autonomously working machines in road construction 4.0 - ROBOT road construction 4.0,Cologne University of Applied Sciences; Consortium; Transport; Germany,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Road construction projects often face labour shortages, safety risks and inconsistent quality control during asphalt paving. The ROBOT road construction 4.0 project aimed to enable construction machines to operate autonomously and in a networked manner, reducing reliance on additional operating personnel while ensuring continuous quality assurance. A detailed 3D digital model of the road served as the basis for automated paving operations. Modern sensor systems and connected IT architectures were integrated into machinery to enable precise positioning, material control and real-time monitoring of paving parameters. Data-driven control systems continuously compared execution with design specifications to maintain consistent quality. Networked coordination between machines improved workflow synchronisation and reduced operational interruptions. The approach enhances occupational safety by limiting manual intervention, improves asphalt durability through precise application and minimises traffic disruption during works. The project demonstrates how automation and digital modelling can increase efficiency, quality and safety in infrastructure construction.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1883,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01405,EU_PSTW_202609_01405,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Norddjurs Municipality,General public services n.e.c.,"Municipal administrations handle large volumes of physical and digital correspondence, and inefficient mail distribution and journaling processes can lead to delays, lack of transparency, and increased administrative costs. The approach significantly reduces manual handling, shortens case processing times, and lowers the risk of misrouting or delays.",Smartmail - intelligent handling of mails,Norddjurs Municipality; Local Government; General public services n.e.c.; Denmark,"This use case addresses these challenges by optimising postal and journaling processes across municipalities, involving administrative staff, caseworkers, and citizens, with the objective of reducing processing times and improving transparency. The solution is implemented through an intelligent mail-handling system that automatically classifies incoming letters and emails, identifies the correct recipient or department, and journals documents immediately within municipal case management systems.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Municipal administrations handle large volumes of physical and digital correspondence, and inefficient mail distribution and journaling processes can lead to delays, lack of transparency, and increased administrative costs. In Norddjurs Municipality, improving internal mail handling was identified as a way to accelerate case processing for citizens while ensuring accountability within municipal workflows. This use case addresses these challenges by optimising postal and journaling processes across municipalities, involving administrative staff, caseworkers, and citizens, with the objective of reducing processing times and improving transparency. The solution is implemented through an intelligent mail-handling system that automatically classifies incoming letters and emails, identifies the correct recipient or department, and journals documents immediately within municipal case management systems. The system integrates with existing digital infrastructure and supports both physical and electronic correspondence. Following successful implementation, the solution was scaled to ten municipalities. The approach significantly reduces manual handling, shortens case processing times, and lowers the risk of misrouting or delays. It improves transparency by ensuring timely registration of correspondence and delivers clear efficiency gains for municipalities, while enhancing service quality and responsiveness for citizens.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK|F06_HIGH_VOLUME_SCALABILITY_BOTTLENECK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1884,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01406,EU_PSTW_202609_01406,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Sønderborg Municipality DTU Compute,General public services n.e.c.,"Manual handling of these cases is time-consuming and may lead to inconsistencies. As a pilot project in Sønderborg Municipality, it demonstrates potential to reduce processing time and administrative burden while improving service quality.",Decision support for handling municipal document access cases,Sønderborg Municipality DTU Compute; Local Government; General public services n.e.c.; Denmark,"The solution uses natural language processing models to analyse large volumes of text, identify relevant documents, and detect information that requires anonymisation.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Municipalities face increasing demand for access to public documents, requiring significant administrative effort to review, redact, and process requests in compliance with legal requirements such as data protection regulations. Manual handling of these cases is time-consuming and may lead to inconsistencies. The objective is to improve efficiency, quality, and consistency in processing document access requests. The solution uses natural language processing models to analyse large volumes of text, identify relevant documents, and detect information that requires anonymisation. These models support case handlers by automating parts of the review process and enabling more efficient file searches. The system is designed to integrate with existing administrative workflows and ensure compliance with data protection requirements. As a pilot project in Sønderborg Municipality, it demonstrates potential to reduce processing time and administrative burden while improving service quality.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1885,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01407,EU_PSTW_202609_01407,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"GovTech Midtjylland, Favrskov, Syddjurs and Aarhus Municipality",Environmental protection n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,Optimised building use with artificial intelligence,"GovTech Midtjylland, Favrskov, Syddjurs and Aarhus Municipality; Local Government; Environmental protection n.e.c.; Denmark",The project aimed to optimise room allocation and building use to reduce energy demand while maintaining service quality.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Many municipal primary schools are ageing buildings with high energy consumption and low space utilisation outside teaching hours, creating inefficiencies and avoidable CO₂ emissions. The project aimed to optimise room allocation and building use to reduce energy demand while maintaining service quality. Building data and Internet of Things sensors were used to monitor occupancy patterns and environmental conditions. Intelligent algorithms analysed usage data to recommend consolidation of activities into fewer rooms during low-demand periods, such as afternoons and weekends. The solution was based on existing technology platforms and datasets from the “Smarte Kvadratmeter” initiative and developed as open source to facilitate wider adoption. Recommendations generated by the system supported targeted heating, lighting and maintenance strategies. The approach enables municipalities to lower operational costs, reduce emissions and improve utilisation of public assets. By providing actionable insights for facility management, the project supports scalable and data-driven optimisation of public building portfolios.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1886,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01408,EU_PSTW_202609_01408,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Syddjurs Municipality,Unemployment.,"The objective is to enable a more flexible and holistic employment pathway by improving transparency, identifying bottlenecks, and supporting informed decision-making across organisational boundaries. Additional benefits include reduced administrative burden, improved scalability, and more consistent, data-driven decision-making.",An effective and holistic employment effort through the artificial intelligence technology Process Mining,Syddjurs Municipality; Local Government; Unemployment.; Denmark,"The use case addresses inefficiencies and fragmentation in public employment services, where multiple stakeholders—including job centres, administrative units, and case workers—operate across disconnected systems, limiting coordinated and citizen-centric support. The solution applies process mining and predictive monitoring techniques to existing log data from case management and financial systems, reconstructing end-to-end citizen journeys and mapping workflows, actors, and interventions.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The use case addresses inefficiencies and fragmentation in public employment services, where multiple stakeholders—including job centres, administrative units, and case workers—operate across disconnected systems, limiting coordinated and citizen-centric support. The objective is to enable a more flexible and holistic employment pathway by improving transparency, identifying bottlenecks, and supporting informed decision-making across organisational boundaries. The solution applies process mining and predictive monitoring techniques to existing log data from case management and financial systems, reconstructing end-to-end citizen journeys and mapping workflows, actors, and interventions. Machine learning models analyse historical data to identify patterns in successful outcomes and predict the most effective interventions for different citizen groups, with integration into existing IT systems for continuous analysis and visualisation. The impact includes enhanced process transparency, improved coordination, and more targeted interventions, leading to increased efficiency and better employment outcomes. Additional benefits include reduced administrative burden, improved scalability, and more consistent, data-driven decision-making.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1887,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01409,EU_PSTW_202609_01409,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Aalborg Municipality, Syddjurs Municipality, Aalborg University",Outpatient services.,"Elderly citizens often experience fragmented home care services involving multiple caregivers, which can reduce perceived safety and continuity of care.",Increased continuity in elderly care using artificial intelligence,"Aalborg Municipality, Syddjurs Municipality, Aalborg University; Local Government; Outpatient services.; Denmark","The solution was implemented in Aalborg and Syddjurs municipalities, allowing comparison across organisational structures and urban–rural contexts.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Elderly citizens often experience fragmented home care services involving multiple caregivers, which can reduce perceived safety and continuity of care. The project aimed to enhance transparency and improve the citizen experience by increasing continuity in the staff assigned to each individual. Building on the existing Greenforce planning solution, the system was adapted to incorporate additional parameters such as actual time spent per visit and limits on the number of different caregivers assigned. Algorithmic optimisation generated visit schedules balancing continuity, working hours and operational constraints. A feedback loop enabled continuous retraining based on user input and operational outcomes. The solution was implemented in Aalborg and Syddjurs municipalities, allowing comparison across organisational structures and urban–rural contexts. Results indicate improved planning transparency, more consistent caregiver assignments and potential reductions in travel time between visits. The initiative strengthens quality of care, supports greener routing and provides a scalable model for data-driven workforce planning in elderly services.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1888,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01410,EU_PSTW_202609_01410,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Aalborg Municipality and Rødovre Municipality,Hospital services.,UNKNOWN,Artificial intelligence to support the visitation of rehabilitation courses (AIR Project),Aalborg Municipality and Rødovre Municipality; Local Government; Hospital services.; Denmark,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Municipal case workers in Denmark faced increasing pressure to assess citizens’ suitability for rehabilitation programmes consistently and efficiently. The AIR pilot in Aalborg aimed to support decision-making while maintaining professional judgement. Stakeholders included municipalities, case workers, healthcare providers, and citizens.Between 2018 and 2021, statistical and predictive models analysed historical rehabilitation data to estimate eligibility, risk of dropout, and expected outcomes. Outputs were presented as decision support within existing case management systems. The pilot improved consistency and efficiency in assessments, supported better allocation of rehabilitation resources, and freed staff time, while keeping humans in control of final decisions.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1889,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01411,EU_PSTW_202609_01411,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Aarhus University Hospital, Aarhus Municipality,MedTech Innovation Consortium,Regional Hospital Horsens and Skanderborg Municipality",Public health services.,UNKNOWN,Artificial intelligence for the prevention of malnutrition and hospitalization of the elderly,"Aarhus University Hospital, Aarhus Municipality,MedTech Innovation Consortium,Regional Hospital Horsens and Skanderborg Municipality; Local Government; Public health services.; Denmark",Health and care data were analysed using predictive modelling techniques to detect patterns associated with nutritional risk.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Malnutrition among older citizens is a major risk factor for prolonged hospital stays, readmissions and increased mortality, yet early identification in community settings remains challenging. The project aimed to prevent emergency hospitalisations by identifying individuals at risk of malnutrition so that timely interventions could be initiated. Health and care data were analysed using predictive modelling techniques to detect patterns associated with nutritional risk. Risk scores were generated to support care professionals in prioritising preventive measures, such as dietary support or follow-up assessments. The system was designed to integrate with existing municipal health records, ensuring that insights could inform routine care processes. By enabling earlier intervention, the solution seeks to improve treatment outcomes, enhance self-reliance and increase quality of life for elderly citizens. Anticipated impacts include reduced hospital admissions, lower long-term care costs and more proactive, data-driven management of elderly health services.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1890,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01412,EU_PSTW_202609_01412,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"CAMES Rigshospitalet, DTU Compute, Region Zealand, Southern Denmark and North Jutland",Public health services.,UNKNOWN,Better diagnostics of pregnant women using artificial intelligence,"CAMES Rigshospitalet, DTU Compute, Region Zealand, Southern Denmark and North Jutland; Consortium; Public health services.; Denmark","Expected impacts include more accurate risk assessment, improved patient safety and more efficient use of clinical resources, contributing to higher overall standards in prenatal care.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Early identification of complications during pregnancy is essential to initiate preventive measures and improve maternal and neonatal outcomes. However, variability in ultrasound image quality can affect diagnostic accuracy and limit the ability to predict adverse developments. This project aimed to enhance the quality and consistency of prenatal ultrasound examinations by supporting clinical staff, including midwives, nurses and doctors, during scanning procedures. Building on a validated prototype capable of assessing completed ultrasound recordings, the solution was further developed into a real-time decision-support tool. Advanced image analysis algorithms evaluate scan quality during the examination, providing immediate feedback to ensure that images meet predefined clinical standards. The system integrates with existing ultrasound equipment and clinical workflows, enabling seamless adoption without disrupting routine practice. By improving image quality at the point of care, the tool increases diagnostic reliability and supports earlier detection of potential complications. Expected impacts include more accurate risk assessment, improved patient safety and more efficient use of clinical resources, contributing to higher overall standards in prenatal care.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1891,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01413,EU_PSTW_202609_01413,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"TeleCare Nord/Region Nordjylland, North Jutland, Aalborg University, TeleCare Nord",Public health services.,"The project focuses on improving care for citizens with multiple chronic conditions, where disease progression is complex and difficult to monitor, often leading to unanticipated exacerbations and hospitalisations.",Artificial intelligence for predicting exacerbations in multidisease with easy-to-understand explanation of complex results,"TeleCare Nord/Region Nordjylland, North Jutland, Aalborg University, TeleCare Nord; Consortium; Public health services.; Denmark","The solution combines clinical data on chronic diseases with contextual information, such as epidemiological trends, using predictive modelling techniques to forecast potential exacerbations. Explainability methods are incorporated to translate complex model outputs into clear, interpretable insights for clinicians and patients, ensuring transparency and ethical use.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The project focuses on improving care for citizens with multiple chronic conditions, where disease progression is complex and difficult to monitor, often leading to unanticipated exacerbations and hospitalisations. Healthcare professionals require better tools to anticipate deterioration and communicate risks clearly to support timely intervention and patient trust. The objective is to enhance clinical decision-making and patient outcomes through early detection and understandable risk assessment. The solution combines clinical data on chronic diseases with contextual information, such as epidemiological trends, using predictive modelling techniques to forecast potential exacerbations. Explainability methods are incorporated to translate complex model outputs into clear, interpretable insights for clinicians and patients, ensuring transparency and ethical use. The system is conceptually integrated into clinical workflows to support decision-making without disrupting existing practices. The impact includes earlier interventions, reduced hospital admissions, and improved patient outcomes. It also enhances trust and usability through transparent communication, while supporting more efficient resource allocation and improved quality of care.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION|S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1892,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01414,EU_PSTW_202609_01414,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Region Zealand, Capital Region of Denmark",Public health services.,"This creates delays, variability in decisions, and administrative burden for clinical staff.",Artificial intelligence to support the visitation of referrals from general practice,"Region Zealand, Capital Region of Denmark; Consortium; Public health services.; Denmark","The use case addressed inefficiencies and inconsistencies in the handling of patient referrals from general practitioners to hospitals, where large volumes of referrals require manual processing, classification, and routing across multiple stages. The solution uses natural language processing and classification models to analyse referral content and automatically assign cases to the appropriate medical specialty and local department.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The use case addressed inefficiencies and inconsistencies in the handling of patient referrals from general practitioners to hospitals, where large volumes of referrals require manual processing, classification, and routing across multiple stages. This creates delays, variability in decisions, and administrative burden for clinical staff. The objective is to improve consistency, efficiency, and patient rights by optimising the referral handling process. The solution uses natural language processing and classification models to analyse referral content and automatically assign cases to the appropriate medical specialty and local department. For selected specialties, the system further associates referrals with predefined patient pathways. It is integrated conceptually with hospital information systems to support seamless workflow automation and human oversight. The impact includes faster processing times, improved consistency in decision-making, and reduced administrative workload. This leads to better patient experience, more efficient use of clinical resources, and enhanced operational scalability within hospital systems.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S6_WORKFLOW_PROCESS_AUTOMATION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1893,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01415,EU_PSTW_202609_01415,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Rigshospitalet-Glostrup,Public health services.,UNKNOWN,Artificial intelligence for treatment support for eye patients,Rigshospitalet-Glostrup; Academic-Research; Public health services.; Denmark,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Ophthalmology departments faced growing demand to assess patients with age-related macular degeneration (AMD), a leading cause of blindness in Denmark. The pilot aimed to support treatment decisions and optimise specialist time. Stakeholders included patients, clinicians, and hospital administrators. From 2017 to 2019, decision-support algorithms analysed clinical imaging and patient data to classify patients requiring treatment, observation, or discharge. The system integrated into hospital workflows with a focus on transparency. The pilot enabled greater delegation of routine assessments, reduced pressure on ophthalmologists, and improved efficiency while maintaining trust and clinical oversight.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1894,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01416,EU_PSTW_202609_01416,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Capital Region of Denmark (Bispebjerg Hospital and Rigshospitalet) DTU and Region Zealand,Public health services.,UNKNOWN,WARD-HOME – Safe discharge from hospital,Capital Region of Denmark (Bispebjerg Hospital and Rigshospitalet) DTU and Region Zealand; Consortium; Public health services.; Denmark,These data are analysed using predictive algorithms to assess patient condition in real time and trigger alerts when intervention is needed.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"WARD-HOME focuses on enabling safe early discharge of patients by addressing the risk of undetected deterioration once patients return home. Healthcare providers require continuous monitoring solutions to ensure patient safety while reducing hospital stays and associated costs. The objective is to support rehabilitation at home through timely detection of health deterioration and rapid clinical response. The solution builds on a wireless monitoring system that continuously collects vital parameters such as heart rate, oxygen saturation, and respiratory rate. These data are analysed using predictive algorithms to assess patient condition in real time and trigger alerts when intervention is needed. The system integrates conceptually with clinical monitoring platforms, enabling healthcare professionals to oversee patients remotely. The impact includes reduced hospital length of stay, improved patient safety, and enhanced quality of care. It also supports healthcare system efficiency by optimising resource utilisation, increasing scalability of remote care, and improving patient experience through home-based recovery.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1895,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01417,EU_PSTW_202609_01417,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Universiteit Twente,R&D education.,"Understanding why and when neural network models learn effectively remains a fundamental scientific challenge with implications for both artificial intelligence and neuroscience. The research builds on a novel interpretation of local parameter updates in Bayesian Neural Networks as a derivative-free optimisation method, challenging the assumption that such approaches are inherently slow.",From A to B: Generalizing the mathematics of artificial neural networks (ANNs) to biological neural networks (BNNs),Universiteit Twente; Academic-Research; R&D education.; Netherlands,"Using nonparametric and high-dimensional statistical theory, the project investigates the convergence properties and generalisation performance of these models.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Understanding why and when neural network models learn effectively remains a fundamental scientific challenge with implications for both artificial intelligence and neuroscience. This project seeks to develop advanced mathematical tools to analyse learning processes in Bayesian Neural Networks as statistical models and to explore parallels with biological neural systems. The research builds on a novel interpretation of local parameter updates in Bayesian Neural Networks as a derivative-free optimisation method, challenging the assumption that such approaches are inherently slow. Using nonparametric and high-dimensional statistical theory, the project investigates the convergence properties and generalisation performance of these models. By establishing rigorous mathematical guarantees, it aims to clarify the statistical mechanisms underlying efficient learning. Insights gained could inform the design of more data-efficient training methods and contribute to the development of neuromorphic computing architectures inspired by biological networks. The project has the potential to open new directions in mathematical statistics while strengthening the conceptual bridge between computational models and brain function.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1897,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01418,EU_PSTW_202609_01418,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,University of Stuttgart,R&D economic affairs,UNKNOWN,"Practical, Learning-Based Tools for Finding and Fixing Bugs",University of Stuttgart; Academic-Research; R&D economic affairs; Germany,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Software defects cause system failures, security vulnerabilities and financial losses, yet traditional static analysis and rule-based tools often miss complex or context-dependent errors. This project aims to translate advanced research on learning-based bug detection into practical tools for everyday software development. Building on techniques developed in the LearnBugs ERC project, the initiative develops intelligent models capable of analysing source code to detect anomalous patterns and suggest corrective changes. Deep learning architectures are trained on large code repositories to recognise bug signatures and generate candidate fixes that align with programming context and style. The proposed tools are designed for integration into common development environments, providing automated feedback during coding. By combining detection and repair functionalities, the system seeks to reduce debugging time and improve software reliability. Anticipated impacts include increased developer productivity, reduced maintenance costs and enhanced security of digital systems, with potential for commercial deployment in the software tools market.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1899,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01419,EU_PSTW_202609_01419,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Università degli Studi di Torino,"R&D recreation, culture and religion.",UNKNOWN,Avifauna in archaeoecological networks,"Università degli Studi di Torino; Academic-Research; R&D recreation, culture and religion.; Italy","Large, heterogeneous datasets are integrated using network analysis and multispecies theoretical frameworks to model relationships between humans, birds and environments.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Human–bird interactions have shaped ecosystems and cultural practices throughout history, yet their complexity remains insufficiently understood. The AviArch project investigates avifauna within past socio-ecological systems, focusing on key transitions such as the shift to agriculture in Southwest Asia and urbanisation in Bronze Age Crete. Researchers employ zooarchaeology, palaeoproteomics and advanced computational analysis to identify bird species and reconstruct their ecological and cultural roles. Large, heterogeneous datasets are integrated using network analysis and multispecies theoretical frameworks to model relationships between humans, birds and environments. Analytical models process morphological, ecological and behavioural data to trace biodiversity patterns and assess how environmental change influenced interspecies dynamics. By generating unprecedented insights into long-term human–avian interactions, the project enhances understanding of adaptability and resilience in past societies. Outcomes contribute to contemporary biodiversity conservation strategies and foster interdisciplinary dialogue between archaeology, ecology and data science.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1900,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01420,EU_PSTW_202609_01420,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Aristotle University Campus,R&D health.,UNKNOWN,AI-enabled healthcare services during cross-border medical emergencies and regular patient services,Aristotle University Campus; Consortium; R&D health.; Greece,"This use case addresses these challenges through the ESCORT project, which brings together healthcare providers, emergency services, patients, and research organisations across multiple European countries. The project is implemented through the integration of connected medical devices, wearable technologies, and advanced data analytics to support pre-clinical, clinical, and post-clinical care.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Healthcare systems face increasing strain when managing medical emergencies across borders and ensuring continuity of care for mobile populations, while also improving routine patient services. Differences in systems, data availability, and coordination can hinder timely and effective treatment. This use case addresses these challenges through the ESCORT project, which brings together healthcare providers, emergency services, patients, and research organisations across multiple European countries. The objective is to improve patient treatment, experience, and system resilience in both emergency and regular care scenarios, including cross-border situations. The project is implemented through the integration of connected medical devices, wearable technologies, and advanced data analytics to support pre-clinical, clinical, and post-clinical care. Digital services are co-designed with stakeholders and validated in real-life scenarios across six countries, building on prior European research initiatives. The system supports real-time data exchange, decision support, and coordination between providers in emergency medicine and routine care. The project enhances the quality and continuity of care, improves response times in emergencies, and strengthens cross-border cooperation. It delivers scalable digital health services that increase resilience of healthcare systems and improve outcomes and experience for patients across Europe.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1901,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01421,EU_PSTW_202609_01421,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Universitair Medisch Centrum Utrecht,R&D health.,UNKNOWN,CLARITY - Causative Link between respirAtory syncytial viRus and chronic lung diseases: Identifying Targets for therapY,Universitair Medisch Centrum Utrecht; Consortium; R&D health.; Netherlands,"Using national cohort data from Estonia and Spain, the project integrates genomic, clinical and epidemiological datasets to examine virus–host interactions.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Respiratory Syncytial Virus (RSV) infections in early life are associated with increased risk of chronic respiratory conditions, including asthma, yet the underlying mechanisms remain unclear. The CLARITY project aims to identify genetic risk factors and molecular pathways linking RSV infection to long-term lung disease. Using national cohort data from Estonia and Spain, the project integrates genomic, clinical and epidemiological datasets to examine virus–host interactions. Advanced computational models analyse cellular networks disrupted by RSV, identifying key pathways associated with inflammatory responses and asthma development. Predictive modelling supports stratification of individuals according to genetic susceptibility and disease risk. The project also screens for drug-like compounds capable of reversing virus-induced molecular alterations. By elucidating causal mechanisms and identifying therapeutic targets, CLARITY seeks to inform personalised prevention strategies and improve treatment options. The findings may extend to other non-communicable diseases triggered by viral infections, enhancing translational impact in respiratory medicine.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1902,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01422,EU_PSTW_202609_01422,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Karolinska Institutet,R&D health.,"However, fragmentation of medical and research data limits comprehensive analysis and personalised care strategies. By enhancing interoperability and analytical capacity, WISDOM seeks to improve patient well-being, optimise therapeutic strategies and reduce the societal burden of chronic immune-mediated diseases.",WISDOM - Well-being improvement through the Integration of healthcare and reSearch Data and models with Out border for chronic iMmune-mediated diseases,Karolinska Institutet; Consortium; R&D health.; Sweden,Computational models are developed to translate complex biological and clinical information into actionable insights across multiple disease use cases.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Chronic immune-mediated diseases affect around 10 % of Europeans and require coordinated, data-driven approaches to improve outcomes. However, fragmentation of medical and research data limits comprehensive analysis and personalised care strategies. The WISDOM project aims to integrate heterogeneous healthcare and research datasets within secure, interoperable infrastructures to support risk stratification and outcome prediction. Computational models are developed to translate complex biological and clinical information into actionable insights across multiple disease use cases. Privacy-preserving data-sharing frameworks and responsible governance mechanisms ensure secure collaboration across institutions. Advanced predictive algorithms identify patient subgroups and forecast disease progression, supporting personalised treatment decisions. The project also promotes critical assessment and responsible deployment of data-driven models in healthcare settings. By enhancing interoperability and analytical capacity, WISDOM seeks to improve patient well-being, optimise therapeutic strategies and reduce the societal burden of chronic immune-mediated diseases.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1903,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01423,EU_PSTW_202609_01423,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Universidad Politecnica de Madrid,R&D environmental protection.,UNKNOWN,ZEBAI - Innovative methodologies for the design of Zero-Emission and cost-effective Buildings enhanced by Artificial Intelligence,Universidad Politecnica de Madrid; Consortium; R&D environmental protection.; Spain,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The construction sector faces growing pressure to reduce emissions while maintaining affordability and high indoor environmental quality. ZEBAI aims to develop innovative methodologies for designing zero-emission and cost-effective buildings through a holistic, data-driven approach. The project first establishes an open-access catalogue of well-characterised construction materials, including environmental and performance indicators. Based on this knowledge base, advanced design algorithms generate optimised building configurations that balance energy efficiency, cost, indoor comfort and environmental impact. Simulation models assess lifecycle performance across different climates and usage scenarios, incorporating resilience to climate change. The methodology integrates architectural, engineering and sustainability parameters within a unified optimisation framework. By enabling systematic comparison of design alternatives, the project supports informed decision-making in early planning stages. Expected impacts include reduced greenhouse gas emissions, improved resource efficiency and scalable pathways towards sustainable building practices across Europe.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1904,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01424,EU_PSTW_202609_01424,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Lunds Universitet,"Agriculture, forestry, fishing and hunting",UNKNOWN,FARMWISE - Future Agricultural Resource Management and Water Innovations for a Sustainable Europe,"Lunds Universitet; Consortium; Agriculture, forestry, fishing and hunting; Sweden","European agriculture faces mounting pressure to optimise water use, increase productivity and reduce environmental impact under climate variability.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"European agriculture faces mounting pressure to optimise water use, increase productivity and reduce environmental impact under climate variability. FARMWISE aims to support farmers and decision-makers with an advanced decision-support system that enhances resource management and sustainable practices. The project integrates remote sensing data, in-field sensors and agronomic datasets to generate actionable insights on irrigation, soil conditions and crop performance. Machine learning models process satellite imagery and environmental indicators to provide field-level recommendations for water allocation and precision farming interventions. The platform facilitates knowledge sharing and co-creation among farmers, researchers and European organisations, ensuring that solutions are adaptable to regional contexts. Interoperable tools enable integration with existing farm management systems, promoting scalability and uptake. Expected impacts include improved water efficiency, reduced input costs and enhanced resilience to climate change. By combining digital technologies with stakeholder collaboration, FARMWISE strengthens sustainable agricultural productivity and supports evidence-based policymaking.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1905,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01425,EU_PSTW_202609_01425,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Kungliga Tekniska Hoegskolan,Fuel and energy,UNKNOWN,EXTRA-BRAIN - Explainable Trustworthy brain-like AI for Data Intensive Applications,Kungliga Tekniska Hoegskolan; Consortium; Fuel and energy; Sweden,"Data-intensive applications increasingly require learning systems that are energy-efficient, adaptable across tasks, and transparent to users, particularly when deployed across edge and cloud environments. Traditional approaches often struggle with high energy consumption, limited generalisation, and lack of explainability, constraining their use in critical or resource-constrained settings.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Data-intensive applications increasingly require learning systems that are energy-efficient, adaptable across tasks, and transparent to users, particularly when deployed across edge and cloud environments. Traditional approaches often struggle with high energy consumption, limited generalisation, and lack of explainability, constraining their use in critical or resource-constrained settings. This use case addresses these limitations through the EXTRA-BRAIN project, involving researchers, technology developers, and application partners, with the objective of developing more universal, trustworthy, and efficient learning models inspired by brain function. The project is implemented by designing novel neural network architectures based on principles from computational neuroscience, enabling functional flexibility and improved hardware compatibility. These models are supported by data optimisation pipelines that enhance data quality and reduce annotation effort, alongside explainability frameworks that allow users to understand and trust system outputs. The solutions are tested across diverse use cases along the edge–cloud continuum, addressing different performance and hardware constraints. The project contributes to reduced energy consumption, improved adaptability across tasks, and greater transparency of advanced data-processing systems. It supports wider adoption of trustworthy, efficient learning technologies in demanding real-world applications, delivering long-term value for industry and society.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1906,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01426,EU_PSTW_202609_01426,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Kobenhayns Universitet,R&D health.,UNKNOWN,IRE - Intelligent Robotic Endoscopes for Improved Healthcare Services,Kobenhayns Universitet; Consortium; R&D health.; Denmark,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Colonoscopy is essential for early detection of colorectal cancer, yet procedural variability and navigation complexity can affect diagnostic success and patient safety. The IRE project seeks to develop intelligent robotic endoscopes that enhance precision, autonomy and clinical outcomes. The system combines robotic control architectures with learning mechanisms derived from human operator data and large datasets of real-life colonoscopies. Biomechanical modelling and digital twin simulations replicate anatomical variability, enabling safe testing and refinement of navigation strategies. Sensory feedback systems and soft robotic components improve manoeuvrability and reduce patient discomfort. Machine learning algorithms support adaptive path planning and obstacle avoidance within complex anatomical structures. Integration with clinician input ensures usability and clinical relevance. The solution aims to increase lesion detection rates, standardise procedure quality and reduce complications. By enhancing autonomy and adaptability in endoscopic navigation, IRE contributes to improved cancer screening effectiveness and more efficient healthcare delivery.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1907,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01427,EU_PSTW_202609_01427,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Twi Ellas Astiki Mi Kerdoskopiki Etaireia,R&D economic affairs,UNKNOWN,ARISE - Advanced AI and RobotIcS for autonomous task pErformance,Twi Ellas Astiki Mi Kerdoskopiki Etaireia; Consortium; R&D economic affairs; Greece,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Labour shortages and hazardous conditions in sectors such as renewable energy and agriculture create demand for adaptable robotic systems capable of performing complex tasks autonomously. ARISE aims to advance automation through reconfigurable robotic manipulators integrating perception, control and learning capabilities. Pneumatic-based manipulators with soft end-effectors are combined with advanced localisation, mapping and semantic perception modules to enable precise interaction with dynamic environments. Hierarchical imitation learning and knowledge representation frameworks allow robots to acquire task strategies from human demonstrations and adapt to new scenarios. An edge-computing framework facilitates deployment of machine learning and computer vision models in real operational settings. The system is validated in solar farm installation and maintenance tasks, as well as hydroponic transplanting and harvesting operations. By improving flexibility, safety and efficiency, ARISE enhances competitiveness in key industries and supports scalable automation solutions tailored to complex real-world environments.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1908,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01428,EU_PSTW_202609_01428,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Language Planning Department of the Government of Iceland,Cultural services.,UNKNOWN,How Iceland is using GPT-4 to preserve its language,Language Planning Department of the Government of Iceland; Central-Government; Cultural services.; Iceland,"The collaboration involved adapting and evaluating GPT-4 using curated Icelandic language data, linguistic expertise and targeted testing to improve comprehension and generation quality.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Small language communities face risks of digital marginalisation when advanced language technologies are predominantly trained on high-resource languages. Iceland sought to ensure that its national language remains represented and usable in emerging digital services. Stakeholders include the Government of Iceland, language experts, technology providers and citizens. The objective is to enhance the performance of large language models in Icelandic and establish resources that support long-term linguistic preservation. The collaboration involved adapting and evaluating GPT-4 using curated Icelandic language data, linguistic expertise and targeted testing to improve comprehension and generation quality. Techniques include fine-tuning, evaluation benchmarking and iterative feedback loops to strengthen performance in low-resource language contexts. Expected impacts include improved access to digital services in Icelandic, strengthened cultural preservation, and a replicable framework for supporting other low-resource languages in advanced language technologies.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1910,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01429,EU_PSTW_202609_01429,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""social_nonprofit""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Reykjavik,General public services n.e.c.,"Cities need practical ways to involve residents in shaping local priorities, yet traditional consultation methods often struggle to attract broad participation or turn large volumes of public input into useful policy signals.",Better Reykjavik,City of Reykjavik; Local Government; General public services n.e.c.; Iceland,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Cities need practical ways to involve residents in shaping local priorities, yet traditional consultation methods often struggle to attract broad participation or turn large volumes of public input into useful policy signals. Better Reykjavik was created as a collaborative platform between the Citizens Foundation, Reykjavik City, and residents to crowdsource ideas, support debate, and strengthen trust and policy development through digital participation. The objective is to give citizens a more direct role in agenda setting, participatory budgeting, and policy discussion, while helping the city process and improve the quality of contributions at scale. The implementation combines an online participation platform with artificial intelligence and machine learning features used to surface relevant information, improve user experience, and support the quality of proposals submitted by participants. The platform also includes structured debate functions, multimedia submissions, and proposal-assistance mechanisms designed to guide users in framing stronger ideas. At a conceptual level, the system integrates crowdsourcing, participation workflows, and intelligent support features to make large-scale civic input more manageable and useful. The impact includes broader citizen engagement, improved quality of submissions, and stronger support for transparent and collaborative urban governance. It also demonstrates how digital participation platforms can scale public input while making it easier for both users and public authorities to navigate complex civic conversations.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F06_HIGH_VOLUME_SCALABILITY_BOTTLENECK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1912,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01430,EU_PSTW_202609_01430,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Derby City Council,General public services n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,Generative AI Transformation Project with Derby City Council and ICS.AI,Derby City Council; Local Government; General public services n.e.c.; United Kingdom,"This use case addresses these challenges through a long-term transformation of customer service operations, involving the Council, residents, and the technology partner ICS.AI, with the objective of streamlining service delivery, improving accessibility, and enabling staff to focus on higher-value tasks. The solution is implemented through generative language technologies embedded into existing digital assistants, Darcie and Ali, which are extended to function as conversational copilots across customer service workflows.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Municipal councils face increasing demand to deliver responsive, high-quality customer services while managing budget constraints and rising case complexity. Derby City Council needed to modernise how residents interact with services, reduce pressure on frontline staff, and improve resolution of more nuanced enquiries across departments. This use case addresses these challenges through a long-term transformation of customer service operations, involving the Council, residents, and the technology partner ICS.AI, with the objective of streamlining service delivery, improving accessibility, and enabling staff to focus on higher-value tasks. The solution is implemented through generative language technologies embedded into existing digital assistants, Darcie and Ali, which are extended to function as conversational copilots across customer service workflows. These systems are trained on council service data, policies, and historical interactions to support natural, context-aware dialogue, case triage, and guided resolution, while integrating with customer relationship management and back-office systems. The initiative improves service consistency, reduces handling times, and enables more complex interactions to be resolved digitally. Over the four-year, £7 million programme launched in 2024, the Council expects improved citizen experience, greater operational efficiency, and scalable digital capability that delivers long-term value for public service transformation.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1913,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01431,EU_PSTW_202609_01431,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,University of Helsinki,Education not definable by level.,"The Elements of AI initiative addresses this challenge by providing a free online course aimed at a wide audience, explaining what artificial intelligence is, what it can and cannot do, and how people can begin to understand or develop related methods.",Elements of AI - free online course designed by the Icelandic government,University of Helsinki; Academic-Research; Education not definable by level.; Finland,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Building broad public understanding of artificial intelligence requires educational offers that are accessible to non-specialists, especially if governments want citizens and workers to engage confidently with technological change. The Elements of AI initiative addresses this challenge by providing a free online course aimed at a wide audience, explaining what artificial intelligence is, what it can and cannot do, and how people can begin to understand or develop related methods. The objective is to expand AI literacy across society and lower the barriers to entry for learners without advanced mathematics or programming backgrounds. The implementation consists of a series of online courses created by MinnaLearn and the University of Helsinki and delivered through digital learning platforms designed for self-paced participation. At a conceptual level, the programme combines structured educational content, practical examples, and accessible explanations so that learners from varied backgrounds can engage with core concepts. The material is presented as open, reusable digital education rather than as a specialist technical qualification. The impact includes broader public access to foundational AI knowledge, improved digital skills awareness, and greater readiness among learners to understand the implications of AI in work and society. It also creates scalable educational value by offering a freely available course model that can reach large and diverse audiences across countries and sectors.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1914,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01432,EU_PSTW_202609_01432,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Wiener Stadtwerke,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.","This pilot addresses the challenge of systematically assessing automated systems used by a large municipal service provider, Wiener Stadtwerke.",Auditing a City of Vienna AI solution under IEEE CertifAIEd,"Wiener Stadtwerke; Local Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Austria","The objective is to validate that deployed systems meet recognised standards for responsible and trustworthy use. The solution applies the IEEE CertifAIEd assessment process to a concrete application, evaluating governance, risk management, transparency, and impact on users.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Public service providers increasingly deploy automated systems that must align with ethical, legal, and societal values. The City of Vienna sought to ensure that its digital services adhere to principles of transparency, accountability, and human-centric design. This pilot addresses the challenge of systematically assessing automated systems used by a large municipal service provider, Wiener Stadtwerke. Stakeholders include public authorities, service users, regulators, and technology developers. The objective is to validate that deployed systems meet recognised standards for responsible and trustworthy use. The solution applies the IEEE CertifAIEd assessment process to a concrete application, evaluating governance, risk management, transparency, and impact on users. Independent auditors analyse documentation, development processes, and operational practices against established criteria. The impact includes increased organisational awareness of ethical and governance requirements, strengthened trust in public digital services, and international recognition of Vienna’s digital humanism strategy. The pilot provides a reference for other cities seeking structured assurance of responsible technology deployment in public services.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1915,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01433,EU_PSTW_202609_01433,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,British Standards Institution,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.","Many lack structured methodologies to assess fairness, robustness and transparency.",British Standards Institution: EU AI Act Readiness Assessment and Algorithmic Auditing,"British Standards Institution; Central-Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; United Kingdom",UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Organisations deploying advanced algorithmic systems face growing regulatory requirements under the EU AI Act, necessitating robust compliance, testing and documentation processes. Many lack structured methodologies to assess fairness, robustness and transparency. Stakeholders include technology providers, regulated organisations, auditors and regulators. The objective is to provide structured readiness assessments and algorithmic auditing services aligned with emerging European standards. The approach combines technical testing tools with conformity assessment frameworks. Automated evaluation platforms measure compliance against technical standards through fairness testing, bias detection, robustness analysis and performance validation. Detailed reporting tools generate evidence to support governance and certification processes, and assessments are designed to integrate with organisational risk management systems. Expected impacts include improved regulatory preparedness, reduced compliance uncertainty, enhanced trust in algorithmic systems and clearer accountability mechanisms across sectors deploying high-risk applications.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1916,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01434,EU_PSTW_202609_01434,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Provincie Fryslân,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.","Governments adopting automated systems face complex ethical, legal, and social considerations that cannot be addressed through technical evaluation alone.",Z-Inspection – Assessment for Responsible Artificial Intelligence,"Provincie Fryslân; Regional Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Netherlands","The objective is to identify risks, ethical dilemmas, and governance gaps early in the lifecycle of automated systems and to build institutional capacity for responsible use. The solution applies the Z-Inspection methodology, bringing together experts from multiple disciplines to analyse the purpose, design, data use, and societal impact of a concrete system.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Governments adopting automated systems face complex ethical, legal, and social considerations that cannot be addressed through technical evaluation alone. The Z-Inspection pilot in the Province of Fryslân addresses the need for holistic assessment methods that support responsible adoption across public administrations. Stakeholders include policymakers, civil servants, citizens, and interdisciplinary experts. The objective is to identify risks, ethical dilemmas, and governance gaps early in the lifecycle of automated systems and to build institutional capacity for responsible use. The solution applies the Z-Inspection methodology, bringing together experts from multiple disciplines to analyse the purpose, design, data use, and societal impact of a concrete system. Structured discussions explore conflicts of interest, accountability, and alignment with public values. The outcomes are documented as lessons learned and shared across government. The impact includes improved understanding of responsible deployment practices, increased digital awareness, and a practical framework for assessing future systems. The pilot supports more confident, transparent, and trustworthy use of advanced technologies in the public sector.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1917,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01435,EU_PSTW_202609_01435,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,European Commission,R&D general public services.,"Music rights are traditionally difficult for creators to monetise directly, with complex royalty chains and intermediaries that can reduce transparency for both artists and fans.",Anotherblock,European Commission; European Institution/Agency; R&D general public services.; Sweden,"The service is implemented by issuing music ownership tokens on Ethereum using the ERC-721 standard, where each token carries a unique identifier and encodes an ownership percentage for a specific song, alongside associated artwork and a linked real-world legal contract defining royalty terms and ownership guarantees.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Music rights are traditionally difficult for creators to monetise directly, with complex royalty chains and intermediaries that can reduce transparency for both artists and fans. Anotherblock addresses this by enabling artists and rights-holders to sell defined portions of track-level rights to their community, creating a clearer route for participation and investment in streaming royalties while maintaining a legally enforceable relationship between parties. Stakeholders include creators, collectors, platforms handling royalty reporting, and regulators seeking clarity on how tokenised intellectual property rights should be structured and supervised, with the objective of making rights transfers simpler, auditable, and fairer. The service is implemented by issuing music ownership tokens on Ethereum using the ERC-721 standard, where each token carries a unique identifier and encodes an ownership percentage for a specific song, alongside associated artwork and a linked real-world legal contract defining royalty terms and ownership guarantees. Transactions and ownership history are recorded on-chain, while off-chain legal documentation and royalty flows are aligned to token holdings through platform governance and reporting integrations. The approach can reduce friction and costs in rights distribution, improve transparency of ownership, and create new, engagement-driven revenue options for artists; participation in the European Blockchain Sandbox is used to test compliance pathways and surface regulatory needs for legal certainty in tokenised IP structures.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1918,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01436,EU_PSTW_202609_01436,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,European Commission,R&D general public services.,"Organisations and individuals increasingly rely on digital documents for contractual, legal, and administrative processes, yet traditional notarisation, signing, and document management remain fragmented, slow, and costly. Ensuring legal validity, integrity, and long-term trust in digital documents is a persistent challenge, particularly in cross-border contexts.",Dedit.io – Blockchain-Based Notarisation and Custodial Wallet Platform,European Commission; European Institution/Agency; R&D general public services.; Italy,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Organisations and individuals increasingly rely on digital documents for contractual, legal, and administrative processes, yet traditional notarisation, signing, and document management remain fragmented, slow, and costly. Ensuring legal validity, integrity, and long-term trust in digital documents is a persistent challenge, particularly in cross-border contexts. Dedit.io addresses this need by providing a unified platform for secure document notarisation, signing, storage, and sharing. Stakeholders include legal professionals, businesses, public authorities, and end users requiring trusted digital transactions. The objective is to streamline document workflows while ensuring high levels of legal certainty and regulatory compliance. The solution provides a distributed notarisation and validation service supporting multiple protocols and development environments, combined with a custodial wallet for managing digital assets and documents. Advanced electronic signatures are integrated, with ongoing development towards stronger digital identity and signature authentication mechanisms. The platform is designed for interoperability and scalability, allowing adaptation to diverse legal and market requirements. The impact includes faster document processing, reduced administrative overhead, and improved trust in digital transactions. Participation in the European Blockchain Sandbox supports regulatory alignment and positions Dedit.io as a scalable, compliant solution for end-to-end digital document management.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1919,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01437,EU_PSTW_202609_01437,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,European Commission,R&D general public services.,Chunk Works addresses this challenge by providing a universal security layer that combines strong encryption with resilient data storage.,Chunk Works – Universal Security Layer for Distributed Data Protection,European Commission; European Institution/Agency; R&D general public services.; Netherlands,"The solution breaks data into encrypted fragments using erasure coding, adds redundancy, and distributes these fragments across multiple locations using distributed ledger technology.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"As organisations increasingly operate across hybrid and decentralised infrastructures, ensuring data security, availability, and resilience has become more complex. Conventional storage systems face risks from cyberattacks, data loss, and emerging threats such as quantum computing. Chunk Works addresses this challenge by providing a universal security layer that combines strong encryption with resilient data storage. Stakeholders include enterprises, public bodies, and service providers handling sensitive data across cloud, on-premise, and edge environments. The objective is to ensure privacy, integrity, and continuous availability of data without compromising performance or deployment flexibility. The solution breaks data into encrypted fragments using erasure coding, adds redundancy, and distributes these fragments across multiple locations using distributed ledger technology. Post-quantum hybrid encryption protects data against future cryptographic threats, while autonomous recovery mechanisms reconstruct data even if parts are lost or unavailable. The impact includes increased resilience to failures and attacks, reduced risk of data loss, and future-proof security. Chunk Works delivers a scalable, infrastructure-agnostic solution that strengthens trust in digital data management and supports secure operations in increasingly decentralised environments.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1920,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01438,EU_PSTW_202609_01438,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,European Commission,R&D general public services.,"The growth of crypto-assets and decentralised finance has created new challenges for combating money laundering, terrorist financing, and financial crime.",Coinfirm – Blockchain Analytics and Investigations for Regulatory Compliance,European Commission; European Institution/Agency; R&D general public services.; United Kingdom,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The growth of crypto-assets and decentralised finance has created new challenges for combating money laundering, terrorist financing, and financial crime. Regulators and financial institutions require sophisticated tools to understand complex transaction flows and assess risk in blockchain ecosystems. Coinfirm addresses this need by providing advanced analytics and investigation capabilities for digital assets. Stakeholders include banks, virtual asset service providers, payment providers, financial intelligence units, regulators, and decentralised platforms. The objective is to support compliance with anti-money laundering and counter-terrorist financing requirements while enabling responsible innovation. The solution aggregates on-chain, off-chain, and shared intelligence data to build detailed models of asset flows and address behaviour. Advanced risk analytics evaluate transaction patterns, identify high-risk entities, and enable real-time monitoring of address clusters. These insights support source-of-funds analysis, transaction screening, and regulatory reporting. The impact includes improved detection of illicit activity, stronger compliance capabilities, and increased transparency in crypto markets. Coinfirm supports safer adoption of digital assets and contributes to the integrity and stability of the evolving financial ecosystem.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1921,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01439,EU_PSTW_202609_01439,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,EUIPO,R&D general public services.,UNKNOWN,Eonpass: stopping counterfeit goods,EUIPO; European Institution/Agency; R&D general public services.; European Union,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Counterfeit goods entering the European Union pose risks to consumer safety, brand integrity, and public revenues, while placing significant strain on customs authorities. Eonpass emerged as a proposed solution following a competition organised by the European Union Intellectual Property Office (EUIPO), aiming to improve the detection of fraudulent goods at borders. Stakeholders included EUIPO, customs authorities, logistics providers, and private-sector partners such as EY and Jet Air Service. The objective was to create a shared, trusted mechanism for verifying the authenticity of goods across complex international supply chains. The proposed system used distributed ledger technology to record product identifiers, shipment data, and verification events, enabling customs officials to cross-check goods against tamper-resistant records at the point of entry. Open standards were emphasised to allow interoperability between manufacturers, freight operators, and enforcement agencies. While Eonpass was recognised as the strongest conceptual model in the EUIPO competition, public sources indicate that it remained at proof-of-concept level and was not rolled out as an operational customs system. Nonetheless, the project demonstrated how shared digital ledgers could enhance transparency and trust in border processes, informing later policy and technical discussions on anti-counterfeiting measures.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PLANNED,계획,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1922,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01440,EU_PSTW_202609_01440,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Alps Blockchain,Economic affairs n.e.c,"Small hydropower plants often struggle with economic viability during periods of low energy demand, leading to underutilised infrastructure.",Hydropower for bitcoin mining,Alps Blockchain; Private sector; Economic affairs n.e.c; Italy,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Small hydropower plants often struggle with economic viability during periods of low energy demand, leading to underutilised infrastructure. In northern Italy, municipalities sought alternative revenue models. The objective of this initiative is to improve the economic sustainability of hydropower plants by coupling energy production with high-performance computing demand. The implementation involves colocating data centres at hydropower facilities, where computing capacity is sold to external operators. This model avoids direct municipal involvement in digital asset markets while monetising surplus capacity. Expected impacts include improved financial sustainability of renewable infrastructure and reduced waste. Qualitative outcomes include regional innovation, repurposing of abandoned assets and alignment between renewable energy and digital infrastructure.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1923,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01441,EU_PSTW_202609_01441,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Lugano,Economic affairs n.e.c,UNKNOWN,Bitcoin and Tether as Legal Tender in Lugano,City of Lugano; Local Government; Economic affairs n.e.c; Switzerland,"Seeking to position itself as a European hub for digital finance, the City of Lugano announced a partnership with Tether to promote the use of cryptocurrencies for everyday transactions. The initiative aimed to allow residents and businesses to use Bitcoin, Tether, and a local token (LVGA Points) for taxes, public services, and retail payments.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Seeking to position itself as a European hub for digital finance, the City of Lugano announced a partnership with Tether to promote the use of cryptocurrencies for everyday transactions. The initiative aimed to allow residents and businesses to use Bitcoin, Tether, and a local token (LVGA Points) for taxes, public services, and retail payments. Stakeholders included the city administration, local businesses, residents, and technology providers. The objective was to stimulate innovation, attract investment, and modernise payment infrastructure. The initiative relied on blockchain-based payment rails and digital wallets to enable peer-to-peer and merchant transactions without traditional intermediaries. While the announcement framed cryptocurrencies as “de facto legal tender,” subsequent reporting indicates a more limited implementation focused on voluntary acceptance, pilot use cases, and municipal payments rather than universal adoption across all businesses. The initiative increased Lugano’s international visibility and led to selective uptake among merchants, but it did not replicate the mandatory legal tender model seen elsewhere. As such, it represents a partial implementation that illustrates both the opportunities and practical constraints of integrating crypto-assets into local economies.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1924,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01442,EU_PSTW_202609_01442,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Central Bank of Norway,Economic affairs n.e.c,UNKNOWN,Central Bank of Norway CBDC Sandbox,Central Bank of Norway; Central-Government; Economic affairs n.e.c; Norway,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Norges Bank established a central bank digital currency (CBDC) sandbox to test technical solutions for potential issuance of digital central bank money. The objective is to evaluate infrastructure for minting, transferring and destroying digital tokens in a controlled experimental environment. The sandbox is built on Ethereum-based technology and includes smart contracts implementing ERC-20 token standards. Access is restricted to authorised participants, with credential-based controls. A custom front-end interface allows interaction with the test network, while monitoring tools such as BlockScout and Grafana provide transaction tracking and performance analytics. The environment enables simulation of issuance, distribution and redemption scenarios without public deployment. By releasing open-source code, the central bank promotes transparency and technical experimentation while assessing feasibility, scalability and security considerations. The sandbox supports evidence-based evaluation of digital currency infrastructure before any policy decision on formal implementation.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1925,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01443,EU_PSTW_202609_01443,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,European Commission,R&D general public services.,"Upcoming EU regulations require manufacturers to provide transparent, verifiable information on product origin, composition and environmental impact, creating significant compliance and data management challenges across supply chains.",Digital Product Passport solution for the Circular Economy,European Commission; European Institution/Agency; R&D general public services.; Luxembourg,The solution uses blockchain-based infrastructure to anchor verified product data and Product Circularity Data Sheets (PCDS) in line with the emerging ISO 59040 standard.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Upcoming EU regulations require manufacturers to provide transparent, verifiable information on product origin, composition and environmental impact, creating significant compliance and data management challenges across supply chains. Compellio, a Luxembourg-based startup, developed a Digital Product Passport (DPP) solution to support circular economy objectives and regulatory readiness under the Ecodesign and Battery Regulations. Stakeholders include manufacturers, regulators, consumers and third-party auditors. The objective is to create a trusted, interoperable mechanism for tracking product lifecycle data while ensuring data integrity and regulatory compliance. The solution uses blockchain-based infrastructure to anchor verified product data and Product Circularity Data Sheets (PCDS) in line with the emerging ISO 59040 standard. Third-party verified data is recorded to ensure traceability and immutability, while off-chain systems manage detailed product information and updates. The platform integrates with enterprise systems and supports selective data disclosure. Selected for the EU Blockchain Regulatory Sandbox, the pilot demonstrates compliance-by-design, improved trust across supply chains and reduced reporting friction. It positions DPPs as a scalable tool for transparency, enabling businesses to meet regulatory demands while supporting greener consumer choices and circular value chains.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1926,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01444,EU_PSTW_202609_01444,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,EIB,Economic affairs n.e.c,"Traditional bond issuance processes involve multiple intermediaries, manual reconciliation steps and settlement delays, which can increase operational complexity and cost.",Digital Bonds on a Public Blockchain – European Investment Bank,EIB; European Institution/Agency; Economic affairs n.e.c; European Union,"The objective is to demonstrate the feasibility of issuing and settling bonds using distributed ledger technology. Smart contract functionalities automate key lifecycle events, and interoperability with existing financial systems is maintained.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Traditional bond issuance processes involve multiple intermediaries, manual reconciliation steps and settlement delays, which can increase operational complexity and cost. Capital market participants seek more transparent and efficient issuance mechanisms. Stakeholders include the European Investment Bank, underwriting banks, central banks and investors. The objective is to demonstrate the feasibility of issuing and settling bonds using distributed ledger technology. The solution records bond registration and settlement transactions on a public blockchain platform, enabling tokenised bond representation and near real-time transaction visibility. In collaboration with Banque de France, central bank digital currency was used to represent payment flows on-chain. Smart contract functionalities automate key lifecycle events, and interoperability with existing financial systems is maintained. Anticipated impacts include reduced intermediary reliance, faster settlement, enhanced transparency of ownership and trading flows, and lower fixed operational costs, contributing to the digitalisation of capital markets.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1927,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01445,EU_PSTW_202609_01445,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Differdange,R&D environmental protection.,UNKNOWN,Survcoin,City of Differdange; Local Government; R&D environmental protection.; Luxembourg,"This use case addresses these objectives by incentivising the use of soft mobility options such as walking, cycling, and public transport, involving municipal employees and local stakeholders. The solution is implemented through a digital reward system based on an alternative local currency, Survcoin, which is issued to participating employees according to their verified reduction in CO₂ emissions.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Urban mobility is a major contributor to carbon emissions, particularly commuting by private car, and local authorities are seeking practical ways to influence behaviour while supporting employee wellbeing. In Differdange, the municipality aimed to reduce emissions generated by daily commuting among its approximately 800 employees and encourage healthier, more sustainable travel choices. This use case addresses these objectives by incentivising the use of soft mobility options such as walking, cycling, and public transport, involving municipal employees and local stakeholders. The solution is implemented through a digital reward system based on an alternative local currency, Survcoin, which is issued to participating employees according to their verified reduction in CO₂ emissions. Mobility data is collected to calculate individual contributions, and rewards are credited in Survcoins that can be used within the local ecosystem. The initiative increases awareness of travel-related emissions, encourages behavioural change, and promotes active mobility. It delivers measurable environmental benefits through reduced car usage, supports employee health and wellbeing, and strengthens local engagement, while providing the city with a scalable and transparent mechanism to link climate goals to everyday mobility choices.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1928,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01446,EU_PSTW_202609_01446,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Regione Calabria,R&D general public services.,"Emergency departments face increasing pressure due to high patient volumes, time-critical decision-making, and limited medical staff, raising the risk of delayed or missed diagnoses.",AI for Emergency information system,Regione Calabria; Regional Government; R&D general public services.; Italy,"Nonetheless, it highlighted emerging directions in emergency care innovation and contributed to policy-level discussions on the responsible use of advanced analytics in high-risk healthcare settings.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Emergency departments face increasing pressure due to high patient volumes, time-critical decision-making, and limited medical staff, raising the risk of delayed or missed diagnoses. This project proposal aimed to explore a new emergency information system to support faster and more accurate triage and intervention. Stakeholders included hospitals, emergency responders, healthcare professionals, and regional authorities. The objective was to improve the overall rescue process by enhancing early assessment of patients’ conditions before or during emergency intake. The proposed system would analyse facial expressions and vocal characteristics captured during emergency interactions, combining these signals with contextual information to identify potential indicators of distress or medical urgency. Conceptually, the system would integrate real-time data capture with analytical models and clinical workflows, providing decision-support signals to medical staff rather than automated diagnoses. Despite its inclusion in Interreg Europe’s project ideas, the initiative remained at the exploratory and proposal stage, with no evidence of operational deployment. Nonetheless, it highlighted emerging directions in emergency care innovation and contributed to policy-level discussions on the responsible use of advanced analytics in high-risk healthcare settings.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PLANNED,계획,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1929,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01447,EU_PSTW_202609_01447,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,MALARDALENS University,R&D general public services.,"The ARTIMATION project, funded under the SESAR Joint Undertaking, addresses the challenge of transparency in artificial intelligence systems used in air traffic management (ATM).",ARTIMATION Project: Building trust in air traffic management AI,MALARDALENS University; Academic-Research; R&D general public services.; Sweden,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The ARTIMATION project, funded under the SESAR Joint Undertaking, addresses the challenge of transparency in artificial intelligence systems used in air traffic management (ATM). Increasing reliance on automation and predictive models in ATM requires high levels of trust, safety and regulatory compliance. ARTIMATION developed explainable AI techniques to make the outputs of neural networks and deep learning models more interpretable for human operators. The project integrates explainability algorithms that highlight relevant input factors and provide traceable reasoning for system recommendations within ATM decision-support tools. By embedding transparency mechanisms into existing AI applications, the solution enables controllers and system managers to understand and validate automated outputs. The approach strengthens operational trust, supports regulatory acceptance and facilitates responsible deployment of AI in safety-critical environments. ARTIMATION contributes to modernising Europe’s ATM infrastructure while ensuring that human oversight remains central to operational decision-making.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1930,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01448,EU_PSTW_202609_01448,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Estonian Information System Authority,General services.,"By automating personal data masking, the solution reduces manual workload, lowers the risk of data breaches and strengthens compliance with data protection regulations while supporting secure training and document handling processes in the public sector.",AI Anonymisator for Estonian public sector,Estonian Information System Authority; Central-Government; General services.; Estonia,"The tool uses named entity recognition models, trained on corpora developed by the University of Tartu, to detect entities such as personal names, identification codes and locations.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Estonian public authorities process large volumes of personal data in appeals, case files and administrative documents, creating risks related to privacy protection and GDPR compliance. To mitigate these risks, an AI-based anonymisation application was developed in 2022 to automatically identify and transform personal data within textual documents. The tool uses named entity recognition models, trained on corpora developed by the University of Tartu, to detect entities such as personal names, identification codes and locations. Detected entities can be replaced with alternative values from the same category, enabling anonymisation or pseudonymisation depending on user requirements. The application can be integrated into existing information systems and used across institutions handling sensitive data. Clients may further train the models with institution-specific datasets to improve accuracy in specialised contexts. By automating personal data masking, the solution reduces manual workload, lowers the risk of data breaches and strengthens compliance with data protection regulations while supporting secure training and document handling processes in the public sector.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1931,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01449,EU_PSTW_202609_01449,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Estonian Public Broadcasting,Communication,"By automating caption generation, the initiative increases inclusion, improves access to public information and reduces production costs associated with manual subtitling.",Automatic real-time subtitles,Estonian Public Broadcasting; Central-Government; Communication; Estonia,"Traditional subtitling requires manual editing and preparation, limiting its use for live events.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"To enhance accessibility of live broadcasts for people who are hard of hearing, Estonia introduced automatic real-time subtitles generated through speech recognition technology. Traditional subtitling requires manual editing and preparation, limiting its use for live events. The autosubtitles system produces captions instantly during broadcasts without human editing, relying on machine speech-to-text models developed within the Quick Writer solution at Tallinn University of Technology. The system processes live audio streams, converts speech into text and displays captions with a short delay of a few seconds. Performance depends on speech clarity, background noise and speaking speed. Users can activate subtitles via their television remote control when supported by their service provider. By automating caption generation, the initiative increases inclusion, improves access to public information and reduces production costs associated with manual subtitling. Although minor inaccuracies may occur, the system significantly expands real-time accessibility across national broadcasting services.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,S3_DOCUMENT_INTELLIGENCE,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1932,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01450,EU_PSTW_202609_01450,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Estonian Information System Authority,General services.,"Citizens interacting with public authorities often need to consult multiple institutions, each with its own website and processes, leading to fragmented experiences and repeated enquiries. Estonia addressed this challenge by creating a unified conversational access layer across government websites.",Bureaucrat on eID website,Estonian Information System Authority; Central-Government; General services.; Estonia,"This use case involves the Information System Authority, multiple public institutions, and citizens, with the objective of providing a single, intuitive way to access public information. The Bureaucrat operates as an interoperable network of chatbots deployed across authority websites, enabling colloquial, natural communication through a shared chat interface.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Citizens interacting with public authorities often need to consult multiple institutions, each with its own website and processes, leading to fragmented experiences and repeated enquiries. Estonia addressed this challenge by creating a unified conversational access layer across government websites. This use case involves the Information System Authority, multiple public institutions, and citizens, with the objective of providing a single, intuitive way to access public information. The Bureaucrat operates as an interoperable network of chatbots deployed across authority websites, enabling colloquial, natural communication through a shared chat interface. The system routes queries to the relevant institution and retrieves accurate information from official sources, while maintaining a consistent user experience. Integration across agencies reduces duplication and improves coherence of responses. The solution enhances accessibility of public services, reduces navigation complexity for users, and improves efficiency for institutions by sharing a common conversational infrastructure. It delivers scalable digital government interaction and strengthens trust in public service delivery.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1933,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01451,EU_PSTW_202609_01451,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Ministry of Economic Affairs and Communications,"General economic, commercial and labour affair","The objective is preventive support rather than enforcement, enabling informed decision-making before financial difficulties escalate.",Prototype of the entrepreneur's early warning service,"Ministry of Economic Affairs and Communications; Central-Government; General economic, commercial and labour affair; Estonia",UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Small and medium-sized enterprises often encounter financial distress too late to take corrective action, leading to insolvency risks with wider economic consequences. Estonia developed a prototype early warning service to help entrepreneurs identify potential liquidity and solvency issues at an early stage. Stakeholders include business owners, public authorities and economic policymakers. The objective is preventive support rather than enforcement, enabling informed decision-making before financial difficulties escalate. The prototype continuously monitors publicly available and submitted financial data, comparing company performance against sector-specific benchmarks and economic principles. When deviations from typical patterns are detected, additional analyses assess trends related to cash flow, obligations and sustainability. If risks persist, the system generates an informational alert to the company. The service is advisory only and carries no legal consequences. The prototype demonstrated feasibility and value as a preventive economic tool, supporting business resilience and reducing the likelihood of sudden failures. Its impact lies in empowering entrepreneurs, improving economic stability and enabling public authorities to offer support earlier, while maintaining a non-intrusive, trust-based approach.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1934,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01452,EU_PSTW_202609_01452,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Environmental Board,R&D environmental protection.,UNKNOWN,Bureaucrat of the Environment Agency,Environmental Board; Central-Government; R&D environmental protection.; Estonia,"The Estonian Environment Agency implemented a “Bureaucrat” chatbot on its website to streamline citizen enquiries and provide accessible digital interaction with public services. The Bureaucrat operates as part of an interoperable network of chatbots deployed across Estonian public authorities, enabling conversational access to services through a unified interface.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Estonian Environment Agency implemented a “Bureaucrat” chatbot on its website to streamline citizen enquiries and provide accessible digital interaction with public services. Residents often require guidance on environmental regulations, permits and public information, generating recurring questions. The Bureaucrat operates as part of an interoperable network of chatbots deployed across Estonian public authorities, enabling conversational access to services through a unified interface. The system uses natural language processing to interpret user queries and retrieve information from institutional databases and knowledge repositories. It provides structured responses and directs users to relevant services, while complex cases remain handled by staff. Integration within the wider Bureaucrat ecosystem allows cross-institutional information exchange and consistent user experience. The chatbot enhances accessibility, reduces administrative workload and supports Estonia’s digital governance strategy by enabling colloquial, user-friendly access to public information services.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1935,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01453,EU_PSTW_202609_01453,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Environment agency,Environmental protection n.e.c.,"Manual snow depth measurement is labour-intensive and may limit data frequency and coverage. By replacing manual readings with continuous digital observation, the system reduces operational costs, increases measurement consistency and enhances temporal resolution of snow data.",Remote snow monitoring,Environment agency; Central-Government; Environmental protection n.e.c.; Estonia,Estonian meteorological services introduced machine vision techniques to automate measurement of snow cover at weather stations. The remote monitoring system uses camera-based image capture combined with computer vision algorithms to detect and quantify snow depth from visual markers.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Estonian meteorological services introduced machine vision techniques to automate measurement of snow cover at weather stations. Manual snow depth measurement is labour-intensive and may limit data frequency and coverage. The remote monitoring system uses camera-based image capture combined with computer vision algorithms to detect and quantify snow depth from visual markers. Image analysis models interpret changes in visible reference scales and surface conditions, converting them into structured meteorological data. The automated measurements are integrated into forecasting databases and contribute to development of improved predictive models. By replacing manual readings with continuous digital observation, the system reduces operational costs, increases measurement consistency and enhances temporal resolution of snow data. The approach supports more accurate weather forecasting and climate monitoring while improving efficiency in environmental data collection.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1936,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01454,EU_PSTW_202609_01454,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Institute of the Estonian Language,Education not definable by level.,"Teachers previously needed to manually design gap-fill exercises and assess responses, which was time-consuming and inconsistent.",GAP: preparation of Estonian language tests easy and the assessment automatic.,Institute of the Estonian Language; Central-Government; Education not definable by level.; Estonia,"GAP uses natural language processing to analyse selected texts and automatically generate gap exercises based on specified criteria such as word type, grammatical form and difficulty level.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Estonian Institute developed GAP to simplify preparation and evaluation of Estonian language tests. Teachers previously needed to manually design gap-fill exercises and assess responses, which was time-consuming and inconsistent. GAP uses natural language processing to analyse selected texts and automatically generate gap exercises based on specified criteria such as word type, grammatical form and difficulty level. The system identifies appropriate lexical items, removes them to create gaps and produces structured test versions for learners. Automated assessment tools evaluate responses according to predefined linguistic rules. By enabling rapid creation of customised exercises, GAP reduces preparation time and supports differentiated learning. The tool enhances consistency in assessment and facilitates scalable language testing aligned with curriculum requirements. It strengthens digital support for language education while preserving teacher oversight in final evaluation and pedagogical decisions.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1937,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01455,EU_PSTW_202609_01455,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Tartu University Hospital,Public health services.,UNKNOWN,Machine translation customer support communication window on the website of Tartu University Hospital,Tartu University Hospital; Local Government; Public health services.; Estonia,"This use case involves the hospital, patients, and support staff, with the objective of enabling multilingual communication through digital channels. The solution is implemented as an online customer support window combining live chat, email submission, FAQs, and machine translation powered by Askly.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Healthcare providers increasingly serve diverse populations with varying language needs, making effective communication essential for safety and patient satisfaction. Tartu University Hospital sought to improve accessibility of its online customer support for non-Estonian speakers. This use case involves the hospital, patients, and support staff, with the objective of enabling multilingual communication through digital channels. The solution is implemented as an online customer support window combining live chat, email submission, FAQs, and machine translation powered by Askly. Incoming messages are translated in real time, allowing staff to respond in their own language while patients communicate in theirs. The system integrates with existing support workflows and hospital information systems. The solution improves inclusivity, reduces misunderstandings, and enhances response efficiency. It delivers better patient experience, supports equitable access to healthcare information, and enables scalable multilingual support without proportionally increasing staffing costs.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1938,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01456,EU_PSTW_202609_01456,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Ministry of Economic Affairs and Communications,Economic affairs n.e.c,"Citizens often struggle to find accurate and relevant information on public authority websites, particularly when content is complex or legalistic. Despite mixed results, the pilot provided valuable insights into localisation challenges, infrastructure needs and language equity in digital public services, informing future investments in Estonian-language models.",Politsei.ee large-language model LLM trained on data,Ministry of Economic Affairs and Communications; Central-Government; Economic affairs n.e.c; Estonia,"The pilot trained a model on State Gazette data and tested it within the Azure OpenAI environment, using a ChatGPT 3.5-based architecture.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Citizens often struggle to find accurate and relevant information on public authority websites, particularly when content is complex or legalistic. The Estonian Police and Border Guard Board tested a pilot to improve information retrieval on politsei.ee by developing a tailored language model trained on official legal texts. Stakeholders include citizens, public service users and government web administrators. The objective was to assess whether customised language models could improve searchability and user interaction in Estonian-language public services. The pilot trained a model on State Gazette data and tested it within the Azure OpenAI environment, using a ChatGPT 3.5-based architecture. The system was evaluated on its ability to answer questions and retrieve relevant information. Results showed that performance in Estonian lagged behind English, highlighting language resource gaps and training data limitations. Despite mixed results, the pilot provided valuable insights into localisation challenges, infrastructure needs and language equity in digital public services, informing future investments in Estonian-language models.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1939,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01457,EU_PSTW_202609_01457,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,National Library of Estonia,Cultural services.,UNKNOWN,RaRa Bureaucrat,National Library of Estonia; Central-Government; Cultural services.; Estonia,The National Library of Estonia implemented a Bureaucrat chatbot on its website to improve access to library services and information.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The National Library of Estonia implemented a Bureaucrat chatbot on its website to improve access to library services and information. Users frequently require assistance with catalogue searches, opening hours, digital collections and service procedures. As part of Estonia’s interoperable Bureaucrat network, the chatbot provides conversational interaction through a web-based chat interface. Natural language processing components interpret user questions and retrieve relevant information from library databases and curated knowledge sources. The system delivers structured responses and directs users to appropriate resources, while complex research queries remain managed by library staff. Integration with the broader Bureaucrat ecosystem ensures consistent functionality across public institutions. The deployment enhances user experience, increases service availability outside office hours and reduces routine enquiry workload. By enabling accessible digital interaction, RaRa Bureaucrat supports the Library’s modernisation and public engagement objectives.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1940,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01458,EU_PSTW_202609_01458,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Health Insurance Fund,Public health services.,"Healthcare professionals frequently encounter complex cases where symptoms do not clearly indicate a single diagnosis, requiring consultation of multiple information sources during limited consultation times. Fragmented access to guidelines and drug databases can delay decision-making.",HIF EE: Information-based decision support,Health Insurance Fund; Central-Government; Public health services.; Estonia,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Healthcare professionals frequently encounter complex cases where symptoms do not clearly indicate a single diagnosis, requiring consultation of multiple information sources during limited consultation times. Fragmented access to guidelines and drug databases can delay decision-making. Stakeholders include physicians, nurses, healthcare institutions and patients. The objective is to provide integrated, real-time decision support during clinical encounters. The system aggregates international and domestic treatment guidelines, drug interaction databases, adverse reaction registries and pregnancy and lactation information into a unified search interface. Natural language search and structured query mechanisms enable rapid retrieval of evidence-based recommendations. Integration with clinical workflows ensures accessibility during patient visits. Expected impacts include reduced time spent searching for information, improved diagnostic accuracy, safer prescribing through interaction checks and enhanced patient-centred care by freeing time for direct consultation.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1941,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01459,EU_PSTW_202609_01459,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Brno Municipality,Waste management.,"Manual inspections are costly and infrequent. Although limited in scale, the pilot demonstrated improved situational awareness, faster intervention by city services and better user experience for residents.",Computer Vision for monitoring the condition of streets and the city environment,Brno Municipality; Local Government; Waste management.; Czechia,"The system detects overflowing containers and visual anomalies, with results displayed on a city map via a React-based frontend using Leaflet.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Municipalities require timely information on the condition of streets, waste containers and public infrastructure to maintain cleanliness and service quality. Manual inspections are costly and infrequent. This pilot addressed the problem by developing a visual monitoring system for overflowing waste containers, with potential extension to other urban anomalies. Stakeholders include city service departments and residents. The objective was to improve responsiveness and optimise maintenance operations. A computer vision model based on fine-tuned ResNet-32 architecture was trained on a dataset of 300 images of waste containers collected from streets and online sources. The system detects overflowing containers and visual anomalies, with results displayed on a city map via a React-based frontend using Leaflet. Although limited in scale, the pilot demonstrated improved situational awareness, faster intervention by city services and better user experience for residents. It highlighted scalability potential to potholes, signage and broader urban monitoring, offering a cost-effective approach to smarter city maintenance.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1942,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01460,EU_PSTW_202609_01460,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,European Commission,General public services n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,Digital Europe Language Tools - eSummary,European Commission; Central-Government; General public services n.e.c.; Belgium,"This use case involves EU institutions, public administrations, and professionals, with the objective of improving efficiency in document analysis. The service is implemented as an online summarisation tool that processes common office and PDF formats and produces concise summaries highlighting key points.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Public administrations and organisations handle large volumes of documents, making it time-consuming to identify relevant information quickly. The European Commission developed eSummary to support faster understanding of documents across languages. This use case involves EU institutions, public administrations, and professionals, with the objective of improving efficiency in document analysis. The service is implemented as an online summarisation tool that processes common office and PDF formats and produces concise summaries highlighting key points. By connecting to the EU’s eTranslation service, it supports all EU languages and additional ones. The system applies advanced text analysis techniques to identify salient information and generate structured summaries returned as Word documents. The solution reduces reading time, supports quicker decision-making, and improves accessibility of information across language barriers. It delivers productivity gains, supports multilingual workflows, and provides scalable value for document-intensive public sector environments.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1943,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01461,EU_PSTW_202609_01461,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,European Commission,General public services n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,Digital Europe Language Tools - eBriefing,European Commission; Central-Government; General public services n.e.c.; Belgium,"The system leverages advanced generative language models and operates in conjunction with eTranslation, enabling submission and output in multiple EU languages. Prompt engineering ensures alignment with institutional conventions, while outputs require human review for accuracy and style before official use.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"eBriefing is an AI-based drafting service developed within the European Commission’s language technology ecosystem to assist in preparing official briefings. EU staff frequently need structured briefing documents summarising complex materials in a standard institutional format. The service allows users to upload documents in docx, txt or text-based PDF format and generates a draft briefing structured with elements such as summary, line-to-take and defensive points. The system leverages advanced generative language models and operates in conjunction with eTranslation, enabling submission and output in multiple EU languages. Prompt engineering ensures alignment with institutional conventions, while outputs require human review for accuracy and style before official use. By automating first-draft preparation, eBriefing reduces drafting time, enhances productivity and supports multilingual document workflows. The tool facilitates consistent formatting and accelerates preparation of policy briefings while maintaining human responsibility for validation and final approval.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1944,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01462,EU_PSTW_202609_01462,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,European Commission,General public services n.e.c.,"By automating transcription tasks, the tool reduces manual workload and shortens turnaround times while enabling scalable processing of multilingual audiovisual material across EU institutions.",Digital Europe Language Tools - Speech Transcription,European Commission; Central-Government; General public services n.e.c.; Belgium,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The eTranslation Speech Transcription service was developed to provide EU institutions and public administrations with an accessible tool for converting audio and video content into written text. Public bodies frequently need transcripts of meetings, speeches and multimedia material for documentation, accessibility and multilingual workflows. The web-based interface allows users to upload sound or video files and receive an automated transcription without specialised software. The system currently relies on customised Microsoft Azure speech-to-text engines, configured to support institutional terminology and multilingual requirements, with plans to integrate alternative engines over time. Uploaded files are processed through secure cloud-based speech recognition models that convert spoken language into structured text output. The service accelerates documentation processes, improves accessibility for hearing-impaired users and supports translation and archiving activities. By automating transcription tasks, the tool reduces manual workload and shortens turnaround times while enabling scalable processing of multilingual audiovisual material across EU institutions.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,S3_DOCUMENT_INTELLIGENCE,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1945,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01463,EU_PSTW_202609_01463,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,European Commission,General public services n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,"Digital Europe Language Tools - Social Media Translator, Anonymization support, Classification",European Commission; Central-Government; General public services n.e.c.; Belgium,"Through a web interface, users compose a message in one language and automatically generate translations using eTranslation’s “General Text” engines, optimised for short-form content. The anonymisation tool replaces or obscures identifiable information using entity recognition and obfuscation techniques derived from the MAPA project, while the classification service assigns Eurovoc descriptors using the JEX tool and returns structured metadata in JSON format.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The eTranslation Social Media Translator enables EU institutions to publish multilingual social media content efficiently while maintaining consistency and reach. Communication teams often need to disseminate short messages across multiple EU languages under tight deadlines. Through a web interface, users compose a message in one language and automatically generate translations using eTranslation’s “General Text” engines, optimised for short-form content. The system accounts for character limits and allows direct posting via linked social media accounts, with an indication that the message was machine translated. Complementary services include anonymisation and document classification. The anonymisation tool replaces or obscures identifiable information using entity recognition and obfuscation techniques derived from the MAPA project, while the classification service assigns Eurovoc descriptors using the JEX tool and returns structured metadata in JSON format. Together, these tools enhance multilingual communication, protect personal data and streamline content management within EU digital workflows.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S3_DOCUMENT_INTELLIGENCE,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1946,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01464,EU_PSTW_202609_01464,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,European Commission,General public services n.e.c.,The services operate on text-based files; scanned PDFs without embedded text are not processed due to lack of standalone OCR functionality.,Digital Europe Language Tools - Named-entity recognition,European Commission; Central-Government; General public services n.e.c.; Belgium,The web interface processes uploaded text-based documents and applies entity recognition models to detect and tag named entities. Complementary anonymisation and classification modules support replacement or masking of personal data and assignment of Eurovoc descriptors using the JEX taxonomy tool.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The eTranslation Named-Entity Recognition (NER) service provides EU institutions with automated extraction of key entities from documents in common formats. Administrations handling multilingual documentation require efficient methods to identify references to persons, organisations and locations. The web interface processes uploaded text-based documents and applies entity recognition models to detect and tag named entities. Results are returned in structured XML format, enabling integration with downstream systems. Complementary anonymisation and classification modules support replacement or masking of personal data and assignment of Eurovoc descriptors using the JEX taxonomy tool. The anonymisation component builds on the MAPA project and allows either entity substitution or visual obfuscation. The services operate on text-based files; scanned PDFs without embedded text are not processed due to lack of standalone OCR functionality. By automating metadata extraction and tagging, the tool enhances document management, improves searchability and supports GDPR-compliant handling of official texts across EU administrations.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1947,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01465,EU_PSTW_202609_01465,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Municipalities of Santarcangelo di Romagna, Riccione and Cattolica",Water supply.,"Water utilities face significant challenges in maintaining ageing distribution networks, where undetected leaks can lead to water loss, infrastructure damage, and costly, disruptive repairs.","Acquedotto 4.0, Hera invests in artificial intelligence","Municipalities of Santarcangelo di Romagna, Riccione and Cattolica; Local Government; Water supply.; Italy","This use case addresses these challenges by enabling early detection of leaks and targeted maintenance, involving the utility operator, municipalities, and residents. The Acquedotto 4.0 pilot is implemented by combining low-cost acoustic sensors with data-driven analysis of water flow sounds to identify patterns associated with leaks.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Water utilities face significant challenges in maintaining ageing distribution networks, where undetected leaks can lead to water loss, infrastructure damage, and costly, disruptive repairs. Hera Group sought to improve monitoring of water networks in several municipalities in Emilia-Romagna while minimising the need for invasive inspections and roadworks. This use case addresses these challenges by enabling early detection of leaks and targeted maintenance, involving the utility operator, municipalities, and residents. The Acquedotto 4.0 pilot is implemented by combining low-cost acoustic sensors with data-driven analysis of water flow sounds to identify patterns associated with leaks. Sensors are deployed across the network to continuously capture data, which is analysed to detect anomalies and localise potential failures. The system integrates with existing maintenance and asset management processes to support timely interventions. The project improves reliability of water supply, reduces non-revenue water, and lowers repair costs by avoiding unnecessary excavation. It also limits traffic disruption and environmental impact, demonstrating how digital monitoring can enhance resilience and efficiency of critical urban infrastructure.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1948,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01466,EU_PSTW_202609_01466,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Vic City Council,Pollution abatement.,"Food supply chains face increasing pressure to quantify and reduce carbon emissions, yet fragmented tools and lack of trust hinder consistent measurement and reporting.",Carboni Zero: minimizing the carbon footprint of the food sector,Vic City Council; Local Government; Pollution abatement.; Spain,The solution uses blockchain infrastructure to create a shared standard for emissions data while ensuring immutability and traceability.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Food supply chains face increasing pressure to quantify and reduce carbon emissions, yet fragmented tools and lack of trust hinder consistent measurement and reporting. The Carboni Zero project in Catalonia addresses this gap by creating an integrated system to quantify and certify emissions across food production processes. Stakeholders include agri-food companies, technology providers, researchers and local authorities. The objective is transparency with confidentiality, enabling optimisation without exposing sensitive production details. The solution uses blockchain infrastructure to create a shared standard for emissions data while ensuring immutability and traceability. Zero-knowledge proof techniques allow verification of total emissions without disclosing underlying proprietary data. The platform is designed by the Centre Blockchain de Catalunya (CBCat), with participation from food companies Bonpreu, Noel and Llet Nostra, technology partner SEIDOR, and academic expertise from UVic. The pilot supports process optimisation, trusted certification and regulatory readiness, demonstrating how secure digital infrastructures can align sustainability goals with business confidentiality and scalability.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1949,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01467,EU_PSTW_202609_01467,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Badalona City Council,Fuel and energy,"Energy self-consumption has grown rapidly in Spain, yet regulatory and market structures have limited the ability of households and communities to exchange surplus renewable energy efficiently.",reWatt: renewable energy standard,Badalona City Council; Local Government; Fuel and energy; Spain,"The platform uses distributed ledger technology to record energy surplus transactions in a peer-to-peer format, removing the need for central intermediaries in surplus management.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Energy self-consumption has grown rapidly in Spain, yet regulatory and market structures have limited the ability of households and communities to exchange surplus renewable energy efficiently. The reWatt Peers project, developed by the Centre Blockchain de Catalunya (CBCat) with the support of Badalona City Council, addresses this gap by enabling the economic exchange of surplus self-consumed energy at both individual and collective levels. Stakeholders include energy prosumers, consumers, energy marketers and local authorities. The objective is to foster local renewable energy communities, increase participation in clean energy generation and improve the economic viability of self-consumption within the framework of Spanish regulation (RD 244/2019). The platform uses distributed ledger technology to record energy surplus transactions in a peer-to-peer format, removing the need for central intermediaries in surplus management. Smart contracts manage allocation and settlement of exchanged energy between participants of the same energy marketer, while metering data provides the basis for accurate accounting. The pilot demonstrates increased transparency, trust and efficiency in surplus energy sharing. Qualitatively, it encourages collaboration, local energy resilience and citizen engagement in the energy transition, while laying the groundwork for scalable community-based renewable energy markets.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1950,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01468,EU_PSTW_202609_01468,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,European Commission,General services.,"European organisations face growing challenges in sharing and monetising data across borders while preserving data sovereignty, intellectual property protection and compliance with EU values. Pontus-X, developed by startup deltaDAO AG, addresses these challenges by providing a federated Web3 ecosystem aligned with Gaia-X principles.",Blockchain-Based Gaia-X Data Infrastructure,European Commission; European Institution/Agency; General services.; Germany,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"European organisations face growing challenges in sharing and monetising data across borders while preserving data sovereignty, intellectual property protection and compliance with EU values. Pontus-X, developed by startup deltaDAO AG, addresses these challenges by providing a federated Web3 ecosystem aligned with Gaia-X principles. Stakeholders include companies, research organisations and public bodies across sectors such as mobility, aerospace, healthcare and manufacturing. The objective is to enable trusted, sovereign data and service exchange within the European digital single market. Pontus-X combines a distributed ledger–based transaction and logging layer with smart contracts for sovereign data exchange, NFT-based metadata catalogues and identity services built on self-sovereign identity and verifiable credentials. Privacy-preserving techniques such as Compute-to-Data allow analytics without exposing raw data. The platform integrates regulated digital payments through the EUROe stablecoin for real-time settlement. Selected for the EU Blockchain Regulatory Sandbox, Pontus-X demonstrates scalable, interoperable data marketplaces that reduce legal and technical friction. Its impact includes improved trust in cross-organisational collaboration, new data monetisation opportunities and stronger alignment with European data strategy objectives, supporting competitiveness and innovation while safeguarding sovereignty.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1951,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01469,EU_PSTW_202609_01469,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,European Commission,Transport,"Industries such as maritime transport generate large volumes of operational data, yet lack of standardisation and data integrity undermines trust, efficiency and regulatory compliance. Selected for the European Blockchain Sandbox, the pilot demonstrates reduced administrative burden, faster verification cycles and improved trust in certified data.",Vidameco for edge data integrity and standardization,European Commission; European Institution/Agency; Transport; Norway,"Smart contracts automate repetitive verification and reporting processes, while identity binding reduces errors and fraud.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Industries such as maritime transport generate large volumes of operational data, yet lack of standardisation and data integrity undermines trust, efficiency and regulatory compliance. The Vidameco research project addresses this problem by providing a unified approach to standardised data collection and tamper-proofing at the edge. Stakeholders include vessel operators, certification bodies, regulators and service providers. The objective is to reduce redundancy, prevent manipulation and simplify compliance with regulations such as the EU’s Monitoring, Reporting and Verification (MRV) framework. Vidameco combines onboard hardware with a microservices-based software architecture to collect, structure and validate data at source. Blockchain technology anchors data fingerprints and stakeholder actions, ensuring traceability and immutability across the value chain. Smart contracts automate repetitive verification and reporting processes, while identity binding reduces errors and fraud. Selected for the European Blockchain Sandbox, the pilot demonstrates reduced administrative burden, faster verification cycles and improved trust in certified data. Its impact extends beyond shipping, offering a reusable model for data integrity and standardisation in other sectors, accelerating digitalisation while maintaining regulatory confidence.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F06_HIGH_VOLUME_SCALABILITY_BOTTLENECK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1952,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01470,EU_PSTW_202609_01470,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""social_nonprofit""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,European Commission,R&D economic affairs,UNKNOWN,Equilibrium Group: Tokenizing real-world assets,European Commission; European Institution/Agency; R&D economic affairs; Finland,"This use case involves financial institutions, regulators, and market participants, with the objective of creating efficient, legally robust digital asset infrastructures. The solution is implemented through distributed ledger systems that represent real-world assets as digital tokens, including EUROe, a fully regulated electronic money instrument issued under EU law.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Financial markets and corporate structures are increasingly exploring digital representations of real-world assets, but face legal, regulatory, and operational uncertainty, particularly in the European context. Equilibrium Group aims to enable compliant tokenisation of assets such as fiat currency and shares of unlisted companies, while ensuring interoperability, transparency, and regulatory alignment. This use case involves financial institutions, regulators, and market participants, with the objective of creating efficient, legally robust digital asset infrastructures. The solution is implemented through distributed ledger systems that represent real-world assets as digital tokens, including EUROe, a fully regulated electronic money instrument issued under EU law. Tokenisation frameworks are designed to support payments, asset registries, and future marketplaces, while integrating identity and compliance mechanisms such as advanced digital identity and zero-knowledge proof-based verification. Participation in the European Blockchain Regulatory Sandbox allows regulatory questions around issuance, trading, and rights to be explored in a controlled environment. The initiative delivers efficiency gains through faster issuance and settlement, improved interoperability, and reduced operational costs, while contributing to regulatory clarity and laying the foundation for scalable, compliant digital asset markets in Europe.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1953,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01471,EU_PSTW_202609_01471,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,European Commission,"General economic, commercial and labour affair",UNKNOWN,Eviden battery passport,"European Commission; European Institution/Agency; General economic, commercial and labour affair; France",UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The transition to electric mobility requires transparent lifecycle information on batteries to support sustainability, safety, repairability and recycling, as mandated by upcoming EU Battery Regulations. Eviden’s Battery Passport initiative addresses this need by providing a shared digital infrastructure for battery data across the automotive ecosystem. Stakeholders include vehicle manufacturers, battery producers, repair centres, recyclers and regulators. The objective is to uniquely identify batteries, enable controlled data sharing and ensure compliance without exposing commercially sensitive information. Each battery is represented by a unique digital passport, with production, performance and usage data securely linked over time. Blockchain technology ensures integrity, traceability and decentralised governance of records, while cloud-based services allow participants to manage their own data. The platform supports selective access rights, enabling downstream actors to retrieve only relevant information. Participation in the European Blockchain Sandbox validates the regulatory relevance of the approach. The pilot enhances transparency, supports circular economy practices and reduces information asymmetries across the value chain, creating long-term value through improved sustainability reporting, compliance readiness and operational efficiency.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1954,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01472,EU_PSTW_202609_01472,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""social_nonprofit""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,European Commission,Transport,"International trade and customs processes remain burdened by paper-based documentation, fragmented data exchange and limited transparency, resulting in delays, higher costs and unnecessary inspections. The project supports more sustainable, efficient international trade by replacing manual processes with verifiable digital workflows.",Open Customs Blockchain,European Commission; European Institution/Agency; Transport; Germany,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"International trade and customs processes remain burdened by paper-based documentation, fragmented data exchange and limited transparency, resulting in delays, higher costs and unnecessary inspections. The Open Customs Blockchain initiative, led by the Open Logistics Foundation with major logistics companies and research partners, aims to modernise customs data exchange across borders. Stakeholders include logistics providers, customs authorities, IT vendors and exporters/importers. The objective is to streamline customs procedures, improve traceability and accelerate cross-border trade. The solution stores customs-relevant information, such as invoice data and export accompanying documents, in a tamper-proof distributed ledger accessible to authorised parties throughout the supply chain. By ensuring consistent data provenance and reusability, the platform reduces duplication and errors. Integration with existing logistics systems enables incremental adoption. Selected for the European Blockchain Sandbox, the pilot demonstrates potential reductions in transit times, fewer physical inspections and improved trust between public and private actors. The project supports more sustainable, efficient international trade by replacing manual processes with verifiable digital workflows.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK|F07_DATA_VISIBILITY_INTEGRATION_GAP,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1955,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01473,EU_PSTW_202609_01473,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Bank of England,R&D economic affairs,UNKNOWN,Privacy Layer for Digital Pound,Bank of England; Central-Government; R&D economic affairs; United Kingdom,The proposed architecture uses decentralised identity and privacy-enhancing technologies to separate user identity from transaction data.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Public trust in a potential UK digital pound depends heavily on assurances around privacy, fraud prevention and proportional oversight. To address these concerns, the Bank of England is collaborating with startup Nuggets to explore a privacy and identity layer for a future central bank digital currency. Stakeholders include central banks, payment providers, consumers and regulators. The objective is to design a system that prevents transaction tracking and correlation by default, while still supporting lawful controls against fraud and money laundering. The proposed architecture uses decentralised identity and privacy-enhancing technologies to separate user identity from transaction data. Cryptographic techniques enable selective disclosure, ensuring that personal data is not centrally stored or routinely accessible. The privacy layer is designed to integrate with potential digital pound core infrastructure rather than replace existing payment systems. Although exploratory, the pilot informs the Bank of England’s ongoing consultation and design choices ahead of any issuance decision post-2025. Its impact lies in demonstrating that strong privacy, regulatory compliance and usability can coexist, shaping public confidence and policy discussions around sovereign digital money.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1956,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01474,EU_PSTW_202609_01474,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""social_nonprofit""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Rovira i Virgili University (URV),R&D education.,UNKNOWN,CAMINS Academic credentials,Rovira i Virgili University (URV); Academic-Research; R&D education.; Spain,"The solution is implemented on the European Blockchain Services Infrastructure (EBSI), enabling credentials to be issued as verifiable digital records anchored to a distributed ledger.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Catalan public universities face growing demand to issue short, modular learning credentials that are easily verifiable across institutions and borders, while giving students greater control over their academic data. The CAMINS initiative brings together five public universities in Catalonia, coordinated by Rovira i Virgili University, with additional universities and shared service bodies acting as observers. The objective is to create a common, decentralised system for issuing, managing and verifying academic microcredentials that is interoperable at European level and aligned with emerging EU standards. The solution is implemented on the European Blockchain Services Infrastructure (EBSI), enabling credentials to be issued as verifiable digital records anchored to a distributed ledger. Universities exchange student data securely through a shared blockchain platform developed by Seidor and Validated ID, which also provides digital identity, electronic signatures and verifiable credentials. Students receive credentials in a personal digital wallet linked to a recognised digital identity, allowing selective sharing with employers or other institutions. The pilot improves trust, reduces administrative overhead and enables cross-border recognition of learning achievements. It strengthens student data sovereignty while laying the foundation for scalable, EU-wide academic credential ecosystems.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1957,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01475,EU_PSTW_202609_01475,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Córdoba City Council,Cultural services.,UNKNOWN,Historical heritage protection,Córdoba City Council; Local Government; Cultural services.; Spain,"This use case involves the municipality, museums, cultural authorities, and citizens, with the objective of certifying and protecting heritage assets. The solution is implemented through a digital certification system where heritage records are stored on a blockchain-based platform, combined with physical tags incorporating geolocation and motion sensors.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Córdoba City Council manages extensive historical and artistic heritage assets that are vulnerable to theft, forgery, and loss of provenance information. Existing tools did not allow rapid verification of ownership, location, and authenticity. This use case involves the municipality, museums, cultural authorities, and citizens, with the objective of certifying and protecting heritage assets. The solution is implemented through a digital certification system where heritage records are stored on a blockchain-based platform, combined with physical tags incorporating geolocation and motion sensors. Each asset is digitally registered with provenance, authorship, and condition data, enabling real-time monitoring of up to 2,000 items. The system improves traceability, prevents fraud, and supports rapid alerts in case of theft or unauthorised movement. It enhances protection of cultural heritage, supports data-driven asset management, and delivers long-term value through improved security, transparency, and preservation.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1958,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01476,EU_PSTW_202609_01476,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Bolsas y Mercados Españoles (BME),R&D economic affairs,"Small and medium-sized enterprises (SMEs) often face limited access to flexible financing instruments, while investors encounter high operational costs and fragmented processes when supporting SME growth. Blockchain records ensure immutability and auditability of transactions, while automated processes reduce manual reconciliation and settlement delays.",Tokenised participatory loans to finance SMEs,Bolsas y Mercados Españoles (BME); Central-Government; R&D economic affairs; Spain,"To address this challenge, the Spanish stock market operator BME tested a pilot for issuing participatory loans to SMEs using tokenisation. The pilot uses Ethereum-based tokenisation to represent participatory loans as digital assets, with smart contracts governing issuance, distribution, settlement and registration.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Small and medium-sized enterprises (SMEs) often face limited access to flexible financing instruments, while investors encounter high operational costs and fragmented processes when supporting SME growth. To address this challenge, the Spanish stock market operator BME tested a pilot for issuing participatory loans to SMEs using tokenisation. The objective was to improve efficiency, transparency and legal certainty across the lifecycle of SME financing instruments, while exploring the regulatory boundaries of distributed ledger–based markets. The pilot uses Ethereum-based tokenisation to represent participatory loans as digital assets, with smart contracts governing issuance, distribution, settlement and registration. Blockchain records ensure immutability and auditability of transactions, while automated processes reduce manual reconciliation and settlement delays. Although secondary trading was restricted under existing regulation, the pilot anticipated future frameworks such as the EU DLT Pilot Regime and updates to Spanish securities law. The results demonstrate operational efficiencies across issuance and administration, reduced processing times and lower costs for market participants. The initiative positions tokenised financing as a viable complement to traditional SME funding channels, supporting innovation in European capital markets while informing future regulatory developments.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1959,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01477,EU_PSTW_202609_01477,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Ecoembes,Waste management.,"Waste management processes involve multiple actors, including public administrations, local entities, sorting plants and recycling facilities, often resulting in fragmented information flows and limited traceability. Lack of transparency can increase costs and hinder environmental performance monitoring.","CircularTrust, waste management system",Ecoembes; Non-governmental; Waste management.; Spain,"The system uses blockchain technology to immutably record transactions related to waste collection, sorting and transfer. Smart contracts automate data validation and, where applicable, financial settlements.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Waste management processes involve multiple actors, including public administrations, local entities, sorting plants and recycling facilities, often resulting in fragmented information flows and limited traceability. Lack of transparency can increase costs and hinder environmental performance monitoring. Stakeholders include recyclers, waste operators, public authorities and environmental organisations. The objective is to enhance transparency, efficiency and trust in the recycling value chain. The system uses blockchain technology to immutably record transactions related to waste collection, sorting and transfer. Smart contracts automate data validation and, where applicable, financial settlements. Real-time monitoring dashboards provide visibility across more than 90 sorting plants and over 70 recycling facilities, processing thousands of monthly transactions. Expected impacts include improved traceability, reduced administrative overhead, enhanced regulatory oversight, lower transaction costs and strengthened confidence among ecosystem participants, contributing to more sustainable waste management practices.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1960,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01478,EU_PSTW_202609_01478,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,European Commission,R&D economic affairs,"Cross-border payments within Europe remain costly and complex due to fragmented identity verification processes, compliance checks and intermediaries.",Globallogic (Nuggets) - European cross-border payments,European Commission; European Institution/Agency; R&D economic affairs; United Kingdom,"This use case, led by startup Nuggets (part of GlobalLogic), aims to simplify and secure European cross-border payments by combining decentralised identity with distributed ledger infrastructure. The solution uses Self-Sovereign Decentralised Identity (SSDID), allowing users to verify themselves once and reuse trusted credentials across payment services.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Cross-border payments within Europe remain costly and complex due to fragmented identity verification processes, compliance checks and intermediaries. This use case, led by startup Nuggets (part of GlobalLogic), aims to simplify and secure European cross-border payments by combining decentralised identity with distributed ledger infrastructure. Stakeholders include individuals, businesses, payment providers and public authorities operating across EU jurisdictions. The objective is to enable faster, more transparent and compliant remittances while reducing friction in customer onboarding and regulatory checks. The solution uses Self-Sovereign Decentralised Identity (SSDID), allowing users to verify themselves once and reuse trusted credentials across payment services. Blockchain infrastructure supports transaction recording and validation, ensuring traceability and integrity without centralised data silos. Verifiable credentials are used to demonstrate compliance attributes, such as identity or eligibility, without exposing unnecessary personal data. Selected for the European Blockchain Sandbox, the pilot enables structured dialogue with regulators on identity, payments and compliance. The initiative shows potential to reduce transaction costs, improve user experience and enhance trust in cross-border payments, while aligning with European data protection and digital identity principles.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1961,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01479,EU_PSTW_202609_01479,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""social_nonprofit""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,European Commission,Community development.,UNKNOWN,Internet Native Organizations,European Commission; European Institution/Agency; Community development.; Estonia,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Decentralised organisations face legal uncertainty in Europe, limiting their ability to operate transparently and engage with public and private institutions. The Internet Native Organization (INO) initiative, led by non-profit startup INO MTÜ in Estonia, seeks to establish a compliant governance framework for decentralised, code-driven organisations. Stakeholders include digital communities, regulators, developers and public administrations interested in new organisational models. The objective is to enable decentralised governance structures that combine legal recognition, accountability and community participation. INO provides a governance architecture where organisational rules are embedded in code and aligned with national legal systems, particularly Estonia’s advanced e-governance environment. Distributed ledger technology is used to record governance actions, membership decisions and organisational changes in a transparent and auditable manner. Smart contracts formalise decision-making processes, while compliance-focused updates ensure alignment with evolving regulation. Selected for the European Blockchain Regulatory Sandbox, INO supports regulatory experimentation around DAOs. Its impact lies in increasing trust, legal clarity and adoption of decentralised governance models, contributing to Europe’s leadership in digital organisational innovation.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1962,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01480,EU_PSTW_202609_01480,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Murcia City Council,"General economic, commercial and labour affair",UNKNOWN,"unBlock, the web3 Smartcity SuperApp","Murcia City Council; Local Government; General economic, commercial and labour affair; Spain",UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Residents and visitors typically navigate multiple disconnected channels to discover services, buy tickets, make bookings, and pay local businesses, which increases friction and limits adoption of digital public services. Murcia’s unBlock aims to provide a single mobile entry point for day-to-day city interactions—such as purchasing event tickets, reserving restaurants or hotels, and paying in participating local commerce—while supporting more seamless authentication for digital access. Stakeholders include the City Council, local businesses and cultural venues, citizens and tourists, and European partners working on digital identity interoperability, with the objective of reducing complexity and improving uptake of city services through one consistent experience. The solution is delivered as a mobile “superapp” connected to participating service providers and payment flows, designed to align with European digital identity initiatives (including wallet-based authentication) and selected for dialogue within the European Blockchain Regulatory Sandbox to explore governance, compliance, and interoperability questions. Conceptually, the platform combines secure user onboarding, a service catalogue, and transaction orchestration, with distributed-ledger components used where auditability or verifiable credentials add value (for example, ticketing integrity and traceable vouchers), while core service fulfilment remains integrated with existing provider systems. The expected benefits include faster access to city services, improved user experience through one-click bookings and ticket display, stronger trust in digital transactions, and a scalable model that can be extended to new municipal services and merchants as adoption grows.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1963,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01481,EU_PSTW_202609_01481,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,European Commission,General services.,European citizens and businesses face fragmented digital identity systems that hinder access to cross-border public and private services.,EU’s Digital Identity Wallet,European Commission; European Institution/Agency; General services.; European Union,"The EUDI Wallet allows users to store and present identity attributes and credentials securely, using distributed ledger technology to support trust and interoperability between national systems. The pilot phase involves more than 250 organisations across 25 Member States and partner countries, testing real-life use cases.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"European citizens and businesses face fragmented digital identity systems that hinder access to cross-border public and private services. The EU Digital Identity Wallet initiative addresses this by creating a unified digital identity solution recognised across Member States. Stakeholders include citizens, businesses, banks, public authorities and service providers across the EU and associated countries. The objective is to enable secure, interoperable and user-controlled digital identity for everyday transactions, from healthcare and education to finance and transport. The EUDI Wallet allows users to store and present identity attributes and credentials securely, using distributed ledger technology to support trust and interoperability between national systems. Biometric verification and cryptographic mechanisms ensure secure authentication, while selective disclosure enables privacy-preserving data sharing. The pilot phase involves more than 250 organisations across 25 Member States and partner countries, testing real-life use cases. The initiative improves user experience, reduces duplication in identity verification and lowers costs for service providers. It represents a foundational step towards a trusted European digital identity ecosystem aligned with EU regulation and values.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1964,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01482,EU_PSTW_202609_01482,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,European Commission,R&D economic affairs,"Capital market transactions remain operationally complex, with fragmented settlement processes and delayed payment cycles. Smart contracts coordinate delivery-versus-payment processes, reducing counterparty risk and settlement delays.",Tokenised securities with payments through stablecoins,European Commission; European Institution/Agency; R&D economic affairs; Netherlands,The solution tokenises securities as regulated digital financial instruments and uses stablecoins or tokenised bank deposits for payment.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Capital market transactions remain operationally complex, with fragmented settlement processes and delayed payment cycles. This pilot, involving startups 2Tokens and Assetblocks alongside ABN AMRO and Rabobank, explores tokenised securities combined with settlement through euro-denominated stablecoins. Stakeholders include banks, investors, issuers and regulators. The objective is to test how distributed ledger–based securities and programmable money can streamline issuance, trading and settlement while remaining compliant with EU financial regulation. The solution tokenises securities as regulated digital financial instruments and uses stablecoins or tokenised bank deposits for payment. Both hosted and non-hosted wallets are supported, enabling flexible access models. Smart contracts coordinate delivery-versus-payment processes, reducing counterparty risk and settlement delays. Selected for the European Blockchain Regulatory Sandbox, the pilot facilitates structured regulatory dialogue on custody, settlement finality and investor protection. The initiative demonstrates efficiency gains, faster settlement and reduced operational risk, while informing future frameworks for digital capital markets in Europe.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1965,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01483,EU_PSTW_202609_01483,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,European Commission,R&D economic affairs,Startup Twinu addresses this challenge by providing a blockchain-based solution for Digital Product Passports that supports upcoming EU regulation.,Circularity solution for Digital Product Passports,European Commission; European Institution/Agency; R&D economic affairs; Germany,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Manufacturers face increasing regulatory and market pressure to demonstrate product sustainability, traceability and circularity. Startup Twinu addresses this challenge by providing a blockchain-based solution for Digital Product Passports that supports upcoming EU regulation. Stakeholders include manufacturers, consumers, recyclers and regulators. The objective is to enable transparent lifecycle tracking of physical products while improving repairability, reuse and recycling. Twinu assigns each product a digital twin recorded on a blockchain, capturing key lifecycle events from production to end-of-life. This digital passport enables secure data sharing, customer engagement and circular business models without centralised control. The solution aligns with EU proposals for Digital Product Passports and was recognised as “Most Sustainable Solution” by Deutsche Telekom and T-Mobile. Selected for the European Blockchain Sandbox, the pilot demonstrates how distributed ledgers can operationalise circular economy principles. The impact includes improved transparency, stronger customer trust and actionable sustainability insights, supporting both regulatory compliance and long-term environmental value creation.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1966,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01484,EU_PSTW_202609_01484,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,5 Spanish public entities,General services.,"Citizens moving between jurisdictions often face administrative friction due to fragmented recognition of official attributes. By embedding citizens as active holders of credentials, the initiative strengthens interoperability, reduces administrative burden and supports seamless public service access across regions.",Public Administrations Interoperability,5 Spanish public entities; Consortium; General services.; Spain,The Public Administrations Interoperability project leverages the European Blockchain Services Infrastructure (EBSI) to enable secure cross-border exchange of verifiable attestations among regional and local governments.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Public Administrations Interoperability project leverages the European Blockchain Services Infrastructure (EBSI) to enable secure cross-border exchange of verifiable attestations among regional and local governments. Citizens moving between jurisdictions often face administrative friction due to fragmented recognition of official attributes. The project develops a presentation and verification platform based on the EU Self-Sovereign Identity trust model. Governments issue verifiable credentials anchored on EBSI, while citizens retain control of their digital attestations through digital wallets. Distributed ledger technology ensures integrity and trust in credential verification without centralised intermediaries. The system supports mutual recognition agreements among participating authorities, enhancing administrative cooperation and service continuity. By embedding citizens as active holders of credentials, the initiative strengthens interoperability, reduces administrative burden and supports seamless public service access across regions.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1967,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01485,EU_PSTW_202609_01485,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,University of Extremadura and Western University of Arad,Tertiary education.,Students studying or moving across EU countries face challenges in presenting and validating academic status.,Educational Identification in Europe,University of Extremadura and Western University of Arad; Consortium; Tertiary education.; European Union,The Educational Identification in Europe project aims to create a cross-border architecture for student identification using verifiable educational credentials.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Educational Identification in Europe project aims to create a cross-border architecture for student identification using verifiable educational credentials. Students studying or moving across EU countries face challenges in presenting and validating academic status. The initiative combines digital wallets, verifiable credentials and blockchain infrastructure within the European Blockchain Services Infrastructure to enable secure issuance, storage and presentation of student IDs. Universities issue credentials in a machine-readable format, which students control and present when required. Verification occurs through decentralised blockchain-based validation mechanisms, ensuring integrity and authenticity without exposing unnecessary personal data. The project involves universities and IT providers from Spain, Romania and Ireland. By enabling portable and secure student identification, the initiative supports seamless cross-border learning, reduces administrative overhead and strengthens trust in digital academic credentials across Europe.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1968,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01486,EU_PSTW_202609_01486,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Universities of Strasbourg, Deusto, Politehnica University of Timisoara and Lideea",Tertiary education.,"Traditional education systems often struggle to recognise short courses and modular achievements, limiting learner mobility.",Cross-border cross-university micro-credentials in Europe,"Universities of Strasbourg, Deusto, Politehnica University of Timisoara and Lideea; Consortium; Tertiary education.; European Union",The project uses eDiplomas formatted as verifiable credentials and anchored on the European Blockchain Services Infrastructure.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"A European consortium of universities, IT providers and research organisations is developing a blockchain-based platform to issue and verify micro-credentials across borders. Traditional education systems often struggle to recognise short courses and modular achievements, limiting learner mobility. The project uses eDiplomas formatted as verifiable credentials and anchored on the European Blockchain Services Infrastructure. Learners receive micro-credentials stored in digital wallets, allowing them to present standalone or bundled achievements to institutions and employers. Distributed ledger validation ensures authenticity and cross-border trust without centralised verification requests. The platform facilitates issuance, recognition and acceptance of digital certificates among participating universities in France, Spain and Romania. By enhancing portability and transparency of skills recognition, the initiative supports lifelong learning and European mobility. It strengthens the credibility of micro-credentials and promotes harmonised digital certification standards across higher education systems.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1969,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01487,EU_PSTW_202609_01487,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Greek Ministry of Education, the Greek University Network (GUNet), and the University of Lausanne",Tertiary education.,Students applying to programmes in other countries often encounter administrative delays and verification challenges.,Formal Accreditation and Recognition,"Greek Ministry of Education, the Greek University Network (GUNet), and the University of Lausanne; Consortium; Tertiary education.; European Union",The initiative uses Web3 technologies and the European Blockchain Services Infrastructure to issue and verify eDiplomas and transcripts as verifiable credentials.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Formal Accreditation and Recognition project brings together universities and public authorities from Spain, Switzerland and Greece to modernise cross-institution degree recognition. Students applying to programmes in other countries often encounter administrative delays and verification challenges. The initiative uses Web3 technologies and the European Blockchain Services Infrastructure to issue and verify eDiplomas and transcripts as verifiable credentials. Digital wallets enable students to present academic records securely, while decentralised verification ensures authenticity without repeated institutional checks. The platform integrates accreditation authorities and university networks to streamline recognition processes. By reducing data protection risks and simplifying cross-border applications, the project enhances mobility within European higher education. It promotes secure, interoperable credential exchange and supports next-generation decentralised public services in academic administration.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1970,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01488,EU_PSTW_202609_01488,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"University of Strasbourg, Chamber of Handicrafts Dresden, RISE, Fraunhofer IFAM and GovPart",R&D education.,"VET systems are governed by networks of social partners, including employers and trade unions, which often rely on fragmented administrative processes and non-standardised data models.",Vocational Education & Training,"University of Strasbourg, Chamber of Handicrafts Dresden, RISE, Fraunhofer IFAM and GovPart; Consortium; R&D education.; European Union",The Vocational Education and Training (VET) project leverages the European Blockchain Services Infrastructure (EBSI) to modernise the issuance and recognition of vocational qualifications across Europe.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Vocational Education and Training (VET) project leverages the European Blockchain Services Infrastructure (EBSI) to modernise the issuance and recognition of vocational qualifications across Europe. VET systems are governed by networks of social partners, including employers and trade unions, which often rely on fragmented administrative processes and non-standardised data models. This creates inefficiencies, prolonged verification timelines and reliance on intermediaries for credential validation. The project develops a Web3-based infrastructure enabling the issuance of vocational certificates as verifiable credentials anchored on EBSI. Learners store credentials in digital wallets and present them directly to employers or authorities, who can verify authenticity through decentralised validation mechanisms. Standardised data models enhance interoperability among chambers, councils and institutions. By reducing administrative complexity and strengthening trust in digital verification, the initiative supports cross-border labour mobility and improves transparency in skills recognition. The approach enhances privacy by allowing individuals to control credential sharing while ensuring reliable authentication of vocational qualifications.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1971,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01489,EU_PSTW_202609_01489,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Ministerie van Justitie en Veiligheid, University of Strasbourg",General services.,"Current hiring processes often rely on paper certificates, PDFs or self-declared CVs, requiring time-consuming manual verification by employers.",Employment credentials,"Ministerie van Justitie en Veiligheid, University of Strasbourg; Consortium; General services.; European Union",The initiative establishes a decentralised system for issuing and validating employment credentials using the European Blockchain Services Infrastructure.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Employment Credentials project aims to simplify verification of professional skills and work experience across European labour markets. Current hiring processes often rely on paper certificates, PDFs or self-declared CVs, requiring time-consuming manual verification by employers. The initiative establishes a decentralised system for issuing and validating employment credentials using the European Blockchain Services Infrastructure. Employers or authorised bodies issue verifiable credentials representing professional experience, which individuals store in secure digital wallets. Verification occurs through cryptographic validation on the blockchain, confirming authenticity without contacting previous employers. The system reduces fraud risks, accelerates recruitment processes and strengthens cross-border mobility by providing a trusted framework for recognising qualifications. By enabling direct, tamper-evident authentication of employment history, the project enhances efficiency in human resources management and supports a more transparent European labour market ecosystem.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1972,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01490,EU_PSTW_202609_01490,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,European Food Safety Authority (EFSA),Public order and safety n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,Development of in Silico Methodologies to Predict the Toxicity of Proteins in Food and Feed Risk Assessment,European Food Safety Authority (EFSA); European Institution/Agency; Public order and safety n.e.c.; Italy,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Assessing the safety of novel proteins in food and feed is complex, time-consuming and often reliant on experimental testing. Regulatory authorities seek faster, reliable methods to support risk assessment. The objective of this initiative is to identify computational methodologies capable of predicting protein toxicity and associated mechanisms. The implementation explores sequence-based, structural and machine-learning approaches to identify homology with known toxic proteins and detect molecular signatures linked to toxicity. The work focuses on methodology identification and validation criteria rather than operational deployment. Expected impacts include improved efficiency of risk assessment, reduced reliance on animal testing and stronger scientific consistency. Qualitative benefits include enhanced regulatory decision support and scalable foundations for future safety assessment tools.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1973,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01491,EU_PSTW_202609_01491,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Fraunhofer Portugal’s,General public services n.e.c.,"Fragmented documentation and limited transparency can undermine trust, particularly in collaborative development contexts involving multiple stakeholders.",AICEBlock,Fraunhofer Portugal’s; Academic-Research; General public services n.e.c.; Portugal,"The solution leverages blockchain technology to register model metadata, training records, versioning information and decision logs in an immutable ledger.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Organisations deploying algorithmic systems in sensitive domains such as healthcare and public administration face stringent requirements for auditability, traceability and regulatory compliance. Fragmented documentation and limited transparency can undermine trust, particularly in collaborative development contexts involving multiple stakeholders. The objective of AICEBlock is to create a certification platform that enhances trust in algorithmic applications by ensuring verifiability of development and operational processes. The solution leverages blockchain technology to register model metadata, training records, versioning information and decision logs in an immutable ledger. Smart contracts manage certification workflows, while cryptographic mechanisms ensure integrity and traceability of updates across training and production environments. The platform supports compliance with regulatory standards, including data protection requirements, and facilitates the integration of interpretability layers. Expected impacts include strengthened user trust, improved audit readiness, reduced compliance risks and enhanced transparency in high-stakes deployments.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1974,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01492,EU_PSTW_202609_01492,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Contracting, Projects and Works Service Dept of Regional Government",Unemployment.,UNKNOWN,AI and ML for the Galician Public Employment Service,"Contracting, Projects and Works Service Dept of Regional Government; Regional Government; Unemployment.; Spain","The Galician Public Employment Service launched a project, funded under REACT-EU within the ESF Operational Programme Galicia 2014–2020, to develop a decision-support system using artificial intelligence and machine learning.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Galician Public Employment Service launched a project, funded under REACT-EU within the ESF Operational Programme Galicia 2014–2020, to develop a decision-support system using artificial intelligence and machine learning. The objective is to enhance labour market policy design and operational decision-making through advanced data analytics. The system integrates employment records, service usage data and socioeconomic indicators into a central analytical environment. Machine-learning models generate predictive insights and classification outputs to support case management, programme evaluation and resource allocation. Visualisation tools are embedded within existing service applications to present actionable indicators to staff. The cloud-based infrastructure supports scalable data processing and secure integration with institutional systems. By automating analysis and providing evidence-based recommendations, the solution improves efficiency, strengthens policy responsiveness and enhances service quality for job seekers. The initiative contributes to digital transformation of employment services in Galicia.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1975,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01493,EU_PSTW_202609_01493,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Interuniversity Earth System Institute - Andalusia,Protection of biodiversity and landscape.,UNKNOWN,SMARTECOMOUNTAINS Project in Andalusia,Interuniversity Earth System Institute - Andalusia; Regional Government; Protection of biodiversity and landscape.; Spain,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The SMARTECOMOUNTAINS project, led by the University of Granada within the LifeWatch ERIC research infrastructure, develops advanced digital tools to study biodiversity and global change in mountain ecosystems. Focusing on the Sierra Nevada as a case study, the project addresses the need for reliable, interoperable and reusable environmental data to support research and policy decisions. The initiative integrates deep learning and data analytics methods to process large-scale ecological datasets, including biodiversity records and ecosystem indicators. A structured Data Management Plan ensures traceability, accessibility and interoperability of collected information. The project also develops technological services facilitating data exchange and analysis among scientists, alongside educational and decision-support tools for policymakers and the public. By combining artificial intelligence with environmental monitoring, SMARTECOMOUNTAINS enhances understanding of climate impacts and ecosystem services in mountainous regions while promoting science-based governance and sustainable development strategies.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1976,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01494,EU_PSTW_202609_01494,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Flanders Research Institute for Agriculture, Fisheries and Food (ILVO)","Agriculture, forestry, fishing and hunting",UNKNOWN,AI4AGRIFOOD platform in Flanders - Precision agriculture and data technology,"Flanders Research Institute for Agriculture, Fisheries and Food (ILVO); Regional Government; Agriculture, forestry, fishing and hunting; Belgium","Within Flanders, the challenge is to democratise the use of advanced analytics across agrifood research while fostering collaboration and knowledge sharing.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Agricultural research institutions increasingly rely on data science and advanced analytics, yet researchers often face barriers in accessing user-friendly tools that accommodate varying levels of technical expertise. Within Flanders, the challenge is to democratise the use of advanced analytics across agrifood research while fostering collaboration and knowledge sharing. Stakeholders include ILVO researchers, innovation managers and technology providers. The objective is to establish a platform that enables both experienced and less technically specialised users to apply machine learning methods in agricultural research contexts. The envisaged solution includes a modular platform with graphical user interfaces, low-code functionalities and integrated machine learning libraries. It supports experiment management, model training and deployment within secure research environments, enabling collaborative workflows. Anticipated impacts include accelerated experimentation, improved research productivity, broader uptake of advanced analytics and strengthened innovation capacity in precision agriculture.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1977,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01495,EU_PSTW_202609_01495,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,CollectiveCrunch Oy,"Agriculture, forestry, fishing and hunting",UNKNOWN,Artificial intelligence in operational forest management planning,"CollectiveCrunch Oy; Private sector; Agriculture, forestry, fishing and hunting; Finland","Managing state-owned forests requires balancing economic use, environmental protection, and social value, while operating across large and diverse territories. This use case involves the Finnish state forestry organisation, planners, and technology providers, with the objective of improving decision-making in forest management.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Managing state-owned forests requires balancing economic use, environmental protection, and social value, while operating across large and diverse territories. Metsähallitus needed more precise data to support operational planning and sustainable timber harvesting. This use case involves the Finnish state forestry organisation, planners, and technology providers, with the objective of improving decision-making in forest management. The solution is implemented through advanced data-driven models that generate assortment-level timber yield predictions, which are integrated into operational planning systems. These predictions complement existing forestry data and enable more accurate planning of harvesting, logistics, and conservation activities. The solution improves planning accuracy, supports sustainability goals, and reduces uncertainty in operational decisions. It enhances efficiency, optimises resource use, and delivers long-term value by supporting responsible management of public land and natural resources.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1978,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01496,EU_PSTW_202609_01496,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Lille European Metropole,General public services n.e.c.,Urban congestion and inefficient parking allocation create traffic delays and environmental impacts.,Lille Metropole Smart Sensors,Lille European Metropole; Regional Government; General public services n.e.c.; France,The project implemented AI-enabled sensors capable of detecting vehicle presence and transmitting real-time occupancy data to central management systems.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Lille Métropole partnered with VizioSense to research, prototype and deploy intelligent parking sensors aimed at optimising management of public parking spaces. Urban congestion and inefficient parking allocation create traffic delays and environmental impacts. The project implemented AI-enabled sensors capable of detecting vehicle presence and transmitting real-time occupancy data to central management systems. Machine-learning algorithms enhance detection accuracy and support predictive analytics for space availability. The initiative followed a phased approach combining research and development with prototype acquisition through a framework agreement. Real-time data feeds inform navigation tools, guiding drivers to available spaces and reducing circulation time. By integrating sensor networks with intelligent analytics, the city improves traffic flow, reduces emissions and enhances user experience. The project demonstrates how smart infrastructure and data-driven parking management can support sustainable urban mobility strategies.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1979,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01497,EU_PSTW_202609_01497,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Imatra Municipality,General public services n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,Imatra Street AI project,Imatra Municipality; Local Government; General public services n.e.c.; Finland,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Street AI Innovation Partnership in Imatra developed a cloud-based platform to enhance street maintenance through real-time data integration and analytics. Municipal authorities require accurate information on traffic volumes, snow cover and road conditions to optimise maintenance activities. The project created a digital environment for data storage, analysis and visualisation, integrating sensor inputs and artificial intelligence models. Real-time dashboards present current street conditions, supporting decision-making and resource allocation. The initiative also tests augmented reality applications and advanced sensor technologies to improve situational awareness. By targeting maintenance activities more precisely, the system reduces operational costs, improves service levels and minimises environmental impact. The platform enhances transparency and efficiency in urban infrastructure management while supporting long-term digital transformation in municipal services.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1980,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01498,EU_PSTW_202609_01498,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Helsinki City Central Library,Cultural services.,"By automating repetitive handling tasks, the solution reduces manual workload, shortens processing times and enhances service quality for visitors.",Robotics and artificial intelligence solutions for Helsinki City Central Library,Helsinki City Central Library; Local Government; Cultural services.; Finland,Helsinki City Central Library (Oodi) implemented robotics and artificial intelligence solutions to improve operational efficiency and user services in a high-traffic public library environment.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Helsinki City Central Library (Oodi) implemented robotics and artificial intelligence solutions to improve operational efficiency and user services in a high-traffic public library environment. Managing large volumes of returned materials and maintaining organised collections requires significant staff resources. The project introduced automated robotic systems for sorting and handling books, integrated with intelligent software capable of recognising and categorising items. AI-supported logistics optimise internal workflows and support accurate shelving and inventory management. The system interfaces with the library’s catalogue and circulation databases to ensure real-time updates. By automating repetitive handling tasks, the solution reduces manual workload, shortens processing times and enhances service quality for visitors. The initiative supports innovative public service delivery and demonstrates how robotics can improve efficiency in cultural and educational institutions while preserving human interaction for user-facing services.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1981,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01499,EU_PSTW_202609_01499,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,The Capital City of Prague,Transport,"The region faces challenges in balancing cost efficiency and accessibility, particularly in less densely populated areas where fixed routes may be underused.",Central Bohemian Region to test on-demand public transport,The Capital City of Prague; Regional Government; Transport; Czechia,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Central Bohemian Region launched an innovation partnership to design and implement a system optimising public transport service settings, including testing on-demand transport solutions. The region faces challenges in balancing cost efficiency and accessibility, particularly in less densely populated areas where fixed routes may be underused. The objective is to develop a data-driven platform capable of aggregating multi-source datasets, including passenger flows, ticketing data and broader mobility indicators. The selected partner is responsible for building the system in line with minimum technical requirements defined by the contracting authority. The solution will process large-scale datasets to model demand patterns, optimise route planning and support adaptive scheduling. By enabling dynamic configuration of services, the platform aims to improve service coverage, reduce operational inefficiencies and enhance responsiveness to passenger needs. The initiative supports evidence-based mobility planning and contributes to more flexible and sustainable regional transport management.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1982,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01500,EU_PSTW_202609_01500,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Herne,Transport,"In Herne, managing a 380 km road network posed challenges in data integration, prioritisation and long-term planning.",City of Herne road maintance with AI,City of Herne; Local Government; Transport; Germany,"The solution applies computer vision and predictive analytics to assess road conditions, integrating collected data into a Building Information Modelling (BIM) environment.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Urban road networks require continuous monitoring and timely maintenance to ensure safety and cost efficiency. In Herne, managing a 380 km road network posed challenges in data integration, prioritisation and long-term planning. Stakeholders include municipal authorities, infrastructure managers and contractors. The objective is to enable proactive maintenance through data-driven analysis and integrated infrastructure modelling. The solution applies computer vision and predictive analytics to assess road conditions, integrating collected data into a Building Information Modelling (BIM) environment. Over time, enriched BIM models contribute to a digital twin of the road infrastructure, enabling scenario simulations and lifecycle planning. By analysing deterioration patterns and maintenance histories, the system supports optimised intervention scheduling. Expected impacts include reduced data acquisition costs, improved decision-making accuracy, enhanced road safety and more efficient allocation of maintenance budgets.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1983,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01501,EU_PSTW_202609_01501,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,The Capital City of Prague,Transport,"Urban congestion at major transport hubs leads to delays, increased emissions and reduced public transport reliability.",City of Prague AI Traffic Management System,The Capital City of Prague; Local Government; Transport; Czechia,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Urban congestion at major transport hubs leads to delays, increased emissions and reduced public transport reliability. Prague seeks to improve traffic flow while maintaining priority for tram services. Stakeholders include municipal transport authorities, road users and public transport operators. The objective is to implement an adaptive traffic management system capable of responding dynamically to real-time conditions. The system employs multi-agent and adaptive control algorithms that process live video and sensor data to assess vehicle counts, flow intensity and directional patterns. Optimisation models generate real-time signalling plans to coordinate intersections and prioritise public transport. Integration with existing traffic infrastructure ensures operational continuity. Anticipated impacts include smoother traffic flow, reduced congestion-related emissions, enhanced tram punctuality and improved overall transport efficiency.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1984,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01502,EU_PSTW_202609_01502,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,RTE - France,Other industries,UNKNOWN,Artificial Intelligence-Enhanced Electricity Transmission Network Planning,RTE - France; Central-Government; Other industries; France,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"France’s electricity transmission operator RTE initiated an innovation partnership to develop advanced analytical tools supporting long-term grid adaptation strategies. The energy transition introduces complexity in forecasting demand, integrating renewables and maintaining grid stability. The project aims to equip research managers with decision-support tools to formulate hypotheses, interpret modelling results and optimise network planning scenarios. The partnership is structured in three phases: prototype design, operational development and testing, followed by acquisition for broader deployment. The system integrates large-scale network data, simulation outputs and predictive modelling techniques to evaluate infrastructure adaptation strategies. By enhancing analytical capabilities, the solution improves scenario assessment, accelerates research workflows and strengthens evidence-based investment planning. The initiative contributes to preparing the transmission network for decarbonisation and increased electrification while ensuring reliability and cost efficiency in grid development.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1985,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01503,EU_PSTW_202609_01503,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,AGENAS - Regione Puglia,Public health services.,"Fragmented information systems can limit efficiency and consistency. Expected impacts include improved care continuity, earlier identification of high-risk patients, reduced administrative burden and enhanced quality of primary healthcare delivery.",AI Platform for to support primary healthcare in Regione Puglia,AGENAS - Regione Puglia; Regional Government; Public health services.; Italy,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Primary healthcare providers manage diverse patient needs, often requiring timely diagnostic support and coordinated care pathways. Fragmented information systems can limit efficiency and consistency. Stakeholders include regional health authorities, general practitioners and patients. The objective is to design, implement and manage a comprehensive platform that enhances decision support and service coordination in primary care. The solution integrates clinical data repositories, predictive analytics models and decision-support algorithms within a secure digital infrastructure. Functionalities may include risk stratification, early detection of chronic conditions and workflow optimisation tools embedded in clinical systems. Interoperability standards ensure integration with existing regional health information systems. Expected impacts include improved care continuity, earlier identification of high-risk patients, reduced administrative burden and enhanced quality of primary healthcare delivery.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1986,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01504,EU_PSTW_202609_01504,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,AMA (Agência para a Modernização Administrativa),General services.,UNKNOWN,Virtual assistant on the eportugal portal for information about the Digital Mobile Key (CMD),AMA (Agência para a Modernização Administrativa); Central-Government; General services.; Portugal,The Portuguese government introduced a virtual assistant on the ePortugal portal to support citizens using the Digital Mobile Key (Chave Móvel Digital).,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Portuguese government introduced a virtual assistant on the ePortugal portal to support citizens using the Digital Mobile Key (Chave Móvel Digital). Users frequently require guidance on activation, unlocking, updating personal data and applying digital signatures. The assistant provides conversational support through voice and text interfaces integrated into the national public services portal. Natural language processing interprets user queries and retrieves structured information from official guidance databases. The system offers step-by-step instructions and directs users to relevant digital procedures, while complex identity or security issues are handled through established support channels. By automating responses to common enquiries, the assistant enhances accessibility, reduces service centre workload and promotes adoption of secure digital identity tools. The initiative strengthens digital public service delivery and improves user experience in accessing e-government services.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1987,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01505,EU_PSTW_202609_01505,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,NAV,Social protection n.e.c.,"NAV processes a very large number of benefit applications each year, and manual handling of all cases can lead to delays and inconsistent outcomes.",Automated evaluations of applications at NAV,NAV; Central-Government; Social protection n.e.c.; Norway,"This use case addresses the need to improve efficiency while ensuring decisions comply with legislation. The solution is implemented through automated decision systems that check applications against legal criteria for benefits such as child benefit, unemployment benefit, pensions, and parental benefits.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"NAV processes a very large number of benefit applications each year, and manual handling of all cases can lead to delays and inconsistent outcomes. This use case addresses the need to improve efficiency while ensuring decisions comply with legislation. It involves NAV, applicants, and public authorities, with the objective of delivering faster and more consistent benefit decisions. The solution is implemented through automated decision systems that check applications against legal criteria for benefits such as child benefit, unemployment benefit, pensions, and parental benefits. The system verifies eligibility, determines benefit levels and duration, and generates formal decisions without human intervention for eligible cases. Integrated with NAV’s case management systems, it ensures transparency and traceability. The solution significantly reduces processing times, frees staff to focus on complex cases, and improves service quality for citizens. It delivers scalable efficiency gains, legal consistency, and sustainable public administration value.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1988,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01506,EU_PSTW_202609_01506,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,UK government’s National Highways,Police Services,UNKNOWN,New AI-Powered Cameras Spot Distracted Drivers in the UK,UK government’s National Highways; Central-Government; Police Services; United Kingdom,"To strengthen enforcement, new roadside camera systems have been deployed to detect motorists using handheld mobile phones or failing to wear seatbelts. High-resolution cameras capture images of passing vehicles, which are processed using computer vision algorithms trained to identify prohibited behaviours.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Road safety authorities in the United Kingdom face persistent risks from distracted driving and seatbelt non-compliance, contributing to preventable accidents and injuries. To strengthen enforcement, new roadside camera systems have been deployed to detect motorists using handheld mobile phones or failing to wear seatbelts. High-resolution cameras capture images of passing vehicles, which are processed using computer vision algorithms trained to identify prohibited behaviours. Suspected violations are reviewed by police officers before penalties are issued, ensuring human oversight in enforcement decisions. The system integrates with existing traffic monitoring infrastructure and supports evidence-based processing of offences. Drivers face fines of up to £500 for seatbelt violations and up to £1,000 with six penalty points for mobile phone use while driving. The technology enhances detection accuracy and enforcement efficiency, acting as a deterrent and contributing to improved compliance, reduced accident risk and safer road environments.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1989,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01507,EU_PSTW_202609_01507,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Charnwood Borough Council,Police Services,UNKNOWN,AI camera system catches litter louts in Loughborough,Charnwood Borough Council; Local Government; Police Services; United Kingdom,"In Loughborough, an intelligent camera system was deployed on Warwick Way to deter and detect individuals discarding waste from vehicles.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Littering in public spaces imposes environmental, financial and social costs on local authorities and communities. In Loughborough, an intelligent camera system was deployed on Warwick Way to deter and detect individuals discarding waste from vehicles. The system operates alongside automatic number plate recognition infrastructure, capturing footage of passing traffic and applying computer vision techniques to identify littering incidents. Detected cases are verified and linked to vehicle registration data for enforcement action. During the trial period, 28 fines of £150 were issued, and a 37 % reduction in litter was recorded in the monitored area. The initiative demonstrates how targeted technological interventions can modify behaviour and improve environmental outcomes. By combining automated detection with established enforcement mechanisms, the project enhances operational efficiency and supports cleaner urban spaces, while providing measurable evidence of impact to inform future deployment decisions.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1990,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01508,EU_PSTW_202609_01508,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,National Health System,R&D health.,"Early detection of breast cancer significantly improves treatment outcomes, yet subtle abnormalities in mammograms can be difficult to identify consistently.",NHS AI test spots tiny cancers missed by doctors,National Health System; Central-Government; R&D health.; United Kingdom,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Early detection of breast cancer significantly improves treatment outcomes, yet subtle abnormalities in mammograms can be difficult to identify consistently. The NHS piloted a diagnostic support tool alongside clinicians to assess its effectiveness in analysing breast screening images. The system processed mammograms from over 10,000 women, the majority of whom were cancer-free, and was evaluated in parallel with standard clinical assessment. Advanced image analysis models examined patterns and anomalies within scans to flag potential cases requiring further review. The tool successfully identified all women diagnosed with cancer and additionally detected 11 cases that had not been initially recognised by human readers. Results indicate potential to enhance sensitivity in screening programmes while supporting clinician decision-making. The technology may reduce missed diagnoses, improve early intervention rates and increase overall efficiency of breast cancer screening services through augmented image analysis.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1991,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01509,EU_PSTW_202609_01509,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Interior Ministry,Police Services,UNKNOWN,French police test AI-powered security cameras ahead of Olympics,Interior Ministry; Central-Government; Police Services; France,"The system was tested at a large concert, analysing live video feeds using pattern recognition algorithms trained to detect eight categories of incidents, including crowd surges, abandoned objects, fires and the presence of weapons.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Large-scale public events such as the Olympic Games require robust security arrangements capable of detecting risks in real time while managing dense crowds. France introduced legislation permitting a temporary trial of intelligent video surveillance to identify predefined abnormal events during major gatherings. The system was tested at a large concert, analysing live video feeds using pattern recognition algorithms trained to detect eight categories of incidents, including crowd surges, abandoned objects, fires and the presence of weapons. Alerts are transmitted to security personnel for assessment and response, maintaining human oversight in operational decisions. An evaluation committee monitors the deployment to safeguard civil liberties during the trial period. The initiative aims to enhance situational awareness, accelerate response times and improve crowd safety management during high-profile events. By integrating automated event detection with existing security frameworks, the system supports proactive risk mitigation while operating within a regulated governance structure.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1992,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01510,EU_PSTW_202609_01510,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Dublin City Council,Cultural services.,UNKNOWN,Dublin City Council and OpenAI Announce Partnership to Show the Potential of AI to Support Europe’s Tourism Industry,Dublin City Council; Local Government; Cultural services.; Ireland,"The solution leverages a large language model to interpret user preferences and generate contextualised recommendations, drawing on curated cultural, historical and event data.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Tourism authorities aim to enhance visitor experiences by providing personalised, engaging recommendations that go beyond static guides. Dublin sought to demonstrate how advanced language technologies could support tailored travel planning. Stakeholders include the city council, tourism agencies, technology partners and visitors. The objective is to develop a proof of concept itinerary planner capable of generating bespoke daily travel plans. The solution leverages a large language model to interpret user preferences and generate contextualised recommendations, drawing on curated cultural, historical and event data. Prompt engineering and content filtering mechanisms ensure relevance and safety, while integration with tourism information databases enriches outputs. Expected impacts include more personalised visitor experiences, increased engagement with local attractions and demonstration of innovative digital tourism services.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1993,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01511,EU_PSTW_202609_01511,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""social_nonprofit""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,NHS,R&D health.,UNKNOWN,OrQA - Organ Quality Assessment - the technology scans images of organs to look at suitability for kidney and liver transplant,NHS; Central-Government; R&D health.; United Kingdom,"The objective is to improve transplant decision-making, make better use of donated organs, and increase the number of patients who can benefit from successful transplants. The implementation uses an image-analysis approach that scans pictures of organs and applies algorithmic assessment techniques to identify features associated with transplant suitability.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Organ transplantation depends on rapid and accurate assessment of donor organs, yet decisions about suitability are complex and time-sensitive, and clinicians must often judge quality under conditions of uncertainty. OrQA was developed to support this process by helping specialists assess whether organs are suitable for kidney and liver transplantation through faster and more consistent image-based evaluation. The objective is to improve transplant decision-making, make better use of donated organs, and increase the number of patients who can benefit from successful transplants. The implementation uses an image-analysis approach that scans pictures of organs and applies algorithmic assessment techniques to identify features associated with transplant suitability. Developed by the University of Bradford and Newcastle upon Tyne Hospitals NHS Foundation Trust, the tool is designed to assist clinicians rather than replace human judgement. At a conceptual level, the system uses large image sets to build an assessment model that can support transplant teams with an additional evidence layer during organ evaluation. Reported expectations for the technology suggest that it could help increase the number of kidney and liver transplants carried out in the UK each year. The impact includes the potential for quicker and more reliable assessment, better use of scarce donor organs, and improved outcomes for patients awaiting transplant, alongside operational gains in a highly specialised clinical pathway.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1995,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01512,EU_PSTW_202609_01512,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,UK Government Digital Service,General public services n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,ChatGPT-powered GOV.UK chatbot on trial,UK Government Digital Service; Central-Government; General public services n.e.c.; United Kingdom,"The prototype uses large language model technology to retrieve and summarise information from existing GOV.UK content, generating responses grounded in official sources.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Citizens accessing GOV.UK often need to navigate large volumes of content to find relevant information about public services, benefits and regulations. To improve accessibility and user experience, the Government Digital Service conducted public trials of a conversational chatbot designed to provide direct, human-like responses to user queries. The objective was to assess feasibility, usefulness and risks before any wider deployment. The prototype uses large language model technology to retrieve and summarise information from existing GOV.UK content, generating responses grounded in official sources. The system was tested with members of the public, and feedback mechanisms were embedded to evaluate answer quality, clarity and reliability. Survey results indicated that around 65 % of participants were satisfied overall and 70 % found the responses useful, while issues of accuracy and consistency were identified. The trial adopted a cautious, iterative approach with human oversight and evaluation. Findings support further experimentation while highlighting the need for robust safeguards, quality assurance and governance to ensure trustworthy digital public services.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1996,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01513,EU_PSTW_202609_01513,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Barcelona City Council,Community development.,UNKNOWN,Barcelona tests with a digital twin if it is a 15-minute city,Barcelona City Council; Local Government; Community development.; Spain,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Urban policymakers seek to ensure that residents can access essential services within a 15-minute walk or cycle, yet measuring such accessibility across complex city environments is challenging. Barcelona tested a digital twin platform to assess whether it meets the criteria of the 15-minute city model. The web-based tool integrates geospatial data on public facilities, transport networks and population distribution into a dynamic urban simulation environment. Accessibility algorithms calculate travel times to schools, healthcare, green spaces and other amenities under different mobility scenarios. The platform enables planners to visualise service gaps and simulate the impact of infrastructure changes in a three-dimensional environment. By combining spatial analytics and interactive modelling, the system supports evidence-based urban planning decisions. The initiative enhances transparency in policy evaluation, enables targeted interventions to reduce inequality in service provision and strengthens long-term strategies for sustainable, liveable neighbourhoods.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-1998,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01514,EU_PSTW_202609_01514,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Helsinki,Community development.,Engaging citizens and local entrepreneurs in urban planning processes can be resource-intensive and limited by traditional consultation formats.,"UrbanistAI, a generative AI platform for participatory planning and co-design: Helsinki use case",City of Helsinki; Local Government; Community development.; Finland,"The City of Helsinki piloted a participatory approach using a generative design platform to broaden stakeholder involvement in shaping neighbourhood development. During moderated workshops, participants explored alternative visions for specific urban areas and expressed preferences regarding functions and land use.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Engaging citizens and local entrepreneurs in urban planning processes can be resource-intensive and limited by traditional consultation formats. The City of Helsinki piloted a participatory approach using a generative design platform to broaden stakeholder involvement in shaping neighbourhood development. During moderated workshops, participants explored alternative visions for specific urban areas and expressed preferences regarding functions and land use. The platform uses generative image models to produce multiple visual scenarios based on user inputs, enabling rapid visualisation of design concepts. City officials and consultants facilitated discussions, ensuring that outputs aligned with planning constraints and policy objectives. The interactive format encouraged dialogue and collective refinement of ideas. The initiative demonstrates how digital co-design tools can enhance inclusivity, stimulate creative thinking and provide structured feedback for planners. By accelerating scenario generation and visual communication, the platform supports more transparent and responsive urban development processes.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2001,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01515,EU_PSTW_202609_01515,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Bologna,Transport,"The City of Bologna is addressing complex urban challenges related to sustainable mobility, energy efficiency, and climate change mitigation, which require coordinated planning across multiple policy domains.",City of Bologna digital twin,City of Bologna; Local Government; Transport; Italy,"The solution is implemented through a digital replica of the city that integrates data from sensors, administrative systems, and qualitative social data.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The City of Bologna is addressing complex urban challenges related to sustainable mobility, energy efficiency, and climate change mitigation, which require coordinated planning across multiple policy domains. City planners, utilities, transport authorities, and citizens all need reliable evidence to support long-term infrastructure investments and behavioural change. The objective of the digital twin initiative is to provide an integrated decision-support environment that allows the city to analyse current conditions and simulate future scenarios before implementing policies or investments. The solution is implemented through a digital replica of the city that integrates data from sensors, administrative systems, and qualitative social data. In mobility, the model combines traffic counts, environmental sensors, and citizen feedback to assess the impact of cycling lanes and tram infrastructure. In the energy domain, it simulates building energy performance to evaluate renovation strategies, incentives, and urban plans aimed at achieving net-zero targets. The digital twin enables scenario testing and cross-sector analysis within a shared platform. As a result, Bologna can design more coherent policies, reduce planning risks, and prioritise investments with higher sustainability impact. The initiative improves transparency, supports data-driven governance, and strengthens the city’s capacity to plan resilient and climate-neutral urban development.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2004,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01516,EU_PSTW_202609_01516,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Amsterdam Institute for Advanced Metropolitan Solutions (AMS),Transport,UNKNOWN,Roboat - an autonomous fleet for Amsterdam,Amsterdam Institute for Advanced Metropolitan Solutions (AMS); Academic-Research; Transport; Netherlands,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Amsterdam’s canal network presents opportunities for innovative transport and urban services, yet managing congestion, waste collection and logistics in dense waterways is complex. The Roboat project, a collaboration between MIT and the Amsterdam Institute for Advanced Metropolitan Solutions, aims to develop a fleet of autonomous boats capable of performing multiple urban functions. The vessels are equipped with sensors, localisation systems and autonomous navigation software to operate safely within busy canal environments. Modular designs allow reconfiguration for tasks such as passenger transport, goods delivery or waste collection. Fleet coordination algorithms optimise routing and docking operations, while data exchange systems integrate with city infrastructure. Prototypes are tested under real conditions to validate safety and operational efficiency. The initiative explores scalable water-based mobility solutions that reduce road congestion and emissions, contributing to more flexible and sustainable urban transport systems.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2005,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01517,EU_PSTW_202609_01517,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Pilsen,Transport,UNKNOWN,Digital twin of the City of Pilsen,City of Pilsen; Local Government; Transport; Czechia,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The City of Pilsen is advancing its Smart City strategy across mobility, environment, security and public participation, requiring integrated tools to support data-driven decision-making. To enhance understanding of urban dynamics, the city is developing a digital twin that interrelates transport patterns and noise pollution within a three-dimensional environment. The platform aggregates traffic data, environmental measurements and spatial information into a unified simulation model. Analytical modules assess correlations between mobility flows and acoustic impact, enabling scenario testing for infrastructure changes or traffic management interventions. Visualisation tools allow planners to explore outcomes in a realistic 3D context, improving stakeholder communication. By linking environmental and transport indicators, the digital twin supports more holistic urban policy evaluation. Expected benefits include improved mobility planning, reduced noise exposure and enhanced quality of life, while providing a scalable framework for integrating additional smart city datasets over time.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2006,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01518,EU_PSTW_202609_01518,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Albanian government,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.","The objective is to reduce the manual burden associated with legal translation and alignment, shorten paperwork timelines, and support faster progress towards EU accession.",Albania to speed up EU accession using ChatGPT,"Albanian government; Central-Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Albania","In Albania, the government presented this use case as a way to accelerate that work by using ChatGPT to translate EU legal measures and assist with incorporating them into Albanian legal structures. The implementation uses a large language model to process legal text, support translation into Albanian, and assist with the comparison and integration of EU provisions into the domestic legislative framework.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Aligning national legislation with the European Union acquis requires the translation, interpretation, and integration of very large volumes of legal and regulatory material, making accession an administratively demanding and time-intensive process. In Albania, the government presented this use case as a way to accelerate that work by using ChatGPT to translate EU legal measures and assist with incorporating them into Albanian legal structures. The objective is to reduce the manual burden associated with legal translation and alignment, shorten paperwork timelines, and support faster progress towards EU accession. The implementation uses a large language model to process legal text, support translation into Albanian, and assist with the comparison and integration of EU provisions into the domestic legislative framework. At a conceptual level, the tool functions as a language and legal-text processing assistant for officials dealing with approximation of legislation, rather than as an autonomous decision-maker. The expected impact includes faster handling of large legal-text volumes, reduced administrative effort, and improved capacity to manage a complex accession workload. It also illustrates how generative language technology can be used in public administration to support large-scale legal harmonisation and strategic policy objectives.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PLANNED,계획,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK|F10_CREATIVE_PRODUCTION_CAPACITY_BOTTLENECK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2020,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01519,EU_PSTW_202609_01519,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,University of Galway,R&D health.,Individuals who have experienced childhood trauma often face persistent cognitive and social difficulties that affect mental health and daily functioning. The SOCIAbLE project aims to develop and evaluate a novel psychosocial intervention designed to address these challenges.,SOCIAbLE - Social Cognitive Interventions following Adverse early Life Experiences,University of Galway; Consortium; R&D health.; Ireland,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Individuals who have experienced childhood trauma often face persistent cognitive and social difficulties that affect mental health and daily functioning. The SOCIAbLE project aims to develop and evaluate a novel psychosocial intervention designed to address these challenges. Researchers are creating a virtual reality-based therapeutic programme that targets social cognition, emotional processing and interpersonal skills. The intervention immerses participants in controlled virtual environments where they can practise social interactions and coping strategies under guided supervision. Data collected during sessions inform adaptive adjustments to therapeutic scenarios, supporting personalised progression. Feasibility studies assess usability, acceptability and preliminary clinical benefits to inform future large-scale trials. By combining immersive technology with evidence-based psychological approaches, the project seeks to enhance resilience and social functioning in individuals affected by early adverse experiences, contributing to improved long-term mental health outcomes.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2022,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01520,EU_PSTW_202609_01520,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Consiglio Nazionale delle Ricerche,"R&D recreation, culture and religion.",UNKNOWN,PERCEIVE - Perceptive Enhanced Realities of Colored collEctions through AI and Virtual Experiences,"Consiglio Nazionale delle Ricerche; Consortium; R&D recreation, culture and religion.; Italy","This use case addresses these challenges by enabling accurate study, reconstruction, and exhibition of coloured cultural heritage, involving researchers, museums, educators, and creative industries. The PERCEIVE project is implemented through advanced image analysis, digital reconstruction techniques, and immersive visualisation technologies that analyse traces of pigment and material data to recreate original colours.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Cultural heritage institutions face growing interest in digitally preserving and presenting ancient artefacts while addressing gaps in historical understanding, particularly around the original appearance of classical sculptures. Many Greek and Roman statues were originally painted, but their polychromy has been lost over time, limiting public understanding and scholarly interpretation. This use case addresses these challenges by enabling accurate study, reconstruction, and exhibition of coloured cultural heritage, involving researchers, museums, educators, and creative industries. The PERCEIVE project is implemented through advanced image analysis, digital reconstruction techniques, and immersive visualisation technologies that analyse traces of pigment and material data to recreate original colours. These reconstructions are rendered through extended reality and hybrid exhibition formats, supported by a service-based digital architecture and dedicated design toolkits for education and creative reuse. The project improves the accuracy and accessibility of cultural heritage interpretation, enriches visitor experience through immersive storytelling, and supports new educational and creative applications. It delivers lasting value by combining scientific research with engaging digital experiences, strengthening preservation, knowledge transfer, and public appreciation of Europe’s cultural heritage.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2023,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01521,EU_PSTW_202609_01521,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Università degli Studi di Milano,Hospital services.,"The TIMELAPSE project aims to prototype and validate a virtual reality application designed to alter subjective time perception, making treatment sessions feel shorter and less burdensome.",TIMELAPSE - A VR application for speeding up time passage during chemotherapy,Università degli Studi di Milano; Consortium; Hospital services.; Italy,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Chemotherapy treatments often involve long, stressful sessions that can negatively affect patients’ psychological well-being. The TIMELAPSE project aims to prototype and validate a virtual reality application designed to alter subjective time perception, making treatment sessions feel shorter and less burdensome. Developed by a multidisciplinary consortium including research, clinical and industrial partners, the application immerses patients in carefully designed virtual environments that modulate attention and emotional engagement. Cognitive science principles guide the design of interactive experiences intended to influence perceived duration. Clinical testing in oncology settings evaluates feasibility, patient acceptance and effects on well-being indicators. Data collected during trials support iterative refinement of the application. By improving patient experience without altering medical procedures, the solution has potential to enhance quality of care, reduce anxiety and support broader adoption of digital therapeutic tools in clinical environments.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2024,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01522,EU_PSTW_202609_01522,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Università degli Studi di Roma La Spaienza,"Medical products, appliances and equipment.","Upper-limb prosthesis abandonment rates can reach 44 %, often due to limited functionality, discomfort and insufficient rehabilitation support.",BRAINmade - Against prostheses abandonment: immersive and low-cost platform for the rehabilitation of upper-limb amputees,"Università degli Studi di Roma La Spaienza; Academic-Research; Medical products, appliances and equipment.; Italy",UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Upper-limb prosthesis abandonment rates can reach 44 %, often due to limited functionality, discomfort and insufficient rehabilitation support. BRAINmade seeks to reduce abandonment by improving motor training and user adaptation through an accessible rehabilitation platform. The project develops immersive virtual reality scenarios controlled by electromyographic (EMG) signals generated by residual limb muscles. Customised, low-cost EMG sensors capture muscle activity and translate it into movements within virtual training environments, enabling patients to practise control strategies even with non-anthropomorphic prostheses. A structured four-week motor training programme reinforces neuromuscular coordination and embodiment of the device. The system integrates hardware and software components designed for affordability and scalability in clinical settings. Early results indicate improved motor performance and user engagement. By enhancing functional outcomes and confidence, the platform aims to lower prosthesis rejection rates, improve quality of life for amputees and modernise rehabilitation practices through cost-effective, technology-enabled training.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2025,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01523,EU_PSTW_202609_01523,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Koc University,R&D health.,UNKNOWN,KATSIM - Cataract Simulator Diagnostics Device Development Using Computational Holographic Displays,Koc University; Consortium; R&D health.; Türkiye,The system uses computational holography to recreate realistic visual distortions associated with different cataract conditions and lens options.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Cataract surgery outcomes depend heavily on selecting the most suitable intraocular lens, yet predicting postoperative visual perception remains challenging. The KATSIM project aims to develop a diagnostic device that simulates how patients will see after surgery, supporting personalised clinical decisions. The system uses computational holography to recreate realistic visual distortions associated with different cataract conditions and lens options. Advanced optical modelling and image processing algorithms generate holographic projections that replicate patient-specific vision scenarios. Clinicians can visualise and compare expected outcomes before intervention, improving lens selection accuracy. The project brings together expertise in computational holography, ophthalmology and vision science to validate the technology in clinical environments. By enabling more precise preoperative assessment, the device has potential to improve patient satisfaction, reduce revision procedures and optimise surgical planning. The innovation enhances shared decision-making between clinicians and patients and supports more predictable, outcome-oriented cataract treatment.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2026,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01524,EU_PSTW_202609_01524,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,ERATOSTHENES Centre of Excellence (ECoE),R&D environmental protection.,UNKNOWN,AI-OBSERVER - Enhancing Earth Observation capabilities of the Eratosthenes Centre of Excellence on Disaster Risk Reduction through Artificial Intelligence,ERATOSTHENES Centre of Excellence (ECoE); Consortium; R&D environmental protection.; Cyprus,"Computational tools are developed to automate hazard mapping, risk modelling and impact assessment workflows.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Effective disaster risk reduction relies on timely and accurate Earth observation data, yet integrating large-scale satellite and environmental datasets into operational decision-making remains complex. AI-OBSERVER aims to strengthen the Earth observation capabilities of the ERATOSTHENES Centre of Excellence in Cyprus by advancing analytical methods for multi-hazard monitoring and assessment. The project combines satellite imagery, geospatial datasets and environmental indicators with advanced machine learning models to improve detection and prediction of natural hazards. Through collaboration with leading research institutions, the initiative enhances technical expertise, research management capacity and innovation potential. Computational tools are developed to automate hazard mapping, risk modelling and impact assessment workflows. Capacity-building activities upgrade infrastructure and support knowledge transfer in disaster risk applications. Expected outcomes include improved scientific excellence, faster hazard assessment and stronger regional resilience. The project positions the centre as a leader in integrating advanced analytics with Earth observation for environmental risk management.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2027,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01525,EU_PSTW_202609_01525,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Universitat de Barcelona,R&D health.,"Radiomics has the potential to support more personalised cancer treatment, but its clinical adoption is limited by the lack of large-scale validation, consistency across centres, and evidence of real-world usability. The RadioVal project addresses this challenge by bringing together hospitals, clinicians, researchers, and technology developers across eight clinical sites in Europe and beyond, with the objective of validating radiomics-based decision support in routine breast cancer treatment planning.",RadioVal - International Clinical Validation of Radiomics Artificial Intelligence for Breast Cancer Treatment Planning,Universitat de Barcelona; Consortium; R&D health.; Spain,"These tools are evaluated using harmonised imaging datasets and clinical data, and assessed across multiple dimensions, including technical robustness, algorithmic fairness, clinical usability, and acceptance by healthcare professionals, with integration into existing clinical workflows.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Radiomics has the potential to support more personalised cancer treatment, but its clinical adoption is limited by the lack of large-scale validation, consistency across centres, and evidence of real-world usability. In breast cancer care, clinicians need reliable tools to predict how patients will respond to neoadjuvant chemotherapy in order to avoid unnecessary treatment and optimise outcomes. The RadioVal project addresses this challenge by bringing together hospitals, clinicians, researchers, and technology developers across eight clinical sites in Europe and beyond, with the objective of validating radiomics-based decision support in routine breast cancer treatment planning. The project implements advanced radiomics pipelines that extract quantitative features from medical imaging data, combined with machine learning models trained to predict treatment response. These tools are evaluated using harmonised imaging datasets and clinical data, and assessed across multiple dimensions, including technical robustness, algorithmic fairness, clinical usability, and acceptance by healthcare professionals, with integration into existing clinical workflows. The project strengthens confidence in data-driven imaging tools, supports more precise treatment decisions, and aims to reduce over-treatment while improving patient outcomes. By providing robust clinical validation and usability evidence, RadioVal contributes to increased trust, scalability, and responsible adoption of advanced imaging analytics in oncology.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2028,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01526,EU_PSTW_202609_01526,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Fundacion para la Investigacion de Malaga en Biomedicina y salud,R&D health.,"Accurate risk prediction following myocardial infarction is essential to guide treatment decisions and prevent adverse outcomes, yet existing models rely on limited clinical variables.",Optimize risk prediction after myocardial infarction through artificial intelligence and multidimensional evaluation,Fundacion para la Investigacion de Malaga en Biomedicina y salud; Academic-Research; R&D health.; Spain,These diverse data streams are combined into a comprehensive dataset analysed using advanced predictive modelling approaches.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Accurate risk prediction following myocardial infarction is essential to guide treatment decisions and prevent adverse outcomes, yet existing models rely on limited clinical variables. This project seeks to enhance postoperative risk stratification by integrating multidimensional patient data. A large cohort of myocardial infarction patients is enrolled to collect information from wearable devices, circulating biomarkers and non-invasive imaging techniques. These diverse data streams are combined into a comprehensive dataset analysed using advanced predictive modelling approaches. The system identifies novel computational biomarkers that capture complex physiological patterns associated with recovery and complications. By refining prediction accuracy beyond current standards, the project supports personalised therapeutic planning and informed decision-making for clinicians and patients. Anticipated benefits include improved survival rates, reduced hospital readmissions and more efficient allocation of healthcare resources. The initiative demonstrates the value of integrating continuous monitoring data with clinical evaluation to advance precision cardiology.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2029,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01527,EU_PSTW_202609_01527,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,University of Santiago de Compostela,Public order and safety n.e.c.,HYBRIDS addresses these challenges by developing hybrid intelligence systems that combine human expertise with advanced computational tools to analyse and counter harmful digital content.,"HYBRIDS - Hybrid Intelligence to monitor, promote and analyse transformations in good democracy practices",University of Santiago de Compostela; Consortium; Public order and safety n.e.c.; Spain,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Online disinformation, hate speech and polarising narratives undermine democratic processes and social cohesion. HYBRIDS addresses these challenges by developing hybrid intelligence systems that combine human expertise with advanced computational tools to analyse and counter harmful digital content. The project integrates insights from social sciences and humanities into natural language processing frameworks and deep learning models. Algorithms detect patterns of misinformation and extremist discourse, while human analysts contribute contextual interpretation and ethical oversight. Interactive tools are designed to support researchers and policymakers in understanding evolving narratives and assessing interventions. By bridging machine scalability with human judgement, the approach mitigates limitations of purely automated systems. The project enhances analytical capacity to monitor transformations in democratic practices and strengthens evidence-based responses to online harms. Outcomes include improved detection accuracy, interdisciplinary collaboration and tools that support resilient democratic ecosystems.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2030,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01528,EU_PSTW_202609_01528,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Engineering - Ingegneria Informatica SPA,Education not definable by level.,"Citizens frequently encounter misleading or false information online, yet lack accessible tools to critically assess claims.",TITAN - AI for Citizen Intelligent Coaching against Disinformation,Engineering - Ingegneria Informatica SPA; Consortium; Education not definable by level.; Italy,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Citizens frequently encounter misleading or false information online, yet lack accessible tools to critically assess claims. The TITAN project aims to empower individuals through an interactive coaching ecosystem that supports critical thinking and fact evaluation. The platform provides personalised question-and-response interactions guiding users to examine sources, logical consistency and contextual evidence behind statements. Advanced language processing models analyse user queries and generate tailored prompts encouraging reflective assessment rather than passive consumption. The system adapts to user profiles and learning progress, delivering customised coaching schemes. By focusing on reasoning skills and media literacy, the initiative promotes informed civic participation. The solution supports scalable citizen engagement while incorporating ethical design principles. Expected impacts include improved resilience to disinformation, enhanced digital literacy and strengthened democratic discourse through proactive, user-centred technological support.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2031,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01529,EU_PSTW_202609_01529,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,UNIVERSITA DI PISA,Public health services.,UNKNOWN,TOLIFE - Combining Artificial Intelligence and smart sensing TOward better management and improved quality of LIFE in chronic obstructive pulmonary disease,UNIVERSITA DI PISA; Consortium; Public health services.; Italy,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD) requires continuous monitoring to prevent exacerbations that significantly impair quality of life and increase hospitalisations. TOLIFE aims to support personalised disease management through unobtrusive sensing and predictive analytics. Wearable and environmental sensors collect physiological and behavioural data relevant to respiratory health, including activity levels and environmental exposure. These data are integrated into predictive models capable of identifying early warning signs of exacerbations. Clinicians receive decision-support insights enabling timely, tailored interventions. The system is designed to fit into routine care pathways and ensure secure data handling. By anticipating health deterioration, the solution seeks to reduce emergency admissions and improve long-term patient outcomes. Anticipated benefits include enhanced quality of life, more efficient healthcare delivery and strengthened preventive management of chronic respiratory conditions through data-driven, patient-centred approaches.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2032,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01530,EU_PSTW_202609_01530,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Università degli Studi di Modena e Reggio Emilia,R&D health.,"Patients with atrial fibrillation and other complex conditions often experience fragmented care due to coexisting diseases, delayed diagnosis, and lack of personalised risk assessment, leading to avoidable hospitalisations and increased mortality. The ARISTOTELES project addresses these challenges by involving hospitals, clinicians, researchers, and patients across multiple countries to improve the management of atrial fibrillation and multimorbidity.",ARISTOTELES - Applying ARtificial Intelligence to Define clinical trajectorieS for personalized predicTiOn and early deTEctiOn of comorbidiTy and muLtimorbidiTy pattErnS,Università degli Studi di Modena e Reggio Emilia; Consortium; R&D health.; Italy,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Patients with atrial fibrillation and other complex conditions often experience fragmented care due to coexisting diseases, delayed diagnosis, and lack of personalised risk assessment, leading to avoidable hospitalisations and increased mortality. Healthcare systems require better tools to manage comorbidities holistically and support timely clinical interventions. The ARISTOTELES project addresses these challenges by involving hospitals, clinicians, researchers, and patients across multiple countries to improve the management of atrial fibrillation and multimorbidity. Its objective is to support personalised prediction, early detection, and coordinated care pathways that improve outcomes and patient adherence. The project establishes a multinational health data platform that integrates heterogeneous patient data, including clinical records, diagnostics, and longitudinal health information. Advanced analytical models are developed to identify disease trajectories, predict risks, and support personalised treatment strategies in an ethical and privacy-aware manner, with a focus on integration into clinical practice. The approach enables earlier diagnosis, better treatment targeting, and improved coordination of care. It aims to reduce disease progression, hospital admissions, and mortality, while enhancing patient engagement and adherence. ARISTOTELES delivers long-term value by supporting more proactive, efficient, and patient-centred management of complex chronic diseases.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2033,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01531,EU_PSTW_202609_01531,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Universiteit Maastricht,R&D health.,"Effective diabetes management requires continuous adjustment of insulin therapy and monitoring of complications, yet many patients lack personalised, data-driven support between clinical visits.",MELISSA - Mobile Artificial Intelligence Solution for Diabetes Adaptive Care,Universiteit Maastricht; Consortium; R&D health.; Netherlands,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Effective diabetes management requires continuous adjustment of insulin therapy and monitoring of complications, yet many patients lack personalised, data-driven support between clinical visits. MELISSA aims to deliver a clinically validated and cost-efficient digital solution to assist insulin-treated patients and healthcare providers in adaptive diabetes care. The platform integrates mobile health data, patient-reported inputs and clinical parameters into advanced predictive models. Reinforcement learning techniques are applied to recommend personalised insulin dose adjustments, while additional analytical tools assess risk of complications based on longitudinal data patterns. The system is designed to operate independently of specific glucose monitoring devices, ensuring interoperability and broad accessibility. Clinicians receive structured insights to support treatment decisions, while patients benefit from tailored guidance via a user-friendly interface. Expected outcomes include improved glycaemic control, reduced risk of adverse events and more efficient clinical workflows. By combining adaptive algorithms with mobile delivery, MELISSA supports scalable, personalised and proactive diabetes management.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2034,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01532,EU_PSTW_202609_01532,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Universitair Medisch Centrum Utrecht,Public health services.,"Breast cancer survivors face elevated risks of chronic conditions such as cardiovascular disease, osteoporosis and chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, yet early identification of these risks is often limited. By repurposing routinely collected imaging data, the approach enhances efficiency without increasing patient burden.",ARTILLERY - Artificial Intelligence for early detection of non-communicable disease risk in people with breast cancer,Universitair Medisch Centrum Utrecht; Consortium; Public health services.; Netherlands,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Breast cancer survivors face elevated risks of chronic conditions such as cardiovascular disease, osteoporosis and chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, yet early identification of these risks is often limited. ARTILLERY seeks to improve long-term health outcomes by leveraging routine imaging data to predict non-communicable disease risks. The project develops advanced image analysis models capable of extracting quantitative biomarkers from standard radiological examinations. These computational tools analyse imaging features alongside clinical data to estimate individual risk profiles for multiple chronic conditions. Outputs provide healthcare professionals with structured, interpretable risk parameters to guide preventive interventions and monitoring strategies. By repurposing routinely collected imaging data, the approach enhances efficiency without increasing patient burden. Anticipated impacts include earlier treatment initiation, reduced long-term morbidity and improved survivorship care planning. The project demonstrates how integrated imaging analytics can support holistic management of cancer survivors and strengthen preventive healthcare strategies.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2035,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01533,EU_PSTW_202609_01533,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""social_nonprofit""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Champalimaud Foundation,Public health services.,"Surgical treatment decisions in breast cancer depend on tumour characteristics, anatomy and patient preferences, yet patients often struggle to visualise potential outcomes.",CINDERELLA - Clinical Validation of an AI-based approach to improve the shared decision-making process and outcomes in Breast Cancer Patients proposed for Locoregional treatment,Champalimaud Foundation; Academic-Research; Public health services.; Portugal,"The application is designed for intuitive use within routine consultations, supporting transparent communication and patient empowerment.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Surgical treatment decisions in breast cancer depend on tumour characteristics, anatomy and patient preferences, yet patients often struggle to visualise potential outcomes. CINDERELLA aims to enhance shared decision-making by developing a digital application that simulates post-surgical results based on clinical and biometric data. The platform integrates a comprehensive clinical database with image processing and biometric matching techniques to generate personalised visual representations of expected surgical outcomes. Patients can compare alternative treatment scenarios, facilitating informed discussions with clinicians. The application is designed for intuitive use within routine consultations, supporting transparent communication and patient empowerment. Clinical validation assesses usability, accuracy and impact on decision quality. Expected benefits include improved patient satisfaction, reduced decisional conflict and more personalised treatment planning. By combining predictive modelling with visual simulation, CINDERELLA strengthens patient-centred care in breast cancer management.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2036,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01534,EU_PSTW_202609_01534,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Universitatea Transilvania Din Brasov,"Agriculture, forestry, fishing and hunting","Romania possesses substantial agricultural potential but faces challenges including soil degradation, fragmentation and limited technological uptake.",AI4AGRI - Romanian Excellence Center on Artificial Intelligence in Earth Observation Data for Agriculture,"Universitatea Transilvania Din Brasov; Consortium; Agriculture, forestry, fishing and hunting; Romania",UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Romania possesses substantial agricultural potential but faces challenges including soil degradation, fragmentation and limited technological uptake. AI4AGRI aims to establish a dedicated research centre focused on applying advanced analytics to Earth observation data for agricultural innovation. Through collaboration with leading institutes in France and Italy, the centre strengthens expertise in processing satellite imagery, particularly data from the Copernicus programme. Machine learning models are developed to generate vegetation status maps and agronomic indicators supporting precision farming decisions. The initiative includes training programmes for young scientists and capacity-building in research management and innovation administration. By integrating geospatial analytics with agricultural applications, the centre enhances evidence-based farm management and policy development. Expected impacts include improved crop monitoring, increased productivity and stronger contributions to European food security and sustainability. The project positions Romania as a regional hub for data-driven agricultural research and innovation.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2038,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01535,EU_PSTW_202609_01535,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Fraunhofer Gesselsaft Zur Forterung Der Angeuaden Forsung Av,Waste management.,"The growing volume of end-of-life photovoltaic modules presents a sustainability challenge, as current recycling processes recover limited materials and fail to support a truly circular solar economy. The APOLLO project addresses these challenges by involving manufacturers, recyclers, technology providers, and market actors to establish a circular framework for photovoltaic modules.",APOLLO - A Proactive Approach to the Recovery and Recycling of Photovoltaic Modules,Fraunhofer Gesselsaft Zur Forterung Der Angeuaden Forsung Av; Consortium; Waste management.; Germany,"The solution is implemented through blockchain-based Digital Product Passports that store secure, trustworthy information on materials, composition, hazards, and lifecycle history of photovoltaic modules.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The growing volume of end-of-life photovoltaic modules presents a sustainability challenge, as current recycling processes recover limited materials and fail to support a truly circular solar economy. To meet environmental and economic goals, the photovoltaic sector needs better traceability, higher-quality material recovery, and stronger links between production, reuse, and recycling. The APOLLO project addresses these challenges by involving manufacturers, recyclers, technology providers, and market actors to establish a circular framework for photovoltaic modules. Its objective is to maximise material recovery, enable reuse and remanufacturing, and reduce environmental impact across the full product lifecycle. The solution is implemented through blockchain-based Digital Product Passports that store secure, trustworthy information on materials, composition, hazards, and lifecycle history of photovoltaic modules. These passports support automated access to data needed for recycling and reuse and are linked to an online marketplace for recovered and refurbished components. The approach improves transparency, traceability, and coordination across the value chain, increases the economic viability of recycling, and supports regulatory compliance. APOLLO enables higher-value material recovery, reduces waste, and creates scalable foundations for a circular photovoltaic industry.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F06_HIGH_VOLUME_SCALABILITY_BOTTLENECK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2039,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01536,EU_PSTW_202609_01536,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Computer Science and Automation Research Institute,R&D economic affairs,"The rapid growth of data-intensive applications challenges traditional centralised cloud computing, as transferring large datasets can be costly, slow and raise security concerns.",Swarmchestrate - Application-level Swarm-based Orchestration Across the Cloud-to-Edge Continuum,Computer Science and Automation Research Institute; Consortium; R&D economic affairs; Hungary,Trust and identity management are supported through blockchain-based mechanisms using self-sovereign identities and distributed identifiers.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The rapid growth of data-intensive applications challenges traditional centralised cloud computing, as transferring large datasets can be costly, slow and raise security concerns. Swarmchestrate addresses this by developing decentralised orchestration mechanisms across the cloud-to-edge continuum. Application microservices are managed by distributed orchestration agents operating within a dynamic orchestration space, where intelligent matchmaking aligns application requirements with available resources. Swarm-based coordination principles enable self-organisation and adaptive workload distribution closer to data sources. Trust and identity management are supported through blockchain-based mechanisms using self-sovereign identities and distributed identifiers. Advanced cryptographic techniques and privacy-preserving analytics ensure end-to-end system security. The approach enhances scalability, reduces latency and optimises resource utilisation in distributed computing environments. By enabling flexible and trustworthy orchestration, the project supports next-generation digital services requiring real-time processing across heterogeneous infrastructures.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2040,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01537,EU_PSTW_202609_01537,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Irish Red Cross,Foreign economic aid.,"Humanitarian aid distribution often suffers from inefficiencies, duplication and limited transparency, particularly in crisis settings involving displaced populations.",Delivery of digital entitlements via Digital Identity for Syrian Refugees in Lebanon,Irish Red Cross; Non-governmental; Foreign economic aid.; Ireland,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Humanitarian aid distribution often suffers from inefficiencies, duplication and limited transparency, particularly in crisis settings involving displaced populations. This project piloted a digital identity solution to deliver aid entitlements to Syrian refugees in Tripoli, Lebanon. In partnership with the International Federation of Red Cross and Red Crescent Societies, beneficiaries received smart cards linked to a blockchain-based identity and entitlement management system. Each card securely stored verified identity information and records of allocated aid, which could be redeemed through authorised local merchants. The distributed ledger ensured transparency and traceability of transactions, enabling aid agencies to monitor distribution in real time. The pilot provided 500 smart cards to 100 families, demonstrating feasibility of secure digital entitlement delivery. Outcomes include improved accountability, reduced fraud risk and enhanced dignity for beneficiaries through controlled access to assistance. The initiative illustrates how digital identity infrastructures can strengthen efficiency and trust in humanitarian operations.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2041,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01538,EU_PSTW_202609_01538,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Università di Pisa,Housing development.,UNKNOWN,BUILDCHAIN - BUILDing knowledge book in the blockCHAIN distributed ledger,Università di Pisa; Consortium; Housing development.; Italy,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Achieving sustainability and energy efficiency targets in the building sector requires transparent documentation of construction, renovation and lifecycle performance data. BUILDCHAIN aims to develop a Digital Building LogBook (DBL) enabling municipalities to manage and administer building portfolios more effectively. The DBL integrates existing and newly generated data within a decentralised knowledge graph built on blockchain technology. Building-specific ontologies structure information related to materials, certifications, energy performance and lifecycle events, ensuring traceability and continuous updates. The platform creates a marketplace environment where stakeholders can share credentials and verified documentation. Distributed ledger mechanisms safeguard data integrity and support trusted information exchange. By centralising and standardising lifecycle data, the solution enhances transparency, regulatory compliance and evidence-based decision-making. Expected benefits include improved asset management, facilitation of renovation strategies and stronger alignment with European sustainability directives.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2042,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01539,EU_PSTW_202609_01539,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,The Lisbon Council for Economic Competitiveness,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.","Through pilot activities in extended reality environments, the initiative tests participatory design approaches and explores challenges related to governance, interoperability, privacy, security and data ownership.",OPENVERSE - OPEN and co-created metaVERSe for Europe,"The Lisbon Council for Economic Competitiveness; Consortium; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Belgium",UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Europe seeks to promote open, inclusive and ethical virtual worlds that safeguard technological sovereignty while fostering innovation and social value. OPENVERSE aims to build a comprehensive knowledge base on European virtual environments and support the development of interoperable and trustworthy ecosystems. The project establishes a multi-stakeholder community including industry, academia, civil society and policymakers to co-create visions and standards for future virtual worlds. Through pilot activities in extended reality environments, the initiative tests participatory design approaches and explores challenges related to governance, interoperability, privacy, security and data ownership. Analytical frameworks and foresight methodologies are used to identify technological gaps and societal implications. The project produces standards proposals, technology roadmaps and policy recommendations to guide sustainable development of virtual ecosystems. Expected impacts include strengthened European leadership in immersive technologies, reduced entry barriers for innovators and enhanced user control and accessibility. By combining technical exploration with co-creation and governance design, OPENVERSE supports a competitive and values-driven European metaverse landscape.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2044,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01540,EU_PSTW_202609_01540,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Patron University,Housing and community amenities n.e.c.,"Cities and regions in South-Eastern Europe face shared challenges related to sustainable mobility, green urban development, and digital transformation, but often lack the coordinated innovation ecosystems needed to scale advanced solutions.",MetaCities Excellence Hub in South-Eastern Europe,Patron University; Consortium; Housing and community amenities n.e.c.; Greece,"These technologies are applied to urban use cases such as mobility optimisation, environmental monitoring, and sustainable spatial planning, with shared experimentation, knowledge exchange, and skills development across regions.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Cities and regions in South-Eastern Europe face shared challenges related to sustainable mobility, green urban development, and digital transformation, but often lack the coordinated innovation ecosystems needed to scale advanced solutions. The METACITIES project addresses this gap by bringing together place-based innovation ecosystems in Cyprus, Greece, and Bulgaria to collaboratively shape future cities and regions. The objective is to strengthen regional capacity, foster cross-border collaboration, and develop practical solutions for smart mobility and green urban planning. The project establishes an excellence hub that promotes advanced digital technologies, including next-generation communication networks, digital twins, and large-scale data analytics, supported by artificial intelligence. These technologies are applied to urban use cases such as mobility optimisation, environmental monitoring, and sustainable spatial planning, with shared experimentation, knowledge exchange, and skills development across regions. The hub supports testing and validation of solutions in real-world contexts and connects public authorities, researchers, industry, and citizens. METACITIES enhances innovation capacity, accelerates adoption of advanced urban technologies, and supports more sustainable, data-driven planning. It delivers long-term value by strengthening regional ecosystems and enabling scalable, future-ready solutions for European cities.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2045,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01541,EU_PSTW_202609_01541,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""social_nonprofit"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,National Quantom Computing Centre,Education n.e.c.,"The objective is to develop quantum computing readiness, address scaling challenges, and help organisations explore how quantum approaches could be translated into usable services and innovation. The implementation is centred on a national platform that works with business, government, and the research community, focusing on the engineering challenges involved in scaling quantum computers and on building the wider quantum ecosystem through facilities, collaboration, and skills development.",National Quantum Computing Centre,National Quantom Computing Centre; Academic-Research; Education n.e.c.; United Kingdom,"Governments, researchers, and businesses increasingly need access to advanced computing capability for problems that may exceed the limits of classical systems, yet building national capacity in quantum computing requires coordinated infrastructure, engineering expertise, and pathways from research into practical use. The impact lies in stronger national capacity, improved coordination between research and application, and greater preparedness for future computing use cases.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Governments, researchers, and businesses increasingly need access to advanced computing capability for problems that may exceed the limits of classical systems, yet building national capacity in quantum computing requires coordinated infrastructure, engineering expertise, and pathways from research into practical use. In the United Kingdom, the National Quantum Computing Centre was established as the national laboratory for quantum computing to support this transition and strengthen the country’s capability across public, industrial, and scientific domains. The objective is to develop quantum computing readiness, address scaling challenges, and help organisations explore how quantum approaches could be translated into usable services and innovation. The implementation is centred on a national platform that works with business, government, and the research community, focusing on the engineering challenges involved in scaling quantum computers and on building the wider quantum ecosystem through facilities, collaboration, and skills development. At a conceptual level, the centre combines technical infrastructure, research translation, and professional development to help turn quantum science into deployable capability. The impact lies in stronger national capacity, improved coordination between research and application, and greater preparedness for future computing use cases. It also creates strategic value by giving the UK a shared institutional foundation for developing quantum capabilities across sectors.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2046,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01542,EU_PSTW_202609_01542,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Espoo,Fuel and energy,"The approach enables automated extraction of input parameters, reducing manual data preparation and improving modelling accuracy.",CityGML-based district energy simulation,City of Espoo; Local Government; Fuel and energy; Finland,"This project leverages the public CityGML data model to streamline district-level energy simulations and support energy-positive urban solutions. Using up-to-date geometric and semantic building information from CityGML datasets, simulation platforms such as Apros model energy systems across buildings and neighbourhoods.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Urban decarbonisation requires robust tools to evaluate renovation strategies and energy supply options at district scale, yet collecting consistent building data is often resource-intensive. This project leverages the public CityGML data model to streamline district-level energy simulations and support energy-positive urban solutions. Using up-to-date geometric and semantic building information from CityGML datasets, simulation platforms such as Apros model energy systems across buildings and neighbourhoods. The approach enables automated extraction of input parameters, reducing manual data preparation and improving modelling accuracy. Planners can test renovation scenarios, alternative heating and cooling systems and renewable integration strategies within a unified simulation framework. The tool supports comparative assessment of energy performance and sustainability impacts across urban blocks. Beneficiaries include energy planners, building consultants and municipal authorities seeking data-driven guidance for renovation programmes. The solution enhances efficiency of planning processes, improves scenario evaluation and supports scalable pathways towards climate-neutral districts.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2065,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01543,EU_PSTW_202609_01543,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Espoo,Fuel and energy,Increasing penetration of renewable energy sources introduces variability and grid management challenges in urban environments.,Virtual Power Station Optimisation in Espoo,City of Espoo; Local Government; Fuel and energy; Finland,The system integrates distributed energy assets through digital control platforms and applies forecasting models and optimisation algorithms to balance supply and demand.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Increasing penetration of renewable energy sources introduces variability and grid management challenges in urban environments. In Espoo’s Leppävaara district, coordinating distributed generation, storage and consumption requires enhanced forecasting and optimisation. Stakeholders include municipal authorities, energy providers, building managers and residents. The objective is to implement and demonstrate a virtual power plant that aggregates and optimises local energy resources to support decarbonisation and grid stability. The system integrates distributed energy assets through digital control platforms and applies forecasting models and optimisation algorithms to balance supply and demand. Advanced predictive analytics support real-time energy dispatch decisions, while smart building systems provide flexible demand response capabilities. Expected impacts include improved energy efficiency, reduced carbon emissions, enhanced grid resilience and more effective utilisation of renewable resources in smart urban districts.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION|S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2066,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01544,EU_PSTW_202609_01544,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Copenhagen Emergency Medical Services,Hospital services.,"Recognising cardiac arrest during emergency calls is a high-stakes operational challenge, because callers may describe symptoms unclearly and dispatchers must make life-critical decisions within seconds.",AI predicts cardian arrest in Copenhagen,Copenhagen Emergency Medical Services; Local Government; Hospital services.; Denmark,"The implementation uses a real-time speech-analysis solution that listens to the ongoing emergency call, compares patterns in the dialogue with historical call data, and calculates the probability that the incident involves cardiac arrest. At a conceptual level, the system applies predictive pattern recognition to live call audio and feeds alerts to the medical dispatcher handling the case, functioning as decision support rather than a replacement for human judgement.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Recognising cardiac arrest during emergency calls is a high-stakes operational challenge, because callers may describe symptoms unclearly and dispatchers must make life-critical decisions within seconds. In Copenhagen, Emergency Medical Services introduced a real-time analytical tool to improve recognition of out-of-hospital cardiac arrest during 112 calls and provide dispatchers with earlier warning when a call matches patterns associated with cardiac arrest. The objective is to increase recognition rates, support faster intervention, and improve survival chances by strengthening dispatcher decision-making during the call itself. The implementation uses a real-time speech-analysis solution that listens to the ongoing emergency call, compares patterns in the dialogue with historical call data, and calculates the probability that the incident involves cardiac arrest. At a conceptual level, the system applies predictive pattern recognition to live call audio and feeds alerts to the medical dispatcher handling the case, functioning as decision support rather than a replacement for human judgement. Reported results indicate that the tool identifies around 85 per cent of cardiac arrests, compared with approximately 75 per cent recognised by dispatchers alone, corresponding to roughly 150 additional patients being recognised before the ambulance arrives. The impact includes faster diagnosis, better quality of emergency response, and more effective use of pre-hospital care resources.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2068,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01545,EU_PSTW_202609_01545,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,ICT Development Programs in the City of Helsinki Education Division,R&D education.,Education systems must respond to increasingly diverse learner needs while supporting teachers with limited resources and rising expectations for personalised learning.,Empowering Learning and well-being in the City of Helsinki using data and AI,ICT Development Programs in the City of Helsinki Education Division; Local Government; R&D education.; Finland,"The City of Helsinki sought to rethink education as a city-wide ecosystem, using data to improve learning outcomes, student wellbeing, and teaching effectiveness. This use case involves city authorities, schools, teachers, students, and technology partners, with the objective of transforming Helsinki into one of the most impactful learning environments globally.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Education systems must respond to increasingly diverse learner needs while supporting teachers with limited resources and rising expectations for personalised learning. The City of Helsinki sought to rethink education as a city-wide ecosystem, using data to improve learning outcomes, student wellbeing, and teaching effectiveness. This use case involves city authorities, schools, teachers, students, and technology partners, with the objective of transforming Helsinki into one of the most impactful learning environments globally. The initiative is implemented through data-driven platforms that integrate learning data, wellbeing indicators, and school information systems, supported by advanced analytics and artificial intelligence. These tools enable personalised learning pathways, early identification of support needs, and evidence-based pedagogical decisions, while respecting ethical and governance frameworks. Teachers are supported with insights that enhance planning and classroom practice, and leadership gains a systemic view of educational performance. The approach improves student engagement, supports wellbeing, and enhances teacher effectiveness. It delivers scalable and sustainable educational innovation by aligning technology, pedagogy, leadership, and governance, creating lasting public value for learners and society.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2072,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01546,EU_PSTW_202609_01546,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,London Borough of Camden,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.",UNKNOWN,Co-creation of a Data Charter for AI in the Camden Council,"London Borough of Camden; Local Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; United Kingdom",UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Public authorities increasingly rely on data-driven technologies, raising concerns about transparency, accountability and community trust. Camden Council partnered with The Alan Turing Institute to co-create a Data Charter defining principles for ethical data collection, processing and sharing. The initiative engaged residents through participatory workshops and consultations, establishing a social contract between the council and the community. Structured deliberation methods and facilitated dialogue were used to capture public expectations and translate them into governance commitments. The resulting charter outlines standards for fairness, transparency and responsible innovation in digital services. The co-creation process itself served as a trust-building mechanism, demonstrating how local governments can embed public values into data governance frameworks. Outcomes include clearer accountability structures, enhanced public confidence and a replicable model for participatory digital governance in municipalities adopting advanced technologies.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2073,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01547,EU_PSTW_202609_01547,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Government of the Republic of North Macedonia,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.",UNKNOWN,North Macedonia’s government launches AI digital assistant,"Government of the Republic of North Macedonia; Central-Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; North Macedonia","The implementation takes the form of an AI-powered digital assistant deployed by the government as a conversational service for business-facing enquiries. At a conceptual level, the solution uses natural-language interaction to interpret company questions and return relevant information on investment and support schemes through a centralised digital channel.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Investors and companies seeking to understand a country’s business environment often need rapid access to clear information on investment conditions, public incentives, and available state support, yet these answers are frequently spread across multiple institutions and documents. In North Macedonia, the government launched its first public-sector digital assistant, ADA, to provide companies with easier access to information about investing in the country and the forms of state aid available. The objective is to simplify access to economic and investment information, improve the responsiveness of public communication with businesses, and strengthen the country’s digital service offering in this area. The implementation takes the form of an AI-powered digital assistant deployed by the government as a conversational service for business-facing enquiries. At a conceptual level, the solution uses natural-language interaction to interpret company questions and return relevant information on investment and support schemes through a centralised digital channel. The impact includes quicker access to official guidance, reduced friction for potential investors, and a more scalable way for public authorities to respond to recurring business enquiries. It also supports more consistent communication and creates value by presenting key investment information through a single, more accessible interface.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2076,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01548,EU_PSTW_202609_01548,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Poste Italiane SpA,General services.,"Large service providers receive high volumes of routine customer enquiries about digital services, products, and website functions, and handling all of them through email or telephone support alone can create avoidable delays and operational pressure.",ChatBot Assistant PosteItaliane,Poste Italiane SpA; Private sector; General services.; Italy,"At a conceptual level, the assistant uses conversational language processing and a structured support knowledge base covering products and services, and it is designed to provide support continuously rather than only during staffed service hours.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Large service providers receive high volumes of routine customer enquiries about digital services, products, and website functions, and handling all of them through email or telephone support alone can create avoidable delays and operational pressure. Poste Italiane introduced its digital assistant to offer users a simpler self-service route for common questions without requiring direct contact with an operator in every case. The objective is to improve the accessibility of customer support, speed up resolution of routine enquiries, and reduce pressure on traditional contact channels. The implementation combines chatbot and voicebot capabilities within Poste Italiane’s assistance environment, allowing users to ask short natural-language questions and either receive guidance directly or, where necessary, be routed onwards to a human operator. At a conceptual level, the assistant uses conversational language processing and a structured support knowledge base covering products and services, and it is designed to provide support continuously rather than only during staffed service hours. The impact includes faster first-line support, better user convenience, and more efficient handling of repetitive customer-service interactions. It also improves scalability by shifting a share of standard enquiries to automated channels while preserving escalation paths for cases needing human assistance.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F06_HIGH_VOLUME_SCALABILITY_BOTTLENECK,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2083,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01549,EU_PSTW_202609_01549,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,National Institute for Social Security (INPS),Sickness and disability.,Social security institutions face the challenge of ensuring that sickness benefits are granted lawfully while avoiding excessive or intrusive medical controls.,SAVIO - Data mining to score sickness certificates for the purpose of controlling their truthfullness,National Institute for Social Security (INPS); Central-Government; Sickness and disability.; Italy,"The objective is to prioritise medical checks where there is a higher likelihood of irregularities, ensuring fairness while optimising the use of inspection resources.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Social security institutions face the challenge of ensuring that sickness benefits are granted lawfully while avoiding excessive or intrusive medical controls. INPS, the Italian National Social Security Institute, reactivated the SAVIO system to support the verification of sickness certificates submitted by workers. Stakeholders include INPS medical services, employers, employees and public health professionals. The objective is to prioritise medical checks where there is a higher likelihood of irregularities, ensuring fairness while optimising the use of inspection resources. SAVIO processes administrative and medical data related to sickness certificates, applying data mining techniques to identify patterns associated with anomalous or inconsistent claims. Based on historical cases and contextual variables, the system assigns risk scores that inform whether ex officio medical examinations should be ordered. The tool does not replace medical judgement but supports decision-making by highlighting cases requiring attention. Expected impacts include improved efficiency of medical checks, reduced unnecessary inspections, better targeting of controls and more consistent handling of sickness benefit claims, while maintaining transparency and proportionality.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2084,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01550,EU_PSTW_202609_01550,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,National Institute for Social Security (INPS),Social protection n.e.c.,"Large public sector websites often struggle to adapt content and interfaces to the real needs of users, particularly when services are complex and audiences diverse.",New Web Analytics System on the INPS official website,National Institute for Social Security (INPS); Central-Government; Social protection n.e.c.; Italy,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Large public sector websites often struggle to adapt content and interfaces to the real needs of users, particularly when services are complex and audiences diverse. INPS, Italy’s national social security institute, manages one of the country’s most heavily used public service portals and requires evidence-based insights to improve usability, accessibility and service effectiveness. The objective of this initiative is to enhance the quality of digital services by identifying navigation patterns, friction points and content gaps experienced by users. The implementation focuses on the design and development of machine learning algorithms that analyse anonymous web navigation data, including page transitions, interaction sequences and usage frequency. These models generate aggregated insights and recommendations for improving information architecture, content clarity and interface design, while fully respecting data protection and anonymity requirements. The system is intended to integrate with existing web management processes and support continuous improvement cycles. Expected impacts include more user-centred digital services, reduced abandonment of online procedures and improved accessibility for different user groups. Qualitative outcomes include better-informed design decisions, increased trust in digital public services and a scalable analytics framework applicable across large public administration websites.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PLANNED,계획,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2085,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01551,EU_PSTW_202609_01551,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,National Institute for Social Security (INPS),Sickness and disability.,"Managing medical examinations for social security purposes requires balancing timely appointments, limited medical resources and citizens’ availability.","Calendaring medical examination management, communication and medical examination",National Institute for Social Security (INPS); Central-Government; Sickness and disability.; Italy,"INPS initiated a project to standardise and automate the scheduling of medical examinations, addressing inefficiencies caused by manual planning and fragmented communication channels. Advanced scheduling logic applies prioritisation rules and planning-and-control principles to dynamically assign appointments.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Managing medical examinations for social security purposes requires balancing timely appointments, limited medical resources and citizens’ availability. INPS initiated a project to standardise and automate the scheduling of medical examinations, addressing inefficiencies caused by manual planning and fragmented communication channels. Stakeholders include INPS medical staff, administrative offices, contracted healthcare providers and citizens subject to medical assessments. The objective is to optimise resource allocation, reduce waiting times and improve communication with patients. The system introduces a centralised and automated calendar that integrates information on medical personnel, facilities and availability. Advanced scheduling logic applies prioritisation rules and planning-and-control principles to dynamically assign appointments. The platform also includes automated notification mechanisms, informing citizens of scheduled visits and enabling rescheduling through INPS digital channels. Anticipated benefits include more efficient use of medical resources, improved appointment adherence, reduced administrative workload and a more transparent and predictable experience for citizens undergoing medical examinations.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2086,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01552,EU_PSTW_202609_01552,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,National Institute for Social Security (INPS),Social protection n.e.c.,"Errors in compilation can lead to delays, corrections and administrative disputes.",Implementation of AI chatbot to guide the compilation of UNIEMENS flows,National Institute for Social Security (INPS); Central-Government; Social protection n.e.c.; Italy,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Private sector employers and intermediaries are required to submit UNIEMENS reports monthly to INPS, detailing employment and contribution data. Errors in compilation can lead to delays, corrections and administrative disputes. INPS launched a project to support users in correctly completing UNIEMENS submissions. Stakeholders include employers, payroll intermediaries, INPS offices and compliance professionals. The objective is to reduce errors, improve understanding of regulatory requirements and streamline reporting processes. The solution consists of a conversational digital assistant designed to provide contextual guidance during UNIEMENS compilation. It draws on regulatory texts, procedural rules and validation logic to answer questions, explain obligations and highlight common errors. The chatbot is intended to integrate with existing INPS digital services, offering real-time support without replacing formal controls. Expected impacts include improved data quality, fewer rejected submissions, reduced support requests to INPS offices and greater transparency for employers navigating complex reporting obligations.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,F08_ERROR_COMPLIANCE_RISK,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2087,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01553,EU_PSTW_202609_01553,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,National Institute for Social Security (INPS),Social protection n.e.c.,"Accessing information on wage-supplementation benefits can be difficult for workers and beneficiaries when rules, procedures, and eligibility conditions are spread across multiple digital channels and documents. It also creates a more scalable front-line assistance channel by reducing dependence on manual information handling for repetitive benefit enquiries.",Chatbot to support workers applying for a wage supplement support,National Institute for Social Security (INPS); Central-Government; Social protection n.e.c.; Italy,"This use case addresses that problem by providing a conversational support service for people seeking information on Wage Supplementation Fund benefits, with the aim of making access to relevant guidance faster, simpler, and more convenient. The implementation takes the form of a chatbot integrated into the relevant service environment, able to use data associated with the people interacting with it, particularly recipients of CIG, in order to provide more appropriate responses.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Accessing information on wage-supplementation benefits can be difficult for workers and beneficiaries when rules, procedures, and eligibility conditions are spread across multiple digital channels and documents. This use case addresses that problem by providing a conversational support service for people seeking information on Wage Supplementation Fund benefits, with the aim of making access to relevant guidance faster, simpler, and more convenient. The objective is to improve user experience, reduce the time needed to find the right information, and make support available continuously beyond traditional service hours. The implementation takes the form of a chatbot integrated into the relevant service environment, able to use data associated with the people interacting with it, particularly recipients of CIG, in order to provide more appropriate responses. The system is also described as incorporating artificial-intelligence and machine-learning functions so that it can learn from past interactions, user behaviour, and correction of previous errors. The impact includes quicker access to benefit-related information, greater service availability through 24-hour support, and improved user satisfaction. It also creates a more scalable front-line assistance channel by reducing dependence on manual information handling for repetitive benefit enquiries.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PLANNED,계획,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2088,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01554,EU_PSTW_202609_01554,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,National Institute for Social Security (INPS),Social protection n.e.c.,"Large public administrations interact with millions of users across multiple channels, often resulting in fragmented data and limited strategic insight.",Customer Relations Management system evolution to support decision-making,National Institute for Social Security (INPS); Central-Government; Social protection n.e.c.; Italy,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Large public administrations interact with millions of users across multiple channels, often resulting in fragmented data and limited strategic insight. INPS initiated the evolution of its Customer Relationship Management system to consolidate user interactions and support more proactive service delivery. Stakeholders include INPS service departments, case officers, management and citizens. The objective is to harmonise data collection across channels, improve monitoring and enable personalised services. The project focuses on integrating all interaction channels into a unified operational CRM platform, complemented by the gradual development of an analytical CRM layer. This includes reporting dashboards, monitoring tools and segmentation models that classify users based on needs, behaviours and life events. The approach follows an iterative and incremental development model, with early releases delivered to operational offices. Expected impacts include improved decision-making, more consistent service provision, better anticipation of user needs and long-term efficiency gains through data-driven service design.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,F07_DATA_VISIBILITY_INTEGRATION_GAP,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2089,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01555,EU_PSTW_202609_01555,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Consell of Formentera,"General economic, commercial and labour affair","Formentera, a small island with limited carrying capacity, needed a mechanism to better distribute visitor flows and reduce overcrowding in highly frequented areas, while engaging both tourists and residents in sustainable behaviour.",Balancing tourist capacity via blockchain in Formentera,"Consell of Formentera; Local Government; General economic, commercial and labour affair; Spain","This use case addresses that challenge by involving the island council, tourism authorities, local businesses, residents, and visitors, with the objective of promoting more balanced tourism patterns and sustainable use of space. The solution is implemented through a decentralised incentive system based on blockchain and smart contracts, which integrates real-time data from the island’s Tourism Intelligence System via oracles.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Tourist destinations with fragile ecosystems face increasing pressure to balance economic benefits from tourism with the protection of natural resources and quality of life for residents. Formentera, a small island with limited carrying capacity, needed a mechanism to better distribute visitor flows and reduce overcrowding in highly frequented areas, while engaging both tourists and residents in sustainable behaviour. This use case addresses that challenge by involving the island council, tourism authorities, local businesses, residents, and visitors, with the objective of promoting more balanced tourism patterns and sustainable use of space. The solution is implemented through a decentralised incentive system based on blockchain and smart contracts, which integrates real-time data from the island’s Tourism Intelligence System via oracles. Based on current capacity and congestion levels, incentives are dynamically issued to encourage visits to less crowded areas or specific activities, such as museums, events, or public transport routes, with transactions recorded on the Alastria network. The approach improves transparency, traceability, and trust in capacity management, enables data-driven redistribution of tourist flows, and enhances visitor experience while protecting sensitive areas. It supports sustainable tourism, reduces pressure on overcrowded sites, and provides a scalable governance model for managing destination carrying capacity.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2091,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01556,EU_PSTW_202609_01556,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Monastery of Santa María La Real,Religious and other community services.,UNKNOWN,Secure visitor management with blockchain,Monastery of Santa María La Real; Community led; Religious and other community services.; Spain,This solution introduces a digital monitoring system to improve visitor management while certifying implemented security measures.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Managing visitor flows in heritage and tourism destinations requires balancing safety, capacity control and preservation of cultural assets. This solution introduces a digital monitoring system to improve visitor management while certifying implemented security measures. The platform integrates telematic capacity control, real-time flow monitoring and environmental indicators such as air quality into a unified management dashboard. Blockchain technology is used to certify compliance with safety protocols and provide tamper-resistant records of operational measures. Information panels and alert mechanisms support on-site staff in preventing overcrowding and mitigating risk situations. Analytical modules generate indicators on visitor behaviour, impact and asset conditions, enabling evidence-based management decisions. The system enhances transparency for authorities and stakeholders while improving safety and visitor experience. By combining monitoring, certification and analytics, the solution supports sustainable tourism management and scalable governance of high-demand heritage sites.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2092,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01557,EU_PSTW_202609_01557,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Trento,Transport,UNKNOWN,Innovation and Digital Transition Service – Mobility and Urban Regeneration Project,Municipality of Trento; Local Government; Transport; Italy,This project aims to develop predictive models capable of forecasting traffic peaks on main urban thoroughfares using integrated transport data.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Effective urban regeneration depends on accurate understanding of mobility patterns and congestion dynamics across public and private transport networks. This project aims to develop predictive models capable of forecasting traffic peaks on main urban thoroughfares using integrated transport data. Data from bus circulation systems and roadside sensors are aggregated into a central analytical platform. Machine learning models analyse temporal patterns, traffic density and mobility behaviour to anticipate congestion events in advance. The system supports scenario analysis and informs traffic management interventions or infrastructure planning decisions. By integrating heterogeneous data sources, the model enhances situational awareness for urban planners and mobility managers. Expected outcomes include reduced congestion, improved public transport coordination and more efficient urban space utilisation. The initiative supports data-driven urban regeneration strategies that align mobility planning with sustainability and quality-of-life objectives.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2094,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01558,EU_PSTW_202609_01558,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Network Rail Limited,Transport,UNKNOWN,Improving trackside worker safety,Network Rail Limited; Private sector; Transport; United Kingdom,"Wearable devices connected to mobile and cloud-based systems use GPS, RFID, Wi-Fi or cellular signals to define virtual boundaries around safe working zones.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Railway maintenance activities expose trackside workers to significant safety risks, particularly when operating near live rail environments. Network Rail is exploring geofencing technology as an additional safeguard to reinforce existing safety protocols. Wearable devices connected to mobile and cloud-based systems use GPS, RFID, Wi-Fi or cellular signals to define virtual boundaries around safe working zones. When a worker approaches or crosses a predefined geofence, the device issues an immediate alert, providing a real-time warning. The system operates alongside established procedures rather than replacing them, adding an extra layer of situational awareness. Data from the platform can also support post-incident analysis and safety planning. The solution aims to reduce accidental incursions into hazardous areas, enhance compliance with safe working limits and improve overall workforce protection. By leveraging location-based technologies, the initiative strengthens preventative safety culture within rail infrastructure operations.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2096,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01559,EU_PSTW_202609_01559,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Bombas Gens Centre d’Arts Digitals,Cultural services.,UNKNOWN,Metaverse-based centre for exibitions in Valencia,Bombas Gens Centre d’Arts Digitals; Non-governmental; Cultural services.; Spain,"In Valencia, Bombas Gens Centre d’Arts Digitals was established as a large-scale digital arts venue designed to host immersive exhibitions using extended reality and related technologies across more than 2,500 square metres of space.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Cultural venues increasingly seek new formats that can attract audiences through immersive and interactive experiences rather than relying solely on traditional exhibition models. In Valencia, Bombas Gens Centre d’Arts Digitals was established as a large-scale digital arts venue designed to host immersive exhibitions using extended reality and related technologies across more than 2,500 square metres of space. The objective is to expand cultural engagement through experimental exhibition formats, create new visitor experiences, and position the venue as a leading site for digital and immersive arts. The implementation combines multiple technological layers, including extended reality, virtual reality, augmented reality, holography, large-format projection, and AI-enabled interactive elements, within four exhibition halls equipped with more than 45 projectors and described by the venue as containing Europe’s largest metaverse room. At a conceptual level, the centre integrates immersive hardware, digital content production, and interactive exhibition design to support large-scale cultural experiences that blend art and emerging technologies. The impact includes richer visitor engagement, broader possibilities for experimental programming, and a scalable platform for immersive cultural production. It also creates strategic value by strengthening Valencia’s profile in digital arts and experience-based exhibition design.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2097,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01560,EU_PSTW_202609_01560,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Bizkaia Regional Council,Transport,"Maintaining road safety requires frequent inspection of infrastructure such as signs, markings, barriers, and work zones, yet manual inspections are costly, infrequent, and expose personnel to risk. The Bizkaia Regional Council faced the challenge of improving road asset monitoring while increasing coverage and responsiveness.",Autonomous Road Inspection for Bizkaia Regional Council,Bizkaia Regional Council; Regional Government; Transport; Spain,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Maintaining road safety requires frequent inspection of infrastructure such as signs, markings, barriers, and work zones, yet manual inspections are costly, infrequent, and expose personnel to risk. The Bizkaia Regional Council faced the challenge of improving road asset monitoring while increasing coverage and responsiveness. The Autonomous Road Inspector addresses this need by enabling continuous, automated assessment of road conditions. Stakeholders include regional authorities, infrastructure operators, and road users. The objective is to enhance safety, optimise maintenance, and support proactive road management. The solution deploys vehicle-mounted sensing systems that capture visual and environmental data during normal traffic operations. Automated analysis identifies damaged or missing signs, degraded markings, unsafe barriers, construction zone conditions, and hazards such as slippery surfaces. Predictive functions support early detection of deterioration and risk. The impact includes more frequent inspections, reduced operational costs, improved worker safety, and faster response to hazards. The pilot demonstrates how automated monitoring can modernise road management, improve public safety, and deliver scalable benefits for regional infrastructure authorities.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2098,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01561,EU_PSTW_202609_01561,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Provincia Autonoma di Trento,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.",UNKNOWN,Public AI Challenge - Hub Innovazione Trentino - Overtourism detection,"Provincia Autonoma di Trento; Regional Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Italy",This use case addresses the need for data-driven forecasting to support sustainable tourism management.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Overtourism places pressure on environmentally sensitive areas, leading to congestion, ecological damage, and reduced quality of life for residents and visitors. The Province of Trento sought tools to anticipate tourism peaks and manage mobility proactively. This use case addresses the need for data-driven forecasting to support sustainable tourism management. Stakeholders include regional authorities, transport planners, local communities, and visitors. The objective is to detect and predict overcrowding in specific areas and support timely mitigation measures. The solution integrates heterogeneous datasets, including road traffic data, mobile network activity, and weather information, to estimate visitor volumes and characterise tourism patterns. Data preparation and harmonisation enable effective analysis, while predictive models identify days and locations at high risk of overtourism. The impact includes improved forecasting accuracy, better traffic management decisions, and reduced environmental pressure on sensitive areas. The initiative supports evidence-based tourism governance and demonstrates how advanced analytics can help balance economic activity with environmental protection and quality of life.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2099,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01562,EU_PSTW_202609_01562,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Messina,Waste management.,UNKNOWN,Municipality of Messina pilots the Recycleye robot to select garbage and improve reciclying,Municipality of Messina; Local Government; Waste management.; Italy,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Messinaservizi Bene Comune partnered with Recycleye to deploy an AI-managed robotic waste sorting system at the Pace sorting centre, the first of its kind in Sicily. Municipal waste management requires precise separation of materials to improve recycling rates and reduce landfill reliance. The Recycleye robot combines computer vision with robotic manipulation to identify and pick specific material classes from waste streams. Trained visual recognition models classify items based on material characteristics, while automated robotic arms perform continuous sorting operations around the clock. The system integrates into existing processing lines, increasing throughput and consistency. By enhancing sorting granularity and operational continuity, the robot improves material recovery rates, reduces labour-intensive tasks and optimises cost efficiency. The deployment strengthens circular economy practices and supports data-driven waste management within the municipality.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2100,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01563,EU_PSTW_202609_01563,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,GETEC ENERGIE GmbH,Fuel and energy,The ETIBLOGG project addresses this challenge by enabling local electricity trading among prosumers and consumers.,ETIBLOGG – Local Energy Trading in Green Microgrids,GETEC ENERGIE GmbH; Consortium; Fuel and energy; Germany,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The transition to decentralised energy systems requires new market mechanisms that allow small-scale producers and consumers to trade electricity efficiently without relying on central intermediaries. In local microgrids, existing market structures often make trading small quantities of energy economically unattractive. The ETIBLOGG project addresses this challenge by enabling local electricity trading among prosumers and consumers. Stakeholders include households, small businesses, and local energy communities. The objective is to make peer-to-peer energy trading economically viable and support decentralised renewable generation. The solution establishes a digital trading framework for microgrids, enabling automated settlement of small energy transactions and transparent coordination between participants. Trading rules and transactions are recorded in a shared digital infrastructure, reducing transaction costs and administrative complexity. The impact includes increased local consumption of renewable energy, improved economic returns for small producers, and reduced reliance on centralised energy markets. ETIBLOGG contributes to more resilient, decentralised energy systems and supports the development of local green grids as part of the future energy landscape.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2101,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01564,EU_PSTW_202609_01564,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Allgäuer Überlandwerk GmbH,Fuel and energy,The Pebbles project addresses this challenge by designing a sustainable and economically viable local energy market within a microgrid.,Pebbles – Peer-to-Peer Electricity Trading in Local Microgrids,Allgäuer Überlandwerk GmbH; Consortium; Fuel and energy; Germany,"Transactions between participants are securely recorded and settled using distributed ledger mechanisms, ensuring transparency and trust without central intermediaries.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Local energy systems with high shares of renewable generation face regulatory and market barriers that limit direct trading between small producers and consumers. In regions such as Wildpoldsried, where many households already generate renewable electricity, surplus energy is often fed into central grids instead of being exchanged locally. The Pebbles project addresses this challenge by designing a sustainable and economically viable local energy market within a microgrid. Stakeholders include households, small and medium-sized producers, prosumers, local companies, and distribution network operators. The objective is to establish a replicable model for regional electricity markets that strengthens local value creation and energy autonomy. The solution implements a regional digital trading platform that enables peer-to-peer exchange of green electricity within the microgrid. Transactions between participants are securely recorded and settled using distributed ledger mechanisms, ensuring transparency and trust without central intermediaries. Market rules support flexible pricing and real-time balancing of supply and demand. The project demonstrates improved local consumption of renewable energy, increased economic participation of small actors, and reduced grid dependency. Pebbles delivers a scalable blueprint for decentralised energy markets, supporting the transition towards resilient, community-based energy systems.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2102,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01565,EU_PSTW_202609_01565,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Madrid,General public services n.e.c.,"Large-scale online participation platforms can suffer from information overload: thousands of proposals and comments make it difficult for citizens to find relevant initiatives, build consensus, and understand debates, while public officials struggle to synthesise input into actionable insights.",Decide Madrid - Citizen Participation and Machine Learning,City of Madrid; Local Government; General public services n.e.c.; Spain,"Research using Madrid’s Decide Madrid platform (built on Consul) examined how to improve discovery, deliberation, and responsiveness so that participation remains meaningful as volume grows. The approach applies natural language processing and machine learning to suggest proposals users may wish to support, cluster citizens by shared interests, summarise comment threads, and assist with drafting or aggregating responses and refined proposals.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Large-scale online participation platforms can suffer from information overload: thousands of proposals and comments make it difficult for citizens to find relevant initiatives, build consensus, and understand debates, while public officials struggle to synthesise input into actionable insights. Research using Madrid’s Decide Madrid platform (built on Consul) examined how to improve discovery, deliberation, and responsiveness so that participation remains meaningful as volume grows. Stakeholders include citizens submitting proposals and comments, city administrators responsible for engagement and policy feedback, and platform maintainers seeking scalable moderation and analysis tools, with the objective of improving user experience and the quality of participation outcomes. The approach applies natural language processing and machine learning to suggest proposals users may wish to support, cluster citizens by shared interests, summarise comment threads, and assist with drafting or aggregating responses and refined proposals. This is achieved by processing the text corpus of proposals and comments, extracting semantic representations, and using similarity, clustering, and summarisation techniques that can be integrated into the platform interface as recommender, navigation, and analyst-support features. Reported evaluations indicate these methods can address participation barriers by reducing noise, improving navigability, and helping users and administrators focus on salient themes, thereby increasing the practicality of digital democracy tools without requiring proportional increases in staffing.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S7_GENERATIVE_CONTENT_CREATION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2103,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01566,EU_PSTW_202609_01566,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Heinrich Heine University Düsseldorf,R&D general public services.,"The results show improved capacity to handle large-scale participation processes, reduced administrative burden and more evidence-based policy insights.",AI in Evaluating Citizens’ Contributions,Heinrich Heine University Düsseldorf; Academic-Research; R&D general public services.; Germany,"The approach applies computational text analysis to identify duplicate submissions, cluster contributions by topic and extract deeper insights such as arguments, sentiment and stakeholder perspectives.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Public administrations increasingly rely on citizen participation processes, yet analysing large volumes of written contributions remains time-consuming and inconsistent. This research-based pilot explores how natural language processing can support public decision-making by structuring and analysing citizen input. Stakeholders include public authorities, policymakers and citizens participating in consultations. The objective is to improve the efficiency, fairness and depth of analysis of public contributions while maintaining transparency. The approach applies computational text analysis to identify duplicate submissions, cluster contributions by topic and extract deeper insights such as arguments, sentiment and stakeholder perspectives. Large collections of textual submissions serve as the primary data source, enabling scalable analysis that complements human review rather than replacing it. The results show improved capacity to handle large-scale participation processes, reduced administrative burden and more evidence-based policy insights. While exploratory, the pilot highlights how advanced language analysis can strengthen democratic participation by ensuring that citizen voices are systematically considered in policymaking processes.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2104,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01567,EU_PSTW_202609_01567,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Deutsches Museum von Meistwerken der Naturwissenschaft und Technik (AdöR),Cultural services.,UNKNOWN,Mission KI - Museum-exhibition services,Deutsches Museum von Meistwerken der Naturwissenschaft und Technik (AdöR); Local Government; Cultural services.; Germany,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The German Museum Bonn initiated the Mission KI project to enhance public understanding of artificial intelligence through interactive exhibition installations. As part of a thematic reorientation of its AI forum, the museum sought to demonstrate the functioning of artificial neural networks in an accessible manner. The project involves acquiring and developing multi-part media demonstrations illustrating how machine learning systems improve performance through data-driven training. Interactive installations visualise single-layer and multi-layer neural network processes, translating complex computational concepts into engaging educational experiences. The exhibition integrates audiovisual displays and interactive components that allow visitors to observe learning mechanisms and model behaviour. By combining scientific content with immersive media, the project strengthens science communication and digital literacy. It enables visitors to grasp abstract AI principles through experiential learning while maintaining academic rigour and educational clarity.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2105,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01568,EU_PSTW_202609_01568,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,TenneT TSO B.V.,Fuel and energy,UNKNOWN,Controlling and balancing public power grids with AI: TenneT usecase,TenneT TSO B.V.; Central-Government; Fuel and energy; Netherlands,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"TenneT initiated a Dynamic Purchasing System to renew and enhance IT systems supporting control and balancing of high-voltage electricity grids between 2021 and 2031. The energy transition increases variability in power generation and requires advanced forecasting and grid management tools. The tender focuses on expertise in EMS/SCADA systems, algorithm development and tool design to support real-time grid operations. Data analytics and optimisation algorithms are integrated into control platforms to monitor system stability, forecast imbalances and coordinate balancing actions. Enhanced analytical capabilities improve responsiveness to fluctuations in supply and demand, supporting reliable grid performance. By modernising operational technology and incorporating advanced modelling tools, TenneT strengthens grid resilience and efficiency while facilitating integration of renewable energy sources.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2106,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01569,EU_PSTW_202609_01569,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Vlaamse Auditautoriteit, Europese Structuurfondsen",General public services n.e.c.,"Traditional auditing relies on periodic manual sampling, which may limit timeliness and coverage.",Computer-related and AI services for CATE project,"Vlaamse Auditautoriteit, Europese Structuurfondsen; Regional Government; General public services n.e.c.; Belgium","The project seeks a private partner to develop innovative approaches using artificial intelligence, machine learning and data mining to assess management and control systems.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Flemish Audit Authority launched the CATE project to modernise audit processes through advanced data analytics and automation. Traditional auditing relies on periodic manual sampling, which may limit timeliness and coverage. The project seeks a private partner to develop innovative approaches using artificial intelligence, machine learning and data mining to assess management and control systems. The solution aims to integrate continuous data monitoring, anomaly detection and predictive analytics into audit workflows. By processing large datasets from administrative systems, the platform supports early identification of irregularities and evidence-based conclusions. The collaboration framework fosters co-development of digital auditing methodologies. The initiative enhances efficiency, improves risk detection and strengthens oversight capabilities within public financial management.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2107,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01570,EU_PSTW_202609_01570,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Eisenbahn-Bundesamt,Transport,"Variability in weather, lighting and surrounding infrastructure creates significant challenges for reliable perception.",DZSF – Object Detection and Classification for Automated Rail Driving,Eisenbahn-Bundesamt; Central-Government; Transport; Germany,"The approach integrates multi-sensor data, such as cameras, radar and LiDAR, and applies machine learning models trained on extensive, curated datasets to identify and categorise relevant objects in track and surrounding areas.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Higher levels of rail automation require technical systems to assume tasks traditionally performed by drivers, including continuous monitoring of the track environment to detect and classify objects that may affect safety or operations. Variability in weather, lighting and surrounding infrastructure creates significant challenges for reliable perception. Stakeholders include railway operators, safety authorities, technology developers and research institutions. The objective is to advance automated rail driving by improving object detection and classification capabilities based on robust data foundations. The approach integrates multi-sensor data, such as cameras, radar and LiDAR, and applies machine learning models trained on extensive, curated datasets to identify and categorise relevant objects in track and surrounding areas. Open databases are further developed to support training, validation and benchmarking of algorithms. The system architecture is designed to interface with onboard control and decision-support modules. Expected impacts include enhanced operational safety, increased network capacity through higher automation levels and improved standardisation of perception datasets across the rail sector.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2108,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01571,EU_PSTW_202609_01571,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Bundesamt für Ausrüstung, Informationstechnik und Nutzung der Bundeswehr",UNKNOWN,"Military training simulations require high levels of visual realism to ensure effective preparation, yet conventional rendering methods may lack photorealistic detail, particularly across different spectral ranges.",Machine Learning in Simulation for Imaging Improvement,"Bundesamt für Ausrüstung, Informationstechnik und Nutzung der Bundeswehr; Academic-Research; Germany",The project applies machine learning techniques to improve visual outputs in both visible and infrared spectra.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Military training simulations require high levels of visual realism to ensure effective preparation, yet conventional rendering methods may lack photorealistic detail, particularly across different spectral ranges. Stakeholders include defence research bodies, simulation software providers and training institutions. The objective is to enhance image quality and realism in simulation environments used by the Federal Armed Forces. The project applies machine learning techniques to improve visual outputs in both visible and infrared spectra. Neural network models are trained to enhance RGB imagery and synthetically generate infrared representations, enabling more accurate thermal simulations. These models are integrated into simulation platforms such as Virtual Battlespace 4, where they augment rendering pipelines and improve scene fidelity. Expected impacts include more immersive and realistic training environments, better representation of operational conditions and increased effectiveness of simulation-based exercises.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2109,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01572,EU_PSTW_202609_01572,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"EMPRESA METROPOLITANA DE ABASTECIMIENTO Y SANEAMIENTO DE AGUAS DE SEVILLA, S.A. (EMASESA)",Water supply.,Fragmented systems can limit predictive capabilities and operational efficiency.,Water Management with AI in Seville,"EMPRESA METROPOLITANA DE ABASTECIMIENTO Y SANEAMIENTO DE AGUAS DE SEVILLA, S.A. (EMASESA); Regional Government; Water supply.; Spain","Analytical models support use cases such as demand forecasting, anomaly detection and network optimisation.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Managing the integral water cycle in large urban areas involves monitoring consumption, distribution networks and environmental conditions across diverse data sources. Fragmented systems can limit predictive capabilities and operational efficiency. Stakeholders include the municipal water utility (EMASESA), city authorities and residents. The objective is to establish a unified analytical platform to support data-driven water management. The solution comprises a cloud-based big data and machine learning platform built around a central data lake that ingests, standardises and classifies data from smart meters, operational systems and third-party sources. Analytical models support use cases such as demand forecasting, anomaly detection and network optimisation. The platform integrates with existing operational systems and enables scalable deployment of new analytics applications. Expected impacts include improved resource efficiency, reduced water losses, enhanced service reliability and data-driven planning for sustainable urban water management.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2110,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01573,EU_PSTW_202609_01573,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,La Generalitat Valenciana,General services.,Traditional digital systems may lack immutable audit trails accessible to citizens.,Digital procedures based on blockchain,La Generalitat Valenciana; Regional Government; General services.; Spain,"The solution records each stage of electronic notifications on a blockchain-based registry, certified by the regional public technology company ISTEC using the BlockchainFUE platform.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Public administrations must ensure transparency, traceability and trust in digital procedures, particularly for legally sensitive communications such as official notifications. Traditional digital systems may lack immutable audit trails accessible to citizens. Stakeholders include the regional government of Valencia, citizens, businesses and public IT entities. The objective is to strengthen transparency and trust in electronic administrative processes. The solution records each stage of electronic notifications on a blockchain-based registry, certified by the regional public technology company ISTEC using the BlockchainFUE platform. Each notification’s lifecycle—from issuance to expiration—is immutably logged, and citizens can access corresponding verification documents through their digital folders. The system integrates with existing e-government platforms while adding a distributed ledger layer to ensure integrity and traceability. Expected impacts include enhanced citizen confidence, improved evidentiary robustness in case of disputes and strengthened digital identity and governance frameworks.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2111,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01574,EU_PSTW_202609_01574,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Polytechnic University of Valencia,Pollution abatement.,UNKNOWN,DivAirCity – Citizen Engagement for Cleaner Air through Incentivisation,Polytechnic University of Valencia; Consortium; Pollution abatement.; European Union,"However, individuals often lack clear feedback, motivation, and incentives to change habits related to mobility, energy use, and lifestyle.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Improving urban air quality requires not only policy measures but also active participation from citizens in adopting greener daily behaviours. However, individuals often lack clear feedback, motivation, and incentives to change habits related to mobility, energy use, and lifestyle. DivAirCity addresses this gap by empowering citizens to actively contribute to cleaner urban environments. Stakeholders include residents, local authorities, environmental organisations, and urban communities. The objective is to promote sustainable behaviour and collective action to improve air quality at city level. The solution provides a decentralised digital application that allows users to monitor air quality, access educational content, and receive personalised incentives for environmentally friendly actions. Transparent recording of actions and rewards builds trust and accountability within the community. The platform fosters engagement by linking individual behaviour to shared environmental outcomes. The project increases public awareness, motivates behavioural change, and supports data-driven environmental governance. DivAirCity demonstrates how citizen-centric digital platforms can strengthen community involvement, improve environmental outcomes, and contribute to healthier, more sustainable cities.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2113,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01575,EU_PSTW_202609_01575,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Split,Economic affairs n.e.c,UNKNOWN,Web3 Quadratic Funding for Public Infrastructure,City of Split; Local Government; Economic affairs n.e.c; Croatia,"Launched in September 2023, the initiative builds on open-source quadratic funding infrastructure used in other public-goods contexts, using blockchain-based tools to manage project submissions, record contributions, and calculate allocations according to quadratic funding rules (where many small contributions can outweigh a few large ones).",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Municipal budgeting can be vulnerable to low engagement and lobbying dynamics that prioritise narrow interests over broadly supported local needs. The City of Split piloted a quadratic funding approach to help allocate resources to community projects in a way that reflects both breadth of support and intensity of preference, aiming to create a fairer and more transparent process for deciding which neighbourhood improvements receive funding. Stakeholders include the city administration, residents, civic groups proposing projects, and ecosystem partners supporting funding rounds, with the objective of increasing participation and producing funding allocations that better match collective preferences. Launched in September 2023, the initiative builds on open-source quadratic funding infrastructure used in other public-goods contexts, using blockchain-based tools to manage project submissions, record contributions, and calculate allocations according to quadratic funding rules (where many small contributions can outweigh a few large ones). Smart contracts and public ledgers support auditable accounting, configurable round parameters, and transparent disbursement logic, while administrative tooling helps the city run rounds and verify outcomes. The pilot demonstrates how verifiable transactions and rule-based allocation can reduce administrative friction, strengthen trust, and encourage wider civic participation, with a model that could be replicated by other municipalities if governance and legal considerations are addressed.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2114,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01576,EU_PSTW_202609_01576,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""social_nonprofit""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,SILO.AI,R&D general public services.,UNKNOWN,PORO an LLM for Finland,SILO.AI; Academic-Research; R&D general public services.; Finland,"The models are trained using large multilingual datasets and high-performance computing resources from the LUMI supercomputer, combined with a dedicated software stack optimised for large-scale training.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"European public institutions, researchers and companies increasingly rely on language technologies, yet most large language models are developed outside Europe and inadequately support smaller European languages. The PORO initiative was launched to strengthen European digital sovereignty by providing open, high-quality language models trained on European data. Led by startup SiloGen, in collaboration with the University of Turku and HPLT, the project aims to democratise access to advanced language models while ensuring alignment with European values, research openness and linguistic diversity. The first PORO model, a 34-billion-parameter system, focuses on English, Finnish and programming code, with plans to expand coverage to all official European languages. The models are trained using large multilingual datasets and high-performance computing resources from the LUMI supercomputer, combined with a dedicated software stack optimised for large-scale training. Open-source checkpoints enable reuse, fine-tuning and transparent evaluation by public and private actors. The initiative delivers strategic benefits by reducing dependency on non-European providers, improving language coverage for public services and research, and enabling innovation across sectors. PORO establishes a scalable foundation for sovereign European language infrastructure.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2115,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01577,EU_PSTW_202609_01577,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,CINECA,R&D general public services.,UNKNOWN,"Modello Italia, the first Italian Foundational Large Language Model",CINECA; Academic-Research; R&D general public services.; Italy,It will be released under an open-source licence and deployed within controlled infrastructures to meet high standards of privacy and governance.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"European public administrations and enterprises require advanced language technologies aligned with national regulatory, privacy and security standards. Dependence on externally developed models may raise concerns regarding data sovereignty and compliance. Stakeholders include iGenius, Cineca, public administrations, regulated industries and research institutions. The objective is to develop a foundational large language model tailored to Italian and European institutional and business contexts. The model is designed as a large-scale neural network trained on several trillion tokens, with a focus on secure, compliant and domain-relevant applications in sectors such as healthcare, finance and national security. It will be released under an open-source licence and deployed within controlled infrastructures to meet high standards of privacy and governance. Expected impacts include strengthened technological sovereignty, improved access to generative language capabilities for public and private sectors and enhanced alignment with European regulatory frameworks.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2116,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01578,EU_PSTW_202609_01578,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Ministry of Justice,Law courts,"Early findings indicate improved drafting speed, clearer public-facing explanations and reduced administrative burden for routine tasks.",Usage of Generative Ai in Spain justice,Ministry of Justice; Central-Government; Law courts; Spain,"Systems are deployed as decision-support tools rather than automated decision-makers, ensuring that judges and clerks remain fully responsible for outcomes.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Judicial systems face growing workloads, complex legal texts and increasing expectations for timely communication with citizens. In Spain, pilot initiatives have explored how generative language technologies can support judicial professionals without compromising legal safeguards. Stakeholders include courts, judicial staff, justice ministries and citizens seeking accessible legal information. The objective is to improve internal efficiency and external communication while maintaining judicial independence and accountability. The pilots, discussed within the framework of the European Commission for the Efficiency of Justice (CEPEJ), focus on two main applications: assisting communication with citizens on legal procedures and summarising lengthy judicial documents. Language models are applied to structured legal texts and official documentation, with strict human oversight and defined usage boundaries. Systems are deployed as decision-support tools rather than automated decision-makers, ensuring that judges and clerks remain fully responsible for outcomes. Early findings indicate improved drafting speed, clearer public-facing explanations and reduced administrative burden for routine tasks. At the same time, limitations related to accuracy, bias and explainability are explicitly acknowledged. The pilots contribute to European best practices by clarifying where generative tools add value in justice systems and where legal or ethical constraints require careful restriction.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2117,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01579,EU_PSTW_202609_01579,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Vël'Ok,Pollution abatement.,UNKNOWN,Vël'OK: Cycling for Survcoins,Vël'Ok; Community led; Pollution abatement.; Luxembourg,"After registering via a mobile application, users earn tokens automatically when using shared electric bicycles.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Local authorities seek practical ways to encourage sustainable mobility and reduce carbon emissions while engaging citizens directly in climate action. In southern Luxembourg, nine municipalities operating the Vël’OK bicycle-sharing scheme introduced Survcoin, a digital reward mechanism designed to incentivise cycling. Stakeholders include municipal authorities, the Vël’OK operator CIGL and registered bicycle users. The objective is to promote low-emission transport while experimenting with alternative, community-based incentive models. Survcoin operates as a blockchain-based virtual currency awarded to users based on kilometres cycled between Vël’OK stations. After registering via a mobile application, users earn tokens automatically when using shared electric bicycles. Importantly, no location tracking is used; distance is calculated by extrapolating trips between stations, protecting user privacy. Blockchain infrastructure ensures transparent issuance, traceability and protection against manipulation. Users can redeem Survcoins within participating local circuits. The pilot demonstrates increased engagement with cycling services and raises awareness of sustainable mobility. It shows how distributed ledger technologies can support local climate objectives, foster behavioural change and enable trust-based incentive systems without intrusive data collection.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2118,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01580,EU_PSTW_202609_01580,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Aleph Alpha,R&D general public services.,UNKNOWN,Generative Ai for German Public Administration,Aleph Alpha; Academic-Research; R&D general public services.; Germany,"Systems are deployed in controlled environments, typically hosted within Germany to meet sovereignty requirements.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"German public administrations face increasing demand for faster, clearer and more consistent public services amid staffing constraints and growing volumes of written communication. Federal, state and municipal authorities have begun piloting generative language technologies to support administrative tasks. Stakeholders include federal ministries, regional governments, municipalities and civil servants. The objective is to improve service quality and productivity while maintaining data protection, legal certainty and public trust. These pilots, often developed with startup Aleph Alpha, focus on tasks such as summarising documents, drafting internal notes, supporting correspondence and assisting research. Systems are deployed in controlled environments, typically hosted within Germany to meet sovereignty requirements. Outputs are reviewed by civil servants, and usage policies define acceptable applications. Early results show time savings in text-heavy processes, improved consistency in communication and better support for staff handling complex information. The pilots also surface organisational and skills-related requirements, informing broader digital transformation strategies. While not yet operational at scale, the initiatives provide valuable insights into how generative technologies can responsibly support public administration without automating decision-making or compromising accountability.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2119,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01581,EU_PSTW_202609_01581,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""social_nonprofit""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"BiDA - Lab, Universidad Autónoma de Madrid (UAM)",R&D general public services.,"The results demonstrate that domain-adapted models can achieve strong performance in classifying specialised documents, significantly reducing manual effort. It also identifies challenges related to multilingual performance and domain specificity, providing a methodological foundation for future operational deployments in public affairs analysis.",Leveraging Large Language Models for Topic Classification in the Domain of Public Affair,"BiDA - Lab, Universidad Autónoma de Madrid (UAM); Academic-Research; R&D general public services.; Spain","A dataset of over 33,000 public affairs documents, comprising more than 22.5 million tokens, was compiled using automated collection techniques.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Public affairs documents shape regulation, compliance and democratic oversight, yet their volume and complexity make systematic analysis challenging for both public institutions and private organisations. This research pilot investigates how large language models can support the classification of public affairs texts. Stakeholders include policymakers, regulatory analysts, companies and researchers. The objective is to improve transparency and accessibility by enabling structured analysis of large document collections. A dataset of over 33,000 public affairs documents, comprising more than 22.5 million tokens, was compiled using automated collection techniques. The task involves multi-label classification across up to 30 policy topics, reflecting the real-world complexity of regulatory texts. Several Spanish-language language models were evaluated under different configurations. The results demonstrate that domain-adapted models can achieve strong performance in classifying specialised documents, significantly reducing manual effort. The study highlights the potential of language models to support regulatory monitoring, policy analysis and corporate compliance. It also identifies challenges related to multilingual performance and domain specificity, providing a methodological foundation for future operational deployments in public affairs analysis.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2120,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01582,EU_PSTW_202609_01582,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,AIACQUA,Waste water management.,"Water utilities need better visibility over the condition and performance of distribution systems, yet smaller operators in particular often lack integrated tools for monitoring, maintenance planning, and rapid response to leaks, interruptions, or inefficient network behaviour. In South Tyrol, AiAqua addresses this challenge by offering a digital platform and app designed to help operators keep their water-distribution systems under closer and more continuous control.",Water monitoring using AI in South Tyrol,AIACQUA; Academic-Research; Waste water management.; Italy,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Water utilities need better visibility over the condition and performance of distribution systems, yet smaller operators in particular often lack integrated tools for monitoring, maintenance planning, and rapid response to leaks, interruptions, or inefficient network behaviour. In South Tyrol, AiAqua addresses this challenge by offering a digital platform and app designed to help operators keep their water-distribution systems under closer and more continuous control. The objective is to improve the efficiency and sustainability of water-network management, reduce waste and service interruptions, and support better operational and investment decisions. The implementation combines a mobile application with a software-as-a-service platform that includes network-modelling functions, heuristic optimisation, machine learning, and data-analysis components. At a conceptual level, the system brings together monitoring data from measurement devices, alarms, images, notes, and network-configuration information to support day-to-day operational oversight and longer-term planning. The impact includes improved water saving, energy efficiency, and maintenance management, alongside better support for renovation planning and network resilience. It also creates value by giving small and medium-sized operators access to a more scalable and integrated water-management capability than they might otherwise be able to build independently.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2121,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01583,EU_PSTW_202609_01583,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Landesbank Baden-Württemberg (LBBW),Economic affairs n.e.c,UNKNOWN,Crypto Custody Services offered by a Federal bank,Landesbank Baden-Württemberg (LBBW); Regional Government; Economic affairs n.e.c; Germany,"The implementation relies on Bitpanda’s investment-as-a-service and custody infrastructure, with LBBW using the partner’s platform, licences, and technical stack as the basis for its own client-facing offering. At a conceptual level, the solution combines regulated banking relationships with specialised digital-asset infrastructure to create a compliant custody model for institutional use.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Large regulated banks are increasingly under pressure to respond to institutional demand for digital-asset services while maintaining compliance, custody security, and integration with established banking operations. In Germany, Landesbank Baden-Württemberg entered into a partnership with Bitpanda to offer crypto custody and procurement services, primarily for corporate clients, reflecting an effort to extend traditional financial infrastructure into regulated digital-asset activity. The objective is to give clients access to secure storage and procurement of assets such as bitcoin and ether through a bank-grade environment, while allowing the bank to remain competitive in a market where digital-asset capabilities are becoming more relevant. The implementation relies on Bitpanda’s investment-as-a-service and custody infrastructure, with LBBW using the partner’s platform, licences, and technical stack as the basis for its own client-facing offering. At a conceptual level, the solution combines regulated banking relationships with specialised digital-asset infrastructure to create a compliant custody model for institutional use. The impact includes expanded service scope for corporate clients, stronger integration of digital assets into regulated finance, and a more scalable route for a large bank to enter crypto custody without building the full stack itself.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2122,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01584,EU_PSTW_202609_01584,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Road Authority Norway (VTS),Transport,UNKNOWN,AI for improvement of AID systems,Road Authority Norway (VTS); Central-Government; Transport; Norway,Video feeds from tunnel cameras are processed using machine learning models trained to distinguish genuine incidents from benign visual variations.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Norwegian highway tunnels rely on Automatic Incident Detection (AID) systems to alert control centres to dangerous events, yet approximately 90 % of alerts are false positives due to misinterpretation of visual changes such as shadows or lighting variations. This pilot project investigates whether advanced image analysis techniques can improve detection accuracy while reusing existing technical infrastructure. Video feeds from tunnel cameras are processed using machine learning models trained to distinguish genuine incidents from benign visual variations. The enhanced system analyses contextual patterns rather than simple pixel changes, reducing misclassification. The pilot assessed integration with current monitoring equipment and evaluated performance during live operation. Results from the test period were highly positive, indicating a significant reduction in false alarms. Expected impacts include improved operational efficiency at traffic control centres, faster response to real incidents and enhanced tunnel safety through more reliable automated detection.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2123,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01585,EU_PSTW_202609_01585,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Trento Museum of Natural Sciences,Protection of biodiversity and landscape.,"Glaciers are critical natural resources and indicators of climate change, yet monitoring their evolution at scale remains complex, resource-intensive, and fragmented. The approach significantly reduces manual analysis effort, improves consistency of measurements across regions, and enables large-scale, repeatable monitoring.",AI-based model to track the impact of the climate crisis on glacial resources,Trento Museum of Natural Sciences; Non-governmental; Protection of biodiversity and landscape.; Italy,"This use case addresses these needs by supporting climate monitoring through systematic analysis of satellite imagery, involving public administrations, researchers, and environmental agencies. The solution is implemented through advanced image analysis models that process satellite data to automatically identify glacier boundaries, generate polygon representations, and detect equilibrium lines and their elevation.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Glaciers are critical natural resources and indicators of climate change, yet monitoring their evolution at scale remains complex, resource-intensive, and fragmented. Public authorities and research institutions require accurate, automated tools to track changes in glacial extent and structure in order to assess long-term impacts on water availability and environmental stability. This use case addresses these needs by supporting climate monitoring through systematic analysis of satellite imagery, involving public administrations, researchers, and environmental agencies. The objective is to improve the accuracy and timeliness of glacier monitoring to better understand the effects of the climate crisis. The solution is implemented through advanced image analysis models that process satellite data to automatically identify glacier boundaries, generate polygon representations, and detect equilibrium lines and their elevation. These models analyse temporal image sequences to identify changes in glacial mass and structure, integrating geospatial data into environmental monitoring systems. The approach significantly reduces manual analysis effort, improves consistency of measurements across regions, and enables large-scale, repeatable monitoring. It provides more reliable evidence for climate policy, supports water resource planning, and strengthens decision-making by delivering timely, data-driven insights into glacial retreat and its environmental consequences.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2124,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01586,EU_PSTW_202609_01586,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Jyväskylä,Community development.,Traditional consultation methods can limit participation and make it difficult for residents to visualise proposed changes.,"UrbanistAI, a generative AI platform for participatory planning and co-design: Jyväskylä use case",City of Jyväskylä; Local Government; Community development.; Finland,"To address this, the city organised a participatory workshop using a generative design platform to facilitate collaborative ideation.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The City of Jyväskylä sought to revitalise public spaces in its city centre and reimagine the future design of the main high street through a more inclusive planning process. Traditional consultation methods can limit participation and make it difficult for residents to visualise proposed changes. To address this, the city organised a participatory workshop using a generative design platform to facilitate collaborative ideation. The platform employs generative image models to transform textual prompts and stakeholder inputs into visual urban design alternatives in real time. Participants explored multiple scenarios for streetscape improvements, public space functions and spatial layouts, supported by moderated discussion. The system enabled rapid iteration and comparison of design concepts, enhancing mutual understanding between citizens and planners. The workshop generated structured qualitative feedback and visual outputs to inform subsequent planning stages. The initiative strengthened civic engagement, improved transparency in early-stage design and demonstrated how interactive visual technologies can support more responsive and creative urban regeneration strategies.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2125,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01587,EU_PSTW_202609_01587,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Narva,Community development.,Conventional planning processes often struggle to engage younger demographics effectively.,"UrbanistAI, a generative AI platform for participatory planning and co-design: Narva use case",City of Narva; Local Government; Community development.; Estonia,"To address this, a three-step participatory workshop series was organised using a generative design platform to stimulate co-creation. The system uses generative image models to translate participants’ ideas into alternative urban visions, enabling students to visualise proposals for public spaces and neighbourhood improvements.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The City of Narva aimed to make urban spaces more attractive and liveable for young people by actively involving students in shaping the city’s future development. Conventional planning processes often struggle to engage younger demographics effectively. To address this, a three-step participatory workshop series was organised using a generative design platform to stimulate co-creation. The system uses generative image models to translate participants’ ideas into alternative urban visions, enabling students to visualise proposals for public spaces and neighbourhood improvements. Workshops combined guided discussion with interactive scenario generation, allowing iterative refinement of concepts. Facilitators structured inputs to align creative exploration with planning constraints. The approach fostered dialogue, empowered youth participation and produced tangible visual material to support municipal decision-making. The initiative enhanced inclusivity in urban planning, strengthened community ownership of development processes and demonstrated the potential of digital co-design tools to engage underrepresented groups in shaping more vibrant and sustainable cities.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2126,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01588,EU_PSTW_202609_01588,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""social_nonprofit""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Technical University of Munich (TUM),Transport,UNKNOWN,Basis of the digitised highway of the future: Highway Real-Time Digital Twin - Providentia++,Technical University of Munich (TUM); Consortium; Transport; Germany,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Managing increasingly complex traffic systems requires real-time situational awareness and predictive capabilities. Providentia++ expanded an existing test field by installing five additional networked stations equipped with over 75 sensors and high-performance computing units across motorway, rural and urban environments. Fibre optic and radio communication technologies connect decentralised processing units capable of handling approximately 20 Gbps of raw sensor data in real time. Advanced computer vision and data fusion algorithms detect anonymised traffic objects, track their trajectories and synchronise multi-modal sensor inputs to generate a dynamic digital twin of traffic conditions. The entire software chain—from object recognition to service interface distribution—was further developed and validated in live operation. Demonstrations showcased integration into virtual environments and driver assistance applications for predictive driving and precise congestion forecasting. The system enhances traffic monitoring accuracy, supports value-added mobility services and provides a scalable foundation for intelligent transport infrastructure and future automated driving ecosystems.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2128,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01589,EU_PSTW_202609_01589,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Università di Pisa,Housing development.,UNKNOWN,BUILDCHAIN - BUILDing knowledge book in the blockCHAIN distributed ledger - School buildings in Florence pilot,Università di Pisa; Consortium; Housing development.; Italy,"In Florence, a pilot within the BUILDCHAIN project applies the Digital Building LogBook platform to a portfolio of school buildings.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Municipal authorities require comprehensive data to improve seismic resilience, safety and energy efficiency of public buildings. In Florence, a pilot within the BUILDCHAIN project applies the Digital Building LogBook platform to a portfolio of school buildings. Existing data from structural surveys, material testing, seismic performance assessments and safety indices are logged within a decentralised knowledge graph supported by blockchain technology to ensure traceability and data integrity. Analytical tools process these datasets to identify priorities for seismic retrofitting and support intervention planning, including classroom management during works. The platform also integrates escape route management and safety monitoring features. For energy optimisation, smart meters, smart plugs and environmental sensors collect operational data to inform efficiency improvements with minimal intrusion. By consolidating structural and operational information, the pilot enhances evidence-based asset management, supports safer learning environments and enables strategic planning for resilience and sustainability in municipal school infrastructure.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2138,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01590,EU_PSTW_202609_01590,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Universidad de Granada,Housing development.,"Cultural heritage buildings face increasing risks from ageing, pollution, and climate-related stress, while maintenance decisions are often reactive and fragmented across institutions.","BUILDCHAIN - Building knowledge book in the blockCHAIN distributed ledger - ""Hospital Real"" in Granada pilot",Universidad de Granada; Consortium; Housing development.; Spain,"The solution develops a Digital Building Logbook that aggregates structural, environmental, and historical data using a distributed ledger to ensure integrity and traceability.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Cultural heritage buildings face increasing risks from ageing, pollution, and climate-related stress, while maintenance decisions are often reactive and fragmented across institutions. The BUILDCHAIN project addresses the need for proactive, data-driven management of public and heritage buildings. In the “Hospital Real” pilot in Granada, stakeholders include municipalities, heritage managers, engineers, and conservation experts. The objective is to improve long-term preservation through integrated monitoring, predictive maintenance, and transparent documentation. The solution develops a Digital Building Logbook that aggregates structural, environmental, and historical data using a distributed ledger to ensure integrity and traceability. Sensor data on variables such as moisture, pollution, and vibration are combined with predictive degradation models to forecast deterioration processes. Hybrid analytical models and advanced system representations simulate the impact of inspection and maintenance actions. The project enables proactive interventions, reduces long-term maintenance costs, and improves resilience of heritage assets. BUILDCHAIN delivers a scalable framework for managing public buildings, transforming maintenance from reactive repair to predictive, evidence-based stewardship.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2139,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01591,EU_PSTW_202609_01591,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,ZAG,Housing development.,UNKNOWN,BUILDCHAIN - BUILDing knowledge book in the blockCHAIN distributed ledger - Hospital building in Slovenia pilot,ZAG; Consortium; Housing development.; Slovenia,"This BUILDCHAIN pilot applies the Digital Building LogBook methodology to a new hospital in Ljubljana, integrating Building Information Modelling (BIM) with decentralised data management.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Designing technologically advanced healthcare facilities requires coordinated management of complex construction, cost and performance data. This BUILDCHAIN pilot applies the Digital Building LogBook methodology to a new hospital in Ljubljana, integrating Building Information Modelling (BIM) with decentralised data management. The project standardises priced bills of quantities (PBoQ) and links them to BIM components through a structured Model Breakdown Structure, ensuring traceability across design phases. The BIM model evolves from conceptual to detailed design and construction, synchronised with cost management processes. Structural health monitoring sensors are incorporated to account for sensitivity of medical equipment to minor displacements. Blockchain-based infrastructure supports secure logging of lifecycle data and interoperability between stakeholders. The approach enhances transparency, improves cost control and strengthens structural performance monitoring. The pilot demonstrates how integrated BIM and distributed ledger technologies can support efficient management of complex hospital infrastructure throughout its lifecycle.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2140,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01592,EU_PSTW_202609_01592,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Sztaki,Housing development.,UNKNOWN,BUILDCHAIN - BUILDing knowledge book in the blockCHAIN distributed ledger - Multi-storey timber buildings,Sztaki; Consortium; Housing development.; United Kingdom,Experimental forced vibration test data are collected and processed to compute modal properties using established analytical tools.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The structural behaviour of tall timber buildings remains an emerging field requiring enhanced monitoring and modelling approaches. This BUILDCHAIN pilot validates integration between the Digital Building LogBook, BIM models and a hybrid digital twin interface for three cross-laminated timber buildings in the United Kingdom and Norway. Experimental forced vibration test data are collected and processed to compute modal properties using established analytical tools. These structural performance indicators are logged within the BUILDCHAIN platform and linked to corresponding BIM elements. An application programming interface aggregates logged data to generate hybrid digital twins reflecting actual structural behaviour. Comparative analysis across the three buildings supports refinement of conceptual design procedures for timber structures. The pilot enhances traceability of structural performance data, supports evidence-based design improvements and demonstrates how decentralised data management combined with digital twins can strengthen confidence in innovative construction methods.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2141,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01593,EU_PSTW_202609_01593,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Estonian Government,General services.,UNKNOWN,KSI blockchain-backed e-Residency in Estonia,Estonian Government; Central-Government; General services.; Estonia,"At a conceptual level, the approach does not use blockchain as a public ledger for transactions, but as an integrity layer that helps ensure records are tamper-evident and auditable while preserving privacy. The impact includes stronger confidence in digital entrepreneurship, improved traceability of data use, and greater resilience of key state systems that support e-Residency.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Cross-border digital entrepreneurship depends on trust in how identities, records, and transactions are secured, especially when users rely on government-backed systems without being physically present in the country. Estonia’s e-Residency programme was built to enable entrepreneurs to interact with the state digitally, and its security model incorporates Keyless Signature Infrastructure to strengthen accountability, integrity, and trust in the underlying digital environment. The objective is to support secure digital business activity, protect data and transactions, and allow users to see how their personal information is used within government systems. The implementation combines digital identity infrastructure with blockchain-based integrity technologies, including KSI, which is used within Estonia’s wider digital state architecture to secure data exchanges, log files, and critical systems. At a conceptual level, the approach does not use blockchain as a public ledger for transactions, but as an integrity layer that helps ensure records are tamper-evident and auditable while preserving privacy. The impact includes stronger confidence in digital entrepreneurship, improved traceability of data use, and greater resilience of key state systems that support e-Residency. It also creates public value by reinforcing the credibility of a fully digital state-backed business environment.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2142,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01594,EU_PSTW_202609_01594,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Centre of Registers and Information Systems (RIK),"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.",UNKNOWN,Estonian National Gazette with KSI-blockchain,"Centre of Registers and Information Systems (RIK); Central-Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Estonia","The implementation uses blockchain as one security layer within the Gazette’s protection model, integrating hash values of published XML laws and regulations together with hash values of digitally signed versions into a cryptographic chain.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"When a country relies entirely on a digital legal gazette as the sole official publication of laws and regulations, the integrity of that platform becomes critical, because any unauthorised change could undermine legal certainty across the whole system. In Estonia, the electronic National Gazette has been the only official publication point for laws and regulations since 1 June 2010, creating a need for especially strong safeguards against tampering. The objective is to ensure the trustworthiness and verifiability of the legal record by detecting any unauthorised alteration to published content as quickly as possible. The implementation uses blockchain as one security layer within the Gazette’s protection model, integrating hash values of published XML laws and regulations together with hash values of digitally signed versions into a cryptographic chain. At a conceptual level, this enables regular validation of the information published on the portal and immediate notification if unauthorised changes occur. The impact includes stronger confidence in the authenticity of legal information, improved resilience of a critical public register, and a more robust mechanism for ongoing verification in a fully digital legal-publication environment. It also creates value by showing how blockchain can support trust and integrity in government records without being the entire application architecture.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2144,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01595,EU_PSTW_202609_01595,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,European Space Agency (ESA),Military defence.,UNKNOWN,EOGuard - KSI-blockchain to protect earth observation data,European Space Agency (ESA); European Institution/Agency; Military defence.; European Union,"The solution is implemented using Guardtime’s Keyless Signature Infrastructure (KSI) blockchain, which generates cryptographic proofs of data integrity, sequence, and time across the entire data lifecycle.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Earth observation data is increasingly critical for environmental monitoring, security, and scientific research, yet its growing volume and complexity expose it to risks such as tampering, corruption, and loss of provenance. Space agencies, data providers, and downstream users require strong guarantees that data has not been altered throughout its lifecycle. The objective of EOGuard is to ensure trust, integrity, and traceability of Earth observation data without disrupting existing mission infrastructures. The solution is implemented using Guardtime’s Keyless Signature Infrastructure (KSI) blockchain, which generates cryptographic proofs of data integrity, sequence, and time across the entire data lifecycle. From data acquisition and processing to distribution and access, integrity proofs are automatically created and verified, independently of data storage formats or satellite missions. The system integrates with existing databases and workflows with minimal overhead, ensuring compatibility and scalability. EOGuard provides continuous assurance against accidental corruption, malicious interference, or security breaches. The impact is increased trust in Earth observation products, reduced risk for critical decision-making, and improved resilience of space data infrastructures. It enables secure reuse of data at scale, supports regulatory and scientific confidence, and delivers long-term value by safeguarding sensitive and mission-critical information.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2145,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01596,EU_PSTW_202609_01596,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Elering,Fuel and energy,UNKNOWN,Elering AS and WePower pilot project to test large scale tokenization of energy data on the blockchain in Estonia,Elering; Central-Government; Fuel and energy; Estonia,Elering AS and WePower conducted a pilot to test large-scale tokenisation of energy data using blockchain technology within Estonia’s national infrastructure.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Transparent and efficient energy markets require secure exchange of consumption and production data, yet traditional systems limit traceability and cross-platform integration. Elering AS and WePower conducted a pilot to test large-scale tokenisation of energy data using blockchain technology within Estonia’s national infrastructure. WePower integrated its smart contract-enabled green energy trading platform with Elering’s Estfeed data exchange system, creating a proof-of-concept for nation-scale tokenisation. Energy production and consumption data were recorded on a distributed ledger, enabling secure, tamper-resistant representation of energy units as digital tokens. Smart contracts facilitated automated settlement and transparent tracking of green energy transactions. The pilot demonstrated feasibility of blockchain-based data management at system-operator scale. Expected benefits include enhanced transparency, streamlined trading processes and strengthened support for renewable energy markets. The initiative illustrates how distributed ledger integration can modernise national energy data ecosystems and promote sustainable energy adoption.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2146,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01597,EU_PSTW_202609_01597,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Elektrilevi (ELV),Fuel and energy,UNKNOWN,CyberSEAS (Cybersecurity in the Electrical Power and Energy System) - Estonian pilot,Elektrilevi (ELV); Central-Government; Fuel and energy; Estonia,"Guardtime’s MIDA technology, based on KSI blockchain infrastructure, was implemented to provide tamper-evident verification and secure key management.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Electrical power and energy systems rely on operational technology devices whose firmware updates are typically supplied by manufacturers, creating potential vulnerabilities if update sources or repositories are compromised. The Estonian CyberSEAS pilot aimed to safeguard the integrity of device configurations within Elektrilevi, Estonia’s largest distribution system operator, by ensuring that only authorised firmware could be installed. The solution introduced a centralised control mechanism requiring all firmware updates to be cryptographically signed and validated before deployment. Guardtime’s MIDA technology, based on KSI blockchain infrastructure, was implemented to provide tamper-evident verification and secure key management. The central management system automatically rejected unauthorised or unsigned firmware versions, preventing accidental or malicious installation. By embedding cryptographic assurance into update processes, the pilot strengthened configuration control and reduced cyber risk exposure. The initiative demonstrates how distributed ledger-based integrity mechanisms can enhance resilience of critical infrastructure, supporting secure and trustworthy operation of national energy systems.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2147,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01598,EU_PSTW_202609_01598,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,University of Piraeus Research Centre,Public health services.,UNKNOWN,CUREX - seCUre and pRivate hEalth data eXchange,University of Piraeus Research Centre; Academic-Research; Public health services.; Greece,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Healthcare providers increasingly exchange sensitive patient data across organisational boundaries, exposing them to cybersecurity and privacy risks. The CUREX project aimed to enable healthcare institutions to assess realistic cyber threats and implement mathematically optimised mitigation strategies tailored to specific applications. The project developed a decentralised architecture incorporating a private blockchain infrastructure to ensure integrity and traceability of risk assessment processes and inter-organisational data exchanges. Advanced risk modelling tools analyse vulnerabilities and recommend safeguard configurations aligned with regulatory and operational requirements. The blockchain layer records transactions and assessment outcomes in a tamper-resistant manner, enhancing accountability among stakeholders. Privacy-preserving mechanisms and secure communication protocols protect sensitive information throughout the lifecycle of data sharing. The solution supports informed decision-making regarding cybersecurity investments and compliance measures. By combining quantitative risk analysis with secure distributed infrastructure, CUREX enhances trust, resilience and data protection in cross-border healthcare ecosystems.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2148,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01599,EU_PSTW_202609_01599,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Madrid,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.","Large municipal administrations process high volumes of internal documents and procedures, and tracing the status and history of those records across departments can be difficult without stronger mechanisms for transparency and integrity.",Madrid City Council uses blockchain to trace documents within the administration,"City of Madrid; Local Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Spain","In Madrid, this use case was developed to make it easier to verify the progress of administrative procedures and trace documents safely within the City Council’s workflow. The implementation uses a blockchain-based document-traceability solution developed with iCommunity, designed to record and verify progress through administrative procedures in a secure and transparent manner.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Large municipal administrations process high volumes of internal documents and procedures, and tracing the status and history of those records across departments can be difficult without stronger mechanisms for transparency and integrity. In Madrid, this use case was developed to make it easier to verify the progress of administrative procedures and trace documents safely within the City Council’s workflow. The objective is to improve transparency, strengthen confidence in document handling, and streamline technical and administrative processes inside the municipal administration. The implementation uses a blockchain-based document-traceability solution developed with iCommunity, designed to record and verify progress through administrative procedures in a secure and transparent manner. At a conceptual level, the solution applies distributed-ledger principles to document workflow events so that records of movement and status can be checked more reliably across the administration. The project description also refers to the use of iCommunity’s utility token in parts of the technical and administrative process, although the core public-sector value lies in the traceability and verification layer. The impact includes more fluid document management, stronger procedural transparency, and improved efficiency in administrative execution. It also creates value by giving a large city administration a more scalable way to monitor and verify document flows.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F06_HIGH_VOLUME_SCALABILITY_BOTTLENECK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2149,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01600,EU_PSTW_202609_01600,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,FUELICS,General public services n.e.c.,"Urban parking management relies on accurate real-time data, yet sensor-based systems are vulnerable to environmental changes that degrade accuracy and require costly manual recalibration. The Swarmchestrate project addresses this operational challenge by enabling self-organising sensor networks that adapt autonomously.",Swarmchestrate EU project - Parking occupancy magnetometer sensor threshold recalibration,FUELICS; Private sector; General public services n.e.c.; Greece,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Urban parking management relies on accurate real-time data, yet sensor-based systems are vulnerable to environmental changes that degrade accuracy and require costly manual recalibration. In particular, magnetometer-based parking sensors are affected by natural fluctuations in the Earth’s magnetic field. The Swarmchestrate project addresses this operational challenge by enabling self-organising sensor networks that adapt autonomously. Stakeholders include municipalities, mobility service providers, and urban infrastructure operators. The objective is to maintain reliable parking occupancy detection while reducing maintenance effort and downtime. The solution connects distributed parking sensors into a coordinated network where decentralised orchestration agents analyse collective magnetic flux data. When abnormal behaviour is detected, sensors automatically recalibrate and reconfigure themselves without manual intervention. Secure coordination and data integrity are ensured through decentralised trust mechanisms and privacy-preserving analytics. The project improves data accuracy, reduces operational costs, and increases system resilience. Swarmchestrate demonstrates how self-organising sensor swarms can support scalable, adaptive urban infrastructure management and provide insights into broader environmental phenomena.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2150,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01601,EU_PSTW_202609_01601,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,UST,General public services n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,Swarmchestrate EU project - Digital Twin of Natural Habitat,UST; Private sector; General public services n.e.c.; Spain,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Managing complex environmental systems requires distributed computing approaches capable of processing real-time data across heterogeneous infrastructures. Within the Swarmchestrate framework, a demonstrator explores the creation of a digital twin of natural habitats supported by decentralised orchestration mechanisms. Interdependent computational units, organised as self-organising swarms, are coordinated by distributed orchestration agents operating within a dynamic orchestration space. Blockchain-based trust mechanisms, self-sovereign identities and advanced cryptographic methods ensure secure interaction and persistent user engagement. Real-time environmental data streams update the virtual representation, creating a persistent and interactive metaverse environment reflecting physical ecosystem changes. Privacy-preserving analytics protect user and environmental data while enabling collaborative exploration. The demonstrator tests scalability, interoperability and resilience of decentralised orchestration across cloud-to-edge infrastructures. By integrating digital twin modelling with secure distributed intelligence, the project advances immersive environmental monitoring and lays groundwork for large-scale, real-time virtual representations of natural systems.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2151,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01602,EU_PSTW_202609_01602,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Agrinio,General services.,"Titormos addresses limited accessibility and inefficiencies in citizen engagement with municipal services, where requests could previously only be submitted via phone during restricted hours. This constrained responsiveness and excluded citizens with limited availability or access to traditional channels.",Titormos - the citizens assist chatbot in the Municipality of Agrinio,Municipality of Agrinio; Regional Government; General services.; Greece,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Titormos addresses limited accessibility and inefficiencies in citizen engagement with municipal services, where requests could previously only be submitted via phone during restricted hours. This constrained responsiveness and excluded citizens with limited availability or access to traditional channels. The objective is to provide a more accessible, efficient, and inclusive interface for citizens to obtain information and submit service requests. The solution implements a conversational interface capable of handling structured requests and free-form queries, supported by natural language processing and integration with municipal service databases. It captures user inputs, generates request tickets, and provides confirmations, while also enabling future integration with administrative and HR systems for analytics and reporting. A voice-based interface extends accessibility to users without digital access. The impact includes improved accessibility, faster response times, and enhanced citizen satisfaction. It also enables better data collection, operational efficiency, and scalability of municipal services, supporting more responsive and data-informed governance.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2152,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01603,EU_PSTW_202609_01603,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Eindhoven,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.",Municipal decision-making often struggles to engage large numbers of citizens in meaningful deliberation processes.,Deliberations @ Scale: Large-scale high-engagement citizen participation with generative AI,"Municipality of Eindhoven; Local Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Netherlands","The Common Ground mobile application, developed in Eindhoven through a consortium of start-ups and local institutions, aims to scale citizen consultations using digital facilitation.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Municipal decision-making often struggles to engage large numbers of citizens in meaningful deliberation processes. The Common Ground mobile application, developed in Eindhoven through a consortium of start-ups and local institutions, aims to scale citizen consultations using digital facilitation. The platform integrates an automated moderation system that structures discussions, summarises viewpoints and prompts balanced exchanges among participants. Natural language processing models analyse contributions in real time, grouping similar arguments and identifying emerging themes. The application has been tested in three consultation processes, evaluating usability and engagement levels. By lowering participation barriers and structuring dialogue, the system supports inclusive and organised civic debate. Future plans include national expansion and integration of moderated consultations into formal decision-making workflows. The initiative demonstrates how digital facilitation tools can enhance democratic participation, increase transparency and strengthen evidence-based policymaking through large-scale, structured citizen input.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2153,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01604,EU_PSTW_202609_01604,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Azienda Provinciale per i Servizi Sanitari di Trento (APSS),R&D health.,UNKNOWN,Predicting Image Quality Degradation in Digital Radiology,Azienda Provinciale per i Servizi Sanitari di Trento (APSS); Local Government; R&D health.; Italy,"The objectives of the project are two-fold: automating processing of radiological image, conducting images analysis using in-house software macros and bash script and discovering trends on an ongoing basis with the aim of detecting and fixing DR system performance problems before clinical operations are affected. The second step was developing a predictive model for assessing the degradation state of DR system using the automatically images-processed database.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Optimal design of medical x-ray imaging systems is essential for delivering maximum image quality while minimizing radiation risk to patients. The performances of imaging devices could degrade over time. For this reason, quality control (QC) programs are in place in the health systems to verify that the clinical image quality remains acceptable over the years guaranteeing the diagnostic capability. The Medical Physicists (MP) is the responsible for the QC program, ensuring high standards in medical diagnostics and maintaining the reliability and safety of medical imaging. Currently MPs perform constancy quality checks on Digital Radiography (DR) system, acquiring radiological images on phantoms and extracting specific information (e.g., non-uniformity). The results are then compared to baseline values and any deviation is evaluated. In such scenario the QC tests only provide a “snapshot” of the system performance at a specific moment over time, until the next inspection or service event not allowing to anticipate malfunctions. The objectives of the project are two-fold: automating processing of radiological image, conducting images analysis using in-house software macros and bash script and discovering trends on an ongoing basis with the aim of detecting and fixing DR system performance problems before clinical operations are affected. Performance characteristics of 19 DR systems from 5 manufacturers have been analyzed over 10 years of monthly and annually QC test. Study has been focused on non-uniformity test, based on 4 image parameters and their tolerances: Local signal non-uniformity, Global Signal non-uniformity, Local Signal to Noise Ratio non-uniformity, Global Signal to Noise Ratio non-uniformity. 1825 images were analyzed running the in-house software macros and bash script on each image to calculate the 4 baseline metrics. The second step was developing a predictive model for assessing the degradation state of DR system using the automatically images-processed database. The model has to predict the pass/no-pass label for each image. The building process involves leveraging the combined strengths of Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs) and Long Short-Term Memory Networks (LSTMs) to analyze sequential radiological images from a images-processed database, grouping images into sequences for effective analysis akin to video sampling. By analyzing the sequential radiological images, this model aims to preemptively identify trends in equipment degradation. This capability is crucial for maintaining high standards in medical diagnostics, ensuring reliability and safety in medical imaging and patient care. The CNN architecture, based on ResNet-18, efficiently extracted spatial features, while the LSTM component, incorporating temporal data, was crucial in detecting patterns of degradation over time. Training and validation of the model were conducted on cloud based machine-learning platform, providing the necessary computational resources and scalability. Data augmentation strategies were implemented to balance the dataset, particularly addressing the scarcity of no-pass labels, thereby enhancing the model’s predictive capability. The model demonstrated a consistent training loss, indicating effective learning, and achieved a validation accuracy of 88.2% after 100 epochs, showcasing its ability to accurately classify the condition of DR systems. This level of accuracy reflects the model’s strong performance and its potential to generalize well to new data. In summary, in this project we proposed a new approach to QC test, starting from the availability of images and data stored through 10 years of activity. A predictive tool, based on AI-model was developed for non-uniformity QC test, with the result of decreasing the interval to detection of substandard performance. This type of approach, in which the primary goal is to preemptively identify trends in equipment degradation, can change the QC scenario.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2154,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01605,EU_PSTW_202609_01605,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Senatskanzlei Hamburg,General public services n.e.c.,"Public administrations in Germany face persistent challenges in digitising internal procedures, often relying on fragmented, manual, or paper-based processes that slow service delivery and burden staff. Developing custom IT solutions is costly and time-consuming, especially for smaller authorities.",MODUL-F: empowering public administration through low-code/no-code,Senatskanzlei Hamburg; Regional Government; General public services n.e.c.; Germany,MODUL-F is implemented as a cloud-based software-as-a-service platform using a low-code/no-code approach.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Public administrations in Germany face persistent challenges in digitising internal procedures, often relying on fragmented, manual, or paper-based processes that slow service delivery and burden staff. Developing custom IT solutions is costly and time-consuming, especially for smaller authorities. MODUL-F addresses this challenge by enabling administrations at federal, state, and local levels to digitise internal specialist procedures quickly and consistently. The objective is to support end-to-end digital workflows in line with the Online Access Act (OZG) while reducing dependency on bespoke software development. MODUL-F is implemented as a cloud-based software-as-a-service platform using a low-code/no-code approach. Administrative staff can create digital procedures by assembling pre-programmed, reusable modules that represent common administrative functions and rules. These modules can be shared across organisations, ensuring standardisation and interoperability. The platform is rule-based, allowing consistent execution of procedures without complex programming. As a result, administrations can digitise processes faster, lower development and maintenance costs, and improve consistency across jurisdictions. MODUL-F increases scalability, empowers non-technical staff, and accelerates digital transformation, delivering measurable efficiency gains and supporting sustainable modernisation of public administration.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2155,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01606,EU_PSTW_202609_01606,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Province of Fryslân and Rijks ICT Gilde,Environmental protection n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,"""Responsible use of AI""- pilot project to enhahnce environmental monitoring within the Province of Fryslân in collaboration with Rijks ICT Gilde and the Z-Inspection® Initiative",Province of Fryslân and Rijks ICT Gilde; Local Government; Environmental protection n.e.c.; Netherlands,The Province of Fryslân conducted a pilot between May 2022 and January 2023 to evaluate a deep learning model designed to map heathland grasslands using satellite imagery.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Environmental monitoring is essential for biodiversity protection, water management and climate mitigation, yet integrating advanced analytics into public administration requires careful governance. The Province of Fryslân conducted a pilot between May 2022 and January 2023 to evaluate a deep learning model designed to map heathland grasslands using satellite imagery. In collaboration with Rijks ICT Gilde and the Z-Inspection® Initiative, the project assessed technical performance, ethical implications and governance considerations. The model processed Earth observation data to classify vegetation patterns within nature reserves, supporting preservation efforts. Evaluation frameworks examined transparency, accountability and potential biases in the algorithm’s outputs. The pilot contributed to structured guidelines for responsible deployment of advanced analytics in environmental contexts. Outcomes include improved mapping accuracy, strengthened institutional capacity and enhanced oversight mechanisms, demonstrating how innovation and ethical assessment can be combined in public-sector digital transformation.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2156,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01607,EU_PSTW_202609_01607,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,VLIZ,R&D environmental protection.,UNKNOWN,MareGraph: Towards an interoperable marine knowledge graph,VLIZ; Consortium; R&D environmental protection.; Belgium,"The MAREGRAPH project will provide an open linked data production and publication of three high impact datasets in the marine domain (the World Register of Marine Species (WoRMS), Marine Regions and EurOBIS (the European Node of the international Ocean Biodiversity Information System) using state of the art technologies.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The aim of MAREGRAPH is to support the EU and its Member States in the achievement of ambitious marine themed objectives by semantically enriching, interlinking and providing high value and foundational datasets. These currently power the “effective biodiversity and conservation knowledge products” of today and will enable building the knowledge products of the future, where knowledge graphs and AI closely interact to support the marine public sector in making and building open knowledge on marine biodiversity. The MAREGRAPH project will provide an open linked data production and publication of three high impact datasets in the marine domain (the World Register of Marine Species (WoRMS), Marine Regions and EurOBIS (the European Node of the international Ocean Biodiversity Information System) using state of the art technologies. This will allow the onboarding of essential marine datasets in the Common European Data Spaces. As such MAREGRAPH will provide a structural component in the digital transition of the marine landscape.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2157,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01608,EU_PSTW_202609_01608,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Flanders Digital Agency,R&D environmental protection.,UNKNOWN,"Parcel Monitoring Platform Flanders, an AI-based geo-imaging tool for data-driven policy formulation and evaluation",Flanders Digital Agency; Regional Government; R&D environmental protection.; Belgium,"Public authorities increasingly need continuous, parcel-level intelligence to support decisions in agriculture, energy, spatial planning, and environmental management, yet raw satellite imagery alone is often too noisy and technically demanding for direct policy use. The objective is to detect relevant changes in land use and parcel conditions more effectively and to give agencies evidence that can support operational monitoring and Green Deal-related policy tasks.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Public authorities increasingly need continuous, parcel-level intelligence to support decisions in agriculture, energy, spatial planning, and environmental management, yet raw satellite imagery alone is often too noisy and technically demanding for direct policy use. In Flanders, Digitaal Vlaanderen and VITO developed the Parcel Monitoring Platform to provide more precise, timely, and actionable monitoring of land parcels in support of data-driven policy formulation and evaluation. The objective is to detect relevant changes in land use and parcel conditions more effectively and to give agencies evidence that can support operational monitoring and Green Deal-related policy tasks. The implementation combines AI and geo-imaging techniques with Sentinel time series, aerial imagery, GIS layers, and field data. Raw satellite images are pre-processed in Terrascope, while the CropSAR time-series service allows users to access derived Sentinel-1 and Sentinel-2 parameters for specific parcels without performing the image processing themselves. At a conceptual level, the platform uses data fusion and change-detection workflows to generate parcel-level notifications relevant to specific policy use cases. The impact includes better monitoring coverage, faster detection of meaningful changes, and stronger support for evidence-based public action. It also improves scalability by turning complex earth-observation data into reusable services for multiple governmental domains.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2158,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01609,EU_PSTW_202609_01609,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Centre de Telecomunicacions i Tecnologies de la Informació de la Generalitat de Catalunya (CTTI),Law courts,"The time dedicated by judges to search for precedents and legal foundations to write a sentence is high and an impediment to being more agile in the drafting of sentences, causing a high response time and a sense of slow justice. Currently, the location of paragraphs of interest of legal or procedural references, of any documentary information or the entirety of the magistrates' own judgments or resolutions previously issued, which may be applicable or reusable in the drafting of new judgments or resolutions, becomes a manual and costly process that causes magistrates to spend a lot of time locating, remembering, and reviewing previous judgments, relying on their memory, both for their content and the date they were issued.","AI4Justice, Intelligent assistant to support judges in drafting sentences by locating texts of previous judgments and jurisprudence",Centre de Telecomunicacions i Tecnologies de la Informació de la Generalitat de Catalunya (CTTI); Regional Government; Law courts; Spain,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The time dedicated by judges to search for precedents and legal foundations to write a sentence is high and an impediment to being more agile in the drafting of sentences, causing a high response time and a sense of slow justice. Currently, the location of paragraphs of interest of legal or procedural references, of any documentary information or the entirety of the magistrates' own judgments or resolutions previously issued, which may be applicable or reusable in the drafting of new judgments or resolutions, becomes a manual and costly process that causes magistrates to spend a lot of time locating, remembering, and reviewing previous judgments, relying on their memory, both for their content and the date they were issued. In Catalonia, the judge usually performs a manual search in the procedural management application for justice (eJusticia.cat), in the archive of the magistrate's own judgments, or through the Judicial Documentation Center (CENDOJ), depending on the General Council of the Judiciary (CGPJ), as well as other repositories or jurisprudential databases. The search is more or less effective depending on what the magistrate remembers at that time from similar cases or parts of an old judgment that may be applicable to the current one. The unmet need is to locate reusable information within judicial documents, such as sentences issued in any instance and jurisdiction, in order to speed up and facilitate the drafting of new sentences by judges. Currently, the location of paragraphs of interest (headings, legal foundations, dispositive part or judgment), legal or procedural references or the entirety of the judges' previous sentences, that are applicable or reusable in the drafting of new sentences, becomes a manual and costly process that causes judges to spend a lot of time locating, remembering and reviewing previous judgments, based on memory, both of their content and of the date they were issued. The solution proposed is an assistant to judges and magistrates that, by entering an open text or prompt, can quickly, accurately, and relatedly obtain all applicable historical information for the sentence being drafted. In the same way, the assistant must provide the applicable legal foundations and sentence draft for a better management of mass litigation cases with hundreds of very similar claims accumulate that has also similar resolutions. It is important that the judicial powers have the necessary resources to ensure agile and efficient justice, such as having digital tools that facilitate the location of reusable information from previous sentences and jurisprudence to draft new sentences.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2159,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01610,EU_PSTW_202609_01610,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Spanish Ministry of the Presidency, Justice and Parliamentary Relation",Law courts,"By combining secure digital workflows with tamper-evident audit trails, EVID enhances accessibility, reduces travel and administrative burdens and supports environmentally sustainable justice services while maintaining procedural security and evidential robustness.",Bringing Justice closer to citizens through digitalisation: Virtual Desktop of Digital Immediation,"Spanish Ministry of the Presidency, Justice and Parliamentary Relation; Central-Government; Law courts; Spain",UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Spanish Ministry of the Presidency, Justice and Parliamentary Relations launched the Virtual Desktop of Digital Immediation (EVID) in 2020 to facilitate remote judicial hearings while ensuring full legal guarantees. Courts needed a secure digital mechanism to conduct proceedings remotely without compromising procedural integrity, identity verification or evidentiary reliability. EVID provides a virtual environment enabling remote participation of judges, lawyers and citizens, integrating secure videoconferencing, document exchange and procedural management tools. To ensure non-repudiation, non-manipulation and non-impersonation, the system anchors key procedural events on a blockchain infrastructure. Cryptographic hashing and timestamping mechanisms record actions, documents and consents in an immutable distributed ledger, guaranteeing traceability and integrity throughout each hearing. Encrypted identity verification components support reliable participant authentication. By combining secure digital workflows with tamper-evident audit trails, EVID enhances accessibility, reduces travel and administrative burdens and supports environmentally sustainable justice services while maintaining procedural security and evidential robustness.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2160,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01611,EU_PSTW_202609_01611,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Autonomous Entitat Autònoma del Diari Oficial i de Publications (EADOP),Law courts,"Legal texts are essential to public life, yet their technical language often makes them difficult for citizens to understand, limiting access to rights, obligations, and public information.",Application of Generative AI to the Generalitat de Catalunya to facilitate citizens' understanding of legal texts,Autonomous Entitat Autònoma del Diari Oficial i de Publications (EADOP); Regional Government; Law courts; Spain,The implementation uses generative AI to produce unofficial summaries of Catalan legal rules in plain language for publication on the Legal Portal.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Legal texts are essential to public life, yet their technical language often makes them difficult for citizens to understand, limiting access to rights, obligations, and public information. In Catalonia, the Entitat Autònoma del Diari Oficial i de Publicacions and the Legal Portal of Catalonia address this issue by making plain-language summaries of legal rules available to the public, with the aim of improving comprehension, accessibility, and transparency in the dissemination of current law. The objective is to reduce the barrier created by specialist legal drafting and to help citizens navigate Catalan legislation more confidently, while making clear that the official legal text remains the only authoritative source. The implementation uses generative AI to produce unofficial summaries of Catalan legal rules in plain language for publication on the Legal Portal. The initial rollout covered 44 rules in Catalan and 44 in Spanish, with the Generalitat stating that the approach would extend to the more than 14,000 legal provisions then in force; later reporting indicated that more than 16,000 summaries had been made available. The summaries are expressly presented as informational, not as legal advice. The impact includes easier public access to legal information, faster large-scale production of citizen-friendly summaries, and improved transparency and inclusiveness in legal communication, while supporting a more scalable model for making complex public information understandable.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2161,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01612,EU_PSTW_202609_01612,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Ministry of Justice and Ministry of Territorial Cohesion,General public services n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,Mapping Portugal: leveraging Machine Learning for land registration,Ministry of Justice and Ministry of Territorial Cohesion; Central-Government; General public services n.e.c.; Portugal,"Accurate land registration is a longstanding challenge in Portugal, particularly for rural and mixed-use properties where ownership information may be incomplete, fragmented, or difficult to access. The implementation combines the BUPi digital platform with machine-learning techniques that support property identification, anomaly detection, land-use classification, and faster processing of geospatial and registry-related information.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Accurate land registration is a longstanding challenge in Portugal, particularly for rural and mixed-use properties where ownership information may be incomplete, fragmented, or difficult to access. The BUPi initiative was created to modernise property management by improving the identification, georeferencing, and registration of land parcels, while also reducing regional disparities in access to information and supporting better territory management. The objective is to make property data more accessible and reliable, increase transparency, and provide a stronger evidence base for land governance, public planning, and sustainable development. The implementation combines the BUPi digital platform with machine-learning techniques that support property identification, anomaly detection, land-use classification, and faster processing of geospatial and registry-related information. At a conceptual level, the system links administrative property records with georeferenced parcel data and analytical tools so that land can be identified more precisely and processed more efficiently. Public reporting on the programme notes the scale of the task, with more than eight million land records still requiring georeferencing during the expansion phase, and highlights the use of advanced technologies to accelerate knowledge about the territory. The impact includes improved data quality, quicker land-registration processes, stronger transparency, and better support for strategic decision-making, creating a more scalable and accurate basis for understanding and managing Portuguese territory.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2162,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01613,EU_PSTW_202609_01613,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Centre de Telecomunicacions i Tecnologies de la Informació de la Generalitat de Catalunya (CTTI),Environmental protection n.e.c.,"Flood risk management requires rapid and accurate assessment of potential river overflows, yet traditional hydraulic models can be computationally intensive and slow to update.",Using Artificial Intelligence to detect and predict river overflows,Centre de Telecomunicacions i Tecnologies de la Informació de la Generalitat de Catalunya (CTTI); Regional Government; Environmental protection n.e.c.; Spain,This initiative explores the feasibility of using advanced predictive algorithms to simulate flood risks more efficiently by combining flow measurements with geospatial datasets.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Flood risk management requires rapid and accurate assessment of potential river overflows, yet traditional hydraulic models can be computationally intensive and slow to update. This initiative explores the feasibility of using advanced predictive algorithms to simulate flood risks more efficiently by combining flow measurements with geospatial datasets. The proof of concept initially focused on the Tordera river basin, integrating hydrological data, terrain models and environmental indicators into a unified analytical framework. Machine learning models were trained to estimate flood-prone areas and generate near real-time predictions of overflow scenarios. The system aims to complement conventional modelling approaches by providing faster decision-support outputs during emergency planning. Early results indicate improved computational efficiency and enhanced situational awareness. The approach supports agile emergency response, better resource allocation and scalable application to additional river basins, strengthening resilience to extreme weather events.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2163,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01614,EU_PSTW_202609_01614,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Centre de Telecomunicacions i Tecnologies de la Informació de la Generalitat de Catalunya (CTTI),R&D health.,"Timely transport of biological samples between healthcare facilities is critical for diagnostic accuracy and patient care, yet conventional road transport can be slow and subject to variability.",Application of drones for the transport of biological samples between a primary health centre and a hospital,Centre de Telecomunicacions i Tecnologies de la Informació de la Generalitat de Catalunya (CTTI); Regional Government; R&D health.; Spain,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Timely transport of biological samples between healthcare facilities is critical for diagnostic accuracy and patient care, yet conventional road transport can be slow and subject to variability. This pilot project in the Girona health region evaluated a drone-based logistics route connecting the Hospital of Olot and the Vall d’en Bas primary care centre. The system conducted recurrent flights transporting blood and urine samples to the hospital and delivering medical equipment in return. Flight operations were assessed for cost, reliability and sample integrity compared with road transport. Results showed a reduction in transport time from 20 minutes by road to 7 minutes by drone, with improved sample quality due to reduced agitation and shorter exposure duration. The solution also demonstrated increased scheduling flexibility and responsiveness for urgent deliveries. The pilot highlights the potential of unmanned aerial logistics to enhance efficiency, reliability and quality in regional healthcare supply chains.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2164,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01615,EU_PSTW_202609_01615,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Ministerio de la Presidencia, Justicia y Relaciones con las Cortes",General services.,"Justice administrations handle large volumes of repetitive administrative tasks that contribute to delays in legal procedures and increased workload for staff. The automated workflows run continuously and consistently, reducing errors and delays.",Streamlining administrative tasks through robotic process automation in the administration of Justice,"Ministerio de la Presidencia, Justicia y Relaciones con las Cortes; Central-Government; General services.; Spain","The solution is implemented through Robotic Process Automation (RPA), which automates structured, rule-based tasks across justice information systems.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Justice administrations handle large volumes of repetitive administrative tasks that contribute to delays in legal procedures and increased workload for staff. In Spain, backlogs in processes such as nationality applications and criminal record cancellations highlighted the need for operational efficiency improvements. The objective of this initiative is to streamline routine administrative activities, reduce processing times, and allow judicial staff to focus on higher-value legal work. The solution is implemented through Robotic Process Automation (RPA), which automates structured, rule-based tasks across justice information systems. Software robots handle activities such as data entry, document verification, system updates, and status checks, operating across multiple legacy systems without altering their core architecture. The automated workflows run continuously and consistently, reducing errors and delays. The impact includes faster processing of applications, improved service delivery to citizens, and reduced administrative burden on justice personnel. The initiative delivers cost savings through efficiency gains, improves reliability and transparency of procedures, and enhances the overall responsiveness of the justice system, contributing to greater public trust and institutional performance.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK|F06_HIGH_VOLUME_SCALABILITY_BOTTLENECK|F08_ERROR_COMPLIANCE_RISK,S6_WORKFLOW_PROCESS_AUTOMATION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2165,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01616,EU_PSTW_202609_01616,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Digitalstadt Darmstadt,General services.,UNKNOWN,Darmstadt im Herzen: a platform promoting sustainability and connected communities in Darmstadt,Digitalstadt Darmstadt; Local Government; General services.; Germany,"The implementation uses an app structured around three main areas: personal services, neighbourhood interaction, and city information.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Urban residents often rely on multiple disconnected applications and service channels for neighbourhood support, local information, waste collection, mobility, and community exchange, which can make everyday participation in city life less convenient and less inclusive. Darmstadt im Herzen was designed to bring these functions together in one platform in order to strengthen community ties, support sustainable behaviour, and improve citizens’ access to local services and information. The objective is to make everyday urban life easier while encouraging neighbourhood cooperation, environmental action, and stronger engagement with what is happening across the city. The implementation uses an app structured around three main areas: personal services, neighbourhood interaction, and city information. It combines practical tools such as a waste calendar and key finder with local sharing and mutual-help functions, event information, weather and webcam data, climate tips, service numbers, and sustainability incentives such as rewards for environmentally friendly mobility. At a conceptual level, the platform integrates local content, user participation, and urban-sharing features within one digital interface, while its further development has been linked to the DELTA energy-transition project in Darmstadt. The impact includes greater convenience for residents, improved access to local services and city updates, stronger neighbourhood interaction, and better support for sustainable everyday choices, offering a scalable model for digitally connected urban communities.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2166,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01617,EU_PSTW_202609_01617,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Emprego en Galicia - la Xunta de Galicia,Unemployment.,"Employment services must support jobseekers and employers in a labour market that changes quickly, where skills gaps, mismatched expectations, and fragmented information can reduce the effectiveness of matching and guidance.",EMi Smart Employment: Emi: AI at the service of job matching and skills development in Galicia,Emprego en Galicia - la Xunta de Galicia; Regional Government; Unemployment.; Spain,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Employment services must support jobseekers and employers in a labour market that changes quickly, where skills gaps, mismatched expectations, and fragmented information can reduce the effectiveness of matching and guidance. In Galicia, EMi Smart Employment was introduced to improve how the public employment system supports unemployed people, employers, and frontline staff by connecting individual profiles more closely with labour-market demand. The objective is to provide more personalised, higher-quality services, guide jobseekers towards the skills the market requires, and give employment-service professionals better tools for decision-making. The implementation combines big data and AI to analyse users’ abilities, labour-market information, and employment patterns in order to suggest relevant skills-development paths and better job matches. At a conceptual level, the platform offers tailored services to jobseekers and employers while also equipping staff in the Galician Public Employment Service with stronger analytical tools to interpret labour-market data and support case management. The system is therefore both a public-facing guidance service and an internal decision-support resource. The impact includes more personalised employability support, stronger skills alignment with market needs, and improved quality of matching and advisory services. It also enhances trust in the employment system by improving service outcomes, while creating a more scalable and data-informed approach to workforce development across the Galician employment ecosystem.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2167,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01618,EU_PSTW_202609_01618,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Barcelona Provincial Council,General services.,"Official gazettes and public-information services handle very large numbers of notices and announcements, yet preparing concise, accurate summaries of those publications manually is resource-intensive and can slow publication workflows and reduce consistency. The objective is to reduce manual effort for both submitters and staff, speed up publication processing, and improve the accessibility of official information for users of the gazette and related public-information services.",Boosting efficiency and transparency in processing of Official Publications with AI tools - AI for summaries,Barcelona Provincial Council; Local Government; General services.; Spain,The implementation uses a supervised machine-learning and neural-network system trained on a large corpus of previous publications to generate summaries automatically in Catalan or Spanish.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Official gazettes and public-information services handle very large numbers of notices and announcements, yet preparing concise, accurate summaries of those publications manually is resource-intensive and can slow publication workflows and reduce consistency. In Barcelona, the Service of the Official Provincial Gazette and Official Documentation and Publications sought to improve the efficiency and transparency of this process by automating the drafting of short summaries for official notices published in the provincial gazette. The objective is to reduce manual effort for both submitters and staff, speed up publication processing, and improve the accessibility of official information for users of the gazette and related public-information services. The implementation uses a supervised machine-learning and neural-network system trained on a large corpus of previous publications to generate summaries automatically in Catalan or Spanish. The project documentation states that the system was trained with 275,000 publications from 2013 to 2022, processes the first two pages of an announcement, and returns a summary of up to 100 words, while also recording manual edits for later retraining. The impact includes faster summary production, lower workload, improved consistency, and better scalability as publication volumes grow. It also supports transparency by making official content easier to understand and reuse within public-information services.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2168,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01619,EU_PSTW_202609_01619,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Barcelona Provincial Council,General services.,"The objective is to reduce the manual burden of classification, increase consistency across large document flows, and make it easier for users to track procedures, regulations, grants, procurement, and other official activity.",Boosting efficiency and transparency in processing of Official Publications with AI tools - AI for thematic classification,Barcelona Provincial Council; Local Government; General services.; Spain,"The implementation uses a neural-network-based deep-learning model trained on human-produced summaries and classifications from a range of official bulletins and journals. The project documentation explains that the model was trained using classified material from several official publications, including provincial bulletins, the Catalan official journal, and the national gazette.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Public administrations publish large volumes of official notices across multiple gazettes and journals, and classifying those notices by topic or procedure is essential if citizens and organisations are to find relevant information quickly. In Barcelona, the same service behind the provincial gazette and official documentation search tools introduced CIDOBOT to support the thematic classification of official publications and improve the accessibility of public information disseminated through the CIDO service. The objective is to reduce the manual burden of classification, increase consistency across large document flows, and make it easier for users to track procedures, regulations, grants, procurement, and other official activity. The implementation uses a neural-network-based deep-learning model trained on human-produced summaries and classifications from a range of official bulletins and journals. At a conceptual level, the system performs documentary analysis of incoming public information and assigns it to thematic or procedural categories such as public procurement, collective agreements, local regulations, grants, and personnel selection. The project documentation explains that the model was trained using classified material from several official publications, including provincial bulletins, the Catalan official journal, and the national gazette. The impact includes faster and more standardised classification, improved discoverability of public information, and stronger transparency for users of CIDO, while also creating a replicable and scalable model for modernising administrative information management.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2169,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01620,EU_PSTW_202609_01620,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Burgas,Water supply.,The Burgas municipality addresses this challenge by modernising water network monitoring to reduce losses and optimise asset management. Continuous automated monitoring enables faster detection than manual inspection methods.,Data-driven water infrastructure maintenance in Burgas,Municipality of Burgas; Local Government; Water supply.; Bulgaria,"Large volumes of sensor data are analysed using predictive models that distinguish normal operation from leak signatures in real time. The project delivers reduced water losses, improved maintenance planning, and more efficient use of public funds.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Ageing water infrastructure causes significant water losses, threatening sustainability, service reliability, and public resources. In some regions, leakage rates reach critical levels, requiring proactive detection and maintenance strategies. The Burgas municipality addresses this challenge by modernising water network monitoring to reduce losses and optimise asset management. Stakeholders include municipal utilities, maintenance operators, and residents. The objective is to detect leaks early, improve water supply reliability, and reduce long-term operational costs. The solution deploys vibration sensors along water pipes to continuously monitor acoustic patterns associated with leaks. Large volumes of sensor data are analysed using predictive models that distinguish normal operation from leak signatures in real time. Continuous automated monitoring enables faster detection than manual inspection methods. The project delivers reduced water losses, improved maintenance planning, and more efficient use of public funds. It strengthens resilience of water infrastructure, enhances service continuity, and supports sustainable water management, offering a replicable model for municipalities facing similar challenges across Europe.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2170,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01621,EU_PSTW_202609_01621,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Ministry of Digital Governance and Ministry of Interior,General public services n.e.c.,"The operational issue is that administrative service catalogues can be difficult for citizens, businesses and public officials to navigate when information is structured around formal terminology rather than the way users naturally express their needs.",MitosLOD: Exporting Linked Open Data from the Greek National Registry of Administrative Public Services,Ministry of Digital Governance and Ministry of Interior; Central-Government; General public services n.e.c.; Greece,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"MitosLOD enhances the Greek National Registry of Administrative Public Services by making information on public services easier to search, retrieve and understand. The operational issue is that administrative service catalogues can be difficult for citizens, businesses and public officials to navigate when information is structured around formal terminology rather than the way users naturally express their needs. The project therefore aims to improve access to the MITOS registry through more intuitive, accurate and multilingual search functions. Its implementation combines Linked Open Data, semantic technologies, natural language processing and artificial intelligence techniques to interpret the meaning and intent behind user queries. Instead of relying only on keyword matching, the semantic search engine analyses relationships between public-service descriptions and translates natural-language questions into structured, machine-readable formats. This allows the system to return more relevant results and support multilingual access to registry content. Conceptually, the solution links public-service metadata, semantic relationships, NLP query interpretation and structured retrieval over the registry. The expected impact is a better user experience, more effective discovery of administrative services and improved reuse of public-sector information. By transforming registry data into semantically searchable content, MitosLOD supports transparency, accessibility and more efficient interaction with public administration.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2171,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01622,EU_PSTW_202609_01622,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,National Transparency Authority,General public services n.e.c.,"The Greek public sector needed a more consistent and transparent way to manage disciplinary proceedings across a very large and fragmented administrative landscape. Before the introduction of a unified system, monitoring disciplinary cases across thousands of public bodies was difficult, limiting oversight, comparability and timely access to evidence for decision-making.",E-peitharxika - an integrated information system to monitor the forms of disciplinary proceedings and disciplinary cases,National Transparency Authority; Central-Government; General public services n.e.c.; Greece,"The solution is implemented as an integrated digital platform covering more than 2,000 public bodies and approximately 520,000 employees. At a conceptual level, the system consolidates case data, supports real-time statistical reporting, interoperates with national human-resources registries and exposes reusable datasets through a REST API, all on a secure, high-availability infrastructure using Azure G-Cloud services and ISO-aligned controls for security, quality and accessibility.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Greek public sector needed a more consistent and transparent way to manage disciplinary proceedings across a very large and fragmented administrative landscape. Before the introduction of a unified system, monitoring disciplinary cases across thousands of public bodies was difficult, limiting oversight, comparability and timely access to evidence for decision-making. E-peitharxika was developed to address this governance problem by creating a single point of supervision for the full lifecycle of disciplinary cases, from investigation and hearings to appeals and enforcement. The stakeholders include public bodies, disciplinary authorities, employees, policymakers and the wider public, with the objective of improving accountability, standardising oversight and strengthening trust in disciplinary justice. The solution is implemented as an integrated digital platform covering more than 2,000 public bodies and approximately 520,000 employees. At a conceptual level, the system consolidates case data, supports real-time statistical reporting, interoperates with national human-resources registries and exposes reusable datasets through a REST API, all on a secure, high-availability infrastructure using Azure G-Cloud services and ISO-aligned controls for security, quality and accessibility. The impact includes stronger transparency, more efficient supervision and better evidence for policymaking, supported by public access to statistical data. More broadly, the platform creates value by improving institutional integrity, enabling uniform and systematic oversight, and modernising a sensitive area of public administration through interoperable digital governance.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2172,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01623,EU_PSTW_202609_01623,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,European Digital Infrastructure Consortium (EDIC),R&D general public services.,"Europe’s language-technology landscape is fragmented by market size, linguistic diversity, and unequal access to high-quality data and infrastructure, which makes it difficult to build competitive large language models and related tools across all European languages. ALT-EDIC addresses this challenge by creating a common European infrastructure for language technologies, with a particular focus on strengthening the foundations for large language models while preserving linguistic diversity and cultural richness.",Alliance for Language Technologies (ALT-EDIC),European Digital Infrastructure Consortium (EDIC); Central-Government; R&D general public services.; European Union,"The implementation uses the European Digital Infrastructure Consortium model, allowing member states to pool funding and resources in a coordinated structure.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Europe’s language-technology landscape is fragmented by market size, linguistic diversity, and unequal access to high-quality data and infrastructure, which makes it difficult to build competitive large language models and related tools across all European languages. ALT-EDIC addresses this challenge by creating a common European infrastructure for language technologies, with a particular focus on strengthening the foundations for large language models while preserving linguistic diversity and cultural richness. The objective is to improve Europe’s competitiveness, expand the availability of European language data, and support interoperable language-technology capacity across participating states. The implementation uses the European Digital Infrastructure Consortium model, allowing member states to pool funding and resources in a coordinated structure. Official ALT-EDIC and related language-data-space materials describe activities including linguistic-data production and curation, evaluation and normalisation support, adaptation of models to user needs, and advice to the wider ecosystem. The impact includes stronger shared infrastructure, more reusable multilingual resources, and better conditions for developing European language technologies at scale. It also creates long-term strategic value by supporting common standards, reducing fragmentation, and reinforcing Europe’s capacity to develop language AI aligned with its regulatory, linguistic, and public-interest goals.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2173,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01624,EU_PSTW_202609_01624,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""social_nonprofit""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Ministry of Economic Affairs and Climate,R&D general public services.,UNKNOWN,GPT-NL - A language model for the Dutch language and culture,Ministry of Economic Affairs and Climate; Central-Government; R&D general public services.; Netherlands,"Many widely used language models are developed outside the Netherlands and may not adequately reflect Dutch language use, legal requirements, or national priorities around privacy, cultural context, and technological independence.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Many widely used language models are developed outside the Netherlands and may not adequately reflect Dutch language use, legal requirements, or national priorities around privacy, cultural context, and technological independence. GPT-NL was launched to address this strategic gap by creating an open Dutch language model designed to strengthen national capability in AI, data science, and data spaces while supporting compliance with European rules. The objective is to improve strategic autonomy, foster domestic expertise, and provide a trustworthy foundation for language applications built around Dutch language and culture. The implementation centres on the development of an open large language model funded by the Ministry of Economic Affairs and Climate Policy through RVO, with EUR 13.5 million allocated to the project after the “Facility for a sovereign Dutch language model” proposal was awarded in October 2023. At a conceptual level, the initiative focuses on building and governing a Dutch-language model in line with the GDPR and the AI Regulation, with an explicit aim of avoiding training sources that infringe privacy or intellectual-property rights. The impact is expected to include a stronger national knowledge base, improved access to compliant Dutch-language AI infrastructure, and greater capacity for public and private organisations to build applications suited to local linguistic and regulatory needs, creating long-term value through sovereign digital capability.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2174,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01625,EU_PSTW_202609_01625,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""social_nonprofit"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"INSAIT – Institute for Computer Science, Artificial Intelligence and Technology",General public services n.e.c.,"Many widely used language technologies provide limited support for smaller European languages, constraining digital inclusion and technological sovereignty. In Bulgaria, public institutions, businesses and citizens lack high-quality language models tailored to national linguistic and cultural contexts.",BgGPT - A bulgarian LLM,"INSAIT – Institute for Computer Science, Artificial Intelligence and Technology; Academic-Research; General public services n.e.c.; Bulgaria","The implementation involves training a series of open and freely available language models using curated Bulgarian-language datasets, supported by advanced computational infrastructure. As the project is still in development, evaluation focuses on linguistic quality, robustness and applicability across use cases.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Many widely used language technologies provide limited support for smaller European languages, constraining digital inclusion and technological sovereignty. In Bulgaria, public institutions, businesses and citizens lack high-quality language models tailored to national linguistic and cultural contexts. The objective of BgGPT is to develop state-of-the-art generative language models designed specifically for the Bulgarian language and its users. The implementation involves training a series of open and freely available language models using curated Bulgarian-language datasets, supported by advanced computational infrastructure. The models are designed to serve a broad range of applications, including public administration, education, research and business services. Emphasis is placed on openness, transparency and alignment with European values. As the project is still in development, evaluation focuses on linguistic quality, robustness and applicability across use cases. Expected impacts include improved access to language technologies for Bulgarian speakers, strengthened national research capacity and reduced dependency on foreign-language-centric solutions. Qualitative benefits include enhanced digital inclusion, support for public sector innovation and a reusable foundation for Bulgarian-language digital services.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2175,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01626,EU_PSTW_202609_01626,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,EU-FarmBook,"Agriculture, forestry, fishing and hunting",EU-FarmBook addresses this challenge by building an open online platform to support knowledge exchange across the European agricultural and forestry innovation ecosystem.,GenAI chatbot on the EU-FarmBook platform,"EU-FarmBook; Consortium; Agriculture, forestry, fishing and hunting; European Union","The objective is to make research-based and practice-oriented resources easier to discover and use, while improving knowledge transfer between actors at regional, national, and European levels. The implementation includes an AI-powered conversational assistant developed with support from TNO, designed to help users retrieve relevant information interactively from the platform.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Farmers, foresters, advisers, researchers, and policymakers often need practical knowledge quickly, but relevant materials are frequently spread across projects, languages, and institutions, making them hard to find and apply. EU-FarmBook addresses this challenge by building an open online platform to support knowledge exchange across the European agricultural and forestry innovation ecosystem. The objective is to make research-based and practice-oriented resources easier to discover and use, while improving knowledge transfer between actors at regional, national, and European levels. The implementation includes an AI-powered conversational assistant developed with support from TNO, designed to help users retrieve relevant information interactively from the platform. At a conceptual level, the chatbot uses generative AI to respond to users in a conversational way while grounding its answers in the platform’s own database of contributions, tools, guides, and other materials. Recent project communications state that the AI technology is European and that the responses are based on content held within EU-FarmBook itself, supporting controlled and domain-relevant retrieval. The impact includes easier access to specialised information for users with different backgrounds, improved usability of a large cross-European knowledge platform, and more efficient dissemination of project results and practice-based insight. It also supports scalability by turning a growing repository into a more navigable and interactive service for rural communities.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2176,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01627,EU_PSTW_202609_01627,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""social_nonprofit""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,LDT CitiVERSE EDIC,R&D general public services.,"Cities across Europe are developing digital twins to support planning and public-service innovation, but these systems often remain local, fragmented, and difficult to connect into a broader European capability.",CitiVERSE - LDT CitiVERSE EDIC,LDT CitiVERSE EDIC; Consortium; R&D general public services.; European Union,"The objective is to strengthen cooperation between participating countries, improve the use of digital twins in smart-city policy, and support new forms of simulation and engagement around issues such as traffic, congestion, air quality, and decarbonisation. The implementation uses the European Digital Infrastructure Consortium model to establish a shared governance and infrastructure framework for networked local digital twins.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Cities across Europe are developing digital twins to support planning and public-service innovation, but these systems often remain local, fragmented, and difficult to connect into a broader European capability. The LDT CitiVERSE EDIC initiative was created to link existing local digital twins and form the foundation of a broader European CitiVERSE, enabling more coordinated development of data-driven and simulation-based tools for urban policy and citizen interaction. The objective is to strengthen cooperation between participating countries, improve the use of digital twins in smart-city policy, and support new forms of simulation and engagement around issues such as traffic, congestion, air quality, and decarbonisation. The implementation uses the European Digital Infrastructure Consortium model to establish a shared governance and infrastructure framework for networked local digital twins. At a conceptual level, the initiative connects data, analytics, simulation, and generative AI applications so that cities can model real-time or future urban conditions and also explore virtual-reality tools that help citizens understand proposed urban changes more easily. The Commission notes that Croatia, Estonia, France, Latvia, Portugal, Slovenia, and Spain initially called for the EDIC to be established. The impact lies in stronger interoperability between local digital twins, greater capacity for collaborative urban innovation, and more scalable smart-city services across Europe, creating both public-sector efficiency gains and better-informed citizen participation.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2177,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01628,EU_PSTW_202609_01628,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""social_nonprofit""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,AI Sweden,R&D economic affairs,UNKNOWN,GPT-SW3 - The first large-scale generative language model for the Swedish language,AI Sweden; Academic-Research; R&D economic affairs; Sweden,"The implementation uses a large-scale generative model based on the same broad technical principles as contemporary transformer-based language models, trained to support Nordic-language generation and downstream applications.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Swedish organisations increasingly need language technologies that reflect local linguistic realities and can be developed within a national ecosystem rather than relying entirely on foreign models. GPT-SW3 was created to address this need by providing a large-scale generative language model for the Nordic languages, with a primary focus on Swedish, through collaboration between AI Sweden, RISE, and WASP WARA Media & Language. The objective is to give organisations in Sweden access to a strong domestic foundation for language applications, while also strengthening the country’s applied AI capability across public, private, and academic sectors. The implementation uses a large-scale generative model based on the same broad technical principles as contemporary transformer-based language models, trained to support Nordic-language generation and downstream applications. At a conceptual level, the project combines national collaboration, technical development, and ecosystem building through AI Sweden, which brings together more than 120 partners and is funded by both the Swedish government and public- and private-sector participants. The impact includes a stronger Swedish-language AI infrastructure, new possibilities for organisations to build local language applications, and improved strategic capability in a domain often dominated by global providers. It also creates long-term value by supporting innovation in Swedish and Nordic language technology through a shared national platform.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2178,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01629,EU_PSTW_202609_01629,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Kungsbacka municipality, Tjörn municipality, Gothenburg city, Region Skåne, Region Halland, and Västra Götaland Region",General public services n.e.c.,"In Sweden, the Svea initiative was created as a joint effort between authorities, municipalities, regions, and industry, coordinated by AI Sweden, to develop shared capability rather than fragmented local solutions.",Svea - A shared digital assistant for the public sector,"Kungsbacka municipality, Tjörn municipality, Gothenburg city, Region Skåne, Region Halland, and Västra Götaland Region; Local Government; General public services n.e.c.; Sweden","Public-sector organisations handle large volumes of text-based work every day, from drafting and revising documents to summarising material, classifying content, and supporting administrative processes, yet these tasks consume significant staff time and are difficult for single organisations to automate alone. The implementation is organised in phases, beginning with data generation, change management, use-case identification, and prototype development, and continuing with large-scale testing and evaluation.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Public-sector organisations handle large volumes of text-based work every day, from drafting and revising documents to summarising material, classifying content, and supporting administrative processes, yet these tasks consume significant staff time and are difficult for single organisations to automate alone. In Sweden, the Svea initiative was created as a joint effort between authorities, municipalities, regions, and industry, coordinated by AI Sweden, to develop shared capability rather than fragmented local solutions. The objective is to support public employees in routine text tasks, free time for more interpersonal and creative work, and establish the conditions for national cooperation on shared AI solutions in the public sector. The implementation is organised in phases, beginning with data generation, change management, use-case identification, and prototype development, and continuing with large-scale testing and evaluation. Svea itself is a web-based chat assistant built on open-source, model-agnostic architecture running on Swedish infrastructure; it can already answer organisation-specific questions, summarise documents, extract information, and rewrite text in easy-to-read Swedish. AI Sweden states that participating organisations collectively spent 2,500 hours annotating training data for the assistant. The impact includes stronger reuse of data and effort across institutions, improved scalability of AI adoption, and better productivity in text-heavy public work, while helping organisations prepare for labour shortages and rising service demand.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2179,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01630,EU_PSTW_202609_01630,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Île-de-France Regional Council,General services.,"Public sector agents often struggle to navigate complex internal documentation and information systems, leading to inefficiencies and increased workload for IT support teams.",LightOn's Paradigm platform - Generative AI at the Île-de-France Regional Council,Île-de-France Regional Council; Regional Government; General services.; France,"The solution deploys a generative AI-powered assistant built on large language model technology, trained and fine-tuned on the Region’s internal documentation corpus.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Public sector agents often struggle to navigate complex internal documentation and information systems, leading to inefficiencies and increased workload for IT support teams. Stakeholders include regional council staff, digital transformation teams and technology providers. The objective is to provide instant, reliable access to technical documentation and internal knowledge resources. The solution deploys a generative AI-powered assistant built on large language model technology, trained and fine-tuned on the Region’s internal documentation corpus. Natural language queries are processed to retrieve and synthesise relevant information, delivering precise responses within existing digital work environments. The platform integrates with knowledge bases and information systems managed by the Digital Transformation Hub. Expected impacts include reduced time spent searching for documentation, lower support demand on IT teams, improved operational efficiency and enhanced digital maturity within public administration.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2180,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01631,EU_PSTW_202609_01631,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,University of Amsterdam,R&D public order and safety.,"In this context, the EU-funded SOLARIS project will focus on the challenges and opportunities posed by GANs in the context of democracy.",SOLARIS - Democratic responses to the challenges of deepfakes and infodemics,University of Amsterdam; Consortium; R&D public order and safety.; European Union,"The project will involve three use cases to simulate the circulation of threatening content, and co-create value-based GANs content to enhance digital citizenship.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Generative adversarial networks (GANs) are generative AI models capable of reproducing realistic images, videos and voice outputs. Because they have the potential to create highly realistic content, they can also pose a threat to democracies through the creation and circulation of deepfakes, which spread false information and undermine trust. In this context, the EU-funded SOLARIS project will focus on the challenges and opportunities posed by GANs in the context of democracy. It will study how deepfakes circulate and are trusted by users, thus threatening democratic processes. It will also develop regulatory guidelines about GANs and generative AI for the promotion of democratic engagement. The project will involve three use cases to simulate the circulation of threatening content, and co-create value-based GANs content to enhance digital citizenship.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2184,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01632,EU_PSTW_202609_01632,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Linkopings Universitet,R&D general public services.,UNKNOWN,TrustLLM - Democratise trustworthy and efficient LLM technology for Europe,Linkopings Universitet; Consortium; R&D general public services.; European Union,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"TrustLLM is a Horizon Europe project designed to develop open, trustworthy and sustainable large language models for European languages, with an initial focus on Germanic languages and underrepresented linguistic communities. The strategic problem is that Europe needs language-model infrastructure that is transparent, reliable, energy-conscious and less dependent on closed non-European systems. Coordinated by Linköping University, the project brings together partners across Europe with expertise in natural language processing, high-performance computing, trustworthy AI, model evaluation and downstream deployment. The implementation will develop large language models trained on substantial multilingual European text resources, while addressing reliability, bias, hallucination, sustainability and accessibility. Conceptually, the project combines large-scale model training, exascale computing, trustworthiness research, open model development and ecosystem-building for modular and extensible European LLMs. The expected impact is stronger European capacity in language technologies, improved human-machine interaction across applications, wider access to open models and better support for industry, public services and research. TrustLLM is explicitly positioned as a response to the need for reliable generative models and mitigation of risks such as hallucinations and bias.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2193,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01633,EU_PSTW_202609_01633,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Alma Mater Studiorum – University of Bologna ALMA-AI,R&D general public services.,UNKNOWN,GENAI4LEX-B,Alma Mater Studiorum – University of Bologna ALMA-AI; Consortium; R&D general public services.; Italy,GENAI4LEX-B was developed to support parliamentary work in Italy by using generative AI for the preparation of legislative initiative acts.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"GENAI4LEX-B was developed to support parliamentary work in Italy by using generative AI for the preparation of legislative initiative acts. The institutional problem is that parliamentarians and parliamentary staff must navigate complex regulatory sources, amendments, policy objectives and legislative drafting requirements under time and information constraints. The project won the “Supporting the preparation of initiative acts” area in a call promoted by the Italian Chamber of Deputies, which selected three winning projects from 28 proposals. Its three prototypes are designed to help users explore regulatory sources, simplify and summarise amendments, and measure the alignment between bills and the policies they intend to pursue. The implementation combines generative AI, legal informatics, regulatory-source analysis, summarisation and legislative-support functions. The consortium is coordinated by Alma Mater Studiorum – University of Bologna / ALMA-AI and includes CNR-Igsg with Aptus.AI, Luiss Guido Carli University with Asimov AI, the University of Turin and the University of Verona. The expected impact is more efficient legislative preparation, improved access to regulatory context, clearer amendment analysis and stronger evidence on whether proposed bills match policy goals. Its value depends on careful human oversight and constitutional safeguards in parliamentary processes.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2194,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01634,EU_PSTW_202609_01634,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,State of Baden-Württemberg,General services.,"The administrative challenge is that civil servants work with large volumes of documents, cabinet templates, notes and research material, which can slow workflows and reduce time available for substantive policy or service tasks.","F13, the LLM used by the State of Baden-Württemberg in collaboration with Aleph Alpha",State of Baden-Württemberg; Regional Government; General services.; Germany,"Its implementation uses generative language-model technology to support several functions: a summary tool with selectable compression levels for pasted texts, a Cabinet Template Note function that converts uploaded cabinet templates into summarised note templates, a research assistant for preparing large volumes of information, and the “Vermerkomat”, which creates comprehensive flow text. Since 2024, all staff with access to the state administration network can use F13, indicating a move beyond experimentation into full administrative deployment.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Baden-Württemberg introduced F13 to support employees of the state administration in drafting, summarising and processing textual information more efficiently. The administrative challenge is that civil servants work with large volumes of documents, cabinet templates, notes and research material, which can slow workflows and reduce time available for substantive policy or service tasks. F13 is designed to relieve staff by providing text assistance functions tailored to administrative routines. The system was developed primarily by the Heidelberg-based company Aleph Alpha and presented by the Innovation Lab Baden-Württemberg, InnoLab_bw. Its implementation uses generative language-model technology to support several functions: a summary tool with selectable compression levels for pasted texts, a Cabinet Template Note function that converts uploaded cabinet templates into summarised note templates, a research assistant for preparing large volumes of information, and the “Vermerkomat”, which creates comprehensive flow text. At a conceptual level, F13 combines large language models, document processing, summarisation, text generation and workflow-oriented user interfaces within the state administration network. The expected impact is faster preparation of administrative documents, reduced repetitive writing work, improved handling of information-intensive tasks and more consistent support for employees. Since 2024, all staff with access to the state administration network can use F13, indicating a move beyond experimentation into full administrative deployment.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F06_HIGH_VOLUME_SCALABILITY_BOTTLENECK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2195,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01635,EU_PSTW_202609_01635,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Île-de-France Regional Counci,General services.,The ALEIA digital assistant responds to the difficulty citizens face when trying to identify the regional aid schemes for which they may be eligible.,ALEIA digital assistant - Generative AI at the Île-de-France Regional Council,Île-de-France Regional Counci; Regional Government; General services.; France,"It uses the French Mistral language model to formulate responses grounded in the region’s corpus of documents, while data are hosted in France and answers are continuously improved.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The ALEIA digital assistant responds to the difficulty citizens face when trying to identify the regional aid schemes for which they may be eligible. Île-de-France has around 350 aid schemes, and this breadth of provision can make access confusing for residents, associations or other potential beneficiaries. The Regional Council therefore sought a tool that could guide users through the available support landscape and provide clear answers based on the region’s own documentation. Developed by the start-up Aleia, the solution combines a search engine and a conversational agent. It uses the French Mistral language model to formulate responses grounded in the region’s corpus of documents, while data are hosted in France and answers are continuously improved. The system is also designed to recognise when a user asks about support that falls outside the region’s responsibilities and to respond negatively rather than providing irrelevant guidance. Conceptually, the assistant combines document retrieval, generative language modelling, conversational interaction, controlled knowledge sources and French-hosted data infrastructure. The expected impact is simpler access to regional support, reduced user confusion, more efficient routing towards relevant schemes and less pressure on administrative staff handling repetitive eligibility questions. By linking conversational access with an official documentary corpus, the system can improve transparency and make regional aid more navigable.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2197,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01636,EU_PSTW_202609_01636,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""social_nonprofit""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Government of Spain,R&D general public services.,"The strategic challenge is that Spanish and Iberian-language communities require generative AI systems that reflect their linguistic diversity, cultural context and public-interest requirements, rather than relying mainly on models optimised for English.",Open Spanish LLM model in collaboration with IBM,Government of Spain; Central-Government; R&D general public services.; Spain,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Spanish Government and IBM collaboration aims to develop open-source foundation models in Spanish and Spain’s co-official languages, including Catalan, Basque, Galician and Valencian. The strategic challenge is that Spanish and Iberian-language communities require generative AI systems that reflect their linguistic diversity, cultural context and public-interest requirements, rather than relying mainly on models optimised for English. The initiative was launched through a Memorandum of Understanding discussed in April 2024 between the Spanish Government and IBM. The implementation draws on IBM’s open-source AI framework and AI stack, with an emphasis on ethical, sustainable and community-driven model development. Conceptually, the project combines large and small language models, open-source development, AI supercomputing, multilingual training and community contribution mechanisms. The expected impact is stronger national and international Spanish-language AI capacity, improved access to generative AI tools for nearly 500 million Spanish speakers, and better support for co-official languages in digital services and innovation. The collaboration also aims to advance responsible generative AI technologies in Spain and support practical deployment in public and private contexts.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2199,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01637,EU_PSTW_202609_01637,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""social_nonprofit"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,OpenGPT-X,R&D general public services.,"The broader challenge is that advanced generative models are increasingly important for business applications and digital transformation, yet strategic dependence on non-European solutions may limit sovereignty, transparency and local innovation capacity.","OpenGPT-X project to create a LLM ""Made in Germany""",OpenGPT-X; Consortium; R&D general public services.; Germany,"This use case therefore brings together partners from industry, research and media across the generative AI value chain, with the objective of advancing cutting-edge research while driving practical adoption in the German economy. The solution is implemented as a collaborative Gaia-X-related project funded by the German Federal Ministry for Economic Affairs and Climate Action, with total funding of around EUR 14 million and a project timeline spanning January 2022 to March 2025.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"OpenGPT-X was launched to strengthen Europe’s capacity in generative-language technologies by developing a large language model ecosystem rooted in German and European research, industrial capability and trusted data environments. The broader challenge is that advanced generative models are increasingly important for business applications and digital transformation, yet strategic dependence on non-European solutions may limit sovereignty, transparency and local innovation capacity. This use case therefore brings together partners from industry, research and media across the generative AI value chain, with the objective of advancing cutting-edge research while driving practical adoption in the German economy. The solution is implemented as a collaborative Gaia-X-related project funded by the German Federal Ministry for Economic Affairs and Climate Action, with total funding of around EUR 14 million and a project timeline spanning January 2022 to March 2025. At a conceptual level, the initiative develops large language model capabilities, supporting infrastructure and practical application pathways that connect foundational research with real business use cases. This includes work across model development, data spaces and deployment-oriented innovation within a trusted digital ecosystem. The expected impact is stronger domestic capability in generative-language technologies, greater readiness for enterprise adoption and improved alignment between research and market application. More broadly, OpenGPT-X supports technological sovereignty, boosts innovation capacity and creates long-term value by establishing a European foundation for advanced language-model development and use.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2200,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01638,EU_PSTW_202609_01638,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Department for Education - UK Government,R&D education.,UNKNOWN,"Development of GenAI-based tool for education by gov.uk - ""Building a proof of concept for Generative AI feedback and resource generation in education contexts""",Department for Education - UK Government; Central-Government; R&D education.; United Kingdom,The National Institute of Teaching and the Department for Education explored this use case as part of a wider effort to transform teachers’ day-to-day work by reducing routine burdens while supporting stronger educational outcomes. The solution is implemented as a proof-of-concept tool based on large language models.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Teachers face substantial workload pressures linked to marking, feedback preparation and the creation of classroom resources, yet these tasks are central to maintaining teaching quality and supporting pupil progress. The National Institute of Teaching and the Department for Education explored this use case as part of a wider effort to transform teachers’ day-to-day work by reducing routine burdens while supporting stronger educational outcomes. The stakeholders include teachers, pupils and education-system leaders, with the objective of helping teachers evaluate work more efficiently, provide useful formative feedback and generate relevant learning activities aligned with the National Curriculum. The solution is implemented as a proof-of-concept tool based on large language models. At a conceptual level, the system uses generative-language capabilities to analyse pupil work against curriculum expectations, draft feedback for formative use and create supporting activities or resources for follow-up teaching. These functions are integrated as exploratory support for professional practice rather than as a replacement for teacher judgement. The expected impact is reduced time spent on repetitive instructional tasks, quicker preparation of feedback and stronger support for lesson adaptation to learner needs. More broadly, the project improves workflow efficiency, enhances the usability of digital tools in education and creates value by testing how generative systems can support teachers in practical, curriculum-linked classroom contexts.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2201,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01639,EU_PSTW_202609_01639,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Leeds Beckett University,"Mining, manufacturing and construction.","The operational problem is that cost overruns are widespread in construction, while early cost planning often requires time-consuming manual work, fragmented data and limited certainty.",Developing a Generative AI platform for construction cost management,"Leeds Beckett University; Academic-Research; Mining, manufacturing and construction.; United Kingdom","Conceptually, the platform will generate construction cost-estimation documents, automate BIM-related cost outputs, provide early cost advice and include accuracy indications to help predict and avoid overruns during planning.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The BIM-GAIcost project develops a generative AI platform to improve productivity and accuracy in construction cost planning. The operational problem is that cost overruns are widespread in construction, while early cost planning often requires time-consuming manual work, fragmented data and limited certainty. Leeds Beckett University’s Futures of Systems, People and Projects laboratory received a £1.18 million Innovate UK grant to develop the platform after a successful feasibility study. The project upgrades the earlier costplan.ai prototype by integrating Building Information Modelling with generative AI and large language models trained on cost data. Conceptually, the platform will generate construction cost-estimation documents, automate BIM-related cost outputs, provide early cost advice and include accuracy indications to help predict and avoid overruns during planning. The expected impact is a major productivity gain, with the source indicating potential improvement of up to 85% by reducing the time required to prepare initial cost plans. The wider consortium includes The Property Box Group, London South Bank University, Birmingham City University, Leeds Beckett University, Milan Consulting, Ardeo and Kier Group.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK|F07_DATA_VISIBILITY_INTEGRATION_GAP,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2202,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01640,EU_PSTW_202609_01640,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,University of Amsterdam,R&D environmental protection.,"The research challenge is that developing new materials for energy storage, industry, food and plastics can be slow, costly and experimentally intensive, while sustainability transitions require faster discovery of safer and lower-impact alternatives.",Amsterdam: Generating sustainable materials with Generative AI,University of Amsterdam; Academic-Research; R&D environmental protection.; Netherlands,"The University of Amsterdam’s AI4SMM initiative applies artificial intelligence to the design of sustainable molecules and materials, shifting generative techniques from language to chemistry and materials science. The implementation uses machine-learning and generative approaches to model molecules and materials, replacing word-based associations with relationships between molecular structures and properties.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The University of Amsterdam’s AI4SMM initiative applies artificial intelligence to the design of sustainable molecules and materials, shifting generative techniques from language to chemistry and materials science. The research challenge is that developing new materials for energy storage, industry, food and plastics can be slow, costly and experimentally intensive, while sustainability transitions require faster discovery of safer and lower-impact alternatives. The initiative is led by Bernd Ensing at the Faculty of Science and connects expertise in computer science, chemistry, ecology, physics and biology. The implementation uses machine-learning and generative approaches to model molecules and materials, replacing word-based associations with relationships between molecular structures and properties. Initial projects focus on areas such as energy-storage salts, sustainable steel, safe plastic and new plant proteins. Conceptually, the approach combines molecular data, computational chemistry, generative modelling, property prediction and experimental validation to propose promising candidates for further study. The expected impact is faster discovery of sustainable materials, better targeting of laboratory work and stronger integration between AI and sustainability research. By applying generative methods to molecules rather than text, the initiative can support innovation in sectors where material performance and environmental impact are central.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2204,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01641,EU_PSTW_202609_01641,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Gdańsk University of Technology,R&D general public services.,"Qra was developed to provide a strong Polish-language generative model trained specifically on Polish text, responding to the limited availability of open large language models optimised for Polish.",Qra - The first Polish open-source LLM,Gdańsk University of Technology; Academic-Research; R&D general public services.; Poland,Gdańsk University of Technology and the AI Lab at the National Information Processing Institute developed Polish generative neural language models using approximately a terabyte of Polish-language data. The models were trained using infrastructure from the PG TASK Computing Centre with 21 Nvidia A100 cards.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Qra was developed to provide a strong Polish-language generative model trained specifically on Polish text, responding to the limited availability of open large language models optimised for Polish. The research and technology problem is that models primarily trained on English or multilingual corpora may underperform in Polish-language understanding, question answering and coherent text generation. Gdańsk University of Technology and the AI Lab at the National Information Processing Institute developed Polish generative neural language models using approximately a terabyte of Polish-language data. The implementation involved training open-source models on Polish text, with the Hugging Face model card noting that published versions were based on Llama 2 checkpoints and further trained on a cleaned, filtered and deduplicated Polish corpus of about 90 billion tokens. The models were trained using infrastructure from the PG TASK Computing Centre with 21 Nvidia A100 cards. The expected impact is improved Polish-language AI capacity, broader reuse by researchers and developers, and reduced reliance on foreign-language models for Polish applications. Because the models are open-source, users can download and adapt them to specific domains or tasks, including question answering and text generation.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2205,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01642,EU_PSTW_202609_01642,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"POLITEHNICA Bucharest, the University of Bucharest and the Institute of Logic and Data Science",R&D general public services.,"OpenLLM-Ro was developed to address the limited availability of large language models specifically trained for Romanian, a gap that can constrain the quality of digital services, research tools and language technologies for Romanian-speaking users.",OpenLLM-Ro - The first LLM in Romanian language,"POLITEHNICA Bucharest, the University of Bucharest and the Institute of Logic and Data Science; Academic-Research; R&D general public services.; Romania",UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"OpenLLM-Ro was developed to address the limited availability of large language models specifically trained for Romanian, a gap that can constrain the quality of digital services, research tools and language technologies for Romanian-speaking users. The initiative brings together academic actors seeking to strengthen national language-technology capacity and provide an open model that can be further refined for different applications. The main stakeholders include POLITEHNICA Bucharest, the University of Bucharest, the Institute of Logic and Data Science, researchers working pro bono, developers, public or private organisations that may reuse the model, and Romanian-speaking end users. Starting in the second half of 2023, the research team worked on developing and training the model, with POLITEHNICA Bucharest also providing the computing power needed for training. The implementation involves large-language-model development, corpus preparation, training infrastructure, model evaluation and ongoing fine-tuning. Conceptually, the project creates a foundational Romanian-language model that can support downstream tasks such as text generation, summarisation, question answering, classification or conversational interfaces. Its expected impact is to reduce dependence on models optimised primarily for larger languages, improve Romanian-language processing quality, and support innovation in education, administration, business and research. The model’s online availability and continued fine-tuning indicate an evolving resource for the Romanian digital ecosystem.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2206,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01643,EU_PSTW_202609_01643,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Government of Ireland,General services.,"The expected impact is easier access to public information, reduced search burden for users, and better scalability for high-volume enquiries.",GenAI-based chatbot to help the public access services on official websites,Government of Ireland; Central-Government; General services.; Ireland,"Conceptually, the service uses generative AI to interpret user questions, retrieve relevant official content and provide conversational answers, while requiring safeguards around accuracy, transparency, human oversight and data protection. A related public-facing Irish implementation is now visible through AskCI, an AI-powered chatbot on Citizens Information that uses generative AI to answer questions based on citizensinformation.ie content and was developed with the OGCIO.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Ireland’s GenAI chatbot initiative was designed to test how generative AI could help members of the public find information on government supports and services through official digital channels. The administrative problem is that citizens and businesses often need to navigate multiple public websites, schemes and eligibility pages, which can create friction and increase demand for direct assistance. The initial project aimed to deliver a “chat agent” able to provide information on business supports offered by the Government, with pilot funding from Microsoft and delivery in collaboration with the Office of the Government Chief Information Officer. Conceptually, the service uses generative AI to interpret user questions, retrieve relevant official content and provide conversational answers, while requiring safeguards around accuracy, transparency, human oversight and data protection. The expected impact is easier access to public information, reduced search burden for users, and better scalability for high-volume enquiries. A related public-facing Irish implementation is now visible through AskCI, an AI-powered chatbot on Citizens Information that uses generative AI to answer questions based on citizensinformation.ie content and was developed with the OGCIO.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,F06_HIGH_VOLUME_SCALABILITY_BOTTLENECK,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2208,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01644,EU_PSTW_202609_01644,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,TurkuGPT - University of Turku,R&D general public services.,"The research challenge is to build models that can handle Finnish linguistic structures effectively and support applications in language technology, digital linguistics and natural language processing.",FinGPT-3 - The open Finnish LLM,TurkuGPT - University of Turku; Academic-Research; R&D general public services.; Finland,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"FinGPT-3 was created to expand the availability of generative language models for Finnish, a language that is less represented than English in many large-scale model ecosystems. The research challenge is to build models that can handle Finnish linguistic structures effectively and support applications in language technology, digital linguistics and natural language processing. The project is led by the TurkuNLP Group at the University of Turku, whose work spans corpus annotation, analysis, machine learning theory and applied language technologies. The National Library of Finland collaborated on the model development, and the project received support from the European Union’s Horizon Europe research and innovation programme under grant agreement No 101070350. In 2023, the team trained GPT-3-like generative models for Finnish. The implementation involves assembling and processing Finnish-language corpora, training large generative architectures, evaluating linguistic performance and making model outputs available for research or downstream application. Conceptually, FinGPT-3 provides foundational language-model capacity that can support Finnish text generation, summarisation, classification, information extraction and other NLP tasks. The expected impact is stronger technological coverage for Finnish, improved resources for researchers and developers, and reduced dependence on multilingual systems that may underperform for smaller language communities. By combining university expertise, national library resources and EU funding, the project contributes to Europe’s capacity for language-specific AI development.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2209,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01645,EU_PSTW_202609_01645,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,NorwAI and NTNU,R&D general public services.,UNKNOWN,"NorGPT - The open LLM ""Made in Norway""",NorwAI and NTNU; Academic-Research; R&D general public services.; Norway,"The core problem is that many large language models are optimised for English or broad multilingual use, while Norwegian users, organisations and public institutions need models that understand Norwegian linguistic structures, terminology and cultural context. The implementation involves collecting and processing Norwegian-language text resources, training large language models, evaluating performance, and making models available for research and experimental use.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"NorGPT forms part of Norway’s effort to develop large language models adapted to Norwegian language needs and national digital capacity. The core problem is that many large language models are optimised for English or broad multilingual use, while Norwegian users, organisations and public institutions need models that understand Norwegian linguistic structures, terminology and cultural context. Developed through NorwAI and NTNU, the initiative supports natural-language interaction so that users can work on language-related tasks through ordinary Norwegian queries rather than artificial syntax. The implementation involves collecting and processing Norwegian-language text resources, training large language models, evaluating performance, and making models available for research and experimental use. Conceptually, NorGPT can support tasks such as text generation, summarisation, translation, question answering and information retrieval. The expected impact is stronger Norwegian-language AI capacity, improved productivity for language-intensive work and reduced reliance on external models not optimised for Norwegian. Current NorwAI material indicates that work on Norwegian LLMs is active, while a recent technical report states that NorwAI models are openly available to Nordic organisations, companies and students for research and experimental use.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2210,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01646,EU_PSTW_202609_01646,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,University of Roma Tre,R&D general public services.,"The democratic problem is that parliamentary information is often technically complex, dispersed across institutional documents and difficult for non-specialist users to interpret.",‘Democratic Engagement through a Public Chatbot’ (DepuChat) - How GenAI can increase transparency,University of Roma Tre; Academic-Research; R&D general public services.; Italy,The implementation uses generative AI to create a conversational and potentially graphical interface over parliamentary data.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"DepuChat proposes a public chatbot to make information on the Italian Chamber of Deputies more accessible, usable and transparent for citizens. The democratic problem is that parliamentary information is often technically complex, dispersed across institutional documents and difficult for non-specialist users to interpret. The project, developed by the IDEAS Research Centre at Roma Tre University in collaboration with the Department of Legal Sciences at the University of Florence, aims to bring citizens closer to institutions by allowing them to query information on the Chamber, its bodies and procedures, individual MPs, and political positions expressed by MPs and the Government. The implementation uses generative AI to create a conversational and potentially graphical interface over parliamentary data. Conceptually, the system combines institutional data retrieval, natural-language interaction, summarisation and visual presentation to help users explore parliamentary activity more easily. The expected impact is improved transparency, broader civic access to legislative information and stronger democratic engagement. The project was announced in July 2024 as a winner of the Chamber of Deputies’ call for ideas, so its current form appears to be a selected proposal or prototype rather than a fully operational public service.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2211,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01647,EU_PSTW_202609_01647,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Ministry of Processing and Function Public Interministerial Directorate for Public Transformation (DITP) and the Interministerial Digital Directorate (DINUM),",R&D public order and safety.,"The expected impact is faster response preparation, reduced administrative burden, improved consistency in written replies and more time for personalised support where human judgement is most valuable.",Supporting Civil Servant with Generative AI,"Ministry of Processing and Function Public Interministerial Directorate for Public Transformation (DITP) and the Interministerial Digital Directorate (DINUM),; Central-Government; R&D public order and safety.; France","The system uses generative language technology to propose draft responses based on anonymised data, meaning confidentiality is intended to be preserved.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"France’s experiment on generative artificial intelligence in public services was launched to assess how such tools could support civil servants in responding to users more efficiently. The administrative problem is that public agents handle large numbers of online opinions, comments and procedural interactions, many of which require clear written replies but can be repetitive and time-consuming. The initiative involved 1,000 volunteer agents equipped with a tool to help draft responses to users during online procedures, while preserving a human-in-the-loop model: agents supervise, modify and approve responses before they are sent. The experiment is led by the Interministerial Directorate for Public Transformation and the Interministerial Digital Directorate, with a solution provided by Allobrain. The system uses generative language technology to propose draft responses based on anonymised data, meaning confidentiality is intended to be preserved. The model supports response writing rather than replacing the public official, and the approach is framed as a way to save time on specific tasks while strengthening human contact between public services and citizens. The planned extension to France services counters would broaden the support to agents assisting users face to face. The expected impact is faster response preparation, reduced administrative burden, improved consistency in written replies and more time for personalised support where human judgement is most valuable.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S7_GENERATIVE_CONTENT_CREATION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2213,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01648,EU_PSTW_202609_01648,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,SNCF,Transport,"The operational problem was that traditional satisfaction surveys were not capturing enough qualitative information, while unsolicited passenger experiences were difficult to process at scale.",SNCF collect 3x more customer feedback with AI,SNCF; Central-Government; Transport; France,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Railway operators need timely and detailed customer feedback to understand service quality, detect weak signals and respond to dissatisfaction before it damages trust. SNCF used AlloReview to improve the collection and analysis of passenger feedback across TER services, particularly in the Grand Est region. The operational problem was that traditional satisfaction surveys were not capturing enough qualitative information, while unsolicited passenger experiences were difficult to process at scale. The implementation combines QR codes, phone numbers, business cards and post-journey feedback mechanisms, allowing travellers to leave comments orally or in writing at different moments of the customer journey. AI-supported real-time analysis processes the resulting verbatims, identifies satisfaction levels and topics raised, and supports scenario-based follow-up. At a conceptual level, the system transforms open-ended voice and text feedback into structured customer intelligence, enabling SNCF to re-engage dissatisfied passengers, reward loyal customers and identify improvement opportunities. The expected impact is richer customer listening, faster detection of recurring problems, better segmentation of passenger satisfaction and stronger customer relationship management. The case demonstrates how AI can be used in transport not only for operations or mobility flows, but also for continuous service-quality improvement through large-scale qualitative analysis.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2214,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01649,EU_PSTW_202609_01649,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,The Polytechnic University of Milan,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.","The institutional problem is that new legislative proposals must be assessed against a large and continuously evolving corpus of laws, from the Constitution onwards, making it difficult to identify relevant precedents, overlaps, inconsistencies or references.",Legislab - monitoring legislative quality with GenAI,"The Polytechnic University of Milan; Academic-Research; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Italy",UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Legislab is designed to support analysis of the Italian legislative system and assist parliamentary offices in monitoring legal quality. The institutional problem is that new legislative proposals must be assessed against a large and continuously evolving corpus of laws, from the Constitution onwards, making it difficult to identify relevant precedents, overlaps, inconsistencies or references. The project, developed by the Politecnico di Milano and the Istituto Einaudi, is intended for the offices of the Chamber of Deputies and combines a database of Italian laws with analytical and generative functions. Its implementation allows users to input a topic or draft title and receive relevant articles and laws that may need to be considered. The system is deliberately constrained to reduce hallucination risks: it relies on existing legal data, objective information and linguistic metrics from scientific literature rather than generating free-form legal text. The expected impact is stronger legislative quality monitoring, faster identification of relevant legal sources and better support for expert legislative drafting, while preserving the role of legal professionals and parliamentarians in writing the law.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2215,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01650,EU_PSTW_202609_01650,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Sant’Anna School of Advanced Studies,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.","This approach is important because many public-sector legal applications require high sensitivity to legal wording and cannot rely solely on models trained on Wikipedia, news or other general corpora.",ITALIAN-LEGAL-BERT: A Pre-trained Transformer Language Model for Italian Law,"Sant’Anna School of Advanced Studies; Academic-Research; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Italy",UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Legal language differs substantially from general Italian because it contains technical terminology, Latin expressions, formulaic constructions and domain-specific references that general-purpose language models may not represent well. ITALIAN-LEGAL-BERT addresses this gap by adapting transformer-based natural language processing to the Italian legal domain. The model was introduced by researchers at the Sant’Anna School of Advanced Studies to improve performance on downstream legal NLP tasks where generic Italian BERT models lose accuracy. The implementation starts from an Italian BERT architecture and performs additional pre-training on Italian civil-law corpora. At a conceptual level, the model learns contextual representations from legal texts, making it better suited to tasks such as legal classification, retrieval, information extraction or document analysis. This approach is important because many public-sector legal applications require high sensitivity to legal wording and cannot rely solely on models trained on Wikipedia, news or other general corpora. The expected impact is improved performance in Italian legal AI systems, stronger foundations for legal analytics, and a reusable research resource for courts, public administrations, legal publishers and academic projects. The published paper reports that ITALIAN-LEGAL-BERT outperforms the general-purpose Italian BERT in domain-specific tasks, while a public Hugging Face entry indicates the model remains available as an open machine-learning resource.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2218,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01651,EU_PSTW_202609_01651,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Athens university of economics and business,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.",UNKNOWN,LEGAL-BERT: The Muppets straight out of Law School,"Athens university of economics and business; Academic-Research; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Greece","The project therefore evaluates different strategies for applying BERT to legal downstream tasks, including using the original model directly, adapting it through additional pre-training on legal corpora and pre-training models from scratch on domain-specific legal data.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"LEGAL-BERT was developed to improve the application of transformer-based language models to legal-domain tasks, where general-purpose models may not perform optimally because of specialised vocabulary, reasoning patterns and document structures. The research problem is that BERT had achieved strong results across many natural language processing tasks, but guidance on how to adapt it to specialised domains had not been sufficiently tested for legal materials. The project therefore evaluates different strategies for applying BERT to legal downstream tasks, including using the original model directly, adapting it through additional pre-training on legal corpora and pre-training models from scratch on domain-specific legal data. The implementation also explores a broader hyper-parameter search space for fine-tuning, recognising that standard configurations may not generalise well to law. The resulting LEGAL-BERT family of models is intended to support legal NLP research, computational law and legal technology applications. Conceptually, the project combines domain-specific pre-training, transformer architectures, legal corpora and systematic evaluation across datasets. Its expected impact is improved model performance for legal text classification, retrieval and analysis, as well as more reliable methodological guidance for researchers and developers working in specialised domains. By releasing LEGAL-BERT, the project provides reusable infrastructure for legal AI experimentation and application development.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2219,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01652,EU_PSTW_202609_01652,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,https://predictice.com/,R&D public order and safety.,"The practical challenge for legal professionals is that judicial documents can be long, technical and time-consuming to read, while some decisions may prove irrelevant only after several minutes of review.",Predictice - Summaries of Court Decisions,https://predictice.com/; Private sector; R&D public order and safety.; France,Predictice uses generative language technology to make legal research more efficient by producing automatic summaries of court decisions. The implementation applies GPT-3 and ChatGPT-style language-model capabilities to the task of document synthesis.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Predictice uses generative language technology to make legal research more efficient by producing automatic summaries of court decisions. The practical challenge for legal professionals is that judicial documents can be long, technical and time-consuming to read, while some decisions may prove irrelevant only after several minutes of review. Predictice provides access to a large legal corpus of around 26 million documents, including court decisions, and aims to help users assess relevance more quickly. The implementation applies GPT-3 and ChatGPT-style language-model capabilities to the task of document synthesis. Rather than asking the model to produce legal advice, the tool focuses on summarising existing decisions, a use case considered more reliable by the Predictice team than generating autonomous legal answers. Conceptually, the system combines large-scale legal-document retrieval, generative summarisation, natural language processing and user-facing legal search functions. The expected impact is faster review of case law, reduced time spent on irrelevant documents, improved productivity for lawyers and legal researchers, and easier navigation of large legal databases. By providing concise summaries of lengthy decisions, Predictice can support legal professionals in prioritising which documents deserve closer reading. However, because legal interpretation remains sensitive, summaries should be treated as decision-support outputs and checked against the original court text.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2221,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01653,EU_PSTW_202609_01653,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,https://henchman.io/,R&D public order and safety.,"The practical challenge in legal work is that lawyers frequently need to adapt existing clauses to specific transactional contexts, client preferences or drafting conventions, while maintaining consistency, precision and speed.",Henchman - contract drafing with AI support,https://henchman.io/; Private sector; R&D public order and safety.; Belgium,"The implementation uses GPT-3.5 to assist with targeted drafting operations, such as changing a clause from singular to plural or adding a new element to an existing provision.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Henchman supports legal professionals in drafting and improving contract clauses by extending the range of available drafting options during contract preparation. The practical challenge in legal work is that lawyers frequently need to adapt existing clauses to specific transactional contexts, client preferences or drafting conventions, while maintaining consistency, precision and speed. The platform is therefore relevant to law firms, in-house legal teams, contract managers and clients relying on efficient document production. Its objective is to make contract drafting more agile by helping users refine language, adjust wording and introduce clause-level variations without starting from scratch. The implementation uses GPT-3.5 to assist with targeted drafting operations, such as changing a clause from singular to plural or adding a new element to an existing provision. Conceptually, the system combines large language model functionality with contract-drafting workflows, allowing users to generate or modify text within a controlled legal-document environment. The expected impact is faster clause adaptation, reduced repetitive editing, improved consistency across contract drafts and greater productivity for legal teams. By supporting incremental drafting rather than replacing legal judgement, the tool can help professionals focus on negotiation strategy, legal risk and client-specific tailoring. Its value depends on appropriate human review, since AI-generated legal text must be checked for accuracy, enforceability and alignment with the intended contractual position.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2222,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01654,EU_PSTW_202609_01654,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,https://www.robinai.com/,R&D public order and safety.,"The expected impact is shorter contract-review cycles, more consistent application of drafting standards and reduced manual workload for routine legal-document tasks.",Robin AI - LLM for supporting Legals,https://www.robinai.com/; Private sector; R&D public order and safety.; United Kingdom,"Its implementation uses large language models, including Anthropic’s Claude, to analyse contract text, support reading and editing, and suggest real-time changes based on client preferences.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Robin AI provides legal teams with a platform for accelerating contract review, drafting and editing through large language model functionality. The operational issue is that legal departments and firms often spend substantial time reading long contracts, identifying risks, suggesting amendments and aligning documents with client preferences. This creates pressure on turnaround times, especially where high volumes of contracts must be reviewed consistently. The platform is relevant to lawyers, in-house legal teams, commercial teams, clients and organisations seeking faster approval workflows. Its implementation uses large language models, including Anthropic’s Claude, to analyse contract text, support reading and editing, and suggest real-time changes based on client preferences. At a conceptual level, the system combines natural language processing, generative text functions, contract analytics, preference-based review logic and workflow integration. It can assist users by identifying relevant provisions, proposing edits and supporting the review process before approval. The expected impact is shorter contract-review cycles, more consistent application of drafting standards and reduced manual workload for routine legal-document tasks. The platform also has potential to improve collaboration between legal and business teams by making contract changes easier to understand and process. However, because the information provided acknowledges the possibility of AI errors, human legal supervision remains essential to validate outputs and ensure that proposed revisions are legally and commercially appropriate.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2223,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01655,EU_PSTW_202609_01655,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,https://www.harvey.ai/,R&D public order and safety.,"Nevertheless, its outputs must be reviewed by qualified professionals, because legal interpretation, strategic judgement and client advice cannot be delegated to automated systems.",Harvey - LLM for supporting contract analysis,https://www.harvey.ai/; Private sector; R&D public order and safety.; United Kingdom,"Harvey was reportedly rolled out by Allen & Overy and uses GPT-4 to help lawyers generate insights, recommendations and predictions.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Harvey is used in the legal sector to support contract analysis, due diligence, research and knowledge work across different practice areas. The business problem it addresses is the high volume and complexity of legal information that lawyers must review, compare and interpret, particularly in transactional matters, investigations or advisory work. Law firms and legal departments need tools that can accelerate document analysis while preserving expert oversight and professional responsibility. Harvey was reportedly rolled out by Allen & Overy and uses GPT-4 to help lawyers generate insights, recommendations and predictions. Its implementation draws on large language model capabilities for natural language understanding, document summarisation, information extraction, legal research support and analytical assistance. Conceptually, the platform can process legal materials, identify relevant issues, support due diligence questions, assist with drafting or research tasks and provide structured outputs for lawyer review. The expected impact is improved efficiency in document-heavy legal work, faster identification of relevant contractual or legal points, reduced time spent on repetitive analysis and stronger support for multi-practice knowledge work. By augmenting lawyers’ ability to navigate complex information, the tool can create business value through faster delivery and more scalable legal services. Nevertheless, its outputs must be reviewed by qualified professionals, because legal interpretation, strategic judgement and client advice cannot be delegated to automated systems.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2224,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01656,EU_PSTW_202609_01656,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Sofia,Transport,"Sofia faces persistent air pollution and traffic congestion, particularly in rapidly developing neighbourhoods with limited public transport coverage.",INNOAIR - Green public transport on-demand in the Municipality of Sofia,Municipality of Sofia; Local Government; Transport; Bulgaria,"The pilot provided residents with alternatives to private car use, contributing to reduced traffic volumes and lower emissions.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Sofia faces persistent air pollution and traffic congestion, particularly in rapidly developing neighbourhoods with limited public transport coverage. The INNOAIR project aimed to improve urban mobility and air quality by piloting greener, on-demand public transport services in the districts of Manastirski Livadi and Buxton. Coordinated by the Municipality of Sofia and managed by the Sofia Development Association, the initiative tested five electric minibuses with a capacity of 20 passengers each during a one-year trial starting in 2023. Advanced routing algorithms were used to determine optimal service routes based on municipal datasets, including registered vehicles, public facilities and demographic information, complemented by data gathered through community engagement and urban walks. While routes remained fixed once defined, departure times operated on a demand-responsive basis. The pilot provided residents with alternatives to private car use, contributing to reduced traffic volumes and lower emissions. The project demonstrated improved accessibility, enhanced user satisfaction and measurable environmental benefits, supporting scalable deployment of intelligent, low-emission mobility services.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2225,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01657,EU_PSTW_202609_01657,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Kortijk,General public services n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,A new virtual assistant for the city of Kortrijk,City of Kortijk; Local Government; General public services n.e.c.; Belgium,"To address this, the city implemented “De Virtuele Assistent”, a digital platform combining transactional chatbot functionality with advanced language processing capabilities.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The City of Kortrijk sought to enhance accessibility to municipal services and streamline citizen interactions as part of a broader regional digitalisation strategy supported by NextGenerationEU funding. Traditional service channels often require in-person visits or complex navigation of online portals. To address this, the city implemented “De Virtuele Assistent”, a digital platform combining transactional chatbot functionality with advanced language processing capabilities. The system enables residents, associations and businesses to obtain information about municipal services, book appointments and request official documents such as certificates and deeds through a single conversational interface. Integrated with existing administrative systems, the assistant supports automated processing of routine requests while escalating complex cases to human staff when necessary. Accessible via smartphone, tablet and desktop devices, the solution enhances inclusivity and service availability. Expected benefits include reduced administrative workload, faster response times and improved user experience, supporting more efficient and citizen-centred digital public services.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2226,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01658,EU_PSTW_202609_01658,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Vaasa,Transport,"Municipal engineering teams need timely, localised information on potholes, damaged signs, and other street defects, yet conventional inspections can miss issues or detect them only after delays. In Vaasa, the Fix the Streets initiative tackled this challenge by drawing directly on geo-located observations submitted by residents, turning citizen-generated reports into a richer operational picture of street conditions.",‘Fix the Streets’ - AI that leverages citizen-produced data to improve the streets,City of Vaasa; Local Government; Transport; Finland,"The objective is to improve infrastructure monitoring, support faster and better-targeted maintenance decisions, and strengthen the city’s use of digital tools within its wider twin-transition and innovation agenda.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Municipal engineering teams need timely, localised information on potholes, damaged signs, and other street defects, yet conventional inspections can miss issues or detect them only after delays. In Vaasa, the Fix the Streets initiative tackled this challenge by drawing directly on geo-located observations submitted by residents, turning citizen-generated reports into a richer operational picture of street conditions. The objective is to improve infrastructure monitoring, support faster and better-targeted maintenance decisions, and strengthen the city’s use of digital tools within its wider twin-transition and innovation agenda. The implementation combined a mobile application with a multi-format knowledge base built over two years from text, image, and video submissions. Working with Crowdsorsa, the Municipal Engineering Department used AI to analyse reported defects and linked the app with Vaasa’s digital signage and mapping systems for more precise localisation and follow-up. At a conceptual level, the solution joins crowdsourced data collection, geospatial mapping, and automated issue analysis in one workflow, while emphasising data minimisation in the handling of citizen input. The impact includes improved visibility of street health, more informed prioritisation of maintenance activity, and stronger evidence for infrastructure decisions. It also demonstrates a scalable model for combining citizen participation with digital monitoring to improve local public services.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S6_WORKFLOW_PROCESS_AUTOMATION|S7_GENERATIVE_CONTENT_CREATION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2227,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01659,EU_PSTW_202609_01659,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,AXA Climate,Environmental protection n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,Satellites for Wilderness Inspection and Forest Threat Tracking (SWIFTT),AXA Climate; Consortium; Environmental protection n.e.c.; Latvia,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Forest ecosystems face increasing risks from climate change, pests and biodiversity loss, yet monitoring large and remote areas remains resource-intensive. The SWIFTT project aims to provide forest managers with accessible and scientifically robust remote sensing tools to monitor and mitigate forest threats. The consortium integrates satellite imagery with advanced analytical models to detect changes in vegetation health, biodiversity indicators and environmental stress factors. Machine learning techniques process multi-temporal Earth observation data to generate risk maps and predictive assessments for forest management. The platform is designed to be user-friendly and cost-effective, enabling practical uptake by forestry professionals. Field validation and deployment are planned in large forest areas in Germany, France and Latvia to test operational feasibility. Anticipated impacts include improved early detection of forest degradation, enhanced biodiversity conservation and more informed climate mitigation strategies. The project strengthens data-driven forest governance and promotes sustainable ecosystem management across Europe.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2228,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01660,EU_PSTW_202609_01660,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Jammerbugt Municipality,Public order and safety n.e.c.,"The tool was developed to tackle the increasing risk of flooding due to extreme weather events, which poses challenges for municipalities, landowners, and urban planners in anticipating and mitigating damage. Existing forecasting tools lack sufficient localisation and lead time for effective decision-making.",The 'Wet Index’ project - an AI tool to predict local floods,Jammerbugt Municipality; Local Government; Public order and safety n.e.c.; Denmark,"The solution uses predictive modelling techniques applied to geospatial and climate data to forecast flood risks at a local level, providing up to 48 hours of advance warning.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The tool was developed to tackle the increasing risk of flooding due to extreme weather events, which poses challenges for municipalities, landowners, and urban planners in anticipating and mitigating damage. Existing forecasting tools lack sufficient localisation and lead time for effective decision-making. The objective is to provide accurate, localised early warnings to support proactive planning and risk management. The solution uses predictive modelling techniques applied to geospatial and climate data to forecast flood risks at a local level, providing up to 48 hours of advance warning. The system integrates multiple data sources and is developed in collaboration with research institutions, with conceptual integration into municipal planning and emergency response systems. The impact includes improved preparedness, reduced damage from flooding, and enhanced decision-making for urban and rural planning. It also increases reliability and scalability of forecasting capabilities, delivering significant value in risk reduction and public safety.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2229,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01661,EU_PSTW_202609_01661,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Nicosia Municipality,Sickness and disability.,UNKNOWN,Policies for Holistic Urban Mobility and Accessibility,Nicosia Municipality; Local Government; Sickness and disability.; Cyprus,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Urban congestion and accessibility barriers disproportionately affect people with disabilities, requiring targeted policy interventions informed by reliable data. The Municipality of Nicosia, within the AI4PublicPolicy initiative, developed a citizen-centric framework to design evidence-based mobility policies. The project focused on improving access to restricted streets during congestion and optimising parking availability for individuals with disabilities. Analytical models processed traffic data, Smart City platform information and inputs from License Plate Recognition cameras to assess access patterns and forecast congestion hotspots. Predictive tools supported dynamic policy adjustments to enhance mobility and parking accessibility. Co-creation workshops with local stakeholders ensured that policy design reflected community needs and practical constraints. The initiative enabled more precise targeting of interventions, reduced travel time for vulnerable users and strengthened inclusive urban planning. By integrating advanced analytics with participatory governance, the project contributes to fairer and more efficient mobility systems.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2230,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01662,EU_PSTW_202609_01662,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Lisbon Municipality,Sickness and disability.,"For visually impaired people, moving independently through unfamiliar indoor and outdoor spaces can be difficult, especially where transport, navigation, and points of interest are not presented in accessible ways. LisNav was developed to address this challenge by improving how users perceive and interact with their surroundings, with particular value in navigating public transport environments and urban spaces.",The AI-based LisNav application improves the lives of the visually impaired,Lisbon Municipality; Local Government; Sickness and disability.; Portugal,"Developed by 3FINERY, the solution applies AR-related capability in a way that supports practical, everyday mobility rather than generic mapping alone.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"For visually impaired people, moving independently through unfamiliar indoor and outdoor spaces can be difficult, especially where transport, navigation, and points of interest are not presented in accessible ways. LisNav was developed to address this challenge by improving how users perceive and interact with their surroundings, with particular value in navigating public transport environments and urban spaces. The objective is to increase independence, accessibility, and confidence by giving users real-time auditory support tailored to their location and immediate context. The implementation combines intelligent algorithms with real-time audio descriptions, drawing on publicly available datasets and user-contributed information such as local points of interest and transport details. In particular, the system provides information about bus stops and the wider bus-stop network, helping users orient themselves and travel more effectively. At a conceptual level, LisNav links location-aware data, accessibility-focused design, and audio interaction to create an augmented navigation layer for visually impaired users. Developed by 3FINERY, the solution applies AR-related capability in a way that supports practical, everyday mobility rather than generic mapping alone. The impact includes better access to transport information, greater autonomy in unfamiliar surroundings, and an improved user experience in navigating cities. It also offers a scalable digital-assistance model for inclusive mobility services.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2231,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01663,EU_PSTW_202609_01663,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Noisy-le-Grand Municipality,Fuel and energy,"Municipal buildings represent a significant share of urban energy consumption, yet fragmented monitoring systems limit proactive management of inefficiencies.",RECITAL' Project: Immediate and Long-Term Energy Reduction,Noisy-le-Grand Municipality; Local Government; Fuel and energy; France,The integration of sensing infrastructure with advanced analytics allows targeted optimisation of energy use and improved performance benchmarking.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Municipal buildings represent a significant share of urban energy consumption, yet fragmented monitoring systems limit proactive management of inefficiencies. The RECITAL project in Noisy-le-Grand introduced an “energy hypervisor” to centralise and analyse ten years of consumption data across municipal buildings. The system aggregates data from smart meters and IoT sensors installed in each facility, enabling daily tracking of electricity and heating usage. Analytical models detect anomalies and trigger alerts when overconsumption patterns occur, supporting rapid corrective action. Historical data analysis informs long-term renovation and decarbonisation strategies, while real-time monitoring enhances operational efficiency. The integration of sensing infrastructure with advanced analytics allows targeted optimisation of energy use and improved performance benchmarking. Expected outcomes include reduced energy expenditure, lower carbon emissions and improved sustainability planning. The project demonstrates how data-driven building management can accelerate municipal energy transition efforts and support climate neutrality objectives.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2232,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01664,EU_PSTW_202609_01664,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,INETUM,R&D economic affairs,"Small and medium-sized enterprises often lack the resources and expertise to implement robust cybersecurity measures, leaving them vulnerable to data breaches and operational disruptions.",CyberKit4SME - Democratizing a Cyber Security Toolkit for SMEs & Mes,INETUM; Consortium; R&D economic affairs; European Union,"CyberKit4SME aimed to provide accessible, affordable and easy-to-use tools enabling SMEs and mid-sized enterprises to better monitor, forecast and manage cyber risks.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Small and medium-sized enterprises often lack the resources and expertise to implement robust cybersecurity measures, leaving them vulnerable to data breaches and operational disruptions. CyberKit4SME aimed to provide accessible, affordable and easy-to-use tools enabling SMEs and mid-sized enterprises to better monitor, forecast and manage cyber risks. The project developed practical encryption and system isolation solutions tailored to constrained IT environments, ensuring data protection without requiring advanced in-house capabilities. In addition, blockchain-based mechanisms were introduced to facilitate secure sharing of threat intelligence and incident reports between companies and Computer Emergency Response Teams. The toolkit integrates risk awareness modules and monitoring functions within a user-friendly interface designed for non-specialist users. By lowering technical and financial barriers, the initiative enhances cyber resilience across supply chains. Expected impacts include improved threat detection, stronger collaboration with response authorities and reduced vulnerability of smaller enterprises to cyberattacks, contributing to a more secure digital economy.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2233,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01665,EU_PSTW_202609_01665,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,University Ca' Foscari,"General economic, commercial and labour affair","Urban food systems face challenges related to sustainability, traceability and coordination among diverse stakeholders.",CITIES2023 - Co-creating resIlient and susTaInable food systEms towardS FOOD2030,"University Ca' Foscari; Consortium; General economic, commercial and labour affair; European Union",UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Urban food systems face challenges related to sustainability, traceability and coordination among diverse stakeholders. The CITIES2030 project brought together researchers, entrepreneurs, civil society and municipalities to transform how food is produced, transported, supplied and reused within urban food system ecosystems. A blockchain-based, data-driven management platform was developed to create a digital twin of the urban food system, integrating information across supply chains. The platform enables secure data exchange, transparency of transactions and monitoring of sustainability indicators throughout the lifecycle of food products. Analytical tools process system-wide data to identify inefficiencies and support evidence-based policy interventions. By fostering collaboration and trust among partners, the project promotes citizen-centred and resilient food governance. Expected outcomes include reduced waste, improved traceability and enhanced sustainability performance. The initiative demonstrates how digital twin modelling and distributed ledger technologies can strengthen urban food system coordination and long-term resilience.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2234,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01666,EU_PSTW_202609_01666,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Universidade da Coruna,",General public services n.e.c.,"PILLAR Robots addresses a fundamental operational challenge in robotics: enabling robots to function with greater autonomy across different domains while ensuring that what they learn remains relevant to real deployment contexts. Many autonomous systems can acquire behaviours through training, yet they often struggle to generalise beyond narrow environments or to focus learning on what truly matters for practical applications.",PILLAR Robots - a project to increase the autonomy of AI-based robots,"Universidade da Coruna,; Consortium; General public services n.e.c.; European Union","This use case therefore involves robotics researchers, developers and application-oriented stakeholders seeking to improve the adaptability and usefulness of autonomous machines in real-world settings. The solution is implemented by operationalising the concept of “purpose”, drawn from cognitive science, within robotic cognitive architectures.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"PILLAR Robots addresses a fundamental operational challenge in robotics: enabling robots to function with greater autonomy across different domains while ensuring that what they learn remains relevant to real deployment contexts. Many autonomous systems can acquire behaviours through training, yet they often struggle to generalise beyond narrow environments or to focus learning on what truly matters for practical applications. This use case therefore involves robotics researchers, developers and application-oriented stakeholders seeking to improve the adaptability and usefulness of autonomous machines in real-world settings. Its objective is to help robots acquire the knowledge and capabilities needed for target applications more efficiently by giving them a stronger internal basis for selecting, prioritising and acting on relevant goals. The solution is implemented by operationalising the concept of “purpose”, drawn from cognitive science, within robotic cognitive architectures. At a conceptual level, the project develops algorithms for purpose acquisition, mechanisms to bias perceptual, motivational and decision systems towards purposeful behaviour, and learning strategies for representations, skills and models that support both deliberative and reactive decision-making. These elements are designed to work together so that learning is not only autonomous but also better aligned with task relevance. The expected impact is increased robot autonomy, stronger domain independence and more effective acquisition of usable skills. More broadly, the project creates value by improving the transferability, reliability and practical utility of autonomous robots in applied settings.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2237,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01667,EU_PSTW_202609_01667,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,DFKI,Environmental protection n.e.c.,"Monitoring marine and freshwater litter is essential for environmental protection, yet manual surveys are labour-intensive and inconsistent.",Aquatic Litter Drones,DFKI; Consortium; Environmental protection n.e.c.; Germany,This use case establishes a service that processes drone imagery to detect and classify floating litter in water bodies and on beaches.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Monitoring marine and freshwater litter is essential for environmental protection, yet manual surveys are labour-intensive and inconsistent. This use case establishes a service that processes drone imagery to detect and classify floating litter in water bodies and on beaches. Unmanned aerial vehicles conduct surveys at varying altitudes, generating high-resolution images that are uploaded to a digital platform. Two convolutional neural network models analyse the imagery to quantify and categorise observed waste, producing standardised datasets for environmental management. The system integrates image storage, processing and analytics into a unified workflow, enabling scalable monitoring across regions. Field applications in collaboration with international organisations have demonstrated operational feasibility and support for clean-up initiatives. The approach enhances accuracy, comparability and timeliness of litter assessments. By providing actionable data for policymakers and local stakeholders, the solution strengthens environmental monitoring, supports targeted interventions and contributes to marine ecosystem preservation.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,S7_GENERATIVE_CONTENT_CREATION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2240,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01668,EU_PSTW_202609_01668,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,SignAvatar,General public services n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,SignAvatar – AI Speech to SignLangauge Translator at the Belgrade airport,SignAvatar; Private sector; General public services n.e.c.; Serbia,"At Belgrade Nikola Tesla Airport, this use case was introduced to improve accessibility by translating passenger announcements into sign language and making key travel information easier to understand within the terminal environment. The implementation uses SignAvatar’s TransportSign solution, which automatically converts speech and text into sign-language output displayed on screens.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Hearing-impaired travellers can be excluded from essential transport information when announcements are delivered only through spoken audio or standard text displays that do not fully meet their communication needs. At Belgrade Nikola Tesla Airport, this use case was introduced to improve accessibility by translating passenger announcements into sign language and making key travel information easier to understand within the terminal environment. The objective is to reduce communication barriers, improve passenger experience for deaf and hard-of-hearing users, and demonstrate how inclusive announcement systems can be integrated into major transport hubs. The implementation uses SignAvatar’s TransportSign solution, which automatically converts speech and text into sign-language output displayed on screens. During the airport trial, five screens were installed across the terminal, and the same solution has also been adopted at Belgrade’s central railway station. At a conceptual level, the system combines speech and text processing with sign-language generation so that operational messages can be communicated in a more accessible visual form. The impact includes improved accessibility of real-time passenger information, a more inclusive travel environment, and reduced dependence on ad hoc assistance for understanding announcements. It also provides a scalable model for transport operators seeking to make station and airport communications more equitable and user-centred.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2242,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01669,EU_PSTW_202609_01669,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Aarhus Municipality,General public services n.e.c.,"Where the chatbot cannot provide sufficient help, it can offer a callback from a call-centre agent.",Inter-municipal Chatbot MUNI - Aarhus,Aarhus Municipality; Local Government; General public services n.e.c.; Denmark,"Citizens often find municipal websites and self-service systems difficult to navigate, particularly when they are unsure which service they need or how to complete a process such as obtaining a passport, using MitID, or arranging childcare.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Citizens often find municipal websites and self-service systems difficult to navigate, particularly when they are unsure which service they need or how to complete a process such as obtaining a passport, using MitID, or arranging childcare. MUNI was created to improve this interface between citizens and local government by offering a more conversational and accessible route into municipal services across a large inter-municipal collaboration in Denmark. The objective is to supplement or partially replace traditional self-service pathways with more efficient and user-friendly AI-supported interaction, while ensuring that people can still reach a human adviser if the automated route does not resolve their need. The implementation combines chatbot technology with integrations into municipal call-centre and self-service systems, including Puzzel and Selvbetjening.nu, and more recent descriptions present MUNI as an AI-driven chat and voicebot. Public descriptions of the project report collaboration across 36 municipalities, with the potential to expand further, and note that since launch citizens have rated the service highly, averaging 2.43 out of 3. Where the chatbot cannot provide sufficient help, it can offer a callback from a call-centre agent. The impact includes faster access to municipal guidance, better user experience, and stronger scalability of citizen support across participating authorities.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2243,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01670,EU_PSTW_202609_01670,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""social_nonprofit""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Itla Children's foundation,Family and children.,"Itla View is a digital tool to help local services address the fragmentation of information across services supporting children, young people, and families, where stakeholders lack a shared, up-to-date understanding of well-being and service needs.",Itla View – Crowdsourcing and AI-based digital tool for knowledge management and systems change in public sector,Itla Children's foundation; Non-governmental; Family and children.; Finland,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Itla View is a digital tool to help local services address the fragmentation of information across services supporting children, young people, and families, where stakeholders lack a shared, up-to-date understanding of well-being and service needs. This limits the ability to identify risks early and coordinate preventive actions. The objective is to create a shared situational awareness to support proactive and coordinated service delivery. The solution aggregates observations from field actors with indicator data and contextual information such as local news, applying analytical techniques to identify patterns and relationships across datasets. The system enables visualisation and interpretation of combined insights, supporting decision-making across organisations. It is conceptually integrated into local service ecosystems to facilitate collaboration and data sharing. The impact includes improved early identification of needs, stronger preventive interventions, and enhanced coordination among stakeholders. It also supports more efficient resource allocation, better service outcomes, and long-term system-level improvements in public sector effectiveness.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2244,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01671,EU_PSTW_202609_01671,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Transport for London Freight Lab,Transport,"Public transport and freight operators in London face a persistent road safety challenge in complex urban environments, where interactions between buses, heavy vehicles, pedestrians, cyclists, and other road users can change rapidly and create significant risk.",Humanising Authonomy - Behavioural AI to improve the safety of public transport,Transport for London Freight Lab; Local Government; Transport; United Kingdom,"Within the context of Transport for London’s Freight Lab and the wider ambition to meet Vision Zero by 2041, this use case aims to strengthen vehicle safety standards and reduce deaths and serious injuries by improving how vehicles detect and respond to human behaviour. The implementation uses real-time intent prediction software that analyses live camera feeds to interpret and anticipate the behaviour of pedestrians, passengers, and surrounding vehicles.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Public transport and freight operators in London face a persistent road safety challenge in complex urban environments, where interactions between buses, heavy vehicles, pedestrians, cyclists, and other road users can change rapidly and create significant risk. Within the context of Transport for London’s Freight Lab and the wider ambition to meet Vision Zero by 2041, this use case aims to strengthen vehicle safety standards and reduce deaths and serious injuries by improving how vehicles detect and respond to human behaviour. The stakeholders include transport authorities, fleet operators, drivers, vehicle technology providers, and road users. The implementation uses real-time intent prediction software that analyses live camera feeds to interpret and anticipate the behaviour of pedestrians, passengers, and surrounding vehicles. Behavioural models and computer vision techniques infer likely movement patterns and provide inputs to vehicle decision systems, supporting functions such as automated emergency braking, front and near-side alerts, and rear-view camera monitoring. The solution is designed to integrate with different levels of vehicle autonomy, from driver assistance in conventional vehicles to more automated environments. The expected impact includes faster hazard recognition, improved driver awareness, and more reliable preventive action, contributing to safer streets, better compliance with evolving safety standards, and reduced operational and social costs linked to collisions.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2245,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01672,EU_PSTW_202609_01672,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Berlin,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.",UNKNOWN,Generative AI Assistant PARLA for the City of Berlin,"City of Berlin; Local Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Germany","The implementation uses AI to search and synthesise information from more than 10,000 public documents, including answers to written parliamentary questions and main committee procedures, and presents results through a question-answer interface.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Public administrations and political institutions generate thousands of documents, but finding the right information quickly across parliamentary papers, committee procedures, and written questions can be time-consuming for civil servants, journalists, associations, and citizens alike. In Berlin, PARLA was developed to make this documentary landscape more searchable and intelligible by supporting research across a large corpus of public documents. The objective is to reduce the effort involved in navigating administrative and parliamentary records, improve access to public information, and provide answer suggestions that remain traceable to their documentary sources. The implementation uses AI to search and synthesise information from more than 10,000 public documents, including answers to written parliamentary questions and main committee procedures, and presents results through a question-answer interface. The system generates proposed answers based on the underlying text and cites the documents used, while also warning users that generated responses are not legally binding and should be checked against the linked sources. At a conceptual level, PARLA combines retrieval, synthesis, and source referencing in one research assistant for public documentation. The impact includes quicker information retrieval, improved transparency through traceable sourcing, and better support for daily work in parliament and administration, while also making complex documentary repositories more usable for external stakeholders.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2252,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01673,EU_PSTW_202609_01673,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,OFFIS - Institute for Computer Science,Sickness and disability.,"For blind and visually impaired people, digital images often remain difficult to access in a meaningful way, because standard image analysis tools are typically designed around the assumptions and preferences of sighted users.",ABILITY - Improving the accessibility of digital images for blind users,OFFIS - Institute for Computer Science; Consortium; Sickness and disability.; Germany,"The objective of this use case is to improve accessibility by developing image interpretation tools tailored to the needs, expectations, and sensory strategies of blind and visually impaired users, with these users involved directly as co-designers and evaluators.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"For blind and visually impaired people, digital images often remain difficult to access in a meaningful way, because standard image analysis tools are typically designed around the assumptions and preferences of sighted users. This creates a gap between what current systems can technically describe and what users actually need in order to form an accurate and useful mental representation of visual content. The objective of this use case is to improve accessibility by developing image interpretation tools tailored to the needs, expectations, and sensory strategies of blind and visually impaired users, with these users involved directly as co-designers and evaluators. The implementation combines computer vision, large language models, and visual-assistant language models with extensive user studies, tactile interaction research, and iterative testing. The partners are also developing dedicated hardware, including a tactile tablet and a pin-display device capable of presenting simplified object outlines, zoomed elements, and other selected outputs derived from image analysis. The system concept therefore links multimodal models with tactile interfaces and user-centred evaluation methods. The anticipated results include more relevant and nuanced image descriptions, improved usability and trust, and a more inclusive digital experience. More broadly, the project supports accessible design innovation, stronger user adoption, and better alignment between emerging image technologies and real user needs.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2253,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01674,EU_PSTW_202609_01674,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Advanced Bionics,R&D health.,"In audiological, occupational health, and cardiological care, clinicians often rely on fragmented observations and periodic assessments, which can limit their ability to identify emerging issues early and respond with appropriately tailored interventions.",A2I - Networked hearing system with innovative sensors,Advanced Bionics; Consortium; R&D health.; Germany,"The purpose of this use case is to create a user-centred socio-technical system that improves prevention, diagnosis, and care processes by using sensor-based data gathered in and around the ear.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"In audiological, occupational health, and cardiological care, clinicians often rely on fragmented observations and periodic assessments, which can limit their ability to identify emerging issues early and respond with appropriately tailored interventions. This is particularly relevant where hearing-related data may also provide insights into wider physiological conditions, yet such information is not routinely captured or connected across care pathways. The purpose of this use case is to create a user-centred socio-technical system that improves prevention, diagnosis, and care processes by using sensor-based data gathered in and around the ear. The implementation combines innovative hearing-related sensors with data analysis methods to process continuous or repeated health signals collected from wearable or ear-based devices. Analytical models are used to detect patterns relevant to audiological status, occupational exposure, and cardiological indicators, while the overall architecture links sensing technologies, health data processing, and evaluation workflows in a conceptually integrated system. The development approach is explicitly user-focused, ensuring that the technical design reflects clinical and practical needs. The expected impact includes earlier identification of health risks, better continuity of care, and more efficient clinical decision-making. Additional value lies in improved service integration, stronger preventive capacity, and the potential to scale monitoring and assessment across multiple healthcare contexts with greater reliability and personalisation.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2254,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01675,EU_PSTW_202609_01675,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Leonard,R&D environmental protection.,"Building renovation projects frequently encounter delays, cost overruns and vulnerability to changing environmental conditions, undermining long-term sustainability objectives. The system supports decision-makers in selecting renovation strategies that minimise delays and reduce the need for premature re-renovation.",RenovAlte - Resilient planning and design of renovation projects,Leonard; Consortium; R&D environmental protection.; Germany,"The project creates a simulation environment capable of modelling renovation scenarios using sensor data and predictive analytics. Expected benefits include improved project efficiency, enhanced climate resilience and more sustainable use of public and private resources in the built environment.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Building renovation projects frequently encounter delays, cost overruns and vulnerability to changing environmental conditions, undermining long-term sustainability objectives. RenovAIte aims to develop resilient planning methodologies for housing renovation and infrastructure maintenance through advanced simulation and data-driven analysis. The project creates a simulation environment capable of modelling renovation scenarios using sensor data and predictive analytics. Adversarial resilience learning techniques are applied to evaluate potential disruptions and optimise project design under uncertain conditions. The system supports decision-makers in selecting renovation strategies that minimise delays and reduce the need for premature re-renovation. Integrated monitoring of streets and buildings enhances situational awareness and long-term asset management. By combining simulation modelling with adaptive optimisation, the project strengthens robustness of renovation planning. Expected benefits include improved project efficiency, enhanced climate resilience and more sustainable use of public and private resources in the built environment.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2256,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01676,EU_PSTW_202609_01676,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Koblenz University of Applied Sciences,Waste water management.,"Municipal authorities require tools to plan emergency drainage routes and improve resilience, but traditional hydraulic modelling approaches are complex, time-intensive, and difficult to scale.",FloReST - Intelligent tools for urban flood resilience,Koblenz University of Applied Sciences; Consortium; Waste water management.; Germany,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Increasing frequency of heavy rainfall events has exposed urban areas to significant risks of flash flooding, causing damage to infrastructure and disrupting communities. Municipal authorities require tools to plan emergency drainage routes and improve resilience, but traditional hydraulic modelling approaches are complex, time-intensive, and difficult to scale. The FloReST project, funded by the German Federal Ministry of Education and Research, aims to enhance urban flood resilience by providing decision-support tools tailored to local conditions. The solution combines hydraulic-numerical modelling with machine learning techniques to simulate and optimise emergency water drainage pathways across urban environments. These models are supported by data from terrain, infrastructure, and environmental sources, enabling scalable analysis of larger catchment areas. The project also includes user-oriented applications designed to support municipalities and infrastructure operators in planning and response. Pilot testing of a smart application with end users took place in 2024. The approach demonstrates improved modelling efficiency and usability, supporting better preparedness and risk mitigation, although full operational deployment remains under evaluation.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2257,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01677,EU_PSTW_202609_01677,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,DINUM - Direction interministérielle du numérique,General public services n.e.c.,"Front-line public-service advisers often need to consult large volumes of guidance and partner documentation in order to answer citizen questions accurately, which can slow down interviews and reduce the time available for direct support.",Albert France Services - GenAI tool to support public servants,DINUM - Direction interministérielle du numérique; Central-Government; General public services n.e.c.; France,"In the France Services version, the tool uses a natural-language response engine that draws on documentation from France Services partners and practical fact sheets from servicepublic.fr, returning sourced answers in French through a search-style interface.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Front-line public-service advisers often need to consult large volumes of guidance and partner documentation in order to answer citizen questions accurately, which can slow down interviews and reduce the time available for direct support. Albert France Services was introduced to assist advisers in the France Services network by giving them quicker access to reliable information tailored to the user situation they are handling. The objective is to support public servants rather than replace them, improve the speed and consistency of responses, and make it easier to navigate the documentation used in day-to-day case support. The implementation forms part of the broader Albert initiative developed by DINUM and Etalab as a sovereign, open, and secure generative AI capability for public administration. In the France Services version, the tool uses a natural-language response engine that draws on documentation from France Services partners and practical fact sheets from servicepublic.fr, returning sourced answers in French through a search-style interface. More broadly, official materials describe Albert as an interministerial inference platform hosted in a secure sovereign environment. The impact includes faster retrieval of dependable information, better support for advisers handling varied citizen needs, and improved operational efficiency in front-line public service. It also creates a reusable model for scaled adoption of generative AI across administrations.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F06_HIGH_VOLUME_SCALABILITY_BOTTLENECK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2259,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01678,EU_PSTW_202609_01678,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""social_nonprofit"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Ministry of Transport and Communications (LVM),"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.",UNKNOWN,GenAI for Legislative Drafting - Ministry of Transports,"Ministry of Transport and Communications (LVM); Central-Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Finland",The objective of the use case was to test whether a practical support service could help drafters navigate legislative content more effectively and retrieve relevant information in a structured manner.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Legislative drafting requires civil servants to examine large volumes of legal, policy, and background material, making the process time-consuming and cognitively demanding, particularly when consistency, traceability, and language quality must be maintained throughout. In this context, the Ministry of Transport and Communications sought to improve the efficiency of drafting work while also reinforcing Finland’s strategic autonomy in digital capability. The objective of the use case was to test whether a practical support service could help drafters navigate legislative content more effectively and retrieve relevant information in a structured manner. The implementation consisted of a service demonstrator built by Futurice around Finnish-language large language models, delivered through a chatbot-style interface. At a conceptual level, the solution allowed legislative drafters to query legislation and related materials in natural language, with the model supporting information retrieval, question answering, and synthesis over large text collections. The system design explored how domestic language models could be incorporated into policy workflows while remaining aligned with public sector requirements. The results indicate potential for reducing time spent searching and reviewing documentation, improving accessibility of complex legal material, and supporting more efficient drafting processes. The wider business value includes strengthened experimentation capacity, greater technological sovereignty, and a foundation for more scalable digital support in public administration.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2260,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01679,EU_PSTW_202609_01679,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Prime Minister's Office (VNK),"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.","This creates an operational challenge in terms of workload, speed, and consistency, especially when consultation material is extensive and diverse in style or content.",GenAI for Legislative Drafting - Prime Minister's Office,"Prime Minister's Office (VNK); Central-Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Finland",The purpose of this use case was to assess whether automated support could help the Prime Minister’s Office process consultation responses more efficiently and accelerate the preparation of summary material for legislative work.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Analysing statements submitted during consultation rounds is a demanding part of legislative preparation, as officials must review substantial volumes of written feedback, identify recurring themes, and produce coherent summaries under time pressure. This creates an operational challenge in terms of workload, speed, and consistency, especially when consultation material is extensive and diverse in style or content. The purpose of this use case was to assess whether automated support could help the Prime Minister’s Office process consultation responses more efficiently and accelerate the preparation of summary material for legislative work. The implementation tested the Finnish Poro language model in a summarisation setting, with the experiment carried out by Silo Gen AI Oy and funded by Sitra. At a conceptual level, the approach used a Finnish large language model to generate condensed summaries from consultation texts, exploring how language technology could support document analysis in a policy environment. The trial focused on the model’s ability to synthesise large volumes of textual input into usable outputs for expert review. The results showed clear potential for increasing efficiency in the analysis of consultation rounds, even though the summaries produced were not yet of sufficient quality for operational adoption. Even so, the exercise generated valuable learning on model limitations, future capability needs, and opportunities to improve productivity, scalability, and evidence handling in legislative processes.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2261,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01680,EU_PSTW_202609_01680,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Heidelberg,General public services n.e.c.,"Municipal residents often need simple guidance on everyday administrative matters, yet city websites can be difficult to navigate when users do not know the exact terminology or service path they need.",LUMI - the Made-in-Europe chatbot for Heidelberg,City of Heidelberg; Local Government; General public services n.e.c.; Germany,"The implementation uses large language models developed by Heidelberg-based company Aleph Alpha and draws on publicly available information from the city of Heidelberg to generate responses. At a conceptual level, the solution combines a public information base with an LLM-powered conversational interface designed for municipal use.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Municipal residents often need simple guidance on everyday administrative matters, yet city websites can be difficult to navigate when users do not know the exact terminology or service path they need. Heidelberg introduced Lumi to create a more natural and convenient way for citizens to interact with the city through a chat-based interface rather than conventional menu navigation. The objective is to support citizens with routine administrative tasks, improve access to public information, and learn from real interactions in order to refine a more tailored digital-assistance service for the city. The implementation uses large language models developed by Heidelberg-based company Aleph Alpha and draws on publicly available information from the city of Heidelberg to generate responses. The city explains that Lumi can already help with issues such as changing address within Heidelberg or finding waste-paper collection times, and that after an internal testing phase it was opened to citizens so the system could learn what residents ask and how they phrase requests. At a conceptual level, the solution combines a public information base with an LLM-powered conversational interface designed for municipal use. The impact includes easier access to city information, a more intuitive user experience, and better scalability of first-line citizen support, while also giving the city a practical way to experiment with European AI capability in public services.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2262,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01681,EU_PSTW_202609_01681,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Rhineland-Palatinate Development Agency,General public services n.e.c.,"Agencies that run many projects and programmes often hold relevant information across numerous internal and public materials, making it difficult for users to obtain quick, targeted answers about activities, tasks, and ongoing work. It also offers a practical model for limited-scope deployment of generative AI in public-sector communication and knowledge access.",GPT-enabled chatbot of the Rhineland-Palatinate Development Agency,Rhineland-Palatinate Development Agency; Regional Government; General public services n.e.c.; Germany,"The implementation uses a GPT-enabled assistant that has been specially configured with information about the agency’s own activities, rather than relying only on the general background knowledge of the underlying model.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Agencies that run many projects and programmes often hold relevant information across numerous internal and public materials, making it difficult for users to obtain quick, targeted answers about activities, tasks, and ongoing work. The Rhineland-Palatinate Development Agency addressed this issue by experimenting with an AI assistant that responds to questions about the agency’s projects and remit through its website. The objective is to improve access to organisational information, reduce the effort required to search through scattered material, and test how conversational interfaces can support communication about public development activity. The implementation uses a GPT-enabled assistant that has been specially configured with information about the agency’s own activities, rather than relying only on the general background knowledge of the underlying model. At a conceptual level, the system combines a large language model with agency-specific material to generate answers about projects and responsibilities through a web-based conversational interface. The impact includes easier access to relevant information, quicker resolution of routine informational queries, and a more user-friendly digital front door for stakeholders interested in the agency’s work. It also offers a practical model for limited-scope deployment of generative AI in public-sector communication and knowledge access.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2263,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01682,EU_PSTW_202609_01682,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Zurich,General public services n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,ZüriCityGPT,City of Zurich; Local Government; General public services n.e.c.; Switzerland,"The implementation uses a chatbot grounded in content published on the City of Zurich website; public descriptions of the project explain that it can answer questions based on that material and also offers a version linked to the Swiss LLM Apertus. The impact includes a more accessible user experience, quicker access to relevant local information, and a scalable way to make complex city websites easier to use.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"People seeking information about city administration often face a familiar problem: official websites contain a great deal of useful content, but search and navigation do not always produce direct answers to practical questions. ZüriCityGPT addresses this by offering a conversational layer over publicly available information about Zurich, allowing users to ask questions in natural language and receive concise answers rather than a list of links alone. The objective is to make local administrative information easier to access, reduce friction in finding relevant guidance, and demonstrate how city-related public information can be made more usable through a question-answer interface. The implementation uses a chatbot grounded in content published on the City of Zurich website; public descriptions of the project explain that it can answer questions based on that material and also offers a version linked to the Swiss LLM Apertus. At a conceptual level, the solution combines retrieval over a defined public corpus with generative answering to provide more precise responses about city services and local administration. The impact includes a more accessible user experience, quicker access to relevant local information, and a scalable way to make complex city websites easier to use. Public project information also makes clear that the tool is Zurich-focused but not officially affiliated with the City of Zurich.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2264,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01683,EU_PSTW_202609_01683,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Netherlands Board of Tourism & Conventions,Community development.,"Streets in many cities remain dominated by cars, limited greenery, and fragmented cycling infrastructure, making it difficult for residents and planners to imagine what a healthier, more bicycle-friendly street could look like in practice. The DutchCycling Lifestyle AI project addresses this challenge by giving people a simple way to visualise how an existing street might be transformed into a greener and more cycle-oriented environment inspired by Dutch design principles.",DutchCycling - Cycling Lifestyle AI,Netherlands Board of Tourism & Conventions; Consortium; Community development.; Netherlands,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Streets in many cities remain dominated by cars, limited greenery, and fragmented cycling infrastructure, making it difficult for residents and planners to imagine what a healthier, more bicycle-friendly street could look like in practice. The DutchCycling Lifestyle AI project addresses this challenge by giving people a simple way to visualise how an existing street might be transformed into a greener and more cycle-oriented environment inspired by Dutch design principles. The objective is to stimulate public imagination, support discussion on liveability and active mobility, and make the Dutch cycling approach more tangible for users anywhere in the world. The implementation combines generative AI with Google Street View imagery and the expertise of Dutch cycling specialists, allowing users to enter an address, select or upload a street view, and generate alternative visualisations of a more sustainable street layout. At a conceptual level, the tool links image-based generation with mobility-design knowledge to create plausible illustrative scenarios rather than detailed engineering plans. Public project material also makes clear that the tool was designed primarily as an inspiration device and that the concept has since been made open source after the original public tool was taken offline. The impact lies in widening public engagement, making policy ideas easier to communicate visually, and providing a scalable digital method for promoting cycling-friendly urban transformation.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2265,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01684,EU_PSTW_202609_01684,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Delft University of Technology Faculty of Technology, Policy and Management",Transport,UNKNOWN,IVORY – ‘AI for Vision Zero in Road Safety’,"Delft University of Technology Faculty of Technology, Policy and Management; Consortium; Transport; Netherlands","In support of the United Nations target to halve traffic deaths by 2030 and the European Vision Zero strategy to eliminate road fatalities by 2050, this use case aims to build a robust framework for responsible innovation in road safety while developing specialist expertise across sectors.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Achieving major reductions in road fatalities requires more than isolated technical improvements, as road safety depends on the interaction between vehicles, infrastructure, human behaviour, governance, and public trust. In support of the United Nations target to halve traffic deaths by 2030 and the European Vision Zero strategy to eliminate road fatalities by 2050, this use case aims to build a robust framework for responsible innovation in road safety while developing specialist expertise across sectors. The stakeholders include universities, industrial partners, associated organisations, researchers, and road safety actors from ten countries. The implementation takes the form of an industrial doctoral network bringing together engineering, data science, and ethics of technology to develop new responsible AI applications for road-user assistance and infrastructure safety management. The programme integrates research training, cross-sector collaboration, and applied experimentation, with 13 researchers working on solutions that combine advanced analytical methods, domain knowledge, and governance considerations. Rather than a single product, the system is a coordinated innovation and learning platform designed to connect research outputs with real-world road safety needs. The expected impact includes stronger interdisciplinary capability, improved methods for safer transport systems, and a more scalable pipeline of expertise. It also creates long-term value through knowledge transfer, international collaboration, and more reliable foundations for future road safety innovation.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2266,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01685,EU_PSTW_202609_01685,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,UNIVERSITAT JAUME I DE CASTELLON,Education n.e.c.,"The e-DIPLOMA project was initiated in response to challenges identified during the rapid transition to online learning caused by the COVID-19 pandemic, including disparities in access to technology, insufficient teacher training, and limitations in existing digital learning environments.","DIPLOMA Project - Electronic, Didactic and Innovative Platform for Learning based On Multimedia Assets",UNIVERSITAT JAUME I DE CASTELLON; Consortium; Education n.e.c.; Spain,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The e-DIPLOMA project was initiated in response to challenges identified during the rapid transition to online learning caused by the COVID-19 pandemic, including disparities in access to technology, insufficient teacher training, and limitations in existing digital learning environments. The objective is to enhance the quality, inclusiveness, and effectiveness of distance education across Europe by developing a next-generation e-learning platform. The project adopts a co-creation approach involving educators, students, families, and policymakers to ensure that the platform addresses real user needs. The solution integrates multiple advanced technologies, including immersive environments, intelligent tutoring components, interactive content delivery, and conversational interfaces, to create engaging and adaptive learning experiences. It also incorporates analytics to monitor learning progress and personalise content. As an ongoing EU-funded research and innovation project, the platform is under development and validation. Expected impacts include improved learner engagement, accessibility, and pedagogical outcomes, although large-scale deployment results are not yet available.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2267,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01686,EU_PSTW_202609_01686,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Federal Ministry of Education, Science and Research ( BMBWF )",Secondary education.,"Schools need accessible, up-to-date teaching materials on artificial intelligence, yet many teachers and families lack the time or specialist expertise to assemble reliable resources independently. The official course page describes the section as an overview of freely available teaching materials for lower and upper secondary education, designed to help schools address both the opportunities and the challenges raised by AI in everyday life and work.",Eduthek - The learning platform on AI for schools,"Federal Ministry of Education, Science and Research ( BMBWF ); Central-Government; Secondary education.; Austria","The implementation uses the Eduthek platform as a central repository and delivery channel for learning content, with a specific AI focus area that is continuously expanded and updated. At a conceptual level, the solution combines digital curation, thematic organisation, and open access to support classroom use without requiring each school to build its own material base.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Schools need accessible, up-to-date teaching materials on artificial intelligence, yet many teachers and families lack the time or specialist expertise to assemble reliable resources independently. Austria’s Eduthek responds to this by providing a dedicated digital space within the Ministry’s wider learning platform where teachers, pupils, and parents can access curated material on AI in a structured and reusable format. The objective is to improve AI literacy in schools, support teaching on a fast-changing topic, and make high-quality educational resources easier to find and apply across different levels of schooling. The implementation uses the Eduthek platform as a central repository and delivery channel for learning content, with a specific AI focus area that is continuously expanded and updated. The official course page describes the section as an overview of freely available teaching materials for lower and upper secondary education, designed to help schools address both the opportunities and the challenges raised by AI in everyday life and work. At a conceptual level, the solution combines digital curation, thematic organisation, and open access to support classroom use without requiring each school to build its own material base. The impact includes broader access to AI-related teaching resources, more consistent support for educators, and a scalable way to strengthen digital and technological understanding across the school system.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2268,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01687,EU_PSTW_202609_01687,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"UK Government - Department of Science, Innovation and Technology",General services.,"Public services often suffer from fragmented data, repetitive administrative work, and slow internal processes, which can delay service delivery and reduce the time civil servants can spend on higher-value tasks. Humphrey was introduced by the UK government to address these operational bottlenecks across the civil service by improving productivity, reducing reliance on manual document handling, and supporting faster, more consistent decision-support processes.","Humphrey - AI tool to streamline public services, eliminate delays through improved data sharing, and reduce costs","UK Government - Department of Science, Innovation and Technology; Central-Government; General services.; United Kingdom",UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Public services often suffer from fragmented data, repetitive administrative work, and slow internal processes, which can delay service delivery and reduce the time civil servants can spend on higher-value tasks. Humphrey was introduced by the UK government to address these operational bottlenecks across the civil service by improving productivity, reducing reliance on manual document handling, and supporting faster, more consistent decision-support processes. The objective is to modernise government operations, improve internal efficiency, and reduce costs through a shared suite of AI tools rather than isolated pilots in individual departments. The implementation consists of a family of government-built AI tools developed by the Incubator for AI for tasks such as summarisation, consultation analysis, and administrative support, with civil servants interacting through secure digital interfaces. Public government sources show that Humphrey has already been used to review consultation responses and, in 2025, was also trialled with 25 councils to support work in areas such as planning and social care, indicating that it has moved beyond concept stage into active experimentation and deployment. The impact includes faster processing of large document volumes, reduced administrative burden, improved scalability of internal government work, and potential savings in consultancy and operational costs, while also building a reusable AI capability across public administration.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK|F07_DATA_VISIBILITY_INTEGRATION_GAP,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2269,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01688,EU_PSTW_202609_01688,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""social_nonprofit"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"UK Government - Department of Science, Innovation and Technology",General public services n.e.c.,"Citizens often need to interact with multiple government services through separate websites and processes, making routine tasks time-consuming and difficult to navigate.",GOV.UK App chatbot,"UK Government - Department of Science, Innovation and Technology; Central-Government; General public services n.e.c.; United Kingdom",The implementation combines the GOV.UK App with GOV.UK One Login and an AI-powered GOV.UK Chat feature that uses generative AI to answer questions based on GOV.UK guidance.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Citizens often need to interact with multiple government services through separate websites and processes, making routine tasks time-consuming and difficult to navigate. The GOV.UK App was created to simplify this experience by giving people a single mobile entry point to government services, while the associated GOV.UK Chat capability is intended to make complex information easier to access through conversational interaction. The objective is to improve usability, reduce friction in service navigation, and provide faster access to relevant guidance across government. The implementation combines the GOV.UK App with GOV.UK One Login and an AI-powered GOV.UK Chat feature that uses generative AI to answer questions based on GOV.UK guidance. Official UK sources state that GOV.UK Chat was piloted with 10,000 business users, answered almost 24,000 questions, achieved relevant answers in about 90 per cent of cases, and blocked all tested prompt-manipulation attempts; the government’s algorithmic transparency record also confirms it as an active GDS system. The impact includes more accessible digital public services, quicker resolution of routine information needs, and a more scalable way to guide users through government content, while creating a foundation for more personalised and proactive service delivery through the app.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2270,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01689,EU_PSTW_202609_01689,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Italian National Institute for Insurance against Accidents at Work (INAIL),Social protection n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,AI Applications within INAIL,Italian National Institute for Insurance against Accidents at Work (INAIL); Central-Government; Social protection n.e.c.; Italy,"INAIL’s AI programme addresses this challenge by using data-driven tools to improve operational efficiency, prevention capability, and service quality across several functions rather than in a single isolated application. The objective is to create public value by modernising workflows, improving decision support, and embedding stronger governance for current and future AI use.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"A large public insurance and prevention body such as INAIL must manage diverse tasks ranging from workplace-accident prevention and grant administration to customer service, medical evaluation, fraud control, and legal support, all under strong regulatory and public-value constraints. INAIL’s AI programme addresses this challenge by using data-driven tools to improve operational efficiency, prevention capability, and service quality across several functions rather than in a single isolated application. The objective is to create public value by modernising workflows, improving decision support, and embedding stronger governance for current and future AI use. The implementation includes adopted and developing AI systems across areas such as predictive analysis for accident prevention, customer-service enquiries, automation of ISI grant-management processes, medical and expert assessment support, fraud detection, knowledge-base management, legal assistance, and IT costing. Recent INAIL public material on its 2025–2027 prevention strategy and innovation agenda confirms that AI and related digital technologies remain part of its active prevention and transformation work. The impact includes better planning, more targeted prevention, faster handling of routine processes, and more scalable service delivery, while also supporting stronger institutional learning and improved alignment between prevention, operational efficiency, and user experience.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION|S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2272,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01690,EU_PSTW_202609_01690,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Fondazione Bruno Kessler,Transport,"The AI@EDGE project addresses challenges in managing traffic involving cooperative, connected, and automated vehicles (CCAVs), particularly in complex scenarios such as roundabouts where coordination and safety remain difficult. Inefficient negotiation of such intersections can lead to congestion, increased accident risk, and higher emissions.",AI @ EDGE: advancing traffic management for cooperative connected and automated vehicles,Fondazione Bruno Kessler; Consortium; Transport; Italy,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The AI@EDGE project addresses challenges in managing traffic involving cooperative, connected, and automated vehicles (CCAVs), particularly in complex scenarios such as roundabouts where coordination and safety remain difficult. Inefficient negotiation of such intersections can lead to congestion, increased accident risk, and higher emissions. The objective is to improve traffic flow and safety by leveraging advanced communication and computing infrastructures. The solution combines edge computing and 5G network capabilities with machine learning models to enable real-time decision-making and coordination among vehicles and infrastructure. These models simulate and optimise vehicle interactions, particularly at bottlenecks, by processing data locally at the network edge to reduce latency. The system supports virtual validation environments to test traffic scenarios and control strategies. As an ongoing research project, the approach is being developed and tested, with expected benefits including improved traffic efficiency, reduced accidents, and lower environmental impact, although real-world deployment remains limited.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2273,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01691,EU_PSTW_202609_01691,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Turin,Public order and safety n.e.c.,"Local authorities need timely information on how public spaces are being used, especially when crowding, footfall, or unsafe proximity may affect public health and safety, yet traditional monitoring methods can be slow, incomplete, or costly to scale.",crowdHEDGE: Using AI to help governments and local authorities keep citizens safe,City of Turin; Local Government; Public order and safety n.e.c.; Italy,"In Turin, crowdHEDGE was piloted to help the city extract actionable insight from drone footage over parks and other public areas so that officials could understand how people were using space and respond more effectively. The implementation uses drone video streams connected to an AI-based image-recognition and analytics system running with edge-computing infrastructure.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Local authorities need timely information on how public spaces are being used, especially when crowding, footfall, or unsafe proximity may affect public health and safety, yet traditional monitoring methods can be slow, incomplete, or costly to scale. In Turin, crowdHEDGE was piloted to help the city extract actionable insight from drone footage over parks and other public areas so that officials could understand how people were using space and respond more effectively. The objective is to improve situational awareness, support safer management of public areas, and provide public-health and city officials with evidence they can explore by location and time. The implementation uses drone video streams connected to an AI-based image-recognition and analytics system running with edge-computing infrastructure. According to Addfor, the solution processes footage close to the source, calculates metrics such as crowd size, approximate distancing, and foot-traffic levels, and presents results through a graphical interface for geographical and temporal analysis. At a conceptual level, the system combines aerial sensing, computer vision, and edge analytics in a workflow designed to reduce bandwidth demands while preserving operational responsiveness. The impact includes faster and more detailed insight into how public spaces are used, better support for local safety decisions, and a scalable model for using drone-based analytics in urban public administration.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2274,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01692,EU_PSTW_202609_01692,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""social_nonprofit""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Autonomous Provice of Trento,General public services n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,AIxPA - Artificial Intelligence for the Public Administration,Autonomous Provice of Trento; Local Government; General public services n.e.c.; Italy,"The solution uses machine learning models, data integration techniques, and simulation tools to analyse large-scale datasets and generate predictive insights. As an ongoing initiative, the project is currently developing and validating use cases, with expected benefits including improved policy design, more efficient resource allocation, and enhanced analytical capacity within the public sector.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The AIxPA project, led by the Bruno Kessler Foundation in collaboration with the Autonomous Province of Trento, aims to strengthen data-driven decision-making capabilities within public administrations. Public authorities face increasing complexity in managing socio-economic systems, territorial dynamics, and public services, requiring advanced tools for analysis and forecasting. The objective is to develop a general-purpose analytical platform tailored to public sector needs, supporting multiple domains such as mobility, environmental management, and economic development. The solution uses machine learning models, data integration techniques, and simulation tools to analyse large-scale datasets and generate predictive insights. It incorporates digital twin concepts to model complex systems and support scenario analysis. The platform is designed to be reusable and scalable across different public administrations. As an ongoing initiative, the project is currently developing and validating use cases, with expected benefits including improved policy design, more efficient resource allocation, and enhanced analytical capacity within the public sector.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION|S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2275,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01693,EU_PSTW_202609_01693,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,eu-LISA,Police Services,"The European Commission, in collaboration with Member State authorities and eu-LISA, initiated the VisaChat pilot to address challenges faced by visa applicants in accessing clear and consistent information on Schengen visa procedures. Applicants often rely on fragmented or complex sources, increasing administrative burden and the risk of errors in applications.",Visa Application Chatbot for the EU countries,eu-LISA; European Institution/Agency; Police Services; European Union,"The objective of the use case was to provide a unified, accessible interface to answer frequently asked questions and improve user experience. The solution consists of a chatbot application designed to respond to general visa-related queries using predefined knowledge bases.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The European Commission, in collaboration with Member State authorities and eu-LISA, initiated the VisaChat pilot to address challenges faced by visa applicants in accessing clear and consistent information on Schengen visa procedures. Applicants often rely on fragmented or complex sources, increasing administrative burden and the risk of errors in applications. The objective of the use case was to provide a unified, accessible interface to answer frequently asked questions and improve user experience. The solution consists of a chatbot application designed to respond to general visa-related queries using predefined knowledge bases. It employs supervised learning techniques based on curated datasets of standard responses in multiple languages, complemented by machine translation and unsupervised approaches to extend multilingual capabilities. The system is modular and built using open-source technologies, allowing flexible deployment either centrally or through Member State portals. Privacy-by-design principles are incorporated, including explicit restrictions on collecting personal data. As a pilot conducted between 2021 and 2022, the project demonstrated feasibility in improving information access, but no evidence confirms transition to full-scale implementation.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F08_ERROR_COMPLIANCE_RISK,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2276,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01694,EU_PSTW_202609_01694,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"UK Government - Department of Science, Innovation and Technology",General public services n.e.c.,Redbox was created inside the UK government to address this administrative burden by giving officials a secure generative AI tool for document interaction and general workplace tasks.,Redbox - Bringing Generative AI to the way the Civil Service works,"UK Government - Department of Science, Innovation and Technology; Central-Government; General public services n.e.c.; United Kingdom","At a conceptual level, the tool combined secure access to large language models with document-processing workflows tailored to government use.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Civil servants work with large volumes of briefings, letters, speeches, meeting minutes, and other documents, and much of their time can be absorbed by locating key information, summarising content, and drafting routine material. Redbox was created inside the UK government to address this administrative burden by giving officials a secure generative AI tool for document interaction and general workplace tasks. The objective was to improve productivity, reduce time spent on repetitive document handling, and help staff engage more efficiently with complex written material so they could focus more on higher-value work. The implementation, developed by the Incubator for AI, provided a chat-based and document-oriented assistant that could summarise files, answer questions about their contents, and support drafting and exploration of official material, including at OFFICIAL-SENSITIVE level. Public information from i.AI states that Redbox was rolled out to more than 6,000 users and used across over 150,000 chats and 1.3 million messages. At a conceptual level, the tool combined secure access to large language models with document-processing workflows tailored to government use. The impact included faster information handling, lower administrative effort, and valuable learning for future public-sector AI deployment, even though the project was later sunset as comparable enterprise tools became more widely available in government.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2277,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01695,EU_PSTW_202609_01695,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Ministry for Digital Transformation and of Civil Service,General public services n.e.c.,"Traditional planning methods can struggle to combine diverse datasets, simulate policy alternatives, and communicate likely outcomes clearly to decision-makers and stakeholders.",V-city - A human-centric platform for Urban Digital Twins,Ministry for Digital Transformation and of Civil Service; Central-Government; General public services n.e.c.; Spain,"The purpose of this use case is to support evidence-based urban planning through a human-centric digital platform that helps public authorities and ecosystem partners explore, predict, and visualise the consequences of different interventions.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Cities increasingly need better tools to test the likely effects of public policies before implementation, especially when addressing complex issues such as climate change, social inequality, mobility, and sustainable development. Traditional planning methods can struggle to combine diverse datasets, simulate policy alternatives, and communicate likely outcomes clearly to decision-makers and stakeholders. The purpose of this use case is to support evidence-based urban planning through a human-centric digital platform that helps public authorities and ecosystem partners explore, predict, and visualise the consequences of different interventions. The implementation centres on an urban digital twin platform led by the Barcelona Supercomputing Center, combining real city data with simulation layers and visual analytics. At a conceptual level, the platform integrates heterogeneous urban datasets, modelling tools, and visualisation capabilities to represent current conditions and assess the projected impact of policies in a virtual environment. The approach also seeks to strengthen the surrounding public-private ecosystem by advancing capability in an emerging field of public service technology. The impact includes improved foresight, better-informed planning decisions, and clearer communication of policy trade-offs. In addition, the platform supports scalability in urban analysis, increases institutional capacity for innovation, and creates strategic value by positioning participating organisations to lead in the development and application of digital twin technologies.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2278,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01696,EU_PSTW_202609_01696,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,CNRS,General public services n.e.c.,"Traditional governance structures often struggle to ensure transparency, accountability, and inclusive participation in digital communities.",BlockchainGov - Blockchain for distributed governance,CNRS; Consortium; General public services n.e.c.; France,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The BlockchainGov project explores how distributed ledger technologies can support new models of governance in decentralised digital environments. Traditional governance structures often struggle to ensure transparency, accountability, and inclusive participation in digital communities. The objective is to design and evaluate governance frameworks that can operate effectively in blockchain-based ecosystems. The project combines theoretical analysis with empirical research to develop models that integrate technical protocols, social norms, and economic incentive structures. These models incorporate both on-chain and off-chain governance mechanisms, including voting systems, dispute resolution processes, and participatory decision-making approaches. Techniques such as smart contracts and consensus mechanisms are analysed and adapted to support governance functions. The project includes participatory action research, where selected communities test proposed models and provide feedback. As an ongoing research initiative, the project is still developing and validating these frameworks, with expected contributions to improved transparency, inclusiveness, and robustness of decentralised governance systems.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2280,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01697,EU_PSTW_202609_01697,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,University of Hamburg,Health n.e.c.,The FeatureCloud project addresses the challenge of enabling collaborative analysis of sensitive healthcare data across institutions while maintaining strict privacy and security requirements.,FutureCloud - Privacy preserving federated machine learning and blockchaining for reduced cyber risks in a world of distributed healthcare,University of Hamburg; Consortium; Health n.e.c.; Germany,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The FeatureCloud project addresses the challenge of enabling collaborative analysis of sensitive healthcare data across institutions while maintaining strict privacy and security requirements. Centralised data sharing models increase the risk of data breaches and limit cross-border collaboration due to regulatory constraints. The objective is to enable secure, decentralised data analysis for medical research and innovation. The solution is based on federated machine learning, where models are trained locally on institutional datasets and only model updates are shared, combined with blockchain technologies to ensure integrity, traceability, and trust in distributed processes. The platform implements a privacy-by-design architecture that avoids central data storage and minimises data exposure. It provides a software toolkit enabling researchers and healthcare providers to perform joint analyses without exchanging raw data. As an EU-funded research and innovation project, the system is under development and validation, with expected impacts including enhanced cybersecurity, compliance with data protection regulations, and improved capacity for collaborative medical research.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2281,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01698,EU_PSTW_202609_01698,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Dublin,Transport,UNKNOWN,Dublin Region Active Travel Dashboard,City of Dublin; Local Government; Transport; Ireland,"Transport planners and policymakers need robust evidence on how people walk and cycle across a city, but active-travel data are often fragmented across multiple sources and difficult for non-specialists to use in one place. Smart Dublin describes it as a one-stop shop for analysing walking and cycling patterns, while later project material notes that it also correlates activity with infrastructure use, health projections, climate impacts, and estimated financial savings.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Transport planners and policymakers need robust evidence on how people walk and cycle across a city, but active-travel data are often fragmented across multiple sources and difficult for non-specialists to use in one place. The Dublin Region Active Travel Dashboard was developed to address this by bringing together key walking and cycling indicators for the capital in a single digital environment that supports evidence-informed mobility planning. The objective is to improve access to active-travel intelligence, support more sustainable transport policy, and make it easier for stakeholders to understand patterns in commuting and route usage. The implementation combines open and operational datasets, including Census information, canal cordon counts, bicycle counters, Google trip data, and Strava-related inputs, into a continuously evolving dashboard. Smart Dublin describes it as a one-stop shop for analysing walking and cycling patterns, while later project material notes that it also correlates activity with infrastructure use, health projections, climate impacts, and estimated financial savings. At a conceptual level, the dashboard integrates heterogeneous mobility datasets into a common visual interface for exploration and comparison. The impact includes better visibility of active-travel behaviour, stronger support for policy and investment decisions, and a scalable platform for sustainable urban-mobility analysis across the Dublin region.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2283,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01699,EU_PSTW_202609_01699,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""social_nonprofit""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Linagora and French OSS Community,General services.,UNKNOWN,Open LLM France and LINAGORA - Open Source LLM model,Linagora and French OSS Community; Private sector; General services.; France,"The objective is to strengthen sovereign and ethical AI capability, provide a reusable base model for French-language applications, and support uses such as meeting summarisation, virtual assistants, and document-related productivity tools.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"European organisations increasingly want language technologies that are transparent, locally governed, and better aligned with national languages and public-sector requirements than many closed commercial models. The OpenLLM France community and LINAGORA developed LUCIE in this context as an open-source French-language large language model intended to serve as a foundation for dialogue generation and understanding tasks. The objective is to strengthen sovereign and ethical AI capability, provide a reusable base model for French-language applications, and support uses such as meeting summarisation, virtual assistants, and document-related productivity tools. The implementation centres on a pre-trained dialogue-oriented language model developed with open and licensed training datasets, community participation, and access to French high-performance computing resources, including the Jean Zay supercomputer. LINAGORA states that the model is designed to reflect the characteristics of spoken interaction and to be fine-tuned for downstream tasks rather than treated as a fully finished product in itself. At a conceptual level, the initiative combines open model development, transparent data governance, and ecosystem collaboration to create a deployable base model for French-language systems. The impact includes greater technological autonomy, better support for French-language digital services, and a scalable open foundation for public and private innovation in language applications.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2284,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01700,EU_PSTW_202609_01700,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,European Commission - DG DIGIT,R&D general public services.,"The challenge is not only to improve everyday productivity, but also to understand where these tools can genuinely strengthen research activity, policy support, and collaboration across teams and expert communities.",GPT@JRC: Generative AI Tool to support Science for Policy at JRC,European Commission - DG DIGIT; European Institution/Agency; R&D general public services.; European Union,"The objective of this use case is therefore to provide a controlled environment in which staff can test practical applications, identify risks and opportunities, and develop guidance for effective use across research and operational settings. At a conceptual level, the platform supports several layers of use, from routine tasks such as summarisation, translation, drafting, proofreading, classification, and sentiment analysis, to the enhancement of existing tools and the development of specialised assistants for knowledge synthesis and foresight.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Within a large scientific and policy-support organisation such as the Joint Research Centre, staff need secure ways to explore emerging language technologies without exposing sensitive information or working outside institutional governance. The challenge is not only to improve everyday productivity, but also to understand where these tools can genuinely strengthen research activity, policy support, and collaboration across teams and expert communities. The objective of this use case is therefore to provide a controlled environment in which staff can test practical applications, identify risks and opportunities, and develop guidance for effective use across research and operational settings. The implementation takes the form of a secure platform that gives staff access to large language models through a governed internal environment. At a conceptual level, the platform supports several layers of use, from routine tasks such as summarisation, translation, drafting, proofreading, classification, and sentiment analysis, to the enhancement of existing tools and the development of specialised assistants for knowledge synthesis and foresight. It is designed to connect experimentation, API-based cooperation, and institutional learning within the wider European Commission ecosystem. The expected impact includes improved efficiency in knowledge work, stronger internal capability in generative technologies, better-informed governance, and greater organisational readiness to scale high-value, policy-relevant applications safely and consistently.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2285,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01701,EU_PSTW_202609_01701,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,European Commission - DG DIGIT,R&D general public services.,UNKNOWN,GPT@EC: Generative AI Tool to support EC administrative task,European Commission - DG DIGIT; European Institution/Agency; R&D general public services.; European Union,The purpose of this use case is to provide European Commission staff with a secure and compliant environment that improves productivity and creativity while allowing internal information to be processed within the Commission context.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Across a large public administration, staff regularly handle time-intensive knowledge tasks such as drafting texts, summarising documents, and producing technical material, often under strict compliance and confidentiality requirements. Reliance on external tools can create risks around data handling, security, and alignment with institutional processes, limiting the safe adoption of advanced digital support. The purpose of this use case is to provide European Commission staff with a secure and compliant environment that improves productivity and creativity while allowing internal information to be processed within the Commission context. The implementation is based on a general-purpose internal platform that provides access to several large language models suited to different tasks and sensitivity levels. At a conceptual level, the service supports text generation, summarisation, code development, and other administrative knowledge tasks, while embedding corporate rules, compliance standards, and secure data handling. It also includes access to open-source and sovereign models, enabling flexibility in model choice according to operational needs and governance constraints. The impact is expected to include faster completion of routine administrative work, improved quality and consistency of outputs, and reduced dependence on third-party services for sensitive tasks. More broadly, the platform strengthens institutional control, supports scalable adoption of trusted digital tools, and creates business value through safer and more efficient administrative processes.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2286,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01702,EU_PSTW_202609_01702,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,BULL SAS,R&D general public services.,UNKNOWN,NExt ApplicationS of Quantum Computing,BULL SAS; Academic-Research; R&D general public services.; European Union,"Quantum computing remains immature for many production uses, yet industry and research actors need to explore practical applications early if they are to understand where near-term quantum systems could create value. NEASQC was created in this context to build a bridge between hardware development and end-user application design, bringing together universities and companies from several European countries to investigate quantum-enabled use cases before the technology fully matures.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Quantum computing remains immature for many production uses, yet industry and research actors need to explore practical applications early if they are to understand where near-term quantum systems could create value. NEASQC was created in this context to build a bridge between hardware development and end-user application design, bringing together universities and companies from several European countries to investigate quantum-enabled use cases before the technology fully matures. The objective is to identify promising applications, develop a shared practical toolset, and prepare industrial and scientific actors to experiment with near-term quantum computing. The implementation took the form of an EU-funded consortium project running from September 2020 to 30 November 2024, combining research partners and industrial users in a collaborative environment for application exploration and software-tool development. CORDIS confirms that the project is closed and frames it as an effort to connect NISQ-era hardware activity with practical end-user investigation. The impact lies in capability-building rather than a single public-facing service: the project generated shared tools, results, and application knowledge that can help organisations assess future quantum opportunities more realistically and at lower entry cost.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2287,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01703,EU_PSTW_202609_01703,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Max Planck Institute for Human Development,R&D general public services.,UNKNOWN,AI competences for or the Max Planck Institute for Human Development in the institute's basic research,Max Planck Institute for Human Development; Academic-Research; R&D general public services.; Germany,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Interdisciplinary basic research increasingly depends on high-end AI, machine learning, and software-engineering capability, especially where projects attract public attention and require robust, secure, high-quality digital components. The Max Planck Institute for Human Development used this procurement to obtain specialist external capacity that could support its AI-related research agenda flexibly and quickly across a range of demanding projects. The objective was not to launch a single citizen-facing service, but to secure access to elite AI and software expertise for research work requiring strong quality control, resilience, and technical excellence. The implementation took the form of a framework contract for specialised teams with advanced skills in areas such as PyTorch, TensorFlow, pattern recognition, NLP, generative models, embeddings, and image processing, with an emphasis on assembling the right experts through match-making and quality control. Current Max Planck sources confirm that the institute remains active in AI-related research, particularly around human-machine interaction, which supports the view that this procurement was part of an ongoing research-capability strategy. The impact is therefore improved research execution capacity, faster access to scarce specialist skills, and stronger quality assurance for technically advanced projects, rather than a discrete public-sector AI service with a direct end-user interface.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2288,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01704,EU_PSTW_202609_01704,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Council of Europe,"R&D recreation, culture and religion.",UNKNOWN,CdE - Pilot Programme for Series Co-Productions Advertising Visuals,"Council of Europe; European Institution/Agency; R&D recreation, culture and religion.; European Union",The objective of this use case is to support the promotion of series co-productions through modernised visual production processes that are better suited to the speed and complexity of today’s audiovisual industry. The implementation centres on the use of generative and algorithmic content creation and management techniques within the production of advertising materials for professional industry events and market-facing communication.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"In the audiovisual sector, promotional content for television series and co-productions must be produced for a highly specialised professional audience across a fast-changing media landscape shaped by multiple distribution channels and new digital production methods. This creates pressure to deliver high-quality advertising visuals efficiently, while adapting to evolving expectations in content creation, branding, and campaign management. The objective of this use case is to support the promotion of series co-productions through modernised visual production processes that are better suited to the speed and complexity of today’s audiovisual industry. The implementation centres on the use of generative and algorithmic content creation and management techniques within the production of advertising materials for professional industry events and market-facing communication. At a conceptual level, the approach combines digital content generation workflows with creative production processes to produce promotional visuals tailored to producers, distributors, sales agents, professional organisations, financial partners, and exhibitors. The solution is intended to fit within the wider communications and campaign ecosystem of the programme, supporting repeatable and adaptable creative production. The expected impact includes faster turnaround in visual asset development, improved responsiveness to market opportunities, and more scalable campaign support across events and formats. It also offers potential cost efficiencies, greater creative flexibility, and stronger visibility for supported series within an increasingly competitive international environment.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S7_GENERATIVE_CONTENT_CREATION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2289,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01705,EU_PSTW_202609_01705,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""social_nonprofit""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Norwegian Meteorological Institute,Environmental protection n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,DestinE Digital Twin Engine (DTE) workflow for weather forecasting.,Norwegian Meteorological Institute; Central-Government; Environmental protection n.e.c.; Norway,"Within Destination Earth, this use case addresses that need by applying advanced generative machine-learning methods to weather forecasting workflows so that public authorities and scientific users can work with richer and more realistic probabilistic scenarios. The implementation uses deep-learning techniques including variational auto-encoders, generative adversarial networks, normalising flows, and diffusion models, with researchers from MET Norway, Météo-France, and SMHI collaborating on ensemble generation and temporal downscaling for numerical weather-prediction data.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Weather forecasting increasingly requires methods that can quantify uncertainty, generate realistic scenario ensembles, and improve temporal detail without unsustainable computational cost. Within Destination Earth, this use case addresses that need by applying advanced generative machine-learning methods to weather forecasting workflows so that public authorities and scientific users can work with richer and more realistic probabilistic scenarios. The objective is to improve forecasting quality, support uncertainty analysis, and make AI-enhanced methods reusable across the DestinE ecosystem. The implementation uses deep-learning techniques including variational auto-encoders, generative adversarial networks, normalising flows, and diffusion models, with researchers from MET Norway, Météo-France, and SMHI collaborating on ensemble generation and temporal downscaling for numerical weather-prediction data. Recent ECMWF and DestinE sources confirm that AI remains an active part of the programme and that Destination Earth has been confirmed for a third phase starting in 2026, indicating continuing institutional commitment beyond the original contract notice. The impact includes more realistic ensembles, improved probabilistic forecasting, better integration of AI into weather and climate digital twins, and a more scalable foundation for future operational and research workflows in European weather forecasting.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2290,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01706,EU_PSTW_202609_01706,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Paris Banliux Sud,Transport,UNKNOWN,WISP - Road Crossing Management supported by AI - Pilot - Paris,Paris Banliux Sud; Academic-Research; Transport; France,"Urban traffic-light control often relies on static or costly automated regulation systems that do not adapt well to real-time traffic conditions, leading to unnecessary delays, higher emissions, and inefficient use of infrastructure.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Urban traffic-light control often relies on static or costly automated regulation systems that do not adapt well to real-time traffic conditions, leading to unnecessary delays, higher emissions, and inefficient use of infrastructure. WISP was developed to address this problem by optimising traffic-light sequencing dynamically, with an initial field deployment at three intersections in the southern suburbs of Paris. The objective is to reduce waiting times, improve flow management, and lower the ecological and budgetary costs associated with conventional automated signal regulation. The implementation combines real-time traffic-data analysis, connected-vehicle data, optimisation algorithms, and a compact hardware module that can be installed in existing signal-control cabinets. The company states that its AI is specialised in reducing driver waiting time through real-time search for the best signal sequence and that the prototypes already showed waiting-time reductions ranging from 10 per cent to 50 per cent depending on the type of junction. At a conceptual level, the system links live digital traffic inputs with adaptive control logic and lightweight roadside hardware to support responsive intersection management. The impact includes faster vehicle movement through junctions, reduced environmental and operating costs, and a scalable model for upgrading traffic control without the expense of wholesale infrastructure replacement.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2291,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01707,EU_PSTW_202609_01707,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Fondazione Bruno Kessler,General public services n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,A Public Administration Canvas for Ethical AI implementation,Fondazione Bruno Kessler; Academic-Research; General public services n.e.c.; Italy,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Public administrations often need structured help to assess legal, ethical, organisational, and technical issues before adopting AI, especially under evolving European regulatory requirements. The Canvas for Ethical AI implementation was developed by FBK with the Autonomous Province of Trento to address this gap by giving public bodies a practical instrument for planning AI adoption responsibly. The objective is to help administrations design AI solutions that are compliant, accountable, and ethically robust from the outset rather than treating ethics as a late-stage add-on. The implementation takes the form of a reusable canvas and guidance framework that prompts users to consider issues such as bias, privacy, accountability, and regulatory fit in a structured way. Current public sources from FBK and the project repository indicate that the tool is available, has been published for broader reuse, and is still being further developed and validated based on the Trento experience. The impact includes better governance readiness, stronger alignment with EU requirements, and a more scalable way for public bodies to assess AI projects consistently before deployment, thereby reducing implementation risk and improving trustworthiness in future AI adoption.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2292,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01708,EU_PSTW_202609_01708,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""social_nonprofit""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Ministere du Travail and Emploi,General public services n.e.c.,"Social and fiscal rules are often complex, difficult to compare, and hard for both citizens and administrations to apply consistently without digital support.","Assess your entitlement to 906 aids: OpenFisca, a tool to writing rules as code",Ministere du Travail and Emploi; Central-Government; General public services n.e.c.; France,"The implementation uses the open-source OpenFisca framework, which models taxes and benefits through formal variables, entities, and rule logic that can be exposed through APIs and reused in services such as Mes Aides and Aides Jeunes.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Social and fiscal rules are often complex, difficult to compare, and hard for both citizens and administrations to apply consistently without digital support. OpenFisca addresses this by turning legal and policy rules into executable code that can be reused to simulate entitlements, compare scenarios, and power public-facing services such as benefit calculators. The objective is to create a common, computable representation of rules so that governments, researchers, developers, and civil society can debate, test, and deliver social and fiscal policy more transparently. The implementation uses the open-source OpenFisca framework, which models taxes and benefits through formal variables, entities, and rule logic that can be exposed through APIs and reused in services such as Mes Aides and Aides Jeunes. The current youth-oriented simulator publicly states that it can estimate eligibility for more than 1,060 aids in a few clicks, while beta.gouv.fr describes OpenFisca as a leading practical implementation of the Rules as Code movement used in multiple countries. At a conceptual level, the solution combines legal modelling, microsimulation, and software engineering to produce machine-executable policy logic. The impact includes faster and more transparent eligibility estimation, easier reuse across public services, and a scalable foundation for policy simulation, digital entitlement tools, and evidence-based reform analysis.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2293,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01709,EU_PSTW_202609_01709,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"University of Copenhagen, Denmark",R&D general public services.,General-purpose language models often struggle in this context because they are not sufficiently exposed to specialised legal corpora or evaluated against tasks that reflect genuine legal knowledge.,LeXFiles and LegalLAMA: Facilitating English Multinational Legal Language Model Development,"University of Copenhagen, Denmark; Academic-Research; R&D general public services.; Denmark",The aim of this use case is to strengthen the development and assessment of legal language models by providing resources that better capture the linguistic and conceptual demands of legal work in a multinational setting.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Legal analysis depends heavily on precise language, domain-specific terminology, and the ability to interpret concepts consistently across jurisdictions and document types. General-purpose language models often struggle in this context because they are not sufficiently exposed to specialised legal corpora or evaluated against tasks that reflect genuine legal knowledge. The aim of this use case is to strengthen the development and assessment of legal language models by providing resources that better capture the linguistic and conceptual demands of legal work in a multinational setting. The implementation combines two complementary components: LeXFiles, a large multilingual legal corpus for model training, and LegalLAMA, a benchmark designed to probe legal knowledge in pre-trained language models. At a conceptual level, the project examines how pre-training objectives, model size, and training data influence performance, comparing newly trained and existing models on legal language understanding tasks. The approach supports domain-specific model development grounded in structured evaluation rather than generic performance measures alone. The results show that performance depends strongly on both the data used for pre-training and the scale of the model, highlighting the value of specialised legal resources. The broader impact lies in enabling more reliable legal text processing, improving the quality of downstream legal applications, and supporting more robust, scalable development of language technologies for multilingual legal environments.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2294,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01710,EU_PSTW_202609_01710,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,National Government,General services.,"Public administrations process large volumes of emails, documents, reports, and routine communications, and this creates pressure to improve productivity without increasing administrative burden.",AI Sweeps new govs in UK,National Government; Central-Government; General services.; United Kingdom,"In the United Kingdom, this use case reflects the growing adoption of Microsoft’s AI product suite, including Copilot, across central and local government as a tool for summarising emails, generating text, and supporting document creation.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Public administrations process large volumes of emails, documents, reports, and routine communications, and this creates pressure to improve productivity without increasing administrative burden. In the United Kingdom, this use case reflects the growing adoption of Microsoft’s AI product suite, including Copilot, across central and local government as a tool for summarising emails, generating text, and supporting document creation. The objective is to streamline routine office work, improve efficiency in day-to-day administrative tasks, and help public servants spend less time on repetitive drafting and information-processing activities. The implementation combines large language model capabilities with office-productivity software used in government environments, embedding AI assistance directly into familiar workflows for document drafting, summarisation, and information handling. Public reporting indicates that more than 100 local councils have adopted the tool and that trials have also taken place in a number of central government departments. The impact includes faster handling of written material, reduced administrative effort, and improved scalability of routine office functions across public bodies. It also shows how AI is being integrated into mainstream workplace software as part of a broader transformation in how government staff manage information and produce content.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,F06_HIGH_VOLUME_SCALABILITY_BOTTLENECK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2296,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01711,EU_PSTW_202609_01711,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,German State Bank KfW,R&D economic affairs,UNKNOWN,Tokenized Bond Issuance,German State Bank KfW; Central-Government; R&D economic affairs; Germany,"This use case involves KfW, the European Central Bank, Boerse Stuttgart Digital, and financial market participants, with the objective of testing secure, efficient digital bond issuance and settlement. The solution is implemented through a blockchain-based digital bond issued under the German Electronic Securities Act, with lifecycle management supported by crypto wallets and digital registers.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Public bond issuance processes involve multiple intermediaries, complex settlement mechanisms, and high operational costs, limiting efficiency and innovation in capital markets. Germany’s development bank KfW sought to explore how digital issuance models could modernise bond transactions while remaining aligned with regulatory frameworks and central bank infrastructures. This use case involves KfW, the European Central Bank, Boerse Stuttgart Digital, and financial market participants, with the objective of testing secure, efficient digital bond issuance and settlement. The solution is implemented through a blockchain-based digital bond issued under the German Electronic Securities Act, with lifecycle management supported by crypto wallets and digital registers. The bond issuance is integrated into ECB wholesale settlement trials, enabling delivery-versus-payment transactions using central bank money, with settlement executed on distributed ledger infrastructure. The approach improves settlement speed, transparency, and operational efficiency, reduces reliance on intermediaries, and supports regulatory-compliant innovation. It provides valuable learning for large-scale adoption of tokenised securities, enhances market resilience, and contributes to the evolution of more efficient and interoperable European capital markets.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2297,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01712,EU_PSTW_202609_01712,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""social_nonprofit"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,The Republic of Slovenia,R&D economic affairs,"Sovereign debt management relies on trusted, transparent, and efficient market infrastructures, yet traditional issuance and settlement processes remain complex and costly.",Digital sovereign bond,The Republic of Slovenia; Central-Government; R&D economic affairs; Slovenia,"This use case focuses on the issuance of the EU’s first digital sovereign bond, involving the Slovenian government, international banks, central banks, and market infrastructure providers, with the objective of increasing efficiency and transparency in sovereign bond markets. The solution is implemented through a distributed ledger-based issuance and settlement framework using the Neobonds platform and Canton blockchain, with settlement carried out via the Banque de France’s interoperability system as part of ECB wholesale experiments.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Sovereign debt management relies on trusted, transparent, and efficient market infrastructures, yet traditional issuance and settlement processes remain complex and costly. Slovenia aimed to modernise its government debt operations and position itself at the forefront of financial innovation within the European Union. This use case focuses on the issuance of the EU’s first digital sovereign bond, involving the Slovenian government, international banks, central banks, and market infrastructure providers, with the objective of increasing efficiency and transparency in sovereign bond markets. The solution is implemented through a distributed ledger-based issuance and settlement framework using the Neobonds platform and Canton blockchain, with settlement carried out via the Banque de France’s interoperability system as part of ECB wholesale experiments. The digital bond lifecycle, from issuance to settlement, is managed through programmable infrastructure that integrates with central bank money. The initiative demonstrates faster settlement, improved auditability, and reduced operational complexity. It strengthens trust in digital financial instruments, supports cross-border interoperability, and establishes a foundation for wider adoption of digital sovereign securities across Europe.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2298,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01713,EU_PSTW_202609_01713,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Cassa Depositi e Prestiti (CDP),R&D economic affairs,UNKNOWN,Digital bonds,Cassa Depositi e Prestiti (CDP); Central-Government; R&D economic affairs; Italy,"This use case involves CDP, the Bank of Italy, commercial banks, and regulators, with the objective of demonstrating compliant issuance and settlement of digital securities without traditional intermediaries. The solution is implemented through the issuance of a €25 million digital bond recorded on a blockchain-based digital register managed by an authorised operator, under Italy’s Fintech Decree.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"European public financial institutions are exploring digital securities to improve efficiency, reduce intermediation, and modernise capital market operations. Italy’s Cassa Depositi e Prestiti sought to test a fully digital bond issuance within a new national regulatory framework, while integrating with European central bank settlement infrastructure. This use case involves CDP, the Bank of Italy, commercial banks, and regulators, with the objective of demonstrating compliant issuance and settlement of digital securities without traditional intermediaries. The solution is implemented through the issuance of a €25 million digital bond recorded on a blockchain-based digital register managed by an authorised operator, under Italy’s Fintech Decree. Settlement is executed through integration with the TARGET2 system using blockchain-enabled payment links as part of ECB wholesale DLT experiments. The approach reduces issuance complexity, shortens settlement cycles, and enhances transparency and control over securities management. It delivers operational efficiency, supports regulatory innovation, and provides a scalable model for future digital capital market instruments, strengthening Italy’s and Europe’s position in financial market digitalisation.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2299,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01714,EU_PSTW_202609_01714,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Landesbank Baden-Württemberg,R&D economic affairs,UNKNOWN,Federal Bank to Offer Crypto Custody Services,Landesbank Baden-Württemberg; Non-governmental; R&D economic affairs; Germany,"The solution is implemented through a partnership with Bitpanda, integrating institutional custody infrastructure with LBBW’s banking systems.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Institutional investors and corporate clients are increasingly seeking secure and regulated access to digital assets, while banks must adapt their service offerings to remain competitive under evolving EU regulations. Landesbank Baden-Württemberg (LBBW) aims to address this demand by offering professional-grade custody services for cryptoassets. The objective is to provide secure, compliant storage and management of digital assets for institutional clients, leveraging the bank’s existing trust and regulatory expertise. The solution is implemented through a partnership with Bitpanda, integrating institutional custody infrastructure with LBBW’s banking systems. The service includes secure key management, wallet infrastructure, and compliance with German and EU regulatory requirements. It is designed to align with upcoming EU cryptoasset regulations, ensuring legal certainty and operational robustness. The impact is the expansion of LBBW’s service portfolio into a growing asset class, improved client trust through bank-grade custody, and strengthened positioning in digital finance markets. The initiative supports scalability, risk management, and regulatory compliance, delivering long-term business value while enabling clients to participate safely in the digital asset economy.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2300,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01715,EU_PSTW_202609_01715,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,European Commission,R&D economic affairs,"Raising capital and managing investor relations for instruments such as equity, debt, or revenue-sharing agreements can be slow, costly, and opaque, particularly for smaller issuers and cross-border offerings.",Tokenization of Real-World Assets (RWAs),European Commission; European Institution/Agency; R&D economic affairs; Spain,"The platform provides end-to-end token issuance and management workflows, using distributed ledger technology to represent assets, automate corporate actions where appropriate, and maintain an auditable register of holdings and transfers.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Raising capital and managing investor relations for instruments such as equity, debt, or revenue-sharing agreements can be slow, costly, and opaque, particularly for smaller issuers and cross-border offerings. Brickken’s platform targets this by enabling organisations to create and manage digitised representations of real-world financial claims and ownership interests, improving transparency and streamlining lifecycle administration for issuers and investors. Stakeholders include issuers, investors, intermediaries, compliance teams, and supervisors concerned with how tokenised instruments fit within EU financial regulation, with the objective of enabling compliant tokenisation at scale and clearer operational processes. The platform provides end-to-end token issuance and management workflows, using distributed ledger technology to represent assets, automate corporate actions where appropriate, and maintain an auditable register of holdings and transfers. It integrates identity, onboarding, and reporting layers so that token operations can align with regulatory obligations (e.g., disclosures, eligibility constraints, and record-keeping), while sandbox participation supports structured dialogue on legal classification and cross-border compliance. Expected impacts include faster issuance and settlement processes, lower administrative overhead, improved transparency for stakeholders, and increased acceptance among institutional clients by validating compliant design choices through regulator engagement in the European Blockchain Sandbox.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2301,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01716,EU_PSTW_202609_01716,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,European Commission,Economic affairs n.e.c,UNKNOWN,Circularise: securing data across the supply chain,European Commission; European Institution/Agency; Economic affairs n.e.c; Netherlands,"The solution uses blockchain-based integrity and access controls to anchor claims and selectively disclose information, conceptually combining on-chain proofs with off-chain secure data storage and permissioning so that partners can confirm provenance, certifications, or material characteristics without full data exposure.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Companies across complex value chains must increasingly share product and sustainability information (including for Digital Product Passports), yet suppliers are often reluctant to disclose sensitive data that could expose intellectual property or competitive advantage. Circularise addresses this by enabling controlled data sharing across supply chains so that downstream partners can verify required attributes without receiving unnecessary proprietary details. Stakeholders include manufacturers, tiered suppliers, brands, auditors, and regulators pushing for traceability and compliance, with the objective of improving trust and verifiability while preserving confidentiality. The solution uses blockchain-based integrity and access controls to anchor claims and selectively disclose information, conceptually combining on-chain proofs with off-chain secure data storage and permissioning so that partners can confirm provenance, certifications, or material characteristics without full data exposure. Data structures can support Digital Product Passport workflows, with cryptographic techniques used to share only what is needed for a given transaction or compliance check. The expected results include reduced friction in compliance reporting, stronger trust between supply-chain actors, improved auditability, and scalable data governance that supports cross-border requirements; participation in the European Blockchain Sandbox is used to clarify legal and regulatory considerations around confidential data sharing, liability, and interoperability.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2302,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01717,EU_PSTW_202609_01717,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,European Commission,R&D housing and community amenities.,"Real estate investment is often constrained by high transaction costs, limited liquidity, and barriers to participation, while issuers face lengthy capital-raising processes and complex administration of ownership and transfers. The anticipated impacts include faster issuance and settlement, improved transparency of holdings, reduced administrative burden through automation, and enhanced liquidity through secondary trading—while sandbox participation helps derisk regulatory uncertainty and supports broader institutional adoption.",Real Estate Trading,European Commission; European Institution/Agency; R&D housing and community amenities.; Denmark,"The platform tokenises real estate assets using distributed ledger technology so that ownership units can be issued, transferred, and tracked with an auditable history, while compliance layers can enforce eligibility rules and reporting requirements.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Real estate investment is often constrained by high transaction costs, limited liquidity, and barriers to participation, while issuers face lengthy capital-raising processes and complex administration of ownership and transfers. DigiShares’ RealEstate.Exchange (RE.X) aims to modernise this by enabling property-related assets to be issued and traded digitally, opening access to a broader range of investors and supporting secondary market liquidity. Stakeholders include real estate companies raising capital, investors seeking fractional exposure, platform operators, custodians, and regulators focused on investor protection and market integrity, with the objective of creating a secure and compliant trading venue for tokenised real estate interests. The platform tokenises real estate assets using distributed ledger technology so that ownership units can be issued, transferred, and tracked with an auditable history, while compliance layers can enforce eligibility rules and reporting requirements. RE.X has been accepted into the European Blockchain Sandbox’s second cohort to engage in structured regulatory dialogue on cross-border legal questions, governance, and operational safeguards. The anticipated impacts include faster issuance and settlement, improved transparency of holdings, reduced administrative burden through automation, and enhanced liquidity through secondary trading—while sandbox participation helps derisk regulatory uncertainty and supports broader institutional adoption.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2303,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01718,EU_PSTW_202609_01718,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,European Commission,Tertiary education.,"Universities and their partners need reliable ways to issue, verify, and store academic credentials, while employers and other institutions require fast authenticity checks to reduce fraud and administrative delays.",DoxyChain: Digital Credentials,European Commission; European Institution/Agency; Tertiary education.; Poland,"The solution uses a blockchain-backed credential registry to anchor authenticity proofs and ensure tamper-evident records, combined with an application layer for issuance, viewing, and verification.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Universities and their partners need reliable ways to issue, verify, and store academic credentials, while employers and other institutions require fast authenticity checks to reduce fraud and administrative delays. DoxyChain focuses on providing a credential lifecycle platform that allows credentials to be issued digitally, verified in real time, and stored in a student portfolio that can be shared when needed. Stakeholders include higher education institutions, students, employers, and credential evaluators, with the objective of improving trust, portability, and efficiency in credential verification. The solution uses a blockchain-backed credential registry to anchor authenticity proofs and ensure tamper-evident records, combined with an application layer for issuance, viewing, and verification. Credentials are created and managed through institutional workflows, then made verifiable via cryptographic signatures and on-chain attestations, while students access and share their records through a secure portfolio interface. The project’s acceptance into the European Blockchain Sandbox’s second cohort supports engagement with regulators on privacy, governance, and cross-border recognition considerations. Expected outcomes include faster verification turnaround, lower administrative costs for institutions, reduced credential fraud, improved user experience for graduates, and scalable interoperability with broader European digital credential and identity ecosystems as standards mature.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2304,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01719,EU_PSTW_202609_01719,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,European Commission,Police Services,"Compliance screening depends on timely, accurate updates to watchlists used for anti-money laundering and counter-terrorism controls, yet the update process can be manual, resource-intensive, and exposed to delays, errors, or manipulation—leaving financial institutions vulnerable. Integration focuses on connecting the ledger layer to existing screening systems via APIs and automated workflows so institutions receive updates without manual intervention.",Eastnets: SafeWatch Screening ChainFeed,European Commission; European Institution/Agency; Police Services; Belgium,"The solution applies distributed ledger technology to publish and synchronise watchlist changes, creating an immutable audit trail of update provenance and timing.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Compliance screening depends on timely, accurate updates to watchlists used for anti-money laundering and counter-terrorism controls, yet the update process can be manual, resource-intensive, and exposed to delays, errors, or manipulation—leaving financial institutions vulnerable. Eastnets’ SafeWatch Screening ChainFeed aims to improve how watchlists are distributed and updated so that banks can rely on consistent, verifiable, near-real-time data feeds. Stakeholders include watchlist providers, banks and compliance teams, auditors, and supervisors, with the objective of automating updates while improving integrity and traceability across the distribution chain. The solution applies distributed ledger technology to publish and synchronise watchlist changes, creating an immutable audit trail of update provenance and timing. Conceptually, it replaces or augments batch-based processes with a ledger-backed “chain feed” where authorised participants can verify that they are operating on the latest approved version, and where update events can be cryptographically validated end-to-end. Integration focuses on connecting the ledger layer to existing screening systems via APIs and automated workflows so institutions receive updates without manual intervention. Expected impacts include reduced operational workload, fewer update errors, stronger resilience against tampering, improved audit readiness, and faster compliance response—supported by participation in the European Blockchain Sandbox to address regulatory expectations for critical compliance infrastructure.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK|F08_ERROR_COMPLIANCE_RISK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2305,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01720,EU_PSTW_202609_01720,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,European Commission,Economic affairs n.e.c,"As decentralised applications and tokenised services expand, security failures and weak controls can expose consumers and investors to losses and undermine trust, while organisations struggle to interpret and meet evolving regulatory requirements.",Hacken: blockchain security auditor,European Commission; European Institution/Agency; Economic affairs n.e.c; Ukraine,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"As decentralised applications and tokenised services expand, security failures and weak controls can expose consumers and investors to losses and undermine trust, while organisations struggle to interpret and meet evolving regulatory requirements. Hacken targets this gap by providing security auditing, assurance, and standards work for Layer 1 protocols, decentralised applications, and tokenisation initiatives, helping projects strengthen defences and demonstrate compliance readiness. Stakeholders include protocol teams, dApp developers, token issuers, users, and regulators concerned with consumer protection and operational resilience, with the objective of improving security baselines and aligning practices with EU requirements such as MiCA and DORA. Hacken’s services combine technical audits (code review, penetration testing, and vulnerability assessment), security monitoring, and governance/control recommendations, alongside participation in standards initiatives to formalise best practices. It also explores tokenisation mechanisms such as the Hacken Equity Share (HES), where security, disclosure, and lifecycle controls are integral to design. Selection for the European Blockchain Sandbox’s second cohort supports structured dialogue on how security assurance, disclosures, and supervisory expectations should apply across decentralised models. The expected benefits include fewer critical vulnerabilities, stronger investor confidence, clearer compliance pathways, and a more mature security culture that lowers systemic risk and supports sustainable adoption of blockchain-based services.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2306,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01721,EU_PSTW_202609_01721,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,European Commission,Economic affairs n.e.c,UNKNOWN,Verifiably Trustworthy IoT Edge Data,European Commission; European Institution/Agency; Economic affairs n.e.c; Germany,"To achieve this, Infineon implemented hardware-based trust anchors at IoT edge devices, combining secure elements with cryptographic signing and time-stamping of sensor data before anchoring integrity proofs to distributed ledgers.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Verifiably Trustworthy IoT Edge Data pilot addressed the growing need for reliable and tamper-proof data generated by connected devices used in public and industrial services, where data integrity is essential for compliance, automation, and cross-organisational trust. The initiative involved technology providers, public authorities, and regulators, with the objective of ensuring that data produced at the edge can be trusted without relying on central intermediaries. The pilot explored how edge-generated data streams could be validated and reused across digital services while meeting regulatory expectations. To achieve this, Infineon implemented hardware-based trust anchors at IoT edge devices, combining secure elements with cryptographic signing and time-stamping of sensor data before anchoring integrity proofs to distributed ledgers. The solution ensured data authenticity, provenance, and immutability while remaining interoperable with blockchain-based applications. System integration focused on linking edge devices, secure key management, and ledger interfaces without exposing raw data. The pilot ran from early 2024 to late 2024 as part of the European Blockchain Sandbox second cohort. Results demonstrated improved trust in decentralised data sharing, reduced verification costs, and higher assurance for regulators evaluating distributed systems. The approach proved scalable across IoT deployments and contributed to legal clarity for trusted edge data in regulated environments.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2307,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01722,EU_PSTW_202609_01722,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,European Commission,Economic affairs n.e.c,"Conventional observation methods can be limited in precision, reducing the ability of public institutions and researchers to capture environmental changes early enough or in sufficient detail to support policy and planning.",Quantum space sensor to fight climate change,European Commission; European Institution/Agency; Economic affairs n.e.c; France,The objective of this use case is to improve Earth observation capacity by developing highly precise sensing technologies for future space missions focused on climate and environmental monitoring. The implementation uses quantum-based acceleration sensors designed to measure motion with exceptional sensitivity.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Monitoring climate-related changes such as glacier loss, sea-level rise, and groundwater variation requires measurement systems capable of detecting extremely subtle shifts in the Earth’s gravity field. Conventional observation methods can be limited in precision, reducing the ability of public institutions and researchers to capture environmental changes early enough or in sufficient detail to support policy and planning. The objective of this use case is to improve Earth observation capacity by developing highly precise sensing technologies for future space missions focused on climate and environmental monitoring. The implementation uses quantum-based acceleration sensors designed to measure motion with exceptional sensitivity. At a conceptual level, these sensors apply quantum technologies to generate more accurate acceleration data, which can in turn support refined measurement of changes in the planet’s gravity field. The resulting data can be incorporated into future space observation infrastructures to improve the monitoring of glaciers, groundwater reserves, and sea-level dynamics. The expected impact includes more precise and timely environmental intelligence, stronger scientific evidence for climate policy, and improved capability to detect and interpret changes affecting natural resources and ecosystems. In the longer term, the technology offers significant business and public value by increasing the reliability of space-based monitoring systems, supporting better decision-making, and strengthening Europe’s capacity for advanced climate-related observation and resilience planning.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2308,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01723,EU_PSTW_202609_01723,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Radio Televisione Italiana,Cultural services.,UNKNOWN,Curve Real Time - Monitoring TV Audience,Radio Televisione Italiana; Central-Government; Cultural services.; Italy,"The use case focuses on tracking TV audiences and analysing real-time reactions to broadcast content, while RAI’s wider AI agenda includes data governance, production-process automation, metadata enrichment, recommendation engines and entity recognition. The implementation uses descriptive, predictive and generative AI methods to process audience-related data and support both content creation and content consumption.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Curve Real Time is part of RAI’s broader strategy to become a more data-driven public broadcasting organisation. The operational problem is that broadcasters need timely insight into audience reactions, content performance and viewer behaviour in order to improve programming, personalise services and manage increasingly complex media environments. The use case focuses on tracking TV audiences and analysing real-time reactions to broadcast content, while RAI’s wider AI agenda includes data governance, production-process automation, metadata enrichment, recommendation engines and entity recognition. The implementation uses descriptive, predictive and generative AI methods to process audience-related data and support both content creation and content consumption. Conceptually, Curve Real Time combines real-time audience monitoring, reaction analysis, data analytics and decision-support dashboards for editorial and operational teams. The expected impact is better understanding of audience engagement, more responsive content strategies, improved operational efficiency and stronger user-centricity in public broadcasting. The available public material confirms that RAI presented AI-supported processes, including this type of use case, in the context of the Politecnico di Milano Artificial Intelligence Observatory, but I did not find detailed evidence of the current operational maturity of Curve Real Time specifically.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2309,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01724,EU_PSTW_202609_01724,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,European Commission - DG DIGIT,Economic affairs n.e.c,UNKNOWN,GPT@EC: Generative AI Tool to support EC administrative task,European Commission - DG DIGIT; European Institution/Agency; Economic affairs n.e.c; European Union,"The organisational challenge is that staff regularly need to draft texts, summarise documents, develop code and process internal information, but using external third-party tools can create risks for confidentiality and compliance. The implementation provides access to several large language models, including models suitable for different tasks and sensitivity levels, with the possibility of using open-source and sovereign models.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"GPT@EC is the European Commission’s secure general-purpose generative AI tool for supporting administrative and knowledge tasks across Commission departments and executive agencies. The organisational challenge is that staff regularly need to draft texts, summarise documents, develop code and process internal information, but using external third-party tools can create risks for confidentiality and compliance. GPT@EC was developed by DG DIGIT as an internal corporate tool adapted to the Commission context, allowing staff to process internal Commission information without sharing it with external parties. The implementation provides access to several large language models, including models suitable for different tasks and sensitivity levels, with the possibility of using open-source and sovereign models. Conceptually, the tool combines secure model access, internal governance rules, compliance controls and task-oriented generative functions such as drafting, summarisation and coding assistance. The expected impact is improved productivity, greater creativity, reduced dependency on unmanaged external tools and safer experimentation with generative AI in the EU administration. The Commission presents the roll-out as aligned with its AI@EC approach for lawful, safe and trustworthy AI, with staff feedback used to improve the tool.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2310,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01725,EU_PSTW_202609_01725,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,European Commission - DG DIGIT,Economic affairs n.e.c,"The operational challenge is that JRC staff routinely handle complex knowledge tasks, including summarising evidence, translating material, drafting briefings, proofreading, classification, sentiment analysis and preparing analytical inputs for policy processes.",GPT@JRC: Generative AI Tool to support Science for Policy at JRC,European Commission - DG DIGIT; European Institution/Agency; Economic affairs n.e.c; European Union,"Using external generative tools can create confidentiality and compliance risks, particularly where internal documents or sensitive policy material are involved. The implementation allows JRC staff to use LLMs for day-to-day productivity tasks and to test how generative functions can be embedded into existing tools and workflows through automated access and processing.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"GPT@JRC was developed inside the European Commission’s Joint Research Centre to give staff a secure environment for experimenting with large language models in science-for-policy work. The operational challenge is that JRC staff routinely handle complex knowledge tasks, including summarising evidence, translating material, drafting briefings, proofreading, classification, sentiment analysis and preparing analytical inputs for policy processes. Using external generative tools can create confidentiality and compliance risks, particularly where internal documents or sensitive policy material are involved. GPT@JRC therefore provides controlled access to language-model capabilities within a secure platform. The implementation allows JRC staff to use LLMs for day-to-day productivity tasks and to test how generative functions can be embedded into existing tools and workflows through automated access and processing. Conceptually, the platform combines secure model access, experimentation facilities, internal governance, prompt-based interaction and potential integration with other JRC systems. The expected impact is improved productivity, faster document handling, enhanced quality of drafts and summaries, and better understanding of how generative AI can support science-for-policy work. It also served as an important precursor to broader Commission-level tools, helping the institution test safe adoption patterns before wider deployment.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2311,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01726,EU_PSTW_202609_01726,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Technical University Munich,Public order and safety n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,AI4POL: Using Artificial Intelligence to Support Regulators & Policy Makers,Technical University Munich; Academic-Research; Public order and safety n.e.c.; Germany,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"AI4POL is a Horizon Europe project designed to support regulators and policymakers in governing artificial intelligence and digital services more effectively. A central focus is consumer protection, particularly whether people understand online-contract information or simply accept long and technical texts without meaningful comprehension. The project aims to generate evidence on how regulatory measures are perceived, how companies and policymakers can receive useful feedback, and how the impact of rules in areas such as digital services and AI can be improved. Its implementation combines AI tools, policy research, human-centred analysis and stakeholder engagement. AI systems may help summarise complex legal or contractual information in simpler language, while the broader project develops tools, insights and frameworks aligned with European values, human rights and citizens’ needs. The consortium is led by Tilburg University and includes TUM and other research partners, with an advisory board involving regulators, policymakers, consumer-protection bodies, civil-society organisations and AI firms. The expected impact is stronger evidence for regulation, better consumer understanding and more effective oversight of AI and digital markets.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2312,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01727,EU_PSTW_202609_01727,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,European Parliament,General public services n.e.c.,"Researchers, policymakers and citizens often face difficulties navigating millions of multilingual legislative documents and parliamentary records.",EU Parliament archive access with Claude.AI,European Parliament; European Institution/Agency; General public services n.e.c.; European Union,"Archibot, built using Claude via Amazon Bedrock, provides a conversational interface enabling users to search, retrieve and interpret archival material rapidly.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The European Parliament introduced “Ask the EP Archives”, known as Archibot, to improve access to its extensive historical archives. Researchers, policymakers and citizens often face difficulties navigating millions of multilingual legislative documents and parliamentary records. Archibot, built using Claude via Amazon Bedrock, provides a conversational interface enabling users to search, retrieve and interpret archival material rapidly. The system processes natural language queries, matches them to indexed archive content and generates contextualised responses, drawing from over 2.1 million official documents. Advanced language models support multilingual understanding and summarisation of complex legislative texts. The deployment has reduced document search time by approximately 80 per cent, increased customer satisfaction by around 60 per cent and expanded monthly query capacity tenfold. By streamlining discovery and interpretation of archival content, Archibot enhances transparency, public engagement and institutional knowledge management within the European Parliament.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2313,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01728,EU_PSTW_202609_01728,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Gladsaxe Municipality,General services.,"The organisational challenge was to help staff navigate municipal information more quickly, draft texts more efficiently and access relevant internal knowledge without relying on dispersed intranet searches or repeated enquiries.",GladGPT for municipality employees in Gladsaxe,Gladsaxe Municipality; Local Government; General services.; Denmark,"Conceptually, the system combines a large language model, internal knowledge retrieval, secure deployment arrangements and a conversational interface adapted to public-sector use. By using a closed environment, the municipality seeks to benefit from generative AI while limiting exposure of internal data.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Gladsaxe Municipality launched GladGPT to provide municipal employees with an internal text and information assistant for everyday administrative work. The organisational challenge was to help staff navigate municipal information more quickly, draft texts more efficiently and access relevant internal knowledge without relying on dispersed intranet searches or repeated enquiries. The main stakeholders include Gladsaxe Municipality, municipal employees, IT and governance teams, and residents who may indirectly benefit from faster administrative work. Introduced in 2023, the chatbot is based on OpenAI ChatGPT-4 technology and operates in a closed environment. It is trained on all content from Gladsaxe’s intranet and selected parts of the municipality’s website, allowing employees to ask questions, locate information and generate draft texts within a municipal context. Conceptually, the system combines a large language model, internal knowledge retrieval, secure deployment arrangements and a conversational interface adapted to public-sector use. The expected impact is improved employee productivity, faster access to organisational information, more efficient drafting of routine texts and reduced time spent searching internal resources. By using a closed environment, the municipality seeks to benefit from generative AI while limiting exposure of internal data. The tool’s value remains linked to appropriate staff guidance, quality control and human review of generated content, particularly where outputs relate to citizens or administrative decisions.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI|S7_GENERATIVE_CONTENT_CREATION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2314,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01729,EU_PSTW_202609_01729,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""social_nonprofit""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,HiTZ,General services.,UNKNOWN,LATXA: a Basque language LLM,HiTZ; Academic-Research; General services.; Spain,The LATXA family consists of base transformer-based LLMs trained on curated Basque corpora using large-scale self-supervised learning.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Basque language, as a low-resource European language, faces structural disadvantages in the development of advanced natural language processing tools, limiting digital inclusion and the availability of high-quality language technologies for public services, education and industry. Existing large language models are predominantly trained on high-resource languages, constraining their effectiveness in Basque and reducing linguistic sovereignty. Stakeholders include language technology researchers, public institutions, educational bodies and technology developers working in the Basque ecosystem. The objective of the LATXA project is to develop foundational large language models specifically pre-trained for Basque, providing a robust technical base for downstream applications rather than a direct-to-consumer chatbot. The LATXA family consists of base transformer-based LLMs trained on curated Basque corpora using large-scale self-supervised learning. Model development was conducted on in-house GPU infrastructure, with final large-scale training executed on the Leonardo supercomputer at CINECA under the EuroHPC Joint Undertaking. These open models are released for expert users who can integrate or fine-tune them for task-specific applications. Expected impacts include strengthened digital presence of Basque, enhanced research capacity, support for public-sector language tools and a scalable foundation for future instruction-tuned and domain-adapted systems.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2315,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01730,EU_PSTW_202609_01730,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""social_nonprofit""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,European Commission,R&D general public services.,"Digital services in Web3 and decentralised finance require trustworthy user authentication mechanisms, yet many current approaches struggle to balance privacy, regulatory compliance, and usability.",Decentralised and Secure Approach to User Authentication,European Commission; European Institution/Agency; R&D general public services.; Germany,"The purpose of this use case is to provide a secure identity solution that enables verified participation in decentralised ecosystems while preserving privacy and supporting compliance requirements. At a conceptual level, the system combines automated and human-assisted identity verification with decentralised token issuance, storing the resulting credential in the user’s wallet for use in on-chain interactions.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Digital services in Web3 and decentralised finance require trustworthy user authentication mechanisms, yet many current approaches struggle to balance privacy, regulatory compliance, and usability. Service providers need ways to verify that users are legitimate without exposing unnecessary personal data, while regulators and enforcement authorities require appropriate safeguards for accountability in cases of fraud or illicit activity. The purpose of this use case is to provide a secure identity solution that enables verified participation in decentralised ecosystems while preserving privacy and supporting compliance requirements. The implementation is based on a Web3 identification solution developed by a consortium including the IOTA Foundation and other partners, and tested within the European Blockchain Sandbox. At a conceptual level, the system combines automated and human-assisted identity verification with decentralised token issuance, storing the resulting credential in the user’s wallet for use in on-chain interactions. The token allows applications to distinguish verified from unverified users without routinely revealing personal information, while maintaining controlled mechanisms for revocation or authorised disclosure. The impact includes stronger trust in Web3 transactions, reduced onboarding friction for compliant services, and improved privacy compared with conventional identification methods. It also supports scalability for regulated decentralised applications, lowers compliance risk, and creates value by enabling more secure and interoperable digital identity management.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2316,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01731,EU_PSTW_202609_01731,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,European Commission,R&D economic affairs,"Traditional disbursement mechanisms can make it difficult to control how money is spent, whether it reaches the intended recipients, and whether it produces the desired economic or social effect. The impact includes lower administrative burden, reduced fraud and misuse, and improved traceability of public or organisational spending.",Purpose Bound Money,European Commission; European Institution/Agency; R&D economic affairs; Netherlands,"The objective of this use case is to provide a more secure and efficient way to distribute earmarked funds so that money is spent only for approved purposes, by approved recipients, within defined limits and timeframes. The implementation is based on a blockchain-enabled model known as Purpose Bound Money, developed and deployed through the MultiKnip project and examined within the European Blockchain Sandbox.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Public authorities and organisations distributing subsidies, grants, and allocated budgets often face operational problems linked to fraud, misuse, unused funds, and heavy administrative processes. Traditional disbursement mechanisms can make it difficult to control how money is spent, whether it reaches the intended recipients, and whether it produces the desired economic or social effect. The objective of this use case is to provide a more secure and efficient way to distribute earmarked funds so that money is spent only for approved purposes, by approved recipients, within defined limits and timeframes. The implementation is based on a blockchain-enabled model known as Purpose Bound Money, developed and deployed through the MultiKnip project and examined within the European Blockchain Sandbox. At a conceptual level, the system allows the issuer to define rules governing who receives funds, how much can be spent, where they may be used, for what purpose, and until when. Payments are then settled only for actual use at approved retailers, linking programmable controls with accountable fund distribution. The impact includes lower administrative burden, reduced fraud and misuse, and improved traceability of public or organisational spending. Additional value comes from more efficient subsidy delivery, better control over outcomes, and positive local economic effects through targeted spending within approved ecosystems.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2317,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01732,EU_PSTW_202609_01732,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""social_nonprofit"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Groningen and the Province of Groningen,Environmental protection n.e.c.,"The challenge is to ensure that public support reaches the intended businesses, is used for eligible products and services, and creates measurable local value without adding excessive administrative effort for the municipality, retailers, or beneficiaries. The impact includes lower administrative burden, reduced misuse, faster and more traceable subsidy distribution, and stronger local economic participation.",Groningen Werkt Slim,Municipality of Groningen and the Province of Groningen; Local Government; Environmental protection n.e.c.; Netherlands,"The objective of this use case is therefore to make targeted sustainability support easier to distribute, easier to use, and easier to control, while encouraging uptake of energy-saving measures and allowing future expansion into other green initiatives. The implementation uses MultiKnip, a blockchain-based wallet for purpose-bound tokens built on IOTA technology, through which Groningen issues €300 in digital funds to eligible SMEs via the Groningen Werkt Slim app.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"In Groningen, small and medium-sized enterprises often face practical barriers when trying to invest in energy-efficiency improvements, particularly where modest subsidy schemes must be distributed quickly, spent only on approved purposes, and linked to local suppliers. The challenge is to ensure that public support reaches the intended businesses, is used for eligible products and services, and creates measurable local value without adding excessive administrative effort for the municipality, retailers, or beneficiaries. The objective of this use case is therefore to make targeted sustainability support easier to distribute, easier to use, and easier to control, while encouraging uptake of energy-saving measures and allowing future expansion into other green initiatives. The implementation uses MultiKnip, a blockchain-based wallet for purpose-bound tokens built on IOTA technology, through which Groningen issues €300 in digital funds to eligible SMEs via the Groningen Werkt Slim app. These tokens can be spent only on approved energy-saving products and services, such as purchases at regional hardware stores and work carried out by “energy-fixers”, with QR-code-based transactions linking users, retailers, and municipal reimbursement flows. The impact includes lower administrative burden, reduced misuse, faster and more traceable subsidy distribution, and stronger local economic participation. It also creates a scalable foundation for extending controlled funding to further measures such as green roofs and electric vehicle charging infrastructure.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2318,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01733,EU_PSTW_202609_01733,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,European Commission,R&D general public services.,"Electronic voting continues to face unresolved challenges around voter anonymity, ballot secrecy, trusted counting, and verifiability, all of which are essential if digital participation is to be accepted in public decision-making. At the same time, public bodies need voting systems that are accessible, compliant with digital identity and data-protection rules, and inclusive for citizens who may struggle with traditional in-person processes.",Electronic voting systems by employing decentralized identities,European Commission; European Institution/Agency; R&D general public services.; Greece,"The objective of this use case is to create a citizen-centred e-voting framework that increases trust, protects privacy, and broadens participation in public consultations and governance processes. A mobile wallet acts as the core interface, enabling interoperable authentication based on trusted public-sector anchors and supporting real use cases in the Greek municipality of Trikala.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Electronic voting continues to face unresolved challenges around voter anonymity, ballot secrecy, trusted counting, and verifiability, all of which are essential if digital participation is to be accepted in public decision-making. At the same time, public bodies need voting systems that are accessible, compliant with digital identity and data-protection rules, and inclusive for citizens who may struggle with traditional in-person processes. The objective of this use case is to create a citizen-centred e-voting framework that increases trust, protects privacy, and broadens participation in public consultations and governance processes. The implementation is represented by TREVO, which combines decentralised identifiers, verifiable credentials, and zero-knowledge proofs to authenticate and authorise voters while preserving anonymity and secrecy. A mobile wallet acts as the core interface, enabling interoperable authentication based on trusted public-sector anchors and supporting real use cases in the Greek municipality of Trikala. The solution is designed around end-user co-creation, linking identity credentials, privacy-preserving cryptographic methods, and voting workflows in a conceptually integrated system aligned with EU guidance on digital identity and data protection. The expected results include stronger voter trust, more secure and auditable digital ballots, and improved accessibility for elderly, disabled, and remote users. It also offers lower process costs, reduced paperwork and travel, and better scalability for future digital participation services.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2319,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01734,EU_PSTW_202609_01734,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Fraunhofer IAIS and Fraunhofer IIS,R&D general public services.,UNKNOWN,OpenGPT-X - 35 GenAI usecases,Fraunhofer IAIS and Fraunhofer IIS; Academic-Research; R&D general public services.; Germany,"OpenGPT-X was created to cover the full generative-AI value chain, from training data and scalable GPU infrastructure to model development and practical prototypes, so that European actors could test and build productive use cases from the beginning. Official Fraunhofer sources confirm that the project has ended, while also showing that its outputs continue through model families such as Teuken and through published use-case and code materials.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Europe faces a strategic need for large language models and related infrastructure that are developed within its own technical, regulatory, and linguistic context rather than relying only on external providers. OpenGPT-X was created to cover the full generative-AI value chain, from training data and scalable GPU infrastructure to model development and practical prototypes, so that European actors could test and build productive use cases from the beginning. The objective is to advance research, strengthen digital sovereignty, and enable industry partners to explore practical generative-AI applications through proofs of concept and deployable models. The implementation was a consortium project funded by the German Federal Ministry for Economic Affairs and Climate Action from January 2022 to 31 March 2025, combining research institutions and industry partners. Official Fraunhofer sources confirm that the project has ended, while also showing that its outputs continue through model families such as Teuken and through published use-case and code materials. The impact includes open multilingual model assets, stronger European capability across the generative-AI stack, and reusable technical results that can support future industrial and public-sector applications even after the formal project period has finished.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2320,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01735,EU_PSTW_202609_01735,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,EBU,Broadcasting and publishing services.,UNKNOWN,GenerativeAI @ TEMS,EBU; Academic-Research; Broadcasting and publishing services.; European Union,"Public-service media organisations need trustworthy, rights-aware ways to engage with generative AI, especially where questions of data use, attribution, verification, and audience trust directly affect their core mission. Within the Trusted European Media Data Space, the GenerativeAI @ TEMS activity addresses this by creating a framework for discussion and collaboration around the use of AI in media-data environments, with the EBU treating AI as a strategic priority.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Public-service media organisations need trustworthy, rights-aware ways to engage with generative AI, especially where questions of data use, attribution, verification, and audience trust directly affect their core mission. Within the Trusted European Media Data Space, the GenerativeAI @ TEMS activity addresses this by creating a framework for discussion and collaboration around the use of AI in media-data environments, with the EBU treating AI as a strategic priority. The objective is to help media actors adopt generative AI responsibly, protect rights and provenance, and ensure that high-quality media data can support trustworthy AI systems. The implementation is not a single chatbot or model, but a coordinated initiative involving publications, stakeholder engagement, data-space development, and collaboration with companies such as Mistral. Recent TEMS and EBU materials show that the topic remains active in 2025, including discussion of AI governance priorities and the role of TEMS as an enabler of trusted, rights-cleared media content for AI-related use cases. The impact lies in shaping governance, data access, and collaboration conditions for future AI deployment in public-service media, creating strategic value through standards, interoperability, and trusted data practices rather than through one direct end-user service.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2321,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01736,EU_PSTW_202609_01736,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,European Commission,General public services n.e.c.,"The European Language Data Space addresses this challenge by building a data market and exchange infrastructure for language resources, with the generative-AI dimension focused on making those resources available as an enabler for European language technologies.",GenerativeAI @ LDS,European Commission; Academic-Research; General public services n.e.c.; European Union,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Europe’s language-technology ecosystem needs much larger volumes of trustworthy language resources if it is to support strong multilingual LLMs and generative-AI applications across official, regional, and minority languages. The European Language Data Space addresses this challenge by building a data market and exchange infrastructure for language resources, with the generative-AI dimension focused on making those resources available as an enabler for European language technologies. The objective is to assemble a broad user and provider community, improve access to language data, and strengthen the foundations for a more inclusive European LLM and GenAI landscape. The implementation centres on the LDS platform and marketplace, which are being developed under the EU Data Strategy as infrastructure for sharing language data and models between public and private actors. Current official sources show that the platform is live, that versions continue to be released, and that the infrastructure and user group are still growing, which matches the description of an initiative still in build-out rather than fully complete. The impact includes improved availability of multilingual language resources, stronger support for European language technologies, and a more scalable market structure for exchanging data needed to train and improve future language models across Europe.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2322,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01737,EU_PSTW_202609_01737,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Fabbrica di San Pietro,"R&D recreation, culture and religion.","Historic and religious landmarks are often difficult to document, interpret, and open up fully to the public, especially in areas that are physically inaccessible or too fragile for close everyday access.",Virtual St. Peter's Basilica with Artificial Intelligence,"Fabbrica di San Pietro; Central-Government; R&D recreation, culture and religion.; Vatican City","The official platform presents this as an AI-powered digital experience, with interactive online exploration and related immersive exhibitions that connect history, art, and spirituality through a virtual model. The impact includes global access to an iconic cultural site, richer educational and visitor experiences, and a scalable model for using advanced digital reconstruction to support heritage accessibility and stewardship.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Historic and religious landmarks are often difficult to document, interpret, and open up fully to the public, especially in areas that are physically inaccessible or too fragile for close everyday access. The digital twin of St. Peter’s Basilica was created to address this by enabling pilgrims, visitors, historians, and preservation specialists to explore the site in a detailed virtual environment while also supporting conservation and communication goals linked to Jubilee 2025. The objective is to make the Basilica more accessible and understandable, extend public engagement beyond physical visits, and create a precise digital representation that can assist interpretation and preservation. The implementation combines photogrammetry, AI, and immersive digital presentation techniques to reconstruct the Basilica in 3D, including areas normally unavailable to visitors. The official platform presents this as an AI-powered digital experience, with interactive online exploration and related immersive exhibitions that connect history, art, and spirituality through a virtual model. At a conceptual level, the solution merges high-resolution capture, 3D modelling, and AI-assisted interpretation into a digital twin environment. The impact includes global access to an iconic cultural site, richer educational and visitor experiences, and a scalable model for using advanced digital reconstruction to support heritage accessibility and stewardship.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2323,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01738,EU_PSTW_202609_01738,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,European Commission,"General economic, commercial and labour affair","Freelance work is often coordinated through fragmented platforms, informal governance, or conventional management structures that can increase transaction costs, create ambiguity over rules and payments, and weaken trust between contributors.",Decentralised Autonomous Organisation for freelance work,"European Commission; European Institution/Agency; General economic, commercial and labour affair; Spain","The purpose of this use case is to provide a more efficient and trust-based operating model for freelance collaboration in Web3 settings, where work, reputation, and funding can be coordinated through programmable rules. The implementation is embodied in MINOS, a platform that operates as a decentralised autonomous organisation and uses blockchain-based code to define and enforce organisational rules.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Freelance work is often coordinated through fragmented platforms, informal governance, or conventional management structures that can increase transaction costs, create ambiguity over rules and payments, and weaken trust between contributors. In decentralised digital work environments, there is a need for new organisational models that allow teams to manage resources, responsibilities, and incentives transparently without relying on a fixed management hierarchy. The purpose of this use case is to provide a more efficient and trust-based operating model for freelance collaboration in Web3 settings, where work, reputation, and funding can be coordinated through programmable rules. The implementation is embodied in MINOS, a platform that operates as a decentralised autonomous organisation and uses blockchain-based code to define and enforce organisational rules. Its architecture is structured around domains and permissions, allowing teams, projects, or departments to organise work and control access to functions, while funding pots and expenditure units manage token-based payments. In parallel, a domain-specific reputation mechanism records the value of contributions over time, supports merit-based decision-making, and aligns reward distribution with recent participation. The impact includes lower coordination costs, stronger transparency in resource allocation, and more reliable collaboration across distributed freelance networks. It also improves organisational flexibility, supports scalable governance, and enables a more structured and incentive-compatible approach to digital work.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2324,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01739,EU_PSTW_202609_01739,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,European Commission,Economic affairs n.e.c,"Across Europe, smaller bond and equity issuances, especially those linked to SMEs and renewable-energy projects, often struggle to attract a broad investor base because traditional securities processes are costly, fragmented, and difficult to scale. A key challenge is to create an infrastructure that connects issuance, registration, custody, trading, and settlement more efficiently while remaining compatible with regulatory requirements and existing market structures.",Digital Asset Platform,European Commission; European Institution/Agency; Economic affairs n.e.c; Germany,"The objective of this use case is to reduce friction in capital formation and help address funding gaps by making tokenised securities easier to issue, manage, and trade.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Across Europe, smaller bond and equity issuances, especially those linked to SMEs and renewable-energy projects, often struggle to attract a broad investor base because traditional securities processes are costly, fragmented, and difficult to scale. A key challenge is to create an infrastructure that connects issuance, registration, custody, trading, and settlement more efficiently while remaining compatible with regulatory requirements and existing market structures. The objective of this use case is to reduce friction in capital formation and help address funding gaps by making tokenised securities easier to issue, manage, and trade. The implementation is delivered through NYALA’s Digital Asset Platform, built on the public Polygon PoS chain, which supports the minting, distribution, registration, and custody of tokenised securities while linking on-chain and off-chain ownership information. The platform functions conceptually as a back-end engine connecting investment platforms into a broader trading ecosystem under the DLT Pilot Regime, with off-chain trading through an existing multilateral trading facility and on-chain settlement infrastructure. It also integrates an e-money token for delivery-versus-payment and automated dividend distribution. The impact includes improved efficiency and transparency across the securities value chain, broader investor access to previously sub-scale instruments, and stronger scalability for digital capital markets. It also offers lower operational friction, faster settlement, and greater potential to mobilise financing for growth-oriented and sustainability-linked projects.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2325,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01740,EU_PSTW_202609_01740,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""social_nonprofit"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,European Commission,R&D economic affairs,UNKNOWN,Verifiable Web for AI,European Commission; European Institution/Agency; R&D economic affairs; Slovenia,"The goal of this use case is to create a more trustworthy foundation for AI by making source information discoverable, verifiable, and attributable across decentralised environments. The technology is intended for a wide spectrum of uses, including industry, infrastructure, science, and misinformation-related applications, and conceptually integrates decentralised data publishing, knowledge graph structuring, and AI retrieval workflows.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Many AI systems rely on external information sources that may be inaccurate, manipulated, or impossible to verify, creating risks around hallucinations, bias, provenance, and misuse of data. For public bodies, infrastructure operators, and large organisations, this weakens trust in generated outputs and limits the safe deployment of AI in knowledge-intensive environments where factual grounding and ownership of information matter. The goal of this use case is to create a more trustworthy foundation for AI by making source information discoverable, verifiable, and attributable across decentralised environments. The implementation is based on OriginTrail’s Decentralized Knowledge Graph, a permissionless network operating above a blockchain layer and designed to provide a trusted knowledge foundation for AI applications. Within this architecture, decentralised retrieval-augmented generation extends conventional RAG by organising external knowledge in a structured graph and associating it with provenance and ownership signals, thereby supporting neuro-symbolic decentralised AI. The technology is intended for a wide spectrum of uses, including industry, infrastructure, science, and misinformation-related applications, and conceptually integrates decentralised data publishing, knowledge graph structuring, and AI retrieval workflows. The impact includes more reliable generated content, stronger resistance to manipulation, and improved transparency about where information comes from. It also supports scalable knowledge sharing, incentivises data ownership, and increases the business value of AI systems that depend on auditable, high-integrity information.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S1_KNOWLEDGE_RETRIEVAL_RAG|S7_GENERATIVE_CONTENT_CREATION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2326,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01741,EU_PSTW_202609_01741,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,European Commission,R&D economic affairs,"Impact investment and ESG-linked fundraising frequently suffer from high transaction friction, fragmented compliance processes, and persistent concerns about the credibility of sustainability claims.",Trrue Chain,European Commission; European Institution/Agency; R&D economic affairs; Italy,The purpose of this use case is to optimise fundraising for impact-focused projects by combining distributed-ledger technology with governance mechanisms that support ethical standards and regulatory compliance.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Impact investment and ESG-linked fundraising frequently suffer from high transaction friction, fragmented compliance processes, and persistent concerns about the credibility of sustainability claims. Investors, project sponsors, and regulators all need mechanisms that improve trust, reduce cost, and maintain legal certainty without losing the benefits of digital infrastructure. The purpose of this use case is to optimise fundraising for impact-focused projects by combining distributed-ledger technology with governance mechanisms that support ethical standards and regulatory compliance. The implementation is centred on Trrue Chain, a blockchain infrastructure designed to verify on-chain data through a network of trusted participants while preserving legal relationships required for regulated financial activity. Built with Polkadot Substrate and Cumulus, the system enables interoperability across blockchains and is intended to connect traditional financial markets with Web3 environments. At a conceptual level, it combines governance protocols, verification layers, and interoperable digital-asset tooling to support compliant fundraising and help address greenwashing risks in financial services. The expected impact includes faster and more secure fundraising processes, lower operational cost than traditional systems, and improved confidence in the quality and integrity of impact investment data. It also supports scalability for responsible investment ecosystems and creates business value by making ESG-positive projects easier to finance within a more transparent and regulation-aware framework.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2327,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01742,EU_PSTW_202609_01742,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,European Commission,R&D economic affairs,"Access to high-value assets such as commercial property and other real-world assets has historically been limited to institutional investors and wealthy individuals, largely because of high entry thresholds, illiquidity, and complex transaction structures.",Tokenization and fractional ownership of real estate and RWAs,European Commission; European Institution/Agency; R&D economic affairs; Ireland,The objective of this use case is to widen access to asset-backed investment opportunities by enabling fractional ownership and more liquid participation in real estate and other tokenised real-world assets.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Access to high-value assets such as commercial property and other real-world assets has historically been limited to institutional investors and wealthy individuals, largely because of high entry thresholds, illiquidity, and complex transaction structures. This restricts participation, narrows sources of capital, and reduces the flexibility with which assets can be financed or traded. The objective of this use case is to widen access to asset-backed investment opportunities by enabling fractional ownership and more liquid participation in real estate and other tokenised real-world assets. The implementation is provided through REClosure’s financial technology platform, which tokenises underlying assets and divides ownership into digital units that can be offered to a broader investor base, including retail participants. At a conceptual level, the platform links asset tokenisation, ownership representation, and marketplace trading functions so that value derived from rental returns or asset appreciation can be associated with fractional digital holdings. The example presented for the sandbox describes a €20 million office property split into 500 units of €20,000, illustrating how tokenisation can lower barriers to entry while preserving tradability. The impact includes broader investor inclusion, improved liquidity for traditionally illiquid assets, and greater flexibility in capital raising. It also supports more scalable market participation, expands access to diversified investments, and increases the efficiency with which real-world assets can be financed and exchanged.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2328,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01743,EU_PSTW_202609_01743,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""social_nonprofit"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,European Commission,"Agriculture, forestry, fishing and hunting","Global trade still depends heavily on fragmented document flows, repeated data entry, manual validation, and paper-based certificates, creating delays, fraud risks, and weak visibility across logistics, mobility, and supply-chain operations, particularly in sensitive sectors such as food and agriculture. The impact includes faster shipment execution, improved supplier management, proactive alerts for non-compliant or expiring certificates, reduced delays and rework, and stronger traceability.",Certificate Management System for the Global Trade dominium,"European Commission; European Institution/Agency; Agriculture, forestry, fishing and hunting; Italy","The purpose of this use case is to create a more trustworthy, efficient, and scalable way for businesses, authorities, and supply-chain partners to exchange, sign, verify, and track certificates and related trade documents while reducing operational friction and improving compliance.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Global trade still depends heavily on fragmented document flows, repeated data entry, manual validation, and paper-based certificates, creating delays, fraud risks, and weak visibility across logistics, mobility, and supply-chain operations, particularly in sensitive sectors such as food and agriculture. The purpose of this use case is to create a more trustworthy, efficient, and scalable way for businesses, authorities, and supply-chain partners to exchange, sign, verify, and track certificates and related trade documents while reducing operational friction and improving compliance. The implementation is based on a fully automated certificate management system developed by ReLOG3P and Morpheus.Network. At a conceptual level, it combines a cloud-based self-service portal, an integrated document-handling database, digitisation tools such as OCR and uploaders, and digital sign-off capabilities, with blockchain back ends on Ethereum and Hyperledger plus IPFS-based immutable trails. Each uploaded document receives a decentralised unique identifier and a timestamped provenance record containing creation, access, signature, and revision data, while APIs allow integration with authorities and ERP systems. The impact includes faster shipment execution, improved supplier management, proactive alerts for non-compliant or expiring certificates, reduced delays and rework, and stronger traceability. It also increases trust in document authenticity, supports food and goods safety, lowers operating costs, and provides a scalable foundation for more transparent and compliant global trade processes.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2329,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01744,EU_PSTW_202609_01744,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,European Commission,R&D economic affairs,"As digital identity, verifiable credentials, and Web3 services expand, public bodies, businesses, and regulators face a growing challenge in determining who can be trusted to issue, verify, and rely on digital attestations across different technical environments.",Universal trust registry,European Commission; European Institution/Agency; R&D economic affairs; Lithuania,"The objective of this use case is to create a more flexible and portable model for managing trusted accreditation structures across distributed environments, so that institutions can operate trusted lists and trust chains without being locked into a single ledger. At a conceptual level, the solution uses a ledger-agnostic architecture for trust registries and sequential, traversable lists of verifiable accreditations built on distributed-ledger infrastructure, drawing on DID Core and DID URL practices and extending the EBSI trust-chain model into a broader universal trust registry approach.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"As digital identity, verifiable credentials, and Web3 services expand, public bodies, businesses, and regulators face a growing challenge in determining who can be trusted to issue, verify, and rely on digital attestations across different technical environments. Existing trust frameworks are often tied to specific ledgers or ecosystems, which limits interoperability and makes it harder to prepare for regulatory frameworks such as eIDAS 2.0, the European Digital Identity ecosystem, and related digital-market requirements. The objective of this use case is to create a more flexible and portable model for managing trusted accreditation structures across distributed environments, so that institutions can operate trusted lists and trust chains without being locked into a single ledger. The implementation is being developed by a consortium led by Soverio with cheqd, Danube Tech, and Netis. At a conceptual level, the solution uses a ledger-agnostic architecture for trust registries and sequential, traversable lists of verifiable accreditations built on distributed-ledger infrastructure, drawing on DID Core and DID URL practices and extending the EBSI trust-chain model into a broader universal trust registry approach. The expected impact includes stronger interoperability across identity ecosystems, improved readiness for future regulatory compliance, and easier cross-platform trust management. It also offers greater scalability for digital credential ecosystems, lower integration friction, and more robust foundations for trusted digital services in both public and commercial settings.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2330,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01745,EU_PSTW_202609_01745,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,European Commission,R&D economic affairs,UNKNOWN,Hosted Wallets Services for non-crypto-asset / non financial related tokens,European Commission; European Institution/Agency; R&D economic affairs; Netherlands,"Organisations using tokenisation for everyday services such as event tickets, museum access, proof-of-attendance records, or fan engagement often face a regulatory problem: although their tokens are not financial instruments or crypto-assets under MiCAR, the hosted wallets embedded in their applications may still technically receive, hold, or transfer crypto-assets, including for transaction fees. This creates uncertainty, potential compliance burdens, and a risk that firms using distributed-ledger technology could face significantly higher operational costs than competitors delivering similar services through non-blockchain technologies.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Organisations using tokenisation for everyday services such as event tickets, museum access, proof-of-attendance records, or fan engagement often face a regulatory problem: although their tokens are not financial instruments or crypto-assets under MiCAR, the hosted wallets embedded in their applications may still technically receive, hold, or transfer crypto-assets, including for transaction fees. This creates uncertainty, potential compliance burdens, and a risk that firms using distributed-ledger technology could face significantly higher operational costs than competitors delivering similar services through non-blockchain technologies. The aim of this use case is to clarify how such wallet services can be provided on a level playing field while preserving innovation in non-financial token use. The implementation is being explored by the 2Tokens consortium with partners including Amdax, Custodiex, Celebratix, Metaseum, TDE, and CMS. Conceptually, the solution examines hosted or white-label wallet models for non-financial tokens, alongside the possibility of lighter regulatory treatment or exemptions, while still accommodating the technical reality that wallets may need to process network fees or hold transferable digital assets. The impact could include lower compliance friction, reduced cost barriers for service providers, and more viable adoption of token-based services in mainstream cultural and consumer applications. It also supports regulatory clarity, fairer competition, and greater scalability for digital-token services whose primary purpose lies outside financial markets.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2331,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01746,EU_PSTW_202609_01746,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Supreme Court of Justice/SCJ,Law courts,UNKNOWN,Three judges under suspicion of ‘cheating’ with artificial intelligence,Supreme Court of Justice/SCJ; Central-Government; Law courts; Portugal,"This case is not a public-service innovation project in the usual sense, but rather a controversy about the alleged use of generative AI in judicial drafting, which raises questions about transparency, accountability, and the acceptable role of automated tools in the justice system. The issue matters because courts depend on trust, legal accuracy, and human responsibility, and any use of AI in judicial reasoning or drafting can affect confidence in the integrity of judgments.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"This case is not a public-service innovation project in the usual sense, but rather a controversy about the alleged use of generative AI in judicial drafting, which raises questions about transparency, accountability, and the acceptable role of automated tools in the justice system. In Portugal, suspicion arose after an appeal-court ruling reportedly contained odd phrasing and citations that lawyers argued might have been produced with the help of an AI system, prompting scrutiny of whether judges had relied on such tools in preparing a decision. The issue matters because courts depend on trust, legal accuracy, and human responsibility, and any use of AI in judicial reasoning or drafting can affect confidence in the integrity of judgments. The reported implementation, insofar as it can be described as one, would have involved a judge or judges using a general-purpose generative AI assistant to help produce legal text rather than a formally authorised court system integrated into judicial workflow. Public reporting indicates that the judges disputed the allegations and that the matter led to official discussion and inquiry rather than confirmation of an endorsed technology solution. The impact of the episode lies in highlighting governance risks: it intensified debate about standards for AI use in courts, the need for human oversight, and the importance of clear rules on whether and how judges may use generative tools.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2332,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01747,EU_PSTW_202609_01747,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Austrian Post Group IT,R&D economic affairs,"Evaluation by 11 professionals across six agile teams indicated significant improvements in comprehensibility and consistency, although challenges related to optimal story length and contextual precision were identified.",LLM-Based Agents for User Story Quality Enhancement in Agile Development,Austrian Post Group IT; Central-Government; R&D economic affairs; Austria,An Autonomous LLM-based Agent System was implemented with specialised agent roles reflecting Product Owner and Requirements Engineer perspectives.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"In agile software development, poorly specified user stories can reduce productivity, create misunderstandings and increase rework. Austrian Post Group IT explored automated approaches to enhance user story quality within development teams. An Autonomous LLM-based Agent System was implemented with specialised agent roles reflecting Product Owner and Requirements Engineer perspectives. Large language models were employed to analyse, rewrite and refine user stories, improving clarity, structure and acceptance criteria. The system operated within existing agile workflows, providing iterative suggestions while preserving contextual alignment. Evaluation by 11 professionals across six agile teams indicated significant improvements in comprehensibility and consistency, although challenges related to optimal story length and contextual precision were identified. The initiative demonstrates how structured language model agents can augment requirements engineering processes, enhance collaboration and improve overall software development quality through targeted automation support.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2333,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01748,EU_PSTW_202609_01748,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Formez,R&D general public services.,"Public recruitment processes often generate very high volumes of candidate questions, and applicants can struggle to interpret open notices, timelines, procedures, and requirements without waiting for manual replies. The impact includes faster applicant support, lower pressure on manual help channels, and a scalable way to improve access to public competition information.","Camilla, Virtual assistant for public competitions",Formez; Central-Government; R&D general public services.; Italy,"The implementation uses a generative-AI virtual assistant accessible through a dedicated web interface, trained and tested extensively by a Formez task force with technical support from CSI Piemonte.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Public recruitment processes often generate very high volumes of candidate questions, and applicants can struggle to interpret open notices, timelines, procedures, and requirements without waiting for manual replies. Camilla was introduced by Formez, in collaboration with CSI Piemonte, to make information about public competitions easier to access and to reduce response times for people interested in ongoing calls. The objective is to provide always-available guidance, improve the applicant experience, and streamline the handling of repetitive questions in a high-demand recruitment environment. The implementation uses a generative-AI virtual assistant accessible through a dedicated web interface, trained and tested extensively by a Formez task force with technical support from CSI Piemonte. Formez reported that the tool went live on an experimental basis after more than 500 tests and that it operates 24/7 to answer questions about competitions managed by the institute; later reporting stated that it had already served over 5,000 users and handled around 22,000 questions, and that its scope had expanded to include ranking-related queries. At a conceptual level, the solution combines generative answering with institution-specific recruitment information in a public-facing assistant. The impact includes faster applicant support, lower pressure on manual help channels, and a scalable way to improve access to public competition information.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK|F06_HIGH_VOLUME_SCALABILITY_BOTTLENECK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2334,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01749,EU_PSTW_202609_01749,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,European Commission,Transport,"Export procedures frequently involve repetitive data sharing, paper-based documentation, fragmented communication between stakeholders, and manual checks that increase processing time, create opportunities for document forgery, and reduce visibility across the supply chain. For exporters, logistics providers, and public authorities, this leads to avoidable administrative effort and slower trade execution, especially when compliance, document authenticity, and secure information exchange must be maintained across organisational boundaries.",Streamlining and digitization of the export process,European Commission; European Institution/Agency; Transport; Austria,"The objective of this use case is to redesign the export process so that it becomes more digital, more secure, and more efficient from end to end. The implementation, developed by VERAG Spedition AG with Atez Yazılım Teknolojileri A.Ş., uses a hybrid distributed-ledger architecture with a custom DLT layer that allows permissioned enterprise participants to build applications and execute smart contracts for eligible users while complying with regulatory and KYC requirements.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Export procedures frequently involve repetitive data sharing, paper-based documentation, fragmented communication between stakeholders, and manual checks that increase processing time, create opportunities for document forgery, and reduce visibility across the supply chain. For exporters, logistics providers, and public authorities, this leads to avoidable administrative effort and slower trade execution, especially when compliance, document authenticity, and secure information exchange must be maintained across organisational boundaries. The objective of this use case is to redesign the export process so that it becomes more digital, more secure, and more efficient from end to end. The implementation, developed by VERAG Spedition AG with Atez Yazılım Teknolojileri A.Ş., uses a hybrid distributed-ledger architecture with a custom DLT layer that allows permissioned enterprise participants to build applications and execute smart contracts for eligible users while complying with regulatory and KYC requirements. Encryption technologies protect confidentiality and integrity, and APIs and integration tools connect the solution with existing networks to support interoperable data exchange. The proof of concept delivered a 40% reduction in work steps, enabled paperless transactions, and removed intermediaries from certain processes. The broader impact includes stronger traceability, lower fraud risk, better cooperation among stakeholders, and reduced environmental burden through dematerialisation. It also improves operational speed, cuts administrative cost, and provides a scalable route towards more resilient and transparent export operations.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2335,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01750,EU_PSTW_202609_01750,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""social_nonprofit""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,UK HM Treasury,R&D economic affairs,"Government bond issuance relies on mature but highly structured market infrastructure that can involve manual processes, fragmented records, and limited programmability across trading, settlement, and post-trade activities. The expected impact includes faster and more automated market processes, stronger auditability, and reduced reliance on manual reconciliation.",Digital Gilt Instrument,UK HM Treasury; Central-Government; R&D economic affairs; United Kingdom,The objective of this use case is to explore how distributed-ledger infrastructure can be applied across the full lifecycle of a sovereign debt issuance and to stimulate wider adoption of such infrastructure in UK capital markets.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Government bond issuance relies on mature but highly structured market infrastructure that can involve manual processes, fragmented records, and limited programmability across trading, settlement, and post-trade activities. In that context, the UK government is testing whether a digitally native sovereign debt instrument can modernise capital-market operations while preserving market integrity and fitting within an appropriate regulatory framework. The objective of this use case is to explore how distributed-ledger infrastructure can be applied across the full lifecycle of a sovereign debt issuance and to stimulate wider adoption of such infrastructure in UK capital markets. The implementation takes the form of the Digital Gilt Instrument pilot, jointly managed by HM Treasury and the UK Debt Management Office, with issuance planned on a platform operating within the Digital Securities Sandbox. Conceptually, the instrument is a new, short-dated, transferable government security held on a DLT platform, designed to test on-chain settlement, over-the-counter trading functionality, interoperability with traditional and DLT-based market participants, and greater transparency of ownership and transaction records. The expected impact includes faster and more automated market processes, stronger auditability, and reduced reliance on manual reconciliation. More broadly, the pilot is intended to improve efficiency, security, and resilience in capital markets, support innovation in digital financial infrastructure, and create a scalable foundation for future forms of digitally issued public debt.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2336,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01751,EU_PSTW_202609_01751,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Danish Municipalities,Outpatient services.,UNKNOWN,Route and shift planning with AI in the health and elderly area,Danish Municipalities; Local Government; Outpatient services.; Denmark,"Conceptually, the system applies optimisation and predictive planning to match staff availability, care visits, geography, time windows and service constraints. The expected impact is more efficient use of personnel, reduced administrative workload, improved continuity of care and better planning quality for citizens receiving home-based services.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Denmark’s route and shift planning initiative aims to improve the organisation of home care and home nursing across municipalities. The operational problem is that planning citizen visits, staff shifts and travel routes in elderly and health care is administratively heavy, complex and time-consuming, especially where municipalities must manage workforce constraints and changing care needs. The initiative supports implementation and dissemination of AI-based solutions for route and shift planning, with the objective of improving resource management and ensuring better planning of visits, duty rosters and home-care routes. Its implementation is organised through municipal implementation partnerships and focuses not only on technical systems, but also on competencies, organisational change and collection of lessons from participating municipalities. Conceptually, the system applies optimisation and predictive planning to match staff availability, care visits, geography, time windows and service constraints. The expected impact is more efficient use of personnel, reduced administrative workload, improved continuity of care and better planning quality for citizens receiving home-based services. The initiative receives DKK 22.8 million in funding.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2337,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01752,EU_PSTW_202609_01752,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Danish Municipalities,Outpatient services.,"Denmark’s speech-to-text initiative aims to reduce the documentation burden for staff working in health, home care and elderly services.",AI-enabled speech-to-text in the health and elderly area,Danish Municipalities; Local Government; Outpatient services.; Denmark,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Denmark’s speech-to-text initiative aims to reduce the documentation burden for staff working in health, home care and elderly services. The operational problem is that employees spend substantial time producing care records and placing documentation correctly in municipal IT systems, which can reduce time available for direct citizen care. The initiative focuses on developing and testing an AI-supported solution that converts audio recordings into medical-record text and helps staff decide where the generated content should be placed in the municipal system. It builds partly on experience from Thisted Municipality and will involve Copenhagen, Aarhus, Aalborg and other municipalities. Conceptually, the system combines speech recognition, natural language processing, documentation assistance and workflow guidance for municipal care records. The expected impact is faster record creation, improved documentation quality, less administrative pressure on care staff and more consistent placement of information in municipal systems. The initiative receives DKK 8.5 million in funding and forms part of Denmark’s wider effort to scale practical AI solutions across municipalities. A related Danish TALT speech-recognition project has reportedly shown time savings and is ready for national roll-out, suggesting that the policy area is moving from testing towards broader implementation.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,S3_DOCUMENT_INTELLIGENCE|S7_GENERATIVE_CONTENT_CREATION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2338,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01753,EU_PSTW_202609_01753,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Montana,Waste management.,"For municipalities and waste-management operators, this creates both a financial and an environmental burden, especially where available operational data is underused and vehicle routing is not dynamically aligned with real needs.",ROSE - Route optimisation module for waste logistic platforms - Montana (BG) pilot,Municipality of Montana; Local Government; Waste management.; Bulgaria,"Municipal waste collection often runs on fixed schedules that do not reflect actual container fill levels, leading to unnecessary journeys, higher operating costs, avoidable fuel use, and excess emissions. The aim of this use case is to improve the efficiency and sustainability of urban waste collection by ensuring that collections take place only when needed and with the most suitable routing and vehicle allocation.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Municipal waste collection often runs on fixed schedules that do not reflect actual container fill levels, leading to unnecessary journeys, higher operating costs, avoidable fuel use, and excess emissions. For municipalities and waste-management operators, this creates both a financial and an environmental burden, especially where available operational data is underused and vehicle routing is not dynamically aligned with real needs. The aim of this use case is to improve the efficiency and sustainability of urban waste collection by ensuring that collections take place only when needed and with the most suitable routing and vehicle allocation. The implementation, led by Theoremus, uses an advanced logistics routing engine that clusters waste containers according to historical fill rates, assigns the most appropriate vehicles to each cluster, and optimises collection schedules using transport data patterns. The solution builds on Digital Waste Monitoring Platforms and has been deployed in Cascais and Montana within a trans-European project context. The results are already measurable: the project anticipated a 10–20% reduction in miles driven and operating cost, and in the Montana pilot weekly collection distance fell by 10.2%, from 173.03 km to 155.36 km, with fuel consumption and CO₂ emissions reduced by a similar margin. This improves operational performance, lowers congestion and heavy-vehicle presence on local streets, and creates stronger long-term value through more data-driven and scalable waste-management practices.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2339,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01754,EU_PSTW_202609_01754,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Cascais,Waste management.,"Traditional collection routes can be inefficient when they do not reflect the actual fill levels of containers or the most suitable deployment of available vehicles, leading to unnecessary mileage, wasted time and avoidable fuel consumption. The expected impact is fewer vehicle miles travelled, lower operational time and reduced costs, together with lower fuel consumption, emissions and traffic burden.",ROSE - Route optimisation module for waste logistic platforms - Cascais (PT) pilot,City of Cascais; Local Government; Waste management.; Portugal,"The solution is implemented as an advanced logistics routing engine that clusters waste containers on the basis of historical fill-rate patterns, assigns the most appropriate vehicles to each cluster and optimises collection schedules using transport data. The system is being deployed in Cascais and Montana, demonstrating its applicability across different urban contexts.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Waste collection services face constant pressure to reduce costs, emissions and congestion while maintaining reliable collection standards across urban areas. Traditional collection routes can be inefficient when they do not reflect the actual fill levels of containers or the most suitable deployment of available vehicles, leading to unnecessary mileage, wasted time and avoidable fuel consumption. ROSE addresses this operational problem by helping municipalities organise waste collection more intelligently and only when necessary. The stakeholders include city authorities, waste-management operators and logistics planners, with the objective of improving the efficiency and sustainability of collection operations through smarter route design and scheduling. The solution is implemented as an advanced logistics routing engine that clusters waste containers on the basis of historical fill-rate patterns, assigns the most appropriate vehicles to each cluster and optimises collection schedules using transport data. At a conceptual level, the module combines route optimisation, demand forecasting and vehicle-assignment logic, building on Theoremus’ experience with Digital Waste Monitoring Platforms and integrating with broader waste-logistics workflows. The system is being deployed in Cascais and Montana, demonstrating its applicability across different urban contexts. The expected impact is fewer vehicle miles travelled, lower operational time and reduced costs, together with lower fuel consumption, emissions and traffic burden. More broadly, ROSE improves scalability, supports cleaner urban operations and creates value through more data-driven and sustainable waste-management practices.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION|S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2340,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01755,EU_PSTW_202609_01755,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""social_nonprofit""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Ricany,Transport,UNKNOWN,AI MOVE - AI mobility optimisation and vehicle efficiency for public transport,Municipality of Ricany; Local Government; Transport; Czechia,"The solution is implemented through enhanced CCTV systems deployed at intersections and transit zones, using computer vision to recognise high-occupancy vehicles and support dynamic prioritisation through existing infrastructure. The expected impact is improved traffic flow for public transport, more cost-effective use of existing infrastructure and broader coverage across transport providers.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Many public-transport-priority systems still rely on transponders installed on vehicles communicating with traffic lights, a set-up that typically benefits only municipal operators and excludes other high-occupancy services. This limits the effectiveness of traffic prioritisation in increasingly mixed mobility environments where demand-responsive transport and other non-municipal providers also play an important role. AI MOVE addresses this gap by seeking to make urban traffic management more inclusive and efficient across a broader set of public and shared transport vehicles. The stakeholders include transport authorities, public-transport operators, demand-responsive providers and city traffic managers, with the objective of reducing congestion and improving journey efficiency by extending priority mechanisms beyond traditional municipal fleets. The solution is implemented through enhanced CCTV systems deployed at intersections and transit zones, using computer vision to recognise high-occupancy vehicles and support dynamic prioritisation through existing infrastructure. At a conceptual level, the system combines video analytics, vehicle-recognition models and traffic-signal integration to identify eligible vehicles in real time and adapt signal behaviour accordingly. This approach avoids the limitations of hardware installed only on selected vehicles and creates a more flexible foundation for priority management. The expected impact is improved traffic flow for public transport, more cost-effective use of existing infrastructure and broader coverage across transport providers. More broadly, the project enhances urban mobility efficiency, supports scalable traffic management and creates value through reduced congestion and more equitable prioritisation.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2341,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01756,EU_PSTW_202609_01756,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Ajka,Transport,"Public-transport planning has often depended on surveys and interviews, which are valuable but slow, expensive and limited in their ability to capture real, constantly changing mobility patterns across a city. As a result, transport authorities and operators may lack the detailed and timely evidence needed to understand current demand, anticipate future shifts and optimise multimodal services effectively.",URBANITE - AI-driven insights to optimise public transport systems,Municipality of Ajka; Local Government; Transport; Hungary,"The solution is implemented through the integration of historical data, GPS traces, mobile-network information and passenger data, analysed using advanced modelling techniques to reconstruct and predict mobility demand across buses, trams, trains and bicycles.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Public-transport planning has often depended on surveys and interviews, which are valuable but slow, expensive and limited in their ability to capture real, constantly changing mobility patterns across a city. As a result, transport authorities and operators may lack the detailed and timely evidence needed to understand current demand, anticipate future shifts and optimise multimodal services effectively. URBANITE addresses this challenge by providing cities and transport organisations with a richer, more dynamic understanding of how people actually move. The stakeholders include municipalities, public transport authorities and public transport operators, with the objective of improving operational planning, anticipating future demand and supporting the creation of safer and more sustainable urban mobility systems. The solution is implemented through the integration of historical data, GPS traces, mobile-network information and passenger data, analysed using advanced modelling techniques to reconstruct and predict mobility demand across buses, trams, trains and bicycles. At a conceptual level, the platform combines data fusion, predictive analytics and movement modelling to produce actionable insights into both present and future transport needs. These outputs can then support network design, service planning and operational adjustment. The expected impact is more precise planning, better alignment between services and demand, and reduced reliance on costly traditional data collection. More broadly, URBANITE improves scalability in mobility analysis, enhances decision quality and creates value through more efficient, sustainable and user-responsive public transport systems.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2342,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01757,EU_PSTW_202609_01757,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Las Rozas de Madrid,Street lighting.,"Municipalities often struggle to identify these lighting-related risks systematically, and conventional inspection methods may not be frequent or detailed enough to support timely maintenance decisions. SAFELIGHT addresses this challenge by helping cities detect high-risk locations and improve illumination where it matters most for safety.",SAFELIGHT project: Analysis of visibility in roads and streets during the night to avoid accidents of vulnerable users,Municipality of Las Rozas de Madrid; Local Government; Street lighting.; Spain,"The solution is implemented through the Autonomous Urban Inspector for Lighting, which analyses road and street visibility using data collected in the urban environment and automatically identifies locations where conditions are unsafe. A second inspection is then carried out once lighting changes are implemented in order to validate the improvement in visibility.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Many urban locations, such as pedestrian crossings, cycle lanes and narrow pavements near traffic, become especially hazardous at night when poor visibility increases the risk of accidents for vulnerable road users. Municipalities often struggle to identify these lighting-related risks systematically, and conventional inspection methods may not be frequent or detailed enough to support timely maintenance decisions. SAFELIGHT addresses this challenge by helping cities detect high-risk locations and improve illumination where it matters most for safety. The stakeholders include host cities, road-maintenance teams, mobility planners and vulnerable users such as pedestrians and cyclists, with the objective of reducing night-time accident risk through better-targeted lighting interventions that can be integrated into ordinary maintenance operations. The solution is implemented through the Autonomous Urban Inspector for Lighting, which analyses road and street visibility using data collected in the urban environment and automatically identifies locations where conditions are unsafe. At a conceptual level, the system combines automated inspection, risk assessment and recommendation functions, after which Flash Lighting proposes the most suitable technical responses for each site. A second inspection is then carried out once lighting changes are implemented in order to validate the improvement in visibility. The expected impact is more precise identification of dangerous locations, more efficient maintenance planning and better evidence for safety interventions. More broadly, SAFELIGHT creates value by providing a scalable, operationally usable method for improving urban lighting and helping reduce accidents involving vulnerable road users.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2343,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01758,EU_PSTW_202609_01758,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Technical University of Cluj-Napoca,Pollution abatement.,"The operational problem is that public, residential and educational buildings often consume energy inefficiently, while building managers require practical tools to reduce consumption, costs and carbon intensity without compromising comfort.",DO IT SMARTer - An AI-based Management and Control System for Energy in Public Buildings,Technical University of Cluj-Napoca; Academic-Research; Pollution abatement.; Romania,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"DO IT SMARTer addresses the need to optimise energy flows in buildings through an AI-based management and control system, with demonstration sites in Romania and Norway. The operational problem is that public, residential and educational buildings often consume energy inefficiently, while building managers require practical tools to reduce consumption, costs and carbon intensity without compromising comfort. The project is an industrial and experimental research cooperation focused on cognitive energy-management techniques. Its implementation aims to design, develop and test energy-management and control algorithms, including monitoring and targeting, measurement and verification, and optimisation tools for smart buildings. The system is demonstrated in three pilot buildings and seeks to reach technology readiness levels 6–7. Conceptually, the solution combines building-energy monitoring, data analytics, AI-based control logic, optimisation of energy flows and integration with heating or power-to-heat technologies. The expected impact is improved energy efficiency, lower operational costs and reduced carbon intensity in public and residential buildings. The project’s stated objectives include reducing household energy consumption and costs by up to 20%, while also supporting bilateral cooperation between Romanian and Norwegian partners in energy management research.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2344,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01759,EU_PSTW_202609_01759,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Burgas,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.","The administrative problem is that citizens often need to navigate municipal decisions, registers, signals, service pages and responsible municipal companies, which can be fragmented and difficult to search.",BgGPT – a cutting-edge Bulgarian artificial intelligence integrates in Municipality of Burgas' services,"Municipality of Burgas; Local Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Bulgaria",BgGPT is intended to make these materials easier to understand and use.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Burgas Municipality introduced BgGPT to improve access to municipal information and services for citizens and businesses, becoming the first Bulgarian municipality to deploy the Bulgarian AI system in local administration. The administrative problem is that citizens often need to navigate municipal decisions, registers, signals, service pages and responsible municipal companies, which can be fragmented and difficult to search. BgGPT is intended to make these materials easier to understand and use. The implementation has two main functions. First, it synthesises decisions of the Burgas Municipal Council and presents them in summarised form, indexed by topics and sessions. Second, an AI chatbot is planned for the municipal website to direct users to the correct electronic service or provide direct information on signals, elections, municipal registers and responsible municipal entities. Conceptually, the system combines Bulgarian-language generative AI, document summarisation, topic indexing, conversational interaction and service-routing functions. The expected impact is improved transparency of local-government decisions, easier access to administrative services, better-informed residents and reduced pressure on staff handling repetitive information requests. By applying a national-language model in a municipal setting, the pilot also demonstrates how locally developed AI can be adapted to public-service delivery.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2345,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01760,EU_PSTW_202609_01760,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Burgas,General public services n.e.c.,"The municipal challenge is that modern cities generate information across transport, infrastructure, environment, services and public spaces, but this data can remain fragmented if it is not integrated into a common platform.",Smart Burgas - integrated platform for urban data,Municipality of Burgas; Local Government; General public services n.e.c.; Bulgaria,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Smart Burgas brings together data from smart devices and urban systems to improve the monitoring, transparency and management of the city environment. The municipal challenge is that modern cities generate information across transport, infrastructure, environment, services and public spaces, but this data can remain fragmented if it is not integrated into a common platform. Residents, visitors, municipal departments, city managers and service operators are the principal stakeholders. The platform is designed to help the municipality observe what is happening in the city, facilitate control, improve service organisation and make urban information more visible to the public. Its implementation consists of an integrated monitoring platform that collects and displays data from smart devices and systems operating in Burgas. Conceptually, the solution combines urban sensing, data aggregation, dashboard visualisation, public information tools and service-management support. By enabling residents and guests to observe city conditions more easily, it also contributes to transparency and civic awareness. The expected impact is improved quality of life, better coordination of municipal services, more responsive urban management and stronger evidence for decision-making. For the administration, integrated urban data can help identify problems, monitor performance and allocate resources more effectively. For citizens and visitors, the platform provides a clearer view of local conditions and supports more informed interaction with the city.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2346,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01761,EU_PSTW_202609_01761,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""social_nonprofit""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Sveta Nedelja,Street lighting.,"The municipal problem is that public lighting is a major operational asset, but conventional lighting systems offer limited remote control, monitoring or capacity to generate wider city data.",Smart Public Lighting in City of Sveta Nedelja (Croatia),City of Sveta Nedelja; Local Government; Street lighting.; Croatia,"The expected impact is reduced energy use, better maintenance insight, improved environmental monitoring and a foundation for broader Smart City services using existing infrastructure.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Sveta Nedelja smart public lighting pilot tested how existing public lighting infrastructure could become a platform for energy management, environmental monitoring and urban sensing. The municipal problem is that public lighting is a major operational asset, but conventional lighting systems offer limited remote control, monitoring or capacity to generate wider city data. The pilot aimed to improve energy efficiency, quality of life, public safety and reduction of light pollution. The implementation, developed with HT Group, connected 80 control and communication modules to existing lighting poles. The solution can operate across several network technologies, including 3G, 4G, 5G and NB-IoT, and is applicable to different types of public lighting. The smart poles enabled remote wireless monitoring and control of lamps, measurement of energy indicators, and additional sensing functions such as temperature, humidity, pressure, UV radiation, air quality, pedestrian passage and traffic noise. Conceptually, the system combines IoT connectivity, smart lighting control, environmental sensing, remote monitoring and city-data collection. The expected impact is reduced energy use, better maintenance insight, improved environmental monitoring and a foundation for broader Smart City services using existing infrastructure.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2347,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01762,EU_PSTW_202609_01762,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,European Regions Network for the Application of Communications Technology (ERNACT),General public services n.e.c.,"Public authorities increasingly recognise the strategic importance of advanced digital technologies, yet many regional and local administrations still lack the practical capacity, frameworks and confidence needed to translate this potential into better public policies and services. ENAIBLER addresses that institutional challenge by helping public-sector organisations across Europe strengthen their readiness to adopt advanced tools in an open, transparent and ethically grounded way.",Enaibler - AI for ethical policies and services across European regions,European Regions Network for the Application of Communications Technology (ERNACT); Consortium; General public services n.e.c.; European Union,"The solution is implemented through interregional cooperation, policy learning and capacity-building activities aimed at supporting organisations in the design and adoption of appropriate approaches.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Public authorities increasingly recognise the strategic importance of advanced digital technologies, yet many regional and local administrations still lack the practical capacity, frameworks and confidence needed to translate this potential into better public policies and services. ENAIBLER addresses that institutional challenge by helping public-sector organisations across Europe strengthen their readiness to adopt advanced tools in an open, transparent and ethically grounded way. The project brings together nine partners from six EU regions, including regional and local authorities, development agencies and a university, all of whom are involved in shaping or supporting policy instruments linked to digital transformation. Its objective is to empower the public sector to unlock the benefits of these technologies while improving the quality, ethics and transparency of the services and policies delivered to citizens. The solution is implemented through interregional cooperation, policy learning and capacity-building activities aimed at supporting organisations in the design and adoption of appropriate approaches. At a conceptual level, the project combines policy exchange, institutional support and shared experimentation to help partners strengthen governance, implementation capability and ethical practice across their territories. The expected impact is better-informed public administrations, stronger regional policy instruments and more responsible deployment of advanced digital services. More broadly, ENAIBLER creates value by improving public-sector capability, supporting trustworthy innovation and helping regions embed more transparent and citizen-oriented approaches to digital transformation.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2348,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01763,EU_PSTW_202609_01763,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Tampere,General public services n.e.c.,"AI4Citizens addresses this challenge by helping local and regional governments strengthen their ability to introduce such technologies safely and effectively, while ensuring that implementation serves the public interest.",AI4Citizens - Supporting ethical and effective AI governance in European cities and regions,City of Tampere; Consortium; General public services n.e.c.; European Union,"The solution is implemented through interregional exchange, co-creation and learning processes focused on good practices, innovative solutions and governance models relevant to public-sector deployment.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Cities and regions face growing pressure to modernise public services through advanced digital technologies, but adoption can be hindered by uncertainty around governance, ethics, risk management and the practical design of enabling policy mechanisms. AI4Citizens addresses this challenge by helping local and regional governments strengthen their ability to introduce such technologies safely and effectively, while ensuring that implementation serves the public interest. The stakeholders include local and regional policymakers, public-sector organisations and partner institutions engaged in interregional cooperation, with the objective of improving and creating policy mechanisms that support responsible adoption and, in turn, enhance citizens’ wellbeing and regional vitality. The solution is implemented through interregional exchange, co-creation and learning processes focused on good practices, innovative solutions and governance models relevant to public-sector deployment. At a conceptual level, the project draws on key elements of governance frameworks and implementation processes, enabling partners to compare approaches, identify gaps and develop policy improvements suited to their own institutional contexts. This process is designed not merely to share examples, but to drive concrete policy change and better governance arrangements. The expected impact is stronger governance capacity, more robust and ethical policy instruments and improved readiness for adoption at local and regional level. More broadly, AI4Citizens creates value by supporting safer innovation, more coherent public-sector transformation and a more trustworthy relationship between digital progress and citizen benefit.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2349,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01764,EU_PSTW_202609_01764,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Innovation Center of the Košice Region (ICKK),Economic affairs n.e.c,UNKNOWN,AI Sandbox in the Košice Self-Governing Region (KSK) - Xploro application,Innovation Center of the Košice Region (ICKK); Regional Government; Economic affairs n.e.c; Slovakia,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Košice Self-Governing Region’s AI Sandbox provides a controlled environment for testing artificial intelligence solutions within regional public-administration infrastructure, and Xploro was one of the selected applications in its first cycle. The broader operational problem is that public administrations often need to experiment with innovative technologies while managing legal, technical and organisational risks before wider adoption. The sandbox, initiated by the Innovation Center of the Košice Region, gives companies, universities and start-ups a structured route to test solutions for public services and internal processes. Participants undergo benefit analysis, technical evaluation, feasibility assessment and legal compliance review. Xploro specifically addresses tourism and visitor experience by providing an AI-supported guide that recommends places of interest and delivers information in audio or text form. Conceptually, the application combines location-aware guidance, recommendation functions, natural language content delivery and digital tourism support. The expected impact is twofold: for the regional administration, the sandbox strengthens capacity to evaluate AI tools responsibly before deployment; for tourism users, Xploro can simplify travel, enrich visits and improve access to information about sites. The inaugural sandbox attracted more than ten applications, with two selected for testing, indicating a selective and structured experimentation process. The project’s value lies in combining innovation testing with public-sector governance and practical service improvement.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2350,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01765,EU_PSTW_202609_01765,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Innovation Center of the Košice Region (ICKK),Economic affairs n.e.c,"The institutional challenge is to improve the digitalisation of regional services and internal processes while ensuring that new tools are assessed for benefits, technical feasibility, implementation readiness and legal compliance before broader deployment.",AI Sandbox in the Košice Self-Governing Region (KSK) - Recruapplication,Innovation Center of the Košice Region (ICKK); Regional Government; Economic affairs n.e.c; Slovakia,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Recru was selected for testing within the Košice Self-Governing Region’s AI Sandbox, a controlled and educational framework created to experiment with artificial intelligence solutions in public-administration settings. The institutional challenge is to improve the digitalisation of regional services and internal processes while ensuring that new tools are assessed for benefits, technical feasibility, implementation readiness and legal compliance before broader deployment. The main stakeholders include the Innovation Center of the Košice Region, the regional administration, participating companies, universities, start-ups, HR teams and applicants who may interact with recruitment processes. Recru focuses on human resources, offering a modern recruitment-management solution intended to simplify and accelerate the hiring process. The implementation is designed to support employers across recruitment tasks, enabling them to focus more directly on candidates. Conceptually, the tool may combine candidate management, workflow automation, recruitment analytics, communication support and AI-assisted processing of hiring information, although the specific technical modules are not detailed in the information provided. The expected impact is faster recruitment administration, more structured candidate handling and reduced repetitive workload for employers. Within the sandbox, the broader value is the ability to test such a system safely in a regional public-sector environment. Since more than ten solutions applied and two were selected, Recru’s inclusion indicates that it met the initial benefit, feasibility and compliance screening requirements.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S6_WORKFLOW_PROCESS_AUTOMATION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2351,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01766,EU_PSTW_202609_01766,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Estonian Health Board,Public health services.,"The operational challenge is that emergency medical systems must respond quickly under uncertain and changing demand, while ensuring that ambulances, staff, dispatch arrangements and service areas are organised effectively.",LifeSaver - enhancing medical services,Estonian Health Board; Central-Government; Public health services.; Estonia,"The project focuses on modernising life-saving assistance by using predictive analytics, real-time monitoring and transparent system insights to support faster and more effective emergency responses.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"LifeSaver aims to strengthen Estonia’s emergency medical services by improving resource allocation, service-area design and system-wide preparedness through data-driven and predictive tools. The operational challenge is that emergency medical systems must respond quickly under uncertain and changing demand, while ensuring that ambulances, staff, dispatch arrangements and service areas are organised effectively. The project focuses on modernising life-saving assistance by using predictive analytics, real-time monitoring and transparent system insights to support faster and more effective emergency responses. The implementation includes the LifeSaver Intelligence Platform and a comprehensive review of Estonia’s emergency medical system by international experts. The platform supports analysis of response patterns, demand forecasting, resource optimisation and identification of improvement projects. The LifeSaver programme identified ten potential innovation projects, from which the Estonian government selected five for future implementation. Conceptually, the initiative combines operational EMS data, predictive modelling, service-area analysis, monitoring dashboards and strategic planning for emergency care. The expected impact is better allocation of emergency resources, faster response capability, improved patient outcomes and stronger public-sector innovation capacity. Estonia was the first country to work with International SOS and Airbus Helicopters on the LifeSaver programme.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION|S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2352,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01767,EU_PSTW_202609_01767,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Pannon Business Network Association,General public services n.e.c.,"The regional problem is that SMEs across the Danube area face uneven digital maturity, labour shortages, cost pressures, industrial transformation and limited internal capacity to adopt advanced technologies.",BrAIn – BRinging Artificial INtelligence towards SMEs,Pannon Business Network Association; Consortium; General public services n.e.c.; Hungary,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"BrAIn supports SMEs in the Danube Region in understanding, adopting and benefiting from artificial intelligence and Industry 4.0 technologies as part of a transition towards more sustainable and human-centric Industry 5.0 models. The regional problem is that SMEs across the Danube area face uneven digital maturity, labour shortages, cost pressures, industrial transformation and limited internal capacity to adopt advanced technologies. The project therefore focuses on knowledge transfer, skills development and targeted transnational interventions in sectors such as manufacturing, agrofood, healthcare and sustainable energy. The implementation is structured as an Interreg Danube Region project that equips stakeholders and SMEs with AI-related knowledge, supports pilot solutions and builds on previous regional innovation results. Conceptually, the initiative combines capacity building, technology transfer, pilot experimentation, stakeholder networking and sector-specific AI adoption support. The expected impact is improved SME competitiveness, stronger readiness for Industry 5.0, more balanced regional development and better alignment between AI adoption and economic, environmental and social sustainability. By working across the Danube Region, BrAIn addresses the fact that AI capability gaps are not only technological, but also organisational, skills-based and territorial.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2354,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01768,EU_PSTW_202609_01768,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Považská Bystrica,Transport,"The main operational pressure was the combination of driver shortages and inefficient scheduling, which can increase costs, reduce service reliability and negatively affect passenger satisfaction. By supporting automated scenario analysis and schedule optimisation, it reduces dependence on manual planning and allows the municipality to respond more flexibly to capacity constraints.",iTranSys - Public Transport Optimisation Powered by AI,City of Považská Bystrica; Local Government; Transport; Slovakia,"The implementation uses an AI-powered optimisation tool that automates aspects of public transport planning, including timetable generation and route scheduling. These results suggest that the tool delivers both operational and environmental value by using resources more efficiently, improving service attractiveness and supporting more sustainable urban mobility.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Považská Bystrica adopted iTranSys to improve the planning and operation of public transport in a town of around 40,000 residents. The main operational pressure was the combination of driver shortages and inefficient scheduling, which can increase costs, reduce service reliability and negatively affect passenger satisfaction. The stakeholders include the City of Považská Bystrica, public transport operators, drivers, passengers, municipal planners and residents who rely on local mobility services. The objective is to make route and timetable planning more efficient while maintaining or improving service quality. The implementation uses an AI-powered optimisation tool that automates aspects of public transport planning, including timetable generation and route scheduling. Conceptually, the system can analyse passenger demand, operational constraints, available drivers, route structures and service-frequency requirements to generate more efficient transport plans. By supporting automated scenario analysis and schedule optimisation, it reduces dependence on manual planning and allows the municipality to respond more flexibly to capacity constraints. The reported impact includes significant annual financial savings, improved passenger satisfaction and reduced per capita CO₂ emissions. These results suggest that the tool delivers both operational and environmental value by using resources more efficiently, improving service attractiveness and supporting more sustainable urban mobility.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2355,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01769,EU_PSTW_202609_01769,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Las Rozas Innova,Transport,"The operational challenge is that freight and last-mile logistics contribute significantly to urban congestion, emissions and fuel consumption, while cities and operators often lack a shared evidence base for managing delivery flows.",LUCID – Data intelligence for cities and urban logistics,Las Rozas Innova; Consortium; Transport; Spain,"LUCID develops a data-driven platform to improve urban logistics by optimising routes, reducing congestion and improving the use of loading and unloading spaces. Its implementation uses advanced analytics and machine-learning methods to support route planning, operational decision-making and more efficient allocation of urban logistics infrastructure.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"LUCID develops a data-driven platform to improve urban logistics by optimising routes, reducing congestion and improving the use of loading and unloading spaces. The operational challenge is that freight and last-mile logistics contribute significantly to urban congestion, emissions and fuel consumption, while cities and operators often lack a shared evidence base for managing delivery flows. The project integrates public city data and private logistics-operator data to provide real-time insights for both city planners and logistics actors. Its implementation uses advanced analytics and machine-learning methods to support route planning, operational decision-making and more efficient allocation of urban logistics infrastructure. Pilot testing is foreseen in Madrid, Las Rozas and Prague, with logistics operators involved to validate the platform in real-world settings. Conceptually, LUCID combines data integration, predictive analytics, route optimisation, loading-space intelligence and city-dashboard functionality. The expected impact is lower delivery costs, reduced environmental footprint, less congestion and better planning capacity for local authorities. By bringing together public and private data, the project supports a more coordinated model of urban logistics management in rapidly growing cities.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2356,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01770,EU_PSTW_202609_01770,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Graz Linien,Public order and safety n.e.c.,"SmartDriveAI was developed to modernise accident reporting in public transport by replacing slow, paper-based processes with real-time digital reporting and predictive safety insights. The operational problem is that conventional accident documentation can delay response, fragment evidence and limit authorities’ ability to identify recurring safety patterns.",SmartDriveAI - digitising accident reporting,Graz Linien; Local Government; Public order and safety n.e.c.; Austria,The implementation integrates external data such as weather conditions and uses analytics to generate proactive safety alerts and insights for city authorities and operators. The initiative also demonstrates how public transport operators can use structured operational data to move from reactive accident handling to evidence-based safety management.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"SmartDriveAI was developed to modernise accident reporting in public transport by replacing slow, paper-based processes with real-time digital reporting and predictive safety insights. The operational problem is that conventional accident documentation can delay response, fragment evidence and limit authorities’ ability to identify recurring safety patterns. In Graz, the project involved Graz Linien and Mapular, with drivers equipped to submit GPS-tagged reports, photographs and incident details through a multilingual digital interface. The implementation integrates external data such as weather conditions and uses analytics to generate proactive safety alerts and insights for city authorities and operators. Conceptually, the platform combines mobile accident reporting, geodata technologies, real-time data capture, predictive modelling and safety-dashboard functionality. Pilot results reported by Mapular indicate a 90% reduction in reporting time, 100% data integrity during the pilot phase and identification of three key accident patterns. The expected impact is faster reporting, better data quality, improved resource allocation, stronger road-safety analysis and more proactive prevention of recurring incidents. The initiative also demonstrates how public transport operators can use structured operational data to move from reactive accident handling to evidence-based safety management.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2357,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01771,EU_PSTW_202609_01771,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Parabol - Mobility for All,Transport,UNKNOWN,SAFELY - Road Safety Analytics System,Parabol - Mobility for All; Local Government; Transport; Bosnia and Herzegovina,"The solution is implemented as a data-driven analytics platform that integrates accident records with traffic patterns, road geometry, environmental data and citizen feedback. At a conceptual level, the system uses clustering techniques such as DBSCAN and ST-DBSCAN, together with geospatial visualisation, to reveal risk hotspots and hidden hazards that are not obvious in raw crash statistics.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Municipalities often rely on recorded accidents alone to understand road-safety problems, yet this reactive approach can miss hidden risks and delay intervention until serious incidents have already occurred. SAFELY was developed to help local authorities move from monitoring past crashes to proactively identifying hazardous conditions before further harm occurs. The platform addresses the need for a more complete understanding of road safety by combining multiple data sources rather than relying only on official accident counts. The stakeholders include municipalities, traffic planners, road-safety teams and local communities, with the objective of prioritising interventions more effectively, allocate resources with greater precision and strengthen evidence-based transport planning. The solution is implemented as a data-driven analytics platform that integrates accident records with traffic patterns, road geometry, environmental data and citizen feedback. At a conceptual level, the system uses clustering techniques such as DBSCAN and ST-DBSCAN, together with geospatial visualisation, to reveal risk hotspots and hidden hazards that are not obvious in raw crash statistics. These insights are presented through actionable hazard maps and planning outputs that fit municipal workflows and align with standards such as CADaS, GDPR and the European Interoperability Framework. The impact has been measurable: in Şanlıurfa, interventions supported by SAFELY were associated with an over 80% reduction in reported accidents and a 90% drop in injuries between 2014–2015 and 2020–2021. More broadly, the platform offers a cost-efficient, scalable and credible model for saving lives through proactive road-safety management.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2358,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01772,EU_PSTW_202609_01772,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,University of Trento,General public services n.e.c.,"ELIAS addresses a strategic challenge facing Europe: how to accelerate advanced technological research and application in ways that contribute directly to sustainability goals while also respecting ethical and societal considerations. Climate change, the energy crisis and related environmental pressures require new forms of collaboration between academia and industry, yet the pathway from research excellence to deployable solutions is often fragmented.",ELIAS - European Lighthouse of AI for Sustainability,University of Trento; Consortium; General public services n.e.c.; Italy,"This use case therefore brings together researchers, emerging talent, industrial partners and innovation stakeholders with the objective of building a strong European ecosystem for sustainability-oriented technological development. The solution is implemented through a programme that combines fundamental academic research, industrial application work, talent development and partnership-building across Europe.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"ELIAS addresses a strategic challenge facing Europe: how to accelerate advanced technological research and application in ways that contribute directly to sustainability goals while also respecting ethical and societal considerations. Climate change, the energy crisis and related environmental pressures require new forms of collaboration between academia and industry, yet the pathway from research excellence to deployable solutions is often fragmented. This use case therefore brings together researchers, emerging talent, industrial partners and innovation stakeholders with the objective of building a strong European ecosystem for sustainability-oriented technological development. The project seeks not only to advance research, but also to ensure that resulting solutions generate practical value for society and the environment. The solution is implemented through a programme that combines fundamental academic research, industrial application work, talent development and partnership-building across Europe. At a conceptual level, ELIAS creates a collaborative structure in which advanced methods are developed, tested and translated into seven pilot use cases over the project’s duration, while also supporting the broader research community and strengthening links between scientific and commercial actors. This model is intended to foster both technical progress and responsible innovation. The expected impact is stronger European leadership in sustainability-focused research, improved pathways from research to application and a more capable innovation ecosystem. More broadly, ELIAS creates value by aligning scientific excellence, industrial relevance and societal benefit around pressing environmental challenges.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2359,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01773,EU_PSTW_202609_01773,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Wrocław University of Science and Technology,General services.,"The problem it addresses is the limited availability of high-quality, open-access language models designed around the linguistic, cultural and administrative needs of Polish users.",PLLuM - Polish Large Language Model,Wrocław University of Science and Technology; Academic-Research; General services.; Poland,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"PLLuM, the Polish Large Language Universal Model, was launched to strengthen Poland’s capacity to develop open and responsible language technologies primarily trained on Polish text. The problem it addresses is the limited availability of high-quality, open-access language models designed around the linguistic, cultural and administrative needs of Polish users. The main stakeholders include Wrocław University of Science and Technology, the wider consortium of six Polish institutions, researchers specialising in artificial intelligence and linguistics, public-service providers, developers and citizens, including those at risk of digital exclusion. The project aims to create the first open Polish large language model and a related smart assistant that can support access to public services electronically and in person. The implementation involves large-scale Polish-language model training, dataset preparation, linguistic expertise, evaluation and the development of assistant functionalities aligned with ethical and responsible AI principles. These include attention to data representativeness, transparency and fairness. Conceptually, PLLuM can support natural-language interaction, information retrieval, text generation and user guidance in public-service contexts. Its expected impact is broader access to digital services, improved quality of Polish-language AI applications and reduced dependence on models developed primarily for other languages. By targeting digitally excluded users, the smart assistant also has potential to improve administrative accessibility and inclusion.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2360,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01774,EU_PSTW_202609_01774,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Slovenian Research and Innovation Agency,"R&D recreation, culture and religion.",UNKNOWN,AID HCH – Advancing Humanities and Cultural Heritage with Artificial Intelligence,"Slovenian Research and Innovation Agency; Central-Government; R&D recreation, culture and religion.; Slovenia","AID HCH is a Slovenian research initiative designed to advance the use of artificial intelligence in humanities research and cultural-heritage preservation. Its implementation combines AI tools, data-space development, digitised cultural and historical sources, predictive conservation methods, heritage storytelling and interdisciplinary frameworks for legal and ethical use.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"AID HCH is a Slovenian research initiative designed to advance the use of artificial intelligence in humanities research and cultural-heritage preservation. The strategic problem is that AI has been widely applied in natural sciences, engineering and medicine, but less systematically in humanities and heritage contexts, where digitised sources, archives, objects and conservation data require specialised methods and ethical safeguards. The project aims to establish Slovenia’s first comprehensive data space for humanities and heritage, strengthening cooperation with infrastructures such as E-RIHS, CLARIN and DARIAH. Its implementation combines AI tools, data-space development, digitised cultural and historical sources, predictive conservation methods, heritage storytelling and interdisciplinary frameworks for legal and ethical use. Conceptually, the project creates shared infrastructure and methodologies for analysing cultural-heritage data, supporting research collaboration and improving protection of heritage assets. The expected impact is stronger digital transformation in humanities research, better access to structured heritage data, improved conservation planning and greater international interoperability. The project also has a capacity-building function by connecting universities, libraries, heritage institutions and technical partners around common data and AI standards.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2361,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01775,EU_PSTW_202609_01775,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,National Council on Prices and Reimbursement of Medicinal Products,"Medical products, appliances and equipment.","The policy challenge is that reimbursement decisions require evidence on clinical effectiveness, patient outcomes and budget impact, yet traditional evaluation processes may not fully exploit large-scale health data.",Enhancing healthcare policy evaluation through AI-driven analytics platform for the National Public Administration,"National Council on Prices and Reimbursement of Medicinal Products; Central-Government; Medical products, appliances and equipment.; Bulgaria",The implementation uses artificial intelligence and big-data analytics to assess the real-world effectiveness of medicines after they enter clinical use.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Bulgaria’s National Council on Prices and Reimbursement of Medicinal Products initiated this project to improve how public authorities evaluate the real-world value of innovative and high-cost medicines. The policy challenge is that reimbursement decisions require evidence on clinical effectiveness, patient outcomes and budget impact, yet traditional evaluation processes may not fully exploit large-scale health data. Stakeholders include the National Council, healthcare policymakers, patients, clinicians, pharmaceutical companies, reimbursement authorities and public-finance actors responsible for sustainable spending. The objective is to support more informed decisions on whether and how medicines should be reimbursed, while balancing patient access with cost-effectiveness. The implementation uses artificial intelligence and big-data analytics to assess the real-world effectiveness of medicines after they enter clinical use. Conceptually, the platform can combine reimbursement records, treatment data, patient outcomes, medicine-cost information and analytical models to compare expected and observed performance. This enables decision-makers to identify which therapies deliver measurable value in practice and where public expenditure may need adjustment. The expected impact is stronger evidence for reimbursement policy, more efficient allocation of public funds, improved accountability for high-cost therapies and better patient care. By linking analytics with reimbursement governance, the project supports a more data-driven and outcome-oriented healthcare administration.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2362,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01776,EU_PSTW_202609_01776,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,District of Recklinghausen,Protection of biodiversity and landscape.,UNKNOWN,Automatic Detection of Impervious Surfaces,District of Recklinghausen; Local Government; Protection of biodiversity and landscape.; Germany,"The implementation uses artificial intelligence, deep learning and computer vision to derive maps of impervious surfaces from aerial images and aggregate the results to different area divisions.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Recklinghausen developed this software to give municipalities a more precise basis for monitoring sealed surfaces and planning climate-adaptation measures. The environmental problem is that impervious surfaces prevent rainwater infiltration and contribute to urban heat accumulation, increasing risks related to drainage, flooding and heat islands. In Germany, around 45% of settlement and transport areas are sealed, according to the Federal Environmental Agency statistics cited in the source material. Stakeholders include the District of Recklinghausen, participating cities, the Westphalian University of Applied Sciences, land registry and geo-information staff, environment and climate-protection departments and local residents. The implementation uses artificial intelligence, deep learning and computer vision to derive maps of impervious surfaces from aerial images and aggregate the results to different area divisions. The project was carried out over 12 months with funding of just under €100,000 for a research associate at the university’s Department of Electrical Engineering and Applied Natural Sciences. Regular meetings between the university and participating cities defined objectives and monitored progress. The expected impact is more detailed assessment of urban land conditions, improved evidence for drainage control, stronger planning of climate-effective measures and better evaluation of municipal land-management interventions. By automating surface classification, the tool helps local authorities move towards more systematic, data-based environmental and climate planning.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2364,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01777,EU_PSTW_202609_01777,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Politecnico di Milano,"Mining, manufacturing and construction.","European manufacturers are under growing pressure to reduce waste, improve resilience and make production systems more sustainable across the full product lifecycle, yet industrial data and decision-making often remain fragmented across suppliers, plants and recovery processes. Circular TwAIn addresses this challenge by helping manufacturing organisations move from linear production models towards more circular and interoperable operations.",Circular TwAIn- AI Platform for Integrated Sustainable and Circular Manufacturing,"Politecnico di Milano; Consortium; Mining, manufacturing and construction.; Italy","The use case involves industrial partners in both discrete manufacturing and process industries, with the objective of improving sustainability, flexibility and resource efficiency while strengthening Europe’s position in advanced manufacturing. The solution is implemented through an integrated platform built around interoperable digital twins, trusted data-sharing mechanisms and advanced analytical models applied across the product lifecycle.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"European manufacturers are under growing pressure to reduce waste, improve resilience and make production systems more sustainable across the full product lifecycle, yet industrial data and decision-making often remain fragmented across suppliers, plants and recovery processes. Circular TwAIn addresses this challenge by helping manufacturing organisations move from linear production models towards more circular and interoperable operations. The use case involves industrial partners in both discrete manufacturing and process industries, with the objective of improving sustainability, flexibility and resource efficiency while strengthening Europe’s position in advanced manufacturing. Its approach spans three industrial pilots, each tackling a different circularity challenge: batteries, waste electrical and electronic equipment, and petrochemical production. The solution is implemented through an integrated platform built around interoperable digital twins, trusted data-sharing mechanisms and advanced analytical models applied across the product lifecycle. At a conceptual level, the platform connects operational, recovery and lifecycle data so that sustainability-related decisions can be supported from design through use, reuse and end-of-life processing. In the battery pilot, it supports new circular value-chain nodes; in the WEEE pilot, it improves recovery and reuse pathways; and in the PETRO pilot, it connects digital intelligence with established industrial processes. The expected impact is more efficient circular manufacturing, better reuse and recycling outcomes, stronger resilience and improved industrial coordination. More broadly, the project enhances scalability, supports trustworthy digital transformation and creates value through more sustainable and competitive production systems.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2365,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01778,EU_PSTW_202609_01778,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,REPLY DEUTSCHLAND SE,"Mining, manufacturing and construction.","Fluently addresses this challenge by making human-robot collaboration more intuitive, inclusive and productive in real manufacturing settings.",Fluently - the essence of human-robot interaction,"REPLY DEUTSCHLAND SE; Consortium; Mining, manufacturing and construction.; Germany","The use case involves shop-floor operators, production managers and industrial technology providers, with the objective of enabling workers of different skill levels to interact more naturally with robotic systems while improving operational performance and worker wellbeing. The solution is implemented through an interface that allows robots to interpret speech and gestures, supported by perception, language-processing and interaction models that translate human signals into machine actions.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Modern manufacturing increasingly depends on effective collaboration between people and machines, yet many industrial systems still require specialised knowledge to operate and can place unnecessary cognitive or physical strain on workers. Fluently addresses this challenge by making human-robot collaboration more intuitive, inclusive and productive in real manufacturing settings. The use case involves shop-floor operators, production managers and industrial technology providers, with the objective of enabling workers of different skill levels to interact more naturally with robotic systems while improving operational performance and worker wellbeing. Rather than requiring users to adapt to rigid machine interfaces, the project seeks to make robots more responsive to human communication and easier to integrate into dynamic factory environments. The solution is implemented through an interface that allows robots to interpret speech and gestures, supported by perception, language-processing and interaction models that translate human signals into machine actions. At a conceptual level, the project combines multimodal interaction, collaborative robotics and a dedicated Robo-Gym training environment where human-robot interaction can be developed and tested before deployment. These capabilities are then validated through real-world industrial pilots. The expected impact is smoother human-robot cooperation, lower barriers to use, improved production efficiency and better worker experience. More broadly, Fluently supports safer and more adaptive manufacturing, increases the practical usability of robotic systems and creates value by aligning automation more closely with human capabilities and needs.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2366,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01779,EU_PSTW_202609_01779,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Madrid,Transport,"In Madrid, last-mile logistics still depends heavily on a conventional, road-based model in which goods move from DHL facilities into the metropolitan centre mainly by truck and van, often through fragmented arrangements involving external operators. This siloed approach contributes to slower deliveries, road congestion and unnecessary environmental impact, especially in dense urban areas where freight and passenger mobility compete for limited space.",DELPHI - Coordinated mobility for passengers and freight - Madrid use case,City of Madrid; Local Government; Transport; Spain,"The Madrid DELPHI use case addresses this problem by integrating passenger and freight transport more strategically and creating a more sustainable logistics model for urban deliveries. The solution is implemented through a multimodal logistics system in which parcels are pre-sorted into roller cages, transferred to dedicated metro depots and moved through the METRO network before reaching microhubs located in selected stations.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"In Madrid, last-mile logistics still depends heavily on a conventional, road-based model in which goods move from DHL facilities into the metropolitan centre mainly by truck and van, often through fragmented arrangements involving external operators. This siloed approach contributes to slower deliveries, road congestion and unnecessary environmental impact, especially in dense urban areas where freight and passenger mobility compete for limited space. The Madrid DELPHI use case addresses this problem by integrating passenger and freight transport more strategically and creating a more sustainable logistics model for urban deliveries. The stakeholders include DHL, metro operators, microhub operators, final-mile couriers and city mobility actors, with the objective of improving delivery efficiency while reducing pressure on the road network. The solution is implemented through a multimodal logistics system in which parcels are pre-sorted into roller cages, transferred to dedicated metro depots and moved through the METRO network before reaching microhubs located in selected stations. From there, KOIKI messengers complete the final delivery on foot or by cargo bike, depending on timing requirements and hub location. At a conceptual level, the system combines data-sharing, multimodal coordination and operational planning within a federated mobility framework. The expected impact is quicker deliveries, lower emissions, reduced congestion and better use of urban transport infrastructure. More broadly, the pilot creates a financially and environmentally more sustainable logistics model for Madrid.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2367,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01780,EU_PSTW_202609_01780,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Athens,Transport,"Freight movement in the Athens metropolitan area is heavily affected by congestion on the Attiki Odos ring road, yet road and metro systems currently operate as separate domains with limited real-time coordination. This disconnect reduces flexibility for logistics providers, especially when delivery services need to respond quickly to traffic incidents or peak-period slowdowns by shifting to faster transport options.",DELPHI - Coordinated mobility for passengers and freight - Attika Use Case,City of Athens; Local Government; Transport; Greece,The Attika DELPHI use case addresses this lack of integration by creating a dynamic multimodal alternative for urban freight movement. The solution is implemented through a coordinated mobility framework that links traffic and transport information from the road and metro systems and supports route suggestions based on current conditions.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Freight movement in the Athens metropolitan area is heavily affected by congestion on the Attiki Odos ring road, yet road and metro systems currently operate as separate domains with limited real-time coordination. This disconnect reduces flexibility for logistics providers, especially when delivery services need to respond quickly to traffic incidents or peak-period slowdowns by shifting to faster transport options. The Attika DELPHI use case addresses this lack of integration by creating a dynamic multimodal alternative for urban freight movement. The stakeholders include Attikes Diadromes, which operates and maintains the ring road, STASY, which manages Athens’ fixed-track public transport network, and logistics operators seeking more resilient delivery routes. The objective is to enable on-demand rerouting from roadway transport to metro-based logistics when traffic conditions make this advantageous. The solution is implemented through a coordinated mobility framework that links traffic and transport information from the road and metro systems and supports route suggestions based on current conditions. At a conceptual level, the pilot uses real-time data exchange, multimodal routing logic and operational coordination to redirect deliveries to designated metro stations, where goods can be loaded onto specialised metro carriages for onward movement. This model is designed for flexible, on-demand use rather than fixed metro freight schedules. The expected impact is faster and more reliable deliveries, better adaptation to congestion and improved use of existing transport assets. More broadly, the pilot strengthens multimodal resilience and supports more efficient urban freight operations.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2368,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01781,EU_PSTW_202609_01781,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Cluj-Napoca,Transport,"Although the city already offers real-time public-transport information through a user-friendly mobile application, a key challenge remains: limited data sharing and coordination between the various public and private operators serving the city and surrounding communes.",DELPHI - Coordinated mobility for passengers and freight - Cluj-Napoca use case,City of Cluj-Napoca; Local Government; Transport; Romania,"The Cluj-Napoca DELPHI use case addresses this fragmentation by aiming to create a more integrated and data-driven mobility ecosystem. The solution is implemented through the development of a centralised IT platform capable of aggregating and standardising data from buses, trams, bike-sharing systems and parking facilities in line with open-data standards.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Cluj-Napoca has experienced rapid population growth and increasing pressure on its transport system, making it harder for the city to maintain efficient, sustainable and coordinated mobility services. Although the city already offers real-time public-transport information through a user-friendly mobile application, a key challenge remains: limited data sharing and coordination between the various public and private operators serving the city and surrounding communes. The Cluj-Napoca DELPHI use case addresses this fragmentation by aiming to create a more integrated and data-driven mobility ecosystem. The stakeholders include the Public Transport Company, municipal authorities, surrounding localities and other mobility-service providers, with the objective of improving planning, interoperability and user responsiveness across the transport network. The solution is implemented through the development of a centralised IT platform capable of aggregating and standardising data from buses, trams, bike-sharing systems and parking facilities in line with open-data standards. At a conceptual level, this platform combines data integration, monitoring and user feedback to support better coordination between services and a more coherent picture of mobility demand and supply. These insights can then inform network adjustments, service improvement and long-term planning. The expected impact is more efficient public transport, improved accessibility and stronger coordination between modes and operators. More broadly, the pilot supports sustainable urban mobility, better use of infrastructure and a more responsive transport system for a growing city.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2369,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01782,EU_PSTW_202609_01782,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Mykonos,Transport,UNKNOWN,DELPHI - Coordinated mobility for passengers and freight - Mykonos use case,City of Mykonos; Local Government; Transport; Greece,"The solution is implemented through an interdisciplinary methodology that combines classical spatial analysis with behavioural studies and digitally supported citizen participation. At a conceptual level, the approach compares the intended design of a place with observed and reported patterns of actual use, then generates practical planning recommendations based on these discrepancies.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Public spaces are often designed according to planning assumptions that do not fully reflect how people actually move, stop, interact and spend time in the urban environment. As a result, spaces intended to support walking, socialising and everyday wellbeing may underperform because their design does not align with real behaviour or user needs. The Good Public Space for Walking project addresses this gap by helping municipalities assess and improve public spaces through a more human-centred understanding of how they are used in practice. The stakeholders include municipal planners, urban designers and residents, with the objective of making public spaces more functional, welcoming and supportive of healthy daily activities such as walking, sitting and social interaction. The solution is implemented through an interdisciplinary methodology that combines classical spatial analysis with behavioural studies and digitally supported citizen participation. At a conceptual level, the approach compares the intended design of a place with observed and reported patterns of actual use, then generates practical planning recommendations based on these discrepancies. The integration of AI-supported participation tools helps ensure that community voices are captured more systematically and incorporated into the planning process. The expected impact is better-targeted urban interventions, improved usability of public space and stronger alignment between planning decisions and lived experience. More broadly, the project supports healthier, more responsive urban environments and creates value by embedding citizen-centred evidence more directly into local design and policy choices.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2370,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01783,EU_PSTW_202609_01783,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Politecnico of Milano,"Mining, manufacturing and construction.","PERKS, formally “Eliciting and Exploiting Procedural Knowledge in Industry 5.0”, addresses the difficulty industrial organisations face in capturing, managing and reusing procedural knowledge. The operational problem is that know-how related to processes, safety procedures, system configuration and energy-management practices is often tacit, poorly digitalised or difficult for workers to access at the moment of need.",PERKS - AI for industrial operational efficiency,"Politecnico of Milano; Consortium; Mining, manufacturing and construction.; Italy",UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"PERKS, formally “Eliciting and Exploiting Procedural Knowledge in Industry 5.0”, addresses the difficulty industrial organisations face in capturing, managing and reusing procedural knowledge. The operational problem is that know-how related to processes, safety procedures, system configuration and energy-management practices is often tacit, poorly digitalised or difficult for workers to access at the moment of need. The project therefore aims to improve industrial efficiency by developing modular, AI-based tools and methodologies that support the full lifecycle of procedural knowledge, from elicitation and management to access and exploitation. Its implementation combines symbolic and subsymbolic AI, data technologies, knowledge-management methods and worker-centred digital tools. The solutions are tested in three industrial scenarios: a white-goods production plant, computer numerical control machines and a microgrid testbed, each presenting different procedural-knowledge requirements. The expected impact is improved productivity, fewer operational errors, better knowledge transfer, stronger worker safety and more efficient energy-related practices. With an overall budget of €3.55 million, the project contributes to Industry 5.0 by prioritising human-centric, flexible and accepted tools for industrial workers rather than automation alone.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2371,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01784,EU_PSTW_202609_01784,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Bronia.AI,General public services n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,Bronia.AI - acoustic technology,Bronia.AI; Private sector; General public services n.e.c.; Bulgaria,"Stakeholders include Bronia.AI, potential industrial clients, technology partners, sectors using acoustic systems and end users benefiting from improved sound processing. The implementation is centred on AI-powered acoustic technologies, including sound optimisation, noise reduction and advanced signal processing.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Bronia.AI is a Bulgarian start-up established in 2024 to develop intelligent acoustic signal-processing technology for complex sound-related applications. The business and technical problem addressed is that many sectors require more advanced tools for managing audio signals, reducing noise, optimising sound quality and extracting usable information from acoustic environments. Stakeholders include Bronia.AI, potential industrial clients, technology partners, sectors using acoustic systems and end users benefiting from improved sound processing. The company’s objective is to build a cutting-edge platform that can transform how sound signals are processed and applied across different business domains. The implementation is centred on AI-powered acoustic technologies, including sound optimisation, noise reduction and advanced signal processing. Conceptually, the platform may combine machine-learning models for audio enhancement, signal separation, pattern recognition, acoustic modelling and real-time processing. Its value lies in addressing situations where conventional audio-processing methods may be insufficient, particularly in complex, noisy or data-rich environments. The expected impact is improved sound quality, more reliable acoustic analysis, new product possibilities and efficiency gains for organisations using audio technologies. As a young company, Bronia.AI’s broader market impact will depend on product maturity, sector adoption and demonstrable performance in real-world settings. Its positioning suggests a focus on innovation in a specialised technological field with potential cross-sector applications.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S3_DOCUMENT_INTELLIGENCE,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2372,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01785,EU_PSTW_202609_01785,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Port Authority of Ravenna,Transport,UNKNOWN,5G connection for the Ravenna Port for enhahnced digital connectivity,Port Authority of Ravenna; Central-Government; Transport; Italy,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Port of Ravenna’s private 5G network is part of a wider digitalisation strategy to make port operations more competitive, secure and sustainable. The operational problem is that ports require low-latency, reliable and secure connectivity to coordinate cargo traffic, intermodal logistics, monitoring systems and future automation across large and complex areas. The planned Mobile Private Network 5G will cover the entire port area and a 15 km canal corridor linking the port to the city, enabling ultra-fast communications and stronger data security. The implementation supports smart-port applications such as intelligent monitoring through cameras and devices, autonomous vehicles, anti-collision systems, drone-based cargo surveillance, augmented reality for maintenance, environmental monitoring and artificial intelligence for managing logistics flows. It is also connected to broader digital tools, including the Port Community System, Rail Management Platform, virtual gates and a future Digital Twin for simulation and planning. The expected impact is optimised cargo traffic, shorter ship waiting times, improved coordination among port stakeholders and a stronger basis for advanced operational planning. In May 2025, the Port Authority awarded the deployment contract to Fastweb and Vodafone, in partnership with Lepida.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2373,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01786,EU_PSTW_202609_01786,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,RTVE – Radiotelevisión Española (Spanish Radio and Television Corporation),Broadcasting and publishing services.,"The impact includes wider electoral coverage, faster production and improved information accessibility, especially in smaller localities that might otherwise receive limited attention.",AI-tool to cover local elections,RTVE – Radiotelevisión Española (Spanish Radio and Television Corporation); Central-Government; Broadcasting and publishing services.; Spain,"RTVE developed this use case to solve a persistent media-coverage challenge: local elections in small municipalities generate official results that are highly relevant to residents, yet producing reliable and accessible reporting for every location can be difficult, especially where traditional newsroom resources are limited. The solution is implemented through a tool that automatically drafts written and audio election content using official election data as its sole input source.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"RTVE developed this use case to solve a persistent media-coverage challenge: local elections in small municipalities generate official results that are highly relevant to residents, yet producing reliable and accessible reporting for every location can be difficult, especially where traditional newsroom resources are limited. The project aimed to ensure that no community would be left without election information, regardless of location, while also improving accessibility for people with visual impairments. The stakeholders include RTVE journalists, audiences in small municipalities and citizens requiring accessible electoral information, with the objective of expanding coverage, improving speed and maintaining trust by basing outputs strictly on official results. The solution is implemented through a tool that automatically drafts written and audio election content using official election data as its sole input source. At a conceptual level, the system combines automated text generation, structured result processing and editorial supervision so that informative, coherent and useful outputs can be produced quickly for municipalities with fewer than 1,000 inhabitants, while remaining extensible to broader electoral coverage. RTVE professionals train and supervise the system to maintain quality and editorial standards. The impact includes wider electoral coverage, faster production and improved information accessibility, especially in smaller localities that might otherwise receive limited attention. More broadly, the tool creates value by supporting public-service media, strengthening democratic information access and reducing the resource barrier to comprehensive local-election reporting.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2374,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01787,EU_PSTW_202609_01787,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,LEADER Region Weinviertel Ost,General public services n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,Smart Assistant - Development of a chatbot for the municipalities of the LEADER Region Weinviertel Ost,LEADER Region Weinviertel Ost; Regional Government; General public services n.e.c.; Austria,Municipalities in the LEADER Region Weinviertel Ost needed a practical way to automate routine online interactions and improve digital service delivery without each authority having to build separate solutions from scratch. The solution is implemented through a chatbot integrated into municipal websites and trained specifically for the needs of Weinviertel Ost.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Municipalities in the LEADER Region Weinviertel Ost needed a practical way to automate routine online interactions and improve digital service delivery without each authority having to build separate solutions from scratch. Smaller municipalities often face capacity constraints in maintaining responsive online communication, even though residents increasingly expect fast answers on common topics such as building permits and local administrative procedures. The project therefore set out to provide a shared digital assistant for 58 municipalities, with the objective of streamlining routine enquiries, improving access to information and supporting more efficient day-to-day online operations. The stakeholders include municipal administrations, residents and the regional bodies coordinating the initiative, with the aim of creating a reusable and locally relevant support tool across the whole region. The solution is implemented through a chatbot integrated into municipal websites and trained specifically for the needs of Weinviertel Ost. At a conceptual level, the system uses commercially available chatbot software, but its local effectiveness depends on targeted training using information drawn from publicly accessible databases through central interfaces and from a dedicated frequently asked questions database covering municipal competencies. This creates a shared but customisable knowledge base aligned with local administrative needs. The expected impact is faster handling of standard requests, lower administrative effort for routine digital communication and improved service consistency across municipalities. More broadly, the project creates value by giving smaller local authorities a scalable and regionally coordinated way to modernise citizen interaction.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2375,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01788,EU_PSTW_202609_01788,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Milan,General public services n.e.c.,"Since taking responsibility for routine road maintenance in July 2023, MM needed a scalable way to monitor a dense and heavily used urban network without relying solely on slower, labour-intensive manual inspections.",3DAI City Mobile Application - AI for Road Maintenance,City of Milan; Local Government; General public services n.e.c.; Italy,"The solution is implemented in collaboration with Univrses AB through a smartphone-based system mounted on MM vehicles’ windscreens. According to MM, the technology entered a nine-month trial in November 2024 and uses moving-vehicle image capture to detect cracks, potholes, other pavement defects and anomalies in vertical road signs, with outputs integrated into MM’s Web-GIS portal.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"MM Spa introduced the 3DAI City mobile application to address a practical infrastructure-management problem: maintaining Milan’s roads more efficiently by identifying defects earlier and improving the quality of maintenance planning. Since taking responsibility for routine road maintenance in July 2023, MM needed a scalable way to monitor a dense and heavily used urban network without relying solely on slower, labour-intensive manual inspections. The stakeholders include MM’s maintenance teams, city infrastructure managers and Milan’s road users, with the objective of reducing emergency repairs, improving visibility of road-surface conditions and making intervention planning more timely and cost-effective. The solution is implemented in collaboration with Univrses AB through a smartphone-based system mounted on MM vehicles’ windscreens. According to MM, the technology entered a nine-month trial in November 2024 and uses moving-vehicle image capture to detect cracks, potholes, other pavement defects and anomalies in vertical road signs, with outputs integrated into MM’s Web-GIS portal. At a conceptual level, the system combines mobile computer vision, georeferenced defect detection and GIS-based asset mapping to create an updated digital view of the city’s road infrastructure. The expected impact is earlier fault detection, fewer emergency interventions and more efficient allocation of maintenance resources. More broadly, the project creates value through lower operational cost, better planning accuracy and a scalable approach to urban road-condition monitoring.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2376,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01789,EU_PSTW_202609_01789,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Riigikogu - Estonian Parliament,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.","By automating these processes, the solution reduces manual workload, minimizes errors, and accelerates decision-making within the legislative framework.",AI to accelerate the legislative power of the Parliament of Estonia,"Riigikogu - Estonian Parliament; Central-Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Estonia",UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Estonia's parliament, the Riigikogu, partnered with Nortal to enhance legislative efficiency by implementing an AI-driven intelligent search solution. This system utilizes natural language processing to swiftly retrieve relevant information from extensive legislative documents, streamlining tasks such as drafting responses and preparing materials for members of parliament. By automating these processes, the solution reduces manual workload, minimizes errors, and accelerates decision-making within the legislative framework.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK|F08_ERROR_COMPLIANCE_RISK,S7_GENERATIVE_CONTENT_CREATION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2378,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01790,EU_PSTW_202609_01790,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Lower Saxony State Health Office (NLGA),Public health services.,"The Lower Saxony State Health Office developed Nova to respond to a recurring public-health communication challenge: during major health events such as the COVID-19 pandemic, public enquiries can rise sharply, overloading staff and making it harder to provide quick, consistent answers through traditional channels alone.",Battling pandemics with the help of AI,Lower Saxony State Health Office (NLGA); Regional Government; Public health services.; Germany,"The solution was implemented with Nortal using its Tark platform to create Nova, a virtual assistant available on the NLGA website.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Lower Saxony State Health Office developed Nova to respond to a recurring public-health communication challenge: during major health events such as the COVID-19 pandemic, public enquiries can rise sharply, overloading staff and making it harder to provide quick, consistent answers through traditional channels alone. The organisation needed a way to offer reliable health information around the clock while preserving staff capacity for more complex work. The stakeholders include the health office, its service teams and citizens seeking guidance on diseases and health-related concerns, with the objective of improving access to official information, reducing pressure on employees and strengthening the administration’s ability to communicate under crisis conditions. The solution was implemented with Nortal using its Tark platform to create Nova, a virtual assistant available on the NLGA website. At a conceptual level, the system combines generative capabilities, large language models and a secure, NLGA-controlled knowledge base to interpret questions, understand contextual meaning and deliver rapid answers on topics such as COVID-19, measles and other public-health concerns. This architecture allows the tool to update quickly as information changes while keeping control of source content within the public authority. The impact includes faster access to health information, better service continuity and lower workload for staff. More broadly, Nova creates value by improving crisis communication, increasing scalability in citizen support and demonstrating a secure model for health-related virtual assistance in public administration.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2379,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01791,EU_PSTW_202609_01791,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Estonian Air Force,Public order and safety n.e.c.,"The defence and civil-protection challenge is that traditional surveillance systems may benefit from additional distributed observations, especially where rapid reporting and corroboration can support situational awareness.",BeReady - AI to enhance Air Raid Warning System,Estonian Air Force; Central-Government; Public order and safety n.e.c.; Estonia,"Stakeholders include the Estonian Air Force, citizens using the application, Estonia-based IT companies, national-security authorities and communities relying on timely air-raid warnings. The implementation integrates an AI-powered air-threat identification feature into the BeReady mobile app.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Estonia’s BeReady initiative strengthens national air-threat detection by involving citizens in the reporting of suspicious aerial activity through a mobile application. The defence and civil-protection challenge is that traditional surveillance systems may benefit from additional distributed observations, especially where rapid reporting and corroboration can support situational awareness. Stakeholders include the Estonian Air Force, citizens using the application, Estonia-based IT companies, national-security authorities and communities relying on timely air-raid warnings. The implementation integrates an AI-powered air-threat identification feature into the BeReady mobile app. Citizens can capture and submit photos of suspicious aerial activities, while the system processes the inputs and consolidates data from multiple users to generate real-time, actionable intelligence for the Air Force. Conceptually, the solution combines mobile crowdsourcing, image analysis, threat classification, data aggregation and operational intelligence workflows. The system was successfully tested during Spring Storm 2024, Estonia’s largest annual joint military exercise. The expected impact is an expansion of surveillance capacity through citizen participation, faster collection of relevant observations and improved support for air-threat assessment. By connecting public reporting with automated analysis, the application can strengthen national security and resilience. Its deployment also requires safeguards to manage false reports, data quality, privacy and the appropriate use of citizen-generated information in defence contexts.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2380,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01792,EU_PSTW_202609_01792,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Municipalities of Den Haag, Groningen, Nijmegen, Rotterdam",Community development.,"This creates a practical challenge for municipalities seeking to make streets, squares and other shared environments more walkable, inclusive and supportive of health and wellbeing.",Good Public Space for Walking pilot in multiple dutch Municipalities,"Municipalities of Den Haag, Groningen, Nijmegen, Rotterdam; Local Government; Community development.; Netherlands","The solution is implemented through an interdisciplinary methodology that combines classical spatial analysis with behavioural studies and digitally supported citizen participation. At a conceptual level, the approach compares the intended function of a place with observed patterns of use, while AI-enabled participation tools help structure and incorporate residents’ views into the assessment process.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Across many towns and cities, public spaces are designed with certain assumptions about how people will move, stop and interact, yet the way these places are actually used often differs from what planners intended. This creates a practical challenge for municipalities seeking to make streets, squares and other shared environments more walkable, inclusive and supportive of health and wellbeing. The Good Public Space for Walking project, developed by Humankind between 2021 and 2022, addresses this gap by helping local authorities understand where the design of a space does not match real behaviour and by translating that insight into usable planning recommendations. The stakeholders include municipal planners, urban designers and residents, with the objective of creating public spaces that better support walking, sitting and social interaction while responding more directly to community needs. The solution is implemented through an interdisciplinary methodology that combines classical spatial analysis with behavioural studies and digitally supported citizen participation. At a conceptual level, the approach compares the intended function of a place with observed patterns of use, while AI-enabled participation tools help structure and incorporate residents’ views into the assessment process. This produces a richer evidence base for design decisions. The expected impact is more user-centred urban planning, better-targeted improvements and stronger alignment between public-space investment and actual community use. More broadly, the project enhances liveability, supports healthier daily habits and creates value through more responsive and effective urban design.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2381,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01793,EU_PSTW_202609_01793,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Dordrecht,Community development.,"Traditional policy development in this area can be constrained by fragmented evidence, static indicators or limited ability to test the likely impact of interventions before they are delivered.",Bikeability.AI to analise bikeability in Municipality of Dordrecht,Municipality of Dordrecht; Local Government; Community development.; Netherlands,"The use case therefore involves the Municipality of Dordrecht and mobility-policy stakeholders, with the objective of generating stronger evidence for cycling policy, improving planning at both district and city level and supporting more informed investment in active mobility. The solution is implemented through Bikeability.ai, described by Horizons as a tool developed by Sobolt with Dordrecht as launching partner.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Dordrecht needed a better way to understand how cycle-friendly different neighbourhoods and city areas actually are, and how planned infrastructure changes might improve the quality of cycling conditions. Traditional policy development in this area can be constrained by fragmented evidence, static indicators or limited ability to test the likely impact of interventions before they are delivered. The use case therefore involves the Municipality of Dordrecht and mobility-policy stakeholders, with the objective of generating stronger evidence for cycling policy, improving planning at both district and city level and supporting more informed investment in active mobility. The solution is implemented through Bikeability.ai, described by Horizons as a tool developed by Sobolt with Dordrecht as launching partner. At a conceptual level, the system uses analytical models to compute and visualise bikeability indicators and to simulate the positive effects of proposed infrastructure interventions. Publicly available technical documentation also shows a bikeability package that works with OpenStreetMap data and spatial indicators such as cycling facilities, greenery and road-network characteristics, suggesting a geospatial analytics approach underpinning the policy tool. This allows city teams to move from descriptive observation towards scenario-based planning. The expected impact is clearer policy insight, better prioritisation of cycling interventions and more evidence-based urban mobility planning. More broadly, the project creates value by supporting healthier, more sustainable transport choices through scalable, data-informed assessment of cycling conditions.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2382,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01794,EU_PSTW_202609_01794,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Cinisello Balsamo,General services.,"This manual work is repetitive, time-consuming and poorly suited to high-volume digital administration, yet it remains essential for ensuring timely response and orderly case handling. Rather than replacing human oversight altogether, the platform aims to reduce the clerical burden at the front end of the process.",AI for certfied emails management in the Municipality of Cinisello Balsamo,Municipality of Cinisello Balsamo; Local Government; General services.; Italy,"The Municipality of Cinisello Balsamo introduced this use case to tackle a significant administrative bottleneck: managing large volumes of certified emails, or PEC messages, which require staff to read, classify and sort incoming communications before they can be directed into the correct municipal processes. The solution was implemented in partnership with Make IT through a machine-learning-based platform for PEC management.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Municipality of Cinisello Balsamo introduced this use case to tackle a significant administrative bottleneck: managing large volumes of certified emails, or PEC messages, which require staff to read, classify and sort incoming communications before they can be directed into the correct municipal processes. This manual work is repetitive, time-consuming and poorly suited to high-volume digital administration, yet it remains essential for ensuring timely response and orderly case handling. The stakeholders include municipal employees, administrative managers and citizens interacting with the municipality through certified email, with the objective of improving operational efficiency, reducing routine workload and allowing staff to concentrate on more strategic and rewarding tasks. The solution was implemented in partnership with Make IT through a machine-learning-based platform for PEC management. At a conceptual level, the system automates the reading, classification and routing of certified emails, creating a structured intake workflow that can integrate with municipal operations while preserving attention to security, data protection and ethical considerations. Rather than replacing human oversight altogether, the platform aims to reduce the clerical burden at the front end of the process. The expected impact is faster handling of incoming PEC traffic, reduced manual effort and improved productivity for municipal teams. More broadly, the project creates value by modernising digital administration, supporting better use of staff time and demonstrating how local authorities can apply automation responsibly in document-heavy service environments.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK|F06_HIGH_VOLUME_SCALABILITY_BOTTLENECK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2383,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01795,EU_PSTW_202609_01795,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Rural Investing Finance Authority (AFIR),General public services n.e.c.,"This administrative complexity can create delays, increase processing costs and discourage applicants, particularly where rural beneficiaries have limited time or resources to navigate public procedures. At a conceptual level, the system combines robotic process automation with AI-enabled enterprise automation to move across institutional systems, collect the necessary records and reduce the amount of manual administrative intervention needed.",UiPath - AI to help farmers get EU funding,Rural Investing Finance Authority (AFIR); Central-Government; General public services n.e.c.; Romania,"The use case therefore involves AFIR, farmers and the state institutions whose documents are needed for funding applications, with the objective of making access to EU support quicker, less burdensome and more efficient at scale. The solution was implemented by AFIR in collaboration with UiPath through automation tools that retrieve required documents and approvals as part of the application workflow.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Romania’s Agency for Financing Rural Investments needed to simplify and speed up the process through which farmers access EU funding, a process that traditionally involves collecting multiple documents and authorisations from different public sources. This administrative complexity can create delays, increase processing costs and discourage applicants, particularly where rural beneficiaries have limited time or resources to navigate public procedures. The use case therefore involves AFIR, farmers and the state institutions whose documents are needed for funding applications, with the objective of making access to EU support quicker, less burdensome and more efficient at scale. The solution was implemented by AFIR in collaboration with UiPath through automation tools that retrieve required documents and approvals as part of the application workflow. At a conceptual level, the system combines robotic process automation with AI-enabled enterprise automation to move across institutional systems, collect the necessary records and reduce the amount of manual administrative intervention needed. AFIR stated in March 2024 that the initiative was expected to benefit more than 50,000 stakeholders, including both farmers and state institutions. The expected impact is shorter processing times, lower administrative cost and improved operational efficiency across the funding chain. More broadly, the project creates value by making public funding more accessible, increasing the efficiency of inter-institutional document handling and supporting a more user-friendly model of agricultural-support administration.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,S6_WORKFLOW_PROCESS_AUTOMATION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2384,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01796,EU_PSTW_202609_01796,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Government of Serbia,General public services n.e.c.,"Serbia launched its national AI supercomputing platform in 2021 to address a strategic capability gap: innovative companies, start-ups and research users often need access to high-performance computing in order to develop advanced models and tackle data-intensive tasks, but such infrastructure is costly and difficult to secure independently.",AI Supercomputing Platform,Government of Serbia; Central-Government; General public services n.e.c.; Serbia,"The solution is implemented through the National AI Platform located in the State Data Center in Kragujevac, where users can access supercomputing resources and training free of charge through a public call process.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Serbia launched its national AI supercomputing platform in 2021 to address a strategic capability gap: innovative companies, start-ups and research users often need access to high-performance computing in order to develop advanced models and tackle data-intensive tasks, but such infrastructure is costly and difficult to secure independently. The platform was therefore designed as a national resource to strengthen the country’s innovation ecosystem and help build a stronger domestic community around advanced digital technologies. The stakeholders include start-ups, innovative companies, research institutions and public bodies responsible for digital development, with the objective of widening access to computational capacity, accelerating model development and supporting a more competitive national AI ecosystem. The solution is implemented through the National AI Platform located in the State Data Center in Kragujevac, where users can access supercomputing resources and training free of charge through a public call process. At a conceptual level, the platform combines GPU-based high-performance computing, training support and organised user access under Serbia’s national AI strategies for 2020–2025 and 2025–2030. Official information indicates that 38 institutions and 35 start-up companies currently have access, with more than 360 user accounts in total, and the platform has also received international recognition from the OECD. The expected impact is stronger innovation capacity, broader access to advanced computing and faster development of complex models. More broadly, the platform creates value by reducing infrastructure barriers, supporting start-up growth and reinforcing Serbia’s long-term digital competitiveness.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2385,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01797,EU_PSTW_202609_01797,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Innovation Center of the School of Electrical Engineering in Belgrade (ICEF),General public services n.e.c.,"Serbian-language digital resources remain relatively limited in several important professional domains, which creates a barrier to building robust language technologies for sectors such as law, administration, finance and healthcare.",COMtext.SR - An AI model for a small-ish language,Innovation Center of the School of Electrical Engineering in Belgrade (ICEF); Academic-Research; General public services n.e.c.; Serbia,"The solution is implemented through the coordinated development of datasets, linguistic resources and processing tools released under a licence that permits all uses, including commercial applications. More broadly, COMtext.SR creates value by reducing resource scarcity, supporting innovation in a smaller language market and enabling broader public and commercial use of Serbian-language tools.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Serbian-language digital resources remain relatively limited in several important professional domains, which creates a barrier to building robust language technologies for sectors such as law, administration, finance and healthcare. Existing public tools and datasets have not adequately covered these areas, leaving both academic and commercial users with fewer resources for high-quality text processing in Serbian, including both ekavica and ijekavica variants. COMtext.SR was developed to address this gap by creating a foundational set of openly available resources and tools for automatic text processing in Serbian. The stakeholders include the academic community, the IT industry and organisations working in domain-specific language environments, with the objective of strengthening the language-technology ecosystem and making advanced Serbian-language processing more broadly usable. The solution is implemented through the coordinated development of datasets, linguistic resources and processing tools released under a licence that permits all uses, including commercial applications. At a conceptual level, the project combines corpus development, language-model building and community contribution across previously under-resourced domains, while also mobilising donated expertise, materials and intellectual property from both academia and industry. The expected impact is improved access to Serbian-language processing tools, stronger support for specialised text analysis and a more capable national language-technology infrastructure. More broadly, COMtext.SR creates value by reducing resource scarcity, supporting innovation in a smaller language market and enabling broader public and commercial use of Serbian-language tools.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2386,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01798,EU_PSTW_202609_01798,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Computing Center of the Slovak Academy of Sciences (CC SAS),General public services n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,Devana Supercomputer in Slovakia,Computing Center of the Slovak Academy of Sciences (CC SAS); Academic-Research; General public services n.e.c.; Slovakia,"The use case therefore involves the Computing Center of the Slovak Academy of Sciences, academic researchers, university teams and commercial users, with the objective of expanding access to modern computational resources, improving research capability and supporting innovation across multiple domains. The solution is implemented through Devana, unveiled in May 2023, with a theoretical performance of around 800 teraflops.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Slovak Academy of Sciences introduced the Devana supercomputer to strengthen national research capacity and replace older computing infrastructure that was no longer sufficient for increasingly data-intensive scientific and technological work. Researchers in universities and academy institutes require high-performance computing to run advanced simulations, process large datasets and support innovation in fields where conventional infrastructure can become a limiting factor. The use case therefore involves the Computing Center of the Slovak Academy of Sciences, academic researchers, university teams and commercial users, with the objective of expanding access to modern computational resources, improving research capability and supporting innovation across multiple domains. The solution is implemented through Devana, unveiled in May 2023, with a theoretical performance of around 800 teraflops. At a conceptual level, the system combines 140 universal computing nodes with 8 GPU-accelerated nodes, enabling large-scale modelling, scientific analysis and advanced applications in areas such as machine learning. The platform is provided free of charge to scientists and researchers from Slovak universities and institutes of the Slovak Academy of Sciences, while also supporting commercial projects. The impact includes stronger computational capacity, broader access to advanced infrastructure and improved support for complex research workflows. More broadly, Devana creates value by modernising Slovakia’s scientific infrastructure, fostering innovation and enabling more scalable and competitive research and development across academic and applied domains.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2387,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01799,EU_PSTW_202609_01799,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Fire Rescue Service of the Czech Republic,Fire-protection services,"The Czech PSAP pilot addressed this challenge by testing whether language-support technology could improve emergency communication in multilingual and tourism-related situations. The results showed encouraging potential, but also highlighted important limits, including uneven performance across languages, difficulties preserving the chronology of fast conversations and problems detecting questions in Czech, where intonation is especially important.",AI pilot in Public Safety Answering Point (PSAP) in Czechia,Fire Rescue Service of the Czech Republic; Central-Government; Fire-protection services; Czechia,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Emergency call centres must gather critical information quickly, even when callers do not speak the local language fluently, yet language barriers can delay understanding at the very moment when speed and precision matter most. The Czech PSAP pilot addressed this challenge by testing whether language-support technology could improve emergency communication in multilingual and tourism-related situations. The stakeholders included call takers, PSAP managers and technology providers, with the objective of strengthening communication quality, supporting operators in multilingual calls and assessing whether the tool could become a practical aid in real emergency environments. The pilot focused on around 70 test calls in different languages and examined how well the solution handled transcription and translation under operational conditions. The implementation used Cestel’s NatalíA natural-language-processing solution, which combines simultaneous language detection, translation, transcription and text-to-speech services. At a conceptual level, the tool was tested as a support layer alongside standard emergency-call handling, helping operators understand incoming speech and access written representations of the exchange. The results showed encouraging potential, but also highlighted important limits, including uneven performance across languages, difficulties preserving the chronology of fast conversations and problems detecting questions in Czech, where intonation is especially important. The impact of the pilot was therefore primarily evaluative: it confirmed the promise of multilingual support in PSAP settings while also identifying clear requirements for better linguistic accuracy, stronger conversation handling and on-premise deployment to meet data-security expectations.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2389,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01800,EU_PSTW_202609_01800,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Emergencies 112 Andalusia,Public order and safety n.e.c.,Background noise also caused delays and disrupted the flow of communication.,AI pilot in Public Safety Answering Point (PSAP) in Andalusia,Emergencies 112 Andalusia; Regional Government; Public order and safety n.e.c.; Spain,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"In a region with tourism, linguistic diversity and time-critical emergency communications, the ability of call centres to understand callers quickly across languages is an important operational concern. The Andalusia PSAP pilot examined whether language-processing technology could reduce friction in emergency interactions and support operators when callers do not communicate clearly in the local language. The stakeholders included emergency call handlers, PSAP leadership and the technology provider, with the objective of improving communication speed, reducing misunderstanding and assessing the usefulness of automated support tools in emergency workflows. The pilot was conducted on a relatively small scale and concentrated on two specific functions that are especially relevant in live calls: transcription and text-to-speech. The implementation used Cestel’s NatalíA solution, which also includes language detection and translation capabilities. At a conceptual level, the system was introduced as a support mechanism alongside normal call-taking processes, helping operators access spoken content in text form and receive automated language assistance where needed. The findings were mixed: transcription and text-to-speech performed well, while translation was criticised for being too literal and insufficiently adapted to emergency terminology. Background noise also caused delays and disrupted the flow of communication. Even so, the pilot demonstrated operational potential and gave clear direction for improvement, particularly around transcription quality and service cohesion. The expected value lies in faster, more accessible multilingual communication, but only once robustness improves sufficiently for demanding emergency-call conditions.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2390,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01801,EU_PSTW_202609_01801,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,SOS Deiak Emergency Coordination Centre,Public order and safety n.e.c.,"However, the system struggled with institution names, certain Basque characters and, crucially, the effects of background noise, especially in hands-free conditions.",AI pilot in Public Safety Answering Point (PSAP) in Basque Country,SOS Deiak Emergency Coordination Centre; Regional Government; Public order and safety n.e.c.; Spain,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Emergency call centres in multilingual environments must capture vital information accurately even when callers speak quickly, switch language or place calls from noisy settings. The Basque Country PSAP pilot explored whether transcription technology could help improve the effectiveness of emergency communications by supporting operators with faster and clearer access to spoken content. The stakeholders included call takers, PSAP managers and the solution provider, with the objective of understanding the practical value of automated transcription in real emergency-call scenarios and identifying the conditions under which it might become operationally useful. The pilot focused on more than 25 simulated calls in Spanish and evaluated the quality of transcription produced during the exercises. The implementation used Cestel’s NatalíA language-processing solution, with attention centred in this case on the transcription service rather than the full multilingual feature set. At a conceptual level, the tool functioned as a real-time aid, converting speech into written text so that operators could track and review key information more easily during a call. The results showed good punctuation, spelling and speaker separation in many cases, indicating real promise. However, the system struggled with institution names, certain Basque characters and, crucially, the effects of background noise, especially in hands-free conditions. The pilot therefore demonstrated that transcription can add value in PSAP contexts, but also made clear that better accuracy and stronger chronology handling are needed before the solution is suitable for operational deployment.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2391,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01802,EU_PSTW_202609_01802,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Integrated Control Centre in Ludwigshafen,Public order and safety n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,AI pilot in Public Safety Answering Point (PSAP) in Ludwigshafen (Germany),Integrated Control Centre in Ludwigshafen; Local Government; Public order and safety n.e.c.; Germany,"However, the beta version was not considered ready for live operational use, especially because of limitations in generative support and insufficient medical knowledge.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Ludwigshafen PSAP explored whether advanced language and call-assistance technologies could improve emergency-call handling in situations where operators must work quickly, accurately and sometimes across multiple languages. Emergency communication centres need tools that support understanding without compromising reliability, privacy or operational control, especially when handling sensitive and time-critical calls. The pilot therefore involved call takers, management and the technology provider, with the objective of evaluating whether automated language services and AI-supported call guidance could improve the operator experience and strengthen information capture during emergencies. The trial tested LiveReader’s ALTERNIS solution for simultaneous language detection, translation and transcription, alongside the NOTITIA tool, which proposes questions and extracts call data to support operators. At a conceptual level, the system combined offline, on-premise detection and transcription for security, browser-based translation without data storage, and AI-assisted prompts to help structure calls. The pilot involved 184 participants working on 1,319 simulated cases, providing a substantial testing base. Participants rated the solution positively, with an average score of 4.1 out of 5, and particularly appreciated the interface and the AI-generated hints. However, the beta version was not considered ready for live operational use, especially because of limitations in generative support and insufficient medical knowledge. Even so, the pilot showed clear value in secure, supportive AI tooling for PSAPs and identified a credible path for future operational maturity.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2392,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01803,EU_PSTW_202609_01803,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Provincia Autonoma of Trento,Public order and safety n.e.c.,"Emergency call audio is often one of the most difficult speech environments to process reliably, as conversations may include screams, overlapping voices, weak connections and high background noise.",AI pilot in Public Safety Answering Point (PSAP) in Trento (Italy),Provincia Autonoma of Trento; Local Government; Public order and safety n.e.c.; Italy,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Emergency call audio is often one of the most difficult speech environments to process reliably, as conversations may include screams, overlapping voices, weak connections and high background noise. The Trento PSAP pilot examined whether automated transcription could still be useful under these demanding conditions and whether it could support future enhancements in emergency-call processing. The stakeholders included call takers, PSAP managers and the technology provider, with the objective of testing the practical value of transcription tools on live emergency calls and understanding how such outputs might support broader operational improvements. The pilot was conducted on approximately 2,500 emergency calls between mid-August and the end of September 2024, and user experience was assessed through several methods, including focus groups. The implementation used Gladia’s transcription tool as a support layer within the call-handling process. At a conceptual level, the technology sought to convert speech into text in real time, creating a basis for later integration with tools capable of summarising calls, suggesting actions or automatically populating CAD systems. In practice, call takers found that transcription quality was not yet sufficient for emergency audio conditions and rated the service poorly. However, the pilot still generated useful insight: it confirmed operational interest in transcription, identified the audio-quality barriers that must be addressed and highlighted downstream opportunities for large-language-model applications. Gladia reported that some issues found during the pilot were corrected in an October 2024 update, suggesting potential for future improvement.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2393,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01804,EU_PSTW_202609_01804,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Rome,Other industries,UNKNOWN,Julia: GenerativeAI as a public service,Municipality of Rome; Local Government; Other industries; Italy,"Julia is the official AI assistant of Roma Capitale: a free, multilingual public service available 24/7 that uses natural language to help citizens, tourists and city users find answers, navigate the city and access digital services.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Julia is the official AI assistant of Roma Capitale: a free, multilingual public service available 24/7 that uses natural language to help citizens, tourists and city users find answers, navigate the city and access digital services. It is the first generative AI developed and governed by a local public authority. Based on certified data from Roma Capitale and qualified partners, it brings information and services together in a single conversational interface. Available on WhatsApp, Telegram, Messenger and Webchat, since June it has also been available on the institutional portal, which hosts more than 160,000 pieces of content, around 90 online services and 18 million annual visits. Julia guides users to official services and sources, providing direct links and related documents. The platform is multi-agent: a Super Agent routes requests to specialized agents for city experiences, authenticated services and CRM. The Azure-centric architecture, with the Fastweb Information Agent integrated across cloud environments, ensures scalability, security and seamless integration.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2394,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01805,EU_PSTW_202609_01805,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,re:edu GmbH & Co. KG,Environmental protection n.e.c.,AI.CAN addresses this challenge by combining citizen participation with scientific monitoring to improve aquatic-ecology data collection and analysis along the Oder River.,AI.CAN: AI Citizen Science Aqua Network,re:edu GmbH & Co. KG; Private sector; Environmental protection n.e.c.; Germany,"The solution is implemented through sensor boxes deployed by non-governmental and citizen actors, whose data are then analysed together with official monitoring information.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Monitoring water quality across a river system is a complex task, particularly when environmental changes can emerge quickly and official measurement points alone may not provide enough spatial or temporal coverage. AI.CAN addresses this challenge by combining citizen participation with scientific monitoring to improve aquatic-ecology data collection and analysis along the Oder River. The stakeholders include citizen initiatives, NGOs, environmental associations, researchers and public-interest organisations, with the objective of creating a richer and more distributed evidence base for understanding water conditions and ecological risks. The project also aims to show that collaborative, data-driven water monitoring can be transferable to other river systems and bodies of water. The solution is implemented through sensor boxes deployed by non-governmental and citizen actors, whose data are then analysed together with official monitoring information. At a conceptual level, the system combines distributed environmental sensing, citizen-science participation and analytical models to identify patterns in water-quality conditions and improve interpretation of aquatic data. The collaboration with the Leibniz Institute of Freshwater Ecology and Inland Fisheries and re:edu provides a bridge between grassroots monitoring and scientific expertise. The expected impact is broader data coverage, improved environmental awareness and stronger capacity to detect and understand water-quality issues. More broadly, AI.CAN creates value by making aquatic monitoring more participatory, scalable and evidence-based, while strengthening cooperation between citizens and scientific institutions in environmental stewardship.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2395,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01806,EU_PSTW_202609_01806,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Senate of Hamburg,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.",UNKNOWN,SUMM AI - Creating accessible information: End to end automated with AI,"Senate of Hamburg; Local Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Germany","This use case, developed by the start-up SUMM AI in collaboration with the press office of the Senate of Hamburg, addresses that operational challenge by helping public-sector employees create accessible information with far less manual effort. The solution is implemented as an automated pipeline that analyses the meaning of a text and suggests suitable images from a curated database, with future functionality intended to generate new visuals where no appropriate image exists.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Public administrations are increasingly expected to provide information that is not only accurate and timely, but also easy to understand and accessible to people with different communication needs. In practice, however, producing barrier-free content can be labour-intensive, particularly when staff must adapt texts, identify suitable illustrations and ensure that materials remain understandable for broad audiences. This use case, developed by the start-up SUMM AI in collaboration with the press office of the Senate of Hamburg, addresses that operational challenge by helping public-sector employees create accessible information with far less manual effort. The objective is to improve equal access to public information across Germany by making the production of clearer, more inclusive content faster and easier within everyday administrative workflows. The solution is implemented as an automated pipeline that analyses the meaning of a text and suggests suitable images from a curated database, with future functionality intended to generate new visuals where no appropriate image exists. At a conceptual level, the system combines text analysis, semantic matching and image recommendation within a content-production workflow tailored to public administration. This allows staff to move more quickly from raw written content to illustrated, accessible materials. The expected impact is reduced preparation time, improved consistency in accessible communication and broader reach for public information. More broadly, the project strengthens inclusion, enhances the usability of official communications and creates value by lowering the practical barriers to producing barrier-free content at scale.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2396,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01807,EU_PSTW_202609_01807,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Berlin,General public services n.e.c.,"Citizens and frontline organisations often struggle to navigate the wide range of social and cultural services available in a community, especially when information is fragmented across providers, difficult to search or inaccessible to people who face language or digital barriers.",Civic-Sync – Mapping platform for social offerings,City of Berlin; Local Government; General public services n.e.c.; Germany,"The solution is implemented as a collaborative database and mapping platform with open interfaces, multilingual access and low-barrier design.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Citizens and frontline organisations often struggle to navigate the wide range of social and cultural services available in a community, especially when information is fragmented across providers, difficult to search or inaccessible to people who face language or digital barriers. Civic-Sync addresses this problem by creating a shared platform through which social and cultural offerings can be collected, structured and made easier to find. The stakeholders include civil-society organisations, public institutions and the people seeking support, with the objective of improving visibility, accessibility and coordination across available services while ensuring that information remains easy to reuse and compliant with data-protection requirements. The solution is implemented as a collaborative database and mapping platform with open interfaces, multilingual access and low-barrier design. At a conceptual level, the platform combines shared data structures, translation tools and speech output to make information available to a broader set of users, while future development focuses on enhancing search, guidance and feedback functions through additional intelligent support features. This creates a common infrastructure through which providers can maintain and share service information more effectively. The expected impact is easier access to relevant social and cultural offerings, better discoverability for target groups and improved coordination between institutions and civil-society actors. More broadly, Civic-Sync supports inclusion, strengthens service visibility and creates value by turning dispersed local information into a more navigable, multilingual and citizen-friendly support ecosystem.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2397,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01808,EU_PSTW_202609_01808,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Berlin,Social protection n.e.c.,"Many people do not access state support simply because the system is difficult to understand, full of administrative language and fragmented across multiple schemes, forms and eligibility rules.",Claim - AI for social claims,City of Berlin; Local Government; Social protection n.e.c.; Germany,"The use case involves citizens, social workers and counsellors, with the objective of reducing bureaucratic and linguistic obstacles, saving time in advisory settings and making access to public support more equitable. The solution is implemented through an interactive application that calculates individual claims on the basis of one structured form and delivers personalised guidance.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Many people do not access state support simply because the system is difficult to understand, full of administrative language and fragmented across multiple schemes, forms and eligibility rules. Claim addresses this barrier by helping users determine, anonymously and more simply, which public benefits or funding they may be entitled to receive. The use case involves citizens, social workers and counsellors, with the objective of reducing bureaucratic and linguistic obstacles, saving time in advisory settings and making access to public support more equitable. Rather than requiring users to navigate numerous official sources, the tool is designed to guide them through a single process that clarifies possible entitlements and next steps. The solution is implemented through an interactive application that calculates individual claims on the basis of one structured form and delivers personalised guidance. At a conceptual level, it combines machine learning and natural language processing to support user interaction, extract relevant information dynamically and offer multilingual access, enabling both self-service use by citizens and guided use in advisory consultations. This allows complex eligibility logic to be translated into a more accessible user journey. The expected impact is quicker identification of relevant support, reduced consultation time for social workers and fewer missed entitlements for citizens. More broadly, Claim improves user experience, lowers access barriers and creates social value by making public financial assistance easier to understand and reach.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2398,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01809,EU_PSTW_202609_01809,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""social_nonprofit""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,IU International University,General public services n.e.c.,"Many social and non-profit organisations need to communicate professionally with the public, funders and local communities, yet they often lack dedicated communications staff, sufficient budgets or the specialist expertise needed to produce high-quality public-relations material. This challenge is particularly acute in organisations that depend on volunteers, where communication work competes with service delivery for time and attention.",Comms4Good - AI for public relations work of social and non-profit organizations,IU International University; Academic-Research; General public services n.e.c.; Germany,"The solution is implemented as a virtual assistant developed through a collaboration between IU International University, Producer Media UG and Phineo.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Many social and non-profit organisations need to communicate professionally with the public, funders and local communities, yet they often lack dedicated communications staff, sufficient budgets or the specialist expertise needed to produce high-quality public-relations material. This challenge is particularly acute in organisations that depend on volunteers, where communication work competes with service delivery for time and attention. Comms4Good addresses this gap by supporting non-experts in carrying out essential PR tasks more effectively and at lower cost. The stakeholders include social organisations, non-profits, volunteers and communication leads, with the objective of improving visibility, strengthening outreach and making everyday communication work more manageable. The solution is implemented as a virtual assistant developed through a collaboration between IU International University, Producer Media UG and Phineo. At a conceptual level, the tool analyses an organisation’s existing public presence and generates practical recommendations and templates, such as draft press releases or poster ideas, to support communication activities. By embedding this support in an accessible digital tool, the project seeks to raise the baseline quality of communications without requiring extensive external expertise. The expected impact is improved visibility for social initiatives, more efficient communication workflows and a better working culture around outreach tasks. More broadly, Comms4Good creates value by helping mission-driven organisations communicate more professionally, consistently and affordably.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2399,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01810,EU_PSTW_202609_01810,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,CORRECTIV,General public services n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,CORRECTIV.Faktenforum - AI to combat disinformation,CORRECTIV; Private sector; General public services n.e.c.; Germany,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"CORRECTIV.Faktenforum is Germany’s first fact-checking community, created to involve citizens in identifying, discussing and countering false claims. The societal problem is the rapid spread of disinformation online, which can outpace traditional journalistic fact-checking and undermine public debate. The platform therefore combines community participation, civic technology and professional guidance to scale prebunking and debunking activities. Stakeholders include CORRECTIV, participating citizens, journalists, online communities, civic-tech actors and the wider public exposed to misleading information. The implementation enables people, regardless of journalistic experience, to contribute from anywhere by identifying suspicious claims, collecting evidence, discussing facts and sharing findings under CORRECTIV’s guidance to maintain journalistic standards. AI technologies support the workflow through data analysis, machine learning and generative AI, while human-in-the-loop tools such as CORRECTIV.Checkbot assist community efforts. Conceptually, the system combines collaborative fact-checking, automated claim-support tools, content analysis, community moderation and editorial oversight. The expected impact is increased capacity to detect and respond to false claims, stronger public participation in information integrity and improved resilience against disinformation campaigns. By combining automation with human review and journalistic standards, the project can expand fact-checking capacity without fully outsourcing judgement to machines. Its value lies in linking civic engagement with structured, technology-supported verification processes.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2400,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01811,EU_PSTW_202609_01811,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Technical University of Cologne,Family and children.,"Finding the right information at the right time can be difficult, and this can make it harder for families to stay in work, access assistance or participate fully in social life. BOTschafft Inclusion addresses this challenge by providing more accessible and responsive guidance for caregiving parents and, in some cases, for employers seeking relevant support information.",BOTschafft Inclusion - AI for families with disability,Technical University of Cologne; Academic-Research; Family and children.; Germany,"The solution is implemented as a chatbot-based support system developed through a collaboration between Cologne University of Applied Sciences, Founders1 GmbH and Health Region CologneBonn e.V. At a conceptual level, the system uses language-understanding capabilities to interpret complex questions, recommend relevant services and adapt its responses over time as it learns from interactions and use patterns.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Parents caring for children with disabilities or chronic illnesses often face a complex and exhausting landscape of support services, rehabilitation options, training opportunities and employment-related questions. Finding the right information at the right time can be difficult, and this can make it harder for families to stay in work, access assistance or participate fully in social life. BOTschafft Inclusion addresses this challenge by providing more accessible and responsive guidance for caregiving parents and, in some cases, for employers seeking relevant support information. The stakeholders include families, employers and support organisations, with the objective of helping parents remain professionally active, better informed and more connected to available services. The solution is implemented as a chatbot-based support system developed through a collaboration between Cologne University of Applied Sciences, Founders1 GmbH and Health Region CologneBonn e.V. At a conceptual level, the system uses language-understanding capabilities to interpret complex questions, recommend relevant services and adapt its responses over time as it learns from interactions and use patterns. This enables more tailored guidance than static information pages or conventional FAQ tools. The expected impact is easier access to counselling and support, reduced time spent searching for relevant assistance and a more personalised experience for caregiving families. More broadly, the project promotes inclusion, supports labour-market participation and creates social value by making support pathways more understandable and usable for parents under considerable practical pressure.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2401,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01812,EU_PSTW_202609_01812,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Deutscher Gehörlosen-Bund e.V.,Sickness and disability.,"These barriers can limit participation, reduce independence and make access to public and private services more difficult.",Deaf.AI - AI for social inclusion,Deutscher Gehörlosen-Bund e.V.; Non-governmental; Sickness and disability.; Germany,"The solution is implemented by training systems on extensive sign-language video data in order to develop avatars capable of automatically rendering sign-language output. A defining feature of the initiative is that it is led by deaf professionals, ensuring that development remains grounded in practical use and community priorities rather than abstract technical assumptions.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Deaf people continue to face significant communication barriers in everyday social and professional life, particularly where information, services and interactions are not readily available in sign language. These barriers can limit participation, reduce independence and make access to public and private services more difficult. Deaf.AI addresses this problem by seeking to improve sign-language accessibility through tools developed around the actual needs of the deaf community. The stakeholders include deaf individuals, advocacy organisations and service environments where communication inclusion matters, with the objective of strengthening participation, reducing exclusion and expanding access to information and interaction in more accessible forms. The solution is implemented by training systems on extensive sign-language video data in order to develop avatars capable of automatically rendering sign-language output. At a conceptual level, the project combines video-based language modelling, avatar generation and translation functionality so that spoken or written information can be conveyed more effectively in sign language. A defining feature of the initiative is that it is led by deaf professionals, ensuring that development remains grounded in practical use and community priorities rather than abstract technical assumptions. The expected impact is improved accessibility, greater autonomy and stronger social and professional participation for deaf users. More broadly, Deaf.AI creates value by advancing inclusive communication tools that are community-led, need-based and focused on reducing structural barriers faced by deaf people.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2402,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01813,EU_PSTW_202609_01813,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,ProFamilia,Family and children.,"People seeking advice on family and parenting issues do not always know which counselling service is appropriate for their situation, and this can delay support, create frustration and increase the burden on organisations that must redirect enquiries manually.",Digitaler Erstkontakt (Digital Initial Contact) - AI for counseling services related to family and parenthood,ProFamilia; Non-governmental; Family and children.; Germany,"The solution is implemented through an automated dialogue system called guide.me – smart paths to counseling, designed for deployment as both a chat bot and a telephone bot. At a conceptual level, the system uses natural-language-processing methods to interpret user input and connect it with curated response pathways and referral logic developed entirely by human experts.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"People seeking advice on family and parenting issues do not always know which counselling service is appropriate for their situation, and this can delay support, create frustration and increase the burden on organisations that must redirect enquiries manually. Digitaler Erstkontakt addresses this problem by creating a constant first point of contact that can guide users more quickly to the counselling facility best suited to their needs. The stakeholders include people seeking advice, counselling organisations and service coordinators, with the objective of improving access, reducing misdirection and ensuring that enquiries are matched more efficiently to the right provider. The solution is implemented through an automated dialogue system called guide.me – smart paths to counseling, designed for deployment as both a chat bot and a telephone bot. At a conceptual level, the system uses natural-language-processing methods to interpret user input and connect it with curated response pathways and referral logic developed entirely by human experts. Unlike generative systems that produce new content on their own, this approach relies on controlled outputs, allowing organisations to retain a high degree of oversight over behaviour and service quality. Development, testing and evaluation are carried out through agile feedback cycles, enabling regular refinement. The expected impact is faster and more accurate referral to counselling, improved user experience and reduced administrative effort for service providers. More broadly, the project creates value by making first contact with counselling services more accessible, dependable and operationally efficient.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2403,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01814,EU_PSTW_202609_01814,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Female Vision,Education n.e.c.,"Many organisations recognise the risk, but lack the practical knowledge and internal capability required to implement diversity-sensitive solutions confidently.",EdTech for Diversity in AI,Female Vision; Non-governmental; Education n.e.c.; Germany,"The solution is implemented through an AI-supported learning platform offering low-threshold digital training packages. At a conceptual level, the project builds on the EdTech tool developed by platform3L, using adaptive digital-learning functionalities to deliver practical content on diversity-sensitive implementation and to support skills development in real workplace settings.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"As companies increasingly adopt digital applications in people-related processes such as recruitment, there is a growing need to ensure that these systems are introduced in ways that do not reproduce or reinforce discrimination. Many organisations recognise the risk, but lack the practical knowledge and internal capability required to implement diversity-sensitive solutions confidently. The DivAI project was created to address this gap by equipping companies and employees with the skills needed to introduce human-centred digital applications more responsibly. The stakeholders include employers, staff and organisations involved in workplace digitalisation, with the objective of reducing discriminatory risk, supporting fairer adoption practices and providing an accessible learning route for organisations that may not otherwise have specialist expertise in this area. The solution is implemented through an AI-supported learning platform offering low-threshold digital training packages. At a conceptual level, the project builds on the EdTech tool developed by platform3L, using adaptive digital-learning functionalities to deliver practical content on diversity-sensitive implementation and to support skills development in real workplace settings. This allows training to be embedded into everyday organisational learning rather than treated as a one-off intervention. The expected impact is better preparedness for fair digital adoption, improved awareness of discrimination risks and stronger internal capability among companies and employees. More broadly, DivAI creates value by supporting more inclusive workplace innovation and making diversity-sensitive implementation more scalable and accessible.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2404,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01815,EU_PSTW_202609_01815,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,HOGENT University of Ghent,Education n.e.c.,"HOGENT University in Ghent addresses this challenge through the 5GENIUS project, which aims to integrate private 5G networks into education and research in order to strengthen both learning and innovation.",Harnessing 5G for innovation in higher education in Belgium,HOGENT University of Ghent; Academic-Research; Education n.e.c.; Belgium,"The solution is implemented through private 5G infrastructure that provides high-speed, low-latency connectivity across educational and research environments. At a conceptual level, this enables use cases such as real-time healthcare training through smart glasses, remote guidance without transmission delays, and continuous data collection from drones and machinery in precision agriculture.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Higher-education institutions increasingly need digital infrastructures that can support advanced teaching, applied research and closer collaboration with industry, particularly in fields where real-time data exchange and connected devices are essential. HOGENT University in Ghent addresses this challenge through the 5GENIUS project, which aims to integrate private 5G networks into education and research in order to strengthen both learning and innovation. The stakeholders include students, teachers, researchers and industry partners, with the objective of familiarising them with the practical potential of 5G while improving the quality of training and experimentation in sectors such as healthcare, logistics and agriculture. The solution is implemented through private 5G infrastructure that provides high-speed, low-latency connectivity across educational and research environments. At a conceptual level, this enables use cases such as real-time healthcare training through smart glasses, remote guidance without transmission delays, and continuous data collection from drones and machinery in precision agriculture. By embedding these capabilities directly into teaching and research workflows, the university links technical experimentation with practical sectoral applications. The expected impact is richer student learning, stronger industry-academia collaboration and improved research capability in connected, data-intensive environments. More broadly, the initiative enhances institutional innovation capacity, helps prepare future professionals for emerging technologies and creates value by positioning higher education as a driver of smart, connected communities.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2405,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01816,EU_PSTW_202609_01816,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Dutch Ministry of Infrastructure and Water Management,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.","The administrative problem is that public consultations can generate large volumes of written feedback, making it difficult for authorities to process responses efficiently, identify recurring themes and close the feedback loop with citizens.",Consultation AI,"Dutch Ministry of Infrastructure and Water Management; Central-Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Netherlands","Its implementation uses AI-driven analytics to support the analysis, synthesis and reporting of consultation responses, while preserving a human decision-making role for policymakers and public officials.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"ConsultationAI was developed to improve how governments analyse and respond to public consultation input. The administrative problem is that public consultations can generate large volumes of written feedback, making it difficult for authorities to process responses efficiently, identify recurring themes and close the feedback loop with citizens. The tool was created during the OECD Co-Creation Bootcamp in Lisbon, which focused on improving civic participation with emerging technologies. Its implementation uses AI-driven analytics to support the analysis, synthesis and reporting of consultation responses, while preserving a human decision-making role for policymakers and public officials. Conceptually, the system combines text classification, clustering, summarisation, theme extraction and reporting support to help governments understand citizen input at scale. The expected impact is lower analysis costs, faster consultation reporting, better traceability of public feedback and more meaningful civic engagement. By helping authorities respond more effectively to citizens’ contributions, ConsultationAI aligns with the wider CivicAI objective of scaling participation through responsible digital public goods. The OECD bootcamp took place on 26–27 February 2025 in Lisbon under an initiative involving Portugal, Spain and the Netherlands.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,F06_HIGH_VOLUME_SCALABILITY_BOTTLENECK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2407,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01817,EU_PSTW_202609_01817,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Technical University of Munich (TUM),Public order and safety n.e.c.,"Forecasting extreme precipitation accurately is a growing operational challenge, particularly as severe weather events place increasing pressure on infrastructure, emergency planning and public safety. EKAPEx addresses this challenge by developing improved precipitation forecasts for Germany, with a particular focus on extreme weather events and the need for methods that are both effective and resource-efficient.",EKAPEx - AI for precipitation forecast,Technical University of Munich (TUM); Academic-Research; Public order and safety n.e.c.; Germany,"The solution is implemented through analytical models trained on a distinctive long-term dataset derived from the Global Navigation Satellite System, covering the past 20 years. At a conceptual level, the project uses these historical atmospheric and positioning-related signals to support the development of new forecasting algorithms capable of identifying patterns linked to precipitation intensity and extreme-weather formation.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Forecasting extreme precipitation accurately is a growing operational challenge, particularly as severe weather events place increasing pressure on infrastructure, emergency planning and public safety. Traditional forecasting approaches can be computationally demanding and may not always extract the full value from long-term observational datasets. EKAPEx addresses this challenge by developing improved precipitation forecasts for Germany, with a particular focus on extreme weather events and the need for methods that are both effective and resource-efficient. The stakeholders include meteorological researchers, forecasting specialists and organisations that rely on timely weather intelligence, with the objective of improving predictive accuracy while controlling computational demands. The solution is implemented through analytical models trained on a distinctive long-term dataset derived from the Global Navigation Satellite System, covering the past 20 years. At a conceptual level, the project uses these historical atmospheric and positioning-related signals to support the development of new forecasting algorithms capable of identifying patterns linked to precipitation intensity and extreme-weather formation. By combining long-run data depth with efficient modelling techniques, the approach seeks to improve forecast quality without relying on unnecessarily heavy computational processes. The expected impact is more accurate precipitation prediction, better anticipation of extreme events and more efficient use of forecasting resources. More broadly, EKAPEx creates value by strengthening weather preparedness, improving the usability of specialised environmental datasets and supporting more resilient responses to high-impact rainfall events.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2408,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01818,EU_PSTW_202609_01818,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,RWTH Aachen University,General public services n.e.c.,Access to information remains uneven when official or public-facing texts are difficult to understand for people with cognitive impairments or limited proficiency in German. ErLeSen addresses this challenge by developing tools that can convert complex German texts into Leichte Sprache so that more people can engage with information independently and effectively.,ErLeSen - Creation and Analysis of Easy Language through AI,RWTH Aachen University; Academic-Research; General public services n.e.c.; Germany,"The solution is implemented through open models and datasets designed to learn from pairs of complex and simplified texts. At a conceptual level, the project uses deep-learning methods based on artificial neural networks, supported by partially synthetic data generation and later refinement through reinforcement-learning techniques.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Access to information remains uneven when official or public-facing texts are difficult to understand for people with cognitive impairments or limited proficiency in German. This creates barriers to participation in education, services and everyday social life, particularly where simplified language is not readily available at scale. ErLeSen addresses this challenge by developing tools that can convert complex German texts into Leichte Sprache so that more people can engage with information independently and effectively. The stakeholders include individuals who benefit from Easy Language, accessibility advocates and organisations seeking to communicate more inclusively, with the objective of improving societal participation through more understandable written content. The solution is implemented through open models and datasets designed to learn from pairs of complex and simplified texts. At a conceptual level, the project uses deep-learning methods based on artificial neural networks, supported by partially synthetic data generation and later refinement through reinforcement-learning techniques. This allows the models to capture patterns of simplification while improving output quality over time. The emphasis on open resources also supports reuse and broader accessibility in the field. The expected impact is easier access to information, stronger inclusion for users with specific language or comprehension needs and reduced effort in producing simplified texts manually. More broadly, ErLeSen creates value by supporting accessible communication as a scalable digital capability rather than a purely manual specialist service.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2409,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01819,EU_PSTW_202609_01819,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Helpme.ai,General public services n.e.c.,"The operational problem is that relevant information is often dispersed across official websites, service providers, assistance organisations, and informal experiences, making it difficult for users to identify accurate answers in a language they understand.",Helpme.ai - assisting asylum seekers with AI,Helpme.ai; Private sector; General public services n.e.c.; Germany,"The implementation uses Large Language Models to deliver multilingual chatbot functionality, supported by advanced natural language processing and a privacy-conscious configuration.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Helpme.ai addresses the information barriers faced by refugees and asylum seekers who need reliable, understandable, and context-specific guidance when navigating administrative procedures, social services, accommodation, rights, and local support options in Germany. The operational problem is that relevant information is often dispersed across official websites, service providers, assistance organisations, and informal experiences, making it difficult for users to identify accurate answers in a language they understand. Stakeholders include asylum seekers, refugees, public administrations, social service providers, civil society organisations, and the Helpme.ai platform team. The objective is to provide a centralised, accessible support channel that can respond to user questions and guide people towards relevant resources. The implementation uses Large Language Models to deliver multilingual chatbot functionality, supported by advanced natural language processing and a privacy-conscious configuration. The system draws on an information vector database containing material from official websites, social service providers, assistance resources, and positive experiences shared by previous refugees and asylum seekers. Through retrieval-based techniques, the chatbot can search relevant content, generate contextual answers, and present information through a web interface. The expected impact includes improved access to support, reduced confusion, faster orientation, and better user experience for vulnerable groups. For service providers, the platform can reduce repeated enquiries, scale multilingual assistance, and improve the consistency of information delivered across different user situations.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,NOT_IN_USE,미사용,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2411,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01820,EU_PSTW_202609_01820,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,University of Nuremberg,General public services n.e.c.,KIA addresses this challenge by developing an assistive system for digital psychosocial counselling that helps practitioners interpret incoming requests and respond more effectively in real time.,KIA - AI-supported assistance for digital psychosocial counseling,University of Nuremberg; Academic-Research; General public services n.e.c.; Germany,The solution is implemented as an assistance system that analyses counselling enquiries as they arrive and provides targeted cues to the counsellor during the exchange.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Digital psychosocial counselling services often require counsellors to assess complex enquiries quickly while maintaining empathy, relevance and consistency in their responses. As demand grows, there is a practical need for tools that can support professionals during consultations without disrupting the human-centred nature of the interaction. KIA addresses this challenge by developing an assistive system for digital psychosocial counselling that helps practitioners interpret incoming requests and respond more effectively in real time. The stakeholders include counsellors, service providers and people seeking support, with the objective of improving the efficiency and quality of digital counselling while preserving professional judgement and relational care. The solution is implemented as an assistance system that analyses counselling enquiries as they arrive and provides targeted cues to the counsellor during the exchange. At a conceptual level, the platform combines several analytical methods to identify relevant themes, interpret user needs and generate support prompts that can help structure the consultation and surface useful response directions. These outputs are designed to augment, not replace, the counsellor’s expertise. The expected impact is more efficient handling of digital enquiries, better support for counsellors in demanding interactions and improved consistency in the consultation process. More broadly, KIA creates value by strengthening the usability and responsiveness of psychosocial counselling services in digital settings while supporting professionals with timely, context-sensitive assistance.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2412,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01821,EU_PSTW_202609_01821,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""social_nonprofit""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Novo Nordisk Foundation,General public services n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,Gefion - the new Supercomputer in Denmark,Novo Nordisk Foundation; Private sector; General public services n.e.c.; Denmark,"The implementation uses NVIDIA DGX SuperPOD infrastructure with 1,528 GPUs, creating a national platform for high-performance AI workloads.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Gefion is Denmark’s national AI supercomputer, established to strengthen the country’s capacity for advanced research and innovation across healthcare, life sciences, biotechnology, environmental sustainability and quantum computing. The strategic problem is that frontier AI research requires large-scale computing infrastructure that may otherwise be inaccessible to Danish public and private researchers. The initiative is led by the Novo Nordisk Foundation and the Export and Investment Fund of Denmark, in collaboration with NVIDIA, and is operated by the Danish Centre for AI Innovation. The implementation uses NVIDIA DGX SuperPOD infrastructure with 1,528 GPUs, creating a national platform for high-performance AI workloads. Conceptually, Gefion provides sovereign AI compute capacity, expert support and access mechanisms for Danish researchers and companies developing advanced AI applications. The expected impact is faster scientific discovery, stronger national technological capacity, more competitive AI research and better support for innovation in domains such as drug discovery, disease diagnosis, climate research and quantum technologies. The system was launched in Copenhagen in October 2024, when it was symbolically turned on by King Frederik X, NVIDIA’s CEO and DCAI’s CEO.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2413,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01822,EU_PSTW_202609_01822,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Autostrade per l'Italia,Transport,"The operational challenge is that motorway safety depends on rapidly identifying risky behaviours and conditions, including dangerous overtaking by heavy vehicles, wrong-way driving, overloaded vehicles, accidents and abnormal traffic situations.",Navigard: the future of road safety on Italian motorways,Autostrade per l'Italia; Central-Government; Transport; Italy,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Navigard is an advanced motorway safety platform developed by Autostrade per l’Italia and the Italian State Police to improve traffic monitoring, enforcement and incident detection on Italian highways. The operational challenge is that motorway safety depends on rapidly identifying risky behaviours and conditions, including dangerous overtaking by heavy vehicles, wrong-way driving, overloaded vehicles, accidents and abnormal traffic situations. The system integrates radars, high-definition cameras, sensors and centrally processed data to monitor traffic and driver behaviour in real time. Its implementation relies on complex algorithms, advanced sensing infrastructure and a central platform able to communicate with competent authorities. Conceptually, Navigard combines real-time data acquisition, automatic risk detection, vehicle monitoring, enforcement support and road-safety intelligence. The expected impact is faster identification of dangerous events, better coordination between infrastructure operators and police, improved road safety and more proactive management of motorway risks. Autostrade per l’Italia also frames Navigard within a broader “intelligent motorway” strategy involving connected roads, smart service areas, drones, infrastructure monitoring and sensor-enabled maintenance.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PLANNED,계획,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2414,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01823,EU_PSTW_202609_01823,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Lantik - Bizkaia Provincial Council,Public order and safety n.e.c.,GovTech4All Pilot 1 focused on the challenge of securely sharing sensitive public-sector data across borders while preparing digital public services for future cryptographic risks.,GovTech4All - Secure information in cross-border data spaces,Lantik - Bizkaia Provincial Council; Consortium; Public order and safety n.e.c.; Spain,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"GovTech4All Pilot 1 focused on the challenge of securely sharing sensitive public-sector data across borders while preparing digital public services for future cryptographic risks. Public administrations increasingly depend on data spaces that must remain confidential, interoperable, and resilient over time. Led by LANTIK with GRNET and DINUM, the pilot involved public authorities, infrastructure providers, and European institutions, aiming to protect shared data while preserving usability and compliance. The technical solution combined post-quantum cryptographic algorithms with fully homomorphic encryption, enabling data to be processed while remaining encrypted. An open-source framework was developed, deployable via Docker and accessible through APIs, allowing public bodies to integrate advanced encryption into existing systems without architectural disruption. Data exchanged within cross-border services could be analysed securely without exposing underlying information. The pilot was conducted between mid-2023 and mid-2024 under the Digital Europe Programme. Outcomes included improved long-term data security, reduced exposure to emerging cryptographic threats, and a reusable framework adaptable across administrations. The project strengthened trust in European data spaces while supporting scalability, interoperability, and regulatory alignment for future digital public services.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2415,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01824,EU_PSTW_202609_01824,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Turin,Hospital services.,"The operational challenge is that transplant logistics are extremely time-sensitive, while road-based transport can be affected by traffic, distance between hospital sites and coordination constraints.",INDOOR – usINg Drones fOr Organ tRansportation,City of Turin; Local Government; Hospital services.; Italy,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"INDOOR tests whether drones can improve medical logistics for the transport of organs and biological material in urban areas. The operational challenge is that transplant logistics are extremely time-sensitive, while road-based transport can be affected by traffic, distance between hospital sites and coordination constraints. In Turin, the project demonstrated a drone connection between two hospital facilities, aiming to create a replicable model for faster and safer transport of organs, blood, medicines and biological samples. The experimental flight on 1 October 2024 covered around 500 metres between the CTO helipad and the Molinette Genetics building, crossing an urban section of the city and part of the Po river. The drone, developed by ABzero, used a dedicated smart capsule able to maintain appropriate transport conditions. Its implementation combines automatic drone navigation, protected biomedical transport, capsule-based monitoring and integrated AI for continuous control of temperature, humidity and material integrity. The expected impact is shorter medical transport times, improved reliability of transplant logistics and stronger capacity to manage urgent biological deliveries across dense urban environments. In Piedmont, the potential beneficiaries include transplant patients, with 496 organ transplants reported in 2024, 449 of them in Turin.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2416,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01825,EU_PSTW_202609_01825,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Hansel,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.",Hankintavälkky was piloted by Hansel to address the difficulty faced by procurement professionals and suppliers in navigating complex public procurement guidance. The context involved increasing regulatory complexity and high information demands on a limited expert workforce.,AI assistant Hankintavälkky - Hilma Procurement Manual Assistant,"Hansel; Central-Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Finland",UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Hankintavälkky was piloted by Hansel to address the difficulty faced by procurement professionals and suppliers in navigating complex public procurement guidance. The context involved increasing regulatory complexity and high information demands on a limited expert workforce. The objective was to make procurement rules easier to access while improving consistency and efficiency for internal and external users. The pilot served public buyers, suppliers, and procurement advisors. The solution used a conversational assistant connected exclusively to the official Procurement Manual, relying on language models hosted on Microsoft Azure and integrated through a third-party platform by twoday. Responses were constrained to verified source content, ensuring reliability and traceability. The system was embedded into Hansel’s digital services and continuously refined through monitored interactions.. Results showed faster access to accurate procurement guidance, reduced manual enquiries, and improved user satisfaction. The assistant increased transparency while lowering operational burden, demonstrating scalability across procurement topics and providing a safe model for digital decision support in regulated public-sector environments.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI|S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2417,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01826,EU_PSTW_202609_01826,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Rome,Community development.,"Previously, relevant data were distributed across fragmented databases, paper records and systems that could not communicate effectively. To address this challenge, Roma Capitale developed the Atlante di Roma Capitale, an integrated digital platform described by the municipality as the city’s digital twin.",Rome's Digital Twin - Atlante di Roma Capitale,City of Rome; Local Government; Community development.; Italy,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Managing Rome’s extensive territory and public assets requires municipal departments to access consistent, accurate and interoperable information. Previously, relevant data were distributed across fragmented databases, paper records and systems that could not communicate effectively. To address this challenge, Roma Capitale developed the Atlante di Roma Capitale, an integrated digital platform described by the municipality as the city’s digital twin. Launched in July 2026 as an expansion of the existing Atlante del Patrimonio, the platform aims to improve knowledge of the urban territory, strengthen public-asset management and progressively integrate data and services from across the municipal administration. The Atlante is based on a new aerial photogrammetric survey covering the entire municipal territory and provides high-definition cartography with a resolution of approximately 5–7.5 centimetres per pixel. The platform integrates three-dimensional mesh models, Building Information Modelling navigation and tools that allow users to perform measurements directly within the digital environment. Data integrity and verifiability are supported through SHA2-256 digital hashes and a precision photogrammetric processing chain. The platform currently incorporates more than 60% of Roma Capitale’s property portfolio, which can be consulted by category, address, condition and surface area. It also integrates the Atlante del Patrimonio, the three-dimensional Schools Atlas and the Underground Utilities Atlas, although access to the latter is restricted for security reasons. Its open architecture is designed to progressively accommodate additional datasets, services and information from all municipal departments. Roma Capitale intends to complete the digitalisation of its entire property portfolio by 2027. Expected benefits include more coordinated planning and maintenance activities, improved access to municipal information, more efficient management of public assets and greater administrative transparency.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F07_DATA_VISIBILITY_INTEGRATION_GAP,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2418,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01827,EU_PSTW_202609_01827,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Stadt Bergisch Gladbach, Gemeinde Niederkruchten and others",General public services n.e.c.,"NeuraBot addresses this challenge by providing continuous, multilingual digital assistance to citizens.",NeuraBot - Chabot for German municipal citizen services,"Stadt Bergisch Gladbach, Gemeinde Niederkruchten and others; Local Government; General public services n.e.c.; Germany",NeuraBot is implemented as a conversational assistant that understands natural language inputs and retrieves answers from a structured municipal knowledge base.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Municipalities receive large volumes of routine enquiries related to administrative procedures, events, and general city information, often overwhelming staff and limiting service availability outside office hours. NeuraBot addresses this challenge by providing continuous, multilingual digital assistance to citizens. The objective is to improve accessibility, reduce response times, and allow municipal staff to focus on complex cases requiring personal interaction. NeuraBot is implemented as a conversational assistant that understands natural language inputs and retrieves answers from a structured municipal knowledge base. It operates 24/7 and supports 95 languages, making services accessible to diverse populations. The system integrates with city websites and information systems, ensuring responses are accurate and up to date. Unresolved or complex enquiries are escalated to human staff through defined workflows. The impact includes faster access to information, reduced waiting times, and lower operational workload for municipalities. NeuraBot enhances user experience, improves service reliability, and provides a scalable, cost-effective solution for modern citizen services, supporting inclusive and efficient digital governance.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2419,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01828,EU_PSTW_202609_01828,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Freiburg,General public services n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,Neuropolis - Information System rethought in the City of Freiburg,City of Freiburg; Local Government; General public services n.e.c.; Germany,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Atlante is based on a new aerial photogrammetric survey covering the entire municipal territory and provides high-definition cartography with a resolution of approximately 5–7.5 centimetres per pixel. The platform integrates three-dimensional mesh models, Building Information Modelling navigation and tools that allow users to perform measurements directly within the digital environment. Data integrity and verifiability are supported through SHA2-256 digital hashes and a precision photogrammetric processing chain.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2420,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01829,EU_PSTW_202609_01829,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Tartu,Transport,"The autonomous shuttle pilot in Tartu addressed the challenge of making emerging automated transport systems safer, more informative, and better integrated into urban mobility networks.",AI System for Autonomous Shuttle in the City of Tartu,City of Tartu; Local Government; Transport; Estonia,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The autonomous shuttle pilot in Tartu addressed the challenge of making emerging automated transport systems safer, more informative, and better integrated into urban mobility networks. The City of Tartu, technology providers, and public transport stakeholders collaborated to test enhancements on a shuttle route between the City Museum and the Estonian National Museum. The objective was to improve passenger safety, service usability, and operational insight during real-world operations. The solution combined on-board and roadside systems, including computer vision for passenger posture detection, sensor fusion for vulnerable road user awareness, and multilingual information services delivered locally. Real-time data from roadside sensors supported collision avoidance, while origin–destination data enabled travel pattern analysis. All components were integrated into the shuttle platform with privacy safeguards and local data processing. Results included improved safety monitoring, richer passenger information, and actionable operational metrics for city planners. The system demonstrated feasibility for wider deployment, supporting safer, more user-friendly automated transport within smart city environments.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2421,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01830,EU_PSTW_202609_01830,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"City of Afragola, City of Bari and others",Transport,UNKNOWN,AI-Traffic solutions used in multiple Italian public administrations to monitor traffic,"City of Afragola, City of Bari and others; Local Government; Transport; Italy",UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The platform currently incorporates more than 60% of Roma Capitale’s property portfolio, which can be consulted by category, address, condition and surface area. It also integrates the Atlante del Patrimonio, the three-dimensional Schools Atlas and the Underground Utilities Atlas, although access to the latter is restricted for security reasons. Its open architecture is designed to progressively accommodate additional datasets, services and information from all municipal departments. Roma Capitale intends to complete the digitalisation of its entire property portfolio by 2027. Expected benefits include more coordinated planning and maintenance activities, improved access to municipal information, more efficient management of public assets and greater administrative transparency.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2422,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01831,EU_PSTW_202609_01831,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Instanbul,Transport,"AI4Life in Istanbul addressed congestion, emissions, and safety risks at urban intersections, where inefficient traffic flow contributes significantly to economic and environmental costs. Models continuously learned from traffic patterns to optimise throughput and minimise delays.",AI4Life - AI-based smart intersection management system in Instanbul,City of Instanbul; Local Government; Transport; Türkiye,"The implemented system analysed live traffic data from cameras and sensors to dynamically adjust signal timing, reduce stop-and-go conditions, and prioritise flow where congestion was highest.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"AI4Life in Istanbul addressed congestion, emissions, and safety risks at urban intersections, where inefficient traffic flow contributes significantly to economic and environmental costs. The city of Istanbul and its municipal company ISBAK aimed to optimise intersection management to improve quality of life and reduce emissions. The pilot focused on adaptive traffic control across selected junctions. The implemented system analysed live traffic data from cameras and sensors to dynamically adjust signal timing, reduce stop-and-go conditions, and prioritise flow where congestion was highest. Models continuously learned from traffic patterns to optimise throughput and minimise delays. The solution was integrated into the city’s traffic management infrastructure without disrupting existing operations. Results showed reduced congestion, shorter travel times, and lower greenhouse gas emissions at monitored intersections, reclaiming hours previously lost in traffic. The system proved scalable across urban areas and delivered measurable environmental and operational benefits for large metropolitan authorities.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2423,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01832,EU_PSTW_202609_01832,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,CCG/ZGDV - ICT INNOVATION INSTITUTE,Transport,UNKNOWN,"BeNeutral- AI-based software solution for ""in-the-city"" multimodal route planning with ecological concerns",CCG/ZGDV - ICT INNOVATION INSTITUTE; Academic-Research; Transport; Portugal,"The implementation is creating an analytical engine that recommends multimodal routes using public and shared transport, optimising not only travel time and distance but also avoided CO₂ emissions.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Urban mobility planning tools traditionally optimise for speed or distance, often overlooking environmental impacts. Cities aiming to reduce emissions need decision-support systems that embed sustainability into everyday travel choices. The objective of BeNeutral is to develop a route-planning solution that prioritises ecological outcomes alongside efficiency. The implementation is creating an analytical engine that recommends multimodal routes using public and shared transport, optimising not only travel time and distance but also avoided CO₂ emissions. The solution is designed to integrate with existing route planners and city platforms, enabling additional features such as incentive schemes for sustainable travel choices. As the system is under development, testing focuses on accuracy of emission estimates, usability and integration potential. Expected impacts include increased adoption of low-emission mobility options, better-informed citizens and support for municipal sustainability goals. Qualitative benefits include behavioural change towards greener travel and scalable integration into smart city ecosystems.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2424,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01833,EU_PSTW_202609_01833,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,CIRCLECHAIN,Waste management.,"The operational problem is that actors involved in waste treatment and material recovery often lack trusted, shared and detailed information on the quality, nature and handling requirements of reusable products and materials.",CIRCLECHAIN - digital platform for the Circular Economy,CIRCLECHAIN; Consortium; Waste management.; France,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"CIRCLECHAIN provides a digital platform for circular-economy processes, with a particular focus on waste disposal sites, construction sites, material traceability and reuse. The operational problem is that actors involved in waste treatment and material recovery often lack trusted, shared and detailed information on the quality, nature and handling requirements of reusable products and materials. This can limit reuse rates, complicate regulatory reporting and reduce confidence among stakeholders. The platform is relevant to waste operators, construction companies, circular-economy actors, regulators, reporting bodies and organisations seeking to measure environmental impact. Its implementation is powered by a consortium blockchain and integrates tools for waste and construction sites. The system supports end-to-end traceability, information sharing on reusable materials, optimisation of waste management, carbon-footprint assessment for treatment and recovery processes, and automated regulatory declarations. Conceptually, CIRCLECHAIN combines distributed ledger technology, data-sharing workflows, material-quality records, carbon accounting and compliance reporting. The expected impact is improved trust between actors, better handling and treatment of reusable materials, higher reuse rates and more efficient regulatory processes. By providing secure and verifiable information, the blockchain layer supports collaboration across organisations that may not otherwise share data easily. The platform’s business value lies in improving circular resource management while strengthening transparency and environmental accountability.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2426,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01834,EU_PSTW_202609_01834,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of České Budějovice,Transport,"The traffic monitoring pilot in České Budějovice responded to growing congestion and limited visibility over real-time mobility patterns. The system reduced manual monitoring efforts, enhanced reliability of traffic data, and demonstrated scalability for medium-sized cities seeking cost-effective intelligent mobility management.",Video-based Intelligent solution for traffic monitoring in the Municipality of České Budějovice,Municipality of České Budějovice; Local Government; Transport; Czechia,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The traffic monitoring pilot in České Budějovice responded to growing congestion and limited visibility over real-time mobility patterns. City authorities sought better tools to manage traffic efficiently while supporting economic activity and public safety. The project involved municipal stakeholders, technology providers, and system integrators. Over 100 strategically placed cameras fed data into a central analytics platform capable of vehicle counting, classification, and congestion detection. Video streams were processed server-side to identify traffic jams, stopped vehicles, and intersection performance. The solution was integrated with existing traffic control systems to support operational decision-making. The pilot ran from 2023 to 2024. Outcomes included improved situational awareness, faster response to congestion, and data-driven planning for infrastructure improvements. The system reduced manual monitoring efforts, enhanced reliability of traffic data, and demonstrated scalability for medium-sized cities seeking cost-effective intelligent mobility management.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2429,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01835,EU_PSTW_202609_01835,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"i.AI - Department for Science, Innovation, and Technology","Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.","Public consultations often generate thousands of written responses, making manual analysis slow, costly, and prone to inconsistency. Consult addresses this challenge by streamlining the analysis of consultation data.",Consult - AI for public engagement (part of the Humphrey toolkit),"i.AI - Department for Science, Innovation, and Technology; Central-Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; United Kingdom","The solution is implemented as a digital tool that processes large volumes of free-text responses to identify themes, patterns, and emerging topics. It uses a two-step approach: first identifying meaningful themes and then mapping them back to individual responses, allowing human analysts to retain control and interpret results.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Public consultations often generate thousands of written responses, making manual analysis slow, costly, and prone to inconsistency. UK policymakers need timely and comprehensive insights to inform decision-making while maintaining transparency and accountability. Consult addresses this challenge by streamlining the analysis of consultation data. The solution is implemented as a digital tool that processes large volumes of free-text responses to identify themes, patterns, and emerging topics. It uses a two-step approach: first identifying meaningful themes and then mapping them back to individual responses, allowing human analysts to retain control and interpret results. The system integrates into policy analysis workflows and produces structured insights that can be reviewed and validated. The impact includes significant reductions in analysis time, improved consistency, and deeper understanding of public sentiment. Consult enables evidence-based policymaking at scale, improves use of public input, and delivers efficiency gains while preserving human oversight and trust in democratic processes.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2432,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01836,EU_PSTW_202609_01836,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"i.AI - Department for Science, Innovation, and Technology","Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.","Parlex was piloted to help policy teams anticipate parliamentary responses to proposed legislation, addressing the challenge of navigating vast volumes of historical debate and political positioning. Results included faster insight generation, improved strategic planning, and reduced manual research time.",Parlex - Forecasting Parliamentary Reactions (part of the Humphrey toolkit),"i.AI - Department for Science, Innovation, and Technology; Central-Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; United Kingdom","Using structured text analysis and large-scale language models, it generated forecasts and summaries showing likely reactions and influential voices.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Parlex was piloted to help policy teams anticipate parliamentary responses to proposed legislation, addressing the challenge of navigating vast volumes of historical debate and political positioning. Stakeholders included civil servants, analysts, and legislative strategists seeking early insight into potential support or opposition. The objective was to improve policy readiness and parliamentary handling strategies. The system analysed years of debate records from MPs and Peers, extracting sentiment, themes, and historical positions related to policy topics. Using structured text analysis and large-scale language models, it generated forecasts and summaries showing likely reactions and influential voices. The tool integrated with policy workflows through a secure interface. The pilot ran from mid-2023 to early-2024. Results included faster insight generation, improved strategic planning, and reduced manual research time. Parlex enhanced decision quality while maintaining transparency and analyst control, demonstrating value as a scalable decision-support tool within government policy development.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2433,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01837,EU_PSTW_202609_01837,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"i.AI - Department for Science, Innovation, and Technology","Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.","Lex addressed the difficulty non-specialists face when researching complex legislation dispersed across statutes, amendments, and case law.",Lex - Simplifying Legislative Research (part of the Humphrey toolkit),"i.AI - Department for Science, Innovation, and Technology; Central-Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; United Kingdom",UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Lex addressed the difficulty non-specialists face when researching complex legislation dispersed across statutes, amendments, and case law. The pilot targeted policymakers, legal advisers, and public servants needing fast, reliable legal insight. The objective was to simplify access to legislation without requiring expert legal search skills. Lex indexed over 1.5 million legislative sections and 63,000 cases within a vector database, combining retrieval, summarisation, and reasoning tools to answer natural language queries. An orchestration layer selected the most appropriate analytical tools to generate structured, source-cited explanations. The system was delivered through the Lexplorer interface and integrated into policy research workflows. Results showed significant reductions in research time, improved accessibility of legal information, and higher confidence in legislative interpretation. Lex demonstrated strong scalability and reliability for complex legal domains, supporting better-informed policymaking.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2434,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01838,EU_PSTW_202609_01838,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"i.AI - Department for Science, Innovation, and Technology","Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.","Minute was piloted to reduce the administrative burden of documenting meetings across public services, where manual note-taking and transcription consume significant staff time.",Minute - AI transcription service (part of the Humphrey toolkit),"i.AI - Department for Science, Innovation, and Technology; Central-Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; United Kingdom",UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Minute was piloted to reduce the administrative burden of documenting meetings across public services, where manual note-taking and transcription consume significant staff time. Public servants and administrative teams were the primary users, seeking accurate and secure summaries. The service converted audio recordings into transcripts and tailored summaries aligned with user needs, integrating securely with government systems. Speech recognition and summarisation models were applied within a controlled environment, ensuring data protection and confidentiality. Users could customise outputs for different meeting types. Results included faster turnaround from meetings to documented actions, improved accuracy, and time savings for staff. Minute proved scalable across departments and reliable for sensitive contexts, delivering clear productivity and quality gains.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2435,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01839,EU_PSTW_202609_01839,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"i.AI - Department for Science, Innovation, and Technology","Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.",UNKNOWN,Caddy - AI powered tool for customer service functions (part of the Humphrey toolkit),"i.AI - Department for Science, Innovation, and Technology; Central-Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; United Kingdom",UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Caddy was piloted to support frontline customer service agents handling complex citizen enquiries under time pressure. Developed with Citizens Advice organisations, the objective was to improve response quality while reducing cognitive load on advisers. The system acted as a secure copilot, analysing queries and suggesting structured guidance drawn from trusted knowledge bases. Language models and retrieval techniques ensured responses were accurate, auditable, and aligned with organisational standards. Caddy integrated into existing case management systems without replacing human judgement. The pilot ran from mid-2023 to mid-2024. Results showed faster response times, improved advice consistency, and higher agent confidence. The tool enhanced service quality while maintaining accountability, demonstrating clear business value and scalability for public-facing advisory services.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2436,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01840,EU_PSTW_202609_01840,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"i.AI - Department for Science, Innovation, and Technology and Infrastructure and Projects Authority (IPA)","Mining, manufacturing and construction.","Review teams within the Infrastructure and Projects Authority face challenges in ensuring consistency, identifying recurring risks and retaining institutional knowledge.",Scout - AI-powered tool to improve government major project delivery,"i.AI - Department for Science, Innovation, and Technology and Infrastructure and Projects Authority (IPA); Central-Government; Mining, manufacturing and construction.; United Kingdom","Two complementary modules support reviewers: one assesses compliance with official review guidelines using traceable citations, while another identifies cross-project patterns and potential risks.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Oversight of major government projects in the United Kingdom requires the analysis of extensive documentation, often under time pressure and with high financial stakes. Review teams within the Infrastructure and Projects Authority face challenges in ensuring consistency, identifying recurring risks and retaining institutional knowledge. The objective of this pilot is to significantly improve the speed and quality of project reviews. The implementation introduces a document analysis system that processes large volumes of review material, extracts structured insights and stores them in a searchable vector-based repository. Two complementary modules support reviewers: one assesses compliance with official review guidelines using traceable citations, while another identifies cross-project patterns and potential risks. The system integrates into existing review workflows and relies exclusively on authoritative guidance. The pilot reduced review preparation time from several days to minutes, improved consistency across assessments and enhanced knowledge reuse. Qualitative benefits include better risk anticipation, more robust decision-making and improved working conditions for reviewers, supporting future scaling across government assurance functions.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2437,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01841,EU_PSTW_202609_01841,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"i.AI - Department for Science, Innovation, and Technology",Fuel and energy,"The transition to renewable energy is constrained by delays in connecting new projects to the electricity grid, creating long queues and uncertainty for investors. In the UK, grid operators require better tools to allocate limited capacity more effectively.",Connect - AI to match capacity to demand in the Grid connections queue,"i.AI - Department for Science, Innovation, and Technology; Central-Government; Fuel and energy; United Kingdom",UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The transition to renewable energy is constrained by delays in connecting new projects to the electricity grid, creating long queues and uncertainty for investors. In the UK, grid operators require better tools to allocate limited capacity more effectively. The objective of Connect is to optimise the matching of available grid capacity with demand from connection applicants. The implementation is developing advanced matching and optimisation algorithms that analyse grid constraints, project readiness and demand characteristics. These tools aim to support planners in prioritising connections that deliver the greatest system and policy benefits. As the project is in development, evaluation focuses on decision-support quality rather than automated execution. Expected impacts include reduced connection delays, improved investment confidence and accelerated deployment of renewable energy. Qualitative outcomes include more transparent planning, better resource utilisation and a scalable approach to managing infrastructure constraints.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2438,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01842,EU_PSTW_202609_01842,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,North Jutland Region,Public health services.,UNKNOWN,AI to analyse X-ray images for bone fractures and joint injuries in the healthcare system,North Jutland Region; Regional Government; Public health services.; Denmark,The solution is currently deployed in pilot settings and is planned for gradual national rollout.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Emergency departments face increasing pressure from high patient volumes, particularly during out-of-hours periods, which can delay diagnosis and treatment. In Denmark, healthcare providers seek tools to support clinicians in analysing injury-related X-rays. The objective of this initiative is to improve diagnostic support for fractures and joint injuries while reducing waiting times. The implementation involves developing systems that analyse X-ray images and identify the likelihood of fractures with high accuracy, supporting clinical decision-making. The solution is currently deployed in pilot settings and is planned for gradual national rollout. Expected impacts include faster triage, reduced workload for staff and improved patient flow. Qualitative benefits include enhanced clinical confidence, improved service resilience and more equitable access to timely care.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2439,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01843,EU_PSTW_202609_01843,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Madrid,Cultural services.,VisitMadridGPT addresses both challenges by enhancing digital tourism services.,VisitMadridGPT - Transforming tourism in Madrid,City of Madrid; Local Government; Cultural services.; Spain,"The solution is implemented as a virtual assistant accessible online, providing real-time information and personalised recommendations in over 95 languages.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Tourists visiting Madrid require timely, accurate, and personalised information to navigate attractions, transport, and services, while the city seeks better insight into visitor needs. VisitMadridGPT addresses both challenges by enhancing digital tourism services. The objective is to improve visitor experience, accessibility, and data-driven tourism management. The solution is implemented as a virtual assistant accessible online, providing real-time information and personalised recommendations in over 95 languages. It integrates official tourism data, city services, and content management systems, while capturing anonymised interaction data for analysis by city officials. The impact is reflected in strong user engagement, with an average session duration of four minutes and 18 seconds and a low bounce rate. The assistant improves accessibility for international visitors, reduces pressure on traditional information channels, and provides valuable insights into tourism behaviour. VisitMadridGPT delivers measurable improvements in user experience, supports smarter tourism planning, and strengthens Madrid’s position as an innovative, visitor-friendly destination.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2442,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01844,EU_PSTW_202609_01844,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Barcelona Supercomputing Center-Centro Nacional de Supercomputación (BSC-CNS),R&D economic affairs,UNKNOWN,ALIA - European Supercomputing Public Infrastructure,Barcelona Supercomputing Center-Centro Nacional de Supercomputación (BSC-CNS); Academic-Research; R&D economic affairs; Spain,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"ALIA is a pioneering initiative in the European Union that seeks to provide a public infrastructure of AI resources, such as open and transparent language models, to boost the promotion of Spanish and co-official languages ​​- Catalan and Valencian, Basque and Galician - in the development and deployment of AI in the world. This is the first European public infrastructure, open and multilingual, which, thanks to the unique supercomputing capabilities of the Barcelona Supercomputing Center, reinforces the technological sovereignty of Spain and Europe in the development of transparent, responsible AI at the service of people. This project is coordinated by the Barcelona Supercomputing Center-Centro Nacional de Supercomputación (BSC-CNS), with the support and leadership of the Secretary of State for Digitalisation and Artificial Intelligence. It is also part of the Artificial Intelligence Strategy 2024 initiated by the Spanish Government",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2446,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01845,EU_PSTW_202609_01845,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,AI-Code,Broadcasting and publishing services.,The rapid proliferation of manipulated and synthetic media has increased the difficulty for journalists to verify content and maintain public trust in news reporting.,AI-Code - GenAi against disinformation,AI-Code; Consortium; Broadcasting and publishing services.; European Union,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The rapid proliferation of manipulated and synthetic media has increased the difficulty for journalists to verify content and maintain public trust in news reporting. Media organisations and fact-checking networks require tools that can operate at the speed of modern information flows while remaining transparent and reliable. The objective of this pilot is to support trustworthy journalism by enhancing the detection and assessment of disinformation across digital channels. The implementation develops a suite of interoperable services integrated into newsroom workflows, enabling the analysis of text, images and audiovisual material for credibility, provenance and signs of manipulation. These services draw on media archives, social platforms and verification datasets, and are evaluated against existing market solutions. The pilot is expected to improve verification speed, detection accuracy and transparency in content handling. Qualitative impacts include stronger editorial confidence, improved collaboration between journalists and fact-checkers, and increased audience trust. The project also contributes shared knowledge to the wider research and media communities, supporting responsible innovation in the fight against disinformation.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2447,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01846,EU_PSTW_202609_01846,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Teaching Institute of Public Health “Dr. Andrija Štampar”,Pollution abatement.,UNKNOWN,Tackling Air Pollution in the City of Zagreb,Teaching Institute of Public Health “Dr. Andrija Štampar”; Academic-Research; Pollution abatement.; Croatia,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Air quality management in large urban areas requires accurate, granular and timely information to protect public health and guide environmental policy. In Zagreb, authorities and health institutions faced limitations in understanding pollution dynamics across the city. The objective of this pilot is to provide a comprehensive, real-time overview of air quality conditions and predictive insights to support decision-making. The implementation deploys a network of environmental sensors collecting data on key pollutants and meteorological factors, combined with modelling techniques that assess exposure levels and forecast trends. Results are presented through analytical tools and public dashboards accessible to policymakers and citizens. The pilot enabled more precise identification of pollution hotspots, improved forecasting capabilities and greater transparency of environmental data. Qualitative benefits include better-informed urban planning, increased public awareness and stronger institutional capacity to respond to environmental risks. The initiative demonstrates how continuous monitoring and predictive analysis can contribute to healthier and more sustainable urban environments.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2450,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01847,EU_PSTW_202609_01847,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Croatian Academic Research Network (CARNET),Pollution abatement.,"Learning environments are directly influenced by indoor air quality, yet many schools lack the tools to monitor and manage classroom conditions effectively.",Transforming Croatian Schools with Indoor Air Quality Monitoring,Croatian Academic Research Network (CARNET); Academic-Research; Pollution abatement.; Croatia,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Learning environments are directly influenced by indoor air quality, yet many schools lack the tools to monitor and manage classroom conditions effectively. In Croatia, education authorities and school administrators sought to improve student wellbeing and learning outcomes through better environmental control. The objective of this pilot is to enhance indoor air quality across 400 schools nationwide. The implementation involves installing sensors that continuously measure parameters such as carbon dioxide levels, temperature and humidity, with real-time data transmitted to a centralised monitoring dashboard. Visual indicators and alerts enable staff to take timely actions, such as ventilating classrooms or adjusting heating systems. The pilot led to measurable improvements in classroom conditions and increased awareness among teachers and administrators. Qualitative outcomes include healthier learning environments, improved concentration for students and a stronger evidence base for school facility management. The project provides a scalable model for smart education infrastructure and supports broader digital transformation in the education sector.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2451,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01848,EU_PSTW_202609_01848,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Electric Power Company of Serbia (EPS),Fuel and energy,"Traditional forecasting methods limited the company’s ability to anticipate demand peaks accurately, leading to inefficiencies in resource allocation and higher operational costs.",AI-augmented system for predicting electricity consumption in Serbia,Electric Power Company of Serbia (EPS); Central-Government; Fuel and energy; Serbia,"This use case involves EPS grid operators, planners, and energy market stakeholders, with the objective of improving demand forecasting to support reliable supply, optimise grid operations, and reduce waste. The solution is implemented through an advanced forecasting system that combines historical consumption data, real-time grid measurements, weather information, and socio-economic indicators.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Electric Power Company of Serbia (EPS) is responsible for balancing electricity generation, distribution, and consumption across a national grid subject to fluctuating demand, seasonal variation, and increasing sustainability requirements. Traditional forecasting methods limited the company’s ability to anticipate demand peaks accurately, leading to inefficiencies in resource allocation and higher operational costs. This use case involves EPS grid operators, planners, and energy market stakeholders, with the objective of improving demand forecasting to support reliable supply, optimise grid operations, and reduce waste. The solution is implemented through an advanced forecasting system that combines historical consumption data, real-time grid measurements, weather information, and socio-economic indicators. Machine-learning models analyse these datasets to generate short- and medium-term demand predictions that are continuously updated as new data becomes available. The system is integrated into EPS operational platforms to support planning, dispatch, and decision-making. The impact includes more accurate demand forecasts, improved grid stability, and better optimisation of generation assets. EPS benefits from reduced operational risk, improved energy efficiency, and stronger support for sustainability objectives, contributing to smarter and more resilient national energy management.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION|S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2452,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01849,EU_PSTW_202609_01849,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Rigas Tehniska Universitate,Fuel and energy,UNKNOWN,exPEDite - Enabling Positive Energy Districts through Digital Twins,Rigas Tehniska Universitate; Consortium; Fuel and energy; Latvia,"Achieving climate neutrality at district level requires coordinated management of energy production, consumption and mobility, which is difficult using fragmented planning tools.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Achieving climate neutrality at district level requires coordinated management of energy production, consumption and mobility, which is difficult using fragmented planning tools. City planners and energy stakeholders need integrated solutions to manage complex urban systems. The objective of this pilot is to support the creation of Positive Energy Districts that produce more energy than they consume. The implementation develops digital twins of urban districts that combine real-time and historical data on buildings, energy networks and transport flows. Simulation and optimisation tools allow stakeholders to test scenarios, optimise energy balance and engage citizens through visualisation and gamification features. The pilot demonstrated improved energy efficiency, reduced emissions and enhanced planning capabilities. Qualitative impacts include better cross-sector collaboration, increased public engagement and transferable guidelines for replication in other cities. The project illustrates the value of digital twins in supporting sustainable urban transformation across Europe.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2454,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01850,EU_PSTW_202609_01850,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Government of Portugal,General public services n.e.c.,"Without such coordination, blockchain adoption can remain fragmented, limiting scale, reuse and broader economic impact. Blockchain.PT addresses this challenge by bringing together a large consortium of partners to create the foundations for a national ecosystem that supports both technological development and market-oriented application.",Blockchain.PT - Decentralize Portugal with Blockchain,Government of Portugal; Central-Government; General public services n.e.c.; Portugal,"The solution is implemented as a collaborative national initiative led by VOID Software and involving 56 partners, including INESC-ID, funded through the Recovery and Resilience Plan and the European Union’s Next Generation EU framework.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Portugal aims to strengthen its position in emerging digital infrastructures by building a coordinated national blockchain ecosystem capable of supporting innovation, interoperability and new business opportunities across multiple sectors. Without such coordination, blockchain adoption can remain fragmented, limiting scale, reuse and broader economic impact. Blockchain.PT addresses this challenge by bringing together a large consortium of partners to create the foundations for a national ecosystem that supports both technological development and market-oriented application. The stakeholders include technology firms, research institutions, sectoral organisations and public and private actors involved in areas such as agriculture, digital asset management and interoperability, with the objective of positioning Portugal at the forefront of blockchain development in Europe. The solution is implemented as a collaborative national initiative led by VOID Software and involving 56 partners, including INESC-ID, funded through the Recovery and Resilience Plan and the European Union’s Next Generation EU framework. At a conceptual level, the project supports ecosystem building, experimentation and cross-sector development of blockchain-enabled solutions, creating shared momentum across research, deployment and business uptake. This model is intended to reduce fragmentation and accelerate capability-building at national level. The expected impact is stronger innovation capacity, improved cross-sector collaboration and greater readiness to capture global business opportunities linked to blockchain. More broadly, Blockchain.PT creates strategic value by supporting technological leadership, ecosystem coherence and long-term digital competitiveness.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2456,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01851,EU_PSTW_202609_01851,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Leuven,General services.,"Urban last-mile delivery places growing pressure on cities, especially where mail, goods movement and mobility services compete for road space and where fragmented operations reduce efficiency.",Smart City Logistics – Automating Last-Mile Delivery in Leuven,City of Leuven; Local Government; General services.; Belgium,"This use case addresses the challenge by exploring how delivery processes can be automated within a smart-city framework so that logistics and mobility operate more smoothly in dense urban environments. The solution is implemented through automation of delivery processes as part of the EU-funded TOKEN project, using a public-private partnership model that connects government actors, the city and a business operator.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Urban last-mile delivery places growing pressure on cities, especially where mail, goods movement and mobility services compete for road space and where fragmented operations reduce efficiency. This use case addresses the challenge by exploring how delivery processes can be automated within a smart-city framework so that logistics and mobility operate more smoothly in dense urban environments. The stakeholders include public authorities, city administrations and business operators working in partnership, with the objective of improving delivery efficiency, supporting better urban mobility and creating a more coordinated model for last-mile operations. The solution is implemented through automation of delivery processes as part of the EU-funded TOKEN project, using a public-private partnership model that connects government actors, the city and a business operator. At a conceptual level, the approach combines digital coordination, automated logistics processes and smart-city service integration to streamline the movement of goods in the final stage of delivery. This allows last-mile operations to be linked more closely with wider urban mobility systems instead of being treated as a separate logistical problem. The expected impact is more efficient urban delivery, smoother interaction between logistics and city transport systems and stronger operational coordination among partners. More broadly, the initiative creates value by demonstrating how automation can support smarter city logistics, reduce friction in urban deliveries and contribute to more effective mobility management in connected city environments.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2457,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01852,EU_PSTW_202609_01852,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Austrian Public Employment Service (AMS),Economic affairs n.e.c,"Job seekers often struggle to navigate complex information on occupations, training pathways, and reskilling opportunities, particularly in a rapidly changing labour market.",AMS Berufs Infomat - personalized career advice for job seekers,Austrian Public Employment Service (AMS); Central-Government; Economic affairs n.e.c; Austria,"The use case involves job seekers, students, career advisors, and AMS staff, with the objective of improving access to labour-market information and supporting lifelong learning. The solution is implemented as an interactive digital assistant that responds dynamically to user questions about professions, qualifications, and further education opportunities.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Job seekers often struggle to navigate complex information on occupations, training pathways, and reskilling opportunities, particularly in a rapidly changing labour market. Austria’s Public Employment Service (AMS) developed Berufs-Infomat to provide accessible, personalised career information to support informed decisions. The use case involves job seekers, students, career advisors, and AMS staff, with the objective of improving access to labour-market information and supporting lifelong learning. The solution is implemented as an interactive digital assistant that responds dynamically to user questions about professions, qualifications, and further education opportunities. It draws on structured labour-market data, occupational profiles, and training catalogues to generate tailored responses aligned with user interests and backgrounds. Integrated into AMS digital services, it complements human counselling rather than replacing it. The impact includes faster access to relevant career information, improved user engagement, and better alignment between individual aspirations and labour-market needs. AMS benefits from more scalable service delivery, while users gain clearer guidance and increased confidence in career planning, supporting employability and workforce adaptability.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2459,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01853,EU_PSTW_202609_01853,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,University of Helsinki,R&D economic affairs,"Many organisations struggle with fragmented information and inefficient knowledge sharing, leading to duplicated work and reduced productivity among knowledge workers. The objective of the pilot is to reduce information bottlenecks and enhance organisational efficiency.",Generative AI-Enhanced Knowledge Management in Business (GAIK),University of Helsinki; Consortium; R&D economic affairs; Finland,The implementation delivers a modular toolkit that enables organisations to deploy intelligent knowledge services using their own business data.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Many organisations struggle with fragmented information and inefficient knowledge sharing, leading to duplicated work and reduced productivity among knowledge workers. Finnish companies participating in this pilot sought practical solutions to improve access to internal knowledge assets. The objective of the pilot is to reduce information bottlenecks and enhance organisational efficiency. The implementation delivers a modular toolkit that enables organisations to deploy intelligent knowledge services using their own business data. Reusable components, templates and process models support rapid integration into existing systems, enabling functions such as contextual search, summarisation and knowledge reuse. Early results indicate reduced time spent searching for information, improved decision-making and higher employee satisfaction. Qualitative benefits include better onboarding, stronger knowledge retention and improved customer-facing processes. The project demonstrates how flexible, reusable solutions can support scalable knowledge management across different organisational contexts.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2460,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01854,EU_PSTW_202609_01854,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,The Cyprus Institute,Education n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,GENAI4ED - A Platform for Assessing and Bridging Generative AI and Human Skills in Secondary Education,The Cyprus Institute; Consortium; Education n.e.c.; Cyprus,"The pilot improves alignment between technology adoption and educational objectives, supporting more informed procurement and classroom use.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Secondary schools face growing pressure to adopt digital tools that enhance learning while supporting teachers rather than increasing their workload. Educators and policymakers require evidence-based guidance to select appropriate educational technologies. The objective of this pilot is to assess and recommend tools that best complement teaching practices and student needs. The implementation provides a digital evaluation platform that reviews widely used educational software against pedagogical, usability and skills-related criteria. Pilot schools involve both teachers and students in structured evaluations, generating comparative insights. The pilot improves alignment between technology adoption and educational objectives, supporting more informed procurement and classroom use. Qualitative outcomes include improved teacher confidence, better student engagement and enhanced learning experiences. The project contributes practical frameworks for responsible integration of advanced digital tools in education.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2461,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01855,EU_PSTW_202609_01855,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Government of Cyprus,General public services n.e.c.,Citizens interacting with public administration often face fragmented information and limited service availability outside office hours.,Generative-AI based chatbot for the Cypriot Government Portal,Government of Cyprus; Central-Government; General public services n.e.c.; Cyprus,"This use case involves government departments, citizens, and businesses, with the objective of enabling faster, clearer, and more immediate communication with the state on a 24/7 basis. The solution is implemented as a generative digital assistant embedded within the government portal, capable of responding to a wide range of citizen enquiries in natural language.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Citizens interacting with public administration often face fragmented information and limited service availability outside office hours. The Cypriot Government sought to improve digital access to public information through its central portal, gov.cy. This use case involves government departments, citizens, and businesses, with the objective of enabling faster, clearer, and more immediate communication with the state on a 24/7 basis. The solution is implemented as a generative digital assistant embedded within the government portal, capable of responding to a wide range of citizen enquiries in natural language. It draws on official government content, structured service information, and continuously updated policy data to provide accurate and consistent answers. The assistant is integrated with the portal’s information architecture and designed to scale as new services are added. The impact includes improved accessibility to government information, reduced pressure on call centres, and higher user satisfaction. Citizens benefit from timely responses and simplified navigation of public services, while the administration gains efficiency and insight into user needs, supporting a more responsive and citizen-centric digital government.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2462,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01856,EU_PSTW_202609_01856,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,European Commission,General public services n.e.c.,"European businesses and public organisations increasingly require access to advanced computing, data, and expertise to develop and deploy competitive digital solutions, yet such resources are often fragmented or inaccessible.",European AI Factories for businesses,European Commission; European Institution/Agency; General public services n.e.c.; European Union,"The use case involves supercomputing centres, universities, SMEs, large industry, financial actors, and public authorities, with the objective of accelerating innovation and adoption across strategic sectors. AI Factories are implemented as ecosystems that combine high-performance computing infrastructure, shared datasets, research expertise, and collaborative environments.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"European businesses and public organisations increasingly require access to advanced computing, data, and expertise to develop and deploy competitive digital solutions, yet such resources are often fragmented or inaccessible. The European AI Factories initiative addresses this gap by creating interconnected innovation hubs across Europe. The use case involves supercomputing centres, universities, SMEs, large industry, financial actors, and public authorities, with the objective of accelerating innovation and adoption across strategic sectors. AI Factories are implemented as ecosystems that combine high-performance computing infrastructure, shared datasets, research expertise, and collaborative environments. They support the development, testing, and scaling of advanced digital models and applications across domains such as health, manufacturing, climate, finance, and space. The network includes national initiatives such as LUMI AIF in Finland, Meluxina-AI in Luxembourg, and IT4LIA in Italy, among others. The impact is increased innovation capacity, reduced barriers to entry for SMEs, and stronger cross-border collaboration. AI Factories enhance Europe’s digital sovereignty, improve competitiveness, and deliver long-term economic and societal value by enabling scalable, trustworthy, and high-impact innovation.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2463,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01857,EU_PSTW_202609_01857,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""social_nonprofit""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Norwegian University of Science and Technology (NTNU),General public services n.e.c.,"Public sector organisations increasingly explore productivity assistants but lack clear guidance on organisational readiness, data protection and responsible deployment.",Norwegian University of Science and Technology testing the utility and safety of Microsoft's AI assistant in public sector organisations,Norwegian University of Science and Technology (NTNU); Academic-Research; General public services n.e.c.; Norway,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Public sector organisations increasingly explore productivity assistants but lack clear guidance on organisational readiness, data protection and responsible deployment. In Norway, public institutions sought evidence-based insights before large-scale adoption. The objective of this pilot is to evaluate the utility, safety and governance implications of deploying Copilot for Microsoft 365. The implementation tests the tool in controlled organisational settings, examining impacts on workflows, information handling and compliance. A structured “readiness toolbox” is developed, providing guidelines, risk assessments and best practices. The pilot delivered practical recommendations, increased organisational awareness and clearer policy guidance. Qualitative benefits include reduced uncertainty, improved governance capacity and a foundation for responsible scaling across the public sector.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2464,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01858,EU_PSTW_202609_01858,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Tampere,General public services n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,x-CITE: Expanding the Experience of Citizens through Extended Reality and Metaverse in the City of Tampere,City of Tampere; Local Government; General public services n.e.c.; Finland,"This use case addresses the need to broaden access to culture, improve engagement in urban planning, and create new forms of social participation, involving the city administration, cultural institutions, event organisers, citizens, and visitors, with the objective of developing a shared CitiVerse as a practical tool for smart city innovation.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Cities increasingly seek new ways to engage citizens, improve access to culture, and support participatory urban development while ensuring that digital innovation remains human-centric and inclusive. The City of Tampere aims to enhance how residents and visitors experience urban spaces, cultural venues, and civic participation by blending physical and digital environments. This use case addresses the need to broaden access to culture, improve engagement in urban planning, and create new forms of social participation, involving the city administration, cultural institutions, event organisers, citizens, and visitors, with the objective of developing a shared CitiVerse as a practical tool for smart city innovation. The x-CITE project implements extended reality and metaverse technologies to create immersive experiences connected to real locations, events, and artworks, integrating physical spaces such as public areas and the Nokia Arena with digital layers. XR applications are developed for urban planning, museums, culture, and tourism, supported by city data platforms and tested through pilots such as immersive museum experiences at Vapriikki. The initiative enhances citizen engagement, reaches new audiences, and improves accessibility to cultural and civic spaces. It delivers richer user experiences, supports inclusive participation, and establishes scalable digital capabilities that strengthen Tampere’s position as a pioneer of human-centric smart cities.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2465,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01859,EU_PSTW_202609_01859,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Government of Navarra,Community development.,"Cities and regions increasingly need collaborative tools to address complex urban challenges while ensuring that innovation delivers tangible benefits to citizens. In the Region of Navarra, public authorities and local companies face fragmentation between policy needs, technological capabilities and skills development.",TwIN - Urban Digital Twin in the Region of Navarra,Government of Navarra; Local Government; Community development.; Spain,"The objective of this pilot is to foster structured collaboration between public administration, industry and the innovation ecosystem through the use of urban digital twin technologies.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Cities and regions increasingly need collaborative tools to address complex urban challenges while ensuring that innovation delivers tangible benefits to citizens. In the Region of Navarra, public authorities and local companies face fragmentation between policy needs, technological capabilities and skills development. The objective of this pilot is to foster structured collaboration between public administration, industry and the innovation ecosystem through the use of urban digital twin technologies. The implementation is organised around two complementary lines of work. TwIN GovTech operates as an open innovation programme that responds rapidly to concrete challenges defined by Pamplona City Council, enabling companies to test digital twin applications in real urban contexts. TwIN Lab functions as a training and experimentation environment, supporting skills development, prototyping and knowledge transfer across the regional ecosystem. Digital twin components integrate urban data, simulation tools and visualisation environments to support analysis and decision-making. The pilot has strengthened collaboration between public and private actors, accelerated experimentation cycles and increased institutional capacity to adopt advanced digital tools. Qualitative results include improved policy design, greater innovation readiness and a scalable framework for citizen-centred urban transformation.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2466,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01860,EU_PSTW_202609_01860,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Elhuyar,General public services n.e.c.,The BasqueLLM research project addresses this challenge by strengthening digital language support for Basque.,BasqueLLM research project launched the Llama-eus-8B neural model in Basque,Elhuyar; Non-governmental; General public services n.e.c.; Spain,"This use case involves researchers, public institutions, technology developers, and Basque-speaking communities, with the objective of enabling high-quality language understanding and generation for Basque in digital applications. The solution is implemented through Llama-eus-8B, a foundational neural language model specifically trained on curated Basque-language datasets.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Digital services often underrepresent minority and regional languages, limiting accessibility and cultural inclusion. The BasqueLLM research project addresses this challenge by strengthening digital language support for Basque. This use case involves researchers, public institutions, technology developers, and Basque-speaking communities, with the objective of enabling high-quality language understanding and generation for Basque in digital applications. The solution is implemented through Llama-eus-8B, a foundational neural language model specifically trained on curated Basque-language datasets. With fewer than 10 billion parameters, it balances performance and efficiency, making it suitable for a wide range of applications. The model is designed as a base layer for downstream tools such as chatbots, content generation systems, and public-sector digital services. The impact includes improved linguistic coverage, reduced dependency on non-European language technologies, and enhanced accessibility of digital services for Basque speakers. The project strengthens digital inclusion, supports cultural preservation, and enables innovation in public and private services using the Basque language.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2467,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01861,EU_PSTW_202609_01861,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Industrial Cybersecurity Center of Gipuzkoa,General public services n.e.c.,Many organisations lack the internal expertise to interpret and apply regulations effectively.,AI tool for industries' application of EU regulations on cybersecurity,Industrial Cybersecurity Center of Gipuzkoa; Non-governmental; General public services n.e.c.; Spain,"The use case involves industrial firms, cybersecurity specialists, and regional authorities, with the objective of simplifying regulatory compliance and strengthening cyber resilience. The solution is implemented as an interactive advisory tool that guides users through regulatory requirements, assesses organisational maturity, and provides tailored recommendations based on company profiles.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Industrial companies face growing regulatory complexity in complying with EU cybersecurity requirements, particularly under new frameworks such as NIS2. Many organisations lack the internal expertise to interpret and apply regulations effectively. ZIUR, the Industrial Cybersecurity Centre of Gipuzkoa, developed a digital assistant to support companies in this process. The use case involves industrial firms, cybersecurity specialists, and regional authorities, with the objective of simplifying regulatory compliance and strengthening cyber resilience. The solution is implemented as an interactive advisory tool that guides users through regulatory requirements, assesses organisational maturity, and provides tailored recommendations based on company profiles. It draws on regulatory texts, best practices, and sector-specific risk scenarios. The impact includes improved understanding of obligations, reduced compliance costs, and more consistent application of cybersecurity measures. Companies benefit from clearer guidance and reduced risk exposure, while the region strengthens its industrial cybersecurity posture and regulatory readiness.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2468,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01862,EU_PSTW_202609_01862,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Danish Environmental Protection Agency,Environmental protection n.e.c.,"Environmental permitting processes are often complex, slow, and resource-intensive, creating uncertainty for project developers while placing pressure on regulators. Automated checks and intelligent assistance help identify issues early and reduce bottlenecks.",Streamlining Environmental Permitting Processes with Multi-Agent AI in Denmark,Danish Environmental Protection Agency; Central-Government; Environmental protection n.e.c.; Denmark,"This use case involves regulators, developers, and environmental stakeholders, with the objective of accelerating permit decisions while maintaining regulatory integrity. The solution is implemented through a fully digital, centrally managed permitting platform that structures workflows, standardises documentation, and supports caseworkers with analytical tools and access to historical data.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Environmental permitting processes are often complex, slow, and resource-intensive, creating uncertainty for project developers while placing pressure on regulators. Denmark’s Environmental Protection Agency modernised this process to improve efficiency and transparency. This use case involves regulators, developers, and environmental stakeholders, with the objective of accelerating permit decisions while maintaining regulatory integrity. The solution is implemented through a fully digital, centrally managed permitting platform that structures workflows, standardises documentation, and supports caseworkers with analytical tools and access to historical data. Automated checks and intelligent assistance help identify issues early and reduce bottlenecks. Applicants can track application status in real time, improving predictability. The impact includes faster processing times, improved coordination between agencies, and higher consistency in decisions. The system supports renewable energy deployment and climate goals, delivering a scalable, replicable model for modern regulatory administration.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2469,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01863,EU_PSTW_202609_01863,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Prague,General public services n.e.c.,"Municipal administrations often struggle to manage large volumes of citizen reports submitted through fragmented communication channels, leading to delays, misrouting and reduced public satisfaction. The pilot has reduced manual handling of reports, shortened response times and improved traceability of issue resolution.",AI-driven citizen engagement platform for the City of Prague,City of Prague; Local Government; General public services n.e.c.; Czechia,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Municipal administrations often struggle to manage large volumes of citizen reports submitted through fragmented communication channels, leading to delays, misrouting and reduced public satisfaction. In Prague, residents and city departments required a more efficient and transparent mechanism for reporting and resolving urban issues. The objective of this pilot is to streamline citizen engagement and improve responsiveness of municipal services. The implementation delivers a digital engagement platform that allows residents to report issues through a single interface, where submissions are automatically categorised and routed to the responsible departments. Natural language processing techniques interpret citizen inputs, while workflow automation integrates with municipal back-office systems. The platform is developed within the CommuniCity initiative in collaboration with the city’s ICT organisation, ensuring alignment with existing governance structures. The pilot has reduced manual handling of reports, shortened response times and improved traceability of issue resolution. Qualitative benefits include increased citizen trust, better inter-departmental coordination and more consistent service delivery. The initiative demonstrates how digital engagement platforms can enhance participation while improving operational efficiency in large cities.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK|F06_HIGH_VOLUME_SCALABILITY_BOTTLENECK,S6_WORKFLOW_PROCESS_AUTOMATION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2471,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01864,EU_PSTW_202609_01864,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Amsterdam,Social exclusion n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,Skendy - Multilingual Chatbot supporting Ukrainian refugees in the City of Amsterdam,City of Amsterdam; Local Government; Social exclusion n.e.c.; Netherlands,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Newly arrived refugees often face significant barriers in understanding administrative requirements, particularly when dealing with unfamiliar languages and complex bureaucratic processes. In the City of Amsterdam, Ukrainian refugees required accessible support to manage official communications and documentation. The objective of this pilot is to reduce administrative stress and improve access to essential services for displaced populations. The implementation adapts an existing digital assistant platform to provide multilingual document support, including translation, organisation and reminder functions. The system processes official letters, utility bills and other documents, presenting clear explanations and deadlines in the user’s preferred language. The solution integrates with mobile devices and existing municipal support services, ensuring ease of access. The pilot improved refugees’ ability to comply with administrative obligations, reduced reliance on informal assistance and eased pressure on frontline municipal staff. Qualitative outcomes include increased autonomy for users, improved inclusion and better access to public services. The project illustrates how targeted digital tools can support vulnerable groups while enhancing the effectiveness of city-level integration policies.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2472,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01865,EU_PSTW_202609_01865,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"DPP, Prague’s Public Transport Company",Transport,UNKNOWN,nShield - Predictive Maintenance for escalators downtimes in Prague,"DPP, Prague’s Public Transport Company; Non-governmental; Transport; Czechia","This use case involves transport operators, maintenance teams, and passengers, with the objective of predicting failures before they occur. The solution is implemented through acoustic sensors installed on escalators, continuously collecting sound data.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Escalator failures in public transport systems cause service disruptions, safety risks, and high maintenance costs. Prague’s public transport operator sought to reduce downtime and improve maintenance efficiency. This use case involves transport operators, maintenance teams, and passengers, with the objective of predicting failures before they occur. The solution is implemented through acoustic sensors installed on escalators, continuously collecting sound data. These signals are analysed in real time using mathematical models that detect anomalies and deviations from normal behaviour. The system identifies likely component failures and provides maintenance recommendations to operators. Integrated into maintenance workflows, it enables condition-based interventions rather than reactive repairs. The impact includes reduced downtime, lower maintenance costs, improved passenger experience, and increased asset lifespan. The system enhances operational reliability and supports smarter management of critical transport infrastructure.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2474,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01866,EU_PSTW_202609_01866,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""social_nonprofit""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Bundesdruckerei Group – Federal AI Competence Center,R&D general public services.,Public sector organisations frequently process confidential documents that cannot be transferred to external systems without risking data breaches or loss of sovereignty.,ADLER: Local Language Models for Sensitive Data,Bundesdruckerei Group – Federal AI Competence Center; Central-Government; R&D general public services.; Germany,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Public sector organisations frequently process confidential documents that cannot be transferred to external systems without risking data breaches or loss of sovereignty. Administrative staff require advanced text-processing capabilities while maintaining strict control over sensitive information. The objective of this pilot is to demonstrate that language models can be executed securely on local devices without network dependency. The implementation explores on-device deployment of compact language models on commercially available, resilient hardware. Documents are processed directly on the user’s workstation, enabling functions such as classification, extraction and summarisation without external data transmission. The approach is designed to comply with strict security classifications, including confidential government information. The pilot strengthens data sovereignty, reduces exposure to cyber risks and opens new usage contexts for secure document handling. Qualitative benefits include increased trust, broader applicability of advanced text processing in sensitive environments and reduced dependency on external infrastructure. The project provides a foundation for responsible deployment of advanced digital tools in security-critical administrative contexts.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2475,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01867,EU_PSTW_202609_01867,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Bundesdruckerei Group – Federal AI Competence Center,R&D general public services.,UNKNOWN,MÖVE: Benchmarking of Language Models for Public Administration,Bundesdruckerei Group – Federal AI Competence Center; Central-Government; R&D general public services.; Germany,"Decision-makers often lack objective criteria to compare available models for administrative use. The framework supports scalable, responsible use of language technologies across public administrations.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Public administrations face growing pressure to adopt language technologies while ensuring reliability, fairness and regulatory compliance. Decision-makers often lack objective criteria to compare available models for administrative use. The objective of this pilot is to establish a robust evaluation framework tailored to public sector needs. The implementation defines standardised benchmarks assessing model performance on administrative tasks in German, alongside governance criteria such as social fairness, hallucination rates and alignment with the EU AI Act. The framework supports transparent comparison of different models and deployment scenarios. The pilot provides evidence-based guidance for procurement and policy decisions. Qualitative impacts include improved risk awareness, stronger governance and increased confidence in technology adoption. The framework supports scalable, responsible use of language technologies across public administrations.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2476,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01868,EU_PSTW_202609_01868,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Bundesdruckerei Group – Federal AI Competence Center,R&D general public services.,"Administrative decision-making often depends on analysing large volumes of unstructured documents, which places significant strain on staff resources.",AI Language Assistant for the German Federal Administration: Efficient Information Processing,Bundesdruckerei Group – Federal AI Competence Center; Central-Government; R&D general public services.; Germany,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Administrative decision-making often depends on analysing large volumes of unstructured documents, which places significant strain on staff resources. Federal authorities require tools to extract relevant information efficiently while maintaining accountability. The objective of this pilot is to improve information processing and support evidence-based decisions. The implementation deploys a language-based assistant capable of summarising documents, identifying key topics and mapping responsibilities. The system integrates with existing document management systems and supports staff workflows. The pilot reduced processing time, improved consistency and enhanced transparency. Qualitative benefits include increased productivity, improved staff satisfaction and better-informed decisions.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F04_UNSTRUCTURED_INFORMATION_COMPLEXITY|F06_HIGH_VOLUME_SCALABILITY_BOTTLENECK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2477,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01869,EU_PSTW_202609_01869,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Bundesdruckerei Group – Federal AI Competence Center,R&D general public services.,Federal ministries often lack a shared infrastructure to support advanced data analysis securely.,PLAIN: a data analysis platform for the entire German federal administration,Bundesdruckerei Group – Federal AI Competence Center; Central-Government; R&D general public services.; Germany,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Effective public policy increasingly depends on the ability to analyse large and heterogeneous datasets across institutional boundaries. Federal ministries often lack a shared infrastructure to support advanced data analysis securely. The objective of this pilot is to establish a standard, cross-departmental data platform. The implementation delivers a secure infrastructure enabling storage, processing and analysis of diverse datasets without thematic restrictions. The platform supports advanced analytical applications and future data-driven projects. The pilot improved analytical capacity, reduced duplication and strengthened inter-ministerial collaboration. Qualitative outcomes include improved policy insight, scalability and strategic alignment with national digital strategies.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2478,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01870,EU_PSTW_202609_01870,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,SOCIB - Balearic Islands Coastal Observing and Forecasting System,R&D environmental protection.,The objective of this pilot is to develop modular digital twin prototypes tailored to specific marine challenges in the Western Mediterranean.,SOCIB 2030: Digital Twin of the Western Mediterranean,SOCIB - Balearic Islands Coastal Observing and Forecasting System; Non-governmental; R&D environmental protection.; Spain,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Understanding complex marine systems is essential for addressing climate change, biodiversity loss and coastal risks. Researchers and policymakers require integrated tools that combine data, modelling and visualisation. The objective of this pilot is to develop modular digital twin prototypes tailored to specific marine challenges in the Western Mediterranean. The implementation delivers three specialised prototypes focusing on marine protected areas, climate adaptation and early warning for extreme events. These integrate observational data, forecasting models and visual analytics. The pilot enhances scientific understanding, supports decision-making and fosters international collaboration. Qualitative impacts include improved preparedness, better environmental governance and scalable contributions to global ocean initiatives.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2479,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01871,EU_PSTW_202609_01871,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,CINECA,R&D economic affairs,UNKNOWN,VELVET - Italian AI Open Source model,CINECA; Academic-Research; R&D economic affairs; Italy,The objective of this pilot is to develop an open-source model prioritising Italian language and content while supporting multilingual use.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"European administrations and organisations require language technologies that reflect linguistic diversity, regulatory requirements and sustainability goals. The objective of this pilot is to develop an open-source model prioritising Italian language and content while supporting multilingual use. The implementation trains a multimodal model on high-performance computing infrastructure, optimised for efficiency and regulatory compliance. The pilot strengthens European technological autonomy, improves language coverage and supports diverse public and private applications. Qualitative benefits include transparency, energy efficiency and alignment with European values.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2483,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01872,EU_PSTW_202609_01872,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""social_nonprofit""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Hafnarfjörður Municipality,Street lighting.,UNKNOWN,Smart Street Lighting,Hafnarfjörður Municipality; Local Government; Street lighting.; Iceland,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Urban lighting represents a major source of energy consumption and maintenance costs for municipalities. Hafnarfjörður sought to modernise its lighting infrastructure to improve efficiency and safety. The objective of this pilot is to enable adaptive, data-driven lighting management. The implementation deploys networked controllers connected via low-power communication, allowing real-time control and monitoring of 6,000 streetlights. Lighting levels adapt to conditions and schedules, and maintenance is optimised through monitoring data. The pilot achieved a 35% reduction in energy consumption and a 42% decrease in maintenance costs, while improving public safety. Qualitative impacts include reduced emissions, operational savings and a scalable foundation for broader smart city services.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2485,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01873,EU_PSTW_202609_01873,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Ministry of Cultural Heritage,Cultural services.,"The pilot improved the ability to identify early warning signs of structural stress, reduced reliance on periodic manual inspections and enhanced situational awareness for conservation teams.",Preserving History with Smart Technology: IoT Monitoring at the Colosseum,Ministry of Cultural Heritage; Central-Government; Cultural services.; Italy,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Protecting world heritage sites requires continuous monitoring while avoiding invasive interventions that could compromise historical integrity. At the Colosseum in Rome, heritage authorities and conservation experts needed a reliable method to detect structural changes and environmental stress without physical alteration of the monument. The objective of this pilot is to support proactive preservation through non-invasive, real-time monitoring. The implementation deploys discreet LoRaWAN®-enabled sensors that collect structural and environmental data and transmit it continuously to a central network server. The system enables real-time detection of anomalies and supports the validation of satellite-based observations, integrating multiple data sources into a coherent monitoring framework. The pilot improved the ability to identify early warning signs of structural stress, reduced reliance on periodic manual inspections and enhanced situational awareness for conservation teams. Qualitative impacts include improved safeguarding of cultural heritage, more informed maintenance planning and a scalable approach for monitoring other sensitive historical sites while preserving their authenticity.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2486,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01874,EU_PSTW_202609_01874,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Region of Tápióság,Water supply.,Ensuring reliable water supply and minimising losses is particularly challenging in areas with limited infrastructure and remote pumping stations.,Water Network Monitoring,Region of Tápióság; Regional Government; Water supply.; Hungary,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Ensuring reliable water supply and minimising losses is particularly challenging in areas with limited infrastructure and remote pumping stations. In Tápióság, Hungary, water utilities required better visibility of network performance without continuous on-site supervision. The objective of this pilot is to improve operational efficiency and sustainability of water distribution systems. The implementation installs autonomous monitoring devices at pumping stations to measure parameters such as pressure, energy consumption, pump operation time and water flow. Data is transmitted via low-power wide-area networks to a central management system, enabling real-time monitoring and alerts. The solution operates continuously without human intervention, even in hard-to-reach locations. The pilot enabled faster detection of faults, improved optimisation of pumping operations and reduced water losses. Qualitative benefits include lower operational costs, improved service reliability and stronger support for sustainable water management practices, demonstrating the value of remote monitoring for utilities with constrained resources.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2487,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01875,EU_PSTW_202609_01875,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Forest Department of Salzburg,Fire-protection services,UNKNOWN,Data-driven Wildfire Prevention System for Forest,Forest Department of Salzburg; Local Government; Fire-protection services; Austria,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Forest authorities face increasing risks from wildfires due to climate change, requiring early detection and accurate risk assessment over large areas. In Salzburg, managing forest safety demanded real-time environmental insights to support preventive action. The objective of this pilot is to enhance wildfire preparedness through continuous risk monitoring. The implementation deploys a dense network of environmental sensors across 350 square kilometres, collecting data on weather conditions and soil moisture. These inputs are processed to calculate the Fire Weather Index, providing real-time assessments of fire risk. Information is made available to authorities to guide prevention measures and resource allocation. The pilot improved situational awareness, enabled faster preventive responses and supported more informed decision-making. Qualitative impacts include increased forest resilience, improved safety for communities and a scalable framework for wildfire risk management in other regions.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2488,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01876,EU_PSTW_202609_01876,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"German Federal Ministry of Research, Technology, and Space (BMFTR)","Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.","Research funding regulations are complex and difficult for applicants to interpret, often leading to incomplete applications and high enquiry volumes. The impact includes reduced administrative burden, faster access to information for applicants, and improved quality of submissions.","German Ministry of Research, Technology, and Space provides a popular chatbot to make project funding regulations more accessible","German Federal Ministry of Research, Technology, and Space (BMFTR); Central-Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Germany","This use case involves researchers, institutions, and ministry staff, with the objective of simplifying access to funding rules and guidance. The solution is implemented as a conversational assistant built on an enterprise-grade platform that retrieves answers from official funding documents and guidance materials.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Research funding regulations are complex and difficult for applicants to interpret, often leading to incomplete applications and high enquiry volumes. The German Ministry of Research, Technology, and Space introduced a digital assistant to improve accessibility of funding information. This use case involves researchers, institutions, and ministry staff, with the objective of simplifying access to funding rules and guidance. The solution is implemented as a conversational assistant built on an enterprise-grade platform that retrieves answers from official funding documents and guidance materials. It supports natural language queries and provides clear, context-specific responses. Integrated into ministry websites, it complements existing support channels. The impact includes reduced administrative burden, faster access to information for applicants, and improved quality of submissions. The ministry benefits from efficiency gains and better stakeholder engagement, while applicants gain clarity and confidence in navigating funding opportunities.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2489,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01877,EU_PSTW_202609_01877,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"City of Naples, Catania, and Perugia",Environmental protection n.e.c.,"Urban EVOLUTION addresses a pressing urban-management challenge: how to reduce air pollution and urban heat-island effects in dense city environments while planning interventions that are practical, evidence-based and replicable.",Urban EVOLUTION (Earth obserVatiOn and nature-based soLUTions agaInst urban pollution),"City of Naples, Catania, and Perugia; Local Government; Environmental protection n.e.c.; Italy","The solution is implemented through a combination of geospatial analysis, remote sensing and analytical models developed with technological partner Latitudo 40 and supported by WiseTown, TeamDev’s R&D division, and the Universities of Perugia and Palermo.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Urban EVOLUTION addresses a pressing urban-management challenge: how to reduce air pollution and urban heat-island effects in dense city environments while planning interventions that are practical, evidence-based and replicable. Piloted over 12 months in Naples, Catania and Perugia, the initiative brings together municipalities, research partners and technical specialists to test how Nature-Based Solutions can respond to different climatic and urban conditions. The operational objective is to help cities understand where green infrastructure can deliver the greatest value in lowering emissions, reducing particulate matter and improving thermal comfort, while supporting longer-term planning aligned with climate targets. The solution is implemented through a combination of geospatial analysis, remote sensing and analytical models developed with technological partner Latitudo 40 and supported by WiseTown, TeamDev’s R&D division, and the Universities of Perugia and Palermo. At a conceptual level, Earth-observation data and urban-environment indicators are processed to assess pollution levels, map spatial patterns and estimate the impact of green spaces on CO₂ reduction, particulate capture and temperature mitigation. These outputs provide a decision-support layer for Nature-Based Solution planning across diverse city contexts. The expected impact is improved urban-planning precision, stronger evidence for climate adaptation measures and a scalable model for greener cities. More broadly, the project supports Paris Agreement goals, with the broader ambition of cutting emissions by 50% by 2030 and reaching carbon neutrality by 2050, while showing that urban green spaces could offset up to 40% of city emissions and materially improve air quality.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2492,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01878,EU_PSTW_202609_01878,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""social_nonprofit""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,State of Baden-Württemberg,Health n.e.c.,"Baden-Württemberg launched MEDI:CUS in 2022 to tackle a structural weakness in Germany’s decentralised healthcare IT environment: fragmented systems, limited interoperability and inefficient data exchange across institutions and care sectors. This fragmentation makes it harder for hospitals and other healthcare actors to share information securely, scale digital services and innovate consistently across the system.",MEDI:CUS – A Multi-Cloud-Based Platform Architecture for a Connected and Intersectoral Healthcare System,State of Baden-Württemberg; Regional Government; Health n.e.c.; Germany,"The solution is being implemented through MEDI:core, a multi-cloud platform architecture designed to function like an app-store model for healthcare.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Baden-Württemberg launched MEDI:CUS in 2022 to tackle a structural weakness in Germany’s decentralised healthcare IT environment: fragmented systems, limited interoperability and inefficient data exchange across institutions and care sectors. This fragmentation makes it harder for hospitals and other healthcare actors to share information securely, scale digital services and innovate consistently across the system. Backed by five state ministries and a broad coalition that includes all university hospitals and the Baden-Württemberg Hospital Association, the initiative aims to establish by 2026 a connected digital foundation that supports more efficient, trusted and interoperable healthcare operations. The solution is being implemented through MEDI:core, a multi-cloud platform architecture designed to function like an app-store model for healthcare. At a conceptual level, the platform combines Infrastructure-as-a-Service and Software-as-a-Service components delivered through standardised interfaces and protocols, enabling interoperability at technical, semantic, organisational and legal levels. The architecture supports secure health-data exchange, cloud-based services and modular digital applications that can be adopted across institutions without each organisation building separate infrastructure from scratch. Early pilot testing has already demonstrated secure hospital connectivity and the deployment of initial services such as cloud storage and Nextcloud. The expected impact is a more scalable and reliable digital-health ecosystem, lower duplication of effort and faster service rollout across participating institutions. By the end of 2026, the project aims to connect 15 to 20 hospitals, provide usable digital solutions and establish a self-sustaining target organisation, creating both a reference model for health-system transformation and a broader blueprint for trusted public-sector platforms.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2497,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01879,EU_PSTW_202609_01879,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Malmö,Community development.,"Major urban-regeneration schemes often struggle to involve residents in a way that is timely, understandable and genuinely influential, which can weaken transparency, reduce public acceptance and leave planners without a clear view of community priorities.",PublicTwin for citizen engagement in Malmo for the renovation of the Nyhamnen harbor,City of Malmö; Local Government; Community development.; Sweden,"The solution is implemented through PublicTwin, a digital twin platform that visualises proposed developments and enables interactive exploration of planning data.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Major urban-regeneration schemes often struggle to involve residents in a way that is timely, understandable and genuinely influential, which can weaken transparency, reduce public acceptance and leave planners without a clear view of community priorities. In Malmö’s Nyhamnen harbour redevelopment, the city sought to address this participation gap by creating a more open and engaging planning process in which citizens could explore proposals and contribute meaningfully to decisions shaping the area’s future. The stakeholders include municipal planners, local residents and other community actors, with the objective of improving dialogue, increasing transparency and ensuring that redevelopment choices are better aligned with public needs and expectations. The solution is implemented through PublicTwin, a digital twin platform that visualises proposed developments and enables interactive exploration of planning data. At a conceptual level, the system combines 3D urban representation, accessible user interfaces and feedback tools that allow residents to review proposed interventions, understand spatial implications and submit suggestions in real time. Public input can then be integrated into planning workflows as part of a more continuous and structured engagement process. The pilot’s impact has been qualitative but significant: it improved transparency, increased citizen participation and strengthened communication between planners and residents. More broadly, the initiative fostered greater trust, supported more inclusive decision-making and demonstrated the business and governance value of digital twins as scalable tools for participatory urban planning and more responsive city development.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2498,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01880,EU_PSTW_202609_01880,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Tampere,Cultural services.,UNKNOWN,AI-Tour guide in the City of Tampere,City of Tampere; Local Government; Cultural services.; Finland,"The solution is implemented through smart glasses equipped with a virtual assistant that delivers location-based information as visitors move through the city. More broadly, the use case enhanced accessibility, supported a more interactive tourism experience and demonstrated a scalable smart-tourism model capable of combining user convenience, local economic value and richer destination storytelling.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"As visitor expectations shift towards immediate, personalised and multilingual support, cities face growing pressure to offer tourism services that are both more engaging and more adaptive than static signage or traditional information points. In Tampere, tourism stakeholders wanted to enrich the visitor journey while also creating stronger links between tourists, attractions and local businesses. The pilot therefore focused on improving how people experience the city in real time, with the objective of delivering accessible, immersive guidance that helps visitors discover services, museums and points of interest more easily and enjoyably. The stakeholders include tourists, tourism authorities, museums and local businesses, all of whom benefit from a more dynamic visitor-information system. The solution is implemented through smart glasses equipped with a virtual assistant that delivers location-based information as visitors move through the city. At a conceptual level, the system combines wearable interfaces, contextual content delivery and continuously updated destination information supplied by tourism organisations and local businesses. This enables visitors to receive relevant guidance in situ rather than searching separately for information, while also allowing business and cultural content to remain current. The pilot improved visitor satisfaction and increased engagement with local services, while also opening new promotional opportunities for participating businesses. More broadly, the use case enhanced accessibility, supported a more interactive tourism experience and demonstrated a scalable smart-tourism model capable of combining user convenience, local economic value and richer destination storytelling.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2499,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01881,EU_PSTW_202609_01881,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Kuopio,Transport,"Keeping shared bicycles available where and when users need them is a persistent operational challenge for cities, particularly when demand fluctuates sharply by area and time of day.",Bike sharing availability optimisation in the City of Kuopio,City of Kuopio; Local Government; Transport; Finland,"The solution is implemented through analysis of historical usage data to identify recurring demand patterns by location and time, followed by predictive forecasting of likely shortages.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Keeping shared bicycles available where and when users need them is a persistent operational challenge for cities, particularly when demand fluctuates sharply by area and time of day. In Kuopio, the electric bike-sharing scheme regularly faced shortages in high-demand zones and surpluses elsewhere, undermining reliability and limiting its effectiveness as a sustainable mobility option. The pilot was designed to address this imbalance by helping the city and service operators manage fleet distribution more intelligently. The stakeholders include mobility operators, logistics teams, municipal transport planners and users of the bike-sharing system, with the objective of improving service reliability, increasing user satisfaction and encouraging wider uptake of cycling as part of the urban transport mix. The solution is implemented through analysis of historical usage data to identify recurring demand patterns by location and time, followed by predictive forecasting of likely shortages. At a conceptual level, these forecasts feed into an operational system that sends real-time redistribution instructions to logistics operators via SMS or digital channels, guiding targeted rebalancing actions. A feedback loop records completed interventions and reported outcomes, allowing the predictive models to be refined continuously. The pilot resulted in more consistent bike availability, fewer service disruptions and better operational efficiency. More broadly, it improved the user experience, strengthened the attractiveness of sustainable transport and demonstrated a scalable, data-driven approach to managing shared mobility assets more effectively across a city.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2500,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01882,EU_PSTW_202609_01882,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Liguria Region,Public health services.,"In healthcare, long waiting lists, uneven demand and constrained resources make it difficult to plan appointments and allocate capacity fairly and efficiently. This pilot was created to address those shared challenges through regionally coordinated innovation hubs that bring together public administrations, service providers and technology partners.",Regional AI Hubs/Centres for Health and Tourism,Liguria Region; Consortium; Public health services.; Italy,"The solution is implemented through analytical tools for healthcare demand forecasting, adaptive resource allocation, appointment scheduling and booking support, combined with tourism-flow management tools designed to personalise visitor experiences and distribute demand more evenly across destinations and seasons. At a conceptual level, the system uses predictive analysis, optimisation techniques and shared governance mechanisms, while embedding ethical and regulatory safeguards into implementation.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Several Italian regions are facing similar operational pressures in two high-impact service areas: healthcare and tourism. In healthcare, long waiting lists, uneven demand and constrained resources make it difficult to plan appointments and allocate capacity fairly and efficiently. In tourism, fluctuating visitor numbers, seasonal concentration and rising expectations for personalised services place strain on destinations and reduce the quality of the visitor experience. This pilot was created to address those shared challenges through regionally coordinated innovation hubs that bring together public administrations, service providers and technology partners. Its objective is to test common approaches that improve planning, service efficiency and user experience across both domains, while building a framework that can be replicated across regions. The solution is implemented through analytical tools for healthcare demand forecasting, adaptive resource allocation, appointment scheduling and booking support, combined with tourism-flow management tools designed to personalise visitor experiences and distribute demand more evenly across destinations and seasons. At a conceptual level, the system uses predictive analysis, optimisation techniques and shared governance mechanisms, while embedding ethical and regulatory safeguards into implementation. Early results show improved planning accuracy, reduced bottlenecks in access to care and stronger coordination in tourism services. More broadly, the pilot supports greater service equity, better quality of care and visitor experience, and a resilient, citizen-centred model for cross-regional public-sector innovation.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION|S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2501,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01883,EU_PSTW_202609_01883,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Lombardy Region,Environmental protection n.e.c.,"Environmental protection and mobility planning increasingly require coordinated action across administrative boundaries, yet data fragmentation often limits policy effectiveness.",Regional AI Hub/Centres for Environmental Data and Sustainable Mobility,Lombardy Region; Regional Government; Environmental protection n.e.c.; Italy,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Environmental protection and mobility planning increasingly require coordinated action across administrative boundaries, yet data fragmentation often limits policy effectiveness. Lombardy and Veneto regions sought to improve environmental and transport governance through shared analytical capabilities. The objective of this pilot is to create a “smart land” by enabling transregional analysis of environmental and mobility data. The implementation establishes a federated data architecture that allows regions to retain control over their datasets while enabling advanced analytical models to operate across jurisdictions. These models support evidence-based policymaking in areas such as emissions reduction, traffic management and sustainable transport planning. The pilot enhances interoperability between regional systems and supports scalable innovation. Qualitative impacts include improved policy insight, stronger coordination between regions and more effective sustainability strategies. The initiative demonstrates how federated data approaches can support advanced analytics while respecting governance and data sovereignty requirements.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2502,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01884,EU_PSTW_202609_01884,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Puglia Region,R&D general public services.,"The pilot improved process efficiency, supported more informed decisions and reduced administrative burdens.",Regional AI Hubs/Centres for the PA,Puglia Region; Regional Government; R&D general public services.; Italy,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Public administrations face increasing complexity in policy design and technical-administrative procedures, placing pressure on staff capacity and decision quality. Several Italian regions recognised the need for shared innovation to support administrative effectiveness. The objective of this pilot is to enhance the quality, consistency and efficiency of public sector work by supporting human decision-making. The implementation introduces analytical and decision-support tools tailored to public policy analysis and administrative processes, integrated into existing workflows. Regional hubs provide shared infrastructure, governance and expertise, enabling experimentation and responsible adoption. The pilot improved process efficiency, supported more informed decisions and reduced administrative burdens. Qualitative benefits include improved working conditions for public servants, stronger institutional capacity and better service outcomes for citizens, offering a scalable model for digital transformation in public administration.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2503,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01885,EU_PSTW_202609_01885,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Tuscany Region,R&D environmental protection.,UNKNOWN,Regional AI Hubs/Centres for Resilience and Security of the Territory with 5G pilot action,Tuscany Region; Regional Government; R&D environmental protection.; Italy,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Preventing environmental risks and ensuring the safety of infrastructure and communities requires timely data, advanced analysis and reliable connectivity. Tuscany and partner regions sought to strengthen territorial resilience and emergency preparedness. The objective of this pilot is to improve risk monitoring, intervention planning and emergency management across regions. The implementation combines advanced analytical models with 5G-enabled connectivity to support real-time data collection from sensors, infrastructure and construction sites. Analytical tools support risk assessment, scenario planning and coordination among authorities. The pilot enhances situational awareness, speeds up response planning and improves cross-agency collaboration. Qualitative impacts include increased safety, stronger resilience of critical infrastructure and improved spatial planning capabilities, demonstrating the value of combining advanced analytics and next-generation connectivity for territorial governance.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2504,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01886,EU_PSTW_202609_01886,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Roma Capitale,Communication,"Rome faces the challenge of supporting digital services, public safety, and connectivity across a large, historic, and densely used urban environment.","""Roma 5G"" - extension of the 5G connection across Rome",Roma Capitale; Local Government; Communication; Italy,"This use case involves the City of Rome, infrastructure providers, transport authorities, and citizens, with the objective of enabling a fast, secure, and sustainable smart city. The solution is implemented through the deployment of advanced 5G infrastructure, including small cells, fibre networks, public Wi-Fi, IoT sensors, and surveillance cameras.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Rome faces the challenge of supporting digital services, public safety, and connectivity across a large, historic, and densely used urban environment. Roma 5G addresses this by upgrading citywide digital infrastructure. This use case involves the City of Rome, infrastructure providers, transport authorities, and citizens, with the objective of enabling a fast, secure, and sustainable smart city. The solution is implemented through the deployment of advanced 5G infrastructure, including small cells, fibre networks, public Wi-Fi, IoT sensors, and surveillance cameras. Coverage is extended across the entire metro system and key public spaces. A central Security Operations Centre coordinates data flows and supports crowd management, particularly for major events such as the 2025 Jubilee. The impact includes improved connectivity, enhanced public safety, better urban monitoring, and support for future digital services. Roma 5G positions Rome as a leading European smart city, delivering long-term social, economic, and environmental value.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2506,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01887,EU_PSTW_202609_01887,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""social_nonprofit""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Luxembourg Air Ambulance,Outpatient services.,"Rapid transport of medical samples and supplies is critical in emergency healthcare, yet traditional logistics can be slow or constrained by infrastructure.",Project Griffin - Drones to transport medical supplies in emergency situations,Luxembourg Air Ambulance; Non-governmental; Outpatient services.; Luxembourg,The objective of Project Griffin is to explore the use of drones for transporting medical supplies in urgent situations.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Rapid transport of medical samples and supplies is critical in emergency healthcare, yet traditional logistics can be slow or constrained by infrastructure. In Luxembourg, emergency services seek innovative solutions to reduce response times. The objective of Project Griffin is to explore the use of drones for transporting medical supplies in urgent situations. The implementation involves testing drone-based delivery routes, operational procedures and coordination with healthcare providers. Future development plans include integrating advanced automation for route planning, obstacle avoidance and fleet coordination. Expected impacts include faster sample transport, improved emergency response and reduced dependency on road transport. Qualitative outcomes include increased system resilience, innovation in healthcare logistics and a scalable foundation for future autonomous delivery networks.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2507,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01888,EU_PSTW_202609_01888,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,University of Nicosia,Tertiary education.,"Universities are increasingly challenged to provide innovative, globally accessible education while ensuring the authenticity and portability of academic credentials.",UNIC – Blockchain- and Metaverse-Based University Course,University of Nicosia; Academic-Research; Tertiary education.; Cyprus,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Universities are increasingly challenged to provide innovative, globally accessible education while ensuring the authenticity and portability of academic credentials. The University of Nicosia (UNIC) addressed this by redesigning how a course is delivered, accessed, and certified. The initiative involves the university, students worldwide, academic staff, and external experts from the digital economy, with the objective of experimenting with new models of online learning, certification, and learner engagement. The course “Introduction to NFTs and the Metaverse” is delivered entirely through decentralised digital environments, removing reliance on traditional learning management systems. Registration is enabled through the minting of a free course token, which grants access to learning materials and activities hosted in virtual spaces. Course participation, progress, and completion are recorded through blockchain-based mechanisms, and learners can optionally obtain a completion certificate issued as a non-fungible token on Ethereum. The impact has been significant, with more than 14,000 registrants, demonstrating global reach and strong engagement. The model enhances transparency, verifiability, and learner ownership of credentials, while reducing administrative overhead. It provides UNIC with a scalable, future-oriented education model and offers learners a portable, tamper-proof record of achievement aligned with emerging digital economies.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2509,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01889,EU_PSTW_202609_01889,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""social_nonprofit""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,European High Performance Computing Joint Undertaking (EuroHPC JU),R&D economic affairs,UNKNOWN,"MareNostrum-Ona, Europe's first quantum annealer",European High Performance Computing Joint Undertaking (EuroHPC JU); European Institution/Agency; R&D economic affairs; Spain,"Europe faces increasing demand for advanced computing capabilities to address complex optimisation problems that are difficult to solve using conventional supercomputing alone, particularly in areas such as logistics, materials science and advanced analytics.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Europe faces increasing demand for advanced computing capabilities to address complex optimisation problems that are difficult to solve using conventional supercomputing alone, particularly in areas such as logistics, materials science and advanced analytics. The objective of MareNostrum-Ona is to strengthen European technological sovereignty by providing researchers, industry and public sector organisations with access to novel quantum computing resources. The implementation involves the development of an analogue quantum annealer hosted at the Barcelona Supercomputing Center and integrated into the MareNostrum 5 infrastructure. The initial system will operate with at least ten physical qubits and will be progressively expanded in subsequent generations. It is designed to complement classical high-performance computing by addressing optimisation tasks through quantum annealing techniques. The project is co-funded by European and national partners and developed in collaboration with leading research institutions. Expected impacts include expanded research capabilities, early industrial experimentation with quantum optimisation and accelerated knowledge transfer within Europe. Qualitative outcomes include improved readiness for quantum adoption, strengthened collaboration between academia and industry, and a scalable foundation for future quantum–HPC hybrid systems.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2510,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01890,EU_PSTW_202609_01890,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,NTT Data,Health n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,DeepHealth - Deep-Learning and HPC to Boost Biomedical Applications for Health,NTT Data; Consortium; Health n.e.c.; European Union,"This use case addresses these needs by supporting advanced biomedical applications through a unified computational framework, involving research institutions, healthcare providers, and technology partners, with the objective of accelerating innovation in diagnosis and treatment.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Biomedical research and healthcare increasingly depend on the ability to analyse large, complex datasets to support diagnosis, monitoring, and treatment, yet existing computing infrastructures often limit performance and scalability. Researchers and clinicians require integrated environments that can efficiently process imaging, genomic, and clinical data across diverse applications. This use case addresses these needs by supporting advanced biomedical applications through a unified computational framework, involving research institutions, healthcare providers, and technology partners, with the objective of accelerating innovation in diagnosis and treatment. The DeepHealth project implements a framework that combines high-performance computing infrastructures with deep learning and computer vision techniques, optimised to run on heterogeneous HPC and big data architectures. It supports data-intensive workflows and enables large-scale training and inference across multiple biomedical domains, piloted through 15 real-world applications. The project improves processing speed, scalability, and analytical accuracy, enabling more efficient analysis of complex biomedical data. It reduces time to insight, supports more precise clinical decision-making, and demonstrates how advanced computing infrastructures can be effectively leveraged to deliver tangible benefits in health research and clinical practice.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2511,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01891,EU_PSTW_202609_01891,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Advanced Center for Aerospace Technologies (CATEC),"Mining, manufacturing and construction.","Ageing infrastructure such as bridges, tunnels and oil and gas facilities requires frequent inspection and maintenance, yet these activities are costly, labour-intensive and often expose workers to hazardous environments. PILOTING was created to address this operational challenge by improving how critical assets are inspected and maintained across Europe.",PILOTING - PILOTs for robotic INspection and maintenance Grounded on advanced intelligent platforms and prototype applications,"Advanced Center for Aerospace Technologies (CATEC); Consortium; Mining, manufacturing and construction.; European Union","The solution was implemented through an integrated robotic platform combining aerial drones, ground robots, crawlers and advanced analytics within a common inspection and maintenance framework.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Ageing infrastructure such as bridges, tunnels and oil and gas facilities requires frequent inspection and maintenance, yet these activities are costly, labour-intensive and often expose workers to hazardous environments. PILOTING was created to address this operational challenge by improving how critical assets are inspected and maintained across Europe. The stakeholders included infrastructure owners, industrial operators, maintenance teams and standardisation actors, with the objective of increasing inspection quality, reducing operating costs and enhancing worker safety in sectors where asset failure can have major economic and public consequences. The solution was implemented through an integrated robotic platform combining aerial drones, ground robots, crawlers and advanced analytics within a common inspection and maintenance framework. At a conceptual level, the system brought together data capture, autonomous or semi-autonomous navigation and defect-analysis capabilities so that robots could operate in complex environments and support the detection of structural or operational issues. These technologies were validated in three large-scale pilots spanning civil infrastructure and oil and gas settings. The expected impact was safer inspections, improved consistency in asset assessment and lower reliance on risky manual intervention. More broadly, PILOTING strengthened the case for robotics in industrial inspection and maintenance, supported the development of standards in the field and created value through more efficient, scalable and safer infrastructure management.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2512,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01892,EU_PSTW_202609_01892,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Forum Virium Helsinki,Transport,FABULOS addressed this challenge by helping cities test whether autonomous minibuses could become a realistic extension of urban transport systems rather than a stand-alone novelty.,FABULOS (Future Automated Bus Urban Level Operation Systems),Forum Virium Helsinki; Consortium; Transport; European Union,"The solution was implemented through a Pre-Commercial Procurement process that engaged several consortia to design, prototype and pilot automated shuttle services in six European cities, including Helsinki, Gjesdal, Tallinn, Lamia and Helmond, as well as a site in Portugal.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Many cities are interested in automated last-mile public transport, yet practical deployment depends on more than vehicle technology alone: it also requires integration with existing networks, regulatory clarity, financial viability and public acceptance. FABULOS addressed this challenge by helping cities test whether autonomous minibuses could become a realistic extension of urban transport systems rather than a stand-alone novelty. The stakeholders included municipalities, public-transport planners, technology suppliers and potential passengers, with the objective of exploring how self-driving shuttle services could improve first- and last-mile connectivity while fitting within real urban operating conditions. The solution was implemented through a Pre-Commercial Procurement process that engaged several consortia to design, prototype and pilot automated shuttle services in six European cities, including Helsinki, Gjesdal, Tallinn, Lamia and Helmond, as well as a site in Portugal. At a conceptual level, the project combined vehicle automation, service design, route integration and operational testing in live urban contexts to assess the technical, economic and societal feasibility of the model. This enabled cities to compare approaches and identify the conditions needed for broader adoption. The expected impact was improved understanding of autonomous public transport in practice, evidence on user acceptance and stronger readiness for future deployment decisions. More broadly, FABULOS created value by reducing uncertainty around automated mobility and supporting cities in planning more connected and innovative transport systems.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2513,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01893,EU_PSTW_202609_01893,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Jutland and Danish Island Bornholm,Fuel and energy,"In regions such as Jutland and Bornholm, energy operators face challenges in optimising district heating and power grid operations simultaneously.","AI-EFFECT, Advancing AI Integration in the European Energy Sector - Multi energy and sector coupling",Jutland and Danish Island Bornholm; Regional Government; Fuel and energy; Denmark,"This use case addresses the need for better coordination between energy systems, involving grid operators, heating network operators, and energy stakeholders, with the objective of improving efficiency and moving towards near-zero-emission energy supply. The approach enables more efficient use of renewable energy, lowers operational costs, and reduces CO₂ emissions.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Energy systems are becoming increasingly complex as electricity, heating, and other energy vectors must be coordinated to reduce emissions while maintaining reliability and affordability. In regions such as Jutland and Bornholm, energy operators face challenges in optimising district heating and power grid operations simultaneously. This use case addresses the need for better coordination between energy systems, involving grid operators, heating network operators, and energy stakeholders, with the objective of improving efficiency and moving towards near-zero-emission energy supply. The initiative implements advanced analytical and forecasting tools to optimise sector coupling between electricity grids and district heating networks. These tools support demand forecasting, plant operation optimisation, and coordinated control across multiple energy vectors, validated through targeted demonstrations. The approach enables more efficient use of renewable energy, lowers operational costs, and reduces CO₂ emissions. It strengthens system flexibility, improves reliability of district heating operations, and provides a validated pathway for deploying advanced optimisation tools across multi-energy systems in Europe.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2514,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01894,EU_PSTW_202609_01894,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,TU Delft,Fuel and energy,UNKNOWN,"AI-EFFECT, Advancing AI Integration in the European Energy Sector - Transmission Congestion Management Laboratory",TU Delft; Academic-Research; Fuel and energy; Netherlands,"This use case addresses these challenges by supporting the development and validation of congestion management solutions, involving transmission system operators, researchers, and control room specialists, with the objective of improving grid resilience and operational effectiveness.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Electricity transmission networks face growing congestion due to renewable integration, electrification, and increased demand, placing pressure on control rooms to manage grid stability in real time. Grid operators require safe environments to test and validate new operational approaches without risking live systems. This use case addresses these challenges by supporting the development and validation of congestion management solutions, involving transmission system operators, researchers, and control room specialists, with the objective of improving grid resilience and operational effectiveness. The Transmission Congestion Management Laboratory extends TU Delft’s Control Room of the Future with capabilities for synthetic grid data generation and algorithm testing. A digital-physical test environment enables rigorous verification of congestion management strategies, including sequential topological reconfigurations, under controlled conditions. The facility improves the robustness and reliability of congestion management solutions, reduces operational risk, and accelerates innovation by allowing experimentation before real-world deployment. It delivers clear value by strengthening grid security, supporting operator confidence, and enabling scalable validation of next-generation grid control tools.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2515,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01895,EU_PSTW_202609_01895,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"INESC TEC - Institute for Systems and Computer Engineering, Technology and Science",Fuel and energy,"Local energy communities are playing a growing role in the energy transition, yet effective energy sharing and optimisation are hindered by fragmented data access, privacy concerns, and limited consumer engagement.","AI-EFFECT, Advancing AI Integration in the European Energy Sector - Energy efficiency, management and sharing for local energy communities","INESC TEC - Institute for Systems and Computer Engineering, Technology and Science; Academic-Research; Fuel and energy; Portugal","This use case addresses these barriers by enabling secure, trusted data sharing for local energy communities, involving consumers, grid operators, service providers, and technology developers, with the objective of improving energy efficiency and supporting new community-based services. It delivers improved energy efficiency, better use of local renewable resources, and scalable foundations for community-driven energy systems.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Local energy communities are playing a growing role in the energy transition, yet effective energy sharing and optimisation are hindered by fragmented data access, privacy concerns, and limited consumer engagement. Traditional data platforms led by system operators are not fully designed for collaborative, user-centric energy services. This use case addresses these barriers by enabling secure, trusted data sharing for local energy communities, involving consumers, grid operators, service providers, and technology developers, with the objective of improving energy efficiency and supporting new community-based services. The initiative implements an Energy Data Space that provides a common framework for sharing energy data with explicit consent, supported by governance, security, and certification mechanisms. This environment enables the development and testing of data-driven services for energy management, e-mobility, and smart appliances, while respecting privacy and data sovereignty. The approach increases consumer trust and participation, supports more flexible grid management, and fosters cross-sector innovation. It delivers improved energy efficiency, better use of local renewable resources, and scalable foundations for community-driven energy systems.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F07_DATA_VISIBILITY_INTEGRATION_GAP,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2516,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01896,EU_PSTW_202609_01896,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Fraunhofer,Fuel and energy,UNKNOWN,"AI-EFFECT, Advancing AI Integration in the European Energy Sector - Distribution Network Congestion Management for Renewable Integration",Fraunhofer; Academic-Research; Fuel and energy; Germany,"This use case addresses these challenges by supporting the integration of advanced decision-support tools into distribution network operations, involving grid operators, engineers, and technology providers, with the objective of enabling efficient renewable integration and improved congestion management as part of business-as-usual operations. The solution is implemented through a flexible and reconfigurable laboratory environment that emulates real cyber-physical distribution systems, allowing advanced analytical and learning-based models to be developed, tested, and validated before deployment.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Electricity distribution networks are under increasing strain as renewable generation, electrification, and decentralised energy resources grow, leading to congestion and operational complexity at local grid level. Distribution system operators need more accurate, scalable, and real-time tools to manage congestion while maintaining high standards of safety, reliability, and security. This use case addresses these challenges by supporting the integration of advanced decision-support tools into distribution network operations, involving grid operators, engineers, and technology providers, with the objective of enabling efficient renewable integration and improved congestion management as part of business-as-usual operations. The solution is implemented through a flexible and reconfigurable laboratory environment that emulates real cyber-physical distribution systems, allowing advanced analytical and learning-based models to be developed, tested, and validated before deployment. The setup integrates simulators, synthetic data generation, and distribution system IT and OT components to assess performance, robustness, scalability, and real-time behaviour under realistic scenarios. This approach reduces operational risk, supports seamless integration with classical engineering methods, and accelerates innovation. It delivers improved grid efficiency, higher hosting capacity for renewables, stronger decision-making capabilities, and a validated framework for safely scaling advanced congestion management solutions across European distribution networks.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2517,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01897,EU_PSTW_202609_01897,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Amsterdam,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.",UNKNOWN,ChatAmsterdam - AI assistant for civil servants,"City of Amsterdam; Local Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Netherlands",The implementation delivers an internal digital assistant deployed within the municipality’s secure infrastructure.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Municipal employees increasingly work with large volumes of text, policy documents and complex information, which places pressure on time, consistency and quality of administrative work. At the same time, public organisations must ensure that sensitive information is handled securely and in line with public values. In the City of Amsterdam, civil servants required a trusted internal tool to support everyday tasks without relying on external commercial services that process data outside municipal control. The objective of this pilot is to improve productivity and information handling for municipal staff while maintaining full data sovereignty and compliance with public sector standards. The implementation delivers an internal digital assistant deployed within the municipality’s secure infrastructure. The system supports tasks such as drafting texts, summarising documents and analysing complex material, while ensuring that all inputs and outputs remain within the city’s digital environment and are not reused externally. The assistant was developed in alignment with the city’s Agenda Digitale Stad and integrated into existing work processes. During the six-month pilot, more than 800 civil servants used the tool for over 34,000 assignments. Results indicate time savings, improved consistency in document handling and high user acceptance. Qualitative impacts include increased trust in digital tools, enhanced staff efficiency and a reusable model that can be shared with other public administrations to support a resilient, human-centred digital public sector.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2518,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01898,EU_PSTW_202609_01898,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Netherlands Entreprise Agency,General public services n.e.c.,"Digital public services increasingly rely on text-based interfaces, which creates significant barriers for people with language deficits, low literacy levels, visual impairments or limited digital skills, including new Dutch citizens and status holders.",LOQUI - participation by speech,Netherlands Entreprise Agency; Central-Government; General public services n.e.c.; Netherlands,"The implementation develops a multilingual speech control tool supporting Dutch, English and Arabic, allowing users to navigate and complete digital services using voice commands. The system recognises speech input, identifies language difficulties in real time and adapts interactions accordingly, enabling more intuitive and accessible service use.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Digital public services increasingly rely on text-based interfaces, which creates significant barriers for people with language deficits, low literacy levels, visual impairments or limited digital skills, including new Dutch citizens and status holders. These barriers reduce access to essential services and limit participation in digital society. The objective of this pilot is to enable inclusive access to digital government services through speech-based interaction. The implementation develops a multilingual speech control tool supporting Dutch, English and Arabic, allowing users to navigate and complete digital services using voice commands. The system recognises speech input, identifies language difficulties in real time and adapts interactions accordingly, enabling more intuitive and accessible service use. Integration with existing digital government platforms allows speech to act as a universal interface layer rather than a separate service. The pilot demonstrated improved accessibility for vulnerable user groups, reduced dependency on intermediaries and greater autonomy in interacting with public services. Qualitative impacts include enhanced digital inclusion, improved user confidence and a scalable approach for making digital public services accessible to diverse populations.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2520,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01899,EU_PSTW_202609_01899,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Netherlands Entreprise Agency,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.",UNKNOWN,VITE-A2 Video and Text AI Analysis for Political Debates,"Netherlands Entreprise Agency; Central-Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Netherlands",UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Municipal council meetings are essential democratic forums, yet their length and complexity often discourage citizen engagement and limit transparency. In the Netherlands, local governments sought ways to make political debates more accessible to the public. The objective of this pilot is to improve understanding of municipal decision-making by transforming complex discussions into clear and engaging summaries. The implementation analyses video and textual recordings of council meetings, extracting key topics, arguments and emotional cues through multimodal analysis techniques. Outputs are presented in simplified formats that highlight relevant discussion points and sentiments, supporting easier interpretation by citizens. The pilot produced a paper prototype and feasibility studies informed by stakeholder feedback. Qualitative results include improved accessibility of political information, increased civic awareness and stronger potential for citizen engagement. The project demonstrates how advanced analysis of public meetings can support transparency and democratic participation at local level.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2521,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01900,EU_PSTW_202609_01900,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,INESC-ID,General public services n.e.c.,"Accelerat.AI addresses this challenge by modernising customer service delivery in both public and private sectors, involving government bodies, enterprises, technology providers, and citizens, with the objective of significantly increasing automation while improving user experience in European Portuguese.",Accelerat.AI - Accelerating digital transformation in Portugal,INESC-ID; Academic-Research; General public services n.e.c.; Portugal,"The initiative is implemented through the development of advanced conversational agents and Contact Center as a Service platforms tailored to linguistic and demographic variation, trained on large-scale, high-quality language and speech datasets.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Public administrations and businesses in Portugal face rising demand for efficient, accessible customer service across multiple channels, while language diversity, regional accents, and ageing populations limit the effectiveness of generic digital solutions. Accelerat.AI addresses this challenge by modernising customer service delivery in both public and private sectors, involving government bodies, enterprises, technology providers, and citizens, with the objective of significantly increasing automation while improving user experience in European Portuguese. The initiative is implemented through the development of advanced conversational agents and Contact Center as a Service platforms tailored to linguistic and demographic variation, trained on large-scale, high-quality language and speech datasets. These systems are integrated into existing customer service infrastructures to support voice and text interactions, automated case handling, and escalation to human agents when required. Funded with €35 million under Portugal’s Recovery and Resilience Plan and Next Generation EU, the project targets automation of up to 80% of customer interactions. The initiative delivers substantial efficiency gains, reduces operational costs, improves service accessibility, and strengthens digital sovereignty by developing language technologies aligned with European values and local needs.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2524,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01901,EU_PSTW_202609_01901,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,AtlanTTic research centre of the University of Vigo,General public services n.e.c.,"In Galicia, the lack of large, high-quality datasets constrained the development of conversational systems in Galician and Spanish.",FalAI - A Dataset for End-to-end Spoken Language Understanding in a Low-Resource Scenario,AtlanTTic research centre of the University of Vigo; Academic-Research; General public services n.e.c.; Spain,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Many European languages remain underrepresented in voice-based digital services, limiting access to technology for speakers of minority languages. In Galicia, the lack of large, high-quality datasets constrained the development of conversational systems in Galician and Spanish. The objective of this pilot is to promote linguistic diversity and digital inclusion by creating open datasets for spoken language understanding. The implementation mobilised large-scale citizen participation through the #A30frases campaign, collecting voice recordings of prototype sentences across public sector domains such as healthcare and social care. The resulting dataset exceeds 260,000 recordings from around 12,000 contributors and is openly available for research and development. The pilot significantly improved the availability of training data for low-resource languages, supporting the development of inclusive voice technologies. Qualitative impacts include strengthened linguistic equality, increased research capacity and long-term benefits for public service accessibility across Europe.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2526,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01902,EU_PSTW_202609_01902,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""social_nonprofit"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Italian Ministry of Foreign Affairs and International Cooperation,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.","Citizens and businesses interacting with diplomatic and consular services often face complex procedures and time-zone constraints, leading to delays and information gaps.",Virtual assistant for services - first step towards artificial intelligence in diplomacy,"Italian Ministry of Foreign Affairs and International Cooperation; Central-Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Italy","Initially deployed across 20 pilot locations, the system integrates with existing digital service channels.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Citizens and businesses interacting with diplomatic and consular services often face complex procedures and time-zone constraints, leading to delays and information gaps. The Italian Ministry of Foreign Affairs sought to improve service accessibility and consistency across its global network. The objective of this pilot is to provide continuous, multilingual guidance for consular and administrative services. The implementation introduces a virtual assistant available on ministry, embassy and consulate websites, supporting six languages and operating around the clock. The assistant guides users through structured decision trees based on frequently asked questions and common procedures, optimised during development through analysis of historical enquiries. Initially deployed across 20 pilot locations, the system integrates with existing digital service channels. The pilot improved response availability, reduced pressure on frontline staff and enhanced user satisfaction. Qualitative benefits include more consistent service delivery, improved accessibility for international users and a foundation for future digital transformation in diplomatic services.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2527,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01903,EU_PSTW_202609_01903,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Aena (Aeropuertos Españoles y Navegación Aérea),Transport,UNKNOWN,OLI Chatbot across spanish airports,Aena (Aeropuertos Españoles y Navegación Aérea); Central-Government; Transport; Spain,"This use case involves airport authorities, passengers, airlines, and service providers, with the objective of delivering timely, accurate information across multiple channels. OLI is implemented as a multilingual digital assistant integrated with operational airport systems, including flight information displays and service directories.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Airports manage complex passenger flows and high volumes of enquiries related to flights, terminals, and services, particularly during peak travel periods. AENA, the operator of Spanish airports, introduced OLI to improve passenger experience and reduce pressure on staff. This use case involves airport authorities, passengers, airlines, and service providers, with the objective of delivering timely, accurate information across multiple channels. OLI is implemented as a multilingual digital assistant integrated with operational airport systems, including flight information displays and service directories. It is accessible via WhatsApp, the AENA website and app, and social media platforms, enabling passengers to receive real-time updates on flights, boarding gates, terminal navigation, and available services. The assistant continuously retrieves and delivers live operational data in multiple languages. By April 2024, OLI had interacted with approximately 940,000 users, supporting its rollout to additional airports. The impact includes improved passenger satisfaction, reduced queues at information desks, and more efficient airport operations. OLI enhances accessibility, supports scalable customer service, and contributes to smoother passenger journeys across Spain’s airport network.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2528,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01904,EU_PSTW_202609_01904,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Ministry of Inclusion,Social protection n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,Spain’s Social Security administration: Using AI to deliver benefits to citizens more efficiently with the ISSA Social Security Virtual Assistant,Ministry of Inclusion; Central-Government; Social protection n.e.c.; Spain,"The use case involves citizens, Social Security staff, and public service managers, with the objective of improving accessibility, reducing administrative burden, and ensuring consistent information delivery. The solution is implemented as an online conversational assistant that provides 24/7 responses on frequently requested procedures and benefits.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Spain’s Social Security administration handles millions of citizen enquiries each year, creating heavy workloads and long waiting times for routine information requests. To improve service delivery, the administration introduced a virtual assistant to provide immediate access to official guidance. The use case involves citizens, Social Security staff, and public service managers, with the objective of improving accessibility, reducing administrative burden, and ensuring consistent information delivery. The solution is implemented as an online conversational assistant that provides 24/7 responses on frequently requested procedures and benefits. Initially covering 37 core services, it retrieves information from official Social Security databases and guidance repositories. Integrated into digital service channels, it allows citizens to self-serve for common enquiries while redirecting complex cases to human agents. In its first month alone, the assistant handled over 2 million interactions, demonstrating high demand and scalability. The impact includes faster access to information, reduced call-centre pressure, and improved operational efficiency. Citizens benefit from immediate, reliable responses, while the administration reallocates staff time to higher-value tasks, delivering measurable improvements in public service performance.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2529,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01905,EU_PSTW_202609_01905,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Red.es,General services.,"Public funding programmes supporting SMEs often involve extensive document verification, which can slow payments and increase administrative costs. Integrated into the grant management workflow, it reduces manual checks and standardises evaluation.",Kit Consulting – AI-Enabled Grant Processing,Red.es; Central-Government; General services.; Spain,"The use case involves Red.es, digital consultants, SMEs, and auditors, with the objective of accelerating document review, improving quality control, and ensuring transparency. The solution is implemented through a digital platform that automatically analyses submitted documentation, detecting plagiarism, duplicates, and quality issues.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Public funding programmes supporting SMEs often involve extensive document verification, which can slow payments and increase administrative costs. Spain’s Kit Consulting programme sought to modernise this process while maintaining rigorous oversight. The use case involves Red.es, digital consultants, SMEs, and auditors, with the objective of accelerating document review, improving quality control, and ensuring transparency. The solution is implemented through a digital platform that automatically analyses submitted documentation, detecting plagiarism, duplicates, and quality issues. Using advanced pattern recognition and language analysis techniques, the system performs large-scale comparisons across documents and flags anomalies for review. Integrated into the grant management workflow, it reduces manual checks and standardises evaluation. Processing time per document has been reduced from around five hours to 1.5 hours—an 80% reduction—enabling the handling of approximately 45,000 documents and up to one billion comparisons. The impact includes faster payments, improved traceability, reduced fraud risk, and significant cost savings. The programme benefits from scalable, secure processing, while SMEs receive quicker support, strengthening trust and efficiency in public funding delivery.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2530,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01906,EU_PSTW_202609_01906,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Valencia,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.",UNKNOWN,City of Valencia chatbot for citizens on tax enquiries,"City of Valencia; Local Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Spain",UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Operating on the millie platform from 1MillionBot, Gestri offers seamless integration into various communication channels, providing instant responses in Valencian, Spanish and English. This platform allows complete data management and personalisation of the assistant, ensuring that each interaction is effective and adapted to the user's needs. The constant availability of Gestri, which operates 24 hours a day through the Provincial Council web portal and other digital media, has optimised taxpayer service. This has allowed the Valencia Provincial Council to free up resources for tasks that require a more human and specialised approach.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2531,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01907,EU_PSTW_202609_01907,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Valencian Regional Government,Social protection n.e.c.,"Rental subsidy programmes are critical social policies but are often slowed by manual document verification and repeated requests for information. Integrated into the subsidy application workflow, it enables straight-through processing for standard cases and flags only complex or ambiguous applications for manual review.",Valencia Provincial Council – Gestri Tax Enquiries Chatbot,Valencian Regional Government; Regional Government; Social protection n.e.c.; Spain,"This use case involves applicants, housing authorities, and administrative staff, with the objective of reducing processing times and administrative burden while improving service quality. The impact includes significantly shorter verification times, faster decisions for applicants, and more efficient use of staff resources.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Rental subsidy programmes are critical social policies but are often slowed by manual document verification and repeated requests for information. The Valencian Regional Government developed a digital solution to streamline these procedures. This use case involves applicants, housing authorities, and administrative staff, with the objective of reducing processing times and administrative burden while improving service quality. The solution automatically verifies rental contracts and payslips submitted by applicants, cross-checking data to avoid redundant documentation requests. Integrated into the subsidy application workflow, it enables straight-through processing for standard cases and flags only complex or ambiguous applications for manual review. The impact includes significantly shorter verification times, faster decisions for applicants, and more efficient use of staff resources. Administrative personnel can concentrate on cases requiring human judgement, improving overall programme effectiveness. The solution enhances scalability, consistency, and fairness in subsidy delivery, strengthening public trust and ensuring timely support for households in need.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2532,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01908,EU_PSTW_202609_01908,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Ministry of Science, Innovation, and Universities",Tertiary education.,UNKNOWN,Recognition of university degrees with AI,"Ministry of Science, Innovation, and Universities; Central-Government; Tertiary education.; Spain","This use case involves applicants, universities, professional bodies, and ministry staff, with the objective of reducing waiting times and simplifying procedures. The solution automates document handling, validation, and comparison against national qualification frameworks, eliminating repetitive paperwork and manual checks.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Lengthy recognition processes for foreign university degrees have historically delayed labour market integration for qualified professionals in Spain. The Ministry of Science, Innovation, and Universities sought to modernise this process to address skills shortages and administrative inefficiencies. This use case involves applicants, universities, professional bodies, and ministry staff, with the objective of reducing waiting times and simplifying procedures. The solution automates document handling, validation, and comparison against national qualification frameworks, eliminating repetitive paperwork and manual checks. Integrated into ministerial systems, it standardises workflows and accelerates decision-making. The impact is a reduction in recognition timelines from years to months, enabling faster entry of foreign professionals into high-value sectors. The ministry benefits from increased efficiency and policy effectiveness, while applicants experience clearer, faster outcomes. The solution supports labour market mobility, improves public administration performance, and contributes to economic competitiveness.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2533,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01909,EU_PSTW_202609_01909,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Spanish Tax Agency (AEAT),"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.","Expected impacts include fewer errors in tax filings, reduced administrative burden for both taxpayers and staff, and more targeted audit activities.",AI for Fiscal Compliance,"Spanish Tax Agency (AEAT); Central-Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Spain","The implementation applies analytical models to identify common reporting errors, inconsistencies and risk patterns in declarations and customs data.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Tax administrations face growing complexity in managing large volumes of data while ensuring compliance, fairness and efficiency. The Spanish Tax Agency aims to modernise tax and customs operations by shifting from reactive enforcement to preventive and supportive compliance models. The objective of this initiative is to simplify taxpayer obligations, improve data accuracy and reduce unnecessary audits. The implementation applies analytical models to identify common reporting errors, inconsistencies and risk patterns in declarations and customs data. These tools are embedded within a human-in-the-loop framework, where tax professionals continuously supervise, validate and refine system outputs. The approach prioritises transparency, accountability and support for taxpayers rather than automated enforcement. As the system is still in development, testing focuses on reliability, explainability and operational integration. Expected impacts include fewer errors in tax filings, reduced administrative burden for both taxpayers and staff, and more targeted audit activities. Qualitative benefits include improved trust in tax administration, better allocation of inspection resources and enhanced effectiveness of fiscal governance.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,F08_ERROR_COMPLIANCE_RISK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2534,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01910,EU_PSTW_202609_01910,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Comisión Nacional de los Mercados y la Competencia (CNMC),Economic affairs n.e.c,UNKNOWN,BRAVA (Bid Rigging Algorithm for Vigilance in Antitrust),Comisión Nacional de los Mercados y la Competencia (CNMC); Central-Government; Economic affairs n.e.c; Spain,"The use case involves procurement authorities, competition regulators, and public bodies, with the objective of identifying anti-competitive behaviour more effectively.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Public procurement is vulnerable to collusion and bid rigging, which undermine competition and waste public funds. Spain’s competition authority, CNMC, developed BRAVA to strengthen oversight of procurement markets. The use case involves procurement authorities, competition regulators, and public bodies, with the objective of identifying anti-competitive behaviour more effectively. BRAVA analyses large datasets from public tenders, examining bidding patterns, pricing behaviours, and relationships between participants. It classifies tenders as competitive or potentially collusive, supporting investigators with risk-based prioritisation. Integrated into CNMC analytical workflows, the tool enhances early detection and evidence gathering. The impact includes improved enforcement capability, more efficient investigations, and stronger deterrence of anti-competitive practices. Recognised with a national award, BRAVA delivers significant public value by protecting competition, improving market integrity, and safeguarding public spending.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2535,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01911,EU_PSTW_202609_01911,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Ministry of Education and Vocational Training, Regional Government of Andalusia",Education n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,HipatIA - AI-driven forecasting tool for educational services,"Ministry of Education and Vocational Training, Regional Government of Andalusia; Regional Government; Education n.e.c.; Spain","This use case involves education departments, schools, and policymakers, with the objective of improving responsiveness and educational outcomes. The impact includes better anticipation of staffing and infrastructure needs, improved student support through targeted tutoring and guidance, and more efficient use of public resources.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Education authorities must anticipate changing student needs to allocate teachers, classrooms, and training programmes effectively. The Andalusian Regional Government introduced HipatIA to support proactive planning. This use case involves education departments, schools, and policymakers, with the objective of improving responsiveness and educational outcomes. HipatIA analyses historical student data to forecast key indicators such as enrolment trends, dropout risks, and demand for vocational training. Integrated into education planning systems, it supports evidence-based decisions on resource allocation and early interventions. The impact includes better anticipation of staffing and infrastructure needs, improved student support through targeted tutoring and guidance, and more efficient use of public resources. Education authorities gain forward-looking insights, while schools benefit from improved planning certainty. The solution enhances service quality, equity, and sustainability in the education system.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION|S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2536,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01912,EU_PSTW_202609_01912,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Barcelona City Council,Transport,"Urban congestion contributes significantly to travel delays, emissions and reduced quality of life in large cities.",Smart lighting with AI in Barcelona,Barcelona City Council; Local Government; Transport; Spain,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Urban congestion contributes significantly to travel delays, emissions and reduced quality of life in large cities. In Barcelona, traffic management required more adaptive solutions to respond to real-time conditions. The objective of this pilot is to reduce congestion and emissions by optimising traffic light operations. The implementation deploys sensor-equipped traffic lights that collect real-time traffic flow data and dynamically adjust signal timings. Analytical models assess traffic density and optimise phase durations to improve throughput during peak hours. The system integrates with the city’s broader mobility infrastructure under the Sustainable Mobility Plan. The pilot aims to reduce congestion by up to 20%, shorten travel times and lower carbon emissions. Qualitative impacts include smoother traffic flows, improved air quality and a scalable framework for intelligent urban traffic management.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2537,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01913,EU_PSTW_202609_01913,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Spanish Ministry of the Presidency, Justice and Parliamentary Relation",Law courts,"The impact includes faster document preparation, reduced manual effort, improved compliance with data protection requirements, and enhanced productivity for legal professionals.",ADIA - the AI Document Assistant for spanish Courts,"Spanish Ministry of the Presidency, Justice and Parliamentary Relation; Central-Government; Law courts; Spain","This use case involves judges, clerks, legal professionals, and court administrators, with the objective of streamlining document handling while ensuring confidentiality. ADIA is implemented as a secure digital service accessible through internal judicial networks.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Judicial systems handle vast volumes of documents containing sensitive personal data, making anonymisation and summarisation time-consuming but essential. Spain’s Ministry of Justice introduced ADIA to improve efficiency and data protection. This use case involves judges, clerks, legal professionals, and court administrators, with the objective of streamlining document handling while ensuring confidentiality. ADIA is implemented as a secure digital service accessible through internal judicial networks. It automatically removes personal identifiers from documents and generates concise summaries highlighting key information. Integrated with existing court systems, it supports nationwide use through a web interface. The impact includes faster document preparation, reduced manual effort, improved compliance with data protection requirements, and enhanced productivity for legal professionals. ADIA delivers measurable efficiency gains, strengthens privacy safeguards, and supports a more modern, responsive justice system.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2538,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01914,EU_PSTW_202609_01914,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Valencian Regional Govermment,General services.,"Businesses and self-employed professionals often face fragmented administrative procedures, duplicated requests for information, and difficulty understanding regulatory obligations across different public bodies. The Generalitat Valenciana launched Canal Empresa to reduce administrative burden and improve the relationship between the business community and public administration.",Canal Empresa - reducing administrative burden and providing regulatory information for businesses,Valencian Regional Govermment; Regional Government; General services.; Spain,"Canal Empresa is implemented as an integrated digital platform that consolidates information and services from the Generalitat and its public-sector organisations, covering the full business lifecycle from start-up to daily operations.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Businesses and self-employed professionals often face fragmented administrative procedures, duplicated requests for information, and difficulty understanding regulatory obligations across different public bodies. The Generalitat Valenciana launched Canal Empresa to reduce administrative burden and improve the relationship between the business community and public administration. The platform involves regional authorities, public-sector entities, companies, and freelancers, with the objective of centralising procedures, guidance, and regulatory information in a single digital access point. Canal Empresa is implemented as an integrated digital platform that consolidates information and services from the Generalitat and its public-sector organisations, covering the full business lifecycle from start-up to daily operations. A built-in virtual assistant provides immediate guidance, helping users navigate procedures, identify applicable regulations, and access the correct forms and services. The platform integrates with existing administrative back-office systems to ensure consistency and up-to-date content. The impact includes faster access to information, fewer errors in submissions, and reduced need for in-person or telephone support. Businesses benefit from clearer processes and time savings, while the administration gains efficiency, improved service quality, and better compliance outcomes, strengthening the region’s overall business environment.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2539,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01915,EU_PSTW_202609_01915,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Andalusian Regional Government,Education n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,PREDUCA - Student learning pathways personalisation,Andalusian Regional Government; Regional Government; Education n.e.c.; Spain,"This use case involves students, teachers, school leaders, and education authorities, with the objective of identifying individual learning needs early and enabling tailored educational interventions. Educators use these insights to adapt lesson plans, materials, and classroom strategies.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Public education systems must address diverse student needs while planning resources efficiently at scale. The Andalusian Government developed PREDUCA to personalise learning pathways and support strategic planning in public education. This use case involves students, teachers, school leaders, and education authorities, with the objective of identifying individual learning needs early and enabling tailored educational interventions. PREDUCA builds on the HipatIA system and is integrated into the regional school management platform. It analyses historical academic performance, attendance, and behavioural data to detect patterns and predict future learning needs. Based on these insights, the system generates recommendations for targeted interventions, such as early support for at-risk students or enriched activities for high achievers. Educators use these insights to adapt lesson plans, materials, and classroom strategies. The impact includes more timely and personalised support for students, improved educational outcomes, and better use of teaching resources. Education authorities gain predictive insights for planning staffing and programmes, while schools benefit from data-informed decision-making, contributing to a more equitable and effective education system.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2540,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01916,EU_PSTW_202609_01916,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Basque Regional Government,Education n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,"ESKOLA ADIMENTSUA - Automation, robotics, and artificial intelligence in teaching",Basque Regional Government; Regional Government; Education n.e.c.; Spain,Eskola Adimentsua is implemented as a comprehensive programme combining curriculum innovation with sustained teacher support.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Education systems must prepare students and teachers for a society shaped by automation, robotics, and advanced digital technologies. The Basque Government launched Eskola Adimentsua for the 2024–25 academic year to embed these competencies into teaching and learning. The initiative involves schools, teachers, students, and education authorities, with the objective of promoting active learning and strengthening digital skills across the education system. Eskola Adimentsua is implemented as a comprehensive programme combining curriculum innovation with sustained teacher support. A core component is professional development, offering mentoring, peer collaboration, and continuous guidance to help educators integrate computational thinking, programming, and digital tools into everyday teaching. Teachers are supported throughout the entire process, from design to classroom implementation. The impact includes increased teacher confidence, improved digital literacy among students, and stronger alignment between education and future labour market needs. By empowering educators as innovation leaders, the programme delivers long-term value, fostering resilient, future-ready schools and positioning education as a driver of digital transformation.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2541,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01917,EU_PSTW_202609_01917,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,La Fe Hospital - Valencian Regional Government,Hospital services.,UNKNOWN,AI to anticipate peaks in Hospital La Fe (Valencia),La Fe Hospital - Valencian Regional Government; Regional Government; Hospital services.; Spain,"This use case involves hospital management, clinicians, and operational teams, with the objective of enabling proactive resource planning and improving patient flow. The impact includes improved preparedness, reduced overcrowding, and more efficient use of clinical resources.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Large hospitals face unpredictable fluctuations in emergency department demand, which can strain staff, facilities, and patient care. Hospital Universitari i Politècnic La Fe in Valencia sought to improve its ability to anticipate peaks in pressure within its Adult Emergency Department. This use case involves hospital management, clinicians, and operational teams, with the objective of enabling proactive resource planning and improving patient flow. The solution combines anonymised historical patient data with real-time operational information to estimate the likelihood of hospital admission for incoming patients. Key factors such as age, triage priority, prior conditions, previous admissions, and diagnostic test results are analysed to support early decision-making. Integrated into hospital information systems, the tool provides staff with actionable insights to anticipate surges and adjust staffing, beds, and workflows accordingly. The impact includes improved preparedness, reduced overcrowding, and more efficient use of clinical resources. Patients benefit from smoother care pathways, while the hospital improves service quality, resilience, and operational efficiency under pressure.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2542,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01918,EU_PSTW_202609_01918,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Spanish hospitals (e.g. La Fe, among others)",Outpatient services.,LOLA addresses this challenge by extending clinical monitoring into patients’ homes.,LOLA Chatbot for healthcare services,"Spanish hospitals (e.g. La Fe, among others); Local Government; Outpatient services.; Spain","This use case involves hospitals, clinicians, chronically ill and elderly patients, and health authorities, with the objective of improving continuity of care and reducing unnecessary in-person interactions. LOLA is implemented as a cloud-based virtual healthcare assistant that conducts automated medical phone conversations according to predefined clinical protocols.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Healthcare systems face growing demand for chronic care and elderly monitoring, while resources remain constrained. LOLA addresses this challenge by extending clinical monitoring into patients’ homes. This use case involves hospitals, clinicians, chronically ill and elderly patients, and health authorities, with the objective of improving continuity of care and reducing unnecessary in-person interactions. LOLA is implemented as a cloud-based virtual healthcare assistant that conducts automated medical phone conversations according to predefined clinical protocols. It integrates with hospital information systems and allows clinicians to configure patient cohorts, monitoring schedules, and escalation rules. The platform can perform tens of thousands of calls per day without local installation, enabling rapid adoption across regions and specialties. The impact includes earlier detection of health issues, reduced workload for healthcare professionals, and improved patient experience through regular, structured follow-up. Hospitals benefit from scalable, cost-effective monitoring, while patients receive personalised attention at home, contributing to better outcomes and more sustainable healthcare delivery.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2543,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01919,EU_PSTW_202609_01919,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Barcelona,Old age.,"Organisations in sectors such as transport, energy and finance face complex optimisation problems that are difficult to solve with conventional computing methods. In Galicia, strategic industries required innovative approaches to manage large-scale combinatorial challenges.",ARI (Intelligent Robotic Assistant),City of Barcelona; Local Government; Old age.; Spain,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Organisations in sectors such as transport, energy and finance face complex optimisation problems that are difficult to solve with conventional computing methods. In Galicia, strategic industries required innovative approaches to manage large-scale combinatorial challenges. The objective of this pilot is to explore advanced optimisation techniques for real-world business scenarios. The implementation investigates quantum-inspired and quantum computing methods to address routing, scheduling and resource allocation problems. Collaborative experimentation between the Galician Supercomputer Centre and industry experts enables testing of algorithms on realistic datasets and constraints. The pilot provided insights into performance improvements, feasibility and future applicability of quantum approaches. Qualitative impacts include enhanced optimisation capabilities, strengthened regional innovation ecosystems and a clearer pathway for integrating next-generation computing into business decision-making.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2544,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01920,EU_PSTW_202609_01920,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Madrid,Old age.,Unwanted loneliness among elderly people is a growing social challenge with significant health and wellbeing implications.,PALOMA - Madrid’s AI-powered virtual assistant,City of Madrid; Local Government; Old age.; Spain,"This use case involves social services, elderly citizens, and care providers, with the objective of detecting vulnerability early and connecting individuals to appropriate support.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Unwanted loneliness among elderly people is a growing social challenge with significant health and wellbeing implications. The City of Madrid introduced PALOMA to proactively identify and support older residents at risk of social isolation. This use case involves social services, elderly citizens, and care providers, with the objective of detecting vulnerability early and connecting individuals to appropriate support. PALOMA systematically contacts residents aged over 75 who live alone, initiating personalised phone conversations based on a short wellbeing questionnaire. The process respects privacy and data protection and maintains human oversight, ensuring that social workers remain involved in assessment and follow-up. When risk indicators are detected, PALOMA triggers referrals to municipal services or community support. The impact includes earlier identification of loneliness, more targeted interventions, and better allocation of social worker time. The initiative improves quality of life for older residents, strengthens preventive social care, and supports a more humane and efficient response to an increasingly pressing societal issue.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2545,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01921,EU_PSTW_202609_01921,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Universitat Oberta de Catalunya (UOC),General services.,"However, limited experimentation spaces and unclear governance frameworks often slow responsible innovation.","ADMAIES Project (Improving administrative decision-making through the use of AI, experimentation, and sandboxes)",Universitat Oberta de Catalunya (UOC); Academic-Research; General services.; Spain,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Public administrations increasingly rely on complex decision-making processes that must balance efficiency, transparency and legal certainty. However, limited experimentation spaces and unclear governance frameworks often slow responsible innovation. The objective of the ADMAIES project is to explore how experimental approaches, including regulatory sandboxes, can support the responsible integration of analytical technologies into administrative decision-making. The implementation is led by academic researchers and focuses on designing, testing and evaluating decision-support tools within controlled environments. These experiments examine how such tools interact with legal frameworks, organisational practices and ethical requirements. Rather than deploying operational systems, the project generates evidence, methodologies and governance models that public administrations can adopt. Expected impacts include improved understanding of risks and benefits, clearer guidelines for responsible experimentation and stronger institutional capacity to innovate. Qualitative outcomes include enhanced transparency, better alignment between technology and administrative law, and a reusable framework for safely testing digital solutions in the public sector.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2546,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01922,EU_PSTW_202609_01922,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Andalusian Regional Government,General services.,"Public-sector recruitment processes often require extensive manual verification of candidates’ qualifications, creating delays and heavy workloads. The impact includes significantly reduced processing times, improved consistency in scoring, and lower administrative burden.",Streamlining talent acquisition and verification with RPA (Robotic Process Automation),Andalusian Regional Government; Regional Government; General services.; Spain,"This use case involves recruitment panels, administrative staff, and job candidates, with the objective of accelerating evaluations while maintaining fairness and accuracy. The solution automates repetitive verification tasks, such as checking academic qualifications, professional experience, and training records against defined criteria.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Public-sector recruitment processes often require extensive manual verification of candidates’ qualifications, creating delays and heavy workloads. The Andalusian Health Service introduced robotic process automation to streamline verification of certificates and documents submitted during selection processes. This use case involves recruitment panels, administrative staff, and job candidates, with the objective of accelerating evaluations while maintaining fairness and accuracy. The solution automates repetitive verification tasks, such as checking academic qualifications, professional experience, and training records against defined criteria. Integrated into recruitment workflows, the system processes large volumes of documentation consistently and efficiently. Since its launch in July 2022, approximately 14,000 merits have been verified. The impact includes significantly reduced processing times, improved consistency in scoring, and lower administrative burden. Staff can focus on complex assessments and decision-making, while candidates benefit from faster, more transparent recruitment processes, improving trust and efficiency in public employment systems.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,S6_WORKFLOW_PROCESS_AUTOMATION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2547,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01923,EU_PSTW_202609_01923,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Galician Supercomputer Centre (CESGA),General services.,"Many strategic sectors, including transport, logistics, energy and finance, face complex optimisation problems where the number of possible solutions grows exponentially, making traditional approaches inefficient or impractical. In Galicia, organisations struggled to optimise routes, schedules and resource allocation while respecting multiple operational constraints.",Optimising businesses: Unlocking the potential of Quantum Computing,Galician Supercomputer Centre (CESGA); Academic-Research; General services.; Spain,Representative industrial problems were modelled mathematically and tested using emerging quantum programming techniques and high-performance computing environments.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Many strategic sectors, including transport, logistics, energy and finance, face complex optimisation problems where the number of possible solutions grows exponentially, making traditional approaches inefficient or impractical. In Galicia, organisations struggled to optimise routes, schedules and resource allocation while respecting multiple operational constraints. The objective of this pilot is to explore advanced optimisation approaches that can deliver better solutions to real-world business problems. The implementation was led by the Galician Supercomputer Centre in collaboration with Ayesa and focused on applying quantum and quantum-inspired algorithms to combinatorial optimisation scenarios. Representative industrial problems were modelled mathematically and tested using emerging quantum programming techniques and high-performance computing environments. The pilot evaluated feasibility, performance gains and integration pathways with existing decision-support systems. Results showed improved solution quality for selected optimisation problems and provided insights into where quantum approaches can complement classical methods. Qualitative impacts include strengthened innovation capacity, increased readiness of regional industries to adopt next-generation computing, and clearer pathways towards future commercial deployment as quantum technologies mature.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2548,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01924,EU_PSTW_202609_01924,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Ibermatica,General services.,"Industrial companies often face shared optimisation and simulation challenges that limit efficiency, sustainability and competitiveness, yet lack practical frameworks to adopt emerging computational approaches.",Q4Real: Quantum Computing for real industries,Ibermatica; Consortium; General services.; Spain,These elements were tested on representative industrial use cases to assess feasibility and performance.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Industrial companies often face shared optimisation and simulation challenges that limit efficiency, sustainability and competitiveness, yet lack practical frameworks to adopt emerging computational approaches. The Basque Government identified the need to accelerate adoption of advanced solutions across the industrial sector. The objective of this pilot is to bridge the gap between experimental quantum technologies and real industrial applications with sustainable impact. The implementation established a collaborative framework bringing together industry, research and technology providers to standardise quantum programming practices. The project developed reusable components, reference architectures and a shared knowledge base for different classes of quantum problems, alongside emerging quantum software engineering methodologies. These elements were tested on representative industrial use cases to assess feasibility and performance. The pilot strengthened technical capacity, reduced barriers to entry and aligned innovation efforts with market needs. Qualitative impacts include improved industrial readiness, shared standards for future development and a clearer route to commercialisation of quantum-enabled solutions, supporting long-term digital leadership.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2549,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01925,EU_PSTW_202609_01925,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Ministry of Economic Affairs and Red.es,Transport,"At María Zambrano train station, managing complex spatial layouts and real-time operational events posed challenges for both travellers and maintenance teams.",5G pilot in Andalusia for the María Zambrano Train Station,Ministry of Economic Affairs and Red.es; Central-Government; Transport; Spain,"The implementation created a detailed digital twin of the station using Building Information Modelling, hosted in a cloud environment and connected via 5G.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Large transport hubs require efficient passenger navigation and proactive infrastructure maintenance to ensure safety, comfort and operational continuity. At María Zambrano train station, managing complex spatial layouts and real-time operational events posed challenges for both travellers and maintenance teams. The objective of this pilot is to enhance passenger experience and asset management through immersive digital tools. The implementation created a detailed digital twin of the station using Building Information Modelling, hosted in a cloud environment and connected via 5G. Two augmented reality applications were developed: a passenger guidance tool providing real-time navigation and points of interest, and a maintenance application supporting preventive and corrective tasks through visual overlays. The high-speed connectivity ensured immediate synchronisation of real-time data, including crowd levels and incidents. The pilot improved navigation efficiency, reduced maintenance response times and enhanced situational awareness. Qualitative benefits include improved service quality, safer operations and a scalable model for smart transport infrastructure.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2550,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01926,EU_PSTW_202609_01926,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Agenzia Italiana del Farmaco,Health n.e.c.,"Public agencies managing complex regulatory domains often struggle to retrieve precise information quickly from large internal document repositories. The impact includes faster response times, reduced manual searching, and improved consistency of information provided.",Margot AI supporting the Agenzia Italiana del Farmaco - document search case,Agenzia Italiana del Farmaco; Central-Government; Health n.e.c.; Italy,"This use case involves AIFA staff, regulated stakeholders, and citizens, with the objective of improving response accuracy and efficiency. Margot AI is implemented using a retrieval-augmented generation architecture that connects natural language queries to internal databases and document collections.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Public agencies managing complex regulatory domains often struggle to retrieve precise information quickly from large internal document repositories. The Italian Medicines Agency adopted Margot AI to support customer service and internal staff in accessing authoritative information. This use case involves AIFA staff, regulated stakeholders, and citizens, with the objective of improving response accuracy and efficiency. Margot AI is implemented using a retrieval-augmented generation architecture that connects natural language queries to internal databases and document collections. It searches, classifies, and retrieves relevant content, delivering precise answers while maintaining alignment with official sources. Integrated across existing systems and communication channels, it automates repetitive requests and supports staff in handling complex enquiries. The impact includes faster response times, reduced manual searching, and improved consistency of information provided. AIFA benefits from more efficient service delivery and better use of institutional knowledge, strengthening regulatory transparency and operational effectiveness.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,S1_KNOWLEDGE_RETRIEVAL_RAG,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2551,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01927,EU_PSTW_202609_01927,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Agenzia Italiana del Farmaco,Health n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,Margot AI supporting the Agenzia Italiana del Farmaco - FarmaciLine assistance,Agenzia Italiana del Farmaco; Central-Government; Health n.e.c.; Italy,"This use case involves citizens, healthcare professionals, and AIFA operators, with the objective of delivering accurate, timely information on medicines.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Citizens frequently seek reliable information on authorised medicines, indications, and regulatory decisions, placing high demand on public health information services. Within AIFA’s FarmaciLine service, Margot AI supports responses to these enquiries. This use case involves citizens, healthcare professionals, and AIFA operators, with the objective of delivering accurate, timely information on medicines. The system retrieves information from official sources such as Summaries of Product Characteristics and AIFA resolutions, ensuring responses remain authoritative. Integrated into FarmaciLine workflows, it handles repetitive questions and supports multilingual, consistent communication. The impact includes improved accessibility to trusted medicines information, reduced workload for operators, and enhanced user satisfaction. Citizens benefit from clear, immediate answers, while AIFA strengthens its role as a reliable source of pharmaceutical information.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2552,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01928,EU_PSTW_202609_01928,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Agenzia Italiana del Farmaco,Health n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,Margot AI supporting the Agenzia Italiana del Farmaco - Monitoring platform,Agenzia Italiana del Farmaco; Central-Government; Health n.e.c.; Italy,"AIFA manages multiple monitoring registries used to track medicine use in clinical practice, which are complex and heterogeneous. This use case supports AIFA staff by simplifying interaction with monitoring registries.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"AIFA manages multiple monitoring registries used to track medicine use in clinical practice, which are complex and heterogeneous. Accessing the correct information quickly is essential for regulatory oversight and operational efficiency. This use case supports AIFA staff by simplifying interaction with monitoring registries. Margot AI retrieves relevant data and guidance from different registry systems, including therapeutic plans and multi-drug registries, through a unified conversational interface. Integrated with internal platforms, it automates information retrieval and supports staff decision-making. The impact includes reduced search time, improved accuracy, and better utilisation of monitoring data. This strengthens regulatory oversight, enhances operational efficiency, and supports evidence-based management of medicines within the Italian healthcare system.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2553,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01929,EU_PSTW_202609_01929,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Government of Albania,General services.,UNKNOWN,AI-powered procurement platform,Government of Albania; Central-Government; General services.; Albania,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Public procurement processes are often time-consuming, error-prone and vulnerable to inefficiencies that undermine transparency and competition. In Albania, authorities seek to modernise procurement practices to improve fairness and operational efficiency. The objective of this initiative is to streamline procurement workflows while maintaining strong human oversight. The implementation is developing a platform that supports drafting terms of reference, estimating costs and generating initial evaluation reports. All outputs are subject to review, with full audit trails tracking modifications. The system integrates procurement data and standard templates to support consistent application of rules. Expected impacts include reduced processing time, fewer errors and increased transparency. Qualitative benefits include fairer competition, improved trust among businesses and stronger governance of public spending.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,S7_GENERATIVE_CONTENT_CREATION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2554,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01930,EU_PSTW_202609_01930,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Government of Albania,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.","Expected impacts include improved compliance rates, faster detection of risks and reduced manual workload.",AI for taxation project in Albania,"Government of Albania; Central-Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Albania","Qualitative outcomes include more effective enforcement, better use of human expertise and increased fiscal integrity.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Combating tax evasion and financial crime requires timely detection of anomalies across large volumes of transactional data. Albania aims to enhance its tax administration by automating routine analysis while strengthening investigative capacity. The objective of this project is to improve detection of irregularities and free staff for complex cases. The implementation develops analytical models that process electronic invoices and customs declarations in near real time to identify unusual patterns. The system supports analysts rather than replacing them, integrating into existing workflows. Expected impacts include improved compliance rates, faster detection of risks and reduced manual workload. Qualitative outcomes include more effective enforcement, better use of human expertise and increased fiscal integrity.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2555,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01931,EU_PSTW_202609_01931,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Government of Albania,Health n.e.c.,"Expected impacts include earlier diagnosis, improved patient outcomes and reduced diagnostic delays.",AI for healthcare project in Albania,Government of Albania; Central-Government; Health n.e.c.; Albania,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Healthcare systems face pressure to detect diseases earlier and manage growing diagnostic workloads. Albania aims to strengthen diagnostic capacity through advanced digital tools. The objective of this project is to support clinicians in radiology by improving early detection and treatment planning. The implementation focuses on developing systems that analyse medical images to identify patterns associated with disease, support personalised treatment decisions and predict progression. These tools are designed to integrate into clinical workflows and support, not replace, medical judgement. Expected impacts include earlier diagnosis, improved patient outcomes and reduced diagnostic delays. Qualitative benefits include improved care quality, increased system resilience and long-term efficiency gains.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2556,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01932,EU_PSTW_202609_01932,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Government of Albania,Community development.,UNKNOWN,Nation-wide AI-powered smart city project,Government of Albania; Central-Government; Community development.; Albania,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Rapid urbanisation and increased mobility place pressure on city infrastructure, traffic management and emergency response. Albania has launched a nationwide initiative to modernise urban management across 20 cities. The objective of this project is to improve safety, mobility and service coordination through real-time data-driven management. The implementation develops an integrated platform that combines urban, traffic and public safety data into a central command and control environment. Analytical tools support traffic optimisation, incident detection and coordinated emergency response. Expected impacts include reduced congestion, improved road safety and faster response times. Qualitative outcomes include more resilient cities, better public service coordination and a scalable model for national smart city deployment.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2557,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01933,EU_PSTW_202609_01933,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Rome,Waste management.,"to address a longstanding operational challenge in Rome’s urban hygiene services: the difficulty of managing a vast, complex and constantly changing city through fragmented information and reactive intervention models.",UCRONIA - Utility Circular Economy Real-Time Operation Navigation Integration Assets,City of Rome; Local Government; Waste management.; Italy,"The solution is implemented through a real-time geographic digital twin that maps Rome’s urban infrastructure, including manholes, bins, tree-lined streets, vehicle fleets, citizen reports and environmental data, and feeds this information into a dedicated control room.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"UCRONIA was developed by AMA S.p.A. to address a longstanding operational challenge in Rome’s urban hygiene services: the difficulty of managing a vast, complex and constantly changing city through fragmented information and reactive intervention models. In a metropolitan context where waste collection, street cleaning, environmental conditions, citizen reports and public events all affect service delivery, AMA needed a system capable of improving coordination, anticipating issues earlier and supporting more consistent decision-making. The stakeholders include operational managers, field teams, municipal authorities, citizens and technology partners, with the objective of moving from a reactive to a preventive model of urban hygiene management while improving service quality, sustainability and circular-economy performance. The solution is implemented through a real-time geographic digital twin that maps Rome’s urban infrastructure, including manholes, bins, tree-lined streets, vehicle fleets, citizen reports and environmental data, and feeds this information into a dedicated control room. At a conceptual level, the platform integrates geospatial intelligence, live operational data and decision-support capabilities to optimise routing, asset management and intervention planning across the city. Operated 24/7 in collaboration with partners such as Present S.p.A. and Esri Italia, the system has already shown its practical value during major events including papal funerals and Jubilee celebrations. The impact includes more efficient operations, stronger service continuity, better situational awareness and a scalable model for smart, data-driven urban environmental management.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2558,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01934,EU_PSTW_202609_01934,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Aarhus Municipality,General public services n.e.c.,"Qualitative impacts include enhanced autonomy for vulnerable users, reduced administrative burden on social services and improved trust in digital public services, demonstrating inclusive innovation in identity management.",IMPULSE Project - NemID card retrieval from lockers (IMPULSE - Denmark),Aarhus Municipality; Local Government; General public services n.e.c.; Denmark,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Access to digital public services in Denmark depends on NemID credentials, which vulnerable citizens frequently lose, limiting their ability to interact with authorities. Aarhus Municipality sought solutions to ensure equitable access for these groups. The objective of this pilot is to support vulnerable citizens by providing secure storage and retrieval of authentication credentials. The implementation introduced personal document lockers where NemID key cards could be safely stored. Access to each drawer is controlled through biometric facial recognition performed via the citizen’s mobile device, ensuring secure and exclusive access. The solution integrates with existing municipal support services and digital identity workflows. The pilot reduced credential loss, improved continuity of service access and increased digital inclusion. Qualitative impacts include enhanced autonomy for vulnerable users, reduced administrative burden on social services and improved trust in digital public services, demonstrating inclusive innovation in identity management.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2559,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01935,EU_PSTW_202609_01935,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City Council of Gijón,General public services n.e.c.,"Secure and user-friendly electronic identity systems are essential for accessing modern public services, yet many citizens face fragmented authentication experiences.",IMPULSE Project - Gijón public services app,City Council of Gijón; Local Government; General public services n.e.c.; Spain,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Secure and user-friendly electronic identity systems are essential for accessing modern public services, yet many citizens face fragmented authentication experiences. The City of Gijón aimed to modernise access to municipal services while ensuring privacy and interoperability. The objective of this pilot is to enhance secure, citizen-centric digital identification within the public sector. The implementation explored integration of advanced identity technologies into municipal platforms, focusing on GDPR-compliant authentication and cross-service usability. The pilot leveraged existing infrastructure to test secure access to public services and evaluate interoperability at local and cross-border levels. Results include improved user experience, stronger data protection and increased confidence in digital services. Qualitative benefits include enhanced accessibility, reduced administrative friction and alignment with European digital identity objectives, supporting scalable adoption across public administrations.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2560,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01936,EU_PSTW_202609_01936,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,UnioneCamere,General public services n.e.c.,"This duplication increases costs, delays procedures and creates inconsistencies in data verification. The pilot demonstrated faster onboarding to public services, reduced administrative burden for businesses and authorities, and improved trust in shared data.",IMPULSE Project - Entreprise digital drawer,UnioneCamere; Central-Government; General public services n.e.c.; Italy,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Businesses frequently face administrative inefficiencies when accessing public services, as they are required to repeatedly provide the same identity documents, registrations and certifications to different authorities, often across borders. This duplication increases costs, delays procedures and creates inconsistencies in data verification. Within the IMPULSE initiative, public administrations and business support organisations sought to simplify and secure enterprise access to digital public services while maintaining regulatory compliance. The objective of this pilot is to streamline business identification processes and enable trusted, reusable digital identities for enterprises. The implementation designed and tested a blockchain-based business identity solution, often referred to as a digital drawer, which enriches traditional business identities with verifiable attributes sourced from official business registers. These attributes can be securely shared with authorised public bodies on demand, with full traceability and user control. The system integrates with existing public service platforms and supports both domestic and cross-border service access. The pilot demonstrated faster onboarding to public services, reduced administrative burden for businesses and authorities, and improved trust in shared data. Qualitative impacts include higher data integrity, improved interoperability between administrations and a reusable framework that supports more efficient, transparent and scalable digital services for enterprises.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2561,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01937,EU_PSTW_202609_01937,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Peshtera,General public services n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,IMPULSE Project - Civil registration & certification in Bulgaria,Municipality of Peshtera; Local Government; General public services n.e.c.; Bulgaria,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Local administrations are under increasing pressure to deliver digital public services that are secure, reliable and compliant with data protection regulations, while maintaining accessibility for citizens. In the Municipality of Peshtera, existing electronic civil registration and certification services required strengthening to meet growing security and GDPR compliance demands. The objective of this pilot is to modernise municipal digital services by enhancing trust, data protection and operational robustness. The implementation built upon the municipality’s existing electronic service platform, integrating identity verification, secure data handling mechanisms and governance principles developed within the IMPULSE project. These enhancements focused on improving authentication, protecting personal data and ensuring that digital interactions between citizens and the municipality complied with European data protection requirements. The pilot enabled the municipality to test and validate these mechanisms in real operational conditions. Results included improved service reliability, stronger protection of citizen data and increased trust in digital civil services. Qualitative benefits include reduced administrative risk, greater efficiency in service delivery and a scalable model that other municipalities can adopt to modernise local e-government services while ensuring compliance and citizen confidence.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2562,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01938,EU_PSTW_202609_01938,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Ertzaintza (ERTZ),Public order and safety n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,IMPULSE Project - Online complaints filing services,Ertzaintza (ERTZ); Non-governmental; Public order and safety n.e.c.; Spain,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Filing a formal police complaint traditionally requires citizens to attend a police station in person, which can be time-consuming, inconvenient and particularly challenging for vulnerable individuals or those living far from urban centres. In the Basque Country, the Ertzaintza sought to modernise its complaint-handling processes while maintaining high standards of security, legal validity and public trust. The objective of this pilot is to enable citizens to securely submit complaints online, reducing administrative barriers and improving access to public security services. The implementation integrated advanced identity management mechanisms into the existing online complaints system, enabling secure authentication and verification of complainants. These mechanisms were designed and tested in collaboration with law enforcement personnel, ensuring alignment with operational requirements and legal constraints. The pilot included end-user needs analysis, system validation and staff training to support adoption. Results showed improved accessibility of police services, reduced need for in-person visits and more efficient handling of non-urgent complaints. Qualitative impacts include improved citizen convenience, better allocation of police resources and strengthened trust in digital public safety services. The pilot demonstrates a scalable approach for modernising law enforcement interactions while preserving security and procedural integrity.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2564,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01939,EU_PSTW_202609_01939,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,DuCoop ESCO,Fuel and energy,"A key challenge in such environments is not only installing low-carbon infrastructure, but also ensuring that residents actively participate in energy management so that local flexibility can be used to support the grid and improve system efficiency.",BRIGHT - Local Energy Cooperative multi-market centralized aggregation for value stacking flexibility services (Belgium),DuCoop ESCO; Consortium; Fuel and energy; Belgium,"The solution was implemented through smart services such as real-time visualisation of energy consumption, billing tools and smart-home features, supported by data-driven methods, blockchain and social-science approaches. The expected impact was greater resident participation, improved grid flexibility and better use of distributed energy resources.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The De Nieuwe Dokken development in Ghent was designed as a highly sustainable urban district with housing, public facilities and a strong renewable-energy and circular-technology component. A key challenge in such environments is not only installing low-carbon infrastructure, but also ensuring that residents actively participate in energy management so that local flexibility can be used to support the grid and improve system efficiency. The BRIGHT pilot addressed this issue by helping turn a residential development into an engaged energy community. The stakeholders included residents, DuCoop ESCO, project partners and energy-system actors, with the objective of increasing energy awareness, encouraging participation in demand-response schemes and assessing the potential for integrating homes into a virtual power plant. The solution was implemented through smart services such as real-time visualisation of energy consumption, billing tools and smart-home features, supported by data-driven methods, blockchain and social-science approaches. At a conceptual level, the pilot combined Digital Twin models, Virtual Power Plant concepts and analytical services to characterise consumer behaviour and identify how residential demand could be coordinated more flexibly. This enabled technical innovation to be paired with user engagement rather than relying on infrastructure alone. The expected impact was greater resident participation, improved grid flexibility and better use of distributed energy resources. More broadly, the pilot created value by demonstrating how citizen-centred energy communities can support sustainability, resilience and more intelligent local energy management.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2565,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01940,EU_PSTW_202609_01940,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,University of Maribor,Fuel and energy,UNKNOWN,BRIGHT - Virtual Community Decentralized Aggregation in Slovenia,University of Maribor; Academic-Research; Fuel and energy; Slovenia,"The energy transition increasingly requires active participation from consumers, yet many households lack the tools to engage effectively in local energy markets or optimise their energy use.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The energy transition increasingly requires active participation from consumers, yet many households lack the tools to engage effectively in local energy markets or optimise their energy use. In Slovenia, energy stakeholders sought solutions to empower consumers while maintaining security, transparency and system efficiency. The objective of this pilot is to enable peer-to-peer energy trading and integrated energy services through a secure, consumer-centric platform. The implementation combines edge-secure gateways, advanced metering infrastructure and digital twin models with a distributed transaction platform to support decentralised energy exchanges. Aggregate measurements are analysed to infer device behaviour without exposing individual consumption patterns, while users interact through a unified interface that integrates energy and non-energy services, including financial and safety-related applications. The pilot demonstrated improved consumer engagement, enhanced visibility of energy usage and new opportunities for local energy optimisation. Qualitative impacts include increased user awareness, support for decentralised energy markets and a replicable national model for virtual energy communities. The project illustrates how integrated digital infrastructures can support sustainable energy systems and active consumer participation.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2566,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01941,EU_PSTW_202609_01941,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,SOCIB - Balearic Islands Ocean Observing and Forecasting System,Protection of biodiversity and landscape.,"Manual observation can be limited in speed, consistency and coverage, particularly when beach conditions change rapidly and multiple indicators must be tracked simultaneously.",iMaging Project - Beach Monitoring,SOCIB - Balearic Islands Ocean Observing and Forecasting System; Non-governmental; Protection of biodiversity and landscape.; Spain,"This use case, developed within the EU-funded iMagine project and led by SOCIB, addresses that challenge by improving the monitoring of beach environments through automated analysis of camera feeds. The solution is implemented through a prototype service that uses deep-learning image-segmentation techniques to analyse video from beach-monitoring cameras.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Coastal authorities need timely and reliable information on beach conditions in order to protect the public, monitor environmental health and respond quickly to changing marine dynamics. Manual observation can be limited in speed, consistency and coverage, particularly when beach conditions change rapidly and multiple indicators must be tracked simultaneously. This use case, developed within the EU-funded iMagine project and led by SOCIB, addresses that challenge by improving the monitoring of beach environments through automated analysis of camera feeds. The stakeholders include coastal managers, environmental researchers and public-safety actors, with the objective of supporting near real-time understanding of shoreline conditions, strengthening beach safety and improving knowledge of ecologically significant coastal features. The solution is implemented through a prototype service that uses deep-learning image-segmentation techniques to analyse video from beach-monitoring cameras. At a conceptual level, the system processes visual data to detect and monitor Posidonia oceanica berms, which are relevant indicators of coastal health, and to identify rip currents, including their location and physical characteristics. These outputs provide actionable information for both environmental observation and risk management. Running from September 2022 to August 2025, the prototype demonstrates how automated image analysis can complement existing beach-monitoring practices. The expected impact includes faster interpretation of coastal conditions, improved support for beach management and stronger public-safety capability, alongside a more scalable approach to continuous shoreline observation.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2567,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01942,EU_PSTW_202609_01942,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""social_nonprofit""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,EMSO ERIC,Protection of biodiversity and landscape.,"Marine ecosystem monitoring relies on continuous observation, yet manual analysis of underwater video footage is time-consuming and limits the ability to detect changes in biodiversity and environmental conditions at scale. The approach significantly reduces manual workload, improves consistency of observations, and enhances the timeliness and scale of ecosystem monitoring.",iMaging Project - Ecosystem Monitoring at EMSO Sites by Video Imagery,EMSO ERIC; Academic-Research; Protection of biodiversity and landscape.; European Union,"This use case addresses these needs by supporting ecosystem monitoring at subsea observatory sites, involving marine researchers, infrastructure operators, and data scientists, with the objective of improving real-time understanding of marine environments. The solution is implemented through a video-based monitoring service that processes underwater imagery using deep-learning models for object detection and quality filtering.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Marine ecosystem monitoring relies on continuous observation, yet manual analysis of underwater video footage is time-consuming and limits the ability to detect changes in biodiversity and environmental conditions at scale. Research infrastructures such as EMSO observatories require automated tools to transform large volumes of raw video into actionable scientific insights. This use case addresses these needs by supporting ecosystem monitoring at subsea observatory sites, involving marine researchers, infrastructure operators, and data scientists, with the objective of improving real-time understanding of marine environments. The solution is implemented through a video-based monitoring service that processes underwater imagery using deep-learning models for object detection and quality filtering. Deployed across EMSO sites including Obsea, the Azores, and SmartBay, the service identifies marine species, flags low-quality footage, and detects notable events such as prawn burrows, converting unstructured video into standardised, analysable datasets. Integrated data pipelines deliver near-real-time outputs to researchers and support reuse across observatories. The approach significantly reduces manual workload, improves consistency of observations, and enhances the timeliness and scale of ecosystem monitoring. It strengthens biodiversity assessment, supports long-term environmental research, and provides a scalable foundation for data-driven marine science.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2568,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01943,EU_PSTW_202609_01943,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,EMSO ERIC,Protection of biodiversity and landscape.,"As part of the EU-funded iMagine project, the EyeOnWater initiative addresses the challenge of ensuring the quality and reliability of citizen-contributed data for environmental monitoring. The automated validation process significantly improves the consistency and reliability of citizen science data while reducing the need for manual review.",iMaging Project - WITOIL Oil spill detection service,EMSO ERIC; Academic-Research; Protection of biodiversity and landscape.; European Union,"The objective of the use case is to validate incoming images efficiently and at scale so that only suitable, scientifically usable photographs are incorporated into long-term environmental datasets. The service uses computer vision techniques to automatically assess the suitability of submitted images before they enter scientific workflows.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"As part of the EU-funded iMagine project, the EyeOnWater initiative addresses the challenge of ensuring the quality and reliability of citizen-contributed data for environmental monitoring. Through a smartphone application, citizens submit photographs of natural water bodies such as rivers, lakes, and seas to support large-scale observation of water colour and related ecological indicators. Key stakeholders include citizens, environmental scientists, and research institutions that rely on consistent, high-quality visual data. The objective of the use case is to validate incoming images efficiently and at scale so that only suitable, scientifically usable photographs are incorporated into long-term environmental datasets. The service uses computer vision techniques to automatically assess the suitability of submitted images before they enter scientific workflows. Image segmentation and object detection models, including YOLOv8, are applied to identify whether an image contains a water body and to detect obstructions or irrelevant content. The models are trained on a labeled dataset of more than 10,000 images categorized into different quality classes. The validation pipeline is integrated into the mobile application backend, enabling near real-time processing and automated decision-making on image acceptance or rejection. The automated validation process significantly improves the consistency and reliability of citizen science data while reducing the need for manual review. Real-time filtering ensures that only high-quality images are added to long-term water colour datasets, increasing their scientific value and usability. The approach enhances scalability by allowing large volumes of submissions to be processed efficiently, supports faster data availability for researchers, and improves the user experience by providing immediate feedback on submissions. Overall, the solution strengthens the contribution of citizen science to environmental monitoring and ecological research.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2569,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01944,EU_PSTW_202609_01944,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,University of Trento,Protection of biodiversity and landscape.,"Marine oil spills are difficult to detect and track quickly across large sea areas, yet delays in identification can worsen environmental damage, complicate response planning and reduce the effectiveness of mitigation efforts. The expected impact is faster and more consistent spill detection, reduced manual screening effort and improved decision support for marine incidents.",iMaging Project - EyeOnWater app,University of Trento; Academic-Research; Protection of biodiversity and landscape.; Italy,"The solution, also known as WITOIL, is implemented on the iMagine platform and uses deep-learning models trained on labelled imagery from Sentinel-1, Sentinel-2 and Landsat 8 satellites.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Marine oil spills are difficult to detect and track quickly across large sea areas, yet delays in identification can worsen environmental damage, complicate response planning and reduce the effectiveness of mitigation efforts. The Oil Spill Detection service within the EU-funded iMagine project addresses this operational problem by improving how spills are identified, monitored and modelled from satellite data. The stakeholders include researchers, environmental authorities and decision-makers responsible for marine protection and spill response, with the objective of strengthening early detection, improving situational awareness and making advanced monitoring tools easier to adopt in practice. The solution, also known as WITOIL, is implemented on the iMagine platform and uses deep-learning models trained on labelled imagery from Sentinel-1, Sentinel-2 and Landsat 8 satellites. At a conceptual level, the service combines satellite-image analysis, automated classification and spill-modelling functions to enhance the capabilities of existing systems such as OKEANOS. In addition to detection, it supports fuel-spill modelling, alert generation, documentation and training resources, allowing users to integrate the service more effectively into operational workflows. The prototype runs from September 2022 to August 2025. The expected impact is faster and more consistent spill detection, reduced manual screening effort and improved decision support for marine incidents. More broadly, the service increases scalability in environmental monitoring and strengthens the practical usability of Earth-observation data for pollution management.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2570,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01945,EU_PSTW_202609_01945,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office (FCDO)","Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.","The system integrates with existing consular contact channels and knowledge bases, enabling rapid response without manual intervention.",Consular Digital Triage - Automating consular enquiry responses,"Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office (FCDO); Central-Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; United Kingdom","The Consular Digital Triage use case was introduced to reduce this operational pressure while ensuring that citizens receive fast, accurate, and safe guidance. The solution is implemented as a digital assistant that classifies incoming written enquiries and matches them to pre-approved guidance templates rather than generating open-ended responses.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"British nationals abroad frequently contact consular services for urgent but routine issues such as lost passports, emergency travel documents, or eligibility for support, creating high volumes of written and telephone enquiries for the Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office (FCDO). The Consular Digital Triage use case was introduced to reduce this operational pressure while ensuring that citizens receive fast, accurate, and safe guidance. The primary stakeholders include British nationals overseas, consular staff, and central FCDO operations teams, with the objective of resolving common enquiries at first contact and allowing human staff to focus on complex or sensitive cases. The solution is implemented as a digital assistant that classifies incoming written enquiries and matches them to pre-approved guidance templates rather than generating open-ended responses. This rules- and model-based approach ensures consistency, policy compliance, and safety. The system integrates with existing consular contact channels and knowledge bases, enabling rapid response without manual intervention. The impact has been substantial: written enquiries were reduced by around 80% and calls by up to 50% within three months of deployment. Over time, the tool is projected to save the FCDO millions of pounds while improving response times, maintaining trust, and enhancing the overall experience for citizens abroad.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2571,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01946,EU_PSTW_202609_01946,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,London Borough of Barking & Dagenham,General services.,"Residents often struggle to navigate council websites or contact centres to find accurate information on local services, leading to high call volumes and repeated enquiries.",Barking and Dagenham AI Search and Webchat - Streamlining access to council services and information through AI-powered search and chat,London Borough of Barking & Dagenham; Local Government; General services.; United Kingdom,"The solution combines an AI-powered search interface and a conversational webchat tool that uses structured service data, including postcode-based logic, to deliver personalised responses.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Residents often struggle to navigate council websites or contact centres to find accurate information on local services, leading to high call volumes and repeated enquiries. Barking and Dagenham Council introduced AI Search and Webchat to streamline access to council services, starting with waste collection and expanding to areas such as housing and council tax. Stakeholders include residents, customer service teams, and service departments, with the objective of resolving most routine questions digitally while preserving accessibility. The solution combines an AI-powered search interface and a conversational webchat tool that uses structured service data, including postcode-based logic, to deliver personalised responses. Both interfaces are integrated with council content systems and continuously refined through community feedback and monitoring. Traditional channels, such as phone and face-to-face support, remain available to ensure inclusivity. The impact has been measurable: the AI Search tool now handles around 18,000 sessions per month with a high first-time hit rate, while the chatbot manages approximately 4,450 conversations monthly with a 93% success rate based on user feedback. Together, the tools have significantly reduced general enquiries and call volumes, improved resident satisfaction, and enabled council staff to focus on more complex cases.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2572,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01947,EU_PSTW_202609_01947,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Department for Education (DfE) / Oak National Academy,Education n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,Aila: AI Lesson Assistant for UK teachers,Department for Education (DfE) / Oak National Academy; Central-Government; Education n.e.c.; United Kingdom,"Aila is implemented as a conversational lesson assistant that combines a large language model with retrieval-augmented generation, drawing exclusively on Oak’s quality-assured lesson repository.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Teachers face significant workload pressures when preparing high-quality, curriculum-aligned lesson plans tailored to diverse student needs. Oak National Academy developed Aila to support teachers in lesson planning while maintaining pedagogical quality and alignment with national curricula. Stakeholders include classroom teachers, school leaders, and curriculum designers, with the objective of saving time, reducing stress, and improving teaching consistency. Aila is implemented as a conversational lesson assistant that combines a large language model with retrieval-augmented generation, drawing exclusively on Oak’s quality-assured lesson repository. Teachers interact with Aila via a chat interface to generate lesson outlines, which can be iteratively refined to suit class context and teaching style. The system is designed to ensure factual accuracy and curriculum relevance by grounding outputs in trusted educational content. The impact has been strong: in its first two months, Aila supported 10,000 users in creating nearly 25,000 lesson plans, with 85% of teachers rating the quality as fairly high or very high. Nearly two-thirds reported reduced workload, with average time savings of around three hours and some saving up to 15 hours. Teachers also reported improved well-being, demonstrating clear educational and workforce value.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S1_KNOWLEDGE_RETRIEVAL_RAG,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2573,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01948,EU_PSTW_202609_01948,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Cabinet Office (CO) / Government Communication Service (GCS),"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.","The system enables users to brainstorm ideas, draft initial content, and review materials more efficiently than manual processes. The impact includes faster turnaround times, improved productivity, and reduced administrative burden, allowing communication professionals to focus more on strategic thinking and stakeholder engagement rather than repetitive drafting tasks.",GCS Assist - Accelerating content creation for government communicators,"Cabinet Office (CO) / Government Communication Service (GCS); Central-Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; United Kingdom","Assist is implemented as a secure internal tool featuring pre-built, task-specific prompts tailored to communication disciplines such as press notices, stakeholder briefings, and campaign planning.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Government communicators must produce high-quality, timely content aligned with established standards and frameworks, often under tight deadlines. GCS Assist was developed in-house to support members of the UK Government Communication Service by accelerating routine content creation and review tasks. The key stakeholders are government communicators across departments, with the objective of saving time while maintaining consistency, accuracy, and best practice. Assist is implemented as a secure internal tool featuring pre-built, task-specific prompts tailored to communication disciplines such as press notices, stakeholder briefings, and campaign planning. The system enables users to brainstorm ideas, draft initial content, and review materials more efficiently than manual processes. Its design embeds Government Communication Service guidance and frameworks directly into workflows, ensuring outputs align with professional standards. Since going live in April 2025, following a prototype phase in late 2023, Assist has enabled communicators to complete tasks in a fraction of the time previously required. The impact includes faster turnaround times, improved productivity, and reduced administrative burden, allowing communication professionals to focus more on strategic thinking and stakeholder engagement rather than repetitive drafting tasks.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2575,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01949,EU_PSTW_202609_01949,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Neath Port Talbot Council,Social protection n.e.c.,"In regional social care services in Wales, there was a need to reduce administrative burden while improving the quality of assessments.",Magic Notes - Assisting social care workers through transcription and note-taking,Neath Port Talbot Council; Regional Government; Social protection n.e.c.; United Kingdom,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Social care professionals spend a significant proportion of their time on administrative documentation, reducing the time available for direct engagement with clients and contributing to workload pressure and burnout. In regional social care services in Wales, there was a need to reduce administrative burden while improving the quality of assessments. The objective of this pilot is to support social workers by automating transcription and structured note-taking during client interactions. The implementation deploys a speech-based system that records conversations, produces accurate transcripts and generates structured summaries aligned with assessment requirements. The tool integrates into existing workflows and allows practitioners to review and edit outputs. During the pilot, practitioners saved on average 25 minutes per assessment and approximately 7.5 hours per week, submitting assessments up to eight hours faster. Accuracy exceeded 97%, and users reported more natural conversations and higher-quality assessments. Qualitative impacts include reduced case backlogs, improved workload distribution and enhanced practitioner wellbeing. The pilot demonstrates significant productivity gains and improved service quality in social care delivery.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2576,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01950,EU_PSTW_202609_01950,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,HM Revenue and Customs (HMRC),Public order and safety n.e.c.,"By automating what was previously a manual and time-consuming process, LDK reduces detection time from days to hours.",Logo Detection and Classification Toolkit (LDK) - Automating the detection of unauthorised logo use on websites,HM Revenue and Customs (HMRC); Central-Government; Public order and safety n.e.c.; United Kingdom,"Unauthorised use of official government logos on fraudulent or misleading websites poses risks to citizens and undermines trust in public institutions. HM Revenue & Customs, working with Central-Government partners, developed the Logo Detection and Classification Toolkit to automate the identification of such misuse.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Unauthorised use of official government logos on fraudulent or misleading websites poses risks to citizens and undermines trust in public institutions. HM Revenue & Customs, working with Central-Government partners, developed the Logo Detection and Classification Toolkit to automate the identification of such misuse. Stakeholders include enforcement analysts, government departments, and the public, with the objective of detecting and removing infringing websites quickly and accurately. LDK is implemented as an automated pipeline that scans the web via search engines, scrapes suspicious sites, and applies a deep-learning-based computer vision model to detect government logos. The system flags likely infringements for analyst review, integrating seamlessly into existing enforcement workflows. By automating what was previously a manual and time-consuming process, LDK reduces detection time from days to hours. The impact is significant: analysts can now take down 10–20 infringing URLs per month, with accuracy exceeding 97%. This improves public protection, strengthens brand integrity, and delivers substantial efficiency gains for enforcement teams.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2577,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01951,EU_PSTW_202609_01951,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,South Cambridgeshire District Council,General public services n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,SAM - Service AI Assistant Module - Streamlining routine queries through AI-powered contact centre tools,South Cambridgeshire District Council; Local Government; General public services n.e.c.; United Kingdom,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Local authorities receive high volumes of routine enquiries about services such as waste collection, council tax and basic administrative procedures, placing sustained pressure on contact centres and frontline staff. Residents increasingly expect immediate, round-the-clock access to information. The objective of this pilot is to improve customer service efficiency while maintaining service quality and accessibility. The implementation introduces a conversational assistant that handles routine enquiries through automated dialogue and integrates with backend council systems to retrieve accurate, up-to-date information. The assistant operates continuously and escalates complex queries to human agents when necessary. Early testing showed response accuracy of approximately 93% and user satisfaction levels between 86% and 90%, based on direct feedback. The pilot reduced call centre workload, shortened response times and improved service availability for residents. Qualitative benefits include better staff focus on complex cases, improved citizen experience and a scalable solution for digital customer service in local government. The pilot highlights the value of automated assistance in enhancing public service responsiveness and operational efficiency.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2579,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01952,EU_PSTW_202609_01952,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,NHS Grampian,Hospital services.,UNKNOWN,Mia - Streamlining breast cancer screening with AI,NHS Grampian; Regional Government; Hospital services.; United Kingdom,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Breast cancer screening programmes face growing demand while radiology departments experience workforce shortages and increasing workloads, which can affect screening throughput and consistency. Radiologists must review large volumes of mammograms, often under time pressure, while ensuring that cases requiring further investigation are reliably identified. The objective of this pilot is to support clinicians in breast cancer screening by improving efficiency and maintaining high diagnostic standards. The implementation introduces an assisted assessment system that automatically analyses mammography images and provides recommendations on whether a case should be recalled for further examination. The system is integrated into existing screening workflows, supporting radiologists during image review rather than replacing clinical judgement. It processes large datasets of anonymised mammograms to detect patterns associated with potential abnormalities and highlights cases that warrant closer attention. The pilot demonstrated improved workflow efficiency, reduced reading time per case and more consistent prioritisation of high-risk images. Qualitative impacts include better workload management for radiologists, increased confidence in screening decisions and enhanced capacity of screening programmes. The pilot supports scalability of breast cancer screening services while preserving clinical oversight and patient safety.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2580,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01953,EU_PSTW_202609_01953,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Department of Health and Social Care (DHSC) / NHS England (NHSE),Public order and safety n.e.c.,"Manual moderation by third-party reviewers led to delays and high costs, and many rejected reviews were never resubmitted, reducing valuable patient feedback. NHS England introduced the Review Automoderation Tool to address these challenges.",Review Automoderation Tool - Automating content moderation on NHS.UK,Department of Health and Social Care (DHSC) / NHS England (NHSE); Central-Government; Public order and safety n.e.c.; United Kingdom,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"NHS.UK receives around 100,000 user reviews each year, all of which must comply with strict moderation policies on personal data, abuse, discrimination, and safeguarding. Manual moderation by third-party reviewers led to delays and high costs, and many rejected reviews were never resubmitted, reducing valuable patient feedback. NHS England introduced the Review Automoderation Tool to address these challenges. Stakeholders include patients, NHS content teams, and service providers, with the objective of accelerating moderation while maintaining safety and quality. The tool automatically assesses reviews against moderation criteria, flagging issues for human oversight where necessary. Integrated into NHS.UK workflows, it processes reviews in seconds rather than days. The impact includes expert-level accuracy, dramatically reduced moderation times, and lower operational costs. Importantly, faster moderation has improved publication rates, preserving more patient feedback and enhancing transparency. The tool supports safer, more responsive digital services while ensuring NHS standards are upheld.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2581,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01954,EU_PSTW_202609_01954,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Department for Energy Security and Net Zero (DESNZ),Fuel and energy,"The result is a fairer, more inclusive system with improved policy effectiveness and reduced administrative burden.",Warm Home Discount Energy Cost Predictor - Modelling energy costs to determine eligibility for Warm Home Discount scheme,Department for Energy Security and Net Zero (DESNZ); Central-Government; Fuel and energy; United Kingdom,"The solution uses a statistical prediction model to estimate household energy costs based on property characteristics such as size, age, and type.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Identifying households eligible for energy cost support is complex, particularly when relying on self-application processes that disadvantage less informed or vulnerable citizens. The Warm Home Discount Energy Cost Predictor was developed to improve targeting of support under the UK’s Warm Home Discount scheme. Stakeholders include the Department for Energy Security & Net Zero, energy suppliers, and low-income households, with the objective of issuing rebates more fairly and efficiently. The solution uses a statistical prediction model to estimate household energy costs based on property characteristics such as size, age, and type. By integrating these estimates with income-related data, the system identifies high-cost, low-income households proactively. The impact has been substantial: in 2023/24, over 90% of the 3 million rebates were issued automatically. This removed the first-come, first-served approach and ensured vulnerable households received support even if they were unaware of the scheme. The result is a fairer, more inclusive system with improved policy effectiveness and reduced administrative burden.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2582,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01955,EU_PSTW_202609_01955,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Department of Health and Social Care (DHSC) / NHS England (NHSE),Health n.e.c.,"Stroke treatment is extremely time-sensitive, and delays in diagnosis can have severe consequences for patient outcomes.",Stroke AI - Interpreting brain scans with AI to improve outcomes for stroke patients,Department of Health and Social Care (DHSC) / NHS England (NHSE); Central-Government; Health n.e.c.; United Kingdom,"The impact is national in scale: all 107 stroke centres in England now use the technology, supporting care for around 80,000 stroke patients each year.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Stroke treatment is extremely time-sensitive, and delays in diagnosis can have severe consequences for patient outcomes. Stroke AI was introduced across England to support clinical teams in rapidly interpreting brain scans and making treatment decisions. Stakeholders include clinicians, hospitals, and patients, with the objective of improving speed, accuracy, and consistency in stroke care. The solution analyses brain imaging data to support identification of stroke type and severity, integrating into existing radiology and clinical workflows. It provides decision support to clinicians rather than replacing clinical judgement. The impact is national in scale: all 107 stroke centres in England now use the technology, supporting care for around 80,000 stroke patients each year. Faster and more accurate assessments enable timely interventions, improving survival rates and long-term outcomes. The system demonstrates how advanced digital tools can enhance clinical capacity and deliver significant public health benefits in critical care settings.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2583,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01956,EU_PSTW_202609_01956,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Aeroporti di Roma (AdR),Transport,UNKNOWN,ADRYX - Chatbot for real-time flight updates,Aeroporti di Roma (AdR); Local Government; Transport; Italy,AdRyX is implemented as part of the Digiport platform and operates via popular messaging channels such as WhatsApp and Facebook Messenger.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Passengers at large international airports often require up-to-date information on flights, services, and facilities, placing pressure on customer service teams. Aeroporti di Roma launched the AdRyX chatbot at Rome Fiumicino Airport to enhance the digital passenger experience. Stakeholders include travellers, airport operators, and commercial partners, with the objective of delivering instant, multilingual information. AdRyX is implemented as part of the Digiport platform and operates via popular messaging channels such as WhatsApp and Facebook Messenger. It provides real-time flight updates, wayfinding support, and recommendations for shops and restaurants, responding in Italian and English. Integrated with airport operational systems, the chatbot delivers accurate, timely responses without human intervention. Since its launch in June 2024, AdRyX has marked a significant step forward in digital service delivery, improving passenger satisfaction, reducing routine enquiries, and supporting smoother airport operations.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2599,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01957,EU_PSTW_202609_01957,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Government of the Canary Islands,Transport,"Qualitative impacts include increased safety for drivers, reduced congestion-related delays and improved reliability of critical transport infrastructure.",Faneque Double Tunnel - improving traffic with Automatic Incident Detection (AID) system,Government of the Canary Islands; Regional Government; Transport; Spain,"The implementation deployed an automatic incident detection system that analyses live video feeds from tunnel cameras to identify events such as stopped vehicles, accidents or abnormal traffic behaviour.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Major road infrastructure projects must ensure smooth traffic flow and rapid incident response to maintain safety and minimise congestion, particularly in confined environments such as tunnels. The completion of the Faneque double tunnel on Gran Canaria’s La Aldea highway required advanced monitoring to manage increased traffic volumes and reduce travel times. The objective of this pilot is to enhance road safety and operational efficiency through early detection of traffic incidents. The implementation deployed an automatic incident detection system that analyses live video feeds from tunnel cameras to identify events such as stopped vehicles, accidents or abnormal traffic behaviour. The system operates continuously and integrates with traffic management platforms to alert operators in real time, enabling faster intervention. The pilot improved incident response times, reduced secondary accidents and supported smoother traffic flow through the tunnel. Qualitative impacts include increased safety for drivers, reduced congestion-related delays and improved reliability of critical transport infrastructure. The project demonstrates how advanced traffic monitoring can enhance resilience and efficiency in complex road environments.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2601,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01958,EU_PSTW_202609_01958,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,University of Milan,Education n.e.c.,"Universities and graduates increasingly need academic credentials that are easy to verify, portable across borders and suited to more flexible forms of learning, yet traditional paper-based diplomas remain bureaucratic, slow to authenticate and poorly adapted to digital mobility. At a conceptual level, universities create secure, portable and verifiable digital credentials that can be shared by graduates and validated independently without relying on repeated manual confirmation from the issuing institution.",Digitising university degrees with blockchain,University of Milan; Academic-Research; Education n.e.c.; Italy,"This use case, launched in September 2019 by Cineca, MIT and Italian universities including Padua and Milano-Bicocca, addresses that mismatch by rethinking how academic qualifications are issued and recognised. The solution is implemented through blockchain-based issuance of academic certificates using the Blockcerts standard.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Universities and graduates increasingly need academic credentials that are easy to verify, portable across borders and suited to more flexible forms of learning, yet traditional paper-based diplomas remain bureaucratic, slow to authenticate and poorly adapted to digital mobility. This use case, launched in September 2019 by Cineca, MIT and Italian universities including Padua and Milano-Bicocca, addresses that mismatch by rethinking how academic qualifications are issued and recognised. The stakeholders include universities, students, graduates, employers and international education institutions, with the objective of reducing administrative friction, supporting learner mobility and enabling a more modern concept of stackable and digitally usable credentials. The solution is implemented through blockchain-based issuance of academic certificates using the Blockcerts standard. At a conceptual level, universities create secure, portable and verifiable digital credentials that can be shared by graduates and validated independently without relying on repeated manual confirmation from the issuing institution. This architecture supports trusted authenticity while simplifying exchange across institutions and countries. By replacing or complementing conventional diplomas with digital credentials, the system aligns academic certification with new patterns of lifelong learning and international movement. The expected impact includes less bureaucracy, faster verification, greater convenience for graduates and improved interoperability across educational contexts. More broadly, the project creates value by modernising credential management and positioning universities to support a more flexible, digital and globally connected education ecosystem.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2602,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01959,EU_PSTW_202609_01959,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,EUIPO,General services.,UNKNOWN,EUIPO connects to TMview and DesignView through blockchain,EUIPO; European Institution/Agency; General services.; European Union,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"In 2021, the European Union Intellectual Property Office integrated blockchain technology into its TMview and DesignView platforms to improve cross-border intellectual property data exchange. Trademark and design information from national IP offices requires secure synchronisation and reliable verification to ensure transparency and trust. The integration connects participating offices through a distributed ledger that records updates in real time and maintains immutable transaction histories. Blockchain-based validation enhances interoperability and ensures that changes to trademark and design records are traceable and tamper-evident. Users can access authenticated histories of intellectual property rights, strengthening confidence in data integrity. Planned developments include downloadable blockchain certificates and an IP Wallet to manage verified records. By embedding distributed ledger infrastructure into existing databases, EUIPO improves efficiency, data consistency and trust in European IP information systems.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2603,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01960,EU_PSTW_202609_01960,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Banque de France,"General economic, commercial and labour affair","Before MADRE, the SEPA Creditor Identifier register operated through a centralised process that could take days to deliver codes to participating banks, creating delays, manual administration and unnecessary operational overhead. This significantly reduces reliance on manual intervention and streamlines the overall process flow.",MADRE: a Banque de France blockchain project,"Banque de France; Central-Government; General economic, commercial and labour affair; France","The solution is implemented through a private Ethereum-based blockchain that decentralises the ICS register and embeds process logic in smart contracts. As the first blockchain system brought into production by a central bank, MADRE also served as an important proof point for the operational use of distributed-ledger technology in regulated financial infrastructure.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Before MADRE, the SEPA Creditor Identifier register operated through a centralised process that could take days to deliver codes to participating banks, creating delays, manual administration and unnecessary operational overhead. Banque de France launched MADRE in April 2018 to address this inefficiency and modernise a core financial-infrastructure process through a more distributed and automated model. The stakeholders include the central bank, participating banks and financial-sector operators relying on timely and reliable management of SEPA Creditor Identifiers, with the objective of accelerating issuance, reducing paperwork and improving the efficiency and robustness of registry operations. The solution is implemented through a private Ethereum-based blockchain that decentralises the ICS register and embeds process logic in smart contracts. At a conceptual level, the platform replaces a single central database with a distributed ledger architecture in which authorised participants can interact with shared, synchronised records while automated rules govern validation and issuance steps. This significantly reduces reliance on manual intervention and streamlines the overall process flow. As the first blockchain system brought into production by a central bank, MADRE also served as an important proof point for the operational use of distributed-ledger technology in regulated financial infrastructure. The impact includes a reduction in issuance time from days to minutes, lower processing costs, less paperwork and improved operational efficiency, demonstrating clear business value in speed, reliability and administrative simplification.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2604,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01961,EU_PSTW_202609_01961,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Comune di Ivrea,General public services n.e.c.,"Smart cities require coordination across energy, mobility and citizen services, yet fragmented systems often limit transparency and trust. The pilot demonstrates how decentralised infrastructure can reduce fragmentation, improve accountability and foster citizen trust.",Smart Ivrea - Blockchain Technology in the Management of Smart Communities,Comune di Ivrea; Local Government; General public services n.e.c.; Italy,"The project uses the Quadrans blockchain platform, an open-source, public and decentralised infrastructure, to record and manage community-related transactions and interactions.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Smart cities require coordination across energy, mobility and citizen services, yet fragmented systems often limit transparency and trust. Smart Ivrea, led by the Italian Agency for Digital Italy (AGiD), explores how distributed ledger technology can support integrated management of smart communities. Stakeholders include local authorities, service providers and citizens. The objective is to enhance transparency, coordination and participation in urban services. The project uses the Quadrans blockchain platform, an open-source, public and decentralised infrastructure, to record and manage community-related transactions and interactions. Blockchain enables verifiable records of service usage, resource distribution and citizen engagement, while smart contracts support automated processes across domains such as energy sharing and mobility services. The pilot demonstrates how decentralised infrastructure can reduce fragmentation, improve accountability and foster citizen trust. Although still experimental, Smart Ivrea provides a replicable model for municipalities seeking to integrate emerging technologies into sustainable urban governance.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2605,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01962,EU_PSTW_202609_01962,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""social_nonprofit""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Planckian - University of Pisa,R&D economic affairs,"In Italy, fragmented efforts and limited shared infrastructure have slowed progress in emerging computing fields.",Two italian universities partner to build the quantum computers of the future,Planckian - University of Pisa; Academic-Research; R&D economic affairs; Italy,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Maintaining competitiveness in advanced computing technologies requires long-term investment, coordinated research and strong collaboration between academic and industrial actors. In Italy, fragmented efforts and limited shared infrastructure have slowed progress in emerging computing fields. The objective of this initiative is to establish a robust national ecosystem capable of designing, developing and scaling next-generation quantum computing technologies. The partnership, launched in June 2025, brings together Planckian—a joint spin-off of the University of Pisa and the Scuola Normale—and the QTLab centre of the Federico II University of Naples, aligning scientific research, engineering capabilities and infrastructure development. The implementation focuses on joint research programmes, shared laboratories and coordinated technology roadmaps covering hardware design, control systems and integration with existing high-performance computing environments. The initiative also fosters collaboration with industry and public research organisations to accelerate technology transfer and skills development. The expected impact includes strengthened national research capacity, improved access to cutting-edge infrastructure and increased attractiveness for industrial partnerships. Qualitative outcomes include accelerated innovation cycles, enhanced talent development and a strategic foundation for Italy’s long-term technological sovereignty in quantum computing.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2606,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01963,EU_PSTW_202609_01963,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Kastav,General services.,"Municipal administrations often manage competitive funding and co-financing processes that involve large volumes of documentation, manual evaluation and tight deadlines, placing strain on staff and reducing transparency.",AI use in the City of Kastav,City of Kastav; Local Government; General services.; Croatia,The objective of this pilot is to partially automate administrative workflows and improve the evaluation of funding applications.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Municipal administrations often manage competitive funding and co-financing processes that involve large volumes of documentation, manual evaluation and tight deadlines, placing strain on staff and reducing transparency. In the City of Kastav, authorities sought to modernise the tender submission and evaluation process while supporting fair and efficient decision-making. The objective of this pilot is to partially automate administrative workflows and improve the evaluation of funding applications. The implementation developed a prototype system that enables digital submission of tender documents, automatic extraction of key information and structured summarisation of project proposals. Applications are assessed against predefined criteria, generating evaluation proposals and ranked outputs that can be exported for further review by commission members. The system integrates with existing administrative tools and supports human oversight throughout the process. The pilot reduced administrative workload, shortened evaluation timelines and improved consistency in scoring. Qualitative benefits include greater transparency, improved traceability of decisions and enhanced efficiency for municipal staff, providing a scalable model for digital transformation in local public administration.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK|F06_HIGH_VOLUME_SCALABILITY_BOTTLENECK,S6_WORKFLOW_PROCESS_AUTOMATION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2607,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01964,EU_PSTW_202609_01964,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,AI-Code,Broadcasting and publishing services.,"Online disinformation increasingly relies on short-form videos, posing challenges for media professionals tasked with identifying coordinated influence campaigns. By reducing manual screening effort, the tools enable faster response to emerging information attacks.",AI-Code - GenAi against disinformation: Use case 3,AI-Code; Consortium; Broadcasting and publishing services.; European Union,"AI-CODE, a Horizon Europe project, addresses this issue through dedicated use cases. Use Case 2 focuses on detecting covert foreign influence in video-based social media content.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Online disinformation increasingly relies on short-form videos, posing challenges for media professionals tasked with identifying coordinated influence campaigns. AI-CODE, a Horizon Europe project, addresses this issue through dedicated use cases. Use Case 2 focuses on detecting covert foreign influence in video-based social media content. Stakeholders include journalists, fact-checkers and media organisations. The objective is to improve the efficiency and accuracy of disinformation monitoring at scale. The solution automates the analysis of large volumes of videos from platforms such as TikTok and YouTube Shorts, extracting visual, audio and contextual features. Advanced models flag anomalies, coordinated patterns and potentially harmful sources for further human investigation. By reducing manual screening effort, the tools enable faster response to emerging information attacks. The pilot strengthens media resilience by combining automation with editorial oversight, contributing to more effective protection of the public information space.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2608,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01965,EU_PSTW_202609_01965,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,AI-Code,Broadcasting and publishing services.,UNKNOWN,AI-Code - GenAi against disinformation: Use case 2,AI-Code; Consortium; Broadcasting and publishing services.; European Union,"AI-CODE Use Case 3 addresses this challenge by supporting responsible use of generative technologies in journalism. The use case delivers tools such as an interactive simulator and an AI-based personal companion that help media professionals test scenarios, identify misinformation and refine editorial judgement.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Media organisations increasingly experiment with generative tools, yet concerns remain about quality, bias and the risk of amplifying disinformation. AI-CODE Use Case 3 addresses this challenge by supporting responsible use of generative technologies in journalism. Stakeholders include journalists, editors and media trainers. The objective is to improve content quality while strengthening professional awareness of generative risks. The use case delivers tools such as an interactive simulator and an AI-based personal companion that help media professionals test scenarios, identify misinformation and refine editorial judgement. Rather than automating publishing, the tools focus on skill development and decision support. Early validation shows improved understanding of generative limitations and increased confidence in identifying misleading content. This use case contributes to building long-term trust in media by ensuring that emerging technologies reinforce, rather than undermine, journalistic standards.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2609,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01966,EU_PSTW_202609_01966,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""social_nonprofit""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Ministry of Digital Transformation,General services.,"Ukraine developed Diia.Engine to address longstanding weaknesses in public-service delivery, including fragmented administrative systems, dependence on paper-based procedures and limited interoperability between existing digital solutions. These structural problems made service development slower, more expensive and harder to scale consistently across institutions.",Diia.Engine - Ukrainian open-source platform for the public sector digitalisation,Ministry of Digital Transformation; Central-Government; General services.; Ukraine,"The stakeholders include national agencies, municipalities, service designers and citizens using digital public services, with the objective of reducing development cost, accelerating delivery and improving standardisation across the public sector. The solution is implemented as a national low-code, open-source platform based on reusable modules and shared digital components, including chatbot functions and automated validators.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Ukraine developed Diia.Engine to address longstanding weaknesses in public-service delivery, including fragmented administrative systems, dependence on paper-based procedures and limited interoperability between existing digital solutions. These structural problems made service development slower, more expensive and harder to scale consistently across institutions. The platform was introduced to provide government bodies with a common foundation for building digital services more quickly and more uniformly. The stakeholders include national agencies, municipalities, service designers and citizens using digital public services, with the objective of reducing development cost, accelerating delivery and improving standardisation across the public sector. The solution is implemented as a national low-code, open-source platform based on reusable modules and shared digital components, including chatbot functions and automated validators. At a conceptual level, institutions build services by assembling standardised blocks rather than developing each system from scratch, which shortens implementation cycles and improves interoperability. Diia.Engine is now used by 23 government agencies and municipalities; more than 40 digital services have been built on the platform, over 80% of them using standard modules, and around 40% of the population has accessed services created this way. Reported estimates indicate possible annual savings of up to USD 50 million if 100 registries are deployed through the platform. More broadly, Diia.Engine creates value through scalability, lower infrastructure use, faster service delivery and a replicable model for public-sector digital transformation.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2610,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01967,EU_PSTW_202609_01967,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Hellenic Police,Police Services,"Law Enforcement Authorities (LEAs) often face fragmentation in accessing and analyzing telecom data, which hinders cross-border cooperation and slows down criminal investigations.",TRACY: A big-data analytics platform for law enforcement investigations,Hellenic Police; Central-Government; Police Services; Greece,"The TRACY project was implemented to address the growing need for secure, efficient, and interoperable use of electronic communications metadata in the investigation of serious and organised crime across EU Member States. TRACY provides an AI-powered data analytics framework tailored for LEAs, improving the timeliness, accuracy, and interoperability of investigations.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The TRACY project was implemented to address the growing need for secure, efficient, and interoperable use of electronic communications metadata in the investigation of serious and organised crime across EU Member States. Law Enforcement Authorities (LEAs) often face fragmentation in accessing and analyzing telecom data, which hinders cross-border cooperation and slows down criminal investigations. TRACY provides an AI-powered data analytics framework tailored for LEAs, improving the timeliness, accuracy, and interoperability of investigations. Preliminary results indicate a significant improvement in processing time and data correlation across jurisdictions. Pilot implementation of TRACY for 6 months in real cases demonstrated a 1000x acceleration in crime resolution. With the use of visual analytics TRACY manages to pinpoint the perpetrators in all cases outperforming manual resolution. TRACY received positive feedback from over 80% of end-users who rated the tools as user-friendly and operationally relevant. Qualitative feedback also highlighted increased confidence in the legal and ethical compliance of the TRACY system, thanks to its embedded privacy-by-design and auditability features. The solution has achieved its core objectives of (1) demonstrating the feasibility of harmonized metadata analysis workflows, (2) equipping LEAs with scalable analytics tools, and (3) informing EU-wide training and standardisation efforts. By addressing both technological and procedural gaps, TRACY contributes to a more resilient, interoperable, and intelligence-led law enforcement ecosystem in Europe.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2611,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01968,EU_PSTW_202609_01968,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Comunidad de Madrid,R&D education.,UNKNOWN,Robic: Inclusive AI for Special Learning,Comunidad de Madrid; Regional Government; R&D education.; Spain,"Implemented at Colegio Gaudem in Madrid, the initiative aims to enhance the personal, cognitive, and emotional development of students with special educational needs through personalised, structured interactions using Inrobic Clinic and Inrobic Virtual.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"This pilot project explores the feasibility and educational impact of an AI-supported social robotics solution in a special education setting. Implemented at Colegio Gaudem in Madrid, the initiative aims to enhance the personal, cognitive, and emotional development of students with special educational needs through personalised, structured interactions using Inrobic Clinic and Inrobic Virtual. Between November 2024 and March 2025, 300 sessions were conducted involving 28 students, mostly in group formats. High satisfaction scores were reported by educators and families, with notable improvements in communication, motor skills, and engagement across subjects like math and language. Students responded positively, treating the robot as a peer and showing increased motivation. Teachers integrated the solution smoothly into their practices, and families expressed strong interest in its continuation. Early findings support the value and effectiveness of AI-assisted robotics in promoting inclusive and adaptive education.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2612,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01969,EU_PSTW_202609_01969,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Digital and Population Data Services Agency (Finnish Digital Agency),General public services n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,Interoperability Platform - Finnish metadata platform for semantic linked data specifications as a service,Digital and Population Data Services Agency (Finnish Digital Agency); Central-Government; General public services n.e.c.; Finland,"When the developers use the same datamodels and code sets, unnecessary variation can be avoided. For example, the idea of OOTS is to use data that is collected by a certain authority, so that there is no need to copy the data for other authorities.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Interoperability platform is a metadata portal, from which the users can find, what kind of data there is available, who provides it, and what is the structure of the data. When the users find useful metadata descriptions, they do not have to re-invent that information, but can reuse existing descriptions, and so save time and money. When the developers use the same datamodels and code sets, unnecessary variation can be avoided. For example, the idea of OOTS is to use data that is collected by a certain authority, so that there is no need to copy the data for other authorities. Those can request the information they need from the authority that owns the data. But one needs to know the contents of the data in order to request it in semantically correct manner.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2613,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01970,EU_PSTW_202609_01970,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Diputació de Barcelona,General services.,UNKNOWN,Local.iA: promoting public and responsible AI,Diputació de Barcelona; Local Government; General services.; Spain,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Local.iA is an ecosystem of resources, materials, and initiatives that provides local authorities—free of charge—with training paths, guides, and communication materials to deploy AI and data governance in an organised manner. In addition, it promotes AI-based proof-of-concept pilots to address local government needs, in collaboration with municipalities. Some of these pilots are already in production, while others are in the analysis phase. All pilots are documented and shared openly.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2614,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01971,EU_PSTW_202609_01971,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""social_nonprofit""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Italian National Institute of Social Security (INPS),Social protection n.e.c.,"This process involves the manual review of millions of documents annually by operators, all within a constrained timeframe. To address these challenges, an innovative solution has been developed, integrating on-premise Generative Artificial Intelligence technologies designed to support operators in document analysis activities.",Document Review in Service Requests powered by Gen AI,Italian National Institute of Social Security (INPS); Central-Government; Social protection n.e.c.; Italy,"The goal of the project was to streamline and enhance the verification process through AI-powered automation, reducing manual workload, ensuring consistency in checks, and improving response times for citizens.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"INPS provides services where users are required to submit various types of documents to support their requests, which must undergo a thorough review to verify their validity. This process involves the manual review of millions of documents annually by operators, all within a constrained timeframe. This involves a wide variety of documents (invoices, contracts, travel tickets, payment receipts, etc), making the process highly time-consuming and error prone. The goal of the project was to streamline and enhance the verification process through AI-powered automation, reducing manual workload, ensuring consistency in checks, and improving response times for citizens. To address these challenges, an innovative solution has been developed, integrating on-premise Generative Artificial Intelligence technologies designed to support operators in document analysis activities. Key features of the solution include: • Automatic classification of documents based on both type and content; • Data extraction and verification, with automated comparison between the information in the documents, the tender requirements, and the associated metadata; • Smart visualization of AI-extracted fields, with automatic highlighting directly within the document for easy reference; • Decision support, offering a detailed summary of the extracted data, flagging any discrepancies, and assisting with manual validation. This solution significantly streamlines the verification process, reducing manual effort and increasing both the speed and consistency of checks, delivering concrete benefits to both staff and citizens. Moreover, the system’s ability to adapt and learn from new data ensures continuous performance improvement, supporting progressively more effective procedure management. The solution aims to deliver benefits for both operators and citizens. Overall, the implementation of Generative AI has proven to be a transformative step in modernizing the document verification process, setting a strong foundation for future innovation and scalability. The next objective is primarily to adopt this solution for the other services within the INPS system.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,S3_DOCUMENT_INTELLIGENCE|S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2615,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01972,EU_PSTW_202609_01972,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Italian National Institute of Social Security (INPS),Social protection n.e.c.,"To address the challenges of bureaucratic language, INPS has launched a structured simplification program aimed at making content understandable to users with a middle school level of education. Over 700 texts have been manually simplified so far, resulting in an increase in the average Gulpease readability score from 57 to 64—a substantial improvement in accessibility.",Text Simplification for Citizens with GenAI,Italian National Institute of Social Security (INPS); Central-Government; Social protection n.e.c.; Italy,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Clear and accessible communication is essential to ensure that citizens can effectively navigate public services. INPS—Italy’s National Institute of Social Security and the largest welfare institution in Europe—serves approximately 45 million citizens through nearly 500 digital services related to pensions and welfare. The clarity of these service descriptions plays a critical role in guaranteeing equitable access and a positive user experience. To address the challenges of bureaucratic language, INPS has launched a structured simplification program aimed at making content understandable to users with a middle school level of education. Over 700 texts have been manually simplified so far, resulting in an increase in the average Gulpease readability score from 57 to 64—a substantial improvement in accessibility. However, this manual process is labor-intensive, requiring specialised linguistic and legal expertise, and is not easily scalable. The need for plain language in public administration has long been acknowledged in Italy. A 2002 directive still in force emphasizes that “all texts produced by administrations must be designed and written to be understood by those who receive them.” Yet institutional communication remains overly formal and complex, hindering citizens’ ability to fully access and understand critical services. While simplification improves transparency and public trust, it must also preserve the legal precision required for official documentation. To overcome these limitations, INPS has integrated Large Language Models (LLMs) into its simplification workflow, developing a semi-automated system—operating in a human-in-the-loop model—that is both scalable and efficient. This solution can process high volumes of content while ensuring readability, semantic accuracy, and legal compliance. A dedicated evaluation framework was introduced to measure key aspects of the output, including readability, fluency, and content preservation. User testing further validated the approach, confirming the quality of AI-generated content in comparison to both the original and manually simplified texts. The AI-based system delivers expert-level results in a fraction of the time and cost required by manual processes. It enhances INPS’s operational efficiency, standardizes quality across service pages, and ensures that content remains accessible and legally sound. Human oversight guarantees accuracy and relevance, while integrated metrics provide transparency and guide ongoing optimization. A large-scale user study involving A/B testing with 1,620 participants confirmed the model’s effectiveness. AI-generated texts were preferred in over 70% of cases when compared to the original versions and outperformed them across all measured dimensions: fluency (+14.1%), readability (+13.5%), clarity (+11.1%), and engagement (+12.3%). The AI semplified versions outperformed also the corresponding human simplified texts across all the qualitative metrics. Citizens are the ultimate beneficiaries of this transformation. By simplifying complex institutional language, INPS reduces barriers to access for individuals with limited language skills, lower educational attainment, or reading difficulties. More inclusive communication strengthens transparency, encourages greater civic engagement, and builds trust between citizens and institutions. Beyond INPS, this AI-driven approach has strong potential for adoption in other sectors where complex language limits accessibility—such as healthcare, legal services, taxation, and social welfare. Its ability to adapt to diverse regulatory and linguistic contexts makes it a powerful tool for modernising public communication across government systems. In summary, the integration of LLMs into INPS’s content simplification strategy demonstrates how generative AI can enhance accessibility, efficiency, and trust in public services—delivering clear, comprehensible, and citizen-centered communication at scale.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,S7_GENERATIVE_CONTENT_CREATION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2616,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01973,EU_PSTW_202609_01973,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Ministry of Justice, Public Administration and Digital Transformation",General public services n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,"Once-Only Common Services: Croatia uses cloud native, cloud agnostic building blocks to make accessing the Single Market simple and easier for EU citizens and businesses","Ministry of Justice, Public Administration and Digital Transformation; Central-Government; General public services n.e.c.; Croatia","Implemented across numerous central government institutions – including ministries, agencies, and national registers – OOTS improves efficiency, transparency, and interoperability in public administration.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Croatia has adopted the Once-Only Technical System (OOTS) to simplify cross-border procedures for citizens and businesses within the EU. The system allows official documents (e.g., certificates, diplomas, permits) to be exchanged securely and automatically between competent authorities, removing the need for individuals or companies to resubmit the same data multiple times. Implemented across numerous central government institutions – including ministries, agencies, and national registers – OOTS improves efficiency, transparency, and interoperability in public administration. By connecting to the EU-wide OOTS Common Services, Croatia helps reduce administrative costs and processing times, supporting a more seamless Digital Single Market and making it easier for citizens and businesses to live, work, study, and operate across borders.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2617,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01974,EU_PSTW_202609_01974,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Generalitat de Catalunya: Centre of Telecommunications and Information Technology (CTTI) together with the “Department of Territory, Housing and Ecologic Transition""",Environmental protection n.e.c.,"This has reduced report preparation time by around 45%, saving nearly 800 hours of manual work per year, while improving consistency, objectivity, and transparency in administrative processes.",Generation of environmental technical reports using Generative AI,"Generalitat de Catalunya: Centre of Telecommunications and Information Technology (CTTI) together with the “Department of Territory, Housing and Ecologic Transition""; Regional Government; Environmental protection n.e.c.; Spain",UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Generalitat de Catalunya has piloted a Generative AI–based system for drafting environmental technical reports, developed by the Centre of Telecommunications and Information Technology (CTTI) and the Department of Territory, Housing and Ecologic Transition. The solution addresses the high volume and repetitive nature of authorisations for activities in protected areas—such as mountain races, drone flights, and forest management—where more than 4,500 reports are prepared annually. By combining visual-language models, geospatial algorithms, and a vectorised legal knowledge base, the tool analyses route data and regulations to automatically generate draft reports. This has reduced report preparation time by around 45%, saving nearly 800 hours of manual work per year, while improving consistency, objectivity, and transparency in administrative processes. The system is fully integrated with the Generalitat’s secure intranet and platforms such as SINATCAT, supports Catalan language outputs, and complies with interoperability, security, and data protection standards. Designed to be modular, scalable, and reusable, it improves efficiency, standardises decision-making, and strengthens public trust by ensuring clearer, more uniform environmental reporting.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,S7_GENERATIVE_CONTENT_CREATION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2618,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01975,EU_PSTW_202609_01975,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Regional Government of Andalusia (Junta de Andalucía),Sickness and disability.,"ALS requires rapid, coordinated support, but traditional processes caused long delays and stress for families. With automated data flows and proactive service triggers, ELA 360 has reduced waiting times from 1 year to under 3 months and eliminated paperwork, ensuring patients receive essential services faster and with less burden.",ELA 360: Humanising Public Services Through Collaborative Innovation,Regional Government of Andalusia (Junta de Andalucía); Regional Government; Sickness and disability.; Spain,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"ELA 360 is a pilot by the Regional Government of Andalusia to improve care for ALS patients under Spain’s new ALS Law (Law 3/2024). ALS requires rapid, coordinated support, but traditional processes caused long delays and stress for families. With automated data flows and proactive service triggers, ELA 360 has reduced waiting times from 1 year to under 3 months and eliminated paperwork, ensuring patients receive essential services faster and with less burden. Developed through co-creation with patients, families, and professionals, the solution integrates health and social systems, improves transparency, and builds trust in public administration. First piloted at Hospital Virgen Macarena, its scalable, modular design makes it adaptable to other municipalities and vulnerable groups, offering a replicable model for more empathetic public services.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2619,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01976,EU_PSTW_202609_01976,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Dublin City Council,Community development.,UNKNOWN,Docklands Digital Twin: Laying the Foundations for Smarter Urban Futures,Dublin City Council; Local Government; Community development.; Ireland,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Docklands Digital Twin, developed by Dublin City Council under the Smart Docklands programme, transforms complex spatial data into an interactive 3D model to support more transparent, evidence-based urban planning. Unlike static maps and siloed reports, the twin integrates multiple datasets into a shared virtual environment, accessible via desktop, web, and VR. Internally, it helps city stakeholders visualise proposed developments and coordinate planning. Externally, it improves public engagement and trust by allowing residents and community groups to explore future changes in an intuitive, accessible format. Developed with interoperability and scalability in mind, the model has already inspired replication beyond Docklands and is positioned to expand into areas such as mobility, climate resilience, and citizen participation. Delivered through collaboration between Dublin City Council, SMEs, and academia, the Docklands Digital Twin lays the groundwork for a citywide digital twin strategy, modernising governance and shaping smarter, more inclusive urban futures.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2620,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01977,EU_PSTW_202609_01977,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Madrid City Council (Spain) - Directorate General of the Digital Office,Communication,UNKNOWN,"Madrid, Digital Capital: City Intelligence, 5G, Interoperability, aimed at improving citizens' well-being through Emerging Technologies",Madrid City Council (Spain) - Directorate General of the Digital Office; Local Government; Communication; Spain,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"As part of its Digital Transformation Strategy, Madrid launched the 5G Agenda to provide advanced connectivity infrastructure, enabling smart, sustainable, and people-centered public services. The initiative modernises internal municipal operations and public-facing services, improving urban mobility, emergency response, waste management, and administrative efficiency for over 3.4 million residents, 5 million daily users, and 11 million annual visitors. Key implementations include 5G urban corridors, IoTMADLab, autonomous vehicles at Mercamadrid, and the EUGENIA emergency network, as well as enhanced connectivity at Valdemingómez Technology Park. The project has led to significant efficiency gains, including €246 million saved over five years, a 95% reduction in paper submissions, and measurable improvements in citizen satisfaction. Beyond operational benefits, the 5G Agenda has strengthened public-private collaboration, fostered innovation, and reinforced Madrid as a European benchmark in advanced connectivity and smart city services.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2621,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01978,EU_PSTW_202609_01978,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Secretary of State of the Free and Hanseatic City of Hamburg (Senatskanzlei),Social protection n.e.c.,"The administrative challenge is that employees handling social benefits requests must interpret complex legislation, locate relevant guidance and provide consistent answers under time pressure. This can slow case processing and create uneven service quality if information is difficult to retrieve.",KIRA - AI research assistant,Secretary of State of the Free and Hanseatic City of Hamburg (Senatskanzlei); Regional Government; Social protection n.e.c.; Germany,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Hamburg’s KIRA Research Assistant was developed to help public administration staff manage large volumes of legal and social-services documentation more efficiently. The administrative challenge is that employees handling social benefits requests must interpret complex legislation, locate relevant guidance and provide consistent answers under time pressure. This can slow case processing and create uneven service quality if information is difficult to retrieve. The main stakeholders include the Free and Hanseatic City of Hamburg, the Senatskanzlei, public administration staff, social-benefit applicants and the InnoTecHH Fonds supporting the project. KIRA operates as an AI-driven knowledge tool and chatbot that retrieves relevant documentation and provides accurate answers to staff queries. Conceptually, it combines document retrieval, question answering, knowledge-base search and conversational access to legal and administrative information. Pilot results indicate strong performance, with an 87% top-k accuracy rate, a usability score of 9.75 out of 10 on the SUS measure, and high satisfaction among staff. The expected impact is shorter research time, more reliable processing of social benefits requests, reduced pressure on employees and more consistent service delivery. By supporting staff rather than replacing their judgement, KIRA improves the administrative knowledge workflow and directly benefits citizens through faster and more dependable handling of requests.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2622,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01979,EU_PSTW_202609_01979,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Ephorate of Antiquities of Western Attica,Cultural services.,"It identified key challenges including limited resources, insufficiently trained staff, incomplete procedures, rigid schedules, and ongoing legislative changes.",ERP And CRM Costumisation for Project Management in the Public Sector,Ephorate of Antiquities of Western Attica; Regional Government; Cultural services.; Greece,The use case by the Ephorate of Antiquities of Western Attica aims to optimise project management at the Ephorate of Antiquities of Western Attica and maximise expenditure absorption per funding source.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The use case by the Ephorate of Antiquities of Western Attica aims to optimise project management at the Ephorate of Antiquities of Western Attica and maximise expenditure absorption per funding source. It identified key challenges including limited resources, insufficiently trained staff, incomplete procedures, rigid schedules, and ongoing legislative changes. The recommended solution was the procurement and customisation of ERP (Enterprise Resource Planning) and CRM (Customer Relationship Management) software, following a thorough modelling and documentation of the Service’s processes and project objectives.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2623,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01980,EU_PSTW_202609_01980,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Technical University of Applied Sciences Wildau,Education n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,Pepper robot as an examination assistant and source of information,Technical University of Applied Sciences Wildau; Academic-Research; Education n.e.c.; Germany,"Pepper at TH Wildau is a deployed library assistant, based on SoftBank’s humanoid platform and the NAOqi software stack. Speech interaction uses an open, componentised chain: automatic speech recognition (speech-to-text), intent and entity extraction for library domains (orientation, lending, services, events), response generation from curated templates and FAQs, and text-to-speech.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Pepper at TH Wildau is a deployed library assistant, based on SoftBank’s humanoid platform and the NAOqi software stack. The project adapts Pepper for a 24/7 academic library, where access is card-controlled outside staffed hours. The robot greets visitors, answers common questions, guides users to locations across three floors, gives short tours, and demonstrates equipment such as the printer–scanner–copier. The solution combines scripted behaviors with a lightweight NLP pipeline. Behaviors are authored in Choregraphe/Python as modular, rule-based flows for gestures, tablet prompts, and navigation sequences. Speech interaction uses an open, componentised chain: automatic speech recognition (speech-to-text), intent and entity extraction for library domains (orientation, lending, services, events), response generation from curated templates and FAQs, and text-to-speech. The team prioritises transparent, locally hosted components over closed assistants. For perception and mobility, Pepper uses classical robotics methods for localisation and path planning; autonomous driving is complemented by tablet teleoperation for safety and elevators. Two units are operated: “Wilma” serves in the library as a mobile information point; “Bernd” is used in the RoboticLab for testing and training. Content and dialogs are updated iteratively, with analytics from interactions feeding refinements to intents, utterances, and tour scripts.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S3_DOCUMENT_INTELLIGENCE,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2624,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01981,EU_PSTW_202609_01981,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,MA01 Wien Digital,General public services n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,Vienna AI with generative search (de: WienKI mit generative Suche),MA01 Wien Digital; Local Government; General public services n.e.c.; Austria,"More than 2,500 employees are already actively using the system, with over 100,000 chat interactions, 85,000 image generations, and 14,000 curated knowledge documents to date.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The City of Vienna has proactively embraced artificial intelligence to modernise public administration and deliver tangible benefits to citizens and employees. Recognising the disruptive potential of generative AI, Vienna developed WienKI, a holistic AI platform designed to improve efficiency, safeguard data sovereignty, and foster responsible innovation. WienKI brings together secure, user-friendly tools such as multilingual assistants, knowledge databases with anonymisation functions, and image generation, supported by a strong governance framework and continuous staff training. More than 2,500 employees are already actively using the system, with over 100,000 chat interactions, 85,000 image generations, and 14,000 curated knowledge documents to date. Built on a modular, open-source architecture, WienKI ensures compliance with GDPR, the EU AI Act, and strict security standards, whilst remaining transparent, scalable, and reusable by other public institutions. It enhances internal efficiency, strengthens accessibility across 103 languages, and encourages a culture of responsible innovation throughout the administration. WienKI demonstrates how public administration can act as a pioneer in times of technological change—balancing innovation and responsibility to create sustainable value for both employees and citizens.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2625,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01982,EU_PSTW_202609_01982,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Berufsgenossenschaft Energie Textil Elektro Medienerzeugnisse (BG ETEM),Sickness and disability.,"The statutory accident insurer BG ETEM faced a sharp rise in electronic business registrations, causing long delays and a five-digit backlog. The tool now automatically categorises most registrations, checks data against external registers, and reduces manual effort, enabling routine cases to be processed quickly while staff focus on complex ones.",AI-Enhanced Processing of Business Registrations for Faster Public Service Delivery,Berufsgenossenschaft Energie Textil Elektro Medienerzeugnisse (BG ETEM); Central-Government; Sickness and disability.; Germany,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The statutory accident insurer BG ETEM faced a sharp rise in electronic business registrations, causing long delays and a five-digit backlog. To resolve this, it introduced the EDA GWA AI solution, which integrates artificial intelligence and robotic process automation into its core system. The tool now automatically categorises most registrations, checks data against external registers, and reduces manual effort, enabling routine cases to be processed quickly while staff focus on complex ones. Since its rollout in 2024, over 100,000 registrations have been handled, response times have improved, and the backlog has already been cut by 13%. Built with open-source components such as Python, Scikit-learn, XGBoost and Docker, the plugin is transparent, explainable, and compliant with strict legal standards. Its modular, containerised design makes it cost-efficient, robust, and transferable to other statutory insurers. By combining automation with human oversight, the solution delivers faster services for businesses, greater efficiency for administration, and strong potential for reuse across the wider social insurance sector.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,S6_WORKFLOW_PROCESS_AUTOMATION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2626,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01983,EU_PSTW_202609_01983,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Berufsgenossenschaft Energie Textil Elektro Medienerzeugnisse (BG ETEM),Social protection n.e.c.,"Previously, such cases were often detected late through manual reminders, leading to delayed interventions, higher treatment costs and longer recovery times. This has replaced the outdated manual reminder process with proactive, automated monitoring, while ensuring case officers remain in control of final decisions.",RehaPlus: AI-Powered Identification of Complex Rehabilitation Cases in Public Health Insurance,Berufsgenossenschaft Energie Textil Elektro Medienerzeugnisse (BG ETEM); Central-Government; Social protection n.e.c.; Germany,RehaPlus applies explainable machine learning models to continuously monitor case data and flag potential high-risk cases weeks earlier than before.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The statutory accident insurer BG ETEM introduced RehaPlus to improve the early identification of complex rehabilitation cases requiring intensive case management. Previously, such cases were often detected late through manual reminders, leading to delayed interventions, higher treatment costs and longer recovery times. RehaPlus applies explainable machine learning models to continuously monitor case data and flag potential high-risk cases weeks earlier than before. This has replaced the outdated manual reminder process with proactive, automated monitoring, while ensuring case officers remain in control of final decisions. The result is improved efficiency, fewer documents to screen or complete, earlier interventions, and better recovery outcomes for insured workers. Since going live in 2021, RehaPlus has delivered high user satisfaction, with more than 90 per cent of staff rating it positively for improving efficiency and quality of work. Built with open-source components such as Python, XGBoost, Scikit-learn, Pandas and Docker, the solution is lightweight, explainable, cost-efficient and fully compliant with GDPR. Its modular architecture makes it portable across infrastructures and scalable to other social accident insurers, with the first reuse planned for 2026. By combining automation with transparency and human oversight, RehaPlus demonstrates how AI can create both internal efficiency gains (G2G) and societal value (G2C) through better rehabilitation outcomes.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2627,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01984,EU_PSTW_202609_01984,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Berufsgenossenschaft Energie Textil Elektro Medienerzeugnisse (BG ETEM),Sickness and disability.,"Around 70 per cent of queries concern recurring issues such as registration or insurance fees, which were previously handled manually, causing long response times and high workloads. By combining automation, explainability, and scalability, MeinAssistent strengthens efficiency and accessibility in public service delivery while reducing manual workload for staff.",AI-Based Virtual Assistant for Faster and Reliable Businesses Support,Berufsgenossenschaft Energie Textil Elektro Medienerzeugnisse (BG ETEM); Central-Government; Sickness and disability.; Germany,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The statutory accident insurer BG ETEM developed a virtual assistant (MeinAssistent) to support its “Members and Fees” department, which has seen a sharp increase in inquiries from businesses. Around 70 per cent of queries concern recurring issues such as registration or insurance fees, which were previously handled manually, causing long response times and high workloads. The assistant provides a three-tiered service: immediate answers from a legally verified FAQ catalogue, context-specific responses generated from official ETEM documents with clear source references, and transparent escalation to staff if no automated answer is possible. This ensures businesses receive reliable, accurate, and timely support while keeping human oversight for complex cases. Early results from the pilot show clear benefits: 24/7 accessibility for businesses, faster responses, reduced interruptions for staff, and high trust thanks to transparent answer paths. Pilot users have praised the clarity, reliability, and seamless handover to human officers. Built with open-source components including Python, LangChain, Hugging Face models, FastAPI, and Docker, the system is modular, containerised, and API-based, making it cost-efficient, portable, and adaptable to other contexts. Although still in pilot, the solution is being integrated into the MeineETEM portal and is designed for transfer to other public sector organisations by connecting it to their own FAQs and documents. By combining automation, explainability, and scalability, MeinAssistent strengthens efficiency and accessibility in public service delivery while reducing manual workload for staff.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2628,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01985,EU_PSTW_202609_01985,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Ministry of Climate Crisis and Civil Protection / Hellenic Fire Service / Fire Service Headquarters / Department of Preventive Fire Protection,Fire-protection services,UNKNOWN,Digital Service for Obligated Citizens to Declare the Cleaning of Their Properties to Prevent Fire Risks or Rapid Fire Spread,Ministry of Climate Crisis and Civil Protection / Hellenic Fire Service / Fire Service Headquarters / Department of Preventive Fire Protection; Central-Government; Fire-protection services; Greece,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The digital platform akatharista.gov.gr is an innovative tool designed to prevent and manage forest and grassland fires in Greece by monitoring the cleaning status of privately owned plots, a key preventive measure against fire outbreaks. The system enables citizens to declare the fire-prevention cleaning activities they have undertaken on their plots each season and allows them to report hazardous plots to supervisory authorities, supporting proactive intervention. For public administration, the platform consolidates and organises critical data—including plot location, ownership, cleaning history, and reported risks—allowing central and local authorities, such as the Fire Service and municipalities, to monitor compliance, assess risk, plan targeted inspections, and take timely preventive action. Since its launch in 2024, over 1.5 million cleaning declarations and 20,000 citizen reports have been processed, resulting in more than 18,000 on-site inspections and more efficient resource deployment. Technologically, the platform integrates with national databases, supports real-time GIS visualisation of plots, and provides automated notifications to citizens. Its interoperable and user-friendly design ensures accessibility, traceability, and transparency, while statistical analysis capabilities inform policy decisions on fire prevention and civil protection. Akatharista.gov.gr exemplifies a digital governance solution that enhances public participation, strengthens accountability, and safeguards human life, property, and ecosystems.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2630,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01986,EU_PSTW_202609_01986,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Amsterdam,General services.,UNKNOWN,ChatAmsterdam - an internally developed generative AI chatbot tailored to the municipality's needs and values to support civil servants in their daily activities,Municipality of Amsterdam; Local Government; General services.; Netherlands,"By providing a safe alternative to commercial generative AI tools, ChatAmsterdam reduces shadow use of external platforms and facilitates responsible AI adoption.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"ChatAmsterdam is an internally developed generative AI assistant designed to support civil servants in the Municipality of Amsterdam. Developed through a six-month research pilot involving over 800 employees, the application provides practical assistance with daily tasks while promoting AI literacy, ensuring digital security, and safeguarding sensitive municipal data. ChatAmsterdam operates within a secure, vendor-neutral environment, prioritising data ownership, privacy, transparency, and compliance with Dutch and European regulations. The platform enables integration with internal data sources, allowing staff to pose domain-specific questions and receive contextually relevant answers. By providing a safe alternative to commercial generative AI tools, ChatAmsterdam reduces shadow use of external platforms and facilitates responsible AI adoption. Its flexible architecture allows future expansion, including additional functionalities, AI-assisted workflows, and integration of new datasets, while ensuring alignment with organisational values and operational goals. The application reflects a user-centred design approach, incorporating continuous feedback, training, and co-creation with staff to maximise usability, trust, and impact.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2631,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01987,EU_PSTW_202609_01987,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,State Consumer Rights Protection Authority (SCRPA),"General economic, commercial and labour affair","This automation has reduced manual depersonalisation time from several hours to under 15 minutes, achieving a 95% efficiency gain, while maintaining 99.8% accuracy in PII detection. Its advanced Natural Language Processing (NLP) and Machine Learning (ML) capabilities allow context-aware recognition of PII in structured and unstructured data, outperforming traditional rule-based methods.",AI tool for depersonalising sensitive data at the State Consumer Rights Protection Authority (SCRPA),"State Consumer Rights Protection Authority (SCRPA); Regional Government; General economic, commercial and labour affair; Lithuania",UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The personal data depersonalisation AI tool is an internally developed solution designed to enhance data privacy, security, and regulatory compliance. By automatically identifying, masking, encrypting, or removing Personally Identifiable Information (PII), the tool ensures that datasets used for development, testing, and analysis are fully anonymised, embedding a “privacy by design” culture across the organisation. This automation has reduced manual depersonalisation time from several hours to under 15 minutes, achieving a 95% efficiency gain, while maintaining 99.8% accuracy in PII detection. The solution is fully interoperable with existing internal systems, integrates seamlessly into data pipelines, and complies with GDPR, CCPA, and organisational policies. Its advanced Natural Language Processing (NLP) and Machine Learning (ML) capabilities allow context-aware recognition of PII in structured and unstructured data, outperforming traditional rule-based methods. The tool has delivered measurable impact, including an 85% reduction in data breaches involving unauthorised PII exposure, significant labour cost savings, and increased user confidence. By providing a reliable, scalable, and user-friendly solution, the AI tool strengthens operational efficiency, data integrity, and transparency, allowing secure data sharing with internal teams and external partners without compromising privacy.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK|F04_UNSTRUCTURED_INFORMATION_COMPLEXITY,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2632,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01988,EU_PSTW_202609_01988,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,State Consumer Rights Protection Authority (SCRPA),"General economic, commercial and labour affair",UNKNOWN,Virtual Market Assistant for the State Consumer Rights Protection Authority (SCRPA),"State Consumer Rights Protection Authority (SCRPA); Regional Government; General economic, commercial and labour affair; Lithuania","It allows barcode scanning for instant product safety checks and simplifies the submission of detailed hazard reports, including photos and descriptions, which are standardised for seamless use by regulatory authorities. Technologically, the app leverages familiar mobile, cloud, and database infrastructure, providing a scalable and user-friendly solution without requiring specialised equipment.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Virtual Market Assistant is an innovative mobile application designed to enhance consumer safety by providing instant access to information on officially listed hazardous products and enabling users to report new risks. By connecting to verified databases such as the EU Safety Gate (RAPEX) and integrating user-submitted reports, the app offers proactive protection and empowers citizens to participate directly in market surveillance. It allows barcode scanning for instant product safety checks and simplifies the submission of detailed hazard reports, including photos and descriptions, which are standardised for seamless use by regulatory authorities. Technologically, the app leverages familiar mobile, cloud, and database infrastructure, providing a scalable and user-friendly solution without requiring specialised equipment. It is designed with full interoperability in mind, adhering to the European Interoperability Framework at legal, organisational, semantic, and technical levels, ensuring seamless integration with national and transnational market surveillance systems. By increasing transparency, accountability, and public engagement, the Virtual Market Assistant strengthens trust between citizens and regulatory authorities, streamlines hazard reporting, and accelerates the removal of unsafe products from the market.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2633,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01989,EU_PSTW_202609_01989,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Idomsot Zrt.,General services.,"Built on open-source AI generative models and service architectures, DONNA integrates with tools like JIRA and Confluence to consolidate fragmented documentation and historical incident data into actionable, contextually enriched recommendations.",DONNA: A digital assistant for IT operations,Idomsot Zrt.; Central-Government; General services.; Hungary,"The solution leverages a modular, containerised architecture (Kubernetes/Kubeflow) with components including vLLM, Milvus, LangChain, LangGraph, Langfuse, and OpenWebUI, ensuring scalability, technical interoperability, and data sovereignty. By using fully self-hosted, open-source technologies, DONNA enhances transparency, auditability, and independence from proprietary vendors, while a user-centred development process involving IT operators ensured high adoption, usability, and alignment with operational realities.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"DONNA is a digital assistant designed to enhance IT operations within public administration, targeting critical services such as vehicle registration and identity card issuance. Built on open-source AI generative models and service architectures, DONNA integrates with tools like JIRA and Confluence to consolidate fragmented documentation and historical incident data into actionable, contextually enriched recommendations. By doing so, it accelerates incident resolution, reduces downtime, and maintains service reliability for citizen-facing digital services. The solution leverages a modular, containerised architecture (Kubernetes/Kubeflow) with components including vLLM, Milvus, LangChain, LangGraph, Langfuse, and OpenWebUI, ensuring scalability, technical interoperability, and data sovereignty. Designed following the European Interoperability Framework (EIF), DONNA aligns with legal, organisational, semantic, and technical interoperability principles, supporting seamless integration with internal IT workflows and potential future cross-government systems. By using fully self-hosted, open-source technologies, DONNA enhances transparency, auditability, and independence from proprietary vendors, while a user-centred development process involving IT operators ensured high adoption, usability, and alignment with operational realities.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2634,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01990,EU_PSTW_202609_01990,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"KITT6, příspěvková organizace",General public services n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,AI agent for Prague 6: Simplifying communication between the office and citizens,"KITT6, příspěvková organizace; Local Government; General public services n.e.c.; Czechia",The Citymind chatbot was implemented by Prague 6 to improve citizen-office communication and streamline access to public services.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Citymind chatbot was implemented by Prague 6 to improve citizen-office communication and streamline access to public services. Its primary function is to provide citizens with a quick, intuitive interface to find information—covering topics such as passport issuance, ID cards, and local police services—while freeing officials from answering routine inquiries. The project, funded under the Horizon Europe CommuniCity programme, combines co-creation with citizens and municipal staff to ensure a user-centred design, resulting in a high user satisfaction rating of 4.05 out of 5. Technologically, the solution is scalable, secure, and user-friendly, integrating seamlessly into municipal websites with minimal technical effort. Its structured data processing ensures semantic interoperability, translating complex administrative content into plain, human-like language. The chatbot also generates analytical reports on common queries, supporting internal service improvements and enhancing organisational efficiency. The innovation process involved participatory development, iterative testing, and a replicability guide to support adoption by other municipalities. Citymind is commercially managed, ensuring ongoing support and continuous development, while being aligned with European interoperability and accessibility principles.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI|S7_GENERATIVE_CONTENT_CREATION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2635,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01991,EU_PSTW_202609_01991,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Magistrat der Landeshauptstadt Linz,General services.,UNKNOWN,AI Literacy in Action: An Interactive and Reusable Prompting Workbook for Public Servants,Magistrat der Landeshauptstadt Linz; Local Government; General services.; Austria,"Technologically, the solution leverages a lightweight web application and structured content (JSON-based exercises), enabling semantic, organisational, technical, and legal interoperability.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The AI Prompting Workbook was developed to rapidly build AI literacy among public administration employees, addressing the limitations of passive training formats and fulfilling capacity-building requirements under the EU AI Act. The interactive, self-paced workbook allows users to engage directly with an internal AI model (“LinzGPT”), experimenting with prompts, executing them in real time, and receiving instant feedback across 50 practical exercises. Internal metrics—voluntary enrolment, course completion, and certificate issuance—demonstrate high uptake and effectiveness, while qualitative feedback from the “AI Pioneers” focus group confirms its practical relevance and engaging format. Technologically, the solution leverages a lightweight web application and structured content (JSON-based exercises), enabling semantic, organisational, technical, and legal interoperability. It is designed for easy scaling and reuse, both internally and as a public Google Gemini Canvas, allowing other administrations to customise or expand the workbook without programming knowledge. The implementation followed a user-centric, agile process, combining in-house prototyping, co-creation with internal focus groups, and rigorous IT validation, with no external procurement required. The dual-delivery model ensures secure internal deployment while maximising public value and societal impact, supporting AI capacity-building and fostering a broader culture of digital empowerment across Europe.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2636,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01992,EU_PSTW_202609_01992,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Luxembourg Space Agency (LSA),Environmental protection n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,Regional Digital Twin Platform for Climate Change,Luxembourg Space Agency (LSA); Central-Government; Environmental protection n.e.c.; Luxembourg,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Regional Digital Twin for Climate Change provides a data-driven platform for climate risk assessment, simulation, and decision support, addressing gaps in integrating EU-scale climate data into local and sector-specific operations. It enhances resilience across public services and critical sectors—energy, finance, agriculture, and emergency planning—by combining diverse datasets, AI and physical simulations, and a climate services marketplace. The platform also federates stakeholders, fostering collaboration and trust. Pilot users in Luxembourg report improved risk understanding, faster and more informed decisions, and strengthened public and financial resilience. Technologically, it integrates satellite, in-situ, topographic, and regional data via AI and HPC-powered cloud solutions, offering real-time insights and multi-sectoral services. Designed according to the European Interoperability Framework (EIF), the platform ensures legal, organisational, semantic, and technical interoperability. Transparency is enhanced through dashboards, open data where possible, and workshops for stakeholders. Its modular architecture allows scalable deployment across sectors, regions, and administrative levels, with the potential for broader adoption by other administrations. The project follows a user-centred innovation process, beginning with a Proof of Concept by Thales Alenia Space, LIST, and PwC, incorporating feedback from ministries and critical infrastructure operators to ensure practical, actionable outcomes.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2637,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01993,EU_PSTW_202609_01993,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Dirección General de Transformación Digital de la Administración de Justicia,General public services n.e.c.,"The Ministry of the Presidency, Justice, and Relations with the Courts has harnessed Artificial Intelligence to manage vast volumes of unstructured legal information and streamline tens of thousands of procedures each year. AI services also produce plain-language and easy-to-read documents, enhancing accessibility for citizens, including those with functional or intellectual difficulties.",Artificial Intelligence Services Oriented to Legal Documents,Dirección General de Transformación Digital de la Administración de Justicia; Central-Government; General public services n.e.c.; Spain,"The AI infrastructure combines natural language processing, supervised neural networks, Transformer-based models, and GPU-enabled servers, deployed within a secure microservices architecture.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Ministry of the Presidency, Justice, and Relations with the Courts has harnessed Artificial Intelligence to manage vast volumes of unstructured legal information and streamline tens of thousands of procedures each year. AI tools support civil servants, judges, magistrates, and prosecutors by extracting and linking relevant information, generating timelines, summarising documents, and assisting with rule-based processes. A key initiative is the automatic transcription of hearings and trials, achieving an estimated 60% time saving, with over 3.28 million transcriptions completed to date, saving more than one million hours of work. AI services also produce plain-language and easy-to-read documents, enhancing accessibility for citizens, including those with functional or intellectual difficulties. The AI infrastructure combines natural language processing, supervised neural networks, Transformer-based models, and GPU-enabled servers, deployed within a secure microservices architecture. Services are accessible via portals such as ADIA and Carpeta Justicia and include anonymisation, document summarisation, intelligent assistants, and translation tools. These tools are integrated across multiple internal applications and are already being reused by other administrations, demonstrating scalability and interoperability. Development followed a user-centred, iterative process involving legal experts, linguists, programmers, and administrative staff. Solutions were piloted on a small scale and then expanded strategically, supported by knowledge-sharing initiatives such as the Justice Digital Transformation Forum. Overall, AI innovations have transformed operations, improved efficiency, fostered a culture of innovation, enhanced accessibility, and strengthened transparency and public trust in the justice system.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F04_UNSTRUCTURED_INFORMATION_COMPLEXITY,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2638,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01994,EU_PSTW_202609_01994,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Ministry of Digital Transformation of Ukraine,General public services n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,Diia.AI: The World's First National AI Agent for Government Services,Ministry of Digital Transformation of Ukraine; Central-Government; General public services n.e.c.; Ukraine,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"On September 1, 2025, Ukraine became the first country in the world to introduce an AI assistant for government services via its Diia portal. Announced by Digital Transformation Minister Mykhailo Fedorov on Telegram, the AI tool is currently in open beta testing. Designed to enhance convenience and efficiency, it guides users through accessing public services, recommends the most suitable options based on their personal situations, and can even provide vital personal data—such as insurance records—within the chat interface.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2639,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01995,EU_PSTW_202609_01995,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Provincial Council of Albacete (Diputación de Albacete),General public services n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,Digital Management Platform for Public Administrations Sedipualb@,Provincial Council of Albacete (Diputación de Albacete); Local Government; General public services n.e.c.; Spain,"Delivered as a Software-as-a-Service (SaaS) solution, the platform is built on open technologies and deployed in the cloud, ensuring scalability, flexibility, and cost-efficiency. Today, more than 300 public entities—including municipalities, consortia, and universities across seven autonomous regions—use the system.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"SEDIPUALB@ is a digital platform created by the Provincial Council of Albacete to enable fully digital administrative management for municipalities. Initially launched to serve local governments within the province, it provides a shared, open, and interoperable system that simplifies administrative processes and eliminates barriers such as licensing costs or dependence on political cycles. Delivered as a Software-as-a-Service (SaaS) solution, the platform is built on open technologies and deployed in the cloud, ensuring scalability, flexibility, and cost-efficiency. Albacete became the only province in Spain where all municipalities adopted a unified digital management platform. Since then, SEDIPUALB@ has expanded well beyond its original scope. Today, more than 300 public entities—including municipalities, consortia, and universities across seven autonomous regions—use the system. It supports over a million citizens and thousands of concurrent users daily. Governance is collaborative, with technical, legal, and administrative bodies advising on improvements, while costs are distributed among participants according to usage. This model ensures both sustainability and transparency, turning a local project into a national benchmark for digital public administration.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2640,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01996,EU_PSTW_202609_01996,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,AI Lab at the German Environment Agency (UBA) and Federal Nature Conservation Agency,Protection of biodiversity and landscape.,"During a six-month pilot, the tool flagged over 50,000 protected animals from more than 1,250 CITES-listed species, transforming an unmanageable manual task into a scalable, data-driven process.",AI for Species Protection - Detection of Illegal Online Wildlife Trade,AI Lab at the German Environment Agency (UBA) and Federal Nature Conservation Agency; Central-Government; Protection of biodiversity and landscape.; Germany,"The system combines AI-powered image classification, rule-based text analysis, and tailored web scraping to scan thousands of online listings daily, identifying species even when disguised or misspelled.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Germany’s Federal Agency for Nature Conservation, together with the AI Lab at the German Environment Agency, developed an AI-driven tool to detect illegal online trading of protected species, one of the fastest-growing environmental crimes worldwide. The system combines AI-powered image classification, rule-based text analysis, and tailored web scraping to scan thousands of online listings daily, identifying species even when disguised or misspelled. During a six-month pilot, the tool flagged over 50,000 protected animals from more than 1,250 CITES-listed species, transforming an unmanageable manual task into a scalable, data-driven process. It frees public servants from repetitive screening, allowing them to focus on high-risk cases, improving efficiency, and strengthening enforcement. Built entirely in-house with open-source technologies, the system ensures digital sovereignty, sustainability, and compliance with European interoperability standards. It has already inspired international interest, including from the Czech CITES authority, and provides a transferable model for EU-wide biodiversity protection.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2641,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01997,EU_PSTW_202609_01997,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Xunta de Galicia,"General economic, commercial and labour affair","The administrative problem is that funding opportunities are often dispersed across different portals, calls and institutional sources, making it difficult for autónomos to understand which schemes are relevant to their activity, situation and eligibility profile. The platform consolidates this information into a single intelligent portal and widget, reducing fragmentation and improving the user journey.",Impulsa Autónomo: Intelligent Platform to Personalise Grants and Subsidies for the Self-Employed in Galicia through AI,"Xunta de Galicia; Regional Government; General economic, commercial and labour affair; Spain","Its implementation uses Retrieval-Augmented Generation–based generative AI, connected to official databases, to provide personalised recommendations rather than static lists of subsidies.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Impulsa Autónomo was developed by the Xunta de Galicia to simplify how self-employed professionals identify and access grants, subsidies and support measures. The administrative problem is that funding opportunities are often dispersed across different portals, calls and institutional sources, making it difficult for autónomos to understand which schemes are relevant to their activity, situation and eligibility profile. The platform consolidates this information into a single intelligent portal and widget, reducing fragmentation and improving the user journey. Its implementation uses Retrieval-Augmented Generation–based generative AI, connected to official databases, to provide personalised recommendations rather than static lists of subsidies. The system combines official information retrieval, conversational guidance, personalised publication of subsidies, automatic alerts, BI dashboards, satisfaction monitoring and two-way feedback channels. Early results indicate reductions in search time of more than 60% and over 100 hours of integrated specialised advisory services. The expected impact is faster access to relevant support, lower administrative costs through automated responses, more transparent communication of available aid, and stronger interaction between the administration and self-employed workers. Its design also supports interoperability and regulatory alignment, making it a potentially replicable model for digital public services. The Xunta’s official Oficina Virtual do Autónomo is currently online and provides information on support and grants for self-employment.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S1_KNOWLEDGE_RETRIEVAL_RAG,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2642,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01998,EU_PSTW_202609_01998,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"DG JUST, B.4",General public services n.e.c.,"By simulating human interaction to overcome obstacles like pop-ups, captchas, and hidden content, it ensures access to data that traditional crawlers cannot reach.",AI-Driven Market Surveillance to Safeguard Consumers in the Digital Single Market,"DG JUST, B.4; European Institution/Agency; General public services n.e.c.; European Union",UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Launched in 2022 by the European Commission’s Directorate-General for Justice and Consumers (DG JUST), this project delivers an EU-wide platform that enables systematic surveillance of online marketplaces across all EU and EEA languages. By simulating human interaction to overcome obstacles like pop-ups, captchas, and hidden content, it ensures access to data that traditional crawlers cannot reach. The platform records dangerous products taken offline, fostering transparency, cross-border cooperation, and a shared view of enforcement progress. With the integration of large language models, the system significantly improves the precision of product identification, reducing false positives and enabling inspectors to focus on genuine risks. This shift accelerates enforcement cycles, supports Member States in handling the growing influx of imported parcels, and enhances consumer protection across the Single Market. Aligned with European interoperability principles and EU product-safety legislation, the platform connects seamlessly with the Safety Gate Rapid Alert System. It provides authorities with a cost-effective, centralised, and secure service that strengthens regulatory enforcement while generating valuable insights for future EU policy-making.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2643,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_01999,EU_PSTW_202609_01999,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Lepida SCpA,General services.,UNKNOWN,The Responsive PIAO: An AI-Enabled Model for Proactive and Continuous Skill Development,Lepida SCpA; Local Government; General services.; Italy,"Using an iterative Plan-Do-Check-Act cycle, it optimises training investment by mapping identified needs to existing courses on national platforms, ensuring both efficiency and value for money.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Launched by LepidaSCpA in collaboration with the Italian public administration, the project introduces a strategic, data-driven approach to staff training, aligning professional development directly with the organisational objectives set out in the Piano integrato di attività e organizzazione (PIAO). Using an iterative Plan-Do-Check-Act cycle, it optimises training investment by mapping identified needs to existing courses on national platforms, ensuring both efficiency and value for money. Generative AI models, employed under human supervision, supported the design and analysis of tools such as the “interests and perceived gaps questionnaire”, which improved communication between management and employees and fostered organisational trust. The pilot phase recorded high levels of user satisfaction, validating the methodology’s potential to shift training strategies from intuition to evidence-based planning. Fully aligned with the European Interoperability Framework, the project demonstrates legal, organisational, semantic, and technical interoperability. It enhances transparency by creating a structured channel for employee feedback and by making its standardised training course index publicly available, offering a replicable model for other administrations.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2645,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02000,EU_PSTW_202609_02000,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Athens,Transport,"The GREEN-LOG Last-Mile Digital Control Tower (DCT), developed as part of the EU co-funded GREEN-LOG project, addresses the growing challenges of urban last-mile logistics driven by e-commerce expansion.",GREEN-LOG Last-Mile Digital Control Tower: An AI-Driven Platform for Real-Time Optimisation and Sustainable Urban Logistics,Municipality of Athens; Local Government; Transport; Greece,"Leveraging AI-powered predictive models, dynamic multi-modal routing, and Logistics-as-a-Service (LaaS) Marketplace metrics, the system optimises fleet utilisation, reduces carbon emissions, and minimises urban congestion while improving delivery efficiency and reliability.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The GREEN-LOG Last-Mile Digital Control Tower (DCT), developed as part of the EU co-funded GREEN-LOG project, addresses the growing challenges of urban last-mile logistics driven by e-commerce expansion. The platform provides real-time monitoring of delivery assets, near real-time forecasting of transport demand and traffic conditions, and advanced data-driven analytics to enhance operational coordination among logistics service providers, city authorities, and other stakeholders. Leveraging AI-powered predictive models, dynamic multi-modal routing, and Logistics-as-a-Service (LaaS) Marketplace metrics, the system optimises fleet utilisation, reduces carbon emissions, and minimises urban congestion while improving delivery efficiency and reliability. Demonstrations across European cities, including Athens, Barcelona, and Flemish urban areas, have shown reductions in kilometres driven (-35%), travel time (-26%), and the number of stops (-12%), delivering both environmental and economic benefits. The DCT’s flexible, modular architecture enables deployment across diverse urban contexts, supports cross-modal routing, and integrates policy constraints and incentives set by local authorities. Its design aligns with the European Interoperability Framework, ensuring legal, semantic, organisational, and technical interoperability, GDPR compliance, and secure, transparent data sharing. Stakeholder engagement and continuous co-creation have driven usability and adoption, with user satisfaction reaching 90% in early 2026, demonstrating the platform’s effectiveness in supporting sustainable and efficient urban logistics.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2646,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02001,EU_PSTW_202609_02001,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Region of Central Greece (Perifereia Stereas Elladas), Greece",General public services n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,Decision Support System Supporting Intelligent Real-time Resource Allocation of Response Teams (RART) for wildfire suppression,"Region of Central Greece (Perifereia Stereas Elladas), Greece; Regional Government; General public services n.e.c.; Greece",UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The RART tool, developed under the SILVANUS GreenDeal project by NETCOMPANY, is a Decision Support System designed to assist fire commanders in optimising wildfire response. By dynamically allocating available firefighting resources, including aerial and ground units, based on fire spread projections, population density, and resource availability, the system enhances situational awareness and enables more effective, timely interventions, reducing the impact on citizens, property, and the environment. The tool integrates open-source population data, AI-driven fire forecasting, and a multi-objective optimisation algorithm to guide resource deployment under complex, time-sensitive conditions. Field tests in October 2024 by the Region of Sterea Ellada, Greece, and simulations in France demonstrated high user satisfaction (>90%) and confirmed the system’s effectiveness in supporting decision-making under real-life constraints.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2647,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02002,EU_PSTW_202609_02002,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Bavarian State Office for Health and Food Safety (Bayerisches Landesamt für Gesundheit und Lebensmittelsicherheit – LGL),Social protection n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,AI-Driven Early Warning and Decision Support for Fraud Prevention in Quality-Labelled Food Supply Chains,Bavarian State Office for Health and Food Safety (Bayerisches Landesamt für Gesundheit und Lebensmittelsicherheit – LGL); Regional Government; Social protection n.e.c.; Germany,"as part of the EU-funded ALLIANCE project, is an AI-powered system designed to detect and mitigate food fraud across complex supply chains.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The ALLIANCE EWDSS, developed by Netcompany S.A. as part of the EU-funded ALLIANCE project, is an AI-powered system designed to detect and mitigate food fraud across complex supply chains. Focusing on sectors such as Feta Cheese, Organic Pasta, Organic Honey, and Fava Beans, the solution combines Artificial Intelligence, fuzzy logic, operations research, and multi-criteria decision analysis to provide early risk detection, transparent risk scoring, and actionable decision support for farmers, auditors, and regulators. The system employs domain-specific monitoring—including real-time pH and temperature data for feta cheese, anomaly detection for honey, and machine learning models for fava beans—to proactively identify potential fraud before it enters production. Developed through a co-creation approach, it ensures usability, transparency, and stakeholder engagement, enabling evidence-based decision-making, consistent risk management, and compliance with food safety and certification standards. By integrating human-in-the-loop decision support and standardised data models, ALLIANCE EWDSS fosters collaboration, accountability, and trust across the food supply chain, representing a significant advancement over conventional single-method detection systems.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION|S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2648,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02003,EU_PSTW_202609_02003,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"National Agency of Information Society, under the Prime Minister’s authority","Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.",UNKNOWN,AI-Driven Harmonisation of Albanian Legislation with the EU Aquis,"National Agency of Information Society, under the Prime Minister’s authority; Central-Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Albania","By combining AI-powered translation (ChatGPT-4 architecture) with automated compliance table generation, the system reduces manual effort, improves accuracy, and tracks thousands of EU measures and national acts requiring alignment. Implemented within the existing PIEA (European Integration and Membership Platform), the solution supports Albanian institutions without restructuring, providing real-time access to CELEX documents and ensuring full interoperability across legal, organisational, semantic, and technical levels.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The AI-assisted platform, developed by Microsoft in partnership with the Albanian Government, accelerates the transposition of EU legislation into Albanian law, enhancing both administrative efficiency (G2G) and public service quality (G2C, G2B). By combining AI-powered translation (ChatGPT-4 architecture) with automated compliance table generation, the system reduces manual effort, improves accuracy, and tracks thousands of EU measures and national acts requiring alignment. Implemented within the existing PIEA (European Integration and Membership Platform), the solution supports Albanian institutions without restructuring, providing real-time access to CELEX documents and ensuring full interoperability across legal, organisational, semantic, and technical levels. Phase 2 introduces AI-based legal analysis to detect overlaps and contradictions, helping Albania stay on track to meet its EU integration objectives by 2030.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2649,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02004,EU_PSTW_202609_02004,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Italian National Institute for Insurance against Accidents at Work (INAIL),Sickness and disability.,UNKNOWN,Archimede (Antifrode) - Evolution of Anti-Fraud Systems and Continuous Internal Auditing,Italian National Institute for Insurance against Accidents at Work (INAIL); Central-Government; Sickness and disability.; Italy,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Archimede is an AI-driven solution developed by INAIL to detect and anticipate errors, evasions, and fraud (EEF), supporting effective risk prevention policies and highlighting process anomalies. During testing, the system demonstrated high accuracy: in six central audits, 100% of flagged cases were confirmed as anomalous, with 44% showing non-compliance in case handling. By integrating with INAIL’s auditing systems and the CRIBIS business information database, Archimede enables the identification of mismanagement and process irregularities, forming a core component of ongoing monitoring and auditing strategies. Built as a versatile, open-source on-premise solution, it ensures secure handling of confidential data and is fully adapted to INAIL’s organisational and analytical context, providing a competitive technological advantage.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2650,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02005,EU_PSTW_202609_02005,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""social_nonprofit""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,National Agency for Information Society (NAIS) – in cooperation with the Ministry of Education and Sports.,General public services n.e.c.,"The Smart Management Information Platform (SMIP) was introduced to modernise Albania’s education governance and overcome the inefficiencies of manual, paper-based processes, fragmented communication, and limited digital access.",The Pre-University Management Information System (SMIP/PUMIS),National Agency for Information Society (NAIS) – in cooperation with the Ministry of Education and Sports.; Central-Government; General public services n.e.c.; Albania,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Smart Management Information Platform (SMIP) was introduced to modernise Albania’s education governance and overcome the inefficiencies of manual, paper-based processes, fragmented communication, and limited digital access. As a unified platform, SMIP brings together education, health, and administration in a way not previously available in the country. It currently integrates more than 407,000 students, 35,000 teachers, and 800,000 parents, providing real-time academic updates for families, digital reporting tools for teachers, and centralised dashboards for education authorities. The platform also supports wider public services, such as issuing over 10,000 digital vaccine cards via the national e-Albania portal, and promoting digital literacy, with more than 74,000 students trained in ICT skills through SmartLabs. By interoperating with national systems like e-Albania, the e-Vaccine system, and the NAIS Government Data Centre, SMIP improves efficiency, reduces bureaucracy, and strengthens transparency in education management. Aligned with the country’s Digital Agenda 2022–2026 and designed in line with EIF interoperability principles, SMIP enables evidence-based policy-making, enhances parental engagement, and lays the foundation for future regional cooperation, including cross-border diploma recognition.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2651,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02006,EU_PSTW_202609_02006,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""social_nonprofit"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Italian National Institute for Insurance against Accidents at Work (INAIL),Sickness and disability.,UNKNOWN,Cruscotto infortuni - Cognitive Systems for Road Accidents,Italian National Institute for Insurance against Accidents at Work (INAIL); Central-Government; Sickness and disability.; Italy,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Road Accident Analysis Dashboard, developed by INAIL, strengthens workplace safety policies by providing structured data and AI-driven insights into incidents that significantly contribute to annual injury statistics. Designed for the Central Health Supervisory Authority, the tool enables the identification of trends, risk factors, and intervention areas, supporting more targeted prevention strategies. Based on Oracle Analytics Server, Cloudera, and IBM Watson, the platform integrates institutional records with predictive analytics to improve data accuracy and uncover previously unclassified accidents. It currently covers incidents from 2018 onwards and will expand to a ten-year overview, with updates every six months. Early adoption shows strong user engagement, high satisfaction, and recognised added value, making it a reliable foundation for evidence-based decision-making and future predictive planning.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2652,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02007,EU_PSTW_202609_02007,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Italian National Institute for Insurance against Accidents at Work (INAIL),Social protection n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,MyInail Chatbot - support tool for INAIL users,Italian National Institute for Insurance against Accidents at Work (INAIL); Central-Government; Social protection n.e.c.; Italy,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The MyInail Chatbot provides users with guided, conversational support across INAIL’s wide range of services, from healthcare and financial benefits to prevention and training. Launched in June 2024, it improves accessibility to INAIL’s knowledge base while directing users to the most appropriate support channels when needed. Integrated into both the INAIL web portal and mobile app, the chatbot reduces contact centre calls, ensures secure and privacy-compliant interactions, and is continuously updated through user feedback. Early adoption shows strong satisfaction and no reported service complaints, confirming its role as a reliable first-level assistance and knowledge management tool.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2653,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02008,EU_PSTW_202609_02008,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Italian National Institute for Insurance against Accidents at Work (INAIL),Sickness and disability.,UNKNOWN,AI-powered Linguistic Mediator: A Phygital Solution for Inclusive and Accessible Public Services at INAIL,Italian National Institute for Insurance against Accidents at Work (INAIL); Central-Government; Sickness and disability.; Italy,"To respond to the needs of an increasingly diverse workforce—20% of insured workers in Italy are foreign-born—INAIL has developed the Linguistic Mediator, an AI-powered tool that provides real-time voice and text interpretation at service counters.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"To respond to the needs of an increasingly diverse workforce—20% of insured workers in Italy are foreign-born—INAIL has developed the Linguistic Mediator, an AI-powered tool that provides real-time voice and text interpretation at service counters. Integrated into INAIL’s “phygital” service model, it helps front-office staff, social workers, and doctors communicate effectively with non-Italian-speaking users, improving access to services while streamlining internal processes. Early pilots in territorial offices have shown strong results, with translation accuracy around 92% and clear efficiency gains for staff. Built on Microsoft Azure AI Services, the solution is modular, secure, and interoperable, with future extensions planned for document translation and Italian Sign Language (LIS).",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2654,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02009,EU_PSTW_202609_02009,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Digital Agency of Andalucía,General services.,UNKNOWN,JuntaGPT: the internal GenAI based assistant for civil servants within the biggest regional administration in Europe.,Digital Agency of Andalucía; Regional Government; General services.; Spain,"Developed by the regional digital agency (ADA) and deployed on the Hércules supercomputer at CICA, the system is designed to ensure digital sovereignty, data protection, and that all information remains securely within the Junta’s environment.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"In June 2025, the Andalusian Regional Government launched JuntaGPT, an on-premises generative AI assistant for its internal personnel — over 240,000 civil servants — to help with document drafting, normative queries, summarising, email writing, and other administrative text work. Developed by the regional digital agency (ADA) and deployed on the Hércules supercomputer at CICA, the system is designed to ensure digital sovereignty, data protection, and that all information remains securely within the Junta’s environment. It allows users to upload documents, ask questions naturally, chain conversational queries, and access pre-loaded information by domain without always having to upload files. Early stages of deployment are underway; the tool is already partly active and intended to improve productivity, efficiency, and internal service quality by streamlining the drafting and information-retrieval tasks of public employees.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2655,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02010,EU_PSTW_202609_02010,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Agency for State Technological Reform Agency (ARTE), Portuguese Institute for Sport and Youth (IPDJ) and the Cascais Municipality",General public services n.e.c.,"Portugal launched the “On Record” platform to address the democratic challenge of politically disengaged youth, despite their digital savviness.","On Record, a tool to help engage youth and other stakeholders in current decision-making processes.","Agency for State Technological Reform Agency (ARTE), Portuguese Institute for Sport and Youth (IPDJ) and the Cascais Municipality; Central-Government; General public services n.e.c.; Portugal","It combines structured dialogue environments, gamified learning tools, AI-powered transcription and summarisation, and user tracking to ensure continuity across participation cycles.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Portugal launched the “On Record” platform to address the democratic challenge of politically disengaged youth, despite their digital savviness. Developed by LabX/ARTE with support from the Portuguese Institute for Sport and Youth (IPDJ) and the Cascais Municipality, the project is part of the EU-funded initiative Improving Civic Participation through Emerging Technologies. On Record is a modular, AI-enabled deliberation and engagement platform embedded in Participa.gov, designed specifically to amplify youth voices (ages 14–30) in policymaking. It combines structured dialogue environments, gamified learning tools, AI-powered transcription and summarisation, and user tracking to ensure continuity across participation cycles. Pilots (2025) have shown its capacity to lower barriers to participation, foster inclusive debate, and enhance transparency by linking proposals to policy follow-up. With interoperability built into its architecture, GDPR compliance, and integration with national digital identity tools, the solution is both scalable within Portugal and transferable across Europe. By co-designing with youth and institutions, Portugal is creating a sustainable digital public good that strengthens trust, accountability, and democratic engagement, while setting the foundations for broader adoption beyond youth participation.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2656,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02011,EU_PSTW_202609_02011,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,German Federal Ministry for Economic Cooperation and Development (BMZ),Foreign economic aid.,UNKNOWN,KIEZ - an Agentic AI Assistant for Development Cooperation,German Federal Ministry for Economic Cooperation and Development (BMZ); Central-Government; Foreign economic aid.; Germany,KIEZ is an AI-powered assistant developed by the Datalab of the German Federal Ministry for Economic Cooperation and Development (BMZ) to support public servants in processing large volumes of text and improving decision-making in the domain of economic cooperation.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"KIEZ is an AI-powered assistant developed by the Datalab of the German Federal Ministry for Economic Cooperation and Development (BMZ) to support public servants in processing large volumes of text and improving decision-making in the domain of economic cooperation. Built as a trustworthy chat interface, KIEZ integrates agentic tools, domain-specific prompt templates, document uploads, controlled internet search, and internal databases, while balancing usability with strict privacy, security, and vendor-independence measures. The platform enables tasks such as document summarisation, translation, and refinement, and operates on a cloud-native, GDPR-compliant infrastructure that supports multiple AI models, including retrieval-augmented generation (RAG). KIEZ has already demonstrated strong impact, with 44% of surveyed staff rating it “important” or “very important” to their work and 51% reporting improvements in efficiency and communication. With over 1.6 million tokens processed daily, KIEZ exemplifies a scalable, secure, and interoperable AI solution for modernising public administration workflows, funded by NextGenerationEU.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S1_KNOWLEDGE_RETRIEVAL_RAG,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2657,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02012,EU_PSTW_202609_02012,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Digital Agency of Andalucía,Cultural services.,UNKNOWN,Alhambra and Generalife Digital Twin Living Lab: Technology to Preserve Beauty.,Digital Agency of Andalucía; Regional Government; Cultural services.; Spain,"The first use cases are expected to launch in the first quarter of 2025, providing actionable insights for both site management and visitors.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Alhambra Living Lab is an initiative led by the Andalusian Digital Agency (ADA) to develop a comprehensive digital twin of the Alhambra and Generalife palaces in Granada. Announced by Antonio Sanz, Regional Minister for the Presidency, Interior, Social Dialogue, and Administrative Simplification, and supported by Patricia del Pozo, Regional Minister for Culture and Sport, and Marifrán Carazo, Mayor of Granada, the project seeks to transform how visitors experience the Nasrid complex while improving its conservation, accessibility, and sustainability. Funded with over €3.2 million (€3,226,546) through the Spain Living Lab initiative within the Territorial Networks of Technological Specialisation (RETECH) programme, the Alhambra Living Lab will run until June 2026. The project integrates advanced technologies, including 3D modeling, IoT sensors, AI, mixed reality, and blockchain, to create a virtual replica of the monuments. This digital twin will allow the testing of innovative solutions to manage visitor flows, monitor environmental and structural conditions, enhance cultural engagement, and support sustainable heritage preservation. The first use cases are expected to launch in the first quarter of 2025, providing actionable insights for both site management and visitors. By combining state-of-the-art digital technologies with real-world heritage conservation, the Alhambra Living Lab represents one of Europe’s most forward-thinking cultural digital twin projects, offering a model for balancing tourism, sustainability, and citizen engagement.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2658,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02013,EU_PSTW_202609_02013,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Ministry for Digital Transformation and the Civil Service,General public services n.e.c.,"ParticipApp lowers barriers to participation, particularly for marginalised groups or those with limited digital literacy, and ensures transparency by providing visible summaries of contributions and clear feedback on how ideas are integrated into decision-making.","ParticipApp - more inclusive, transparent, and effective citizen participation in government decision-making",Ministry for Digital Transformation and the Civil Service; Central-Government; General public services n.e.c.; Spain,"Using AI-powered tools, ParticipApp clusters and summarizes contributions thematically, helping participants and policymakers make sense of large volumes of input.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"ParticipApp is a national digital platform designed to make citizen participation in government decision-making more inclusive, transparent, and effective. The platform allows contributions in all co-official languages of Spain, and supports multiple input types, including text, voice, and images of handwritten notes. Using AI-powered tools, ParticipApp clusters and summarizes contributions thematically, helping participants and policymakers make sense of large volumes of input. The platform also provides AI-assisted drafting, enabling users to refine proposals while retaining full control over their content. Highly supported ideas can then be routed to a voting phase and formally considered in policy processes. For administrators, ParticipApp offers comprehensive dashboards to monitor participation, track emerging trends, moderate contributions, and measure engagement and impact. Built on open-source frameworks like Decidim and Consul and hosted on GDPR-compliant EU infrastructure, the platform is modular, scalable, and interoperable with existing digital public services. ParticipApp lowers barriers to participation, particularly for marginalised groups or those with limited digital literacy, and ensures transparency by providing visible summaries of contributions and clear feedback on how ideas are integrated into decision-making. By combining AI with human oversight, the platform strengthens public trust, enhances the quality and legitimacy of consultations, and establishes a sustainable digital public infrastructure for participatory governance, with potential for adoption across other European countries.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2659,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02014,EU_PSTW_202609_02014,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Public Institution CPO LT (Viešoji įstaiga CPO LT),Economic affairs n.e.c,UNKNOWN,"Integration of an Innovative Product – AI Technology – into Public Procurement Object Price Analysis and Forecasting, and Centralized Public Procurement Savings Identification Procedures",Public Institution CPO LT (Viešoji įstaiga CPO LT); Central-Government; Economic affairs n.e.c; Lithuania,"Led by CPO LT, the national Central Purchasing Body, this project uses a generative AI solution — including large language models (LLMs) and advanced analytics — to analyse procurement prices, forecast costs, and calculate actual savings.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Republic of Lithuania is pioneering the integration of artificial intelligence (AI) into centralised public procurement to improve efficiency, transparency, and cost-effectiveness. Led by CPO LT, the national Central Purchasing Body, this project uses a generative AI solution — including large language models (LLMs) and advanced analytics — to analyse procurement prices, forecast costs, and calculate actual savings. In 2024, CPO LT managed €1.245 billion in procurements, representing around 15% of Lithuania’s total public procurement.The AI tool enables real-time market price analysis, identifies overpriced offers, predicts procurement values with high accuracy, and optimises supplier selection. These capabilities result in lower prices, reduced resource requirements for contracting authorities, increased supplier competition, and greater trust in procurement processes. The project complies with EU interoperability and procurement regulations, aligns with the European AI Act, and integrates open data sources such as CPO LT’s catalogue and kainos24.lt. Developed by NFQ Technologies, it is the first AI-based public procurement solution in Lithuania and represents a scalable model for other EU Member States. The pilot version is currently undergoing testing, with full deployment expected in December 2025, marking a significant step towards more transparent and efficient public procurement in the country.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2660,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02015,EU_PSTW_202609_02015,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,ARTE - State Technological Reform Agency,General public services n.e.c.,"Portugal is making it easier for citizens to live, study, work, or retire in other EU countries through the Once-Only Technical System (OOTS), an EU-wide framework that reduces the administrative burden of cross-border procedures.","Once-Only Common Services: Portugal uses cloud native, cloud agnostic building blocks to make accessing the Single Market simple and easier for EU citizens and businesses",ARTE - State Technological Reform Agency; Central-Government; General public services n.e.c.; Portugal,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Portugal is making it easier for citizens to live, study, work, or retire in other EU countries through the Once-Only Technical System (OOTS), an EU-wide framework that reduces the administrative burden of cross-border procedures. With OOTS, citizens and businesses only need to submit required documents (“evidences”) once, as these can be securely exchanged between public authorities across Member States, including permits, certificates, diplomas, or licenses. In December 2023, Portugal went live with the OOTS Common Services, which enable interoperability between procedure portals across the EU, covering around 80,000 national competent authorities. The system reuses existing technical components like eID and eDelivery, and is developed as open-source, ensuring scalable, secure, and GDPR-compliant operations. OOTS simplifies life events such as registering a vehicle abroad, studying, or running a business, while improving administrative efficiency, trust in shared data, and transparency. Early studies suggest that SMEs and self-employed workers could save up to 50% of costs and time when completing cross-border procedures. Students applying to study abroad can also save days or weeks in administrative processes. Portugal is now progressing to level 5 on the OOTS Acceleratormeter, aiming for the first live transactions in collaboration with the Directorate-General for Higher Education. Through OOTS, Portugal contributes to a seamless EU digital single market, enabling faster, more transparent, and citizen-centric public services across Europe.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2661,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02016,EU_PSTW_202609_02016,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Agency for Technological Modernization of Galicia (AMTEGA) – Xunta de Galicia,Fire-protection services,"The system has already proven especially valuable in depopulated rural areas where traditional surveillance is limited, outperforming commercial alternatives in both accuracy and speed.",(AILUME) - Artificial Intelligence-Based Application for the Early Detection of Forest Fire Outbreaks,Agency for Technological Modernization of Galicia (AMTEGA) – Xunta de Galicia; Regional Government; Fire-protection services; Spain,"Using advanced deep learning models, optimised YOLO detectors, and vision-language reasoning, the system identifies smoke, filters false alarms, and provides explainable alerts to operators at the Central Coordination Center for Forest Fire Defense.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"AILUME is an artificial intelligence system developed to strengthen Galicia’s response to one of its greatest environmental threats: forest fires. The region’s vast forest coverage, rural depopulation, and increasing climate change impacts make early detection essential. AILUME addresses this by continuously analysing video streams from the Xunta de Galicia’s forest surveillance camera network, strategically placed across the territory. Using advanced deep learning models, optimised YOLO detectors, and vision-language reasoning, the system identifies smoke, filters false alarms, and provides explainable alerts to operators at the Central Coordination Center for Forest Fire Defense. The platform combines state-of-the-art AI with robust software architecture: a Django-based modular backend, Apache Kafka for distributed real-time processing, PyAV/OpenCV for video capture, and PostgreSQL for structured data management. It includes a human-in-the-loop feedback system, ensuring that operator validation contributes to continuous learning and higher detection accuracy. Designed for resilience and scalability, the solution can handle dozens of concurrent video streams with sub-second data latency, operating 24/7 under real production conditions. Pilot testing launched in August 2025 has confirmed its effectiveness: 86.1% detection success in real-world cases, precision of 98.2%, recall of 99.9%, and detection lead times averaging 18 minutes earlier than official notification channels. The system has already proven especially valuable in depopulated rural areas where traditional surveillance is limited, outperforming commercial alternatives in both accuracy and speed.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2662,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02017,EU_PSTW_202609_02017,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Polytechnic University of Milan and the Einaudi Institute for Economics and Finance,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.","For the Chamber of Deputies, this creates an operational challenge in making legislative production easier to explore, analyse and verify, both for institutional users and for those supporting parliamentary work.",NORMA - virtual assistant for the analysis of legislative production,"Polytechnic University of Milan and the Einaudi Institute for Economics and Finance; Academic-Research; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Italy",The solution is implemented as a natural-language virtual assistant developed from the Legislab proposal by the Polytechnic University of Milan and the Einaudi Institute for Economics and Finance.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Accessing and interpreting parliamentary data can be complex, particularly when users need to navigate large volumes of legislative information spread across amendments, draft laws, procedural stages and official reports. For the Chamber of Deputies, this creates an operational challenge in making legislative production easier to explore, analyse and verify, both for institutional users and for those supporting parliamentary work. NORMA was developed to simplify that access by enabling more intuitive consultation of the Legislative Observatory. The stakeholders include parliamentary staff, researchers and institutional users who need reliable information on the lawmaking process, with the objective of improving the accessibility, clarity and analytical usability of legislative data. The solution is implemented as a natural-language virtual assistant developed from the Legislab proposal by the Polytechnic University of Milan and the Einaudi Institute for Economics and Finance. At a conceptual level, the system queries certified parliamentary archives and allows users to retrieve information on amendments, legislative texts and the parliamentary progress of bills through conversational interaction. It can also present outputs in charts and tables, making complex legislative information easier to interpret. Because it relies on official archival sources, results can be verified against certified documentation. The expected impact is faster access to legislative information, improved analytical capability and stronger support for the Chamber’s institutional work, while also creating value for statistical and research activities related to legislative production.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2663,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02018,EU_PSTW_202609_02018,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Alma Human AI,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.","For deputies and parliamentary groups, this can make amendment preparation slow, error-prone and heavily dependent on specialist drafting expertise. MSE was designed to address this operational difficulty by supporting the preparation of formally correct amendments directly within the parliamentary drafting process.",MSE - assisted text system for parliamentary amendments,"Alma Human AI; Academic-Research; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Italy","The solution is implemented as an assisted drafting system developed by the Alma Human AI inter-university consortium, involving the Universities of Bologna, LUISS, the CNR, and the Universities of Verona and Turin.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Drafting parliamentary amendments is a technically demanding task because proposals must not only reflect the intended policy change, but also comply precisely with strict formal and syntactic rules. For deputies and parliamentary groups, this can make amendment preparation slow, error-prone and heavily dependent on specialist drafting expertise. MSE was designed to address this operational difficulty by supporting the preparation of formally correct amendments directly within the parliamentary drafting process. The stakeholders include deputies, parliamentary commissions and political groups, with the objective of reducing drafting friction, improving formal accuracy and making legislative work more efficient and accessible. The solution is implemented as an assisted drafting system developed by the Alma Human AI inter-university consortium, involving the Universities of Bologna, LUISS, the CNR, and the Universities of Verona and Turin. At a conceptual level, MSE provides a text-editor interface that works directly on draft bills and automatically converts modifications into correctly structured amendment proposals in real time. It also includes an internal chatbot that retrieves relevant supporting documentation, such as dossiers, hearing transcripts and deposited memoranda, and links these to official sources. A further module under development will suggest more refined technical phrasing through natural-language interaction. Currently used on a trial basis by parliamentary groups, MSE is expected to be officially rolled out by the end of 2025, with benefits in drafting speed, reliability and overall legislative workflow efficiency.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2664,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02019,EU_PSTW_202609_02019,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Roma Tre University and the University of Florence,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.","Citizens often find it difficult to obtain clear, trustworthy and easily searchable information about the work of Members of Parliament, even though much of that information is already published in official records.",DEPUCHAT - chatbot to facilitate citizens' access to MPs' activities,"Roma Tre University and the University of Florence; Academic-Research; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Italy","The solution is implemented as an experimental generative chatbot developed from a proposal by Roma Tre University and the University of Florence in response to a call from the Chamber of Deputies. At a conceptual level, it uses official parliamentary data, including roles, legislative proposals, speeches, committee work and oversight acts, to support topic-based, person-based and issue-based queries.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Citizens often find it difficult to obtain clear, trustworthy and easily searchable information about the work of Members of Parliament, even though much of that information is already published in official records. Parliamentary activity is spread across proposals, speeches, committee work, oversight acts and institutional roles, which can make it hard for non-specialists to understand what representatives are doing or to explore parliamentary action on a specific topic. DEPUCHAT was created to reduce that barrier and improve public access to parliamentary information. The stakeholders include citizens, parliamentary institutions and civic users interested in transparency and democratic participation, with the objective of providing fast, certified and intelligible access to MPs’ official activities while maintaining strict limits around the scope of the information provided. The solution is implemented as an experimental generative chatbot developed from a proposal by Roma Tre University and the University of Florence in response to a call from the Chamber of Deputies. At a conceptual level, it uses official parliamentary data, including roles, legislative proposals, speeches, committee work and oversight acts, to support topic-based, person-based and issue-based queries. The system is explicitly confined to parliamentary activity and excludes personal questions. Still in testing, it will undergo further data integration, stress testing and security checks before public release. The expected impact is improved transparency, easier public understanding of parliamentary work and stronger civic engagement through more direct and reliable access to institutional information.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2665,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02020,EU_PSTW_202609_02020,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Informatica Alto Adige SpA,R&D general public services.,UNKNOWN,Lab.ai: the heart of artificial intelligence in public administration,Informatica Alto Adige SpA; Local Government; R&D general public services.; Italy,"Based at NOI Techpark in Bolzano, the initiative addresses the need for a central coordination point capable of linking research, development and skills-building across both public-sector use cases and local economic actors. The solution is implemented as a coordinated innovation hub, supported by partnerships with Microsoft and Eurac Research and reinforced by a dedicated competence centre for strategic oversight, compliance and capacity-building.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Public administrations and local businesses increasingly need structured support to explore advanced digital solutions responsibly, yet innovation efforts are often dispersed across separate projects without shared coordination, training or ethical oversight. Lab.ai was launched by Informatica Alto Adige under a broader strategy for introducing advanced digital capabilities into public administration in South Tyrol. Based at NOI Techpark in Bolzano, the initiative addresses the need for a central coordination point capable of linking research, development and skills-building across both public-sector use cases and local economic actors. The stakeholders include public administrations, local businesses, research partners and innovation leaders, with the objective of accelerating practical adoption, strengthening organisational capability and ensuring that innovation proceeds under clear strategic and ethical guidance. The solution is implemented as a coordinated innovation hub, supported by partnerships with Microsoft and Eurac Research and reinforced by a dedicated competence centre for strategic oversight, compliance and capacity-building. At a conceptual level, Lab.ai connects experimentation, training and application development in a shared framework that allows public-sector and local-business use cases to benefit from common expertise and infrastructure rather than operating in isolation. The expected impact is stronger innovation coordination, improved institutional readiness and better-quality deployment of advanced digital solutions. More broadly, Lab.ai creates value by building a sustainable ecosystem for public-sector modernisation, strengthening skills and governance, and making innovation more structured, scalable and aligned with public interest.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2666,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02021,EU_PSTW_202609_02021,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Informatica Alto Adige SpA,General public services n.e.c.,"Public administrations often struggle to plan and deliver services efficiently when information about citizens, territories and service environments is fragmented across multiple systems and difficult to interpret as a whole. This limits transparency, slows decision-making and makes it harder to design services that are inclusive, responsive and grounded in real conditions.",Digital Twin: a digital replica for efficient management,Informatica Alto Adige SpA; Local Government; General public services n.e.c.; Italy,"The solution is implemented through digital-replica models that mirror relevant characteristics and behaviours of real systems. Depending on the use case, this may involve modelling individual service users, territorial systems or public-service environments so that administrators can analyse conditions, detect changes and assess options more effectively.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Public administrations often struggle to plan and deliver services efficiently when information about citizens, territories and service environments is fragmented across multiple systems and difficult to interpret as a whole. This limits transparency, slows decision-making and makes it harder to design services that are inclusive, responsive and grounded in real conditions. The Digital Twin initiative led by SIAG addresses this challenge by creating virtual models of real entities, such as users or geographic areas, to improve monitoring, planning and service management. The stakeholders include public administrators, service planners and decision-makers responsible for territorial and citizen-facing services, with the objective of enabling more data-driven, transparent and efficient public administration. The solution is implemented through digital-replica models that mirror relevant characteristics and behaviours of real systems. At a conceptual level, these twins integrate data from operational environments and translate them into virtual representations that can be used for simulation, monitoring and decision support. Depending on the use case, this may involve modelling individual service users, territorial systems or public-service environments so that administrators can analyse conditions, detect changes and assess options more effectively. The expected impact is improved service efficiency, better situational awareness and more informed public decision-making. More broadly, the initiative creates value by supporting inclusive and responsive governance, strengthening the practical use of data in administration and providing a scalable framework for smarter management of both people-centred and place-based public services.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2667,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02022,EU_PSTW_202609_02022,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Ajuntament de Barcelona,Transport,"Bus reliability in dense urban corridors is frequently undermined by vehicles that stop or park illegally in reserved bus lanes or at bus stops, disrupting service regularity and slowing public transport operations. In Barcelona, this creates an operational challenge for Transports Metropolitans de Barcelona, which must keep buses moving efficiently while also understanding how frequently such obstructions occur and where they have the greatest impact.",Automated bus lane and bus stop enforcement pilot with Hayden AI,Ajuntament de Barcelona; Local Government; Transport; Spain,"The solution is implemented on four buses running on the H12 and D20 routes through cameras mounted inside the front of the vehicles and supplied by Hayden AI. At a conceptual level, the system uses computer-vision techniques to identify static violations, such as unauthorised stopping or parking in restricted areas, while avoiding the recording of licence plates or people during the trial phase.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Bus reliability in dense urban corridors is frequently undermined by vehicles that stop or park illegally in reserved bus lanes or at bus stops, disrupting service regularity and slowing public transport operations. In Barcelona, this creates an operational challenge for Transports Metropolitans de Barcelona, which must keep buses moving efficiently while also understanding how frequently such obstructions occur and where they have the greatest impact. The six-month pilot was therefore launched to improve evidence on bus-lane and bus-stop misuse and to assess whether a larger-scale monitoring approach could support future operational improvements. The stakeholders include TMB, bus operators, city mobility authorities and public transport users, with the objective of enhancing bus circulation, reducing interference with service and gathering robust information for possible future enforcement action. The solution is implemented on four buses running on the H12 and D20 routes through cameras mounted inside the front of the vehicles and supplied by Hayden AI. At a conceptual level, the system uses computer-vision techniques to identify static violations, such as unauthorised stopping or parking in restricted areas, while avoiding the recording of licence plates or people during the trial phase. No fines are issued; instead, the system collects statistical and contextual data for evaluation. The expected impact is improved understanding of obstruction patterns, better-informed decisions on rollout and a scalable model for supporting faster, more reliable bus services across the network.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2668,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02023,EU_PSTW_202609_02023,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Ajuntament de Barcelona,General public services n.e.c.,"Barcelona City Council created Decidim.Barcelona to address a core governance challenge: how to involve citizens more meaningfully, transparently and inclusively in municipal decision-making. Traditional participation processes can exclude parts of the population, limit visibility of how contributions are used and make it difficult for residents to engage consistently across different policy issues.",Decidim - digital and democratic platform for citizen participation,Ajuntament de Barcelona; Local Government; General public services n.e.c.; Spain,"The solution is implemented through an open-source digital participation platform that supports structured consultations, deliberation spaces, proposal management and voting mechanisms.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Barcelona City Council created Decidim.Barcelona to address a core governance challenge: how to involve citizens more meaningfully, transparently and inclusively in municipal decision-making. Traditional participation processes can exclude parts of the population, limit visibility of how contributions are used and make it difficult for residents to engage consistently across different policy issues. The platform was therefore designed to give citizens a more direct role in shaping the city’s development by enabling debate, proposal submission and voting on public initiatives. The stakeholders include residents, municipal departments, civic organisations and elected representatives, with the objective of strengthening democratic engagement, improving transparency and making participation more accessible through a combination of online and in-person channels. The solution is implemented through an open-source digital participation platform that supports structured consultations, deliberation spaces, proposal management and voting mechanisms. At a conceptual level, the system integrates digital workflows with physical participation methods, allowing contributions gathered through different channels to be managed within a common and transparent framework. Because the software is open source, it can be continuously improved, adapted and reused according to changing civic and institutional needs. The impact includes broader citizen participation, greater visibility of decision-making processes and improved trust in municipal governance. More broadly, Decidim creates public value by supporting scalable, transparent and inclusive democratic participation while giving the city a flexible platform for long-term civic engagement.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2669,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02024,EU_PSTW_202609_02024,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Rome,Fuel and energy,"The Italian PlatOne pilot, led by Areti in the Rome metropolitan area, addressed this challenge by exploring how a local flexibility market could help manage electricity flows more intelligently.",PlatOne - PLATform for Operation of distribution Networks (Italian Pilot),Municipality of Rome; Local Government; Fuel and energy; Italy,"The stakeholders included the distribution-system operator, households, commercial users, technology providers and energy-service actors, with the objective of supporting reliable electricity supply while enabling greater use of distributed energy resources and more active citizen participation in energy management. The solution was implemented through a pilot that connected advanced grid automation and monitoring with flexible assets such as cogenerators, residential renewable systems with storage, smart office energy management and electric-vehicle charging infrastructure.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"As renewable generation, storage technologies and electrified demand become more common, distribution networks need new ways to balance local supply and demand without compromising reliability. The Italian PlatOne pilot, led by Areti in the Rome metropolitan area, addressed this challenge by exploring how a local flexibility market could help manage electricity flows more intelligently. The stakeholders included the distribution-system operator, households, commercial users, technology providers and energy-service actors, with the objective of supporting reliable electricity supply while enabling greater use of distributed energy resources and more active citizen participation in energy management. The solution was implemented through a pilot that connected advanced grid automation and monitoring with flexible assets such as cogenerators, residential renewable systems with storage, smart office energy management and electric-vehicle charging infrastructure. At a conceptual level, the platform coordinated these distributed resources through market-oriented and technical mechanisms that allowed flexibility to be identified, exchanged and used in support of network operations. This created a practical environment in which local balancing services could be tested under real operating conditions. The expected impact was more efficient management of electricity demand and supply, improved integration of renewables and stronger operational visibility for the grid operator. More broadly, the pilot created value by demonstrating a pathway towards smarter, more flexible distribution networks that can accommodate electrification and decentralised generation while keeping citizens and local users actively involved.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2670,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02025,EU_PSTW_202609_02025,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,RWTH Achen University - Institute for Automation of Complex Power Systems,Fuel and energy,UNKNOWN,PlatOne - PLATform for Operation of distribution Networks (Germany Pilot),RWTH Achen University - Institute for Automation of Complex Power Systems; Academic-Research; Fuel and energy; Germany,"Before local flexibility markets can be deployed widely, core technical components must be validated in a secure and controlled setting to ensure that distributed energy resources, measurement systems and market platforms can interact reliably. The solution was implemented by connecting key elements of the PlatOne architecture, including the Blockchain Access Layer and the DSO Technical Platform, with a real-time power-system simulator, a prototype Synchronized Measurement Unit and development work on a low-cost phasor measurement unit.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Before local flexibility markets can be deployed widely, core technical components must be validated in a secure and controlled setting to ensure that distributed energy resources, measurement systems and market platforms can interact reliably. The German PlatOne reference implementation addressed this need by creating a laboratory-based environment in which the project’s open framework could be tested before real-world deployment. The stakeholders included RWTH Aachen University, Engineering Ingegneria Informatica, distribution-system operators and technology developers, with the objective of building a scalable and secure framework capable of supporting local flexibility markets and improving the integration of renewable energy into distribution grids. The solution was implemented by connecting key elements of the PlatOne architecture, including the Blockchain Access Layer and the DSO Technical Platform, with a real-time power-system simulator, a prototype Synchronized Measurement Unit and development work on a low-cost phasor measurement unit. At a conceptual level, this environment enabled secure data exchange, interoperability testing and validation of flexibility-service tools under realistic electrical conditions without the risks of direct field deployment. It also helped raise the maturity of enabling technologies before use in European pilots. The expected impact was stronger technical readiness, more reliable coordination of distributed energy resources and better assurance that the framework could function securely at scale. More broadly, the reference implementation created value by reducing deployment risk and strengthening the foundations for flexible, efficient and sustainable electricity distribution.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2671,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02026,EU_PSTW_202609_02026,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Barnsley Council,General public services n.e.c.,"Barnsley Council introduced Microsoft Copilot to tackle a familiar public-sector challenge: high volumes of administrative work that consume staff time and reduce capacity for more meaningful, citizen-facing tasks.",Pioneering Use of Microsoft Copilot - Barnsley Council,Barnsley Council; Local Government; General public services n.e.c.; United Kingdom,"The solution is implemented through Microsoft Copilot embedded into staff workflows, supported by training, stakeholder engagement and a governance model that includes an ethics board and specific protocols for higher-risk areas such as children’s social care. At a conceptual level, the system uses generative-assistance capabilities within office productivity environments to help staff draft, summarise and organise information more efficiently while maintaining organisational oversight of where and how the tool is used.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Barnsley Council introduced Microsoft Copilot to tackle a familiar public-sector challenge: high volumes of administrative work that consume staff time and reduce capacity for more meaningful, citizen-facing tasks. The council began with 300 licences in late 2023, quickly expanded to 2,000, and by January 2024 had rolled the tool out across the organisation. The initiative involved a wide range of internal stakeholders, including teams in finance, business support and social care, with the objective of improving staff efficiency, automating routine work and supporting wider digital transformation without compromising governance in sensitive public-service settings. The solution is implemented through Microsoft Copilot embedded into staff workflows, supported by training, stakeholder engagement and a governance model that includes an ethics board and specific protocols for higher-risk areas such as children’s social care. At a conceptual level, the system uses generative-assistance capabilities within office productivity environments to help staff draft, summarise and organise information more efficiently while maintaining organisational oversight of where and how the tool is used. Early adoption has already improved productivity in several service areas and reduced time spent on repetitive administration. The impact includes better use of staff capacity, stronger confidence in digital working practices and improved organisational readiness for responsible adoption of emerging tools. More broadly, the programme creates value through efficiency gains, more purposeful work and faster public-service modernisation.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F06_HIGH_VOLUME_SCALABILITY_BOTTLENECK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2672,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02027,EU_PSTW_202609_02027,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Birmingham City,General public services n.e.c.,"The impact includes faster compliance, reduced manual sorting, lower fine risk, improved staff efficiency and better citizen response times.",Foundry - Birmingham City,Birmingham City; Local Government; General public services n.e.c.; United Kingdom,"The solution is implemented as an internal transformation engine that co-develops and deploys digital and automation tools across priority back-office functions. At a conceptual level, the Foundry combines workflow redesign, user-centred development and a portfolio of technologies, including Microsoft Copilot for administrative automation, UiPath for automated redaction, SharePoint-based translation for simple documents in 30 languages, and an AI-powered communications-mining solution that analyses emails and routes thousands of incoming messages to the right teams.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Birmingham City Council launched the Foundry in 2023 to respond to a combination of budget pressure, rising service demand and the need to build stronger internal digital capability while reducing reliance on external consultants. Rather than pursuing isolated technology projects, the council created a collaborative model in which multidisciplinary teams work with frontline staff to identify practical interventions that improve efficiency, reduce cost and enhance the experience of work. The stakeholders include service teams, operational staff, digital specialists and council leadership, with the objective of embedding a culture of data-informed improvement under the principle of “think big, start small, learn and scale quickly”. The solution is implemented as an internal transformation engine that co-develops and deploys digital and automation tools across priority back-office functions. At a conceptual level, the Foundry combines workflow redesign, user-centred development and a portfolio of technologies, including Microsoft Copilot for administrative automation, UiPath for automated redaction, SharePoint-based translation for simple documents in 30 languages, and an AI-powered communications-mining solution that analyses emails and routes thousands of incoming messages to the right teams. These solutions are integrated into day-to-day operations rather than treated as stand-alone pilots. The impact includes faster compliance, reduced manual sorting, lower fine risk, improved staff efficiency and better citizen response times. More broadly, the Foundry creates business value by building internal capability, cutting avoidable cost and fostering a digitally confident workforce.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2673,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02028,EU_PSTW_202609_02028,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Buckinghamshire Council,General public services n.e.c.,"The council’s operational challenge was to improve productivity and work quality while also reducing risk, identifying savings and supporting staff wellbeing in a context of growing service pressure.",Buckinghamshire Council: Early adoption of Microsoft Copilot,Buckinghamshire Council; Local Government; General public services n.e.c.; United Kingdom,"The rollout therefore aimed not only to test a new productivity tool, but also to uncover practical use cases that would align with strategic priorities across the organisation. The solution is implemented through Copilot embedded in staff workflows, supported by a structured engagement model that included a “Dragon’s Den”-style event where teams pitched high-value use cases for the technology.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Buckinghamshire Council became one of the first UK local authorities to adopt Microsoft Copilot in October 2023, granting early access to 300 staff across digital services, business support, customer service and other departments. The council’s operational challenge was to improve productivity and work quality while also reducing risk, identifying savings and supporting staff wellbeing in a context of growing service pressure. The rollout therefore aimed not only to test a new productivity tool, but also to uncover practical use cases that would align with strategic priorities across the organisation. The stakeholders included staff from multiple service areas, senior leaders and governance teams, with the objective of ensuring that adoption was purposeful, collaborative and well controlled. The solution is implemented through Copilot embedded in staff workflows, supported by a structured engagement model that included a “Dragon’s Den”-style event where teams pitched high-value use cases for the technology. At a conceptual level, this was combined with a governance board meeting fortnightly to oversee implementation, share lessons and address emerging issues. This governance-and-engagement structure allowed the council to pair experimentation with oversight and cross-departmental learning. The impact includes improved staff productivity, stronger collaboration between teams and earlier identification of valuable operational uses. More broadly, the initiative creates value by reducing implementation risk, supporting digital confidence and demonstrating how early adoption can be aligned with strategic public-sector outcomes.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2674,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02029,EU_PSTW_202609_02029,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Cambridge City Council,General public services n.e.c.,"Greater Cambridge Shared Planning, a partnership between South Cambridgeshire District Council and Cambridge City Council, faced a substantial administrative burden in processing around 9,500 public comments for each Local Plan consultation. Summarising this feedback manually took approximately 18.5 hours of officer time, limiting the capacity available for deeper analysis, stakeholder engagement and planning judgement.",Greater Cambridge Shared Planning: AI-Driven Summarisation for Local Plan Consultations,Cambridge City Council; Local Government; General public services n.e.c.; United Kingdom,"The use case was therefore developed to reduce the time and effort required to process consultation responses while preserving the ability of officers to focus on higher-value planning work. The solution is implemented through a summarisation tool developed with support from the Ministry of Housing, Communities and Local Government’s PropTech Innovation Fund.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Greater Cambridge Shared Planning, a partnership between South Cambridgeshire District Council and Cambridge City Council, faced a substantial administrative burden in processing around 9,500 public comments for each Local Plan consultation. Summarising this feedback manually took approximately 18.5 hours of officer time, limiting the capacity available for deeper analysis, stakeholder engagement and planning judgement. The use case was therefore developed to reduce the time and effort required to process consultation responses while preserving the ability of officers to focus on higher-value planning work. The stakeholders include planning officers, partner councils and members of the public who contribute to consultations, with the objective of making the consultation process more efficient and improving the quality of how feedback is handled. The solution is implemented through a summarisation tool developed with support from the Ministry of Housing, Communities and Local Government’s PropTech Innovation Fund. At a conceptual level, the system processes large volumes of consultation text and produces structured summaries in under an hour, compared with the previous 18.5 hours of officer effort. This allows response data to be organised and reviewed much faster within the planning workflow. The impact includes a major reduction in administrative time, quicker turnaround in consultation processing and better use of professional planning capacity. More broadly, the tool creates value through efficiency gains, improved responsiveness and a more scalable consultation process for complex planning exercises.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2675,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02030,EU_PSTW_202609_02030,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Halton Borough Council,Family and children.,"Families seeking support often need timely, clear and accessible information, yet service pathways can be difficult to navigate, especially outside standard working hours. Halton Borough Council addressed this challenge by creating a “Digital Front Door” for Family Hub Services so that parents and carers could access guidance and practical support more easily and without unnecessary delay.",The Family Hub in Halton Borough Council: Digital front door in family hub services,Halton Borough Council; Local Government; Family and children.; United Kingdom,The solution is implemented in partnership with Beebot AI as an interactive platform offering 24/7 access to family and children’s services.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Families seeking support often need timely, clear and accessible information, yet service pathways can be difficult to navigate, especially outside standard working hours. Halton Borough Council addressed this challenge by creating a “Digital Front Door” for Family Hub Services so that parents and carers could access guidance and practical support more easily and without unnecessary delay. The stakeholders include families, children’s services teams and service administrators, with the objective of improving accessibility, simplifying contact with family services and making routine transactions more efficient for both users and staff. The solution is implemented in partnership with Beebot AI as an interactive platform offering 24/7 access to family and children’s services. At a conceptual level, the system combines conversational interfaces with automated service functions, including personalised guidance through chat-based interactions and automated booking processes for appointments. This creates a single, always-available access point that can handle common enquiries and direct users towards appropriate support pathways. By integrating these digital interactions into Family Hub operations, the platform is intended to reduce friction in first contact and routine service access. The impact includes improved accessibility for families, faster handling of standard requests and more streamlined service delivery. More broadly, the initiative creates value by improving the user experience, supporting a more responsive family-services model and freeing staff capacity for more complex cases requiring direct human support.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2676,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02031,EU_PSTW_202609_02031,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Hertfordshire County Council,Transport,"Hertfordshire County Council, working with Robotiz3d and the University of Liverpool, set out to address a costly and persistent infrastructure problem: small cracks in roads are often left untreated until they develop into potholes, by which point repairs are more expensive, more disruptive and more damaging to road quality. At a conceptual level, the robot combines sensing, defect-identification models and automated repair functions so that it can inspect road surfaces, locate issues likely to become potholes and carry out preventative treatment without relying on entirely manual workflows.",Hertfordshire County Council: AI road maintenance robot,Hertfordshire County Council; Local Government; Transport; United Kingdom,The solution is implemented through an autonomous road-maintenance robot capable of detecting and repairing defects such as cracks.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Hertfordshire County Council, working with Robotiz3d and the University of Liverpool, set out to address a costly and persistent infrastructure problem: small cracks in roads are often left untreated until they develop into potholes, by which point repairs are more expensive, more disruptive and more damaging to road quality. The objective of the project is to intervene earlier by identifying minor defects before they worsen and by carrying out preventative maintenance in a more efficient way. The stakeholders include highway maintenance teams, local government infrastructure managers and road users, all of whom benefit from more reliable roads and lower lifecycle maintenance costs. The solution is implemented through an autonomous road-maintenance robot capable of detecting and repairing defects such as cracks. At a conceptual level, the robot combines sensing, defect-identification models and automated repair functions so that it can inspect road surfaces, locate issues likely to become potholes and carry out preventative treatment without relying on entirely manual workflows. This shifts maintenance activity from reactive repair to earlier intervention. The impact includes improved maintenance efficiency, better targeting of preventative repairs and reduced likelihood of more severe road deterioration over time. More broadly, the project creates value by lowering long-term maintenance costs, improving asset reliability and demonstrating a scalable model for proactive highway management that can reduce disruption for both councils and road users.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2677,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02032,EU_PSTW_202609_02032,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Kent County Council,Social protection n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,Kent County Council: Using predictive analytics to prevent homelessness in Maidstone and Kent,Kent County Council; Local Government; Social protection n.e.c.; United Kingdom,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Kent County Council leveraged predictive analytics to identify individuals and households at risk of homelessness in Maidstone and across Kent. By analysing data from multiple sources, the council can proactively intervene, provide targeted support, and allocate resources more effectively, aiming to reduce homelessness and improve community well-being.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2678,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02033,EU_PSTW_202609_02033,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""social_nonprofit""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Lancashire County Council,Transport,UNKNOWN,Lancashire County Council – A strategic approach to deploying AI in transport,Lancashire County Council; Local Government; Transport; United Kingdom,"Lancashire County Council is taking a structured approach to a persistent transport-management challenge: how to improve traffic flow, reduce congestion and make better use of existing infrastructure in a context of rising demand, constrained budgets and growing expectations for smarter mobility. The stakeholders include transport planners, highways managers, operational teams and road users, with the objective of improving journey reliability, supporting more efficient use of the road network and strengthening the council’s ability to respond to changing mobility patterns.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Lancashire County Council is taking a structured approach to a persistent transport-management challenge: how to improve traffic flow, reduce congestion and make better use of existing infrastructure in a context of rising demand, constrained budgets and growing expectations for smarter mobility. Rather than treating digital tools as isolated experiments, the council is positioning this work as part of a broader transport-planning and network-management strategy. The stakeholders include transport planners, highways managers, operational teams and road users, with the objective of improving journey reliability, supporting more efficient use of the road network and strengthening the council’s ability to respond to changing mobility patterns. The solution is being implemented through analytical and predictive technologies designed to support traffic optimisation and infrastructure management. At a conceptual level, these tools use transport and operational data to identify congestion patterns, model likely network behaviour and support decisions on planning, intervention and traffic management. By embedding these capabilities within existing transport processes rather than operating them separately, the council is creating a more strategic and scalable basis for deployment. The expected impact is improved traffic efficiency, better-informed planning decisions and more effective use of public infrastructure. More broadly, the initiative creates value by supporting smarter urban mobility, reducing avoidable delay and building a stronger foundation for data-driven transport management across the county.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2679,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02034,EU_PSTW_202609_02034,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Northumberland County Council,Public order and safety n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,"Northumberland County Council- FloodAI, using AI to detect flash floods",Northumberland County Council; Local Government; Public order and safety n.e.c.; United Kingdom,The solution is implemented as a live monitoring and early-warning system that analyses environmental data to identify signs of flash flooding as they emerge.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Northumberland County Council launched FloodAI to address a serious public-safety and resilience issue: flash floods can develop rapidly, leaving little time for authorities and communities to respond. In a changing climate, local authorities need earlier and more reliable warning mechanisms to reduce disruption, protect people and support a more coordinated response to local flooding events. This pilot, funded by Defra through the £200 million Flood and Coastal Innovation Programmes managed by the Environment Agency, involves council teams, resilience planners and public-safety stakeholders, with the objective of detecting flood conditions in real time and enabling faster operational action. The solution is implemented as a live monitoring and early-warning system that analyses environmental data to identify signs of flash flooding as they emerge. At a conceptual level, the system draws on relevant local data streams, processes them through detection and forecasting models, and converts these outputs into warning signals that can support operational response and risk mitigation. By linking analysis to real-time monitoring, the pilot is intended to shorten the time between environmental change and public action. The expected impact is earlier flood detection, improved readiness and stronger public safety outcomes. More broadly, FloodAI creates value by enhancing local climate resilience, supporting more proactive flood management and providing a scalable model for data-driven adaptation in vulnerable areas.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2680,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02035,EU_PSTW_202609_02035,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,North Yorkshire Council,Family and children.,"The council’s proof-of-concept project was developed to address the challenge of modernising case management so that staff can make better decisions, allocate resources more effectively and tailor support more closely to the needs of children and families.",North Yorkshire Council: Using AI to reimagine Children’s Social Care,North Yorkshire Council; Local Government; Family and children.; United Kingdom,"The solution is implemented through a case-management proof of concept built on Microsoft Azure and designed to support data-driven analysis within children’s social care workflows. At a conceptual level, the system uses analytical and decision-support capabilities to organise case information, surface relevant patterns and support more informed judgements about intervention, prioritisation and support planning.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"North Yorkshire Council is exploring how to improve children’s social care in a service environment where case complexity, workload pressures and the need for timely, well-informed decisions place heavy demands on professionals. The council’s proof-of-concept project was developed to address the challenge of modernising case management so that staff can make better decisions, allocate resources more effectively and tailor support more closely to the needs of children and families. Funded by the Department for Education’s Data and Digital Solutions Fund, the initiative involves social-care practitioners, service managers and digital teams, with the objective of improving outcomes while making the service more responsive and better equipped for complex casework. The solution is implemented through a case-management proof of concept built on Microsoft Azure and designed to support data-driven analysis within children’s social care workflows. At a conceptual level, the system uses analytical and decision-support capabilities to organise case information, surface relevant patterns and support more informed judgements about intervention, prioritisation and support planning. This is intended to complement professional expertise rather than replace it. The expected impact is better-quality decision-making, more effective deployment of resources and more personalised support for children and families. More broadly, the project creates value by strengthening service capability, improving workflow efficiency and laying the groundwork for a more modern and evidence-informed model of children’s social care.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2681,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02036,EU_PSTW_202609_02036,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Peterborough City Council,General public services n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,"Peterborough City Council: Hey Geraldine, a personalised AI assistant",Peterborough City Council; Local Government; General public services n.e.c.; United Kingdom,This use case therefore focuses on improving resident engagement and making council information easier to access in a more personalised way. The solution is implemented through a personalised digital assistant that interprets user needs and preferences in order to provide tailored information and guidance.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Peterborough City Council introduced “Hey Geraldine” to improve how residents access information and guidance from the council, particularly in situations where generic web content or traditional contact routes may not provide a quick or sufficiently tailored response. Local authorities increasingly need to offer services that feel more responsive to individual needs, while also managing service demand efficiently. This use case therefore focuses on improving resident engagement and making council information easier to access in a more personalised way. The stakeholders include residents, customer-service teams and council departments, with the objective of providing more relevant support, improving satisfaction and making interactions with the council simpler and more effective. The solution is implemented through a personalised digital assistant that interprets user needs and preferences in order to provide tailored information and guidance. At a conceptual level, the system combines conversational interaction with user-context understanding, enabling it to respond in a way that is more specific and useful than a standard one-size-fits-all information service. This allows the council to offer a more adaptive digital front end while maintaining consistency in information delivery. The expected impact is stronger resident engagement, higher satisfaction and easier access to council support. More broadly, “Hey Geraldine” creates value by improving the user experience, supporting service modernisation and offering a scalable model for more personalised local-government interaction.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2682,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02037,EU_PSTW_202609_02037,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,South Cambridgeshire,General public services n.e.c.,"Residents increasingly expect immediate answers for straightforward questions, yet traditional contact-centre models often struggle to provide this without increasing cost or pressure on staff.",South Cambridgeshire - Contact centre chatbot to improve resident satisfaction,South Cambridgeshire; Local Government; General public services n.e.c.; United Kingdom,"South Cambridgeshire District Council deployed a contact-centre chatbot to address a common local-government challenge: high volumes of routine enquiries can slow response times, reduce service efficiency and leave staff with less capacity to deal with complex or sensitive cases. The solution is implemented through a chatbot integrated into the contact-centre environment and configured to respond instantly to routine enquiries.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"South Cambridgeshire District Council deployed a contact-centre chatbot to address a common local-government challenge: high volumes of routine enquiries can slow response times, reduce service efficiency and leave staff with less capacity to deal with complex or sensitive cases. Residents increasingly expect immediate answers for straightforward questions, yet traditional contact-centre models often struggle to provide this without increasing cost or pressure on staff. The initiative was therefore designed to improve resident satisfaction while making the council’s service model more efficient. The stakeholders include residents, contact-centre teams and service managers, with the objective of providing faster responses to common queries, reducing unnecessary waiting and allowing staff to focus their effort where human judgement adds the greatest value. The solution is implemented through a chatbot integrated into the contact-centre environment and configured to respond instantly to routine enquiries. At a conceptual level, the system uses conversational question handling and service-routing logic to identify common issues, provide immediate answers and direct unresolved matters towards the appropriate teams. This creates a layered service model in which simple requests can be handled automatically while more complex cases continue to receive staff attention. The expected impact is quicker response times, improved service efficiency and better resident satisfaction. More broadly, the chatbot creates value by reducing friction in frontline service delivery and supporting a more scalable, responsive contact-centre operation.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2683,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02038,EU_PSTW_202609_02038,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,St Helens Borough Council,Outpatient services.,"St Helens Council launched its Technology Enabled Care Hub in 2024 as part of its 2024–2027 Adult Social Care strategy and wider “reablement – home first” approach, responding to the challenge of supporting people more proactively after hospital discharge and helping them regain confidence in everyday life.",St Helens Borough Council – augmenting TEC with AI,St Helens Borough Council; Local Government; Outpatient services.; United Kingdom,"The solution is implemented through in-house tools developed with Microsoft Copilot Studio, including predictive analytics used to match individuals with the most suitable TEC solutions and to build cost models for care planning.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"St Helens Council launched its Technology Enabled Care Hub in 2024 as part of its 2024–2027 Adult Social Care strategy and wider “reablement – home first” approach, responding to the challenge of supporting people more proactively after hospital discharge and helping them regain confidence in everyday life. Adult social care services need to identify the right support quickly, plan effectively and avoid unnecessary dependency on reactive models of care. This initiative therefore focuses on improving how Technology Enabled Care is selected, planned and embedded within care pathways. The stakeholders include adult social care teams, people leaving hospital, care planners and digital service leads, with the objective of shifting from reactive support to more preventative and person-centred intervention. The solution is implemented through in-house tools developed with Microsoft Copilot Studio, including predictive analytics used to match individuals with the most suitable TEC solutions and to build cost models for care planning. At a conceptual level, the system combines person-level data, predictive matching and planning support within the council’s digital-care framework so that TEC can be used more strategically and consistently. The expected impact is better alignment between individual need and care technology, improved planning efficiency and stronger support for reablement outcomes. More broadly, the initiative creates value by optimising the impact of TEC, strengthening the council’s next-generation digital strategy and enabling more sustainable adult social care delivery.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2684,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02039,EU_PSTW_202609_02039,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Swindon Borough Council,Sickness and disability.,"Swindon Borough Council developed Simply Readable to tackle a significant accessibility gap in public communication: producing Easy-Read documents for residents with learning disabilities can be slow, expensive and difficult to scale, even though accessible information is essential for fair access to public services.","Swindon Borough Council: Simply Readable, an AI easy-read solution",Swindon Borough Council; Local Government; Sickness and disability.; United Kingdom,"The solution is implemented as an Easy-Read generation tool developed in collaboration with residents and designed to produce simplified documents using clear language, larger fonts and helpful visuals. At a conceptual level, the system automates document adaptation and supports translation into 75 languages, allowing accessible content to be created rapidly while still reflecting the practical needs identified through co-design.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Swindon Borough Council developed Simply Readable to tackle a significant accessibility gap in public communication: producing Easy-Read documents for residents with learning disabilities can be slow, expensive and difficult to scale, even though accessible information is essential for fair access to public services. Traditional production methods often take weeks and cost hundreds of pounds, which limits how much information can realistically be adapted. The council therefore set out to create a faster and more inclusive way to generate accessible documents. The stakeholders include residents with learning disabilities, council communication teams and wider community users who benefit from clearer information, with the objective of improving understanding, reducing production barriers and extending access across more languages and formats. The solution is implemented as an Easy-Read generation tool developed in collaboration with residents and designed to produce simplified documents using clear language, larger fonts and helpful visuals. At a conceptual level, the system automates document adaptation and supports translation into 75 languages, allowing accessible content to be created rapidly while still reflecting the practical needs identified through co-design. The impact is substantial: production time has fallen from weeks to minutes and costs from hundreds of pounds to pennies per page. More broadly, Simply Readable creates value through major efficiency gains, stronger inclusivity and a scalable model for accessible public information.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2685,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02040,EU_PSTW_202609_02040,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Tunbridge Wells Borough Council,Cultural services.,"At The Amelia Scott cultural hub, Tunbridge Wells Borough Council sought to address two linked challenges: preserving historical artefacts more efficiently and creating richer ways for visitors and local communities to engage with collections.","Tunbridge Wells Borough Council: The Amelia Scott, Collecting Tomorrow",Tunbridge Wells Borough Council; Local Government; Cultural services.; United Kingdom,The solution is implemented by integrating generative tools into 3D-scanning workflows to accelerate and refine the creation of digital twins of historical objects.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"At The Amelia Scott cultural hub, Tunbridge Wells Borough Council sought to address two linked challenges: preserving historical artefacts more efficiently and creating richer ways for visitors and local communities to engage with collections. Traditional digitisation workflows can be time-consuming and resource-intensive, limiting how quickly objects can be documented, interpreted and reused in exhibitions or educational settings. The project therefore involved museum and cultural staff, local residents, learners and visitors, with the objective of improving preservation processes, widening access to heritage and building new skills and opportunities around digital culture. The solution is implemented by integrating generative tools into 3D-scanning workflows to accelerate and refine the creation of digital twins of historical objects. At a conceptual level, scanning, modelling and digital-content production are combined so that artefacts can be transformed into high-quality virtual representations for immersive exhibitions, tactile displays and virtual-reality experiences. The project also includes training activity that helps community participants develop practical digital skills linked to cultural preservation and interpretation. The impact includes faster and more accurate production of digital replicas, improved visitor engagement and broader accessibility to collections through interactive formats. More broadly, the initiative creates value by opening commercial opportunities, strengthening local capability and demonstrating how cultural institutions can combine preservation, education and innovation in a scalable and socially meaningful way.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2686,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02041,EU_PSTW_202609_02041,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Warwickshire County Council,Social exclusion n.e.c.,"The operational challenge was that identifying and classifying local food-related businesses and services manually required significant staff effort and limited the speed with which the council could generate useful insights for policy and service design. The stakeholders included council analysts, service planners and communities facing barriers to food access, with the objective of improving the evidence base for local decision-making while exploring whether new tools could reduce manual workload and support future analysis.",Warwickshire County Council: “Access to Food” – exploring LLMs for citizen needs,Warwickshire County Council; Local Government; Social exclusion n.e.c.; United Kingdom,"The solution was implemented through an in-house experiment using the open-source Llama 2 model to classify local businesses, allowing the council to retain full data sovereignty.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Warwickshire County Council developed the “Access to Food” project to better understand how residents, particularly those living in deprived areas, access healthy and affordable food options. The operational challenge was that identifying and classifying local food-related businesses and services manually required significant staff effort and limited the speed with which the council could generate useful insights for policy and service design. The stakeholders included council analysts, service planners and communities facing barriers to food access, with the objective of improving the evidence base for local decision-making while exploring whether new tools could reduce manual workload and support future analysis. The solution was implemented through an in-house experiment using the open-source Llama 2 model to classify local businesses, allowing the council to retain full data sovereignty. At a conceptual level, the project tested large language model capabilities against more conventional data-science methods in a controlled environment, examining performance, infrastructure needs, governance implications and operational fit. Although traditional analytical methods proved more accurate in this case, the pilot showed that language-model tools may still have value for automating parts of labour-intensive classification tasks. The impact lies less in immediate performance gains and more in strategic learning: the project generated valuable insight on hardware, security and governance requirements, while highlighting where future time savings and productivity improvements might be realised through carefully controlled adoption in local government settings.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2687,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02042,EU_PSTW_202609_02042,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Wigan Council,Old age.,"The initiative therefore involved adult social care practitioners, housing teams and transformation partners, with the objective of reducing administrative burden, improving workflow efficiency and allowing staff to focus more on frontline support.",Wigan Council: QuickAction in adult social care,Wigan Council; Local Government; Old age.; United Kingdom,"The solution was implemented in partnership with Agilisys Transform as a suite of tools designed around real service needs, with social workers involved directly in the design process. At a conceptual level, the system automates core administrative processes, supports case documentation and analysis, and includes an AI chatbot to assist service users and staff.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Wigan Council introduced QuickAction to address a pressing operational issue in adult social care: professionals were spending substantial time on repetitive administrative tasks, reducing the time available for direct support to residents. Activities such as writing needs assessments, recording meeting minutes, carrying out case audits and analysing survey responses are essential, but they can absorb large amounts of staff capacity in already pressured services. The initiative therefore involved adult social care practitioners, housing teams and transformation partners, with the objective of reducing administrative burden, improving workflow efficiency and allowing staff to focus more on frontline support. The solution was implemented in partnership with Agilisys Transform as a suite of tools designed around real service needs, with social workers involved directly in the design process. At a conceptual level, the system automates core administrative processes, supports case documentation and analysis, and includes an AI chatbot to assist service users and staff. The approach has also been extended beyond social care into housing services, creating wider organisational value. Robust governance arrangements were embedded to support secure and ethical use throughout deployment. The impact has already been measurable, with more than 100 staff days saved, alongside qualitative gains in productivity, consistency and user experience. More broadly, QuickAction demonstrates how carefully designed automation can improve service quality, reduce operational pressure and create a more sustainable and responsive model for care delivery.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2688,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02043,EU_PSTW_202609_02043,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Scottish Government’s Directorate for Equality, Inclusion & Human Rights",Sickness and disability.,"Access to British Sign Language interpreters is essential for Deaf BSL users to participate fully in work, healthcare, public services and social life, yet existing booking systems are often fragmented, inefficient and unable to match users with available interpreters reliably. This creates a practical barrier even where interpreter capacity exists, because the process of finding the right person at the right time can be slow and opaque.",SignPort: Transforming access to BSL interpreters through technology,"Scottish Government’s Directorate for Equality, Inclusion & Human Rights; Central-Government; Sickness and disability.; United Kingdom","The solution is implemented as a mobile app that matches users to interpreters according to need, availability and timing.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Access to British Sign Language interpreters is essential for Deaf BSL users to participate fully in work, healthcare, public services and social life, yet existing booking systems are often fragmented, inefficient and unable to match users with available interpreters reliably. This creates a practical barrier even where interpreter capacity exists, because the process of finding the right person at the right time can be slow and opaque. SignPort was developed to address this access problem, with Deaf Action and Burrotech focusing on choice, control and equity for Deaf BSL users rather than simple administrative efficiency alone. The stakeholders include Deaf BSL users, interpreters, public-service organisations and booking agencies such as the NHS, with the objective of improving interpreter access and making the process more user-centred and scalable. The solution is implemented as a mobile app that matches users to interpreters according to need, availability and timing. At a conceptual level, the platform combines booking logic, user preferences and service coordination into a digital matching workflow that reduces fragmentation and improves responsiveness. The team is also extending the service through additional features, including a fully integrated web version, to make it more attractive for interpreters and easier for agencies to adopt. The expected impact is faster and more reliable access to interpreters, improved user autonomy and a more efficient matching process, with broader value in accessibility, inclusion and service scalability.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2689,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02044,EU_PSTW_202609_02044,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""social_nonprofit"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Scottish Government’s Directorate for Equality, Inclusion & Human Rights","Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.","The Scottish Government identified a significant structural problem in the design and delivery of business support: information about businesses was fragmented across multiple organisations, making it difficult to generate a coherent view of need, target support effectively or evaluate outcomes across the public sector. This lack of integration weakened both efficiency and policy responsiveness, particularly during periods of economic uncertainty when timely evidence is critical.",DeepMiner: Transforming business support through data integration,"Scottish Government’s Directorate for Equality, Inclusion & Human Rights; Central-Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; United Kingdom","The solution is implemented through the Data Spine, a platform designed as a single point of truth for businesses in Scotland. The impact includes stronger policy intelligence, better targeting of support services and improved efficiency in the use of economic data.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Scottish Government identified a significant structural problem in the design and delivery of business support: information about businesses was fragmented across multiple organisations, making it difficult to generate a coherent view of need, target support effectively or evaluate outcomes across the public sector. This lack of integration weakened both efficiency and policy responsiveness, particularly during periods of economic uncertainty when timely evidence is critical. DeepMiner addressed this challenge by creating a common data foundation that could improve how business support services are designed, delivered and assessed. The stakeholders include policymakers, business-support teams, public agencies and the wider Scottish economy, with the objective of making business data more accessible, connected and useful for decision-making. The solution is implemented through the Data Spine, a platform designed as a single point of truth for businesses in Scotland. At a conceptual level, it integrates and structures economic data from diverse sources, creating a unified and queryable environment that supports insight generation, early identification of economic shocks and more targeted policy responses. Originally founded through CivTech in 2017 and refocused in 2020 on advanced data technologies, DeepMiner has built a model that reduces data silos and increases analytical capability. The impact includes stronger policy intelligence, better targeting of support services and improved efficiency in the use of economic data. More broadly, the platform creates societal and economic value by enabling smarter, faster and more coordinated public decision-making.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2690,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02045,EU_PSTW_202609_02045,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Bath and North East Somerset Council,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.","Processing such a large volume of qualitative feedback manually would normally require weeks of officer time, slowing decision-making and making it harder to provide clear, consistent and accountable reporting on what residents had said. The system completed the full analysis within three days, compared with an estimated six weeks of manual effort.",ConsultAI: Bath and North East Somerset Council parking charge proposals,"Bath and North East Somerset Council; Local Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; United Kingdom","The solution was implemented through ConsultAI, deployed in partnership with The Future Fox. More broadly, the use case creates value by improving transparency and accountability in public consultation, while giving councils a scalable way to handle large volumes of citizen feedback more efficiently.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"When Bath and North East Somerset Council consulted on proposed parking-charge changes in 2024, it received more than 4,300 responses and over 18,000 free-text comments. Processing such a large volume of qualitative feedback manually would normally require weeks of officer time, slowing decision-making and making it harder to provide clear, consistent and accountable reporting on what residents had said. The council therefore needed a way to accelerate analysis without losing visibility of the issues raised. The stakeholders included council officers, decision-makers and residents participating in the consultation, with the objective of improving the speed, clarity and reliability of consultation analysis. The solution was implemented through ConsultAI, deployed in partnership with The Future Fox. At a conceptual level, the platform processes large volumes of consultation responses, identifies themes, analyses longer comments and produces editable reports in Excel and Word format, enabling officers to review and refine outputs within familiar working tools. The system completed the full analysis within three days, compared with an estimated six weeks of manual effort. The impact was therefore both substantial and measurable: major time savings, quicker decision support and more consistent reporting. More broadly, the use case creates value by improving transparency and accountability in public consultation, while giving councils a scalable way to handle large volumes of citizen feedback more efficiently.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK|F06_HIGH_VOLUME_SCALABILITY_BOTTLENECK,S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2691,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02046,EU_PSTW_202609_02046,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Northamptonshire,Housing development.,"Large developments often struggle to sustain meaningful engagement, especially when conventional consultation formats are slow to launch, difficult to access or unable to provide timely insight into what communities actually think. At a conceptual level, the platform combines accessible digital polling, targeted online promotion and built-in analytics so that engagement can be broadened while results are analysed in real time, rather than through lengthy manual review.",PlaceBuilder - Unlocking community-driven development,City of Northamptonshire; Local Government; Housing development.; United Kingdom,"The solution was implemented through The Future Fox’s PlaceBuilder platform, a mobile-first digital engagement tool that enabled a branded consultation to be designed and launched in under seven days.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Hanwood Park, one of the UK’s largest planned housing developments, required an approach to consultation that could keep local communities involved throughout a complex and long-term planning process. Large developments often struggle to sustain meaningful engagement, especially when conventional consultation formats are slow to launch, difficult to access or unable to provide timely insight into what communities actually think. The developers therefore sought a more agile and inclusive method of involving residents in discussion about key themes such as sustainability, wellbeing and the masterplan itself. The stakeholders included Hanwood Park LLP, local communities and planning teams, with the objective of capturing broad, representative feedback and remaining responsive to local needs during the planning process. The solution was implemented through The Future Fox’s PlaceBuilder platform, a mobile-first digital engagement tool that enabled a branded consultation to be designed and launched in under seven days. At a conceptual level, the platform combines accessible digital polling, targeted online promotion and built-in analytics so that engagement can be broadened while results are analysed in real time, rather than through lengthy manual review. The impact included strong participation, faster interpretation of feedback and improved visibility of community priorities. More broadly, PlaceBuilder creates value by reducing consultation friction, improving the quality and representativeness of engagement, and giving developers a scalable method for community-driven planning.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2692,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02047,EU_PSTW_202609_02047,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Ministry of Housing, Communities & Local Government (MHCLG)",General public services n.e.c.,The operational challenge was to gather meaningful input across a very large and diverse geography while ensuring that participation extended beyond the usual respondents and generated usable evidence for strategic planning.,Strategic spatial planning: inclusive engagement and AI in strategic spatial planning,"Ministry of Housing, Communities & Local Government (MHCLG); Central-Government; General public services n.e.c.; United Kingdom",The solution was implemented through The Future Fox’s PlaceBuilder platform using a two-phase engagement model: visually led polls to attract younger audiences and a formal consultation site for wider participation.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"In 2020 and 2021, the Ministry of Housing, Communities and Local Government commissioned a large-scale digital consultation across the Oxford-Cambridge Arc, a region spanning five counties and 3.3 million residents, to inform the development of a long-term Spatial Framework. The operational challenge was to gather meaningful input across a very large and diverse geography while ensuring that participation extended beyond the usual respondents and generated usable evidence for strategic planning. The initiative therefore aimed to improve both the inclusiveness and analytical strength of spatial consultation. The stakeholders included national and local policymakers, residents, underrepresented groups and planners shaping the region’s future growth and sustainability, with the objective of obtaining richer, broader and more actionable public insight. The solution was implemented through The Future Fox’s PlaceBuilder platform using a two-phase engagement model: visually led polls to attract younger audiences and a formal consultation site for wider participation. At a conceptual level, targeted social-media campaigns were combined with automated analysis of over 66,000 responses, producing spatial heatmaps that visualised public sentiment on themes such as environment, economy, connectivity and place-making. The project achieved a 28% conversion rate, significantly higher than traditional surveys, and successfully engaged groups often underrepresented in consultation, including people under 35 and ethnic minorities. The impact was a more inclusive, data-rich and strategically useful consultation process, demonstrating the value of digital engagement and analytical tools in large-scale spatial planning.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2693,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02048,EU_PSTW_202609_02048,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Simplyhealth,Health n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,Simplyhealth supports their digital transformation with PolyAI,Simplyhealth; Private sector; Health n.e.c.; United Kingdom,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Simplyhealth, a leading UK health insurer, partnered with PolyAI to develop a customer-led voice assistant aimed at modernising their customer service operations. The voice assistant efficiently handles common FAQs and identifies vulnerable callers, directing them to appropriate departments for personalised support. This AI-driven solution has successfully resolved 25% of calls, enhancing customer experience and optimising resource allocation.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI|S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2694,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02049,EU_PSTW_202609_02049,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Carnegie Mellon University,General public services n.e.c.,"Pittsburgh needed a more effective way to manage congestion caused by a difficult urban road network shaped by hills, bridges and irregular street layouts. Traditional pre-programmed traffic signals were poorly suited to these changing conditions and contributed to delays, excess idling and inefficient traffic flow.",AI traffic system in Pittsburgh,Carnegie Mellon University; Academic-Research; General public services n.e.c.; United Kingdom,"The use case therefore focused on improving mobility through smarter signal control, with the objective of reducing travel time, cutting emissions and creating a more responsive traffic-management system. The solution is implemented through a decentralised signal-management platform that uses sensors and cameras to detect traffic in real time, predict short-term flows and adjust signal timings dynamically.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Pittsburgh needed a more effective way to manage congestion caused by a difficult urban road network shaped by hills, bridges and irregular street layouts. Traditional pre-programmed traffic signals were poorly suited to these changing conditions and contributed to delays, excess idling and inefficient traffic flow. The use case therefore focused on improving mobility through smarter signal control, with the objective of reducing travel time, cutting emissions and creating a more responsive traffic-management system. The stakeholders include city authorities, transport planners, drivers, pedestrians and researchers at Carnegie Mellon University, who developed the SURTRAC system to support a more adaptive model of urban traffic control. The solution is implemented through a decentralised signal-management platform that uses sensors and cameras to detect traffic in real time, predict short-term flows and adjust signal timings dynamically. At a conceptual level, intersections communicate with one another while still making local decisions, creating a coordinated network without relying on a single central controller. First deployed in 2012 across nine intersections in East Liberty, the system quickly demonstrated strong results: travel times fell by about 25%, idling by more than 40%, braking by 30% and emissions by roughly 21%. The project was later expanded to dozens of intersections and spun off into Rapid Flow Technologies for wider deployment. More broadly, the case shows how adaptive traffic control can improve mobility, reduce environmental impact and offer a scalable model for smarter urban infrastructure.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2695,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02050,EU_PSTW_202609_02050,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,North Lanarkshire Council,Social protection n.e.c.,"Many people experiencing distress do not engage easily with traditional services, especially when support is difficult to access outside normal service hours or when stigma discourages direct contact.",Chatbot for Mental Health and Wellbeing Support,North Lanarkshire Council; Local Government; Social protection n.e.c.; United Kingdom,"North Lanarkshire introduced this use case in response to rising suicide rates and the need for more accessible, immediate and low-barrier routes into mental health support. The solution is implemented through interactive signage in public locations and a linked digital chat platform that offers tailored guidance and resource signposting through conversational interaction.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"North Lanarkshire introduced this use case in response to rising suicide rates and the need for more accessible, immediate and low-barrier routes into mental health support. Many people experiencing distress do not engage easily with traditional services, especially when support is difficult to access outside normal service hours or when stigma discourages direct contact. The initiative, delivered through Hello Lamp Post as part of the council’s “Let’s Talk” campaign, sought to provide constant and approachable guidance in both public spaces and online. The stakeholders include the council, local communities, adults, under-18s and mental-health support services, with the objective of improving access to timely advice, identifying people at risk earlier and directing them towards appropriate support. The solution is implemented through interactive signage in public locations and a linked digital chat platform that offers tailored guidance and resource signposting through conversational interaction. At a conceptual level, the system uses rule-based and adaptive dialogue flows to identify user needs and route them towards relevant support pathways, including urgent services where necessary. The early impact was notable: within 24 hours of launch in Airdrie, one person self-identified as being at risk; about a third of users were directed to critical mental-health support and 23% were guided to emergency services. More broadly, the initiative creates value by providing always-available support, strengthening early intervention and demonstrating a scalable model for community-based mental-health engagement.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2696,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02051,EU_PSTW_202609_02051,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,NHS Royal United Hospital Bath,Outpatient services.,"The Royal United Hospital in Bath needed a more effective way to gather patient feedback without overloading staff or relying on low-response manual survey processes. In urgent care and orthopaedic outpatient settings, traditional feedback collection created administrative burden for nurses and produced very limited response volumes, making it difficult for the hospital to gain representative insight into patient experience.",Chatbot for Collecting Patient Feedback,NHS Royal United Hospital Bath; Local Government; Outpatient services.; United Kingdom,"The use case therefore focused on improving both engagement and operational efficiency through a more accessible and automated feedback mechanism. The solution is implemented through Hello Lamp Post’s interactive chat platform, structured around the Friends and Family Test and deployed directly in the relevant hospital wards.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Royal United Hospital in Bath needed a more effective way to gather patient feedback without overloading staff or relying on low-response manual survey processes. In urgent care and orthopaedic outpatient settings, traditional feedback collection created administrative burden for nurses and produced very limited response volumes, making it difficult for the hospital to gain representative insight into patient experience. The use case therefore focused on improving both engagement and operational efficiency through a more accessible and automated feedback mechanism. The stakeholders include patients, nurses, departmental managers and hospital administrators, with the objective of raising response rates, reducing staff workload and generating more useful evidence for service improvement. The solution is implemented through Hello Lamp Post’s interactive chat platform, structured around the Friends and Family Test and deployed directly in the relevant hospital wards. At a conceptual level, the system automates survey delivery through conversational interaction, allowing patients to respond more easily while the hospital receives structured and analysable feedback without depending on manual administration. The results were substantial: completion rates rose from just 2% to 70%, and each department saved around 300 staff-hours annually, representing staffing-cost savings calculated at GBP 11 per hour. The hospital also obtained richer insight into care quality, with 58% of orthopaedic patients rating their care as good and 5% identifying areas for improvement. More broadly, the project creates value by transforming patient engagement, improving operational efficiency and strengthening service improvement through better-quality feedback.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2697,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02052,EU_PSTW_202609_02052,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Network Rail,Transport,UNKNOWN,ExploreStation - Virtual Reality (VR) in Designing the Stations of the Future,Network Rail; Central-Government; Transport; United Kingdom,"The use case is significant because it applies VR to democratic infrastructure design, using immersive technology to make complex built-environment decisions more accessible and participatory.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Railway stations are public spaces used by passengers, communities, businesses and staff, so their future design requires engagement beyond technical rail specialists. ExploreStation used virtual reality to involve the public in shaping the design of small and medium-sized railway stations in the UK. The operational problem was that proposed station layouts, accessibility features and community functions can be hard for people to understand from drawings or consultation documents alone. The implementation created immersive VR experiences in which participants could walk through proposed station environments, experience platforms and waiting areas, and consider how design choices affected comfort, safety, accessibility and integration with local places. At a conceptual level, virtual architectural visualisation turns design consultation into embodied experience: users can form opinions based on spatial perception rather than abstract plans. The expected impact is more meaningful public feedback, better-informed station design, stronger inclusion of passenger and community needs, and improved collaboration between designers, planners and users. The use case is significant because it applies VR to democratic infrastructure design, using immersive technology to make complex built-environment decisions more accessible and participatory.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2698,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02053,EU_PSTW_202609_02053,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Nottinghamshire Hospice,Hospital services.,"Palliative and end-of-life care settings need approaches that support comfort, emotional wellbeing and meaningful experience for patients who may have limited mobility or cannot easily access outdoor environments.",Virtual Reality (VR) in the Nottinghamshire Hospice,Nottinghamshire Hospice; Local Government; Hospital services.; United Kingdom,"The use case is relevant because it shows how immersive technology can be applied in compassionate care, not merely for training or entertainment.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Palliative and end-of-life care settings need approaches that support comfort, emotional wellbeing and meaningful experience for patients who may have limited mobility or cannot easily access outdoor environments. Nottinghamshire Hospice worked with Digital Urban on a virtual-reality experience designed to bring natural landscapes into the care environment. The operational problem was to enhance patient experience and wellbeing without requiring physical travel or demanding activity. The implementation used a pop-up VR installation themed “Adding Life to Days”, allowing patients and residents to enter a virtual natural landscape with mountains, a pond, meadow, birds, butterflies, bees and other countryside elements. At a conceptual level, VR provides an immersive sensory environment that can support relaxation, reminiscence, emotional connection and temporary escape from clinical surroundings. The expected impact is improved wellbeing, reduced stress or anxiety, richer engagement for patients and families, and a more accessible way to experience nature within a hospice setting. The use case is relevant because it shows how immersive technology can be applied in compassionate care, not merely for training or entertainment. Its value depends on careful facilitation, sensitivity to patient comfort and integration with broader palliative-care support.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2699,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02054,EU_PSTW_202609_02054,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Chatsworth House,Cultural services.,"Lost heritage structures can be difficult for visitors to understand when no physical building remains, even where archival drawings and historic descriptions survive.",Virtual Reality (VR) in the Paxton’s Conservatory at Chatsworth House,Chatsworth House; Local Government; Cultural services.; United Kingdom,"The use case is important because it shows how VR can revive heritage spaces without physical reconstruction, supporting education and interpretation while preserving the integrity of the historic landscape.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Lost heritage structures can be difficult for visitors to understand when no physical building remains, even where archival drawings and historic descriptions survive. The Chatsworth project used virtual reality to reconstruct Joseph Paxton’s Great Conservatory, a major nineteenth-century glasshouse completed in 1840 and demolished in 1920. The operational objective was to make a vanished architectural and engineering landmark visible again for contemporary audiences, allowing visitors to experience its scale, iron-and-glass structure, planting and spatial drama. The implementation used detailed 3D modelling, historic drawings, archival research and immersive visualisation to create a virtual reconstruction of the conservatory. At a conceptual level, the project turns documentary evidence into a spatial experience: users can step inside a building that no longer exists and understand how its design, heating systems and botanical displays worked together. The expected impact is richer heritage interpretation, stronger public appreciation of Paxton’s engineering innovation, improved accessibility to lost architectural history and a more memorable visitor experience. The use case is important because it shows how VR can revive heritage spaces without physical reconstruction, supporting education and interpretation while preserving the integrity of the historic landscape.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2700,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02055,EU_PSTW_202609_02055,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Wirral Local Authority,General public services n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,Virtual Reality (VR) in Seacombe,Wirral Local Authority; Local Government; General public services n.e.c.; United Kingdom,"The case is significant because it uses immersive technology as a consultation method, not merely as a visual presentation tool, helping local authorities gather reactions from people who might not otherwise engage with planning processes.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Regeneration proposals can be hard for residents to assess when plans are presented as maps, drawings or abstract policy language. The Seacombe VR project used immersive 3D modelling to support community engagement around possible future changes to Seacombe and nearby waterfront areas. The operational objective was to help residents and stakeholders visualise regeneration ideas before decisions were finalised, especially in a context where public trust and early involvement were important. The implementation created a virtual experience that allowed participants to move through imagined urban, waterfront and public-realm spaces, including a playful classroom-based experience in which children could virtually pedal through future scenarios. At a conceptual level, VR converts masterplanning concepts into embodied experience: users can see scale, spatial relationships and design possibilities in a more intuitive way than through traditional consultation documents. The expected impact is broader participation, more informed feedback, better engagement with younger residents and stronger alignment between regeneration proposals and community expectations. The case is significant because it uses immersive technology as a consultation method, not merely as a visual presentation tool, helping local authorities gather reactions from people who might not otherwise engage with planning processes.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2701,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02056,EU_PSTW_202609_02056,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Wirral Borough Council,General public services n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,Virtual Reality (VR) in New Brighton,Wirral Borough Council; Local Government; General public services n.e.c.; United Kingdom,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Seaside regeneration involves sensitive choices about density, waterfront design, tourism, community identity and public space, so residents need ways to understand proposals before they become fixed plans. The New Brighton VR project used immersive visualisation to support early engagement with the town’s regeneration process. The operational problem was that people may react differently to proposals once they can see how buildings, promenades and public spaces might feel in situ. The implementation created a 3D model and VR experience for New Brighton, including a virtual journey along the promenade on a simulated open-top bus. Ideas from local young people and wider engagement were incorporated into the experience, making it a participatory tool rather than a purely technical model. At a conceptual level, the system turns planning options into an interactive environment where residents can assess scale, movement, views and atmosphere. The expected impact is more informed consultation, better representation of youth perspectives, reduced misunderstanding about regeneration proposals and stronger community influence on masterplanning. The case is relevant because it shows how VR can help move consultation beyond static boards and surveys, making future urban change easier to debate collectively.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2702,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02057,EU_PSTW_202609_02057,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Farnham,General public services n.e.c.,"High-street regeneration programmes need public input, but consultation can struggle to reach younger or less-engaged residents when proposals are shown through conventional documents.",Virtual Reality (VR) in Visualising the Future of Farnham High Street,City of Farnham; Local Government; General public services n.e.c.; United Kingdom,"The implementation created a 3D model of the town centre and deployed pop-up VR experiences directly on the high street, including playful modes where participants could “fly” through the future town using a jet-pack metaphor or explore the space through joystick-controlled alternatives.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"High-street regeneration programmes need public input, but consultation can struggle to reach younger or less-engaged residents when proposals are shown through conventional documents. The Farnham project used a digital twin and immersive VR experiences to help the public explore proposed changes to the town centre. The operational objective was to make future high-street designs easier to understand, more engaging and more inclusive as part of the Farnham Infrastructure Programme. The implementation created a 3D model of the town centre and deployed pop-up VR experiences directly on the high street, including playful modes where participants could “fly” through the future town using a jet-pack metaphor or explore the space through joystick-controlled alternatives. At a conceptual level, the project converts transport and public-realm proposals into a navigable digital environment, allowing people to experience scale, movement and design implications before providing feedback. The expected impact is broader consultation participation, more informed responses, stronger engagement with younger demographics and better evidence for planners and local decision-makers. The case demonstrates how immersive consultation can make urban-design choices more accessible, helping residents interact with planned changes in the place where those changes would occur.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2703,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02058,EU_PSTW_202609_02058,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Maggioli S.p.A,General public services n.e.c.,"AI4Gov addresses this governance challenge by helping policymakers and public institutions develop tools and frameworks that support automated, evidence-based decision-making while protecting democratic values and fundamental rights.",AI4Gov - Trusted AI for Transparent Public Governance fostering Democratic Values,Maggioli S.p.A; Consortium; General public services n.e.c.; European Union,"Public administrations are increasingly interested in using advanced analytical systems to improve policy design and service delivery, yet this raises difficult questions around transparency, bias, discrimination, legality and public trust. The solution is implemented through a European programme that develops governance models, practical tools and pilot use cases to improve transparency, explainability and ethical oversight in public-sector applications.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Public administrations are increasingly interested in using advanced analytical systems to improve policy design and service delivery, yet this raises difficult questions around transparency, bias, discrimination, legality and public trust. AI4Gov addresses this governance challenge by helping policymakers and public institutions develop tools and frameworks that support automated, evidence-based decision-making while protecting democratic values and fundamental rights. The stakeholders include public authorities, policymakers, researchers and citizens, with the objective of making technological adoption in government more trustworthy, interpretable and aligned with frameworks such as GDPR and the EU AI Act. The solution is implemented through a European programme that develops governance models, practical tools and pilot use cases to improve transparency, explainability and ethical oversight in public-sector applications. At a conceptual level, the project combines technical methods for trustworthy systems with policy-oriented design and user-friendly interfaces, enabling public bodies to test how advanced automation can be introduced without sacrificing accountability or fairness. This also supports greater citizen understanding and confidence in the use of digital systems in governance. The expected impact is better-quality public decision support, reduced risk of harmful bias and improved institutional readiness for responsible adoption. More broadly, AI4Gov creates value by strengthening democratic legitimacy, supporting more transparent public innovation and helping governments modernise in a way that is both effective and rights-conscious.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2704,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02059,EU_PSTW_202609_02059,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Diputación Provincial de Badajoz (DPB) is a local government body coordinating municipalities to promote economic and social development across the province. As part of the AI4Gov project, the DPB pilot focuses on sustainable water cycle management, covering both drinking and wastewater. The council oversees 63 drinking water treatment plants serving 137,910 residents, along with wastewater treatment. The pilot aims to develop intelligent tools that leverage existing water management systems and data to identify inefficiencies—such as leaks, water losses, or off-hour energy use—enabling technicians to implement non-obvious, day-to-day solutions. The insights will also support policymakers in infrastructure planning, budget allocation, and optimising water services by training AI models to detect system malfunctions.",Waste water management.,"In the province of Badajoz, water-cycle management spans both drinking water and wastewater services, creating an operational challenge in identifying inefficiencies across a distributed infrastructure of treatment plants and networks.",AI4Gov - Trusted AI for Transparent Public Governance fostering Democratic Values (Spain),"Diputación Provincial de Badajoz (DPB) is a local government body coordinating municipalities to promote economic and social development across the province. As part of the AI4Gov project, the DPB pilot focuses on sustainable water cycle management, covering both drinking and wastewater. The council oversees 63 drinking water treatment plants serving 137,910 residents, along with wastewater treatment. The pilot aims to develop intelligent tools that leverage existing water management systems and data to identify inefficiencies—such as leaks, water losses, or off-hour energy use—enabling technicians to implement non-obvious, day-to-day solutions. The insights will also support policymakers in infrastructure planning, budget allocation, and optimising water services by training AI models to detect system malfunctions.; Local Government; Waste water management.; Spain","The provincial council oversees 63 drinking-water treatment plants serving 137,910 residents, alongside wastewater responsibilities, and needs better ways to detect leaks, losses, malfunctioning equipment and avoidable energy use. This pilot addresses that problem by helping technicians and policymakers use existing operational data more effectively in day-to-day management and long-term planning.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"In the province of Badajoz, water-cycle management spans both drinking water and wastewater services, creating an operational challenge in identifying inefficiencies across a distributed infrastructure of treatment plants and networks. The provincial council oversees 63 drinking-water treatment plants serving 137,910 residents, alongside wastewater responsibilities, and needs better ways to detect leaks, losses, malfunctioning equipment and avoidable energy use. This pilot addresses that problem by helping technicians and policymakers use existing operational data more effectively in day-to-day management and long-term planning. The stakeholders include technical staff, infrastructure planners and provincial decision-makers, with the objective of improving service efficiency, targeting investment more intelligently and strengthening sustainable water management. The solution is implemented by building intelligent tools on top of existing water-management systems and datasets. At a conceptual level, the pilot uses analytical models to identify non-obvious inefficiencies, abnormal patterns and possible system malfunctions, translating these findings into operational insights for maintenance and service optimisation. The same evidence base can also inform budget allocation and infrastructure planning decisions. The expected impact is earlier detection of leaks and losses, more efficient energy use and better-informed technical interventions. More broadly, the pilot creates value by improving reliability in public water services, supporting sustainable infrastructure management and showing how existing municipal data can be turned into practical governance and operational benefits.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2705,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02060,EU_PSTW_202609_02060,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Jožef Stefan Institute (JSI),Environmental protection n.e.c.,"Many promising initiatives claim to contribute to the Sustainable Development Goals, but comparing them systematically and evaluating their ethical quality and practical impact at a global level is difficult. The Top100 Projects pilot, led by the Jožef Stefan Institute in partnership with UNESCO’s IRCAI, addresses this challenge by strengthening the application and evaluation process behind IRCAI’s annual showcase of one hundred initiatives linked to the 17 UN Sustainable Development Goals.",AI4Gov - Trusted AI for Transparent Public Governance fostering Democratic Values (Slovenia - Pilot1),Jožef Stefan Institute (JSI); Academic-Research; Environmental protection n.e.c.; Slovenia,"The solution is implemented by enhancing the digital system through which initiatives are submitted, assessed and compared. At a conceptual level, the pilot applies structured analysis and evaluation support to make project selection more transparent, consistent and evidence-based, while giving greater visibility to considerations such as impact, fairness and responsible implementation.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Many promising initiatives claim to contribute to the Sustainable Development Goals, but comparing them systematically and evaluating their ethical quality and practical impact at a global level is difficult. The Top100 Projects pilot, led by the Jožef Stefan Institute in partnership with UNESCO’s IRCAI, addresses this challenge by strengthening the application and evaluation process behind IRCAI’s annual showcase of one hundred initiatives linked to the 17 UN Sustainable Development Goals. The stakeholders include researchers, project applicants, evaluators and policymakers interested in socially beneficial innovation, with the objective of creating a more robust global platform that highlights projects not only for technical novelty but also for ethical practice and real-world value. The solution is implemented by enhancing the digital system through which initiatives are submitted, assessed and compared. At a conceptual level, the pilot applies structured analysis and evaluation support to make project selection more transparent, consistent and evidence-based, while giving greater visibility to considerations such as impact, fairness and responsible implementation. This transforms the showcase from a listing exercise into a stronger governance and learning instrument. The expected impact is improved quality and comparability of applications, better visibility for ethically grounded initiatives and more useful insights for organisations tracking innovation for the SDGs. More broadly, the pilot creates value by supporting a more credible and globally relevant framework for recognising technology-enabled projects with genuine societal benefit.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2706,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02061,EU_PSTW_202609_02061,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Jožef Stefan Institute (JSI),Environmental protection n.e.c.,The JSI SDG Observatory pilot addresses this challenge by creating a clearer and more usable evidence base for tracking sustainability progress.,AI4Gov - Trusted AI for Transparent Public Governance fostering Democratic Values (Slovenia - Pilot2),Jožef Stefan Institute (JSI); Academic-Research; Environmental protection n.e.c.; Slovenia,The solution is implemented as a web-based toolkit that aggregates and displays SDG-related indicators in a structured and accessible format. This turns fragmented sustainability information into a more coherent monitoring environment and supports more timely use of evidence in governance.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Policymakers and researchers need accessible ways to monitor progress towards the Sustainable Development Goals, yet relevant indicators are often dispersed across different sources and not always presented in a form that supports rapid understanding or action. The JSI SDG Observatory pilot addresses this challenge by creating a clearer and more usable evidence base for tracking sustainability progress. The stakeholders include researchers, policymakers and institutions working on SDG implementation, with the objective of making indicators easier to gather, visualise and interpret so that they can inform strategy, monitoring and public decision-making more effectively. The solution is implemented as a web-based toolkit that aggregates and displays SDG-related indicators in a structured and accessible format. At a conceptual level, the observatory combines data collection, harmonisation and visual analytics so that users can explore trends, compare indicators and identify areas requiring policy attention. This turns fragmented sustainability information into a more coherent monitoring environment and supports more timely use of evidence in governance. The expected impact is better visibility of SDG progress, improved usability of indicator data and stronger support for evidence-based policy discussions. More broadly, the pilot creates value by enhancing transparency in sustainability monitoring, reducing friction in the use of complex datasets and giving policymakers and researchers a more practical tool for understanding where action is succeeding and where additional intervention may be needed.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2707,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02062,EU_PSTW_202609_02062,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Jožef Stefan Institute (JSI),Environmental protection n.e.c.,"The OECD AI policy-analysis pilot addresses this challenge by helping institutions examine national strategies in a more systematic way, with particular attention to how bias is treated in policy frameworks.",AI4Gov - Trusted AI for Transparent Public Governance fostering Democratic Values (Slovenia - Pilot3),Jožef Stefan Institute (JSI); Academic-Research; Environmental protection n.e.c.; Slovenia,The solution is implemented through analytical and visual tools that examine national policy documents and generate comparative summaries of how bias-related questions are addressed.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"National strategies on advanced digital technologies are now widespread, but understanding how these policy documents address issues such as bias, fairness and responsible governance remains a complex analytical task. The OECD AI policy-analysis pilot addresses this challenge by helping institutions examine national strategies in a more systematic way, with particular attention to how bias is treated in policy frameworks. The stakeholders include policymakers, governance researchers and organisations interested in responsible digital regulation, with the objective of improving understanding of policy quality, highlighting best practices and supporting more informed development of future governance approaches. The solution is implemented through analytical and visual tools that examine national policy documents and generate comparative summaries of how bias-related questions are addressed. At a conceptual level, the pilot combines policy-text analysis, thematic categorisation and visual presentation methods to reconcile large numbers of documents and make differences in emphasis, completeness and good practice easier to identify. These outputs can then support reflection and policy learning rather than replacing expert interpretation. The expected impact is clearer visibility of policy strengths and gaps, better benchmarking across national strategies and stronger support for more ethical policy development. More broadly, the pilot creates value by improving the transparency and usability of policy analysis, helping decision-makers move from broad strategy statements towards more concrete, informed and responsible approaches to governance.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2708,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02063,EU_PSTW_202609_02063,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Vari-Voula-Vouliagmeni (VVV),Transport,"The municipality faces the challenge of optimising policies across transportation, waste management, sanitation, healthcare, security, and public works to adapt dynamically to varying citizen flows, including spatiotemporal patterns and ad-hoc situations like severe weather.",AI4Gov - Trusted AI for Transparent Public Governance in Transport (Greece - Pilot1),Vari-Voula-Vouliagmeni (VVV); Local Government; Transport; Greece,"Within AI4Gov, VVV proposed a use case focuing on parking ticket monitoring.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Vari-Voula-Vouliagmeni (VVV) is a municipality in eastern Attica with 50,585 residents and a significant influx of seasonal and weekend visitors drawn to its coastline, cultural sites, and events. The municipality faces the challenge of optimising policies across transportation, waste management, sanitation, healthcare, security, and public works to adapt dynamically to varying citizen flows, including spatiotemporal patterns and ad-hoc situations like severe weather. Coordinating updates across domains—for example, aligning parking management with waste collection—is crucial. Within AI4Gov, VVV proposed a use case focuing on parking ticket monitoring. The aim is to predict citizen flows and design efficient, multi-domain policies to manage people and vehicle movement effectively.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2709,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02064,EU_PSTW_202609_02064,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Vari-Voula-Vouliagmeni (VVV),Waste management.,"The municipality faces the challenge of optimising policies across transportation, waste management, sanitation, healthcare, security, and public works to adapt dynamically to varying citizen flows, including spatiotemporal patterns and ad-hoc situations like severe weather.",AI4Gov - Trusted AI for Transparent Public Governance in Waste Management (Greece - Pilot2),Vari-Voula-Vouliagmeni (VVV); Local Government; Waste management.; Greece,"Within AI4Gov, VVV proposed a use case focusing on waste management.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Vari-Voula-Vouliagmeni (VVV) is a municipality in eastern Attica with 50,585 residents and a significant influx of seasonal and weekend visitors drawn to its coastline, cultural sites, and events. The municipality faces the challenge of optimising policies across transportation, waste management, sanitation, healthcare, security, and public works to adapt dynamically to varying citizen flows, including spatiotemporal patterns and ad-hoc situations like severe weather. Coordinating updates across domains—for example, aligning parking management with waste collection—is crucial. Within AI4Gov, VVV proposed a use case focusing on waste management. The aim is to predict citizen flows and design efficient, multi-domain policies to manage people and vehicle movement effectively.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2710,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02065,EU_PSTW_202609_02065,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Turing Institute,Fuel and energy,UNKNOWN,Machine Learning towards a greener grid,Turing Institute; Academic-Research; Fuel and energy; United Kingdom,"The implementation developed improved solar and wind forecasting approaches using data science methods created with researchers from the Alan Turing Institute. The use case is significant because it applies AI to a critical infrastructure function where forecast accuracy has direct operational, environmental and economic consequences.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Electricity systems with high shares of wind and solar generation require more accurate forecasting because renewable output changes with weather and time of day. The “Towards a Greener Grid” project applied machine-learning and statistical forecasting to help National Grid Electricity System Operator balance the British electricity system more efficiently. The operational problem is that inaccurate renewable forecasts can force the system operator to procure more reserve or rely on backup generation, increasing costs and emissions. The implementation developed improved solar and wind forecasting approaches using data science methods created with researchers from the Alan Turing Institute. At a conceptual level, the models learn from historical generation, weather and system data to predict renewable output more accurately, especially for day-ahead operational planning. The expected impact is better balancing of supply and demand, reduced reliance on fossil-fuel backup, lower consumer costs and increased ability to integrate renewable energy into the grid. The use case is significant because it applies AI to a critical infrastructure function where forecast accuracy has direct operational, environmental and economic consequences. It demonstrates how machine learning can support decarbonisation not by producing energy directly, but by helping the grid absorb cleaner generation more reliably.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2711,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02066,EU_PSTW_202609_02066,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Italian Trade Agency,Economic affairs n.e.c,UNKNOWN,TrackIT Blockchain - The value chain for the traceability of Made in Italy,Italian Trade Agency; Central-Government; Economic affairs n.e.c; Italy,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Exporting companies often need to prove origin, quality, sustainability and production history to international buyers, while the “Made in Italy” brand is exposed to counterfeiting and misleading imitation. TrackIT Blockchain, promoted by the Italian Trade Agency, supports selected Italian exporters by providing blockchain-based supply-chain traceability connected to consumer-facing product information. The objective is to help companies, especially SMEs, communicate the authenticity and value of their products in sectors such as agri-food, fashion, cosmetics, furniture and design. The implementation records key production and provenance data on a blockchain and links that information to goods through QR codes or smart tags. At a conceptual level, the platform connects supply-chain events, company narratives, certification information and immutable distributed-ledger records, allowing consumers or business partners to scan a product and view its verified story. The expected impact is stronger consumer trust, better protection of the Made in Italy brand, improved export promotion and more transparent communication of quality and sustainability characteristics. Official ICE material presents TrackIT as a service that registers production data in blockchain and allows consumers to visualise the history of the product and company by scanning a smart tag. ICE also states that the project makes a standard traceability service available to 500 companies, with current participation deadlines extended into 2026.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2712,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02067,EU_PSTW_202609_02067,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Centro Ceramico,Economic affairs n.e.c,UNKNOWN,Block4Mat - Blockchain technology for the ceramics and construction supply chain,Centro Ceramico; Consortium; Economic affairs n.e.c; Italy,"Construction-material supply chains involve multiple actors, technical certifications, environmental requirements and lifecycle information that can be difficult to trace reliably from raw materials to final use or disposal. BLOCH4MAT applies blockchain technology to strategic construction-material sectors in Emilia-Romagna, including ceramic tiles, bricks and composite materials.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Construction-material supply chains involve multiple actors, technical certifications, environmental requirements and lifecycle information that can be difficult to trace reliably from raw materials to final use or disposal. BLOCH4MAT applies blockchain technology to strategic construction-material sectors in Emilia-Romagna, including ceramic tiles, bricks and composite materials. The project’s objective is to analyse where blockchain can bring effective advantages to producers and stakeholders, then develop a prototype platform able to make product information traceable, secure, unambiguous and non-falsifiable across the lifecycle. The implementation is coordinated by Centro Ceramico and involves scientific partners such as TEKNEHUB, CIRI-EC and Consorzio Futuro in Ricerca, together with companies including InnovationCHAIN and other industry actors. At a conceptual level, the platform records supply-chain information on materials, production steps, processing, certification and possibly end-of-life phases, while also exploring integration with BIM and other digital construction tools. The expected impact is greater transparency, anti-counterfeiting protection, process optimisation, improved coordination among construction actors and better availability of verified material data for digital building workflows. Public project material states that the central activity is the creation of a prototype blockchain platform for ceramic slabs, brick products and fibrous composite materials.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2713,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02068,EU_PSTW_202609_02068,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,University of Florence,"Mining, manufacturing and construction.","Large public real-estate portfolios require reliable information on buildings, maintenance needs, systems, spaces and performance, but asset data are often fragmented across static drawings, spreadsheets and facility-management systems.",BIM-To-Digital Twin: towards predictive and intelligent management of real estate assets,"University of Florence; Academic-Research; Mining, manufacturing and construction.; Italy","The implementation uses Infocad.FM and BIM tools to integrate property data, facility-management workflows and sensor information. At a conceptual level, BIM provides structured building geometry and asset information, while IoT data adds time-varying information on use, condition or performance.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Large public real-estate portfolios require reliable information on buildings, maintenance needs, systems, spaces and performance, but asset data are often fragmented across static drawings, spreadsheets and facility-management systems. The University of Florence’s BIM-To-Digital Twin work explores how Building Information Modelling and Internet of Things data can support predictive and intelligent management of university real estate. The operational problem is to move from static building records to dynamic information environments that help managers understand current conditions and anticipate interventions. The implementation uses Infocad.FM and BIM tools to integrate property data, facility-management workflows and sensor information. At a conceptual level, BIM provides structured building geometry and asset information, while IoT data adds time-varying information on use, condition or performance. Combining these layers creates the basis for a digital twin that can support maintenance planning, data interoperability and lifecycle decision-making. The expected impact is more efficient facility management, safer and more reliable building data, improved maintenance scheduling and better long-term management of university assets. The use case is important because it applies digital-twin concepts to everyday building operations, where value comes from connecting existing asset knowledge with live data and predictive management.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2714,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02069,EU_PSTW_202609_02069,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Parma,Community development.,"The operational problem is that many municipal decisions require field surveys, fragmented datasets or partial information, which can slow down planning and reduce transparency.",Digital Twin of the City of Parma,City of Parma; Local Government; Community development.; Italy,"The implementation uses panoramic surveys, geolocated point clouds and high-precision data acquisition across road and pedestrian areas, creating a shared digital model for municipal departments. The project is also connected to Parma’s carbon-neutrality objectives, because a shared digital model can help administrators evaluate the environmental consequences of policies and infrastructure decisions before they are implemented.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Urban administrations need reliable spatial data to evaluate planning decisions, manage public assets, support maintenance and assess environmental impacts before interventions are carried out. Parma’s Digital Twin creates a detailed three-dimensional representation of the city, including buildings, roads, green areas, infrastructure, technological networks and public assets. The operational problem is that many municipal decisions require field surveys, fragmented datasets or partial information, which can slow down planning and reduce transparency. The implementation uses panoramic surveys, geolocated point clouds and high-precision data acquisition across road and pedestrian areas, creating a shared digital model for municipal departments. At a conceptual level, the digital twin allows technical staff to inspect, measure, classify and simulate parts of the city from the office, while also supporting predictive analysis for weather events, flood risk, urban greenery and maintenance priorities. The expected impact is more efficient territorial management, faster asset inventories, better planning of public services, stronger climate-adaptation capacity and improved citizen participation. The project is also connected to Parma’s carbon-neutrality objectives, because a shared digital model can help administrators evaluate the environmental consequences of policies and infrastructure decisions before they are implemented.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F07_DATA_VISIBILITY_INTEGRATION_GAP,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2715,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02070,EU_PSTW_202609_02070,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,University of Bologna,"Mining, manufacturing and construction.",BeTwin addresses this challenge by developing digital-twin methods and tools for performance-based management of built heritage and complex real-estate portfolios.,BeTwin - Building Digital Twin for Built Heritage Performance-based Management,"University of Bologna; Academic-Research; Mining, manufacturing and construction.; Italy","At a conceptual level, the project creates digital twins that connect building characteristics, occupancy, comfort, energy use and performance indicators, making it possible to assess trade-offs between functional demand, environmental performance and operational cost. The expected impact is better building management, improved energy efficiency, more informed maintenance planning and easier use of advanced analytics by non-technical users.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Large building portfolios and heritage assets require management decisions that balance maintenance, energy performance, comfort, functional needs, costs and environmental impact. BeTwin addresses this challenge by developing digital-twin methods and tools for performance-based management of built heritage and complex real-estate portfolios. The operational problem is that facility managers often work with disconnected sources of information: BIM models may describe geometry and assets, energy simulations may model performance, IoT systems may capture real-time conditions, and financial or functional data may be managed separately. BeTwin proposes an integrated digital ecosystem that combines Building Information Modelling, Building Performance Simulation and Internet of Things data to support decision-making. At a conceptual level, the project creates digital twins that connect building characteristics, occupancy, comfort, energy use and performance indicators, making it possible to assess trade-offs between functional demand, environmental performance and operational cost. The expected impact is better building management, improved energy efficiency, more informed maintenance planning and easier use of advanced analytics by non-technical users. The use case is particularly relevant for universities, schools, museums, flexible offices and other multifunctional buildings where space use is intermittent and management decisions have both economic and sustainability implications.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2716,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02071,EU_PSTW_202609_02071,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Tarquinia,Cultural services.,UNKNOWN,4D digital model for the archaeological site of Cencelle,City of Tarquinia; Local Government; Cultural services.; Italy,"The use case is significant because it applies digital-twin methods to cultural heritage management, where the objective is not only visualisation but also systematic safeguarding of fragile archaeological remains over time.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Archaeological sites require long-term monitoring because environmental exposure, structural decay and excavation activity can progressively change the condition of ruins. The Cencelle project develops a 4D digital model of the archaeological site near Tarquinia to support conservation, documentation and preventive management. The operational problem is that traditional site records may not capture change over time with enough precision to identify degradation patterns or plan interventions proactively. The implementation combines drone-based multisensor surveys, photogrammetry, thermal imaging, laser scanning, on-site documentation and historical-architectural analysis. At a conceptual level, the model creates a digital twin of the site that includes spatial and temporal dimensions: researchers can compare conditions across survey periods and identify changes in structures, materials or environmental impacts. The expected impact is better conservation planning, more precise monitoring, improved documentation for researchers and administrators, and a stronger basis for restoration or preventive maintenance decisions. The use case is significant because it applies digital-twin methods to cultural heritage management, where the objective is not only visualisation but also systematic safeguarding of fragile archaeological remains over time.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2717,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02072,EU_PSTW_202609_02072,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""social_nonprofit""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Milan,General public services n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,"Milan Smart City Project: 2,500 km of roads surveyed by Cyclomedia mapping",City of Milan; Local Government; General public services n.e.c.; Italy,The use case is relevant because it shows that a digital twin often begins with high-quality baseline mapping before advanced simulations or AI services can be built on top.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Smart-city governance depends on accurate, up-to-date information about the physical public realm, including roads, signage, lighting, traffic signals, street furniture, accessibility features and infrastructure assets. Milan worked with Cyclomedia and other geospatial partners to map approximately 2,500 kilometres of urban roads and establish the basis for a city digital twin. The operational problem was that planning, maintenance and street management require detailed spatial data, but urban information can become outdated quickly and may be distributed across multiple municipal databases. The implementation used mobile mapping technology, 360-degree panoramic imagery and geospatial data capture to record the city’s street environment at scale. At a conceptual level, the project creates an immersive, measurable digital representation of Milan’s road network and associated assets, enabling staff to inspect, update and analyse the city without always needing field visits. The expected impact is more efficient maintenance, better urban planning, improved management of public space, stronger support for mobility and safety projects, and a more complete foundation for future smart-city applications. The use case is relevant because it shows that a digital twin often begins with high-quality baseline mapping before advanced simulations or AI services can be built on top.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2718,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02073,EU_PSTW_202609_02073,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,French Ministry of Culture,Cultural services.,"Major heritage monuments require detailed documentation for conservation, maintenance and public interpretation, especially where restoration history, structural complexity and visitor pressure create long-term management challenges.",Digital twins in cultural heritage conservation: Notre-Dame Cathedral,French Ministry of Culture; Central-Government; Cultural services.; France,The Notre-Dame digital twin project aims to create a highly detailed virtual replica of the cathedral using AI-supported processing of imagery and spatial data.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Major heritage monuments require detailed documentation for conservation, maintenance and public interpretation, especially where restoration history, structural complexity and visitor pressure create long-term management challenges. The Notre-Dame digital twin project aims to create a highly detailed virtual replica of the cathedral using AI-supported processing of imagery and spatial data. The operational objective is to build an accurate digital record of the cathedral’s interior and exterior, including roof, vaults, stonework, stained glass and architectural details, so that heritage professionals can support conservation and future maintenance. The implementation combines drone imagery, laser scanning, photogrammetry, high-resolution photographs and AI algorithms to integrate the data into a coherent digital model. At a conceptual level, the digital twin acts as both a conservation record and an immersive access tool: it can document physical condition, support analysis of the building fabric and allow remote users to explore the monument virtually. The expected impact is improved heritage preservation, more informed maintenance planning, broader public access and stronger resilience against future damage or loss. The case illustrates how AI and digital twins can support cultural heritage by making complex architectural information usable for both specialists and the public.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2719,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02074,EU_PSTW_202609_02074,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,House of Emerging Technologies of Matera (CTE Matera),Cultural services.,UNKNOWN,Digital Twin in the City of Matera,House of Emerging Technologies of Matera (CTE Matera); Academic-Research; Cultural services.; Italy,"The use case is significant because it links digital-twin technology with a UNESCO heritage urban environment, where innovation must also support cultural and environmental stewardship.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Urban governance requires integrated knowledge of mobility, infrastructure, energy, waste, environmental risks, built heritage and public services, but these domains are often managed through separate datasets and systems. The Digital Twin of Matera creates a virtual representation of the city to support planning, management and sustainable regeneration. The operational problem is to provide city authorities, researchers, businesses and citizens with a shared digital environment where real-time and historical urban information can be analysed together. The implementation is developed through the Casa delle Tecnologie Emergenti di Matera and the Italian National Research Council, integrating sensors, geospatial information, software modules, data storage, computational services and user interfaces. At a conceptual level, the digital twin functions as a living lab: it combines data and models to simulate urban phenomena, monitor conditions and support decisions about mobility, buildings, energy efficiency, waste flows, extreme weather and cultural heritage. The expected impact is more proactive city management, better forecasting of urban dynamics, improved resource efficiency and stronger public understanding of the city’s systems. The use case is significant because it links digital-twin technology with a UNESCO heritage urban environment, where innovation must also support cultural and environmental stewardship.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2720,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02075,EU_PSTW_202609_02075,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Italian Ministry of Environment and Energy Security (MASE),Environmental protection n.e.c.,"The operational problem is that environmental planning and risk prevention depend on consistent, accurate and up-to-date geophysical data, but such data are often fragmented across surveys, agencies and local systems.",Digital Twin of the Italian soil,Italian Ministry of Environment and Energy Security (MASE); Central-Government; Environmental protection n.e.c.; Italy,"The implementation uses aerial LiDAR, aerogravimetric surveys and geospatial processing to build detailed maps of terrain elevation, ground surfaces and subsurface features. The expected impact is stronger prevention of natural hazards, better planning of mitigation measures, more informed land-use policy and improved resilience of infrastructure and communities.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Land and soil management require detailed information on terrain morphology, surface features and subsurface characteristics, especially in a country exposed to landslides, floods, erosion and complex geological risks. The Digital Twin of the Italian Soil project aims to create a high-resolution national model of Italy’s territory through collaboration between Leonardo, its subsidiary e-GEOS and the Italian Ministry of Environment and Energy Security. The operational problem is that environmental planning and risk prevention depend on consistent, accurate and up-to-date geophysical data, but such data are often fragmented across surveys, agencies and local systems. The implementation uses aerial LiDAR, aerogravimetric surveys and geospatial processing to build detailed maps of terrain elevation, ground surfaces and subsurface features. At a conceptual level, the digital twin becomes a national monitoring and prediction layer that can support analysis of land stability, hydrogeological risk, environmental conditions and territorial change. The expected impact is stronger prevention of natural hazards, better planning of mitigation measures, more informed land-use policy and improved resilience of infrastructure and communities. The use case is significant because it applies digital-twin thinking at national territorial scale, moving from city or building models to a strategic environmental data infrastructure.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2721,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02076,EU_PSTW_202609_02076,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Spindox SpA,General public services n.e.c.,"The operational problem is that advanced digital-twin and AI applications often require programming expertise, limiting adoption by engineers, designers and industrial professionals who understand the process but cannot build complex software pipelines.",DESIGN-IT (DEcision Science desIGN platform for digItal Twins),Spindox SpA; Consortium; General public services n.e.c.; Italy,"The use case is relevant because it treats digital-twin design itself as a serviceable workflow, making advanced analytics easier to reuse and adapt across industrial domains.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Industrial organisations increasingly need to model products, machines and processes while integrating simulation, optimisation, machine learning and predictive maintenance into operational decision-making. DESIGN-IT was created to develop a Decision Science Design Platform that enables users to build and manage digital twins through a low-code approach. The operational problem is that advanced digital-twin and AI applications often require programming expertise, limiting adoption by engineers, designers and industrial professionals who understand the process but cannot build complex software pipelines. The implementation aims to provide a drag-and-drop platform where users can combine modelling, simulation, data management, optimisation and explainable AI components. At a conceptual level, the system acts as a configurable environment for creating digital twins at product, component and process levels, then connecting them with AI pipelines for prediction, maintenance and operational optimisation. The expected impact is faster development of industrial AI applications, greater accessibility for non-programmers, improved human-machine collaboration and smarter decision-making in automated manufacturing and robotics contexts. The use case is relevant because it treats digital-twin design itself as a serviceable workflow, making advanced analytics easier to reuse and adapt across industrial domains.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2722,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02077,EU_PSTW_202609_02077,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Rome Chamber of Commerce,Cultural services.,UNKNOWN,Adriano - first robot employee of the Italian Public Administration,Rome Chamber of Commerce; Local Government; Cultural services.; Italy,"The use case is significant because it moves AI assistance from a website or call centre into a physical public space, testing how humanoid robots can support service interaction and cultural communication.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Public-facing offices and cultural-institutional venues often need to welcome visitors, provide orientation and answer routine questions while also presenting public administration as innovative and accessible. Adriano was introduced by the Rome Chamber of Commerce on 28 march 2025 as an embodied AI robot designed to support reception and information services at the Temple of Vibia Sabina and Hadrian. The operational problem is to assist citizens and tourists in a high-profile public setting through a system that can recognise surroundings, communicate naturally and guide users towards relevant information. The implementation combines robotics, computer vision, artificial intelligence, conversational capabilities and advanced robotic hands or actuators. At a conceptual level, Adriano perceives people and objects, adapts its movement and behaviour in real time, and interacts with users in Italian and English to provide information about the Chamber of Commerce, the building and related services. The expected impact is improved visitor reception, experimentation with embodied public-service AI, stronger accessibility of institutional information and public demonstration of robotics within administration. The use case is significant because it moves AI assistance from a website or call centre into a physical public space, testing how humanoid robots can support service interaction and cultural communication.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2723,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02078,EU_PSTW_202609_02078,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Ca’ Foscari University of Venice,Cultural services.,"Archaeological restoration can involve thousands of damaged fragments whose manual reassembly is extremely slow, uncertain and resource-intensive. RePAIR addresses this problem by combining artificial intelligence, computer vision and robotics to support the reconstruction of fragmented artefacts such as frescoes, vases and amphorae.",RePAIR – Reconstructing the Past: Artificial Intelligence and Robotics meet Cultural Heritage,Ca’ Foscari University of Venice; Academic-Research; Cultural services.; Italy,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Archaeological restoration can involve thousands of damaged fragments whose manual reassembly is extremely slow, uncertain and resource-intensive. RePAIR addresses this problem by combining artificial intelligence, computer vision and robotics to support the reconstruction of fragmented artefacts such as frescoes, vases and amphorae. The operational challenge is that many cultural objects are excavated as large collections of worn, incomplete or irregular pieces, making traditional puzzle-solving methods impractical at scale. The implementation develops technologies that digitise fragments, analyse their shape and visual features, identify possible matches and physically assist reconstruction through robotic systems. At a conceptual level, the project creates a human-in-the-loop restoration workflow: AI helps reduce the search space and propose fragment relationships, while archaeologists and conservators retain expert control over interpretation and validation. The expected impact is faster restoration, recovery of artefacts that would otherwise remain inaccessible, improved documentation and new research capacity for cultural heritage institutions. The project’s significance lies in bringing together advanced robotics and archaeological expertise, demonstrating how automation can support restoration without replacing scholarly judgement or conservation ethics.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2724,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02079,EU_PSTW_202609_02079,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Creative Studio EBB,Cultural services.,UNKNOWN,EBB_AI : Co-creation-as-a-Service,Creative Studio EBB; Consortium; Cultural services.; France,"Cultural institutions and artists face growing demand to use generative AI creatively while protecting copyright, attribution and fair compensation. The operational problem is that generative AI can remix or derive value from cultural datasets without clear governance, making it difficult for institutions and artists to control use, monetise outputs or ensure recognition.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Cultural institutions and artists face growing demand to use generative AI creatively while protecting copyright, attribution and fair compensation. EBB_AI proposes a Co-creation-as-a-Service platform that allows museums, art schools, contemporary artists, film producers and other cultural partners to create AI-assisted outputs while maintaining rights-holder traceability. The operational problem is that generative AI can remix or derive value from cultural datasets without clear governance, making it difficult for institutions and artists to control use, monetise outputs or ensure recognition. The implementation combines co-creation tools, cultural partner databases, AI content-generation workflows and a blockchain- or NFT-based copyright-management layer. At a conceptual level, users interact with curated cultural assets through AI tools, while the platform records rights information, supports automated compensation and enables digital merchandise or poster creation under controlled conditions. The expected impact is more responsible use of AI in the cultural sector, new revenue models for institutions and artists, improved user engagement and stronger safeguards against untraceable reuse of protected works. The case is important because it addresses one of the central tensions in creative AI: enabling experimentation while embedding mechanisms for copyright, inclusivity, bias reduction and stakeholder compensation.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2725,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02080,EU_PSTW_202609_02080,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,CINECA,R&D economic affairs,"Advanced AI development requires large-scale computing infrastructure, high-quality data services and technical support that many public administrations, SMEs, start-ups and research organisations cannot build independently.",IT4LIA – Italy for Artificial Intelligence,CINECA; Academic-Research; R&D economic affairs; Italy,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Advanced AI development requires large-scale computing infrastructure, high-quality data services and technical support that many public administrations, SMEs, start-ups and research organisations cannot build independently. IT4LIA is Italy’s AI Factory initiative, designed to provide national and European users with AI-optimised supercomputing capacity, data-processing tools and related services. The operational problem is that AI adoption in strategic sectors depends on access to high-performance infrastructure and expert support, not only on algorithms. The implementation centres on the CINECA data centre in Bologna, where a new AI-optimised supercomputer is being integrated with existing high-performance computing and cloud systems. At a conceptual level, the factory provides a shared infrastructure layer for developing, training, testing and deploying AI applications in sectors such as agritech, cybersecurity, manufacturing, climate, earth sciences and public administration. The expected impact is stronger Italian and European AI competitiveness, wider access to advanced computing, accelerated innovation by SMEs and start-ups, and better translation of research into operational AI services. The initiative is significant because it treats AI capacity as public strategic infrastructure, linked to EuroHPC and national industrial-policy objectives.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2726,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02081,EU_PSTW_202609_02081,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Riga State City municipal Agency “Riga Digital Agency”,Social protection n.e.c.,"Public-safety and environmental-monitoring teams need rapid situational awareness across beaches, parks, waterways and other large open areas, but manual patrols cannot continuously cover all locations. The operational problem is that accidents, dangerous behaviour or environmental risks can develop quickly in public spaces, while response teams may lack real-time visibility over wide zones.",5G4LIVES – 5G for a Better Tomorrow: Protecting Lives and the Environment in Riga,Riga State City municipal Agency “Riga Digital Agency”; Local Government; Social protection n.e.c.; Latvia,"5G4LIVES applies 5G-enabled automated drone operations to support the Riga Municipal Police and improve emergency detection, prevention and response. The implementation uses drones equipped with high-resolution and thermal cameras, connected through 5G infrastructure to transmit data and support automated or semi-automated flights.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Public-safety and environmental-monitoring teams need rapid situational awareness across beaches, parks, waterways and other large open areas, but manual patrols cannot continuously cover all locations. 5G4LIVES applies 5G-enabled automated drone operations to support the Riga Municipal Police and improve emergency detection, prevention and response. The operational problem is that accidents, dangerous behaviour or environmental risks can develop quickly in public spaces, while response teams may lack real-time visibility over wide zones. The implementation uses drones equipped with high-resolution and thermal cameras, connected through 5G infrastructure to transmit data and support automated or semi-automated flights. At a conceptual level, the system combines UAVs, high-bandwidth connectivity, remote monitoring, image capture and command-centre workflows. This allows authorities to inspect areas more efficiently, detect incidents faster and coordinate response without relying exclusively on ground patrols. The expected impact is improved public safety, faster emergency response, better environmental monitoring and reduced workload for municipal police. The case is relevant because it links 5G deployment to concrete public-interest services rather than connectivity as an abstract infrastructure upgrade.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2728,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02082,EU_PSTW_202609_02082,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Turin,Social protection n.e.c.,"The operational problem is that emergency teams need reliable real-time information from difficult or risky areas, while drone missions require secure, low-latency communication, accurate tracking and integration with other data sources.",5G4LIVES – 5G for a Better Tomorrow: Protecting Lives and the Environment in Torino,Municipality of Turin; Local Government; Social protection n.e.c.; Italy,"In Turin, 5G4LIVES applies next-generation connectivity, drones and remote-sensing technologies to improve public safety and environmental monitoring. The implementation uses 5G-enabled UAV operations, infrared sensors, satellite-data interoperability and anti-drone-hacking technologies to support aerial monitoring and vulnerability assessment.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Urban emergency management in hilly and environmentally exposed areas requires rapid situational awareness, especially where floods, landslides, fires or missing-person incidents can evolve faster than conventional ground patrols can respond. In Turin, 5G4LIVES applies next-generation connectivity, drones and remote-sensing technologies to improve public safety and environmental monitoring. The operational problem is that emergency teams need reliable real-time information from difficult or risky areas, while drone missions require secure, low-latency communication, accurate tracking and integration with other data sources. The implementation uses 5G-enabled UAV operations, infrared sensors, satellite-data interoperability and anti-drone-hacking technologies to support aerial monitoring and vulnerability assessment. At a conceptual level, the system creates an aerial situational-awareness layer: drones collect visual, thermal and environmental information, while 5G connectivity supports command and control, mission planning, real-time tracking and rapid transmission of evidence to emergency operators. The expected impact is faster search-and-rescue response, better fire and hazard detection, improved assessment of vulnerable areas and stronger preparedness for natural disasters. The case is significant because it links 5G deployment to concrete municipal resilience needs, demonstrating how advanced connectivity can support both emergency operations and environmental protection.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2729,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02083,EU_PSTW_202609_02083,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,University of Ghent,General public services n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,5GENIUS – A Next-Generation Network for the University College of Ghent,University of Ghent; Academic-Research; General public services n.e.c.; Belgium,"At a conceptual level, the campus becomes an experimentation environment where 5G supports connected sensors, drones, augmented and virtual reality, data-intensive laboratory work, e-health applications and smart-farming use cases.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Universities increasingly need connectivity infrastructure that can support data-intensive research, immersive teaching and experimentation across multiple applied domains. 5GENIUS creates a private 5G network at University College Ghent, turning the campus into a smart laboratory for education, research and innovation. The operational problem is that advanced applications in healthcare, agriculture, logistics, mobility, Geo-ICT and 3D prototyping require reliable bandwidth, low latency and secure local connectivity that conventional campus networks may not always provide. The implementation deploys a private 5G non-standalone network at the Schoonmeersen campus, enabling researchers, students and innovation partners to test digital services under realistic conditions. At a conceptual level, the campus becomes an experimentation environment where 5G supports connected sensors, drones, augmented and virtual reality, data-intensive laboratory work, e-health applications and smart-farming use cases. The expected impact is improved training, stronger applied research capacity, faster testing of digital services and closer links between education, technology providers and sectoral innovation. The case is relevant because it treats university infrastructure as a platform for wider regional innovation, allowing students and researchers to work with connectivity conditions similar to those required in future industrial and public-service environments.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2730,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02084,EU_PSTW_202609_02084,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,WINGS ICT Solutions Technologies Pliroforikis Kai Epikoinonion Anonymi Etaireai,Social protection n.e.c.,"The operational problem is that communities with limited broadband access may struggle to benefit from telemedicine, remote monitoring, immersive learning or real-time emergency-alert systems.",5G-TERRA – 5G infrastructure and services for public interest and social inclusion,WINGS ICT Solutions Technologies Pliroforikis Kai Epikoinonion Anonymi Etaireai; Private sector; Social protection n.e.c.; Greece,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Remote rural areas and islands often face weaker connectivity, making it harder to provide modern healthcare, education and civil-protection services. 5G-TERRA addresses this digital divide by deploying 5G infrastructure in sparsely populated areas of central Greece and Greek islands. The operational problem is that communities with limited broadband access may struggle to benefit from telemedicine, remote monitoring, immersive learning or real-time emergency-alert systems. The implementation combines new 5G radio access equipment, upgraded backhaul links, cloud and edge resources, and a transition from non-standalone to standalone 5G architecture. At a conceptual level, the network becomes an enabling layer for three public-interest domains: digital healthcare, smart and green schools, and public protection. In healthcare, it supports wearable monitoring, video links for paramedics, remote medical support, electronic records and clinical decision support. In education, it enables connectivity, environmental monitoring and AR/VR learning. In civil protection, it supports wildfire and flood detection through IoT, AI, cameras, drones and risk-prediction tools. The expected impact is more equal access to essential services, better emergency preparedness, improved educational quality and stronger social inclusion for communities that would otherwise remain digitally underserved.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2731,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02085,EU_PSTW_202609_02085,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Oulu University Hospital,Hospital services.,"The operational problem is that clinicians often need to retrieve patient data, imaging and vital-sign information through fixed terminals or fragmented systems, which can reduce time spent with patients and complicate workflows during procedures.",Hola 5G Oulu - Private 5G network at an operating European Hospital,Oulu University Hospital; Local Government; Hospital services.; Finland,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Hospitals require secure, resilient and high-capacity wireless connectivity to support real-time clinical information, wearable devices and advanced visualisation in sensitive environments such as operating theatres and wards. Hola 5G Oulu establishes a private 5G network at Oulu University Hospital to create a healthcare environment where wireless wearable solutions and data-sharing applications can be safely tested and used. The operational problem is that clinicians often need to retrieve patient data, imaging and vital-sign information through fixed terminals or fragmented systems, which can reduce time spent with patients and complicate workflows during procedures. The implementation provides a robust private network initially covering selected operating theatres and a patient-ward floor, supporting research and development of wearable and visualisation solutions. At a conceptual level, the network acts as a secure wireless backbone for AR/VR devices, live patient-data access, medical imaging retrieval and remote specialist guidance. The expected impact is faster clinical information access, reduced workload for nurses and doctors, improved support during surgery and stronger resilience for digital hospital operations. The case is important because it tests private 5G in a functioning hospital, where connectivity must meet strict safety, reliability and continuity requirements.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2732,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02086,EU_PSTW_202609_02086,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Port of Ploce Authority,Environmental protection n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,SmartPort Ploce - Enchancing public services through a private 5G network,Port of Ploce Authority; Local Government; Environmental protection n.e.c.; Croatia,"The case is relevant because it applies 5G not only as general connectivity, but as an enabling infrastructure for port automation, safety management and environmental supervision in a strategic maritime-logistics setting.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Ports need reliable real-time information on cargo, vehicles, vessels, safety risks and environmental conditions, but traditional communications infrastructure may not provide the latency, resilience or coverage needed for advanced smart-port applications. SmartPort Ploče aims to transform the Port of Ploče into a 5G-enabled smart port through a dedicated private standalone network across the port area. The operational problem is that port authorities and operators must coordinate logistics, safety, security and environmental monitoring across a complex industrial space with moving assets and high operational risk. The implementation combines private 5G connectivity with real-time location systems, video analytics, sensor-based monitoring, smart cameras, thermal imaging and weather-monitoring tools. At a conceptual level, the network becomes a secure digital layer connecting port assets, vehicles, vessels, cargo operations and safety systems. The expected impact is more efficient cargo handling, better traffic coordination, faster incident detection, improved fire and pollution monitoring, and stronger operational resilience. The case is relevant because it applies 5G not only as general connectivity, but as an enabling infrastructure for port automation, safety management and environmental supervision in a strategic maritime-logistics setting.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2733,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02087,EU_PSTW_202609_02087,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"NOS Comunicações, S.A.",Health n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,5G in Healthcare – Northern Region (PT) (5G Healthcare (PT)),"NOS Comunicações, S.A.; Private sector; Health n.e.c.; Portugal",UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Healthcare systems need rapid information exchange between emergency vehicles, hospitals, specialists and remote patients, particularly where time-critical decisions depend on video, imaging or live vital-sign data. The 5G Healthcare project in Portugal’s Northern Region deploys standalone 5G infrastructure to support advanced clinical services across the Porto district and surrounding healthcare network. The operational problem is that ambulances, helicopters, hospitals and remote-care environments require secure, high-capacity and low-latency communication for medical data, video streaming and specialist input. The implementation supports connected emergency vehicles, robotic-surgery live streaming, telemedicine, remote patient monitoring, virtual-reality therapy in intensive care and off-site anatomical pathology diagnosis. At a conceptual level, the 5G network acts as a clinical connectivity layer: it enables real-time data exchange, supports medical training, connects IoT monitoring devices and allows high-resolution diagnostic material to be transmitted to specialists. The expected impact is better emergency triage, faster diagnosis, improved patient monitoring, reduced avoidable hospitalisation, more effective medical training and enhanced recovery support for conscious ICU patients. The case is significant because it combines emergency medicine, hospital innovation, remote diagnostics and immersive therapeutic applications within one regional 5G healthcare ecosystem.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2734,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02088,EU_PSTW_202609_02088,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Dstelecom S.A.,"Agriculture, forestry, fishing and hunting","5G.RURAL addresses this problem by deploying shared 5G connectivity in parts of the Alentejo region of Portugal, with the objective of reaching villages and low-density areas that lack adequate digital infrastructure. The operational problem is that conventional operator-led deployment may be commercially difficult in sparsely populated territories, while public services and local businesses increasingly require reliable high-speed connectivity.",5G.RURAL - Rural connectivity,"Dstelecom S.A.; Private sector; Agriculture, forestry, fishing and hunting; Portugal",UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Rural communities often face persistent connectivity gaps that affect access to healthcare, education, business development, tourism, cultural services and smart-agriculture applications. 5G.RURAL addresses this problem by deploying shared 5G connectivity in parts of the Alentejo region of Portugal, with the objective of reaching villages and low-density areas that lack adequate digital infrastructure. The operational problem is that conventional operator-led deployment may be commercially difficult in sparsely populated territories, while public services and local businesses increasingly require reliable high-speed connectivity. The implementation is based on a neutral-host model, allowing infrastructure to be shared by multiple operators or service providers rather than duplicated. At a conceptual level, 5G becomes a common rural-service backbone supporting telemedicine, remote care for older people, immersive learning, smart farming, livestock monitoring, energy-efficiency tools, tourism and local economic development. The expected impact is reduced territorial inequality, improved access to essential services, stronger rural innovation and better conditions for digital public-service delivery in areas that would otherwise remain underserved. The case is important because it frames 5G as an inclusion and territorial-cohesion tool, not only as an urban or industrial technology.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2735,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02089,EU_PSTW_202609_02089,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Stockholm,Outpatient services.,UNKNOWN,5GMHI – Smart healthcare,City of Stockholm; Local Government; Outpatient services.; Sweden,"5GMHI deploys a private 5G network in Stockholm to support medical services, emergency response and building-management use cases. The implementation enables five main use cases: drone delivery of automated external defibrillators, real-time location of hospital beds and infusion pumps, video communication between ambulances and emergency departments, AR/VR integration of BIM and GIS for building management, and connected HVAC monitoring through IoT and digital twins.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Urban healthcare and hospital-estate management both require fast, reliable and secure data exchange, from ambulance triage and equipment tracking to smart-building control and emergency response. 5GMHI deploys a private 5G network in Stockholm to support medical services, emergency response and building-management use cases. The operational problem is that hospitals and emergency services need low-latency video, real-time asset location, resilient communication and connected sensors, while hospital buildings also require better monitoring of energy and indoor-air conditions. The implementation enables five main use cases: drone delivery of automated external defibrillators, real-time location of hospital beds and infusion pumps, video communication between ambulances and emergency departments, AR/VR integration of BIM and GIS for building management, and connected HVAC monitoring through IoT and digital twins. At a conceptual level, the private 5G network supports both clinical and infrastructure intelligence, linking people, equipment, vehicles, buildings and emergency responders through a secure connectivity layer. The expected impact is improved patient safety, faster emergency response, better hospital preparedness, reduced staff time spent locating equipment and more efficient management of buildings and energy use. The case is relevant because it integrates healthcare innovation with smart-estate management rather than treating them as separate digital-transformation tracks.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2736,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02090,EU_PSTW_202609_02090,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Technical University of Košice (TUKE),Economic affairs n.e.c,UNKNOWN,TUKE 5GSC - TUKE 5G Infrastructure for Smart Communities,Technical University of Košice (TUKE); Academic-Research; Economic affairs n.e.c; Slovakia,"The implementation uses C-band spectrum, indoor radio units, antennas, basebands, network controllers and cloud-based management to create a dedicated 5G environment. At a conceptual level, the network enables two principal use cases.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Research and industrial environments need connectivity that can support real-time location, automated workflows, energy monitoring and low-latency data exchange. TUKE 5GSC deploys a private standalone 5G network at the Technical University of Košice to support smart-community applications in logistics and energy management. The operational problem is that manufacturing and campus environments require accurate tracking of assets, production flows and energy consumption, but conventional networks may not provide the performance and reliability required for industrial-grade automation. The implementation uses C-band spectrum, indoor radio units, antennas, basebands, network controllers and cloud-based management to create a dedicated 5G environment. At a conceptual level, the network enables two principal use cases. The smart-logistics use case uses real-time location systems to optimise manufacturing and delivery processes, while the smart-metering use case provides real-time analytics on energy use, efficiency and automated workflows supported by machine learning. The expected impact is more efficient production logistics, better visibility of energy consumption, improved reliability of monitoring systems and a replicable 5G model for university-industry experimentation. The case is significant because it positions a technical university as a testbed for industrial 5G applications that can later inform smart-community or manufacturing deployments.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2737,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02091,EU_PSTW_202609_02091,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Cities of Luleå, Boden, Skellefteå and Uppsala",General public services n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,ED5GE - 5G Edge Enabled Smart Communities for Green Transformation in the North Project,"Cities of Luleå, Boden, Skellefteå and Uppsala; Local Government; General public services n.e.c.; Sweden","The implementation combines 5G connectivity, edge-computing nodes, local traffic breakout, public network integration and smart applications across five use-case areas.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Northern Sweden’s green and digital transition requires connectivity that can support emergency response, port logistics, waste management, city traffic monitoring and precision agriculture across different types of communities. ED5GE deploys a 5G edge network across Luleå, Boden, Skellefteå and Uppsala to enable smart-community applications where low latency and local data processing are important. The operational problem is that many public-safety, transport and environmental services need real-time data from cameras, drones, sensors, autonomous machines and digital twins, but cloud-only architectures may introduce delay or reduce resilience. The implementation combines 5G connectivity, edge-computing nodes, local traffic breakout, public network integration and smart applications across five use-case areas. At a conceptual level, edge processing brings computation closer to data sources, allowing video streams, sensor data and machine-learning outputs to be analysed rapidly for operational decisions. The expected impact is improved emergency response in port areas, more efficient port traffic, better waste and recycling management, real-time traffic intelligence, and stronger support for sustainable farming through drones, autonomous machinery, digital twins and AR. The case demonstrates how 5G and edge computing can underpin multi-sector smart-community services rather than a single isolated application.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2738,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02092,EU_PSTW_202609_02092,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Poznan Supercomputing and Networking Center (PSNC),R&D economic affairs,"European research and industry need access to quantum-computing resources to explore problems that may be difficult for classical computers alone, including optimisation, materials science, chemistry, machine learning and complex simulation.",PIAST-Q - First European Quantum Computer,Poznan Supercomputing and Networking Center (PSNC); Academic-Research; R&D economic affairs; Poland,"The implementation is based on trapped-ion technology, using electromagnetic fields to trap charged atoms and precision lasers to perform quantum operations. The use case is significant because it is not a single research prototype in isolation, but part of a distributed European quantum-computing infrastructure designed to complement existing supercomputers.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"European research and industry need access to quantum-computing resources to explore problems that may be difficult for classical computers alone, including optimisation, materials science, chemistry, machine learning and complex simulation. PIAST-Q is a EuroHPC quantum computer hosted at the Poznań Supercomputing and Networking Center in Poland. The operational objective is to provide a European quantum-computing capability integrated with high-performance computing resources, so users can test hybrid classical-quantum workflows. The implementation is based on trapped-ion technology, using electromagnetic fields to trap charged atoms and precision lasers to perform quantum operations. At a conceptual level, the system operates as a gate-based quantum computer connected to the wider EuroHPC infrastructure, allowing researchers and eventually industrial or public-sector users to access quantum resources through a shared European framework. The expected impact is stronger European sovereignty in quantum technologies, broader access to advanced computing, faster experimentation with quantum algorithms and support for future scientific and industrial breakthroughs. The use case is significant because it is not a single research prototype in isolation, but part of a distributed European quantum-computing infrastructure designed to complement existing supercomputers.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2739,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02093,EU_PSTW_202609_02093,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""social_nonprofit""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Copernicus Marine Service,R&D related to environmental protection,UNKNOWN,EDITO-Infra project: EU Public Infrastructure for the European Digital Twin Ocean,Copernicus Marine Service; European Institution/Agency; R&D related to environmental protection; European Union,"The use case is significant because it treats the ocean as a complex dynamic system requiring shared European digital infrastructure, rather than isolated datasets or individual models.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Ocean governance requires integrated knowledge about climate change, biodiversity, pollution, coastal pressures, marine resources and human activity, but marine data are distributed across many observation, modelling and service systems. EDITO-Infra develops the core public infrastructure for the European Digital Twin Ocean, providing a shared foundation for local and thematic ocean twins. The operational problem is to integrate ocean observations, forecasts, modelling tools, data services and computational capacity into a framework that can support interactive simulations and science-based decision-making. The implementation builds on Copernicus Marine Service and EMODnet, combining existing and emerging data sources with next-generation ocean modelling, artificial intelligence and high-performance computing. At a conceptual level, the infrastructure creates a digital environment where users can explore ocean data, simulate scenarios, build specialised digital-twin applications and assess the impacts of climate change or human activity. The expected impact is better marine-policy design, stronger support for restoration and conservation, improved public understanding of ocean systems and more accessible tools for researchers, policymakers and innovators. The use case is significant because it treats the ocean as a complex dynamic system requiring shared European digital infrastructure, rather than isolated datasets or individual models.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2740,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02094,EU_PSTW_202609_02094,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Copernicus Marine Service,R&D related to environmental protection,"These resources will enable scientists, policymakers, businesses and other stakeholders to design their own local or thematic digital twins tailored to specific challenges.",EDITO-ModelLab - European Digital Twin Ocean,Copernicus Marine Service; European Institution/Agency; R&D related to environmental protection; European Union,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"EDITO-Model Lab is a Horizon Europe project launched in 2022 which forms a central component of the European Digital Twin Ocean initiative. Its purpose is to develop the next generation of ocean numerical models and the software infrastructure that will power a highly detailed, interactive digital representation of the ocean. The project combines state-of-the-art ocean modelling, artificial intelligence, satellite and in situ observations, and high-performance or cloud computing to create a “Virtual Ocean Model Lab” where advanced simulations can be built, tested and shared. This environment will allow users to explore a wide range of scenarios, such as pollution dispersion, coastal risks, climate-change impacts, biodiversity responses and sustainable marine-resource management. EDITO-Model Lab delivers a suite of high-resolution global and coastal models, covering physical, biogeochemical and ecological processes, alongside toolkits that support both developers and end users. These resources will enable scientists, policymakers, businesses and other stakeholders to design their own local or thematic digital twins tailored to specific challenges. By providing accessible modelling tools and a unified computing and data framework, the project supports informed decision-making, environmental protection, innovation in the blue economy and broader public understanding of the marine environment.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2741,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02095,EU_PSTW_202609_02095,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Rotterdam Port Authority,Transport,UNKNOWN,A Digital Twin for the Port of Rotterdam,Rotterdam Port Authority; Non-governmental; Transport; Germany,"The use case is significant because it applies digital-twin technology to one of Europe’s most complex logistics environments, where even small improvements in planning and situational awareness can have substantial operational value.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Large ports depend on precise coordination between vessels, quay infrastructure, weather conditions, hydrological data, logistics flows and safety operations. The Port of Rotterdam digital twin translates this complex physical environment into a dynamic virtual representation of the harbour area, supporting more informed and timely operational decisions. The operational problem is that port managers need a continuously updated view of ship movements, infrastructure status, water levels, wind, visibility, currents and other variables that influence navigation and logistics. The implementation integrates real-time sensor data, infrastructure information, shipping movements and environmental readings through a cloud-based IoT platform developed with technology partners. At a conceptual level, the digital twin functions as an operational intelligence layer: it combines spatial, environmental and maritime data so that port authorities can monitor conditions, improve berth planning, support safer vessel movements and prepare for more automated port operations. The expected impact is greater efficiency, improved safety, better environmental monitoring and stronger readiness for autonomous shipping. The use case is significant because it applies digital-twin technology to one of Europe’s most complex logistics environments, where even small improvements in planning and situational awareness can have substantial operational value.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2743,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02096,EU_PSTW_202609_02096,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Munich City Council,Economic affairs n.e.c,"The operational problem is that urban decisions often rely on separate datasets held by different departments, utilities and transport operators, making it difficult to assess combined impacts before action is taken.",Digital Twin Munich,Munich City Council; Local Government; Economic affairs n.e.c; Germany,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Cities need integrated data environments to plan mobility, manage growth, adapt to climate risks and involve residents in decisions that affect the urban fabric. Digital Twin Munich provides a virtual representation of the city that supports analyses, simulations and “what-if” scenarios for municipal planning. The operational problem is that urban decisions often rely on separate datasets held by different departments, utilities and transport operators, making it difficult to assess combined impacts before action is taken. The implementation centres on Munich’s Urban Data Platform, which connects geodata, 2D and 3D city models, sensor data and specialised municipal information. At a conceptual level, the digital twin acts as a shared analytical infrastructure: planners can visualise change, test policy scenarios and integrate real-time information such as weather, traffic, air-quality, noise and environmental data. The expected impact is better traffic planning, stronger climate-adaptation capacity, improved urban-development analysis and more transparent citizen involvement. The case is particularly relevant because Munich presents the digital twin not as a single application, but as a permanent digital backbone for future smart-city services.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2744,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02097,EU_PSTW_202609_02097,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Maggioli SpA,Health n.e.c.,"Cancer diagnosis increasingly depends on combining imaging, clinical information and specialist interpretation, but medical data are often fragmented across institutions and difficult to reuse securely for AI development. INCISIVE addressed this challenge by developing AI-based decision-support tools and a federated data repository for cancer imaging and associated clinical data.",INCISIVE - Improving cancer diagnosis and prediction with AI and big data,Maggioli SpA; Consortium; Health n.e.c.; European Union,"The use case is significant because it links AI performance to trustworthy data infrastructure, recognising that clinical AI depends as much on secure, representative and interoperable datasets as on model design.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Cancer diagnosis increasingly depends on combining imaging, clinical information and specialist interpretation, but medical data are often fragmented across institutions and difficult to reuse securely for AI development. INCISIVE addressed this challenge by developing AI-based decision-support tools and a federated data repository for cancer imaging and associated clinical data. The operational problem was to improve the accuracy, sensitivity, specificity, interpretability and cost-effectiveness of cancer-imaging methods while respecting data-protection requirements. The implementation created an interoperable repository of anonymised medical images and clinical data, allowing AI models to be trained and validated across distributed European datasets without relying on a single centralised data pool. At a conceptual level, INCISIVE combines federated data governance, explainable AI services and clinical decision-support pipelines for breast, lung, prostate and colorectal cancer. The expected impact is better detection, staging, monitoring and risk assessment, as well as stronger European capacity to develop reliable AI tools for oncology. The use case is significant because it links AI performance to trustworthy data infrastructure, recognising that clinical AI depends as much on secure, representative and interoperable datasets as on model design.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2745,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02098,EU_PSTW_202609_02098,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,National Agency of Information Society,General services.,UNKNOWN,Diella: AI - Driven Assistant for Citizen - Centric Digital Services,National Agency of Information Society; Central-Government; General services.; Albania,Diella is an AI-powered virtual assistant designed to improve citizens’ interaction with digital public services.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Diella is an AI-powered virtual assistant designed to improve citizens’ interaction with digital public services. Integrated into the e-Albania ecosystem, Diella provides intelligent, real-time assistance by helping users navigate public services, access relevant information, and better understand administrative procedures. Beyond providing information and guidance, Diella enables a more proactive and interactive service experience by generating documents in real time based on citizens’ requests. Through natural language interaction, users can request specific documents or services, and Diella can support the immediate preparation and delivery of the relevant documentation, simplifying processes and reducing the time and effort required to access public services. Its main purpose is to simplify the user experience, reduce administrative complexity, and provide fast, consistent, and accessible support. By leveraging artificial intelligence and natural language processing, Diella enables more intuitive communication between citizens and digital government platforms, contributing to a more efficient, responsive, and citizen-centric public administration.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2746,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02099,EU_PSTW_202609_02099,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Kaunas University of Technology (KUT),Health n.e.c.,"Depression diagnosis often relies on interviews, questionnaires and clinical judgement, which can be affected by subjectivity, limited appointment time and differences in how symptoms are expressed.",AI Model Combining Speech and Brain Activity for Depression Diagnosis,Kaunas University of Technology (KUT); Academic-Research; Health n.e.c.; Lithuania,"The implementation uses voice recordings and EEG signals converted into spectrograms, then analysed through a modified DenseNet-121 deep-learning architecture. At a conceptual level, the model treats audio and brain signals as complementary representations of mental-state information, using deep learning to classify whether a person is healthy or affected by depression.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Depression diagnosis often relies on interviews, questionnaires and clinical judgement, which can be affected by subjectivity, limited appointment time and differences in how symptoms are expressed. Researchers at Kaunas University of Technology developed a multimodal AI model that combines speech patterns and brain activity to support more objective recognition of major depressive disorder. The operational problem is that single-source diagnostic signals may be insufficient: speech can capture changes in pace, intonation and energy, while EEG data can reflect brainwave activity under controlled conditions. The implementation uses voice recordings and EEG signals converted into spectrograms, then analysed through a modified DenseNet-121 deep-learning architecture. At a conceptual level, the model treats audio and brain signals as complementary representations of mental-state information, using deep learning to classify whether a person is healthy or affected by depression. The expected impact is faster assessment, potential support for remote screening and reduced reliance on purely subjective interpretation. The case should be framed as research rather than clinical deployment, because further clinical trials, explainability improvements and validation are required before such a system could be used safely in healthcare settings.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2747,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02100,EU_PSTW_202609_02100,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,HTWG Konstanz,Fuel and energy,UNKNOWN,AI4grids - AI - based planning and operational management of distribution grids,HTWG Konstanz; Academic-Research; Fuel and energy; Germany,"The expected impact is better integration of renewable energy, reduced need for costly grid over-dimensioning, improved reliability and more efficient use of existing infrastructure. The case is significant because it applies AI to a concrete energy-transition bottleneck: distribution grids must become more intelligent if decentralised generation and flexible demand are to be integrated at scale.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Electricity distribution networks face increasing pressure from renewable generation, electric vehicles, heat pumps and changing consumption patterns. AI4Grids developed AI-based methods to support the planning and operational management of medium- and low-voltage distribution grids and microgrids. The operational problem is that solar and wind generation fluctuate, while new flexible loads can create local congestion or instability if not coordinated intelligently. The implementation develops algorithms for grid-state monitoring, load forecasting, renewable-generation prediction and operational recommendations. At a conceptual level, the system combines automated network-structure monitoring with weather and consumption data to build a more complete picture of grid conditions. In the event of a fault or abnormal operating state, AI-based control logic can generate recommendations for the control centre, helping operators restore stability more quickly. The expected impact is better integration of renewable energy, reduced need for costly grid over-dimensioning, improved reliability and more efficient use of existing infrastructure. The case is significant because it applies AI to a concrete energy-transition bottleneck: distribution grids must become more intelligent if decentralised generation and flexible demand are to be integrated at scale.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2748,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02101,EU_PSTW_202609_02101,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,KIWA,Protection of biodiversity and landscape.,"KIWA addresses this challenge by combining drones, artificial intelligence and decision-support tools to support earlier detection of forest and wildfires and more effective coordination of response resources. Traditional observation methods may not provide sufficiently fast or detailed information, especially when fires spread across difficult terrain or visibility is limited.","KIWA, an AI-based forest monitoring system",KIWA; Private sector; Protection of biodiversity and landscape.; Germany,KIWA uses unmanned aerial vehicles equipped with cameras to collect images and videos of monitored forest areas.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Forest fires can spread rapidly and create simultaneous risks for people, infrastructure, biodiversity and local emergency services. KIWA addresses this challenge by combining drones, artificial intelligence and decision-support tools to support earlier detection of forest and wildfires and more effective coordination of response resources. The operational problem is that municipal fire brigades and disaster-control authorities often need real-time information on the location of a fire, the direction and speed of spread, and the resources required to contain it. Traditional observation methods may not provide sufficiently fast or detailed information, especially when fires spread across difficult terrain or visibility is limited. KIWA uses unmanned aerial vehicles equipped with cameras to collect images and videos of monitored forest areas. These data are transmitted through interfaces and analysed by AI models in real time to identify fire or smoke, while the decision-support system helps emergency services interpret the current situation and match available personnel and equipment to the threat. At a conceptual level, the system creates a continuous forest-monitoring and response-support loop: drones collect situational data, AI detects and forecasts fire-related risks, and municipal authorities receive actionable information through a dashboard or operational interface. The expected impact is faster detection, better deployment of firefighting resources, reduced ecological damage, improved safety for people and animals, and lower financial burden for municipalities affected by wildfire events. The case is significant because it connects environmental monitoring, civil protection and AI-enabled decision support in a scalable model for regions exposed to increasing climate-related fire risks.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION|S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2749,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02102,EU_PSTW_202609_02102,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Lepida ScpA,General services.,UNKNOWN,MarghERita: Emilia-Romagna’s High-Performance Computing and Big Data Platform,Lepida ScpA; Non-governmental; General services.; Italy,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Regional administrations increasingly need high-performance computing capacity to process large datasets, run predictive models and support rapid decision-making in areas such as civil protection, environmental risk and public-service planning. MarghERita is the Emilia-Romagna Region’s HPC and Big Data platform, developed with Lepida to provide regional institutions with computing infrastructure that can support advanced modelling and data-intensive applications. The operational value of the platform is particularly clear in civil protection, where extreme weather events require rapid simulations and evidence-based prevention measures. After the severe floods that affected Emilia-Romagna in May 2023, the Civil Protection, the University of Parma, the University of Padua and the Region used MarghERita’s computing power to implement algorithms capable of simulating flood hydrographs in real time. These simulations applied predictive models of possible bank breaks along the Lamone River to assess and mitigate potential flooding consequences for surrounding territory. At a conceptual level, MarghERita acts as a shared computational backbone for public authorities: instead of each administration building separate high-capacity infrastructure, the regional platform provides the computing power needed for complex simulations, environmental modelling and decision support. The expected impact is improved prevention of hydrogeological risk, faster response during weather-hydrological alerts, stronger coordination between universities and public authorities, and more data-driven management of extreme climate events. The case is relevant because it shows how regional HPC infrastructure can move beyond research support and become part of operational civil-protection capacity.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2750,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02103,EU_PSTW_202609_02103,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Lepida ScpA,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.","Regional legislation can be difficult for both public officials and citizens to navigate because legal texts, implementing acts and territorial effects are often distributed across multiple documents and administrative layers.",SAVIA project: Artificial Intelligence for law quality,"Lepida ScpA; Consortium; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Italy","SAVIA addresses this challenge by using artificial intelligence to support consultation of Emilia-Romagna regional laws and their implementing measures. The operational problem is that legislative assemblies and local administrations need timely, accurate and comprehensible information about what regional laws require, how they are implemented by the Regional Council, municipalities and provinces, and what effects they produce on the territory.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Regional legislation can be difficult for both public officials and citizens to navigate because legal texts, implementing acts and territorial effects are often distributed across multiple documents and administrative layers. SAVIA addresses this challenge by using artificial intelligence to support consultation of Emilia-Romagna regional laws and their implementing measures. The operational problem is that legislative assemblies and local administrations need timely, accurate and comprehensible information about what regional laws require, how they are implemented by the Regional Council, municipalities and provinces, and what effects they produce on the territory. SAVIA is developed by the Legislative Assembly of Emilia-Romagna in collaboration with CINECA and is supported by the MarghERita Big Data platform. At a conceptual level, the system uses large language models and retrieval-augmented generation to answer natural-language questions on regional law. The aim is not to replace legal judgement or legislative interpretation, but to provide a decision-support and information-access tool that can help institutional users and the public retrieve relevant legal information more easily. Between the end of 2024 and the beginning of 2025, activities were carried out to test SAVIA’s performance on MarghERita, including configuration work and expansion of GPU resources available to the project. The expected impact is improved legislative quality, easier access to regional law, greater transparency of institutional activity and stronger support for local administrations in carrying out their functions. The case is significant because it applies generative AI to legal accessibility while grounding answers in official regional legal texts.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,S1_KNOWLEDGE_RETRIEVAL_RAG,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2751,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02104,EU_PSTW_202609_02104,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Lepida ScpA,General public services n.e.c.,"The operational problem is that decision-makers need rapid evidence on the likely environmental effects of mobility measures, but full modelling exercises can be slow and technically complex.","Proof of Concept (POC) of VERA, the Regional Digital Twin",Lepida ScpA; Non-governmental; General public services n.e.c.; Italy,"The case is relevant because it uses a digital twin as a policy simulator rather than only as a visual model, supporting environmental governance with quantified scenario outputs.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Regional air-quality policy requires an understanding of how traffic flows, vehicle fleets and emission-reduction measures affect pollutant concentrations across municipalities. VERA, the Regional Digital Twin of Emilia-Romagna, is a proof-of-concept tool designed to simulate the air-quality impacts of traffic and road-policy scenarios. The operational problem is that decision-makers need rapid evidence on the likely environmental effects of mobility measures, but full modelling exercises can be slow and technically complex. The implementation allows expert users to generate scenarios in less than ten minutes through three input modes: changing traffic flows on the regional road graph, modifying the composition of vehicle fleets in selected municipalities, and applying hypothetical emission-reduction measures. At a conceptual level, VERA connects transport-policy assumptions with emissions calculations and concentration modelling for pollutants such as NO₂, PM10 and PM2.5. The expected impact is faster scenario assessment, stronger evidence for traffic and air-quality policy, and better coordination between regional and municipal decisions. The case is relevant because it uses a digital twin as a policy simulator rather than only as a visual model, supporting environmental governance with quantified scenario outputs.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2752,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02105,EU_PSTW_202609_02105,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Italian National Institute for Insurance against Accidents at Work (INAIL),Sickness and disability.,"INAIL’s experimental chatbot addresses the problem that legal and technical texts can be difficult to consult, especially for users who need to understand practical implications for occupational health and safety.",Chatbot for the consultation of the AI Act,Italian National Institute for Insurance against Accidents at Work (INAIL); Central-Government; Sickness and disability.; Italy,"The EU AI Act introduces a complex regulatory framework that affects public administrations, employers, technology providers and organisations using AI systems in sensitive contexts. At a conceptual level, the chatbot uses retrieval-augmented generation: the language model’s ability to generate coherent responses is combined with selected legal sources so that answers remain linked to the relevant regulatory material.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The EU AI Act introduces a complex regulatory framework that affects public administrations, employers, technology providers and organisations using AI systems in sensitive contexts. INAIL’s experimental chatbot addresses the problem that legal and technical texts can be difficult to consult, especially for users who need to understand practical implications for occupational health and safety. The chatbot was developed by INAIL’s Department of Occupational and Environmental Medicine, Epidemiology and Hygiene as a specialised tool for answering questions about Regulation (EU) 2024/1689. The operational objective is to make the AI Act easier to understand and apply by allowing users to interact with the legal text through a structured question-and-answer interface. The tool is based on a pre-trained large language model, GPT-4o, customised through the GPT Store and supplied with a targeted knowledge base including the Italian version of the AI Act, a summary of the regulation, its index and detailed instructions for structuring responses. At a conceptual level, the chatbot uses retrieval-augmented generation: the language model’s ability to generate coherent responses is combined with selected legal sources so that answers remain linked to the relevant regulatory material. The system was tested with particular attention to health and safety at work, where AI regulation may affect workplace technologies, risk management and worker protection. The expected impact is easier consultation of complex regulation, better informed compliance, improved awareness of AI-related obligations in occupational contexts and a reusable methodology for other legal or technical domains. The case is relevant because it demonstrates controlled public-sector experimentation with LLMs for regulatory assistance.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2753,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02106,EU_PSTW_202609_02106,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Polish Government,General public services n.e.c.,"Digital public-service portals and wallet applications can simplify access to administration, but citizens may still struggle to identify which service is relevant, what documents are needed or how to complete a procedure. Poland’s planned AI assistant for mObywatel addresses this challenge by integrating the Polish Large Language Model, PLLuM, into the country’s national digital-wallet and public-service ecosystem.",Open Source AI Assistant in Polish digital-wallet app mObywatel,Polish Government; Central-Government; General public services n.e.c.; Poland,"The operational problem is that public administration uses formal language, legal terminology and complex workflows, while citizens often express needs in everyday language. The assistant is expected to support access to public information, automate parts of clerical work, assist document processing and help officials respond to citizens’ enquiries.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Digital public-service portals and wallet applications can simplify access to administration, but citizens may still struggle to identify which service is relevant, what documents are needed or how to complete a procedure. Poland’s planned AI assistant for mObywatel addresses this challenge by integrating the Polish Large Language Model, PLLuM, into the country’s national digital-wallet and public-service ecosystem. The operational problem is that public administration uses formal language, legal terminology and complex workflows, while citizens often express needs in everyday language. An AI assistant can help bridge this gap by answering questions in Polish, guiding users through procedures and making public information more accessible. PLLuM is specifically relevant because it was trained on Polish datasets and designed to handle Polish inflection, syntax and administrative language more effectively than generic models. At a conceptual level, the system acts as a sovereign language layer for digital government: instead of relying solely on foreign commercial assistants, Poland can embed an open Polish model into public-service channels. The assistant is expected to support access to public information, automate parts of clerical work, assist document processing and help officials respond to citizens’ enquiries. The expected impact is simpler navigation of administrative processes, improved accessibility for users with limited digital skills, reduced workload for public-administration staff and stronger digital sovereignty through open-source national AI infrastructure. The case is significant because it links language-model development with a concrete government application in mObywatel, rather than treating PLLuM as a research model alone.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2754,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02107,EU_PSTW_202609_02107,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Berufsgenossenschaft Energie Textil Elektro Medienerzeugnisse (BG ETEM),"General economic, commercial and labour affair","Very small businesses must comply with occupational health and safety requirements, but they often lack specialised staff, time or technical knowledge to prepare structured risk assessments. The operational problem is that even low-risk businesses need to identify hazards, assess working conditions and define suitable protective measures, while conventional documentation can be burdensome for owners and managers.",A Web App to Support Risk Assessments for Small Businesses,"Berufsgenossenschaft Energie Textil Elektro Medienerzeugnisse (BG ETEM); Central-Government; General economic, commercial and labour affair; Germany",UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Very small businesses must comply with occupational health and safety requirements, but they often lack specialised staff, time or technical knowledge to prepare structured risk assessments. BG ETEM’s web app supports micro-enterprises in completing the German “Gefährdungsbeurteilung”, a core requirement for effective workplace safety. The operational problem is that even low-risk businesses need to identify hazards, assess working conditions and define suitable protective measures, while conventional documentation can be burdensome for owners and managers. The implementation provides a browser-based, questionnaire-style application tailored to small establishments with relatively limited hazard potential, such as opticians, hearing-aid businesses, tailoring alteration shops and copyshops. At a conceptual level, the app translates occupational-safety requirements into guided steps covering safety organisation, general workplace aspects and, where relevant, office and screen-based work. The expected impact is reduced administrative burden, easier compliance, improved awareness of workplace hazards and better preventive protection for employees. The case is significant because it shows how digital tools can simplify regulatory compliance for micro-enterprises without replacing professional responsibility for health and safety.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2755,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02108,EU_PSTW_202609_02108,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Italian Ministry of Labour and Social Policies,Social protection n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,AppLI: AI-powered guidance and skills development for professional transitions,Italian Ministry of Labour and Social Policies; Central-Government; Social protection n.e.c.; Italy,"Integrated into Italy’s public ecosystem for employment, training and social inclusion, it uses multi-agent generative AI to transform needs expressed in natural language into personalised pathways for guidance, learning and professional preparation.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"AppLI is a national public platform created by the Italian Ministry of Labour and Social Policies to support citizens in skills development and professional transitions. Integrated into Italy’s public ecosystem for employment, training and social inclusion, it uses multi-agent generative AI to transform needs expressed in natural language into personalised pathways for guidance, learning and professional preparation. AppLI helps users define goals, identify learning needs, access regional, digital and AI training opportunities, undertake microlearning, prepare and edit CVs and cover letters, and practise for interviews. Citizens access the service securely through Italy’s national digital identity schemes and interact through a conversational interface. Operational nationwide since September 2025, AppLI is free and voluntary and works alongside Public Employment Services, combining public governance, user control and continuous support for skills development.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2756,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02109,EU_PSTW_202609_02109,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""social_nonprofit"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,TildeAI,R&D economic affairs,UNKNOWN,TildeOpen LLM – AI solution for European languages,TildeAI; Consortium; R&D economic affairs; Latvia,"The implementation uses European supercomputing resources to train a model with more than 30 billion parameters, with particular attention to Baltic languages, Ukrainian, Turkish and other languages that are less well covered by dominant models. The use case is significant because it treats language diversity as a strategic AI infrastructure issue.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"European public administrations, businesses and researchers need language technologies that work reliably in smaller and under-represented European languages, not only in English or other high-resource languages. TildeOpen LLM addresses this gap by providing an open-source large language model specialised for European languages. The operational problem is that many commercial LLMs are trained primarily on English-language data and may produce weaker grammar, awkward syntax or inaccurate terminology in smaller languages. The implementation uses European supercomputing resources to train a model with more than 30 billion parameters, with particular attention to Baltic languages, Ukrainian, Turkish and other languages that are less well covered by dominant models. At a conceptual level, TildeOpen provides a foundation model that can be fine-tuned or customised for specific organisational tasks, including multilingual assistants, public-service support, translation workflows or sector-specific language applications. The expected impact is stronger linguistic inclusion, greater European data sovereignty, more secure deployment options and wider access to generative AI for administrations and organisations that require local or EU-compliant hosting. The use case is significant because it treats language diversity as a strategic AI infrastructure issue.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2757,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02110,EU_PSTW_202609_02110,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,University of Applied Arts Vienna,Tertiary education.,UNKNOWN,Flynn - first AI-art university student,University of Applied Arts Vienna; Academic-Research; Tertiary education.; Austria,"The operational purpose of the project is not to automate art education, but to use the university environment as a live setting for examining authorship, creativity, collaboration and the limits of machine agency. Flynn was developed using existing large language models and open-source image-generation tools, including Claude Sonnet and a Stable Diffusion model, and participates in classes through a laptop or tablet.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Art and design schools are increasingly confronted with a practical and conceptual question: whether generative AI should be treated only as a tool used by human students, or whether AI systems can themselves become participants in artistic education. Flynn addresses this question through an experimental enrolment at the University of Applied Arts Vienna, where a non-human AI agent successfully passed the same admission process as human applicants, including portfolio submission, interviews and an artistic suitability test. The operational purpose of the project is not to automate art education, but to use the university environment as a live setting for examining authorship, creativity, collaboration and the limits of machine agency. Flynn was developed using existing large language models and open-source image-generation tools, including Claude Sonnet and a Stable Diffusion model, and participates in classes through a laptop or tablet. At a conceptual level, the system listens to lectures and interactions, processes them as part of its learning environment, keeps diary entries and produces visual work that responds to its experiences. The expected value is primarily educational, artistic and critical: Flynn allows students, professors and audiences to engage directly with the role of AI agents in creative processes, while testing whether AI can become a collaborator, medium or institutional actor within higher education. The case is significant because it does not present AI as a simple productivity tool, but as an object of pedagogical, social and aesthetic inquiry.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2758,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02111,EU_PSTW_202609_02111,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Government of Luxembourg,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.","Legal systems are difficult for citizens and even professionals to navigate when legislative texts are dispersed, technically written and frequently amended. Luxembourg’s planned language model trained on national legislation addresses this challenge by making the country’s legal framework easier to search, interpret and understand.",Comprehensive language model specifically trained on Luxembourg legislation,"Government of Luxembourg; Central-Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Luxembourg",The case is significant because it applies generative AI to legal accessibility while explicitly retaining human oversight for decisions affecting citizens.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Legal systems are difficult for citizens and even professionals to navigate when legislative texts are dispersed, technically written and frequently amended. Luxembourg’s planned language model trained on national legislation addresses this challenge by making the country’s legal framework easier to search, interpret and understand. The operational problem is twofold. Legal professionals such as judges, lawyers, public officials and jurists need faster ways to interrogate legislative materials, while citizens need more accessible explanations of rights, obligations and procedures written in language that is easier to understand than formal legal drafting. The initiative described in Luxembourg’s public-sector AI strategy context focuses on developing a large language model specifically trained on Luxembourgish legislation. At a conceptual level, the model would act as a legal-information assistant: users could ask questions in natural language and receive responses grounded in national legal texts, helping them identify relevant provisions or understand the structure of a legal issue. The source stresses that Luxembourg’s approach to AI in the public sector is human-centred and does not aim to let AI decide on matters such as subsidies or social-programme access. Instead, AI is framed as decision support and information support that makes processes more transparent and comprehensible. The expected impact is more efficient legal research, improved access to law, greater transparency of public rules and stronger digital sovereignty through locally adapted AI tools. The case is significant because it applies generative AI to legal accessibility while explicitly retaining human oversight for decisions affecting citizens.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2759,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02112,EU_PSTW_202609_02112,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Government of Luxembourg,Education not definable by level.,UNKNOWN,AI in Education in Luxembourg,Government of Luxembourg; Central-Government; Education not definable by level.; Luxembourg,"Luxembourg is deploying artificial intelligence to support personalised learning in the education system, creating tools that help pupils learn more effectively while also teaching them how to use AI responsibly.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Luxembourg is deploying artificial intelligence to support personalised learning in the education system, creating tools that help pupils learn more effectively while also teaching them how to use AI responsibly. The initiative seeks to provide tailored guidance to students, accelerate their progress and ensure that all applications respect strict data-protection standards so that children’s personal information remains secure.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2760,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02113,EU_PSTW_202609_02113,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Government of Luxembourg,Cultural services.,"The operational challenge is that high-performing language and speech models need large quantities of high-quality text and audio data, but Luxembourgish has fewer publicly available resources than global languages.",High-performing multilingual model that includes Luxembourgish in both written and spoken form,Government of Luxembourg; Central-Government; Cultural services.; Luxembourg,"The case is significant because it recognises that AI infrastructure is also cultural infrastructure: if a language is not represented in models, it becomes harder to use in modern digital environments. The available source frames the project as ongoing development rather than a mature deployed service.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Many smaller or less widely spoken languages are under-represented in modern AI systems, which can reduce digital inclusion and weaken the presence of national languages in public services, speech interfaces and language technologies. Luxembourg’s multilingual language and speech model addresses this problem by aiming to include Luxembourgish at a level comparable to better-supported languages such as English and French. The operational challenge is that high-performing language and speech models need large quantities of high-quality text and audio data, but Luxembourgish has fewer publicly available resources than global languages. The project described in Luxembourg’s public-sector AI strategy involves collecting local audio sources, including voice samples from television, to train systems capable of recognising and generating Luxembourgish in both written and spoken form. At a conceptual level, the initiative is about linguistic digital sovereignty: the national language should not be absent from AI-enabled search, voice assistants, transcription, translation or public-service tools simply because commercial models focus on larger markets. The model could support public administrations, educational services, accessibility tools, speech recognition, multilingual communication and citizen-facing digital services. The expected impact is stronger inclusion of Luxembourgish speakers, better language coverage in digital government, improved speech-to-text and text-generation capabilities, and preservation of linguistic diversity in the age of AI. The case is significant because it recognises that AI infrastructure is also cultural infrastructure: if a language is not represented in models, it becomes harder to use in modern digital environments. The available source frames the project as ongoing development rather than a mature deployed service.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI|S3_DOCUMENT_INTELLIGENCE,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2761,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02114,EU_PSTW_202609_02114,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Government of Luxembourg,R&D economic affairs,UNKNOWN,MeluXina-AI: High-performance supercomputer to test AI applications on a large scale,Government of Luxembourg; Central-Government; R&D economic affairs; Luxembourg,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Advanced AI development requires infrastructure capable of training, testing and deploying large models under secure, sovereign and high-performance conditions. Luxembourg’s MeluXina-AI initiative addresses this requirement by creating an AI-optimised supercomputer and an associated Luxembourg AI Factory. The operational problem is that start-ups, SMEs, researchers and public-sector innovators often need computing capacity for AI and data-intensive workloads that exceeds ordinary cloud or local infrastructure, while sensitive data and intellectual property require secure environments. MeluXina-AI will be hosted and operated by LuxProvide as part of the Luxembourg AI Factory, with Luxinnovation coordinating AI Factory activities and a consortium including the Luxembourg National Data Service, the University of Luxembourg and the Luxembourg Institute of Science and Technology. The new system will support training, testing and pre-production deployment of advanced AI models, with a software ecosystem designed for AI users from start-ups to researchers and industry. At a conceptual level, the infrastructure combines supercomputing, sovereign cloud capabilities, multi-tenant isolation, data bridges and GPU-as-a-Service delivery. It will be installed across the Bissen and Bettembourg sites and is expected to deliver an estimated AI peak performance of more than 20 exaflops. The expected impact is stronger Luxembourg and European AI capacity, better support for trustworthy AI development, improved access for SMEs and researchers, and closer integration into the EuroHPC AI Factory network. The case is significant because it treats AI compute as strategic public infrastructure, not just a technical resource.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2762,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02115,EU_PSTW_202609_02115,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Sofia Tech Park,R&D economic affairs,It supports users that need large-scale computing but cannot rely on ordinary institutional servers or commercial cloud services alone.,Discoverer+ Supercomputer,Sofia Tech Park; Academic-Research; R&D economic affairs; Bulgaria,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Scientific research, industrial innovation and public-sector digital transformation increasingly depend on access to high-performance computing infrastructure capable of processing large simulations, data-intensive workloads and artificial-intelligence models. Discoverer+ strengthens Bulgaria’s role in the EuroHPC ecosystem by upgrading the original Discoverer petascale supercomputer hosted at Sofia Tech Park. The operational problem is that scientific communities and businesses need more powerful and AI-ready infrastructure, particularly for workloads such as neural-network training, complex modelling, large-scale data analysis, medicine, astronomy and materials science. The upgrade introduces GPU partitioning for data-intensive operations, expands memory capacity and improves resilience through an uninterruptible power-supply system. At a conceptual level, Discoverer+ acts as an enhanced national and European computing platform that combines conventional high-performance computing with stronger AI-oriented capabilities. It supports users that need large-scale computing but cannot rely on ordinary institutional servers or commercial cloud services alone. The expected impact is broader access to advanced computing for researchers, SMEs and public-sector users, stronger Bulgarian participation in European AI and HPC initiatives, and improved capacity to support large scientific and industrial projects. The case is relevant because it shows that AI adoption depends not only on algorithms and data, but also on strategic computational infrastructure that can be shared across the research and innovation ecosystem.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2763,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02116,EU_PSTW_202609_02116,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Vilnius University,R&D economic affairs,"The operational problem is that AI adoption across public services, cybersecurity, personalised medicine, industrial automation, green transformation, energy and transport requires coordinated infrastructure rather than fragmented project-level experimentation.",LitAI - Lithuania AI Factory,Vilnius University; Academic-Research; R&D economic affairs; Lithuania,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"National AI ecosystems need shared infrastructure, data access and technical expertise if companies, researchers and public institutions are to move from experimentation to reliable deployment. Lithuania’s LitAI Artificial Intelligence Factory is intended to become a national competence and technology centre bringing together high-performance computing, data resources, scientific research and specialised expertise. The operational problem is that AI adoption across public services, cybersecurity, personalised medicine, industrial automation, green transformation, energy and transport requires coordinated infrastructure rather than fragmented project-level experimentation. LitAI will operate as a one-stop platform providing computing infrastructure together with technical and research-based consulting services, enabling the public and private sectors to develop, test and implement reliable AI solutions more quickly. The initiative will be coordinated by Vilnius University, with partners including Kaunas University of Technology, VILNIUS TECH, Vytautas Magnus University, the State Data Agency, the Lithuanian Radio and Television Centre and the Innovation Agency. At a conceptual level, LitAI links advanced computing capacity, applied research, data environments and practical support services into a national AI innovation hub. Its implementation will be connected to the LRTC VDC3 Third State Data Centre, and the estimated project value is around EUR 130 million, most of which is dedicated to high-performance computing infrastructure. The expected impact is stronger scientific capacity, faster AI adoption in strategic sectors, more than 100 high-skilled jobs and improved Lithuanian competitiveness within Europe’s AI and HPC ecosystem. The case is significant because it positions AI as national innovation infrastructure shared across academia, industry and government.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2764,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02117,EU_PSTW_202609_02117,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Vilnius City Municipality,General public services n.e.c.,"City services require timely information on infrastructure, cleanliness, mobility, waste, weather impacts and public-space conditions, but municipal staff often work with fragmented datasets and manual inspections. The operational problem is that city managers need to know, for example, whether streets have been cleared after snow, whether waste bins are full, where maintenance is required, and how mobility patterns are changing, but field checks alone are slow and resource-intensive.",Dìgital Twin in the City of Vilnius,Vilnius City Municipality; Local Government; General public services n.e.c.; Lithuania,The implementation uses drone flights to collect imagery that is analysed by AI models and displayed in maps within the city’s digital twin.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"City services require timely information on infrastructure, cleanliness, mobility, waste, weather impacts and public-space conditions, but municipal staff often work with fragmented datasets and manual inspections. Vilnius has developed a digital-twin approach that combines GIS, drone imagery and artificial intelligence to monitor city functions and support operational decisions. The operational problem is that city managers need to know, for example, whether streets have been cleared after snow, whether waste bins are full, where maintenance is required, and how mobility patterns are changing, but field checks alone are slow and resource-intensive. The implementation uses drone flights to collect imagery that is analysed by AI models and displayed in maps within the city’s digital twin. For example, streets can be classified according to whether snow has been cleared, while municipal trash receptacles can be highlighted if they need emptying. At a conceptual level, the digital twin is not only a 3D planning tool but an operational awareness platform, combining real-time or near-real-time geospatial information with service-management workflows. The expected impact is faster prioritisation of municipal tasks, better transparency for residents, improved evidence for public-transport planning and more efficient maintenance of the built environment. The case is significant because Vilnius extends the digital-twin model beyond long-term urban planning into everyday service delivery and municipal accountability.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK|F07_DATA_VISIBILITY_INTEGRATION_GAP,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2765,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02118,EU_PSTW_202609_02118,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""social_nonprofit""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Translated,R&D economic affairs,"Current artificial-intelligence systems remain strong at processing digital representations such as text, images and video, but they are still limited when they need to understand real-world context across several sensory channels at once. The operational challenge is that many high-value applications require contextual awareness rather than isolated signal processing: simultaneous translation in a noisy environment requires knowing who is speaking; cardiology applications may require linking imaging data with physiological patterns; disaster-management tools may need to combine satellite, drone and environmental signals.",DVPS ('Diversibus Viis Plurima Solvo') - creating a new generation of artificial intelligence models,Translated; Consortium; R&D economic affairs; Italy,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Current artificial-intelligence systems remain strong at processing digital representations such as text, images and video, but they are still limited when they need to understand real-world context across several sensory channels at once. DVPS, or “Diversibus Viis Plurima Solvo”, addresses this limitation by developing multimodal foundation models that combine linguistic, visual, auditory and sensory inputs to build more grounded representations of the physical world. The operational challenge is that many high-value applications require contextual awareness rather than isolated signal processing: simultaneous translation in a noisy environment requires knowing who is speaking; cardiology applications may require linking imaging data with physiological patterns; disaster-management tools may need to combine satellite, drone and environmental signals. DVPS therefore focuses on models that can learn across modalities, transfer knowledge between domains and adapt more efficiently when labelled data are limited. The project will develop AutoDVPS, an open-source toolkit for multimodal foundation-model design, pre-training, fine-tuning and modality expansion, as well as DVPSBench, a benchmarking suite for assessing performance, robustness and ethical implications. The expected impact is to strengthen Europe’s capacity in next-generation AI by producing reusable methods, open assets and demonstrators in language communication, cardiology and geo-intelligence. The case is relevant because it goes beyond language-model deployment and targets foundational AI capabilities for real-world interaction. Translated announced the initiative with EUR 29 million in backing and 20 partners across nine countries, while CORDIS lists the Horizon Europe grant agreement as running from 1 July 2025 to 30 June 2029.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2766,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02119,EU_PSTW_202609_02119,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Lithuanian Post,General public services n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,Mantas – chatbot created by Lithuanian Post together with Tilde,Lithuanian Post; Central-Government; General public services n.e.c.; Lithuania,"The chatbot uses natural-language understanding to interpret freely formulated Lithuanian-language questions, including stylistic variation and imperfect grammar, and provides answers based on the knowledge base prepared with Lithuanian Post. The case is important because it shows how a national service provider can use language technology in a practical, high-volume public-service context, where the value lies less in spectacular AI capabilities and more in dependable handling of everyday citizen and customer enquiries.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Postal operators handle a high volume of repetitive customer questions about parcels, delivery status, sending rules, opening hours, service conditions and problem resolution. Mantas was developed by Lithuanian Post with Tilde to improve digital customer service and reduce pressure on human consultants. The operational problem is that customers increasingly expect immediate online support, while written enquiries can accumulate rapidly and require staff to answer similar questions repeatedly. The chatbot uses natural-language understanding to interpret freely formulated Lithuanian-language questions, including stylistic variation and imperfect grammar, and provides answers based on the knowledge base prepared with Lithuanian Post. At a conceptual level, Mantas functions as a first-line customer-service layer: it receives incoming requests, understands the user’s intent, supplies standard information and allows human staff to focus on cases requiring judgement or escalation. The intended impact is shorter response time, more consistent answers, greater 24/7 accessibility and reduced workload for the contact centre. The case is important because it shows how a national service provider can use language technology in a practical, high-volume public-service context, where the value lies less in spectacular AI capabilities and more in dependable handling of everyday citizen and customer enquiries. Continued development of the chatbot also reflects the need to keep responses aligned with changing postal services and user expectations.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2767,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02120,EU_PSTW_202609_02120,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Central Electoral Commission of Lithuania (VRK),General public services n.e.c.,The VRK chatbot was developed for Lithuania’s Central Electoral Commission to provide AI-supported election information while reducing the operational burden on officials.,VRK chatbot: AI-powered support during the elections in Lithuania,Central Electoral Commission of Lithuania (VRK); Central-Government; General public services n.e.c.; Lithuania,"The case is significant because it applies chatbot technology in a democratic process where reliability, neutrality and auditability are particularly important.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Election administrations face intense peaks in public communication before and during voting periods, when voters, candidates, parties, campaigners and local election commissions all require accurate and timely answers. The VRK chatbot was developed for Lithuania’s Central Electoral Commission to provide AI-supported election information while reducing the operational burden on officials. The operational problem is that electoral information is highly sensitive: inaccurate guidance can undermine trust, confuse voters or create legal risks, while staff must also manage organisational work during election periods. The chatbot was trained in close collaboration with VRK, with attention to accuracy, cybersecurity and the risks of misleading answers. At a conceptual level, the assistant acts as a controlled conversational gateway to official election knowledge, answering frequently asked questions, routing users towards relevant information and operating continuously during periods when human staff may be unavailable or overloaded. The expected impact is greater accessibility of election guidance, faster responses to routine queries, reduced workload on VRK employees and improved consistency in public communication. The case is significant because it applies chatbot technology in a democratic process where reliability, neutrality and auditability are particularly important. It also illustrates why public-sector AI tools require domain-specific governance: election information is not simply customer support, but part of the institutional infrastructure of democratic participation.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2768,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02121,EU_PSTW_202609_02121,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,University of Latvia,Education n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,Digne – AI chatbot with LLM for academic communication and learning processes,University of Latvia; Academic-Research; Education n.e.c.; Lithuania,"Digne is an AI-powered virtual assistant developed by Tilde in cooperation with Latvian universities and businesses to support the mandatory “Civil Protection” course in Latvian higher education. Digne is integrated with Moodle, uses user authentication and combines guided dialogues with large-language-model functionality.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Universities need to support students with course information, study progress, exam preparation and learning resources, while academic staff face growing administrative and communication workloads. Digne is an AI-powered virtual assistant developed by Tilde in cooperation with Latvian universities and businesses to support the mandatory “Civil Protection” course in Latvian higher education. The operational problem is that students often ask recurring questions about grades, progress, course content and materials, while staff must respond to individual queries and prepare reports alongside teaching duties. Digne is integrated with Moodle, uses user authentication and combines guided dialogues with large-language-model functionality. At a conceptual level, the chatbot draws on course materials, lecture content, presentations, videos and instructions to answer open-ended questions when no predefined response exists. It can therefore act both as an administrative assistant and as a study-support tool, helping students find resources, review course material and prepare for examinations. The expected impact is improved learning efficiency, easier access to course-specific information, reduced repetitive workload for academic staff and a more personalised digital learning environment. The case is important because it moves beyond a generic chatbot model: Digne is embedded in a learning-management system and trained on real course content, making it a domain-specific assistant for higher education. It also demonstrates how LLM-based tools can support teaching when combined with institutional authentication, structured content and human academic oversight.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2769,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02122,EU_PSTW_202609_02122,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Rīgas namu pārvaldnieks (RNP),Housing and community amenities n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,Rīgas namu pārvaldnieks’ Juris – the first chatbot in the property management field,Rīgas namu pārvaldnieks (RNP); Local Government; Housing and community amenities n.e.c.; Lithuania,"Rīgas namu pārvaldnieks introduced Juris, an AI-powered virtual assistant developed with Tilde, to modernise customer communication and support residents across multiple digital channels. The case is significant because it applies conversational AI to municipal-property management, an area where citizen-facing service interactions are frequent, practical and often time-sensitive.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Large property-management organisations receive substantial volumes of recurring questions about payments, maintenance, ownership changes, emergency reports and building-management decisions. Rīgas namu pārvaldnieks introduced Juris, an AI-powered virtual assistant developed with Tilde, to modernise customer communication and support residents across multiple digital channels. The operational problem is that RNP serves around 160,000 customers and manages approximately 4,000 apartment buildings, creating a heavy and often repetitive workload for customer-service staff. Juris is available on the RNP website, customer self-service portal and Facebook, providing support 24 hours a day and seven days a week. At a conceptual level, the chatbot acts as a conversational front office for property-management services: it answers routine questions, guides users through common procedures, registers emergency notifications and can support further contact with the customer-service centre. More advanced functionality includes customer recognition after login, personalised support linked to a specific property or invoice data, bilingual understanding in Latvian and English, and image attachment functionality for reporting issues. The expected impact is faster access to information, reduced email and phone pressure, better support during peak communication periods such as storms or technical breakdowns, and more convenient digital engagement for residents. The case is significant because it applies conversational AI to municipal-property management, an area where citizen-facing service interactions are frequent, practical and often time-sensitive.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2770,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02123,EU_PSTW_202609_02123,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City Hall of Sector 3 in Bucharest,General public services n.e.c.,"Local administrations receive frequent citizen questions about services, documents, procedures and contact channels, but municipal websites can be difficult to navigate and staff capacity is limited outside office hours. The operational problem is that citizens often need quick answers to administrative questions, while traditional communication channels such as email, social media or in-person offices can be slower or less convenient.",Sector 3 City Hall's AI-powered chat system,City Hall of Sector 3 in Bucharest; Local Government; General public services n.e.c.; Romania,Sector 3 City Hall in Bucharest introduced an AI-powered chat system on its official website to make municipal information more accessible and to streamline direct communication with residents.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Local administrations receive frequent citizen questions about services, documents, procedures and contact channels, but municipal websites can be difficult to navigate and staff capacity is limited outside office hours. Sector 3 City Hall in Bucharest introduced an AI-powered chat system on its official website to make municipal information more accessible and to streamline direct communication with residents. The operational problem is that citizens often need quick answers to administrative questions, while traditional communication channels such as email, social media or in-person offices can be slower or less convenient. The chatbot is designed to provide 24/7 assistance, offering rapid responses to information requests and guiding citizens towards relevant municipal services. At a conceptual level, the system functions as a conversational entry point to the local administration: users ask questions in ordinary language and the AI system learns progressively from interactions, improving its ability to respond over time. The expected impact is faster access to municipal information, reduced pressure on front-office channels, improved citizen experience and stronger visibility of digital innovation within local government. The case is relevant because it shows how even a city-district administration can introduce AI as part of everyday public-service communication. At the same time, because the municipality itself described the tool as being in a learning phase, the system should be understood as an incremental digital-service improvement rather than a fully mature automated administrative decision system.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2771,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02124,EU_PSTW_202609_02124,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Galicea Commune,General public services o.a.l.,"Small municipalities and communes often face the same citizen-service expectations as larger administrations, but with fewer staff and more limited digital capacity. The operational problem is that residents may need quick guidance on local services, administrative procedures, contact points or municipal information, while smaller public administrations may struggle to maintain extensive digital helpdesks or call-centre-style support.",Galicea Commune's Chatbot,Galicea Commune; Local Government; General public services o.a.l.; Romania,"At a conceptual level, AI Galicea appears to function as a citizen-facing virtual assistant, using conversational technology to improve access to municipal information.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Small municipalities and communes often face the same citizen-service expectations as larger administrations, but with fewer staff and more limited digital capacity. Galicea Commune’s “AI Galicea” chatbot is cited as an example of Romanian local government adopting conversational AI at commune level to provide citizens with information and support. The operational problem is that residents may need quick guidance on local services, administrative procedures, contact points or municipal information, while smaller public administrations may struggle to maintain extensive digital helpdesks or call-centre-style support. The chatbot model offers a lightweight way to expand availability, allowing citizens to ask questions digitally and receive basic guidance outside traditional office interactions. At a conceptual level, AI Galicea appears to function as a citizen-facing virtual assistant, using conversational technology to improve access to municipal information. The expected impact is improved responsiveness, reduced routine workload for local staff, and broader diffusion of AI-enabled public-service tools beyond large cities. The case is relevant because it shows that public-sector AI adoption is not confined to national ministries or metropolitan administrations; smaller local authorities can also experiment with digital assistants. However, the available evidence is limited. The cited source identifies AI Galicea as an advanced chatbot available to citizens of Galicea, but it does not provide detailed information on technical architecture, usage volumes, procurement arrangements or integration with specific municipal systems. The entry should therefore be framed as an early-adoption example with limited public documentation.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2772,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02125,EU_PSTW_202609_02125,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Simula Research Laboratory,Transport,"AI4CCAM addresses the problem that automated-vehicle AI cannot be evaluated only on technical performance: it must also be explainable, robust, unbiased, secure and acceptable to users, pedestrians, cyclists and other road actors.","AI4CCAM - Trustworthy AI for Connected, Cooperative and Automated Mobility",Simula Research Laboratory; Academic-Research; Transport; European Union,"At a technical level, AI4CCAM works on AI-based scenario management, pedestrian and cyclist behaviour anticipation using elements such as visual-gaze estimation, explainable ego-car trajectory prediction and simulation of ethical dilemmas. The project also uses generative adversarial networks and metamorphic testing techniques to multiply and stress-test scenarios.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Connected, cooperative and automated mobility depends increasingly on AI systems that must interpret complex road environments, anticipate the behaviour of vulnerable road users and support safe vehicle decision-making. AI4CCAM addresses the problem that automated-vehicle AI cannot be evaluated only on technical performance: it must also be explainable, robust, unbiased, secure and acceptable to users, pedestrians, cyclists and other road actors. The project develops an open environment for integrating trustworthy-by-design AI models into CCAM scenarios, with particular attention to vulnerable road-user behaviour anticipation in urban traffic conditions. At a technical level, AI4CCAM works on AI-based scenario management, pedestrian and cyclist behaviour anticipation using elements such as visual-gaze estimation, explainable ego-car trajectory prediction and simulation of ethical dilemmas. The project also uses generative adversarial networks and metamorphic testing techniques to multiply and stress-test scenarios. At a governance level, it includes a participatory space where stakeholders and the public can explore risks, biases, cybersecurity issues and ethical questions related to automated driving. The expected impact is safer and more socially acceptable deployment of AI in automated mobility, supported by validated use cases and evidence on user acceptance. The case is significant because it treats AI for automated vehicles as a socio-technical system: the model must not only predict movement, but also operate transparently in environments where people need to trust and understand automated behaviour.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2773,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02126,EU_PSTW_202609_02126,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Var Department in Southern France,General public services n.e.c.,"The operational problem is that the Var Departmental Council employs around 5,000 staff, and HR teams receive many repetitive requests that are already answered somewhere in internal documentation but are difficult for employees to find.",Transforming Public Services with AI in the Var Department,Var Department in Southern France; Regional Government; General public services n.e.c.; France,"The clearest operational example is the HR chatbot deployed on the Department’s intranet, which responds to recurring employee questions on leave, part-time arrangements, career progression, compensation schemes, workplace accidents, sick leave and retirement. The implementation uses a conversational assistant accessible 24/7, developed from frequently asked questions and organised around guided paths for key events in an employee’s career.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Regional administrations manage complex portfolios of services for citizens, employees, social policy, territorial planning and local development, which creates many opportunities for targeted AI support. The Var Department has been developing an AI-oriented approach to public-service efficiency, with documented activity around internal conversational assistance and wider workshops on administrative simplification. The clearest operational example is the HR chatbot deployed on the Department’s intranet, which responds to recurring employee questions on leave, part-time arrangements, career progression, compensation schemes, workplace accidents, sick leave and retirement. The operational problem is that the Var Departmental Council employs around 5,000 staff, and HR teams receive many repetitive requests that are already answered somewhere in internal documentation but are difficult for employees to find. The implementation uses a conversational assistant accessible 24/7, developed from frequently asked questions and organised around guided paths for key events in an employee’s career. At a conceptual level, the system transforms internal rules and HR knowledge into a navigable dialogue, helping employees find information in seconds while allowing HR staff to focus on more complex cases. Beyond the HR chatbot, the Department has also organised AI workshops with public and private partners to identify locally testable use cases for administrative simplification, services for users, planning, environment and support for vulnerable people. The expected impact is reduced internal workload, faster employee support, more structured experimentation with AI and a stronger basis for responsible deployment across departmental services.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2774,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02127,EU_PSTW_202609_02127,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Athens,General public services n.e.c.,"Municipal websites often contain large amounts of information, but residents and visitors may still struggle to identify the correct service, procedure or contact point quickly. The operational objective is to make access to municipal information faster, more intuitive and available at any time, reducing dependence on manual navigation or direct staff assistance for routine queries.",AI Chatbot in the City of Athens,City of Athens; Local Government; General public services n.e.c.; Greece,"The City of Athens AI chatbot addresses this problem by adding an AI-powered search engine and digital agent to the municipality’s official website. The implementation was developed by Mindseed on behalf of DAEM and the Municipality of Athens, in collaboration with Google, and uses Gemini AI integrated into the WordPress-based municipal website.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Municipal websites often contain large amounts of information, but residents and visitors may still struggle to identify the correct service, procedure or contact point quickly. The City of Athens AI chatbot addresses this problem by adding an AI-powered search engine and digital agent to the municipality’s official website. The operational objective is to make access to municipal information faster, more intuitive and available at any time, reducing dependence on manual navigation or direct staff assistance for routine queries. The implementation was developed by Mindseed on behalf of DAEM and the Municipality of Athens, in collaboration with Google, and uses Gemini AI integrated into the WordPress-based municipal website. The system allows users to ask questions about city services in natural language and receive immediate answers, while the AI digital agent provides an interactive conversational interface available 24/7. From a technical and governance perspective, the solution uses Google Cloud, BigQuery and Looker Studio to support logging, storage and visualisation of anonymised questions, answers and errors. At a conceptual level, the chatbot turns a static municipal website into a responsive service gateway: users no longer need to know the administrative structure of the municipality before asking for help. The expected impact is faster information retrieval, improved citizen and visitor experience, lower administrative workload for routine enquiries and better continuous improvement through analysis of anonymised interaction logs. The case is relevant because it shows how generative AI can be embedded into local-government websites as a practical interface layer for everyday public-service access.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2775,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02128,EU_PSTW_202609_02128,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Inria,Family and children.,"The operational challenge is subtle: anonymity is not achieved simply by removing a name, because free-text reports can contain clues about a pupil’s class, schedule, relationship network, writing style, location or personal circumstances.",A chatbot to protect the identities of those reporting school bullying,Inria; Academic-Research; Family and children.; France,"The case is relevant because it applies AI not to surveillance or discipline, but to safer reporting and privacy-preserving child protection.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"School bullying is often under-reported because victims and witnesses fear reprisals, social exposure or being identified indirectly through the details of their report. The Petscraft project led by Inria addresses this problem by exploring an AI-based chatbot capable of receiving reports while protecting the identity of the person submitting them. The operational challenge is subtle: anonymity is not achieved simply by removing a name, because free-text reports can contain clues about a pupil’s class, schedule, relationship network, writing style, location or personal circumstances. A large language model might also infer personal traits or identity markers from apparently harmless text. The project therefore focuses on privacy-enhancing technologies that can filter, rewrite or transform the report so that the school receives useful information about the incident while reducing the risk that the reporter can be identified. The approach is first tested on public datasets before being applied to sensitive bullying-reporting material, which is important given the vulnerability of minors and the seriousness of the information involved. At a conceptual level, the chatbot acts as an anonymisation layer between the reporter and the institution: it encourages disclosure, protects the person raising the alert and helps schools detect and respond to bullying more effectively. The case is relevant because it applies AI not to surveillance or discipline, but to safer reporting and privacy-preserving child protection. Petscraft itself is an Inria project team created with INSA Centre Val de Loire and the University of Orléans, focused on privacy-enhancing technologies.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2776,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02129,EU_PSTW_202609_02129,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Ministry of Digital Governance,General services.,UNKNOWN,mAIgov - AI-powered digital assistant of Greece's unified public administration portal,Ministry of Digital Governance; Central-Government; General services.; Greece,"National digital-government portals can still be difficult for citizens to use when services are spread across many administrative categories, legal procedures and institutional responsibilities. Greece’s mAIgov addresses this problem by integrating an AI-powered digital assistant into gov.gr, the country’s unified public-administration portal.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"National digital-government portals can still be difficult for citizens to use when services are spread across many administrative categories, legal procedures and institutional responsibilities. Greece’s mAIgov addresses this problem by integrating an AI-powered digital assistant into gov.gr, the country’s unified public-administration portal. The operational objective is to simplify access to public services by allowing citizens to ask questions in natural language, rather than searching only through predefined keywords or navigating complex service menus. The assistant is positioned as a conversational layer above the existing gov.gr service infrastructure, helping users identify the relevant public service, understand the steps involved and move towards the correct digital procedure. The system is available directly on the gov.gr homepage, where users can ask the digital assistant questions; the portal currently presents the assistant as a beta service and reports more than 3.9 million dialogues. At a conceptual level, mAIgov supports a shift from document-centred e-government to conversational public-service navigation: the citizen expresses a need, and the system interprets it in relation to existing services. The expected impact is easier access to digital public services, lower search friction, improved inclusion for users unfamiliar with administrative terminology and reduced pressure on traditional support channels. The case is significant because it applies generative AI at national scale within a core government portal, where reliability, transparency and public trust are essential.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2777,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02130,EU_PSTW_202609_02130,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Government of Slovenia,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.","Public administrations increasingly need to base decisions on integrated, timely and comparable data rather than fragmented spreadsheets or isolated departmental reports. The operational problem is that public-sector data are often distributed across different authorities and systems, making it difficult to produce a consistent “single source of truth” for decision-making.",Use of AI in Business Intelligence System - Skrinja,"Government of Slovenia; Central-Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Slovenia",UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Public administrations increasingly need to base decisions on integrated, timely and comparable data rather than fragmented spreadsheets or isolated departmental reports. Slovenia’s Skrinja platform provides a central business-intelligence environment for public authorities, supporting analysis of policy areas such as public-sector salaries, public procurement and administrative procedures. The operational problem is that public-sector data are often distributed across different authorities and systems, making it difficult to produce a consistent “single source of truth” for decision-making. Skrinja already serves as a shared analytics platform, allowing authorities to generate dashboards, visualisations and structured reports. The planned AI extension aims to move the system beyond descriptive reporting towards predictive analytics, scenario analysis and forward-looking insights. At a conceptual level, the system combines public-administration data warehousing with business intelligence and AI-supported forecasting, helping officials understand current patterns and anticipate future developments. This can support more responsive policy design, better oversight of administrative processes, more accurate resource planning and earlier detection of anomalies or emerging pressures. The expected impact is stronger data-driven governance, reduced manual reporting, improved transparency and more coherent evidence across the Slovenian public sector. The case is important because it treats AI not as a stand-alone chatbot or model, but as an analytical capability embedded into a wider public-sector data infrastructure. Its value depends on data quality, interoperability, privacy safeguards and the ability of public officials to interpret predictive outputs responsibly.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI|S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2778,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02131,EU_PSTW_202609_02131,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Digital Austria,General services.,"The EU AI Act creates new obligations for developers, deployers, public institutions and businesses, but its legal and technical requirements can be difficult to interpret for non-specialists. Austria’s AI Act Chatbot, provided through Digital Austria and the KI-Servicestelle, addresses this challenge by offering an accessible tool for asking questions about the AI Act and related national guidance.",AI Act Chatbot,Digital Austria; Central-Government; General services.; Austria,"The operational problem is that organisations need practical regulatory orientation, while legal texts and technical documentation are often too complex for quick operational use. The chatbot uses relevant expert documents, including the EU AI Act and information from the Austrian AI Service Desk, to prepare understandable answers.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The EU AI Act creates new obligations for developers, deployers, public institutions and businesses, but its legal and technical requirements can be difficult to interpret for non-specialists. Austria’s AI Act Chatbot, provided through Digital Austria and the KI-Servicestelle, addresses this challenge by offering an accessible tool for asking questions about the AI Act and related national guidance. The operational problem is that organisations need practical regulatory orientation, while legal texts and technical documentation are often too complex for quick operational use. The chatbot uses relevant expert documents, including the EU AI Act and information from the Austrian AI Service Desk, to prepare understandable answers. What makes the use case particularly distinctive is its emphasis on transparency: the project explicitly exposes the internal functioning of the chatbot and shows the advantages and limitations of such systems. The published approach includes transparency from prompt to energy consumption, extensive legal and technical documentation, digital sovereignty through operation on RTR hardware, use of a European Mistral language model, and publication of source code and the knowledge base under open conditions. At a conceptual level, the chatbot is both a regulatory support service and a demonstrator of responsible public-sector AI implementation. The expected impact is lower barriers to understanding the AI Act, better informed compliance, improved trust in public AI services and reuse potential for other regulatory or advisory domains. The case is relevant because it makes transparency itself part of the service design, not merely a governance statement added afterwards.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2779,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02132,EU_PSTW_202609_02132,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Digital Austria,"Agriculture, forestry, fishing and hunting","agrifoodTEF addresses these challenges by providing testing and experimentation facilities where companies, start-ups, researchers and users can test AI and robotics solutions under real or realistic conditions.",agrifoodTEF - innovative AI and robotics applications for the Austrian agricultural and food industry,"Digital Austria; Central-Government; Agriculture, forestry, fishing and hunting; Austria",UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Agriculture and food production are facing simultaneous pressure from climate change, biodiversity loss, labour shortages, resource constraints, animal-health needs and increasing demands for sustainability. agrifoodTEF addresses these challenges by providing testing and experimentation facilities where companies, start-ups, researchers and users can test AI and robotics solutions under real or realistic conditions. The operational problem is that many AI and robotics tools for agriculture remain stuck between laboratory development and market adoption because they need validation in heterogeneous field, farm and food-production environments. agrifoodTEF provides physical and digital test environments that help developers assess whether their technologies work reliably in practice before wider commercial deployment. In Austria, the initiative creates national test environments for innovative AI and robotics applications tailored to the needs of the agricultural and food sectors. The Austrian consortium includes Josephinum Research, Raumberg-Gumpenstein Research & Development and the University of Applied Sciences Wiener Neustadt. At a conceptual level, agrifoodTEF is an innovation infrastructure rather than a single AI product: it helps validate tools for precision agriculture, food-quality monitoring, robotics, animal-health applications and resource optimisation. The expected impact is stronger collaboration between users, technology providers and research centres, improved competitiveness of the agri-food sector and more sustainable production processes. The case is relevant because it shows how public-sector innovation policy can support AI adoption by reducing the testing and validation gap between prototype and market readiness.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2780,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02133,EU_PSTW_202609_02133,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""social_nonprofit""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Transport Malta,Transport,"Without an integrated communication layer, it is difficult to provide interoperable mobility information, coordinate traffic management or prepare for more advanced forms of automated decision support.",Intelligent Transport Systems (ITS) - AI for urban mobility,Transport Malta; Central-Government; Transport; Malta,"Transport Malta describes the ITMS platform as part of an effort to improve the safety and efficiency of road-based transport systems by making better use of digital technologies and information services, while encouraging a shift to lower-emission transport modes. The platform is intended to integrate with existing intelligent transport systems and accommodate future systems deployed under Malta’s National Transport Strategy and Transport Master Plan.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Transport authorities need integrated data and digital infrastructure to manage congestion, public transport, road safety, emissions and multimodal travel information. Malta’s Integrated Transport Management Systems initiative addresses this need by developing an ITMS platform that will support the country’s National Access Point and interact with existing intelligent transport systems. The operational problem is that transport data are often distributed across traffic-management tools, travel-information services, infrastructure systems and policy-planning processes. Without an integrated communication layer, it is difficult to provide interoperable mobility information, coordinate traffic management or prepare for more advanced forms of automated decision support. Transport Malta describes the ITMS platform as part of an effort to improve the safety and efficiency of road-based transport systems by making better use of digital technologies and information services, while encouraging a shift to lower-emission transport modes. The platform is intended to integrate with existing intelligent transport systems and accommodate future systems deployed under Malta’s National Transport Strategy and Transport Master Plan. Importantly, the source frames AI as a future capability: the communication layer may later enable advanced computer analysis and automated decision support using AI to simulate, predict and plan traffic-management operations in a multimodal environment. The expected impact is better mobility-data interoperability, stronger traffic-management capability and a foundation for future AI-enabled planning. The case should therefore be presented as a digital transport infrastructure project with potential AI functionality, not as a confirmed operational AI deployment.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2781,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02134,EU_PSTW_202609_02134,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Cluj,General public services n.e.c.,"Local public administrations face increasing pressure to provide faster, more accessible and more responsive services while managing repetitive administrative procedures with limited staff capacity.",Virtual civil servant - Local public administration in Cluj,City of Cluj; Local Government; General public services n.e.c.; Romania,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Local public administrations face increasing pressure to provide faster, more accessible and more responsive services while managing repetitive administrative procedures with limited staff capacity. The Cluj virtual civil servant initiative explores how AI-based assistants can support the digitisation of local administrative processes and improve interactions between citizens and municipal services. The operational problem is that many citizen requests require basic information, document verification, routing to the correct office or guidance through procedures that can be repetitive for staff but confusing for users. An AI-based “virtual civil servant” can act as a first-line interface, helping citizens understand procedures, prepare requests and obtain information without immediately requiring direct human intervention. At the same time, it can support civil servants by reducing repetitive workloads and enabling staff to focus on more complex cases requiring judgement, legal interpretation or personal assistance. The OECD.AI entry states that the initiative began in Cluj as part of the digitisation of procedures and citizen services, before similar experimentation appeared in Galați. At a conceptual level, the Cluj model represents a conversational administrative support layer, not an autonomous public authority: the system can provide guidance and process support, while public officials remain responsible for formal decisions. The expected impact is shorter response times, better service accessibility, more efficient administrative workflows and increased user-friendliness in local government. The case is relevant because it shows how Romanian municipalities are experimenting with natural-language AI in everyday public governance rather than only in national digital strategies.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2782,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02135,EU_PSTW_202609_02135,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Galati City Hall,General public services n.e.c.,"Municipal service desks often receive recurring questions about planning, taxes, fees, local revenues and procedural requirements, creating pressure on front-office staff and delays for citizens.",Virtual civil servant - Galati City Hall,Galati City Hall; Local Government; General public services n.e.c.; Romania,"The operational challenge is that local public-service information can be fragmented across departments and written in technical or legal language, while citizens need immediate and understandable answers about what applies to their situation. It also shows that municipal AI adoption is still at an experimental stage, where training, testing and content validation are central before wider citizen-facing use.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Municipal service desks often receive recurring questions about planning, taxes, fees, local revenues and procedural requirements, creating pressure on front-office staff and delays for citizens. Galați City Hall’s virtual civil servant initiative addresses this problem by testing an AI-based assistant that can provide information to citizens on local administrative topics. The operational challenge is that local public-service information can be fragmented across departments and written in technical or legal language, while citizens need immediate and understandable answers about what applies to their situation. The Galați virtual assistant is intended to offer a conversational interface for common enquiries, particularly in areas such as town planning, taxes, fees and other local revenues. According to the OECD.AI entry, the assistant was launched after the Cluj initiative and was still being trained and tested at the time of reporting. At a conceptual level, the tool functions as an AI-supported public-information assistant: it can help users navigate administrative topics, but it should not be treated as making binding administrative decisions or replacing official case handling. The expected impact is easier access to municipal information, reduced workload for employees handling routine questions and faster guidance for citizens who otherwise need to search across web pages or contact offices directly. The case is relevant because it reflects the early diffusion of “virtual civil servant” models across Romanian local government. It also shows that municipal AI adoption is still at an experimental stage, where training, testing and content validation are central before wider citizen-facing use.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2783,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02136,EU_PSTW_202609_02136,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Agency for Administrative Modernisation,General public services n.e.c.,"Citizen complaints, suggestions and compliments are a critical source of feedback for improving public services, but paper-based or handwritten submissions can be slow to process, difficult to classify and harder to analyse systematically. The operational problem is that handwritten submissions require manual transcription and interpretation before they can be evaluated, routed and analysed.",Digitalised Yellow Book,Agency for Administrative Modernisation; Central-Government; General public services n.e.c.; Portugal,The case is relevant because it applies AI to a foundational accountability mechanism in public administration.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Citizen complaints, suggestions and compliments are a critical source of feedback for improving public services, but paper-based or handwritten submissions can be slow to process, difficult to classify and harder to analyse systematically. Portugal’s Digitalised Yellow Book initiative addresses this problem by modernising the traditional Livro Amarelo, the formal channel through which citizens provide feedback on public services. The broader electronic service is already available through gov.pt, where citizens can submit complaints about public services or public entities, and the complaints are sent directly to the responsible entity with legal value equivalent to the physical complaints book. The AI-specific project described by OECD.AI focuses on digitising handwritten complaint reports. The operational problem is that handwritten submissions require manual transcription and interpretation before they can be evaluated, routed and analysed. By applying AI to recognise and classify handwritten text, the initiative can convert unstructured citizen input into structured data more quickly and consistently. At a conceptual level, the system supports administrative accountability by improving the data pipeline behind complaints management: it does not replace the citizen’s right to complain or the authority’s duty to respond, but helps process and evaluate submissions more efficiently. The expected impact is shorter processing times, improved evaluation standards, more consistent support for users and better analytical insight into recurring service problems. The case is relevant because it applies AI to a foundational accountability mechanism in public administration.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2784,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02137,EU_PSTW_202609_02137,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Ministry of Economics of the Republic of Latvia,R&D economic affairs,UNKNOWN,AI Factories Latvia,Ministry of Economics of the Republic of Latvia; Central-Government; R&D economic affairs; Latvia,"At a conceptual level, the programme functions as an AI-enabling ecosystem rather than a single application: it is intended to connect research, companies, infrastructure and priority use cases so that AI solutions can move from experimentation to deployment.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Latvia’s AI ecosystem needs stronger support for companies and researchers developing market-ready AI products, especially in areas where local language, security and regional-context requirements are not well served by generic international solutions. AI Factories Latvia is a national initiative designed to support start-ups and established companies in developing and commercialising AI products and services. The operational problem is that AI innovation requires not only technical talent, but also access to computing capacity, data resources, commercialisation support and sectoral collaboration. The initiative focuses particularly on large language models tailored to regional languages, which can improve translation, communication tools and content-generation services for Latvian and other under-represented language contexts. It also supports digital-twin projects with applications in cybersecurity and regional security simulations, reflecting Latvia’s strategic interest in both digital innovation and resilience. At a conceptual level, the programme functions as an AI-enabling ecosystem rather than a single application: it is intended to connect research, companies, infrastructure and priority use cases so that AI solutions can move from experimentation to deployment. The expected impact is stronger national AI capacity, new products and services, improved language-technology sovereignty, better support for entrepreneurship and enhanced regional-security innovation. The case is significant because it treats AI industrial policy as a national capability-building process, where start-ups, established companies and public-sector priorities are linked through shared infrastructure and targeted support.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2785,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02138,EU_PSTW_202609_02138,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,IQInAbox ApS,"Agriculture, forestry, fishing and hunting",The operational problem is that traditional feed trials can be slow and may struggle to keep pace with rapid genetic development in slaughter pigs.,AI-based feed optimisation – real-time processing of large amounts of data in pig production,"IQInAbox ApS; Consortium; Agriculture, forestry, fishing and hunting; Denmark","The Danish AI-based feed-optimisation project addresses the challenge of continuously identifying optimal feeding strategies for growing pigs using real-time data from across the production chain. The expected impact is higher productivity, reduced feed costs, better feed utilisation and lower greenhouse-gas emissions from food production through more efficient resource use.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Pig production depends heavily on feed efficiency because feed composition affects productivity, animal growth, production costs and the climate footprint of meat production. The Danish AI-based feed-optimisation project addresses the challenge of continuously identifying optimal feeding strategies for growing pigs using real-time data from across the production chain. The operational problem is that traditional feed trials can be slow and may struggle to keep pace with rapid genetic development in slaughter pigs. Producers need more dynamic tools that can account for changing growth patterns, feed composition, herd performance and commercial conditions. The project will develop an advanced AI-based system that integrates data from feed suppliers, slaughterhouses and pig producers, enabling real-time monitoring and continuous calculation of optimal feed compositions. At a conceptual level, the system works as a data-driven alternative or supplement to conventional feed trials: instead of relying only on periodic experimental results, the model learns from large-scale operational data and updates recommendations as conditions change. The expected impact is higher productivity, reduced feed costs, better feed utilisation and lower greenhouse-gas emissions from food production through more efficient resource use. The case is relevant because it applies AI to a concrete environmental and economic bottleneck in agriculture, where small improvements in feed efficiency can have significant aggregate effects. The project is led by IQinAbox ApS with the University of Copenhagen and is supported by Denmark’s Green Development and Demonstration Programme.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2786,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02139,EU_PSTW_202609_02139,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Duotec A/S,Waste water management.,"Wastewater treatment plants can emit nitrous oxide, a greenhouse gas with a high climate impact, but emissions are difficult to manage because they depend on complex biological and operational conditions inside process tanks.",AI Assisted Process Understanding for Management of N2O Reduction from Wastewater Treatment Plants (AIAP-N2O),Duotec A/S; Consortium; Waste water management.; Denmark,"AIAP-N2O addresses this challenge by using sensor data, AI, data analysis and process knowledge to understand and reduce N₂O formation in wastewater treatment. The operational problem is that treatment plants need practical control strategies that can be implemented across different hardware and software environments, rather than solutions that work only in one specific plant or vendor system.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Wastewater treatment plants can emit nitrous oxide, a greenhouse gas with a high climate impact, but emissions are difficult to manage because they depend on complex biological and operational conditions inside process tanks. AIAP-N2O addresses this challenge by using sensor data, AI, data analysis and process knowledge to understand and reduce N₂O formation in wastewater treatment. The operational problem is that treatment plants need practical control strategies that can be implemented across different hardware and software environments, rather than solutions that work only in one specific plant or vendor system. The project will collect data from three wastewater treatment plants over one to one and a half years, including measurements from two types of N₂O sensor technologies. These data will be used to develop an open and detailed description of how process-tank control can avoid or reduce nitrous oxide formation. At a conceptual level, the project converts plant-level measurements into generalisable process knowledge: AI helps identify the conditions and parameters that drive emissions, while the resulting guidance can be used by treatment plants or third-party control providers to build their own control strategies. The expected impact is lower greenhouse-gas emissions, better environmental performance in water and wastewater management, and easier adoption of N₂O reduction practices across the sector. The case is relevant because it uses AI not simply to optimise efficiency, but to make climate mitigation operational in public environmental infrastructure. The project is led by Duotec A/S with the Danish Technological Institute, DTU Sustain and several wastewater utilities.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2787,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02140,EU_PSTW_202609_02140,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Danish Ministry of Climate, Energy, and Utilities",Fuel and energy,"The operational challenge is that conventional de-icing methods often rely on indirect external indicators, such as temperature and humidity, which may trigger de-icing even when ice formation is not actually present.",Energy-efficient de-icing with camera detection and artificial intelligence,"Danish Ministry of Climate, Energy, and Utilities; Central-Government; Fuel and energy; Denmark",This leads to unnecessary energy use and higher operating costs. AICID uses camera detection together with humidity and temperature sensors to identify real ice formation more accurately and control de-icing cycles only when needed.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Ventilation systems are critical for energy-efficient buildings because heat exchangers can recover a large share of the heat from exhaust air, reducing the need for additional heating. However, ice formation on heat exchangers can reduce efficiency, increase energy consumption and damage the system if not managed correctly. The Danish AICID project addresses this problem by developing an AI-based control algorithm for more precise de-icing in ventilation systems. The operational challenge is that conventional de-icing methods often rely on indirect external indicators, such as temperature and humidity, which may trigger de-icing even when ice formation is not actually present. This leads to unnecessary energy use and higher operating costs. AICID uses camera detection together with humidity and temperature sensors to identify real ice formation more accurately and control de-icing cycles only when needed. The project includes analysis of camera and sensor technologies, testing, development and implementation of the algorithm on EXHAUSTO ventilation systems, demonstration in homes managed by Haderslev Boligselskab and lifecycle assessment to confirm environmental benefits. The expected results are substantial: the project aims to reduce heat loss in ventilation systems by at least 40%, reduce de-icing periods from around 40% to between 5% and 0.5% annually, and lower the temperature threshold for de-icing from 5°C to between -5°C and -10°C. The wider expected impact is improved building energy efficiency, lower operational costs, longer equipment lifetime and significant national energy-saving potential.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2788,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02141,EU_PSTW_202609_02141,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Danish Ministry of Climate, Energy, and Utilities",Housing and community amenities n.e.c.,"HabitAi addresses this challenge by exploring how dynamic and contextual building data can be collected, structured and made usable for homeowners and construction-sector actors. The operational problem is that many decisions about single-family homes are still taken on the basis of fragmented, static or siloed data.",HabitAi - intelligent data to support sustainable decisions for homeowners,"Danish Ministry of Climate, Energy, and Utilities; Central-Government; Housing and community amenities n.e.c.; Denmark","Renovation and maintenance decisions in residential buildings have a major influence on resource use, emissions and the long-term sustainability of the construction sector.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Renovation and maintenance decisions in residential buildings have a major influence on resource use, emissions and the long-term sustainability of the construction sector. HabitAi addresses this challenge by exploring how dynamic and contextual building data can be collected, structured and made usable for homeowners and construction-sector actors. The operational problem is that many decisions about single-family homes are still taken on the basis of fragmented, static or siloed data. Information about building characteristics, condition, maintenance needs, climate impact and renovation options may exist across different registries or professional assessments, but it is not necessarily available in a form that supports practical decision-making by the homeowner. The HabitAi preliminary project examined whether it is technically possible to carry out cross-cutting data collection and digitalisation of currently locked or fragmented building data for Danish single-family houses. The intended outcome is a digital toolbox that can help homeowners and the building sector decide when maintenance, renovation or preservation is preferable to demolition, and how to achieve the greatest environmental benefit within the homeowner’s financial constraints. At a conceptual level, the project is about converting building data into decision support: rather than treating each renovation as an isolated case, data can help identify sustainable pathways for preserving resources, extending building lifetimes and reducing climate impact. The case is relevant because it focuses on the residential building stock, where individual decisions collectively have large environmental consequences. However, the official source describes HabitAi as a preliminary project and does not confirm a fully operational AI product; it should therefore be presented as a feasibility and data-structuring initiative.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2789,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02142,EU_PSTW_202609_02142,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Danish Ministry of Climate, Energy, and Utilities",Housing and community amenities n.e.c.,"Renovation, repair and deconstruction of masonry buildings are labour-intensive activities that can expose workers to repetitive strain, dust, noise and physically demanding manual work. The operational challenge is that brick buildings often have long potential lifetimes, but repairing or selectively dismantling them requires careful removal of joints without damaging reusable materials.",Robotic technologies for the construction industry,"Danish Ministry of Climate, Energy, and Utilities; Central-Government; Housing and community amenities n.e.c.; Denmark",UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Renovation, repair and deconstruction of masonry buildings are labour-intensive activities that can expose workers to repetitive strain, dust, noise and physically demanding manual work. The Danish project on robotic removal of masonry joints addresses this problem by developing and testing robotic technologies that can remove mortar joints in masonry more precisely and efficiently. The operational challenge is that brick buildings often have long potential lifetimes, but repairing or selectively dismantling them requires careful removal of joints without damaging reusable materials. Conventional manual methods can be slow and physically demanding, while demolition may lead to unnecessary material loss and reduce the possibility of circular reuse. The project therefore focuses on full-scale development, testing, dissemination and implementation of robotic technologies for the construction sector, with specific attention to extending building lifetime and enabling more effective deconstruction. At a conceptual level, the solution combines robotics, sensing and control methods to identify and remove joints accurately, supporting both selective repair and recovery of building materials. The expected impact is improved productivity in construction and renovation, reduced wear and tear on craft workers, better occupational safety, longer building lifetimes and increased reuse of masonry materials in the circular economy. The case is relevant because it shows how automation can support the green transition in construction not only through new materials, but also by making repair and deconstruction more practical. The official project page describes the initiative as an environmental-technology development, testing and demonstration project within the MUDP programme.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2790,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02143,EU_PSTW_202609_02143,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Vejle Municipality,General services.,UNKNOWN,Supportbot for Vejle Municipality,Vejle Municipality; Local Government; General services.; Denmark,"The solution implemented by CleverCall provides an AI-powered telephone support assistant that answers calls when the human IT support desk is closed. The case is relevant because it demonstrates an internal public-sector AI use case rather than a citizen-facing chatbot: the direct users are municipal employees, and the public value arises through improved organisational efficiency and service continuity.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Large municipalities depend on stable internal IT support because service disruption affects schools, administrative offices and front-line staff. Vejle Municipality’s Supportbot addresses a practical operational problem: its more than 8,500 employees may experience IT issues outside normal support hours, and unresolved problems can delay work, teaching preparation or service delivery. The solution implemented by CleverCall provides an AI-powered telephone support assistant that answers calls when the human IT support desk is closed. Instead of requiring employees to wait until the next working day or adding pressure to the support team at opening time, the Supportbot provides concrete answers, sends links to written instructions and video guides, and helps users solve common issues such as forgotten passwords or malfunctioning equipment. At a conceptual level, the tool functions as an always-available first-line support layer for municipal employees. It captures recurring questions, provides standardised guidance and continuously improves through training on daily enquiries. The expected impact is reduced downtime, fewer interruptions for human IT-support staff, faster resolution of simple problems and improved continuity of municipal work. The case is relevant because it demonstrates an internal public-sector AI use case rather than a citizen-facing chatbot: the direct users are municipal employees, and the public value arises through improved organisational efficiency and service continuity. It also shows how conversational AI can be useful in narrow, high-frequency support domains where many problems follow repeatable patterns.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2791,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02144,EU_PSTW_202609_02144,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Kalundborg Kommune,General services.,"The operational challenge identified by Kalundborg Municipality was not the absence of AI, but fragmentation.",CostAI Kalundborg,Kalundborg Kommune; Local Government; General services.; Denmark,"As municipalities adopt more AI tools, employees can face a practical usability problem: the number of separate assistants, chatbots, transcription tools, translation services and specialist applications grows faster than the organisation’s ability to make them easy to find and use. The expected impact is easier uptake of AI by staff, reduced confusion, better governance of AI use and improved internal productivity across municipal service areas.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"As municipalities adopt more AI tools, employees can face a practical usability problem: the number of separate assistants, chatbots, transcription tools, translation services and specialist applications grows faster than the organisation’s ability to make them easy to find and use. CostAI Kalundborg addresses this problem by creating a single app through which municipal employees can access different AI tools from one consolidated entry point. The operational challenge identified by Kalundborg Municipality was not the absence of AI, but fragmentation. Staff had to navigate too many links, tools and interfaces, which risked reducing the productivity benefits that AI was supposed to deliver. In response, the municipality worked with the Danish technology start-up Promte, formerly Applai, to develop a user-friendly platform for municipal AI assistants. The app is designed to work across PC, tablet and mobile devices and to bring together tools such as intranet chat, specialised professional-domain assistants, interpretation or translation support, meeting transcription and minute-generation functions. At a conceptual level, CostAI acts as an internal AI hub or orchestration layer: rather than creating one large general-purpose assistant, it gives staff a structured gateway to different controlled tools. This supports safer and more coherent organisational adoption, because employees can access AI services through an approved municipal channel rather than through dispersed, informal solutions. The expected impact is easier uptake of AI by staff, reduced confusion, better governance of AI use and improved internal productivity across municipal service areas.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2792,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02145,EU_PSTW_202609_02145,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Ukraine's Ministry of Digital Transformation,Education n.e.c.,"Education systems need digital tools that support students, parents and teachers without adding new administrative burdens or fragmenting learning information across incompatible platforms.","Digital assistant ""Mriya"" for learning",Ukraine's Ministry of Digital Transformation; Central-Government; Education n.e.c.; Ukraine,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Education systems need digital tools that support students, parents and teachers without adding new administrative burdens or fragmenting learning information across incompatible platforms. Ukraine’s Mriia is a state educational ecosystem designed to help pupils learn, parents follow progress and educators manage teaching processes more efficiently. The operational problem is particularly acute in Ukraine’s wartime education context, where schools may operate remotely, in shelters or under disrupted conditions, and where families need reliable digital access to schedules, assignments, grades and communication. Mriia provides a web portal and mobile application that connect schools, students, parents and teachers through a common educational environment. The official platform presents functions including homework, schedules, learning plans, progress tracking, educational content, secure chats based on Signal, support at all stages and a digital education document called Mriia ID. Artificial intelligence is described as supporting personalised user experience inside the ecosystem. At a conceptual level, Mriia acts as a unified educational operating layer: it reduces bureaucracy for teachers, gives parents clearer visibility of children’s learning and helps students organise their educational pathway. The expected impact is more coherent school communication, better continuity of learning, reduced paperwork and stronger personalisation of educational support. The case is significant because it links AI-enabled personalisation and digital administration to a nationwide public-education ecosystem, rather than treating learning technology as a collection of isolated school-level tools.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2793,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02146,EU_PSTW_202609_02146,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Government of Odesa,Health n.e.c.,"Rapid diagnosis of brain injuries and neurological conditions is critical because delays can directly affect survival, treatment options and long-term patient outcomes. The operational challenge is that radiologists and emergency clinicians must detect pathological changes quickly, especially when patients may have trauma, stroke-related signs or other acute brain conditions.",Brainscan Telemedicine - AI for brain diagnostics,Government of Odesa; Local Government; Health n.e.c.; Ukraine,The BrainScan telemedicine pilot in Odesa addresses this problem by using artificial intelligence to analyse computed tomography images of the brain and provide doctors with a faster diagnostic “second opinion”. BrainScan uses a neural network to process CT images automatically and identify possible pathological changes.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Rapid diagnosis of brain injuries and neurological conditions is critical because delays can directly affect survival, treatment options and long-term patient outcomes. The BrainScan telemedicine pilot in Odesa addresses this problem by using artificial intelligence to analyse computed tomography images of the brain and provide doctors with a faster diagnostic “second opinion”. The operational challenge is that radiologists and emergency clinicians must detect pathological changes quickly, especially when patients may have trauma, stroke-related signs or other acute brain conditions. Manual analysis can take longer, and in wartime or strained healthcare settings specialist capacity may be limited. BrainScan uses a neural network to process CT images automatically and identify possible pathological changes. According to the source, results can be available around five minutes after image processing, allowing doctors to decide on further treatment more quickly than in ordinary workflows. At a conceptual level, the AI does not replace the clinician; it provides rapid image analysis and decision support, helping reduce the risk of missed findings and supporting faster triage. The project was implemented within Ukraine’s national telemedicine strategy, with support from USAID’s LHSS project, and the Odesa experience was subsequently extended to frontline Kramatorsk in Donetsk region. The expected impact is faster diagnosis, fewer medical errors, more timely treatment decisions and stronger access to specialist-style diagnostic support in locations under pressure. The case is significant because it links AI diagnostics with telemedicine and emergency care in a high-stakes wartime health context.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2794,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02147,EU_PSTW_202609_02147,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Public Health Center of the Ministry of Health of Ukraine (PHC),Outpatient services.,"Tuberculosis screening and diagnosis require rapid reading of chest X-rays, but radiology capacity can be limited, especially in regions where mobile outreach and specialised services are needed. The operational problem is that radiologists must review large volumes of images and detect subtle changes, while delays can slow the start of treatment and reduce the effectiveness of public-health interventions.",AI helps diagnose tuberculosis and other lung pathologies,Public Health Center of the Ministry of Health of Ukraine (PHC); Central-Government; Outpatient services.; Ukraine,"Ukraine’s Public Health Center reports the use of AI-enabled systems to assist the diagnosis of tuberculosis and other lung pathologies by analysing chest X-ray images. The system is particularly relevant for mobile screening teams, where AI-supported portable X-ray equipment can be deployed in territorial communities and underserved areas.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Tuberculosis screening and diagnosis require rapid reading of chest X-rays, but radiology capacity can be limited, especially in regions where mobile outreach and specialised services are needed. Ukraine’s Public Health Center reports the use of AI-enabled systems to assist the diagnosis of tuberculosis and other lung pathologies by analysing chest X-ray images. The operational problem is that radiologists must review large volumes of images and detect subtle changes, while delays can slow the start of treatment and reduce the effectiveness of public-health interventions. The AI systems support automated analysis of chest images, highlighting potentially affected lung areas with heat maps and helping identify primary tuberculosis, residual changes after disease, pneumonia, tumours and other thoracic pathologies. At a conceptual level, the tool works as a diagnostic-support layer: it does not replace medical professionals, but accelerates image interpretation and helps clinicians focus attention on suspicious findings. The system is particularly relevant for mobile screening teams, where AI-supported portable X-ray equipment can be deployed in territorial communities and underserved areas. The expected impact is faster diagnosis, reduced radiologist workload, earlier detection of lung disease and improved access to screening outside large hospitals. The case is significant because it shows practical AI deployment in public health under difficult operating conditions, where speed, portability and scarce specialist capacity are central constraints.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F06_HIGH_VOLUME_SCALABILITY_BOTTLENECK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2795,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02148,EU_PSTW_202609_02148,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,CheckEye Ltd.,Health n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,CheckEye: The first AI-based platform in Europe implementing a national chronic disease screening,CheckEye Ltd.; Private sector; Health n.e.c.; Ukraine,"CheckEye enables screening through retinal image analysis, using AI to identify signs of diabetic retinopathy and prioritise patients who require ophthalmological follow-up. The expected impact is earlier detection, prevention of avoidable vision loss, more efficient use of ophthalmologist capacity and stronger resilience of screening services during crisis.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Chronic diseases such as diabetes can lead to severe complications when early signs are not detected, and diabetic retinopathy is a major preventable cause of vision loss. CheckEye addresses this problem through an AI-based platform for large-scale screening of diabetic retinopathy and, more broadly, chronic-disease complications. The operational problem is that many patients do not receive regular specialist eye examinations, particularly under wartime conditions where access to healthcare facilities is disrupted. CheckEye enables screening through retinal image analysis, using AI to identify signs of diabetic retinopathy and prioritise patients who require ophthalmological follow-up. At a conceptual level, the platform shifts screening from a scarce specialist-service model towards a scalable preventive-health model: images can be collected through distributed screening points, analysed by AI and used to identify patients needing further care. In Ukraine, CheckEye reports implementation across six regions, including Chernivtsi, Zakarpattia, Vinnytsia and Kyiv regions, in cooperation with the Ukrainian Diabetes Federation under the “Seeing a Colourful World-2” project. The reported results suggest that around 30% of screened patients had diabetic retinopathy without knowing it, while the platform reports 95% sensitivity and 90% specificity. The expected impact is earlier detection, prevention of avoidable vision loss, more efficient use of ophthalmologist capacity and stronger resilience of screening services during crisis. The case is relevant because it applies AI not to one-off diagnosis, but to population-level preventive screening.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2796,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02149,EU_PSTW_202609_02149,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Government of Estonia,General services.,UNKNOWN,"New cross-government, AI-powered data management asset",Government of Estonia; Central-Government; General services.; Estonia,"Estonia’s digital-government model already relies on interoperability, secure data exchange and the ability to deliver services across institutional boundaries, but the next challenge is making public-sector data easier to search, connect and use from the citizen’s point of view. The new cross-government AI-powered data management asset addresses this problem by aiming to create a unified, AI-enabled layer for accessing information and resources held by different ministries and agencies.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Estonia’s digital-government model already relies on interoperability, secure data exchange and the ability to deliver services across institutional boundaries, but the next challenge is making public-sector data easier to search, connect and use from the citizen’s point of view. The new cross-government AI-powered data management asset addresses this problem by aiming to create a unified, AI-enabled layer for accessing information and resources held by different ministries and agencies. The operational issue is that citizens do not organise their needs according to government structures: a person may need information related to health, education, benefits, family services, taxation or another public function, but may not know which institution holds the relevant data or how to retrieve it. Estonia’s planned tool is intended to reduce this friction by making information from different parts of government searchable and accessible through a more integrated interface. At a conceptual level, the initiative builds on Estonia’s wider approach to proactive and data-driven public services: instead of requiring citizens to navigate administrative silos, AI can help identify relevant information, connect datasets and support more intuitive service access. The expected impact is improved accessibility of public information, reduced administrative complexity, more efficient use of government data and a stronger basis for proactive citizen services. However, the available evidence describes the asset as still being developed, with initial components expected to become operational first and then improved iteratively. It should therefore be framed as an implementation pathway rather than a fully mature, routinely operating national platform",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2797,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02150,EU_PSTW_202609_02150,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Government of Estonia,Secondary education.,Estonia’s collaboration with OpenAI addresses this challenge by introducing ChatGPT Edu into the national secondary-school system as part of the country’s AI Leap 2025 initiative.,ChatGPT Edu into Estonia's education system,Government of Estonia; Central-Government; Secondary education.; Estonia,"The operational problem is that students are already using generative AI for learning, writing, programming and tutoring, but access and responsible-use frameworks are uneven. Schools therefore need structured deployment, teacher support and education-specific safeguards rather than leaving AI use to individual choice.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Education systems increasingly need to equip students and teachers with practical AI literacy while ensuring that access to advanced tools is equitable, secure and pedagogically supported. Estonia’s collaboration with OpenAI addresses this challenge by introducing ChatGPT Edu into the national secondary-school system as part of the country’s AI Leap 2025 initiative. The operational problem is that students are already using generative AI for learning, writing, programming and tutoring, but access and responsible-use frameworks are uneven. Schools therefore need structured deployment, teacher support and education-specific safeguards rather than leaving AI use to individual choice. Under the initiative, Estonia planned to provide students and teachers in the secondary school system with access to ChatGPT Edu, starting with 10th and 11th graders by September 2025. The collaboration also includes OpenAI API services, technical support and knowledge-sharing for custom GPTs focused on teaching and learning. Potential use cases include feedback assistance, student support, study assistance and lesson planning, with the aim of reducing administrative burden for teachers and supporting students’ creativity and critical thinking. At a conceptual level, the project treats generative AI as part of national education infrastructure, comparable to earlier waves of school digitalisation. The expected impact is broader AI literacy, more personalised learning support, greater teacher productivity and a more systematic approach to responsible AI adoption in education. The case is significant because OpenAI describes it as the first government initiative to provide nationwide access to ChatGPT Edu across a national education system",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2798,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02151,EU_PSTW_202609_02151,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Government of Albania,R&D economic affairs,"The operational and policy rationale is that a smaller economy such as Albania cannot compete with larger technology ecosystems through domestic scale alone, but it can position itself within the global AI landscape by backing high-profile frontier-AI ventures, strengthening links with internationally recognised Albanian talent and signalling long-term commitment to advanced technology.",Thinking Machines Lab – Albanian strategic investment in advanced AI,Government of Albania; Central-Government; R&D economic affairs; Albania,"At a conceptual level, the case is less about immediate service automation and more about industrial policy, technology diplomacy and ecosystem positioning: the public sector is using investment as a lever to connect Albania with frontier AI research and potentially attract expertise, visibility and future collaboration opportunities. It is an implemented strategic investment decision in an advanced AI company",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Albania’s investment in Thinking Machines Lab should be understood as a strategic AI and innovation-policy initiative rather than as the deployment of an AI tool within public administration. The corrected name of the company is Thinking Machines Lab, an artificial-intelligence research and product company founded and led by Mira Murati, the Albanian-American former CTO of OpenAI. The operational and policy rationale is that a smaller economy such as Albania cannot compete with larger technology ecosystems through domestic scale alone, but it can position itself within the global AI landscape by backing high-profile frontier-AI ventures, strengthening links with internationally recognised Albanian talent and signalling long-term commitment to advanced technology. Public reporting indicates that the Albanian government approved an investment of around EUR 8.8 million, equivalent to approximately 1 billion lek or USD 10 million, through the 2025 budget revision. At a conceptual level, the case is less about immediate service automation and more about industrial policy, technology diplomacy and ecosystem positioning: the public sector is using investment as a lever to connect Albania with frontier AI research and potentially attract expertise, visibility and future collaboration opportunities. The expected impact is therefore indirect, including stronger national branding in AI, possible knowledge spillovers, increased investor attention and support for Albania’s ambition to participate in emerging technology markets. The entry should avoid presenting this as a government AI implementation, because there is no evidence that Thinking Machines Lab is currently delivering an AI system for Albanian public services. It is an implemented strategic investment decision in an advanced AI company",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2799,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02152,EU_PSTW_202609_02152,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Ministry of Digital Transformation and Ministry of Higher Education, Science and Innovation",R&D economic affairs,"Advanced AI development depends on access to high-performance computing, data, technical expertise and innovation support, but smaller national ecosystems may struggle to compete if they lack shared infrastructure. Slovenia’s AI Factory initiative addresses this challenge by combining a new supercomputer with services intended to support AI research, development and deployment.",A new supercomputer and AI factory to boost technological power,"Ministry of Digital Transformation and Ministry of Higher Education, Science and Innovation; Central-Government; R&D economic affairs; Slovenia",UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Advanced AI development depends on access to high-performance computing, data, technical expertise and innovation support, but smaller national ecosystems may struggle to compete if they lack shared infrastructure. Slovenia’s AI Factory initiative addresses this challenge by combining a new supercomputer with services intended to support AI research, development and deployment. The operational problem is that companies, researchers, start-ups and public-sector innovators need infrastructure capable of training and testing large AI models, while Europe also seeks to strengthen digital sovereignty and reduce dependence on external computing capacity. The Slovenian initiative forms part of the wider EuroHPC AI Factories network, which is designed to bring together computing power, data and talent around European supercomputers. At a conceptual level, the AI factory is not only a machine room: it is an ecosystem service that gives users access to compute, expertise and support for developing trustworthy and ethical AI models. It is expected to serve researchers and companies, including SMEs and start-ups, and to contribute to national and European AI strategies. The expected impact is stronger Slovenian AI capacity, improved innovation infrastructure, better opportunities for companies to commercialise AI applications and enhanced participation in the pan-European AI ecosystem. The case is significant because it treats AI as a strategic public infrastructure agenda, linking supercomputing investment to industrial competitiveness, scientific research and European technological sovereignty.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2800,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02153,EU_PSTW_202609_02153,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Helsinki,Transport,"Urban mobility around major transport hubs is difficult to manage because cars, trams, buses, bicycles, electric scooters, pedestrians and ferry passengers interact dynamically, particularly during peak arrival and departure periods. The operational challenge is that transport planners need to understand not only aggregate traffic volumes, but also how passengers move between ferries, trams, metro lines, trains, taxis, scooters, bicycles and walking routes.",Drones and AI to tackle congestion,City of Helsinki; Local Government; Transport; Finland,"These outputs are then converted into anonymised trajectory data and integrated into AI-powered digital twins, allowing city regulators and engineers to test traffic-management strategies virtually before implementing them in the real world.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Urban mobility around major transport hubs is difficult to manage because cars, trams, buses, bicycles, electric scooters, pedestrians and ferry passengers interact dynamically, particularly during peak arrival and departure periods. Helsinki’s drone- and AI-supported traffic-monitoring initiative addresses this problem by collecting high-resolution mobility data around the West Harbour, one of Europe’s busiest ferry-port areas. The operational challenge is that transport planners need to understand not only aggregate traffic volumes, but also how passengers move between ferries, trams, metro lines, trains, taxis, scooters, bicycles and walking routes. Since 2023, drones have been used above the harbour area to collect bird’s-eye-view video of multimodal traffic. AI systems process the footage to detect and classify vehicles and track movement patterns, including speed, direction and interaction between transport modes. These outputs are then converted into anonymised trajectory data and integrated into AI-powered digital twins, allowing city regulators and engineers to test traffic-management strategies virtually before implementing them in the real world. At a conceptual level, the initiative turns temporary aerial observations into simulation-ready data for urban planning. It also raises important governance questions, since even GDPR-compliant drone monitoring can create public concerns about surveillance, transparency and cybersecurity. The project aims to test soft measures such as route recommendations and incentives to travel at off-peak times, and to trial an app suggesting alternative routes or modes. The wider expected impact is reduced peak congestion, smoother intermodal connections, better planning evidence and support for sustainable urban mobility",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2801,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02154,EU_PSTW_202609_02154,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Milan,Economic affairs n.e.c,UNKNOWN,Gemello Digitale Esteso - Milan's Digital Twin,City of Milan; Local Government; Economic affairs n.e.c; Italy,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Milan is developing a comprehensive digital twin of its metropolitan area under a municipal smart‑city initiative. The project, led by the Municipality of Milan with four geoinformation partners, combines high‑resolution aerial imagery, LiDAR point clouds, and terrestrial mobile mapping data to build a detailed dynamic model of the city and surrounding municipalities. This virtual representation supports urban planning, resource management and decision‑making by providing interactive geospatial datasets and software tools for analysis. The digital twin covers about 1,575 km² and includes thousands of urban objects, helping city authorities make better data‑driven choices and improve quality of life for residents and stakeholders.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2803,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02155,EU_PSTW_202609_02155,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Florence,Cultural services.,"Historic city centres face a difficult balance between welcoming visitors and protecting residents’ quality of life, heritage spaces and urban functionality. The expected impact is improved liveability in the historic centre, better management of tourism flows, stronger support for sustainable urban-tourism policy and a replicable operational manual for other European contexts.",TOURISMO Project - Florence,City of Florence; Local Government; Cultural services.; Italy,"At a conceptual level, the system creates a real-time evidence base for sustainable tourism management: rather than relying only on periodic surveys or anecdotal complaints, Florence can use data to assess pressure points and plan interventions. The case is relevant because it applies AI and sensor systems to tourism governance in a UNESCO-sensitive urban environment, where public acceptance and privacy safeguards are essential.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Historic city centres face a difficult balance between welcoming visitors and protecting residents’ quality of life, heritage spaces and urban functionality. Florence’s TOURISMO pilot addresses this issue by testing smart technologies to monitor tourism flows in highly visited areas of the city, including Piazza Duomo, Ponte Vecchio and Piazza Pitti. The operational problem is that overtourism is not only a matter of total visitor numbers; it also depends on where people concentrate, how long they stay, how they move and how visitor behaviour affects local liveability. The implementation combines thermal cameras and “sniffer” sensors with AI-based processing to collect anonymised data on pedestrian movement and presence. Thermal cameras installed in Piazza Duomo and additional sensors across strategic points gather information that can help city authorities understand crowding patterns without identifying individuals. The initiative also includes QR-code tools to collect visitor feedback, adding a qualitative layer to quantitative flow monitoring. At a conceptual level, the system creates a real-time evidence base for sustainable tourism management: rather than relying only on periodic surveys or anecdotal complaints, Florence can use data to assess pressure points and plan interventions. The expected impact is improved liveability in the historic centre, better management of tourism flows, stronger support for sustainable urban-tourism policy and a replicable operational manual for other European contexts. The case is relevant because it applies AI and sensor systems to tourism governance in a UNESCO-sensitive urban environment, where public acceptance and privacy safeguards are essential.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2804,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02156,EU_PSTW_202609_02156,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Malta Regional Development,Protection of biodiversity and landscape.,"Tourism pressure in environmentally sensitive coastal areas creates a difficult management problem for local authorities and protected-area managers: high visitor numbers can damage fragile habitats, reduce visitor experience and make it harder to plan cleaning, enforcement, traffic management and conservation measures. The TOURISMO pilot at Golden Bay, also known as Ir-Ramla tal-Mixquqa, responds to this challenge by introducing a beach-monitoring system designed to provide evidence on visitor flows and behaviour.",TOURISMO Project - Malta,Malta Regional Development; Regional Government; Protection of biodiversity and landscape.; Malta,"The implementation uses GDPR-compliant people-counting CCTV and deep-learning algorithms to monitor pedestrian and vehicular traffic, producing insights on visitor density and movement during peak tourist seasons. The case is significant because it applies AI-enabled monitoring to sustainable tourism governance rather than to promotion or visitor marketing.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Tourism pressure in environmentally sensitive coastal areas creates a difficult management problem for local authorities and protected-area managers: high visitor numbers can damage fragile habitats, reduce visitor experience and make it harder to plan cleaning, enforcement, traffic management and conservation measures. The TOURISMO pilot at Golden Bay, also known as Ir-Ramla tal-Mixquqa, responds to this challenge by introducing a beach-monitoring system designed to provide evidence on visitor flows and behaviour. The site is one of the Mediterranean pilot areas of the TOURISMO project, which focuses on innovative and sustainable management of tourism flows. The operational problem is that overcrowding at Golden Bay affects both the natural environment and the quality of the tourism experience, but without systematic data it is difficult to know when, where and how visitor pressure is developing. The implementation uses GDPR-compliant people-counting CCTV and deep-learning algorithms to monitor pedestrian and vehicular traffic, producing insights on visitor density and movement during peak tourist seasons. At a conceptual level, the system turns the beach into a monitored management environment where real-time or near-real-time data can support decisions on conservation, services and visitor-flow control. The expected impact is better protection of the area’s dune ecosystems and flora and fauna, improved NATURA 2000 management planning, more informed local stakeholder coordination and a stronger basis for balancing tourism with environmental preservation. The case is significant because it applies AI-enabled monitoring to sustainable tourism governance rather than to promotion or visitor marketing.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2805,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02157,EU_PSTW_202609_02157,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,European Commission,Broadcasting and publishing services.,"The Open Rights Data Exchange addresses the persistent problem that rights data are often fragmented, inconsistent, difficult to verify and poorly interoperable across platforms, distributors and collecting systems. The expected impact is faster licensing, reduced disputes, more transparent royalty allocation, lower administrative burden and stronger interoperability in European cultural and creative markets.",Open Rights Data Exchange - A Common European data space for copyright,European Commission; European Institution/Agency; Broadcasting and publishing services.; European Union,"The case is significant because it applies blockchain to the governance of digital creative assets, where reliable metadata and identity binding are essential for fair remuneration and lawful reuse.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Copyright markets depend on reliable information about who created a work, who owns or administers rights, what licences apply, and how royalties should be allocated. The Open Rights Data Exchange addresses the persistent problem that rights data are often fragmented, inconsistent, difficult to verify and poorly interoperable across platforms, distributors and collecting systems. This creates friction for creators, rights holders, publishers, online services and licensing intermediaries, increasing transaction costs and weakening monetisation opportunities. ORDE proposes a common European rights-data infrastructure built on the European Blockchain Services Infrastructure. The operational model allows creators and rights holders to register a digital work by linking a content fingerprint with a verified identity and rights metadata. This binding can then be timestamped and stored immutably on the ledger, creating a verifiable reference point for authorship, ownership and licensing information. At a conceptual level, ORDE functions as a trusted rights-data exchange layer: it does not replace copyright law or licensing contracts, but makes rights information more discoverable, machine-readable and verifiable. Platforms, distributors and users could query this information to understand whether a work can be used, under what conditions and who should be compensated. The expected impact is faster licensing, reduced disputes, more transparent royalty allocation, lower administrative burden and stronger interoperability in European cultural and creative markets. The case is significant because it applies blockchain to the governance of digital creative assets, where reliable metadata and identity binding are essential for fair remuneration and lawful reuse.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2806,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02158,EU_PSTW_202609_02158,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Cyclades Fast Ferries (CFF),Transport,"The operational challenge is that passengers may need to manage different paper and digital documents, while ferry companies must verify identity and discount eligibility reliably and prevent misuse of boarding passes. At a conceptual level, the project demonstrates how digital identity infrastructure can streamline cross-border mobility services by replacing manual document checks with trusted, standardised and privacy-preserving verification.",Digital Ferry Onboarding Experience,Cyclades Fast Ferries (CFF); Non-governmental; Transport; Greece,"The Digital Ferry Onboarding Experience addresses this problem by using EBSI-based digital identity wallets and verifiable credentials to simplify the journey from ticket booking to embarkation. During booking, the ferry company verifies the authenticity of these credentials against the EBSI ledger, automatically fills relevant booking information and applies the appropriate student discount.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Ferry travel can involve several identity, eligibility, booking and boarding checks, which may be particularly cumbersome for students who need to prove their status to access discounts. The Digital Ferry Onboarding Experience addresses this problem by using EBSI-based digital identity wallets and verifiable credentials to simplify the journey from ticket booking to embarkation. The operational challenge is that passengers may need to manage different paper and digital documents, while ferry companies must verify identity and discount eligibility reliably and prevent misuse of boarding passes. Cyclades Fast Ferries, the University of the Aegean and the European Digital Identity Wallet Consortium are working on a model in which students can store educational identity credentials in a personal digital wallet. During booking, the ferry company verifies the authenticity of these credentials against the EBSI ledger, automatically fills relevant booking information and applies the appropriate student discount. The boarding pass can then be issued as a verifiable credential and stored in the same wallet. At embarkation, the passenger presents the boarding pass from the wallet and the ferry officer verifies it directly. At a conceptual level, the project demonstrates how digital identity infrastructure can streamline cross-border mobility services by replacing manual document checks with trusted, standardised and privacy-preserving verification. The expected impact is faster booking, fewer errors in student-discount validation, stronger security against ticket misuse, reduced paper handling and a smoother passenger experience. The case is relevant because it applies EBSI to a practical mobility scenario linking education credentials, transport services and digital wallets. (ebsi.eu",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2807,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02159,EU_PSTW_202609_02159,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Italian Ministry of Infrastructure and Transport (MIT),"Mining, manufacturing and construction.","The operational problem is that Olympic construction projects involve multiple sites, contractors, design inputs, schedules and safety requirements, while citizens and stakeholders may have limited visibility of progress before the venues and infrastructure are completed.",Milan-Cortina 2026: Digital twin applied to construction site safety and transparency,"Italian Ministry of Infrastructure and Transport (MIT); Central-Government; Mining, manufacturing and construction.; Italy","The Milan-Cortina 2026 digital-twin use case applies dynamic virtual modelling to Olympic construction sites so that infrastructure works can be monitored, communicated and experienced in a more transparent way.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Large sporting events require complex infrastructure works to be delivered under strict deadlines, intense public scrutiny and high expectations for safety, legacy and accountability. The Milan-Cortina 2026 digital-twin use case applies dynamic virtual modelling to Olympic construction sites so that infrastructure works can be monitored, communicated and experienced in a more transparent way. The operational problem is that Olympic construction projects involve multiple sites, contractors, design inputs, schedules and safety requirements, while citizens and stakeholders may have limited visibility of progress before the venues and infrastructure are completed. The implementation described by the Italian Ministry of Infrastructure and Transport integrates design data, sensors and 3D models into a digital twin of the infrastructure, creating an interactive representation that can be updated in real time. At a conceptual level, the digital twin acts both as a construction-management tool and as a public-transparency interface: technical teams can follow the evolution of works, while the public can access immersive experiences that allow them to “enter” the construction sites virtually. The expected impact is more efficient monitoring of works, improved oversight of construction progress, stronger safety and risk-management capacity, and a clearer public narrative about the Olympic infrastructure legacy. The case is relevant because it connects digital-twin technology not only with engineering control, but also with civic transparency around major public investment.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2808,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02160,EU_PSTW_202609_02160,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Municipality of Utrecht,Community development.,UNKNOWN,Utrecht Digital Twin,Municipality of Utrecht; Local Government; Community development.; Netherlands,"Urban planning in dense and climate-exposed cities increasingly requires tools that can connect spatial development, infrastructure constraints, climate adaptation and citizen evidence before decisions are implemented in the physical environment.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Urban planning in dense and climate-exposed cities increasingly requires tools that can connect spatial development, infrastructure constraints, climate adaptation and citizen evidence before decisions are implemented in the physical environment. Utrecht’s digital twin addresses this need by providing an interactive 3D model of the city that brings together buildings, parks, above-ground urban form, underground infrastructure and climate-related data. The operational problem is that planning decisions often require several types of information that climate-related data. The operational problem is that planning decisions often require several types of information that are not naturally visible together: a proposed surface-level intervention may conflict with underground utilities, while green-space choices may need to respond to heat-island patterns or neighbourhood-level climate evidence. Utrecht’s model allows city officials, researchers and residents to visualise planned changes, assess environmental impacts and test urban-design scenarios before implementation. The twin can be described as a “living data platform” used collaboratively by the municipality, knowledge institutions, technology companies and local communities. Also, residents from four neighbourhoods contributed local climate data on temperature variation, humidity and heat hotspots, helping to create a comprehensive heat map and inform climate-adaptation planning. The expected impact is faster and better-informed infrastructure decision-making, stronger integration of above- and below-ground planning, more evidence-based tree planting and cooling measures, and greater citizen participation in shaping climate-resilient urban space",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2809,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02161,EU_PSTW_202609_02161,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,SNCF,Transport,UNKNOWN,Digital Twin for Monte Carlo Station,SNCF; Central-Government; Transport; Monaco,"The Monte-Carlo Station digital twin applies AI, simulation and sensor integration to support smarter operation of transport infrastructure. SNCF Gares & Connexions partnered with Akila and NVIDIA to deploy a real-time AI and simulation platform at Monte-Carlo train station, using Akila’s digital-twin platform and NVIDIA Omniverse technologies.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Railway stations are complex public environments where passenger flows, energy performance, building systems, safety, maintenance and emergency planning need to be managed together. The Monte-Carlo Station digital twin applies AI, simulation and sensor integration to support smarter operation of transport infrastructure. The operational problem is that station managers need to understand how people, heat, airflow, equipment and building systems interact in real time, but traditional facility-management systems often treat these elements separately. SNCF Gares & Connexions partnered with Akila and NVIDIA to deploy a real-time AI and simulation platform at Monte-Carlo train station, using Akila’s digital-twin platform and NVIDIA Omniverse technologies. At a conceptual level, the system merges several computational layers: a 3D digital twin for simulation, AI training based on historical and real-time data, and AI agents that can generate operational insights from cross-system analytics. The platform ingests data such as crowd movement, solar heating, airflow and IoT sensor feeds, allowing operators to visualise and interact with station conditions. The expected impact is better energy management, preventive maintenance, reduced intervention times, lower downtime, improved emergency simulation and new safety, security and crowd-control capabilities. The case is significant because it presents the station as a “physical AI” environment where built infrastructure can be continuously simulated, analysed and optimised rather than managed only through static building information.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2810,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02162,EU_PSTW_202609_02162,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Mercator Ocean International,R&D environmental protection.,UNKNOWN,AI-Powered Digital Twin of the Ocean,Mercator Ocean International; European Institution/Agency; R&D environmental protection.; European Union,"The European Digital Twin of the Ocean provides an AI-powered platform for exploring “what-if” scenarios and translating complex marine data into actionable intelligence. The case is significant because it treats digital twins as decision infrastructure for planetary-scale environmental governance, linking scientific modelling with practical policy and community use.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Ocean governance requires the ability to understand how climate change, pollution, coastal erosion, biodiversity loss and human activity may affect marine systems over different timescales. The European Digital Twin of the Ocean provides an AI-powered platform for exploring “what-if” scenarios and translating complex marine data into actionable intelligence. The operational problem is that policymakers, environmental agencies, researchers and communities need to anticipate future ocean conditions, but relevant information is spread across satellite observations, in-situ measurements, numerical models and specialised marine services. The EU DTO integrates data from the Copernicus Marine Service, EMODnet and other sources, combining them with artificial intelligence and advanced GPU computing. At a conceptual level, the platform creates a dynamic virtual representation of marine systems that can simulate scenarios such as Sargassum drift, turtle movements and fishing-risk exposure, coastal erosion protection, plastic-pollution drift and local coastal-risk projections. AI models can accelerate forecasting, producing results in seconds rather than hours and improving ocean-current prediction accuracy. The expected impact is more informed marine policy, better coastal and biodiversity planning, stronger pollution response, increased transparency and wider access to ocean intelligence as a public good. The case is significant because it treats digital twins as decision infrastructure for planetary-scale environmental governance, linking scientific modelling with practical policy and community use.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2811,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02163,EU_PSTW_202609_02163,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Erasmus Universiteit Rotterdam,R&D general public services.,"Democratic backsliding is difficult to analyse because it develops through complex interactions between institutions, political actors, citizens, media systems, legal frameworks and informal norms. The operational problem is that traditional democracy-monitoring tools can identify institutional deterioration, but they often struggle to simulate how different drivers of democratic erosion interact over time or how specific policy interventions might strengthen resilience.",TWIN4DEM: Strengthening Democratic Resilience Through Digital Twins,Erasmus Universiteit Rotterdam; Academic-Research; R&D general public services.; Netherlands,"TWIN4DEM addresses this challenge by using digital twin technology and computational social science to prototype dynamic representations of democratic systems in Czechia, France, Hungary and the Netherlands.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Democratic backsliding is difficult to analyse because it develops through complex interactions between institutions, political actors, citizens, media systems, legal frameworks and informal norms. TWIN4DEM addresses this challenge by using digital twin technology and computational social science to prototype dynamic representations of democratic systems in Czechia, France, Hungary and the Netherlands. The operational problem is that traditional democracy-monitoring tools can identify institutional deterioration, but they often struggle to simulate how different drivers of democratic erosion interact over time or how specific policy interventions might strengthen resilience. TWIN4DEM therefore brings together political scientists, computational social scientists, humanities scholars, technologists and democracy stakeholders to model the pathways leading to executive aggrandisement and threats to rule-of-law institutions. At a conceptual level, the project treats democratic systems as complex adaptive systems that can be represented through data aggregation, natural-language processing, agent-based modelling and dynamic simulation. These digital twins are intended to help researchers and stakeholders test hypotheses, simulate policy scenarios and anticipate democratic risks before they become entrenched. The project also emphasises GDPR-compliant, open and FAIR tools, participatory engagement with policymakers and civil society, and the creation of an open-source community around democracy research. The expected impact is stronger analytical capacity for identifying early warning signs of democratic decline, more evidence-based policy responses, and improved democratic resilience across Europe. The case is significant because it extends digital twin methods beyond physical infrastructure into institutional and political systems.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2812,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02164,EU_PSTW_202609_02164,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,University of Athens,Environmental protection n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,Dryads - AI-powered platform to manage forest resilience and mitigate climate change impacts,University of Athens; Academic-Research; Environmental protection n.e.c.; Greece,"Dryads addresses this challenge by developing an AI-powered environmental-monitoring platform for forest resilience and wildfire-risk management in Greece. The platform uses multimodal sensing and AI to extract microclimate profiles, identify anthropogenic risk factors, monitor fire outbreaks and predict fire evolution.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Wildfires are becoming more frequent and severe in Mediterranean regions, creating urgent pressure for better early warning, prevention and operational response. Dryads addresses this challenge by developing an AI-powered environmental-monitoring platform for forest resilience and wildfire-risk management in Greece. The operational problem is that fire risk is shaped by many interacting factors, including weather, microclimate, vegetation, human activity, terrain, sensor signals and real-time visual information. Traditional monitoring approaches may not combine these data sources quickly enough to detect danger, assess vulnerability or predict how a fire could evolve. Dryads is a cooperation project between the SCAN Laboratory of the National and Kapodistrian University of Athens and the Greek Ministry of Environment and Energy, funded by ICLEI with support from Google.org. The platform uses multimodal sensing and AI to extract microclimate profiles, identify anthropogenic risk factors, monitor fire outbreaks and predict fire evolution. At a conceptual level, the system combines IoT sensors, satellite imagery, weather data, real-time video streams and geospatial modelling into an AI-enabled forest-management environment. The expected impact is earlier wildfire detection, more accurate risk assessment, improved prevention planning, stronger protection for communities and better climate-adaptation capacity for forests. The case is significant because it moves wildfire governance from reactive response towards continuous sensing and predictive forest-resilience management.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2813,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02165,EU_PSTW_202609_02165,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Valencia,Public order and safety n.e.c.,"Extreme rainfall and flash floods create urgent information needs for emergency services, especially when roads, infrastructure and affected communities are difficult or dangerous to access.",Valencia shows how integrated geospatial systems can be used to build AI-empowered cities,City of Valencia; Local Government; Public order and safety n.e.c.; Spain,"The Valencia case concerns the use of drones, scientific expertise and high-resolution geospatial information after the DANA event that struck the province on 29 October 2024.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Extreme rainfall and flash floods create urgent information needs for emergency services, especially when roads, infrastructure and affected communities are difficult or dangerous to access. The Valencia case concerns the use of drones, scientific expertise and high-resolution geospatial information after the DANA event that struck the province on 29 October 2024. The operational problem was that emergency responders needed to assess damage rapidly, locate debris and vehicles, identify pollution and infrastructure impacts, and support decision-making in areas where ground access was constrained. CSIC activated its Disaster and Emergency Advisory Group on 30 October 2024, mobilising around 200 professionals from multiple research centres and deploying drones in the Barranco del Poyo and nearby municipalities such as Paiporta, Picanya, Catarroja and Massanassa. The implementation used low-altitude drone flights to collect high-resolution images, which were then used to detect dragged vehicles, contaminating waste and infrastructure damage, as well as to support three-dimensional modelling of affected areas. At a conceptual level, the case illustrates how integrated geospatial systems can turn emergency data into operational intelligence, giving responders and planners a rapid spatial understanding of disaster impacts. The expected impact is faster situational assessment, better prioritisation of emergency action, stronger scientific support for civil protection and improved preparation for future climate-related disasters. The case should be framed as emergency-response geospatial intelligence rather than as a fully institutionalised city digital twin unless stronger municipal evidence is added.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2814,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02166,EU_PSTW_202609_02166,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Nottingham City Council,Community development.,"The operational problem is that traditional two-dimensional drawings and isolated planning documents can make it difficult to assess the visual, spatial and cumulative effects of new development, particularly across a city centre undergoing major renewal.",Nottingham shows how integrated geospatial systems can be used to build AI-empowered cities,Nottingham City Council; Local Government; Community development.; United Kingdom,"Nottingham City Council’s digital-twin work uses 3D geospatial modelling to support planning, redevelopment and public engagement. Nottingham’s approach combines a 3D object model, real-world scans of buildings and location-specific planning data, using ArcGIS Urban and related GIS technology.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Urban regeneration requires planners, developers, elected officials and residents to understand how proposed projects will affect the physical city before construction begins. Nottingham City Council’s digital-twin work uses 3D geospatial modelling to support planning, redevelopment and public engagement. The operational problem is that traditional two-dimensional drawings and isolated planning documents can make it difficult to assess the visual, spatial and cumulative effects of new development, particularly across a city centre undergoing major renewal. Nottingham’s approach combines a 3D object model, real-world scans of buildings and location-specific planning data, using ArcGIS Urban and related GIS technology. At a conceptual level, the digital twin acts as a shared planning environment: it allows planners to visualise existing conditions, test development proposals, discuss schemes more interactively with developers and communicate future scenarios in a way that is easier for non-specialists to understand. The expected impact is a faster planning process, better-quality discussions with applicants, improved transparency, stronger investor confidence and more accessible public consultation. The case is relevant because it shows how a city-wide 3D model can become a practical planning tool rather than a purely visual showcase. It also demonstrates that AI-empowered or digitally enabled cities often depend first on high-quality geospatial data infrastructure, into which more advanced analytics and simulation functions can later be integrated.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2815,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02167,EU_PSTW_202609_02167,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,European AI Office,Housing development.,UNKNOWN,Digital Twin for the Reconstruction of Ukraine,European AI Office; European Institution/Agency; Housing development.; Ukraine,"The planned platform will generate 3D virtual replicas of Ukrainian cities and use AI models to categorise damage, compare reconstruction strategies and calculate costs. The case is significant because it applies AI-enabled digital twins to post-war reconstruction, where speed, accuracy, public trust and financial accountability are essential.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Reconstruction after large-scale destruction requires rapid damage assessment, transparent cost estimation, scenario comparison and coordination between municipalities, citizens, national authorities and international donors. The European Commission’s Digital Twin for the Reconstruction of Ukraine is being developed to support Ukrainian authorities across the full reconstruction cycle. The operational problem is that damage information can come from many sources, including satellite imagery, drone footage and written inspection reports, while planners need reliable and comparable assessments at building, neighbourhood and city scale. The planned platform will generate 3D virtual replicas of Ukrainian cities and use AI models to categorise damage, compare reconstruction strategies and calculate costs. At a conceptual level, the digital twin functions as a reconstruction-planning environment: it brings together spatial imagery, inspection data, AI-based damage analysis, scenario generation and financial estimation so that different rebuilding options can be evaluated against cost and policy objectives. The expected impact is faster and more consistent assessment of damage, more transparent reconstruction planning, improved municipal decision-making and stronger accountability to citizens and donors. The first phase will focus on residential buildings, with potential later extension to infrastructure such as rail, roads, bridges and energy substations. The case is significant because it applies AI-enabled digital twins to post-war reconstruction, where speed, accuracy, public trust and financial accountability are essential.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2816,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02168,EU_PSTW_202609_02168,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Cretan Government,General public services n.e.c.,"The operational problem is that hotel check-in, activity booking and verification of visitor information can create repetitive administrative work, delays at reception desks and errors in manual data handling.",CretanPass,Cretan Government; Local Government; General public services n.e.c.; Greece,"In the described model, travellers would use a digital identity and wallet to simplify interactions across the island, allowing hotels and service providers to verify information more efficiently and reduce duplicated paperwork. The available source is a secondary market article rather than an official public-administration, project or technology-provider source, and I did not find independent confirmation that CretanPass is operated by the Cretan Government or implemented at scale.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Tourism destinations need to streamline visitor services while maintaining trust, data security and efficient coordination across hotels, activity providers and local experiences. CretanPass is described in the available source as a blockchain-based tourism solution for Crete that provides tamper-proof digital identities and a unified wallet for tourists. The operational problem is that hotel check-in, activity booking and verification of visitor information can create repetitive administrative work, delays at reception desks and errors in manual data handling. In the described model, travellers would use a digital identity and wallet to simplify interactions across the island, allowing hotels and service providers to verify information more efficiently and reduce duplicated paperwork. At a conceptual level, blockchain is used as a trust layer for identity and transaction verification: the visitor’s credentials and booking-related information can be presented in a way that is harder to tamper with and easier to reuse across services. The expected impact is faster check-in, fewer administrative errors, improved guest satisfaction and smoother integration of tourism services. However, the evidence base for this case is weak. The available source is a secondary market article rather than an official public-administration, project or technology-provider source, and I did not find independent confirmation that CretanPass is operated by the Cretan Government or implemented at scale. For catalogue purposes, the case should therefore be treated cautiously and not presented as a confirmed public-sector government deployment unless a stronger source is added.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK|F08_ERROR_COMPLIANCE_RISK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2817,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02169,EU_PSTW_202609_02169,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Random Red Ltd,General public services n.e.c.,"Across borders, this trust can be slowed by manual verification, paper certificates, fragmented databases, semantic differences and uncertainty about whether a laboratory or instrument credential is valid. The operational challenge is that traditional certification and accreditation processes can be lengthy, costly and vulnerable to human error or data manipulation, while users often lack an immediate view of certificate or accreditation status.",Random Red Ltd. - Measurements and Digital Calibration Certificates,Random Red Ltd; Non-governmental; General public services n.e.c.; European Union,"Random Red Ltd.’s EBSI use case addresses these problems by applying blockchain-based verifiable credentials to metrology and calibration. The case is significant because it applies blockchain to a narrow but economically important public-interest function: reliable measurement underpins trade, safety, consumer protection, health and environmental monitoring.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Scientific measurement, calibration and conformity-assessment processes depend on trust in the credentials of laboratories, instruments and certification bodies. Across borders, this trust can be slowed by manual verification, paper certificates, fragmented databases, semantic differences and uncertainty about whether a laboratory or instrument credential is valid. Random Red Ltd.’s EBSI use case addresses these problems by applying blockchain-based verifiable credentials to metrology and calibration. The operational challenge is that traditional certification and accreditation processes can be lengthy, costly and vulnerable to human error or data manipulation, while users often lack an immediate view of certificate or accreditation status. The proposed infrastructure combines digital wallets, verifiable credentials and blockchain verification through the European Blockchain Services Infrastructure. At a conceptual level, national accreditation bodies, laboratories and companies can issue, store, present and verify calibration certificates and laboratory credentials in machine-readable form. For example, a laboratory technician can receive a laboratory-accreditation credential, issue a digital calibration certificate for an instrument, and allow a company or authority in another Member State to verify the certificate against the EBSI ledger. The expected impact is faster cross-border recognition, reduced administrative overhead, stronger transparency, greater resistance to fraud and improved trust in precision-dependent industries. The case is significant because it applies blockchain to a narrow but economically important public-interest function: reliable measurement underpins trade, safety, consumer protection, health and environmental monitoring.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK|F07_DATA_VISIBILITY_INTEGRATION_GAP,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2818,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02170,EU_PSTW_202609_02170,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""social_nonprofit""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,European Commission,General public services n.e.c.,"Cross-border e-commerce creates large volumes of parcels and customs declarations, making it difficult for customs authorities, marketplaces, sellers and brokers to verify transaction data quickly and reliably. The operational challenge is that fraud can occur through undervaluation, incorrect origin information, false declarations or misuse of IOSS numbers, while legitimate sellers and brokers also face delays, penalties and administrative friction when data are incomplete or hard to verify.",e-Origin - Building the foundation of a cross-border e-commerce verification services for customs declarations using EBSI,European Commission; European Institution/Agency; General public services n.e.c.; Belgium,e-Origin addresses this problem by using EBSI-based verifiable credentials and digital wallets to strengthen trust in commercial and shipment information used for customs procedures. The case is significant because it applies blockchain to a concrete Single Market and customs-control bottleneck: how to verify high-volume parcel data across borders while preserving interoperability and trust.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Cross-border e-commerce creates large volumes of parcels and customs declarations, making it difficult for customs authorities, marketplaces, sellers and brokers to verify transaction data quickly and reliably. e-Origin addresses this problem by using EBSI-based verifiable credentials and digital wallets to strengthen trust in commercial and shipment information used for customs procedures. The operational challenge is that fraud can occur through undervaluation, incorrect origin information, false declarations or misuse of IOSS numbers, while legitimate sellers and brokers also face delays, penalties and administrative friction when data are incomplete or hard to verify. e-Origin proposes a blockchain-based trust framework where relevant actors can issue, hold and verify credentials related to shipments, commercial transactions and compliance data. At a conceptual level, the system turns customs-relevant information into structured credentials that can be checked by authorised parties without relying solely on paper documentation or repeated manual verification. Customs brokers can receive and present trusted data, online sellers and marketplaces can demonstrate compliance more efficiently, and authorities can improve auditability. The expected impact is faster clearance, reduced fraud risk, lower administrative burden, fewer disputes and a more transparent e-commerce import ecosystem. The case is significant because it applies blockchain to a concrete Single Market and customs-control bottleneck: how to verify high-volume parcel data across borders while preserving interoperability and trust. It should be framed as a customs-compliance infrastructure pilot rather than a fully operational EU-wide service.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F06_HIGH_VOLUME_SCALABILITY_BOTTLENECK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2819,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02171,EU_PSTW_202609_02171,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""social_nonprofit"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,European Commission,Fishing and hunting,UNKNOWN,TRACE4EU - Building the foundation of traceable seafood supply-chain infrastructure,European Commission; European Institution/Agency; Fishing and hunting; European Union,"TRACE4EU Seafood Tracing uses the European Blockchain Services Infrastructure to build a traceability model for seafood products, with a focus on Norwegian king crab and related supply-chain actors. TRACE4EU uses verifiable credentials, QR codes and blockchain timestamping to create an auditable chain of events for seafood products.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Seafood supply chains require reliable traceability because illegal, unreported and unregulated fishing, mislabelling and weak documentation can undermine food safety, sustainability and consumer trust. TRACE4EU Seafood Tracing uses the European Blockchain Services Infrastructure to build a traceability model for seafood products, with a focus on Norwegian king crab and related supply-chain actors. The operational problem is that information about catch origin, fisher identity, product handling, customs clearance, packaging, transport and final sale may be stored in separate systems or exchanged through documents that are hard to verify. TRACE4EU uses verifiable credentials, QR codes and blockchain timestamping to create an auditable chain of events for seafood products. At a conceptual level, each step in the product journey can be recorded and linked: a fisher receives an identity credential, catch information is registered, product and sales-order credentials are issued, packaging and customs credentials are added, and downstream buyers or consumers can verify provenance against the ledger. This creates a digital traceability layer that supports authorities, intermediaries, retailers and end customers. The expected impact is stronger detection of illegal fishing, improved confidence in catch certificates, better food-safety oversight, more transparent supply-chain documentation and support for future Digital Product Passport capabilities. The case is relevant because it applies EBSI to supply-chain integrity in a sector where public regulation, market trust and environmental sustainability are closely connected. It should be treated as an EBSI pilot infrastructure case rather than a fully scaled operational system.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2820,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02172,EU_PSTW_202609_02172,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,European Commission,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.","Innovative SMEs and start-ups often struggle to access cross-border financing because investors, public funding bodies and support organisations need to verify business credibility, eligibility, documents, due-diligence information and “investability” before committing resources. The operational problem is that investment readiness data are often fragmented across incubators, accelerators, public agencies, funders and company documents, creating repeated checks and slow onboarding.",OnePass: SME Financing,"European Commission; European Institution/Agency; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; European Union","OnePass uses EBSI to create a trusted framework in which SMEs can hold and present digital credentials, while investors and funding intermediaries can verify them more efficiently. The case is significant because it applies blockchain-based identity and credentials to financing rather than to token speculation.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Innovative SMEs and start-ups often struggle to access cross-border financing because investors, public funding bodies and support organisations need to verify business credibility, eligibility, documents, due-diligence information and “investability” before committing resources. OnePass SME Financing addresses this problem by proposing a verifiable-credentials framework for start-ups, investors and trusted operators in the European innovation ecosystem. The operational problem is that investment readiness data are often fragmented across incubators, accelerators, public agencies, funders and company documents, creating repeated checks and slow onboarding. OnePass uses EBSI to create a trusted framework in which SMEs can hold and present digital credentials, while investors and funding intermediaries can verify them more efficiently. At a conceptual level, the model turns business credentials into reusable, tamper-resistant digital proofs: rather than repeatedly submitting the same documents to different funding actors, an SME can build a trusted digital profile that demonstrates relevant attributes such as participation in recognised incubators, eligibility for funding, or verification by trusted service operators. The expected impact is lower due-diligence friction, reduced transaction costs, faster cross-border investment, improved trust between investors and SMEs, and more scalable support for European innovation. The case is significant because it applies blockchain-based identity and credentials to financing rather than to token speculation. Its public value lies in strengthening the European start-up support ecosystem, especially where cross-border investment is limited by trust and verification barriers. It should be treated as a pilot or standard-building use case rather than a fully deployed financing marketplace.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2821,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02173,EU_PSTW_202609_02173,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,CSC – IT Center for Science,R&D economic affairs,UNKNOWN,LUMI AI Supercomputer and High-Performance Computing (HPC),CSC – IT Center for Science; Academic-Research; R&D economic affairs; Finland,"The operational problem is that many organisations have promising AI use cases but lack the infrastructure, expertise or data pipelines needed to test and scale them responsibly. At a conceptual level, it brings together compute, data and human expertise so that AI models can be trained, evaluated and deployed more effectively.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"AI development increasingly depends on access to computing resources that exceed the capacity of ordinary organisational IT systems, especially when models require large-scale training, simulation, optimisation or processing of sensitive scientific and industrial datasets. The LUMI AI Factory addresses this need by combining one of Europe’s most powerful supercomputing environments with expert support, curated datasets and an innovation ecosystem for companies, researchers and public-sector actors. Hosted by CSC in Kajaani, Finland, LUMI provides AI-optimised computing capacity for users working in sectors such as manufacturing, healthcare, language technologies, digital twins and climate research. The operational problem is that many organisations have promising AI use cases but lack the infrastructure, expertise or data pipelines needed to test and scale them responsibly. The LUMI AI Factory therefore functions as an enabling platform rather than a single AI application: it gives users access to high-performance computing, tailored training, expert services and “Datasets as a Service”, including Finnish and Swedish speech data, pathology imaging and European web content. At a conceptual level, it brings together compute, data and human expertise so that AI models can be trained, evaluated and deployed more effectively. The expected impact is faster experimentation, stronger AI adoption by SMEs and start-ups, improved research capacity, better support for public-sector innovation and stronger European technological sovereignty in AI and high-performance computing.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2822,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02174,EU_PSTW_202609_02174,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Luxprovide SA,R&D economic affairs,"The Luxembourg AI Factory addresses this challenge by creating a trusted national AI infrastructure built around MeluXina-AI, an AI-optimised supercomputer intended to support large-scale model training and AI services. The operational problem is that start-ups, SMEs, researchers and public-sector organisations may lack access to the compute power, datasets, financial instruments and compliance support needed to move from experimentation to production-grade AI.",L-AIF - Luxembourg AI Factory,Luxprovide SA; Non-governmental; R&D economic affairs; Luxembourg,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Advanced AI development requires secure computing capacity, reliable access to data, specialist support and governance conditions that allow companies and public institutions to train, fine-tune and deploy models responsibly. The Luxembourg AI Factory addresses this challenge by creating a trusted national AI infrastructure built around MeluXina-AI, an AI-optimised supercomputer intended to support large-scale model training and AI services. The operational problem is that start-ups, SMEs, researchers and public-sector organisations may lack access to the compute power, datasets, financial instruments and compliance support needed to move from experimentation to production-grade AI. L-AIF is designed to provide end-to-end AI services, tailored support for adopters, secure supercomputing access and high-quality datasets, with a focus on sectors where Luxembourg has strategic strengths, including finance, space, cybersecurity and the green economy. At a conceptual level, the AI Factory acts as a national AI adoption platform: it combines infrastructure, data access, research expertise, business engagement and skills development in one coordinated ecosystem. L-AIF is supported by a consortium of LuxProvide, Luxinnovation, the Luxembourg National Data Service, LIST and the University of Luxembourg, with LuxProvide as coordinator. The expected impact is stronger national digital transformation, more secure and trusted AI adoption, improved access for start-ups and SMEs, and better integration of Luxembourg into the European network of AI Factories. The case is relevant because it treats AI compute, data governance and support services as strategic public-interest infrastructure",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2823,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02175,EU_PSTW_202609_02175,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,High-Performance Computing Centre Stuttgart (HLRS),R&D economic affairs,"The operational problem is that SMEs, start-ups, industrial users and academic researchers may know where AI could create value, but lack the computing infrastructure or specialist support needed to train, scale and deploy robust applications.",HammerHAI - New AI-optimised Supercomputer,High-Performance Computing Centre Stuttgart (HLRS); Academic-Research; R&D economic affairs; Germany,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Industrial and scientific AI adoption depends on more than access to models: organisations need high-performance computing, suitable datasets, open-source tools, technical expertise and support across the full AI lifecycle. HammerHAI addresses this need by establishing Germany’s AI Factory for manufacturing, engineering and research, coordinated by the High-Performance Computing Center Stuttgart and operated by a consortium of German academic research centres. The operational problem is that SMEs, start-ups, industrial users and academic researchers may know where AI could create value, but lack the computing infrastructure or specialist support needed to train, scale and deploy robust applications. HammerHAI provides access to hardware, tools, training and personalised consulting, including a “Concierge Service” intended to connect users with resources, knowledge and partners. Its sectoral positioning is particularly relevant for manufacturing and engineering, where AI can support simulation, optimisation, predictive maintenance, product development and research workflows. At a conceptual level, HammerHAI is designed as a one-stop AI adoption environment: it combines high-performance computing systems, data-management capabilities, open-source software, pre-trained models, datasets, training curricula and an expertise network. The initiative also plans to host a cloud-accessible, AI-optimised supercomputer at HLRS, with data handling framed as secure and compliant with the European AI Act. The expected impact is lower barriers to industrial AI adoption, faster development of applied AI services and stronger European capacity for trustworthy, scalable AI in research and industry",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2824,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02176,EU_PSTW_202609_02176,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,INSAIT,R&D economic affairs,"The operational problem is that advanced AI development requires infrastructure, talent, domain expertise and support services that individual organisations often cannot assemble alone.",BRAIN++ New AI-optimised Supercomputer,INSAIT; Academic-Research; R&D economic affairs; Bulgaria,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Bulgaria’s BRAIN++ initiative is designed to strengthen the country’s position in the European AI ecosystem by combining a new AI-optimised supercomputer with a national AI hub for government, education and private-sector users. The operational problem is that advanced AI development requires infrastructure, talent, domain expertise and support services that individual organisations often cannot assemble alone. BRAIN++ responds by creating a modern GPU-based AI data centre in Sofia, linked to Discoverer++, a next-generation supercomputer tailored for AI workloads, and a broader Bulgarian AI Factory offering one-stop-shop support for responsible AI adoption. The initiative is led by INSAIT in collaboration with Sofia Tech Park and has a reported value of EUR 90 million. At a conceptual level, BRAIN++ acts as both infrastructure and ecosystem: it will provide compute capacity while also supporting Bulgarian-language large language models, robotics AI, space-observation AI, product manufacturing, trustworthy AI compliance tools, federated AI data lakes and collaborative physical and virtual workspaces through the BulgAI Sandbox. The expected impact is to support start-ups and SMEs, develop national AI talent, accelerate responsible AI entrepreneurship and position Bulgaria as a regional AI innovation leader. The case is relevant because it links frontier AI infrastructure to specific national priorities, including language technology and robotics, while also connecting Bulgaria to the wider EuroHPC AI Factory network. It should be framed as an AI Factory under development rather than a mature recurring public service",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2825,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02177,EU_PSTW_202609_02177,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Swedish National AI Innovation Hub,R&D economic affairs,"Organisations that want to adopt AI often face a gap between strategic ambition and practical implementation: they may lack computing resources, model-development expertise, trusted AI governance, data-management capability or staff training.",MIMER New AI-optimised Supercomputer,Swedish National AI Innovation Hub; Academic-Research; R&D economic affairs; Sweden,"At a conceptual level, Mimer acts as an intermediary between AI users and the European high-performance computing ecosystem: it helps users identify the right access pathway, apply for compute resources, structure their projects and move from proof of concept to scalable use.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Organisations that want to adopt AI often face a gap between strategic ambition and practical implementation: they may lack computing resources, model-development expertise, trusted AI governance, data-management capability or staff training. Mimer, Sweden’s AI Innovation Factory, addresses this gap by providing free access to powerful GPUs and expert support for companies, researchers and public-sector organisations. The operational problem is that advanced AI projects require more than software tools; they need suitable compute allocations, guidance on model optimisation, support for production-scale deployment and compliance with emerging expectations for trustworthy AI. Mimer brings these elements together through a service portfolio covering expert support, access to national and European supercomputing resources, secure data pipelines, training and upskilling, trustworthy AI services and wider ecosystem navigation. At a conceptual level, Mimer acts as an intermediary between AI users and the European high-performance computing ecosystem: it helps users identify the right access pathway, apply for compute resources, structure their projects and move from proof of concept to scalable use. The expected impact is faster AI experimentation, reduced infrastructure barriers for SMEs and researchers, stronger uptake of trustworthy AI practices and improved competitiveness of Sweden’s AI ecosystem. The case is relevant because it frames AI infrastructure as a service layer that combines hardware, expert guidance and governance support, rather than simply as a new supercomputer.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2826,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02178,EU_PSTW_202609_02178,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,IZUM – the Institute of Information Science,R&D economic affairs,UNKNOWN,SLAIF New AI-optimised Supercomputer,IZUM – the Institute of Information Science; Academic-Research; R&D economic affairs; Slovenia,"It will support use cases in energy optimisation, environmental monitoring, smart agriculture, healthcare, biotechnology, pharmaceuticals, language technologies, materials science and creative industries.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Slovenia’s AI ecosystem requires shared infrastructure capable of supporting large-scale model development, advanced scientific computing and industrial deployment across sectors where individual organisations may not have sufficient capacity. The Slovenian AI Factory, known as SLAIF, responds to this need by combining an AI-optimised EuroHPC supercomputer with a broader ecosystem for research, industry and public institutions. The operational problem is that AI expertise in Slovenia is distributed across universities, research institutes and companies, while access to advanced computing, data and deployment support remains essential for scaling AI applications. SLAIF is designed as a coordinated hub that bridges academia and industry, providing access to AI infrastructure, expertise, data, pre-trained models and tailored cloud-based AI services. The new supercomputer will be hosted by IZUM in collaboration with the Jožef Stefan Institute and Arnes, with the wider AI Factory involving Slovenian universities, research bodies, industry organisations and innovation actors. At a conceptual level, SLAIF connects hardware, datasets, domain expertise, training programmes and business support into a single national AI-enabling environment. It will support use cases in energy optimisation, environmental monitoring, smart agriculture, healthcare, biotechnology, pharmaceuticals, language technologies, materials science and creative industries. The expected impact is stronger national AI capacity, improved competitiveness, more accessible AI adoption for companies and researchers, and Slovenia’s closer integration into the European network of AI factories.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2827,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02179,EU_PSTW_202609_02179,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Poznań Supercomputing and Networking Center (PCSS),R&D economic affairs,"The operational challenge is that AI development in areas such as cybersecurity, space technologies, robotics, health, life sciences, sustainability and public services requires access to specialised compute resources, expert support and an ecosystem capable of translating research into applications. It is expected to support knowledge-sharing and applied AI development, helping Polish companies, researchers and public institutions build and test AI solutions at a scale that would otherwise be difficult to access.",PIAST AIF New AI-optimised Supercomputer,Poznań Supercomputing and Networking Center (PCSS); Academic-Research; R&D economic affairs; Poland,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Poland’s PIAST-AI Factory is intended to bridge the gap between advanced computing infrastructure and practical AI adoption across academia, industry and the public sector. The operational challenge is that AI development in areas such as cybersecurity, space technologies, robotics, health, life sciences, sustainability and public services requires access to specialised compute resources, expert support and an ecosystem capable of translating research into applications. PIAST-AI will be hosted at the Poznań Supercomputing and Networking Center and developed in cooperation with Poznań University of Technology, Adam Mickiewicz University, Nicolaus Copernicus University, regional clusters and innovation hubs. The initiative includes the acquisition of an AI computing server equipped with more than 1,000 graphics cards and integrated with Poland’s wider quantum and computing ecosystem. At a conceptual level, PIAST-AI is designed as a national platform for AI innovation: it will combine infrastructure, research expertise, industry engagement, ethical AI principles and sustainability considerations. It is expected to support knowledge-sharing and applied AI development, helping Polish companies, researchers and public institutions build and test AI solutions at a scale that would otherwise be difficult to access. The expected impact is stronger national AI capacity, improved digital competitiveness and closer integration of Poland into Europe’s technological sovereignty agenda",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2828,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02180,EU_PSTW_202609_02180,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Genci,R&D economic affairs,UNKNOWN,AI2F AI-ready Supercomputer,Genci; Central-Government; R&D economic affairs; France,"The operational problem is that advanced AI development requires not only national talent and research excellence, but also access to high-performance computing, support services and coordinated pathways for translating models into use across sectors.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"France’s AI Factory France, or AI2F, is designed to accelerate AI adoption by building on the country’s decentralised AI ecosystem of research laboratories, universities, schools, large companies, start-ups, data centres, venture capital actors, incubators and AI associations. The operational problem is that advanced AI development requires not only national talent and research excellence, but also access to high-performance computing, support services and coordinated pathways for translating models into use across sectors. AI2F responds by leveraging state-of-the-art supercomputing infrastructure and support services to foster AI in research, innovation and public services in France and Europe. The consortium is led by GENCI, with partners including AMIAD, CEA, CINES, CNRS, France Universités, Inria, The French Tech, Station F and HubFranceIA. At a conceptual level, AI2F acts as a national AI infrastructure and service platform, connecting existing French computing resources with the forthcoming Alice Recoque EuroHPC exascale supercomputer. Because Alice Recoque is expected to start deployment in 2026, AI2F will initially provide access to existing national infrastructures such as Jean Zay, Adastra and Joliot-Curie. The initiative is open to all sectors and explicitly targets defence, energy, aerospace, edtech, agriculture, finance, humanities, robotics, health, earth sciences, materials science and mobility. The expected impact is faster AI adoption, stronger collaboration between research and industry, and enhanced French and European technological sovereignty",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2829,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02181,EU_PSTW_202609_02181,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,GRNET (National Infrastructures for Research and Technology),R&D economic affairs,UNKNOWN,Pharos AI Factory,GRNET (National Infrastructures for Research and Technology); Consortium; R&D economic affairs; Greece,"It will use the DAEDALUS supercomputer as its computing platform, with the source stating that DAEDALUS will offer 89 Pflops of computing power and that a significant share will be reserved for AI applications through Pharos.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Greece’s Pharos AI Factory is intended to create a national AI ecosystem that connects academia, industry and the public sector around high-performance computing resources and responsible AI development. The operational problem is that Greek researchers, start-ups, public organisations and companies need access to advanced compute capacity and AI expertise if they are to develop competitive applications in fields of national relevance. Pharos focuses on three priority areas: health, Greek language and culture, and sustainable development. It will use the DAEDALUS supercomputer as its computing platform, with the source stating that DAEDALUS will offer 89 Pflops of computing power and that a significant share will be reserved for AI applications through Pharos. The consortium is led by GRNET under the Ministry of Digital Governance, with partners including the National Centre for Scientific Research “Demokritos”, the National Technical University of Athens, the Athena Research Center, Growthfund and several affiliated research and university entities. At a conceptual level, Pharos is a national one-stop environment for AI innovation: it provides access to computing resources and AI tools, supports new business models, and helps public-sector bodies and researchers move from experimentation towards applied solutions. The expected impact is stronger Greek technological autonomy, new jobs, support for brain regain, improved competitiveness and contribution to the broader European AI Factory network. The case is relevant because it connects AI infrastructure to culturally and socially specific priorities, including Greek-language technologies and health applications",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2830,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02182,EU_PSTW_202609_02182,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Advanced Computing Austria (ACA),R&D economic affairs,"The case is significant because it treats AI adoption as a systemic challenge: access to hardware must be paired with skills, services and governance",AI:AT New AI-optimised Supercomputer,Advanced Computing Austria (ACA); Academic-Research; R&D economic affairs; Austria,"The expected impact is stronger national capacity for trustworthy AI applications, more efficient training of complex models, faster transfer of AI into practical use cases and improved cooperation between research and industry.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Austria’s AI Factory Austria, or AI:AT, is designed to expand the country’s AI ecosystem by combining a new AI-optimised supercomputer with an AI Factory Hub that supports research, industry and public administration. The operational problem is that many organisations require greater computing capacity, technical support and access to AI expertise before they can train larger models or deploy AI solutions in production-relevant settings. AI:AT builds on the Vienna Scientific Cluster and will acquire new AI-optimised supercomputing infrastructure available to researchers, start-ups, companies and public administrations. The project is led by Advanced Computing Austria and the AIT Austrian Institute of Technology, with a broader network of academic and industrial partners. At a conceptual level, AI:AT is both infrastructure and ecosystem: it provides computational capacity while also expanding support services, software solutions, training, project assistance and cross-sector collaboration. The initiative is particularly relevant to manufacturing, where AI can improve production planning, quality assurance, efficiency and sustainability, but it also supports broader Austrian and European AI competitiveness. The expected impact is stronger national capacity for trustworthy AI applications, more efficient training of complex models, faster transfer of AI into practical use cases and improved cooperation between research and industry. The case is significant because it treats AI adoption as a systemic challenge: access to hardware must be paired with skills, services and governance",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2831,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02183,EU_PSTW_202609_02183,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,VSB – Technical University of Ostrava,R&D economic affairs,"The operational challenge is that many AI applications require specialised computing resources, secure data environments, software tools, expert teams and training that are difficult for individual organisations to access alone.",KarolAIna - petascale EuroHPC supercomputer in Czechia,VSB – Technical University of Ostrava; Academic-Research; R&D economic affairs; Czechia,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Czech AI Factory is intended to strengthen the Czech Republic’s ability to develop, test and deploy artificial intelligence across industry, research, public administration and start-ups. The operational challenge is that many AI applications require specialised computing resources, secure data environments, software tools, expert teams and training that are difficult for individual organisations to access alone. CZAI addresses this by establishing a national AI Factory led by VSB – Technical University of Ostrava, with a consortium that includes the Czech Technical University in Prague, Charles University, the International Neurodegenerative Disorders Research Center, the Institute of Organic Chemistry and Biochemistry of the Czech Academy of Sciences and Brno University of Technology. At the centre of the initiative is KarolAIna, a new AI-optimised supercomputer building on the Karolina EuroHPC petascale system at IT4Innovations. KarolAIna will include approximately 340 state-of-the-art AI chips and deliver around 850 PFlop/s in AI operations. At a conceptual level, the Factory acts as a national AI adoption and innovation hub, offering compute, support services, training and expertise across manufacturing, energy, healthcare, public administration, cybersecurity and mobility. It will also establish AI campuses in Prague and Brno to complement the existing Ostrava ecosystem and support talent development. The expected impact is stronger Czech AI sovereignty, better industry uptake, improved public-sector capacity and closer integration into the European network of AI Factories",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2832,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02184,EU_PSTW_202609_02184,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Handary,R&D economic affairs,"Traditional shelf-life management often relies on empirical estimates, laboratory testing at limited points in time and conservative date-marking assumptions that may not fully reflect real production and distribution conditions.",Shelfex AI - Predictive AI transforming shelf life management,Handary; Private sector; R&D economic affairs; Belgium,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Food manufacturers need reliable methods to determine shelf life, prevent spoilage, ensure regulatory compliance and maintain consumer trust across complex supply chains. Traditional shelf-life management often relies on empirical estimates, laboratory testing at limited points in time and conservative date-marking assumptions that may not fully reflect real production and distribution conditions. Shelfex AI, developed by the Belgian food safety technology company Handary, addresses this problem by combining biotechnology, analytical science and artificial intelligence into a predictive framework for food integrity. The operational problem is that spoilage and microbial risk are dynamic: they depend on ingredient quality, processing parameters, packaging, storage conditions and distribution environments. Shelfex AI integrates laboratory data and production parameters to authenticate ingredients, predict spoilage before it occurs and support compliance with date-marking requirements. It is complemented by Microfier smart labels, which provide real-time microbial signals directly on packaging, and Hékôn intelligent micro-perforated membranes, which regulate gas balance and monitor storage conditions during distribution. At a conceptual level, the system turns shelf-life management from a retrospective or judgement-based process into a data-driven predictive control system. The expected impact is improved food safety, reduced waste, more accurate date marking, stronger supply-chain transparency and greater trust between manufacturers, regulators and consumers. The case is relevant because it shows how AI can support food safety and sustainability through predictive monitoring of product integrity, although it is primarily a private-sector technology rather than a public-administration deployment.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2833,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02185,EU_PSTW_202609_02185,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Kaunas University of Technology (KTU),"Agriculture, forestry, fishing and hunting","The operational problem is that conventional certification and paperwork can be manipulated, and authorities or buyers may struggle to verify whether timber was harvested legally, transported through legitimate channels and processed according to sustainability rules.",Blockchain for trade,"Kaunas University of Technology (KTU); Academic-Research; Agriculture, forestry, fishing and hunting; Lithuania",UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Illegal timber trade creates environmental, regulatory and geopolitical risks, particularly where sanctioned wood products may enter European supply chains through indirect routes or falsified documentation. The Lithuanian blockchain-for-trade case addresses this problem by proposing a blockchain-based traceability system for timber, with specific relevance to Russian and Belarusian wood entering EU markets. The operational problem is that conventional certification and paperwork can be manipulated, and authorities or buyers may struggle to verify whether timber was harvested legally, transported through legitimate channels and processed according to sustainability rules. Researchers from Kaunas University of Technology and Vytautas Magnus University developed a model that assigns each log a unique identifier and records its journey on a blockchain, from permits through transport and sale. Smart contracts are used to validate each action, including blocking attempts to harvest without permits or introduce fictitious suppliers. At a conceptual level, the system creates a tamper-resistant audit trail for timber, combining product-level identification, sustainability scoring and automated rule enforcement. The source reports that simulation tests processed up to 550 transactions per second and that participants with higher sustainability scores achieved up to 95% successful trades. The expected impact is stronger enforcement against illegal timber, better supply-chain transparency, improved buyer confidence and more effective implementation of sanctions and deforestation-related due diligence",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2834,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02186,EU_PSTW_202609_02186,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,31Third,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.","In on-chain finance, these challenges become more pronounced because digital assets, tokenised securities and decentralised exchanges operate with different execution mechanisms and transparency expectations from traditional markets. The operational problem is that managers need to maintain portfolio allocations in line with predefined strategies, but manual trading across multiple assets can be costly, slow and operationally risky.",Blockchain for Strategy Portfolio Rebalancing,"31Third; Private sector; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; European Union","31Third’s strategy-based portfolio rebalancing use case addresses this operational complexity by providing blockchain-native infrastructure for institutional on-chain asset managers and index funds. At a conceptual level, the system uses blockchain to make portfolio adjustment more transparent, programmable and efficient: strategic allocation rules are translated into automated execution logic, while routing mechanisms seek efficient execution across decentralised exchanges, market makers and liquidity pools.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Institutional asset management often involves complex operational processes when portfolios must be rebalanced across multiple assets, trading venues and compliance requirements. In on-chain finance, these challenges become more pronounced because digital assets, tokenised securities and decentralised exchanges operate with different execution mechanisms and transparency expectations from traditional markets. 31Third’s strategy-based portfolio rebalancing use case addresses this operational complexity by providing blockchain-native infrastructure for institutional on-chain asset managers and index funds. The operational problem is that managers need to maintain portfolio allocations in line with predefined strategies, but manual trading across multiple assets can be costly, slow and operationally risky. The platform allows users to define target allocations and then automatically rebalance portfolios in a single transaction, supported by smart contracts, smart order routing and compliance features. At a conceptual level, the system uses blockchain to make portfolio adjustment more transparent, programmable and efficient: strategic allocation rules are translated into automated execution logic, while routing mechanisms seek efficient execution across decentralised exchanges, market makers and liquidity pools. The expected impact is lower operational cost, reduced manual intervention, more accurate alignment with investment strategies and improved transparency for institutional investors. The case is relevant for public-sector innovation catalogues mainly as a regulatory-sandbox example, because it demonstrates how blockchain infrastructure may reshape institutional finance under supervision and dialogue with regulators rather than as a direct government-service deployment.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2835,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02187,EU_PSTW_202609_02187,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,31Third,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.","In traditional fund operations, this process can involve intermediaries, manual reconciliation and delayed portfolio adjustment. At a conceptual level, the platform connects fund-share issuance, redemption logic and smart-contract-powered portfolio execution: when fund shares are created or redeemed, the system adjusts the underlying asset basket so that allocations continue to reflect the fund’s target composition without requiring separate manual intervention.",Blockchain for Issuance- and Redemption-Triggered Rebalancing,"31Third; Private sector; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; European Union","31Third’s issuance- and redemption-triggered rebalancing use case applies blockchain automation to this specific operational problem. The case is particularly relevant because it illustrates how blockchain can be used not merely to represent assets digitally, but to automate specific financial-market operations that are traditionally handled through administrative and intermediary-heavy workflows.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"On-chain exchange-traded funds and index products require the underlying asset basket to remain aligned with the fund’s intended composition when new shares are issued or existing shares are redeemed. In traditional fund operations, this process can involve intermediaries, manual reconciliation and delayed portfolio adjustment. 31Third’s issuance- and redemption-triggered rebalancing use case applies blockchain automation to this specific operational problem. The system is designed for on-chain ETFs and similar portfolio products where subscription and redemption events automatically trigger rebalancing of the underlying assets. At a conceptual level, the platform connects fund-share issuance, redemption logic and smart-contract-powered portfolio execution: when fund shares are created or redeemed, the system adjusts the underlying asset basket so that allocations continue to reflect the fund’s target composition without requiring separate manual intervention. This creates a more direct link between investor activity and asset management. Smart order routing can optimise execution across decentralised exchanges, market makers and liquidity pools, while compliance features are intended to support institutional-grade operation. The expected impact is more efficient fund administration, lower execution friction, better transparency for investors and stronger scalability for on-chain index products. The case is particularly relevant because it illustrates how blockchain can be used not merely to represent assets digitally, but to automate specific financial-market operations that are traditionally handled through administrative and intermediary-heavy workflows. As with the related 31Third portfolio-rebalancing use case, it should be treated as a sandboxed financial-innovation case rather than a public-service deployment.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2836,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02188,EU_PSTW_202609_02188,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Blocksquare,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.","Real estate is traditionally illiquid, capital-intensive and difficult for smaller investors to access, while fractional ownership models often face legal, registry and enforceability challenges. The operational challenge is that real-estate tokenisation must provide more than a digital representation of value: investors need enforceable rights, transparency on the underlying asset and a legally recognised structure that can operate across European jurisdictions.",Blocksquare – Notarised Real Estate Tokenisation Framework,"Blocksquare; Private sector; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Slovenia",UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Real estate is traditionally illiquid, capital-intensive and difficult for smaller investors to access, while fractional ownership models often face legal, registry and enforceability challenges. Blocksquare’s notarised real-estate tokenisation framework addresses this problem by combining blockchain tokens with legally grounded property claims, notarised loan agreements and registered mortgages. The operational challenge is that real-estate tokenisation must provide more than a digital representation of value: investors need enforceable rights, transparency on the underlying asset and a legally recognised structure that can operate across European jurisdictions. Blocksquare’s framework enables property owners or marketplace operators to fractionalise economic exposure to real-estate assets while anchoring investor protection in notarised documentation and euro-denominated mortgage registration. At a conceptual level, the token becomes a digital instrument connected to a legally documented claim rather than merely a speculative blockchain asset. This structure can support decentralised trading, branded marketplaces and broader investor participation while attempting to preserve compliance with EU legal expectations. The expected impact is increased liquidity in real-estate markets, wider access to property-backed investment, more efficient marketplace creation and stronger transparency around tokenised assets. The case is relevant because it demonstrates a bridge between real-world assets and blockchain infrastructure. However, it is primarily a private-sector tokenisation framework, and the source should be used cautiously because it is industry media rather than a public regulator or government confirmation.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2837,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02189,EU_PSTW_202609_02189,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,CHARONIUM GmbH,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.","Digital assets such as cryptocurrencies, NFTs and tokenised real estate create practical and legal challenges for inheritance, because heirs, notaries, lawyers and financial institutions may not know what assets exist, how to verify ownership or how to transfer them securely after death. The expected impact is reduced legal uncertainty, lower administrative burden, fewer disputes, improved fraud resistance and clearer procedures for the inheritance of digital assets.",CHARONIUM GmbH - Legacy - Digital Inheritance Platform,"CHARONIUM GmbH; Private sector; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; European Union",UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Digital assets such as cryptocurrencies, NFTs and tokenised real estate create practical and legal challenges for inheritance, because heirs, notaries, lawyers and financial institutions may not know what assets exist, how to verify ownership or how to transfer them securely after death. CHARONIUM Legacy addresses this problem by providing a blockchain-powered platform for digital estate discovery, verification and transfer. The operational problem is that traditional probate and inheritance procedures were designed for bank accounts, physical property and registered financial assets, while decentralised digital assets may be held across wallets, exchanges and tokenised platforms. CHARONIUM Legacy is designed for legal professionals, notaries and financial institutions, offering automated asset verification across blockchains such as Bitcoin and Ethereum, centralised exchanges and tokenised platforms. At a conceptual level, the platform converts digital inheritance into a structured workflow: assets can be discovered, tokenised into proofs of ownership, registered in tamper-proof records and transferred through smart-contract-based processes under legal conditions. The system also supports beneficiary and investor management, automated notifications, probate processing, cross-border compatibility, AML alignment and GDPR compliance. The expected impact is reduced legal uncertainty, lower administrative burden, fewer disputes, improved fraud resistance and clearer procedures for the inheritance of digital assets. The case is significant because digital estate management is becoming increasingly important as crypto-assets and tokenised property enter mainstream wealth portfolios. For public-sector cataloguing, it should be presented as a regulated blockchain innovation case involving legal-sector processes, not as a government-run inheritance platform.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2838,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02190,EU_PSTW_202609_02190,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Traced Systems,Waste management.,UNKNOWN,Blockchain-Powered Digital Product Passport (DPP) for Battery Lifecycle Management,Traced Systems; Private sector; Waste management.; European Union,"The operational challenge is that batteries move through production, use, collection, recycling and secondary markets, but critical data may not follow the physical product in a secure, standardised and privacy-preserving way. The project developed and deployed a Digital Product Passport application over six months, with Instagrid providing manufacturing data, Batteriretur contributing collection and recycling logistics, Cling Systems supporting secondary battery transactions and Traced Systems deploying the Chromia blockchain for decentralised data management.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Battery value chains involve manufacturers, users, collectors, recyclers, marketplaces and regulators, each of whom needs reliable information about product origin, chemistry, condition, ownership, usage and end-of-life handling. The blockchain-powered Digital Product Passport pilot involving Traced Systems, Instagrid, Batteriretur and Cling Systems addresses this information problem in the downstream phase of the battery lifecycle. The operational challenge is that batteries move through production, use, collection, recycling and secondary markets, but critical data may not follow the physical product in a secure, standardised and privacy-preserving way. The project developed and deployed a Digital Product Passport application over six months, with Instagrid providing manufacturing data, Batteriretur contributing collection and recycling logistics, Cling Systems supporting secondary battery transactions and Traced Systems deploying the Chromia blockchain for decentralised data management. At a conceptual level, the Digital Product Passport acts as a controlled data layer for the battery: different actors receive role-based access to relevant information, while blockchain supports traceability, provenance and integrity. Manufacturers can gain downstream insight into battery use, collectors can access chemistry and lifecycle information to improve sorting and recycling, and secondary-market participants can verify battery health and provenance. The expected impact is improved circularity, better recycling efficiency, stronger regulatory alignment, greater confidence in second-life markets and more transparent sustainability reporting. The case is relevant because it demonstrates how blockchain can support practical industrial data exchange under emerging European product-passport and circular-economy requirements.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2839,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02191,EU_PSTW_202609_02191,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Multi,"General economic, commercial and labour affair",UNKNOWN,"Distributed ledger technology (DLT) to create a transparent, community-driven financial ecosystem","Multi; Private sector; General economic, commercial and labour affair; European Union","Multi, developed by Kunveno Teknologos, uses distributed ledger technology to create a community-driven financial ecosystem governed through on-chain proposal voting.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Decentralised finance aims to reduce reliance on centralised institutions, but many systems still face governance, transparency, liquidity and manipulation risks. Multi, developed by Kunveno Teknologos, uses distributed ledger technology to create a community-driven financial ecosystem governed through on-chain proposal voting. The operational problem is that digital-asset reserves and basket tokens need credible mechanisms for asset selection, governance and liquidity provision if they are to operate transparently and sustainably. Multi’s design includes two core tokens: a governance token for the decentralised autonomous organisation, and a fully collateralised basket token backed by a dynamic reserve of crypto assets. Participants propose asset allocations, while the reserve structure is determined through a meritocratic, game-theoretic competition mechanism. At a conceptual level, the system transforms portfolio governance into a decentralised process: participants compete to improve reserve allocation, the DAO defines the rules of the competition, and rewards are distributed through staking mechanisms. The platform also functions as a decentralised exchange through an automated multi-party trade algorithm, issuing fully collateralised tokens according to liquidity needs and dynamically adjusting supply to maintain market balance. The expected impact is greater transparency, more resilient decentralised governance, reduced dependence on intermediaries and a new model for community-managed financial infrastructure. The case should be framed as a blockchain sandbox and decentralised-finance innovation case, because it concerns private-sector financial infrastructure rather than a public-service tool.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2840,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02192,EU_PSTW_202609_02192,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Dowsers,"General economic, commercial and labour affair","The operational problem is that testing can identify known bugs, but it cannot prove the absence of entire classes of vulnerabilities across all possible execution states.",DOWSERS - blockchain for smart contracts,"Dowsers; Private sector; General economic, commercial and labour affair; European Union","DOWSERS provides a suite of software tools and algorithms that analyse source code from repositories and bytecode already deployed on blockchain networks. At a conceptual level, the platform applies mathematical reasoning to prove or disprove whether a smart contract satisfies specified properties, drawing on formal-verification techniques used in safety-critical sectors such as aerospace, nuclear systems, rail and automated transport.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Smart contracts can execute financial and operational logic automatically, but bugs or vulnerabilities in their code can lead to irreversible losses, exploitation or loss of trust in blockchain systems. DOWSERS addresses this problem by applying formal verification methods to smart contracts before or after deployment. The operational problem is that testing can identify known bugs, but it cannot prove the absence of entire classes of vulnerabilities across all possible execution states. This is particularly important in decentralised finance and token ecosystems, where a small error can expose developers, investors and institutions to major financial risk. DOWSERS provides a suite of software tools and algorithms that analyse source code from repositories and bytecode already deployed on blockchain networks. At a conceptual level, the platform applies mathematical reasoning to prove or disprove whether a smart contract satisfies specified properties, drawing on formal-verification techniques used in safety-critical sectors such as aerospace, nuclear systems, rail and automated transport. It can identify vulnerabilities including reentrancy attacks, race conditions, authorisation errors, buffer overflows and unsafe external calls, while also detecting ambiguities, inconsistencies and poor security practices. The expected impact is stronger trust in blockchain applications, better developer guidance, more informed investment decisions and reduced cybersecurity risk across decentralised systems. The case is relevant to public-sector and regulatory discussions because smart-contract assurance is a prerequisite for safe adoption of blockchain in finance, public registries, digital credentials and other high-trust domains.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2841,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02193,EU_PSTW_202609_02193,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Enoda ENSEMBLE,Fuel and energy,UNKNOWN,Decentralised Coordination of Distributed Energy Resources,Enoda ENSEMBLE; Private sector; Fuel and energy; European Union,"Enoda ENSEMBLE™ addresses this challenge by using distributed ledger technology to coordinate decentralised energy assets so that they can provide frequency and ancillary services to the grid. The implementation uses a private-permissioned blockchain, smart contracts, cryptographic safeguards and compliance features covering issues such as AML, KYC, GDPR and data protection.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Electricity systems are becoming harder to balance as renewable generation, heat pumps, electric vehicles and distributed energy resources create more variable patterns of supply and demand. Enoda ENSEMBLE™ addresses this challenge by using distributed ledger technology to coordinate decentralised energy assets so that they can provide frequency and ancillary services to the grid. The operational problem is that grid stability has historically depended on large centralised plants, including fossil-fuelled peaker plants, which can respond to frequency deviations but are expensive, carbon-intensive and poorly aligned with decarbonisation objectives. ENSEMBLE™ proposes a more decentralised model in which distributed assets can participate in balancing markets through secure, near-real-time coordination. The implementation uses a private-permissioned blockchain, smart contracts, cryptographic safeguards and compliance features covering issues such as AML, KYC, GDPR and data protection. At a conceptual level, the platform creates a trusted coordination layer between grid operators, aggregators and distributed assets, allowing performance data, transactions and balancing contributions to be recorded transparently. The expected impact is lower transaction costs, reduced barriers for renewable and distributed assets, greater grid flexibility and less reliance on conventional balancing generation. The case is relevant because it applies blockchain to a concrete energy-transition bottleneck: how to integrate decentralised clean-energy resources into operational grid services without losing trust, compliance or system stability. The source frames the case as part of the European Blockchain Sandbox third cohort rather than as evidence of routine market-wide deployment.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2842,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02194,EU_PSTW_202609_02194,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Fair Squares,Housing development.,"Affordable housing is a persistent challenge across the European Union, with many households facing high rents, limited supply and housing systems that can favour speculative returns over long-term social affordability.",Fair Squares: Affordable Housinf Funding Protocol,Fair Squares; Private sector; Housing development.; European Union,"The case is significant because it applies blockchain to a social-policy problem, not only to financial trading, but it should still be treated as a sandbox-stage financial and governance model rather than a confirmed operational public-housing programme.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Affordable housing is a persistent challenge across the European Union, with many households facing high rents, limited supply and housing systems that can favour speculative returns over long-term social affordability. Fair Squares proposes a blockchain-enabled funding and governance protocol designed to make housing investment more transparent, community-driven and affordability-oriented. The operational problem is that traditional affordable-housing delivery often depends either on constrained public budgets or large private investors whose return expectations may not align with tenants’ needs. Fair Squares introduces a distributed digital funding model in which investors hold small, traceable shares of a diversified pool of real housing assets, while tenants, investors and housing organisations participate in a more collaborative governance framework. At a conceptual level, tokenisation is used to distribute ownership or economic participation across multiple investors, reduce concentration risk and make the financing layer more transparent. Community-powered rent control is central to the model: expected returns influence rent levels, and the system is designed to keep investor returns modest and stable rather than speculative. Digital identifiers support secure and verifiable records of ownership and tenancy. The expected impact is a more stable housing-finance ecosystem, better alignment between investor incentives and tenant affordability, increased transparency and broader participation in affordable-housing funding. The case is significant because it applies blockchain to a social-policy problem, not only to financial trading, but it should still be treated as a sandbox-stage financial and governance model rather than a confirmed operational public-housing programme.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2843,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02195,EU_PSTW_202609_02195,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""social_nonprofit""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Frictionless Market,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.","Institutional finance still relies on settlement, custody, distribution and foreign-exchange processes that can be slow, intermediary-heavy and limited by market opening hours. The operational problem is that cross-border, multi-currency institutional transactions may involve settlement delays, counterparty risk, reconciliation work and fragmented access to tokenised assets.",Frictionless Markets Sarl (Frictionless Group Holdings Sarl),"Frictionless Market; Private sector; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; European Union","At a conceptual level, the system uses distributed ledger technology, multi-chain interoperability and decentralised oracle networks to connect traditional capital-market functions with blockchain-based settlement and transparency. However, within the catalogue it should be presented as a regulatory-sandbox use case, not as proof that all described capabilities are already operating at full market scale.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Institutional finance still relies on settlement, custody, distribution and foreign-exchange processes that can be slow, intermediary-heavy and limited by market opening hours. Frictionless Markets’ OnFi platform proposes a blockchain-based infrastructure for institutional digital capital markets, aiming to combine regulated financial operations with on-chain execution. The operational problem is that cross-border, multi-currency institutional transactions may involve settlement delays, counterparty risk, reconciliation work and fragmented access to tokenised assets. OnFi addresses this by enabling instant, borderless, 24/7 settlement across multiple currencies and by converting ERC20 assets and regulated stablecoins into fully backed institutional deposit tokens. The platform also supports access to tokenised money-market funds, yieldcoins, on-chain foreign exchange, order-book management, digital capital raising and on-chain issuance. At a conceptual level, the system uses distributed ledger technology, multi-chain interoperability and decentralised oracle networks to connect traditional capital-market functions with blockchain-based settlement and transparency. Chainlink is used for features such as proof of reserves and atomic cross-chain transfer support. The expected impact is faster settlement, lower operational risk, more efficient distribution of private-market products, improved transparency and a scalable foundation for institutional tokenised finance. The case is relevant because it sits at the intersection of traditional finance, regulated digital assets and DeFi infrastructure. However, within the catalogue it should be presented as a regulatory-sandbox use case, not as proof that all described capabilities are already operating at full market scale.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2844,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02196,EU_PSTW_202609_02196,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,EMERALD,Public order and safety n.e.c.,"Cloud-service certification depends on evidence that must remain trustworthy over time, but evidence-management processes can be complex, manual and vulnerable to doubts about integrity. Authorised users, including internal or external auditors, can also perform manual verification through a graphical interface.",EMERALD Blockchain-based trustworthiness system,EMERALD; Private sector; Public order and safety n.e.c.; European Union,"The case is relevant because it applies blockchain to assurance and certification, where trust depends on evidence integrity rather than on tokenisation or financial exchange. For catalogue purposes, it should be presented as a sandbox-stage cybersecurity assurance use case.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Cloud-service certification depends on evidence that must remain trustworthy over time, but evidence-management processes can be complex, manual and vulnerable to doubts about integrity. EMERALD addresses this problem through a blockchain-based trustworthiness system that supports cybersecurity certification and auditing of cloud services. The operational problem is that cloud service providers, certification bodies and auditors need to collect, store and verify evidence demonstrating security controls, while also preserving privacy and protecting sensitive technical information. EMERALD provides a framework in which evidence collectors can automatically generate proofs of integrity, while the wider system can automatically check whether stored evidence has been tampered with. Authorised users, including internal or external auditors, can also perform manual verification through a graphical interface. At a conceptual level, the blockchain layer does not need to expose all evidence publicly; rather, it records integrity proofs and supports long-term verifiability, traceability and resistance to modification. This allows certification evidence to remain auditable without compromising confidentiality. The expected impact is more efficient cybersecurity certification, stronger trust in cloud-service evidence, reduced administrative burden for auditors and providers, and improved capacity to demonstrate compliance over time. The case is relevant because it applies blockchain to assurance and certification, where trust depends on evidence integrity rather than on tokenisation or financial exchange. For catalogue purposes, it should be presented as a sandbox-stage cybersecurity assurance use case.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2845,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02197,EU_PSTW_202609_02197,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Billions Network,Public order and safety n.e.c.,"Online age verification creates a difficult policy trade-off: platforms need to restrict access to age-limited content or services, but conventional checks often require users to disclose sensitive personal information such as date of birth, identity documents or national identification numbers.",Blockchain-Based Age Estimation with Privado ID and Privately,Billions Network; Private sector; Public order and safety n.e.c.; European Union,"The Billions Network, Privado ID and Privately use case addresses this challenge through a privacy-preserving age-verification model using self-sovereign identity, verifiable credentials, zero-knowledge proofs and on-device AI age estimation. The case is significant because it uses blockchain and zero-knowledge cryptography to solve a concrete regulatory and rights-balancing problem, but it remains a sandbox-stage use case rather than a confirmed EU-wide operational standard.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Online age verification creates a difficult policy trade-off: platforms need to restrict access to age-limited content or services, but conventional checks often require users to disclose sensitive personal information such as date of birth, identity documents or national identification numbers. The Billions Network, Privado ID and Privately use case addresses this challenge through a privacy-preserving age-verification model using self-sovereign identity, verifiable credentials, zero-knowledge proofs and on-device AI age estimation. The operational problem is especially relevant for online services operating across multiple EU Member States, where legal requirements differ and platforms must also comply with GDPR and the European Digital Identity Framework. The implementation combines Privado ID’s decentralised identity technology with Privately’s AI-based age-estimation approach. Trusted entities can issue digitally signed age credentials, which are stored in a user-controlled wallet. When accessing an age-restricted service, the user can generate a proof that they meet the required threshold without revealing their exact age, date of birth or broader identity data. At a conceptual level, the system separates eligibility verification from identity disclosure, reducing data exposure while maintaining regulatory accountability. The expected impact is stronger privacy protection, better cross-border interoperability, lower risk of data misuse and a more proportionate approach to online safety and age-restricted access. The case is significant because it uses blockchain and zero-knowledge cryptography to solve a concrete regulatory and rights-balancing problem, but it remains a sandbox-stage use case rather than a confirmed EU-wide operational standard.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2846,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02198,EU_PSTW_202609_02198,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Oneboard,Public order and safety n.e.c.,"The operational problem is that onboarding and monitoring processes are costly, repetitive and difficult to keep aligned with changing regulatory frameworks such as MiCA, DORA and anti-money-laundering rules. The expected impact is faster onboarding, lower compliance costs, reduced manual workload, improved regulatory readiness and easier cross-jurisdictional operation for financial institutions.",Oneboard AG,Oneboard; Private sector; Public order and safety n.e.c.; European Union,The case is relevant because it applies blockchain-inspired identity and data-management mechanisms to a highly regulated operational function.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Financial institutions devote significant resources to Know Your Customer and Know Your Business processes, but these checks are often duplicated whenever a customer interacts with a new institution or when documents expire. Oneboard addresses this inefficiency through reusable, dynamically updated compliance profiles for financial institutions. The operational problem is that onboarding and monitoring processes are costly, repetitive and difficult to keep aligned with changing regulatory frameworks such as MiCA, DORA and anti-money-laundering rules. Oneboard proposes a model in which customers complete verification once, while their compliance data are stored securely, updated when documents expire and refreshed as regulatory requirements change. The platform integrates with existing banking and financial systems so that institutions can verify and monitor business customers without rebuilding compliance workflows from scratch. At a conceptual level, the system creates a portable compliance profile supported by decentralised storage, a compliance wallet and cryptographic security. This allows data integrity and privacy to be maintained while reducing repeated collection of the same documentation. The expected impact is faster onboarding, lower compliance costs, reduced manual workload, improved regulatory readiness and easier cross-jurisdictional operation for financial institutions. The case is relevant because it applies blockchain-inspired identity and data-management mechanisms to a highly regulated operational function. However, the available source describes the case within the European Blockchain Sandbox, so it should be treated as a regulatory-dialogue pilot rather than as evidence of full regular deployment across financial institutions.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2847,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02199,EU_PSTW_202609_02199,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Parfin,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.","Parfin’s Rayls platform addresses this challenge by providing an institutional-grade blockchain designed for banks, asset managers, financial market infrastructures and central banks.",Parfin – Institutional Blockchain Infrastructure for the European Blockchain Sandbox,"Parfin; Private sector; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; European Union","Rayls uses a hub-and-spoke architecture with private subnets, allowing institutions to operate in controlled environments while maintaining interoperability with broader blockchain ecosystems. The platform uses zero-knowledge proofs to protect transaction confidentiality while enabling regulatory oversight, and it is designed to support GDPR, KYC/AML and MiCA alignment.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Regulated financial institutions are exploring tokenisation, digital currencies and blockchain-based settlement, but they require privacy, compliance, interoperability and scalability before digital-asset infrastructure can be used in institutional environments. Parfin’s Rayls platform addresses this challenge by providing an institutional-grade blockchain designed for banks, asset managers, financial market infrastructures and central banks. The operational problem is that public blockchains offer transparency and programmability, but they may not provide the confidentiality, governance and regulatory controls required by regulated finance. Rayls uses a hub-and-spoke architecture with private subnets, allowing institutions to operate in controlled environments while maintaining interoperability with broader blockchain ecosystems. The platform uses zero-knowledge proofs to protect transaction confidentiality while enabling regulatory oversight, and it is designed to support GDPR, KYC/AML and MiCA alignment. At a conceptual level, Rayls provides a regulated blockchain layer for issuing digital currencies and tokenised assets, conducting cross-border transactions, enabling wholesale CBDCs, supporting on-chain trading of tokenised securities and facilitating bank-issued stablecoins. The expected impact is faster settlement, improved liquidity, reduced costs, more efficient cross-border payments and a stronger bridge between traditional finance and digital-asset markets. The case is significant because it addresses institutional adoption barriers rather than consumer crypto use. For catalogue purposes, it should be framed as a sandbox-stage financial infrastructure case selected for regulatory dialogue, not as a fully deployed public-sector blockchain system.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2848,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02200,EU_PSTW_202609_02200,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,PixelPai,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.","Home care is a labour-intensive sector facing recruitment, retention and workforce-sustainability challenges, while care providers often operate under tight financial margins. The operational problem is that home-care workers may receive limited additional reward for verified hours of care, while small and medium-sized care providers may struggle to attract investment or demonstrate compliance in a trusted way.",PIXELPAI APS - financial compliance infrastructure platform,"PixelPai; Private sector; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; European Union","The case is significant because it applies tokenisation to a social-care workforce problem rather than only to financial assets. However, given its source, it should be treated as a sandbox-stage use case selected for regulatory dialogue.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Home care is a labour-intensive sector facing recruitment, retention and workforce-sustainability challenges, while care providers often operate under tight financial margins. CareCredits.io proposes a blockchain-enabled model that links verified care work to tokenised finance, creating incentives for care workers and new financing routes for compliant care SMEs. The operational problem is that home-care workers may receive limited additional reward for verified hours of care, while small and medium-sized care providers may struggle to attract investment or demonstrate compliance in a trusted way. The CareCredits smart contract, built on the KXCO.io platform, integrates with care scheduling software through an API and rewards workers with wrapped utility tokens for every verified hour of care. These tokens are designed to supplement wages and can be redeemed through existing supermarket loyalty schemes, without increasing employer costs. At a conceptual level, the model connects real-world verified labour, utility tokens, security-token-backed investment and digital identity. LEI-based KYC/AML, ISO-standard vLEI digital identities and cryptographic verification are used to establish trust in participating companies, officers and care workers. The expected impact is improved worker retention, stronger recruitment incentives, additional financing options for care SMEs and greater transparency in the care ecosystem. The case is significant because it applies tokenisation to a social-care workforce problem rather than only to financial assets. However, given its source, it should be treated as a sandbox-stage use case selected for regulatory dialogue.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2849,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02201,EU_PSTW_202609_02201,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,CareCredits.io,Social protection n.e.c.,"Home care is a labour-intensive sector facing recruitment, retention and workforce-sustainability challenges, while care providers often operate under tight financial margins. The operational problem is that home-care workers may receive limited additional reward for verified hours of care, while small and medium-sized care providers may struggle to attract investment or demonstrate compliance in a trusted way.",Revolutionising home care system by linking verified care work to tokenised finance,CareCredits.io; Private sector; Social protection n.e.c.; European Union,"The case is significant because it applies tokenisation to a social-care workforce problem rather than only to financial assets. However, given its source, it should be treated as a sandbox-stage use case selected for regulatory dialogue.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Home care is a labour-intensive sector facing recruitment, retention and workforce-sustainability challenges, while care providers often operate under tight financial margins. CareCredits.io proposes a blockchain-enabled model that links verified care work to tokenised finance, creating incentives for care workers and new financing routes for compliant care SMEs. The operational problem is that home-care workers may receive limited additional reward for verified hours of care, while small and medium-sized care providers may struggle to attract investment or demonstrate compliance in a trusted way. The CareCredits smart contract, built on the KXCO.io platform, integrates with care scheduling software through an API and rewards workers with wrapped utility tokens for every verified hour of care. These tokens are designed to supplement wages and can be redeemed through existing supermarket loyalty schemes, without increasing employer costs. At a conceptual level, the model connects real-world verified labour, utility tokens, security-token-backed investment and digital identity. LEI-based KYC/AML, ISO-standard vLEI digital identities and cryptographic verification are used to establish trust in participating companies, officers and care workers. The expected impact is improved worker retention, stronger recruitment incentives, additional financing options for care SMEs and greater transparency in the care ecosystem. The case is significant because it applies tokenisation to a social-care workforce problem rather than only to financial assets. However, given its source, it should be treated as a sandbox-stage use case selected for regulatory dialogue.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2850,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02202,EU_PSTW_202609_02202,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Renewable Energy Communities (RECs),Fuel and energy,UNKNOWN,Blockchain to transform decentralised energy management and distribution,Renewable Energy Communities (RECs); Non-governmental; Fuel and energy; European Union,"The Stichting 2Tokens use case explores how blockchain technology, tokenisation, smart contracts and decentralised autonomous organisations can support transparent and participatory management of decentralised energy systems. The proposed model uses tokenisation to represent different rights and instruments, including energy tokens, governance tokens, membership shares, ownership participations, loans and bonds.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Renewable Energy Communities need governance, financing and operational tools that allow households, businesses and public bodies to co-own, manage and benefit from local energy assets. The Stichting 2Tokens use case explores how blockchain technology, tokenisation, smart contracts and decentralised autonomous organisations can support transparent and participatory management of decentralised energy systems. The operational problem is that local energy communities must coordinate ownership, energy offtake, voting rights, financing, membership and investment returns among multiple participants, while also respecting energy regulation, data protection, tax rules and financial-market requirements. The proposed model uses tokenisation to represent different rights and instruments, including energy tokens, governance tokens, membership shares, ownership participations, loans and bonds. Participants use digital wallets to store and manage these assets, track energy value and participate in community governance. A DAO structure supports democratic decision-making, allowing members to develop and vote on proposals, allocate resources and shape the community’s strategy. At a conceptual level, the blockchain system becomes a governance and accounting layer for the energy community, making rights, obligations and transactions more transparent and auditable. The expected impact is stronger local resilience, broader access to investment, improved operational transparency and more inclusive participation in decentralised energy management. The Amelander Energie Coöperatie is highlighted as the practical community context, demonstrating how DLT could support community ownership and financing of shared energy systems. The case remains best classified as a sandbox pilot rather than a mature recurring implementation.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2851,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02203,EU_PSTW_202609_02203,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Tree,Economic affairs n.e.c,"Tree Protocol addresses this challenge by providing a decentralised and tokenised knowledge-graph infrastructure for creating and managing Digital Product Passport data. The operational problem is that product information is often fragmented across brands, suppliers, logistics providers, repair services, resale platforms and recycling companies, making it difficult to share high-quality data across the full lifecycle.","Blockchain to power intelligent, scalable Digital Product Passport (DPP) infrastructure",Tree; Private sector; Economic affairs n.e.c; European Union,"Tree uses blockchain-based smart contracts, API interfaces and developer SDKs to allow DPP applications to be built and connected across platforms. At a conceptual level, the protocol turns product data into a structured and shareable knowledge layer: brands can provide verified product information, while circular-economy platforms can use it to improve repair, resale, recycling and consumer-facing services.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Product value chains increasingly require structured, trustworthy and interoperable data on origin, materials, lifecycle impacts, repairability, resale potential and recycling pathways. This need is becoming more urgent as the EU moves towards Digital Product Passport requirements under the Ecodesign for Sustainable Products Regulation. Tree Protocol addresses this challenge by providing a decentralised and tokenised knowledge-graph infrastructure for creating and managing Digital Product Passport data. The operational problem is that product information is often fragmented across brands, suppliers, logistics providers, repair services, resale platforms and recycling companies, making it difficult to share high-quality data across the full lifecycle. Tree uses blockchain-based smart contracts, API interfaces and developer SDKs to allow DPP applications to be built and connected across platforms. At a conceptual level, the protocol turns product data into a structured and shareable knowledge layer: brands can provide verified product information, while circular-economy platforms can use it to improve repair, resale, recycling and consumer-facing services. The use case is particularly relevant for textiles, where product composition, traceability and lifecycle information are essential for compliance and circular business models. Tree’s Shopify plugin and collaboration with Vaayu further support automated traceability-data collection through API integration. The expected impact is smoother DPP implementation, better compliance readiness, stronger circular-economy services and the conversion of compliance data into operational and commercial value. The case should be framed as a blockchain sandbox use case rather than a fully mature public infrastructure deployment.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2852,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02204,EU_PSTW_202609_02204,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Bridge10X,Environmental protection n.e.c.,"Bridge10X, operating through the Tokenforge framework, addresses this challenge by tokenising real-world ESG assets such as solar farms, geothermal plants and reforestation projects into compliant tradable digital securities. The operational problem is that many sustainability projects require substantial upfront investment, but access to such investments can be fragmented, illiquid and limited to specialised investors.",Platform to deliver a blockchain-agnostic framework for tokenising real-world ESG assets,Bridge10X; Private sector; Environmental protection n.e.c.; European Union,"AI is used to assess fair market value by analysing market data and ESG metrics, while smart contracts automate investment flows and help verify environmental impact against recognised standards. The case is relevant because it shows how tokenisation can be applied to environmental finance, but it remains a sandbox-stage use case and should not be presented as a fully operational public investment platform.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Financing sustainable infrastructure often requires matching long-term environmental projects with investors who need transparency, liquidity, regulatory assurance and credible valuation. Bridge10X, operating through the Tokenforge framework, addresses this challenge by tokenising real-world ESG assets such as solar farms, geothermal plants and reforestation projects into compliant tradable digital securities. The operational problem is that many sustainability projects require substantial upfront investment, but access to such investments can be fragmented, illiquid and limited to specialised investors. The proposed model converts physical ESG assets into digital securities backed by the underlying asset, while also linking them to carbon-allowance-backed digital units to strengthen liquidity and environmental value. At a conceptual level, the platform combines blockchain-agnostic tokenisation, AI-driven asset valuation, smart-contract automation and regulated investment infrastructure. AI is used to assess fair market value by analysing market data and ESG metrics, while smart contracts automate investment flows and help verify environmental impact against recognised standards. The model is designed to support retail and institutional participation through a cooperative structure, while preserving compliance through AML/KYC, custody, liability coverage and registrar services. The expected impact is broader access to green investment, improved transparency of ESG asset performance, more liquid markets for sustainability-linked assets and more scalable financing for climate-positive infrastructure. The case is relevant because it shows how tokenisation can be applied to environmental finance, but it remains a sandbox-stage use case and should not be presented as a fully operational public investment platform.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2853,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02205,EU_PSTW_202609_02205,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,University of Turku,Health n.e.c.,"The operational problem is that trustworthy AI systems for preventive healthcare, clinical decision support, education and health-technology assessment need extensive training and validation data, while hospitals and research organisations cannot simply pool sensitive health records in conventional centralised datasets. The case is significant because it addresses one of the central bottlenecks of medical AI: how to make data useful while keeping it protected.",Phase IV AI - Advanced AI Models for Health Data Privacy,University of Turku; Academic-Research; Health n.e.c.; Finland,PHASE IV AI addresses this structural barrier by developing privacy-compliant methods for using health data in AI development without requiring direct access to identifiable real-world patient data.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Healthcare AI depends on large, high-quality and representative datasets, but health data are often distributed across institutions and subject to strict legal, ethical and privacy constraints. PHASE IV AI addresses this structural barrier by developing privacy-compliant methods for using health data in AI development without requiring direct access to identifiable real-world patient data. The operational problem is that trustworthy AI systems for preventive healthcare, clinical decision support, education and health-technology assessment need extensive training and validation data, while hospitals and research organisations cannot simply pool sensitive health records in conventional centralised datasets. The project advances synthetic-data generation, testing and validation metrics, and multi-party computation protocols that allow AI development in privacy-preserving conditions. At a conceptual level, PHASE IV AI aims to create a “Health Data as a Service” model, where AI developers can access useful anonymous or synthetic data resources generated from decentralised de-identified sources, without exposing underlying patient information. The project also seeks to integrate data-market and data-service components into a cross-European health-data hub aligned with the European Health Data Space. The expected impact is improved access to usable health data for AI developers, stronger privacy protection, better validation of AI tools and greater adoption of health AI in high-impact disease areas, including cancer. The case is significant because it addresses one of the central bottlenecks of medical AI: how to make data useful while keeping it protected.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2854,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02206,EU_PSTW_202609_02206,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,City of Florence,General public services n.e.c.,"Municipal administrative procedures can be difficult for citizens to navigate, especially when they involve documentary requirements, eligibility conditions, deadlines and procedural steps that are spread across several web pages or offices.",Florence's First AI-powered chatbot,City of Florence; Local Government; General public services n.e.c.; Italy,FestinaLente is designed to answer natural-language questions using official municipal documentation validated by the relevant staff.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Municipal administrative procedures can be difficult for citizens to navigate, especially when they involve documentary requirements, eligibility conditions, deadlines and procedural steps that are spread across several web pages or offices. Florence’s FestinaLente chatbot addresses this problem by providing first-level AI assistance for citizens who need to change their official residence. The operational problem is that residence-change procedures generate many repetitive questions for demographic-services staff, while citizens may otherwise rely on phone calls, emails or in-person visits to understand what documents are required and how the process works. FestinaLente is designed to answer natural-language questions using official municipal documentation validated by the relevant staff. It supports 14 languages, making it useful not only for Italian residents but also for international users living in or moving to Florence. At a conceptual level, the chatbot acts as a structured conversational layer between the user and the municipal knowledge base: it does not replace administrative officials, but translates validated information into accessible guidance available at any time. The system is built on a modular AI platform that allows the city to choose among different large language models, with ChatGPT used as the main engine. The expected impact is reduced routine workload for municipal employees, clearer access to demographic-service information, fewer procedural errors by citizens and a better user experience for residents interacting with the municipality. The case is relevant because it shows how generative AI can be applied to a narrow, high-demand municipal procedure in a controlled and document-based way.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2855,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02207,EU_PSTW_202609_02207,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Finance Department's Economic-Fiscal Studies and Research Directorate,"Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.","Tax administrations process millions of declarations containing income, expenses, VAT data and other fiscal indicators, and manual review cannot efficiently identify all relevant patterns or inconsistencies. The operational problem is that traditional tax-control workflows depend on automatic checks, formal checks and deeper substantive review, but the increasing volume and complexity of tax data make it difficult for officials to identify discrepancies, risk indicators or broader fiscal patterns quickly.",Chatbot to revolutionise tax return analysis,"Finance Department's Economic-Fiscal Studies and Research Directorate; Central-Government; Executive and legislative organs, financial and fiscal affairs, external affairs.; Italy",UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Tax administrations process millions of declarations containing income, expenses, VAT data and other fiscal indicators, and manual review cannot efficiently identify all relevant patterns or inconsistencies. Italy’s Finance Department chatbot is intended as an internal AI assistant for analysing tax-return data and supporting more timely fiscal analysis. The operational problem is that traditional tax-control workflows depend on automatic checks, formal checks and deeper substantive review, but the increasing volume and complexity of tax data make it difficult for officials to identify discrepancies, risk indicators or broader fiscal patterns quickly. The reported system was developed with Sogei and the Department of Finance’s Economic-Fiscal Studies and Research Directorate, and is described as capable of analysing documents, aggregated data, expenses, income and VAT information. At a conceptual level, the chatbot functions less as a public-facing assistant and more as an analytical interface for officials: users can query large fiscal datasets, carry out calculations, compare information and surface anomalies that may require further human assessment. The expected impact is faster analysis of declarations, better targeting of potential irregularities, improved understanding of fiscal trends and more efficient allocation of official attention to complex cases. The case should be handled carefully because secondary sources describe the system’s capabilities, but I did not find a directly accessible official MEF technical page confirming all operational details. It should therefore be presented as an internal fiscal-analysis AI tool supported by media reporting, not as an autonomous enforcement decision-maker.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2856,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02208,EU_PSTW_202609_02208,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,German Government,General public services n.e.c.,"The operational problem was that the previous chatbot relied heavily on predefined questions and decision-tree logic, which limited its ability to understand varied wording or respond flexibly to citizens’ needs.",German government hotline 115: AI-based chatbot for 24/7 support,German Government; Central-Government; General public services n.e.c.; Germany,"The case is relevant because it illustrates a typical public-sector AI upgrade path: a rule-based assistant is introduced first, its limitations become visible in practice, and it is then replaced or enhanced by a more flexible AI-powered version.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Citizens often need simple, reliable answers about administrative procedures, but public-service information in Germany is distributed across federal, state and municipal responsibilities. The 115 service is intended to make access to administrative information easier, and its chatbot upgrade responds to the limitations of earlier rule-based digital assistance. The operational problem was that the previous chatbot relied heavily on predefined questions and decision-tree logic, which limited its ability to understand varied wording or respond flexibly to citizens’ needs. The new AI-based version is designed to interpret natural-language queries more effectively and provide more relevant guidance on public services, administrative procedures and contact points. At a conceptual level, the chatbot acts as a conversational access layer to the knowledge base behind the 115 public-service information system. Instead of requiring citizens to formulate their question in exactly the expected way, the system can process broader phrasing and guide users towards the appropriate service information. The expected impact is easier access to administrative guidance outside office hours, reduced frustration for users, better handling of routine questions and lower pressure on human support channels. The case is relevant because it illustrates a typical public-sector AI upgrade path: a rule-based assistant is introduced first, its limitations become visible in practice, and it is then replaced or enhanced by a more flexible AI-powered version. The main value lies in improving accessibility and usability, not in automating administrative decisions.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2857,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02209,EU_PSTW_202609_02209,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,French Government,General services.,"France’s sovereign AI partnership with Germany, Mistral AI and SAP SE is designed to address a strategic public-administration challenge: how to adopt AI at scale while preserving European control over data, infrastructure, governance and critical digital dependencies.",Sovereign AI for Public Administration in France,French Government; Central-Government; General services.; France,"For France, the initiative is framed as a strategic shift towards more efficient, resilient and sovereign public administration, enabling civil servants to use generative agents for drafting, scenario simulation, budget justification, compliance support and eligibility guidance.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"France’s sovereign AI partnership with Germany, Mistral AI and SAP SE is designed to address a strategic public-administration challenge: how to adopt AI at scale while preserving European control over data, infrastructure, governance and critical digital dependencies. The operational problem is that public administrations need AI tools for productivity, decision support, financial management, service guidance and compliance, but reliance on non-European technology stacks can create concerns around sovereignty, interoperability, security and long-term control. The planned partnership focuses on four pillars: an intelligent sovereign ERP platform tailored to French and German administrative requirements; AI-augmented financial management, including invoice classification and audit checks; digital agents for civil servants and citizens; and joint training and innovation capacity through AI-ERP labs. For France, the initiative is framed as a strategic shift towards more efficient, resilient and sovereign public administration, enabling civil servants to use generative agents for drafting, scenario simulation, budget justification, compliance support and eligibility guidance. At a conceptual level, the partnership aims to create a European-controlled AI stack that combines Mistral AI’s models with SAP’s enterprise systems and sovereign infrastructure principles. The expected impact is improved public-sector efficiency, stronger auditability and traceability of AI systems, better service responsiveness and reduced dependence on external technology providers",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2858,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02210,EU_PSTW_202609_02210,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,German Government,General services.,UNKNOWN,Sovereign AI for Public Administration in Germany,German Government; Central-Government; General services.; Germany,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Germany’s sovereign AI initiative with France, Mistral AI and SAP SE is intended to create a trusted European framework for applying AI in public administration while preserving digital sovereignty and institutional control. The operational problem is that German administrations, like their French counterparts, increasingly require AI-enabled enterprise systems and digital agents for financial workflows, compliance support, drafting, service guidance and internal productivity. However, deploying such systems in government requires interoperability with existing administrative architecture, secure handling of public data, explainability, auditability and long-term resilience. The announced partnership focuses on a sovereign ERP platform with embedded AI, AI-supported financial management, digital agents for public services and shared training and innovation capacity. In the German context, SAP’s role is particularly relevant because of its long-standing presence in enterprise software used by public and private organisations, while Mistral AI contributes European model capabilities. At a conceptual level, the initiative aims to place AI inside the administrative operating system rather than deploying isolated chatbots. It is designed to create tools that help civil servants classify invoices, check audits, draft documents, simulate scenarios, justify budgets and guide citizens through forms or eligibility checks. The expected impact is more efficient administration, stronger interoperability, lower dependence on non-European providers and better alignment with European sovereignty objectives",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2859,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02211,EU_PSTW_202609_02211,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""social_nonprofit"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Greek Government,Secondary education.,"OpenAI for Greece addresses this challenge through a partnership between OpenAI, the Government of the Hellenic Republic, the Onassis Foundation and Endeavor Greece, with an education component centred on ChatGPT Edu in upper-secondary schools.",OpenAI For Greece - ChatGPT Edu in Secondary Education,Greek Government; Central-Government; Secondary education.; Greece,"The Onassis Foundation will lead implementation with local partners, including The Tipping Point, while OpenAI will co-design teacher training, provide technical support and share best practices for safe classroom use. At a conceptual level, the initiative treats AI not merely as a student tool, but as a system-level capacity for teacher training, lesson support, responsible use and national skills development.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Education systems need to prepare teachers and students for AI while ensuring that adoption is structured, responsible and aligned with pedagogical goals. OpenAI for Greece addresses this challenge through a partnership between OpenAI, the Government of the Hellenic Republic, the Onassis Foundation and Endeavor Greece, with an education component centred on ChatGPT Edu in upper-secondary schools. The operational problem is that generative AI is already entering learning environments informally, but teachers need training, secure tools and clear guidance if they are to integrate AI constructively into classroom practice. Greece’s pilot will start with teachers from upper-secondary schools selected to reflect regional and socio-economic diversity. The first phase focuses on AI literacy, teacher productivity and responsible integration of AI into education. ChatGPT Edu is described by OpenAI as a version of ChatGPT built for academic institutions, offering access to OpenAI’s latest models with enterprise-grade security and controls that support GDPR compliance. The Onassis Foundation will lead implementation with local partners, including The Tipping Point, while OpenAI will co-design teacher training, provide technical support and share best practices for safe classroom use. At a conceptual level, the initiative treats AI not merely as a student tool, but as a system-level capacity for teacher training, lesson support, responsible use and national skills development. The expected impact is improved AI literacy, more confident teacher adoption, support for classroom innovation and a pathway to wider national rollout if the pilot demonstrates successful outcomes",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2860,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02212,EU_PSTW_202609_02212,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Greek Government,"General economic, commercial and labour affair","The programme responds to a structural innovation challenge: Greece has a strong base of STEM graduates and technical talent, but many highly skilled professionals pursue opportunities abroad, weakening the domestic pipeline for advanced digital entrepreneurship.",OpenAI For Greece - Greek AI Accelerator to support local AI‑focused start‑ups,"Greek Government; Central-Government; General economic, commercial and labour affair; Greece",UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Greece’s AI Accelerator is designed to strengthen the country’s start-up ecosystem by helping local founders build, test and scale AI-driven products in sectors aligned with national priorities. The programme responds to a structural innovation challenge: Greece has a strong base of STEM graduates and technical talent, but many highly skilled professionals pursue opportunities abroad, weakening the domestic pipeline for advanced digital entrepreneurship. The accelerator creates a pathway for AI-focused start-ups to access technical resources, international expertise and investor networks while remaining connected to the Greek innovation ecosystem. Selected companies receive access to OpenAI technology and credits, technical mentorship from OpenAI engineers and global experts, tailored workshops on scaling, compliance and safety-by-design, and international exposure, including links to leading investors and innovators. The programme gives particular attention to education, public services, healthcare and climate, where AI can generate both economic and public-interest value. At an operational level, the accelerator helps start-ups move from prototype development to scalable products by combining model access, technical advice, policy alignment and market visibility. At ecosystem level, it supports talent retention, creates stronger links between Greek founders and global AI networks, and contributes to the government’s wider objective of positioning Greece as a competitive hub for applied artificial intelligence.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2861,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02213,EU_PSTW_202609_02213,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Greek Ministry of Health,Hospital services.,"The Digital Doctor Assistant addresses this challenge by allowing healthcare professionals to interact with the patient record through natural-language queries, including spoken interaction. Through the MyHealthDoc system, doctors can request summaries of a patient’s medical history, ask which medicines were administered over a given period, retrieve personalised results and navigate clinical information without manually searching across the full record.",Digital Doctor Assistant,Greek Ministry of Health; Central-Government; Hospital services.; Greece,"Greece’s Digital Doctor Assistant introduces artificial intelligence into the national electronic health-record environment to help doctors access, interpret and use patient information more efficiently.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Greece’s Digital Doctor Assistant introduces artificial intelligence into the national electronic health-record environment to help doctors access, interpret and use patient information more efficiently. Public health systems generate large volumes of clinical data, including histories, medications, laboratory results, diagnostic information and previous treatments, but this information is not always easy to retrieve quickly during a consultation. The Digital Doctor Assistant addresses this challenge by allowing healthcare professionals to interact with the patient record through natural-language queries, including spoken interaction. Through the MyHealthDoc system, doctors can request summaries of a patient’s medical history, ask which medicines were administered over a given period, retrieve personalised results and navigate clinical information without manually searching across the full record. The tool is intended to reduce bureaucracy, shorten the time needed to access relevant information and support medical decision-making inside the National Health System. Its value lies in improving the clinical information workflow: doctors remain responsible for diagnosis and treatment, while the assistant helps surface relevant data faster and in a more usable form. The initiative also supports broader digital-health transformation by embedding AI directly into a national public-health platform rather than treating it as a separate experimental application.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2862,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02214,EU_PSTW_202609_02214,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Greek Ministry of Health,Hospital services.,"The initiative addresses a common public-health challenge: citizens often need guidance on where to seek care, which service to contact, or how to navigate the health system, while frontline teams face high volumes of routine and urgent enquiries.",Greek Government and Sword Health team up for AI-driven initiative,Greek Ministry of Health; Central-Government; Hospital services.; Greece,"Greece’s partnership with Sword Health uses AI to strengthen the national healthcare access pathway, starting from the National Health Information Line 1566.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Greece’s partnership with Sword Health uses AI to strengthen the national healthcare access pathway, starting from the National Health Information Line 1566. The initiative addresses a common public-health challenge: citizens often need guidance on where to seek care, which service to contact, or how to navigate the health system, while frontline teams face high volumes of routine and urgent enquiries. By integrating AI into triage and coordination processes, the programme aims to increase the capacity of the health information line, improve response times and provide more consistent support across the country. The AI layer is designed to work alongside healthcare professionals rather than replace them, absorbing repetitive and high-volume tasks, helping direct citizens towards appropriate services and giving health-system leaders better visibility over emerging needs. This is particularly relevant for a national system that must serve urban, rural and remote populations with different levels of access to healthcare resources. At operational level, the initiative creates an AI-enabled “front door” to healthcare: citizens can be guided more efficiently, hospitals can face lower avoidable pressure, and frontline staff can focus on cases requiring human judgement or clinical escalation. The wider ambition is to build a more responsive and resilient public-health access model that combines AI-based coordination with human care.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,F06_HIGH_VOLUME_SCALABILITY_BOTTLENECK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2863,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02215,EU_PSTW_202609_02215,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Administration of Częstochowa,General services.,UNKNOWN,City of Częstochowa will become the first in Poland to begin using the Polish Large Language Model (PLLuM),Administration of Częstochowa; Local Government; General services.; Poland,"Częstochowa’s pilot use of PLLuM, the Polish Large Language Model, addresses the practical question of how a national language model can support municipal administration while preserving linguistic quality and public-sector control. Under a pilot with Poland’s Ministry of Digital Affairs, Częstochowa became the first city to use PLLuM in local administration.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Local administrations process large volumes of routine text, including residents’ enquiries, official letters, reports, summaries and internal procedural materials. Częstochowa’s pilot use of PLLuM, the Polish Large Language Model, addresses the practical question of how a national language model can support municipal administration while preserving linguistic quality and public-sector control. The operational problem is that generic commercial AI models may not be optimised for Polish administrative language, legal terminology or public-sector workflows, while staff need safe tools that can help with repetitive drafting and analysis without replacing human judgement. Under a pilot with Poland’s Ministry of Digital Affairs, Częstochowa became the first city to use PLLuM in local administration. The model is intended to support officials in tasks such as drafting official correspondence, summarising long or complex documents, analysing residents’ requests and providing quick internal guidance. At a conceptual level, the system acts as a Polish-language productivity assistant adapted to public administration: it helps transform incoming or internal information into structured drafts and summaries, which staff can then review and approve. The expected impact is reduced processing time, improved consistency in written communication, faster analysis of citizen enquiries and better evidence on the practical value and limitations of national LLMs in government. The case is relevant because it tests generative AI in a real municipal setting while supporting a broader national objective: developing sovereign Polish-language AI capacity rather than relying only on foreign-language general-purpose tools.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2864,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02216,EU_PSTW_202609_02216,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Social Security Malta,General public services n.e.c.,"The Life Events Robot addresses this challenge through a bilingual conversational assistant that combines retrieval-augmented generation, curated public-service information and natural-language interaction.",Bilingual AI Chatbot and Mobile Robot,Social Security Malta; Central-Government; General public services n.e.c.; Malta,"It also supports speech-to-text and text-to-speech functions, enabling voice-based interaction and improving accessibility for users who may find written digital services difficult to use.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Malta’s Life Events Robot provides AI-supported assistance for citizens dealing with major civil life events, including marriage, birth, divorce and other registration-related procedures. These services generate high volumes of recurring questions, and citizens often need clear guidance in both Maltese and English. The Life Events Robot addresses this challenge through a bilingual conversational assistant that combines retrieval-augmented generation, curated public-service information and natural-language interaction. The system retrieves relevant administrative and legal content from authoritative life-event datasets and presents it in a conversational format that is easier for users to understand than navigating multiple web pages or contacting a helpdesk. It also supports speech-to-text and text-to-speech functions, enabling voice-based interaction and improving accessibility for users who may find written digital services difficult to use. The mobile-robot component extends the same assistance into physical service environments, allowing citizens to receive on-site guidance through a Temi V3 robot in government service centres. At operational level, the solution reduces repetitive demand on staff, improves consistency of answers, supports 24/7 availability and creates a reusable platform that can later be extended to other public-service domains. Its bilingual design is particularly important in Malta’s administrative context, where digital public services must remain accessible in both national service languages.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI|S3_DOCUMENT_INTELLIGENCE,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2865,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02217,EU_PSTW_202609_02217,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Malta Information Technology Agency (MITA),General services.,"The initiative responds to a common administrative challenge: public officers spend significant time drafting documents, summarising information, preparing communications, managing email and performing repetitive knowledge-work tasks that can be accelerated with AI support.",Introduction of Microsoft 365 Copilot within the public sector,Malta Information Technology Agency (MITA); Central-Government; General services.; Malta,"Through a EUR 4 million investment, Microsoft 365 Copilot is being made available to more than 8,000 public officers over a two-year period, with direct deployment on employees’ laptops and structured training to support effective use. A distinctive feature is the integration of Maltese-language support, enabling public officers to use Copilot across core Microsoft applications in Maltese as well as English.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Malta’s public-sector rollout of Microsoft 365 Copilot introduces generative-AI productivity support across the administration to reduce routine workload and modernise daily work practices. The initiative responds to a common administrative challenge: public officers spend significant time drafting documents, summarising information, preparing communications, managing email and performing repetitive knowledge-work tasks that can be accelerated with AI support. Through a EUR 4 million investment, Microsoft 365 Copilot is being made available to more than 8,000 public officers over a two-year period, with direct deployment on employees’ laptops and structured training to support effective use. The initiative also includes a Centre of Excellence operated through collaboration between Microsoft, the Government and MITA, providing a hub for guidance, best practice, governance and innovation. A distinctive feature is the integration of Maltese-language support, enabling public officers to use Copilot across core Microsoft applications in Maltese as well as English. This improves accessibility, reinforces the use of Maltese in digital administration and supports language inclusion within the public-sector workforce. At operational level, Copilot assists with drafting, summarisation, document preparation and routine administrative work, while employees remain responsible for reviewing and validating outputs. The initiative is part of Malta’s broader digital-transformation agenda and is positioned as a workforce-empowerment measure rather than a replacement for public officials.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2866,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02218,EU_PSTW_202609_02218,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Ministry of Justice, Paris Public Prosecutor's Office",Law courts,"Users can also interrogate the procedure directly, making it easier to search through large files without manually reading every page in sequence.",Mon parquet assistant - My assistant prosecutor,"Ministry of Justice, Paris Public Prosecutor's Office; Central-Government; Law courts; France",UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Mon parquet assistant supports criminal-case processing in the courts under the Paris Court of Appeal by helping magistrates review, search and analyse procedural files more efficiently. Criminal files can be lengthy, heterogeneous and administratively demanding, particularly in high-volume proceedings where magistrates need to identify the nature of offences, locate relevant information and understand the structure of a case quickly. The tool is designed to allow users to upload one or more procedures, whether natively digital or scanned, into a dedicated interface. It then supports the detection of the offence types under investigation, offers a rapid view of the case by answering predefined questions linked to the relevant offence category, and enables deeper review by displaying source passages in a PDF reader and highlighting relevant sections. Users can also interrogate the procedure directly, making it easier to search through large files without manually reading every page in sequence. At operational level, the tool reduces repetitive, low-value document-handling tasks and gives magistrates faster access to the information needed for procedural analysis. It supports both overview and detailed review, while preserving the magistrate’s responsibility for legal assessment and prosecutorial decision-making. The initiative forms part of the Justice incubator approach to building practical digital tools around real professional workflows in the justice system.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2867,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02219,EU_PSTW_202609_02219,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,SNCF,Transport,UNKNOWN,SNCF’s AI Maintenance Scheduler Reduces Train Downtime by 45%,SNCF; Central-Government; Transport; France,"Rail operators need to reduce unexpected failures, improve punctuality and use maintenance resources efficiently across large fleets and infrastructure assets. SNCF’s predictive-maintenance work addresses this challenge by using data and AI to detect technical problems before they become operational disruptions.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Rail operators need to reduce unexpected failures, improve punctuality and use maintenance resources efficiently across large fleets and infrastructure assets. SNCF’s predictive-maintenance work addresses this challenge by using data and AI to detect technical problems before they become operational disruptions. The operational problem is that time-based maintenance can lead to two opposite inefficiencies: some components may be serviced too early, wasting parts and labour, while emerging faults may still be missed if they develop between scheduled inspections. SNCF’s official predictive-maintenance material describes the use of sensors and 4G/5G connectivity to collect train data and assess equipment in real time. The organisation reports being able to analyse thousands of train variables, including variables collected in real time, across more than 1,100 trains simultaneously. At a conceptual level, predictive maintenance converts rolling stock from a passive asset into a data-generating system: sensor streams, historical failure patterns and operating conditions are analysed to identify risks, prioritise interventions and plan maintenance before faults affect passengers or freight. The expected impact is improved reliability, fewer delays, better use of maintenance staff and parts, and reduced service disruption. The specific claim in the user-provided source that downtime was reduced by 45% should be treated cautiously because I found stronger official evidence for SNCF’s predictive-maintenance capability, but not an official confirmation of that precise figure in the available sources. The catalogue entry should therefore emphasise SNCF’s implemented predictive-maintenance programme without over-relying on unverified performance metrics.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S4_PREDICTION_DETECTION,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2868,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02220,EU_PSTW_202609_02220,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,"Ministry of Research, Innovation and Digital Policy",General services.,"The initiative also includes data-security considerations and is connected to a wider EUR 5 million “AI in Government” programme, which seeks to encourage AI-driven solutions for state challenges such as labour-market matching and climate response.",Microsoft Copilot in Cyprus' Public Administration,"Ministry of Research, Innovation and Digital Policy; Central-Government; General services.; Cyprus","Copilot provides an AI assistant integrated into the Microsoft 365 environment, enabling civil servants to draft reports, manage routine tasks, analyse large information sets and automate simple actions while remaining within the productivity tools already used by the administration. The first phase provides 350 user licences and dedicated training, ensuring that public servants can use the tool safely and effectively.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Cyprus is introducing Microsoft 365 Copilot across public administration to accelerate routine work, improve productivity and support the wider digital modernisation of government services. Public officials manage large volumes of written material, administrative data, reports and internal processes, much of which requires drafting, summarisation, analysis and coordination. Copilot provides an AI assistant integrated into the Microsoft 365 environment, enabling civil servants to draft reports, manage routine tasks, analyse large information sets and automate simple actions while remaining within the productivity tools already used by the administration. The first phase provides 350 user licences and dedicated training, ensuring that public servants can use the tool safely and effectively. The initiative also includes data-security considerations and is connected to a wider EUR 5 million “AI in Government” programme, which seeks to encourage AI-driven solutions for state challenges such as labour-market matching and climate response. At operational level, the rollout helps public officials complete repetitive knowledge-work tasks more quickly, freeing staff capacity for higher-value work and direct service improvement. At strategic level, it creates an initial public-sector user base for generative AI and helps the administration develop the skills, governance practices and organisational experience needed for broader AI integration across government.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2869,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02221,EU_PSTW_202609_02221,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Norwegian University of Science and Technology,Outpatient services.,"Hypertension is a major public-health challenge because it contributes to heart failure, kidney disease, stroke, dementia and substantial healthcare costs, while ageing populations increase the demand for personalised and preventive care. MyMDT addresses this challenge by creating a “living” digital model of the patient that can evolve over time as new data become available.",MyMDT - My Medical Digital Twin,Norwegian University of Science and Technology; Academic-Research; Outpatient services.; Norway,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"MyMDT develops a personalised medical digital twin for hypertension, combining physiological modelling, machine learning, statistical methods, biosensor data and population-based research to support patient-specific prevention and treatment. Hypertension is a major public-health challenge because it contributes to heart failure, kidney disease, stroke, dementia and substantial healthcare costs, while ageing populations increase the demand for personalised and preventive care. MyMDT addresses this challenge by creating a “living” digital model of the patient that can evolve over time as new data become available. The platform integrates physiological simulations of cardiovascular function with machine-learning methods and data streams from sensors or wearable devices. It also draws on exercise physiology, cardiovascular modelling, biobanks and population studies, including NTNU’s research expertise in physical activity, arterial compliance and hypertension risk. At operational level, MyMDT is designed to support diagnostic, prognostic and treatment decisions by modelling how individual physiology and lifestyle factors interact. This can help identify prevention schemes, guide targeted data collection and support more personalised management of primary and secondary hypertension. At research level, the initiative brings together multiple disciplines—medicine, biomechanics, machine learning, statistics, biosensors and population health—to create a clinically meaningful digital-twin platform. The expected impact is better prevention, more personalised intervention, improved health outcomes and reduced healthcare costs for a common chronic condition.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2870,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02222,EU_PSTW_202609_02222,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Barcelona Supercomputing Center,R&D environmental protection.,"Urban pollution affects exposure, respiratory health, quality of life and environmental justice, but policymakers often lack tools that connect environmental monitoring, mobility behaviour, health indicators and long-term policy impacts.",HARMONIE project (Health Assessment Refinement for Mitigating Noise and Air Quality Effects),Barcelona Supercomputing Center; Academic-Research; R&D environmental protection.; Spain,HARMONIE uses digital twin technologies to help cities understand and reduce the health impacts of air and noise pollution.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"HARMONIE uses digital twin technologies to help cities understand and reduce the health impacts of air and noise pollution. Urban pollution affects exposure, respiratory health, quality of life and environmental justice, but policymakers often lack tools that connect environmental monitoring, mobility behaviour, health indicators and long-term policy impacts. HARMONIE addresses this by combining real-world pilot studies with advanced digital simulations across five pilot cities: Barcelona, Lausanne, Sarajevo, Gävle and Barakaldo. The project collects data on air and noise pollution, including emerging indicators such as oxidative potential, and links them with citizen mobility patterns, exposure levels and health indicators, with attention to vulnerable groups. These data feed two complementary digital twins: a Lung Digital Twin, which models the interaction between pollutants and the human respiratory system, and a Citizen-Centric Digital Twin, which models exposure patterns and policy impacts at urban scale. The initiative gives policymakers a safer analytical environment for testing interventions before implementing them in the real city. Measures such as traffic restrictions, green infrastructure, behavioural interventions and urban redesign can be assessed for their environmental and health effects. HARMONIE’s added value lies in connecting environmental science, public health and digital modelling into a practical decision-support framework for cleaner, healthier and more equitable cities.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2871,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02223,EU_PSTW_202609_02223,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Barcelona Supercomputing Center,R&D health.,UNKNOWN,HARMONIE project: Lung Digital Twin,Barcelona Supercomputing Center; Academic-Research; R&D health.; Spain,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Lung Digital Twin within HARMONIE focuses on the physiological effects of air pollution by modelling how inhaled pollutants move through and affect the human respiratory system. Air-quality policy often relies on ambient pollution measurements, but individual health risk depends on how pollutants enter the airways, where particles are deposited, how susceptible a person is and how exposure contributes to respiratory disease over time. The Lung Digital Twin addresses this gap by creating high-resolution simulations based on patient-specific geometries and detailed models of inhaled pollutants. It simulates airflow, particle transport and deposition inside the airways, helping researchers assess inhaled dose, susceptibility and health-impact pathways linked to oxidative stress. At operational level, the model connects environmental exposure data with physiological mechanisms, allowing health authorities and researchers to move beyond general pollution maps towards more precise understanding of respiratory risk. The Lung Digital Twin can support the assessment of how different pollutants affect vulnerable populations and how exposure-reduction policies may translate into health benefits. It also complements the urban-scale Citizen-Centric Digital Twin by adding a direct human-health layer to city-level environmental modelling. The expected impact is improved evidence on pollution-related respiratory risks, stronger prevention strategies, better-informed urban policy and more targeted protection for citizens most exposed or most biologically vulnerable to air pollution.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2872,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02224,EU_PSTW_202609_02224,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Barcelona Supercomputing Center,R&D environmental protection.,"The Citizen-Centric Digital Twin integrates pollution mapping, mobility information, social dynamics and exposure modelling to show how environmental burdens are distributed across urban populations.",HARMONIE project: Citizen-centric Digital Twin,Barcelona Supercomputing Center; Academic-Research; R&D environmental protection.; Spain,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"The Citizen-Centric Digital Twin within HARMONIE models how people experience air and noise pollution across urban environments, linking environmental conditions with mobility patterns, social behaviour and health exposure. Cities need to understand not only where pollution is generated, but also where and how citizens encounter it during daily life. A street with high emissions may affect residents, commuters, pedestrians, schoolchildren or vulnerable groups differently depending on mobility routines, time of day, housing conditions and access to green or quieter spaces. The Citizen-Centric Digital Twin integrates pollution mapping, mobility information, social dynamics and exposure modelling to show how environmental burdens are distributed across urban populations. It enables “what-if” simulations in which policymakers can test measures such as traffic restrictions, green infrastructure, zoning changes or behavioural interventions before implementing them. At operational level, this allows local authorities to compare policy alternatives according to both environmental effectiveness and equity of health benefits. The twin also supports citizen engagement by making individual and collective contributions to pollution reduction more visible and understandable, including through personalised information and behavioural feedback mechanisms. The expected impact is more adaptive, community-driven and evidence-based urban policy, helping cities reduce exposure to harmful pollutants while improving liveability and fairness. The Citizen-Centric Digital Twin gives urban planners a practical way to connect environmental data with real human experience.",NON_AI_OR_UNCONFIRMED_TECHNOLOGY,PILOT,시험·파일럿,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2873,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02225,EU_PSTW_202609_02225,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,French Ministry of Culture,General public services n.e.c.,"Compar:IA addresses this challenge by creating a public chatbot arena where users can compare two anonymised AI models, judge their answers and contribute to open preference datasets.",Compar:IA - the chatbot arena,French Ministry of Culture; Central-Government; General public services n.e.c.; France,UNKNOWN,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Generative AI models are increasingly used by citizens, professionals and public administrations, but many users rely on single systems without understanding differences in quality, bias, environmental footprint, language performance or cultural alignment. Compar:IA addresses this challenge by creating a public chatbot arena where users can compare two anonymised AI models, judge their answers and contribute to open preference datasets. The operational problem is that high-quality human preference data are scarce for French and other European languages, while AI literacy among citizens also needs to improve as generative AI becomes more common. The platform allows users to ask questions, interact with two anonymous models, vote on the preferred answer and later learn more about the models’ characteristics. These interactions generate anonymised preference data that can be published as open datasets and used to improve future models, especially for French and other European languages. At a conceptual level, Compar:IA combines public education, model evaluation and data collection in one interface: citizens learn not to trust one AI answer uncritically, while their feedback contributes to a shared resource for language-model improvement. The platform also highlights the environmental footprint of AI queries, adding an awareness dimension that goes beyond output quality. The expected impact is stronger AI literacy, better understanding of model differences, more open language-specific preference datasets and support for European digital sovereignty. The case is significant because it treats the public not only as AI users but also as contributors to model evaluation and linguistic diversity.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2874,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02226,EU_PSTW_202609_02226,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Virgin Media O2,Public order and safety n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,"Daisy - an AI ""granny"" that will waste scammers' time",Virgin Media O2; Private sector; Public order and safety n.e.c.; United Kingdom,"Daisy is an AI-powered anti-fraud tool developed by Virgin Media O2 to disrupt phone scammers by keeping them engaged in long, unproductive conversations. Phone fraud often targets vulnerable people, particularly older citizens, by using social engineering, urgency and deception to extract money or personal information.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Daisy is an AI-powered anti-fraud tool developed by Virgin Media O2 to disrupt phone scammers by keeping them engaged in long, unproductive conversations. Phone fraud often targets vulnerable people, particularly older citizens, by using social engineering, urgency and deception to extract money or personal information. Daisy reverses this dynamic by acting as a convincing potential victim who can absorb scammers’ time without exposing a real person to harm. The system combines several AI models that listen to callers and respond in real time, imitating the voice and conversational style of an elderly person. Daisy tells rambling stories, discusses family, knitting and everyday topics, and provides false or confusing information to keep fraudsters on the line. During testing, the AI was able to keep scammers engaged for up to 40 minutes, reducing the time they could spend contacting genuine victims. At operational level, Daisy functions as a scam-baiting and awareness tool rather than a conventional customer-service chatbot. Its role is not to investigate or prosecute fraud directly, but to waste criminal resources, expose scammer tactics and demonstrate how AI can be used defensively in consumer protection. The initiative also raises public awareness of fraud techniques, especially the manipulation methods used against older or vulnerable people. Its broader value lies in showing how conversational AI can be deployed creatively to protect citizens from social-engineering threats.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2875,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02227,EU_PSTW_202609_02227,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,Greek Ministry of Tourism,General public services n.e.c.,UNKNOWN,mAIGreece - the digital AI assistant for visitors,Greek Ministry of Tourism; Central-Government; General public services n.e.c.; Greece,"mAiGreece addresses this need by providing an AI-powered digital assistant for visitors to Greece, created by the Ministry of Tourism and the Ministry of Digital Governance.",UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Tourists often need fast and reliable information during travel, particularly on health services, emergency support, consular offices, police stations, museums, beaches and official visitor guidance. mAiGreece addresses this need by providing an AI-powered digital assistant for visitors to Greece, created by the Ministry of Tourism and the Ministry of Digital Governance. The operational problem is that travellers may not know which official source to consult, may face language barriers, or may need urgent information while moving through unfamiliar locations. The platform brings together information on hospitals, health centres, embassies and consulates, police stations, Blue Flag beaches, museums, content from Visit Greece and frequently asked questions. It also includes an important safety function: users can send an SMS with their geographical location to the Ministry of Civil Protection’s operations centre through the 112 emergency number, supporting rapid assistance where necessary. At a conceptual level, mAiGreece acts as a multilingual travel companion and official public-service gateway, combining AI-driven question handling with location-aware safety support. The system supports written or spoken natural-language questions and offers information in 31 languages, improving accessibility for a diverse international visitor base. The expected impact is a better visitor experience, easier access to official information, stronger tourist safety and improved coordination between tourism promotion and public-service guidance. The case is relevant because it shows AI being used not only for marketing, but also for practical visitor support and emergency-related information in a national tourism context.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,,,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2876,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02228,EU_PSTW_202609_02228,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work"", ""education_research"", ""enterprise_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,GesetzeFinden.at,General public services n.e.c.,"Legal language is highly specialised, context-dependent and difficult for non-experts to understand, while legal professionals also spend considerable time searching, interpreting and structuring complex information.",AmigaAI - Chatbot for the legal sector,GesetzeFinden.at; Private sector; General public services n.e.c.; Austria,This design aims to improve output quality while allowing the use of smaller and more resource-efficient models.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"AmigaAI is an Austrian legal-AI initiative designed to improve access to legal information and increase the efficiency of legal workflows through a domain-specific large language model. Legal language is highly specialised, context-dependent and difficult for non-experts to understand, while legal professionals also spend considerable time searching, interpreting and structuring complex information. General-purpose AI models are not always suitable for this environment because they may fail to capture the nuances, terminology and legal context of Austrian law. AmigaAI addresses this by developing a legal-specific language model capable of understanding and interpreting Austrian legal language more accurately. The initiative is led by the Vienna-based legal-tech company GesetzeFinden.at and brings together an interdisciplinary consortium including the Psychological Faculty of Sigmund Freud Private University, CERHA HEMPEL and Fraunhofer Austria. The project combines legal expertise, psychological research on human-machine interaction and technology acceptance, and applied technical development. A distinctive feature is the “Society of Minds” approach, in which several AI chatbots communicate internally and collaborate on the answer before presenting a result to the user. This design aims to improve output quality while allowing the use of smaller and more resource-efficient models. At operational level, AmigaAI is intended to support both professionals and the wider public by making legal information easier to retrieve, understand and apply, while maintaining reliability, usability and alignment with European AI governance expectations.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,IN_DEVELOPMENT,개발 중,,S2_AGENT_CONVERSATIONAL_AI,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2877,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
AXALL_EU_PSTW_202609_02229,EU_PSTW_202609_02229,SOURCE_REPORTED_USE_CASE,SOURCE_REPORTED_PROJECT_OR_USE_CASE,European Commission Joint Research Centre,public_work,공공기관·공공서비스,"[""public_work""]",PUBLIC_AGENCY,La Justice - Luxembourg,Law courts,"JUANO addresses this challenge by supporting the anonymisation of court decisions before they are made available on Luxembourg’s justice internet portal. Manual anonymisation can be slow, repetitive and vulnerable to inconsistency, particularly when large volumes of decisions need to be processed.",JUANO - app to anonymise court decisions for publication on the justice internet portal,La Justice - Luxembourg; Central-Government; Law courts; Luxembourg,JUANO uses an artificial-intelligence engine to recognise entities that should be anonymised and to suggest categories based on the content of the text.,UNKNOWN; 원천의 기대효과와 별도 관측 성과는 구분해야 합니다.,UNKNOWN,"Publishing court decisions improves transparency, legal certainty and access to justice, but it also requires careful protection of personal data. JUANO addresses this challenge by supporting the anonymisation of court decisions before they are made available on Luxembourg’s justice internet portal. The operational problem is that court judgments often contain names, addresses, personal identifiers and other sensitive details that must be removed or generalised consistently before publication. Manual anonymisation can be slow, repetitive and vulnerable to inconsistency, particularly when large volumes of decisions need to be processed. JUANO uses an artificial-intelligence engine to recognise entities that should be anonymised and to suggest categories based on the content of the text. At a conceptual level, the application functions as decision support for legal publishing: it identifies potentially sensitive elements, proposes how they should be treated and facilitates a more standardised anonymisation workflow. The expected impact is faster preparation of judgments for publication, more consistent data-protection practice, better access to case law for lawyers and interested parties, and stronger compliance with privacy requirements. The system should not be framed as replacing legal or judicial responsibility, because anonymisation in legal texts still requires careful validation and contextual judgement. Its value lies in reducing the burden of repetitive entity recognition and improving consistency across decisions. The case is relevant to digital justice because anonymisation tools are a practical prerequisite for expanding access to case law while respecting fundamental rights and data-protection obligations.",SOURCE_CLASSIFIED_AI,DEPLOYED,원천 보고: 운영,F01_REPETITIVE_MANUAL_WORK|F06_HIGH_VOLUME_SCALABILITY_BOTTLENECK,S5_OPTIMIZATION_DECISION_SUPPORT,https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/public-sector-tech-watch/pstw-cases-viewer-data-download,PSTW-2878,CC BY 4.0,SOURCE_REPORTED_NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED,STRUCTURED_SOURCE_ROW_CHECKED,True,2.5.0-inclusive,False
